1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 {Mickey claims he was inspired to try this line based on Gawain's successes with it and the coverage in his book - that move order runs 3.Qe2 but we soon transpose.} cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Qd2 {An interesting position which was tested by Kuzubov in 3 games.} Qxb2 {After this Black is left in the type of passive position against which Adams in normally lethal.} 9. Nb3 (9. Rb1) 9... Nc6 (9... Qa3 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Be2 Nc6 12. O-O h5 13. Nb1 Qb4 14. Qe3 d5 15. exd5 Ne7 16. Nc3 Nf5 17. Qd3 Qb6+ 18. Rf2 Bd6 19. Ne4 Bxf4 20. Nxf6+ Kf8 21. Qc3 Bxh2+ 22. Kxh2 Qxf2 23. Nd7+ Kg8 24. Nf6+) 10. Bxf6 (10. Bd3 d5 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Na4 Qa3 13. Nb6 d4 14. O-O Rb8 15. Qe2 Qb4 16. Nc4 b5 17. a3 Qe7 18. Ncd2 h5 19. Rf3) 10... gxf6 11. Na4 Qa3 12. Nb6 Rb8 13. Nc4 {Black is faced with a difficult choice of where to move the knight.} Qa4 (13... Qb4 14. c3) 14. a3 (14. Kf2 Be7 15. a3 b5 16. Nxd6+ Kf8) (14. O-O-O Bd7 15. Kb1 d5 16. c3 Ne5 17. fxe5 dxc4) (14. Be2 b5 (14... d5 15. exd5 exd5 16. Nb6 Qb4 17. Nxd5 Qxd2+ 18. Kxd2 Bg7 19. Ke3) 15. Nxd6+ Bxd6 16. Qxd6 Qb4+ 17. Qxb4 Nxb4) 14... b5 15. Nxd6+ {Provoking ...e5 which, as noted by Adams, weakens the light squares (particularly d5) and blocks the f6-bishop.} Bxd6 16. Qxd6 Qxe4+ 17. Be2 Qd5 (17... Qxg2 18. O-O-O Bb7 (18... Qxe2 19. Qxc6+ Kf8 20. Qd6+) 19. Qd7+ Kf8 20. Qd6+ Ke8 21. Bh5) 18. Qxd5 {Adams had already seen the possibility of Bb6 (preventing ...Bd8 since e5 is hanging prepares Rac1).} exd5 19. O-O-O Ne7 (19... Be6 20. Bf3) 20. Bf3 Be6 (20... O-O 21. Rhe1 Re8 22. Bxd5 (22. Rxd5 Bb7 23. Rd3 Bxf3 24. Rxf3 Nd5 25. Rd1 Nb6) 22... Bg4 23. Rd3 Nxd5 24. Rxe8+ Rxe8 25. Rxd5 Re1+ (25... Be6 26. Rd6 Bxb3 27. cxb3 Re2 (27... Re6 28. Rxe6 fxe6 29. g4 Kf7 30. Kd2 Kg6 31. Ke3) 28. Rxa6 Rxg2 29. Ra5) 26. Kb2 Re2 27. Rd2 (27. h3 Be6 28. Rd8+ Kg7 29. g4 Bxb3 30. Kxb3 Re4) 27... Rxd2 28. Nxd2 Kg7 29. Nf1 Kg6 30. Ne3) 21. Rhe1 O-O (21... Rg8 22. Nd4 Rb6 23. f5 Bc8 24. Rd3 Kd8 25. g4 Bb7 26. Kd2) 22. g4 (22. Nc5 Rb6 23. Nd7 Bxd7 {with perhaps an edge for White, but Black was very solid in Y.Kuzubov (2627) - M.Dzhumaev (2465), Tashkent 2011 (1-0 in 62).} {Adams noted that} 24. Rxe7 {was an alternative. White has scored poorly after} Be6 25. Bxd5 (25. g4 f5 26. gxf5 Bxf5 27. Bxd5 Be6) 25... Bxd5 26. Rxd5 Rc8 (26... Rc6)) (22. Nd4 Rb6 23. g4 f5 24. gxf5 Nxf5 25. Nxf5 Bxf5 26. Rxd5 Be6) 22... f5 23. gxf5 Nxf5 {Adams didn't like this (d7 possibilities later) but didn't suggest alternatives.} 24. Bxd5 (24. Nc5 Rbe8 25. Re5 {and Black had no complaints in Y.Kuzubov (2615) - H. Gretarsson (2460), Reykjavik 2012 (1/2-1/2 in 58).} (25. Nxa6 Ra8 26. Nc7 Rxa3 27. Bxd5 {also puts the brakes on d4.} Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Ra1+ 29. Kd2 Rxe1 30. Kxe1 Ne3) {Black is fine after} 25... Nh4 26. Bxd5 Bxd5 (26... Ng6 27. Nxe6) 27. Rexd5) 24... Rbe8 (24... Rb6 25. Nc5 Bc8 {looked okay for Black in Y.Kuzubov, Y (2627) - K.Miton (2600), Reykjavik 2011 (1-0 in 37).} (25... Bxd5 26. Rxd5 {is "certainly a good Benoni" according to Adams, though he does not sound sure that it's enough for an advantage.} Ng7 $5 {is a dynamic attempt to prevent d4.} 27. f5) 26. Bb3) 25. Bxe6 (25. Bb7 Bc8 {(intending ...Bc6)} (25... Rb8 26. Nc5 {"starts to get a bit random". Continuing a bit further,}) (25... Bxb3 26. cxb3 Rxe1 27. Rxe1 Nd6 {still looks nice for White.}) 26. Bxc8 Rxc8 27. Re5 Nh4) (25. Nc5 Bxd5 {is excellent for White.} {After} 26. Rxe8 {Adams planned} Rxe8 {intending c4} 27. Rxd5 Ne3 (27... Re1+ 28. Kd2 $1 {is a cute tactic pointed out by Mickey} Rd1+ {was Adams' home analysis.} 29. Kxd1 Ne3+ 30. Kd2 {with a reversed Benoni.} Nxd5 31. Nxa6 f5 32. Nc5 Nxf4 33. Nb7) 28. Rg5+ Kf8 29. Nxa6 Nc4) 25... Rxe6 26. Rxe6 (26. Nc5 Rc6 27. Re5 Rfc8 28. Rdd5 {wins (Adams)} Ng7 {was "maybe the best chance" according to Adams, since}) 26... fxe6 27. Nc5 Ne3 $8 28. Nxe6 (28. Re1 Nc4) 28... Rc8 (28... Re8 {and Black's kingside is shattered.} 29. Rd6) (28... Rf6 29. Rd8+ Kf7 30. Ng5+ Kg6 (30... Ke7 $5 31. Rd4) 31. Rd4 Nf5 (31... Nc4 $5 32. a4 $5) 32. Rd7 h6 33. Ne4 {leaves White with several active possibilities,}) 29. Rg1+ {Now Polgar is playing for tricks.} (29. Rd8+ Rxd8 30. Nxd8 Nc4) 29... Kf7 (29... Kh8 {where, despite the two bishops, Black has insufficient compensation for the exchange.} 30. Kd2) 30. Ng5+ Kf6 (30... Kg6 31. Ne4+) 31. Ne4+ (31. Nxh7+ Kf5 32. Kd2 Kxf4) (31. Kd2 Nc4+ 32. Kd3 Kf5) 31... Ke6 32. Rg5 (32. Kd2 {.} Nc4+ $4 {allows} 33. Kd3 {with a draw, as noted by Adams.} Nxa3) (32. c3 Rc4 33. Ng5+ {(Adams)} Kf5 {(Adams) "looks dubious but I didn't completely refute it". Let's try:}) 32... Rxc2+ 33. Kb1 Rc6 ({Adams was going to meet} 33... Re2 {with the simple}) 34. Re5+ Kd7 35. Nc5+ Kd6 36. Nb7+ {An excellent win by Adams, in a line which has caused some problems for Black. The alternatives at move 10 certainly appear preferable to Polgar's treatment.} 1-0

