1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. d4 dxc4 7. Qc2 a6 {*} 8. a4 Bd7 9. Qxc4 (9. Rd1 Bc6 {[%cal Rd2a5] a5} 10. Nc3 Bxf3 $11 11. Bxf3 $6 Nc6 $36 12. Bxc6 bxc6 $11) 9... Bc6 10. Bg5 Bd5 11. Qd3 $146 {A novelty in this concrete position, although White has already earlier retreated with his  to d2 instead of h4 in this line.} Be4 (11... c5 $15) 12. Qe3 Nbd7 (12... Bc6 13. Nc3 Nbd7) 13. Nc3 Bc6 14. Qd3 (14. a5 $11) 14... Rb8 $1 {Otherwise Black's  would have to remain in the .} (14... Bb4 15. Rfe1 (15. Rfd1 $14 {More circumspect was} h6 $11 16. Bf4 $5 $132 {deserved attention.} a5 17. Nb5 Be4 18. Qc4 c6 19. Nc3 Bc2 $11 {The weakness of the d6 is illusory, as it can be sufficiently protected.} 20. Rdc1 Nb6 21. Qa2 Bh7 22. Qb3) 15... h6 $5 $15 (15... Bxf3 16. Qxf3 c6 17. Red1 Qa5 18. h4 {-26.fe5 fe5} h6 $2 19. Bd2 $1 $17 e5) 16. Bf4 Rc8 17. Qc2 b6 18. e4 Bb7 19. Rad1 Re8 20. Ne5 Nxe5 21. Bxe5 (21. dxe5 Nd7 22. Re3 Qe7 23. Na2 Bc5 24. Red3 $17 Nb8 $142 $1) 21... Nd7 22. Bf4 $15 Qe7 23. Re2 $36 c5 24. d5 (24. Red2 cxd4 25. Rxd4 Nf6 26. Bd6 Bxd6 $5 27. Rxd6 b5 28. axb5 axb5 $132 29. Qb3 Bc6 30. Qb4 Qb7 $11 31. h3 Rb8 32. Kh2) 24... e5 25. Be3 Qd6 26. Bh3 Rc7 27. Nb1 $17 Bc8 28. Nd2 $5 b5 29. Nf3 c4 30. Nh4 g6 31. Bxh6 Nc5 32. Bxc8 Rexc8 33. axb5 axb5 34. Nf3 $15) 15. Qc2 (15. e4 Nc5) 15... b5 (15... h6 16. Bxf6 Nxf6 17. e4 Bb4 $15 18. Rfe1) 16. axb5 axb5 $8 17. Ne5 (17. Rac1) 17... Bxg2 {'=' Stohl: '='} 18. Kxg2 Nxe5 $11 {Simplification has lead to approximately equal play, but Akopian after a 1-st round loss with White shows more ambitions than his position can finally stand.} 19. dxe5 Nd5 (19... Nd7 $17 20. Bxe7 {-29.gf4} Qxe7 21. f4) 20. Bxe7 Qxe7 $5 21. Ne4 b4 22. Rfc1 b3 23. Qc5 Qd7 {Polgar wants to prepare any central or flank activity first, but once again} (23... Qxc5 24. Nxc5) 24. Qc6 $6 {Tempting, as it stops d5. Now however White's  are biting into granite and the c-file opens for Black.} Qe7 (24... Qxc6 25. Rxc6) 25. h4 h6 $1 $36 {This important move prevents b4.} 26. Ra7 Qb4 {[%cal Gc7c1] c} 27. Qc4 (27. Rc4 $5 $17 Qe1) 27... Rfd8 $1 (27... Qe7 {-32.f1} 28. Nc3) (27... Qb6 28. Qc5) 28. Qxb4 Rxb4 29. Nc5 Rd4 30. Nd3 $6 (30. Nxb3 Rb4 31. Nc5 Rxb2 32. e4 {Stohl: '-32.f1'} Ne3+ $17 33. Kf3 Nd1) 30... Rb8 $1 $17 {Now the doubled extra pawn is very important. White's back rank is weak, and taking the f3 will cost White 2 other pawns.} (30... h5) (30... g6 $17) 31. h5 g6 (31... Kh7 $142 32. Rca1) 32. hxg6 fxg6 33. Rc6 Rb6 (33... Kf7 34. Nc5 $17) 34. Raxc7 Rxc6 (34... Nxc7 35. Rxb6) 35. Rxc6 Kf7 36. Rd6 {Akopian still can't resign himself to the inevitable and unpleasant 4 a central pawn down.} g5 (36... Ke7 37. Nc5 $17) 37. Rd7+ $2 Kg6 38. Rb7 Rc4 $19 {Despite the desperate liberating attempt White's  remains in a mating trap. The natural try to activate the f1 loses quickly.} 39. Rxb3 Rc2 $1 40. Kf1 (40. Kf3 h5 41. e4 g4+ 42. Kg2 $19 Ne3+ 43. Kg1 {[%cal Rh5h4] h4-+}) 40... h5 $1 41. e4 Ne7 42. Rc3 Rxc3 (42... Rd2 43. Ke1) 43. bxc3 h4 {Stohl: '0-1 (43) Akopian,V (2703)-Polgar,J (2715) Enghien les Bains 2003 CBM 96 [Stohl]'} 44. gxh4 gxh4 $1 45. Kg2 0-1

