{John Emms: 'Worth A Pawn:- White grabs an early pawn in return fro the initiative: A dangerous strategy against such an attacking player as Judit Polgar.'} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nf3 c5 3. g3 b6 4. Bg2 Bb7 5. O-O g6 6. d4 cxd4 7. Qxd4 {John Emms: 'This is less common than 7 Qc2 or 7 e3.'} Bg7 8. Nc3 d6 9. Be3 Nbd7 10. Rac1 a6 {John Emms: 'Black loses a move by moving his bishop twice, but manages to obtain more space by exchanging a pair of minor pieces.'} 11. Nd5 Rb8 12. Qd2 O-O 13. Nd4 {John Emms: '!?'} b5 {John Emms: '!?' Black must play actively, as the centre is to White's advantage.} 14. Nxf6+ Bxf6 15. cxb5 axb5 16. Bh6 Bg7 {John Emms: 'This position has been assessed as "unclear". Certainly Black has some compensation the pawn and this is very much the type of position which Polgar like to play.' The fact that White cannot castle is a considerable handicap, but the black bishop could become bad, and a pawn is a pawn.} 17. Bxg7 $1 {White wants to keep d4 free for the knight, but must be ready to meet Qg2 with Qf3.} Kxg7 {John Emms: '!'} 18. Bxb7 Rxb7 {John Emms: 'Akopian now struggles to find an effective plan and it does seem hard to come up with a solution to White's development problems.'} 19. Nc6 $2 Qe8 20. Na5 Rb6 21. Rc7 e5 {With the back rank problem covered the rook threatens to cause damage.} 22. Nb7 Qe6 23. Rd1 Nf6 $1 {John Emms: 'Preparing ...Bh3.' A neat switchback breaks down the defenses.} 24. Nxd6 Kg8 25. Nb7 Qxa2 {John Emms: '!'} 26. Nc5 {John Emms: 'This is desperate, but'} Qa8 27. b4 Rc6 28. Re7 Re8 29. Rxe8+ Qxe8 30. Qd3 Kg7 31. Qxb5 (31. Ra1 {wins.}) 31... Rd6 32. Ne6+ (32. Qxe8 {and Rg1 is no longer available.} Rxd1+ 33. Kg2 Nxe8) 32... Qxe6 33. Ra1 Rd5 34. Qa4 Qh3 35. f3 e4 {and there are no more checks.} 36. Qa7 exf3 {John Emms: '!'} (36... exf3 {looks desperate, but after the piece is lost its all over.} 37. exf3 {leaves White struggling to find a decent move.} Rd2) 0-1

