1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 d5 3. e3 c5 4. Bxf6 gxf6 5. Nc3 (5. dxc5) 5... Nc6 6. Nge2 (6. dxc5 e6 $11 {I prefer} 7. Qh5 {. For example,} b6 $5) (6. a3 e6 7. Nf3 a6 8. Be2 cxd4 9. exd4 Bg7 ({Gavrikov suggests the immediate} 9... Bh6 $1 {intending Bb4, and this looks like a significant improvement} {there's nothing wrong with} 10. Qd3 {intending ....Rfd8, ...Rac8, ...Na5, etc} f5 {prevents ...e5. It's certainly White's most ambitious option here, and there are some interesting possibilities:}) 10. O-O $2 O-O 11. Re1 Qd6 $15 {and Black's ambitious play left him with the edge in M.Sadler-M. Chandler, London 1991, but overall I don't think White should be so eager to prevent ... e4.}) 6... Bg4 7. h3 (7. dxc5 $5 e6 8. f3 Bh5 9. Ng3 Bg6 10. Bd3 $11 {or even} Bxc5 11. Qd2 Qb6) 7... Bh5 8. Qd2 (8. g4 $2 {is strongly answered by} Bg6 $1 {not ideal, but} 9. f4 {allows Black to use tactics on the c-file:} f5 10. Qd2) 8... e6 9. dxc5 Bxc5 {The most common choice for Black. If the queen is going to settle on c7, it makes sense to do so now because it sets up an x-ray attack on the c4-bishop and White has to deal with this straight away.} 10. Nf4 Bg6 {Preparing ...e5.} 11. a3 Qe7 {I can't see any point in delaying this move any longer, and if Black does White might be tempted to play f4.} 12. Be2 {White wants to prevent the possibility of ...e4, but I feel maybe this move should be encouraged rather than prevented! The problem with 12 Qc2 is that it allows Black tactical opportunities on the c-file.} O-O-O $1 {Simple stuff. Black plans ...Rc8 and it's difficult for White to hold his centre. It has to be said that Aleksandrov doesn't even try. ..} (12... d4 13. exd4 Bxd4 14. O-O-O Rd8 $11 {, K.Lerner-F.Hellers, New York 1990. Note here that}) 13. Nxg6 $6 $15 {Surely there must be something better than this!} (13. b4 Bd6 14. O-O {with an iron grip on the light squares, E.Gausel-M.Petursson, Gausdal 1990.} {I feel that} Kb8 {preparing Ba3 looks the most sensible, although after} 15. Nb5 $1 {White has to face up to the problems of his queen being on c2:} Bxf4 $5 16. exf4 e5) 13... hxg6 14. O-O-O (14. b4 Bd6 15. Bb5) 14... d4 15. exd4 Nxd4 $1 {Black manages to dominate the light squares even without his bishop. By the time White is able to activate his dormant c1-bishop, Black is in complete control.} 16. Kb1 f5 17. Bd3 (17. Na4 Nc6 18. Qe1 Rxd1+ 19. Bxd1 Bd4) 17... a6 18. f4 Kb8 19. Rhe1 Rd6 20. Na4 (20. Ne2 Nxe2 21. Qxe2 Qc7 22. c3 Rb6) 20... Ba7 21. c4 Rhd8 22. c5 Rd5 23. Bc4 Nf3 24. Qe2 Nd2+ 25. Ka2 Rd4 26. Bb3 $6 Qd7 27. Bc2 Qd5+ 28. Ka1 Bxc5 29. Nxc5 $6 Qxc5 $1 30. Rc1 $2 Rc4 $1 {Winning material.} 31. Qd1 Nb3+ 32. Bxb3 Rxd1 33. Rcxd1 Rxf4 34. Rd7 Qb5 {A model game from Black's point of view.} {It's not} 35. Rd8+ $6 {, though:} Kc7 $1 0-1

