{Anand: 'This game was played in the 9th round. Kramnik had 5.5 points, I had 5 and Topalov, Karpov and Polgar had 4.5. Kramnik played Topalov. I knew that if they drew, then I would have a chance to win the tournament.'} 1. e4 {Anand Anand Anand Anand: 'Huzman '} c6 2. d3 d5 {Anand: 'I had studied the King's Indian quite well, since I knew that if I played 1.f3, then there would be a few players who would play the King's Indian. Shirov opted for the Slav, but finally a KID appears! '} 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Bd6 5. Qe2 (5. Be2 Ne7 6. O-O O-O 7. c3 $11) (5. g3 $1 Ne7 (5... Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 8. Re1 $18 Nbd7 9. c3 dxe4 10. dxe4 $18 Qc7 11. Qc2 a5 12. Nc4 Bf8) 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O $16 {Anand: 'Black has some , but eventually the extra piece should tell.'} Nd7 {(Anand) Huzman} 8. c4 f5 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. exd5) (5. d4 $1 $18 exd4 6. exd5 { Nb6 Anand: ' b6'} cxd5 $142 {(Anand) Huzman} 7. Bb5+ $16 Nc6 8. O-O Nge7) 5... Qe7 (5... Nd7 6. exd5 cxd5 7. Nxe5 Nxe5 8. d4 $16 (8. f4 {Anand} Bg4) 8... Qe7 9. dxe5 $16 Qxe5 10. Nf3 $13) 6. d4 exd4 7. exd5 cxd5 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. N2b3 {Anand: 'Quite the rage these days. I like it because there are only a few critical positions to study, and so far, there doesn't seem a clear way for Black.'} (9. N2f3 Bd7 $1 10. Bg5 Qxe2+ {Anand: 'Huzman '} 11. Bxe2 {Anand} Nge7 12. O-O-O Rd8 13. Rhe1 f6 $132 14. Be3 Bb8) 9... Nf6 (9... Bg4 10. f3 $1 $16 {Anand: 'Stepping out of any tricks with the s.'} Bd7 $5 {(Anand) Huzman} 11. Bd2 $16 Qxe2+ 12. Bxe2 Nge7 13. Nb5 $1 $18 {Anand: '+-!'} Bb8 {(Anand) Huzman} 14. O-O-O) (9... Nxd4 $14 10. Nxd4 $1 {Anand: '!'} Be5 $1 {Anand: 'Is what I had missed.'}) 10. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {Anand: 'Looked new for a second, but I didn't see any difference between this and 10...axb4, (except that the same position can arise after move 11 or move 12!) so...'} 11. Bd2 Re8 12. O-O-O a6 13. f3 Kf8 {Anand: 'This doesn't seem right. 13...e8 is played more often and rightly so, in my opinion. Now c7 is weak.'} 14. Bc3 {Anand: 'During the game, I thought that it was important to just go ahead, but now I think that this is imprecise.'} Ne5 15. Kb1 b6 16. Bd3 $146 {Anand: 'Once again, just e1 was better. Black has to decide right away whether she is going to take the bishop.'} Nxd3 $1 {Anand: 'Of course fairly obvious, but I was mesmerised by the "x-ray" on d8! When she played this, I suddenly felt uncomfortable on the h3 square.'} 17. Rxd3 Bd7 $2 {[f6] Anand: 'A really bad move. Even passing would have been better.' A really bad move. Even passing would have been better. [×f6]} 18. Rdd1 Rac8 19. Nc1 $1 {Anand: 'This would not work with the bishop on g7.'} Nh5 (19... Ng8) 20. g3 $1 g6 21. Nd3 a5 22. Bd2 f6 23. Rhe1 $6 {Anand: 'I saw that d6 would win a piece, but decided to try for more...'} Rxe1 $1 {Anand: 'And wound up with less.'} 24. Bxe1 $6 {Anand: '?!'} (24. Rxe1) 24... Ng7 (24... Kf7 25. g4) 25. Bf2 $1 Bc7 26. b4 {Anand: 'White has to start all over again, but at least I don't have to push the d- to d6 again!'} (26. Re1 $1 $16 {Anand: 'Huzman '}) 26... Kf7 27. b5 Nf5 28. a4 (28. Nxf5 Bxf5 $18 29. Nb2 {(Anand) Huzman} Bxc2+ $16 30. Kxc2 {(Anand) Huzman} Bxg3+ $16) 28... Ne7 29. Ne2 g5 30. g4 Ng6 (30... Bxh2 31. Rh1) 31. h3 $1 {Anand: 'Now the queens go.'} Be6 32. Nd4 Bd7 33. Rh1 Rh8 $1 34. Rc1 (34. h4 $1 h5 35. hxg5 hxg4 36. Rxh8 $1 Nxh8 37. fxg4 $18 Bxg4 38. gxf6 $18) 34... Ne5 35. Nxe5+ fxe5 36. Ne2 $1 {[צf8] Anand: 'Black's  has nowhere to go.' Black's  has nowhere to go. [צf8]} h5 37. Nc3 Be6 38. Rd1 $2 {Anand: 'Another one. After f1, Black would have no defence to cd5, but now she gets some relief by exchanging one pair of s.'} hxg4 39. hxg4 Rd8 $1 (39... Rh2 $18 40. Nxd5 Bxd5 $1 $18 {Anand: '+-' '+-'} (40... Rxf2 $16 {[%cal Yd6d7]} 41. Nxc7 {Anand: 'This doesn't help Black.'} Rxf3 $1 {( d7) Anand: ' d7'  d7 ( d7)} 42. Rd6) 41. Rxd5 {(Anand) Huzman} Rxf2 42. Rd7+) 40. Be3 Kg6 41. Bc1 Rd7 {Anand: 'Now I realized that White was in danger of losing his advantage entirely. From here on however, I think I played quite well.'} 42. Bb2 d4 43. Ne4 Rf7 44. Bc1 {[צf7] Anand: 'White must keep the  boxed in as long as possible.' White must keep the  boxed in as long as possible. [צf7]} Bd8 45. Rh1 Rxf3 46. Rh8 Bf6 47. Nxf6 $1 {[ Nd5-c7-e6-d8] Anand: 'White eventually wants to get his  to d8 to dislodge the Black rook.' White eventually wants to get his  to d8 to dislodge the Black rook. [ d5-c7-e6-d8]} (47. Rb8 Bd5 48. Nd2 e4 49. Rxb6 $1 Rf2 50. Rd6 e3 51. Rxd5 $18 {Anand: '+-'} exd2 (51... exd2 52. Bxd2 Rxd2 53. b6 d3 $18 {Anand: '+-'} 54. cxd3 Rb2+ $18 {Anand: 'Huzman '} 55. Kc1 {Anand} Rxb6 56. Rxa5 Rb4)) 47... Rxf6 48. Rd8 (48. Rh5 Rf1 49. Rxg5+ Kf6 50. Rh5 $11 e4) 48... Bxg4 49. Rg8+ {[ 50. Ne6 Kf6 51.Nd8!] Anand: ' 50.e6+ f6 51.d8!'  50.e6+ f6 51.d8! [ 50.e6 f6 51.d8!]} Kf7 {Anand: 'Black has cleared d7 for her , but now her bishop is running out of squares. When I saw that after e6+, Black has to play f6, White's next move became apparent.'} 50. Rxg5 $1 {[ 51.Ne6 Kf6 52.Bg5#] Anand: ' 51.e6+ f6 52.g5#'  51.e6+ f6 52.g5# [ 51.e6 f6 52.g5#]} Bf5 51. Rh5 $1 e4 52. Bb2 Kg6 53. Rh1 $1 {Anand: 'The Black king must be forced to h7 for the subsequent fork on f6.'} Rd6 54. Rd1 $1 d3 55. cxd3 e3 56. Be5 $1 $18 Re6 57. Bd4 e2 58. Re1 Bxd3+ 59. Kb2 Bf5 (59... Kf5 60. Kc3 Ke4) 60. Bxb6 Rxb6 61. Rxe2 Rd6 62. Kc3 Rd3+ 63. Kc4 Ra3 64. Kc5 Rxa4 65. b6 $1 Rb4 66. Ra2 Rb1 67. Rxa5 Be4 68. Rb5 Rc1+ 69. Kd6 Bb7 70. Rc5 Rb1 71. Kc7 Bh1 72. Rc6+ {Anand: '. With this win, my score went to 6 points. I then remembered the tournament in Madrid 1993 where I won my last round game against Judit and tied with Kramnik and Topalov, who had just drawn their last round game...!'} Bxc6 73. Kxc6 Kf7 74. b7 $18 {Anand: '+-' Anand: 'Huzman '} Ke7 {Anand} 75. Kc7 $18 Rc1+ $4 {(Anand) Huzman} 76. Kb6 1-0

