1. d4 {Krasenkow,M Krasenkow} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 d6 7. e3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Qe7 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. Nd2 (10. Bd3) 10... O-O {This line was examined by GM Evgeny Postny in his theoretical survey in CBM116. See also my annotations to Bologan-A.Ivanov in CBM117.} (10... b6 11. Be2 Bb7 12. e4 g5 13. Bg3 e5 14. Qb1 Kd8 15. Nf1 Kc7 16. Ne3 Nf8 17. Nf5 Qd7 18. f3 Ng6 19. Bf2 Qa4 20. Qb3 Qa5 21. Kd2 Bc8 22. Ne3 Bd7 23. a4 Nf4 24. Bd1 h5 25. Bc2) 11. Be2 Ne5 12. dxe6 (12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. f4 Ng6 14. Ne4 Qe7 15. dxe6 f5 16. Ng3 Bxe6 17. Qd3 Rad8 18. O-O Nh4 19. Rae1 d5 20. Qc2 Kh8 21. Nh1 dxc4 22. g3 Bd5 23. gxh4 Qxe3+ 24. Nf2 Qxf4 25. Ng4 Qd6 26. Rxf5 Qe6) (12. Ne4 g5 13. Nxf6+ Qxf6 14. Bg3 Nxc4 15. O-O exd5 (15... Ne5 16. f4 Nd7 17. fxg5 Qxg5 18. Bxd6 Re8 19. dxe6 Qxe3+ 20. Kh1 Rxe6 21. Bc4 Rf6 22. Qh5 Qg5 23. Bxf7+ Kg7 24. Be7 Rxf1+ 25. Rxf1 Qxh5 26. Bxh5 a5 27. Be2 b6 28. Bf3 Ra7 29. Bd5 Bb7 30. Be6) 16. Qxd5 Be6 17. Qxb7 Rab8 18. Qxa7 Rb2 19. Bxc4 Bxc4 20. Rfd1 Be2 21. Re1 Bd3 22. Qc7 c4 23. Qxd6 Rxf2 24. e4 Rd8 25. Qc7 Rb2 26. a4 Re8) 12... Qxe6 $146 (12... fxe6 13. O-O) 13. O-O Re8 14. Bg3 Bd7 15. Qc2 Bc6 16. Rad1 Rad8 17. h3 Nfd7 {White's d6-pawn is the main strategical problem in this kind of position. It can be weak or strong. As Black's h8 rook is out of play, the former possibility is more probable.} (17... Ng6 18. Bd3 Ne4 19. Nxe4 Bxe4 $44 {0-1 Loeffler,S-Sakaev,K/Dresden GER 2007/The Week in Chess 648 (38)} 20. Bxe4 $44 Qxe4 21. Qb3) 18. f4 $6 {A dubious exchange sac.} Ng6 $6 {Black "believes" her opponent - and wrongly so!} (18... Qg6 19. Qxg6 Nxg6 20. Bf2 (20. Kf2 Nf6 21. Bd3 Be4) 20... Nf6 21. Bf3) 19. e4 Qe7 (19... f5 $3 20. Bh5) (19... Bxe4 20. Nxe4 Qxe4 21. Qxe4 Rxe4 22. Bf3 Rxc4 $17 {½-½ Aronian,L-Leko,P/Moscow RUS 2006/The Week in Chess 627 (27)} 23. Rxd6 $5 Ngf8 24. Rfd1) 20. Rde1 Bxe4 (20... Nf6) 21. Nxe4 Qxe4 22. Bd3 Qc6 23. Bxg6 fxg6 24. Qxg6 Nf6 $14 {[%mdl 32] In this position White's bishop is very strong. Compared to the game Gelfand-Kasimdzhanov from this issue, there is much more material on the board. Still, if Black's h8 rook safely comes out without her having to make any concessions, the position will almost equalize.} (24... Nb6 25. f5) (24... Nf8 25. Qd3) 25. Bh4 $1 Rxe1 26. Rxe1 Re8 27. Bxf6 Rxe1+ {[%mdl 4096]} 28. Kf2 Qd7 $6 {Cutting off his own rook once again. Black's further attempts to free it take valuable time and the invasion of White's rook, combined with the advance of his pawns, decides. The lesser evil was to remove his strong knight from d4:} 29. Kxe1 (29. Bxg7 Qe8) 29... Qe6+ 30. Kd2 Qxf6 31. Qxf6 gxf6 {[%csl Rg5]} 32. f5 {[%cal Re1e8,Re8e7]} a6 33. Kd3 Kf7 34. Ke4 Ke7 35. g4 Ke8 36. Kd5 Kd7 37. a4 (37. h4 Ke7 38. Ke4 b5 39. cxb5 axb5 40. Kd5 Kd7 41. a3 Kc7 42. Ke6 {1-0 Carlsen,M-Ivanchuk,V/Monte Carlo MNC 2007/The Week in Chess 646} Kc6 43. g5 $44) 37... a5 38. h4 $5 Ke7 39. Ke4 Kd7 $6 40. Ke3 Ke7 (40... Kd8 41. Kf4 d5 42. cxd5 b5 43. g5 hxg5+ 44. hxg5 fxg5+ 45. Ke5 (45. Kxg5 b4 46. f6 b3 $16 47. d6 Ke8 48. Kg6 b2 49. Kg7) 45... bxa4 46. f6 $18 a3 47. d6 a2 48. f7 a1=Q 49. f8=Q+ Kd7 50. Qe7+ Kc6 51. Qc7+ Kb5 52. Qb7+ $18 Kc4 53. Qe4+ Kxc3 54. d7 Qd1 55. Ke6) (40... Kc7 {[%cal Yd5d6]} 41. Kf3 $142 $1 (41. Kf4 d5 42. cxd5 b5 43. axb5 $1 a4 44. g5 hxg5+ $6 45. hxg5 fxg5+ 46. Kxg5 a3 47. f6 a2 48. f7 a1=Q 49. f8=Q Qg1+) 41... Kd7 (41... d5 42. g5 hxg5 43. hxg5 fxg5 44. f6 $1 Kd7 45. cxd5 b5 46. axb5 a4 47. f7 $15 {[%csl Rd6]} Ke7 $14 48. b6) 42. Ke4 Kc7 (42... Ke7 43. Kf4) (42... Kd8 $14 43. Kf4 $1) 43. g5 hxg5 44. hxg5 fxg5 45. f6 Kd7 $16 46. Kf5 $142) 41. Kf4 d5 42. cxd5 b5 43. axb5 {The advantage of the bishop over the knight is now obvious.} a4 44. b6 Kd7 45. b7 Kc7 46. g5 hxg5+ 47. hxg5 fxg5+ 48. Kxg5 a3 49. f6 a2 {Desperation.} 50. f7 a1=Q 51. b8=Q+ Kxb8 52. f8=Q+ Kb7 53. Qxc5 Qc1+ 54. Kg6 Qc2+ 55. Kf6 Qh2 56. Qe7+ Kb6 57. Qe6+ Kb5 (57... Ka5) 58. Qc6+ Ka5 59. c4 Kb4 60. d6 Qh7 (60... Qh4+ 61. Kf7) 61. Qb5+ Ka3 62. d7 Qh6+ 63. Ke7 Qh4+ (63... Qg7+ 64. Kd8 Qg4) 64. Kd6 Qd8 65. c5 Qf6+ 66. Kc7 {[%cal Ra4a5]} Qe5+ 67. Kb7 Qd5+ 68. Ka7 1-0

