1. Nf3 c5 2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. g3 g6 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. d4 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4 O-O 9. O-O d6 10. Qd3 Bf5 11. e4 $6 {This is not too promising for White, compared to} Be6 {This move may look strange but Black avoids creating weaknesses in her position after the "main"} {And the white g5 bishop will have to retreat anyway.} 12. Bd2 {Too "academic" but} Nd7 $1 $146 {[%mdl 8] A novelty in a well-known theoretical position! This move was recommended by GM Alon Greenfeld but not tested in practice before the present game.} (12... Rc8 13. b3 $1) 13. b3 a6 14. Qe2 (14. Rac1 Ne5 15. Qe2 b5 16. cxb5 axb5 17. Rfd1 b4 18. Na4 Bg4 19. f3 Bd7 20. Be3 Bxa4 $16 {1-0 Topalov,V-Hansen,C/Istanbul 2000/EXT 2001/[Tsesarsky] (65)} 21. bxa4 Qd7 22. f4 Ng4 23. e5 Nxe3 24. Qxe3 Rxa4 25. Bc6 Qa7 26. Qxa7 Rxa7 27. exd6 exd6 28. Bd5 $14 {½-½ Kramnik,V-Anand,V/Monte Carlo 1995/CBM 046 ext (50)} Bc3 29. Bb3 Rd8 30. Rc2 Kf8 31. Kf2 Ra5 32. Rd5 Rxd5 33. Bxd5 Re8 34. Re2 Bd4+ 35. Kf3 Rc8 36. Bb3 Rc1 37. Rc2 Rf1+ 38. Ke2 Rh1 39. Rc7 Rxh2+ 40. Kd3 Bc5 41. Rxf7+ Ke8 42. Rg7 {1-0 Schandorff,L-Greenfeld,A/Saint Vincent 2005/CBM 110} Rh3 43. Ke4 Rxg3) 14... Rb8 {The same idea as after 12...h6 but Black's rook is already on e8, which is, of course, favorable for her.} 15. Rac1 Ne5 16. h3 Nc6 17. Nd5 Bxd5 (17... Re8 18. Be3 Bd4 19. Bxd4 Nxd4 20. Qb2 $16 Nc6 $142 $14 21. Rfd1 {was still possible, e.g.} b6 22. f4 $44 Qd7 23. Kh2 Bxd5 {is not good now due to} 24. cxd5 $1 Na7 25. h4 Rbc8 26. Bh3 e6 27. h5 Qe7 $142 {[%cal Yf5e4] , e.g.} 28. h6 Qf8 29. dxe6 fxe6 $1 $36 30. Rxd6 $2 Rxc1 31. Rd7 $2 Qxh6 32. Qxc1 $2 Nc8 33. Kg2 g5 34. f5 $15 {was even stronger.} Ne7 $6 35. fxe6 Qg6 36. Kg1 g4 37. Bf1 $17 b5 38. Rd6 Qxe4 39. Qf4 Qg6 40. Bd3 Qg7 41. Qd4 Qg5 42. Qf4 $15 Qg7 {is the main theoretical move but White keeps an edge:} 43. Be2 Ng6 44. Qf7+ $5 {The most dangerous plan in such positions.} Qxf7 45. exf7+ Kxf7 46. Bxg4 Ra8 47. Bf3) 18. exd5 Ne5 $6 {An unnecessary move, after which White consolidates his position and Black's d5 pawn becomes weak.} 19. Rfe1 Re8 $2 20. Bc3 (20. f4 $1 Nd7 21. Kh2 $16) 20... Nd7 21. Bxg7 {[%csl Rb7,Rd5] Suddenly Black is in trouble.} Kxg7 22. Rc3 b5 23. h4 Nf6 24. cxb5 Qa5 25. Qb2 (25. Rec1) 25... Rxb5 26. Rce3 (26. h5) 26... Kg8 27. R1e2 {and White gradually converted his extra pawn:} Nxd5 28. Bxd5 Rxd5 29. Rxe7 Rxe7 30. Rxe7 Re5 31. Rxe5 dxe5 32. Qe2 Qa3 33. h5 gxh5 34. Kg2 f6 $1 {was even simpler:} 35. Qc4+ Kg7 36. Qc7+ Kg6 $16 37. Qc2+ {is too late due to} 1-0

