1. g3 d5 2. Bg2 c6 3. Nf3 Bf5 4. O-O e6 5. d3 h6 6. b3 Nf6 7. Bb2 Be7 {Diagram } 8. Nbd2 {A relatively calm line. White prevents ...b5.} Bh7 9. c4 O-O {Diagram } 10. a3 a5 11. Ra2 Nbd7 12. Qa1 {As a rule, having a weakened b4 square, White is not pretending for a serious advantage, but may still have hopes to develop a slight pressure.} Re8 $146 13. Ne5 Bf8 14. Nxd7 Nxd7 15. Bc3 {Diagram } Qb6 ({Completely hopeless is} 15... Nc5 16. d4 Na6 17. Rc1 {, etc.} {Or} b5 18. cxb5 cxb5 19. Nf3 Rc8 {and White should gradually win after, say,} 20. Qb2 $5 {or maybe even} Qb6 $5 21. Raa1 b4 {sooner or later followed by f4!} {Black had to play} 22. axb4 $1 {after which White is still better but there is surely a fight ahead.} {Missing a stronger continuation:} Nxb4 $1) 16. b4 Qa6 17. bxa5 {Diagram  Black's position, likely, remains playable.} Nc5 18. Bb4 Na4 $6 19. Bxf8 Rxf8 20. cxd5 $1 exd5 21. Nb3 $1 {Diagram  Black does not get enough for a pawn, mainly because his knight is far away from the kingside.} Rfe8 22. Rc1 Re7 23. Bf3 Rae8 24. Qd4 $1 Qb5 $6 25. Qb4 $16 Bf5 {Diagram } 26. Qxb5 $6 cxb5 $2 27. Nd4 $18 {Diagram } Bd7 28. Bxd5 $1 Rb8 {This tactic does not help, but anyway Black has a bad position.} 29. Rc7 Kf8 30. Rac2 Be8 31. Rxe7 $1 $18 {Diagram } Kxe7 32. Rc7+ Kd6 33. Rxb7 Rc8 34. Nf5+ Ke5 35. e4 g6 36. f4+ Kf6 37. Nd6 Rc1+ 38. Kf2 Bc6 39. Rxf7# $1 {Diagram } 1-0

