1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 c5 5. g3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 Ne4 7. Qc2 Nxc3 8. bxc3 Be7 $1 {Black removes the bishop, rather than allow White to exchange on d4 and obtain a bit of pressure after 10 Ne2.} 9. Bg2 a6 (9... O-O 10. O-O Nc6 (10... d6 11. Rd1 a6 12. c5 Qc7 13. cxd6 Bxd6 14. c4 Nd7 15. Nb3 Nc5 16. Ba3 Be7 17. Rab1 Ra7 18. Nxc5 Bxc5 19. Bxc5 (19. Qb3) 19... Qxc5 20. Qd3) 11. Rd1 (11. Bf4) 11... Ne5 12. Qe4 Qa5 (12... Nxc4 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. Qxc4 d5 15. Qd3) 13. Bf4 {gave the second player good counterplay in Buscher-Naiditsch, European Club Cup, Plovdiv 2010.} f6 (13... Nxc4 14. Nxe6) (13... Ng6 14. Nb3 Qxc3 15. Rac1 Qb2 16. Rc2 Qa3 17. Bc7) 14. Rab1) (9... Nc6 10. O-O O-O) (9... Qc7 10. Nb5 Qxc4 11. Qb3 Qxb3 12. axb3 Na6 13. Rxa6 bxa6 14. Nc7+ Kd8 15. Nxa8 d5 16. Bf4 g5 17. Be5 f6 18. Bb8 Bb7 19. Nc7 Kd7 20. Nxe6 Rxb8 21. Nd4 (21. Bh3) 21... Bc5 22. Kd2) 10. Bf4 (10. O-O {Black was pretty comfortably placed in Voronov-S.Zhigalko, Alushta 2010.} Qc7 $5 {, with the idea of} (10... d6 {in Tseshkovsky-Vaisser, Arco 2010.} {We'll consider the more important option,} 11. c5 {, in Kovacevic-Lenic.} {I'm less convinced by d3 here than a move earlier, though, and neither should} dxc5 {be too problematic for Black:} 12. Nf3) 11. Qa4 (11. Rb1 d6) 11... Nc6 (11... O-O 12. Nb5) 12. Nb3 d6 13. c5 Bd7 {and decent pressure for the pawn, could well be a stiffer test} (13... d5 14. c4 dxc4 $1 {enabled Black to solve his problems with} 15. Qxc4 O-O 16. Bb2 e5 17. Bd5) {Polgar is happy to play by analogy with the 2 Nc3 Nc6 3 Bb5 Nd4 variation. Here too one can make a case for} 14. Qg4 {, although after} O-O 15. Bh6 Bf6 16. cxd6 {White must be slightly for choice with} Qxd6 {followed by f4,} {whereas} 17. Be3 Qc7 18. Nc5 Bc8 19. Rab1) (10. Rb1 Qc7 $1 11. Qa4 Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 (12... dxc6) 13. Bf4 e5 14. Be3) 10... O-O (10... d6 11. Nf3 Nd7 {when White has definite chances to obtain the upper hand} {This quickly gives rise to an unbalanced, complex middlegame. An alternative approach is} 12. O-O {and after} Qc7 13. Rfd1 h6 (13... O-O 14. Ng5) 14. Qd3 e5 15. Be3 $1 O-O) 11. Rb1 (11. Rd1 {while matters wouldn't have been at all clear, it would be a surprise if Black was worse with his light-squared bishop an especially useful asset.} {White's position looks a little overextended if anything after} d6 12. Nb3 Qc7 13. c5 {better than} e5) 11... Nc6 (11... d6 12. Bxb7 Bxb7 13. Rxb7 Bf6 (13... e5 14. Nf5)) 12. O-O Na5 $1 {Polgar develops her second bishop as actively as possible and now we have a classic tussle between White's knights and slightly better structure and Black's bishops. I can think of several players who would be happy to slowly manoeuvre in White's shoes, but Black's position is hardly bad and does contain a fair amount of dynamic potential.} 13. Qd3 $6 d6 14. Nb3 Nc6 (14... Nxb3 $1) 15. Rfd1 Ne5 $5 (15... e5 16. Be3 {, which looks like a pretty decent version of an Open Sicilian for Black, and only here changed his mind?} {This doesn't achieve too much. Better would have been the immediate} Qc7 {, aiming to expand while restricting Black's bishops with Nh4 and f4.} {White often goes here in the closely-related 2 Nc3 Nc6 3 Bb5 system. Here he might have played more aggressively in the centre, but after} 17. Nd2 $5) 16. Qd4 (16. Bxe5 {when} dxe5 17. Qxd8 {would have kept the lid on Black's bishops and potential for the time being.} {Was Edouard really initially intending} Rxd8 18. Rxd8+ Bxd8 19. Nc5) 16... f6 (16... Bf6 $5 17. Kf1 Qc7 18. c5 (18. Nd2) 18... d5 {then one possibility, offering Black some pressure and full compensation for the pawn.} {Black's aggressive-looking last causes White to reroute his other knight, but he might also have followed through with} 19. h4) 17. c5 d5 (17... dxc5 18. Qxd8 Rxd8 19. Rxd8+ Bxd8 {keeps everything defended and seems fine for White.} {I'm not particularly convinced by White's knight manoeuvre, especially if going to g2 is the best he can do. By now} 20. Nxc5 {was no longer such a good option, though, with}) 18. Bxe5 (18. e4) 18... fxe5 19. Qxe5 Bf6 20. Qe3 Qc7 $1 {Having improved her king and then queen, it's time for Polgar to begin to lever open White's camp, solid though his defences still appear.} 21. Qd3 $2 a5 $1 22. c4 $5 (22. a4 Bd7 {then} 23. c4 Bxa4 24. cxd5 {would have seen the second f-pawn coming into play with some effect.} {White's play over the last five or so moves has likely not been too accurate, although we shouldn't forget that this was a rapid game. Here he would have done better with} exd5 $5 {, one important idea of which is that} 25. Qxd5+ Rf7) 22... a4 23. Nd2 $2 (23. Nd4 Qxc5 24. cxd5 exd5 {.} {The Frenchman goes for counterplay and who can blame him? Had White defended with, say,} 25. Rb5) 23... d4 (23... Qxc5 {, but no doubt both players were low on time by this point and Edouard may well also have been pretty demoralised.} {This seems strong, but gives White a resource. Instead} 24. cxd5 exd5 (24... Qxf2+ 25. Kxf2 {would have concluded matters on the spot.} {Once again} Bd4+ {would have been a stiffer defence, rather promising for Black though this would have been with her extra pawn and line-moving pieces. Edouard tries to obtain some counterplay, but only makes matters worse for himself.} {I dare say that Black's slightly-cheeky 24th came as a surprise to White who was no doubt mainly focussed on the pressure down the g-file. Here he had to go} 26. Ke1 {, preparing counterplay down the d-file with}) 25. Bxd5+ $1 Kh8 26. Ne4) 24. Ne4 e5 $1 {Polgar's keen eye has spotted that White's powerful-looking central pawns are actually pretty weak.} 25. e3 Be7 (25... Bf5 26. Rxb7) 26. exd4 Bf5 {Winning a pawn.} 27. Rb5 exd4 28. Qxd4 $6 Rad8 (28... Bxe4 29. Bxe4 {would also have done the business.} {Missing the chance to grovel with} Rad8 30. Bd5+) 29. Nd6 Bc8 30. Bxb7 Bxb7 $6 31. Rxb7 $6 Qxb7 32. Nxb7 Rxd4 33. Rxd4 Rc8 34. Rd5 a3 $1 {Now Polgar hits on the right idea and goes after White's rather shaky king.} 35. Kf1 Rb8 36. c6 Kf7 37. c5 Ke6 38. Rd6+ Ke5 39. c7 Rc8 40. f4+ $5 Kf5 41. Rc6 0-1

