1. e4 {Psakhis,L} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nd7 4. d4 (4. O-O a6 5. Bd3 $14) 4... cxd4 5. Qxd4 a6 6. Be2 (6. Bxd7+ $5 {'!'}) 6... Ngf6 {*} (6... e5 7. Qe3 $13 {<xml:en>with reasonable compensation for the queen.</:en/xml>} {Continuing to play for the attack, although the simpler} Ngf6 $1 {'!?'} 8. O-O) 7. O-O (7. c4 g6 8. Nc3 Bg7 9. O-O {<xml:en>due to the extra d-pawn and to the kingside threats. In the game Fressinet does force some kingside weaknesses, but Polgar defends strongly and in the end it's White who's left having to force the draw:</:en/xml>} O-O $2 10. Qd3 Nc5 11. Qc2 b5 12. cxb5 $18 {The exchanges don't help Black and so she should have tried to change the course of the struggle with Possibly Judit could play superbrave} axb5 $5 {'!' <xml:en>, intending</:en/xml> for example:} 13. Bxb5 Bb7 14. Re1 $140 Rc8) 7... e5 (7... e6 $140 $1 8. c4 $140 Be7 9. Nc3 O-O $18 10. Bg5 $5 {bringing queen to the king side.} {<xml:en>Here Kaufman's</:en/xml> Black experiences much more difficulties after} Qc7 $5 {[%cal Gd1d8,Gf1f7] <xml:en>was much stronger when f7 falls and Black is close to being lost after</:en/xml> for example} 11. Rfe1 $140 Rd8 12. Qd2 b6 13. Bd3 Bb7 $18) (7... g6) 8. Qe3 Nc5 (8... Be7 9. c4 O-O 10. Nc3 $14 {although, the lion's share of advantage had been already missed.}) 9. Nc3 Be7 10. b3 O-O 11. Ba3 Ne6 {<xml:en>Black hopes that the rook will prove useful here, with the pressure down the c-file assisting a successful ...b5 or ...d5 break. However, this doesn't have to be played immediately and Black can, and quite possibly should, prefer to first decide where to deploy his queen:</:en/xml>} 12. g3 (12. Rfd1) 12... Qa5 (12... b5 13. Rad1 Qa5 14. Bb2 Nc5 $16 {Worth considering} 15. a3 $5 Qc7) 13. Bb2 Nc5 14. Nd2 Be6 (14... b5) 15. a4 {Both sides have developed consistently with their overall approaches and we are now at a critical juncture with Black needing to find a way to deal with 16 Nf3 and 17 e5.} Rfe8 {'?!'} 16. Nc4 $146 Qc7 17. a5 {'!'} Rad8 18. Rfd1 Qc6 19. Nd2 {'!'} (19. f3 Bf8 20. Qf2 g6 21. Ne3 $13) 19... h6 {'!'} (19... d5 $16 20. exd5 $5 {The following does not give anything to White} Bxd5 21. Qxe5 Bd6 22. Qf5 Be5) 20. Bf3 Bf8 $6 21. Nf1 Bg4 22. Bxg4 Nxg4 $5 23. Qf3 $6 (23. Qe2 Nf6 24. Nd2 (24. f3 d5) 24... Ne6 25. Nf3 $8) 23... Nf6 24. Re1 $6 {Facing stubborn defense, Fressinet loses his rate of attack.} Ne6 25. Ra4 Ng5 $8 26. Qg2 d5 27. exd5 Nxd5 $5 28. Rc4 Qf6 (28... Nf4 {[%cal Gh3d7]} 29. Qxc6 Ngh3+ 30. Kh1 Nxf2+ $1 31. Kg1 N2h3+) 29. Nxd5 $5 {It looks like White has managed to cause serious threats, but it is just an illusory thing.} Nf3+ $1 30. Kh1 Rxd5 31. Re3 Rd1 32. Qxf3 Qxf3+ 33. Rxf3 Rxf1+ 34. Kg2 Rb1 35. Bc3 Rc1 (35... Rd1 36. Rc7 $2 f6 37. Rxb7 Rc1 38. Bd2 Rxc2 $18 39. Rd3) 36. Bd2 Rd1 37. Rd3 Be7 38. Kf3 Bg5 39. Bxg5 Rxd3+ 40. cxd3 hxg5 41. Ke4 f6 (41... Re7 42. Kd5) 42. Rc7 Rb8 43. b4 Kh7 44. Kd5 b6 45. b5 axb5 46. a6 Rd8+ {½-½ (46) Fressinet,L-Polgar,J Enghien les Bains (Game 26) 2003 White queen and Black king had strange and very long dance together!} 47. Kc6 b4 48. Kb7 b3 49. Rc3 1/2-1/2

