1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 d5 5. Bf4 O-O 6. Rc1 dxc4 7. e3 Be6 8. Ng5 Bd5 9. e4 h6 {Joe Gallagher: 'Against 9 Nd2 Black does best to hold up White's queenside play. If he gets involved in a direct race (kingside v queenside attack) then he usually comes out second best as the knight on d2 can be extremely powerful when it emerges on c4 (after White has played c5 of course).'} 10. exd5 hxg5 {Joe Gallagher: 'Played to further slow down White on the queenside as now a3 is met by a4.10. . . Nd7 is the other mainline'} 11. Bxg5 Nxd5 12. Bxc4 Nb6 13. Bb3 Nc6 14. Ne2 Rc8 15. O-O Qd7 (15... Nxd4 16. Nxd4 Qxd4 (16... Bxd4 17. Re1 Bxb2 (17... Bf6 18. Qc2 Kg7 19. Rcd1) 18. Qc2 Qd4 $13 (18... Bxc1 19. Qxg6+ Kh8 20. Qh6+ {Black is thought to be Ok} Kg8 $5 21. Bxc1) 19. Rb1 {is the other possibility in order to exchange off the 'bad bishop'. After} Bc3 20. Rxe7) 17. Qf3 (17. Bxe7 Rfe8 18. Ba3 $11 Qf4 {Ü 64/556}) 17... Qe5 (17... e6) 18. h4 $11 Bf6 19. Bxf6 Qxf6 20. Qxb7 $14 c5 21. Rfe1 c4 22. Bc2 $11 Qxb2 23. Qxa7) 16. Qd2 (16. Be3 Rfd8 17. Qd3 e6 18. Bg5 Re8 19. Qf3 Na5 20. Bc2 Qd5) (16. d5 $14 Na5 (16... Nb4 $14 17. Nc3 $6 Bxc3 18. bxc3 N4xd5 $14 19. c4 $1 Nf6 20. Qf3 Qc6 21. Qxc6 bxc6 22. Rfe1 $11) 17. Rc5 Nxb3 $14 18. Qxb3 Rfd8) 16... Nxd4 (16... Rfd8 17. Rfe1) 17. Nxd4 (17. Rcd1 c5) 17... Qxd4 (17... Bxd4 18. Bxe7 Qxe7 $11 (18... Bxf2+ 19. Rxf2 Qxe7 20. Rcf1 Qc5 21. Qd3 $17 Kh8 22. Qh3+ Kg7 23. Qc3+ Qxc3 24. Rxf7+) 19. Qxd4) 18. Qxd4 $146 {Joe Gallagher: 'This looks like a new move. Normally White plays'} (18. Qa5 a6 (18... Qe5 19. Rc5 $17 Qd6 20. Rd1 Bd4 21. Rc2 a6 $17 22. Bxe7 {Joe Gallagher: 'here but then realised that the reply'} Qxe7 {Joe Gallagher: 'is awkward for him. For example,'} 23. Rxd4) 19. Bxe7 Rfe8 $17 20. Bc5 Qxb2 $1 21. Bxb6 cxb6 22. Bxf7+ Kf8 (22... Kxf7 23. Qd5+ Kf8 24. Qd6+ Kg8 25. Rxc8 Rxc8 26. Qe6+) 23. Rxc8 Rxc8 24. Qd5 Qe5 $19) 18... Bxd4 19. Bxe7 Rfe8 (19... Bxb2 20. Bxf8 (20. Rcd1 $16 Rfe8 $5 21. Bc5 Bf6) 20... Bxc1 21. Bc5 Bf4 22. Rd1 Re8 23. g3) 20. Bc5 Bxb2 (20... Bxc5 21. Rxc5 Re2 22. Rc2) 21. Rcd1 (21. Rc2 Be5 22. Rd1 $11 Rcd8 $2) 21... Bf6 $6 (21... Rcd8 22. Rxd8 $15 (22. f4 a5 23. f5 g5 $3 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Be7 Re8 26. Bxg5 Bd4+ 27. Kh1 c5) 22... Rxd8 23. Bxb6 (23. Re1 Nc8 24. Kf1 a5) (23. Rd1 Rxd1+ 24. Bxd1 $15 Nc8 25. Bg4 f5 26. Bf3 c6 $17 27. Kf1 b6 $1 28. Be3 Ne7) 23... axb6 24. Rd1 Bd4 25. a4 $15) 22. f4 Be7 {Joe Gallagher: 'The game is about level with each side having one excellent central White now heads for complications he would have been well advised to steer clear of.'} (22... Rcd8 23. Rxd8 (23. f5 g5 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Rd1 Rxd1+ 26. Bxd1 $19 Nc8 27. Bf3 c6 $4 28. Kf2 $1 {is a trap that had to be sidestepped} b6 $5 29. Be3 c5) 23... Rxd8 24. Rd1 $17 Rxd1+ 25. Bxd1 Nd5 26. Bxa7 Nxf4 $15 27. Be3 $2) 23. Bxb6 (23. Bxe7 Rxe7 24. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 25. Rxe1 Kf8 $17) 23... axb6 24. f5 {Joe Gallagher: '?!'} g5 $1 (24... Kg7 25. Rd7) (24... Bc5+ 25. Kh1 $19 gxf5 26. Rxf5 Re7 27. Rdf1 Rf8 28. g4 $19) 25. f6 $2 {Joe Gallagher: '!' Joe Gallagher: 'Ftacnik must have planned'} Bc5+ 26. Kh1 Rcd8 27. Rxd8 Rxd8 28. Rf5 {Joe Gallagher: 'The b-pawn and the fact that the bishop on g7 is so passive gives White hope'} g4 (28... Be3 29. Re5 Bf4 30. Re7 Rf8 31. a4 Bd6 32. Rd7) (28... Rd6 29. Rxg5+ Kf8 30. Rf5 $17 Bd4 31. g4 Rxf6 32. Rxf6 Bxf6 33. a4) 29. Rg5+ Kf8 30. Rxg4 (30. Rh5 {#} Ke8 $1 31. Rh8+ Bf8 32. Rh7 Rd7) 30... Bd4 31. Rf4 $6 b5 (31... Rd6 32. g4 b5 33. g5) 32. g4 c5 33. g5 c4 $2 34. Rh4 (34. Bc2 $11 Rd5 $6 35. Rh4 $1 Ke8 (35... Kg8 36. Bh7+ Kf8 $18 37. Bf5 Kg8 $1 38. Bh7+) 36. Rh8+ $17 Kd7 37. g6 fxg6 38. f7 Bc5 39. f8=Q $11 Bxf8 $2 40. Rxf8 Rd2) 34... Kg8 (34... Ke8 $14 35. Bc2 Bc5 $1 36. Rh8+ Bf8 37. h4) 35. Bc2 Be3 36. Bh7+ (36. Rh7 Rd1+ 37. Bxd1 (37. Kg2 Rd2+ 38. Kf3 Rxc2 39. Rg7+ $15 Kf8 $2 40. Rh7 $1 Ke8 41. Kxe3 Rg2) 37... Kxh7 38. h4 Bf2 39. h5 Bh4 40. g6+ Kg8 $11 41. Bc2) 36... Kf8 37. Bf5 $1 Kg8 38. Bh7+ (38. Rh7 {Joe Gallagher: 'Ftacnik was a little lucky after he mishandled the early middlegame but it is not unusual for a rook to be an equal of two minor pieces in the King's Indian.'} Rd1+ 39. Kg2 Rg1+ 40. Kf3 Rxg5) 38... Kf8 39. Bf5 Kg8 40. Bh7+ 1/2-1/2

