1. d4 {Krasenkow,M Krasenkow} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qa4 Bb7 6. Bg2 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O O-O 9. Nc3 Be7 10. Rd1 Ne4 $6 $146 {[%mdl 8] A new but hardly good continuation in this well-known position. White seizes the d6 square and initiative.} (10... Na6 {is usually played}) (10... d6) (10... a6) ({and} 10... Qc8 {have also been seen in GMs' practice.}) 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. Bf4 Qc8 13. Rac1 (13. Bd6 $5 Bxd6 14. Rxd6 {may be even more precise as White's a1 rook will go to d1.}) 13... Rd8 14. Bd6 Bxd6 15. Rxd6 Qc5 16. Qd1 (16. Rcd1 $5) 16... Bd5 $2 {It was relatively better to give up the d7 pawn to seize the d-file:} (16... Nc6 17. Rxd7 h6 $16) 17. Rxd5 $1 {[%mdl 64]} exd5 18. Ng5 {[%cal Rg2d5,Rd1d5,Gd5f7,Gd5a8]} (18. Ne5 $142 $1 $18 {[%cal Rg2d5,Rd1d5,Gd5f7,Gd5a8]} Rf8 $140 19. Bxd5 Nc6 20. Nxd7) 18... Rf8 19. Bxd5 Nc6 20. Qd3 g6 21. Ne4 Qd4 $1 {Black now holds a material balance by tactical means but his position remains compromised.} (21... Qe7 22. Qc3 Qe5 23. Qxe5 Nxe5 24. Bxa8 Rxa8 $18 {}) 22. Qxd4 Nxd4 23. Rd1 $1 (23. Bxa8 Nxe2+) 23... Nc6 24. Nf6+ Kg7 25. Nxd7 Rfc8 26. Rb1 $1 {[%cal Rb2b4]} Rc7 $8 27. Ne5 Nxe5 28. Bxa8 Rxc4 {White has won a pawn (although not in the best version) and gradually converts his material advantage:} 29. Bd5 Rc2 30. Kf1 g5 31. Bb3 Rd2 32. Ke1 Rd7 33. h3 h6 34. f4 Ng6 35. e3 Nf8 36. Rd1 Re7 37. Kf2 Nd7 38. Kf3 Nc5 39. Bc2 Rc7 40. Rd2 Nd7 41. e4 Nf8 42. e5 Nd7 43. Kg4 b5 44. h4 gxf4 45. gxf4 a5 46. Kh5 a4 47. Rg2+ Kf8 48. Kxh6 Rc4 49. Kg5 Nc5 50. Bf5 Rc1 51. h5 Ke7 52. h6 Rh1 53. h7 Ne6+ 54. Kg4 Nf8 55. Rc2 Nxh7 56. Rc7+ Ke8 57. Rb7 Nf8 58. Rb8+ Ke7 59. Rb7+ Ke8 60. Rxb5 Rg1+ 61. Kf3 Rf1+ 62. Ke4 1-0

