{In the previous game, centralization took place very early. This was partly because of the rapid development and open lines typical of e-pawn openings. In our next game we have a King's Indian Defense - which normally is a closed opening with few open lines early on. In this game, however, play opens up early and with some deft, timely centralizing moves. Judith Polgar, the world's youngest GM, stirs up a winning attack.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 O-O 5. e4 d6 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 ({Another popular choice is the older line} 7... Nbd7) 8. Be3 {(Gligorich's system, which aims to retain the central tension.)} ({The main alternative is} 8. d5) 8... Ng4 9. Bg5 f6 10. Bc1 f5 {[%csl Yd6,Ye5,Yf5,Yg6] (With four pawns directly involved, play becomes sharp.)} 11. dxe5 Ngxe5 12. exf5 Bxf5 {[%csl Yd4,Yd5,Ye4,Ge5][%cal Rf5e4,Rg7d4,Rc6d4,Rc6e5] (Now all of Black's minor pieces are well developed and trained upon central squares.)} 13. Be3 Qf6 14. Rc1 Rae8 15. b3 h6 $1 {[%cal Yg6g5,Yg5g4] (Planning Kingside aggression with g5 and g4.)} 16. Qd2 g5 17. Nd4 {(White struggles for central squares.)} Nxd4 18. Bxd4 g4 19. Kh1 Qg6 {[%cal Yg6h5] (Heading for an effective post on h5.)} 20. Rg1 Qh5 21. Nd5 {[%csl Rc7][%cal Gd5c7] (Centralization with an attack on the c-pawn.)} Be4 {(Ignoring the attack on c7, Black also centralizes while drawing attention to White's cornered King.)} 22. Nxc7 Nf3 $1 {(The centralized Knight shows the way to crack open lines for attack.)} 23. gxf3 Rxf3 $1 {[%cal Rf3f2,Rf3h3,Ge4h1] (With the threat of 24...Rh3+, or 24...Rxf2+)} 24. Rg2 (24. Bxf3 Bxf3+ 25. Rg2 {transposes into the actual game.}) 24... Qh3 $1 25. Rcg1 ({Now if} 25. Nxe8 Rxf2 {is crushing.}) {(For the moment White holds on. But Black's next centralizing-attacking move proves too tough to handle.)} 25... Re5 $1 (25... Re5 {threatening} 26. Z0 Rh5 {which in turn would threaten} 27. Z0 Qxh2+ 28. Rxh2 Rxh2+ 29. Kxh2 Rh3#) 26. Bxf3 ({On} 26. Bxe5 Bxe5 {(replacing one centralized piece with another) Black has a winning attack, e.g.} 27. Rxg4+ Kh7 28. R4g2 (28. Rxe4 Qxh2#) 28... Rxf2 {is a winner.}) 26... Bxf3 27. Bxe5 Bxe5 28. Qd5+ Kh8 {Now 28...Qxh2 mate is threatened, and there is no good defense: 29.Qxe5+ dxe5 is best, but is clearly losing. White resigns.} 0-1

