1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 b5 8. Nxc6 Qxc6 9. f3 Bb7 10. O-O-O Ne7 11. Kb1 Ng6 12. h4 b4 13. Ne2 Ne5 14. Nf4 Rc8 15. Bd4 f6 (15... Nc4 16. Bxc4 Qxc4 17. Rh3 $14) 16. Nh5 Qd6 17. Qe1 (17. Rh3 Nc6 18. Be3 Qxd2 19. Rxd2 Ne7 $11) 17... Qc7 18. Rd2 Bc5 $2 {Polgar was living under impression, that it is not possible to eat the pawn on g7, but it is anything but truth.} (18... Qa5 19. Be3 d5 (19... Nxf3 20. Nxg7+ Bxg7 21. gxf3 f5 $11) 20. Qg3 Kf7 $13) 19. Nxg7+ (19. Bxc5 Qxc5 20. Nxg7+ Ke7 (20... Kf7 21. f4 Ng6 22. Qe2 Qc6 23. Nh5 Qxe4 24. Rxd7+ Ne7 25. Qd1 $16) 21. Qd1 {Ftacnik: '' Ftacnik,L: ''} (21. Nh5 Nxf3 22. gxf3 Qxh5 23. Rg1 $16) 21... Rc7 22. Nh5 $16) 19... Ke7 20. Qe3 $6 (20. Bxc5+ Qxc5 21. Qd1 $16) 20... h5 $1 21. Be2 (21. f4 Ng4 22. Qd3 (22. Bxc5+ Qxc5 23. Qd3 Bc6 24. e5 fxe5 25. Qg6 Rcg8 $15) 22... Bxd4 23. Qxd4 Rh7 24. Qxb4+ Kf7 25. Nxh5 Rxh5 26. Be2 Rg8 $11) 21... Rh7 22. Nxh5 Rxh5 23. f4 $6 {Grischuk has completely lost the track and for the caught piece he will be left with a mere two connected pawns in the middlegame.} (23. Bxc5+ $5 Qxc5 24. Qxc5+ Rxc5 25. f4 Ng6 (25... Rh7 26. fxe5 Rxe5 27. Rhd1 Ke8 $11) 26. Rhd1 Nf8 27. Bxh5 Rxh5 28. Rd4 a5 29. g3 e5 $15) 23... Bxd4 24. Qxd4 Nc6 25. Qe3 (25. Qxd7+ Qxd7 26. Rxd7+ Kxd7 27. Bxh5 Ke7 $17) 25... Rh7 26. g4 Rg8 27. g5 (27. h5 Ke8 (27... e5 28. fxe5 Qxe5 29. Rhd1 Ke8 30. Qb6 Nd8 31. Qxb4 Bxe4 32. Qa4 $11) 28. g5 fxg5 29. fxg5 Qe5 $15) 27... fxg5 28. fxg5 Qe5 29. Rdd1 (29. Qb6 Bc8 30. Rdd1 Rgh8 $15) 29... Kd8 $6 (29... Ke8 $1 $17) 30. Qb6+ (30. Bxa6 Bxa6 31. Qb6+ Ke7 32. Qxa6 Qxe4 33. Qb7 Rd8 $17) 30... Kc8 31. Qf2 $2 {The last serious mistake in the somewhat mysteriously played game, it is very difficult to understand why white refrained from eating a6 pawn.} (31. Bxa6 $1 Bxa6 32. Qxa6+ Kc7 33. Qe2 $13) 31... a5 32. Rhg1 a4 $1 33. g6 a3 34. c3 (34. bxa3 bxa3 35. Qb6 Rhg7 36. h5 Qxe4 $17) 34... Rh6 35. g7 (35. Bd3 bxc3 36. Rg5 Qh8 $19) 35... bxc3 36. bxa3 (36. Qf8+ Kc7 37. Qxa3 (37. Qxg8 c2+ 38. Kxc2 Qxb2+ 39. Kd3 Ba6+ 40. Ke3 Qxe2+ 41. Kf4 Qf2+ 42. Kg4 Ne5+ 43. Kh3 Rxh4#) 37... Rxg7 $19) 36... Rf6 37. Qb6 Rf7 (37... Qxe4+ 38. Bd3 Qxh4 $19) 38. Rg5 (38. Bd3 Rfxg7 $19) 38... Qxe4+ 39. Bd3 Qf3 {Somewhat pradoxically this active move turns out to be slightly inaccurate, but the black's position is already completely winning.} (39... Qxh4 40. Rb5 d6 $19) 40. Kc2 Rgxg7 41. Rxg7 Rxg7 42. Rf1 Rg2+ 43. Kc1 (43. Kxc3 Ne7 $1 44. Rxf3 Nd5+ $19) 43... Rb2 $1 44. Qxb7+ (44. Rxf3 Rxb6 45. h5 Ne5 $19) 44... Kxb7 45. Rxf3 Rh2 46. Rf4 (46. Rf7 Ne5 47. Be4+ Kb6 48. Rf4 d5 $19) 46... d5 47. h5 e5 {By the way in the final position both parties have three pawns, but their's potential is different as night and day.} (47... e5 48. Rg4 Nd4 $19) 0-1

