1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bg5 d5 {Despite being a Queen's Indian player, whom one might expect would head for a Hedgehog set-up, Polgar is happy to counter Harikrishna's favourite weapon with a classical arrangement.} 4. e3 c5 5. c3 Nbd7 6. Nbd2 Be7 7. Bd3 b6 8. Ne5 Nxe5 {As we've seen before, the knight must go lest White support it with f2-f4.} 9. dxe5 Nd7 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. f4 Bb7 12. O-O ({An interesting moment. The text is the standard move, but Harikrishna had earlier preferred} 12. Qe2 $5 {, keeping White's options open while waiting to see where Black will castle. After} f6 (12... O-O-O 13. a4 $1 {reveals another point behind White's move order}) 13. exf6 gxf6 14. e4 {White must have been quite happy in Harikrishna-Moradiabadi, Doha 2006. I was sufficiently impressed to make that an illustrative game in 'The Torre Attack: Move by Move', but maybe the Indian star has discovered a downside to the move or was just blitzing at this stage and so made the more natural move.}) 12... O-O ({This is often avoided for fear of coming under a kingside attack, but is quite playable. Instead,} 12... O-O-O 13. Nf3 g5 $1 {reaches quite a critical position - see Eric's analysis to Dreev-Azarov, Internet 2004, in the Archive.}) 13. Qe2 ({Only now does he move the queen to e2. White has designs on the e4-square and wants to avoid} 13. Nf3 f6 14. exf6 gxf6 15. Qe2 f5 {.}) 13... Rad8 ({Polgar is happy too to improve her pieces, but the critical move must be} 13... f6 {. After} 14. exf6 Nxf6 ({now} 14... gxf6 {is well met by} 15. e4) 15. Nf3 {Black is very solid, but White might be able to claim a small pull based on the weakness of e5,} ({while avoiding the early complexities of} 15. e4 c4 16. Bc2 e5 $5 {.})) 14. Nf3 f6 {It's not clear what else Black can do bar this break, but I dare say that Harikrishna didn't mind seeing it.} 15. exf6 Nxf6 ({Once again,} 15... gxf6 16. e4 {with useful central pressure, is not what Black wants to be doing.}) 16. Rad1 g6 17. Rfe1 {White isn't in a hurry, but why should he be with Black devoid of an effective pawn break? Harikrishna is content to improve his pieces and only then decide whether to go in for Ne5 or aim for e3-e4.} Rd6 18. Qf2 Qg7 19. Bc2 Qe7 {Polgar is unable to find a way to strengthen her position and is hoping for Ne5 when ...Nd7 may lead to a welcome simplification. Harikrishna, however, finds a stronger continuation.} 20. Qg3 Nh5 21. Qg4 Nf6 22. Qh3 Bc8 $6 23. Qh4 {More manoeuvring, but good manoeuvring by White as the black knight is now pinned against its queen.} Bb7 24. Ne5 {White is quick to exploit the pin, although admittedly his advantage still isn't huge as Black remains very solid.} Kg7 25. Rf1 Qd8 26. Qg5 Nd7 27. Qg4 {Following the classic principle that the side with more space and the safer king should keep pieces on.} Nf6 28. Qg5 Nd7 29. Qg3 $2 ({A slip, but Harikrishna may have been getting frustrated at being unable to break Black's passive if solid set-up. White should have repeated and then gone} 29. Qg4 Nf6 (29... Nxe5 $2 {is much weaker than in the game as e6 is loose and} 30. fxe5 Rc6 $2 31. Rxf8 Qxf8 32. Rf1 Qd8 33. Rf6 {would see White's pieces pouring in}) 30. Qg3 $1 {. After, say,} Qe7 ({this doesn't particularly help, but it's not so easy to find a good move Black must avoid} 30... Nd7 $4 31. Nxg6 $1) ({and} 30... Nh5 $2 31. Qg5 $1 {is also rather awkward, playing against the knight on the rim}) 31. Bxg6 hxg6 32. Qxg6+ Kh8 33. Rf3 Qg7 34. Rh3+ Nh7 35. Qh5 {White would have enjoyed a strong initiative.}) 29... Nxe5 $1 30. fxe5 Rd7 31. Qg4 Rdf7 {As ever, Polgar is quick to seize her chance, but she remains a touch worse on account of having the wrong-coloured bishop.} 32. Rf4 Qc7 $1 33. Qg5 ({Black's idea was} 33. Qxe6 Rxf4 34. exf4 Rxf4 {when the e-pawn isn't all that useful, as shown by} 35. Re1 Rf8 36. Qg4 Bc8 $1 37. Qg3 Be6 {with a firm blockade.}) 33... Qe7 34. Qg3 $2 ({White has underestimated Black's counterplay and failed to appreciate that the trend is against him. It was time to simplify with} 34. Qxe7 Rxe7 35. Rdf1 {, even if the pure bishop ending is surely just a draw.}) 34... Ba6 $1 {Removing the f1-square from the white rooks while teeing up a powerful pawn break.} 35. Rd2 d4 $1 36. h4 $4 ({Harikrishna suffers an uncharacteristic collapse, although such rushes of blood to the head are hardly uncommon when one misses a strong idea and has little time in which to regroup. The initiative may have changed hands, but White might still have maintained a rough balance with} 36. cxd4 cxd4 37. Rdxd4 Qc5 {and then the only move} 38. Qf2 $1 {.}) 36... dxe3 37. Rxf7+ Rxf7 38. Rd6 ({Now the newly-minted e-pawn is a killer, but so would} 38. Qxe3 Rf1+ 39. Kh2 Qxh4+ 40. Qh3 Qf4+ {have been.}) 38... e2 39. Kh2 Rf1 40. Bxg6 hxg6 41. h5 Qf7 42. Rd7 Qxd7 43. Qxg6+ Kh8 0-1

