1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 ({In the fourth game of this mini-match Polgar adopted Grivas' preferred move order,} 4... Qb6 {, which usually leads to a transposition after} 5. Nb3 ({otherwise,} 5. Nb5 $5 {is a sharp sideline}) ({and} 5. Nf3 {a fairly harmless retreat following} Nf6 6. Bd3 {Black might just move his e-pawn after all, but in A.Sokolov-Hamdouchi, French League 2010, he preferred an Accelerated Dragon-like approach:} d6 7. h3 g6 $5 8. c4 Bg7 9. Nc3 O-O 10. O-O Nd7 11. Nd5 Qd8 {and here already a draw was agreed, with Black clearly comfortably placed}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 Nf6 {.}) 5. Nc3 Qb6 $6 {There is a reason why most adherents of the Grivas Sicilian follow in their master's footsteps with 4...Qb6, rather than employ this risky move order.} 6. Nb3 ({Ivanchuk is happy to transpose, but} 6. Be3 $1 {looks like a rather dangerous pawn sacrifice, as we'll see in Lastin-Kononenko.}) 6... e6 7. Qe2 {Quite a critical test. White prepares to go long and doesn't commit his bishop to e3, so that he can meet ...Bb4 with Bd2. However, in this position he has quite a wide range of options, including too:} ({a)} 7. Bd3 a6 ({Grivas usually prefers} 7... d6 {, leading to a Scheveningen centre and a variation which receives plenty of coverage in his monograph on his pet opening}) 8. Qe2 ({White can also go short indeed,} 8. O-O Qc7 9. f4 d6 10. a4 {was preferred in Mista-Movsesian, Warsaw (rapid) 2009, when Black should prioritise kingside development, since} b6 11. Qe2 Bb7 12. Bd2 g6 $6 13. f5 Bg7 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Bc4 {saw Movsesian coming under pressure}) 8... d6 9. g4 $5 {is a sharp line, not dissimilar to the approach chosen by Ivanchuk, and one which we considered in Smits-Golod, Belgian League 2007.}) ({b)} 7. Be3 Qc7 8. f4 ({otherwise,} 8. a3 {prevents ... Bb4, but is a little slow and in Stavnjak-Kozul, Bihac 2010, Black obtained a comfortable-enough version of the Classical Scheveningen after} a6 9. Be2 Be7 10. O-O d6 11. f4 O-O 12. Bf3 b5 $1 13. g4 Re8 14. g5 Nd7 15. Qe1 Rb8 {,}) ({but even worse for White was the course of Strygin-Kononenko, Alushta 2010:} 8. Be2 $6 Bb4 9. f3 d5 10. O-O dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. fxe4 O-O {and Black was already at least fully equal}) 8... Bb4 ({here too a Scheveningen approach with} 8... d6 9. Qf3 a6 {is the alternative Reinhart-Hamdouchi, French League 2010, continued} 10. g4 h6 $5 11. h4 h5 12. g5 Ng4 13. Bh3 $1 Nxe3 14. Qxe3 g6 15. O-O-O Bd7 16. f5 O-O-O 17. Rhf1 {when White was slightly better, his pressure against e6 outweighing Black's control of the e5-square}) 9. Bd3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 {is another fairly critical line and one which subscribers will find in Caruana-Zhang Pengxiang, Merida 2008, in the Archive.}) ({c) If White is going to rule out ...Bb4 then} 7. a3 $5 {is his best method and after the De la Villa-endorsed} d6 8. Bf4 $1 {Black's position isn't so easy to handle.}) 7... Bb4 ({The most ambitious move and the one which Black would really like to make work. Again} 7... d6 {is the alternative and after} 8. g4 ({previously in Tukhaev-Oleksienko, Poltava 2008, we've covered the relatively slower} 8. Be3 Qc7 9. g4) 8... a6 ({as I've noted before,} 8... h6 9. h4 a6 10. Bh3 $1 g6 11. Be3 Qc7 12. O-O-O {looks to be in White's favour}) 9. Bg2 (9. Be3 Qc7 {would transpose to Tukhaev-Oleksienko,}) ({but perhaps here too White can delay Be3 for a while with} 9. g5 Nd7 10. h4 $5) 9... Be7 10. a4 {(not exactly the most consistent follow-up to the g4-advance)} Qc7 11. a5 O-O 12. O-O Nd7 $1 {Black was OK in Cabrera-Korneev, Donostia 2010.}) 8. Bd2 O-O 9. e5 $5 ({Ivanchuk ploughs ahead in the centre, but one might make a good case for putting the question to Black's bishop with} 9. a3 {, which we'll consider in Robson-Yermolinsky.}) 9... Ne8 {I wonder just how deeply Polgar had prepared this variation, since Black has two sharp and likely superior alternatives at this juncture:} ({a)} 9... Nd5 $5 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. O-O-O d6 {looks pretty suspicious for Black after} 12. Be3 $1 {, but} d4 $1 13. Bxd4 ({or} 13. Nxd4 dxe5 {when White should exchange on c6, since} 14. Ne6 $4 Qa5 15. Nxf8 Qxa2 16. c3 Bf5 $1 {gives Black a crushing attack}) 13... Nxd4 14. Rxd4 dxe5 15. Qxe5 Be6 {gave Black the bishop-pair and some compensation for the pawn in Nepomniachtchi-Haznedaroglu, Budva 2009. My suspicion is that it shouldn't be quite enough, but after Nepo's} 16. Qb5 {Black can do better than tamely exchange the queens on b5, with} Be1 $5 17. f3 Bf2 {one more challenging possibility.}) ({b)} 9... Bxc3 $5 10. bxc3 ({Black obtains decent counterplay with his knights too after} 10. Bxc3 Nd5 11. Bd2 Qc7 {, such as with the continuation given by Van Delft and Ris for ChessVibes:} 12. c4 Nde7 13. f4 a5 $1) 10... Nd5 11. c4 Nde7 12. Qe4 f5 13. exf6 Rxf6 (13... d5 $1 {looks more to the point, after which} 14. Qe3 Rxf6 15. Qxb6 axb6 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Bd3 Bf5 {may give Black just enough activity to offset his positional weaknesses}) 14. c5 Qc7 15. Bd3 {was unbalanced, but slightly better for White in Mazi-Kurajica, Sarajevo 2010.}) 10. O-O-O ({This natural novelty turns out well, but in the fourth game of this mini-match Ivanchuk was to prefer} 10. a3 $5 {and after} Be7 11. O-O-O a6 ({Black might break with} 11... d5 $5 {, as he did in Khamroev-Golod, Tashkent 2010, and after} 12. exd6 Nxd6 13. Be3 Qc7 14. Bf4 a6 15. g3 b6 16. h4 Bb7 17. Bg2 Na5 $1 {he was able to equalise, but White's play can likely be improved}) ({the other break is} 11... f6 {, but here} 12. exf6 Nxf6 13. Be3 Qc7 14. f4 {should enable White to claim an edge with g2-g4-g5 on its way}) 12. f4 Qc7 13. g3 b5 14. Bg2 {the position was exactly the same as in our main game, but for the inclusion of a3 and ...Be7. One might have thought that this would favour Black, but perhaps Ivanchuk considered the possibility of ...b4 less annoying than that of ...Nb4. More to the point, the early a3 makes an early ...f6 break less effective. In any event, though, after} Rb8 15. Ne4 $1 a5 ({White had prepared to meet} 15... b4 {with} 16. a4 Na5 17. Kb1 {, thereby keeping the queenside closed and so retaining a pleasant pull,}) ({but perhaps Black might have tried to break out with} 15... f6 $5) 16. Be3 a4 17. Nd4 Nxd4 18. Rxd4 {Ivanchuk had taken control of the position, although he eventually lost after a further 97 moves!}) 10... a6 $6 ({One reason for Ivanchuk's later preference for 10 a3 may well be that he had discovered in the interim that} 10... f6 $1 11. exf6 (11. f4 $6 fxe5 12. fxe5 Qc7 {sees e5 dropping off}) 11... Nxf6 {would have given Black reasonable counterplay: for instance, after} 12. g3 {Black might go} a5 $5 ({or} 12... Bxc3 13. Bxc3 Nd5 14. Be1 a5 {, making good use of the bishop's active location on b4 in both cases.})) 11. f4 $1 {Now, however, White begins to take control, as it quickly becomes clear that Polgar's plan to untangle her queenside, while quite natural, is just too slow here.} Qc7 12. g3 b5 13. Bg2 Bb7 14. Ne4 $1 {White's position is a model of harmony, whereas it's clear that Black is going to suffer from her weaknesses down the d-file.} Rc8 15. Kb1 Be7 16. Be3 Nb4 $5 ({True to her style Polgar tries to counterattack. The alternative was to try to untangle with} 16... d6 {, but then White has} 17. exd6 (17. c3 $5 {is also quite tempting, since then} dxe5 18. Nbc5 {threatens to invade on d7}) 17... Nxd6 18. Nec5 {, netting the bishop-pair while retaining a pretty pleasant advantage.}) 17. Rd2 ({Covering d2 while preparing to double rooks, but one can also make a pretty good case for} 17. c3 $5 Nd5 18. Bd4 d6 {and now White might well decide that it's time to sound the charge with} 19. h4 $1 {.}) 17... Bd5 $1 {Polgar is quick to seize the chance to activate her bishop, but even this isn't enough to bring her close to enjoying rough equality.} 18. Rhd1 $6 ({A little too routine. Instead White should have offered to exchange the bishops and after} 18. Nc3 $1 Bc4 19. Qf2 {he would have retained control and the upper hand.}) 18... Bc4 19. Qf2 Nd5 20. Bc5 $1 {Just when it appeared that White was perhaps beginning to drift, Ivanchuk jolts awake and prudently plays for exchanges.} Bxb3 $2 ({The bishop on c4 actually becomes a liability after the natural} 20... Bxc5 21. Nbxc5 f5 $2 22. exf6 Nexf6 23. b3 {.}) ({However, as the text doesn't really weaken White's king, Black should have preferred the natural} 20... d6 {which would have given her reasonable counterplay: for example,} 21. Bd4 $5 (21. exd6 Nxd6 22. Nxd6 Bxd6 23. Bxd6 Qxd6 {doesn't really give White anything, especially with} 24. Na5 $6 {well met by} Qb4) 21... a5 $1 22. Nc3 Nxc3+ 23. Bxc3 b4 24. Bd4 a4 {and Black will open lines on the queenside, although whether this would have been enough to give her the upper hand is somewhat more debatable.}) 21. Bxe7 Bxa2+ 22. Kxa2 Nxe7 23. Rxd7 Qa5+ 24. Kb1 Nd5 {It must have been this return of the knight to d5 which attracted Polgar to the sequence of exchanges. Unfortunately for her, though, White is able to deal with the threat of 25... Nb4 while taking control of the board once again.} 25. Rd3 Rc4 $6 ({Polgar wants to keep b4 free for a knight, but she should really have preferred} 25... b4 {, preventing White's rook from swinging across to a3, even if this wouldn't have been all that pleasant after} 26. Nd6 Rc7 27. Rd8 $1 Rxc2 28. Qxc2 Qxd8 29. Bxd5 exd5 30. Rxd5 {.}) 26. Qd2 $2 ({However, this is a clear slip. Necessary and very logical would have been} 26. Ra3 $1 {and after} Ra4 (26... Qb6 27. Qxb6 Nxb6 28. Rdd3 {also leaves White in full control}) 27. Rxa4 bxa4 28. Nc5 $1 {it transpires that Black will come up badly short on the queenside.}) 26... Qb6 ({Hardly terrible, but Black would likely have done even better with the consistent} 26... Nb4 $1 27. Ra3 Qb6 {, threatening a large check on g1 as well as the c-pawn. White can try} 28. Bf3 $1 {, but after} Qg1+ $1 (28... Nxc2 29. Rc3 Nb4 30. Rxc4 bxc4 31. Qd4 {enables White to regain his pawn with some advantage}) 29. Qd1 Qxd1+ 30. Rxd1 Rxc2 31. Rb3 Rc4 32. Nd6 Nxd6 33. exd6 Rd8 {White has no more than sufficient play for the pawn.}) 27. b3 Rc8 $6 ({Black wants to trap the rook, but I wouldn't have been so keen to provoke a dangerous-looking exchange sacrifice on d5. Instead after} 27... Rc7 28. Rxc7 Nexc7 29. Kb2 {White is a little better, but Black remains pretty solidly placed and not without counterchances.}) 28. Kb2 Qc6 29. Ra7 ({This move is hardly a mistake, but may well not have been the best practical decision, assuming that both players were already running low on time. Easier to play would have been} 29. R3xd5 $1 exd5 30. Rxd5 Qb6 31. f5 {, leaving White dominating the position and with excellent play for the exchange.}) 29... b4 30. Qf2 Qb5 $6 31. f5 $6 ({One shouldn't be too critical, as it appears that time trouble was by now playing a major factor. Here, though, White missed a good chance with} 31. Rd1 $1 Qb8 32. Ra1 {, picking up the a-pawn, as pointed out by Van Delft and Ris.}) 31... Nc3 $5 32. Nxc3 bxc3+ 33. Kb1 Qxe5 34. Rxa6 $2 ({One can understand Ivanchuk's desire not to open the f-file, but with} 34. fxe6 fxe6 35. Qe3 {he would have retained a pull, such as with} Qxe3 36. Rxe3 Rf2 37. Bh3 Kf8 38. Rxa6 Rd8 39. Rxc3 {.}) 34... exf5 {Now Black is a pawn to the good and with the safer king. White faces a tough defensive task, but for the time being Ivanchuk shows that he is up to it:} 35. Qe3 Qb5 36. Ra4 Rb8 ({Natural enough, but Black might have preferred to invade down the e-file with} 36... Nf6 $5 37. Rxc3 Rxc3 ({or} 37... Rce8) 38. Qxc3 Qe2 {, thereby retaining the initiative.}) 37. Ka2 Nf6 38. Rxc3 Rfe8 39. Qf4 $1 {Good defence, keeping an eye on both the f5-pawn and the rook on b8, not to mention various central squares.} Qe2 40. Bc6 Red8 41. Qxf5 ({Here Ivanchuk must have lost on time for the result is certainly correct, or perhaps the live-board feed couldn't cope with whatever frantic blitzing may have followed. Actually White isn't doing too badly here, with} 41. Qxf5 Qxh2 42. Kb2 Qe2 43. Ra7 {a double-edged position in which his queenside majority and superior minor piece may even count - objectively, not practically speaking - for more than his more exposed king position.}) 0-1

