1. d4 {Psakhis,L Psakhis Psakhis Seirawan,Yasser} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. O-O Nc6 {It was a very serious question what to do in open ing against Vasiliy Ivanchuk. His opening knowledge are really tremendous. After long preparation we choose very rare variation in Gruenfeld defence.} 7. Nc3 {This move was a big suprise for us, but not unpleasant one. Usual 7.cxd5 xd5 8.c3 looks more promising for White.} (7. Ne5 {(Seirawan,Yasser) Ü 67/533}) 7... dxc4 8. d5 Nb4 $1 {Stronger than} (8... Na5 9. Qa4 c6 10. dxc6 Nxc6 11. Qxc4 Qa5 12. Qh4 Rd8 13. Bh6 Qh5 14. Qxh5 Nxh5 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Rfd1 Be6 17. Ng5 $14 {1-0 Roos,J-Schmitt,A/French tch 1994 (29)}) 9. e4 $2 {[%csl Rd3] N/ New and not very good move.White produced weakness on d3 and closed diagonal h1-a8. It was played before N/ New and not very good move.White produced weakness on d3 and closed diagonal h1-a8. It was played before Seirawan,Yasser: 'N'} (9. Ne5 {and still Black has to fight for equality. For example:} c6 (9... e6 {Seirawan,Yasser: '!'} 10. dxe6 (10. e4 $2 {Seirawan,Yasser} Nxe4 $1) 10... Bxe6 11. Bxb7 (11. Qa4 Nfd5 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Nxc4 Nb6 14. Nxb6 axb6 15. Qc2 c6 $10 {0-1 Di,M-Maier/Switzerland 1986 (46)}) 11... Rb8 12. Bg2 $14 (12. Bf3 {Seirawan,Yasser} Nfd5 $1 13. Nxd5 Bxe5 14. Nxb4 Rxb4 $17)) (9... Nfxd5 $2 {Seirawan,Yasser} 10. Nxd5 Nxd5 11. Nxf7 Rxf7 12. Bxd5 $16) 10. dxc6 Nxc6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qa4 (12. Bxc6 Bh3) 12... Be6 13. Bxc6 Rc8 14. Be3 a5 15. Rfd1 Qc7 16. Bf3 $14 {½-½ Voskanian,V-Howell,J/Cannes op 1995 (35)}) 9... e6 $1 {Very strong and unexpected move for Ivanchuk and it made big influence for a game. He waits just natural 9... c6.} 10. Bg5 (10. Qa4 a5 11. a3 Bd7 12. Qd1 Nd3 $17) 10... h6 11. Be3 (11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. e5 Qd8 13. dxe6 Bxe6 {also not to promising for White. also not promising for White}) 11... Nd3 {Seirawan,Yasser: '!'} 12. dxe6 (12. Ne1 {Maybe stonger} Nxe1 (12... Nxb2 13. Qd2 exd5 14. exd5 (14. Qxb2 d4 15. Bd2 dxc3 16. Bxc3 c6 $17) 14... Nd3 15. Nxd3 cxd3 16. Bxh6 Bxh6 17. Qxh6 Bf5 18. h3 $13) 13. Rxe1 exd5 14. exd5 Bf5 $15) 12... Bxe6 13. h3 {Seirawan,Yasser: '?!'} (13. Nd2 $2 Ng4 $19) (13. Qc2 {Seirawan,Yasser} Ng4 14. Bd2 $17) (13. Nd4 $6 {Seirawan,Yasser} Bg4 14. f3 Nxe4 $1) 13... Qd7 $5 ({Good alternative was also} 13... Nxb2 14. Qxd8 (14. Qb1 Nd3 15. Qxb7 Qd7 $17) 14... Rfxd8 15. Rab1 Ng4 $5 16. Bc1 (16. Bd2 Rxd2 17. Nxd2 Bxc3 18. hxg4 Bxd2 19. Rxb2 c3 20. Rc2 Bc4 $19) (16. Rxb2 Nxe3 17. fxe3 Bxc3 18. Rxb7 Ba5) (16. hxg4 Bxc3) 16... Bxc3 17. Bxb2 (17. hxg4 Nd3 18. Rxb7 c5 19. Bxh6 Bxg4 $17)) 14. Qd2 {What else?} (14. Kh2 Nxb2) (14. e5 Nd5 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 $19) 14... Bxh3 15. Bxh6 Rae8 $17 {I'll be very surprised if Judit even dreamed about position like this, just after 15 moves , before game. Ivanchuk was so disappointed now, that I suspect it was mutial desire to finish game as soon as possible.} 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Ng5 {Lose immediately, but} (17. Bxh3 Qxh3 18. Ng5 Qg4 19. Kg2 Nh5 {not much better.}) 17... Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Nh5 $1 {[%cal Rd3f4,Rh5f4,Rf8h8]} 19. Qe3 (19. Rfd1 Qg4 20. Nh3 f5 $5 21. exf5 (21. Qg5 Ndf4+ $1 $19) 21... Rxf5 $19) 19... Ndf4+ $1 {Vasili decided stop his sufferings and resign immedeately.Any case his position after} (19... Ndf4+ 20. Kf3 (20. gxf4 Qg4+ 21. Kh2 Rh8 {Seirawan,Yasser: '-+'} 22. Nh3 Nxf4 $19) (20. Kh2 Rh8) (20. Kg1 Qg4 21. Nf3 Nxg3 $19) 20... f6 21. Nh7 {Ivanchuk} (21. gxf4 fxg5 22. f5 Rxf5+ $1 23. exf5 Qxf5+ 24. Kg2 Nf4+ $19) 21... Kxh7 (21... g5 22. gxf4 Qh3+ 23. Ke2 Nxf4+ 24. Kd2) 22. gxf4 Qh3+ 23. Ke2 Qg4+ $19 {totally hopeless.}) 0-1

