1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. c3 Bg7 13. Nxb5 $5 axb5 14. Bxb5 {A critical and fairly topical continuation, albeit one in sore need of tests at elite level.} Bd7 15. exf5 Nb8 ({I've quite liked this in the past, but Kaidanov's active response may mean that it isn't more promising than the more common} 15... O-O {. Then} 16. O-O {leads to an important divide:} (16. Qg4 Kh8 $1 {was OK for Black in Istratescu-Moiseenko, Montreal 2008 - see the Archive}) 16... Z0 (16... Nb8 $2 {is now too late on account of} 17. f6 $1 Bxf6 18. Nxf6+ Qxf6 19. Bxd7 Nxd7 20. Qg4+ Kh8 21. Qxd7 {, which regained the piece with a near-decisive advantage in Mauron-Lehmann, Lenk 2010. Black was rated 2321 in that encounter, so evidently the delayed piece sacrifice on b5 is still catching some players out.}) (16... Rb8 17. a4 Re8 ({Tony has suggested that} 17... Qg5 $5 {might not be so clear, but after} 18. f4 $5 exf4 19. h4 $1 Qd8 20. Rxf4 {White has the initiative indeed, the line} Bh6 21. Rc4 Ne5 22. Qh5 $1 Nxc4 $2 23. Qxh6 Bxf5 24. Rf1 {is one which Black must not follow in its entirety}) 18. Qg4 Kh8 19. Qe4 $1 f6 $1 20. b4 ({having restricted Black's bishops and obtained a bind, I suspect that White may do better with} 20. Rfd1 $5 Ne7 21. Nxe7 Qxe7 22. c4 {followed by a queenside advance}) 20... Ne7 21. Bxd7 Qxd7 22. Nxe7 Rxe7 $1 ({a better defence than} 22... Qxe7 23. a5 Rec8 24. Rfb1 $1 {, which left White very fast on the queenside in Almasi-Wang Yue, Paks 2006, as shown by Tony's notes}) 23. Rfd1 Qc7 24. a5 (24. Rd3 $6 d5 $1 25. Qxd5 e4 26. Re3 Rd8 {, seizing the initiative is Black's big idea,}) ({but Tony's} 24. Qd5 $5 {is surely more challenging after} Qxc3 25. Qxd6 {I can only assume that Timofeev has found that} Qc7 {ought to hold: for example,} 26. b5 Qxd6 27. Rxd6 Reb7 28. Rc1 Kg8 {and it's not obvious that White can make progress}) 24... Qxc3 25. b5 Ra7 $1 {(again we see how well placed the king's rook is on the second rank for defensive purposes, especially in contrast to Almasi-Wang Yue)} 26. Qa4 {(trying to keep the pawns and threatening to advance with 27 b6)} Bh6 $1 27. Rxd6 ({now} 27. b6 Rxb6 28. Qe8+ Kg7 29. axb6 Rxa1 30. Qd7+ $1 Kg8 31. Qe6+ {only leads to a draw}) 27... Bf8 $5 ({Black is happy to give up a fourth pawn to activate his bishop, but he may well also be able to go} 27... Rxb5 28. a6 Bf8) 28. Rxf6 Bc5 29. Rc6 Rg8 {(the counterattack arrives)} 30. Rf1 Rag7 31. Rg6 $1 {(essential)} hxg6 32. Qh4+ Rh7 33. Qf6+ Rhg7 34. Qh4+ Rh7 {1/2-1/2 Cheparinov-Timofeev, Moscow 2010. An important clash between two noted theoreticians and the ball appears to be in White's court after 16...Rb8.}) (16... e4 $5 {has been theory's main recommendation and was recently adopted by Kotronias:} 17. a4 Re8 18. Qg4 ({White can also explore} 18. Re1 $5 {, but in Yeo-Kwiatkowski, British League 2010,} Re5 ({Cox's} 18... Kh8 19. Qg4 Re5 20. Ne3 Qf6 {is another decent set-up for Black}) 19. Ne3 Ne7 $1 20. Bxd7 Qxd7 21. f6 Bxf6 22. Qg4+ Qxg4 23. Nxg4 Re6 24. Nxf6+ Rxf6 25. Rxe4 Re6 {saw Black hold the endgame without any real difficulty}) 18... Kh8 19. Rad1 Rc8 $1 ({Kotronias' idea against his own favourite line for why I believe that} 19... Re5 $6 20. Ne3 $1 {is imprecise see Kotronias-Papadopoulos, Rhodes 2008, in the Archive}) ({we've also previously considered} 19... Rb8 20. Rfe1 Qc8 {when I've suggested that} 21. Rxe4 $5 Rxe4 22. Qxe4 {may be best, but is hardly a total panacea as Black's bishops then become quite active after} Bxf5 {and if} 23. Qf4 $5 {, then maybe} Bc2 24. Rd2 Qe6) 20. Rfe1 ({it seems strange to retreat with} 20. Ne3 {here, and after} Ne5 $1 21. Qxe4 Nc4 22. Qc2 {Black has a few decent options, including the simple} Bxb5 23. axb5 Nxe3 24. fxe3 Bh6) 20... Re5 21. Ne3 Qf6 {(in comparison with Kotronias-Papadoulos, White's rook has moved to e1 and Black's to c8 that should be in Black's favour as his d7-bishop is free to move, but matters are hardly at all clear here)} 22. Qh5 $6 ({played a la Kotronias-Papadoulos, but I'm not convinced and feel that White should investigate the complex late middlegames arising from} 22. Qe2 $5 Bxf5 23. Nxf5 Qxf5 24. Rxd6) 22... Bxf5 23. Nxf5 Rxf5 24. Qh3 Ne5 $1 {(the pin isn't going to pose any problems for Black)} 25. Rxe4 Rg8 26. Rf1 Qg6 27. Re3 $2 {(a blunder, but White was already under pressure)} Rh5 28. Qg3 Qh6 29. Re4 Rxh2 $1 {0-1 Delchev-Kotronias, French League 2009.})) 16. a4 $1 ({Undoubtedly critical. We've considered a number of alternatives here before with the most important being} 16. Qg4 {- see Almasi-Ni Hua, Reggio Emilia 2009, in the Archive. Since that loss Almasi appears to have given up the piece sacrifice and was last seen preferring 11 g3 when faced with the Sveshnikov.}) 16... O-O ({The critical continuation} 16... Bxb5 $5 17. axb5 Rxa1 18. Qxa1 {has been shown to help White seize the initiative. That said, matters are hardly totally clear here and Tregubov has been happy to defend the black cause:} Nd7 ({or} 18... O-O 19. Qa3 $1 e4 20. O-O Be5 21. Qa4 {with advantage}) 19. Qa7 O-O 20. O-O e4 21. Re1 Be5 $5 ({previously we've noted that after} 21... Re8 22. b6 {Black is probably in trouble, but might try} Be5 $5 23. Qc7 Nf6 24. Nxf6+ Bxf6 25. Qc6 Qb8 26. Ra1 Rc8 27. Qd7 Kg7 28. Ra7 Rf8 {still with chances to defend}) 22. b6 (22. Rxe4 $5 Nf6 23. Ne7+ Kg7 24. Rc4 {is a critical alternative, but} Qb8 $1 25. Qxb8 Rxb8 26. f4 d5 {seems OK for Black thanks to his precise king move: with the king on h8} 27. Rc6 {would win material}) 22... Nf6 23. Ne7+ Kg7 24. Qc7 $6 ({unimpressive, although} 24. Nc6 Qe8 25. Nxe5 Qxe5 26. b7 Nd7 {halts the b-pawn and prepares to annexe the f5-pawn, which should be OK for Black}) 24... Qd7 25. Qxd7 Nxd7 26. Nd5 Nf6 27. Nxf6 Kxf6 28. g4 $2 d5 {left White in some trouble with all his weak pawns in Najer-Tregubov, Russian Team Championship 2009.}) 17. O-O Bxf5 $2 ({We've previously criticised this move. Dare I say that Polgar didn't perhaps prepare this version of the piece sacrifice on b5 in as much detail as she should have done for this Sicilian-themed match? Instead} 17... Bxb5 18. axb5 Rxa1 19. Qxa1 Nd7 20. Qa7 {would still have taken play back into our last note.}) 18. Qf3 Be6 19. Ne7+ $5 ({Kaidanov is happy to rely on his a-pawn after grabbing the rook and this might be even more precise than} 19. Nf6+ Qxf6 20. Qxa8 Nd7 21. Qc6 $1 {, as I've previously endorsed.}) 19... Qxe7 20. Qxa8 d5 21. a5 Nd7 22. Qa7 {Black's centre is impressive, but that a-pawn is just too quick.} Qd6 23. a6 e4 ({Thematic, but again too slow. Indeed, Black had to seize her chance to complicate the struggle with} 23... Nc5 $1 24. b4 Nb3 25. Ra3 d4 {, although after} 26. Rd1 {White should remain on top.}) 24. Bxd7 $6 ({I wouldn't have wanted to cede the bishop-pair so lightly and} 24. Qb7 $1 {would have been strong:} Rb8 (24... Ne5 25. a7 Nf3+ 26. gxf3 Bh3 {looks dangerous, but comes up short after} 27. Kh1 Qf4 28. Be2 exf3 29. Rg1 fxe2 30. Qxd5 {, as later pointed out by Kaidanov}) 25. Qc6 Qf8 26. a7 Ra8 27. Rfd1 {and Black is unlikely to survive.}) 24... Bxd7 25. Qe3 f5 {Black must obtain some counterplay.} 26. Rfd1 Bc6 27. a7 Ba8 28. Qe2 {The queen must return to the queenside, but I suspect that Kaidanov may have been a little uncomfortable around here, as Black's own pawn mass must not be underestimated.} Qc5 29. Qa6 f4 $1 {White's major pieces are invading and he is still somewhat better, so Black cannot avoid to delay.} 30. Ra5 Qe7 31. Raxd5 $2 ({A typical human decision and... a mistake! Instead the calm} 31. Rb5 e3 32. Rb8 exf2+ 33. Kf1 {would have maintained control, although one can understand why Kaidanov was reluctant to allow this in the run up to the time control.}) 31... e3 $5 ({Rolling the dice, but Polgar should have inserted the obvious} 31... Bxd5 32. Rxd5 {before going} e3 $1 {when White must take care:} 33. Kf1 (33. a8=Q $2 {gives White an extra queen, but} e2 $1 {will force him to return the queen without delay}) 33... Ra8 34. Qc4 Qf7 {and now White should force a draw with} 35. Qb3 $1 Rxa7 36. Qb8+ Bf8 37. Rg5+ Kh8 38. Qe5+ Bg7 39. Qb8+ Bf8 {.}) 32. Qd6 exf2+ ({Removing the dangerous pawn with} 32... Qxa7 {was also quite possible, but time trouble was already playing a role and Polgar's choice creates a neat threat. Then} 33. Qe6+ Qf7 (33... Kh8 34. Rd8 {can only be dangerous for Black}) 34. Qxf7+ Kxf7 35. Rd7+ Kg6 {would have left everything to play for.}) 33. Kf1 $1 ({Essential, as Kaidanov avoids the little trap:} 33. Kxf2 $4 Qe3+ 34. Kf1 f3 {with a devastating onslaught.}) 33... Qe3 $6 ({I dare say that the ever-combative Polgar rejected} 33... Qe4 34. Rg5 Re8 {on account of} 35. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 36. Qd4+ Qxd4 37. Rxd4 Re7 {, but Black should have been happy enough with this level endgame.}) 34. R5d3 Bxg2+ $2 ({Tempting, but too optimistic and Black had to prefer the more restrained} 34... Qe4 $1 35. Rf3 Qa4 {, which would have kept matters still far from clear.}) 35. Kxg2 f3+ {This looks very dangerous, but the king has a square.} 36. Kh3 $1 Qe2 37. a8=Q f1=Q+ 38. Rxf1 Qxf1+ 39. Kh4 Qe1+ $2 ({After this Black is out of checks and White wins, just as happened in every game of the match! The last try was} 39... Bf6+ $1 {and after} 40. Qxf6 Qf2+ 41. Kg4 Qg2+ 42. Kf4 Qxh2+ 43. Kxf3 Rxa8 44. Qg5+ Kh8 45. Rd8+ Rxd8 46. Qxd8+ Kg7 {Black would still have been able to pose a few technical difficulties in the queen ending.}) 40. Kh5 $1 1-0

