[EventCountry "RUS"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. Nbd2 Be7 10. c3 O-O 11. Bc2 f5 12. Nb3 Qd7 13. Nbd4 Nxd4 14. Nxd4 c5 15. Nxe6 Qxe6 16. f3 Ng5 17. a4 Rad8 18. axb5 axb5 19. Ra7 Rd7 20. Rxd7 Qxd7 {Judith has some experience with the white pieces here.} 21. f4 $5 {A novelty. It seems that Karjakin's idea was to prevent Carlsen's 21...f4, but it gives Black an option of 21...Ne4.} ({Earlier White played} 21. Be3 {Naiditsch, A  (2693)-Mikhalevski, V (2608)/Budva 2009/[Mikhalevski, V]. Polgar tested this line against Carlsen, who played} f4 {in 2007.} ({However} 21... Ne6 {looks like a safer way to equality.} 22. f4 g6 23. g3 (23. g4 $5) 23... Rd8 24. Bf2 d4 25. cxd4 Nxd4 26. Bxd4 Qxd4+ 27. Qxd4 Rxd4 28. Rd1 $11 {and soon a draw was agreed in Gashimov, V (2730)-Sutovsky, E (2660)/Poikovsky 2009.})) ({Judith also tried} 21. Kh1 {in Polgar, J (2710)-Sokolov, I (2670)/Hoogeveen 2006.}) 21... Ne4 {A necessity.} (21... Ne6 $6 {would be strongly met by} 22. g4 $1 {with the initiative.}) 22. Bxe4 (22. g4 $5 {also looks interesting, but Karjakin had a different idea in mind.}) 22... fxe4 23. f5 {This idea is definitely the fruit of Sergey's home preparation.} d4 $1 {Black has to compensate White's play on the kingside by counterplay in the centre.} (23... Rxf5 $2 24. Rxf5 Qxf5 25. Qxd5+ Kf8 26. Be3 $16 {is clearly in White's favor.}) 24. f6 $1 gxf6 $5 {It's difficult to blame Black for this natural move even if the cold-blooded 24... Bd8 is slightly better.} (24... Bd8 $1 25. Be3 $1 gxf6 26. cxd4 cxd4 27. Bxd4 Qe6 $1 {A difficult move, which Black would have to find.} (27... fxe5 $2 {leads to White's advantage after both} 28. Rxf8+ (28. Qb3+ Rf7 29. Qg3+ Kf8 30. Rxf7+ Kxf7 31. Qxe5 $14) 28... Kxf8 29. Bc5+ Ke8 30. Qh5+ Qf7 31. Qxe5+ Kd7 32. Qxe4 $14) 28. exf6 Bxf6 29. Bxf6 Rxf6 30. Qd8+ Kg7 31. Qxf6+ Qxf6 32. Rxf6 Kxf6 33. Kf2 Ke5 34. Ke3 h5 $1 $11 {and Black seems to keep a draw.}) 25. Bh6 Rf7 ({The only alternative is the following exchange sacrifice:} 25... fxe5 26. Bxf8 Bxf8 {Despite Black's strong centre White seems to be able to obtain the better chances.} 27. cxd4 exd4 28. Qb3+ $1 c4 29. Qg3+ Qg7 30. Qb8 $1 b4 31. Qc8 $1 c3 32. Qe6+ Kh8 33. Qe8 $1 Kg8 34. Qxe4 $14) 26. cxd4 (26. Qe1 $1 {seems to secure an extra pawn as the following line proves.} Bf8 $1 27. Qg3+ Kh8 28. Bxf8 Rxf8 29. exf6 Qf7 (29... e3 30. Qe5 $1 Qf7 31. cxd4 cxd4 32. Qxd4 {transposes to 29...Qf7.}) 30. cxd4 cxd4 31. Qe5 e3 32. Qxd4 e2 33. Re1 Qxf6 34. Qxf6+ Rxf6 35. Rxe2 $14 {where White's chances for a win are equal to Black's chances for a draw.}) 26... fxe5 $1 {This intermediate move is most precise.} ({The obvious alternative} 26... cxd4 {is slightly worse and promise White better chances after} 27. Qe1 Bf8 28. Qg3+ Kh8 29. Bxf8 Rxf8 30. exf6 d3 $5 (30... e3 31. Qe5 $1 $14 {transposes to 26. Qe1.}) 31. Qe5 $1 Qe8 $1 32. Qe7 $1 Rg8 $1 33. Qb4 $1 Rf8 34. g3 $1 $14 {and Black experience certain problems.}) 27. Rxf7 Kxf7 28. Qh5+ Kg8 29. dxe5 (29. d5 Bf6 $1 {also looks ok for Black.}) 29... c4 {The most logical.} 30. e6 {White doesn't seem to have anything better.} ({For instance,} 30. Be3 b4 31. Qe2 Qd3 32. Qg4+ Kh8 33. Qc8+ Kg7 34. Qg4+ {with the perpetual.}) 30... Qa7+ 31. Kf1 Qa1+ 32. Kf2 ({or} 32. Ke2 Qxb2+ 33. Bd2 Qf6 34. Qe8+ Bf8 35. Bg5 Qb2+ 36. Bd2 Qf6 $11) 32... Qxb2+ 33. Kg3 Qf6 (33... Bd6+ 34. Kh3 Qf6 35. Qe8+ Bf8 36. Bxf8 Qf5+ {transposes to the game.}) 34. Qe8+ Bf8 35. Bxf8 Qg5+ {Black forces a draw by perpetual.} 36. Kh3 (36. Kf2 Qd2+ $11) 36... Qf5+ 37. Kh4 Qf4+ 38. Kh3 Qf5+ 39. g4 {This attempt to play for a win doesn't change much.} Qxf8 40. Qd7 Qf3+ 41. Kh4 Qf6+ 42. g5 Qf4+ 43. Kh5 Qf3+ 44. Kh4 Qf4+ {Almost a perfectly played game by both players. I can only mention two slight inaccuracies: Black's 24...gxf6 (24...Bd8!) and 26. cxd4 (26.Qe1!). It seems that after Karjakin's novelty the move 22.g4!? is White's best chance to fight for the advantage.} 1/2-1/2

