1. d4 {Psakhis,L Psakhis} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nc6 7. d5 {Old move, nowdays more common 7.c3 and just after 7... a6 8.d5} Na5 8. Nfd2 c5 ({Deserves attention} 8... c6 $5 {for example} 9. a3 cxd5 10. cxd5 Ng4 11. Ra2 Ne5 12. b4 Nac4 13. Nxc4 Nxc4 14. a4 Bd7 $11 {0-1 Glavica,Z-Kosanski,S/Zagreb 1995 (40)}) 9. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4]} Nd7 ({White,s chances are slightly better after} 9... b6 10. b4 (10. Ra2 Ba6 11. Qc2 b5 12. b3 bxc4 13. bxc4 Rb8 14. Bb2 Qb6 15. Bc3 Nd7 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 $13 {½-½ Vucic,M-Goldenberg,D/Toronto op 1998 (36)}) 10... Nb7 11. Bb2 {for example} e5 12. dxe6 fxe6 13. Qb3 Qe7 14. Nc3 Rb8 15. Rad1 cxb4 16. axb4 a5 17. Nce4 $14 {1-0 Burmakin,V-Lobzhanidze,D/Kstovo op 1997 (47)}) 10. Ra2 Ne5 $5 11. Qc2 (11. b3 Rb8 12. a4 a6 13. Na3 e6 14. Bb2 exd5 15. cxd5 f5 16. f4 Ng4 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Qa1+ Kg8 $13 {1-0 Kharitonov,A-Fedorov,A/Sochi 1997 (46)}) 11... Bf5 (11... Bd7 $5 12. h3 (12. b4 cxb4 13. axb4 Naxc4 14. Nxc4 Rc8 15. Nbd2 Bb5 $5 $11) 12... b5 13. cxb5 Bxb5 14. Nc3 Qb6 15. b4 (15. b3 Ba6 16. Rb2 {½-½ Kasimdzhanov,R-Antoniewski,R/Wch B20 Zagan 1997}) 15... cxb4 16. axb4 Nac4 17. Nxb5 Qxb5 18. Nxc4 Nxc4 19. Bf4 Rfc8 $11 {½-½ Kochiev,A-Popovic,P/Krk 1976 (37)}) (11... Bg4 12. Re1 b5 13. h3 Bd7 14. b3 bxc4 15. bxc4 Qc8 16. f4 Bf5 17. e4 Bxh3 18. fxe5 Bxe5 $132 {0-1 Husek,Z-Kozma,K/HUN t-ch 1995 (60)}) 12. e4 Bd7 13. b3 $5 {N} (13. h3 b5 14. b3 bxc4 15. bxc4 Qe8 16. a4 Rb8 $15 {0-1 Grushka-Roselli,B/Progreso op 1986 (37)}) 13... b5 14. Bb2 (14. f4 Ng4 15. Nf3 bxc4 16. bxc4 f5 $5 {[%csl Ya5,Yg7]}) 14... bxc4 15. bxc4 Rb8 16. Bc3 (16. f4 $2 Rxb2 $1 17. Rxb2 Nexc4 18. Nxc4 Nxc4 $17) 16... Qb6 {Black pieces are active, but, unfortunately for them, Karpov finds good way to finish development, and ,due to advantage in space, get initiative.} (16... f5 17. f4 Ng4 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Qc3+ Kg8 20. e5 $36) 17. a4 $1 {[%cal Gb1a3]} (17. f4 Nexc4 (17... Ng4 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. h3 $14) 18. Nxc4 Nxc4 19. Bxg7 Ne3 20. Qc1 (20. Qf2 Qxb1 (20... Nxf1 21. Rb2 Qd8 22. Bxf8 Ne3 $1 23. Bxe7 $1 Rxb2 24. Qxb2 Qxe7 $13) 21. Rxb1 Rxb1+ 22. Bf1 Rxf1+ 23. Qxf1 Nxf1 24. Bxf8 Nxg3 25. hxg3 Kxf8 26. Rb2 a5 $1 $11) 20... c4 (20... Nxf1 21. Rb2 $1) 21. Rb2 (21. Bxf8 Nxf1+ 22. Kxf1 Qxb1 23. Rc2 Qxc1+ 24. Rxc1 Kxf8 $17) 21... Qc5 22. Bc3 $1 (22. Bxf8 $2 Rxb2 23. Qxb2 $140 Nd1+ $19) 22... Rxb2 23. Bxb2 Rb8 $132) 17... e6 (17... Bh6 $5) 18. h3 {[%cal Rf2f4]} exd5 19. cxd5 Rfc8 {[%cal Gc5c4]} (19... f5 20. f4 Nf7 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Qc3+ Kg8 23. e5 $16) 20. Kh2 {[%cal Gc3a5,Gf2f4]} (20. Bxa5 $5 Qxa5 21. f4 c4 $1 22. fxe5 Bxe5 23. Kh2 c3 24. Nf3 $14) (20. f4 c4+ (20... Nec4 21. Nxc4 Nxc4 22. Bxg7 Ne3 23. Qc3 Nxf1 24. Bh6 $18) (20... Nd3 $2 21. Bxa5 $18) 21. Kh2 Nd3 22. Bxa5 (22. Bxg7 Nb4 $1 $17) 22... Qxa5 23. Nxc4 Nb2 $5 24. Nxa5 Rxc2 $44) 20... Nb3 21. a5 (21. f4 Nd4 $1 22. Qd1 (22. Bxd4 $2 cxd4 23. Qd1 Nd3 $17) 22... Nd3 23. Nc4 Qb3 24. Qxb3 Rxb3 25. Nxd6 Rcb8 26. Rd2 Nb4 $5 $13) 21... Qb7 $2 {Serious mistake, Judit forgot about 23.d1!. Much stronger was to play} (21... Qb5 $1 {with possible continuations} 22. Rb2 (22. f4 Nd4 $1 23. Qd1 Nd3 24. Na3 $8 Qa6 25. Ndc4 Nb4 $13) 22... Nxd2 $5 23. Rxb5 (23. Nxd2 Qe2 $36) 23... Nxf1+ 24. Bxf1 Bxb5 25. Bxb5 Rxb5 26. Nd2 Rcb8 (26... c4 27. Qa4 a6 28. Bxe5 Bxe5 29. Nxc4 $14) 27. Bxe5 Bxe5 28. Nc4 Kf8 $13) 22. f4 $1 Nd4 23. Qd1 $1 {[%csl Re5]} Bb5 $8 (23... Nd3 24. Nc4 {[%csl Rd6]}) 24. fxe5 Bxf1 25. Qxf1 Bxe5 26. Na3 $1 $16 {[%cal Ga3c4] White advantage is clear, and Judit had not too many chances to avoid first defeat, but clocks, her big ally in this match, helped her again.} Qe7 27. Nac4 Bg7 28. a6 $1 h5 29. h4 Rf8 30. Qd3 f5 {Just produce additional weakness, but it is difficult to give good advice.} 31. Bxd4 Bxd4 32. exf5 gxf5 (32... Rxf5 $5) 33. Nf3 {[%csl Re6][%cal Ga2e2]} Qf6 34. Nxd4 cxd4 35. Rf2 Rbe8 36. Bh3 $18 Qg7 37. Bxf5 Kh8 38. Rf4 Rf6 39. Qxd4 {It is very difficult to comment last part of the game.White position is easy winning and Karpov had enough time, but Judit continued to fight and her stubborness got the price in the end: Anatoly, having 20-25 seconds left on his clocks, instead of giving mate in 3 moves blunder stalemate! Karpov played very well this game and surely he deserves victory.} Re2+ 40. Kh3 Re1 41. Kg2 Re2+ 42. Kf3 Re1 43. Ne3 Rg1 44. g4 hxg4+ 45. Bxg4 Rxf4+ 46. Qxf4 Re1 47. h5 Rh1 48. Qf5 Rc1 49. Qe6 Qf8+ 50. Bf5 Rc7 51. h6 Re7 52. Qg6 Rf7 53. Kf4 Re7 54. Ng4 Rf7 55. Nf6 Rc7 56. Be6 Rc4+ 57. Kg5 Rc7 58. Kh5 Re7 59. Qg8+ Qxg8 60. Nxg8 Rc7 61. Nf6 Rc1 62. Ne8 Rh1+ 63. Kg6 Rg1+ 64. Kf7 Rf1+ 65. Ke7 Kh7 66. Nxd6 Kxh6 67. Nf7+ Kg6 68. d6 Rd1 69. d7 Kh5 70. Nd6 Rxd6 71. Kxd6 Kg5 72. d8=Q+ Kf4 73. Qh4+ Ke3 74. Qg3+ Kd4 75. Qf3 $4 {stalemate} (75. Bb3 Ke4 76. Bc2+ Kd4 77. Qd3#) 1/2-1/2

