1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qa4 {John Emms: 'Less popular than 5 b3 or 5 Nbd2, but quite fashionable recently.'} Bb7 ({For} 5... Be7 {see Piket-Ivanchuk, Montecatini Terme 2000.}) 6. Bg2 c5 7. dxc5 bxc5 ({The other way to recapture is also possible -} 7... Bxc5 8. O-O O-O 9. Nc3 Be7 10. Bf4 Na6 11. Rac1 Nc5 12. Qc2 Qc8 {with an equal position (NCO).}) 8. O-O Be7 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Bf4 Qb6 11. Rfd1 {John Emms: 'A case of which is the best rook for the job?'} ({For the alternative} 11. Rad1 {see the game Lalic-Emms, Redbus KO 2001 in ChessPub (ECO code E15).}) 11... Rd8 (11... d6 $5 12. Rd2 (12. Qb3 $5) 12... Nc6 $5 13. Bxd6 Bxd6 14. Rxd6 Qxb2 15. Rb1 Qxc3 $11 16. Rxb7 Nd4 17. Nxd4 $4 (17. Qd1 {is the only move}) 17... Qe1+ 18. Bf1 Ne4 $1 {and White had to resign in the game Dautov-Palac, Vienna 1996.}) 12. Qb3 $5 (12. Rd2 {Ü 80/523}) 12... d6 ({The immediate} 12... Qxb3 {is also possible.}) 13. Rd2 Na6 $146 {[ c7]} ({I prefer giving White the doubled pawns with} 13... Qxb3 14. axb3 {for example,} Nc6 15. Rad1 Ne8 16. Ne1 Rdb8 17. Nd3 Nd4 18. b4 cxb4 19. Na4 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Nb3 21. Rc2 Nd4 {led to an eventual draw in Olafsson-Lautier, Antwerp 1998, although at this stage Black is a clear pawn up.}) 14. Qxb6 axb6 15. Rad1 Ne8 16. h4 h6 17. Nh2 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Nac7 19. e4 $1 {John Emms: 'White's pressure on d6 gives him a small advantage.'} Kf8 20. b3 Rdb8 (20... g5 $5 {(Polgar,Ju)} 21. hxg5 hxg5 22. Be3 Bf6 23. Na4 b5 24. cxb5 Nxb5 25. Nf3 Rxa4 $5 26. bxa4 Nc3 $44) 21. e5 $1 $14 {John Emms: '!'} d5 (21... dxe5 22. Bxe5 Rd8 23. Rd7 {is promising for White.}) 22. cxd5 exd5 23. Nxd5 Nxd5 24. Rxd5 Rxa2 25. Rd7 Raa8 26. Ng4 Rd8 27. Ne3 Rac8 (27... g5 $5 28. Nf5 Rxd7 29. Rxd7 gxf4 30. Nxe7 fxg3 31. Nf5 Rc8 $1 32. Nxh6 Rc7 33. Rxc7 Nxc7 34. Kxg3) 28. Nf5 Rxd7 29. Rxd7 Bd8 30. Rb7 Bc7 31. h5 $1 Rd8 32. Ne3 Rd3 (32... Rd7 $2 33. Nc4 Ke7 34. Nxb6 Bxb6 35. Rxb6 Nc7 36. Be3 Ne6 37. Kf3 $16) 33. e6 $1 fxe6 34. Bxc7 Rd7 35. Rxb6 Rxc7 (35... Nxc7 $6 36. Rc6 Nd5 37. Nc4 Rb7 38. Nd2) 36. Rxe6 {John Emms: 'White has won a pawn and Karpov's technique is enough to convert his advantage.'} Rb7 37. Re5 Rxb3 38. Rxc5 {[ 9/h]} Nf6 39. g4 Ra3 40. Rc8+ Kf7 41. Nc4 Ra7 42. f3 Ke6 43. Rc5 Nd5 44. Rc6+ Ke7 45. Ne5 Nf4+ 46. Kg3 Ra4 47. Rc2 Kf6 48. Nd7+ Ke7 49. Nc5 Rd4 50. Ra2 Kf7 51. Ra7+ Kf8 52. Ne4 Ne6 53. Ra6 Ke7 54. Kf2 Rc4 (54... Rb4 $142 55. Ra7+ Kf8 56. Nd6 Rb3 57. Nf5 Rb2+) 55. Ke3 Rc1 56. Ra7+ Rc7 57. Ra4 Rb7 58. Ra3 Rc7 59. Ng3 Kf6 60. Ne4+ Ke7 61. Nc3 Rd7 62. f4 Nc5 63. Ne2 Kf7 64. Nd4 Ne6 65. Nf3 Rb7 66. Ne5+ Kf6 67. Nd3 Ke7 68. Ke4 Rd7 $2 (68... Rb5) 69. Kf5 $1 $18 Rd6 70. Ra7+ Kf8 71. Ne5 Nd4+ 72. Ke4 Ne6 (72... Nc6 73. Ra6 Rd4+ 74. Ke3) (72... Rd8 73. Nd7+) 73. Ng6+ Kg8 74. Ke5 Rb6 75. Re7 (75. Re7 Nf8 76. Re8 Rf6 77. Nxf8 Rxf8 78. Rxf8+ Kxf8 79. Ke6 Ke8 80. f5 Kf8 81. f6 {is a winning king and pawn ending for White.}) 1-0

