{John Emms: 'Here we see two super-GMs tackling the main line 4 g3 Ba6 5 b3 variation. Polgar's becoming quite an adherent of the Queen's Indian Defence, which gives Black a solid base, but still offers chances for dynamic counterplay.'} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 Bb4+ {John Emms: 'This is the most solid line for Black against 5 b3, and a favourite of many of the top GMs. In fact Karpov has had much experience of this line from both sides of the board.'} 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Bg2 c6 8. Bc3 d5 9. Ne5 ({The other main line for White runs} 9. Nbd2 Nbd7 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Bb7 {see Karpov-Anand, GSM World Blitz Cup, Warsaw 2000}) 9... Nfd7 10. Nxd7 Nxd7 11. Nd2 O-O 12. O-O Nf6 {John Emms: 'Once again Black has many choices here, including'} (12... Rc8 {and}) (12... b5) 13. e4 b5 14. Re1 dxe4 15. Qc2 (15. Rc1 Rb8 $5 16. c5 b4 17. Bb2 Bd3 18. Nxe4 Bxe4 19. Bxe4 Nxe4 20. Rxe4 Bf6 21. Qe2 Qa5 22. Ra1 Qb5 23. a3 Qxe2 24. Rxe2 h5 25. Kg2 Rfd8 26. axb4 Rxb4 27. Rxa7 Rxb3 28. Rc7 $11 {was equal in Karpov-Anand, Frankfurt rapid 1999.}) 15... Rc8 16. Rad1 ({The Slovakian GM Stohl gives the line} 16. Nxe4 bxc4 17. Nc5 cxb3 $5 18. axb3 Bb5 19. Rxa7 Ra8 $11) 16... Nd5 {John Emms: '!'} 17. Bb2 {John Emms: '!?' John Emms: 'This is a new move.'} (17. Bxe4 Nxc3 18. Qxc3 Bf6 19. Qc2 $14 {is probably enough to give White a tiny edge.}) (17. cxd5 $6 cxd5 {gives Black excellent compensation for the piece, for example} 18. Bf1 b4 $1 19. Bxa6 Rxc3 20. Qb1 Qb6 $17 {d4} 21. Bf1 Bf6 {and the d4-pawn will fall.}) 17... f5 $146 {John Emms: '!?'} (17... Nb4 {Ü 77/(506) Ü 77/(506) Ü 77/(506)}) 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Qb1 b4 {John Emms: 'The opening phase is over and we can conclude that Black has some compensation for the knight in the shape of a powerful pawn diamond in the centre of the board.'} 20. Nf1 Qb6 21. f3 (21. Ne3 $5 f4 22. Ng4 Bd6 $44) 21... Bf6 22. Ne3 Rc3 $1 {John Emms: '!?' John Emms: 'Polgar continues in aggressive fashion.'} 23. Bxc3 bxc3 24. Nc2 $2 (24. fxe4 fxe4 25. Nc2 $1 (25. Bh3 Bxd4 26. Rxd4 Qxd4 27. Bxe6+ Kh8 28. Bxd5 Bd3 29. Qc1 Rf3 $44) 25... Bd3 $1 (25... Bxd4+ $2 26. Nxd4 e5 27. Bxe4 $16) (25... e5 26. Ne3 Bb7 27. Nxd5 Bxd5 28. Bxe4 Bxe4 29. Qxe4 exd4 30. Kg2 $14) 26. Bf1 Bxd4+ 27. Kh1 Rf3 $1 28. Be2 Bxe2 29. Rxe2 e5 $44) 24... e5 $6 {John Emms: '!'} (24... Bxd4+ $1 25. Nxd4 (25. Rxd4 $2 e5) 25... e5 26. fxe4 (26. Kh1 exd4 27. Qc1 Qf6 (27... Qc5 $15)) 26... exd4 27. Kh1 (27. exd5 d3+ 28. Kh1 c2 29. Qc1 cxd1=Q 30. Rxd1 Re8 $15) 27... fxe4 $44) 25. Ne3 exd4 26. Nxd5 Qc5 27. Nxf6+ Rxf6 {John Emms: 'Black is a rook down now, but those pawns are becoming a major threat.'} 28. b4 Qc4 (28... Qb6 $2 29. Qb3+ $18) 29. Qb3 d3 (29... Qxb3 30. axb3 d3 31. fxe4 d2 32. e5 dxe1=Q+ 33. Rxe1 Rb6 34. Rc1 Rxb4 35. Rxc3 Bb7 $11) 30. Qxc4+ (30. fxe4 $5 d2 31. e5 Re6 $1 32. Qxc4 (32. Bd5 $2 Qd4+ 33. Kg2 Kh8 $1 34. b5 (34. Bxe6 dxe1=N+ 35. Rxe1 Qd2+) 34... dxe1=N+ 35. Rxe1 Qd2+ 36. Kf1 c2) 32... Bxc4 33. Rxd2 cxd2 34. Rd1) 30... Bxc4 31. Bf1 Rc6 {John Emms: 'The pawns give Black enough counterplay to reach a drawn position.'} (31... Ba6 $5 32. fxe4 $8 (32. Kf2 c2 33. Rc1 (33. Rd2 $4 e3+) 33... Rd6 $1 34. fxe4 fxe4 35. Be2 d2 36. Bxa6 d1=Q) (32. b5 Bxb5 33. Kf2 c2 34. Rc1 Rd6 { a4  a4  Ba4}) 32... fxe4 33. Rxe4 c2 34. Ree1 cxd1=Q 35. Rxd1 Rf3 36. Kg2 Bb7 37. Kg1 $11) 32. fxe4 d2 33. b5 Rc5 34. Bxc4+ Rxc4 35. exf5 (35. Rf1 Rxe4 $1 36. Kf2 Kf7 { e6-d5  e6-d5  Ke6-d5}) 35... dxe1=Q+ 36. Rxe1 Kf7 37. Rc1 Kf6 38. Kf2 Ra4 39. Rc2 Ra5 40. Ke3 Kxf5 41. Rxc3 Rxa2 42. Rc7 Kf6 {is a dead draw ½-½ (42) Karpov,A (2699)-Polgar,J (2656) Buenos Aires 79/482 2000 CI1-130 [Polgar,Judit]} 1/2-1/2

