{Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'The Classical King's Indian:''} 1. d4 {Dautov Karpov,Anatoly Karpov,Anatoly} Nf6 2. Nf3 (2. c4 {Karpov,Anatoly} g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 {(see Black's 8th move for comparison).}) 2... g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nc6 7. Nc3 e5 ({Against the most popular move} 7... a6 {(Karpov,Anatoly) , Karpov has generally steered clear of the critical variations after} 8. d5 ({and preferred the solid system with} 8. Re1 Rb8 9. Rb1 {; for instance,} Bd7 10. b4 e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 dxe5 13. b5 Be6 $6 (13... axb5 {gives Black good chances to equalize}) 14. Ba3 Re8 15. bxa6 bxa6 $6 ({White's queen will be much more effective than Black's, so} 15... Qxd1 {was better}) 16. Bc6 ({here} 16. Rxb8 {, avoiding the queen exchange, was more precise}) 16... Nd7 $2 17. Rxb8 Qxb8 18. Nd5 Qd8 19. Qa4 {and White had built up a winning advantage through simple means in A.Karpov-A.Shirov, Eurotel Trophy (rapid; 4th matchgame), Prague 2002 (1-0 in 50).})) 8. d5 Ne7 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'Black's play is consistent with the main lines of the Classical King's Indian; for example, 1 d4 Nf6 2 c4 g6 3 Nc3 Bg7 4 e4 d6 5 Nf3 0-0 6 Be2 e5 7 0-0 Nc6 8 d5 Ne7. QUESTION: What is the difference in this position, and whom does it favour? ANSWER: Obviously, the difference lies in the placement of White's light-squared bishop, which is on g2 rather than e2. At the risk of oversimplifying, the fianchettoed bishop makes it much more difficult for Black to carry out a standard King's Indian attack with ...Nd7, ...f7-f5-f4, and pushing the g-pawn. We see Polgar trying to carry out a similar plan in this game, and getting nowhere. ''} 9. e4 ({As noted by Mikhalchishin, after} 9. c5 {Karpov,Anatoly} Ne8 10. cxd6 {, rather than the apparently automatic recapture} cxd6 {,} ({Black gets good play with} 10... Nxd6 $1 {. Judit successfully defended this position twice: in A.Adorjan-J.Polgar, Hungarian Championship, Budapest 1991 (½-½ in 20) and L.Portisch-J.Polgar, Veterans vs. Women, Monte Carlo 1994 (½-½ in 43).})) ({Mikhalchishin also suggests the interesting} 9. a4 {(Karpov,Anatoly) , aiming to gain space on the queenside with a5.}) 9... Nd7 10. Ne1 f5 11. Nd3 Nf6 12. Bg5 fxe4 ({Karpov,Anatoly: 'White's idea after'} 12... h6 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'is to attack the centre with'} 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. f4 {Karpov,Anatoly: '. Karpov had this in a game the month before, which proceeded'} exf4 15. Nxf4 Be5 16. exf5 Bxf5 17. Qd2 c6 18. Kh1 Qb6 19. Rae1 $14 {Karpov-Gelfand/Dos Hermanas/1994 Karpov,Anatoly: ', when Dautov suggests that White holds a small advantage in view of the weakness of the e6-square, A.Karpov-B.Gelfand, Dos Hermanas 1994 (½-½ in 59).'}) 13. Nxe4 Nf5 14. Re1 $1 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'N!' 'N!' Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'A strong move and a novelty, entirely in Karpov's style – he continues development, increases his central control and anticipates any Black counterplay.''} (14. Qd2 Nxe4 15. Bxe4 Qe8 16. Rae1 Nd4 17. Kg2 b5 $132 {Epishin-Polgar,J/Dos Hermanas/1994}) (14. Kh1 h6 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Bd2 Qd7 17. Bc3 (17. f4 e4 $1 18. Bxe4 Nxg3+ 19. hxg3 Qh3+ 20. Kg1 Qxg3+ 21. Kh1 Qh3+ $10) 17... Qg7 18. Qb3 b6 19. a4 a5 20. Rae1 g5 21. Qd1 Bd7 22. Be4 Rae8 $10 {Epishin-Polgar,J/Las Palmas/1994}) ({As it happens, White has been very successful with the more concrete} 14. g4 $5 {(Karpov,Anatoly) , when the rook can prove effective on f1 after a subsequent f2-f4, trying to exploit the pin on the f6-knight. Karpov would not play such a move unless he was sure of the consequences (probably through prior preparation).}) 14... h6 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Bd2 (16. Bxf6 {Karpov,Anatoly} Qxf6 {seems comfortable for Black.}) 16... Kh7 {Karpov,Anatoly: '?!' '?!'} (16... Qd7 17. f4 exf4 18. Nxf4 Qg7 (18... Bxb2 19. Nxg6 Rf6 20. Nf4 Bxa1 21. Qxa1 $40) 19. Bc3 Bxc3 20. bxc3 Bd7 21. Ne6 Bxe6 22. dxe6 c6 23. Qd3 Rae8 24. Re2 $16) (16... g5 $5 {} 17. Bc3 (17. Qh5 Kg7 (17... Qe8 $2 18. Qxe8 Rxe8 19. f4 gxf4 20. gxf4 $16) 18. Bc3 Bd7 {e8-g6}) 17... Bd7 18. c5 Qe7 19. b4 Qg7 20. a4 $14) (16... Bd7 {Karpov,Anatoly}) 17. Bc3 Bd7 18. c5 $1 {Karpov,Anatoly: '!' '!' Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: '!' Sam Collins: 'This is a thematic break for White in many King's Indian positions, and there is no reason to refrain from it here.''} (18. f4 exf4 19. Nxf4 Bxc3 20. bxc3 Qf6 21. Ne6 (21. Qd3 Rae8 $10) 21... Bxe6 22. dxe6 Rae8 $15) 18... b6 $2 {Karpov,Anatoly: '?!' '?!' Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'QUESTION: What's wrong with this move? ANSWER: In general terms, it violates an important strategic rule (which, like all rules in chess, has some exceptions!): namely, not to make pawn moves on the side where you're weaker. In the long run, White is winning on the queenside, and no arrangement of the black pawns can establish parity on this side of the board once White gets c4-c5 in. More concretely, this move allows White to play c5-c6, when the pawn severely cramps Black's queenside, while White retains pawn control over the e6-square. I think Polgar would not have played in this fashion if she had seen a way for White to break through to e6. However, it must be acknowledged that Black's position is already deeply unpleasant.''} (18... Bb5 19. f4 Bxd3 (19... exf4 20. Nxf4 Bxc3 21. bxc3 $36) 20. fxe5 Bxe5 21. Bxe5 dxe5 22. Qxd3 Qf6 23. Rf1 Qg7 24. Be4 $14) (18... g5 $5) ({Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'For instance, Karpov gives''} 18... Nd4 {Karpov,Anatoly} 19. Bxd4 exd4 20. c6 bxc6 21. dxc6 Bf5 22. Nf4 $16 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'as clearly better for White.''}) 19. c6 ({Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'Very strong, although White had a promising alternative: Mikhalchishin assesses''} 19. cxd6 {Karpov,Anatoly} cxd6 20. Nb4 $16 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'as strategically winning for White.''}) 19... Be8 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'EXERCISE: How should White proceed? ANSWER:''} 20. f4 $16 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: '!' Sam Collins: 'Breaking down Black's centre and gaining access to e6 for the white knight. The f2-f4 break is a very typical resource in the Fianchetto King's Indian, which allows White to fight for space on the kingside. The fact that Black cannot obtain a kingside space advantage is one of the main reasons that the white king tends to be much safer in the Fianchetto lines than in the Classical Variation with the bishop on e2.''} exf4 21. Nxf4 Bf7 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'Black hurries to protect the horribly weak e6-square.''} (21... Bxc3 {Karpov,Anatoly} 22. bxc3 $16 (22. Ne6 $6 Bd4+)) 22. Qd3 Bxc3 23. bxc3 {Karpov,Anatoly: '!' '!'} (23. Qxc3 {Karpov,Anatoly} Bg8 $8 (23... Qg5 24. Nh3 $1 Qh5 25. Nf2 $18)) 23... Qg5 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'EXERCISE: How should White continue? ANSWER:''} (23... Qf6 {Karpov,Anatoly} 24. Bh3 (24. Ne6 Bxe6 25. Rxe6 Qg7 (25... Qf7 26. Rf1 $16) 26. Be4 Ne7 27. Re1 $16) 24... Rae8 (24... Ng7 25. Bd7 $18) 25. Bxf5 gxf5 26. Rxe8 Rxe8 27. Rf1 $16) 24. h4 $1 {Karpov,Anatoly: '+-!' '+-!' Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: '!' Sam Collins: 'White has a range of strong continuations, but this one is clearly the best.''} (24. Bh3 {Karpov,Anatoly} Bg8 {( g3)} 25. Bxf5 Rxf5 26. h4 $16) 24... Qf6 (24... Nxh4 25. gxh4 Qxf4 26. Re4 (26. Re7 {Karpov,Anatoly} Rae8 27. Rxe8 $1) 26... Qf5 (26... Qf6 27. Rf1 Qd8 28. Qe3 Kg7 29. Re7 $18) 27. Rf1 Qh5 (27... Qxd5 $2 28. Qxd5 Bxd5 29. Re7+ $18) 28. Re7 Kg8 29. Qg3 $16 { f3,c7}) ({Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'If Black takes on g3, then after''} 24... Qxg3 25. Qxg3 Nxg3 26. Re7 $16 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: ', the c7-pawn drops, leaving White with a monster passed pawn on c6.''} Kg8 {Karpov,Anatoly} 27. Kh2 $1 (27. Rxc7 g5 28. hxg5 (28. Ne6 Bxe6 29. dxe6 Ne2+ 30. Kh2 Rf4 $132) 28... hxg5 29. Nh3)) 25. Ne6 Bxe6 26. Rxe6 Qg7 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: 'EXERCISE: How can White finish the game? ANSWER:''} 27. h5 $18 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: '!' Sam Collins: 'Cracking open the light squares and decisively undermining the knight on f5.''} Ne7 (27... gxh5 28. Be4 $18) 28. Rae1 {Karpov,Anatoly: 'Sam Collins: '. As with many of Karpov's strategic masterpieces, Polgar resigns in a position with equal material, but facing irresistible threats.''} (28. Rae1 Rf7 29. Qe4 $18) 1-0

