ge – just compare the quality of the respective minor pieces.}) 17. Qxd4 {Gawain Jones: 'Now Kasparov is level on material and definitely up on position. Now Kasparov is level on material and definitely up on position.'} h4 18. Nab5 {[%cal Gb5a7,Gb5c3]} Rh5 19. Qe3 {Gawain Jones: 'White plays a little superficially, as one might expect in rapid chess, and decides to put all his minor pieces on pretty squares in the centre of the board. White plays a little superficially, as one might expect in rapid chess, and decides to put all his minor pieces on pretty squares in the centre of the board.' Kasparov got a big advantage after the opening. Black can't complete the development and must suffer now for nothing.} ({As Psakhis and Palliser point out, there was nothing wrong with As Psakhis and Palliser point out, there was nothing wrong with} 19. Nxa7 $1 {when Black can keep material equality with} Bxd6 20. exd6 Qd7 21. Be3 Qxd6 {, but the a- and b-pawns will roll through without any meaningful opposition. , but the a- and b-pawns will roll through without any meaningful opposition.}) 19... Qd7 20. Nd4 Bd8 21. Qd2 {Gawain Jones: 'An indication that Kasparov isn't sure how to proceed, but luckily his position is good enough to give away a couple of tempi. An indication that Kasparov isn't sure how to proceed, but luckily his position is good enough to give away a couple of tempi.'} (21. f3 $1 {was the most precise way of continuing. was the most precise way of continuing.}) 21... Ne7 22. b5 Ng6 23. Nc6 {Gawain Jones: '!' Gawain Jones: 'Very visual knights and psychologically damaging for Black as those knights seem to suffocate her. However, she has played well over the last few moves and her pieces have started to coordinate (thanks to the couple of tempi which Kasparov gave her with his Qd4-e3-d2 rather than capturing the a7-pawn). However, here Polgar plays too aggressively. Very visual knights and psychologically damaging for Black as those knights seem to suffocate he