1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nc6 4. Bxc6+ bxc6 5. O-O {Having doubled Black's c-pawns one might expect White to simply develop, intending to later exploit his structural advantage. However, by playing too slowly White risks leaving himself without a plan while Black takes over the centre and then the initiative after ...e5. Thus White usually aims to open the centre with c3 and d4 when his superior development may well allow him to exert pressure against c6 and d6. <xml:en>Having doubled Black's c-pawns one might expect White to simply develop, intending to later exploit his structural advantage. However, by playing too slowly White risks leaving himself without a plan while Black takes over the centre and then the initiative after ...e5. Thus White usually aims to open the centre with c3 and d4 when his superior development may well allow him to exert pressure against c6 and d6.</:en/xml>} e5 {Traditionally the main line, but this certainly commits Black to having to play pretty aggressively at any early stage.} ({Instead <xml:en>Traditionally the main line, but this certainly commits Black to having to play pretty aggressively at any early stage. Instead</:en/xml>} 5... Nf6 {is a little careless as it allows <xml:en>is a little careless as it allows</:en/xml>} 6. e5 $1) ({while <xml:en>while</:en/xml>} 5... Bg4 {is the subject of Game 3. <xml:en>is the subject of Game 3.</:en/xml>}) 6. c3 {Preparing to quickly blast the centre open, while once again it's risky for White to play too slowly.} ({Preparing to quickly blast the centre open, while once again it's risky for White to play too slowly.} 6. Re1 {is useful, but then} g5 $1 {(a recurring theme, while clearly now 7 h3? h5 only helps Black)} 7. d3 {doesn't look like too appetising a position for White and} g4 8. Nfd2 Ne7 {, intending 9...Ng6 with a kingside bind, is awkward for him. <xml:en>, intending 9...Ng6 with a kingside bind, is awkward for him.</:en/xml>}) 6... g5 $6 {Optimism and hope may have been in the air at Prague, but Polgar was under pressure having lost the first game of this mini-match; hence this aggressive choice. However, this thrust is too weakening and Kasparov powerfully and classically responds by opening the centre. <xml:en>Optimism and hope may have been in the air at Prague, but Polgar was under pressure having lost the first game of this mini-match; hence this aggressive choice. However, this thrust is too weakening and Kasparov powerfully and classically responds by opening the centre.</:en/xml>} ({She should thus have preferred <xml:en>She should thus have preferred</:en/xml>} 6... f5 $5 {, the subject of our next game <xml:en>, the subject of our next game</:en/xml>}) ({or the relatively solid <xml:en>or the relatively solid</:en/xml>} 6... Nf6 {However, White can still then play aggressively should he wish and} 7. d4 $5 ({instead} 7. Re1 {is fairly well met by Akopian's} Bg4 {, although} 8. d4 $5 cxd4 9. cxd4 exd4 $5 10. Qxd4 Bxf3 11. gxf3 Be7 12. e5 $1 {wasn't so comfortable for Black in O.Salmensuu-V.Akopian, Linares 2001; however, he should have earlier strong-pointed e5 with 9...Qc7 or have now opted for} Nd5 $5 {(Akopian) when} 13. exd6 Qxd6 14. Qxg7 {is the only way to try and trouble Black, but then simply} Qg6+ $1 15. Qxg6 hxg6 {appears to supply full compensation for the pawn}) 7... exd4 {critical,} ({while Black should though avoid critical, while Black should though avoid} 7... Nxe4 $6 8. Re1 f5 9. dxe5 d5 10. Qa4 {when his e4-steed doesn't fully prevent White's pressure with c4 imminent}) 8. Re1 $5 ({Nisipeanu's sacrificial idea which aims to breathe new life into 7 d4 as White has been struggling after} 8. cxd4 Nxe4 9. Re1 d5) 8... dxc3 {reaches a sharp and important position where Nisipeanu and Stoica's suggestion of Nisipeanu and Stoica's suggestion of} 9. Qa4 $5 (9. Nxc3 Be7 10. e5 dxe5 11. Qxd8+ Bxd8 12. Nxe5 Be6 $1 {, preparing to meet 13 Be3 with 13...Bc7, saw Black equalise in L.Nisipeanu-M.Parligras, Curtea de Arges 2002}) 9... cxb2 10. Bxb2 {deserves serious attention; White is two pawns down, but 11 e5 is next up and Black must tread carefully, although he does have} Be7 11. e5 dxe5 12. Nxe5 O-O $1 {when} 13. Nxc6 Bd7 14. Nxe7+ Qxe7 15. Rxe7 Bxa4 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Nc3 Bc6 {will probably lead to a draw.}) 7. d4 $1 g4 8. Nfd2 cxd4 9. cxd4 exd4 10. Nc4 $1 c5 {Polgar decides that she might as well have a pawn for her suffering,} ({while instead Polgar decides that she might as well have a pawn for her suffering, while instead} 10... Bg7 {Polgar decides that she might as well have a pawn for her suffering, while instead} 11. Bf4 Be6 12. Nxd6+ Kf8 13. Nd2 Ne7 14. Nb3 Qb6 15. Rc1 {simply gave White a large advantage in S.Rublevsky-A.Stripunsky, Kazan 1995.}) 11. Re1 $1 {Eyeing up the black king and preparing to open the position to exploit White's superior development. <xml:en>Eyeing up the black king and preparing to open the position to exploit White's superior development.</:en/xml>} Be6 12. Nba3 Be7 13. e5 $1 d5 14. Nd6+ $1 Kf8 {Criticised by Kasparov,} ({although one can easily appreciate why Polgar didn't like the look of Criticised by Kasparov, although one can easily appreciate why Polgar didn't like the look of} 14... Bxd6 15. exd6 Qxd6 {when she would have been very weak on the dark-squares; something which Kasparov's} 16. b4 $1 {then neatly exploits.}) 15. Bf4 h5 16. b4 $1 {Destroying the black centre and winning back the gambited pawn after which White retains a strong bind on the position while Polgar struggles to coordinate her ragged position. <xml:en>Destroying the black centre and winning back the gambited pawn after which White retains a strong bind on the position while Polgar struggles to coordinate her ragged position.</:en/xml>} c4 17. Qxd4 h4 18. Nab5 Rh5 19. Qe3 ({Being a rapid game, it would be surprising if White were to convert his large advantage flawlessly, but the world no.1 does begin to drift around here, with one good option simply being} 19. Nxa7 $5 Bxd6 20. exd6 Qd7 21. Be3 {(Psakhis) when White's queenside pawns should decide.}) 19... Qd7 20. Nd4 Bd8 $1 21. Qd2 Ne7 22. b5 Ng6 23. Nc6 c3 24. Qc1 {White has two colossal knights, but Polgar has resisted well thus far although now she becomes too ambitious in her bid for counterplay. <xml:en>White has two colossal knights, but Polgar has resisted well thus far although now she becomes too ambitious in her bid for counterplay.</:en/xml>} g3 $6 25. fxg3 hxg3 26. Bxg3 Bb6+ 27. Kh1 Kg7 28. Qxc3 {The g3-bishop holds the kingside together and White is now two pawns up, but there's still no forced winning line in sight. <xml:en>The g3-bishop holds the kingside together and White is now two pawns up, but there's still no forced winning line in sight.</:en/xml>} Rah8 29. Rf1 Kg8 30. Rae1 $6 f5 $1 31. exf6 Qh7 32. f7+ ({Having lost control a little one would expect White to calmly prefer} 32. Nd4 $1 Rxh2+ 33. Kg1 {, but Kasparov has seen something flashy.}) 32... Bxf7 33. Qxh8+ $6 {An impressive idea, but objectively this does throw away White's advantage and allow Black right back into the game.} ({Instead White should have calmly sacrificed the bishop to block the h-file, according to Kasparov, whereupon <xml:en>An impressive idea, but objectively this does throw away White's advantage and allow Black right back into the game. Instead White should have calmly sacrificed the bishop to block the h-file, according to Kasparov, whereupon</:en/xml>} 33. Bh4 $1 Rxh4 34. h3 {should be winning for White due to his huge pressure against f7 and the black monarch. <xml:en>should be winning for White due to his huge pressure against f7 and the black monarch.</:en/xml>}) 33... Nxh8 34. Ne7+ Kf8 35. Nec8 $2 {Kasparov has just a rook for the black queen, but he does have a fair amount of pressure and is much better coordinated.} ({Now he should have maintained his impressive compensation with Kasparov has just a rook for the black queen, but he does have a fair amount of pressure and is much better coordinated. Now he should have maintained his impressive compensation with} 35. Nef5 {rather than allow the extra queen into the game...}) 35... Qd3 $2 {...except that Polgar chooses the wrong way.} ({Instead <xml:en>...except that Polgar chooses the wrong way. Instead</:en/xml>} 35... Bc5 $1 36. Re8+ Kg7 37. Re7 Qd3 $1 {looks rather good for Black and would have completed a quite remarkable turnaround. <xml:en>looks rather good for Black and would have completed a quite remarkable turnaround.</:en/xml>}) 36. Rf3 Qc2 $2 ({She should at least have threatened to capture on h2 with} 36... Qd2 {, and after this second mistake White quickly regains his material and is left with an easy win as both players blitzed out their remaining moves.}) 37. Nxf7 Nxf7 38. Nd6 Qg6 39. Rxf7+ Qxf7 40. Nxf7 Kxf7 41. Re5 $1 Rxe5 42. Bxe5 d4 43. Kg1 Ke6 44. Bb8 Kd5 45. Kf1 Kc4 46. Ke2 d3+ 47. Kd1 Kxb5 48. h4 a5 49. Be5 Bf2 50. h5 Be3 51. Bg7 1-0

