1. d4 {Gershon Gershon} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Nbd2 d5 (5... Bb4 {Gershon} 6. Qc2 Bb7 7. Bg2 Be4 8. Qd1 O-O 9. O-O Bxd2 10. Qxd2 a5 11. b3 d6 12. Bb2 Nbd7 13. Rfe1 a4 14. Bf1 h6 15. Qc3 Bb7 16. Nd2 c5 17. e4 {½-½ Hellsten,J-Hansen,S/Luebeck GER 2000 (33) White's position looks slightly better.But still black has nice pawn structure and "good" pieces what gives him good chances to equalize.}) 6. Bg2 Be7 7. O-O O-O {This position is gaining in popularity lately, and, somehow, Khalifman seems to take on himself to prove white better here. Gershon: 'This position is gaining in popularity lately, and, somehow, Khalifman seems to take on himself to prove white better here.'} 8. cxd5 {many other moves have been tried here, but they all lead to same positions. Gershon: 'many other moves have been tried here, but they all lead to same positions.'} (8. Ne5 {Gershon}) (8. Qc2 {Gershon}) (8. b3 {Gershon}) 8... exd5 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. b3 c5 (10... a5 {Gershon} 11. Qc2 (11. Bb2 Na6 12. Rc1 {Lautier prefered to place the  on c2 and  on d1.} c5 13. Nd3 {[%cal Gd3f4]} (13. e3 Rc8 14. Ndf3 Bd6 $11) 13... Re8 14. Nf4 cxd4 $6 {This move leads to a slightly worse position,while after 14...c8 black has a good  pawn structure and well placed pieces.} (14... Rc8 $11) 15. Nf3 (15. Bxd4 Ba3 16. Ra1 Ne4 {And black is fine.}) 15... Nb4 16. a3 Na2 {This was the black's idea.} 17. Rc2 Nc3 $13 {½-½ Khalifman,A-Leko,P/New Delhi/Theran 2000/CBM 81/[Rabinovich,A] (49)}) 11... Na6 12. Bb2 c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. Rad1 a4 15. bxa4 Nb4 $11 {0-1 Lautier,J-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 2000/CBM 76/[Gershon] (34). (34)}) (10... Na6 {Gershon} 11. Bb2 c5 {is the other alternative} 12. Rc1 {Gershon} Nc7 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. Nd3 Nd7 15. Nf4 Rb8 16. e4 d4 17. Nc4 Bg5 18. Rc2 Bxf4 19. gxf4 Qh4 20. Qf3 Ne6 $11 {0-1 Khalifman,A-Ponomariov,R/Moscow RUS 2002/The Week in Chess 409 (59)}) 11. Bb2 Nbd7 12. Nxd7 (12. dxc5 {Gershon} bxc5 13. Rc1 Nxe5 14. Bxe5 Qb6 15. Qc2 Rfe8 16. e3 Rac8 17. Rfd1 Nd7 18. Bb2 Bf6 19. Bxf6 Nxf6 20. Qf5 a5 21. Qf4 a4 {½-½ Yermolinsky,A-Ivanov,A/Washington DC 1997/EXT 99 (21).}) 12... Qxd7 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. e4 {In such a setup, all white can do, is play e4, force black to move his pawn to d4, and then, after blockading the pawn pair, try to capture them, however, it's easier said than done Gershon: 'In such a setup, all white can do, is play e4, force black to move his pawn to d4, and then, after blockading the pawn pair, try to capture them, however, it's easier said than done' the only way for white to target black pawns, however, this move has its own obvious drawbacks} d4 (14... dxe4 $5 15. Bxf6 $1 {Gershon} (15. Nxe4 $2 {Gershon: '?'} Qxd1 16. Nxf6+ (16. Raxd1 Bxe4 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Bxe4 Bd4 $11) 16... Bxf6 17. Rfxd1 Bxb2 (17... Bxg2 18. Bxf6 Bf3 19. Rd3 $14) 18. Bxb7 Rab8 19. Rab1 Rxb7 20. Rxb2 c4 $11) (15. Re1 Rad8 16. Nxe4 Qxd1 17. Nxf6+ Bxf6 18. Raxd1 Bxb2 19. Bxb7 Bd4 $11) 15... Bxf6 16. Nxe4 Qxd1 17. Nxf6+ gxf6 18. Rfxd1 $16) 15. Re1 ({white cannot let black knight to the  side:} 15. e5 {Gershon} Nd5 {[%cal Yd5c3,Gd5b4,Gb4d3] with a better game for black}) 15... a5 16. a4 Qe6 {[%csl Rb3]} 17. Rc1 {[%csl Rc5] both sides have many weaknesses in this position, which compensate for each other. However, white has less space, therefore, he's the one who should play carefully Gershon: 'both sides have many weaknesses in this position, which compensate for each other. However, white has less space, therefore, he's the one who should play carefully'} Ng4 {[%cal Gg4e5,Ge5d3]} 18. Ba3 (18. Nc4 {Gershon} Rfd8 $15 {[%cal Gd4d2]}) 18... Ne5 19. Bf1 Rfc8 $11 20. f4 Nd7 {playing on the safe side Gershon: 'playing on the safe side'} (20... Ng4 $5 {Gershon} 21. f5 Qh6 22. Qxg4 Qxd2 {with an unclear game}) 21. Nc4 Rcb8 22. Bg2 {white was able to push black back, and now, neither side can improve his position, without weakening himself too much. Gershon: 'white was able to push black back, and now, neither side can improve his position, without weakening himself too much.'} 1/2-1/2

