1. Nf3 {Hazai} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. d4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Ne8 10. Be3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bf2 h5 13. c5 g5 14. cxd6 (14. a4 Rf6 $5 (14... dxc5 15. Bxc5 Rf6 {Here White is not forced to play 16.a5.} 16. Nd3 Rg6 17. Bf2 $1 $14 {clearing the square for the .}) 15. cxd6 (15. a5 {Hazai} Rg6 (15... dxc5 16. Bxc5 Nd6 17. Nb5 a6 18. Nxd6 cxd6 19. Bb6 Qe8 20. Rc1 $14) 16. cxd6 Nxd6 $1 {This is the main line nowadays. The main idea of Black is to blockade the d6 square with the  defending the queenside.} (16... cxd6 17. Nb5 a6 18. Bb6 Qd7 19. Qa4 $1 g4 20. Nc3 $16 {After exchanging the queens in this Kings Indian main line the endgame usually favours for White.})) 15... Nxd6 16. Nd3 {comes into consideration with the threat of c1.} (16. Nb5 $14 {comes into consideration with the threat of c1.}) 16... Rg6 $13) 14... cxd6 (14... Nxd6 {Hazai} 15. Nd3 {threatening c5} b6 16. a4 a5 17. b4 Bd7 18. bxa5 Rxa5 19. Nb4 $14 {The fight is continuing on the queenside, which is not very encouraging for Black, since he has to attack on the kingside.}) 15. Kh1 $5 $146 (15. a4 {Kortschnoj,Viktor Lvovich: '!' '!' '!' 'Kortschnoj: '!' Kortschnoj: 'Hazai '' Kortschnoj,Viktor Lvovich: 'Kortschnoj: 'would have transpose to the main lines with 14.a4 with an important difference: White has avoided the d6 lines!''} Ng6 16. a5 Bh6 17. b4 Kh7 18. Nb5 $13) (15. a4 $1 {Hazai  would have transpose to the main lines with 14.a4 with an important difference: White has avoided the d6 lines!}) 15... Ng6 16. Rc1 {This plan is dubious here, even here 16.a4 was worthy of  consideration.} Bd7 {Black was afraid of 17.b5} (16... Bh6 $5) (16... Bh6 {Hazai} 17. Nb5 a6 18. Na7 Bd7 (18... Rxa7 $2 19. Bxa7 b6 20. Bxb6 $1 {explains the idea behind 15.h1}) 19. Qb3 Rb8 (19... b5 20. a4) 20. Bb6 Qe7 21. Bc7 Nxc7 22. Rxc7) 17. a4 Bh6 {This move is threatening with18... g4 19.fxg4 f3 making profit from the inferior position of the  on c1.} 18. Nd3 (18. Bb5 {Hazai} g4 (18... Bc8 19. Bxe8 Rxe8 20. Nb5) 19. Bxd7 Qxd7 20. Bg1 {was more to the point. The exchange of the light-squared bishops helps for White in most of the cases.}) 18... g4 $6 (18... Nf6 $2 19. Nb5 $16) (18... Rf7 $5 19. Qb3 Rg7 20. Qxb7 g4 $40) 19. fxg4 (19. Bg1 $2 {Hazai} g3 $17 {Black has a ferocious attack here.}) 19... f3 20. gxf3 Bxc1 21. Qxc1 hxg4 (21... Kh7 $5 {Hazai}) 22. Qh6 (22. fxg4 Nf4 23. Nxf4 Rxf4 (23... exf4 24. Be1 Qg5 25. Bd2 $16) 24. Be3 Rxf1+ 25. Qxf1 Qh4 26. Qf2 Qh3 27. Qf3 $14) 22... Qf6 23. fxg4 Qg7 (23... Nf4 $2 24. Qxf6 Nxf6 25. Nxf4 exf4 26. Bh4 $16) 24. Qxg7+ Kxg7 25. Kg2 (25. h4 $2 {Hazai} Nxh4) 25... Rh8 26. Be3 Nf6 27. h3 (27. Kg3 $142) 27... a6 {White has a slightly better position, but a breakthrough is not easy.} 28. Kg3 (28. b3 {Hazai} Rac8 29. Bd2 {plan is b2-c4}) 28... Rac8 29. Nf2 Rcf8 (29... b5 $5) 30. Rh1 Nf4 31. Bd1 Rh6 (31... b5 32. axb5 axb5 33. Ne2 $14) 32. h4 Rg6 33. g5 N6h5+ 34. Kh2 Rh8 35. Bg4 (35. Ng4 Nd3 36. Nh6 Nhf4 $13) 35... Bxg4 36. Nxg4 Kf7 (36... Nf6 $5 37. Kg3 (37. gxf6+ $2 Kf7 38. Kg3 Rhg8 $17) (37. Nh6 Ng4+ $1 $19) 37... N6h5+ 38. Kf3 Rf8 39. Nh6 $14) 37. Rg1 (37. Rf1 $1 Rg7 38. Nh6+ Kg6 39. Bxf4 Nxf4 40. Kg3 $14) 37... Rg7 (37... Nf6 38. Kg3 $14 (38. Nh6+ $2 Rhxh6 39. gxh6 Ng4+ $17)) 38. Nh6+ Kg6 39. Rf1 (39. Nf5 Rd7 40. Bxf4 Nxf4 41. Kg3 $14) 39... Rc8 40. Nf5 Rd7 41. Ng3 $4 (41. Bxf4 Nxf4 42. Kg3) 41... b5 42. axb5 axb5 $17 43. Bxf4 Nxf4 44. Nf5 Rc5 (44... b4 $5 45. Nb5 Rc2+ 46. Kg3 Rxb2) 45. Kg3 b4 46. Nd1 Rc2 47. Kg4 Rg2+ 48. Kf3 Rd2 49. Kg4 Nh5 50. Nde3 Rxb2 51. Nc4 Rg2+ 52. Kf3 Rc2 53. Nfxd6 Rc3+ 54. Kg2 Kh7 $4 (54... Ra7 55. Nxe5+ Kh7 (55... Kg7 56. Ne8+ Kg8 57. Nf6+ Nxf6 58. gxf6 Ra2+ 59. Kg1 $10) 56. Rf7+ Rxf7 57. g6+ Kg7 58. gxf7 Nf4+ 59. Kf2 b3 60. Nd7 Ng6 61. h5 Rc7 62. Nf5+ Kxf7 63. hxg6+ Kxg6 64. Ne5+ Kf6 65. Nd3 $10) (54... Nf4+ $1 55. Kh2 (55. Rxf4 exf4 56. Ne5+ Kh5 57. Nxd7 b3 58. Nf6+ Kxh4 59. e5 Rg3+ 60. Kf2 b2 $19) 55... Kh5 56. Nxe5 Rxd6 57. Rxf4 Ra6 58. g6 b3 $17) 55. Ra1 $1 $13 Nf4+ 56. Kf2 Rc7 57. Ne3