1. d4 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow Krasenkow: 'Krasenkow,M''} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bd2 Bg7 6. e4 Nb6 7. Be3 O-O 8. Be2 Nc6 9. Nf3 Bg4 10. d5 Bxf3 11. gxf3 Na5 {This looks stronger than Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: 'This looks stronger than''} (11... Ne5 {, after which the black knight disturbs his other pieces. The simplest way to refute it is probably} 12. Qb3 {, e.g.} c6 (12... Qc8 13. h4 h5 14. O-O-O c6 15. Kb1 Ned7 16. d6 Qe8 17. Rhg1 Kh7 18. f4 Rd8 19. e5 {1-0 Boensch,U-Gyorkos,L/Szekszard op 1989 (27)}) 13. f4 Ned7 14. Rd1 (14. dxc6 bxc6 15. Rd1 e6 16. h4 Qe7 17. h5 Rab8 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Qc2 $16 {Hausrath,D-Mohandesi,S/Interteam ch, BEL 1996/1-0 (54)}) 14... Qc7 15. O-O Rfd8 16. a4 Nf6 17. a5 Nbd7 18. dxc6 bxc6 19. e5 Nd5 20. Nxd5 cxd5 21. Rxd5 $18 {Dao Thien Hai-Zaw Oo/Yangon zt 1998/1-0 (46)}) {This is one of the rare occasions when a knight is better placed at the edge of the board than in the centre! Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: 'This is one of the rare occasions when a knight is better placed at the edge of the board than in the centre!''} 12. Bd4 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: 'Krasenkow: 'This is one of the rare occasions when a knight is better placed at the edge of the board than in the centre!'''} c5 $5 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: '!?'' Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: '!?' Krasenkow: '!?''} (12... e5 13. Bc5 Re8 14. Bb5 c6 15. dxc6 bxc6 16. Ba6 Bf8 (16... Qf6 $5) 17. Bxf8 Rxf8 18. Qxd8 Raxd8 19. Rd1 Rd4 20. Ke2 Rfd8 21. b3 $14 {Soppe,G-Malisauskas,V/Erevan olm 1996/1-0 (74)}) (12... Bh6 13. Nb5 $5 (13. h4 Nac4 14. h5 Bd2+ 15. Kf1 Bf4 16. hxg6 fxg6 $14) 13... Nac4 (13... a6 14. Na3 $16) (13... c6 14. Bc3 cxb5 15. Qd4 f6 16. Bxa5 $16) 14. Bc3 Nd6 15. Qd4 f6 16. Qd3 Nxb5 17. Qxb5 $16) 13. Bxg7 (13. Bxc5 Nac4 14. Bxc4 Nxc4 15. Qe2 Nxb2 16. Qxb2 Qc7 17. Bb4 (17. Qa3 Rfc8) 17... a5 $17 {Atalik}) 13... Kxg7 14. Qc2 e5 {Black closes down the position. Now White castles to the q-side and mutual pawn attacks start. However, the poor position of the black a5 knight enables White to come first. Korchnoi nicely underlines the drawbacks of Black's plan. Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: 'Black closes down the position. Now White castles to the q-side and mutual pawn attacks start. However, the poor position of the black a5 knight enables White to come first. Korchnoi nicely underlines the drawbacks of Black's plan.''} (14... e6 $5) 15. Nd1 {[%cal Yd1e3,Ye3g4]} Nd7 16. Qc3 $1 $146 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: ''' Krasenkow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '''' An interesting novelty. White has two ideas: 17.f4 and 17.b5 Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Krasenkow,Michal: 'An interesting novelty. White has two ideas: 17.f4 and 17.b5''} (16. Ne3 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Ü 71/485''Ü 71/485 Krasenkow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: 'Ü 71/485'''} c4 $1 17. h4 b5 18. h5 Nb7 19. O-O-O Nd6 20. Rdg1 g5 21. Qd2 Kh8 22. Kb1 f6 $13 {Sokolov,I-Atalik,S/New York 1997/½-½(42)}) 16... a6 $6 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: '?' '?' '?''Krasenkow,Michal: '?!' Krasenk'Krasenkow,Michal: '?!' Krasenkow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '?'' Krasenkow: '?!'' ow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '?'''} (16... f6 17. Bb5 {to exchange the "bad" bishop} b6 18. Bxd7 Qxd7 19. h4 {But after the text move Whie starts a strong attack.}) (16... c4 17. f4 f6 18. b4 cxb3 19. axb3 b6 20. b4 Rc8 21. Qh3 Nc4 22. Rxa7 $18) (16... b6 $5 17. Ne3 $14 (17. f4 $2 Qh4 18. Bb5 Qxf4 $17)) 17. f4 f6 (17... Nf6 18. fxe5 Nxe4 19. Qe3 Ng5 (19... Qxd5 $2 20. f3 $18) (19... Qh4 20. O-O $1 $16) 20. Rg1 h6 21. f4 Nh7 22. Nc3 b5 23. O-O-O $16) 18. f5 $1 g5 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: '?!' '?!' '?!''Krasenkow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '?!'''} (18... gxf5 19. Ne3 $5 {Kortschnoj,Viktor: '!' '!' '!''Krasenkow,Michal: '!?' Krasenk'Krasenkow,Michal: '!?' Krasenkow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '!'' Krasenkow: '!?'' ow,Michal: 'Kortschnoj,Viktor: '!'''} fxe4 (19... f4 20. Nf5+ $16 { Kortschnoj,Viktor: ' Krasenkow,Michal: '''}) 20. Bg4 (20. Nf5+ Kh8 21. Rg1 Rg8 22. Rxg8+ Kxg8 23. 