{Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: 'Inf 93/449''} 1. d4 {Tyomkin,D Tyomkin,Dimitri Tyomkin,Dimitri} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 b6 7. Nf3 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. Be2 Nbd7 10. O-O Ne4 11. Qc2 f5 12. b4 Rf6 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Larsen and later Smyslov made this line popular for Black;'} 13. d5 Rg6 14. Nd4 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: '!' '!' Tyomkin,D: '!' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'The only way for White to fight for the opening advantage. White allows Black to get his queen to g5, while White manages to repulse Black's attack there. Kramnik was definitely familiar with this line, not sure about Judit..'} Qg5 $5 {Kramnik,V: 'N' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'That's a novelty, and definitely an improvement of Matveeva-Maric game where Black got in trouble very soon.'} ({Judit diverts from} 14... exd5 {(Matveeva - Maric, Budva 2003).} 15. f3 Qg5 16. Bd3 dxc4 17. Bxc4+ d5 18. Bb5 $16 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '''} c6 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 19. fxe4 $16 cxb5 20. exf5 Rc8 21. Qf2 Rf6 22. Bb2 Rf7 23. Ne6 Qg4 24. h3 Qh5 25. Bd4 g6 $2 26. Qg3 Rc2 27. Nd8 {1-0 Matveeva,S-Maric,A/Budva 2003/CBM 096 ext}) 15. g3 exd5 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: '15...f8 is the way to keep tension in the centre, although this doesn't bring black an equal play.'} (15... Nf8 $142 $14 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '''} 16. Bb2 $14 {Tyomkin,Dimitri}) 16. cxd5 Bxd5 17. Bc4 $1 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '!'' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Exchanging Black's bishop, White gets access to Black's queenside.'} ({Kramnik does not trust} 17. Qxc7 Ne5 $1 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: '!'} 18. f4 Qh4 (18... Qf6) 19. Nxf5 Qh3 20. Ne7+ Kh8 21. Nxg6+ hxg6 22. Qc2 Nxg3 23. Rf2 Rc8 24. Qd2 Nxe2+ 25. Qxe2 Nf3+ 26. Rxf3 Bxf3 27. Qf2 Be4 $19) 17... Bxc4 18. Qxc4+ Kh8 19. Qc6 $1 {Black is forced to play a weakening move. Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Important accuracy- Black's rook is worse on d8 than on a8;'} (19. Qxc7 Ne5 $13 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: ''' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'same as in the game, but Black's queen doesn't need to guard the rook;'}) 19... Rd8 (19... Qd8 20. Nxf5 Ng5 21. f4 $16) 20. Qxc7 Ne5 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Black pawns are targets, but he still has some hopes on the kingside;'} 21. Ra2 $3 {White develops an eminent defender. Tyomkin,Dimitri: '!' '!' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '!!'' Tyomkin,D: '!' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Bringing the rook to a defense- white intends f4 now. Immediate f4 would lead to a draw only.'} (21. Qxa7 $2 Qh5 $40 22. Qxb6 Rf8 23. Ra2 Ng4 24. Nf3 Ng5 25. Nh4 Nxh2 26. Nxg6+ hxg6 27. f4 Nxf1 $19) (21. f4 Qxg3+ $1 22. hxg3 Rxg3+ 23. Kh1 (23. Kh2 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} Ng4+) 23... Rh3+ $11 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '=''} 24. Kg1 $11 {Tyomkin,Dimitri}) 21... Rf8 (21... Qf6 22. f3 Ng5 23. f4 Nh3+ 24. Kg2 Ng4 25. Nxf5 Re8 26. e4 Qe6 27. Re2 $18) (21... Ng4 22. Nf3 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: '' '' Tyomkin,D: ''} Qf6 23. Bb2 Qf8 24. Nh4 Rg5 25. f4 $18) (21... Rd7 22. Qc8+ Rd8 23. Qxf5 Qxf5 24. Nxf5 Rf6 25. Nd4 $16) (21... Re8 22. f4 Qg4 23. Rg2 Nd3 24. Qxa7 $16 h5 $140 25. Qf7) (21... Nd3 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 22. Qxa7 Nxc1 23. Rxc1 f4 24. exf4 Qxf4 25. Qc7 Rg8 26. Rd1 {and again Black fails to create any serious threats against the white king;}) 22. f4 $1 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'This move freezes Black's attack, as well as a2 joins the defense;'} Qg4 {Kramnik,V: ''} 23. Qe7 {Kramnik,V: '!'} (23. fxe5 $2 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} Nxg3 24. Rg2 Ne2+) 23... Rg8 (23... Nd7 24. Rg2 Nef6 25. Qe6 $16) (23... Kg8 $142 24. Rg2 (24. Ne6 Rb8) 24... Nf7 25. Qxa7 d5) 24. Rg2 Nd3 (24... Nc4 25. Qxa7 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'The same idea as in the game - Black doesn't have anything on the kingside, so white can safely grab pawns;'} (25. Qf7 d5 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 26. Qxf5 Qxf5 27. Nxf5 $16) 25... d5 26. Qf7 Nf6 27. Qe6 $16) 25. Qxa7 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'White is safe on the kingside, so Vlad simply picks Black pawns;'} h5 (25... Qh3 26. Qa6 (26. Qxb6 $4 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} Nxg3 {and Black even wins here!} 27. Rxg3 (27. hxg3 Rh6) 27... Rxg3+ 28. hxg3 Qxg3+ 29. Kh1 Qh3+ 30. Kg1 g5 $19) (26. Qb7 $5 {Tyomkin,Dimitri the move which wasn't mentioned by any other commentators of this game.} h5 27. Rd1 {[%cal Gb7g2] and again black doesn't have anything;}) 26... Nxc1 (26... Nxg3 27. Qxd3 $1 $18 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Kramnik,V: '+-!''} Nxf1 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 28. Qxf1 $18) 27. Rxc1 Nxg3 28. hxg3 Rxg3 29. Rxg3 Qxg3+ 30. Kf1 Qxe3 31. Ne2 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: '!' '!' Tyomkin,D: '!'} Qf3+ 32. Ke1 $18) 26. Qa6 {Kramnik,V: '!' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'My Fritzy doesn't understand why Kramnik didn't grab another one....'} (26. Qxb6 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} h4 27. Qb5 hxg3 28. hxg3 Nxc1 29. Nxf5 $1 $18) 26... Nxc1 (26... h4 27. Qxd3 hxg3 28. hxg3 Rh6 29. Nf3 Nxg3 30. Nh2 Qh5 31. Re1 Ne4 32. Ree2 $18) 27. Rxc1 h4 28. Qe2 {Kramnik,V: '' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'An extra pawn for Kramnik in endgame means a point in the crosstable in the most cases;'} Qxe2 (28... Ra8 29. Qxg4 fxg4 30. Ra2 hxg3 31. Nf5 Rf6 32. Nxg3 $16) 29. Rxe2 hxg3 (29... Rf8 30. Rec2 $5 hxg3 (30... Kh7 31. Rc8 Rff6 32. Re8 hxg3 33. h3 Rh6 34. Kg2 $16) 31. Rc8 gxh2+ 32. Kxh2 $16) 30. Nxf5 gxh2+ 31. Kh1 {Kramnik,V: '!'} Rg1+ 32. Rxg1 hxg1=Q+ 33. Kxg1 {Kramnik wins the endgame. Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'White has an extra pawn, all black pawns are weak... And Vlad is sitting in front of you... sounds bad.'} Ra8 34. Ra2 (34. Ne7 g5 $1) (34. Kg2 $5 Rxa3 35. Kf3 Nf6 36. Nxd6 $16) 34... Nc3 (34... Kg8 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 35. Ne7+ Kf7 36. Nd5 b5 37. Kg2 {[%cal Gg2f3]}) 35. Rh2+ Kg8 36. Rg2 Kf7 {Kramnik,V: '!'} (36... Rxa3 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 37. Rxg7+ Kf8 38. Rb7) 37. Nxd6+ Ke6 38. Nc4 (38. Rg6+ Kd5 39. Nf5 b5 40. Rxg7 Rxa3 41. Nd4 Kc4 $132) 38... b5 39. Na5 Kf6 40. Rd2 $1 {Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'following with d3. Vlad makes it looking easy.'} (40. Kf2 Nb1 41. e4 Nxa3 42. Ke3 Rc8 43. e5+ Kf7) (40. Rc2 $1 Ne4 41. Kg2 $18) 40... g5 {Kramnik,V: '!' Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'Pawn exchanges are favoing the weaker side, but here nothing can help Black;'} 41. Rd3 Ne4 42. fxg5+ (42. Kg2 Kf5 $1 43. Rd5+ (43. fxg5 Rc8 $1 $132) 43... Kg4 $132 44. fxg5 (44. Re5 Rc8 $3) 44... Rc8 45. g6 Rc2+ 46. Kf1 Kf3 47. Rf5+ Kxe3 48. Kg1 Ng3 49. Rc5 Ne2+ 50. Kf1 Rb2 51. Re5+ Kf3 52. Ke1 Nf4 53. Kd1 Nd3 $11) (42. Rd5 gxf4 43. exf4 Rg8+ 44. Kh2 Rg4 45. Rxb5 Rxf4 $132) 42... Kxg5 43. Kg2 Rf8 44. Rd5+ Kg4 45. Rd4 (45. Re5 $5) 45... Kf5 (45... Rf2+ 46. Kg1 Kg3 47. Rxe4 Ra2 48. Kf1 Kf3 49. Rf4+ Kxe3 50. Rf5 Rxa3 51. Rxb5 Ra2 52. Nc6 Rc2 53. Rc5 $18) 46. Nc6 Rg8+ 47. Kf1 Ra8 (47... Rg3 $142 48. Rd3) 48. Ne7+ Ke5 49. Nc6+ Kf5 50. Ne7+ Ke5 51. Ng6+ Kf5 52. Nh4+ Ke5 53. Nf3+ Kf5 54. Nh4+ Ke5 55. Nf3+ Kf5 56. Rd5+ Kf6 (56... Kg4 57. Kg2 $1 Rxa3 58. Nh2+ (58. Ne5+ {Tyomkin,Dimitri} Kf5 59. Nc4+ Ke6 60. Rxb5 $18) 58... Kh4 59. Rd4 $1 Rxe3 60. Nf1 Re2+ (60... Re1 61. Ng3 $18) 61. Kf3 Rf2+ 62. Kxe4 Rxf1 63. Kd5+ Kg5 64. Kc6 $18) 57. Rd3 Rh8 58. Ke2 Ke7 (58... Rh1 59. Rd4 Kf5 60. Rd5+ Kg4 61. Ne5+ Kf5 62. Nc6+ Ke6 63. Rd8 Ra1 64. Nd4+ Ke7 65. Ra8 $18) 59. Nd4 Rh2+ (59... Rh5 60. Kf3 Nd6 (60... Nf6 {Tyomkin,Dimitri} 61. Rc3 {[%cal Gc3c5]}) 61. Rc3 $18) (59... Nd6 60. Rc3 $18) 60. Kf3 Nd6 61. Rc3 Rh3+ (61... Kd7 62. Rc5 Rh3+ 63. Kf4 Rh4+ 64. Kg3 $18) 62. Kg4 {Both players complained about tiredness. Tyomkin,Dimitri: 'The pawn b5 falls as well.... One more very long and exhausting game for both players in Sofia; From Sofia reports: "Both players looked very tired at the press conference and agreed that one additional day of rest would have been a good idea." '} 1-0

