{Vladimir Kramnik demonstrated that Black's task to equalise in the Symmetrical Tarrasch might not be as easy as it's supposed to be.} 1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. e3 e6 5. d4 d5 {As a rule, nowadays in this symmetrical Tarrasch Black prefers to have the move ...a7-a6 played rather than ...Nb8-c6 but this is not possible if it arises via this line of the English Opening.} 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bb5 {This line might promise a small advantage for White.} (7. Be2 {is a common option, too, though it gives Black more options, such as} cxd4 $5 ({or even} 7... c4 $5 8. O-O Bb4)) 7... Bd6 (7... a6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. O-O {is supposed to be somewhat better for White. Actually it would be a transposition to the line discussed below:} Bd6 10. dxc5 (10. Qa4 $5 Qc7 11. e4 $5 cxd4 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. exd5 O-O 14. Qxc6 Bb7 15. Qxc7 Bxc7 16. Nxd4 Bxd5 {gave Black decent chances to hold balance in Kasparov - Agzamov, USSR-ch Frunze 1981}) 10... Bxc5 11. b3 O-O 12. Bb2 Bg4 13. Rc1 {- see the comments to 10. ..Bg4.}) 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. O-O O-O 10. b3 {This is in fact the Rubinstein variation of the Nimzo-Indian with reversed colours and White's extra tempo would really make Black's activity not quite sufficient to compensate the weakness of the isolated pawn.} Bd6 {This continuation is not very popular.} ({Black usually plays} 10... Bg4 11. Bb2 a6 ({or} 11... Rc8 12. Rc1 Bd6 13. h3 Bh5 14. Be2 a6 15. Nh4 Bxe2 16. Nxe2 Be5 17. Bxe5 Nxe5 18. Qd4 Re8 19. Nf5 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Qd7 21. Nfg3 {with a small but stable advantage, Grachev - Chadaev, RUS-chT Loo 2013}) 12. Bxc6 $5 bxc6 13. Rc1 Ba7 (13... Bd6 14. Ne2 Bxf3 (14... Rc8 15. Ne5 $14 {P. Jaracz - Duppel, Boeblingen 2002}) 15. gxf3 Rc8 {and here in the game Ashley - De Firmian, New York 1996, White could have thought about} 16. Ng3 $5 Re8 17. Qd3 a5 18. f4 $14 {, maintaining some pressure}) 14. Ne2 Qd6 15. Be5 (15. Ne5 $5) 15... Qe7 16. Ned4 Bxf3 (16... c5 $5 17. Nc6 Qe6 18. Bxf6 Qxc6 19. Be7 Rfc8 20. h3 Bxf3 (20... Bf5 21. Bxc5 Bxc5 22. b4 $14) 21. Qxf3 $14) 17. Nxf3 Rfc8 18. Qd3 a5 19. Bxf6 Qxf6 20. Rc2 $14 {with advantage, Carlsen - Radjabov, Sandnes 2013.}) (10... Be6 11. Bb2 Qe7 12. Ne2 Rac8 13. a3 (13. Nf4 $5) (13. Rc1 $5) 13... Rfd8 14. Ned4 Bg4 15. Be2 Ne4 {was okay for Black in T. Petrosian - Botvinnik, WCh (m/25) Moscow 1963, but White seems to be able to maintain some pressure in this line, too.}) 11. Bb2 Bg4 12. Be2 ({Kramnik deviates from a previous game against the same opponent:} 12. h3 Bh5 13. Be2 a6 14. Nh4 Bxe2 15. Nxe2 Re8 16. Rc1 Ne4 17. Nf5 Be5 18. Bxe5 Rxe5 19. Nfd4 Qd7 20. Nf4 {with an edge, Kramnik - J.Polgar, Baku (rapid) 2010.}) 12... a6 {In some games this position has been reached with Black to play, but it just means White was a bit careless in the opening.} (12... Rc8 {is a natural option, too. The position after} 13. Rc1 {has been tested in practice many times. For example,} Bb8 14. h3 (14. Nb5 Ne4 15. Nbd4 Re8 16. h3 Bxf3 17. Nxf3 Qd6 {was fine for Black in Sunye - Kasparov, Graz 1981}) 14... Bh5 15. Nh4 $1 Qd6 (15... Bxe2 16. Nxe2 Re8 17. Nf5 Qd7 18. Neg3 Be5 $14 {Voiska - I.Farago, Bolzano 2000}) 16. g3 Bxe2 (16... Bg6 {does not equalise:} 17. Nb5 Qe6 18. Bxf6 Qxf6 19. Nxg6 hxg6 {and here in the game Seirawan - De Firmian, Key West 1994, White could have obtained the better chances by a straightforward} 20. Qxd5 Qb2 21. Bc4) 17. Nxe2 Rfd8 ({in the game Tomashevsky - Romanishin, EU-ch Legnica 2013, Black tried} 17... Qe6 18. Bxf6 Qxf6 {and here White unnecessarily rejected} 19. Qxd5 $1 {and if} Qb2 {then} 20. Qb5 $1) 18. Nd4 {and White has managed to obtain an edge, Zvjaginsev - Lutz, Essen 2002.}) 13. Nd4 {A new and possibly the best continuation. White is trying to simplify the position - a common strategy while playing against an isolated pawn.} ({White tried other options, such as} 13. Rc1 {and, curiously enough, in some games this position has been reached with White to play!}) 13... Bd7 ({Both} 13... Nxd4 14. Qxd4) ({and} 13... Bxe2 14. Ncxe2 {could only promote White's plans.}) 14. Bf3 Be6 (14... Qc7 $5 {might have been stronger:} 15. h3 (15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Bxh2+ 17. Kh1 Be5 18. Qh5 g6 (18... Nxd4 $6 19. exd4 Bf6 20. Ba3) (18... Bxd4 19. exd4 $14) 19. Qf3 Qd8 $5 20. Kg1 Qg5 $13) (15. g3 Bh3 16. Re1 Nxd4 17. Qxd4 Be5 $13) 15... Nxd4 16. Qxd4 Bh2+ 17. Kh1 Be5 18. Qd2 {with a slight edge.}) 15. Nxc6 $1 {One of the typical methods of play against an isolated pawn – White transposes to a position with an isolated pawn couple.} bxc6 16. Rc1 Rc8 17. Na4 c5 {Black is trying to get counterplay at any cost but this pawn push does not solve her problems.} ({Yet Black was already under pressure:} 17... Qe7 18. Qd3 a5 19. Rc2 {seemed even more difficult but otherwise White's knight could have been installed on c5.}) 18. Qe2 (18. Bxf6 Qxf6 19. Nb6 ({surely not} 19. Bxd5 $2 Qe5 20. f4 Qxe3+ $1 21. Kh1 Bxf4) 19... Rcd8 20. Nxd5 {could have been met by} Bxh2+ 21. Kxh2 Qe5+ 22. Kg1 Bxd5 23. Bxd5 Rxd5 24. Qe2 Qe6 {and White could hardly get anything substantial from Black's damaged Q-side pawn structure as his king was not quite safe.}) 18... c4 19. g3 (19. Rfd1 $5 {was probably more precise:} Qa5 20. Bc3 Bb4 21. Bd4 Nd7 22. Qb2 {with advantage.}) 19... Qa5 $5 {Following the same strategy - only activity can compensate Black's poor pawn structure.} 20. Bc3 (20. Bxf6 gxf6 21. Rfd1 Rfd8 {was okay for Black}) ({but} 20. Rfd1 $5 {was still worth considering.}) 20... Qb5 $6 {This retreat promotes White's plans.} (20... Bb4 $1 {should have been played though White still had chances to maintain a slight edge after} 21. Qb2 $5 (21. Bxf6 gxf6 22. Rfd1 Rfd8 $13) 21... Bxc3 22. Nxc3 Rb8 $5 23. Rfd1 Rfc8 24. Ne2) 21. Bxf6 gxf6 22. Nc3 Qa5 23. bxc4 $1 ({The interesting} 23. Bxd5 {could be well met by} cxb3 $1 ({but not} 23... Bxd5 $2 24. Qh5 $1 Be5 25. b4 $1 $16 Qd8 $2 26. Rfd1 $18) 24. Ne4 Rxc1 25. Rxc1 Bxd5 (25... Qxa2 26. Qxa2 bxa2 27. Bxa2 $1 $14) 26. Nxf6+ Kg7 27. Qb2 $1 Kg6 $1 28. Nd7 {with unclear play, according to Mikhail Golubev.}) 23... dxc4 (23... Rxc4 $2 {failed to} 24. Nxd5 $1 Rxc1 (24... Bxd5 25. Rxc4) 25. Nxf6+ Kg7 26. Nh5+ {and Rf1xc1.}) 24. Rfd1 Ba3 $6 ({Black should have kept her dark-squared bishop in the center by} 24... Be5 $1 {with reasonable chances to hold:} 25. Nd5 Qc5 {and now:} 26. e4 (26. Be4 $5 Rfd8 ({not} 26... f5 $2 27. Bxf5 $1 Bxf5 28. Qh5 $16) 27. Qf3 (27. f4 Bb2 $1 28. Rc2 (28. Qxb2 Bxd5 29. Bxd5 Rxd5 30. Rxd5 Qxd5 $13) 28... c3 29. Nxf6+ Kg7 30. Nd5 Bxd5 31. Qg4+ Kf8 32. Bxd5 Rc7 $13) 27... Kg7 28. Bf5 c3 29. Qg4+ Kf8 30. Bxe6 (30. e4 c2 31. Rd2 Qb5 32. Bxe6 Qb2 $1) 30... fxe6 31. Qxe6 (31. Nf4 Bxf4 32. Rxd8+ Rxd8 33. Qxf4 Kf7 34. Qe4 Rd2 35. Qxh7+ Kf8 36. Qg6 Qe5 $44) 31... Rc6 32. Nf4 $1 Rxd1+ 33. Rxd1 Qb6 {and the strong pieces and passed pawn on c3 help Black to hold on. For example,} 34. Qf5 c2 35. Rc1 Bxf4 36. Qxf4 Qb2 37. e4 Kg7 {and so on}) 26... c3 27. Bg4 Rfd8 28. Qf3 c2 29. Rd2 Qb5 30. Rdxc2 Rxc2 31. Rxc2 Qb1+ 32. Qd1 Qxd1+ 33. Bxd1 f5 (33... Bxd5 34. Rd2 $16) 34. Bf3 fxe4 35. Ne7+ Kf8 36. Nc6 Rd6 37. Bxe4 $14 {with an extra pawn, which would be rather difficult to convert.}) 25. Rc2 Rfd8 (25... Rcd8 {couldn't prevent} 26. Bd5 $1 {as after} Bxd5 {White has} 27. Qg4+ Kh8 28. Qf5) 26. Bd5 $1 {This nice trick secures White's superiority.} Bb4 ({After} 26... Bxd5 {White could simply play} 27. Nxd5 {since Black's rook on c8 comes under attack from g4.}) (26... Kh8 $5 {might have been more accurate though White kept a tangible advantage after, for example,} 27. Bxe6 $5 fxe6 28. Rd4) 27. Bxe6 $6 ({Another trick} 27. Ne4 $1 {was much more energetic:} Bxd5 (27... Be7 28. Bxe6 fxe6 29. Rxd8+ Rxd8 30. Qg4+ Kf7 31. Rxc4) 28. Rxd5 $1 Rxd5 (28... Qxd5 29. Nxf6+) 29. Qg4+ $1 {followed by Qg4xc8, as was pointed by Golubev.}) 27... fxe6 28. Ne4 f5 29. Nf6+ $1 {White complicated matters a little bit and so has to play precisely.} (29. Rxd8+ Qxd8 30. Rxc4 Rxc4 31. Qxc4 fxe4 32. Qxb4 {was less convincing:} Qd1+ 33. Kg2 Qf3+ 34. Kf1 Qh1+ 35. Ke2 Qf3+ 36. Ke1 Qh1+ 37. Kd2 Qxh2 38. Qb8+ Kg7 39. Qe5+ Kf7 40. Ke2 Qh1 {with chances to escape.}) (29. Ng5 $5 {was also better for White but Black could fight:} Rxd1+ 30. Qxd1 Qd5 31. Qh5 Qd7 32. Qh6 Rc6 33. Qh4 $1 Qd1+ $1 (33... e5 34. Nf3 Be7 35. Qh5 Bf6 36. Rd2 $16) 34. Kg2 h5 35. Rb2 a5 36. f3 $1 c3 37. Qxh5 Qd7 38. Rc2 e5 $14 {etc.}) 29... Kf7 (29... Kh8 30. Rd4 $1 Be7 31. Nh5 c3 32. Rc4 $1 $16 {Golubev}) 30. Nd7 $1 Kg6 $6 (30... Be7 31. Rxc4 Rxc4 32. Qxc4 {was bad for Black but the text move loses quicker.}) 31. Rxc4 ({Opening the center with} 31. e4 $5 {was also strong enough.}) 31... Qb5 32. a4 $1 Qxa4 ({Obviously, the rook was untouchable:} 32... Qxc4 $2 33. Ne5+) 33. Rcd4 {Kramnik prefers a safe approach.} ({There was a quick mate after} 33. Rh4 $1 Rxd7 34. Qh5+ Kf6 35. Qh6+ Ke5 (35... Ke7 36. Qxh7+ Kd8 37. Qg8+ Kc7 38. Rc1+ $18) 36. Re4+ $3 {(Golubev) but it was not so easy to find in a rapid game.}) 33... Qa5 34. Ne5+ ({But here he should really have played} 34. Rh4 $1 {as it was checkmate for free.}) 34... Kf6 35. Qh5 Rxd4 (35... Kxe5 36. f4+ Kf6 37. Rxd8 Rxd8 38. Rxd8 Qxd8 39. Qg5+ $18) 36. exd4 (36. Qh6+ $1 Kxe5 37. Rxd4 $18 {followed by f2-f4 was quicker.}) 36... Qc7 37. Qh6+ Ke7 38. Qh4+ Kd6 39. d5 $1 {White can hardly spoil his win in such a position.} Kxe5 40. Qxb4 ({It was more precise to play with checks:} 40. Qd4+ $1 Kd6 41. Qxb4+ Qc5 42. Qf4+ {etc.}) 40... Kf6 41. Qh4+ Kg6 42. dxe6 Qc2 43. Qd4 Qe2 44. Qd7 Rc7 45. Qe8+ Kf6 46. Qf8+ Kg6 47. Qd6 $2 {Missing the following reply.} (47. Qg8+ Rg7 ({or} 47... Kf6 48. Qd8+ Kg6 49. h4 $1) 48. Qe8+ Kh6 49. Rd4 {was much stronger.}) 47... Rc2 $2 {Black returns the favour.} ({After} 47... Rc6 $1 48. Qxc6 Qxd1+ 49. Kg2 Kf6 {White's task could have become much more complicated. Yet, he was still winning, as Mikhail Golubev showed in his comments:} 50. Qb7 $5 Kxe6 51. Qxa6+ Kf7 52. Qh6 Qd5+ 53. Kh3 Kg8 54. f4 {followed by the king's advance to g5.}) 48. e7+ Kf7 49. Qd5+ {White finally launches the decisive attack.} Kg6 ({Grabbing the pawn on e7 was fatal for the king:} 49... Kxe7 50. Qd8+ Kf7 51. Rd7+ {etc.}) 50. Qg8+ Kf6 51. Qf8+ Kg5 52. Qg7+ Kh5 53. Qxh7+ Kg5 54. Qg8+ Kf6 55. Rd6+ Kxe7 56. Qd8+ Kf7 57. Rf6+ {and Black resigned.} 1-0

