{Kramnik,V: 'Inf 67/346'} 1. e4 {Kramnik,V Knaak} e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. c4 Nf6 ({John Watson suggests} 5... c6 $5 {when the sequence} 6. Nc3 Ne7 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 O-O 9. O-O Nd7 {planning ...Nb6 is solid enough for Black.}) 6. Nc3 c6 7. Bg5 {A very interesting move. The pin sets Black problems by indirectly threatening d5. Polgar has no wish to concede the centre directly with 7...dxc4 8 Bxc4: she would like White to waste a move with Bd3 or Be2 first and only then play ... dxc4 when after Bxc4 she has gained a tempo. Instead Polgar comes up with a fine response. She simply ignores the threat to d5! In view of his lack of success in the opening, Kramnik was later critical of his bishop move and in Informator 67 recommended A very interesting move. The pin sets Black problems by indirectly threatening d5. Polgar has no wish to concede the centre directly with 7...dxc4 8 Bxc4: she would like White to waste a move with Bd3 or Be2 first and only then play ... dxc4 when after Bxc4 she has gained a tempo. Instead Polgar comes up with a fine response. She simply ignores the threat to d5! In view of his lack of success in the opening, Kramnik was later critical of his bishop move and in Informator 67 recommended Kramnik,V: 'N?!' A very interesting move. The pin sets Black problems by indirectly threatening d5. Polgar has no wish to concede the centre directly with 7...dxc4 8 Bxc4: she would like White to waste a move with Bd3 or Be2 first and only then play ... dxc4 when after Bxc4 she has gained a tempo. Instead Polgar comes up with a fine response. She simply ignores the threat to d5! In view of his lack of success in the opening, Kramnik was later critical of his bishop move and in Informator 67 recommended Knaak: '?!' '?!' Kramnik,Vladimir: 'N?!' Knaak,Rainer: '?!' Knaak,R: '?!' Knaak: 'Weiß versucht, das Schlagen auf c4 zu erzwingen, bevor der f1 gezogen hat, um mit f1xc4 ein Tempo zu sparen.'} (7. c5 {or or Kramnik,V: '- 53/246' Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Ü 53/246'}) (7. cxd5 {as preferable.}) 7... O-O $1 {This pawn sacrifice should solve Black's opening problems. Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak: '!''} (7... dxc4 {Knaak} 8. Bxc4 $14) 8. cxd5 h6 {Knaak: '!' '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' Knaak,R: '!' Knaak: 'Die Bedeutung dieses Zwischenzuges wird gleich ersichtlich.'} (8... Qb6 $5 {Kramnik,V} 9. Bxf6 {Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak'} Qxb2 10. Qc1 Qxc1+ (10... Ba3 $2 11. Bg5 $18) 11. Rxc1 gxf6 12. Be2 $11 {Kramnik,Vladimir: '=' Knaak,Rainer: '=' Knaak,R: '='} cxd5 $6 13. Nxd5 Be6 $6 14. Nxf6+ Kg7 15. d5 $14 {½-½ Teske,H-Knaak,R/DDR-ch Stralsund 1988 (33)}) 9. Bh4 $6 {White should try} (9. Bxf6 Qxf6 {Knaak: 'Schwarz hat auf jeden Fall genug Kompensation für den Bauern, welchen Weiß zudem kaum verteidigen kann.'} 10. Be2 (10. dxc6 {Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak'} Nxc6 11. Be2) 10... Rd8 {Kramnik,V: '!?' Kramnik,Vladimir: '!?'} 11. dxc6 Nxc6 12. O-O {-Kramnik} Nxd4 {Knaak} 13. Nxd4 Be5 $15) 9... Qb6 {Kramnik,V: '!' Knaak: '!' '!' Kramnik,Vladimir: '!' Knaak,Rainer: '!' Knaak,R: '!' Knaak: 'Weiß muß um Ausgleich kämpfen.'} (9... cxd5 {Kramnik,V} 10. Be2 Be6 11. O-O $14) 10. Qd2 {Kramnik,V: '!' Kramnik,Vladimir: '!'} (10. Bxf6 $2 {Kramnik,V Knaak} Qxb2 11. Rc1 (11. Qc1 Ba3 $1 $19 {Kramnik,Vladimir: '!' Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak: '-+!'' Da sieht man den Sinn von 8...h6!, denn 12.g5 ist verhindert! Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak: 'Da sieht man den Sinn von 8...h6!, denn 12.g5 ist verhindert!''}) (11. Ne4 Bb4+ 12. Ned2 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 gxf6 $17) 11... Bb4 12. Qd2 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2 gxf6 $17) (10. Qb3 {Kramnik,V} Qxb3 11. axb3 Nxd5 $15) 10... Nxd5 (10... Re8+ {Kramnik,V} 11. Be2 Ne4 12. Nxe4 Rxe4 13. O-O) 11. Bg3 {Knaak: 'Die schwarze Stellung ist scheinbar so gut, daß man nur nach Varianten sucht, die klaren Vorteil bringen.'} Bxg3 {Knaak: 'Solche Züge sind schnell gemacht, weil Schwarz den Entwicklungsvorsprung nicht einbüßen will.'} (11... Bb4 $5 {Kramnik,V Knaak: 'war eher geeignet, auf Vorteil zu spielen.'} 12. Be2 {Kramnik,Vladimir: 'Knaak'} Re8 (12... Qa5 