{[%mdl 8256] A36: Symmetrical English vs ...g6: 4 Bg2 Bg7} {Balogh Csaba: 'A good fighting game. Judit Polgar, true to her style, bravely sacrificed a piece. However, by playing excellent chess, Kramnik showed that there was not sufficient compensation for the material.'} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 c5 3. g3 Nc6 {Balogh Csaba: 'At this moment, this is not a great move. White gets a good version in any of the possible systems.'} (3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nc7) ({Or} 3... e6 4. Nf3 b6 5. Bg2 Bb7 {are the two main lines, and both are completely playable for Black.}) 4. Bg2 d6 5. e3 (5. a3 Bd7 6. b4 cxb4 7. axb4 Nxb4 8. Bxb7 Rb8 9. Bg2 a5 10. Nf3 g6 11. d3 Bg7 12. Bd2 O-O 13. O-O Bc6 14. Qc1 Re8 15. Bh6 Bh8 16. Ra3 Nd7 17. Qd2 Nc5 18. Be3 Qc7 19. Rfa1 Ra8 {Bareev,E (2709)-Polgar,J (2676) Cannes 2001 1-0 (48)}) 5... e5 {Balogh Csaba: 'Quite an ugly move, weakening the d5 square. It's especially bad now that White has already focused his minor pieces around this point. The g1 knight is also heading to e2, where it will support his colleague after it jumps to d5. However, it's already difficult to suggest something clever...'} (5... Bg4 6. Nge2 Qc8 7. h3 Bd7 8. b3 g6 9. Bb2 Bg7 10. Ne4 Ne5 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. d4 Nc6 13. Qd2 cxd4 14. exd4 O-O 15. Rd1 a5 16. Nf4 Bg7 17. O-O Bh6 18. d5 Ne5 19. Nxg6 Nxg6 20. Qxh6 {Kortschnoj,V (2691)-Franklin,M (2373) Hastings 1971 1-0}) (5... Bg4 6. Nge2 Qc8 7. h3 Bd7 8. d3 a6 9. a4 Rb8 10. g4 g5 11. Ng3 Rg8 12. b3 Ne5 13. a5 Bc6 14. e4 h6 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 Bd7 17. Be4 Bg7 18. Ra3 Rf8 19. f4 gxf4 20. Nf5 {Paschall,W (2352)-Rajlich,I (2411) Budapest 2007 0-1 (51)}) ({Balogh Csaba: 'For instance, after'} 5... g6 6. Nge2 (6. d4 {White gets a good version of the g3 system of the King's Indian.}) 6... Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8. d4 cxd4 9. exd4 Bg4 10. Be3 Rc8 11. h3 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 Nd7 13. Qd2 a6 14. Rac1 Qa5 15. b3 f5 16. Rfe1 Nf6 17. d5 Ne5 18. Bd4 Nh5 19. f4 Bh6 20. Qf2 Nd3 21. Qe3 Ne5 22. Kh2 Qc7 23. Qe2 Nf7 24. Bf3 Bg7 25. Bxg7 Nxg7 26. Qxe7 Qb6 27. Qe3 Qa5 28. Qd2 b5 29. Be2 Qb6 30. Bf1 bxc4 31. Bxc4 h5 32. Na4 Qa7 33. Qe3 Qxe3 34. Rxe3 h4 35. gxh4 {Flores,D (2558)-Galego,L (2529) Morelia 2008 1-0}) 6. Nge2 Be6 {Balogh Csaba: 'Diagram'} (6... Be7 7. O-O $11) 7. Nd5 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'This is a known idea! Black was ready to free his position with d5, so White had to parry this threat. It's also important to mention that White only installs his knight on d5 when Black can't take it with the knight, because of the fork after cxd5.'} ({The careless} 7. d3 {allows Black to get a good position after} d5 $1 8. cxd5 Nxd5) 7... Be7 (7... g6 8. O-O Bg7 9. h3 Qd7 10. Kh2 O-O 11. Nec3 Nh5 12. d3 f5 13. Rb1 Rf7 14. a3 Raf8 15. f4 exf4 16. gxf4 Bh6 17. b4 Ng7 18. Bb2 g5 19. b5 Ne5 20. fxe5 dxe5 21. Qe2 g4 22. hxg4 {Gonzalez Rodriguez,J (2375)-Shirazi,K (2455) Saint John 1988 ½-½ (136)}) 8. O-O O-O 9. Nec3 $146 {Balogh Csaba: 'White has successfully taken the d5 hole under control. His next goal is to initiate play on the queenside with d3, a3, Rb1 and b4. Judit didn't want to wait for this to happen and decided to complicate matters.'} (9. d4 Bf5 10. dxc5 dxc5 11. Nec3 Re8 12. b3 Qd7 13. Qe2 Rad8 14. Rd1 Qc8 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Nd5 Bg4 17. f3 Bh3 18. Bb2 Bxg2 19. Kxg2 Ne7 20. Nxe7+ Rxe7 21. e4 Red7 22. Rd5 a6 23. Rad1 Qc6 24. Bc3 b6 25. h4 g6 26. Qb2 Qe6 27. f4 Qg4 28. Rxd7 Rxd7 29. Rxd7 Qxd7 30. Bxe5 Bxe5 31. Qxe5 Qd2+ 32. Kh3 h5 33. Qd5 Qxa2 34. Qd3 Qf2 35. e5 Qg1 36. Qe4 Qd1 37. Kh2 Qxb3 38. e6 Qb2+ {Huettemann,K (2220)-Kohnert,A (2220) Ruhrgebiet 1999 ½-½}) 9... Nb4 {Balogh Csaba: 'Of course, the d5 knight was very annoying, so it's understandable that Black wants to kick it out.'} 10. Nxe7+ {Balogh Csaba: 'Taking the advantage of the bishop pair. Classical Russian school.'} Qxe7 11. d3 {Balogh Csaba: 'Diagram'} d5 $2 {Balogh Csaba: 'A brave decision! Black prefers to sacrifice a piece than to suffer in a worse position. However, there is nothing to justify that this offer could be correct...'} (11... Bg4 $142 12. f3 Bd7 $14) (11... Rad8 {with the idea of freeing the position with d5. This would be met with} 12. a3 Nc6 13. Nd5 $1 {The same motif as before, but now Black is forced to give up his bishop.} Bxd5 14. cxd5 Nb8 15. b4 {White is better. An unpleasant plan could be Bb2, followed by e4, f4 and Bh3. The White center supported by the strong bishop is overwhelming.}) 12. a3 $18 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Kramnik accepts the challenge!'} dxc4 13. axb4 cxb4 {Balogh Csaba: 'Black only has two pawns for the piece, but Rfd8 is coming next, which forces White to push d4, after which the Black pawn phalanx on the queenside becomes really scary...'} 14. Ne4 {Balogh Csaba: '!'} ({The sacrifice would have been justified after a careless move, for instance} 14. Ne2 cxd3 15. Qxd3 Rfd8 {The queen does not find its place.} 16. Qb5 {and the game could finish with an unusual repetition after} (16. Qb1 {allows} Bc4) 16... Rac8 $1 {with Bc4 threats, and on} 17. Qxe5 {Diagram#} Rc5 $1 18. Qf4 Rf5 {!} 19. Qh4 Rh5 $1 20. Qf4 Rf5 {and draw.}) 14... Nxe4 (14... Rfd8 $2 {did not work immediately} 15. Nxf6+ Qxf6 16. Bxb7) 15. Bxe4 (15. dxe4 $6 a5 $11) 15... f5 16. Bg2 Rfd8 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Forcing White to push d4.'} 17. d4 a5 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Judit had foreseen this position before playing d5! Black is intending to play a4, followed by a possible breakthrough with a3 bxa3 b3 and c3 next, or c3 bxc3 b3 and a3 next. White must take it very seriously.'} (17... e4 $2 {would also have been principled, in order to close the strong bishop, but White can open the diagonal with} 18. f3 $1 {which means that the e4 move is premature.}) (17... exd4 $2 {is another big mistake, allowing the c1 bishop to escape.} 18. exd4) 18. Qe2 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Very well played! White threatens to push d5 and collect on c4. Black must hold this pawn, otherwise the position collapses. However, Rac8 doesn't work because of the a5 pawn.'} (18. Qa4 exd4 19. exd4 Bd5 20. Bxd5+ Rxd5 $18) 18... e4 {Balogh Csaba: 'Diagram'} (18... Qc7 {Balogh Csaba: 'meets by'} 19. Rd1 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'with the idea of d5 Bf7 e4, bringing the c1 bishop to the game.'} e4 20. Bd2 $16 (20. f3)) 19. f3 $18 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Of course, the bishop must be activated.'} Bd5 {Balogh Csaba: 'Black must try to hold the e4 pawn in order to lock the c1 bishop.'} (19... exf3 20. Qxf3 {and a possible e4 break would destroy Black's resistance.}) 20. fxe4 fxe4 21. Rf5 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'Another great move with a double threat of Re5, and winning the e4 pawn and Rxd5, eliminating the c4 pawn.'} ({On a "normal" move like} 21. Bd2 {Black plays} a4 {with the idea of c3 bxc3 b3 and a3 with very unclear play.}) 21... Qd7 (21... b5 {was an alternative, but White gets the advantage after} 22. Re5 Qb7 23. Qh5 $1 a4 24. Bd2 $1 {and Black is unable to mobilize the queenside pawns. The dark-sqaured bishop blocks their way.} c3 (24... b3 $2 25. Bb4 {would be a huge success for the bishop.}) 25. bxc3 b3 26. Bc1 $1 {This is the point, and the bishop becomes a perfect blockader. The active White pieces on the 5th rank deprive Black from creating real threats.}) 22. Re5 {Balogh Csaba: 'Going for the e4 pawn.'} Re8 {Balogh Csaba: 'Diagram'} 23. Bh3 $1 {Balogh Csaba: 'A great resource to activate the bishop with tempo, since Black can't give up his d5 bishop as it is the piece that holds the whole position together.'} Qc6 (23... Qxh3 24. Rh5 {Decoy Double attack} (24. Rxd5 {Deflection})) (23... Z0 $140 24. Bxd7 {Wins material}) 24. Rxe8+ Qxe8 25. Bd2 {Balogh Csaba: 'Developing and preventing the a4 move at the same time.'} Qe7 $4 {Balogh Csaba: '?' Balogh Csaba: 'To refresh a4 plan, but this is a huge blunder that allows White to activate his pieces too fast.'} (25... h6 $142 $18) (25... b5 {would have been the only move, although White's position remains clearly better after} 26. Qg4 {as the break through is not really effective with} c3 27. bxc3 b3 28. Bc1 $1 {creating the usual strong blockade!}) 26. Qh5 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'This was probably missed by Judit. Now she is unable to keep her position together.'} Qd8 (26... Bf7 27. Qb5 {and one of the pawns will fall soon.}) 27. Rf1 (27. Qe5 $142 b6 $18) (27. Bf5 $5 {was also winning.} g6 28. Bxg6 $1 hxg6 29. Qxg6+ Kh8 30. Rf1 $1 {and the rook joins the attack. Black can't defend properly.} Ra6 31. Qh5+ Kg7 32. Rf5 {+-}) 27... g6 {Balogh Csaba: 'It was already hard to recommend something else...'} (27... h6 $18) 28. Qe5 Ra6 {Balogh Csaba: 'Kramnik finds the most beautiful way to finish the game, to kick the d5 bishop out from its great place.'} 29. Bd7 $1 {Balogh Csaba: '!!' Balogh Csaba: 'Really nice line and closing combination.'} Bf7 (29... Qxd7 {Balogh Csaba: '?'} 30. Qb8+ {Mate attack} Kg7 31. Qf8# {was the point of the sacrifice.}) 30. Rxf7 $1 {Balogh Csaba: 'After playing Bd7, Kramnik felt that he had to finish the game in the same style.'} (30. Bb5 $1 {was even stronger} Re6 31. Qf4 {+-}) 30... Kxf7 (30... Kxf7 31. Qf4+ {Double attack}) (30... Z0 $140 31. Qg7# {Mate threat}) 31. Bb5 {Balogh Csaba: 'Black's position collapses. The pawns start to fall one after another and the bishop pair soon takes control over the board.'} Rf6 $2 (31... Rc6 32. Bxc6 bxc6 33. Qxe4 $18) 32. Bxc4+ Kg7 33. Qxe4 {Balogh Csaba: 'The game is practically over. Black can't create any real threats.'} (33. g4 $142 b5 34. Bxb5 $18) 33... Qc7 (33... Qd7 $18) 34. Qd5 {Balogh Csaba: '!'} Rf8 35. e4 {Balogh Csaba: '!' Balogh Csaba: 'And the dark-squared bishop decides the battle. There was no need for the rest of the game.'} b5 (35... Rd8 36. Qg5 Qd6 37. Be3 $18) 36. Qxb5 Qd6 (36... Qe7 37. Bf4 h5 38. Qxa5 $18) 37. Qb7+ Kh8 38. Qa7 {Balogh Csaba: 'Bh6 and Bf4-e5 are the main threats.'} g5 39. Bxg5 Qc6 40. Qe7 1-0

