{D83: Grünfeld: 4 Bf4 Bg7 5 e3 0-0} 1. d4 {Glatman Glatman,Victor Glatman,Victor Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan'} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bf4 {Angus Dunnington: 'Rather than build an imposing pawn centre only to see Black seek to undermine it throughout the opening (and middlegame), White gives his own dark-squared bishop an active role, after which the d4-pawn can be bolstered with e2-e3. As we shall see in this chapter, Bf4 facilitates an early entry into the game of the queen's rook, which is well placed on the c-file. Moreover, by ignoring the 'knights before bishops' general rule White is free to post his king's knight on either e2 or f3, whichever is the most appropriate when the time comes. This flexibility is an important characteristic of this variation and, I believe, an underestimated advantage over the more popular 4 Nf3, 5 Bf4 system.'} Bg7 {Angus Dunnington: 'The actual move order was'} 5. Rc1 (5. e3 O-O {1-0 (53) Lalic,B-Polgar,J Yerevan Olympiad 1996 Attacking with 1 d4 [Angus Dunnington]}) 5... O-O 6. e3 c5 {Angus Dunnington: '.'} (6... dxc4 7. Bxc4 Nbd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 9. Bb3 c6 10. O-O {does nothing to make White pay for the luxury of having his dark-squared bishop in play – as opposed to being walled in on c1 by the e3-pawn.}) ({Nor is} 6... c6 {what Black wants, e.g.} 7. Nf3 Bg4 8. Qb3 $5 ({White can also play in more sober fashion with} 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 {, enjoying extra protection of the e5-square and not having to worry about the b2-pawn since} Qb6 10. Rc2 {holds nicely}) 8... Bxf3 9. gxf3 Qd7 10. h4 e6 11. Be5 {which was comfortably better for White in Lukacs-Kolbus, Budapest 1996.}) 7. dxc5 (7. cxd5 $2 cxd4 8. Qxd4 (8. exd4 Nxd5 {favours Black}) 8... Nxd5 $1 {and} 9. Qxd5 $4 {loses to} Bxc3+ {etc.}) 7... Qa5 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '?''} ({After} 7... dxc4 $6 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bxc4 {Black will have problems regaining the pawn}) ({but the slightly odd} 7... Be6 $1 {is in fact the move that causes White the most inconvenience. White should avoid opening the long diagonal for his opponent by trading on d5 and instead opt for the mobilisation of his kingside. Glatman,Victor: '(Lalic,Bogdan) Ü 65/(456)'} 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. Be2 Ne4 {is typical, and now} 10. Nd4 {leaves the centre bursting with possibilities:} Z0 (10... Nxd4 11. exd4 Nxc3 12. bxc3 dxc4 13. O-O Bd5 14. Bf3 {can only be slightly advantageous for White, who can lodge his rooks on b1 and e1. In Kraidman-Moberg, Gausdal 1996, Black challenged the c5-pawn, perhaps prematurely –} b6 $6 15. c6 $1 Rc8 16. c7 Qd7 17. Re1 Rfe8 18. Bxd5 Qxd5 19. Qa4 {and Black was under pressure.}) (10... Nxc3 11. bxc3 Qa5 12. O-O Rad8 13. Nxe6 fxe6 {is T.Paunovic-Djuric, Liosia 1998. Giving up the light-squared bishop for a knight in this fashion is good only when the added support of the centre helps generate counterplay for Black. This is not the case here because the central configuration is yet to be decided and, in the meantime, White has a target in the shape of the b7-pawn.} 14. Qb3 $1 Qxc5 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Qxb7 {and the queen was safe in enemy territory:} e5 17. Bg5 Rb8 18. Qd7 Bf6 19. Bxf6 Rxf6 20. c4 $1 {(softening Black up on the light squares)} d4 21. Qd5+ Qxd5 22. cxd5 {with an extra pawn.} (22. Z0))) 8. cxd5 Rd8 (8... Ne4 9. Nge2 Rd8 (9... Nxc5 10. a3 Ne4 11. f3 Nxc3 12. Nxc3 e6 13. Bc4 exd5 14. Qxd5 Qb6 15. Qd2 Be6 16. Na4 Qc6 17. Bxe6 Qxe6 18. O-O Na6 19. Rfd1 Qb3 20. Qc2 Qe6 21. b4 Rfc8 22. Qd3 Rxc1 23. Rxc1 Re8 24. b5 {Marzolo,C (2459)-Pinter,E (2394)/Szeged 2007/CBM 118 ext/ 1-0 (40)}) (9... Na6 {White has two promising continuations:} 10. Z0 (10. f3 Nexc5 11. a3 {. In Eslon-Insua Mellado, Seville 1994 Black at least addressed the pawn deficit by muddying the waters with} e5 ({now} 11... Nd7 12. Nd4 Nf6 13. e4 Bd7 14. Be3 Ne8 15. Be2 Nd6 16. O-O {left White a clear pawn up in Eslon-Chiburdanidze, Seville 1994}) 12. Bg3 e4 13. Nd4 exf3 14. gxf3 Re8 {. On first impressions the plan seems to have worked for Black, but the calm} 15. Kf2 Bh6 16. f4 Ne4+ 17. Nxe4 Rxe4 18. Bg2 Re8 19. Rc3 $1 {saw White emerge in control, pushing Black back after} Bd7 20. b4 Qd8 21. Qc2 Rc8 22. Rxc8 Bxc8 23. Rc1 Qe7 24. e4 {etc.}) (10. Qa4 Qxa4 11. Nxa4 Bd7 12. c6 bxc6 13. dxc6 Nb4 14. Nd4 {was equally effective for White in P.Nikolic-Lautier, Melody Amber (rapid) 1999.})) 10. Qa4 Qxa4 11. Nxa4 Rxd5 12. f3 Nf6 13. Nec3 Rd8 14. Bc4 Bd7 15. Ke2 Nc6 16. Rhd1 Rac8 17. a3 Nh5 18. Bg5 h6 19. Bh4 g5 20. Be1 Ne5 21. Bd5 Rb8 22. Bb3 g4 23. f4 {Dreev,A (2683)-Sutovsky,E (2656)/Internet ICC 2002/EXT 2003/ 1-0 (47)}) 9. Bc4 ({Better than} 9. Qd2 $6 Nxd5 10. Bc7 (10. Nxd5 Qxd2+ 11. Kxd2 Rxd5+ {favours Black}) 10... Qxc7 11. Nxd5 Rxd5 12. Qxd5 Be6 13. Qd2 Nc6 {when Black is way ahead in development and certainly not without an initiative in return for the sacrificed exchange. Tolush-Botvinnik, USSR Ch. 1939, went} 14. Rd1 Rd8 15. Qc1 Qa5+ 16. Rd2 Rd5 $1 17. Ne2 Rxc5 18. Nc3 Bxc3 19. bxc3 Rxc3 20. Qb2 Ra3 21. Qb5 Qc3 22. Qb2 Qc5 {and there was no respite for White,} 23. Qb1 (23. Qxb7 Qc1+ 24. Ke2 Bc4+ 25. Kf3 Qxd2 26. Bxc4 Ne5+ 27. Kg3 Rxe3+ $1) 23... Bxa2 24. Rxa2 Qa5+ 25. Rd2 Ra1 26. Bd3 Rxb1+ 27. Bxb1 Ne5 {soon leading to a decisive advantage for Black.}) 9... Qxc5 $146 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: 'N''} (9... Be6 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: 'Ü 46/610''} 10. Nge2 ({Angus Dunnington: '. Another option for White is '} 10. b4 {Angus Dunnington: '!?'} Qxb4 11. Qb3 Qxb3 12. Bxb3 {Angus Dunnington: ', e.g. '} Nxd5 (12... Bxd5 $2 13. Rd1 e6 14. e4) 13. Nxd5 Rxd5 (13... Bxd5 $2 14. Rd1 e6 15. e4) 14. Bxb8 {Angus Dunnington: '!'} Rxb8 15. Bxd5 Bxd5 16. Nf3 Bxa2 17. Ke2 {Angus Dunnington: 'with a pull for White in Milic-Shaitar, Yugoslavia 1947.'} b5 18. cxb6 axb6 19. Nd4 b5 20. Rhd1 Bc4+ 21. Ke1 Ra8 22. Rb1 Ra2 23. Rd2 Bxd4 24. exd4 Ra6 {Milic,B-Sajtar,J/Zagreb 1947/EXT 2004/1/2-1/2}) (10. Z0) ({, invites} 10. e4 {, the point being that Black has} Nxe4 {. Then} 11. Nge2 Z0 (11... Nxc5 12. O-O Bd7 13. Bg5 {leads to a position in which White's pieces are more effectively placed, with Black's less co-ordinated. Glatman,Victor: '' '' Glatman: ''}) ({. However, this is a lesser evil than} 11... Nxf2 $6 {Glatman,Victor: '!?' '!?' Glatman: '!?'} 12. Qa4 $1 Qxa4 13. Nxa4 Nxh1 14. dxe6 {which was the course taken in Pinter-K.Allen, Thessaloniki Olympiad 1988. Black's material lead is only temporary, the game continuing} fxe6 {Glatman,Victor: '?!' '?!' Glatman: '?!'} (14... Nc6 $142 {Glatman,Victor}) 15. Kf1 $1 Rf8 16. Kg1 Nf2 17. Be3 {Glatman,Victor: '+-' '+-' Glatman: '+-'} (17. Kxf2 g5) 17... Be5 (17... Ng4 18. Bxe6+ {,}) ({or} 17... Ne4 18. Bxe6+ Kh8 19. Bd5) 18. Bxf2 Bxh2+ 19. Kxh2 Rxf2 20. Kg3 Rf6 21. Nac3 Kg7 22. Nf4 Nc6 23. Nxe6+ {and White eventually won.})) 10... Nxd5 11. Qb3 Na6 12. O-O Nxc5 13. Qb5 Qxb5 14. Nxb5 Nxf4 15. Nxf4 Bxc4 16. Rxc4 Na6 17. b3 Rd2 18. a3 e5 19. Nh3 Rd3 20. b4 Rad8 21. Ng5 h6 22. Ne4 f5 23. Ned6 Bf8 24. Nc8 {Vaitonis,P-Yanofsky,D/Arvida 1949/EXT 2000/1/2-1/2 (37)}) 10. Bb3 ({Also possible is Pinter's} 10. Qb3 $5 {Glatman,Victor: 'Pinter/'} a6 11. a4 Nbd7 12. e4 (12. d6 $5) 12... Qa5 13. Bd2 Nc5 14. Qc2 {. Both this and the game continuation suggest that Black does not have enough play for the pawn once White establishes his pawns on e4 and d5. Nonetheless ...Qa5 remains a popular general theme in the Grünfeld at all levels. Glatman,Victor: '!' '!' Glatman: '!'}) 10... Nc6 11. Nf3 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '?!''} (11. Nge2 $16 {(Glatman,Victor) Lalic,Bogdan}) 11... Na5 12. O-O Bg4 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '!''} ({Unfortunately for Black} 12... Nxb3 {Glatman,Victor: '?' Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan'} 13. Qxb3 Nxd5 $2 {fails to} 14. Rfd1 (14. Nxd5 {Glatman,Victor} Qxd5 15. Rcd1 $1 Qa5 16. Qb4 Qxb4 17. Rxd8+ Bf8 18. Bh6 e5 19. Rc1 e4 20. Nd4 $18 { a3, Rcc8}) 14... Bxc3 15. Rxc3 Qa5 16. Bc7 $1 {. Consequently Black is left trying to obtain play by exerting pressure on White's centre in order to generate something on the dark squares, relying on the long-range bishop.}) 13. e4 Qb4 14. Bc7 {Glatman,Victor: '!' '!' Glatman: '!'} Bxf3 (14... Nxb3 15. Qxb3 Qxb3 16. axb3 Rd7 17. Bg3 Bxf3 18. gxf3 {gives Black nothing for the pawn, White's new f-pawn serving to support the centre. Glatman,Victor: '' '' Glatman: ''}) 15. Bxa5 ({Not} 15. Qxf3 Nxb3 16. axb3 Rd7 17. Bg3 Qxb3 {. Glatman,Victor: '=' '=' Glatman: '='}) 15... Qxa5 (15... Bxd1 16. Bxb4 Bxb3 17. axb3 Nd7 {.}) (15... Qxc3 $5 {(Glatman,Victor) Lalic,Bogdan} 16. bxc3 Bxd1 17. Rfxd1 b6 18. Bb4 Nxe4 19. Bxe7 Nxc3 20. Bxd8 Ne2+ 21. Kf1 Nxc1 22. Rxc1 Rxd8 23. Rc7 $16) 16. Qxf3 {Glatman,Victor: '' '' Glatman: ''} Nd7 17. Qe3 Rac8 (17... Nc5 {Glatman,Victor} 18. Bc4 (18. e5 Nxb3 19. axb3 Qb4 $132) 18... Qa3) 18. f4 Qc5 19. Qxc5 Nxc5 20. e5 ({After} 20. Bc2 Bxc3 21. bxc3 b6 {White has a bishop and Black a knight. Instead Lalic prefers to keep his knight and frustrate Black's bishop. Glatman,Victor: '' '' Glatman: ''}) 20... Nxb3 (20... f6 $5 {(Glatman,Victor) Lalic,Bogdan} 21. d6+ $140 $2 Nxb3 22. dxe7 Nxc1 23. exd8=Q+ Rxd8 {Polgar,Ju}) 21. axb3 f6 22. Rce1 ({Refusing to be drawn into} 22. e6 f5 23. Rfd1 Rc5 {when Black goes on the offensive. Glatman,Victor: '' '' Glatman: ''}) 22... Rc5 23. g3 fxe5 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '?!''} (23... a5 $1 {(Glatman,Victor) (Lalic,Bogdan)  b5 Polgar,Ju}) 24. fxe5 {Angus Dunnington: 'this time using the rook along the rank to meet '} e6 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '?''} (24... a5 {(Glatman,Victor) Lalic,Bogdan} 25. Rf4 $1 b5 26. b4 axb4 27. Rxb4 $16) 25. b4 {Glatman,Victor: '!' '!' Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '!'' Glatman: '!'} (25. dxe6 {with} Bxe5 {. By now it is clear that the e5-pawn's role in demoting the bishop to spectator status is a key factor in White's winning strategy, hence White's next.}) 25... Rc4 26. dxe6 Re8 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '''} (26... Rxb4 {Glatman,Victor: 'Lalic,Bogdan: '?''} 27. e7 (27. Nd5 $1 $18 {(Glatman,Victor) Lalic,Bogdan} Rxd5 $140 $2 28. e7) 27... Re8 28. Nd5 Rxb2 29. Nf6+ Bxf6 30