1. d4 {Hazai Hazai} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O 5. Be2 d6 6. O-O (6. h3 {is the usual reaction here against 6...h5.}) 6... Nfd7 { e5} (6... Nh5 $5 {deserves attention, although after} 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 {White will not move the , but his f3 !} 9. Nfd2 (9. Ne1 Nf4 10. exf4 gxh4 { c5} 11. c3 {and although Black has a strong fianchettoed , his  structure is seiously weakened.}) 9... gxh4 (9... Nf4 10. exf4 gxh4 11. c3) 10. Bxh5 c5 11. c3 {keeping the stronghold on d4.}) 7. e4 {This is a rather original idea. White loses a tempo, but the  on d7 ruins the harmony in the black camp.} (7. h3 {This usually arises by transposition.} e5 8. Bh2 Nc6 (8... Qe7 $6 {- Moldovan-Nevednichy, Targoviste 2001 CBM 87}) (8... f5 {This agressive move seems to be a bit risky, but it is not easy for White to exploit the lack of development in Black's camp.} 9. c4 (9. dxe5 Nxe5) 9... Nc6 10. Nc3 g5 11. dxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {For the sake of development it is important to exchange the  first.} (11... dxe5 {- 8...c6}) (11... dxe5 $2 12. Nd5 Nc5 13. b4 Ne6 14. b5 e4 15. bxc6 exf3 16. Bxf3 Bxa1 17. Nxc7 $1 Qxd1 18. Rxd1 Nxc7 19. Bxc7 $16 {Bosgue Ortega-Gonzalez Valez, Barbera-A 1997}) (11... dxe5 {- 8...c6}) 12. Nxe5 dxe5 13. Qxd8 Nxd8 14. e4 f4 {The h2  causes again some headache for White.} 15. Bg4 Bxg4 16. hxg4 Ne6 $11 {Payen-Nijboer, Cafe op Koszalin 1998}) 9. c3 (9. Nc3 Re8 10. Bc4 Nb6 11. Bb3 exd4 12. exd4 Na5 13. Re1 Bf5 14. Rxe8+ Qxe8 15. Bf4 h6 16. Be3 c6 17. Nd2 d5 $15 {Z.Nikolic-Damljanovic, Novi Sad 1995}) (9. dxe5 {This is again rather passive and overcautious.} dxe5 (9... Ndxe5 $11) 10. Nc3 f5 11. Bb5 Kh8 12. Qd5 Qf6 (12... Ne7) 13. Bxc6 bxc6 14. Qa5 Rb8 15. Na4 Rb7 16. Rad1 f4 17. exf4 exf4 18. b3 h6 19. Rd4 g5 20. Rfd1 $14 {Arencibia-Paneque, Cuba 1989}) (9. Na3 f5 $5 (9... e4 10. Nd2 f5 11. c4 g5 12. Rb1 Nf6 13. b4 Ne7 14. Nb5 Bd7 15. c5 Bxb5 $1 (15... Ned5 $6 16. Qb3 Kh8 17. cxd6 Bxb5 18. Bxb5 cxd6 19. Bc4 $14 {Rodgaard-R.Gruenberg, Hamburg 1980}) 16. Bxb5 d5 $132)) (9. c4 exd4 $1 10. exd4 Qf6 {and Black has a real pressure on × d4.} 11. Nc3 $5 (11. d5 Nd4 $11) 11... Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Qxd4 13. Nb5 Qxd1 14. Raxd1 c6 15. Nc7 Rb8 16. Bxd6 Be5 $11 {Lopushnoy-Kopylov, RUS-ch Novosibirsk 1996}) 9... f5 (9... b6 $5 10. Nbd2 Bb7 11. Rc1 h6 12. Re1 f5 13. Bc4+ Kh8 14. Nb3 Qf6 15. Qc2 g5 $17 {Eriksson-Bracjunova, Herculane 1994}) 10. a4 (10. Na3 e4 11. Nd2 Nf6 12. f3 (12. b4 $5) 12... exf3 $5 (12... d5 $143 $6 13. c4) (12... Ne7 13. fxe4 fxe4 14. Qb3+ Kh8 15. c4 {(Alterman-Avrukh, Tel Aviv 1999 CBM 71)} h5 $1 {Avrukh} 16. Rf2 Nf5 17. g4 (17. Raf1 h4) 17... hxg4 18. hxg4 Nh4 $36) 13. Bxf3 Be6 $11 {is the simple solution.} (13... Re8 $6 14. Qb3+ Kh8 15. Rae1 $16) (13... g5 14. e4 $140 f4)) (10. dxe5 {Here} dxe5 {looks satisfactory:} (10... Ndxe5 11. Nbd2 Be6 12. Nd4 Bf7 $142 (12... Bd5 $2 13. N2f3 Bf7 14. Bf4 h6 15. Bg3 a6 16. Qc2 Qf6 17. h4 Ne7 18. Rad1 Rab8 19. c4 c5 $13 {Van Heste-De Kok OHRA-B Amsterdam 1985})) 11. Bb5 Qf6 12. Na3 Kh8 13. e4 (13. Nc4 e4 $13) 13... f4 (13... Nc5 14. exf5)) 10... g5 11. Na3 $15 (11. a5 $2 a6 $1 $17 {A.Hoffman-Strikovic, Sevilla 1994 60/64})) 7... c5 ({On} 7... e5 8. Bg5 {is a successful provocative move.} f6 (8... Qe8 9. Nc3 $14) (8... Bf6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Nc3 $14) 9. Be3 $14) 8. c3 {Black now has problems by attacking White's strong  .} Nc6 (8... Qb6 9. Na3 {and now grabbing for pawns is unhealthy after} Qxb2 $2 10. Nc4 $16) (8... cxd4 9. cxd4 Nc6 {allows the strong developing move} 10. Nc3) 9. d5 Nce5 10. Nbd2 b6 (10... f5 11. exf5 Rxf5 12. Bg3 $14 {leaves Black with a bad  structure, see the backward  on e7.}) 11. a4 a6 12. Bg3 (12. Nxe5 $1 {was the clear-cut strategical solution.} Nxe5 13. Bg3 { f4} g5 (13... f5 14. exf5 Bxf5 15. Nc4 $16) 14. Nc4 $14) 12... Rb8 (12... Nxf3+ $1 13. Nxf3 Ne5 14. Nxe5 dxe5 {was the lesser evil, if Black can hold × d6 and e5.}) 13. Nxe5 Nxe5 14. f4 Nd7 15. Nc4 $16 {Now White has got an ideal Benoni type of position with a strong  and chances for a breakthrough by e4-e5. It is not easy to find a counterplay by Black, because b6-b5 is always answered by a5.} Qc7 16. Re1 ({Immediately} 16. Bg4 $1 {looks more precise.} Nf6 (16... h5 17. Bf3 $16) (16... b5 17. axb5 axb5 18. Na5 $16) 17. Bf3 b5 18. axb5 axb5 19. Na5 Ra8 20. Nc6 Rxa1 21. Qxa1 $16) 16... Bb7 (16... Nf6 $6 17. Bf3 $16 {and soon e4-e5 comes.}) 17. Bg4 $1 (17. Bf3 {allows the freeing move} b5 18. axb5 axb5 19. Na5 Ba8) 17... b5 18. Bxd7 (18. axb5 axb5 19. Na5 {is again answered by} Ba8) 18... Qxd7 19. Nb6 Qc7 20. a5 Rbd8 { e6} (20... e6 $2 {immediately is too early on account of} 21. dxe6 fxe6 22. f5 $16) 21. f5 $1 c4 22. Qd2 Bc8 23. Rf1 {Black is completely pushed to the wall, White's space advantage is enormous.} Kh8 24. Kh1 Bf6 25. Qh6 (25. Bf4 gxf5) (25. Bf2 $5 { d4 deserved attention} gxf5 26. Nxc8 Rxc8 27. Bd4 Bxd4 28. Qxd4+ f6 29. exf5 {with a strong pressure on the e , but Black has exhanged the light pieces.}) 25... Rg8 26. Bh4 Bg7 27. Qe3 gxf5 28. exf5 Be5 29. Bg5 f6 30. Bf4 {White has time to improve his position slowly but surely, Black has no real counterplay.} Bb7 31. Rf3 Rg4 32. h3 Rg7 33. Bh6 Rgg8 34. Qe4 Rb8 35. Rd1 Qd8 36. g4 Qe8 37. Rg1 Qf7 38. h4 e6 $1 {This is the only chance for fishing in troubled waters!} 39. fxe6 Qxe6 40. Rf5 Rbe8 41. Qf3 Qe7 42. g5 Qf7 43. h5 b4 44. gxf6 $18 Rxg1+ 45. Kxg1 Rg8+ 46. Bg7+ Rxg7+ 47. fxg7+ Qxg7+ 48. Kf1 Kg8 49. Nxc4 Bc8 50. Nxe5 dxe5 51. h6 Qxh6 52. Qg3+ 1-0

