1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. b3 Bg7 4. Bb2 O-O 5. Bg2 d5 6. c4 c6 7. O-O a5 8. d3 Na6 9. Nbd2 Be6 (9... a4 10. Bc3 axb3 (10... Ne8 $6 11. Bxg7 Nxg7 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. bxa4 Nc5 {1-0 (57) Villamayor,B (2469)-Nolte,R (2522) Manila 2008} 14. d4 Nxa4 15. Qb3 $36) 11. axb3 b6 12. b4 $1 Bb7 13. Qc2 c5 14. bxc5 bxc5 15. Rfb1 Qc7 16. Qb2 $16 (16. cxd5 $5) 16... Bc6 17. Be5 Qc8 18. Ra5 d4 19. Nb3 $18 {1-0 (80) Timman,J (2575)-Giri,A (2701) Leiden 2011}) 10. a3 Qc8 {We've seen this plan of exchanging the light-squared bishops with ...h3 before. It's not really an issue but we should remember to play c1 fairly soon as lining up the rook against the queen limits Black's options.} (10... Rc8 11. Rc1 b5 {1-0 (45) Vogler,T (2254)-Vogel,C (2152) Germany 2013} 12. cxb5 cxb5 13. Rxc8 Qxc8 14. Qa1 $14) 11. Re1 (11. Rc1 Rd8 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Nc4 Qc7 15. Nd4 $14 {White has a quite beautiful position.}) 11... Bh3 12. Bh1 (12. cxd5 Bxg2 13. Kxg2 cxd5 14. Rc1 Qd7 $11) (12. Rc1 $1 {was the best, threatening cxd5 (exchanging the central pawn for a wing pawn).} Bxg2 13. Kxg2 e6 14. Qc2 $14 {It's worth pointing out that now e4 can be more effective (as it doesn't block in a bishop on g2).}) 12... h6 (12... Rd8 $5 {followed by ...f5-h5 is interesting and may offer a fair share of the play.}) 13. Qc2 Qd8 $6 14. Rac1 Nc7 15. cxd5 Ncxd5 16. Nc4 $16 {Black's last moves were not so precise and now White is just better.} Nb6 17. Nfe5 Nfd7 18. Nxb6 Nxb6 19. Qb1 Qd6 20. Nc4 Nxc4 21. bxc4 e5 22. Bc3 Qxa3 23. Qxb7 Rfc8 24. Qb2 Qc5 25. Bg2 Bxg2 26. Kxg2 a4 27. Bb4 Qa7 28. Qa3 c5 29. Bd2 Qb7+ 30. e4 Bf8 31. Rb1 Qd7 32. Be3 h5 33. h3 f5 34. Rb6 Rc6 35. Reb1 h4 1-0

