1. d4 {Wells,P Milov,Vadim Milov,Vadim Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim''} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 c5 9. Bd3 {This is Vadim Milov's latest try in an opening system which he has really made his own. Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'This is Vadim Milov's latest try in an opening system which he has really made his own.''} ({My guess is that Milov has tired of the positions arising after} 9. Bb2 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K'} Qa5 $1 (9... Qc7) (9... Nc6) {when white must choose between attempting to keep open the option of capture towards the centre with the rather clumsy and time-consuming} 10. Qd2 ({or acquiescing in the inconvenient exd4 with} 10. Nf3 cxd4 11. exd4 b6 12. O-O (12. a4 Ba6 13. Nd2 Rc8 14. Bxa6 Qxa6 15. c4 (15. Qe2 $5 {Lautier.}) 15... Nbd7 16. O-O Qa5 17. Re1 Rc7 {with comfortable play for black in Milov,V-Lautier,J/Biel 1996/Inf 67 1-0 (34)}) 12... Ba6 $1 13. Bd3 Rc8 14. Rc1 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 b5 {[%csl Gc4]} 16. Nd2 Nbd7 (16... Qa4 $5) 17. a4 b4 18. c4 Qxa4 19. Ra1 Qc6 $15 {½-½ Milov,V-Granda Zuniga,J/Buenos Aires 1996/CBM 53 (60) white clearly has some compensation for the pawn, but I am a bit sceptical that it sould be enough.}) 10... Nbd7 11. Rc1 b5 12. Bd3 Bb7 13. Nf3 Rac8 14. c4 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 bxc4 16. Rxc4 Ne4+ 17. Bxe4 Bxe4 18. Rhc1 Bd5 19. Ra4 $2 Nb6 $17 {0-1 Milov,V-Tiviakov,S/Buenos Aires Najdorf 1996/CBM 53 (43)}) 9... Qc7 {This move seems to me very sensible in that it restricts white's options. Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'This move seems to me very sensible in that it restricts white's options.''} ({There is of course nothing wrong with} 9... Nc6 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K' either. After} 10. Nf3 Qc7 {however, white can still choose between} 11. O-O ({but not} 11. e4 $2 cxd4 12. cxd4 Nxd4) ({and} 11. Bb2 e5 {which is covered in the note to 10...Nc6 below.}) 11... e5 12. Qc2 {which is the old main line,}) 10. Bb2 (10. Nf3 $2 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K' is of course ruled out due to} cxd4 11. cxd4 $140 $2 Qc3+ $19) (10. Ne2 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K'}) 10... b6 $6 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: '?!'' Nothing at all wrong in principle with playing on the light-squares, but after white's logical reply, black feels obliged to close the centre after which the move ...b6 plays just about no role at all. Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'Nothing at all wrong in principle with playing on the light-squares, but after white's logical reply, black feels obliged to close the centre after which the move ...b6 plays just about no role at all.''} (10... Nc6 $5 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K'} {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'and''} 11. Nf3 (11. e4 $6 {might appear a tempting alternative, but} Qa5 ({stronger than} 11... Qb6 {when} 12. Rb1 $1 {covers everything due to the tactic} cxd4 $6 13. cxd4 Nxd4 $2 14. e5 $18) 12. Qd2 Rd8 {looks highly inconvenient. If white is forced to play} 13. e5 Nd5 {then the structure is not favourable at the best of times, but without queens it is dictinctly bad.}) 11... e5 {leads to a highly interesting position which can really only arise by the 6 a3 move order, since it is the fact that white has not castled which uniquely gives him time to place his bishops on b2 and d3. However, whilst it is clearly true that d3 is a more active square than e2 ( comparing the line 4 e3 0-0 5 Bd3 d5 6 Nf3 c5 7 0-0 Nc6 8 a3 Bxc3 9 bxc3 dxc4 10 Bxc4 Qc7 11 Bb2 e5 12 Be2 ) it is also a more vulnerable square. It is this that leads me to feel that I would play black here. Practice seems to support this hunch, although it is to me strange that black has not headed for this more often. Milov,Vadim: 'leads to a highly interesting position which can really only arise by the 6 a3 move order, since it is the fact that white has not castled which uniquely gives him time to place his bishops on b2 and d3. However, whilst it is clearly true that d3 is a more active square than e2 ( comparing the line 4 e3 0-0 5 Bd3 d5 6 Nf3 c5 7 0-0 Nc6 8 a3 Bxc3 9 bxc3 dxc4 10 Bxc4 Qc7 11 Bb2 e5 12 Be2 ) it is also a more vulnerable square. It is this that leads me to feel that I would play black here. Practice seems to support this hunch, although it is to me strange that black has not headed for this more often. Wells,Peter K: 'which is covered in the note to 10...Nc6 below.''} 12. dxe5 (12. Qc2 $5 cxd4 13. cxd4 e4 $5 14. Bxe4 Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Qa5+ 16. Nd2 Bf5 17. Qf4 Qb5 18. Bc3 Qd3 19. Rc1 Rac8 20. Qf3 Rfe8 21. Nb3 b6 (21... Nxd4 22. Bxd4 (22. Nxd4 Be4) 22... Qxb3 23. O-O Rxc1 24. Rxc1 $13) 22. Qe2 Nb4 (22... Ne5 $5 23. dxe5 Rxc3 24. Qxd3 Rxd3 25. Nd4 Bg6 $132) 23. Bxb4 Qxb3 24. O-O Bd3 25. Rxc8 Rxc8 26. Qd1 Rc2 27. Re1 $14 {½-½ Addison,W-Reshevsky,S/Palma de Mallorca 1970/IZT (32)}) 12... Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Qxe5 14. Qc2 {[%cal Yc3c4]} c4 $1 {An excellent thematic light-square pawn sacrifice the logical consequences of which can be enjoyed some moves later.} 15. Bxc4 Bf5 16. Qe2 Rac8 17. O-O Ng4 18. g3 Qc5 19. Bd3 (19. Ba2 Ne5 $44 {[%cal Gf5d3,Gf5g4]}) 19... Bxd3 20. Qxd3 Qh5 21. h4 Ne5 22. Qd1 Qf5 {with easily sufficient play to hold the balance in ½-½ Milov,V-Lautier,J/Biel 1997/CBM 60 (31)}) 11. e4 $1 {Milov,Vadim: 'N' 'N''Wells,Peter K: '!' Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim: 'N'''} (11. Qe2 {(Milov,Vadim) Ü 29/(546) Milov,Vadim: 'Ü 29/(546) Wells,Peter K: '(Milov,Vadim) Ü 29/(546)''}) (11. Nf3 $5 {Milov,Vadim Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim''}) 11... e5 (11... Ba6 {Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K'} 12. e5 $5 (12. Nf3 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Qb7 $132) 12... Bxd3 $140 $2 13. exf6 $40) 12. d5 (12. dxe5 $2 {Milov,Vadim Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim''} Qxe5 (12... Ng4 13. f4 c4 14. Bb1 $1 $16) 13. Nf3 Qe7 14. O-O Nc6 $15) 12... c4 13. Bc2 Nbd7 14. Ne2 {Milov,Vadim: '?!' '?!''Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim: '?!'''} (14. Nf3 {Milov,Vadim Milov,Vadim: 'Wells,Peter K: 'Milov,Vadim''} Nc5 15. Nd2 (15. Qe2 Ba6 16. O-O Nd3 17. Bc1 Ne8 18. a4 Nd6 19. Ba3 f5 $13) 15... Ba6 16. O-O Nd3 $140 17. Bxd3 cxd3 18. Qa4 $14) 14... Nc5 15. Ng3 b