1. d4 {Tyomkin,D Tyomkin,D The first win for Morozevich in Corus 2005 came only in round 8- Moro managed to come back in the tournament after an horrible start for him( Alex had scored 2 draws only in first seven rounds of Wijk aan Zee 2005).} Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. g3 c5 4. Bg2 cxd4 5. Nxd4 d5 6. O-O e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. Bg5 Nbd7 9. Nc3 h6 {Very risky move- black isn't developed and wastes time.. 9...Be7 is much safer and probably gives black an equality.} (9... Be7 10. e3 (10. e4 dxe4 (10... d4 11. Nd5 h6 12. Bd2 (12. Bxf6 $142) 12... O-O 13. c3 Bg4 14. Qe1 (14. Nxf6+ Nxf6 15. f3 Be6 16. cxd4 exd4 17. e5 Nd5 18. f4 Ne3 $15) 14... Nxd5 15. exd5 d3 $15 {0-1 Acosta,J-Damaso,R/Dos Hermanas 2004/EXT 2005 (39)}) 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. Nxe4 Qc7 $15 {½-½ Goric,E-Brkic,A/Vinkovci 2004/CBM 099 ext (26)}) 10... h6 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. Qe2 (12. f4 $5) 12... Qb6 13. e4 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Rc8 15. Rfd1 O-O 16. Nxf6+ Bxf6 17. Bd5 e4 (17... a5 $5 18. Bxe6 fxe6 19. a4 e4 $15 {[%csl Rf2]}) 18. Bxe4 (18. c3 Bxd5 19. Rxd5 Rfe8 20. Re1 $11) 18... Rfe8 19. Qf3 Rc4 (19... a5 20. c3 $1 (20. a4 Qb4 $1 $15) 20... a4 21. Nd4 Qxb2 22. Nxe6) 20. c3 Bg4 21. Qxg4 Rcxe4 22. Qf3 Re2 {½-½ Yandemirov,V-Lugovoi,A/St Petersburg 2003/EXT 2004}) 10. Bxf6 Nxf6 11. Qd3 {11.f4 is premature.} (11. f4 d4 12. Ne4 Ng4 13. Qd3 Ne3 (13... Qb6 $5) 14. Qb5+ Qd7 15. Qxe5 f6 16. Qxd4 Nxg2 17. Kxg2 Bh3+ 18. Kf3 Qxd4 19. Nxd4 f5 {½-½ Itkis,B-Prokopchuk,E/Alushta 1997/EXT 99}) 11... Qd7 {11...Rc8 doesn't solve black problems completely, however it's worth a try.} (11... Rc8 12. Rad1 d4 13. Qb5+ Nd7 14. Nd5 Rxc2 15. Rc1 Rxc1 16. Rxc1 Bd6 17. Qxb7 (17. Na5 $5 a6 18. Qxb7 $14) 17... O-O 18. Qxa7 Qb8 (18... Nf6 $1) 19. Qa6 Rd8 20. Rc6 Bf8 21. Rxe6 {1-0 Simic,R-Horvath,J/Igalo 1994/EXT 2002 (45)}) 12. Rad1 d4 {Black can sacrifice an a7 pawn by Rd8, but black completes the development and gets a counterplay in that case.} (12... Rd8 $5 13. Qe3 Qc7 14. Qxa7 Be7 15. f4 $1 O-O 16. fxe5 Qxe5 17. Qd4 Qg5 {and this position isn't very clear.}) 13. Ne4 Nxe4 $146 {This move is definitely better than 13...Rc8 previously played in this position, however black suffers in this case as well.} (13... Rc8 14. c3 (14. Nxf6+ gxf6 15. Qf3 Be7 16. c3 Bg4 17. Qe4 Bf5 {0-1 Panelo Munoz,M-Hernando Rodrigo,J/Castellar del Valles 2004/CBM 101 ext (27)}) 14... dxc3 15. Qf3 $16 {1-0 Anikaev,Y-Totsky,L/Smolensk 1991/EXT 2000 (21)}) 14. Qxe4 Bd6 15. f4 (15. c3 f5 16. Qd3 (16. Qxb7 Qxb7 17. Bxb7 Rb8 18. Bc6+ Ke7 19. cxd4 Bxb3 20. axb3 Rxb3 21. dxe5 Bxe5 {with some chances for a draw.}) 16... Rd8 17. cxd4 e4 18. Qe3 O-O {and black has a compensation here.}) 15... Bxb3 $2 {This move leads to serious troubles. 15...f5 looks very risky but that's the best black can do here.} (15... f5 $142 16. Qd3 (16. Qxb7 Qxb7 17. Bxb7 Rd8 18. fxe5 Bxe5 19. Nc5 Ke7 {and black has a strong counterplay.}) 16... Rc8 $1 (16... O-O 17. fxe5 Bxe5 18. Nxd4 $16) 17. fxe5 Bxe5 18. e3 O-O 19. Qd2 $14) 16. axb3 Qe7 17. Qxb7 $2 {17.fxe5 would cause black much more troubles!} (17. fxe5 $1 Bxe5 (17... Qxe5 18. Rxd4 O-O 19. Qxe5 Bxe5 20. Rd7 $16 {[%csl Rb7,Rf7]}) 18. e3 dxe3 19. Qa4+ Kf8 20. Rd7 e2 21. Rxe7 exf1=Q+ 22. Kxf1 Kxe7 23. Qe4 $18) 17... Qxb7 18. Bxb7 Rd8 19. Bc6+ Ke7 20. fxe5 Bxe5 21. Ra1 Bb8 {Judit could try 21...d3 here however white had a way for an advantage in this line.} (21... d3 $5 22. cxd3 (22. exd3 Rc8 23. Ra6 Bd4+ 24. Kh1 Bb6 25. Bd5 Rxc2 26. Rxf7+ Kd8 $16 {but this was an excellent chance for black to fight for a draw.}) 22... Bd4+ 23. Kg2 Rd6 24. Bb5 $16) 22. Ra4 (22. Rf5 g6 23. Rb5 Rd6 24. Rb7+ Ke6 {and black survives.}) 22... h5 23. Rb4 {Moro blew off almost all his advantage with his next moves.} (23. c3 $5 Rh6 (23... dxc3 24. bxc3 $16 {[%cal Ga4e4]}) 24. Be4 Rhd6 25. Bd3 $16) 23... Rc8 24. Be4 Bd6 25. Rc4 Rxc4 26. bxc4 g6 {Draw is very likely here but Judit messed it up in a time trouble.} (26... Rb8 $142 27. b3 g6 28. Ra1 Bc5 29. Kg2 {but white has almost nothing left from his advantage.}) 27. Bd5 Rf8 {Hard to explain this move of Judit....I think black could safely advance the f-pawn.} (27... f5 28. Ra1 Bc5 29. Kf1 h4 $132) 28. Ra1 Bc5 29. Kf1 f5 30. Ra5 Bb6 31. Ra6 f4 32. Ke1 fxg3 33. hxg3 Rf6 34. Kd2 $6 {I am not sure why Moro rejected b2-b4 move- seems like white gets good winning chances that way. While after the game move black stops b2-b4.} (34. b4 g5 (34... Bc5 35. Ra4 $16) 35. Kd1 $1 $16 (35. c5 $2 Bxc5 36. Rxf6 Bxb4+)) 34... Bc5 35. Ra4 a5 $1 36. Kd3 Bb6 {Judit missed a big chance here! Paradoxial 36...Ra6 freezes the queenside and probably gives black a draw.} (36... Ra6 $1 37. Ra1 $140 a4 $1) 37. c5 $1 {Now Moro breaks in.} Bxc5 38. Rxa5 Bb6 39. Rb5 Bc7 $6 {One more mistake in a time trouble. 39...Rd6 is much more resistant.} (39... Rd6 40. b4 Kd8 41. Be4 {but white must work hard to win this ending.}) 40. Rb7 Kd8 41. Kxd4 Bxg3 42. c4 h4 43. e4 (43. Rh7 Rb6 $1) 43... Rf1 $2 {This was necessary to cut off white rook from the kingside!} (43... Bc7 $1 {and white's win is still a big question here!}) 44. Rh7 Rb1 45. e5 $1 $18 {Black king is vulnerable now as well as white pieces cooperate perfectly.} Rd1+ (45... Rxb2 46. e6 Re2 47. Be4 Re1 48. Rd7+ Kc8 49. Rg7 Kd8 50. Kd5 Rd1+ 51. Kc5 Rd6 52. e7+ Kd7 53. Bxg6 $18) 46. Kc5 Bxe5 47. b4 Bg3 {47...g5 was a last chance for Polgar in this game, but black loses anyhow.} (47... g5 48. Kc6 $1 $18 {[%cal Gc4c5,Gb4b5]}) 48. b5 Rb1 49. Kc6 Bf2 50. Rd7+ (50. c5 $2 Rc1) 50... Ke8 (50... Kc8 51. Rf7 {[%cal Gf7f8,Gf7f2]}) 51. Bf7+ Kf8 52. Bxg6 {The rest is simple for white- two connected passers of white decide the game easily.} Rb2 53. Rf7+ Kg8 54. Rxf2 $1 {Moro chooses the most beautiful way to win the game!} Rxf2 55. b6 $1 Kg7 (55... Rf6+ 56. Kc5 $18) 56. b7 Rb2 57. Be4 Kf6 58. c5 Rb4 59. Bh1 1-0

