1. Nc3 {Mikhalevski,V Mikhalevski,V . . . We can expect this move from nobody else but Morozevich.} c5 (1... d5 {would be a mistake as it allowed Alex to get his favourite Chigorin with an extra tempo.} 2. d4 $1) 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e4 d6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 a6 {Eventually the game transposes to the very popular variation of Naidorf.} 6. Be3 Ng4 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Bg7 {A very popular setup. Most of the top players including Kasparov played it both with the white and the black pieces. Also this tournament hasn't become an exception.} 10. Be2 {At the beginning of this setup White made this move almost automatically.} ({Svidler and Kasimdzhanov preferred the more fashionable} 10. h3 {in San Luis.} Ne5 11. Nf5 (11. f3 Nbc6 12. Bf2 Ng6 13. Qd2 Qa5 14. O-O-O Bd7 15. Kb1 Rc8 16. g3 Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 Qe5 19. Qf2 Rxc3 $5 20. bxc3 Qxc3 21. Rd3 Qa5 22. Qe3 Be6 23. Ra3 Qb4+ 24. Ka1 Kd7 25. Be2 Qc5 26. Rb1 $1 $16 {[%csl Ra6,Rb7,Rd7] and Black started to experience problems with the queenside. 1-0 Svidler,P-Polgar,J/San Luis ARG 2005 (59)}) 11... Bxf5 12. exf5 Nbc6 13. Nd5 e6 14. fxe6 $1 {An essential exchange.} (14. Ne3 Qa5+ 15. c3 Nf3+ $3 16. Qxf3 Bxc3+ 17. Kd1 Qa4+ 18. Nc2 Bxb2 19. fxe6 fxe6 20. Qb3 Qxb3 21. axb3 Bxa1 22. Nxa1 Ke7 $44 {0-1 Svidler,P-Topalov,V/San Luis ARG 2005 (44) Mikhalevski,V: 'see Svidler-Topalov.'}) 14... fxe6 15. Ne3 O-O ({Anand didn't know yet Topalov's smashing idea of the next round. Otherwise he might have tried} 15... Qa5+ 16. c3 Nf3+ 17. Qxf3 Bxc3+ 18. Kd1 Qa4+ 19. Nc2 (19. Kc1 $5 {Now this move may work thanks to an intermediate check on h5, which was impossible with the pawns on f5 and f7 in Topalov's game.} Bxb2+ 20. Kxb2 Qb4+ 21. Kc1 Nd4 22. Qh5+ Kd8 {In any cases Black's attack shouldn't be underestimated.}) 19... Bxb2 20. Qb3 Qxb3 21. axb3 Bxa1 22. Nxa1 Ke7 {see Svidler-Topalov.}) 16. Be2 Qe7 17. O-O Rad8 18. Bh5 Kh8 19. Re1 d5 20. a4 $14 {1-0 Kasimdzhanov,R-Anand,V/San Luis ARG 2005 (38). (37)}) 10... h5 11. h4 gxh4 12. Rxh4 Nc6 13. Nb3 Be6 14. Qd2 Rc8 $5 {A very uncommon line. Black's not in a hurry with 14...Qb6 which allows 15.Nd5, but waits for White to weaken the f2-pawn and only then comes with Qb6.} (14... Qb6 {is the main line.} 15. Nd5 Bxd5 16. exd5 Nce5 17. c3 (17. O-O-O {was more popular.}) 17... Ng6 18. Rh3 h4 19. Bxg4 hxg3 20. Rxh8+ Bxh8 21. Qe3 gxf2+ 22. Kxf2 Qxe3+ 23. Kxe3 Ne5 24. Be2 Kd7 25. Nd2 Rg8 26. Rh1 Bf6 $15 {and Black is better thanks to a more compact pawn structure. 0-1 Topalov,V-Kasparov,G/Geneve 1996 (64)}) 15. O-O-O ({Black won a nice game after} 15. Qg5 $2 Bf6 $1 {[%csl Rg5,Rh4]} 16. Rxh5 Kd7 $1 17. Nc5+ $2 (17. Rxh8 Qxh8 18. Qd2 Nb4 {[%csl Rc3] is also very dangerous for White, but that's what White had to do.}) 17... dxc5 18. Qd2+ Nd4 19. Bxg4 Bxg4 $1 20. Rd5+ Ke8 21. Qf4 ({It turns out that after} 21. Rxd8+ Rxd8 $19 {[%csl Re1][%cal Gh8h1] White can't stop Rh1.}) 21... Bd7 $19 22. O-O-O e6 {and White resigned in 0-1 Smikovsky,I-Yudasin,L, Chigorin mem 1997./CBM62[Atlas].}) 15... Qb6 $5 {[%cal Gg4f2] Only this move is new. Black attacks the f2-pawn and thus provokes the exchange of the queens.} ({The only previous game saw} 15... Bf6 16. Rhh1 h4 17. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 18. exd5 Nce5 19. Bxe5 Nxe5 20. Qb4 Qc7 21. c3 Rg8 {and Black should be OK. Movsesian,S-Kalod,R After GP Mladi 1997/CBM61[Gershon].}) 16. f3 (16. Na4 {is senseless in view of} Qa7) 16... Qe3 $1 {After the exchange of queens the black king feels safer.} 17. Bf4 $1 {White will try to play on the weakness of the h5-pawn, which is the only clear target in Black's position.} (17. Nd5 $6 {looks very risky.} Bxd5 18. exd5 Nb4 19. c3 Nxd5 (19... Bh6 20. Kb1 $1) (19... Nxa2+ $5 {also deserves attention.} 20. Kb1 Bxc3 21. Qxe3 Nxe3 22. Kxa2 Nxd1 23. Bxd1 Bf6 $13) 20. fxg4 Qxg3 $15) 17... Qxd2+ 18. Rxd2 {White takes his rook away from a fork on f2.} ({In case of} 18. Bxd2 Bf6 $1 {[%cal Gg4f2] White would have to give up an exchange since Nf2 is coming.}) 18... Nge5 {[%cal Ge5g6,Ge5c4] The knight goes to c4 creating on the way a threat of Ng6. White has to move either rook or bishop.} (18... Bf6 {was interesting with the idea} 19. Rh1 Bxc3 20. bxc3 Nge5 {[%csl Ra2,Rc2,Rc3] and Black compensates the weakness of the h-pawn by White's weaknesses on the queenside.}) 19. Be3 Bf6 20. Rh1 Nc4 {Forcing an exchange of one of the white bishops. Black obtains an advantage by the bishop's pair.} 21. Bxc4 Bxc4 {[%cal Gb7b5]} 22. Na4 $1 {Just in time. Otherwise Black would play b5 herself.} b5 $1 {Anyway. The bishop on c4 will be untouchable.} 23. Nb6 Rb8 24. Kb1 $5 {White wants to bring his passive knight into play by means of Nc1-e2-f4.} (24. Nxc4 bxc4 25. Nd4 c3 $1 26. bxc3 Ne5 $1 {with very strong compensation for a pawn.}) 24... h4 {[%csl Rg2] Judit fixes the g-pawn on g2.} 25. f4 {[%cal Gb6d5] White has in mind Nd5 and so he restricts the black pieces and may at some point break through with e5.} Rb7 26. Nc1 {White creates a hidden threat of f5 followed by b3, but in fact it's not really a threat because even after capturing the bishop the knight will be pinned on the b-file and most likely lost.} (26. Nd5 {White could try to exploit the weakness of the h-pawn by means of} Bxd5 27. exd5 Nb8 28. Bd4 {but Black is just in time to counterattack the centre.} Nd7 29. g3 h3 30. Rdh2 Bxd4 31. Nxd4 Nf6 32. Rxh3 Rxh3 33. Rxh3 Nxd5 {and Black should be able to retain the balance.}) 26... Rg8 $1 {[%cal Gg8g3]} 27. Rh3 {[%csl Gg3] Covering the g3-square.} (27. f5 Rg3) 27... Rg4 $1 {Black's position looks very comfortable while White has to find a way how to unpin his pieces.} 28. b3 Be6 $5 (28... Bf1 $5 {[%csl Rg2] deserves serious attention.} 29. Rh2 b4 $1 {Preparing to meet Rf2 with Bb5.} 30. Nd3 Rg3 (30... Bxd3 31. cxd3 (31. Rxd3 $2 Rxb6 $1 32. Bxb6 Rxf4 {and Black picks up the e4-pawn and obtains a clear advantage.}) 31... Nd4 $1 32. Nd5 Bg7 {[%cal Ge7e6] with the idea of e6 and the initiative is on Black's side.})) 29. Ne2 Rxg2 30. f5 {It starts to seem that White's position is getting better. He's going to play Nd5, to exchange Black's dark-squared bishop and to win back the h-pawn.} Bd7 ({With a fantastic positional blow} 30... Rxb6 $1 31. Bxb6 Bd7 {[%csl Re4,Rf5][%cal Gd6d5] preparing d5! Black could maintain the initiative although White seems to be able to escape to a draw.} 32. Be3 d5 $1 (32... e6 $5) 33. exd5 Bxf5 34. Rf3 Bg4 35. Rxf6 $1 (35. dxc6 Rxe2 $3 36. Rd1 (36. Rxe2 Bxf3 37. c7 Kd7 38. Rf2 Bb7 39. Bf4 Kc8 $15) 36... Re1 $1 37. Rxe1 Bxf3 38. c7 Kd7 $15) 35... exf6 36. dxc6 Rxe2 37. Rxe2 Bxe2 $11) 31. Nd5 Ne5 $2 {This is a risky continuation from the positional point of view. There was no point in destroying her own pawn structure.} ({The position after} 31... Be5 32. Rxh4 e6 33. Ndf4 {is close to equality.}) 32. Nxf6+ exf6 33. Nc3 $1 {[%csl Rd6,Rf6][%cal Gc3d5] Suddenly the knight finds a very nice square on d5. Now it's getting clear that Black misplayed her nice position.} ({Certainly not} 33. Rxh4 $2 Nf3 {[%csl Rd2,Rh4]} 34. Rh8+ Ke7 {and White loses an exchange.}) 33... Rg3 (33... Rxd2 34. Bxd2 Bc6 35. a3 $1 {and the position is already slightly better for White.}) 34. Rxg3 hxg3 35. Nd5 (35. Bf4 $5 {looks better.}) 35... Bc6 $1 {[%csl Re4]} 36. Nxf6+ Ke7 37. Nh5 f6 {Black gives up a pawn which wasn't necessary.} (37... Bxe4 $2 {would be a blunder in view of} 38. Bg5+ $1 {and suddenly the king can't find a good square.} Kf8 39. Rxd6 $1 {[%cal Rd6d8,Gh5g3] and in view of the threat of mate in one Black loses also the g3-pawn.}) ({After the correct} 37... Nf3 38. Re2 g2 $1 39. Rxg2 Bxe4 40. Ng3 Bc6 {Black is not worse.}) 38. Nxg3 Rb8 $1 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gb8g8,Gg8g4] As some compensation for the sacrificed pawn she activates the rook and creates some threats to the e4-pawn.} 39. Rh2 {[%cal Gh2h7]} ({It's hard to believe that the passive} 39. Bf4 Rg8 40. Rd4 Rg4 41. Ne2 {can promise any advantage.}) 39... Rg8 40. Rh7+ Nf7 41. Bf4 Rg4 42. Nh5 ({In case of} 42. Bxd6+ Kxd6 43. Rxf7 Rxg3 44. Rxf6+ Kc7 {Black at least shouldn't be worse.}) 42... Bxe4 {Judit still looks for initiative.} (42... Rh4 {leads to a forced draw.} 43. Bd2 Bxe4 44. Nxf6 Rxh7 45. Nxh7 Bxf5 46. Ng5 $11) 43. Bxd6+ $1 Kxd6 44. Rxf7 Rg2 $1 {[%csl Rb1,Rc2] The idea behind 42...Bxe4.} 45. Rxf6+ Ke5 46. Re6+ ({The immediate} 46. Rxa6 Bxc2+ 47. Ka1 Bxf5 {doesn't change much. Black compensates a pawn by better placed pieces.}) 46... Kxf5 47. Rxa6 Bxc2+ 48. Kc1 Be4 $11 49. Ra5 Bd3 50. a4 Rc2+ 51. Kd1 Ra2 {[%csl Rd1,Rh5][%cal Gd3e2] The threat of Be2+ forces White to repeat the moves.} 52. Kc1 Rc2+ 53. Kd1 Ra2 54. Kc1 Rc2+ {An interesting fighting draw.} 1/2-1/2

