{[%mdl 1]} 1. e4 {Rogozenco,D Rogozenco,Dorian Rogozenco,Dorian Wells Wells Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit Wells'} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qb6 5. Nb3 Nf6 6. Nc3 e6 7. Qe2 Bb4 8. Bd2 O-O 9. a3 Be7 10. O-O-O d5 (10... d6 {is more "sicilian". On the other hand after 10...d6 White can easier organise the kingside attack, since the center is not open yet.} 11. g4 a6 12. g5 Nd7 13. h4 Qc7 14. f4 b5 15. Kb1 b4 16. axb4 Nxb4 17. f5 Rb8 18. Bf4 exf5 19. exf5 Ne5 20. Bh3 Re8 21. g6 $1 $36 {Anand,V-Kramnik,V/Mainz 2001 (blitz)}) 11. exd5 (11. Be3 Qc7 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'transposes into 11.Be3.'} 14. g4 Ne5 15. h3 Nc4 16. Bd4 Be6 17. Bg2 Rfc8 18. Rhe1 Bf8 $13 {Konguvel,P-Sherbakov,R/Calcutta 2002}) (11. e5 Nd7 12. f4 a6 13. Qh5 f5 14. Bd3 Nd4 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 16. Rhe1 Qb6 17. Re3 g6 18. Qh6 Nc5 19. Rh3 Nxd3+ 20. Rxd3 Rf7 {½-½ Mulyar,M-Yermolinsky,A/Seattle 2002}) 11... Nxd5 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Bc3 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: 'N''} (13. Be3 Qc7 {transposes into 11.Be3.}) (13. Qf3 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: 'Ü 82/(133)''} Bf6 14. Be3 d4 15. Bf4 Be6 16. Bd3 Rfc8 17. Kb1 $13 {Gofshtein-Grivas, Panormo 2001 (rapid)}) 13... Be6 14. Qf3 (14. Nd4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bf6 15. Nxc6 (15. Qd2 Rfe8 $13) 15... Bxc3 (15... Bg5+ 16. Kb1 bxc6) 16. Ne7+ Kh8 17. Qb5 (17. bxc3 Qc5) 17... Bd4 18. Nxd5 Qxb5 19. Bxb5 Bxf2 $11) 14... Rac8 15. Qg3 (15. Nd4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bc5 17. Bxc5 (17. Qe3 $2 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 Rxc2+ $1) 17... Qxc5 18. Bd3 b5 $36) (15. Bd3 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Rfd8) 15... g6 16. h4 {Here and on the next move I would rather prefer to block the d-pawn with Nd4.} (16. Nd4 {Wells} Bf6 $1 {also leads nowhere fast for white.}) (16. Be2 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Rfd8 17. Nd4 Nxd4 18. Bxd4 Bc5) 16... Rfd8 17. h5 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Looks like White has a strong attack, but in fact after the next move Black is the one who creates threats quicker.} (17. Nd4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Nxd4 18. Bxd4 Bc5 19. Bxc5 Qxc5 20. Bd3 d4 $13) 17... d4 $1 {Wells: 'Most obviously this enhances the light-squared bishop, but there are tactical elements to consider here, and Judit is really setting herself up a very pleasing exchange sacrifice which is really much more about the dark squares.'} 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Nxd4 (19. Bd2 Bxb3 20. Qh3 (20. cxb3 Na5+ 21. Kb1 Qxb3 $17) (20. Qxb3 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qxb3 21. cxb3 Na5+ 22. Kb1 Nxb3 $17) 20... Bf6 21. cxb3 d3 $1 (21... Na5+ 22. Kb1 Qxb3 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Bb4+) 22. Bc3 Ne5 {and White's position looks critical anyway.} 23. b4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} (23. Bxd3 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Rxc3+ 24. bxc3 Qxb3 $19) 23... Rxc3+ 24. bxc3 Qxf2 25. Bxd3 Qa2 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Bg7 28. Qe3 Qxa3+ 29. Kc2 Rc8 30. Be2 Ng4 $19) 19... Nxd4 20. Rxd4 (20. Qe5 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Nb3+ $1 21. cxb3 Rxc3+ 22. Qxc3 Bg5+ 23. Kc2 Qxf2+ $19) 20... Rxc3 $1 {[%mdl 896] Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '!!'' Wells: 'The point. It is not too hard to see, especially when the long-diagonal combines with the open h-file that this is the absolute key piece in the position. Still, the way in which the necessary defensive aspects of this sacrifice combine with very potent attacking resources too is a delight to watch. '} ({Wells: 'Of course not'} 20... Rxd4 {Wells: '?' Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '?''} 21. Qe5 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '''} Bg5+ $8 22. Qxg5 Rxc3 23. bxc3 Qd8 $14 (23... Rd5 {Wells} 24. Qh6 {with some advantage to white.} (24. Qf6 $2 {Wells} Rd1+ $1))) 21. bxc3 {Wells: 'Regrettably forced from white's standpoint. There is a rather better hidden tactical point underlying black's sacrifice namely that'} (21. Rxd8+ Qxd8 22. Qxc3 (22. bxc3 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bxa3+ $19) 22... Bg5+ $19) (21. Qe5 {(Wells) fails to} Bf6 $1 22. Qxf6 Rxc2+ $1 23. Kxc2 Bb3+) 21... Rxd4 (21... Bxa3+ {Wells} 22. Kd2 Rxd4+ {is also possible, but the text has a very unhurried feel about it which emphasises black's utter confidence that her dark-square domination will carry the day.} 23. cxd4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qb4+ (23... Qxd4+ 24. Bd3 Bb4+ 25. Ke2) 24. Ke2 $8 Bc4+ 25. Kf3 Bd5+ (25... Qc3+ 26. Bd3 Bxd3 27. Qb8+ Bf8 28. Rh8+ Kxh8 29. Qxf8+ Kh7 30. Qxf7+) 26. Ke2 $11) 22. cxd4 Qxd4 {Black is objectively better and from the practical point of view White has almost no chances to survive against Black's strong attack.} 23. c3 (23. Qb8+ Bf8 $17 24. Kb1 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qc3 $17) (23. Qd3 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bg5+ 24. Kd1 (24. Kb1 Qe5 25. Qe2 Qc3 $19) 24... Qa1+ 25. Ke2 Qe5+ 26. Kd1 Bg4+ 27. f3 Bf5 28. Qc4 Qe3 29. Qd5 Be6 30. Qd3 Qc1+ 31. Ke2 Bc4 $19) 23... Qc5 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} (23... Bxa3+ 24. Kc2 Qb6 25. Qe5 $1 {for another pawn White trades queens, which keeps him out of danger.} Qxf2+ (25... f6 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 26. Qd4 Bf5+ 27. Kd1) 26. Be2 Qf5+ 27. Qxf5 Bxf5+ 28. Bd3 {and the draw is most likely result.}) (23... Qd5 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 24. Qb8+ Bf8 25. Qxa7 Qa2 26. Qd4) (23... Bg5+ {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 24. Qxg5 Qxc3+ 25. Kd1 Bb3+ 26. Ke2 Bc4+ $11) 24. Kd2 ({preferable is} 24. Be2 Qxa3+ 25. Kd2 Qb2+ 26. Ke1 {although Black is better here too.}) (24. c4 $2 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qd4 $1 25. Qb8+ Bf8 26. Qxb7 Bxa3+ 27. Kb1 Bd6 28. Qa8+ Bf8 $19) (24. Kd1 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bf6 $17) 24... Bg5+ {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '?!''} (24... Bf6 $5 {Rogozenco,Dorian: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} 25. Be2 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} (25. Qb8+ {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bc8) 25... Kg7 $17) 25. Kc2 (25. Ke1 {again he had to run to e1.} Bf6 26. Be2 $15 {[%cal Ge1f1] /} Kg7 $1 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 27. c4 (27. Kf1 Qxa3) 27... Qd4 28. Qd3 Qa1+ 29. Bd1 Qc1 30. Qc2 Qxa3 $17) 25... Kg7 {'!' Wells. Wells: '!' Rogozenco,Dorian: ''!' Wells. Polgar,Judit: '!'' Wells: 'The patience that comes from such a qualitative superiority of force. I'm sure Judit didn't feel as if she was material down here. Her pieces have an enduring superiority which belies the simple number-crunching of relative material values.'} 26. Bd3 (26. Be2 Qd5 $1 {[%cal Gd5a2]} (26... Bf6 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 27. Ra1 Bf5+ 28. Kd2 Qb6 $17) (26... Qa5 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} 27. Kd1 $8)) 26... Bf6 27. Rb1 (27. Be2 Qxa3 $19) 27... Qxc3+ 28. Kd1 Bg5 29. Ke2 (29. Qxg5 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qxd3+ 30. Kc1 Bf5 31. Rb2 Qc3+ $19) (29. f4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Bf5 30. Rc1 Bg4+ 31. Qxg4 Qxd3+ 32. Ke1 Qe3+ 33. Kd1 Bxf4 $19) 29... Bg4+ $1 30. Kf1 (30. Qxg4 {(Rogozenco,Dorian) Polgar,Judit} Qd2+ 31. Kf1 Qxd3+ $19) 30... Bf4 $1 {The triumph of black bishops.} (30... Bf4 31. Qxf4 Qxd3+) 0-1

