1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 Ne4 3. Bf4 d5 ({Another recent high-level rapid game saw instead} 3... c5 4. f3 Qa5+ 5. c3 Nf6 6. d5 e6 $5 ({this advance continues to gain some support at the cost of the traditional main line,} 6... Qb6) 7. e4 exd5 8. exd5 d6 {. Here we've tended to focus on the main move,} 9. Qd2 {, most recently in Palliser-A.Zhigalko, European Club Cup, Rhodes 2013.} ({Instead, Grischuk-Giri, Beijing (rapid) 2013, continued} 9. Na3 Be7 10. Ne2 $5 ({a new move for us previously, we've noted that} 10. Nc4 Qd8 11. a4 O-O 12. Ne2 Re8 13. Kf2 Na6 14. Bc1 $6 Nc7 15. Ne3 Bf8 $1 {left Black slightly for choice in Stips-Korneev, Porto San Giorgio 2011 it's notable how Grischuk refrains from driving the black queen back, instead preferring to focus on the other flank}) 10... Nh5 ({being a rapid game, Giri is keen to seize the initiative and he may also have realised that} 10... b5 11. g4 O-O 12. Ng3 {leaves White will placed on both the kingside and to react to either of Black's queenside advances}) ({however,} 10... O-O $5 11. g4 Qd8 12. Ng3 Re8 {might be the critical test of Grischuk's idea when one important line runs} 13. Kf2 a6 14. Nc4 b5 15. Ne3 {and while the knight should be happy on e3 here it permits} g5 $5 {, and if} 16. Bxg5 Nxg4+ 17. fxg4 Bxg5) 11. Be3 O-O 12. g4 $1 ({a new move and a stronger plan than the previously played} 12. Nc4 Qd8 13. a4) 12... Bh4+ 13. Bf2 Bxf2+ 14. Kxf2 Nf6 15. Ng3 {(Black has exchanged off a potentially problem bishop, but lacks counterplay, partly due to the excellent defensive work performed by the knight on a3 Giri now sacrifices a pawn for some activity)} c4 $6 16. Nxc4 Qc5+ 17. Qd4 (17. Ne3 {looks even stronger, and if} Re8 {only then} 18. Qd4) 17... Nxd5 18. Qxc5 dxc5 19. Rd1 Be6 20. Ne4 {and White was pressing, eventually winning on move 60.})) 4. e3 c5 5. Bd3 Nc6 $6 ({Polgar didn't appear too well prepared in London. She had played the text before, but there's a reason why pretty much every other GM has abandoned the move, in large part for} 5... Nf6 {.}) 6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. d5 e5 ({Practice has also shown that} 7... Nb4 8. Nc3 e6 9. d6 $1 {is in White's favour.}) 8. Bg3 Ne7 9. Nc3 f6 ({A new try for the former top-ten player and nowadays acclaimed author. Previously} 9... Ng6 {had been well met by} 10. h4 $1 {in Radjabov-Polgar, Benidorm (rapid) 2003 - see Eric's notes in the Archive.}) 10. Qd2 $6 ({A natural enough move, but one would have expected the world no.3 to know about} 10. Qh5+ $1 Ng6 11. O-O-O {, which is just very strong, as shown by Eric's notes to K.Berg-Navara, Morso 2002, and mine to Laznicka-Huzman, European Club Cup, Eilat 2012.}) 10... Bf5 11. f3 ({Freeing the g3-bishop to an extent and undermining the cramping pawn on e4, but White might have preferred} 11. O-O-O $5 {, and if} Nc8 12. f4 {, gaining a move over the game, albeit at the cost of committing the king to a side of the board where it might be attacked.}) ({Note too that} 11. d6 $2 Nc8 {would just drop a pawn for insufficient compensation.}) 11... Nc8 $1 ({This pawn sacrifice is a novelty, but it makes excellent sense. Instead,} 11... exf3 12. Nxf3 Nc8 13. e4 Bg6 14. O-O {looks quite pleasant for White, with Nh4 and Qe2-g4 one plan.}) 12. f4 $1 ({Nakamura really is determined to free his bishop. White can cling on to an extra pawn with something like} 12. fxe4 Bg6 13. Nh3 Nd6 14. Nf2 {, but after} b5 {Black is for choice: not only is e4 a problem, but White is devoid of a good plan and has a rather unhappy bishop.}) 12... exf4 13. Bxf4 Nd6 ({Watching live at Olympia I rather liked the idea of} 13... h5 $5 {, gaining some useful space and making it hard for White to go Ne2-g3.}) 14. Nge2 Nc4 {A bit sub-optimal, but necessary if Black wanted to maintain the pawn on e4 while completing her development.} 15. Qc1 Bd6 ({Facilitating castling, but Polgar has got away without completing development thus far and might have continued in that vein with} 15... Bg4 $5 {. The idea is that d5 is as weak as e4 and, unfortunately for White,} 16. Ng3 $2 {runs into} g5 {.}) 16. O-O Bg6 17. Ng3 Qe7 $6 ({Nakamura had played the last few moves pretty quickly, as is his wont, and I'm sure that with more time Polgar would have found something better. Pushing the f-pawn looks pretty sensible and there was also} 17... Bxf4 18. Rxf4 Nd6 {, although after} 19. Qd2 Qe7 20. Qe2 {White should be slightly for choice, since h4-h5 is very much an idea.}) 18. b3 $1 {Prodding the knight at the most opportune moment.} Be5 ({The best try. Black might like to go} 18... Nb6 {, but after} 19. Nf5 $1 Bxf5 20. Bxd6 Qxd6 21. Rxf5 {White will safely bag the e4-pawn, since} Qe7 {can be met by} 22. Rf4 {.}) 19. Bxe5 Qxe5 20. Ncxe4 ({Going for the extra pawn makes a lot of sense, although} 20. Nce2 Nd6 21. c4 {would also have left White doing pretty well on the positional front, with a queenside advance one plan.}) 20... Nb6 21. Nxc5 Nxd5 22. Nd3 Bxd3 23. cxd3 O-O {Of course, the e-pawn was immune, but now at last Polgar is castled. Moreover, her centralised pieces exert a fair amount of pressure and it isn't so easy for Nakamura to realise his extra pawn.} 24. e4 $6 ({Too ambitious, but perhaps the American superstar was either over confident or just not in the mood to play having already qualified for the quarter-finals. Here} 24. Re1 {followed by Qd2 would have been somewhat more circumspect.}) 24... Qd4+ 25. Kh1 Rac8 26. Qd2 ({Now Black's queen and knight team generate some threats to the white king, but in the case of} 26. Nf5 Rxc1 27. Nxd4 Rxf1+ 28. Rxf1 Nb4 {Polgar would have regained her pawn with full equality.}) 26... Ne3 27. Rfc1 Ng4 28. h3 $6 ({Whether this was a rare outright blunder or just Nakamura trying too hard isn't clear. What is clear is that objectively White should have settled for} 28. Nf5 Nf2+ 29. Kg1 Nh3+ 30. Kh1 Nf2+ {with a draw by repetition.}) 28... Qxd3 $1 29. Qf4 Qe3 30. Qxe3 Nxe3 31. Rxc8 Rxc8 32. Re1 Rc3 {Suddenly it's Black who is the one pressing. Now White should have offered a trade of knights on f5, but instead committed a second oversight.} 33. e5 $2 Nc2 34. Rc1 ({Better to pin than to play the pawn-down knight ending which would have arisen after} 34. Ne4 Nxe1 35. Nxc3 fxe5 {.}) 34... fxe5 35. Nf5 b5 36. Ne3 $1 {A good recovery from Nakamura. Due to the position of Black's rook in front of the e-pawn, Polgar will struggle to shield her queenside pawns from attack and White can draw without too much trouble.} Rxe3 37. Rxc2 Re1+ 38. Kh2 Ra1 39. Kg3 ({Now Black's pawns live, but presumably Nakamura wasn't too sure about} 39. Rc8+ Kf7 40. Rc7+ Kf6 41. Rxa7 e4 42. Kg3 Ke5 {, although this does just seem to be a draw after, say,} 43. Rxg7 Rxa2 44. Rxh7 Rb2 45. Rh5+ Kd4 46. Rxb5 e3 47. Rb7 Rb1 48. Kf3 {.}) 39... a5 40. Rc5 Rxa2 ({I'm not convinced that this rook ending was terribly well played, but then again it was a rapid game. Here} 40... a4 $5 41. Rxb5 ({or} 41. bxa4 bxa4 42. Rc2 Kf7) 41... a3 42. Rxe5 Rxa2 43. b4 Rb2 44. Ra5 Rb3+ 45. Kf2 Kf7 {would still have left Black with some chances to press.}) 41. Rxb5 Ra3 42. Kf2 $6 ({And here White might have drawn by force:} 42. Kg4 $1 e4 43. Kf4 e3 44. Kf3 Kf7 45. Re5 {and by evading the tactics along the third rank, White picks off the extra black pawn.}) 42... e4 43. b4 a4 44. Re5 ({Easy to criticise, but the damage had already been done, as shown by} 44. Ra5 Kf7 45. b5 Ke6 46. b6 Kd6 47. Ra7 Kc6 {and Black's passed pawns carry much the greater threat.}) 44... e3+ ({Maintaining the pawn as White can hardly enter a pawn ending, but one might also make a decent case for} 44... Kf7 $5 45. Rxe4 Rb3 {.}) 45. Kf3 Rb3 46. b5 $2 ({A further error. Indeed,} 46. Ra5 Rxb4 47. Kxe3 {should be a pretty easy draw for someone of Nakamura's level.}) 46... a3 $2 ({Returning the favour. Based on another small tactic along the third rank,} 46... Kf7 $1 47. Rxe3 Rxb5 {would have prevented White from getting his rook behind the a-pawn and so retained good winning chances.}) 47. Re8+ Kf7 48. Ra8 {Suddenly the draw becomes clear as White is just going to push his b-pawn.} Kf6 49. b6 Kg5 50. Ra5+ Kf6 51. b7 Rxb7 52. Rxa3 h5 1/2-1/2

