1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 Qb6 7. Nb3 e6 8. Bxf6 gxf6 9. Qd2 a6 (9... Be7 $2 10. a4 a6 11. a5 Qc7 12. Na4 Bd7 13. Nb6 Rd8 14. Be2 $16) 10. f4 {Now a4 is far less effective because Black has enough time to castle long.} (10. a4 Bd7 11. a5 Qc7 12. Na4 O-O-O 13. Nb6+ Kb8 $13 {The knight on b6 looks good, but it is not doing anything. White has lost a lot of time but has not achieved anything.}) 10... Bd7 11. Be2 (11. O-O-O O-O-O 12. Be2 {is another move order.}) 11... h5 $1 {Black has to prevent White from playing h5.} (11... O-O-O 12. Bh5 Be8 13. O-O-O $14 {There is no counterplay for Black in this position at all.}) 12. Rf1 O-O-O 13. O-O-O Kb8 14. Kb1 Be7 15. Rf3 Rdg8 (15... h4 16. a3 Rc8 17. Qe1 Na5 18. Ka2 Nxb3 19. cxb3 Bc6 20. Rfd3 $14 {White is better because Black is very passive and he cannot meaningfully change the pawn structure in the centre.}) 16. Bf1 Rc8 17. Qe1 Nb4 18. Qd2 Nc6 19. a3 Rc7 20. Na2 h4 21. Rh3 $14 {The h4-pawn is subject to an attack by the queen from e1. Black still cannot do anything with his pawn structure and his bad bishop on e7.} Rcc8 22. Qe1 Qc7 23. Qf2 Ka8 24. Nc3 Na5 25. Nc1 Rh5 26. N1a2 Rch8 27. Be2 R5h7 28. Nb4 Be8 29. f5 Qc5 30. Qf1 Nc6 31. Bxa6 bxa6 32. Nxa6 Qa5 33. Nb5 Bd8 34. Rb3 Bb6 35. Nxd6 Ne5 36. Nxe8 Rxe8 37. Rd6 Ka7 38. Rdxb6 Qxb6 39. Rxb6 Kxb6 40. a4 Rc8 41. Qb5+ 1-0

