1. d4 {Stohl,I Stohl} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. c4 d6 6. O-O Nc6 7. Nc3 a6 8. e4 $5 {This move has been largely ignored due to the following unpleasant pin. But in fact the arising positions are quite interesting and merit closer attention.} Bg4 {Very logical} ({But an interesting and nearly untested idea in the Yugoslav spirit is} 8... Na5 $5 9. Qd3 c5 10. h3 (10. d5 b5 11. cxb5 axb5 12. Nxb5 Ba6 $44) 10... cxd4 11. Nxd4 Nd7 12. b3 Ne5 13. Qc2 $2 (13. Qd1 $11 {/}) 13... Nexc4 14. Nde2 Ne5 15. Be3 Nac6 16. f4 Nd7 17. Rad1 Qa5 $17 {Pantebre,J-Sharif,M/Thessaloniki olm/1984/}) (8... Rb8 9. h3 {leads to lines, which nowadays Black tends to avoid-after 8.h3 the most popular move has become 8...d7.}) 9. Be3 (9. h3 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Nd7 {also enables Black to occupy d4 with his , moreover White has spent a move on the more or less useless h3.} (10... e5 $11 {is very similar.})) 9... Nd7 10. Qd2 ({White can continue his fight for d4 only by the rather unnatural} 10. Ne2 Na5 $142 $1 ({Illogical is} 10... e5 $6 11. d5 Ne7 12. Ne1 (12. Nd2 Bxe2 13. Qxe2 c5 14. Rfb1 f5 15. f3 Qc7 16. Bh3 Rab8 17. Rf1 b5 18. b3 b4 19. Rae1 a5 20. Bg5 Bf6 21. Bh6 Bg7 22. Bxg7 Kxg7 23. f4 exf4 24. gxf4 fxe4 25. Nxe4 Rbe8 26. Qb2+ $40 {Barus,C-Hon Kah Seng,C/Asia-chT/1987/}) 12... Nb6 13. Rc1 c5 14. Nd3 f5 15. h3 Bxe2 16. Qxe2 fxe4 17. Bxe4 Nd7 (17... Nf5 $8) 18. g4 $1 b5 19. b3 bxc4 20. bxc4 Rb8 21. f4 Qa5 22. fxe5 Nxe5 23. Bf4 Qa3 24. Bxe5 Bxe5 25. Nxe5 Qg3+ 26. Bg2 dxe5 27. d6 $18 {Krasenkow,M-Smirin,I/Polanica Zdroj/1995/}) 11. Rc1 c5 12. b3 Nc6 (12... cxd4 13. Nexd4 Nc6 $11) 13. d5 Nb4 14. a3 Nd3 $5 15. Rc2 (15. Qxd3 $142 Bxf3 16. Bxf3 Ne5 $11) 15... b5 16. Ne1 Nxe1 17. Qxe1 bxc4 18. bxc4 Bxe2 19. Qxe2 Qa5 20. Bh3 Nb6 21. Qd3 Rab8 22. Bd2 Qa4 23. Rb1 Nd7 24. Rxb8 Rxb8 $36 {[%cal Gb8b1] b,Hallmark,A-Penrose,J/York/1959/}) 10... Bxf3 ({Deserving attention is the preparatory} 10... Rb8 {The idea is that after e.g.} 11. Rab1 $142 $5 {[%csl Gb4,Gc4,Gd4][%cal Yb2b4] b4} (11. Rac1 e5 12. d5 (12. Ne2 $2 Bxf3 13. Bxf3 exd4 14. Nxd4 Qf6 15. Nxc6 bxc6 $17 {Foulon,G-Giffard,N/Le Touquet/1998/}) 12... Bxf3 13. Bxf3 Nd4 14. Bg2 c5 15. dxc6 bxc6 16. b3 $13 {White's expansion on the  is stopped for the time being.})) (10... e5 11. d5 Bxf3 {gives White the additional possibility} 12. dxc6 (12. Bxf3 {-10...f3}) 12... Bxg2 13. cxb7 Rb8 (13... Bxf1 14. bxa8=Q Qxa8 15. Rxf1 Re8 16. Qd5 Nf8 17. Qxa8 Rxa8 18. c5 dxc5 19. Na4 Ne6 20. Nxc5 Nxc5 21. Bxc5 Rd8 22. Be3 $36 {[%csl Ra6,Rc7] ׫,Jasnikowski,Z-Hazai,L/Espoo/1989/}) 14. Kxg2 Rxb7 15. b3 Qb8 16. Rac1 f5 17. c5 Nf6 18. Bg5 Rb4 19. cxd6 cxd6 20. f3 Rd4 21. Qe2 Qb7 22. Bxf6 Bxf6 23. Nd5 $14 {Jasnikowski,Z-Sznapik,A/POL-chT Cetniewo/1991/ (26)}) 11. Bxf3 e5 12. d5 {Stohl: '-10...f3'} Nd4 13. Bg2 {[%csl Gd4][%mdl 32] So Black has a beautiful d4. But if the  should have some practical effect, he must open the position. And the favours White with his . As we will see from the examples, White can play on both flanks, sort of "around" the d4. An interesting strategical concept.} c5 (13... a5 $6 14. Nb5 $36) (13... Rb8 $143 14. Ne2 c5 15. dxc6 Nxe2+ 16. Qxe2 bxc6 17. Rfd1 Qc7 18. Qd3 Rxb2 19. Qa3 Rfb8 20. Qxd6 Qxd6 21. Rxd6 Nf8 22. Bh3 $1 $16 {Debnar,L-Struhar,J/Trencin SVK-ch/1991/}) 14. dxc6 bxc6 15. b4 $146 {[%csl Gb4,Gc4] A new idea in this position. Formerly White used to set his sights on the  with success.} (15. f4 $5 Rb8 (15... Qa5 16. Rac1 (16. Rad1 $2 Nb6 {} 17. b3 Qxc3 $19 {Stantic,S-Atalik,S/Nova Gorica/1999/}) 16... Qb4 17. b3 a5 18. Kh1 Nc5 19. f5 Rfe8 20. f6 Bh8 21. Bxd4 exd4 22. Qxd4 Rad8 23. Rcd1 Nd7 24. Bh3 Ne5 25. Na4 h5 26. Nb2 Qa3 27. Nd3 $16 {Begun,S-Hasanov,M/Minsk/1985/}) 16. f5 (16. Rf2 $5 Nf6 17. Bh3 h5 18. Re1 Ng4 $6 19. Bxg4 hxg4 20. Bxd4 exd4 21. Nd1 Qb6 22. b3 Rfe8 23. Qd3 Rb7 24. Rfe2 Rbe7 25. Nf2 f5 26. Kg2 $16 {Cao Sang-Roselli Mailhe,B/Moscow olm/1994/}) (16. Rab1 a5 17. Ne2 c5 18. Nc3 Nb6 19. b3 a4 20. f5 axb3 21. axb3 Qe7 22. Qd3 f6 23. b4 Qf7 24. Bxd4 exd4 25. Nd5 Nxd5 26. exd5 Bh6 27. bxc5 dxc5 28. Rxb8 Rxb8 29. Rb1 {½,Alzate,J-Ciocaltea,V/La Habana olm/1966/}) 16... a5 17. Rf2 Nb6 18. b3 a4 19. Nxa4 Nxa4 20. bxa4 c5 21. Raf1 f6 22. h4 Rb4 (22... Ra8 $142 $5 $14 {}) 23. Bxd4 $1 exd4 24. fxg6 hxg6 25. e5 $1 dxe5 26. Bd5+ Kh7 27. h5 $40 {[%csl Gd5,Rg7] ,Jasnikowski,Z-Penzold,A/BL2-N 9899/1999/ Typically an attack with opposite-coloured  is very dangerous, Black's light squares are fatally weakened.}) 15... Rc8 (15... Nb6 16. Qd3 c5 17. bxc5 (17. b5 $6 axb5 18. cxb5 Ra3 $40) 17... dxc5 18. Nd5 Rb8 19. Rab1 Qd6 20. Bd2 $5 {[%cal Yd2a5] a5}) 16. Rfc1 Qe7 17. Rab1 Qe6 18. Qd3 Nb6 19. Bxd4 {Black's  was getting real, so White has to exchange the  after all.} (19. Ne2 c5 20. a3 (20. Bxd4 $2 cxd4 $15) (20. bxc5 dxc5 $11) 20... Na4 $5 $132) 19... exd4 20. Ne2 c5 21. Nf4 Qe5 $8 (21... Qf6 $6 22. bxc5 dxc5 23. e5 Qd8 24. e6 $36 {[%csl Gg2,Rg7][%cal Re6e7] e7,}) 22. Qa3 ({Simpler seems} 22. bxc5 Qxc5 23. Qb3 Rb8 $1 24. Nd3 Qa5 25. Qb4 $5 $14) 22... cxb4 23. Rxb4 (23. Qxa6 Nd7 24. Nd3 (24. Rxb4 $143 Nc5 25. Qb6 Bh6 $36) 24... Nc5 25. Nxe5 Nxa6 26. Nd7 (26. Nd3 Rb8 {[%cal Yb8b6,Yf8b8,Ya6c5] b6,fb8,c5=}) 26... Rfe8 27. Nb6 Rc5 28. Nd5 d3 $5 $132) 23... Nd7 24. Bh3 {[%csl Gd4,Gd7][%cal Gd7c5] White understandably doesn't want Black's  to come to c5 or e5, where it would further the possible advance of the d4. Although White will have some edge due to the pawn structure, Polgar still retains  against the c4 and e4.} (24. Nd3 Qe6 25. Rb7 Nc5 26. Rb6 a5 $13) 24... Rcd8 25. Bxd7 Rxd7 {[%cal Rg7h6] h6} 26. Re1 Bh6 27. Nd3 $14 Qe6 28. Rb6 (28. Ra4 Rc8 29. c5 Qe8 (29... Bd2 $5) 30. Rxd4 dxc5 31. Rc4 (31. Rxd7 Qxd7 32. Qxa6 c4 $36 {[%csl Gc4] c}) 31... Bf8 $11) (28. c5 $2 Bd2 $17) 28... Qxc4 29. Qxa6 Qc2 (29... Rc8 30. Qxc4 Rxc4 31. Rd1 $14) 30. Rb2 Qc3 31. Rb3 Qc7 (31... Qc2 32. Qa4 Rdd8 33. Ra3 $14 (33. Qxd4 Qxa2 $11 {/})) 32. Nb4 Qa7 33. Qd3 (33. Qxa7 Rxa7 34. Re2 Rc8 $11) 33... Bg7 (33... Rc8 $143 34. Nd5 $36 {[%cal Rd5b6] f6,b6}) 34. Rc1 Qa5 35. Rc6 (35. a3 $5 $14 {d5}) 35... h5 (35... f5 36. exf5 Rxf5 37. Rc8+ $40) 36. Ra6 Qc5 37. Rc6 Qa5 38. h4 Kh7 $5 39. Qc4 f5 {[%mdl 256] Active defence. Black first safeguarded his  before this double-edged advance.} 40. Qd5 Qd8 (40... Qxd5 $2 41. exd5 $14 {[%csl Ga2,Rd6,Rg7] /,,ץg7,d6,a}) 41. Nd3 Qe7 42. exf5 Rxf5 43. Qc4 Rf3 44. Rc8 Qf6 45. Rc7 {White exchanges a pair of  to lessen the potential danger for his .} (45. a4 Rf7 46. Qc2 Qf5 $11 {[%csl Rf2,Rg1,Rg3] ׻}) 45... Rxc7 46. Qxc7 Qf5 47. Rb7 Qf6 48. Qc2 Qf5 {Stohl: '=' Stohl,I: '='} 49. Qc7 Qf6 50. Rb3 (50. Qc2 Qf5 $11) 50... Qf5 51. Qc4 {White persists, but Black already has  play.} g5 $6 {Again Polgar plays the most risky move, but now she takes it a bit too far.} (51... d5 52. Qc2 Qe4 {} 53. a4 g5 $132) 52. hxg5 Qxg5 (52... h4 53. Nf4 Rxb3 54. g6+ Kh6 55. Qxb3 Be5 56. Qf7 $1 $18) 53. Ne1 Rc3 54. Qe2 Rc1 (54... h4 55. Qe4+ Kh6 56. Nf3 $1 $16) 55. Kg2 Qd5+ 56. Rf3 $6 ({More testing was} 56. Nf3 $142 $1 Bf6 (56... Rc3 57. Rb5 Rc5 (57... d3 58. Qb2 $1 (58. Rxd5 dxe2 59. Rxh5+ Kg6 60. Rh1 Rc2 $44) 58... Rc5 59. Rb7 Qg8 60. Qd4 $18) 58. Qd3+ Kh6 59. a4 $36) 57. Qb5 $5 $14) (56. f3 $14) 56... Rc3 57. Nd3 Be5 58. a4 (58. Kg1 $5) 58... h4 59. g4 ({White's pieces are in a nasty pin even after} 59. gxh4 $5 Ra3 60. Ne1 Ra1 $44) 59... Kg7 60. Ne1 Rc1 $1 $11 {Now Black is finally saved, as White's attack against his exposed  leads only to a perpetual due to the pin of f3.} 61. Qd2 (61. Kf1 Ra1 $15) 61... Rxe1 62. Qg5+ (62. Qxe1 $2 h3+ $19) 62... Kh7 63. Qxh4+ Kg7 (63... Kg6 64. Qh5+ $11) 64. Qg5+ Kh7 65. Qf5+ Kg7 (65... Kg8 66. Qf8+ Kh7 67. Qe7+ Kg6 68. Qe8+ $11) 66. g5 $5 Qe4 (66... Qg8 $2 67. Qd7+ Kg6 68. Qh3 $1 $18 Qh7 (68... Bg7 69. Qf5+ Kh5 70. Rh3#) 69. Qf5+ Kg7 70. Qf7+ Kh8 71. Qf8+ Qg8 72. Rh3#) 67. Qf8+ Kh7 68. Qf7+ Kh8 69. Qh5+ Kg7 70. Qh6+ Kg8 71. Qe6+ Kg7 72. Qh6+ 1/2-1/2

