1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 5. Nb5 a6 6. Nd6+ Bxd6 7. Qxd6 Qf6 8. Qxf6 {Nisipeanu is content to follow his earlier game with Vallejo Pons. White doesn't have to play for only a small edge, though, and we will consider some more ambitious - and dare I say critical - ideas in Amanov-Shtyrenkov.} Nxf6 9. Nc3 Nb4 ({Best. Indeed, subscribers should know by now that I'm not a fan of the black position after} 9... d5 10. exd5 $1 ({for whatever inexplicable reason,} 10. Nxd5 $6 Nxd5 11. exd5 Nb4 12. Bd3 Nxd3+ $1 13. cxd3 Bf5 {keeps being played and gave Black full equality in, for example, Lama-Gillani, New Delhi 2009}) 10... Nb4 11. Bd3 Nxd3+ $6 ({better to restrict White to just an edge with} 11... Nfxd5 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Bd2) 12. cxd3 Bf5 {.}) 10. Bd3 d6 ({In their third rapid play-off game (and fifth in the match), Polgar switched to my recommendation from 'Dangerous Weapons: The Sicilian',} 10... h6 {, but with unfortunate consequences:} 11. Be3 {(White's main move, albeit a new one for us on the site alternatives can be found in the notes to both Nispeanu-Vallejo and Al Qudaimi-Turov in the Archive)} d6 $6 ({oh dear! indeed, as explained in the aforementioned DW work, Black should insert} 11... Nxd3+ 12. cxd3 {and only then go} d6 {with good chances to equalize}) 12. O-O-O Be6 13. a3 $5 ({White might also keep the bishop-pair with} 13. Be2 {since} d5 $6 {runs into} 14. a3 {, as pointed out in my DW coverage}) 13... Nxd3+ 14. Rxd3 Ke7 15. Rhd1 Rhd8 16. Bb6 $1 Rd7 17. f3 Rc8 18. a4 {left Black low on counterplay. Indeed, White enjoys a pleasant edge here and it might be instructive to see how Nisipeanu wrapped things up whilst maintaining control:} Rc6 (18... Nh5 $5) 19. a5 Bc4 20. Re3 Ne8 21. b3 Be6 22. g4 $1 Rc8 23. Kb2 Nf6 24. Red3 Ne8 25. h4 f6 26. h5 Nc7 27. R1d2 Na8 28. Na4 Rc6 29. Bf2 Rdc7 30. c4 Rc8 31. Rd1 Nc7 $2 32. Nb6 Rb8 33. Nd5+ Kf7 34. Nb4 Ne8 35. Nxc6 bxc6 36. Kc3 Ke7 37. b4 Rc8 38. Bb6 Ra8 39. c5 d5 40. exd5 Bxd5 41. Rxd5 $1 cxd5 42. Rxd5 Ke6 43. Kc4 Rc8 44. Rd1 f5 45. gxf5+ Kxf5 46. b5 {1-0.}) 11. Bg5 $1 {Critical. White must play to exploit the absence of ...h6.} Be6 12. O-O-O ({A logical choice, although as I pointed out in my earlier notes to Nisipeanu-Vallejo,} 12. f4 $5 {gives White decent chances for an edge:} Ng4 ({perhaps Black has to try his chances after} 12... Rc8 $5 13. f5 Bc4 14. Bxf6 Bxd3 15. cxd3 gxf6 16. Kd2 Kf8) 13. f5 f6 14. Bd2 Nxd3+ 15. cxd3 Bf7 16. Ke2 {and White must be better with options right across the board.}) 12... Ng4 $1 {Avoiding any exchange on f6. Back in 2008 I felt that Black was OK here and still think so, although presumably Nisipeanu disagreed - or did he just repeat this line having nothing else ready when surprised by the Lowenthal?} 13. Bh4 g5 14. Bg3 ({Black is fine too in the event of} 14. h3 Ne3 15. fxe3 gxh4 16. Be2 Rg8 17. Bf3 O-O-O {.}) 14... Ke7 15. Be2 $5 ({The novelty, although whether this was well-remembered preparation or over-the-board inspiration is debatable. Previously} 15. h4 Nxd3+ 16. Rxd3 h6 17. f3 Nf6 18. Bf2 Nh5 ({Black might prefer the more solid} 18... Rag8 $5 19. Rhd1 Ne8 {, intending to develop counterplay with ...g4 or even ...f5, as I pointed out at the time}) 19. Be3 {had been seen in Nisipeanu-Vallejo Pons, Heviz 2008, and now Black should have overprotected d6 with} Rad8 $1 {- see the Archive for a full explanation.}) 15... Nf6 ({The knight must go back, as} 15... h5 $6 16. h4 $1 {would just weaken Black on the kingside.}) 16. f3 $1 ({Bringing the bishop back into play makes a fair amount of sense, whereas my earlier notes only considered} 16. h4 h6 {when Black keeps the kingside closed, and will look for counterplay with .. .Rac8 and ...b5.}) 16... Nh5 ({Bringing the knight to f4 is a natural-enough plan, but with more time on the clock I'm sure that Polgar would have preferred} 16... Rac8 $1 {. Indeed, this move gains a useful tempo as} 17. Kb1 ({or} 17. a3 Na2+ 18. Nxa2 Bxa2 19. Bf2 {and now, rather than pointlessly manoeuvre the knight to f4, Black might well deprive White of the bishop-pair with} Bc4 $1) 17... Nh5 ({or just} 17... Bc4 $5) 18. Bf2 $2 {now runs into} (18. Be1 {is required}) 18... Nxa2 $1 19. Nxa2 Bxa2+ 20. Kxa2 Rxc2 21. Rhe1 Nf4 {, regaining the piece with some advantage.}) 17. Bf2 Nf4 $6 ({It still wasn't too late for} 17... Rac8 {, but now Polgar begins to run into difficulties.}) 18. Bf1 $1 {This, rather than White's 16th, is the real star move. Nisipeanu doesn't mind having to retreat his bishop to its start square. From there it usefully defends g2 and, with his rook still on h1, White is now ready to drive Black backwards with a combination of g2-g3 and h2-h4.} Rhc8 19. a3 Na2+ 20. Nxa2 Bxa2 21. h4 {Having deployed his bishops to excellent squares, White is now in a position to turn his attention to the classic undermining of Black's extended kingside.} h6 22. g3 Ne6 23. hxg5 hxg5 24. Bh3 Rc6 25. Be3 Rg8 26. Bf5 {Not a bad square for the bishop! Indeed, things have clearly gone wrong for Polgar who now finds herself under some pressure.} a5 27. Rh5 $6 ({Nisipeanu hurries to win a pawn, but this enables Black to obtain sufficient counterplay. I suspect that White would have done better to exploit the placement of Black's pieces with} 27. Rd5 $5 {, intending to slowly grind away with the two bishops in the event of} b6 28. Rh6 {.}) 27... Ng7 $1 {Essential defence. Black does now go a pawn behind, but obtains a rolling central pawn-mass and the comfort of opposite-coloured bishops in return.} 28. Rxg5 f6 $6 ({Hardly a total disaster, but Black would have done somewhat better to have kept the rooks on:} 28... Nxf5 $1 29. exf5 Rh8 {and the counterattack should not be underestimated, as shown by the line} 30. c3 {(avoiding the loss of material after 30...Bb3)} Bb3 31. Rf1 Kf6 $1 32. f4 Rc4 {and if anyone must be careful to maintain the balance it's White.}) 29. Rg4 Nxf5 30. exf5 Rxg4 31. fxg4 d5 32. Re1 ({White might have moved the bishop out of the way of the d-pawn, retaining some grinding chances after} 32. Bd2 {, with the idea of} d4 33. Bxa5 Bb3 (33... Bd5 $1 {is probably a better defence}) 34. Rd2 d3 35. Bb4+ Ke8 36. Bc3 {followed by undermining with g4-g5.}) 32... Bb3 33. c3 Rc4 $2 ({No doubt both players were playing on increment alone or close to by this point. Forcing the pace is tempting, but Black would have done better to redeploy her rook for defensive purposes with} 33... Rc8 $1 34. g5 Rh8 {.}) 34. g5 Rg4 35. gxf6+ $2 ({Now it's just a draw. I dare say Nisipeanu saw that} 35. g6 Rxg3 36. Bc5+ Ke8 {was OK for Black who will obtain sufficient counterplay with ...Rg2,}) ({but he presumably missed the strength of the intermediate check,} 35. Bc5+ $1 {:} Kf7 (35... Ke8 $2 36. gxf6 {threatens both Rxe5+ and Rh1}) 36. g6+ Ke8 37. Rg1 Bc4 38. Kd2 {and with the g6-pawn secure, Black would still have had some defending ahead.}) 35... Kxf6 36. Bb6 Rxg3 37. Bd8+ Kxf5 38. Rxe5+ $1 {Wisely settling for a draw.} Kxe5 39. Bc7+ Ke4 1/2-1/2

