{It was 10 years ago when I last played in the European championship in Ohrid, Macedonia. On that occasion I tied for 3rd but the play-off did not go in my favour so I could not reach a medal. I was looking forward to playing in Aix les Bains the 12th European Championship, I had not been playing in classical chess events since the Olympiad, so I was keen to play and challenge myself to see what can I show in such a tense event where nearly 400 participants were taking place. Obviously the main reason for most of the strong players is to qualify for the World Cup which will be held in Khanty-Mansiysk next August. There were a few players qualified from the previous European Championship, but they were competing to get a medal. It was clear from the first moment that to qualify one has to make at least plus 4 but for a sure spot plus 5 was needed. After round 5 I was quite happy with my 3 wins and 2 draws. In round 6 I got a bad opening and sacrificed a knight to complicate things but it backfired. In round 7 I played with Black against the Georgian young talent Pantsulaia.  Polgar,J: 'It was 10 years ago when I last played in the European championship in Ohrid, Macedonia. On that occasion I tied for 3rd but the play-off did not go in my favour so I could not reach a medal. I was looking forward to playing in Aix les Bains the 12th European Championship, I had not been playing in classical chess events since the Olympiad, so I was keen to play and challenge myself to see what can I show in such a tense event where nearly 400 participants were taking place. Obviously the main reason for most of the strong players is to qualify for the World Cup which will be held in Khanty-Mansiysk next August. There were a few players qualified from the previous European Championship, but they were competing to get a medal. It was clear from the first moment that to qualify one has to make at least plus 4 but for a sure spot plus 5 was needed. After round 5 I was quite happy with my 3 wins and 2 draws. In round 6 I got a bad opening and sacrificed a knight to complicate things but it backfired. In round 7 I played with Black against the Georgian young talent Pantsulaia. '} 1. c4 {Polgar,J Polgar,J} e6 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. g3 d5 4. Qc2 $6 {I had the feeling that my opponent's main idea was to surprise me in the opening, this is a premature move for the queen. Polgar,J: 'I had the feeling that my opponent's main idea was to surprise me in the opening, this is a premature move for the queen.'} (4. b3 {Ü 109/(7)}) 4... c5 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 {I realised quickly that after my next move there is only one difference between this position and the game I played against Gelfand in Wijk aan Zee 2008, which I won in memorable style. In that game the bishop was on g2 instead of the badly placed queen. Polgar,J: 'I realised quickly that after my next move there is only one difference between this position and the game I played against Gelfand in Wijk aan Zee 2008, which I won in memorable style. In that game the bishop was on g2 instead of the badly placed queen.'} e5 $1 7. Nb3 $146 {After the game I found one other game with this position where White continued with 7.f3: Polgar,J: 'After the game I found one other game with this position where White continued with 7.f3:'} (7. Nf3 d4 {Polgar,J was my plan to continue} (7... Nc6 {Polgar,J The game between Takacs against Alekhine at the famous tournament Budapest 1927 is also very tempting for Black.} 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. a3 Be6 10. Bd2 Be7 11. Nc3 O-O {with a better game for Black} 12. Bg2 Rc8 13. Rd1 Nd4 14. Qb1 Bf6 15. O-O Nxc3 16. bxc3 Nxe2+ 17. Kh1 Qc7 18. Qd3 Nxc3 19. Rc1 e4 {0-1 Takacs,S-Alekhine,A/Kecskemet 1927}) 8. Nxe5 Qa5+) 7... Nc6 $17 {Here I realised that I am better already which to tell you the truth I did not even dream of being the case after move 7!  Polgar,J: 'Here I realised that I am better already which to tell you the truth I did not even dream of being the case after move 7! '} 8. Bg2 {Obviously it is very difficult to make a choice with White when the position is worse already at a such an early stage. Polgar,J: 'Obviously it is very difficult to make a choice with White when the position is worse already at a such an early stage.'} Nb4 {This was the right move to point out the drawback of the early queen move which was made on move 4.  Polgar,J: 'This was the right move to point out the drawback of the early queen move which was made on move 4. '} 9. Qd1 dxc4 $6 {I did not really notice during the game the difference between the immediate ...dxc4 and then ...f5 or the other way around. As usual one is more precise as the other. Here the bishop move was more forcing. Polgar,J: 'I did not really notice during the game the difference between the immediate ...dxc4 and then ...f5 or the other way around. As usual one is more precise as the other. Here the bishop move was more forcing.'} (9... Bf5 10. Na3 {and now 10...dxc4 11.d2 and transposing to the game.} dxc4) 10. N3d2 Bf5 11. Na3 {Ü 9...dc4 Yes, this is the logical move but White could have got chances to get away from the very bad position by sacrificing the rook! Polgar,J: 'Yes, this is the logical move but White could have got chances to get away from the very bad position by sacrificing the rook!'} (11. O-O $1 {I was simply was not paying enough attention to the fact that White can sac material for an initiative. Polgar,J: 'I was simply was not paying enough attention to the fact that White can sac material for an initiative.'} Nc2 $2 {is not the best because of the move I play mostly in move 1 with White: e4! Polgar,J: 'is not the best because of the move I play mostly in move 1 with White: e4!'} (11... Qc7 $17) 12. e4 $1 {suddenly the passive bad position which White had few moves ago is gone and White is turning the path of the game! Polgar,J: 'suddenly the passive bad position which White had few moves ago is gone and White is turning the path of the game!'} Nxa1 13. exf5 {and Black will not be able to get the knight out of the corner. Black should react to castling with 11...c7 or 11...b8 with a nice game for Black. Polgar,J: 'and Black will not be able to get the knight out of the corner. Black should react to castling with 11...c7 or 11...b8 with a nice game for Black.'}) 11... b5 $1 {This move came to my mind immediately and I loved it, so I did not hesitate too much and played it.  Polgar,J: 'This move came to my mind immediately and I loved it, so I did not hesitate too much and played it. '} 12. Bxa8 {White kind of has to take it, if 12.0-0 I just defend the b5-pawn by ...b8 and beside the fact I have a pawn up I also have the initiative. Polgar,J: 'White kind of has to take it, if 12.0-0 I just defend the b5-pawn by ...b8 and beside the fact I have a pawn up I also have the initiative.'} Qxa8 13. Nf3 {Logical continuation. Polgar,J: 'Logical continuation.'} (13. O-O Bh3 $17 {and Black has a huge advantage. Polgar,J: 'and Black has a huge advantage.'}) 13... Nd3+ $1 {It is hard to judge this move I could have played simply ...c6 or ...a6 and have a great advantage. On the other hand with this idea I can stop White from castling and it is almost a mating attack, it looked so good and tempting to me that I could not stop myself trying it out. Polgar,J: 'It is hard to judge this move I could have played simply ...c6 or ...a6 and have a great advantage. On the other hand with this idea I can stop White from castling and it is almost a mating attack, it looked so good and tempting to me that I could not stop myself trying it out.'} (13... Qc6 $1 {Polgar,J: '!'} 14. O-O (14. Bd2 Bc5 15. O-O Bh3 16. Bxb4 Bxb4 $17 17. Nc2 {Polgar,J} Bc5) 14... Bh3 15. Re1 Ng4 $1 $19) (13... a6 {Polgar,J this was Garry's suggestion after the game when we met him in the park.}) 14. exd3 Bxd3 {This was my idea so I did not spend too much time on ...g4 which was also interesting. Polgar,J: 'This was my idea so I did not spend too much time on ...g4 which was also interesting.'} 15. Nxb5 $8 {The only move to bring the a3-knight into defence to c3. Polgar,J: 'The only move to bring the a3-knight into defence to c3.'} Bb4+ 16. Nc3 O-O $1 {This is the position I was looking forward to when I sacrificed my knight. All my pieces are playing; only the rook is missing but not for long. White has a rook up against only one little pawn, but what an initiative for Black! Polgar,J: 'This is the position I was looking forward to when I sacrificed my knight. All my pieces are playing; only the rook is missing but not for long. White has a rook up against only one little pawn, but what an initiative for Black! '} (16... Qe4+ {was too early: Polgar,J: 'was too early:'} 17. Be3 $13 {and I made him develop for no reason. Polgar,J: 'and I made him develop for no reason.'}) 17. Rg1 $8 {Again the only continuation for White to get out from the pin. Polgar,J: 'Again the only continuation for White to get out from the pin.'} (17. Be3 {would be practically losing on the spot to Polgar,J: 'would be practically losing on the spot to'} Be4 {with the following moves for example: Polgar,J: 'with the following moves for example:'} 18. O-O Bxf3 19. Qd2 Qc8 $19 {and next ...h3 with mate on the next move. Polgar,J: 'and next ...h3 with mate on the next move.'}) (17. Bd2 Bxc3 18. bxc3 Qe4+ 19. Be3 Nd5 $19) 17... Ne4 (17... Qe4+ {was still not good to Polgar,J: 'was still not good to'} 18. Be3 Nd5 {and now shows why g1 was important; Polgar,J: 'and now shows why g1 was important;'} 19. Nd2 {and White saves himself for sure. Polgar,J: 'and White saves himself for sure.'} Nxc3 20. Nxe4 Nxd1+ 21. Kxd1 Bxe4 $44) 18. Bd2 Rd8 {It is very difficult for White to get out from this tight position, the continuation was a logical one though there was an other option too: Polgar,J: 'It is very difficult for White to get out from this tight position, the continuation was a logical one though there was an other option too:'} ({Here I saw the following line Polgar,J: 'Here I saw the following line'} 18... Nxc3 19. Bxc3 Bxc3+ 20. bxc3 Qe4+ 21. Kd2 Qb7 22. Ke1 $1 (22. Kc1 $2 Rb8 23. Qd2 Qb1+ 24. Rxb1 Rxb1# {#}) 22... Qe4+ $11 {with only a draw, so I decided to continue and bring my last piece into play - my rook! Polgar,J: 'with only a draw, so I decided to continue and bring my last piece into play - my rook!'}) 19. Rc1 {White's plan is to play e3 next and if he had time for d2 afterwards then I would be in big trouble, but it was my turn. Polgar,J: 'White's plan is to play e3 next and if he had time for d2 afterwards then I would be in big trouble, but it was my turn.'} (19. Ng5 $5 {my intention was during the game to play Polgar,J: 'my intention was during the game to play'} Nxd2 20. Qxd2 {and Polgar,J: 'and'} h6 21. Nh3 Bxc3 22. bxc3 Be4 $1 23. Qe3 Bf3 $1 24. Kf1 Bg4 $17 {I had seen until this point and I was happy! Polgar,J: 'I had seen until this point and I was happy!'}) 19... Nxc3 20. bxc3 Ba3 {It is unbelievable how strong the d3-bishop can be. It was interesting that in a later round I had Black against Lupulescu and I got a similarly unpleasant situation with the other side, but I made an incredible defence and won the game.  Polgar,J: 'It is unbelievable how strong the d3-bishop can be. It was interesting that in a later round I had Black against Lupulescu and I got a similarly unpleasant situation with the other side, but I made an incredible defence and won the game.  '} 21. Be3 {White has to continue to rearrange his pieces. Polgar,J: 'White has to continue to rearrange his pieces.'} (21. Ra1 {was not a good idea due to Polgar,J: 'was not a good idea due to'} Bb2 $19 {and I am not only interested in taking the rook on a1 but in attacking the c3-pawn as well! Polgar,J: 'and I am not only interested in taking the rook on a1 but in attacking the c3-pawn as well!'}) 21... Bxc1 22. Bxc1 Rb8 $1 {[ b1] I was very happy with this move to force White to play d2 before playing e3.  Polgar,J: 'I was very happy with this move to force White to play d2 before playing e3. '} 23. Nd2 {White has to stop the possibility for Black to play  to b1 and control the 1st rank. Polgar,J: 'White has to stop the possibility for Black to play  to b1 and control the 1st rank.'} Qd5 {[ a5] Finally my poor queen got out with the threat ...a5 to put pressure on the c3-pawn. ...c6 was also possible.  Polgar,J: 'Finally my poor queen got out with the threat ...a5 to put pressure on the c3-pawn. ...c6 was also possible. '} 24. Qa4 {Stopping Black from ...a5 and attacking the pawn at the same time. Polgar,J: 'Stopping Black from ...a5 and attacking the pawn at the same time.'} a5 {The main idea for Black is to focus on not allowing White to untangle himself.  Polgar,J: 'The main idea for Black is to focus on not allowing White to untangle himself. '} 25. Qd1 {White had other opportunities like Polgar,J: 'White had other opportunities like'} (25. g4 {Polgar,J trying to get some air was also possible, but in all cases I prefer to play it with Black}) (25. Qa3 {this is also an idea to control the black squares and defend the c3-pawn Polgar,J: 'this is also an idea to control the black squares and defend the c3-pawn'} e4 $40 {and I really like blacks position Polgar,J: 'and I really like blacks position'}) (25. Nf1 {but then I would play Polgar,J: 'but then I would play'} Qf3 26. Qd1 Qf6 $40 {and with at least great compensation. Polgar,J: 'and with at least great compensation.'}) 25... h6 $1 $17 {I kind of knew what was going to happen on the next move, or I just hoped for it?  Polgar,J: 'I kind of knew what was going to happen on the next move, or I just hoped for it? '} 26. Qf3 $2 {This practically loses the game. White could have tried f1 or g4 but it is really unpleasant to play such a position with White specially in time trouble as well. Polgar,J: 'This practically loses the game. White could have tried f1 or g4 but it is really unpleasant to play such a position with White specially in time trouble as well.'} Rb1 $3 {After this move my opponent got very upset, he quickly realised that his position going to fall apart. Polgar,J: 'After this move my opponent got very upset, he quickly realised that his position going to fall apart.'} 27. Kd1 (27. Qxd5 Rxc1# {#}) (27. Qd1 Ra1 {and the rook on a1 is killing already. Polgar,J: 'and the rook on a1 is killing already.'} 28. a3 e4 $17 {Polgar,J: ''}) 27... e4 (27... Qd7 $5 {was also winning but I did not see after Polgar,J: 'was also winning but I did not see after '} 28. Qa8+ (28. Nxb1 Be4+ {would win the queen Polgar,J: 'would win the queen'}) 28... Kh7 29. Qxa5 {the move Polgar,J: 'the move '} Qf5 $1 $19 {which is winning on the spot. Polgar,J: 'which is winning on the spot.'}) 28. Qf4 Ra1 {White can't stop my queen from helping my rook and the bishop in the attack and once they work together it will be hopeless for White.  Polgar,J: 'White can't stop my queen from helping my rook and the bishop in the attack and once they work together it will be hopeless for White. '} 29. Qb8+ $138 {White is already in timetrouble, but that is not the only problem he has.  Polgar,J: 'White is already in timetrouble, but that is not the only problem he has. '} Kh7 30. g4 ({There was a beautiful line after Polgar,J: 'There was a beautiful line after'} 30. Qb2 Rb1 $1 {and if Polgar,J: 'and if'} 31. Nxb1 Bc2+ $3 32. Kxc2 (32. Ke2 Qd3+ 33. Ke1 Qd1# {#}) 32... Qd3# {# mate Polgar,J: 'mate'}) 30... Qd7 {It was interesting to notice that this was the only move in the game where I went backwards!  Polgar,J: 'It was interesting to notice that this was the only move in the game where I went backwards! '} 31. Qe5 e3 {The last touch. Polgar,J: 'The last touch.'} (31... Qa4+ 32. Nb3 {here e3 still wins but taking the knight would allow White to give a perpetual check by f5-c8. Polgar,J: 'here e3 still wins but taking the knight would allow White to give a perpetual check by f5-c8.'} cxb3 $2 (32... e3 $1) 33. Qf5+ Kg8 34. Qc8+ $11) 32. fxe3 (32. Qxe3 {would not help either Polgar,J: 'would not help either'} Qa4+ 33. Nb3 Rxa2 $19 {wins Polgar,J: 'wins'}) 32... Qa4+ 33. Ke1 Rxc1+ 34. Kf2 Rxg1 {...and if White continues and takes the rook, then d1 wins a piece.  It was such a nice feeling to play this game, that I won my next 3 games and made a draw in the last round. I won my first European Bronze Medal. Polgar,J: '...and if White continues and takes the rook, then d1 wins a piece.  It was such a nice feeling to play this game, that I won my next 3 games and made a draw in the last round. I won my first European Bronze Medal.'} 0-1

