1. d4 {Marin,M Marin,M} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 Nc6 10. d5 Ne7 {Marin,M: 'The position is similar to that arising from the Mar del Plata variation, but with two important differences. The h4-bishop and the g4-knight are placed in different, less favourable ways and will need some time for regrouping. Who will be able to adjust to the slightly changed circumstances? The result of the game greately depends on the answer to this question.' The position is similar to that arising from the Mar del Plata variation, but with two important differences. The h4-bishop and the g4-knight are placed in different, less favourable ways and will need some time for regrouping. Who will be able to adjust to the slightly changed circumstances? The result of the game greately depends on the answer to this question.} 11. Nd2 {Marin,M: 'Immediately attacking the enemy knight with a generally harmonious move. Otherwise, Black would get to play ...f5 under favourable circumstances.' Immediately attacking the enemy knight with a generally harmonious move. Otherwise, Black would get to play ...f5 under favourable circumstances.} f5 $5 {Marin,M: 'This changes the character of the position drastically. Black spoils the mobility of his structure, but open the f-file for his attack and gets a threatening kingside space advantage. besides, he will also be rewarded with the bishops pair.' This changes the character of the position drastically. Black spoils the mobility of his structure, but open the f-file for his attack and gets a threatening kingside space advantage. besides, he will also be rewarded with the bishops pair.} ({Marin,M: 'On the next day, Judit switched to the more solid' On the next day, Judit switched to the more solid} 11... Nh6 12. O-O (12. f3 {Marin,M: 'opens the bishop's way for retreat, but closes the e2-h5 diagonal for his colleague. Besides, it is a bit slow.' opens the bishop's way for retreat, but closes the e2-h5 diagonal for his colleague. Besides, it is a bit slow.} c5 {Marin,M: 'Having been given a tempo, Black usually spends it on trying to close the queenside. A recent game a top level went' Having been given a tempo, Black usually spends it on trying to close the queenside. A recent game a top level went} 13. dxc6 $5 bxc6 14. b4 Be6 15. Nb3 d5 16. cxd5 cxd5 17. exd5 Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. O-O Nf5 20. Bf2 Bh6 $132 {Marin,M: 'and Black had active play in Gelfand,B (2741)-Radjabov,T (2740)/Astrakhan 2010/ CBM 137 (1/2, 40)' and Black had active play in Gelfand,B (2741)-Radjabov,T (2740)/Astrakhan 2010/CBM 137 (1/2, 40)}) 12... g5 13. Bg3 f5 14. exf5 Bxf5 $5 $146 {Marin,M: 'A natural developing move, consolidating the kingside and putting some pressure on the e4-square.' A natural developing move, consolidating the kingside and putting some pressure on the e4-square.} (14... Nhxf5 {Marin,M: 'allows White set up a domination on light squares with' allows White set up a domination on light squares with} 15. Bh5 Nd4 16. f3 {Marin,M: 'The counterplay starting with' The counterplay starting with} b5 $5 17. cxb5 Rb8 {Marin,M: 'eventually was successfull in Onischuk,A (2652)-Bologan,V (2679)/Poikovsky 2004/CBM 100 (0-1, 31), but its soundness is not entirely clear.' eventually was successfull in Onischuk,A (2652)-Bologan,V (2679)/Poikovsky 2004/CBM 100 (0-1, 31), but its soundness is not entirely clear.}) 15. c5 Ng6 16. cxd6 cxd6 17. Nc4 Nf4 18. Re1 Bg6 19. Rc1 Nxe2+ 20. Qxe2 Nf5 21. Ne4 h5 $6 {Marin,M: 'This may be too slow. Traditionally, Black is supposed to attack on the kingside, but play on two wings also is part of the opening's spirit.' This may be too slow. Traditionally, Black is supposed to attack on the kingside, but play on two wings also is part of the opening's spirit.} (21... b5 $5 22. Ncd2 Qa5 {[%cal Gb5b4,Ga5d5,Ya2a3] Marin,M: 'would cause White some problems defending d5 and a2.' would cause White some problems defending d5 and a2.} 23. Nxg5 Nd4 24. Qd1 Qxa2 $132) 22. f3 Bh6 23. Bf2 Kh7 24. g4 $1 {Marin,M: 'White stabilizes the kingside now and has a pleasant position.' White stabilizes the kingside now and has a pleasant position.} Nh4 25. Rc3 hxg4 26. fxg4 Bxe4 27. Qxe4+ Kh8 28. Bg3 $14 {Marin,M: 'Black had to suffer for a while before reaching a draw in Hracek,Z (2633)-Polgar,J (2682)/ Khanty-Mansiysk 2010/CBM 139 (1/2, 59)' Black had to suffer for a while before reaching a draw in Hracek,Z (2633)-Polgar,J (2682)/Khanty-Mansiysk 2010/CBM 139 (1/2, 59)}) ({Marin,M: 'The third possibility, also tried at the Olympiad, is' The third possibility, also tried at the Olympiad, is} 11... h5 {Marin,M: 'This fights for space, but in the long run the weakening of the kingside may tell.' This fights for space, but in the long run the weakening of the kingside may tell.} 12. h3 Nh6 13. f3 c5 14. g4 hxg4 15. hxg4 Nf7 16. Bd3 Bd7 17. Rb1 $1 $146 {Marin,M: 'Black's kingside is well defended so far, because most of his minor pieces are massed around the king. This leaves the other wing insufficiently well defended, which justifies opening the position there.' Black's kingside is well defended so far, because most of his minor pieces are massed around the king. This leaves the other wing insufficiently well defended, which justifies opening the position there.} ({Marin,M: 'Playing for attack offers White little:' Playing for attack offers White little:} 17. Qe2 Nc8 18. Bf2 Bh6 19. Be3 Bxe3 20. Qxe3 Kg7 21. O-O-O Rh8 22. Rdg1 Ne7 23. Qe1 Qa5 24. Rxh8 Rxh8 25. Rh1 Qd8 $11 {Marin,M: 'Black equalized and even took over the initiative in the ening, Bacrot,E (2720)-Nakamura,H (2729)/Sestao 2010/CBM 138 (1/2, 97)' Black equalized and even took over the initiative in the ening, Bacrot,E (2720)-Nakamura,H (2729)/Sestao 2010/CBM 138 (1/2, 97)}) 17... Nc8 18. b4 b6 19. Bc2 Bh6 20. a3 Kg7 21. Ba4 Bxa4 22. Qxa4 Rh8 23. Ke2 Ne7 24. Bf2 $14 {Marin,M: 'The kingside is stable, while on the queenside the initiative is White's, Topalov,V (2803)-Matamoros Franco,C (2542)/Khanty-Mansiysk RUS 2010/CBM 139  (1-0, 42)' The kingside is stable, while on the queenside the initiative is White's, Topalov,V (2803)-Matamoros Franco,C (2542)/Khanty-Mansiysk RUS 2010/CBM 139 (1-0, 42)}) 12. Bxg4 fxg4 13. Bg5 {Marin,M: 'The main continuation.' The main continuation.} (13. O-O h6 {[%cal Gg6g5] Marin,M: 'would leave the bishop on a passive position.' would leave the bishop on a passive position.}) 13... h6 ({Marin,M: 'The extravagant, but possibly half correct' The extravagant, but possibly half correct} 13... Rf4 {Marin,M: 'was also tried at grandmaster level. White is invited to either open the long diagonal or weaken his kingside with g2-g3.' was also tried at grandmaster level. White is invited to either open the long diagonal or weaken his kingside with g2-g3.} 14. O-O (14. g3 {Marin,M: 'offers Black good counterplay:' offers Black good counterplay:} Rf7 15. b4 c6 16. O-O h6 17. Be3 g5 18. Nb3 cxd5 19. cxd5 Ng6 20. a4 Nf4 $132 {Sakaev,K (2555)-Poluljahov,A (2510)/Alushta 1994/CBM 042 (1/2, 37)}) 14... h6 15. Bxf4 exf4 16. Nb3 g5 17. Nd4 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 Ng6 19. Ne2 f3 20. Ng3 Ne5 21. Rae1 Qf6 22. Re3 Bd7 23. Nf5 fxg2 24. Kxg2 Bxf5 25. exf5 Qxf5 $44 {Marin,M: 'Shulman,Y  (2435)-Poluljahov,A (2485)/Gausdal 1994/EXT 97 (1/2, 35). Of course, the correctness of this exchange sacrifice should be checked still.' Shulman,Y (2435)-Poluljahov,A (2485)/Gausdal 1994/EXT 97 (1/2, 35). Of course, the correctness of this exchange sacrifice should be checked still.}) 14. Be3 b6 $5 {Marin,M: 'Slowing down the queenside attack and opening some diagonals for the c8-bishop.' Slowing down the queenside attack and opening some diagonals for the c8-bishop.} (14... g5 {Marin,M: 'allows White a pleasant queenside attack with' allows White a pleasant queenside attack with} 15. c5 $14) 15. O-O g5 16. b4 Ng6 17. a4 $146 {[%cal Ga4a5] Marin,M: 'White does not feel like letting the bishop emerge on the a6-f1 diagonal.' White does not feel like letting the bishop emerge on the a6-f1 diagonal.} (17. c5 bxc5 $5 $146 ({Marin,M: 'The extravagant' The extravagant} 17... Nh4 $6 18. Nc4 Rf4 $6 {Marin,M: 'failed to generate any concrete threats and left White free hands on the queenside:' failed to generate any concrete threats and left White free hands on the queenside:} 19. Rc1 Bf8 20. a4 Rb8 21. Nb5 Rxe4 22. Nxa7 Qe8 23. Nxc8 Rxc8 24. Qd3 Qg6 25. cxd6 cxd6 26. Nxe5 $1 $18 {Brenninkmeijer,J (2475)-Van Wely,L (2445)/Wijk aan Zee 1990/CBM 017 (1-0, 32)}) 18. bxc5 Ba6 19. Re1 h5 {[%csl Gg4]} 20. Qa4 Bd3 21. Nb3 Nf4 {Marin,M: 'Black's threats start looking more concrete. White should find a way to harrass the intruding bishop.' Black's threats start looking more concrete. White should find a way to harrass the intruding bishop.} 22. Red1 Bc2 23. Rd2 Bxb3 24. axb3 h4 25. Qc6 Qf6 $132 {Marin,M: 'The strong threat of ...Nxg2, followed by ...Qf3+ and either ...g3 or ...h3 is not easy to parry.' The strong threat of ...xg2, followed by ...f3+ and either ...g3 or ...h3 is not easy to parry.}) 17... a5 $1 {Marin,M: 'The natural recation, fighting back for space, although the blockade is impossible to maintain.' The natural recation, fighting back for space, although the blockade is impossible to maintain.} 18. bxa5 Rxa5 19. Nb3 Ra6 20. a5 bxa5 21. c5 (21. Nxa5 c5 {Marin,M: 'closes the queenside.' closes the queenside.}) 21... Nf4 22. Nxa5 h5 23. Nc4 Rxa1 24. Qxa1 Qf6 25. cxd6 cxd6 {Marin,M: 'A critical moment of the game. Each side has its trumps. White's strategic advantage on the queenside is obvious, but Black maintains tactical threats on the kingside.' A critical moment of the game. Each side has its trumps. White's strategic advantage on the queenside is obvious, but Black maintains tactical threats on the kingside.} 26. Nb6 {Marin,M: 'Possibly, the safest continuation, maintaining the overall coordination and preparing the thematic exchange of the c8-bishop. Despite the engines' initial optimism, each of the ways of continuing the queenside attack has a hidden drawback.' Possibly, the safest continuation, maintaining the overall coordination and preparing the thematic exchange of the c8-bishop. Despite the engines' initial optimism, each of the ways of continuing the queenside attack has a hidden drawback.} (26. Qa3 $6 {[%csl Gf1] Marin,M: 'leaves the f1-rook undefended, allowing a favourable form of the sacrifice on g2.' leaves the f1-rook undefended, allowing a favourable form of the sacrifice on g2.} Nxg2 $1 27. Kxg2 $2 Qf3+ 28. Kg1 g3 $1 {[%cal Gc8h3]} 29. Nd2 gxh2+ 30. Kxh2 Qh3+ 31. Kg1 Qg4+ 32. Kh2 Qh4+ 33. Kg1 Bh3 34. Ne2 Rf3 $3 35. Nxf3 Qg4+ 36. Ng3 Qxf3 $19) (26. Rb1 {Marin,M: 'simply abandons the kingside to Black's mercy.' simply abandons the kingside to Black's mercy.} Nxg2 $1 27. Kxg2 Qf3+ 28. Kf1 (28. Kg1 $2 g3 $19) 28... h4 $40 {[%cal Gg4g3]}) (26. Nb5 {Marin,M: 'leaves the e2-square undefended, allowing' leaves the e2-square undefended, allowing} g3 $5 (26... Nxg2 $2 {Marin,M: 'does not work out well now:' does not work out well now:} 27. Kxg2 Qf3+ 28. Kg1 g3 29. fxg3 $18) 27. hxg3 Ne2+ 28. Kh2 (28. Kh1 $2 Nxg3+ $1) 28... h4 29. gxh4 gxh4 30. Nbxd6 h3 $40) 26... h4 27. Qd1 Qf7 28. Nxc8 Rxc8 29. Bd2 Qd7 {Marin,M: 'Black has eliminated a dangerous attacking piece (Bc8), but Black has perfect coordination now and is out of strategic dangers.' Black has eliminated a dangerous attacking piece (c8), but Black has perfect coordination now and is out of strategic dangers.} 30. g3 Nh3+ 31. Kg2 Rf8 32. f3 Qa7 $6 {Marin,M: 'Slightly over optymistic.' Slightly over optymistic.} (32... hxg3 33. hxg3 gxf3+ 34. Rxf3 g4 35. Rxf8+ Bxf8 {Marin,M: 'should reach for equality.' should reach for equality.} 36. Nb5 Be7 {[%cal Ge7g5]} 37. Nc7 $5 Kh8 (37... Qxc7 38. Qxg4+ $16) 38. Ne6 Qa7 39. Qe1 Qa6 $132) 33. gxh4 $6 {Marin,M: 'Unnecessarily freeing the knight.' Unnecessarily freeing the knight.} (33. Nb5 Qc5 34. Nxd6 $1 Qxd6 35. fxg4 Nf4+ 36. gxf4 exf4 37. Qe2 Be5 $14 {Marin,M: 'White may well have no chance to win this, but Black definitely is on the defending side.' White may well have no chance to win this, but Black definitely is on the defending side.}) 33... Nf4+ 34. Bxf4 $6 (34. Kh1 $5) 34... exf4 $17 {Marin,M: 'The bishop becomes a monster now.' The bishop becomes a monster now.} 35. e5 Bxe5 36. Ne4 Qh7 37. Nd2 (37. hxg5 Qh3+ 38. Kg1 g3 39. Qe2 Rb8 $40 {[%cal Gb8b2]}) 37... Qxh4 38. fxg4 f3+ 39. Nxf3 Qxg4+ 40. Kh1 Qe4 41. Kg1 Bc3 42. h3 Rf5 43. Rf2 Kg7 44. Kg2 Bf6 45. Qc2 Qxd5 46. Qc7+ Kh6 47. Qd7 Bd4 48. Rf1 Qa2+ 49. Kg3 Be3 50. Kg4 Rf6 51. Nh4 Qc4+ 0-1

