1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 {White has won some space, but Black can evolve counterplay - that's the French Defence.} 5. Nce2 {[%cal Gc2c3] Intending to keep the pawn chain, though slowing down the development. This move is having a revival it seems.} (5. f4 {is main line.}) 5... c5 6. c3 cxd4 {Black wants to start to undermine the opponent's centre with f6, but he can not to do it immediately due f4.} ({The normal} 6... Nc6 {allows White to build up the pawn chain on the kingside too; e.g.:} 7. f4 Qb6 8. Nf3 f6 9. g3 $5 Be7 10. Bh3 {[%cal Gh3e6]}) ({Therefore a more sophisticated try is} 6... Qb6 {if we want to compare the example here above.} 7. f4 Be7 8. Nf3 f6 9. g3 $6 (9. f5 $5) 9... O-O {[%csl Rd4,Re5,Rf2,Rf3][%cal Gf6e5,Gc5d4] and} 10. Bh3 {[%cal Gh3e6,Yb6e6] makes no sense.}) (6... f6 $2 7. Nf4 {[%cal Gf4e6,Gd1h5]}) 7. cxd4 f6 8. exf6 {To give the centre back is safe and not bad at least at this moment.} ({After} 8. Nf4 {can Black solve his problems with} Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ ({or} 9... Qb6) 10. Qxd2 Qe7) ({And after} 8. f4 {(in the spirit of the White's plan, but it looks risky to me). The White Lady could find herself in Black's preparation because she had already played 8.f4...} fxe5 (8... Be7) 9. fxe5 Bb4+ (9... Qh4+ $5) 10. Kf2 O-O+ 11. Nf3 Nc6 12. a3 Ndxe5 (12... Qh4+ $1 13. Kg1 Rxf3 14. gxf3 Ndxe5 15. Ng3 Nxd4 16. axb4 Nexf3+ 17. Kg2 e5 $40 {1/2 Nikel,B (2289)-Siewert,W (2278)/Chessfriend.com 2005/Corr 2008 (49)}) 13. axb4 Qh4+ 14. Kg1 Nxf3+ 15. gxf3 Rxf3 16. Ng3 Nxd4 17. Bg2 Rf7 $44 {1-0 Polgar,J (2658)-Hernandez,G (2560)/Merida 2000/CBM 078 (32)} (17... Ne2+ $5)) 8... Nxf6 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Nc3 {[%csl Re5] There is a hole on e5. However, Black should be more or less ok, if this  moves up and then down...} Bd6 11. Bd3 O-O 12. O-O Qe8 {Of course, Black has many moves here... and the choice of Mr Korchnoi would not surprise anybody from his fan club and never ever me. I like his aggressive style. And the flank operation is logical with the closed centre. (Black only can decide to open it with e6-e5, but will not want in the chosen setup.)} ({Another setup - to rid of the weak e-pawn and move} 12... e5 $5 {is possible naturally, though it' just play for draw. And White is a bit better.} 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Be2 $14 {[%csl Rd5]}) 13. Re1 Qh5 14. Be2 (14. h3 {is more common.}) 14... Qg6 15. Ng5 $6 {This looks like a waste of time.} (15. a3) 15... h6 $1 16. Bd3 Qe8 $6 ({Why not} 16... Qh5 $1 17. Qxh5 (17. Bh7+ Kh8 18. Qxh5 Nxh5 19. Bg6 hxg5 20. Bxh5 Nxd4 {is pretty ok for Black.}) 17... Nxh5 18. Nxe6 Re8 19. Bg6 Rxe6 20. Rxe6 Bxe6 21. Bxh5 Nxd4 $11) 17. Nf3 Qh5 18. h3 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Gh6g5] Interesting, we have the same position as after 14.h3, but with the pawn on h6. Anyway I hardly can extrapolate that this was the idea of one of the players...} Bd7 19. a3 {White has the better pawn stucture, so Black has to act...} g5 $5 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Gg5g4] You can shout: "wow!", but it is not so criminal (maybe just a little). Remember, the centre is closed...} 20. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Gb5d6,Yb5d4] Ouch, Judit found an exact sequence to solve her problems with chances to claim an advantage.} (20. Qc2 Kg7 21. Nb5 (21. Ne5 $2 Nxd4) {allows} 21... g4 $13) 20... Bb8 (20... g4 $143 21. Nxd6 gxf3 22. Bc2 {[%cal Gd1d3]} (22. Be3 $5 {[%cal Yd1h5,Rf3g2] and the white  is not in real trouble yet.})) 21. Qc2 $1 {Avoiding  exchange} Kg7 22. Ne5 $1 {No more g5-g4.} Nxe5 23. dxe5 Ne4 24. Bxe4 Bxb5 25. Qb3 $6 (25. a4 $142 $1 $16 {[%cal Ya1a3]} Bd7 (25... Bc6 26. Bd3 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Yc2h7]}) (25... Ba6 26. Bf3 $1 Rxf3 27. gxf3 {[%csl Ra8,Rb8,Rg7][%cal Ga1a3]}) 26. Bd3 {[%cal Yc2h7]} (26. Bf3 $5 Rxf3 $5 27. gxf3 Qxh3 28. Ra3 {[%csl Ra8,Rb8,Rg7]})) 25... dxe4 $2 ({Black coud play the very strong move} 25... a6 $142 $1 {[%cal Ga6b5,Yb8a7]} 26. a4 {[%csl Rb4]} Bc4 $1 27. Qxb7+ Qf7 {[%cal Gf7f2]} 28. Qxf7+ Rxf7 29. Bf3 Ba7 30. Be3 Rb8 $44) (25... Bc4 $143 26. Qxb7+ Qf7 27. Qxf7+ Rxf7 28. Bf3 a5 29. Bd2 $14) 26. Qxb5 Qf7 27. Be3 a6 28. Qb4 Bxe5 29. Qxe4 $16 {[%csl Re6,Rg7]} Bf6 30. Rad1 Rad8 31. Bc5 $1 Rfe8 32. Rxd8 Rxd8 (32... Bxd8 33. Bd4+ Bf6 34. Bxf6+ Kxf6 35. Re3 {[%csl Rf6]}) 33. Qxe6 {Winning the e6 and making the transition to an endgame.} Qxe6 34. Rxe6 $16 {White is a pawn up, however she still has a long way to go before signing the score sheets. It would be good to exchange the rooks for her. Black should try to exchange the pawns on the queenside and possibly the bishops (under reasonable circumstances).} Rd5 (34... Bxb2 $5 {[%csl Ra3,Ra8][%cal Ra3a8] with the drawish idea White , a3,  /Black } 35. Re7+ Kg6 36. Rxb7 {[%cal Gb7b2,Gb7b6,Yb6a6,Yb6h6]} Rd1+ 37. Kh2 Be5+ $1 {tempo} 38. g3 Rd5 39. Rb6+ Kf5 40. Rc6 (40. Be3 Rd6) (40. Bf8 Rd2) 40... h5 {and Black has improved the material structure on the board. The question is if his pawns are not too weak now...} 41. Kg2 g4 (41... a5 42. Be3 Bd6 (42... g4 $2 43. Rh6) 43. a4 g4 44. hxg4+ (44. Bb6 $5) 44... hxg4 45. Rc4 Rd3 46. Bb6 Rd5) 42. hxg4+ hxg4 43. a4 a5 (43... Bf6 $5 44. Be3 Ra5 45. Rc4 Bg5 46. Bc5) 44. Be3 Bf6 45. Rc4 Bg5 46. Bb6 Bh6 {White is again still very far from the victory.}) 35. b4 $1 Rd1+ 36. Kh2 h5 37. g4 $1 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Yc5e3,Ye3g5]} hxg4 38. hxg4 Rd3 39. a4 Kf7 40. Rb6 {It's always right to drag the opponents piece to a passive positon.} Rd7 41. Kg2 Bd8 42. Rh6 Kg7 43. Re6 Kf7 44. Re5 Bf6 45. Re3 Bd8 46. a5 $1 {[%csl Ra6,Rb7,Yc5] Black can not exchange the queenside pawns and the white  has a strong outpost now.} Rd5 47. Kf3 $1 Bf6 48. Ke4 $1 {[%csl Ye4]  belongs in the centre!} Ke6 49. Rh3 Rd7 50. Rh1 Kf7 51. Rh7+ Bg7 52. Rh3 Ke6 53. Rd3 $1 $18 {[%cal Gd3d7,Yd3d6]} Rxd3 $8 (53... Rc7 54. Rd6+ Kf7 55. Kf5) 54. Kxd3 Kd5 55. Be7 Bh6 56. f3 Ke6 57. Bc5 Bg7 58. Be3 Bf6 59. Kc4 Kd6 60. f4 gxf4 61. Bxf4+ Ke6 62. g5 {Please imagine this position without the b-pawns. Black could draw by taking on g5. Look the line 34...xb2.} Bd8 63. Be3 Bc7 64. g6 Kf6 65. Kd5 Bg3 66. Bc5 {[%cal Gg6g7,Gd5e6,Ge6d7,Gd7c8]} 1-0

