1. e4 {Rogozenco,D Rogozenko} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. c3 Bg7 12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nc2 O-O 14. Nce3 Be6 15. g4 {An old move, appeared in 1976 and at the beginning considered to give White advantage. The intention to prevent f7-f5 is logical, but the drawbacks are obvious too - White weakens too much his own structure and will have problems with the king in all tactical complications.} b4 $1 {Most energetic. Black immediately starts counterplay on the queenside. Moves 15...Qh4 or 15...Ne7 are good as well, but the text is strongest.} 16. Bg2 (16. cxb4 Rb8 17. a3 a5 18. b5 Nd4 19. a4 f5 20. gxf5 Bxd5 21. Nxd5 Rxf5 $36 {0-1 Rodriguez-Jussupow, Amsterdam 1978 (79)}) (16. Nxb4 Nxb4 17. cxb4 e4 18. Qd2 Qf6 $36 {½-½ Sieberg,R-Gromotka,E/W-ch M495 corr 1990 (41)}) 16... bxc3 17. bxc3 Rc8 18. Qd3 $146 (18. O-O Ne7 19. Nxe7+ Qxe7 20. Qd3 Qg5 $17 {[%csl Rc3,Rg4][%cal Ge5e4]}) 18... e4 $5 (18... Ne7 {is also good for Black.}) 19. Bxe4 (19. Qxe4 $2 Bxd5 $19 20. Qxd5 (20. Nxd5 Re8 $19) 20... Bxc3+ 21. Ke2 Nd4+ $19) 19... Ne5 20. Qe2 $2 (20. Qxa6 {This was better for the simple reason that 20.Qe2 does not protect pawn g4 anyway. With 20.Qxa6 White not only takes the pawn, but also keeps square e2 available for the king.} Nxg4 (20... Re8 21. h3 (21. O-O Nxg4 22. Nxg4 Bxg4 $44) 21... Nc4 22. O-O Nd2 23. Bxh7+ $1 Kh8 (23... Kxh7 24. Qd3+) 24. Bc2 Nxf1 25. Qxf1 $13) 21. Nxg4 Bxg4 $44 (21... Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Bxc3+ 23. Ke2 Re8+ 24. Ne3 Bxa1 25. Rxa1 Rc2+ {[%cal Gd8f6]} 26. Kd3 Rxf2 {in spite of the exposed king on d3, White's chances are preferable in this complicated position. He can bring the queen to d4 after 27.Qa4, also start an attack with Ra1-g1.})) 20... Nxg4 $1 21. Rg1 (21. Nxg4 Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Bxc3+ {and because the king cannot step on e2, Black will simply take on a1 next, remaining an exchange up.}) 21... Nxe3 22. Qxe3 Kh8 $17 {The material is equal, only that White's pawn weaknesses are more vulnerable and his king will always be under attack. A complete failure of White's opening strategy.} 23. Kf1 (23. Rxg7 Kxg7 24. Qd4+ f6 $19) 23... Qh4 24. Rd1 Rfe8 25. Qg3 Bh3+ 26. Bg2 Qc4+ 27. Qd3 Qxd3+ 28. Rxd3 Bf5 {Of course Black has a huge advantage in all variations, but here he could win an exchange right away:} (28... Bd7 $1 29. Re3 (29. c4 Bb5 $1 $19) 29... Bb5+ 30. Ke1 Bh6 31. Rxe8+ Rxe8+ 32. Ne3 (32. Kd1 Ba4# {a nice checkmate!}) 32... Bxe3 33. fxe3 Rxe3+ $19) 29. Re3 Rxe3 30. fxe3 Rb8 31. e4 (31. Kf2 Rb2+ 32. Kf3 Rxa2 $19) 31... Be6 32. Bf3 Rb1+ 33. Kf2 Rb2+ 34. Be2 Rxa2 35. h4 a5 36. h5 h6 37. Ke3 Bxd5 38. exd5 Bxc3 {The win is a matter of time. White cannot create counterplay at all because of his "bad" bishop.} 39. Bb5 Rb2 40. Be8 Rb7 41. Bc6 {On c6 the bishop guards pawn a5, but it is cut off from the kingside, where Black will make progress soon by activating the king.} Re7+ 42. Kf4 Re5 43. Rh1 Kg7 44. Rg1+ Kf8 45. Rh1 Ke7 46. Kf3 Bb4 47. Rh2 Kf6 48. Kf4 Rf5+ 49. Kg4 Rg5+ 50. Kf4 Bc3 51. Ke4 Rg4+ 52. Kf3 Rb4 53. Rc2 Bd4 54. Be8 Rb8 55. Bc6 Kg5 56. Rg2+ (56. Rh2 Rb3+ 57. Ke4 Bc5 $19 {[%cal Gb3e3]}) 56... Kxh5 57. Kf4 Be5+ 58. Kf5 Kh4 59. Ra2 Rb4 60. Rf2 Kg3 61. Rf1 Kg2 0-1

