1. e4 {Rogozenco,D Rogozenko} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 Bg7 11. c3 f5 12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nc2 O-O 14. Nce3 Be6 15. Bd3 f5 16. O-O {This move is relatively new. It was first met in the game Ivanchuk-Kramnik/London PCA rapid/1994 and now 16.0-0 became one of the main continuations here. The alternatives are 16.Bc2 and 16.Qh5.} Kh8 $5 $146 {Since the above mentioned game a lot of other games were played, but nobody played this.} (16... e4 17. Nf4 (17. Bc2 $2 f4) 17... Bf7 18. Bc2 Be5 19. Nfd5 Qg5 $5 $13 {Anand,V-Kramnik,V/Linares/1998}) (16... Ra7 17. Qh5 Raf7 18. f4 $146 (18. g4 {is probably dubious, as it allows Black to create a strong attack, e.g.} e4 19. Bc2 Ne5 20. Nf4 Bd7 21. Bb3 fxg4 22. Ne6 Bxe6 23. Bxe6 Qf6 24. Bxf7+ Rxf7 25. h3 Qe6 26. hxg4 Rf6 27. Kg2 Nd3 28. Qh4 Nf4+ 29. Kg1 h5 30. g5 Nh3+ 31. Kh1 Rf3 32. Qxh5 Nxf2+ 33. Kg2 Rh3 34. Qe2 Qe5 35. Rg1 Rh2+ 36. Kf1 Nh3 37. Qg4 Nxg1 38. Kxg1 Rxb2 39. Qc8+ Bf8 40. Qh3 Qxg5+ 41. Kh1 Qh6 $19 {Svidler,P-Kasparov,G/ICC blitz/1998}) 18... Ne7 19. Nxe7+ Qxe7 20. Kh1 (20. fxe5 Qa7 21. Rae1 dxe5 22. Kh1 e4 23. Bb1 $13) 20... e4 21. Bc2 b4 $5 $13 {Asrian,K-Rogozenko,D/Elista (ol)/1998} (21... d5 22. Bb3 Qc5 23. Qe2 a5 $15)) 17. Qh5 (17. Bc2 {also comes into consideration.}) 17... Bf7 18. Qh3 e4 19. Bc2 Ne5 20. f3 $6 {Now after a forced line Black achieves better prospects.} (20. Rad1 {was necessary. Only the practice can give a more or less precise evaluation to this complicated position.}) 20... exf3 21. gxf3 Bxd5 22. Nxd5 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 (23. Qg2 Qxg2+ 24. Kxg2 $11) 23... Qd2 24. Bb3 $6 (24. Nb4 a5 25. Rad1 Qe2 26. Rde1 Qd2 $11) 24... Nd3 $1 (24... Qxb2 {was also possible, but the game move looks stronger.}) 25. Qh5 $2 (25. Qh4 Qxb2 $17) 25... Rae8 26. Qh4 Qxb2 $19 27. Rad1 Qe2 28. Nf4 {What else? The Knight on d3 must be exchanged, but Black has enough material to win the game.} Nxf4 29. Qxf4 Bxc3 30. Qg3 Be5 31. Qh3 Qe3 32. Rg1 Rf6 33. Rde1 (33. Rg2 Ref8 {34. ... Rh6 -+.}) 33... Rh6 34. Rxe3 Rxh3 35. Re2 (35. Rg2 Rc8 $19) 35... Rxf3 36. Reg2 Rxb3 $6 {This was not necessary. Now White has some hopes to save the game.} (36... h6 37. Rg8+ (37. Rg6 Rh3 38. R1g2 a5 $19) 37... Rxg8 38. Rxg8+ Kh7 39. Ra8 Rf2 {is winning, of course.}) 37. axb3 a5 38. Rc1 $6 (38. Ra2 Ra8 39. Rc1 $17) (38. Rd1 a4 39. bxa4 bxa4 40. Ra2 Ra8 41. Rd3 $17) 38... a4 39. bxa4 bxa4 40. Ra2 a3 41. Rf1 Bb2 42. Rxf5 Kg7 43. Rd5 (43. Kg2 Rf8 $1) 43... Re6 44. Kg2 Rf6 $1 {Very good move, cutting the King.} 45. Rd3 Kf7 46. Kg3 {Sooner or later White will have to take on a3 and it seems that there is no real difference if doing it now or later, as in the game.} (46. Raxa3 Bxa3 47. Rxa3 Ke6 48. Ra7 h6 $19) (46. Rf3 Rxf3 47. Kxf3 d5 48. Kf4 Kf6 49. h4 h5 $19) 46... Ke6 47. Kg4 Ke5 48. Re3+ (48. Kg5 h6+ 49. Kh5 d5 $19) 48... Kd4 49. Rexa3 Bxa3 50. Rxa3 d5 $19 {Black is too active, so the position is easy winning.} 51. Ra7 h6 52. h4 Ke5 53. Re7+ Kd6 54. Ra7 Ke5 55. Re7+ Re6 56. Rf7 d4 57. Kf3 Kd5 58. Rd7+ Kc4 59. Rc7+ Kb3 60. Rd7 Kc3 61. Rc7+ Kd2 0-1

