1. e4 {Psakhis,L Psakhis,Lev Psakhis,Lev Psakhis,Lev Psakhis,Lev: 'Psakhis Psakhis '} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. Qg4 Kf8 {Nikolic,s speciality. He played this move against strongest chessplayers ( Kasparov is included) with reasonable results. We decided not to follow main lines ( 8.h4, 8.a4) and to choose rare continuation.} 8. Bd2 Qc7 {N/ New and may be good move. Nikolic likes to put Q on c7 in many variations in French defence.} (8... Qa5 9. a4 Nbc6 10. Nf3 Qb6 11. dxc5 Qxc5 12. Bd3 h6 13. O-O Qb6 14. Rfe1 $14 {½-½ Short,N-Nikolic,Pr/London PCA 5' 1994 (49)}) (8... Qb6 9. dxc5 Qc7 (9... Qb2 10. Qd1 Nd7 11. Nf3 Nxc5 12. c4 Qb6 13. Be2 Bd7 14. O-O Rc8 15. cxd5 Nxd5 16. c4 $16 {1-0 Orlov,V-Karpeshov,O/Elista 1994 (36)}) 10. Nf3 Nd7 11. Be2 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 13. O-O h5 14. Qf3 Qe4 15. Qd3 Nc6 16. Rfe1 h4 17. h3 f6 18. Rab1 $16 {1-0 Kamsky,G-Ljubojevic,L/Melody Amber blind 1995 (59)}) 9. Rc1 $5 {It is not in character of Judit Polgar to worry to much about her pawns. Normal chessplayer will play 9.Bd3.} Qa5 $1 10. h4 Qxa3 11. h5 h6 12. Rh3 c4 $1 {Just in time! White's light bishop was ready to jump on d3 and later on g6.} 13. Rf3 $6 {Typical plan of attack, but not the best one. After clever} (13. Qd1 $1 Qa5 14. Ra1 Qd8 15. Bc1 {[%cal Yc1a3,Ya3f8] Judit could get reasonble compensation fora pawn.}) 13... Nbc6 $17 14. Rf4 b5 {Sergey Dolmatov, coach of Kramnik, told me now " You can stop to worry, she has nothing to lose already ". It was not so easy to stop to worry, but, unfortunately, he was right in estimation of  position.} 15. Qf3 Nd8 16. g4 ({And now desevrs attention} 16. Qd1 Qa5 17. Ra1 Qb6 18. Bc1 a5 19. Ba3 b4 $140 $2 20. Bxb4) 16... Ke8 17. Nh3 Rb8 (17... a5 $5 18. Rb1 (18. Qd1 Nec6) 18... Bd7 19. Qd1 Nc8) 18. g5 (18. Qd1 Qa6 19. Ra1 Qb6 20. Bc1 a5) 18... Nf5 19. Bg2 (19. Rxf5 $5 exf5 20. gxh6 gxh6 21. Qxd5 $17) 19... a5 $1 20. gxh6 Nxh6 21. Qg3 Kf8 (21... Qf8 22. Ra1 Nc6) (21... Nf5 $143 22. Rxf5 exf5 23. Qxg7 Rxh5 24. Nf4 $132) 22. Rf3 Nc6 (22... Nf5 $5) 23. Ng5 Rb7 24. Bh3 Nd8 {Nikolic defended all squares around his king,his pawns are ready to move, White needs to do something quickly.I was sure that Judit will play now 25.Ke2 with idea to use passive rook c1 in attack, but she choose strange..} 25. Qg1 {[%cal Ye1e2,Yc1a1]} Ng8 26. Bg4 Qb2 27. Nh3 {[%cal Rh3f4,Rf4g6]} b4 ({After the game Judit show me fanny variation} 27... Nc6 28. Ke2 Ke8 (28... Nge7 $5) 29. Bf5 Nge7 (29... Kf8 30. Rb1 Qa3 31. Ra1 Qe7 32. Bg6 $132) 30. Qxg7 $1 Rg8 $2 31. Qxf7+ $3 Kxf7 32. Bg6+ Kg7 33. Rf7+ Kh8 34. Rh7# {but this is just a dream.}) 28. Nf4 Ne7 29. Bh3 (29. Ng6+ Nxg6 30. hxg6 b3) 29... b3 (29... bxc3 $5 30. Rxc3 (30. Be3 $5) 30... Ndc6) 30. Ke2 (30. cxb3 cxb3 31. Nd3 Qa2) ({I think, that stronger was} 30. Qg5 $5 {with many tactical possibilities} Ke8 (30... Kg8 $6 31. Rg3 Rh7 32. h6 g6 33. Nh5 $40) (30... a4 $2 31. Ng6+ $1 $18) 31. Qxg7 Rg8 32. Qh7 Rg1+ 33. Bf1 a4) 30... a4 31. Ng6+ (31. cxb3 axb3) (31. Qg5 $5) 31... Nxg6 32. Qxg6 Qa3 (32... Kg8 33. Qg5 Nc6 34. Rg1 Rh7 35. Rfg3 Qxc2 36. Qf6 Kf8 37. Rxg7 Qd3+ 38. Ke1 $132) 33. h6 {Finally White manage to organize strong attack, but, unfortunately black pawns too strong.} (33. Rg1 Ke8 34. Qxg7 Qf8 35. h6 b2 $19) 33... Rxh6 $1 34. Bxh6 gxh6 35. Qf6 (35. Rg1 Ke8) (35. Qxh6+ Ke8 36. Rg1 Qf8) 35... Ke8 36. Rg1 Qf8 {At that moment each player had 1-1,5 minutes left. Honestly, I was completely desperate and thought already what I have to say to Judit after game. But she doesn't lose her head and admit really brave decision-kihg must to block pawns himself!} (36... Qe7 $5 37. cxb3 (37. Qh8+ Kd7 38. Rg8 Kc7) 37... cxb3 38. Kd2 a3 39. Qxe7+ Kxe7 40. Kc1) (36... Qf8) 37. cxb3 $1 cxb3 38. Kd2 $1 b2 (38... a3 39. Kc1 a2 40. Kb2 Ra7 41. Ka1) 39. Kc2 a3 40. Kb1 a2+ $2 {Last move in time troubles and very serious mistake.} ({Black could easy win after} 40... Bd7 {For example} 41. c4 (41. Rfg3 a2+ 42. Kxa2 Ra7+ 43. Kxb2 Qa3+ 44. Kc2 Qa2+ 45. Kd3 Bb5+ 46. Ke3 Qe2+ 47. Kf4 Qe4# {Matt!}) 41... a2+ 42. Kxa2 b1=Q+ $1 (42... Ra7+ 43. Kxb2 Qb4+ 44. Rb3 Qd2+ 45. Kb1 Qa2+ 46. Kc1 Qa1+ 47. Kc2 Ra2+ 48. Kd3 dxc4+ 49. Ke3 Qxg1 50. Qh8+ Ke7 51. Qf6+ Kf8 52. Qh8+ (52. Qxd8+ Be8 $19) 52... Qg8 53. Qxg8+ Kxg8 54. Rb8 Kf8 $1 55. Rxd8+ Ke7 {[%csl Ga2][%cal Ya2a3,Ya3h3]}) 43. Rxb1 Ra7+ 44. Kb3 (44. Kb2 Qb4+ 45. Kc1 Qe1+ 46. Kc2 Ra2+ 47. Rb2 Qe2+ $19) 44... Qa3+ 45. Kc2 Ba4+ 46. Kd2 Qa2+ 47. Ke3 Qxb1 48. Qh8+ Kd7 49. Bxe6+ fxe6 50. Qg7+ Kc8 51. Qxa7 Qc1+ 52. Kd3 dxc4+ $19) 41. Kxa2 Ra7+ $6 {Second mistake and now White's chances are better!} ({Better was} 41... Bd7 $1 42. Rb1 Ra7+ 43. Kxb2 Qa3+ 44. Kc2 Ba4+ 45. Kd3 Qa2 46. Rg1 Qc2+ 47. Ke3 {and Black have draw but maybe nothing more.}) 42. Kxb2 Qa3+ 43. Kc2 Qa2+ 44. Kd3 Qc4+ 45. Ke3 Qxc3+ 46. Kf4 Qxd4+ 47. Kg3 {According to material balance Black is OK, but knight and bishop are very passive.} Re7 48. Bf1 $1 {[%cal Rf1b5]} (48. Rc1 Ba6 49. Rfc3 Bc4) 48... Qb2 49. Bd3 Nc6 50. Re3 d4 51. Rf3 {Simply and strong.} (51. Be4 Nb4 (51... dxe3 52. Bxc6+ Kd8 53. Rd1+ Kc7 54. Qxe7+ Kxc6 55. Qd6+ Kb7 56. fxe3 $18) 52. Rd1 Rc7) 51... Qb8 52. Re1 Kd7 53. Kh3 {Black is hopeless now.All white pieces will take part in final hunt.} Qb4 54. Rc1 $1 Qa4 (54... Qb6 55. Be4 Bb7 56. Rb1 Qa7) 55. Be4 Bb7 56. Rd3 Qa7 57. Rc4 $18 Qa1 (57... Qa8 58. Rb3) 58. Bxc6+ Bxc6 59. Rdxd4+ (59. Rdxd4+ Bd5 60. Rxd5+ exd5 61. Qd6+ Ke8 62. Rc8# {Nice end (for a White!) of long and dramatical fight.}) 1-0

