1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qd6 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Ne5 Nbd7 7. Nc4 Qc7 8. a4 g6 9. g3 Bg7 10. Bg2 Nb6 11. Nxb6 axb6 {RR: 'N'} (11... Qxb6 {Ü 106/32}) 12. Bf4 Qd8 13. O-O Be6 (13... O-O {Ü 107/(31)}) 14. Re1 Qd7 (14... O-O {Ü 107/(31)}) 15. Qd3 {RR: '!?'} (15. Be5 O-O {Ü 107/(31)}) 15... O-O 16. Rad1 Nd5 (16... Rfe8 17. Be5 Rad8 $13) 17. Nxd5 Bxd5 18. b3 Bxg2 19. Kxg2 Rfd8 20. Qe4 Bf6 21. h4 h5 22. c4 {The same amount of pieces is left on board as in my Alekhine analysis. White's space advantage is less obvious for the time being, but Black's structure is damaged.} b5 {Black uses the same break as in my analysis and, although here it does not offer counterplay along the b-file, it allows the simplification of the position.} 23. axb5 cxb5 24. d5 Rac8 25. Be3 bxc4 26. bxc4 {RR: ''} Qa4 27. Bb6 Rd7 28. c5 Qxe4+ 29. Rxe4 {White's space advantage is bigger than in the Alekhine line already, but Black has managed to trade queens.} e6 30. d6 Bd8 $1 {Solving Black's main problem, the uselessness of his bishop.} 31. Bxd8 Rdxd8 32. Re5 Rd7 33. Rb1 {[%cal Gg2f3,Gf3f4,Gf2f3,Gg3g4]} f6 $1 {A well timed pawn sacrifice, forcing a draw.} 34. Rxe6 Rxc5 35. Rxf6 Kg7 36. Re6 Kf7 37. Rbe1 Rc6 38. Re7+ Rxe7 39. Rxe7+ Kf6 40. Rxb7 Rxd6 41. Kf3 Rd2 42. Ke3 Ra2 43. Rd7 Ra5 44. Rd6+ Kf5 45. f3 Ra3+ 46. Rd3 Ra4 47. Rd5+ Kf6 48. Rd4 Ra3+ 49. Ke4 Ra6 50. g4 Re6+ 51. Kf4 hxg4 52. fxg4 g5+ 53. hxg5+ Kg6 1/2-1/2

