1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Anand,Viswanathan} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. Bb3 Be6 11. Bg5 Nd7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Nd5 Qd8 14. Qe2 Nc5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?!' Anand,Viswanathan: '[פc6]'} (14... Na5 {Anand,Viswanathan} 15. Rad1 Rc8 (15... Nxb3 16. axb3 Bxd5 17. Rxd5 Nf6 18. Rd3 Qc7 19. Rfd1 Rfd8) 16. Ne3 Nxb3 17. axb3 Rc6) 15. Rad1 {Anand has a compact opening repertoire, so finding an advantage is not easy. Polgar has made a sensible choice, Black has to fight for complete equality.} (15. Rfd1 {(Anand,Viswanathan) Ü 98/182}) 15... b5 {Anand,Viswanathan: 'N'} (15... f5 16. exf5 Rxf5 17. Ne3 Rf7 18. Nc4 Nxb3 19. axb3 Bxc4 20. Qxc4 Qf6 21. Qd5 Rd8 22. c3 h6 23. Nd2 Kh8 24. Ne4 Qg6 {1/2 Solodovnichenko,Y (2537)-Czakon,J (2484)/Castelldefels 2006 (79)}) 16. Ne3 {Anand,Viswanathan: '!'} Nxb3 (16... Nxe4 17. Qd3 Ng5 18. Nxg5 Qxg5 19. Bxe6 fxe6 20. Qxd6 Nd4 21. c3 $14) 17. axb3 Qc7 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?'} (17... Qe7 {Anand,Viswanathan} 18. Rd3 Rfd8 19. Rfd1 Rd7 (19... Nb4 20. R3d2 Nc6 $14) 20. Qd2 Rad8 21. Ra1 Ra7 22. Nd5 Qb7 $14) 18. Rd3 Nb4 {Black is hurrying with the knight move as otherwise the white queen would complicate the counterplay.} (18... Rfd8 19. Qd2 $14 (19. Rfd1 {Anand,Viswanathan} Nb4 20. R3d2 Nc6 21. Ng5 $36)) 19. Rd2 Qe7 (19... f6 20. Rfd1 Rfd8 21. h4 $14) 20. Rfd1 Rad8 21. Nf5 $6 {Anand,Viswanathan: '!' The idea of forcing the bishop to take on f5 is not bad, but somewhat better chances were promised by the more standard move to d5.} (21. Ra1 Qb7 22. c3 Nc6 23. Nd5 $14) 21... Bxf5 22. exf5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '[ Qe4]'} Rfe8 23. c3 Nc6 24. Qe4 Na5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?!'} (24... Qc7 25. h3 Na5 26. Qb4 Nc6 27. Qg4 $14) 25. Qd5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '!'} Qf6 $6 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?' Anand was struggling to get his tournament going and this dubious decision reflects his desire to fight. It was objectively better was to equalise with the help of the 25...e4! advance.} (25... e4 $1 26. Nd4 (26. Re2 {Anand,Viswanathan} Qb7 27. Qxb7 Nxb7 28. Nd4 d5 29. b4 Nd6 $14) 26... Qe5 27. Nc2 Qxd5 28. Rxd5 Nxb3 29. Nb4 (29. Rxd6 {Anand,Viswanathan} Rxd6 30. Rxd6 Nc5 31. Rc6 Nd3 32. Rxa6 Nxb2 33. Nd4 Kf8 34. Nxb5 Rb8 35. Nd6 Na4 36. h4 Nxc3 37. Rc6 Rb3 $14) 29... Nc5 $11) (25... Qb7 {Anand,Viswanathan} 26. b4 (26. Nxe5 Qxd5 27. Rxd5 Nxb3 $16) 26... Qxd5 27. Rxd5 Nb7 28. Ng5 $16) 26. Re2 (26. Ra1 e4 $1 27. Rxa5 exf3 28. Rd1 fxg2 29. Rxa6 $11) 26... Rc8 (26... Qxf5 $2 27. Ra1 $18) 27. Nd2 $6 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?' We should not be too harsh on Judit, since the relocation of the knight is not bad. The sad part lies in a missed chance to punish her great opponent with the precise 27.a1! gaining a serious advantage.} (27. Ra1 $1 Rc5 (27... e4 28. Rae1 Qd8 29. g4 Qd7 30. Rxe4 Rxe4 31. Rxe4 Qc6 32. Qxc6 Nxc6 33. Kg2 $16) 28. Qd1 Nb7 29. Rxa6 Qxf5 30. b4 Rc7 31. Qd5 $16) 27... Qxf5 28. Ne4 {Anand,Viswanathan: '?!'} (28. b4 $1 Nc6 29. Ne4 Qe6 30. Red2 {Anand,Viswanathan: ''} Qxd5 31. Rxd5 Red8 32. f3 $14) 28... Qe6 29. Nxd6 Rcd8 $1 {Anand,Viswanathan: '=!' The flow of the game has changed and suddenly White has to look for ways to equalise matters.} (29... Qxd5 30. Rxd5 Rcd8 31. Red2 Rf8 32. b4 Rxd6 33. Rxd6 Nc4 34. Kf1 $14) 30. Red2 (30. Qxe6 $2 Rxe6 31. Red2 Nxb3 $17) (30. f4 $11 {Anand,Viswanathan}) 30... Nxb3 {Anand,Viswanathan: '!'} 31. Nxe8 (31. Rd3 h5 32. Nxe8 Rxd5 33. Rxd5 Qg4 $15) 31... Rxd5 32. Rxd5 h6 33. Rd8 (33. Rd6 Qf5 34. Rxa6 Qc2 35. Rad6 Qxb2 $15) (33. Nd6 {Anand,Viswanathan} Nd4 $1) (33. Nc7 {Anand,Viswanathan} Qg4 34. h3 Qe2 35. Nxa6 Nd4 36. Rf1 Ne6 37. Nb4 Nf4 38. Rd8+ Kh7 39. Nd5 $1 $11) 33... Kh7 34. Nd6 $6 {[%mdl 1024] The mislocated knight will not decide the issue, Anand was better in any line. On the other hand the idea to counterattack the f7-square will turn into a pipe dream and White will go under.} (34. Nc7 Qg6 (34... Qg4 {Anand,Viswanathan} 35. h3 (35. Nxa6 Nd4 $1) 35... Qe2 36. Nxa6 Nd4 37. Rf1 Ne6 $36) 35. R8d3 Nc5 36. Rd6 Qc2 37. R6d2 Qb3 $17) 34... Nc5 {Anand,Viswanathan: ''} (34... e4 $6 35. Re8 Qg4 36. Re1 f5 37. Nf7 $11) 35. h3 (35. g3 Qb3 36. Rd2 Na4 $17) 35... Qg6 {The other very strong idea was to assault the b2-pawn with both black pieces.} (35... Qb3 $1 36. Rd2 Na4 $17 37. h4 {Anand,Viswanathan} Nxb2 38. h5 $1 (38. Nxf7 Qxf7 39. Rxb2 Qc4 $1) 38... Na4 (38... e4 39. Nxe4 Nc4 40. R2d4 $132) 39. c4) 36. g3 (36. Nc8 Ne6 (36... e4 {Anand,Viswanathan} 37. R8d6 Qh5 (37... Qf5 38. Ne7 Qg5 39. Nd5 Nd3 $36 { Qe5, f5}) 38. R6d5 (38. R1d5 Qe2 $1 39. Rxc5 e3 $19) 38... f5 39. Nd6 Nd3 40. Rd2 Qg6 41. Nxf5 $1 Nf4 42. Ne3 Nxd5 43. Rxd5 $15) (36... Qc2 {Anand,Viswanathan} 37. R8d2 Qb3 38. Nd6) 37. R8d6 Qg5 38. Kh2 e4 $17) 36... Ne6 $1 37. Rd7 (37. Ra8 Ng5 38. h4 Nf3+ $19) 37... Ng5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '-+'} 38. h4 Qe6 {Black could already choose between three different lines with variable levels of catastrophe for White.} (38... Qc2 39. Rf1 (39. Ne8 Nh3+ 40. Kg2 Nxf2 41. R7d2 Qxd1 42. Rxd1 Nxd1 $19) 39... Nf3+ 40. Kg2 Qd3 $19) (38... Qh5 39. Ne8 Nf3+ 40. Kg2 Nd4 $19) 39. Rb7 Nf3+ 40. Kg2 (40. Kf1 Qh3+ 41. Ke2 Qg4 42. Ke3 Nxh4 $19) 40... Qg4 {Repeatedly Anand refused to contemplate the move b3, even though here it was the strongest line winning outright.} (40... Nxh4+ 41. Kf1 Qh3+ 42. Ke2 Qg4+ 43. Kd2 Nf5 44. Nxf5 Qxf5 45. Ke3 $17) (40... Qb3 $1 41. Ra1 Qd5 42. Kf1 Qxd6 $19) 41. Rd5 (41. Rh1 Nd2 42. Kh2 Qf3 43. Rxf7 Qd5) 41... Nxh4+ 42. Kf1 Qf3 43. Rd2 Nf5 {Anand,Viswanathan: '!'} 44. Nxf5 $8 (44. Rd7 Nxg3+ 45. Ke1 Qh1#) 44... Qxb7 {In the end the decision will fall in the endgame, where White has perilously little material for the enemy queen.} 45. Ke2 a5 46. Ne3 a4 47. Rd5 (47. Nd5 Kg6 48. f3 Kg5 49. Rd1 h5 $19) 47... b4 48. cxb4 (48. Rxe5 bxc3 49. bxc3 Qa6+ 50. c4 a3 $19) 48... Qxb4 49. Rd2 f5 50. Rc2 (50. Nxf5 $2 Qg4+ $19) 50... Qb3 51. f4 exf4 52. gxf4 Qb4 53. Nc4 (53. Nxf5 Qb5+ $19) 53... g5 {White has no more hopes even for resistance.} 0-1

