1. e4 {Psakhis,L Psakhis,Lev Psakhis,Lev Psakhis,Lev  Psakhis,Lev: 'Psakhis  '} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {It was correct time to start to play Panov attack, but after 5th game Judit was shocked and I ask her to play as solid as possible.} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 Ngf6 6. Bd3 e6 7. N1f3 Bd6 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Qc7 11. Qg4 Kf8 12. Be3 Nf6 $5 {N Strong move, white,s queen hasn,t chance to move to e2, what,s happen in 4th game.} (12... c5 13. dxc5 Bxc5 14. O-O-O Nf6 15. Qh4 Bxe3+ 16. fxe3 Bd7 17. Rhf1 Rc8 18. Kb1 Bc6 19. e4 Kg8 20. c3 Nd7 21. Bc2 $36 {1-0 Polgar,J-Karpov,A/Budapest m 1998 (49)}) (12... b6 13. Nd2 Nf6 14. Qe2 Nd5 15. Nc4 Ba6 16. Nxd6 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 Qxd6 18. O-O Rd8 19. Rad1 b5 20. a4 a6 $13 {½-½ Polgar,J-Karpov,A/Budapest m rapid (04) 1998 (57)}) 13. Qh4 b6 14. O-O-O Bb7 15. Rhe1 c5 $11 {I suppose, that Black has at least equal chances.} 16. dxc5 bxc5 {[%cal Ga8b8,Gc5c4]} 17. Nd2 {[%cal Gd2c4]} Nd5 (17... Bxg2 18. Rg1 $44) 18. Nc4 Nxe3 (18... Be7 $5 19. Qh3 Nxe3 20. Nxe3 Rb8 $13) 19. Nxe3 Rb8 20. Qc4 $5 {[%cal Gd3e4] White will be happy to exchanged light bishops, but immediately} (20. Be4 $2 {lose after} g5 $1 21. Qg4 h5 22. Qxg5 Bxe4 $19) 20... h5 $5 {[%cal Gd6h2]} 21. h3 (21. g3 $5) 21... Be5 22. c3 Bf6 23. Be4 Bc8 24. Qa4 (24. Rd2 $5 g6 25. Red1 Kg7 $13) 24... g6 25. Qc6 ({Deserves attention} 25. Rd2 Kg7 26. Red1 $132 {with complicated position, but Judit decided to exchanged queens- unusual decition for her!}) 25... Qxc6 26. Bxc6 Kg7 27. Nc4 {[%cal Gc4e5]} (27. Bd7 Bb7) 27... Ba6 28. Ne5 Rhd8 29. b3 (29. Nd7 Bg5+ 30. Kc2 Rbc8 31. Bf3 Bb5 32. Ne5 Bf6) 29... Rxd1+ 30. Kxd1 Rb6 {[%csl Ra6,Rf6] Black has pair of strong bishops and Judit must to play very carefully to keep balance.} (30... Rd8+ 31. Kc2 Bg5 32. Rd1) 31. Bf3 Rd6+ 32. Kc2 Bh4 $5 (32... Bg5 33. Rd1 Rxd1 34. Kxd1 Bf6 35. Nc6 (35. Nd7 Bxc3 36. Nxc5 Bf1) 35... Bxc3 36. Nxa7 $13) 33. g3 Bg5 34. Rd1 Rxd1 35. Kxd1 (35. Bxd1 h4 36. Nc6 (36. g4 $2 Bf1 $19) 36... Bf1 37. Nxa7 Bxh3 $132) 35... Bf6 36. Nc6 $5 (36. Nd7 Bxc3 37. Nxc5 Bf1 38. h4 Bd4 39. Ke1 Bb5 $15) 36... Bxc3 (36... Bb7 37. Ke2 $11) 37. Nxa7 {[%csl Ra2]} e5 (37... Bd4 38. Ke1) 38. Be2 Bb7 39. Nb5 Ba5 40. Nd6 Bg2 41. h4 f5 42. Nc4 Bc7 43. a4 {Pawn moved and it,s clear, that White out of danger now!} Bd5 44. Ke1 (44. a5 Bxc4 45. Bxc4 Bxa5 46. Ke2 $11) 44... f4 45. a5 $1 Bxc4 46. Bxc4 Bxa5+ 47. Ke2 Kf6 48. Bd3 Bc7 49. Kf3 1/2-1/2

