1. e4 {Ftacnik,L} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {Kramnik was able to eliminate Kasparov's white pieces in the London 2000 match with the help of this ambitious defence. Black is playing without castling and with the worse pawn structure, but he can lean back on the advantage of the bishop pair.} 9. Nc3 Ke8 10. h3 h5 11. Rd1 {So far there is no patent which leadis to the gaining of an advantage, so in every game white players try to squeeze from the position some concrete benefits.} (11. Bg5 Be7 12. Rfd1 Be6 13. g3 (13. Rd2 h4 (13... Rd8 14. Rad1 Rxd2 15. Rxd2 h4 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Ne2 Bd5 18. Nfd4 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 g6 20. b3 a5 21. c4 Be6 $11 {1-0 Anand,V (2788)-Karjakin,S (2723)/Moscow 2009 (51)}) 14. Rad1 Rh5 15. Bxe7 Nxe7 16. Nd4 Bd5 17. f4 a6 18. b3 b6 19. Kf2 Rc8 20. Rd3 $14 {½-½ Robson,R (2560)-Bacrot,E (2710)/Khanty Mansiysk 2011 (26)}) 13... Rd8 14. Rxd8+ (14. Kg2 f6 15. Bf4 Bb4 16. exf6 Bxc3 17. f7+ Bxf7 18. bxc3 Rxd1 19. Rxd1 Bxa2 20. Ra1 Bd5 21. Rxa7 Rf8 22. Rxb7 $2 Nh4+ $19 {0-1 Zhigalko,A (2591)-Matlakov,M (2603)/Sestao 2010 (26)}) 14... Bxd8 15. Rd1 Be7 16. b3 b6 17. Bxe7 Kxe7 18. Ne2 a5 19. Nf4 c5 20. Ng5 Rh6 21. c3 $16 {1-0 Navara,D (2722)-Kryvoruchko,Y (2635)/Reykjavik 2010 (44)}) 11... Be7 {Bologan,Viktor: '*'} 12. Ne4 Bd7 {Bologan,Viktor: 'N' 'N'} (12... Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 14. Ng5 Bf5 15. c4 Rf8 16. Rd4 Bxg5 17. Bxg5 f6 18. exf6 gxf6 19. Re1+ Kf7 20. Rf4 Kg6 21. Bh4 Rad8 22. Re3 $14 {1-0 Balogh,C (2595)-Delorme,A (2434)/Arinsal 2009 (68)}) (12... Be6 13. Nfg5 Bd5 (13... Rh6 14. b3 Rg6 15. Bf4 Nh4 16. Nxe6 Rxe6 17. Bh2 Ba3 18. Rab1 a5 19. c3 Be7 20. a4 Rb8 21. b4 axb4 22. cxb4 $11 {½-½ Szabo,K (2523)-Kovacs,G (2500)/Szombathely 2010 (126)}) 14. b3 Rd8 15. c4 Bxe4 16. Rxd8+ Bxd8 17. Nxe4 h4 18. Bb2 Be7 19. Rd1 Rh6 20. Rd3 c5 21. Kf1 $14 {1-0 Vachier Lagrave,M (2710)-Gozzoli,Y (2524)/Marseilles 2010 (41)}) (12... Bd6) 13. b3 h4 {Nobody is able to tell Black sensibly which danger should be addressed as a priority. The fixing of the kingside pawns lowers the danger of the advance of his majority. The other competing concept was to gradually advance the queenside with the hope of generating real counterplay.} (13... c5 $5 14. Bg5 b6 $14) 14. Bg5 Rd8 15. c4 b6 (15... Bxg5 16. Nfxg5 b6 17. Rd2 Be6 $14 {Bologan,Viktor: ''} 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Rxd8+ Kxd8 20. Rd1+ $14) 16. Rd2 Bc8 (16... Bxg5 17. Nfxg5 Be6 $14) 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 (17... Bxd8 18. Bf4 (18. Rd1 Bxg5 19. Nfxg5 Ke7 $11) 18... c5 19. Rd1 Rh5 $14) 18. Rd1+ Ke8 19. Bf4 {Bologan,Viktor: '!' '!'} c5 (19... Be6 20. Kf1 $14) 20. e6 $5 {[%mdl 32] A remarkable moment in the battle of two concepts. The white player is seemingly illogically giving up the pawn majority on the kingside; the black bishops automatically gain in importance. The curse of the defence is at this moment the passive location of the rook on the other side of the board.} (20. Nc3 $5 c6 21. Ne4 $14) 20... Bxe6 $2 {Karjaking is a brilliant talent from the Ukraine, but currently playing for Russia. The change of the federation did not come cheap - the conditions for him had an implicit guarantee of the Russian government. The goal is to groom a player with the potential to win the world championship title. In World Cup he had the bad luck of meeting Judit Polgar who was coming back after longish maternity leave at the height of her powers. The strongest woman player in history is not afraid of any opponent. The Russian hope missed the chance to apply an unusual solution. After the less appealing recapture with a pawn chances would have remained balanced.} (20... fxe6 21. Bxc7 Bb7 22. Nfg5 Rh6 (22... Nd4 23. Bb8 Rf8 $13) 23. Bb8 Rg6 24. f4 a6 $11) 21. Bxc7 f6 (21... Bc8 22. Nc3 Bb7 23. Nd5 Rh5 24. Bb8 Nd4 25. Nxd4 Bxd5 26. Nb5 Bb7 27. Bxa7 Rg5 28. Nc7+ Kf8 29. Nd5 $16) 22. Bb8 a6 (22... Bd8 23. Bxa7 Bc7 24. Nc3 Kf7 25. Nb5 Ra8 26. Nxc7 Rxa7 27. Nxe6 Kxe6 28. a4 $16) 23. Ba7 Bd8 (23... b5 24. cxb5 axb5 25. Bxc5 $16) 24. Nc3 Kf7 (24... Bc7 25. Na4 b5 26. Nxc5 Bc8 27. Rc1 $16) 25. Na4 $1 {Bologan,Viktor: '' '' The position has significantly moved towards understandable problems without original features. Black is losing a pawn and has to look for the most promising form of resistance.} b5 26. Nxc5 Bc8 (26... bxc4 27. Nxe6 Kxe6 28. bxc4 Be7 29. Kf1 $16) 27. cxb5 axb5 28. a4 $5 (28. Ne4 Be7 29. Nc3 $14) 28... bxa4 (28... b4 29. Nd2 Be7 30. Nc4 Bxc5 31. Bxc5 Be6 32. Na5 Kg6 33. Rd3 Rb8 34. g4 hxg3 35. fxg3 $18) 29. bxa4 Re8 {Knowledge of the course of the game enables us to guess, that somewhat better chances for a real fight were offered by the idea of opening the d-file for counterplay.} (29... Bc7 $5 30. Rb1 Rd8 31. Bb6 Rd5 32. Bxc7 Rxc5 33. Bb6 Rc2 34. Rb4 g5 35. a5 Bb7 36. Nd4 $16) 30. Rb1 $1 g5 (30... Bc7 31. Bb8 Bd8 32. Rb5 Be7 33. Bc7 $16) 31. Bb6 Be7 (31... Bxb6 32. Rxb6 Re2 33. a5 Ra2 34. Ne4 $18) 32. a5 Bxc5 {When the position is sliding the defender should try keep das far away from the abyss as possible. Defence with both bishops would have enabled Polgar to dream about the ideal location for the knight on the c7-square.} (32... Nd6 33. Nd2 Nb7 34. Na6 Bf5 35. Ra1 Rc8 36. Nc7 Bc5 37. Bxc5 Nxc5 38. a6 Rxc7 39. a7 Rxa7 40. Rxa7+ $18) 33. Bxc5 Re6 34. Rb6 (34. Rb8 Ba6 35. Bb4 Rc6 $16) 34... Ng7 {The blockade on the light square is seriously complicating the advance of the a-pawn, but Polgar will easily refocus on the pawn weaknesses of the kingside.} (34... Rxb6 35. Bxb6 Ke6 36. Nd2 Nd6 37. f3 Ba6 38. Bd4 f5 39. Nb3 $16) 35. Be3 (35. Nd4 Rxb6 36. axb6 Ne6 37. Nb3 Ke8 38. Bb4 f5 39. Na5 Nd8 40. f3 Kd7 41. Bd2 f4 42. Bc3 $18) 35... Nf5 (35... Rxb6 36. axb6 Nf5 37. Bc5 Ke6 38. Nd2 Kd5 39. Ba3 $16) 36. Rb8 Re8 (36... Nxe3 37. Rxc8 Nd5 38. Rc5 $18) 37. Ra8 Bb7 {The choice of the endgame, in which the defender could stand a chance to save the difficult position is a very complicated task. The battle of the bishop against the knight in the event of an exchange on e3 would not have been pretty for Black.} (37... Nxe3 38. fxe3 Bf5 39. Rxe8 Kxe8 40. Nd2 Kd7 41. e4 Be6 42. e5 fxe5 43. Ne4 Kc6 44. Nxg5 Bd5 45. Nf3 $18) 38. Ra7 Re7 39. Bc5 Rd7 40. a6 Bc6 41. Rxd7+ Bxd7 42. Nd2 (42. Nd4 Nxd4 43. Bxd4 Ke6 44. a7 Bc6 45. f3 f5 46. Kf2 $16) 42... Ke6 43. Nc4 Bc6 {The last moment when the final part of the game might have developed differently. Unfortunately also the fight of the black knight against the bishop would have been hopelessly lost.} (43... Kd5 44. Na5 $1 Bc6 45. Nxc6 Kxc6 46. Kf1 Ng7 47. Be7 Nh5 (47... Ne8 48. Bd8 $18) 48. Ke2 $18) 44. Nb6 Nd6 (44... Ng7 45. a7 Kf5 46. a8=Q Bxa8 47. Nxa8 Ne6 48. Bd6 $18) 45. Bxd6 Kxd6 46. a7 Kc7 47. a8=Q Bxa8 48. Nxa8+ Kb7 49. f4 $1 {Bologan,Viktor: '1-0 (49) Polgar,J (2699)-Karjakin,S (2788) Khanty-Mansiysk 2011 CBM 145 [Ftacnik]' The charming finish is reminiscent of the sharp intake of breath you experience at the moment, when everything is beautiful around you. White will win the pawn endgame without any effort, since the black king has to go to the far away a8-square.} (49. f4 $1 Kxa8 (49... gxf4 50. Kf2 Kxa8 51. Kf3 Kb7 52. Kxf4 Kc6 53. Kf5 $18) 50. fxg5 fxg5 51. Kf2 Kb7 52. Kf3 Kc6 53. Kg4 $18) 1-0

