1. e4 {Marin,M Marin,M} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 {(!?!) Throughout his carrier, Karpov has been playing all kind of closed systems of the Ruy Lopez. He is supposed to know the open variation very well, too, but mainly from White's point of view, after having faced it repeatedly during his World Championship matches against Korchnoi from 1978 and 1981. With Black, Karpov only captured on e4 twice, between the aforementioned matches. Although his overall result was satisfactory, Karpov must have felt that the opening did not suit his style perfectly. Until now...} 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 (8. Nxe5 {Hort,V (2595)-Karpov,A (2725)/Bugojno 1980/MCD (½-½, 16)}) 8... Be6 9. Nbd2 (9. Qe2 {Kavalek,L (2590)-Karpov,A (2705)/Montreal 1979/MCL/[ChessBase] (0-1, 41)}) 9... Be7 10. c3 O-O {This variation had been played by Korchnoi starting with the '50s and has been revitalized by him recently.} 11. Qe2 {Caught by surprise, Judit deviates from the main course of theory, where, she supposed, Karpov had prepared something.} ({In fact, she had two recent experiences with the main line:} 11. Bc2 f5 12. Nb3 Qd7 13. Nfd4 Nxd4 14. Nxd4 c5 15. Nxe6 Qxe6 16. f3 Ng5 17. a4 Rad8 18. axb5 {Logical. What else would a4 be good for?! White opens lines, activates his rook and attempts to become active. Logisch, wozu soll a4 sonst gut sein?! Weiß macht die Linien auf, aktiviert seinen Turm und versucht aktiv zu werden.} axb5 19. Ra7 Rd7 {The new move. It is questionable whether it is an important innovation, more an improvement on the previous game. Der neue Zug. In wiefern das eine wichtige Neuerung ist, ist fraglich. Das ist eher Verstärkung der Vorgängerpartie.} 20. Rxd7 Qxd7 21. Be3 ({Aiming to improve over} 21. Kh1 {, where the final result of the game had little to do with the outcome of the opening, Polgar,J (2710)-Sokolov,I (2670)/Hoogeveen 2006/CBM 115/[Kritz] (1-0, 48)}) 21... f4 22. Bf2 g6 23. Qd2 Ne6 24. Rd1 Rd8 25. Be4 Qc7 26. Bxd5 Qxe5 27. b3 Kg7 28. c4 bxc4 29. bxc4 Nc7 30. Qa2 Nxd5 31. cxd5 Kf8 32. h3 Qd6 $11 {Polgar,J (2707)-Carlsen,M (2710)/Biel 2007/CBM 120 (½-½, 43)}) 11... Nxd2 $5 {Still following the footsteps of his historical rival.} ({The main alternative is} 11... Nc5 {, which had been employed by Korchnoi, too, on the occasion of his Candidates' match against Mecking...} 12. Nd4 Nxb3 13. N2xb3 Qd7 14. Nxc6 Qxc6 15. Be3 Bf5 16. Rfd1 Qg6 17. Nd4 Bd7 18. b4 Qe4 $132 {Mecking,H (2615)-Kortschnoj,V (2670)/Augusta 1974/Candidates (½-½, 35). True, several dozens of games have been played in the meanwhile...}) 12. Bxd2 Na5 13. Bc2 Nc4 ({Another historical game went} 13... c5 14. Qd3 (14. b3 {deprives the knight of the c4-square, but weakens the c3-square prematurely.} Nc6 15. Qd3 g6 16. Bh6 Re8 17. Qd2 b4 18. cxb4 Nxb4 19. Bd1 Nc6 20. Qf4 Rb8 $132 {Carlsen,M (2646)-Predojevic,B (2566)/Sarajevo 2006/TWIC 602 (½-½, 88)}) ({However, from the modern era the following game deserves serious attention:} 14. Rad1 Qd7 15. h3 Nc6 16. Bg5 Rfe8 17. Rfe1 {White is in no hurry to break Black's position, but simply develops and activates his pieces.} Rad8 18. Qe3 Bxg5 19. Nxg5 Bf5 20. e6 fxe6 21. Bxf5 exf5 22. Qxc5 $14 {Sokolov,A (2628)-Predojevic,B (2566)/Turin 2006/CBM 113 (½-½, 27)}) 14... g6 15. Bh6 Re8 {Now, the simplest and probably best continuation would have been} 16. Rfe1 {consolidates the central pawn and continues the development of the pieces. I believe that in this case White would have maintained an advantage, for instance} (16. Qd2 {led to incalculable complications after} Nc4 17. Qf4 Nxb2 18. Bg5 d4 $13 {Tal,M-Kortschnoj,V/Riga 1955/EXT 99 (0-1, 33)}) 16... f6 (16... Bf5 {solves only part of Black's problems. After} 17. Qe2 Bxc2 18. Qxc2 f6 19. Rad1 {White's pressure is unpleasant.}) 17. Nh4 $1 {This is not the start of an atack actually, but a way to force Black to a significant positional concession.} f5 ({Sad necessity. Black is forced to block the position, thus adding force to the white central pawn. After, for instance} 17... Bf8 $6 18. Bxf8 Kxf8 {White would have obtained a very powerful attack with} 19. Nxg6+ hxg6 20. Qxg6 {when, compared to a line examined above, the presence of the light squared bishops on board makes a lot of difference in White's favour.}) 18. Nf3 Nc6 19. Rad1 {and White has completed his development, maintaining a positional advantage. True, Black's position remains quite solid.}) 14. Nd4 (14. b3 Nxd2 15. Qxd2 Rc8 $5 ({I believe that there are not many players who, now or later, would refrain from} 15... c5) 16. a4 Qd7 17. axb5 axb5 18. Ra5 g6 19. Bd3 c6 20. Rfa1 Rc7 21. Nd4 f6 $132 {Aronin,L-Kortschnoj,V/Riga 1954/EXT 2000 (½-½, 27). After having more or less neutralised the enemy bishop, Black opens new diagonals for his own dark-squared bishop.}) (14. Bc1 Qd7 (14... Bg4 $1 $132) 15. b3 Nb6 16. Qd3 $14 {Hecht,H-Langeweg,K/Hengelo 1968 (1-0, 45)}) 14... Nxd2 {It seems that Karpov likes clarity lately.} ({Actually, Black can ignore the bishop for the time being and simply continue his development with} 14... Qd7 15. f4 c5 16. Nxe6 Qxe6 17. Rae1 f5 18. Bc1 Qg6 19. b3 Nb6 20. Be3 Rad8 21. Qf2 Qc6 22. Rd1 (22. Qf3 Na8 $5 {[%csl Ge6][%cal Ga8c7,Gc7e6]}) 22... d4 $1 23. cxd4 Nd5 24. Kh1 (24. dxc5 $2 Nxe3 25. Qxe3 Bxc5 $19) (24. b4 c4 25. a3 a5 $132) 24... cxd4 25. Bxd4 Nxf4 26. Qxf4 (26. Bb1 Ne6 27. Bxf5 Nxd4 28. Rxd4 Rxd4 29. Qxd4 g6 30. Bd3 Rxf1+ 31. Bxf1 Qc1 $132 32. Qd3 Qf4 33. e6 Bc5 34. Be2 Qe3 $11) 26... Qxc2 27. Rd2 Qe4 $15 {Lee,P-Langeweg,K/Bad Aibling 1968/EXT 2001 (0-1, 48)}) (14... Bg4 $2 {was recommended but is just bad:} 15. Qxg4 $1 ({The old analysis went} 15. Qe1 f6 $15) 15... Nxd2 16. Rfd1 Nc4 17. Nf5 g6 18. Rxd5 $1 $18) 15. Qxd2 Qd7 16. f4 c5 17. Nxe6 fxe6 {Black has strengthened his centre, but also lost some kingside flexibility. The thematic pawn break ...f6 is not available any more, but on the queenside his pawn majority could become threatening. Although objectively speaking chances might be equal, it is mainly White who will do the attacking in the next phase of the game.} (17... Qxe6 $2 18. Rae1 $16 {[%csl Rd5][%cal Gf4f5]}) 18. Rad1 (18. f5 Rad8 $5 (18... exf5 19. Bxf5 $1 Rxf5 $2 20. Rxf5 Qxf5 21. Qxd5+ $18)) 18... Rad8 19. Qd3 g6 20. Qg3 Rf7 21. h4 Rg7 22. Qh3 c4 {Restricting the enemy bishop by placing the last pawn on a light square and hoping for a favourable endgame in case of successful defence and mass exchanges.} 23. Kh1 a5 24. h5 Bc5 25. Rf3 Qe8 26. Rg3 {White's attack looks a bit scary, but his king is also slightly vulnerable, which restricts his active possibilities.} gxh5 27. f5 Rxg3 28. Qxg3+ Kh8 29. Qh4 Qe7 30. f6 Qf7 31. Rf1 Rg8 32. Bd1 Qg6 33. Qh3 Qd3 $1 $11 34. Qxd3 cxd3 35. f7 Rf8 36. Bxh5 Kg7 37. Rd1 Be3 38. Rxd3 Bf4 39. a4 bxa4 40. Rd4 a3 41. bxa3 (41. Rxf4 $2 axb2 42. Rf1 a4 $19) 41... Bxe5 42. Rg4+ Kh6 (42... Kf6 43. c4 $11) 43. Rg8 Bd6 44. Rxf8 Bxf8 45. Bg4 e5 46. a4 Kg6 47. Be6 d4 48. cxd4 1/2-1/2

