1. e4 {Salov} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. g4 h6 7. g5 hxg5 8. Bxg5 Nc6 9. Bg2 Bd7 10. Nb3 $5 {white is planning to develop her queen on e2 where it will support the 'h'pawn in the future; however now black is not forced to waste time on the maneuvre Qd8-b6 - the knight d4 had to leave the centre voluntarily} a6 11. Qe2 Qc7 12. O-O-O O-O-O 13. h4 Kb8 14. h5 Be7 15. f4 Ng8 $1 $146 {this method of neutralizing white's activity on the kingside was introduced by Ulf Andersson - one of the greatest masters of the positional play Salov,V: '!'} (15... Nh7 16. Bxe7 Nxe7 17. e5 $14) (15... Bc8) 16. Nd4 Bf6 $5 {and this seems to be something new; normally the maneuvre Nf6-g8 is connected with the idea Bg5 and f6} (16... Nxd4 $5 {Salov: '!?'} 17. Rxd4 Bxg5 18. fxg5 Ne7 19. h6 gxh6 20. gxh6 Nc6 21. Rd3 Ne5 22. Rg3 $36) 17. Nf3 Nge7 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Qd2 Rh6 $3 {the most difficult move of the game; now after 20.Nd4 Nd4 21.Qd4 the pawn f6 is protected and black is able to activate his bishop by 21...Bc6, obtaining excellent counterplay} (19... Bc8 $2 20. Nd4 $16) 20. Qxd6 {white is more or less forced to accept the pawn sacrifice} Qxd6 21. Rxd6 Kc7 22. e5 Rdh8 (22... Rg8 $1 $15) 23. Rhd1 Bc8 24. Ne4 $1 {Judith is trying her best to maintain the initiative; white's extra pawn is of no importance, after black's inaccuracy on the 22nd move the chances are about equal} fxe5 25. Nxe5 Nxe5 26. fxe5 Nc6 27. Bf3 $2 {leads to grave problems for white; curiously Judith didn't even consider the best defence 27.Rc6!} (27. Rxc6+ $8 $8 {Salov: '!' Salov,V: '!'} bxc6 28. Bf3 Rxh5 29. Bxh5 Rxh5 30. Rf1 Rf5 31. Rxf5 exf5 $11 {Salov: '=' Salov,V: '='}) 27... Nxe5 28. Be2 Rg8 $17 29. b4 Bd7 30. Nc3 Rg2 31. R6d4 f5 32. Kb2 Rh7 33. Kb3 Rg3 34. Kb2 Rg2 35. Kb3 Rh2 36. a4 Rg7 $1 {now the second black rook enters the game and the pressure becomes unbearable- white is forced to give up some material} 37. b5 axb5 38. Nxb5+ (38. axb5 Rg3 39. b6+ Kxb6 40. Rxd7 Nxd7 41. Rxd7 Rxe2 $19) 38... Bxb5 39. Bxb5 Rxh5 40. Rd6 (40. Re1 Nf3 41. Rc4+ Kd6 42. Rd1+ Ke5 $19) 40... Rh1 41. R1d2 Rh2 42. R2d4 Rh4 43. Rd2 {allows an elegant combination} (43. Rd1 $142) 43... Re4 $1 $19 44. c4 (44. Rxe6 Nf3 $1 45. Rxe4 Nxd2+ $19 {exterminating all the white rooks}) (44. a5 Rg3+ (44... Rg2 45. Rxg2 Kxd6 46. Rg7 Nc6 $19) 45. c3 Ree3 46. Rxe6 Rxc3+ 47. Ka2 Ra3+ 48. Kb1 Rgb3+ 49. Rb2 Rxb2+ 50. Kxb2 Rxa5 51. Rxe5 Kb6 $19) 44... Rg2 45. Rxg2 (45. Rd1 b6 $19) 45... Kxd6 46. Rg7 b6 (46... Re3+ 47. Kb4 b6 $19) 47. Kb4 Nd3+ 48. Kc3 Nc5 {0-1 (48) Polgar,J (2630)-Salov,V (2710) Buenos Aires 1994 CBM 44 [Salov]} (48... Nc5 49. Kb4 Re3) *

