1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. Bd3 Bc5 6. Nb3 ({Invariably played, but presumably in a bid to avoid theory} 6. c3 {was the world no.1's recent choice in Carlsen-Crumiller, Manhattan (simul) 2010:} Qc7 ({there's no need to commit the queen so early and} 6... d6 7. O-O Ne7 $5 {is Hellsten's recommendation in his excellent 'Play the Sicilian Kan'}) 7. O-O Nf6 8. f4 d6 9. Kh1 Nc6 10. Nxc6 $1 bxc6 11. Nd2 {and White was slightly for choice.}) 6... Ba7 ({Of course, a very important alternative is} 6... Be7 {, but this featured in another recent exhibition event, Short & Vujatovic-Kasparov & Crumiller, London 2010, and is worth a quick look here for matters quickly became rather sharp:} 7. Qg4 g6 8. Qe2 d6 9. O-O Nd7 10. a4 b6 11. Na3 $1 Qc7 $6 ({this appears to be the route of Black's future problems unsurprisingly Hellsten prefers} 11... Ngf6 12. Bh6 Bb7) 12. Nc4 Bb7 13. a5 $1 b5 14. Nb6 Nxb6 $6 ({Black hasn't time for this grab his position already doesn't inspire confidence, but} 14... Rd8 {was likely a better try}) 15. axb6 Qxb6 16. Na5 Bc8 17. Be3 ({the white team have followed Khalifman's recommendation thus far, but here the Russian prefers} 17. c4 $5 {, which also looks pretty dangerous for Black}) 17... Qc7 18. c4 b4 19. e5 $1 Rb8 ({perhaps} 19... dxe5 $5 {is a better try, as Black employed having fallen for the same trap in Lindberg-Akesson, Vaxjo 2008, and after} 20. Be4 $6 (20. Qf3 Rb8 21. Qc6+ $1 {improves and} Qxc6 22. Nxc6 Rb7 23. Rxa6 Rc7 24. Rb6 {sees White regaining his pawn while retaining a clear plus}) 20... Rb8 21. Nc6 f5 $1 22. Nxb8 Qxb8 23. Bc2 Nf6 {Akesson had escaped with reasonable compensation for the exchange}) 20. Bd4 f6 {and now there was no good reason to help Black untangle his kingside with an exchange on f6. Instead} 21. f4 $1 dxe5 22. fxe5 Bc5 ({or} 22... f5 23. Qe3) 23. Qf2 {would have left Kasparov and Crumiller badly grovelling.}) 7. O-O ({Avoiding} 7. Qg4 Nf6 {, but there have also been recent developments elsewhere:}) ({a)} 7. Qe2 Nc6 8. c4 $1 ({after} 8. Be3 d6 9. N1d2 Nf6 10. f4 Bxe3 11. Qxe3 e5 $1 12. f5 {Black took over the initiative in Escobar Forero-Felgaer, Khanty-Mansiysk Olympiad 2010, with} O-O 13. Rg1 $6 d5 14. g4 Nb4) 8... d6 9. O-O Nge7 10. Be3 e5 $1 {(a thematic and common idea Black increases his control over the dark squares, especially d4)} 11. Bxa7 Rxa7 12. Nc3 Be6 13. Rfd1 b6 $5 ({this might well be a better move order than} 13... O-O 14. Qe3 Qb8 {, as we've previously considered, and after} 15. Be2 Rd8 16. a4 $1 b6 17. Nd5 Rb7 18. h3 {in Belov-A.Muzychuk, Athens 2009, White had a definite edge}) 14. Qe3 O-O 15. Rd2 ({I'm not sure this is best White might prefer} 15. Rac1 {with ideas of Nd5}) ({or to play a la Belov with} 15. a4 $5 Qb8 16. Be2) 15... Qb8 16. Rad1 Rd8 17. Bf1 Rb7 18. Nd5 h6 19. Qg3 Kh8 {(Black is firmly entrenched)} 20. Qf3 b5 21. c5 Rbd7 {and here White decided that he had nothing better than to repeat the position with} 22. Nb6 Rb7 23. Nd5 Rbd7 {in Hammer-Andreikin, Chotowa 2010.}) ({b)} 7. c4 Nc6 8. Nc3 d6 9. O-O Nge7 ({again we see this development of the king's knight instead} 9... Nf6 10. Qe2 {would transpose to our main game}) 10. Qe2 O-O 11. Be3 e5 12. Rfd1 Nd4 $5 ({Black must always time such a leap carefully, but here I think the timing is good moreover, Balogh had previously preferred} 12... Be6 {, so the knight leap was likely the product of preparation}) 13. Bxd4 exd4 14. Nd5 Nc6 15. f4 Be6 16. Qh5 f6 $1 {gave Black a very solid position in Pavlidis-Balogh, European Championship, Rijeka 2010, and now White should definitely have held back on} 17. e5 $6 f5 {.}) 7... Nc6 8. c4 ({A critical alternative is again} 8. Qg4 $5 {, as we last considered in Rublevsky-Zubov, European Club Cup, Ohrid 2009.}) 8... Nf6 ({Even Andreikin, the current World Junior Champion, has developed the knight classically here, although Black can still consider too the plan of} 8... d6 9. Nc3 Nge7 {.}) 9. Nc3 d6 10. Qe2 e5 ({In the same city Polgar had previously faced} 10... O-O {and after} 11. Be3 Bxe3 12. Qxe3 e5 13. Rfd1 Be6 14. Be2 $1 Qb8 15. Rd2 b5 16. Nd5 {she had a definite edge due to Black's long-term problems down the d-file in J.Polgar-Nisipeanu, Khanty-Mansiysk 2009.}) 11. Be3 O-O ({I think that Smirin is correct not to exchange on e3, but the text isn't Black's only choice. The alternative being to hold back on castling with} 11... Be6 $5 12. Bxa7 Rxa7 13. Qe3 b6 {and after} 14. Rfd1 Nd7 $1 {(commencing a typical regrouping)} 15. Rab1 Nc5 16. Nd2 a5 17. b3 O-O 18. Nb5 Rd7 {Black was fine in Sjugirov-Andreikin, Chotowa 2010. Overall, I believe that Black should be able to restrict White to at most a small pull in these lines. Indeed, he has excellent drawing chances, but as for winning chances... Well, I would have said not that many, but then again, just look at our main game!}) 12. Rac1 ({Polgar improves over... Polgar! In J. Polgar-Martorelli, Italian Team Championship 2008, she had preferred} 12. Rad1 {and after} Nd4 $5 ({I doubt Smirin would have repeated this, and here too} 12... Be6 {is unsurprisingly a viable alternative}) 13. Bxd4 exd4 14. Nd5 Ng4 ({possibly Black was worried about d4 simply dropping off, but it's here that one should look to improve an idea is} 14... Re8 $5 {, intending} 15. Bc2 Nxd5 16. cxd5 Bd7 17. Nxd4 Qf6 {with the two bishops and some long-term compensation on the dark squares - I doubt that it's quite enough for the pawn, but gradually improving the white position is not such an easy task}) 15. h3 $1 {(driving the knight to e5, so that f4 will come with tempo White definitely has the upper hand here)} Ne5 16. Nd2 $1 {(White is happy to cede a second bishop and is now all set to push forward with f4)} f5 17. f4 fxe4 $6 ({too routine Black had to find counterplay and as such something like} 17... Nxd3 18. Qxd3 Be6 19. Nf3 b5 $5 {suggests itself}) 18. Nxe4 Nxd3 19. Qxd3 Bf5 20. Rde1 {was well on the way to destroying much lower-rated opposition with her rather powerful knight-pair.}) 12... Be6 ({A logical approach, but I wonder just what Polgar had in mind against} 12... Nd4 $5 {. After} 13. Bxd4 (13. Qd1 {would be a radical way of exploiting the free d1-square, but unfortunately for White there's no great follow-up after} Bd7) 13... exd4 14. Nd5 {it makes a little more sense to have the queen's rook on c1 rather than d1, but here too I'd be surprised if Black was all that much worse after} Re8 {.}) 13. Nd5 Nd7 14. Bxa7 Rxa7 15. Qe3 b6 $1 {This idea should be familiar by now from the earlier notes. Black simply plays around the d5-knight, slowly improving his pieces and gaining space.} 16. Nd2 ({White didn't have to retreat the knight, but otherwise after} 16. Rfd1 Nc5 {Black is all set to bring a knight to d4.}) 16... Nc5 17. Bb1 ({It's not entirely clear that the bishop is worth preserving, but in any case it's far from easy to find a good plan for White. For instance,} 17. f4 {is met by} exf4 18. Rxf4 Ne5 19. Be2 a5 {with at least equality for Black, and likely more in view of the threat to trade on d5, leaving White with a potentially bad bishop.}) ({Likewise, if White tries to fight for d4 with} 17. Nf3 {, then after} a5 18. h3 {(prudently preventing ... Bg4)} Nb4 $1 19. Bb1 f5 $5 {Black has decent counterplay.}) 17... a5 ({Naturally played to prevent White from expanding with b4, but now Polgar spots a new home for her knight. However, Smirin's choice was clearly correct, especially as} 17... Bxd5 $6 18. cxd5 Nd4 19. b4 Nd7 20. Nb3 Nxb3 21. Qxb3 {would have only played into White's hands the bishop will return to the action on d3 and an invasion on c6 is imminent.}) 18. Nc3 $1 h6 ({Smirin continues in very solid vein. Black might have activated his second knight with} 18... Nd4 {, but with} 19. f4 {White begins some play of her own. Note that after} f6 {the knight might even return with} 20. Nd5 $5 {, as} Bxd5 21. exd5 {would open up the remaining bishop to a certain extent.}) 19. Rfd1 Rd7 $1 ({Continuing his plan. Again} 19... Nd4 {was an option, but after} 20. Ne2 $1 Nxe2+ (20... Qf6 $5 {might be a more combative response}) 21. Qxe2 Rd7 22. Nf1 $1 Qg5 23. Ne3 {White still has chances to be better with a knight set to return to d5.}) 20. Nb5 Qg5 {The local conclusion of both sides' mini-plans has been reached. White has invaded b5 and placed d6 under some pressure, but Black can defend his one weakness and retains an extremely solid position.} 21. Nf1 ({Polgar only wants to exchange queens on her terms. Of course,} 21. Qxg5 hxg5 22. Nf1 {was an option too, but after} Rfd8 {followed by ...Na7 it's again hard to see any real problems for Black.}) 21... Rfd8 22. Rd2 Na7 $1 {Smirin knows that he will be able to defend d6 all day and that there's no getting passed his monster knight on c5.} 23. Na3 Nc6 24. Nb5 Na7 25. Nxa7 ({Not a mistake and a move typical of Judit Polgar's fighting spirit, but White clearly has nothing here and it might have been wiser to settle for} 25. Na3 {and a repetition.}) 25... Rxa7 26. Qe1 $6 ({Now Black gets to push ... b5 and swiftly assumes the initiative. Having spurned the repetition White's position was not so easy, though, as shown by, for instance,} 26. f3 Rb7 27. b3 b5 28. cxb5 Rxb5 {when White should be able to hold after} 29. Bd3 $1 Nxd3 30. Qxd3 Rb4 {, but it is clear that Black is pretty active with both ...a4 and ... d5 high on the agenda.}) 26... Rb7 27. Qe2 ({An admission that something has gone wrong, but perhaps} 27. Ne3 $5 {, meeting} b5 {with} 28. cxb5 Rxb5 29. Qd1 {, was a better defensive try.}) 27... Rdb8 28. Ne3 ({Not an easy move to criticise, but I wonder if Polgar had already misassessed the position after Black's 30th. Indeed, there's little doubt that White is slowly sliding down the slippery slope. It wasn't nice to have to admit it, but perhaps now was already the time to grimly dig in, beginning with} 28. Qe3 $5 {. Then} Qxe3 29. Nxe3 b5 {gives Black undoubted pressure, but after} 30. b3 $1 (30. Rxd6 $2 bxc4 31. b3 {isn't such a good idea on account of} cxb3 $1 32. Rxc5 bxa2 33. Bxa2 Bxa2 {with an extra outside passed pawn for Black}) 30... a4 31. cxb5 Rxb5 (31... axb3 32. Rxd6 bxa2 {is no longer so good, in view of simply} 33. Bxa2 {, leaving the c5-knight en prise}) 32. Rxd6 axb3 33. axb3 Nxb3 34. Rcd1 Nd4 35. Bd3 {he is restricted to no more than a slight pull.}) 28... b5 29. cxb5 Rxb5 30. Rxd6 $2 ({Definitely a mistake. White had to hang tight with something like} 30. g3 {, restricting Black to just an edge. Then Black might decide to re-route his queen, as} Rb4 31. Rxd6 Rxb2 32. Qe1 {no longer seems so effective with the f4 entry square closed off.}) 30... Rxb2 31. Qe1 Qf4 $1 ({Simple and strong, and, indeed, there was no need to fish in more murky waters with} 31... Bxa2 $6 32. Bxa2 Nxe4 33. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 34. Rd7+ Kg8 {when} 35. h4 $5 {is but one tricky resource at White's disposal.}) 32. Rcd1 {Now a key pawn simply drops off, but I'm stumped to suggest anything all that much better.} Bxa2 (32... Nxe4 $5 33. Bxe4 Qxe4 34. Qxa5 Rxa2 {was an option too, but why give White hopes of a draw by leaving all the pawns on one wing when you can have a passed a-pawn?}) 33. Bc2 Bb3 34. Bxb3 R2xb3 35. Rc6 ({It's all gone horribly wrong for the Hungarian. Indeed, neither would activating her knight have helped:} 35. Nc4 a4 36. R6d5 Nxe4 37. Nxe5 Nc3 38. Rd8+ Rxd8 39. Rxd8+ Kh7 40. Nd7 $5 h5 $1 41. Nf8+ Kh6 {and the happiest monarch is the one on h6.}) 35... Nd3 36. Qf1 $1 Qxe4 $6 ({A second pawn drops off, but in return White will manage to drive the black queen to a sub-optimal square, as Polgar begins to display most impressive resourcefulness. Indeed. White is now back in the struggle, but would not have been had Black found the blow} 36... Nxf2 $1 {when} 37. Nd5 (37. Qxf2 Rxe3 {gives White no hope}) 37... Qxe4 (37... Nxd1 $5 38. Nxf4 Ne3 39. Qa6 exf4 {is another approach}) 38. Rc4 $5 Qxc4 $1 39. Qxc4 Nxd1 40. Ne7+ Kh7 41. Qxf7 Ne3 {is an especially cool way to defend and also counterattack.}) 37. Rc4 $1 Qh7 ({Black would like to keep his queen active, but I dare say that the line} 37... Qg6 38. Rg4 Qa6 $1 39. Nf5 {caused some nervous seconds with the flag hanging. However, with more time Smirin might well have gone in for this line, since} Qe6 $1 40. Rxg7+ Kh8 41. Rg3 Qxf5 42. Rgxd3 Rxd3 43. Rxd3 a4 {is surely a winning endgame.}) 38. Rc6 Kh8 $6 ({Polgar has done very well to complicate and it's no real surprise that Smirin errs a second time, especially as his flag was likely hanging. Instead} 38... e4 {was correct, as then} 39. f3 Nb4 40. Rcd6 Rf8 {appears to leave everything covered for now. One problem that White faces is that as soon as her rook probes the back rank, such as with} 41. Qe2 a4 42. Rd8 {, Black can extricate his queen when the counterattack shouldn't be quite enough, here after} Qg6 43. Rxf8+ Kxf8 44. Rd8+ Ke7 45. Qd1 Nd3 {.}) 39. Rd6 e4 40. f3 Qg8 $5 ({I can't see anything too much wrong with} 40... f5 {, but I dare say that Smirin was still low on time at this point and just wanted to avoid any back-rank dramas.}) 41. fxe4 Ne5 $2 ({With an incremental time control and a digital clock Smirin should have realised that the time control had been reached, but perhaps he didn't or found it hard to readjust. Black is still clearly better, but had to find the precise} 41... Nb2 $1 42. Re1 a4 43. Nf5 Qf8 {, keeping everything covered. White still has some tricks, but from what I can see after} 44. e5 a3 {she can't quite manage to out-trump Black's major trump, an a-pawn two squares from touchdown.}) 42. Nf5 Qc8 ({The alternative defence was} 42... f6 $5 {, but this wouldn't have stopped the threat. Still after} 43. Nxh6 $1 Qc4 $1 44. Qxc4 (44. Rd8+ Rxd8 45. Rxd8+ Kh7 46. Qf5+ $2 {comes up badly short after just} g6) 44... Nxc4 45. Rd8+ Rxd8 46. Rxd8+ Kh7 47. Nf5 Rb7 48. Ra8 Kg6 49. Nd4 Rb2 {Black would have retained a definite edge in the endgame, although with care White should likely hold.}) 43. h3 $2 ({I must confess that I don't really understand this unPolgar-like move. Why did she reject} 43. Nxh6 $1 {? Black can keep a degree of control with} Qc7 ({otherwise,} 43... gxh6 $4 44. Qf6+ Kh7 45. Qxe5 {gives White a winning attack,}) (43... Qc5+ 44. Kh1 {doesn't appear to allow the second player any sort of devastating counterattack}) ({and} 43... f6 44. Rxf6 $1 gxf6 45. Qxf6+ Kh7 46. Nf5 {gives White enough attack to draw}) 44. Qf5 f6 {, but this would hardly have been clearly winning for him after} 45. Ng4 Nxg4 46. Qxg4 {.}) 43... Qc4 $1 {Simple and strong. White must now either accept a pretty bad endgame by trading queens or allow Black to assume control and run his a-pawn.} 44. Qe1 a4 45. Kh2 $6 (45. Qd2 $5 {was likely a trickier defence, but presumably Polgar was by now dispirited having seen the game drift away from her for a second time.}) 45... Qc3 46. R1d2 $2 {And now she misses a killer tactic.} Nc4 47. Rd8+ Kh7 48. Rxb8 Nxd2 $1 {White can't save both queen and rook.} 0-1

