1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik Ftacnik Ftacnik: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Ftacnik''} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 a5 11. f3 a4 12. Rc1 Qa5 13. Rc2 {White has to play very patiently against the clever development by Black. Mamedov has chosen a line that is very solid despite the fact that development of the light-squared bishop has been postponed.} Bd7 (13... Be6 14. Nd5 Nxd5 (14... Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Nxd5 16. cxd5 Bc8 17. Rb1 Bf6 18. Bb5 Ra5 19. Bd3 Ra8 20. Rc4 e6 21. Rb4 exd5 22. exd5 Bd8 {1/2 Begnis,N (2293)-Coenen,N (2353)/Belgium 2011}) 15. cxd5 Rfc8 16. Qxa5 Rxa5 17. Kd2 Rxc2+ 18. Kxc2 Bd7 19. Kb1 a3 20. Rc1 axb2 21. Rc7 $16 {1-0 Zubarev,A (2478)-Shalimov,V (2440)/Kharkov 1999 (23)}) (13... Re8 14. O-O Nd7 15. Nd5 Nc5 16. Rd1 Be6 17. Bd4 Bxd5 18. cxd5 Qxd2 19. Rdxd2 Rec8 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. Kf2 {1/2 Arribas Lopez,A (2312)-Aroven,M (2201)/Ronda 2007 (71)}) 14. O-O Bc6 15. Rfc1 Nd7 16. Nd5 Bxd5 {The first truly critical position in the game. The defender had to decide, if he prefers the solid position without queens or the more flexible fight after the retreat to d8.} (16... Qd8 $5 17. Bd4 Rc8 $11) 17. Qxa5 $1 (17. cxd5 Qxd2 18. Rxd2 Rfc8 19. Rxc8+ Rxc8 20. Kf2 Nc5 $13) 17... Rxa5 18. cxd5 Nc5 19. Kf1 b6 20. Rb1 f5 {The real problems for the second player will take some time to become truly visible. If he can not find any counterplay White will slowly advance her pawns on the kingside and at the suitable moment play b2-b4.} (20... Ra7 21. Bb5 $14) (20... Rc8 21. g4 Ra7 22. Kg2 $14) 21. exf5 Rxf5 (21... gxf5 22. g3 Kf7 23. Kg2 $16) 22. Rd1 {Polgar has been enjoying her successful comeback to the chess scene. With greater maturity she sometimes plays more cautiously than was the case in her younger years.} (22. g4 $5 Rf8 (22... Re5 23. Bd2 Ra8 24. Bf4 $16) 23. Kg2 Be5 24. Bd2 Ra7 25. Bh6 Bg7 26. Be3 Be5 27. Rc4 $16) 22... Ra8 (22... Re5 23. Kf2 Ra8 24. f4 Rf8 25. Bf3 Ref5 26. g3 g5 27. g4 Rxf4 28. Bxf4 Rxf4 29. Kg2 $16) 23. h4 (23. f4 $5 Be5 24. g3 g5 25. Bg4 Rff8 26. f5 h6 27. Kg2 $16) 23... a3 $6 {Mamedov had every reason to worry about the future, but the advance of a pawn will not bring the counterplay he was hoping for. White will get concrete play for her well coordinated pieces.} (23... Be5 24. g4 Rf7 25. Kg2 Raf8 26. Rb1 Bf4 27. Bf2 $16) 24. b4 Na4 (24... Nd7 25. g4 Rff8 26. Kg2 $16) 25. Rd3 Nb2 (25... Bb2 26. f4 h5 27. g4 Rf6 28. gxh5 gxh5 29. Bd2 $16) 26. Rb3 Rxd5 (26... Rb8 $6 27. Rxa3 Rxd5 28. Bc1 $18) 27. Bxb6 Rb8 (27... Bd4 28. Ba5 Rf5 29. Rxa3 d5 30. Rb3 $18) 28. Ba7 $1 Rb7 (28... Ra8 29. Rxa3 Rh5 30. g3 d5 31. b5 Nc4 32. Rxc4 dxc4 33. b6 $18) 29. Be3 Bd4 (29... Bf6 30. b5 Bxh4 31. b6 $18) 30. Bc1 $6 {Tactics in the service of strategic ideas is a well known and very efficient tool. Judit plays a solid but academic move completely ignoring the golden chance to decide the game immediately with the help of tactical flair.} (30. Rd2 $1 e5 (30... Bxe3 31. Rxd5 Bc5 32. bxc5 Rxb3 33. cxd6 Rb8 34. d7 Rd8 35. Bb5 $18) 31. Rdxb2 Bxb2 32. Bc4 Rbb5 33. Ke2 Kf7 34. Bxd5+ Rxd5 35. b5 Bd4 36. b6 $18) 30... Ra5 (30... Kf7 31. f4 Ra7 32. Bd2 $16) 31. bxa5 Rxb3 32. Bc4+ {The strongest woman player in the history falls in love with clever tactics again, but not at the best moment. It was much easier to simply advance the a-pawn, as the black rook has abolutely no squares.} (32. Rc8+ $5 Kg7 33. a6 Rb4 (33... d5 34. axb3 a2 35. a7 a1=Q 36. a8=Q $18) 34. Bd2 Ra4 35. Bb5 $18) 32... Nxc4 33. axb3 Nb2 $6 {Black should have punished the great opponent by taking the queenside pawns. Winning in a technical ending would be an unpleasant task for White.} (33... Nxa5 $1 34. Bxa3 Nxb3 35. Bb2 $16) 34. a6 a2 35. a7 $1 a1=Q 36. Rc8+ Kf7 37. a8=Q {It is not so common for the stronger party to queen the pawn after the opponent with a decisive effect.} Qxa8 38. Rxa8 Nd3 39. Bg5 (39. Bd2 Bf6 40. Ke2 $18) 39... h5 40. Ke2 Nb4 41. Ra4 Nc6 (41... Bc3 42. Bd2 Bxd2 43. Kxd2 $18) 42. Rc4 Bc5 43. Be3 {In the end the technical ending is very easy to win, since White enjoys the presense of the passed pawn b3.} Ne5 (43... Bxe3 44. Kxe3 Ne5 45. Rc7 $18) 44. Rf4+ Ke6 45. b4 (45. Bxc5 dxc5 46. Ke3 Kd5 47. Rf8 $18) 45... Bxe3 46. Kxe3 Kd5 47. Rd4+ Kc6 48. Re4 (48. Re4 Kb5 49. Rxe5+ dxe5 50. Ke4 $18) 1-0

