1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 6. Bb3 Bc5 7. a4 {The second most popular line. White opens the a-file for his rook.} (7. c3 {with the subsequent 8.d4 is more popular.}) 7... Rb8 8. axb5 axb5 9. Nxe5 (9. c3 {in Adams, M (2737)-Leko, P (2763)/Miskolc HUN 2005/[Olivier Renet] is the main alternative.}) 9... Nxe5 10. d4 Bxd4 11. Qxd4 {It seems that White has achieved a better position as he has the advantage of a bishop pair and a pawn in the centre, which doesn't have a counterpart, though things are not so simple as the position of White's queen in the centre yields Black chances for counterplay.} d6 12. f4 Nc6 (12... c5 $6 {is dubious due to} 13. Qd2 Nc4 ({or} 13... Nc6 14. e5 $1 dxe5 15. fxe5 Qxd2 16. Bxd2 Nxe5 17. Bf4 $1 Nfd7 18. Bxf7+ $16 {with a big advantage for White in Kotronias, V (2535)-Kalaitzoglou, P  (2210)/Chania 1999.}) 14. Bxc4 bxc4 15. e5 $14 {with an initiative.}) 13. Qc3 Ne7 14. Nd2 $5 {A very rare line, which was introduced at the GM level by Volokitin. White protects the e4-pawn and prepares 15.e5.} ({The immediate} 14. e5 {used to be the main line. Black usually meets it with} Ne4 {transferring the knight to c5.}) ({Other interesting lines include} 14. Ra7) ({and} 14. Qd3 {- the latter was introduced by Kasparov.}) 14... O-O 15. e5 Nfd5 $1 ({An exchange on e5} 15... dxe5 16. fxe5 $14 {opens the f-file for the White's rook.}) 16. Bxd5 $6 {I don't like this exchange. Judith parts with the pair of bishops deliberately, but I don't understand the motives behind this decision.} (16. Qd3 $5 Nb4 17. Qe2 dxe5 18. Nf3 $1 {with an initiative looks like a better idea.}) ({The aforementioned game continued} 16. Qf3 dxe5 17. fxe5 c5 18. c4 Nc7 (18... Nb4 $5) 19. Ne4 Ne6 20. cxb5 c4 21. Ba4 Bb7 22. Rd1 Qc7 {Volokitin, A (2687)-Ragger, M (2568)/Austria 2010. Now} 23. Qe2 {looks like an interesting attempt to improve upon White's play.}) 16... Nxd5 17. Qd4 Bb7 18. Ne4 f5 {Nakamura's idea is to fight for the a8-h1 diagonal, though Black could have done it with less drastic steps.} (18... Ne7 $5 19. exd6 Nf5 $1 20. Qe5 Nxd6 21. Nxd6 cxd6 {and Black is more than fine thanks to his strong light-squared bishop.}) 19. Ng5 Qd7 20. Nf3 {The knight finds another square on the big diagonal in order to protect his king.} Ra8 21. Bd2 c5 {Hikaru is faithful to his aggressive style.} ({Some players would have preferred the more cautious} 21... Qc6 {with roughly equal chances.}) 22. Qd3 c4 {Black takes some space on the queenside, but gives away central squares, such as d4.} 23. Qd4 dxe5 24. Nxe5 Qc7 25. Rfd1 $1 h6 $5 {Prophylaxis. The h7-square might be useful for the black king.} 26. Be1 $6 {A slight inaccuracy. Polgar misses that after the text she will have to come back to protect the f4-pawn.} (26. h3 $5) 26... Rfe8 $1 {Now Black is threatening a trade of rooks, which is followed by 28...Nxf4.} 27. Bd2 (27. Rxa8 $5 {deserved attention, deflecting Black's rook from e5.} Rxa8 ({or} 27... Bxa8 28. Ra1 {taking the a-file.}) 28. h3 {with a defendable position.}) 27... Red8 (27... Rad8 $5 {was a possible alternative, but Nakamura prefers to retain the tension on the a-file.}) 28. Rxa8 Rxa8 29. h3 $5 {White may also need a luft.} Ra2 $1 {Black creates a threat of 30...Qb6.} 30. Rb1 $6 {This looks passive.} (30. Ng6 $1 {deserved attention, looking for counterplay.} Qd6 ({After} 30... Qb6 $6 31. Qxb6 Nxb6 32. Bc3 {White creates serious counterplay on the kingside.}) 31. Nh4 Qf6 32. Qxf6 Nxf6 33. Bc3 Be4 34. Rd8+ Kf7 35. Rb8 Ra1+ 36. Kf2 Rc1 37. Rxb5 Rxc2+ 38. Ke3 {with sufficient counterplay.}) 30... Nf6 $1 $15 {A strong move. Black prepares the annoying 31...Be4 and it turns out that there is no good way to protect the c2-pawn.} 31. Bb4 (31. Bc3 $5 {looks preferable.}) 31... Be4 32. Re1 Bxc2 33. g4 {A desperate attempt to create counterplay on the kingside.} ({Once again} 33. Ng6 $5 {deserved attention.}) 33... Qa7 {Black simplifies into an endgame.} ({The coldblooded} 33... Ra6 $1 {was better.}) 34. Qxa7 Rxa7 35. Nc6 Ra6 (35... Ra2 $5 36. Bc3 Be4) 36. Ne7+ Kf7 (36... Kh7 $1 {would be more accurate as the king gets an escape square on h6 after} 37. g5 hxg5 38. fxg5 Ne4 39. g6+ ({or} 39. Nxf5 Ra4 $17 {and the king doesn't come under attack.}) 39... Kh6 40. Nxf5+ Kxg6 $15) 37. g5 ({After} 37. Nxf5 Bxf5 38. gxf5 ({or} 38. Re7+ Kg6 39. gxf5+ Kxf5 40. Bc3 Kxf4 41. Rxg7 Ne4 $17) 38... Nd5 $17 {Black retains good winning chances.}) 37... hxg5 38. fxg5 Ne4 39. Nxf5 Nxg5 ({The difference with 36...Kh7 can be seen here:} 39... Ra4 40. Nd6+ Kg8 41. Nxe4 Rxb4 42. Nd6 Rxb2 43. Re5 $15 {and due to the limited material on the board Black's win isn't guaranteed.}) (39... Kg6 $5 {deserved attention, avoiding a check from d6.}) 40. Re7+ ({After} 40. Nd6+ $5 Kg6 41. Re3 {it would be very difficult for Black to make progress due to the misplaced knight on g5.}) 40... Kg6 41. Nd4 Bd3 42. Rb7 (42. h4 $5 Ne4 43. Re6+ {exchanging the rooks looks like a better defence.}) 42... Nxh3+ 43. Kh2 Ng5 44. Nxb5 Rf6 {Black is starting to spin a mating net.} 45. Bc5 $2 {A serious mistake.} (45. Nd6 $1 {was necessary.}) 45... Rf4 $1 {The rook is coming to g4.} 46. Nc3 $2 {A decisive mistake in a difficult position.} (46. Rb8 $1 {was necessary.} Rg4 47. Be3 Nf3+ 48. Kh3 Ne5 49. Rf8 $1 {taking away the f1-square and White is still in the game, even though her position is already difficult.}) 46... Rg4 $1 $19 {Now it's over. White can't stop the ...Bf1 and ...Nf3 moves.} 47. Nd1 Bf1 {and White resigned as she gets mated. The rare line with 14.Nd2 deserves attention, but I believe White has to follow it with either 16.Qd3 or 16.Qf3.} 0-1

