1. e4 {Rogozenco,D Rogozenko} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 Bg7 11. g3 $5 {A quiet, but rather tricky continuation in order to avoid current theoretical discussions in the main lines after 11.Bd3 Ne7. The idea behind 11.g3 is to get a certain type of positions and limit Black's choice. By the way, although 11.g3 is played quite often, some of the theoretical publications fail to mention this move at all.} ({The following variation will help understand better 11.g3.} 11. c3 f5 12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nc2 Be6 {this continuation is perfectly playable, but it is less attractive for many Black players than 13...0-0.} (13... O-O {currently one of the most fashionable lines.} 14. Nce3 Be6 15. Bd3 f5 $11) 14. g3 O-O 15. Bg2 {Playing this position is a matter of taste. Generally, this was the main reason why Black players switched again to 13...0-0 instead of 13...Be6, because not everybody liked the position after 15.Bg2 with Black. Rogozenko: 'Now we have the same position from the previous comment (11.c3) in which White avoided 13...0-0.'}) 11... Ne7 {Black is making most natural moves and soon will end up in a typical position for Sveshnikov Sicilian, which is close to equal.} ({The most principled continuation is considered} 11... f5 {although White has his arguments here too:} 12. exf5 Bxf5 (12... e4 13. c3 Ne5 14. Be2 {is a different variation, which scores very well for White in the practice.}) 13. Bg2 {[%cal Gd5e3]} Be6 14. c3 O-O 15. Nc2 {Now we have the same position from the previous comment (11.c3) in which White avoided 13...0-0.}) 12. Nxe7 Qxe7 13. Bg2 O-O 14. O-O ({The immediate 14.c3 has its logic - to bring the knight into play before castling:} 14. c3 Rb8 (14... f5 15. exf5 e4 16. Nc2 Bxf5 17. O-O Rac8 18. Re1 Rfe8 19. a4 Qd7 20. axb5 axb5 21. Ne3 Bg6 22. Nd5 Rc5 23. Ra6 f5 24. f3 $36 {1-0 Tolnai,T-Simon,S/Budapest 1990 (31)}) 15. Qh5 (15. Nc2 f5 16. exf5 Bxf5 17. Nb4 $14) 15... b4 16. Nc2 bxc3 17. bxc3 d5 $6 (17... Qc7 18. O-O Qxc3 19. Ne3 $44 {[%cal Gg2h3]}) 18. exd5 f5 19. O-O e4 20. Rad1 Bxc3 21. d6 Qe6 22. Ne3 Bd7 23. Qg5+ Kh8 24. Bh3 Rb5 25. Nd5 Rxd5 26. Rxd5 Qxd5 27. Qe7 Kg7 28. Qxd7 Be5 29. Qxf5 Qxd6 30. Qg5+ Kh8 31. Bf5 Bg7 32. Qh4 h6 33. Qxe4 $16 {1-0 Szieberth,A-Atakisi,U/Budapest 2000(37)}) 14... Rb8 15. c3 f5 16. exf5 Bxf5 17. Nc2 a5 {Black is in time to prevent Nc4 now.} 18. Ne3 Be6 19. Qd3 f5 20. Rad1 (20. Rfd1 $5) 20... b4 (20... Bxa2 $2 21. Nxf5 $16) (20... Rb6 21. Bd5 $14) 21. Bd5 Kh8 22. Bxe6 Qxe6 {White might be slightly better, but not more than that.} 23. Nc4 $5 (23. Qxd6 Qxd6 $1 (23... Qxa2 24. Nxf5 $1 Bf6 (24... Rxf5 25. Qxb8+ $18) (24... Qxb2 $2 25. Nxg7 Kxg7 26. Qxe5+ Kg8 27. Rd7 $18) 25. cxb4 axb4 26. Ra1 Qxb2 27. Ra7 {[%csl Rh7][%cal Gd6d7]} Qb3 {[%cal Gb3g8]} 28. Rc1 $1 $16 {[%cal Gc1c7]}) 24. Rxd6 bxc3 25. bxc3 Rb2 $44) 23... f4 $6 {This weakens too much light squares, especially square e4. Black relies on the attack which will never happen.} (23... e4 {it looks more logical to open the diagonal for the bishop and although White keeps an edge in endgame after} 24. Qd5 Qxd5 25. Rxd5 bxc3 26. bxc3 Bxc3 27. Rc1 $1 Bb4 {Black must be able to hold different kind of possible rook endgames.}) 24. Nxd6 bxc3 $2 (24... f3 $2 25. Rfe1 Qh3 (25... Rf6 $2 26. Ne4 Rf5 27. Qd7 $18) 26. Qf1 $16) (24... Qxa2 {was necessary:} 25. c4 (25. Rd2 a4) 25... Qxb2 26. Qd5 $44) 25. bxc3 Qxa2 26. c4 $16 Bf6 (26... Rb2 27. Qd5 $16) 27. Qd5 Qe2 28. c5 {The c-pawn supported by the knight and queen will soon decide.} (28. Qxa5 Rb2 29. Qd5 $16) (28. Nf7+ $5 Kg7 29. Rde1 $16 {[%csl Re5]}) 28... Rb2 29. Ne4 {Black failed to create any counterplay and is lost now.} fxg3 (29... Rd8 30. Qf7 Rxd1 31. Qf8#) 30. hxg3 Kg7 31. Qc6 (31. c6 Rd8 32. Nd6 $18) 31... Qf3 32. Rd7+ Kg6 33. Qd6 Rbb8 (33... Qxe4 34. Qxf8 $18) 34. Qd5 h5 35. Rd6 Kg7 (35... h4 36. Nxf6 $18) 36. Nxf6 Rxf6 37. Qxe5 $18 Qf5 38. Qe7+ Rf7 39. Qe3 Kg8 40. c6 Rbf8 41. Qd4 Qg5 42. Qc4 Kg7 43. Rd5 Rf5 44. Rd7+ Kh6 45. c7 Rc5 46. Qa6+ 1-0

