1. e4 {Stohl,I Stohl,Igor Stohl,Igor Stohl,Igor Stohl,Igor: 'Blatny,P' Stohl,Igor: 'Adams,Michael Blatny,P'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 {Stohl,Igor: 'Adams was one of the main exponents of the Neo-Arkhangelsk with'} (5... b5 {Stohl,Igor} 6. Bb3 Bc5 $5 {However this line is under pressure nowadays, so he returned to the more classical continuations.}) 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 {Stohl,Igor: 'Judit is not afraid of the Marshall!'} (8. a4 {(Stohl,Igor) -C88}) ({Of the various anti-gambit attempts let us mention especially} 8. h3 $5 {Stohl,Igor as it was played by Kasparov himself. However, after} Bb7 9. d3 d6 10. a3 Na5 11. Ba2 c5 12. Nc3 Nc6 13. Bg5 Qd7 14. Nh2 Ne8 15. Bd2 Nc7 16. Nf1 Kh8 17. Ng3 Nd4 18. Nce2 Nde6 19. b4 $6 d5 20. bxc5 Bxc5 21. Bb4 Rfe8 $15 {Kasparov,G-Adams,M/Linares/1999/ it was only White, who had problems.}) 8... d5 {Stohl,Igor: '!' '!' '!' Stohl,Igor: 'This move appeared for the first time in 1918 in the famous Capablanca-Marshall game and is very much alive even nowadays. (As we saw above, even Garri avoids it.)' Stohl,I: '!'} 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 {Stohl,Igor: 'Marshall's former idea'} (11... Nf6 {Stohl,Igor} 12. d4 Bd6 13. Re1 Ng4 14. h3 Qh4 15. Qf3 Nxf2 16. Bd2 $1 $16 {hasn't survived the test of time, but it has been successfully replaced by the positional continuation in the text.} (16. Re2 $5 Bg4 17. hxg4 Bh2+ 18. Kf1 Bg3 19. Rxf2 Qh1+ 20. Ke2 Bxf2 21. Bd2 Bh4 22. Qh3 Rae8+ 23. Kd3 Qf1+ 24. Kc2 Bf2 25. Qf3 Qg1 26. Bd5 c5 27. dxc5 Bxc5 28. b4 Bd6 29. a4 a5 30. axb5 axb4 31. Ra6 bxc3 32. Nxc3 Bb4 33. b6 Bxc3 34. Bxc3 h6 35. b7 Re3 36. Bxf7+ {1-0, Capablanca,J-Marshall,F/New York/1918/})) 12. d3 {Stohl,Igor: 'Nowadays the most fashionable.'} ({The declining popularity of} 12. d4 {(Stohl,Igor) has been caused amongst others by the fact, that after} Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 15. Be3 Bg4 16. Qd3 Rae8 17. Nd2 Re6 18. a4 Qh5 $1 19. axb5 axb5 {it's not clear how White can get any advantage.} 20. Ne4 (20. Nf1 Bf5 $5 21. Qd2 Rfe8 22. Bd1 Qg6 23. Bf3 h5 24. Qd1 h4 25. Bd2 Rxe1 26. Bxe1 Bc2 27. Qc1 Nf4 $44 {Kruppa,Y-Ginsburg,G/Simferopol/1990/}) 20... Bf5 $1 21. Bd2 Rxe4 22. Rxe4 Nf6 23. f3 Qg6 $1 {[%cal Rf6e4,Rd6g3] e4,g3} 24. Qf1 Nxe4 25. fxe4 Bxe4 26. Bf4 Bd3 27. Qf2 Bxf4 28. Qxf4 h6 29. Qf2 Re8 30. Re1 Rxe1+ 31. Qxe1 Be4 32. Qf2 $11 {½,Svidler,P-Kamsky,G/Groningen/1995/}) 12... Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 (13... Bf5 $143 {Stohl,Igor allows White to return the pawn and play for a win without any risk after} 14. Qf3 Qd7 (14... Re8 15. Rxe8+ Qxe8 16. Nd2 Qe1+ 17. Nf1 Bg6 18. g3 Re8 19. Bd1 Qe6 20. Bd2 Qh3 21. a4 b4 {Dolmatov,S-Kamsky,G/Dortmund/1993/} 22. c4 $14) 15. Bxd5 cxd5 16. Bf4 $5 Bxf4 17. Qxf4 Bxd3 18. Nd2 Rfe8 19. h3 h6 20. Qd4 Bg6 21. Nb3 Qc6 22. Nc5 $36 {Anand,V-Adams,M/Paris Immopar/1992/}) 14. g3 Qh3 15. Re4 {Stohl,Igor: 'White prevents g4 and attempts to chase away Black's annoying . One of the benefits of d3 is that now 15...g5? is impossible.'} Qf5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Possibly the most active move. '} ({Adams has had some bad experience with} 15... Nf6 {Stohl,Igor} 16. Rh4 Qf5 17. Nd2 $1 (17. Bc2 Qg6 18. Nd2 Bg4 19. Nf3 Rfe8 20. Kg2 Nd5 21. d4 Bf5 22. Bxf5 Qxf5 $44 {[%csl Rh4] צh4} 23. Bd2 Nf6 24. Bf4 Bf8 25. Bd2 Bd6 26. Bf4 Bf8 27. Bd2 Bd6 {½,Leko,P-Adams,M/Madrid/1998/}) (17. Bf4 Be7 $1 18. d4 h6 19. Bc2 Qd5 20. Be5 Bb7 21. Bb3 Qd7 22. Nd2 c5 $40 {[%csl Rf3,Rg2,Rh3,Rh4] צh4,,Ariskin,A-Gorbunov,A/Kstovo/1998/}) 17... g5 18. Rh6 $1 (18. Rd4 $143 $6 Bc5 19. Nf3 h6 20. Bf4 {0-1 Ivanchuk,V-Timman,J/Wijk aan Zee 1999 (51)} Re8 $1 21. Bd6 Bb6 {[%cal Rc6c5] c5}) 18... Ng4 19. Ne4 Nxh6 20. Nxd6 Qg6 (20... Qh3 21. Qe2 $5 $44) 21. Ne4 Ng4 22. Bxg5 Bf5 23. Bf4 Rad8 24. Qe2 Rfe8 25. Re1 Kg7 26. f3 Ne5 27. Bc2 c5 28. Qe3 $36 {Svidler,P-Adams,M/Elista olm/1998/}) ({And he also didn't want to defend a slightly inferior endgame after} 15... Qd7 {Stohl,Igor} 16. Nd2 Bb7 17. Re1 c5 18. Ne4 Be7 19. a4 b4 20. Bg5 Bxg5 21. Nxg5 bxc3 22. bxc3 Rad8 23. Ne4 Qc6 24. Qh5 Nxc3 25. Qxc5 Rxd3 26. 