1. e4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net) Huzman: 'Huzman,Alexander Huzman,Alexander: 'Huzman Huzman''} e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. c4 Nb4 9. Be2 O-O 10. a3 Nc6 11. cxd5 Qxd5 12. Nc3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Bf5 14. Re1 Rfe8 15. Bf4 Rac8 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): ' The main opening tabia in Sofia. After two painful losses Kramnik repeats the line once again. In an interview to the Bulgarian TV he said that he did not want to finish using the opening, which served him well for years, with two losses. His aim was to avoid defeat in the his last Black game in Sofia and then, after returning home, to decide what to do with the Petroff in the future. At the press conference, I asked Vladimir what he would do if, hypothetically, the tournament would start again: to play the Sveshnikov or to stay with Petroff. Kramnik answered that of course he would playthe Sveshnikov - he can not look anymore at these Petroff positions! Below I will recall all previous Kramnik's games, played in the position after 15...ac8.  '} 16. Bd3 (16. Bg3 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Bf6 ({Note that} 16... Bd6 {was tried in another game of the 9th round: Adams-Anand. Please find it in today's game file!}) 17. Nd2 Qa5 18. Qc1 Bxd4 19. cxd4 Nxd4 20. Bc4 Nc2 21. Rxe8+ Rxe8 22. Rb1 Re1+ 23. Qxe1 Nxe1 24. Rxe1 Kf8 25. Nf3 f6 26. Rd1 Qc5 27. Bf1 Ke8 28. Nd4 Bd7 29. Rd3 a5 30. h3 b5 31. Nb3 Qxa3 32. Bxc7 a4 33. Bd6 Qb2 34. Nc5 a3 35. Re3+ Kf7 36. Nd3 Qb1 37. Bxa3 Be6 38. Nf4 b4 39. Bxb4 Qxb4 40. Nxe6 g6 41. g3 {1-0 Adams-Kramnik, Sofia 2005}) (16. Qc1 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Na5 17. c4 Qe4 18. Bd1 Qd3 19. Re3 Qxc4 20. Re5 {1-0 Anand-Kramnik, Sofia 2005}) (16. c4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Qe4 17. Be3 Qc2 18. d5 Na5 19. Nd4 Qxd1 20. Rexd1 Bd7 21. Bd2 Bf6 22. Bxa5 Bxd4 23. Rxd4 Rxe2 {½-½ Leko-Kramnik, Brissago 2004 (3)}) (16. h3 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Be4 (16... Bf6 17. Nh2 Qa5 18. Bd2 Rcd8 19. Bf3 h6 20. Ng4 Bxg4 21. hxg4 Bg5 22. Bxg5 hxg5 23. Rxe8+ Rxe8 24. Qd3 Nd8 25. g3 c6 26. Kg2 Ne6 27. Rb1 Re7 28. c4 Qc7 29. Rh1 Nf8 30. Qf5 Rd7 31. d5 cxd5 32. Bxd5 Qd8 33. a4 b6 34. a5 bxa5 35. Ra1 Qf6 36. Rxa5 Rc7 37. Qxf6 gxf6 38. Kf3 Ne6 39. Ke3 Kf8 40. f4 gxf4+ 41. gxf4 Nc5 42. Rb5 a6 43. Rb8+ Ke7 44. Ra8 Kd6 45. Kf3 Ke7 46. Kg3 Rd7 47. Rc8 Nd3 48. Be4 Nb4 49. c5 Rd8 50. Rc7+ Rd7 51. Rc8 Rd8 {½-½ Kramnik-Anand, Wijk aan Zee 2003}) 17. Be3 Na5 18. c4 Nxc4 19. Bxc4 Qxc4 20. Nd2 Qd5 21. Nxe4 Qxe4 22. Bg5 Qxe1+ 23. Qxe1 Bxg5 24. Qa5 Bf6 25. Qxa7 c5 26. Qxb7 Bxd4 27. Ra2 c4 28. Re2 Red8 29. a4 c3 30. Qe4 Bb6 31. Qc2 g6 32. Qb3 Rd6 33. Rc2 Ba5 34. g4 Rd2 35. Kg2 Rcd8 36. Rxc3 Bxc3 37. Qxc3 R2d5 38. Qc6 Ra5 39. Kg3 Rda8 40. h4 R5a6 41. Qc1 Ra5 42. Qh6 Rxa4 43. h5 R4a5 44. Qf4 g5 45. Qf6 h6 46. f3 R5a6 47. Qc3 Ra4 48. Qc6 R8a6 49. Qe8+ Kg7 50. Qb5 R4a5 51. Qb4 Rd5 52. Qb3 Rad6 53. Qc4 Rd3 54. Kf2 Ra3 55. Qc5 Ra2+ 56. Kg3 Rf6 57. Qb4 Raa6 58. Kg2 Rf4 59. Qb2+ Raf6 60. Qe5 Rxf3 61. Qa1 Rf1 62. Qc3 R1f2+ 63. Kg3 R2f3+ 64. Qxf3 Rxf3+ 65. Kxf3 Kf6 {0-1 Leko-Kramnik, Brissago 2004 (1)}) 16... b5 $146 17. Qb1 (17. a4 $5) 17... Bxd3 18. Qxd3 a6 19. a4 b4 20. Rac1 (20. c4 $5 Qd7 21. d5 Bf6 22. Rad1 Rxe1+ 23. Rxe1 Ne7 (23... a5 $5 24. Bg5 Bc3 25. Bd2 Bxd2 26. Nxd2 Ne7 27. Nb3 $14) 24. Be5 (24. Ne5 Bxe5 25. Bxe5 Ng6 $11) 24... Bxe5 25. Nxe5 Qxa4 26. Qe2 Rf8 27. Nd3 Re8 (27... Ng6 28. Nc5 $1 Qa3 29. Nd7 $18) 28. Qb2 b3 29. Qc3 Ng6 30. Rxe8+ Qxe8 31. Qxb3 Nf4 $11) (20. Qxa6 bxc3 21. Qd3 Bf6 22. Be3 c2 23. Qxc2 Nxd4 24. Bxd4 Bxd4 25. Nxd4 Qxd4 $11) 20... Bf6 21. Rxe8+ ({White have real chances for advantage after} 21. cxb4 $5 Rxe1+ (21... Nxb4 22. Qb1 a5 23. Rxc7 Rxe1+ 24. Qxe1 $16) 22. Rxe1 Nxb4 23. Qb1 $1 c5 (23... Nc6 24. Qb7 $16) 24. dxc5 $16) 21... Rxe8 22. cxb4 Nxb4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): ''} 23. Qc3 ({Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): 'Both players mentioned'} 23. Qb1 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): '!?' Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): 'as a serious alternative.'} a5 24. Rxc7 (24. Bxc7 Bxd4 25. Re1 Rxe1+ 26. Qxe1 Bc5 27. Qe8+ Bf8 28. h3 Nd3 $11) 24... Bxd4 $11) 23... Na2 (23... Qf5 24. Bg3 Nd5 25. Qc6 Qe6 26. Qxe6 fxe6 27. Kf1 Rb8 28. Bxc7 Rb4 29. a5 Rb5 $14) 24. Re1 (24. Qc4 $5 Qe4 25. Qxa2 Qxf4 26. Qc4 g6 27. a5 Rd8 28. Qxc7 Qxc7 29. Rxc7 Rd5 $1 (29... Bxd4 30. Kf1 $14 Bf6 31. Rc6 Ra8 32. Nd2 $14) 30. Rc5 Rxc5 31. dxc5 Kf8 $1 32. Nd2 Ke7 33. c6 Bc3 34. Ne4 Bxa5 35. Nc5 Bb6 36. Nxa6 Kd6 $11) 24... Rxe1+ 25. Qxe1 h6 26. Bxc7 Bxd4 $11 27. h3 Bf6 28. Qe8+ Kh7 29. Be5 Qd1+ (29... Bxe5 30. Qxe5 (30. Nxe5 Qe4 $15) 30... Qd1+ 31. Kh2 Qxa4 32. Qf5+ $11) 30. Kh2 Qe2 31. Qb8 Bxe5+ 32. Qxe5 Qxe5+ 33. Nxe5 f6 34. Nd3 Kg6 35. Kg3 Kf5 36. Kf3 Nc3 37. a5 Nb5 38. g4+ Kg5 39. Nc5 f5 40. Nxa6 fxg4+ 41. hxg4 Nd4+ 42. Kg3 Nc6 43. Nc7 Nxa5 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): 'The players were allowed to agree to a draw. Indeed, the continuation'} 44. Ne8 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): '='} g6 $4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} 45. f4# {was not likely to happen. At the press conference, Polgar said that it was really nice to pose some problems for Kramnik, but she hardly thought at any moment that she should win. She does not feel either especially happy or unhappy with her overall performance (50%). Somewhere she missed chances, somewhere her opponents did. "It's balanced. It's fifty per cent", said Judit. She also said that Topalov seems to play the most interesting chess among top players. "He also makes mistakes, but he fights, and he has news ideas"... Kramnik confessed that he still not fully [physically] recovered after the Brissago match. Of course, he feels better than after the match, but still not everything is OK. He needs to improve and he will work on improvement. He hopes to play in Dortmund "much better" than in Sofia. He was asked, when, during the last 10 years, did he share the last place in a tournament. Kramnik recalled Novgorod-1996, the similar tournament where he scored -1 and shared 4th-6th places. But he still has one game to play in Sofia, said Vladimir.} 1/2-1/2

