1. e4 {Marin,M Marin,M} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. O-O {Marin,M: 'I examined this Anti-Berlin variation in my Opening Database from CBM 120.' I examined this Anti-Berlin variation in my Opening Database from CBM 120.} d6 {Marin,M: 'In the article, I recommended Black to delay this move for a while, in order to be able to react with ...d5 under certain cricumstances. However, the game move was not considered unplayable either.' In the article, I recommended Black to delay this move for a while, in order to be able to react with ...d5 under certain cricumstances. However, the game move was not considered unplayable either.} 6. c3 O-O 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 ({Marin,M: 'I considered' I considered} 9. Nxg5 {Marin,M: 'to be the critical line, as in Akopian,V (2704)-Georgiev, K (2645)/Moscow 2006/CBM 112/[Marin; Postny,E] (0-1, 38)' to be the critical line, as in Akopian,V (2704)-Georgiev,K (2645)/Moscow 2006/CBM 112/[Marin; Postny,E] (0-1, 38)}) 9... Nh5 $146 {Marin,M: 'Strangely enough, this natural move, aiming to obtain the advantage of the pair of bishops and increase the sphere of activity of Black's own dark-squared bishop is a novelty.' Strangely enough, this natural move, aiming to obtain the advantage of the pair of bishops and increase the sphere of activity of Black's own dark-squared bishop is a novelty.} ({Marin,M: 'In a game played after my article was published' In a game played after my article was published} 9... Bg4 {Marin,M: 'was played.' was played.} 10. Nbd2 Qe7 11. Re1 Nd8 12. d4 exd4 13. e5 $1 dxe5 14. cxd4 Bxd4 15. Qc2 Bxf3 16. Nxf3 Ne6 17. Nxe5 $44 Nh5 ({Marin,M: 'Maybe he should have continued developing with' Maybe he should have continued developing with} 17... Rad8) 18. Ng6 $1 $14 {1-0 Akopian,V (2713)-Gyimesi,Z (2610)/Crete 2007/CBM 121 (29). Marin,M: '1-0 Akopian, V (2713)-Gyimesi,Z (2610)/Crete 2007/CBM 121 (29). One small remark: if Akopian did not repeat the experiment of sacrificing on g5, something might be wrong with it.' One small remark: if Akopian did not repeat the experiment of sacrificing on g5, something might be wrong with it.}) 10. d4 Bb6 11. dxe5 (11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. dxe5 Nxg3 13. hxg3 g4 14. Nh4 dxe5 {Marin,M: 'leads to similar consequences as in the game. The strong b6-bishop and the possible plan of advancing the g- and h-pawns compensate for the pawn weaknesses.' leads to similar consequences as in the game. The strong b6-bishop and the possible plan of advancing the g- and h-pawns compensate for the pawn weaknesses.}) 11... Nxg3 12. hxg3 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 14. Nd2 ({Marin,M: 'If White exchanges queens with' If White exchanges queens with} 14. Qxd8 Rxd8 {Marin,M: 'in order to avoid the attacking plan, the development of the queenside would suddenly become difficult to achieve, because the d2-square is not protected.' in order to avoid the attacking plan, the development of the queenside would suddenly become difficult to achieve, because the d2-square is not protected.}) 14... Qf6 15. Qe2 ({Marin,M: 'Unfortunately, White cannot maintain the control of the d1-h5 diagonal with' Unfortunately, White cannot maintain the control of the d1-h5 diagonal with} 15. Be2 Rd8 16. Bh5 $2 {Marin,M: 'because of' because of} g4 $1 17. Bxg4 Qg5 $19 {[%csl Gd2,Gg4]}) 15... c6 16. Bc4 {Marin,M: 'This square would be useful for the knight's transfer to e3.' This square would be useful for the knight's transfer to e3.} (16. Ba4 $5 Kg7 17. Nc4 {[%csl Gb6,Gf5][%cal Gc4e3,Gc4b6] Marin,M: 'and White should not be worse.' and White should not be worse.}) 16... Kg7 17. Bb3 (17. g4 $2 {[%cal Gg2g3,Gg1g2]} Qf4 $1 $19 {[%csl Gg4]}) 17... h5 $36 {Marin,M: 'Black's attacking plan is hard to counter now.' Black's attacking plan is hard to counter now.} 18. Nc4 (18. Qxh5 $2 Rh8 19. Qf3 {[%csl Gf7]} Qg6 {[%cal Gg5g4,Gg6h5]} 20. g4 Rh4 $19 {[%csl Gg4]}) 18... Bg4 19. Qe1 Bc5 $1 {Marin,M: 'Black should keep this bishop on the active diagonal, no matter how many tempi he will lose in achieving this aim.' Black should keep this bishop on the active diagonal, no matter how many tempi he will lose in achieving this aim.} 20. Ne3 Rh8 21. Bd1 Be6 22. Bb3 Bg4 23. Bd1 Rh7 $1 {Marin,M: 'It appears that the bishop is taboo, because White cannot afford to open the h-file.' It appears that the bishop is taboo, because White cannot afford to open the h-file.} 24. b4 (24. Bxg4 hxg4 25. Nxg4 Rah8 $19) (24. Nxg4 hxg4 25. Bxg4 Rah8 26. Bh3 Rxh3 27. gxh3 Qf3 28. Kh2 g4 29. h4 Rxh4+ 30. gxh4 Qh3+ 31. Kg1 g3 $19) 24... Bb6 25. a4 a6 26. a5 Ba7 27. Ra2 Rah8 28. Rd2 Bc8 {Marin,M: 'Both sides have completed their regroupment, but White's king's position remains dangerous.' Both sides have completed their regroupment, but White's king's position remains dangerous.} 29. Nf5+ {Marin,M: 'The only way to reduce Black's attacking potential.' The only way to reduce Black's attacking potential.} Bxf5 30. exf5 g4 31. Qe4 {Marin,M: 'It is hard to criticize this move, because the only possibility of staying alive was far from obvious.' It is hard to criticize this move, because the only possibility of staying alive was far from obvious.} ({Marin,M: 'The king should have been evacuated from the dangerous diagonal with' The king should have been evacuated from the dangerous diagonal with} 31. Kh2 $1 {Marin,M: ', when' , when} h4 {Marin,M: 'does not yet have deadly effects, for instance' does not yet have deadly effects, for instance} 32. Bxg4 hxg3+ 33. Kxg3 {Marin,M: 'and, for once, the presence of opposite coloured bishops favours the defending side, because the a7-bishop has lost its main target already.' and, for once, the presence of opposite coloured bishops favours the defending side, because the a7-bishop has lost its main target already.}) 31... h4 $19 {Marin,M: 'Now, Black is just winning.' Now, Black is just winning.} 32. Bxg4 hxg3 33. Bh3 Rh4 34. Qd3 Qg5 35. f6+ Kxf6 36. Rfd1 Bxf2+ 37. Rxf2+ gxf2+ 38. Kxf2 Kg7 39. Qe3 Rf4+ 40. Ke2 Rhh4 41. Qd3 Re4+ 42. Kf2 Qf4+ 43. Kg1 Re3 0-1

