1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. b3 Ng6 {The knight covers f4 and e5 from here, while it also enables Black to blunt the long diagonal without having to worry about Nh4 in reply in reply to ...f6.} ({However, as well as The knight covers f4 and e5 from here, while it also enables Black to blunt the long diagonal without having to worry about Nh4 in reply in reply to ...f6. However, as well as} 5... a6 6. Bxc6 Nxc6 7. Bb2 {which we'll deal with in the next two games, which we'll deal with in the next two games, Black has also tried to close down the bishop with}) ({Black has also tried to close down the bishop with} 5... Nd4 $6 6. Nxd4 cxd4 {, but then} 7. c3 a6 ({or} 7... Qb6 8. Na3 Ng6 9. Be2 Be7 10. Nb5 $1 dxc3 11. Nxc3 {with an edge as White will regain the d4-square after} Qd4 12. Bb2 O-O 13. Qc2 {- Golubev – Golubev}) 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. cxd4 $5 Nxd4 ({or} 9... d5 10. exd5 Qxd5 11. Bb2 Be7 12. Na3 $1 {with the better development for White, whose his advantage grew after} Bf6 $2 13. Nc4 Bxd4 14. Be4 $1 Qd8 15. Ba3 {in V.Jansa-E.Sveshnikov, Gausdal 1992}) 10. Bb2 Bc5 11. Qh5 d6 12. e5 $1 {is fairly promising and makes good use of the white bishops, while even} Nf5 $1 13. Bxf5 exf5 14. d4 Ba7 15. Rd1 {left White with the edge in A.Delchev-B.Macieja, European Ch., Silivri 2003.}) 6. Bb2 f6 $1 {The best way of handling the position,} ({whereas The best way of handling the position whereas} 6... d6 7. Re1 Bd7 8. Bxc6 $1 Bxc6 9. d4 cxd4 10. Nxd4 {leaves it difficult for Black to unravel his kingside and} Qg5 $6 11. Re3 h5 12. Nd2 $1 Bd7 13. N4f3 Qd8 14. a4 $1 Qc7 15. Nc4 {also left d6 vulnerable in V.Laznicka-A.Czebe, Steinbrunn 2005.}) 7. Re1 Qc7 $5 {Continuing to fight for control of e5,} ({but an immediate <xml:en>Continuing to fight for control of e5, but an immediate</:en/xml>} 7... Be7 {is also playable when White has preferred <xml:en>is also playable when White has preferred</:en/xml>} 8. c3 {, but} ({White hasn't been keen to try} 8. Bxc6 bxc6 9. e5 {, probably because of} O-O $1 {, followed by exchanges on e5 and then ...d6 and ...e5}) {, but} 8... O-O 9. d4 a6 10. Bf1 d5 {is a reasonable response which has been used by Wells and Krasenkow. The IQP positions have been shown to be fine for Black and so White shouldn't hurry to exchange on d5 and c5, while} 11. Nbd2 $5 cxd4 12. cxd4 Bd7 13. a3 $1 Bd6 14. b4 {, intending to exchange on d5 and then go Nd2-b3-c5 or Nd2-b1-c3, gave White good chances for an edge in V.Akopian-P.Wells, Lausanne 2004. , intending to exchange on d5 and then go Nd2-b3-c5 or Nd2-b1–c3, gave White good chances for an edge in V.Akopian-P.Wells, Lausanne 2004.}) 8. Bxc6 $6 {Now play is similar to the 4 Bxc6 lines and Black soon gains a strong centre.} ({Superior is Now play is similar to the 4 Bxc6 lines and Black soon gains a strong centre. Superior is} 8. c3 Be7 9. d4 O-O 10. Bf1 $1 {when Black is a little cramped and} a6 11. g3 b5 12. h4 $1 Bb7 13. h5 Nh8 14. d5 {saw White increase his useful space advantage and retain the advantage in V.Akopian-A.Fominyh, Ubeda 2001.}) 8... bxc6 9. d3 {Continuing to drift, and if even a member of the world's elite can then drifting is all too easy to do in this structure!} ({Instead White had to challenge in the centre with Continuing to drift, and if even a member of the world's elite can then drifting is all too easy to do in this structure! Instead White had to challenge in the centre with} 9. e5 $5 Be7 10. d4 {.}) 9... d6 10. Nbd2 Be7 11. Nc4 O-O 12. Bc3 e5 {The ideal central advance and already Black is a little better with White forced to try and contain the opposition centre. <xml:en>The ideal central advance and already Black is a little better with White forced to try and contain the opposition centre.</:en/xml>} 13. Ba5 Qb7 14. Bd2 f5 15. Bg5 Be6 16. Na5 $1 Qd7 17. Bxe7 Qxe7 $1 18. b4 {Polgar has deprived Black of the bishop-pair and contains to play actively to distract Tsesarsky from the kingside,} ({whereas <xml:en>Polgar has deprived Black of the bishop-pair and contains to play actively to distract Tsesarsky from the kingside whereas</:en/xml>} 18. Nxc6 $2 Qc7 19. Ng5 Nf4 $1 20. g3 h6 {would have trapped and won the c6-knight. <xml:en>would have trapped and won the c6-knight.</:en/xml>}) 18... cxb4 19. Nxc6 Qb7 20. Na5 Qb6 $6 {Tsesarsky was understandably unhappy with this which squanders most of his advantage as now the knight gets to come to c4 with tempo,} ({whereas his Tsesarsky was understandably unhappy with this which squanders most of his advantage as now the knight gets to come to c4 with tempo, whereas his} 20... Qc7 $1 21. Ng5 Nf4 22. Nxe6 Nxe6 {would have prevented} 23. Nc4 $2 {due to simply} ({but White is left somewhat worse after} 23. Nb3 a5) {due to simply} 23... fxe4 {.}) 21. Ng5 $1 Nf4 22. Nxe6 Nxe6 23. Nc4 Qc5 24. exf5 Rxf5 25. Ne3 Rf4 26. f3 Nd4 27. a3 Nf5 ({White now gets to make a draw and the last chance was} 27... bxa3 $1 28. Qc1 Nf5 29. Qxa3 Qxa3 30. Rxa3 Nxe3 31. Rxe3 a5 {(Tsesarsky), retaining an edge due to the a-pawn.}) 28. axb4 Rxb4 29. c3 Rb2 30. d4 Nxe3 31. dxc5 Nxd1 32. Raxd1 dxc5 33. Rxe5 a5 34. Rd7 a4 35. Ree7 a3 36. Rxg7+ Kh8 37. Rxh7+ Kg8 38. Rhg7+ Kh8 39. Rh7+ Kg8 40. Rdg7+ Kf8 41. Rb7 $5 Kg8 $1 42. Rbg7+ 1/2-1/2

