1. e4 {Wedberg Wedberg Ftacnik} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 {Milos tries something new against the amazon.} (11... Bb7 12. Nbd2 cxd4 13. cxd4 exd4 14. Nxd4 Re8 15. b3 Bf8 16. Bb2 g6 17. Qf3 Bg7 18. Rad1 Rc8 19. Bb1 Nc6 {1-0 Polgar,J-Milos,G/Buenos Aires 2000/CBM 80/[Wedberg] (33)}) 12. Nbd2 cxd4 13. cxd4 Rd8 {This is a dangerous line for White if he continues with the standard manouvre f1. But Judits response is the best, giving White a slight plus.} 14. b3 $1 {White has time for this since there is no immediate pressure on the c-file. In lines where Black can play c8, and threaten c2 followed by c3, b3 is a mistake. Here it solves all White's problems.} (14. Nf1 $6 exd4 15. Nxd4 d5 16. e5 Ne4 17. f3 Bc5 $1 $36 {very difficult for White is the verdict after several tests.}) (14. d5 {Preventing a black d5, but Black has no problems when the tension is released so early.} Bd7 15. Nf1 Rdc8 16. Bd3 Nb7 17. Ng3 Nc5 $11) 14... Nc6 15. Bb2 exd4 16. Nxd4 Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Bb7 (17... Be6 18. Rc1 Qa5 19. Bb1 $14 {in a number of games.}) 18. Rc1 (18. b4 $5 Rac8 19. Rc1 Qb8 20. a3 Re8 21. Nf1 Bd8 22. Ng3 $36 {With nice play on the -side. Black has no counterplay since d5 always is punished by e5. 1-0 Korneev,O-Fernandez Garcia,J/Elgoibar 2000/CBM 81 (48)}) 18... Qa5 (18... Rac8 $2 {allows a little tactic} 19. e5 $1 Qc6 20. Bxh7+ $18 {1-0 Wang Pin-Garcia,G/Ubeda 1998/CBM 62 ext (36)}) 19. Bb1 Rac8 20. Rxc8 Rxc8 21. Nf1 Re8 (21... g6 22. Qe2 Re8 23. Rd1 Qc7 $13 {½-½ Moreno Carnero,J-Fernandez Garcia,J/Dos Hermanas 2001/CBM 83 (48)}) 22. Ne3 $1 $146 {[%mdl 8]} (22. Re3 Qd8 23. Ng3 g6 24. Qd2 Bf8 25. Qb2 Bg7 26. f4 Rf8 27. Kh2 h5 28. b4 h4 29. Nh1 d5 30. e5 Ne4 $132 {1-0 Dolmatov,S-Polgar,Z/New York 1989/CBM 12 (61)}) 22... g6 {Ftacnik: 'The whole variation promises black active pieces, but the daigonal a1-h8 and the attacking potential of the white's army favorize Polgar.'} (22... Nxe4 $2 23. Nf5 Bf6 24. Bxe4 Rxe4 25. Rxe4 Bxe4 26. Qe2 $18) (22... Bxe4 $2 23. Bxf6 Bxf6 24. Nd5 $18) 23. Re2 Qd8 {Black prudently avoids taking the offered  on e4. Even if there is no immediate refutation for greed, Black has a long defence to prove his point.} (23... Nxe4 $6 24. Ng4 $1 $40) 24. Bb2 Bf8 (24... Bxe4 $2 25. Bxe4 Nxe4 26. Nd5 $18) 25. b4 Nh5 (25... Bxe4 $6 26. Bxe4 Rxe4 27. Nd5 $16) (25... Bg7 $5) 26. Ng4 Qg5 27. Bc2 Bc8 {This leads to the exchange of a  for a . The simplification of the position doesn't help Black, since White has clear plus in the ending as well. Ftacnik: 'Black may not have a road to equality, but at least he is doing maximum in an attempt to complicate the opponent start of an attack.'} (27... d5 28. e5 Bxb4 29. Qd4 Nf4 30. Re3 Be7 (30... Bc5 $2 31. Qxc5 Rc8 32. Qd4 Rxc2 33. e6 Rxb2 34. Nf6+ $18) 31. e6 f6 {[%cal Yh7h5] Not a tempting position for Black, but White has the burden of proof.}) 28. Re3 Bg7 (28... Bxg4 29. Qxg4 Qxg4 30. hxg4 $14 {[%csl Gb2,Gc2]}) 29. Bxg7 Kxg7 30. Qd4+ {'!' Ftacnik.} Kg8 31. Qxd6 Bxg4 32. hxg4 Nf4 $2 {'!' Ftacnik.} (32... Qxg4 33. g3 $5 $14 (33. Qxa6 $2 Nf4 34. g3 Rc8 $19) (33. Bd1 Qg5 34. Bxh5 Qxh5 35. Qxa6 Qd1+ 36. Kh2 Qh5+ $11)) 33. g3 Nh3+ 34. Kg2 Qxg4 35. f3 Qg5 {'?' Ftacnik. Ftacnik: 'The tragic mistake, black queen remains optically close to the straited knight, but now he will never get out.'} (35... Qc8 $1 {Ftacnik} 36. Qc5 (36. Bb3 Ng5 $13) 36... Ng5 37. Qxc8 Rxc8 38. Bb3 Kf8 39. Rd3 $14) 36. Re2 $16 {Black's pieces are all on bad circuits now. White dominates.} Qh5 37. Qd7 Re6 38. Qc8+ {'!' Ftacnik.} Kg7 39. Qc3+ Kg8 40. Bb3 Re8 41. Re1 (41. Re1 Ng5 42. Rh1 $18) 1-0

