1. e4 {Marin,M Lukacs/Hazai Lukacs/Hazai} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. Nf1 Bf8 14. Ng3 g6 15. b3 ({This modern approach is a worthy alternative to the traditional} 15. a4 c5 16. d5 c4) 15... d5 $5 {RR: 'Marin,M: '!?'' A logical reaction. RR: 'Marin,M: 'A logical reaction.''} ({White's main idea is that after} 15... Bg7 16. d5 {the move b3 proves useful by enabling a further consolidation of the centre by means of c4, while the bishop move has left the d6-pawn undefended, which causes Black some delay with the planned ...c6.}) 16. Bg5 {The start of an interesting phase of the game, in which neither side should hurry to release the tension in the centre. RR: 'Marin,M: 'The start of an interesting phase of the game, in which neither side should hurry to release the tension in the centre.''} h6 17. Bh4 {This provocative move is the only way to fight for an advantage. RR: 'Marin,M: 'This provocative move is the only way to fight for an advantage.''} g5 ({Black has nothing else left than accept the challenge. The pin of the f6-knight is quite unpleasant.} 17... dxe4 $2 18. Nxe4 g5 19. dxe5 $1 {1-0 Polgar,J (2595)-Spassky,B (2565)/Budapest 1993/CBM 033/[Ftacnik] (42)}) 18. Nxg5 hxg5 19. Bxg5 exd4 {Lukacs/Hazai: '!' '!' RR: '!?'} (19... Be7 20. Nf5 $40) (19... dxe4 {RR: 'Ü 58/377'} 20. Bxe4 $1 {1-0 Sherzer,A (2500)-Lesiege,A (2485)/Biel 1993/CBM 037/[Wolff] (47)}) 20. e5 $1 {RR: 'Marin,M: '!'' The only ay to keep the attack going. RR: 'Marin,M: 'The only ay to keep the attack going.''} Rxe5 21. Rxe5 Nxe5 22. cxd4 (22. Qxd4 Qd6 23. Re1 Ne4 24. Bf4 Re8 $17 {0-1 Navara,D (2663)-Socko,B (2615)/Ermioni Argolidas 2005/CBM 107 ext (41)}) 22... Nc6 {Lukacs/Hazai: '!' '!' RR: '!' RR: '*' Not only keeping the d4-pawn under pressure, but allowing the free circulation of the own queen as well. Lukacs/Hazai: 'This looks best, 22...ed7 is simply passive. Now Black has pressure against × d4 and sometimes ...b4 may come. ' RR: 'Marin,M: 'Not only keeping the d4-pawn under pressure, but allowing the free circulation of the own queen as well.''} ({It might seem more logical to keep the f6-knight overdefended with} 22... Ned7 {[%csl Yd8] , but this move seems to be too passive and leave White with dangerous initiative.} 23. Qf3 (23. Nf5 Bg7 24. Qd3 $40 {Rogers,I}) 23... Qe7 24. Bf5 {[%csl Rd7,Yf5,Rf6,Yg5]} (24. Nh5 $5 Bg7 25. Nxg7 Kxg7 26. Qg3 $36) 24... Qd6 25. Bf4 Qe7 26. Rc1 c6 $2 (26... c5 $5 {[%cal Yd6b6]} 27. Bg5 Qd6) 27. Bg5 Qd6 28. Nh5 $1 $40 {[%csl Gd6]} Bg7 (28... Re8 29. Bf4 Qe7 30. Qg3+ Kh8 31. Qh4 Kg8 32. Qg5+ Kh8 33. Nxf6 Nxf6 34. Be5 Bg7 35. Rc3 $18) (28... Nxh5 29. Qxh5 Bg7 30. Qh7+ Kf8 31. Bxd7 Qxd7 32. Bh6 Bxh6 33. Qxh6+ Ke7 34. Re1+ Kd8 35. Qf6+ Kc8 36. Re7 $18) 29. Nxg7 Kxg7 30. Bf4 Qe7 31. Qg3+ Kh8 32. Qh4+ Kg8 33. Bxd7 Qxd7 34. Qxf6 Qe6 35. Qg5+ {1-0 Navara-Socko, Ustron 2006}) 23. Nf5 $6 $146 {Lukacs/Hazai: '?' '?' RR: 'N?' RR: 'Marin,M: 'N?!'' An unfortunate novelty. Lukacs/Hazai: 'Judit wanted to avoid the main lines which usually end with perpetual check, but this over the board novelty was a bad choice.' RR: 'Marin,M: 'An unfortunate novelty.''} ({The critical continuation is} 23. Nh5 {Lukacs/Hazai: '!' '!' when White has several ways of reaching a draw by perpetual, but has failed to prove a method of retaining a concrete advantage yet.} Be7 24. Bxf6 Bxf6 25. Qd3 Bxd4 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Ke8 28. Re1+ Ne7 29. Qg5 (29. Ng7+ Kd7 30. Nf5 Nc6 31. Nxd4 Nxd4 32. Qf4 Nxc2 33. Qxf7+ Kc6 34. Qg6+ Kc5 35. Qxc2+ Kb6 36. Re6+ c6 $17 {0-1 Fedorov,A (2602)-Inarkiev,E (2612)/Sochi 2005/CBM 107 (60)}) 29... Kf8 30. Bh7 {[%csl Gg8][%cal Ye1e7]} Bc3 (30... Bh8 $2 31. Qh6+ Ke8 32. Bf5 Bc3 33. Ng7+ Kf8 34. Ne6+ Ke8 35. Qf8+ Kd7 36. Nxd8+ Nxf5 37. Qe8+ Kc8 38. Nxf7#) 31. h4 {[%csl Gg5][%cal Ye1e7]} (31. Rxe7 {[%csl Ge7][%cal Yh5g3,Yg3f5,Rg5h6]} Bh8 $1 32. Ng3 $6 f6 $1 {[%csl Gg7]}) 31... Qd6 (31... Bxe1 $2 32. Qg7+ Ke8 33. Nf6#) (31... a5 {[%cal Ya8a6]} 32. Qh6+ Ke8 33. Bf5 $1 {[%cal Yh5g7]} Bc8 34. Qc6+ Kf8 35. Qxc3 $16) 32. Rxe7 Re8 {White has managed to win the sacrificed material back, but Black can also be satisfied with his achievements: he has completed his development and the pair of bishops could become dangerous in the long run. However, the main factor remains the complex tension surrounding the e7-square, which reminds us to mutual zugzwang. 1/2-1/2 Landa,K (2574)-Kosyrev,V (2539)/playchess.com 2005/CBM 106 ext (48).}) (23. a3 $143 {Lukacs/Hazai} Qd6 24. Nf5 Qe6 25. Qf3 Ne4 $19) (23. Rc1 $143 {Lukacs/Hazai} Qd6 24. Qf3 Ne4 (24... Nxd4)) (23. Qd2 $143 {Lukacs/Hazai} Qd6 24. Nf5 Qe6 25. Qf4 Ne4) (23. Qd3 {Lukacs/Hazai}) 23... Bc8 $1 $17 {Lukacs/Hazai: '!' '!' RR: '!' RR: 'Marin,M: '!'' After the exchange of the white knight, the attack will be easier to beat off. RR: 'Marin,M: 'After the exchange of the white knight, the attack will be easier to beat off.''} 24. Rc1 Bxf5 25. Bxf5 Qd6 {RR: '-+' Lukacs/Hazai: 'White has no real attacking chances for the sacrificed material. '} 26. Bh4 Ne7 27. Bg3 Qb4 28. Bb1 c6 29. Be5 Bg7 {Lukacs/Hazai: '' '' Lukacs/Hazai: 'and the black  is well-protected.'} 30. a3 {Lukacs/Hazai: ' c3'} Qxa3 31. Rc3 Nd7 32. Rg3 (32. Bxg7 {Lukacs/Hazai} Kxg7 33. Rg3+ Kf8 34. Qh5 Ke8 $19) 32... Nxe5 33. dxe5 Qb4 34. Rg4 Qc3 35. f4 d4 36. Kh2 Nd5 37. f5 d3 38. Qxd3 Qxe5+ 39. Rg3 Nf6 40. Qf3 Rd8 {RR: '0-1 (40) Polgar,J (2710)-Mamedyarov,S (2728) Hoogeveen 2006 CBM 115 [Marin,M]'} 0-1

