1. e4 {Wedberg Wedberg} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 c5 13. d5 (13. Nf1 {is more common, but often it's just a transposition, since d5 comes later anyway.}) 13... g6 14. Nf1 a5 $146 {[%mdl 8] In the main line White often takes the initiative on the -side with a timely a2-a4. So Sokolov concentrates on the -side right away.} (14... Nh5 15. Bh6 Ng7 16. Ne3 Nf6 17. a4 Kh8 18. b3 Rb8 19. Qe2 Bc8 20. axb5 axb5 21. Ra7 $14 {1-0 Karpov,A-Gligoric,S/Leningrad 1973/IZT/[ChessBase] (62)}) 15. a4 b4 {White is invited to close the -side.} 16. Bd3 (16. c4 {is possible, but it gives Black a clear picture of what to do (play on the -side). Judit prefers to keep more options.}) 16... Qc7 17. Ne3 bxc3 18. bxc3 c4 19. Bc2 (19. Nxc4 Ba6 $132) 19... Ba6 20. Nd2 Rfc8 21. Ba3 Bf8 22. Qf3 Bg7 23. g3 Rab8 24. Kg2 Nb6 25. g4 $1 {White must start to attack before Black manages to open lines on the other side.} Nfd7 26. h4 Nc5 {[%cal Yc5d3]} 27. Bxc5 $1 {Else Black jumps in on d3 with serious play coming.} Qxc5 28. h5 Nd7 $6 (28... Bh6 $5 29. Qf6 (29. Rh1 Bf4 30. hxg6 fxg6 31. Qh3 Rc7) 29... Nd7 30. Qh4 Bf4 31. hxg6 fxg6 32. g5 Rb2 33. Rh1 Nf8 34. Ndf1 Qc7 $15) 29. Rh1 Nf8 30. g5 $1 $14 {Black is subdued on the -side and can only wait to see how White is going to increase the pressure against the .} Rb2 31. Ng4 gxh5 (31... Rxc2 32. h6 f5 (32... Bh8 33. Nf6+ $18) 33. gxf6 Bxf6 (33... Bh8 $4 34. f7#) 34. Nxf6+ Kh8 35. Nd7 $1 Nxd7 36. Qf7 Rg8 37. Qxd7 Qb6 38. Rhb1 Qd8 39. Qxd8 Rxd8 40. Rb6 Rxd2 41. Rxa6 $16) 32. Nf6+ Bxf6 33. gxf6 Ng6 (33... Rxc2 34. Qxh5 Rxd2 35. Qg5+ Ng6 36. Qxd2 Nf4+ 37. Kf3 $18 {Black is helpless against the attack on the h-file.}) 34. Kf1 h4 35. Qh5 Kh8 36. Nf3 Rxc2 37. Rh2 $1 Rxf2+ (37... Nf4 38. Qxh4 h5 39. Qg5 Rxf2+ 40. Rxf2 Qxf2+ 41. Kxf2 Nh3+ 42. Ke3 Nxg5 43. Nxg5 $18) 38. Rxf2 Nf4 39. Qxh4 Rg8 40. Rh2 1-0

