1. e4 {Ftacnik} c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 Qb6 {Black is reacting to the less standard line of his opponent with an equally atypical idea - the first piece developed is the queen.} 4. Nc3 dxe4 5. fxe4 e5 {The fight against the mighty pawn centre is part of the compulsory program for Black.} 6. dxe5 (6. Nf3 exd4 7. Qxd4 (7. Nxd4 Bc5 8. Na4 Qa5+ 9. c3 Be7 10. b4 Qe5 11. Bd3 Nf6 12. O-O O-O 13. Bf4 Qh5 14. Qe1 Re8 15. Qg3 $16 {1-0 Vitiugov,N (2555)-Janjgava,L (2479)/Martuni 2006 (35)}) 7... Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Nf5 (9. Nb3 Bd6 10. Be3 Nf6 11. O-O-O Be5 12. Nd4 Nbd7 13. Bc4 Ng4 14. Bg1 Bf4+ 15. Kb1 Nde5 16. Bb3 $11 {½-½ Gergacz,A (2425)-Kreisl,R (2376)/Austria 2010 (43)}) 9... g6 10. Be3 Bxe3 11. Nxe3 Be6 12. Nc4 Bxc4 13. Bxc4 Nd7 14. O-O Ne5 15. Bb3 Rd8 16. Rf2 Ne7 17. Raf1 Rf8 18. Ne2 Nc8 19. Nf4 $11 {½-½ Benjamin,J (2575)-Kacheishvili,G (2606)/ICC INT 2009 (75)}) 6... Be6 $146 {The weakened diagonal a7-g1 can have a magic attraction, but even the battery of two pieces would not promise any gains.} (6... Bc5 7. Nf3 (7. Bc4 {Ftacnik,L: '!'} Bxg1 8. Qf3 Be6 9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. Bg5 (10. Ne2) 10... Qf2+ 11. Qxf2 Bxf2+ 12. Kxf2 Nd7 13. Ne2 Nxe5 14. Nd4 Nf6 15. Rhe1 Kf7 16. Nf3 Nxf3 17. gxf3 e5 $11 {½-½ Christiansen,L (2585)-Benjamin,J (2556)/ICC INT 2010 (42)}) 7... Be6 8. Bd3 Be3 9. Na4 Qa5+ 10. b4 Qxa4 11. Bxe3 Qxb4+ 12. Bd2 Qa4 13. Rb1 Nd7 14. Rxb7 Ne7 15. Bb4 c5 16. Bxc5 Qc6 17. Bxe7 Qxb7 18. Bd6 Qb6 19. a4 $14 {1-0 Bosiocic,M (2503)-Predojevic,B (2651)/Plovdiv 2008 (36)}) 7. Nf3 Nd7 {*} 8. Bd3 (8. Nd4 Nxe5 (8... O-O-O 9. Nxe6 fxe6 10. Qg4 Bc5 11. Bc4 Ne7 12. Rf1 Ng6 13. Bxe6 Ngxe5 14. Qg3 Bd4 15. Ne2 $14) 9. Nxe6 fxe6 10. Qh5+ Ng6 11. Bd3 O-O-O $11) 8... Ne7 {Ragger is still improving in his chess development. His patient method of counterplay against the e5-pawn makes a very logical impression.} 9. Qe2 $6 (9. Na4 Qa5+ (9... Qc7 10. O-O O-O-O $11) 10. Qd2 Qxd2+ 11. Bxd2 Ng6 12. Nd4 Ngxe5 13. Nxe6 fxe6 $11) 9... Ng6 $11 10. Bd2 Ndxe5 (10... Qxb2 11. Rb1 Qa3 12. Rxb7 Bb4 13. Nb1 Qc1+ 14. Qd1 Qxd1+ 15. Kxd1 Bc5 16. Nc3 $11) 11. Nd5 $2 {Polgar's solution to positional problems is often tied to a tactical mess created on the board. The sharp blow in the centre has no positional grounds, it was better to live with relative failure and castle.} (11. O-O-O O-O-O 12. Kb1 $15) 11... Nxd3+ $6 {[%mdl 256] Black did not find the courage to seize the tigress by the tail and refute her main line. Objectively the best was to daringly take the pawn b2 with a nearly winning position.} (11... Qxb2 $1 12. Bc3 (12. O-O cxd5 13. Bb5+ Kd8 14. exd5 Nxf3+ 15. Qxf3 Qxb5 16. dxe6 fxe6 $19) 12... Nxf3+ 13. gxf3 Bb4 14. O-O (14. Kd2 cxd5 $19) 14... Bxc3 15. Rab1 Bd4+ 16. Kh1 Qxa2 17. Nc7+ Kd8 18. Nxa8 Kc8 $19) 12. cxd3 (12. Qxd3 cxd5 $19) 12... cxd5 13. exd5 Qxb2 14. O-O $2 {White plays consequently and refuses to bow her head and live with a clear disadvantage after the best move 14.d1.} (14. Rd1 Nf4 15. Bxf4 Qxe2+ 16. Kxe2 Bxd5 $17) 14... Nf4 15. Qe4 Nxd5 16. Rab1 (16. Rfb1 Qf6 17. Bg5 Qc3 $19) 16... Qa3 17. Rxb7 Nf6 {Black enjoys the advantage of an extra piece, but all the white pieces are in attacking squares. The danger should not be underestimated and thus the defender has abstained from the best line 17...a6.} (17... Qa6 18. Rxf7 (18. Rfb1 Bc5+ 19. d4 Nf6 20. Qe1 Bb6 21. Rxf7 Bxd4+ 22. Nxd4 Kxf7 23. Nxe6 Qxe6 24. Rb7+ Nd7 $19) 18... Kxf7 19. Ng5+ (19. Nd4+ Nf6 20. Qxa8 Bc5 21. Qxh8 Bxd4+ 22. Kh1 Qxd3 $19) 19... Kg8 20. Nxe6 Qc6 $19) 18. Qc6+ Nd7 19. d4 {Judit is not willing to play the sad endgame after 19.xd7 and prefers to fish in murky waters a piece down.} (19. Rxd7 Qc5+ 20. Qxc5 Bxc5+ 21. d4 Kxd7 22. dxc5 Rhc8 23. Rc1 f6 $17) 19... Qd6 20. Qa4 Qd5 21. Rfb1 (21. Rxa7 Rxa7 22. Qxa7 Be7 $19) 21... Bd6 22. Qa6 Be7 {Ragger could have castled, since the charming combination linked with the move of the knight to c5 would promise him a winning counter attack.} (22... O-O $5 23. R1b5 Nc5 $1 24. dxc5 (24. Qa5 a6 25. Rxc5 Qxb7 $19) 24... Bxc5+ 25. Kh1 (25. Kf1 Qd3+ 26. Ke1 Rfe8 $19) 25... Qd3 26. h3 Qf1+ 27. Kh2 Bd5 $19) 23. R1b5 Qe4 (23... Qxa2 24. Qc6 Qc4 25. d5 Bc5+ 26. Rxc5 Qxc5+ 27. Qxc5 Nxc5 28. Rb5 Bxd5 29. Rxc5 Bxf3 30. gxf3 Kd7 $19) 24. Rxd7 Bxd7 25. Re5 Qb1+ 26. Kf2 {Yet another moment, when White firmly refuses the transition to the lost endgame without a trace of hope.} (26. Re1 Qb6 27. Qxb6 axb6 28. Bb4 Be6 29. Bxe7 Kxe7 30. d5 Rxa2 $19) 26... Qb6 27. Qe2 (27. Qxb6 axb6 28. Bb4 Be6 29. Bxe7 Kxe7 30. d5 Rxa2+ $19) 27... O-O {Black could not wait for the moment, when he will finally be able to remove the king from the centre and when the main danger from the fierce attacker will be over. The stronger line was to punish White with the cool move 27...e6.} (27... Be6 28. Be3 Qb7 29. d5 Bxd5 30. Bc5 Be6 31. Bxe7 Qxe7 32. Qb5+ Qd7 $19) 28. Rxe7 Be6 29. Qe5 {Polgar is diligently looking for some chances. Unfortunately the more tempting centralisation of the queen did not help either.} (29. Qe4 Rad8 (29... Rae8 30. Ng5 g6 31. Qh4 h5 32. Nf3 Bxa2 $19) 30. Be3 Bd5 $17) 29... Rad8 30. a4 Qb2 31. Kg3 (31. Rxa7 Bd5 $19) 31... Qb3 32. Rxa7 Rd5 33. Qf4 g5 $1 {Black has finally decided to solve the task of realising his advantage in a radical fashion. The weakening of the king is worth the win of a piece, as White's forces for any counterattack are depleted.} 34. Qc7 (34. Qf6 Rf5 35. Qe7 g4 $19) 34... g4 35. Rb7 Qd3 36. Rb8 gxf3 37. Rxf8+ Kxf8 38. Bh6+ Ke8 39. Qc6+ Ke7 {Ftacnik,L: '0-1 (39) Polgar,J (2686)-Ragger,M (2614) Aix-les-Bains 2011 CBM 142 [Ftacnik,L]' Not every player would have the lion heart to fight on without restraint. Unfortunatelly in this game Polgar had no trumps in her hand...} (39... Ke7 40. Qc7+ Kf6 $19) *

