{The game was played in the penultimate round. While Svidler had to win to retain some theoretical chances for the title, Polgar had a bad tournament and brought up the rear.} 1. e4 {Mikhalevski,V} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O {Let's make the first stop for some statistics. This position was rather popular in San Luis with not less than nine games played. All the players excluding Morozevich had it on the board while Svidler played it not less than 5(!) times, 4 of them with the black pieces.} 8. c3 {The brave lady accepts the challenge. Nowadays most of the players are trying to avoid the sharp and well-explored variation of Marshall. This tournament hasn't become an exception and only two more games featured the Marshall.} ({White deviated from 8.c3 six times, three times with each} 8. h3 {scoring 2.5 out of 3}) ({and} 8. a4 {with all three games drawn.}) 8... d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 {*} 12. d3 {A novelty for this tournament.} ({In two earlier Marshalls in San Luis White played d4, which is the main alternative to 12.d3.} 12. d4 Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 15. Re4 (15. Be3 Bg4 16. Qd3 Rae8 17. Nd2 Re6 18. a4 Qh5 19. axb5 axb5 20. Qf1 Rfe8 21. Bxd5 Qxd5 22. h3 Bh5 23. Qg2 Qxg2+ 24. Kxg2 f5 $44 {½-½ Polgar,J-Adams,M/San Luis ARG 2005 (37)}) 15... g5 16. Qf1 Qxf1+ 17. Kxf1 Bf5 18. f3 h6 19. Nd2 Bxe4 20. fxe4 $44 {½-½ Anand,V-Svidler,P/San Luis ARG 2005 (39). (38)}) 12... Bd6 13. Re1 Bf5 14. Qf3 Qh4 15. g3 Qh3 16. Nd2 {A very uncommon continuation, which seems to lead to almost a forced draw.} (16. Bxd5 {is the main line.Here are a few recent examples:} cxd5 17. Be3 {The latest fashion in the 12.d3-line.} (17. Bf4 {was tested several times without big successes.} Bg4 (17... Bxf4 18. Qxf4 Bxd3 19. Nd2 $14 {Rodrigez Cespedes,A-Peng Xiaomin,WchT 3rd Luzern 1993/ CBM38[Wolff].}) 18. Qg2 Bxf4 19. Qxh3 (19. gxf4 d4 20. Qg3 (20. Qxh3 {transposes to 19.Qxh3.}) 20... Rad8 21. c4 Rd6 22. Re5 f5 23. Nd2 Rg6 24. Kh1 Qh5 25. f3 Bh3 26. Qf2 Bg2+ 27. Qxg2 Rxg2 28. Kxg2 bxc4 29. dxc4 Qh4 $19 {0-1 Kotronias,V-Grischuk,A/Tripoli LBA 2004/The Week in Chess 503 (37)}) 19... Bxh3 20. gxf4 d4 21. cxd4 (21. Nd2 dxc3 22. bxc3 Rfd8 23. Re3 Bf5 24. Ne4 Rac8 25. a4 g6 26. axb5 axb5 27. h4 Rc6 28. Ra5 Rb6 29. Ra7 h6 30. Kh2 b4 31. cxb4 Rxb4 32. h5 Rbd4 33. hxg6 $11 {½-½ Bauer,C-Adams,M/Senat FRA 2003.}) 21... Rfd8 22. Re3 Bf5 23. a4 b4 24. Nd2 Rxd4 25. Ne4 g6 26. Rc1 Rad8 27. Rc4 Rxd3 28. Rxd3 Rxd3 29. Rxb4 Bh3 {with more than sufficent compensation for a pawn. Grischuk,A-Tkachiev,V/Prague CZE 2002.}) 17... Bxd3 18. Qxd5 Rad8 $1 19. Qf3 (19. Qg2 {provides a nice square on f5 for the black queen.} Qf5 20. Nd2 b4 21. Bd4 bxc3 22. bxc3 h6 23. a4 Rfe8 24. Qc6 {½-½ Grischuk,A-Khalifman,A/Rethymnon GRE 2003.}) 19... Qf5 ({Black can also play with the queens:} 19... Bc4 20. Nd2 Be6 21. Bd4 h6 22. a3 Bb8 23. Qg2 Qf5 24. f3 Rfe8 25. Ne4 Bd5 26. Re2 Re6 27. Rae1 {½-½ Kasimdzhanov,R-Adams,M/Linares ESP 2005.}) 20. Qxf5 Bxf5 21. Nd2 Rfe8 22. Bd4 f6 23. Nb3 (23. f3 b4 24. Ne4 Bc7 25. Kf2 bxc3 26. bxc3 Rb8 27. Re2 Kf7 28. Rd1 h5 29. Bc5 Kg6 30. Bd4 {½-½ Carlsen,M-Anand,V/Leon ESP 2005/The Week in Chess 553 (30)}) 23... Kf7 24. Bb6 Rc8 25. Rxe8 Rxe8 26. Rd1 Bf8 27. Nd4 Be4 28. f3 Bd5 29. a3 Rb8 30. Bc7 Rb7 31. Bf4 Bc5 $44 {½-½ Kasimdzhanov,R-Onischuk,A/Calvia ESP 2004. All these games show that a pair of bishops is usually a sufficient compensation for a pawn.}) 16... Rae8 17. Ne4 Bg4 18. Qg2 Qxg2+ $1 {A precise move demonstrated earlier this year by Levon Aronian. Black needs the white king on g2 in order to have a check on f3 after the exchange on e4.} ({In case of} 18... f5 {Black experiences problems.} 19. Qxh3 Bxh3 20. Bd2 {After the exchange on g2 neither this move nor} (20. c4 $5 {is possible.}) 20... fxe4 21. dxe4 Bc5 (21... Be6 22. exd5 Bxd5 23. Rxe8 Rxe8 24. Be3 $16 {1-0 Pariente Lopez,F-Rubio Doblas,R/Malaga 1995.}) 22. Be3 Rxe4 23. Bxc5 Rfe8 24. Rxe4 Rxe4 25. Be3 $1 Kf8 26. Re1 {and White ends up a pawn for nothing in both cases. 1-0 Kotronias,V-Nakamura,H/Port Erin IOM 2004.}) 19. Kxg2 f5 {[%csl Rd5,Re1,Re4,Rg8]} 20. Bf4 {Formally this is a novelty, but in fact the difference with Aronian's game is only in the position of the h-pawn which doesn't seem to matter.} (20. h3 Bh5 (20... fxe4 $2 {is bad in view of} 21. hxg4 exd3 22. Rxe8 Rxe8 23. Be3 $1 {Preparing Rd1.} Re5 24. Bf4 $1 Re6 25. Bxd5 cxd5 26. Bxd6 Rxd6 27. Rd1 $16) 21. Bf4 $1 Bxf4 22. gxf4 fxe4 23. dxe4 Bf3+ $1 {A nice tactical trick, which allows Black to restore material equality.} 24. Kxf3 Rxf4+ 25. Kg3 Rfxe4 26. Rxe4 Rxe4 27. f3 Re2 28. c4 bxc4 29. Bxc4 Rxb2 30. Bxa6 g5 {½-½ Nakamura,H-Aronian,L/Caleta ENG 2005. and Black successfully compensated White's passed a-pawn by counterplay on the kingside.}) ({Now} 20. Bd2 $2 {would be a blunder in view of} fxe4 21. dxe4 Bf3+ $1 {That's why 18...Qxg2 was played!}) 20... Bxf4 21. gxf4 fxe4 22. dxe4 Bf3+ $1 {Coping Aronian's play in the above-mentioned game.} 23. Kxf3 Rxf4+ 24. Kg3 Rfxe4 25. Rxe4 Rxe4 26. f3 Re5 {Only now Svidler deviates from Aronian's Re2. Probably he considered the position which arose in Nakamura-Aronian as dangerous for Black.} 27. c4 $1 {The only try to play for a win. This way White succeeds to destroy Black's pawn structure on the queenside.} (27. a4 Kf8 28. axb5 axb5 29. Ra6 ({or} 29. h4 Ke7) 29... c5 {with equality in both cases.}) 27... bxc4 {The knight can't move in view of a discovered check.} 28. Bxc4 a5 29. Rc1 $1 {[%csl Rc6] I believe Judit is still in her home preparation. White wants to exploit weakness of the c6-pawn by means of Bb3.} Kf8 $1 {'N!?' Unpinning the knight.} (29... Rg5+ {Ü 93/285}) 30. Bxd5 {Probably too tired of the tournament Judit forces a draw.} (30. Bb3 Ne7) ({If White wanted to set some problems she had to try to cover the g5-square and take her h-pawn away from the second rank with} 30. h4 $1 {[%csl Gg5] though even in this case White chances for a win are not big.}) 30... Rg5+ $5 {[%csl Rh2] An interesting intermediate move, which forces White to leave the h-pawn unprotected.} ({I believe there was also nothing wrong with} 30... cxd5 31. Rc5 a4 32. Kf2 Rh5 33. Kg3 Re5 $11 {with an obvious draw.}) 31. Kf4 ({Certainly the passive} 31. Kf2 Rxd5 32. Ke1 Rh5 {is fine for Black.}) 31... Rxd5 32. Rxc6 Rd2 {[%csl Rb2,Rh2]} 33. Ra6 Rxh2 {The point of the intermedite check on the 30th move.} 34. Rxa5 Rxb2 $11 {Now it's a dead draw.} 35. a4 Kf7 36. Kf5 g6+ 37. Ke5 {With the positional threat of Ra7+. So} Re2+ 38. Kf4 h5 39. Ra7+ {The game leaves the question where to look for an advantage in the 12.d3-line open.} 1/2-1/2

