1. e4 {[%mdl 512] Lukacs Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This variation is rather risky, and cannot be the objectively best move, but may lead to immense tactical complications favouring Judit's style. Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs: 'This variation is rather risky, and cannot be the objectively best move, but may lead to immense tactical complications favouring Judit's style.''} e6 4. Nc3 (4. Be3 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} Qb6 $5 {- Mitkov-Dreev, EUCup Neum BIH 2000 CBM 80}) 4... Bb4 5. Bf4 Nf6 (5... Qa5 $143 $6 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs' is useless because of} 6. Nge2 Nf6 7. a3 Be7 {and Black had to retreat.} 8. Ng3 h5 9. Bd3 h4 10. Nge2 Nbd7 11. e5 Nh5 12. Be3 b5 13. g4 $1 $16 {Gallagher-Preziuso, Ticino 1994}) (5... dxe4 $143 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs' gives up the tension in the  too early.} 6. fxe4 Nf6 7. Qd3 $14 O-O 8. a3 (8. Nf3 c5) 8... Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qa5 10. e5 $1 Nd5 11. Bd2 $14) (5... Ne7 $5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs' is safe and logical.} 6. Nge2 (6. Qd3 {The  is rather vulnerable here.} b6 { a6 activating the passive .} 7. Nge2 a5 8. a3 Ba6 9. Qe3 Bd6 10. Ng3 Bxf1 11. Rxf1 Bxf4 12. Qxf4 Ng6 13. Qe3 O-O 14. O-O-O b5 $132 {Geenen-Arlandi, Athens 1997}) (6. Qd2 O-O 7. Nge2 {Polgar,Judit: '- 6.d2' Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs: '- 6.d2''} Nd7 8. a3 Ba5 9. e5 (9. g3 {This is not a quiet positional system!} c5 $1 10. exd5 cxd4 11. Qxd4 (11. dxe6 $2 {is not correct:} dxc3 12. exf7+ Rxf7 13. Nxc3 Kh8 14. O-O-O Rf8 15. Bc4 Ng6 {White has no compensation for the piece.} 16. Bd6 Rxf3 17. Qe2 Qg5+ 18. Kb1 Qe3 $19 {W.Watson-Sigfusson, Reykjavik 1989}) 11... Nxd5 $11) 9... c5 {In a French type of position the  on f4, the  on e2 are misplaced.} 10. dxc5 Bc7 11. Nd4 Bxe5 12. Bxe5 Nxe5 13. f4 Nd7 14. b4 b6 15. c6 Nb8 16. b5 a6 $15 {and White cannot hold his "Vorpost" anymore, Gallagher-Khenkin, Geneve 1995.}) 6... Ng6 (6... O-O 7. a3 (7. Qd2 {- 6.d2}) 7... Ba5 8. Qd3 { 0-0-0 making things more wild.} Nd7 9. Bd6 Bc7 10. Bxc7 Qxc7 11. O-O-O b5 12. Qe3 a5 $13 {making profit from the weakness of a3, Murrey-Kharitonov, Moscow 1989.}) (6... Nd7 7. a3 Ba5 8. Qd3 dxe4 9. fxe4 e5 {Black wants to gain control over × e5.} 10. dxe5 Ng6 11. e6 fxe6 12. Bd6 Nde5 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. Qg3 Ng6 $15 {Ainsa-Izeta, Benasque 1996}) 7. Bg3 dxe4 8. fxe4 e5 9. Qd3 Bg4 10. a3 Ba5 11. dxe5 Nd7 12. h3 Bxe2 13. Bxe2 Ngxe5 {The  is excellent on e5.} 14. Qe3 Qb6 15. Qxb6 Bxb6 16. O-O-O f6 $15 {Mitkov-Magem Badals, San Sebastian 1993}) 6. Qd3 b6 (6... c5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} 7. a3 Qa5 8. Bxb8 {Usually this exchange is not a big deal.} Rxb8 9. e5 Nd7 10. Rb1 Bxc3+ 11. Qxc3 Qb6 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. Qd4 Bd7 14. f4 {We have reached a typical classical French position.} Rc8 15. Qb4 Qc7 16. Nf3 O-O 17. Rd1 $11 (17. c3 $2 {is met by the activization of the black .} Ba4 $1 { c2} 18. Rc1 b5 19. Nd2 f6 $17 {Vallejo Pons-Garcia Padron, Ponferrada 1997})) (6... O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} 7. Nge2 c5 (7... b6 {- 6...b6}) 8. O-O-O cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nbd7 10. Ndb5 e5 11. Bg5 d4 12. Nd5 Be7 13. Nxe7+ Qxe7 14. Qa3 Qxa3 15. Nxa3 Nb6 16. Nc4 Nxc4 17. Bxc4 {The  is a bit better for White because of the .} Nd7 18. Be7 Re8 19. Ba3 $14 {Murey-Hodgson, Sevilla op 1987}) 7. Nge2 (7. O-O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} Ba6 8. Qe3 Bxf1 9. Rxf1 O-O 10. e5 Nfd7 11. Bg5 Be7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. f4 {This is another French structure with more real chances for White on the , than Black's on the other flank.} c5 14. Nf3 Nc6 15. Nb5 (15. h4) 15... a6 16. Nd6 Nxd4 17. Nxd4 cxd4 18. Qxd4 f6 $13 {Hodgson-Orr, London 1988}) 7... O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs: '- 6...b6''} (7... Ba6 {Polgar,Judit: 'Lukacs'} 8. Qe3 O-O (8... Nbd7 $6 {is met by} 9. e5 {and the  has to retreat.} Ng8 10. a3 Be7 {Now the  follows him...} 11. g4 Bh4+ 12. Bg3 Bxg3+ 13. Nxg3 Bxf1 14. Rxf1 Qe7 15. O-O-O b5 16. Nb1 a5 17. f4 b4 18. f5 $16 {Gallagher-Dunn, London 1993}) (8... Bxc3+ 9. Nxc3 Bxf1 10. Rxf1 O-O (10... Qd7 11. O-O-O O-O 12. g4 {Here in the  race White comes first on the .} Rc8 13. Bg5 Ne8 14. f4 dxe4 15.