{At this point, I had drawn 5 games and lost 2. I had also failed to win an excellent position against Ivanchuk in round 7. Judit on the other hand had had a good tournament so far and was on -2 even after losing to Kramnik. Still, I was feeling good (at least there were only 4 rounds left!) and I could try to have some fun playing chess - there wasn't much else that could go wrong anymore!} 1. e4 {Anand Anand Anand} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Bc5 {Topalov has already drawn twice with this system against me and I figured it was time to try it with Black! Black is trying to get into an Archangelsk without giving White the chance to play d3 and a4. If White plays d3 then Black plays simply d6. If not, then Black plays b5 and transposes} 6. c3 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. a4 Rb8 9. d3 {Here she deviates from her game against Shirov two rounds earlier. Anand: 'Here she deviates from her game against Shirov two rounds earlier. Anand,V: 'N''} (9. d4 {Anand: 'Anand,V: 'Ü 69/(317)''} Bb6 10. axb5 axb5 11. Na3 O-O 12. Nxb5 Bg4 13. Bc2 {And here Shirov improved on his game against Topalov (Wijk aan Zee 1996) by taking on f3.}) 9... O-O 10. axb5 axb5 11. Re1 Bb6 12. Nbd2 {I was surprised that she did this - in the Guico Piano (or Italian game, I am not sure exactly what it is called!) this move order was ruled out by the discovery that g4 followed by h8 and h6 gave Black an edge. After some thought I concluded that White was better placed against it. The open a-file and b5 make it difficult for Black to concentrate on the kingside. Still, Black is doing fine now.} Ng4 $1 13. Re2 Kh8 14. h3 Nh6 15. Nf1 f5 16. Bg5 (16. Bxh6 gxh6 17. d4 Qf6 $1 {This looks fine for Black.} (17... fxe4 18. Rxe4 Bf5 (18... d5 19. Nxe5 $1 dxe4 20. Nxc6 {Since g8 is off limits to Black's pieces, White can allow the  to f2.} Qf6 21. Nxb8 Qxf2+ 22. Kh1) 19. Re3 { g3 Anand: ' g3 Anand,V: '( g3)''} e4 20. Bd5 $1 exf3 21. Bxc6 fxg2 22. Bxg2)) 16... Qe8 17. Bxh6 gxh6 18. Ng3 Ne7 (18... f4 {Anand: 'Anand,V: '!?''} 19. Nf5 Bxf5 (19... Rxf5 20. exf5 Bxf5 21. d4 $1 $16) 20. exf5 Rxf5 {During the game I thought that White should be OK here, but maybe this was worth a try. For eg..} 21. Qc2 $1 { d4 Anand: ' d4 Anand,V: '( d4)''} (21. Bd5 Ne7 22. Be4 Rf8 23. d4 d5 $1) (21. d4 e4 { d5}) 21... Qh5 $15) 19. d4 f4 20. Nf5 Nxf5 21. exf5 e4 22. Nd2 (22. Bd5 $1 Bxf5 23. Nd2 $1 {Anand: '!' '!' Anand: 'Anand,V: '!''} (23. Bxe4 $2 Bxe4 24. Nd2 d5 25. f3 Qg6 26. fxe4 f3 27. Rf2 dxe4 $19) 23... e3 $1 (23... f3 24. Nxf3 $1 c6 25. Bxe4 Bxe4 26. Nd2 d5 27. f3) 24. fxe3 fxe3 $11) 22... Bxf5 23. Nxe4 $6 (23. Bd5 $1) 23... Qg6 24. f3 Bxh3 25. Qd2 Rbe8 {Anand: 'Anand,V: '''} 26. Rae1 {Even after this error White is still fine here, as it is quite difficult to break through White's kingside. Judit however started to commit small errors.} Re7 (26... b4 $1 {Anand}) 27. Kf1 {This is not really necessary - 27.c2 looks OK. Anand: 'This is not really necessary - 27.c2 looks OK. Anand,V: '?!''} (27. Bc2 $142 {Anand}) 27... Rg7 28. Rf2 Bd7 29. Bc2 Qh5 30. Ke2 $2 (30. b4 $1 {Sealing up the b6. White can proceed with e2-d1-c2-b2. Anand: 'Sealing up the b6. White can proceed with e2-d1-c2-b2. Anand,V: '( e2-d1-c1-b2)''} d5 (30... c6 31. Ke2 $1 Bd8 32. Kd1 $1 {Judit had overlooked that h4 could be met by h1} Bh4 $140 {Anand} 33. Rh1) 31. Nc5 Bxc5 32. dxc5 Qh1+ 33. Ke2 Qxg2 34. Kd1 Qg5 35. Qd4 $15) 30... b4 $1 $17 {Grabbing his chance. Only now is Black really better.} 31. Kd1 bxc3 32. bxc3 Be8 {Once Black moves his , plays h5 and g6, his s will be free to double on the e-file.} 33. Rff1 Qa5 34. Rh1 h5 35. Nf6 $2 {This leads to a forced loss, though White had a tough defence ahead of her.} (35. Rh4 {Anand} Qa1+ 36. Qc1 Qxc1+ 37. Kxc1 Rxg2 { g6, d5-+}) 35... Qa1+ 36. Qc1 Qxc3 $1 37. Nxe8 (37. Rxe8 Rxe8 38. Nxe8 Rxg2 $19) 37... Rxg2 {My first win of 1997 - I was still on -1, but at least I wasn't in last place anymore!} 0-1

