1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 d6 3. f4 Nc6 ({If Black wants to reduce White's options he should consider the move order} 3... g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 {when the bishop really has to go to c4, as} 5. Bb5+ Bd7 {is fine for Black,}) 4. Nf3 g6 5. Bb5 {Placing the bishop on c4 is hardly bad, but the text gives White quite an easy game.} Bd7 6. O-O Bg7 7. d3 a6 8. Bxc6 Bxc6 9. Qe1 {The players follow one of the main lines. Black shouldn't be worse here, but there's no doubt that White's position is the easier to handle and he has scored well in recent practice.} Qd7 10. a4 {Polgar decides to direct her immediate energies against the ...b5 break. White can also consider:} ({a)} 10. Kh1 b5 ({Black can also go} 10... Nh6 11. Bd2 {and only then} b5 {- see David Vigorito's notes to Roy Chowdhury-Gupta, Bad Wiessee 2009, in the Archive}) 11. f5 $5 {(very ambitious, even by Grand Prix Attack standards)} b4 12. Ne2 gxf5 $5 ({Black should also be OK after} 12... Nf6 {if the queen goes to h4 then Black gets an even better opportunity to take on f5, but after} 13. Ng3 O-O {how is White supposed to continue the attack?}) 13. Ng3 fxe4 14. dxe4 O-O-O $6 ({the king won't be safe here the position isn't clear, but after} 14... Bb5 $5 15. Rf2 Nf6 16. Nf5 Bf8 {I'd be surprised if White had more than enough play for his pawn}) 15. a3 $1 {gave White good attacking chances on the queenside in Andreikin-Dominguez Perez, Baku 2011.}) ({b)} 10. Bd2 {was covered by Gary Lane in Grabarska-Zawadzka, Ostrow 2002. Perhaps here too Dominguez was intending} b5 $5 {.}) 10... b5 $6 {It's often a good idea to give up a pawn for the initiative and/or long-term compensation in a rapid game, but here it seems that Black doesn't get quite enough play. Thus he should look to the alternatives:} ({a)} 10... b6 {has been quite common, keeping open Black's castling options and challenging White to come up with a good plan. Perhaps} 11. Bd2 {is best,} ({whereas} 11. b3 $6 Nf6 12. h3 Nh5 $1 13. Bd2 f5 {saw Black seizing the initiative in Ljubojevic-Kasparov, Linares 1991, as we've seen before.})) ({b)} 10... f5 $6 {was a typically vigorous Topalov counter-strike in Carlsen-Topalov, Monaco (rapid) 2011, but after} 11. Nd5 $1 fxe4 12. dxe4 Rb8 13. Ng5 $1 {Black was already under pressure. Will the day ever come when even leading theoreticians are properly prepared for the Grand Prix? I've certainly lost track of how many haven't been over the years.}) ({c) Four games after our main one here, Dominguez tried} 10... Nh6 $5 {, but} 11. a5 f5 12. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 13. exd5 Nf7 14. Rb1 O-O 15. b4 {left White slightly for choice in Polgar-Dominguez Perez, Khanty-Mansiysk (blitz) 2011. This all rather begs the question what do Topalov and Dominguez have against 10...b6? I'm afraid I just can't work it out!}) 11. e5 $1 {A classic central riposte to Black's ambitious flank attack.} Nh6 {Black's last didn't actually have to entail a pawn sacrifice, but the alternatives to completing his kingside development weren't ideal:} ({a)} 11... bxa4 12. exd6 (12. Ne4 $5 {is possible too}) 12... Qxd6 13. Ne5 {and White will regain his pawn with the better structure.}) ({b)} 11... b4 12. Ne4 f5 ({how else to deal with the pressure down the d-file it's hard to believe Black will survive after} 12... O-O-O 13. c3) 13. Ned2 {looks quite pleasant for White,} ({but} 13. Neg5 h6 $1 14. e6 Qc8 15. Nf7 Rh7 {doesn't seem so clear.})) 12. exd6 Qxd6 13. axb5 axb5 14. Nxb5 $1 {Netting the aforementioned pawn.} Qb8 15. Rxa8 Bxa8 16. c4 $1 {If the knight had to retreat then Black would have obtained decent pressure down the b-file, but now the onus is very much on him to show that he has enough compensation.} Nf5 ({This may not be fast enough. Thus we should investigate} 16... Bc6 $5 {, although after} 17. Qa5 (17. Nc3 Bxf3 18. Rxf3 Nf5 {gives Black definite positional compensation}) 17... O-O 18. Ne5 $1 (18. Be3 {works well in our main game, but here} Ng4 $1 19. Bxc5 Qxf4 {gives Black a strong counterattack}) 18... Qb7 (18... Bxe5 19. fxe5 Ng4 {no longer works so well in view of} 20. Bf4) 19. Nxc6 Qxc6 20. Re1 {it seems that Black doesn't have enough compensation, since White might remove the queens via c7. That said, it does seem that this was likely a better try for Dominguez.}) 17. Be3 $1 {Offering a second bishop to augment his grip on the position.} Bc6 $2 ({There's no time for this in view of White's powerful riposte. After} 17... Nxe3 18. Qxe3 O-O 19. b3 {Black's bishops would only have given him some hope of holding a pawn-down ending,}) ({and} 17... Qc8 18. Qf2 Nxe3 19. Qxe3 O-O 20. Qxe7 Bxb2 21. Qe3 Bxf3 22. Qxf3 {would also have left White with decent chances to grind.}) 18. Bxc5 Bxb5 19. Qb4 $1 {Dominguez may have missed this. Now White will regain his piece and obtain a powerful passed pawn in the bargain.} O-O 20. cxb5 Rd8 21. b6 $1 h6 ({There would have been no defence after} 21... Rxd3 $2 22. Ra1 Rd7 23. Qe4 {. Thus Dominguez has to go into contortions to sidestep White's invasion plan on a8.}) 22. Ra1 Kh7 23. Qe4 {Black has avoided disaster on the back rank for now, but his major pieces are too tied down for him to be able to resist for long.} Bxb2 24. Rb1 Bf6 25. Ne5 Nd6 26. Bxd6 Rxd6 27. b7 {The pawn gets ever closer to touchdown.} Qa7+ 28. Kf1 Rb6 29. Rxb6 Qxb6 30. Nd7 $1 {Polgar has realised that Black will quickly run out of checks.} Qb1+ 31. Ke2 Qb2+ 32. Ke3 Qc1+ 33. Kf2 Qb2+ 34. Kg3 Bd4 35. b8=Q Qf2+ 36. Kh3 1-0

