{Jones, Gawain: 'An interesting match where the players were obliged to play the Sicilian every game. For round 2, Kaidanov plumped for the Dragon.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Rc8 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. Kb1 Re8 {Jones, Gawain: 'So we reach possibly the most important Dragon position from the last couple of years.'} 13. h4 h5 14. g4 (14. Bh6 {was played in the only other recent game I could find and seems to be a relatively risk-free way of playing for White.} Nc4 {Carlsen's choice but Black has to be happy to defend so maybe it's here that Black can investigate the other choices.} 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Nd5 e5 18. Nxf6 Qxf6 19. Nb3 (19. Ne2 {was Karjakin's choice against Carlsen but didn't get any real advantage.}) 19... Re6 20. Qa5 Ra4 21. Qc7 Bc6 22. Rd3 (22. Rd2 {is suggested by Ponomariov and Komarov (and de la Villa in Dismantling the Sicilian) with the conclusion that White is slightly better.}) 22... Bb5 $6 {Seeing as Black just has to retreat his bishop next move this seems dubious.} (22... Ra6 {seems very passive but it's not clear how White improves either} 23. Rhd1 $5 Bb5 24. R3d2 Rb6 25. Qb8 Qxh4 26. Qxa7 Qd8 27. Nc5 Re7 {and Black seems to be holding.}) 23. Rd2 Bc6 24. a3 Ra6 25. Nc1 (25. Rhd1 Qxh4 26. Rxd6 {is starting to be unpleasant}) 25... Qf4 26. Rhd1 Qxh4 27. Rxd6 Qe7 28. Qb8 Rxd6 29. Rxd6 Bb5 30. Rd5 (30. Rd8 $5 $16) 30... Bf1 31. Rxe5 $6 Qd6 32. Qe8 Rb6 $6 (32... Bxg2 33. Re7 Qf6 34. e5 Qxf3 {is fine for Black as} 35. e6 $2 Rxe6 $17) 33. Rd5 Qf6 34. e5 $6 (34. Nb3 Bxg2 35. Nd4 $16) 34... Qe6 35. Qxe6 Rxe6 36. Nd3 Bxg2 37. Nf4 Bxf3 38. Nxe6+ fxe6 {This trade has left Black in a winning endgame with his two connected passed pawns and he went on to win although not due to the opening, Agopov-Fruebing, Berlin 2010}) 14... hxg4 15. h5 Nxh5 16. Rdg1 {Jones, Gawain: 'Deviating from 16.Bh6 which we've seen a fair amount of in the archives, most recently in Dominguez-Smerdon in November where David held very comfortably. White switches her moves around although it's not clear what difference this makes. On the one hand Kasimdzhanov's 16...Kh7 is now no longer playable, although it's been somewhat discredited recently. On the other hand Black now has more options here.'} e6 (16... Qa5 $5 17. fxg4 Bxg4 (17... Nxg4 $2 18. Nf5 $1 $40 {and Black's extra couple of pawns aren't going to help when he gets mated.}) 18. Bh6 e6 (18... Bf6 19. Bg5 Bg7 {gives White at least a draw}) 19. Rh4 Bxh6 20. Qxh6 Rxc3 21. bxc3 Qxc3 22. Nxe6 Rxe6 (22... Bxe6 23. Rxh5 Nc4 24. Qh7+ Kf8 25. Qh8+ Qxh8 26. Rxh8+ Ke7 27. Rxe8+ Kxe8 28. Bxc4 Bxc4 29. Kb2 {gives White a slightly better ending although Black should hold.}) 23. Rhxg4 (23. Bxe6 Bxe6 24. Rxh5 Bxa2+ 25. Kxa2 (25. Kc1 Qa1+ 26. Kd2 Qd4+ {would turn the tables on White}) 25... Qxc2+ $11) 23... Nxg4 24. Rxg4 {and Black has held on}) (16... Rxc3 {would be the logical choice although} 17. bxc3 (17. Qxc3 Nxf3 $15) 17... a5 18. Bh6 {looks too scary to be playable.} Bxh6 19. Qxh6 a4 20. Rxh5 gxh5 21. fxg4 $1 $18) (16... gxf3 {is far too dangerous} 17. Rxh5 gxh5 18. Bh6 $18) 17. Bh6 {Jones, Gawain: 'So we've transposed back to Dominguez-Smerdon but Black doesn't have the opportunity to play Kasimdzhanov's 16...Kh7 although that has been somewhat discredited.'} (17. fxg4 Nf6 18. g5 (18. Bh6 Nfxg4 19. Bxg7 Kxg7) 18... Nfg4 (18... Nh5)) 17... Qf6 18. fxg4 Bxh6 19. Qxh6 Qg7 20. Qd2 $146 {Jones, Gawain: 'And this finally deviates from the aforementioned game with Polgar's second novelty of the game! Polgar, true to her nature, goes for the most aggressive plan.'} (20. Qe3 {Ü 107/71 Ü 107/71} Nf6 21. g5 Nh5 22. Ndb5 Bxb5 23. Nxb5 Red8 24. Qxa7 Nf3 25. Rg2 Qe5 26. Qe3 Ne1 $1 {was a strong novelty against which Dominguez failed to find any advantage.}) 20... Nf6 21. g5 Nh5 22. Nce2 {Jones, Gawain: 'So White shows her hand. She plans on trading the h5 knight and tripling on the h file. Initially when I looked at this game I thought this idea was too basic but the deeper I look the more problems I find for Black. The extra pawn doesn't seem very useful.'} Nc4 $2 (22... a5 {was another of my attempts for counterplay.} 23. Qxa5 Nc6 (23... Bc6 24. Rh4 Nd7 25. Qd2 Nc5 26. Qe3 {and I can't really see Black's counterplay.}) 24. Qd2 Qe5 25. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 26. g6 {is another killing attack for White.}) ({I' prefer to keep the knight on e5 to try and protect the king. However the problem is it's hard to generate any aggressive potential without trading the b3 bishop.} 22... b5 23. Ng3 Nxg3 24. Rxg3 Nc4 (24... Kf8 25. Rgh3 Ke7 26. Qf4 $1 (26. Rh7 Rh8 {exploits White's back rank problems.}) 26... a5 27. Rh7 Rh8 28. Rxh8 Rxh8 29. Qf6+ Qxf6 30. gxf6+ Kxf6 31. Rxh8 a4 32. c4 axb3 33. cxb5 $18) 25. Bxc4 Rxc4 26. Nf3 {and it seems unlikely Black will survive long.} (26. Rgh3 e5)) 23. Bxc4 (23. Qb4 $2 a5 24. Qxb7 Bc6) 23... Rxc4 24. b3 Rc5 25. Ng3 Nxg3 (25... Nf4 $2 {is an attempt to slow the White attack but after} 26. Rh2 {followed by Rgh1 is winning.}) 26. Rxg3 Rec8 (26... e5 27. Nf5 Bxf5 (27... gxf5 28. Qxd6) 28. exf5 gxf5 29. g6 $1 (29. Rgh3 Re6 30. Qh2 Kf8 31. Rh8+ Ke7 32. Qh4 {and Black's king can't survive, for example} Kd7 (32... Rg6 33. Qa4 Rxg5 34. Qe8+ Kf6 35. Qd8+ Ke6 36. Re8+ Kd5 37. Rd1+ Ke4 38. Qxd6 $18) 33. Qa4+ Kc7 34. b4 $1 (34. R1h7 {also looks good}) 34... Rc6 (34... Rc3 35. Qa5+) 35. b5 $1 Rc5 36. Qa5+ {with mate next move}) 29... fxg6 30. Rgh3 $1) 27. Rgh3 $1 {Jones, Gawain: '+-' Jones, Gawain: 'Funnily enough, all Black's moves have matched the computer's, and then in this position it suddenly switches to White winning. Indeed there's no defense.'} e5 (27... Kf8 28. Rh7 Qe5 29. Qf2 $18) 28. Rh4 exd4 (28... Bg4 29. Qh2 Bh5 30. Nf5 $18) 29. Qh2 Kf8 30. Qxd6+ Kg8 31. Qxd7 d3 (31... Rxc2 32. Qh3 {followed by 33.Rh8+ wins}) 32. c4 (32. cxd3 Rc1+ 33. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 34. Kxc1 Qa1+ 35. Kd2 Qb2+ 36. Ke3 Qc1+ 37. Kd4 Qg1+ 38. Kc4 Qc1+ 39. Kb4 Qe1+ 40. Kc5 Qxh4 41. Qe8+ Kg7 42. Qe5+ Kg8 43. Kd6 $18) 32... Qc3 33. R4h2 b5 {Jones, Gawain: 'It looks like Black has suddenly got some counterplay but after'} (33... R5c7 34. Qd6 Rc6 35. e5 Qc2+ 36. Rxc2 dxc2+ 37. Kxc2 Rxd6 38. exd6 $18) 34. e5 $1 Qxe5 35. Rh7 R5c7 (35... Rf8 36. Qd6 d2 37. Rh8+ Qxh8 38. Rxh8+ Kxh8 39. Qxf8+ Kh7 40. Qxf7+ Kh8 41. Kc2) 36. Qd6 {Jones, Gawain: '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Black resigned as it's forced mate. Polgar's idea looks very dangerous and I have to admit I can't find a good solution for Black. Against 16.Rg1, 16...Qa5 is a possibility but this idea seems to refute 16... e6 and 17...Qf6. Let's hope not!'} (36. Qd6 Qc3 (36... Qxd6 37. Rh8+ Kg7 38. R1h7#) 37. Qf6 {Jones, Gawain: '!'} Qc2+ 38. Ka1) 1-0

