1. e4 {Mihail Marin Mihail Marin Mihail Marin: 'Marin,Mihail Marin,Mihail: 'Mihail Marin''} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. O-O d6 6. c3 O-O 7. Nbd2 Bb6 $5 8. Nc4 {a4} (8. Re1 Ng4 9. Re2 f5 $132 {is an idea known from the Italian Game. The difference in the white bishop's placement clearly favours Black here, because he can do without the move ...h8.}) (8. h3 Ne7 9. Re1 Ng6 {also leaves the b5-bishop looking a bit strange.}) 8... Ne7 $5 {[%csl Yb5]} 9. Ba4 (9. a4 c6 10. Nxb6 axb6 11. Bc4 b5 12. Bb3 bxa4 13. Rxa4 Rxa4 14. Bxa4 Ng6 15. Re1 h6 16. h3 Be6 $11 {Mueller,R (2370)-Schlindwein,R (2425)/Germany 1998/EXT 99 (0-1, 53)}) (9. Re1 Ng6 10. d4 c6 11. Nxb6 axb6 12. Bd3 {might be a more constructive way of winning the pair of bishops and occupying the centre, although Black's flexible placement of pieces enables him to look to the future with certain optimism.}) 9... Ng6 10. Bc2 Re8 {Preventing the occupation of the centre with d4.} (10... Be6 11. Nxb6 axb6 12. a3 (12. d4 $1) 12... d5 $1 $132 {Kabisch,T (2270)-Nikolac,J (2435)/Germany 1986/GER-chT (0-1, 50)}) 11. h3 c6 12. Nxb6 (12. Re1 Bc7 {[%csl Yc4][%cal Gd6d5]}) 12... axb6 13. Re1 h6 14. d4 b5 {A natural move, but the pawn can also be useful on b6, restricting the dark-squared bishop.} (14... Qc7 15. Bd2 Bd7 16. c4 c5 17. d5 (17. dxe5 dxe5 {[%cal Yg6f8,Yf8e6,Yg6f4,Yf4e6]} 18. Bc3 Rad8 19. Qe2 Nh5 $132) 17... Qd8 18. Kh2 Nh7 19. g3 Rf8 20. Be3 Qc8 21. Ng1 f5 $132 {½-½ Vasiukov,E (2505)-Krogius,N (2470)/Bad Liebenzell 1996/EXT 98}) 15. a4 bxa4 16. Rxa4 Rxa4 17. Bxa4 {In the variation initiated with 9.e1 from the comment to White's 9th move, White would have not only spared a tempo, but also been able to capture on a4 with the queen, leaving the e4-pawn safely defended.} exd4 $5 18. Qxd4 $8 {[%csl Ye4]} c5 19. Qd3 Bd7 20. Bb3 (20. Bxd7 Qxd7 21. c4 Qc6 22. Nd2 d5 $1 $32 {[%csl Ye1]}) 20... Qb6 (20... Bc6 21. e5 $5 Bxf3 (21... dxe5 $4 22. Qxg6 $1 $19) (21... Nxe5 22. Nxe5 dxe5 23. Qxd8 Rxd8 24. Rxe5 c4 25. Bc2 $14) 22. e6 (22. Qxg6 Bd5 23. Bxd5 Nxd5 24. Qd3 Nc7 $5 $11) 22... d5 23. Qxf3 (23. exf7+ Kxf7 24. Rxe8 Qxe8 25. Qxf3 Qe1+) 23... Rxe6 24. Rxe6 fxe6 25. Bc2 $44) 21. Bc2 Qc7 {It is not easy for White to develop the queen's bishop and maintain the e4-pawn well defended at the same time.} 22. Re3 Bc6 23. Qe2 Ra8 24. c4 Ra1 25. Qd1 Qe7 $36 {Black has a very active position, which proves more than sufficient compensation for White's pair of bishops.} 26. Nd2 Nf4 27. Re1 Ne6 28. Nb3 Ra8 29. Bd2 Nd7 30. Bc3 Ne5 31. Nd2 Nd4 32. Bxd4 cxd4 33. Nf3 Nxf3+ 34. Qxf3 Qe5 35. Rd1 1/2-1/2

