1. e4 {Wedberg Wedberg} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Bb7 12. Nbd2 cxd4 13. cxd4 exd4 14. Nxd4 Re8 15. b3 Bf8 16. Bb2 g6 17. Qf3 Bg7 18. Rad1 Rc8 19. Bb1 Nc6 $146 (19... b4 $5 20. Nf1 d5 21. e5 (21. exd5 Qxd5 22. Qxd5 Nxd5 $15 {0-1 Popovic,P-Romanishin,O/Stara Pazova 1988/EXT 97 (35)}) 21... Ne4 22. Ng3 Rxe5 23. Ndf5 {was vey unclear in 1-0 Velimirovic,D-Romanishin,O/Vrsac 1989/CBM 15 (68)}) (19... Qe7 20. Qe3 Nc6 21. Nf1 Nxd4 22. Bxd4 Qf8 23. f3 Nd5 24. Qf2 Nc3 25. Bxg7 Kxg7 26. Rd3 Nxb1 27. Rxb1 Re6 28. Rbd1 $16 {1-0 Short,N-Thipsay,P/Torquay 1998/CBM 66 ext (65)}) (19... Nd7 20. Nf1 b4 21. Ne3 Qg5 22. Qe2 Qc5 23. Qd2 Rcd8 24. Rc1 Qb6 25. Ng4 Ne5 $13 {½-½ Sorokin,M-Ramesh,R/Sangli 2000/EXT 2001 (63)}) 20. Nf1 Ne5 21. Qe3 Ned7 22. Qf4 d5 23. Ng3 Qc7 24. e5 $1 {[%mdl 16] A surprising sacrifice of a . The idea is to keep the Black pieces (especially b7) blocked in by the d5- and then to build play around the excellent blockading  on d4.} Rxe5 25. Rxe5 Qxe5 26. Qd2 $5 {This is a bit speculative, but Judit is confident in her attacking abilities.} ({Good for an advantage was} 26. Qxe5 Nxe5 (26... Nxe5 27. Ndf5) 27. Ndf5 $1 (27. Nxb5 Nf3+ 28. gxf3 axb5 $11) 27... gxf5 28. Bxe5 $14) 26... Nh5 27. Nxh5 (27. Bf5 $5 Nxg3 28. Bxd7 Ne4 29. Nf5 $1 Qc7 30. Nh6+ Kh8 31. Nxf7+ Kg8 32. Nh6+ Kh8 $11) 27... Qxh5 28. Re1 Bh6 $6 {Leaving the long diagonal creates new options for White. Black is already a  to the good, so he could have chosen something less provocative.} (28... Nc5 $5) 29. f4 Qh4 30. Bf5 $3 {[%mdl 512] A beautiful move by the bold Judit - or the other way around.} (30. Rf1 Nc5 (30... Re8 31. Qc3 Bg7 32. Qc7 Bc8 33. Qc6 $13) 31. Ne2 Ne6 $17) 30... Qd8 (30... Bxf4 31. Qe2 Qd8 32. Rf1 $1 Bg5 (32... Bd6 33. Be6 $18) 33. Be6 Nf6 34. Bxc8 Bxc8 35. Qe5 $16) (30... gxf5 31. Nxf5 Bxf4 32. Qe2 Qd8 33. Ne7+ Kf8 34. Qg4 Be5 35. Bxe5 Nxe5 36. Rxe5 $18) 31. Qe3 Rb8 $4 {Losing on the spot.} (31... Qc7 32. Nxb5 axb5 33. Bxd7 Qxd7 34. Qe5 f6 35. Qxf6 Bg7 36. Qxg7+ Qxg7 37. Bxg7 Kxg7 38. Re7+ Kf6 39. Rxb7 $18) (31... b4 $142 {creating the possibility c3}) ({or} 31... Ra8 {to keep the  out of harms way.}) 32. Bxd7 Qxd7 33. Qe5 {[%cal Ye5b8,Yb2h8]} 1-0

