[EventType "rapid"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4 e6 7. O-O ({Last month we considered the more aggressive and, in my view, ultimately less promising} 7. Bb3 b5 8. Bg5 {- see Khandelwal-Palliser. Polgar prefers to calmly build up before going after the black king, at least for a bit!}) 7... b5 8. Bb3 Be7 9. Qf3 Qb6 $5 {Slightly the more ambitious of the two queen moves.} 10. Be3 Qb7 11. a3 ({Slow. White has more often preferred} 11. Qg3 {, as indeed Mickey Adams did en route to winning the recent British Championship:} b4 ({forcing the pace in Navara-Svidler, Sochi 2008, we considered the important alternative} 11... Nbd7) 12. Na4 Nbd7 13. f3 O-O 14. a3 $5 ({opening the queenside without delay White often moves a rook at this juncture, while Judit Polgar herself had previously tried} 14. c4 bxc3 15. bxc3 $5 {in Polgar-Sieciechowicz, Warsaw (rapid) 2010, seizing the advantage after} Nc5 $6 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Ne2 Bd7 18. e5 $1 Ne8 19. Rab1 Qc7 20. Bc2 {, but Black should surely have moved his knight to e5}) 14... bxa3 15. Rxa3 $1 Nc5 ({White's point is that} 15... d5 16. exd5 Bxa3 17. bxa3 Nxd5 ({and not} 17... exd5 $2 18. Nf5 g6 19. Bd4 {with an overwhelming attack}) 18. Bh6 g6 19. Bxf8 Kxf8 {regains the exchange while retaining the initiative after} 20. c4) 16. Ne2 Nxa4 17. Bxa4 $5 Bd7 ({it seems that Black's queen somehow lives after} 17... Qxb2 18. Rb3 Qxc2 19. Nd4 Qa2 $5 {and following} 20. Bc6 e5 21. Qf2 Qxf2+ 22. Bxf2 Bd7 23. Bxa8 Rxa8 24. Nf5 {he might well survive the ending}) 18. Rb3 Qc8 19. Bxd7 Qxd7 20. Bh6 Ne8 21. Rd1 {White had a definite pull in Adams-Gormally, Sheffield 2011, before Black collapsed with} Rc8 22. Rd2 Kh8 23. Bg5 Nf6 $2 24. e5 Qa7+ $2 25. Be3 {1-0. Opening the queenside is very logical and I'm surprised that Adams hasn't yet received more followers, especially as he first introduced his idea against Dominguez back in 2009.}) 11... O-O ({It feels rather unnatural to play} 11... Nc6 {having spent two tempi with the queen, rather than just one placing it on c7. However, with White's last a little slow this may be playable and is worth brief consideration, if only to see a thematic recent game:} 12. Rfe1 (12. Qg3 $5 {would be more vigorous I'm not sure Black can still sacrifice the g-pawn having lost time and} O-O 13. Nxc6 Qxc6 14. Bh6 Ne8 15. Nd5 $1 Bd8 16. Rad1 {must be a little better for White}) 12... O-O 13. Rad1 Bd7 14. Qg3 Rfd8 15. Bh6 Bf8 16. Bg5 ({strong, although it's also tempting to swing the knight over to the kingside with} 16. Nf3) 16... Be7 17. Re3 Ne5 $2 ({a definite mistake Black had to free his position with} 17... Nxd4 18. Rxd4 Bc6) 18. f4 Nc4 19. Bxc4 bxc4 20. e5 $1 dxe5 21. fxe5 Nd5 22. Nf5 $1 Bxg5 23. Qxg5 exf5 24. Nxd5 {saw White's calm build-up result in a powerful attack in Liepold-Sand, Basle 2011.}) 12. Rae1 ({Creative and rather unexpected. Unsurprisingly Kiril Georgiev doesn't consider this in 'The Sharpest Sicilian', focussing here only on} 12. Rfe1 {, whereupon} Nbd7 13. Bg5 Ne5 14. Qg3 Ng6 15. Rad1 Nh5 16. Qg4 Bxg5 ({Black should keep pieces on and} 16... Nf6 $1 17. Qf3 h6 18. Be3 Bd7 {would promise him a reasonable set-up, as the Bulgarian Grandmaster points out}) 17. Qxg5 Nf6 18. Nf3 {gave White a pull in Nisipeanu-Agrest, Mainz (rapid) 2005.}) 12... Bd7 ({It can't have been easy trying to puzzle out White's aims without much time available, but the text was by no means forced. I certainly can't see too much wrong either with} 12... Nbd7 {: for example,} 13. Qg3 (13. g4 $2 Ne5 {now costs White a pawn}) 13... Ne5 14. f4 (14. Bh6 Nh5 15. Qh3 Nf6 {doesn't particularly help White}) 14... Nc4 15. e5 $5 (15. f5 e5 $1 {keeps lines closed}) 15... Ne4 16. Nxe4 Qxe4 17. c3 Bb7 18. Bc2 Qd5 {with a tense middlegame in progress.}) 13. g4 $5 ({With Black yet to develop his queen's knight, Polgar decides to get on with it. One would assume that} 13. Qg3 {followed by f2-f4 was the point behind White's last, but moving the queen can wait. Besides, here} Nc6 14. f4 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 Bc6 {looks like a decent Scheveningen set-up for Black and after} 16. e5 dxe5 17. Bxe5 g6 18. f5 Bc5+ 19. Kh1 Nh5 $1 20. Qg4 $2 exf5 21. Rxf5 Bxg2+ 22. Qxg2 Qxg2+ 23. Kxg2 gxf5 {the attack had been neatly rebuffed in Jensen-Hansson, Copenhagen 1981.}) 13... Nc6 14. g5 Ne5 $5 ({The leading Cuban Grandmaster isn't afraid to sharpen the struggle. With the ideal retreat to d7 unavailable,} 14... Ne8 {was the alternative, but after} 15. Qg3 {I'd prefer to take White: f4-f5 is coming and even} Na5 16. f4 Nxb3 17. cxb3 f6 {doesn't totally solve Black's problems.}) 15. Qg2 Nh5 $1 {Keeping the knight active and presenting a definite obstacle in the way of White's goal of blowing open the kingside.} 16. f4 Nc4 17. Bxc4 bxc4 18. f5 ({Annotating for ChessVibes, Van Delft and Ris point out that White could have won a pawn with} 18. Qe2 g6 19. Qxc4 {and that} Qxb2 (19... Rfc8 $5 {is another idea, driving White's queen offside in the event of} 20. Qb3 Qc7) 20. Qc7 Bd8 21. Qxd7 Qxc3 22. Qxd6 Rc8 {would have given Black decent counterplay. One can certainly imagine the exposed nature of White's kingside coming back to haunt her here.}) 18... g6 19. Nce2 $2 ({Polgar must have underestimated the coming closing of the centre or perhaps overestimating her attacking chances. Instead} 19. Rb1 {'keeps the position more or less balanced' (Van Delft and Ris). Extending this with} exf5 $5 20. Nd5 Be6 21. Nxe7+ Qxe7 22. exf5 Bxf5 23. Nxf5 gxf5 {we find Black's position looking a little precarious, bit actually this might be OK for him with ...Qe4 up next.}) ({Another idea is} 19. f6 $5 Bd8 20. Nde2 {, aiming to exchange off that monster knight on h5. However, Black's counterplay is quite far advanced here and after} Bb6 (20... Qxb2 $2 21. Rb1 Qxa3 22. Ra1 Qb2 23. Rfb1 $1 Qxc2 24. Qf2 $1 {leaves the black queen in a spot of bother,}) ({but} 20... h6 $5 {might be considered}) 21. Ng3 Bxe3+ 22. Rxe3 Qb6 23. Nd1 Nxg3 24. Qxg3 e5 $1 25. Qh4 Kh8 26. Qh6 Rg8 {it's not at clear how White can increase the pressure on the kingside.}) 19... e5 $1 {A thematic counterstrike and one which leaves White beginning to look a little overextended.} 20. f6 Bd8 21. Nf5 $5 ({A decent-enough practical try. Instead after} 21. Nf3 {Black can either munch on e4 or pick up the exchange with the crafty} Qc8 $5 {.}) 21... gxf5 {Refusing to be bluffed. White will now be able to remove the knight from h5, but is that really going to be worth a piece?} 22. Nc3 Kh8 ({Dominguez sees no reason why he shouldn't be the one attacking on the kingside. That's fair enough, but perhaps a better way to begin was the calm} 22... f4 $5 23. Bd2 ({after} 23. Qf3 fxe3 24. Qxh5 Kh8 {Black's light-squared bishop does a sterling job of protecting its king}) 23... Qc8 {followed by ...Kh8 and ...Rg8 (Van Delft and Ris), leaving White in pretty desperate trouble.}) 23. Qh3 Nf4 ({Black must have been very short of time by this point, as he had a number of pretty easy ways to win, including} 23... Nxf6 $5 24. gxf6 f4 {, with favourable simplification. Don't forget that Black is a whole piece ahead!}) 24. Bxf4 exf4 25. Rxf4 Rg8 $5 ({Arguably consistent and certainly risky. Simpler would have been} 25... fxe4 26. Qh6 Rg8 {when I don't really see what White does next, with her own king so exposed, as highlighted by} 27. Ref1 {(trying to get in Rh4)} Bb6+ 28. Kg2 ({or} 28. Kh1 e3+) 28... Be3 {.}) 26. Rh4 {White's only hope is to throw everything at Black's king and hope for a mistake.} Rxg5+ 27. Kf1 Bxf6 $4 ({Black has made it hard for himself, but would still have been winning and fairly easily after} 27... Kg8 $1 28. Rxh7 Bxf6 {: for instance,} 29. Nd5 Bg7 30. Rxg7+ $5 Kxg7 31. Qc3+ Kh6 32. Qh3+ Kg6 33. Nf4+ Kg7 {and the checks begin to dry up.}) 28. Nd5 Qxb2 $2 ({Collapsing, but presumably only now did Dominguez realise that} 28... Bg7 $2 29. Ne7 {would have led to utter disaster on the kingside (} Bh6 30. Rxh6 Rg7 {doesn't help after} 31. Qc3 $1 {, threatening mate in two).}) ({Black might, however, have moved his bishop with} 28... Be5 {when} 29. Rxh7+ (29. Ne7 $2 Rg7 {now keeps everything covered}) 29... Kg8 30. Ne7+ Kf8 31. Qh6+ Kxe7 32. Qxg5+ Kf8 {would have left White struggling to do more than repeat moves.}) ({However, with more time on the clock I'm sure Dominguez would have found the cleanest solution:} 28... Rg1+ $1 29. Kxg1 Bxh4 30. Qxh4 Rg8+ {, as pointed out by Van Delft and Ris. After} 31. Kf1 fxe4 {Black's queen is a long way from the action, but} 32. Nf6 Bf5 33. Nxg8 Kxg8 34. Qf6 Qb5 {activates it in time and leaves White having to take the draw in view of his rather fragmented position.}) 29. Rxh7+ Kg8 30. Qh6 $1 {As ever Polgar is deadly when it comes to finishing off a badly-exposed king.} Bd8 ({This runs into an interference tactic, but} 30... Bg7 {would have run into} 31. Rh8+ $1 Bxh8 32. Ne7# {.}) 31. e5 Rg1+ {There's only going to be one more proper check after this, but in any case there was no defence.} 32. Kxg1 Qd4+ 33. Kf1 1-0

