1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bd3 Nf6 (5... Nc6 6. Nge2 Nge7 7. O-O Bf5 $10) 6. Nge2 {6.g5 6.g5 6.g5 Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Logical. We don't mind blocking our queen since we don't have access to h5 and we would lose time with our queen on f3. '} h6 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Stops the pin, but creates a big sac target on h6. White also has access to simple plans like a future f3, g4, h4 and g5.'} ({Preferable was} 6... O-O {, although even here} 7. O-O c6 8. Bg5 {left Black wishing his bishop was on e7 rather than on b4, I.Nataf-V.Rosinovsky, Aubervilliers 2001.}) 7. Be3 c6 (7... O-O 8. Qd2 Ne4 9. Bxe4 dxe4 10. a3 Bxc3 11. Nxc3 $14) 8. Qd2 a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is nervous about sacs on h6 and so refrains from castling kingside. His last move compounds his troubles by making queenside castling next to impossible. Rivas Pastor is under the mistaken impression that queenside castling by White is imminent and begins attacking the non-existent king on that side of the board. '} ({He should play something like} 8... Be6 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Nf4 O-O $1 {with a slightly worse but playable position.}) 9. a3 Be7 { 9...e7} 10. f3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's problem: where to put his king? Either wing is unsafe. Meanwhile Polgar keeps Black guessing about the status of her own king and keeps it in the centre for now, while also annexing large amounts of territory on the kingside. '} Na6 11. g4 Nh7 12. Ng3 $16 Ng5 {12...0-0} 13. Qe2 b5 14. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Nh7 (14... Ne6 15. f5 Nf8 16. Nh5 $16) 15. g5 $1 hxg5 16. fxg5 $16 g6 17. h4 b4 $2 { 17...f8} 18. Na4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} ({It would be unwise to allow Black's knight to trade itself off for White's bishop on d3 with} 18. axb4 $2 Nxb4 {.}) 18... bxa3 19. bxa3 Nf8 20. Bf4 $1 Ne6 21. Be5 Rg8 (21... Rf8 22. O-O Ra7 23. Rxf7 $1 Kxf7 24. Bxg6+ $18) 22. O-O {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hiding in plain sight. Black's passivity ensures that White's king remains safe out in the open. '} Ra7 23. c4 $1 Bd6 24. cxd5 Bxe5 25. Qxe5 Qxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Trading queens at the high cost of granting White's knight access to e4.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'However, after'} 25... cxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'it's just a matter of time before d5 falls: for example,'} 26. Bb5+ (26. Rac1 Nac7 27. Nb6 Ba6 28. Bxa6 Rxa6 29. Rc6 $1 Rf8 30. Ne2 $1 {and Black is tied up in a knot and d5 is doomed.})) 26. Ne4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The knight is an octopus, spreading its power over f6, d6 and c5. '} Qxe5 27. dxe5 Kd8 ({Hopeless, as is} 27... Kf8 28. Nd6 Kg7 29. Nb6 $1 Rc7 30. Nbxc8 {.}) 28. Rad1 Nb8 29. Bc4+ Bd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black is helpless. For example:'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'b)'} 29... Ke7 30. Nd6 Rf8 31. Rxf7+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Rxf7 32. Nxc8+ $18 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) ({a)} 29... Rd7 30. Nf6 {.}) 30. Rxf7 Re8 31. Nb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attackers all across the board are drawn to the d7-square like flies to a garbage can! '} Rb7 32. Nxd7 Rxd7 33. Rfxd7+ {Judit Polgar Judit Polgar Judit Polgar Cyrus Lakdawala: '34 Nf6 follows. Summary: The tip from this game is to stall castling if you can and keep Black guessing about the address of your king.'} 1-0

