1. e4 {Postny,E Postny} e5 2. Bc4 {Judith Polgar avoids the Petroff Defence as she needed to win this game to level the score.} Nf6 3. d3 c6 4. Qe2 {This move is directed to prevent the advance d7-d5.} ({In case of} 4. Nf3 {Black can play} d5 $11) 4... Be7 ({Actually} 4... d5 $5 {is possible at this point too, but Black has to be ready to sacrifice a pawn for an initiative. For instanse:} 5. exd5 cxd5 6. Qxe5+ (6. Bb5+ Nc6 7. Nf3 Bd6 8. Nxe5 O-O 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc6 Bg4 11. f3 Rc8 12. fxg4 Rxc6 13. O-O Qc7 $44 {[%csl Rc2,Rh2]}) 6... Be7 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. Nf3 O-O 10. O-O Bd6 $44) 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bb3 d6 (6... d5 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Na6 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Re1 Bf6 12. Nd2 Nc5 13. Ne4 Nxb3 14. axb3 Be7 15. Ng3 $14 {0-1 Rathore,S (2372)-Sasikiran,K (2525)/Nagpur 1999/CBM 069 ext (40)}) 7. O-O Nbd7 8. c3 a5 9. a4 b5 {Boris is going for an immediate queenside expansion.} (9... Nc5 10. Bc2 Ne6 {was another logical option.}) 10. Bc2 Ba6 11. axb5 cxb5 12. Nbd2 Qc7 13. d4 a4 (13... b4 $6 14. c4 $14 {The a5 might become a weakness.}) 14. Bd3 Rfb8 15. Nh4 g6 16. f4 {The situation in the match urges Judith to play aggressively without really concerning about the risk.} exf4 17. Ndf3 Nh5 18. Bd2 Nb6 19. g4 $1 {The only way to continue the initiative.} fxg3 20. Ng5 Nc4 $2 {Boris Gelfand played superbly in Khanty-Mansiysk, but in the current game he lost control over the position.} (20... gxh2+ 21. Qxh2 Bxg5 22. Bxg5 b4 {Black must exchange as many pieces as possible to reduce white's attacking potential.} 23. Bxa6 Rxa6 24. Nf5 f6 25. Nh6+ Kg7 {and Black defends successfully.}) 21. Nf5 $2 {This is tempting, but the simple 21.xf7 could yield White already a crushing attack.} (21. Rxf7 gxh2+ (21... Rf8 22. Rxh7 $18) 22. Kxh2 $3 {This recapture is amazing for a human, but in such a sharp position an engine knows better...} (22. Qxh2 $2 Nxd2 23. Rxh7 (23. Nf5 $4 Bxg5 24. Rxc7 Nf3+ $19 {reveals the drawback of 22.xh2 compared to the correct 22.xh2.}) 23... b4 24. Bxa6 Qa5 25. Rxh5 gxh5 26. Nf5 Bxg5 27. Qxh5 Qd8 28. Qg6+ Kf8 29. Qg7+ Ke8 30. Qg6+ Kf8 $11) 22... Nxd2 23. Nf5 $1 $18) 21... Bxg5 22. Bxg5 f6 (22... gxf5 $2 23. Qxh5 $18 {[%cal Gg5f6]}) 23. Bh4 gxh2+ 24. Qxh2 Rf8 (24... gxf5 25. Rxf5 Ng7 26. Rf3 $1 Qf7 (26... f5 27. exf5 Bb7 28. Rg3 $40) 27. Bxf6 Nh5 28. e5 Nxf6 29. Rxf6 Qe7 30. Kf2 Nxb2 31. Qg2+ Kh8 32. Bxh7 Qxh7 33. Rh1 Ra7 34. Rxh7+ Rxh7 35. Qf3 Rg8 36. Rf8 $18) 25. Be2 gxf5 (25... Nd2 26. Bxh5 Nxf1 27. Qg2 Kh8 28. Bxg6 b4 29. Rxa4 hxg6 30. Qxg6 Qf7 31. Bxf6+ Qxf6 32. Qh5+ Kg8 33. Nh6+ Kg7 34. Nf5+ Kg8 $11) 26. Bxh5 fxe4 $2 {Too greedy. Boris had to bring his queenside pieces into action as soon as possible.} (26... Qg7+ 27. Kh1 Bb7 28. Qf4 (28. d5 Qe7 29. Rae1 fxe4 30. Qg2+ Kh8 31. Rxe4 Qd7 32. Re2 Ne5 $1 33. Bxf6+ Rxf6 34. Rxf6 Rg8 {with fine compensation for an exchange.}) 28... Kh8 29. Kh2 Bxe4 30. Rg1 Qe7 31. Qh6 a3 $1 32. bxa3 Nb6 33. Bg6 Nd5 34. Rae1 Rxa3 35. Bxh7 Ra2+ 36. Kh3 Nf4+ 37. Kg3 $1 Nh5+ $1 38. Kh3 $11 {Another spectacular line by an engine!} (38. Qxh5 $2 Qxh7 39. Qxh7+ Kxh7 $19)) 27. Qf4 $1 (27. Bxf6 {would allow Black to sacrifice an exchange:} Rxf6 28. Rxf6 Qg7+ 29. Qg2 Qxg2+ 30. Kxg2 Nxb2 $13) 27... f5 (27... e3 28. Kh2 $40 {[%cal Gf1g1]}) (27... Qg7+ 28. Kh2 Kh8 29. Rg1 Qe7 30. Qh6 $40 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Ga1f1]}) 28. Kh1 Kh8 29. Rg1 {Most of black's pieces got stuck on the queenside and can't help his king.} Rf7 (29... Nb6 30. Qh6 Nd5 31. Rg2 Rg8 32. Bf6+ Nxf6 33. Qxf6+ Rg7 34. Rag1 Rag8 35. Rxg7 Rxg7 36. Qf8+ $18) 30. Bxf7 Qxf7 31. Qh6 Rf8 32. Rg6 $1 {[%csl Rf6] There is no defense against 33.f6+ or 33.f6.} 1-0

