1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. Be3 Be7 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. c3 Qd7 12. Bc2 {The main drawback to the immediate 12.Bc2 is that White allows ...f5 under favourable circumstances for Black. 12 Re1 is considered to be more precise as Black doesn't then have such a useful move.} ({Earlier I only analysed} 12. Re1 {for you, Leko, P (2735) -Akopian, V (2694)/Astrakhan RUS 2010/[Mikhalevski, V].}) 12... f5 13. exf6 Nxf6 14. Bg5 $5 {An extremely rare continuation. I've found only one preceding game with two unknown players. Probably White's idea is to vacate the e-file for the rook.} (14. Qb1 {taking control of the important light squares and preventing ...Bf5 is the mainline.}) 14... Bd6 {A typical and useful move in this position. Black improves the position of the bishop and also vacates the e-file for her rooks.} 15. a4 {Judith tries to create an object of attack on b5 hoping for a combined attack along the e-file and on b5.} ({The aforementioned game continued} 15. Re1 Bg4 $6 (15... Rae8 $5) 16. Nf1 ({In case of} 16. h3 {Black can sacrifice a piece and obtain a strong attack.} Bxh3 17. gxh3 Qxh3) 16... Ne7 {with roughly equal chances in Hofrichter, J  (2113)-Kleindienst, T/Germany 2001.} (16... Bxf3 $5)) 15... b4 $1 {In order to avoid the attack on b5 Black advances the pawn.} 16. a5 {White takes some space and creates a positional threat of 17.Ba4 with an annoying pin.} bxc3 17. bxc3 Qf7 $1 {This way Black makes the 18.Ba4 move ineffective. We can say that Black has already equalised.} 18. Re1 (18. Ba4 $6 {makes no sense due to} Nxa5 ({or} 18... Ne5)) 18... h6 $1 {A useful move. Black attacks the bishop, takes the g5-square under control and makes a luft at the same time.} 19. Bh4 Ng4 {Black wants to connect the knights on e5, though the king's knight could have been employed better.} (19... Nh5 $1 {transferring the knight to f4 was better and promised good play. For example,} 20. Ba4 $1 Nd8 $1 21. Nd4 Nf4 $1 {with the idea of 22...Bd7 and Black looks ok.}) 20. Bg3 $5 {After the exchange of the dark-squared bishops Black experiences some problems with the dark squares c5 and e5.} (20. Nf1 $1 {with the idea of 21.Bg3 would be more precise.} Nge5 (20... Rab8 21. Bg3 {with the idea} Bxg3 $2 22. Nxg3 {and the knight on g4 is misplaced.}) 21. Nxe5 Nxe5 22. Ne3 (22. Bg3 $5) 22... Nc4 23. Qd3 g5 24. Bg3 $14 {and Black has problems to solve, in particular, the vulnerable position of the king.}) 20... Bxg3 21. hxg3 Bf5 {Koneru offers a typical exchange of the light-squared bishops.} (21... Rae8 $1 {deserved serious attention as it could have justified a knight jump to g4.} 22. Nb3 (22. Ba4 $5 Bd7 23. Rxe8 Rxe8 24. Qf1 Nb8 $1 25. Bxd7 Qxd7 {with just a marginal advantage for White.}) 22... Nce5 23. Rxe5 (23. Nxe5 Qxf2+ 24. Kh1 Nxe5 25. Rxe5 Qxg3 26. Rxe6 Rxe6 27. Qxd5 Qh4+ 28. Kg1 Qf2+ {with a perpetual.}) 23... Nxe5 24. Nxe5 Qxf2+ 25. Kh2 Bh3 $1 26. Qxd5+ $1 (26. Kxh3 $2 Rxe5 $19 {is hopeless for White.}) 26... Be6 27. Qd2 Rd8 $1 28. Qxf2 Rxf2 29. Nc5 Rxc2 30. Nxe6 Re8 31. Nd4 Rxc3 32. Nec6 Re4 {and Black isn't worse.}) 22. Ba4 $5 {A natural attempt to avoid an exchange of bishops.} ({However} 22. Bxf5 $1 Qxf5 23. Nb3 {could have also set some problems.} Qh5 {Black creates a threat of 24...Rxf3.} (23... Kh8 $5 {might be more accurate.}) 24. Ra4 $1 {A clever defence.} Rxf3 25. Rxg4 $1 (25. Qxf3 $2 {loses to} Qh2+ 26. Kf1 Qh1+ 27. Ke2 Re8+ $19) 25... Qxg4 26. gxf3 Qc4 27. f4 $1 Rb8 28. Nd4 Nxd4 29. cxd4 $14 {Black experiences some problems due to the vulnerable position of her king.}) 22... Nxa5 $1 {Black has to accept the sacrifice.} 23. Ne5 $2 {After this mistake Black is doing fine. Indeed it looks wrong to exchange the misplaced knight on g4.} (23. Nd4 $1 {may look dangerous in view of} Nxf2 $2 {, but in fact the move doesn't work.} (23... Bc8 $1 {is correct.} 24. N2f3 c5 $1 25. Bc2 $1 Nc4 $1 (25... cxd4 $6 26. Rxa5 $14 {with the idea} dxc3 $6 27. Rxd5 $16 {and Black's position is very unpleasant.}) 26. Nc6 Be6 $1 27. Qd3 Nf6 28. Nce5 Nxe5 29. Nxe5 Qc7 30. Ng6 Rfe8 31. Ba4 Bd7 32. Ne7+ Kh8 33. Nxd5 Nxd5 34. Qxd5 Bxa4 35. Rxe8+ Rxe8 36. Rxa4 a5 {and Black should be able to hold.}) 24. Kxf2 Bc2+ (24... Bg4+ 25. N2f3 $18) 25. Qf3 Qg6 ({or} 25... Bxa4 26. Qxf7+ Rxf7+ 27. Kg1 c5 28. N4f3 $16 {and White wins a piece.}) 26. Bxc2 Rxf3+ 27. gxf3 Qb6 28. Re6 $1 Qc5 29. Re3 $1 $16 {and White's extra material will tell.}) 23... Nxe5 24. Rxe5 Rad8 $1 25. Qe2 Qf6 $1 {With the last two moves Black has consolidated the position, she just needs to bring the misplaced knight into the game to claim an advantage.} 26. Re1 $1 {The control of the e-file allows White to partly compensate the pawn.} Kh8 $1 {Black secures the king.} 27. g4 $5 {White sacrifices two more pawns to maintain the initiative.} (27. Qf3 {deserved attention.} c6 28. Re7 {with some initiative for the pawn.}) 27... Be4 $1 {Humpy decides to avoid complications. A good practical decision.} ({It turns out that after} 27... Bxg4 $5 28. Qxg4 Qxf2+ 29. Kh2 Qxd2 30. R1e3 $1 {it's not easy for Black to extinguish White's initiative.} (30. Re7 Qf4+ $17) 30... Qf2 $1 31. Rg3 Qf7 32. Bc2 Nc6 33. Rg5 $1 Rg8 34. Bg6 $1 {with compensation for the sacrificed material.}) 28. Rh5 Bg6 29. Re5 Be4 {Koneru agrees to a draw.} ({Black could have played on:} 29... Qf4 $1 30. Ra1 Nb7 (30... Nc4 $5) 31. Nb3 Be4 32. Rh5 Rd6 33. f3 Bg6 34. Re5 c5 $15 {with a slight edge for Black.}) 30. Rh5 Bg6 31. Re5 Be4 {The 12.Bc2 line doesn't seem to set many problems, though Judith's idea is interesting and requires more practical tests.} 1/2-1/2

