1. e4 {Gelfand,B Rogozenko} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. c3 {Rogozenko: 'Having lost a principled opening battle against Leko few rounds earlier in the same tournament, Judit changes the variation and plays a line she used years ago. This time she was more successful with it, although the opening outcome was certainly not completely satisfactory for White.'} (11. Bd3 {Rogozenko} Be6 12. Qh5 Rg8 13. g3 Rg5 14. Qd1 Bxd5 15. exd5 Ne7 {was met in round 5, Polgar,J-Leko,P/Budapest 2003.}) 11... Bg7 12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nc2 O-O 14. Nce3 Be6 15. Bd3 f5 16. O-O Ra7 ({Judit's previous game in this line brought White little joy:} 16... Kh8 {Rogozenko} 17. Qh5 Bf7 18. Qh3 e4 19. Bc2 Ne5 20. f3 $6 (20. Rad1 $142) 20... exf3 21. gxf3 Bxd5 22. Nxd5 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 Qd2 24. Bb3 Nd3 25. Qh5 Rae8 $17 {0-1 Polgar,J-Illescas Cordoba,M/Dos Hermanas 1999 (61).}) (16... e4 {(Rogozenko) is another possible option.}) 17. f4 {Rogozenko: '17.a4 and 17.Qh5 are the main options for White, the former being probably stronger. Although White scored good results in practice after 17.f4, it can hardly set any problems for Black.'} (17. a4 {Ü 83/130}) 17... Ne7 $1 $11 {= Rogozenko: 'Gelfand deviates first from his own game and easily achieves a comfortable position.'} (17... e4 {Rogozenko} 18. Bc2 Kh8 19. Kh1 a5 20. Qe2 b4 21. Rad1 bxc3 22. bxc3 a4 23. g4 fxg4 24. Nxg4 Ne7 25. Bxe4 Nxd5 26. Bxd5 Bxg4 27. Qxg4 Bxc3 $11 {½-½ Voitsekhovsky,S-Gelfand,B/Belgrade 1998 (29)}) (17... Raf7 {Rogozenko} 18. Kh1 (18. a4 $5) 18... Qb8 19. Bc2 b4 20. Ba4 $1 $14 {1-0 Gashimov,V-Skripchenko,A/Dubai 2003 (41)}) 18. Bc2 (18. Nxe7+ Rxe7 {( 19.fe5 b6)} 19. Bc2 $11 (19. fxe5 $140 Qb6)) 18... Nxd5 19. Nxd5 a5 (19... Qc8 {Rogozenko} 20. Bb3 Kh8 21. Kh1 Qc5 22. Qd3 a5 23. Ne3 e4 24. Qd2 d5 25. a3 Rd7 26. Nc2 Rd6 27. Rad1 Rfd8 28. Qe1 Qb6 29. Qh4 Bc8 30. Qh5 b4 31. axb4 axb4 32. Rxd5 {½-½ Timofeev,A-Batsanin,D/Togliatti 2003.}) 20. a4 ({Rogozenko: Stronger is} 20. a3 {'' Rogozenko.} Qc8 21. Bb3) 20... Qc8 {'!' Rogozenko. Rogozenko: 'Black has a nice position. It turns out that White's activity on the queenside started with the move 20.a4 only favours Black, who will soon create pressure along the open "a" and "b" files.'} (20... Rb7 $5) 21. Bb3 Qc5+ 22. Kh1 (22. Rf2 bxa4 23. Rxa4 Rb7 $17) 22... Kh8 (22... Rb7 23. Ne3 $1) 23. axb5 Qxb5 24. Rf2 (24. Nb4 $2 Bxb3 25. Qxb3 axb4) (24. Ra3 a4 (24... Qc5 $5) 25. Nc7 Rxc7 26. Bxe6 e4 27. Rxa4 Qxb2 $11) 24... a4 25. Ba2 $6 (25. Rd2 Rfa8 26. Ba2 a3 27. b4 e4 28. Rc1 $13) 25... a3 {'!' Rogozenko.} 26. Nb4 $2 (26. b4 $15) 26... axb2 $2 (26... Qc5 27. Nd3 Qxf2 $3 {[%mdl 512]} 28. Nxf2 axb2 29. Rb1 Rxa2 30. Qd2 (30. Qxd6 Ra1) 30... exf4 $1 (30... e4 31. Nd1 (31. Rxb2 e3) 31... Bb3 32. Rxb2 (32. Ne3 Rc8) 32... e3 (32... Rxb2 33. Nxb2 d5 34. Qe3) 33. Qe1 Rxb2 34. Nxb2 $15) (30... Bb3 31. Nd3 (31. Nd1 e4) 31... e4 32. Rxb2 (32. Nxb2 Rc8 $17 (32... e3 33. Qe2)) 32... Ra1+ 33. Nc1 Be6 34. Rb7 Rc8 35. Rxg7 Kxg7 36. h3 Kf7 37. Kh2 $15) 31. Nd1 (31. Nd3 Rb8 32. Qe1 (32. Nb4 Rxb4 33. cxb4 Ra1 34. Qc2 Be5 35. b5 Ba2) 32... Bg8 33. Nxf4 Ra3 $19) (31. h3 Rc8 (31... Rg8 32. Nd3 (32. Rxb2 Ra1+ 33. Nd1 Be5) (32. Nd1) 32... Bd5 33. Ne1 Be4 34. Rxb2 $15) 32. Nd1 Be5 33. Rxb2 Ra1 $17) 31... Be5 32. Rxb2 (32. Nxb2 Rb8 $19) 32... Ra1 33. Kg1 f3 $1 34. gxf3 Rg8+ 35. Kf2 (35. Kh1 f4 $19) 35... f4 $1 $19 (35... Bxh2 36. Qh6 Bg3+ 37. Ke2 Bc4+ 38. Kd2 $13) 36. Rb5 Bh3 37. Ke2 Rg2+ 38. Nf2 Rxh2 39. Rb8+ Kg7 40. Rb7+ Kf6 41. Kd3 d5 42. Rb2 Kf5) 27. Rxb2 Rfa8 {Rogozenko: 'This is somewhat superficial. Black could have tried to achieve more:'} (27... Rxa2 {Rogozenko} 28. Rbxa2 Bxa2 29. Rxa2 (29. Nxa2 Qb2 $36) 29... Qc4 {[%csl Rc3,Rf4] White still has problems to solve.}) 28. Rab1 $1 {Rogozenko: 'Thanks to this small tactical trick White escaped the pin along the a-file.'} Bxa2 29. Nxa2 Qe8 $11 (29... Qe8 30. Qxd6 $2 {Rogozenko} (30. Nb4 {Rogozenko} exf4 $5 $11 {/}) (30. Qxd6 $2 Rxa2 31. Rxa2 Rxa2 32. Rb8