1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 5. Nb5 d6 {Having said last month that the Kalashnikov hadn't been too popular at 2600+ level this year, along came Radjabov at the recent President's Cup to uphold his old favourite.} 6. c4 ({Two rounds later Mamedov preferred} 6. N1c3 {against Radjabov, and after} a6 7. Na3 Be7 $5 ({a fashionable move order Black can also consider} 7... b5 8. Nd5 Nge7 ({or} 8... Nf6 {a la Radjabov when I feel that White should advance his c-pawn without delay, since in L.Milov-Piscopo, Calvi 2010,} 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 10. c4 Qg6 $1 11. f3 Be7 12. Be3 O-O 13. cxb5 $6 axb5 14. Nxb5 d5 {gave Black dangerous play for the pawn}) 9. c4 Nd4 10. Be3 Nxd5 11. cxd5 g6 12. Bd3 Bg7 13. O-O O-O 14. Qd2 f5 15. Bxd4 exd4 16. exf5 Bxf5 17. Nc2 Kh8 {, as in Pavlovic-Tadic, Serbian Championship, Kragujevac 2010 at first I thought that White should be better here, as his knight has some good outposts, but on closer consideration Black may well be fine, since he will be quick to activate his queen and take aim at d5}) 8. Nd5 Nf6 9. Nxf6+ ({a more critical choice than} 9. Be3 Nxd5 10. exd5 Nd4 $1 {which, as we've seen in Socko-Radjabov, European Team Ch., Novi Sad 2009, is a straightforward equalizer}) 9... Bxf6 10. Nc4 Be7 ({the solid choice Black might, though, prefer to sacrifice with} 10... O-O $5) 11. Be3 Be6 12. Nb6 Rb8 13. Nd5 ({as we've considered before, Black is close to equality after} 13. Qd2 O-O 14. Bd3 Bg5 $1) 13... O-O 14. c3 ({taking control of d4 instead Zelcic-Pilaj, Austrian League 2005, saw the related} 14. Be2 Bg5 15. Bb6 Qd7 16. O-O {and now Black might have considered the straightforward} Ne7 $5 17. c4 f5) 14... Bg5 15. Bb6 Qd7 16. Bc4 Ne7 $1 {(now that White can no longer bring a pawn to c4, this makes a lot of sense)} 17. O-O Rbc8 18. Nxe7+ (18. Qd3 Nxd5 19. Bxd5 Bxd5 20. Qxd5 Qc6 {would be very comfortable too for Black}) 18... Qxe7 {1/2-1/2. Play might have continued} 19. Bd5 Bxd5 20. Qxd5 Rc6 21. Be3 Bxe3 22. fxe3 Rc5 {with full equality.}) ({Just a reminder that last month we also considered the Sveshnikov transposition} 6. a4 Nf6 7. N1c3 a6 8. Na3 Bg4 $1 {in Panarin-Moiseenko.}) 6... Be7 7. Be2 ({A slightly unusual and committal move order. White often prefers} 7. N1c3 a6 8. Na3 {and now} Nf6 (8... f5 $5) ({and} 8... Be6 {are, of course, important alternatives}) 9. Be2 {would transpose to the game.}) 7... Nf6 8. N1c3 a6 9. Na3 Be6 ({Radjabov develops along typical Kalashnikov lines, but I wonder why he rejected the more forcing} 9... Nd4 $5 {. Then, as we saw in the notes to Ponkratov-Moiseenko last month,} 10. O-O O-O 11. Nc2 Nxe2+ 12. Qxe2 Be6 13. b3 Rc8 {gave Black good counterplay with ...b5 imminent in Erdogdu-Grigore, Ankara 2010.}) 10. Be3 O-O 11. O-O Rc8 12. Rc1 h6 13. Qd2 ({Polgar continues to delay the standard regrouping of the knight from the rim. I dare say that she considered} 13. Nc2 {(at least in her preparation), but it allows Black to get in} Na5 $5 ({manoeuvring with} 13... Nd7 {is also possible}) 14. b3 b5 {. Here White might try} 15. Nb4 $5 bxc4 16. Nbd5 {, but after} Nxd5 17. Nxd5 Bg5 18. b4 Nc6 {Black should be OK.}) ({White might also facilitate her next while preventing . ..Ng4 with} 13. f3 {, but then} Nd4 $5 {seems OK for Black, as} ({or} 13... Nh5 {preparing ...Bg5}) 14. Bxd4 $6 exd4 15. Qxd4 $2 {runs into the powerful} d5 $1 {.}) 13... Ng4 ({Radjabov chases down the bishop-pair, which is quite a modern handling of the scarcely old Kalashnikov. This seems a reasonable choice here, although it does lead to quite a solid position without too many winning prospects for Black. I'm not totally convinced by the temporary sacrifice} 13... Nxe4 $6 14. Nxe4 f5 {as} 15. Nc3 f4 16. Bf3 {followed by Bd5 should give White an edge.}) ({However, Black might activate his queen with} 13... Qa5 $5 {perhaps followed by ...Nd7-c5.}) 14. Bxg4 Bxg4 15. Nd5 ({A novelty and quite a natural one at that as Polgar aims for control of d5. Previously} 15. f3 Be6 16. Rfd1 Bg5 17. Bxg5 hxg5 18. Kh1 Nd4 $1 19. Ne2 Nxe2 20. Qxe2 Rc6 21. Rc3 Qb6 {had given Black enough queenside pressure to compensate for his d-file weaknesses in Arnold-Lobron, German League 1996,}) ({but} 15. Nc2 $5 Be6 16. b3 f5 17. exf5 Rxf5 (17... Bxf5 18. Nd5 Rf7 {might improve}) 18. Rfd1 Qe8 (18... b5 19. cxb5 axb5 20. Nd5 $1 {also retains a pleasant pull}) 19. Ne4 {had given White an edge in Leko-Nataf, Istanbul Olympiad 2000.}) 15... Bg5 {Now that White can no longer pressure d6 so quickly, it makes sense to free Black's position with this thematic trade.} 16. Nc2 Be6 17. Rfd1 ({Not the only option and White might have exchanged:} 17. Bxg5 $5 Qxg5 ({the correct recapture, as} 17... hxg5 $6 18. f3 {looks quite nice for White}) 18. Qxg5 hxg5 19. Rfd1 Bxd5 20. cxd5 Ne7 {and while Black should hold with care, there's no doubt that White has a pull after} 21. Ne3 {.}) 17... Bxe3 18. Ndxe3 Qh4 19. f3 Rfd8 {Back in the Seventies this would have been assessed as a pretty pleasant advantage to White, but nowadays the weakness of d5 is held in less high regard. White is still a touch better here, but Black is very solid and Radjabov now held without difficulty.} 20. a4 Qg5 21. b4 ({Vigorous. Something like the more restrained} 21. b3 {was possible, but after} Ne7 22. Nb4 Nc6 {Black would have been very solid again the weakness of d4 compensates for that of d5.}) 21... Ne7 22. Ne1 Nc6 23. N1c2 Ne7 24. Ne1 ({White is happy to repeat. Instead} 24. Qd3 {would have overprotected c4 and prepared a queenside advance, but then the black knight would have gone the other way:} Ng6 $1 25. Ne1 ({and not} 25. b5 $2 Nf4 26. Qf1 Rxc4 $1) 25... Rc7 {and Black will double on the c-file with good counterplay.}) 24... Nc6 25. N1c2 1/2-1/2

