{[%mdl 1]} 1. e4 {Finkel,A Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian Polgar,Judit Ftacnik Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 (4. e5 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian is the another main continuation.}) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bxf6 Bxf6 7. Nf3 O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} (7... Nd7 {Polgar,Judit} 8. Qd2 {1-0 (24) Polgar,J (2715)-Berkes,F (2578) Budapest 87/278 2003 CI1-130 [Polgar,Judit]}) 8. Qd2 {Polgar,Judit: '1-0 (24) Polgar,J (2715)-Berkes,F (2578) Budapest 2003 CBM 95 [Finkel,A]'} Nd7 9. O-O-O Be7 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian: 'This variation used to be a specialty of Evgeny Bareev, who has since retired from tournament activity. Nowadays Matthias Bluebaum plays it many times.'} ({In view of the debacle that follows it seems as if Black should play the other line with an immediate} 9... b6 {Have a look at the examples on ChessPub if you want to try it as Black.}) 10. Bd3 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian: 'A natural move,'} ({but} 10. Bc4 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian}) ({and} 10. h4 {(Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian) are also popular.}) 10... b6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (12s): ' C13: French: Classical System: 4 Bg5 Be7, Alekhine-Chatard Attack'} ({Just a few weeks earlier, went} 10... c5 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian} 11. Nxc5 Nxc5 12. dxc5 Qd5 13. Kb1 Qxc5 14. h4 {Black should have played the prophylactic} h6 (14... b6 $2 {was careless, as} 15. Ng5 $1 h6 16. Bh7+ Kh8 17. Be4 Rb8 18. Qf4 $1 $18 {with attacking the b8-rook and the f7-pawn simultaneously, Topalov,V (2743)-Bareev,E (2729) Monte Carlo blind 2003.}) 15. g4 Bd7 16. g5 h5 {with a defensible position, Carmeille,L (2297)-Poetsch,H (2198) Germany 2007.}) 11. Neg5 $5 {Neil McDonald: '!' Neil McDonald: 'It may be that this puts Black's last move out of business.' A new move in very popular position. White is starting an actions on the kingnside without preliminary preparations. Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian: 'White is starting an actions on the kingside without preliminary preparations. A new move in very popular position. White is starting an actions on the kingnside without preliminary preparations.'} ({Neil McDonald: 'The slower'} 11. h4 {Neil McDonald: 'gives Black time to mobilise his queenside pieces and counterattack in the centre:' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A' Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian: 'is the another even more popular continuation.'} Bb7 12. Rh3 (12. Qe2 Nf6 13. Neg5 Bxf3 14. Nxf3 Qd6 15. Ne5 c5 16. dxc5 Qxc5 17. Kb1 Bd6 18. Nc4 Rfd8 19. g4 Bf8 20. Nd2 $14 {Van den Doel,E-Sakalauskas,V/Bled 2002}) (12. c3 c5 13. Neg5 (13. dxc5 Qc7 14. Neg5 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qf4+ 16. Kb1 Bxc5 17. g3 Bxf3 18. Nxf3 Qc7 $11 {Al Modiahki,M-Arizmendi Martinez,J/Biel 2002}) 13... Nf6 (13... h6 14. Bh7+ Kh8 15. Bc2 Bxf3 16. Nxf3 Nf6 {and Black held the draw against a renowned French killer in Anand,V-Bareev,E/ Monte Carlo MNC 2003.}) 14. Qc2 g6 15. h5 Nxh5 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Bxg6 Nf4 18. Bxh7+ $40 {Ivanchuk,V-Radjabov,T/Moscow 2002} Kg7 19. Be4 Bxe4 20. Qxe4 {½-½ (49) Ivanchuk,V (2711)-Radjabov,T (2610) Moscow 2002 CBM 090 [Finkel,A]}) 12... c5 13. Rg3 Qc7 14. dxc5 Rfd8 15. Nfg5 $1 bxc5 16. Nd6 Bxd6 17. Bxh7+ Kf8 18. Qxd6+ Qxd6 19. Rxd6 $16 {Gashimov,V-Dizdar,G/Dubai 2003}) (11. Qf4 $5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} Bb7 12. h4 Nf6 13. Neg5 Qd6 14. Ne5 Rad8 15. Rh3 h6 16. g4 c5 17. Ngxf7 Qxd4 18. Qxd4 Rxd4 19. g5 $36 {Guliev,L-Gouliev,N/Baku 2001}) 11... h6 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian: 'This is not a mistake yet, but it shows Black reluctance to exchange his bishop for the g5-knight, an attitude that will soon makes problems for him.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (12s): 'LiveBook: 3 Games'} ({White has a very dangerous initiative after both} 11... Nf6 12. Ne5 {and}) (11... g6 12. h4) (11... Bxg5 {Finkel,Alexander Szabo,Krisztian was the safer answer,} 12. Nxg5 Nf6 13. Nf3 Bb7 $11 {with a balanced position, Gashimov,V (2594)-Bareev,E (2688) Gothenburg 2005.}) 12. Bh7+ $1 {Neil McDonald: 'This looks like a blunder, but Judith Polgar has everything worked out.' F