{ChessCalisthenics: 'C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and 9 Nbd2' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and 9 Nbd2'' Navara,David: 'Podľa Judit Polgár v New in Chess 6/2016, strana 76.'} {Navara,David: 'My notes are not exactly original, as this game has been annotated by Judit Polgár herself for New in Chess 6/2016 (page 76). Moreover, GM Victor Mikhalevski had mentioned the relevant lines in his comments to the game Svidler - Anand, Dos Hermanas 1999. Funnily enough, Dos Hermanas means "two sisters" in Spanish! I am sure that Judit Polgár's two sisters loved the following game!'} 1. e4 {[%emt 0:00:09] Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics' Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics' C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and Nbd2} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 ({It is worth noting that the ancient line} 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 $6 6. Nxf7 (6. d4 $5) 6... Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 {resembles the game.}) 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. Nbd2 Nc5 10. c3 d4 11. Ng5 {Navara,David: 'This surprising move was first played in the World Championship match Karpov - Kortschnoj, Baguio 1978.'} Bd5 $6 {Mikhalevski,Viktor: '?' '?' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'It's interesting that this objectively bad move brought good results. In fact, black suffered first defeat in this game after winning 2 and drawing 5 games!'} ({Navara,David: 'By now it has become clear that black can hold after'} 11... Qxg5 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor} 12. Qf3 {ChessCalisthenics} O-O-O 13. Bxe6+ fxe6 14. Qxc6 Qxe5 15. b4 (15. a4 dxc3 16. Nf3 Qd5 17. axb5 Rd6 18. Qe8+ Rd8 19. Qc6 Rd6 20. Qe8+ Rd8 21. Qf7 Nb3 22. Rxa6 Nxc1 23. Rxc1 Bc5 24. Re1 cxb2 25. h3 Rhf8 26. Qxg7 Rxf3 27. gxf3 Qxf3 28. Qg2 Bxf2+ 29. Qxf2 Rg8+ {Iordachescu,V (2603)-Golod,V (2576)/Pori Erin 2006/CBM 114 ext/0-1}) 15... Qd5 16. Qxd5 exd5 17. bxc5 dxc3 18. Nb3 d4 19. Ba3 g6 {Navara,David: '!?'} (19... Be7) 20. Bb4 Bg7 21. a4 {Navara,David: '' Navara,David: '. The lines are complex and the ensuing positions are hard to evaluate. Back then the computers were very weak for today's standards.'} Kd7 22. axb5 axb5 23. Rfd1 Ke6 24. Rac1 Rhe8 25. Kf1 Kf5 26. c6 {Kasparov,G (2849)-Shirov,A (2718)/Linares 2001/CBM 082/1-0 (38)}) ({and} 11... dxc3 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor are the main alternatives.} 12. Nxe6 {ChessCalisthenics} fxe6 13. bxc3 Qd3 14. Bc2 Qxc3 15. Nb3 Rd8 16. Bd2 Qxe5 17. Re1 Qd5 18. Qg4 Nd4 19. Nxc5 Bxc5 20. Qxg7 Rf8 21. Bxh7 Rd7 22. Qg6+ Kd8 23. Bh6 Nf3+ 24. gxf3 Qxf3 25. Qg5+ Kc8 26. Qxc5 {Naiditsch,A (2611)-Mamedyarov,S (2660)/Pamplona 2004/CBM 105/1/2-1/2}) 12. Nxf7 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' Mikhalevski,Viktor: '!' '!' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N'' Navara,David: '!' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'This tactical blow combined with Svidler's 14.g4+ is a refutation of 11...d5? It's strange that Black tried to repeat this line after Svidler-Anand.' Navara,David: 'This sacrifice does not win by force, but gives white a very strong attack.'} (12. c4 {ChessCalisthenics (Mikhalevski,Viktor) ½-½ Mamedov,N-Mamedyarov,S/Baku 2001/EXT 2003 (25)} bxc4 {ChessCalisthenics} 13. Bxc4 h6 14. Ngf3 Be7 15. b3 Bxc4 16. Nxc4 Qd5 17. Ba3 Rd8 18. Bxc5 Bxc5 19. Rc1 O-O 20. Ne1 d3 21. Qxd3 Qxd3 22. Nxd3 Rxd3 23. Nb2 Rd5 24. Na4 Bxf2+ 25. Rxf2 Nxe5 {1/ 2-1/2 Mamedov,N (2392)-Mamedyarov,S (2479)/Baku 2001/EXT 2003}) ({In the beginning White tried to look for advantage after} 12. Bxd5 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor '!?' must be considered} Qxd5 {''} 13. Nb3 Nxb3 14. axb3 Be7 15. Nf3 d3 (15... Nxe5 {Anand-Sokolov,Lion,1994 CBM 45[Korchnoi]}) 16. Be3 O-O 17. b4 {0-1 McDonald,N-Gyimesi,Z/Budapest 1995/EXT 97 (32). (32)} (17. Bd4 {Onischuk-Sokolov,1997 CBM 57[Wedberg]}) 17... Rfd8 {ChessCalisthenics} 18. Re1 a5 19. bxa5 Rxa5 20. Rxa5 Nxa5 21. Nd4 Nc4 22. b3 Nxe5 23. f4 Ng6 24. b4 Qc4 25. Qd2 c5 26. bxc5 Bxc5 {McDonald,N (2425)-Gyimesi,Z (2525)/Budapest 1995/EXT 1997/ 0-1 (32)}) 12... Kxf7 {''} ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'After'} 12... Bxf7 $2 {Navara,David: 'White would regain the material after'} 13. Bxf7+ Kxf7 14. Qf3+ Kg8 {Mikhalevski,Viktor} 15. Qxc6 {Black's a pawn down in a bad position.}) 13. Qf3+ Ke6 {Mikhalevski,Viktor: '' ''} 14. Qg4+ {Mikhalevski,Viktor: '!' '!'} (14. Ne4 $6 {Mikhalevski,Viktor is less convincing:} Nxb3 (14... Nxe4 {½-½ Dimitrov,V-Kurcubic,A/Tivat 1995/EXT 2002 (45). (45)}) 15. Qg4+ Kf7 16. Qf5+ Kg8 17. e6 h6 (17... Ne5 $5) (17... Qe7 $5) 18. Qf7+ Kh7 19. Ng5+ $1 hxg5 20. Qh5+ Kg8 21. Qf7+ {½-½ Kalod,R-Virostko,P/CZE 2001/EXT 2002 (21)}) 14... Kf7 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: '??' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: '??'' Navara,David: 'This move loses by force. Shakhriyar was already a strong grandmaster, but still lacked experience with top-level chess and his opening repertoire was not impressive back then. Later he eliminated these weaknesses and became one of the best players in the world.' ChessCalisthenics: '??' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: '??'' Navara,David: 'This move loses by force. Shakhriyar was already a strong grandmaster, but still lacked experience with top-level chess and his opening repertoire was not impressive back then. Later he eliminated these weaknesses and became one of the best players in the world.' weakening the position} (14... Ke7 {Ü 75/311 ChessCalisthenics: '=' Ü 75/311 Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: '='' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Svidler-Anand,1999 CBM 71[Mikhalevski,V]'} 15. e6 $1 Bxe6 {''} 16. Re1 Qd7 17. Bxe6 Nxe6 18. Nf3 Re8 19. Ng5 Ncd8 20. Bd2 {, I warmly recommend GM Mikhalevski's notes to the Svidler - Anand game. Black managed to hold, but even the final position was lost in a study-like way!} h6 21. Nf3 Qd5 22. Re5 Qd6 23. cxd4 h5 24. Qe4 Kf7 25. d5 Kg8 26. Qg6 Re7 27. Rae1 Nd4 28. Qxd6 Nxf3+ 29. gxf3 cxd6 30. Rxe7 Bxe7 31. Rxe7 Nf7 32. Ra7 h4 33. Ra8+ Kh7 34. Rxa6 Kg6 35. Rb6 Rc8 36. Rxb5 Rc2 37. Bc3 h3 38. Kf1 Ng5 39. Rb7 Nxf3 40. Rxg7+ Kf5 41. Rg3 Ke4 42. Rxh3 Rc1+ 43. Ke2 Rxc3 44. bxc3 Ng1+ 45. Kf1 Nxh3 46. a4 Kxd5 47. Kg2 Ng5 48. h4 Ne4 49. h5 Ke6 50. c4 Nd2 51. a5 Nxc4 52. a6 Nb6 53. a7 Kf5 54. f4 Na8 55. Kf3 d5 56. Ke3 Nc7 57. Kd2 Kf6 58. Kc2 Kf5 59. Kb3 d4 60. Kc4 Kf6 61. Kd3 Kf5 62. Ke2 Kf6 63. Kf2 Kf5 64. Kf3 Na8 65. Ke2 Nc7 66. Kd3 Kf6 67. Ke4 Kf7 68. f5 Kf6 69. h6 Kf7 {˝-˝ (69) Svidler,P (2713)-Anand,V (2781) Dos Hermanas 1999 CBM 071 [Mikhalevski,V] Even this position is winning for white in a study-like way!} 70. Kxd4 $1 Nb5+ 71. Kc5 $1 Nxa7 72. Kb6 Nc8+ 73. Kc7 {The knight is dominated, one of white's pawns will queen.} Na7 (73... Ne7 74. h7 Kg7 (74... Nd5+ 75. Kd6 Kg7 76. Kxd5 Kxh7 77. Ke6 $18) 75. f6+) 74. Kd7 $1 Nb5 75. h7 Kg7 76. f6+ Kxh7 77. f7 Kg7 78. Ke7 $18) 15. Qf5+ $1 $146 {Mikhalevski,Viktor: '+-!' '+-!' Mikhalevski,Viktor: '+-!' '+-!' '+-'} ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 15. e6+ Bxe6 16. Bxe6+ Nxe6 17. Qf3+ Qf6 18. Qxc6) 15... Ke7 ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 15... Kg8 {ChessCalisthenics: '' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: ''' Navara,David: '?'} 16. e6 $18 {Navara,David: '!'} Ne5 {ChessCalisthenics (Mikhalevski,Viktor) ChessCalisthenics} (16... Nxe6 17. Qxe6+ Bxe6 18. Bxe6# {would have led to a beautiful mate.}) 17. Qxe5 Nxb3 18. axb3 dxc3 19. bxc3 Bc6 $18) (15... Ke8 16. e6 $18 Bxe6 17. Re1 dxc3 18. Bxe6 (18. Rxe6+ $6 Nxe6 19. Qxe6+ Ne7 $16) 18... Nxe6 19. Rxe6+ Ne7 20. bxc3 $18) 16. e6 $1 {Navara,David: 'This move opens the e-file against black's king.' Navara,David: 'This move opens the e-file against black's king.' Theme: Clearance for f5-c5} (16. Nc4 $2 {even loses to} Be6 {, e.g.} 17. Bg5+ Ke8 $1 (17... Kd7 $4 18. Nb6+ $1 cxb6 19. Bxe6+ Nxe6 20. Bxd8 Rxd8 21. Rad1 $18) 18. Nd6+ (18. Qf3 Qd7 $19) 18... Qxd6 $1 $19) (16. Nf3 $6 h6 $1 17. e6 $1 Nxb3 18. Bf4 $1 {is also very good for white, but black could still fight on after} Bxe6 19. Rfe1 $1 Nc5 $1 20. Qxc5+ Kf7 21. Qxc6 Bd5 22. Ne5+ Kg8 $16 {.}) 16... Bxe6 {A pinning theme} ({Navara,David: 'After' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 16... Nxb3 {Navara,David: ', the simple move'} 17. Nxb3 {Navara,David: '!' Navara,David: 'wins easily:'} Bxe6 18. Re1 Qc8 (18... Qd6 19. Nc5 $5 $18) 19. Bg5+ Kd6 20. Bf4+ Ke7 21. Nc5 Nd8 22. Rxe6+ $1 Nxe6 23. Re1 $18) (16... Nxe6 17. Bxd5 {Theme: Double Attack}) 17. Re1 $1 {Navara,David: '!?' Navara,David: 'This was a well-calculated continuation which lead to a brilliant finale, but it is a bit impractical to play like this, as white needs to play precisely.'} (17. Qxc5+ {also wins and is much more practical, as white does not need to calculate anything!} Qd6 18. Qh5 $18 {and black's king is simply too weak.} Bxb3 19. Nxb3 Kd7 $18) 17... Qd6 {ChessCalisthenics: '?' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: '?''} ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 17... Qd7 18. Bxe6 (18. Qxc5+ {ChessCalisthenics (Mikhalevski,Viktor) ChessCalisthenics} Kd8 19. Qg5+ Be7 20. Qxg7 Rg8 21. Qxh7 Bd5 $18 22. Bxd5 Qxd5 $18) 18... Nxe6 19. Nb3 g6 20. Qf3 $1 $18) 18. Bxe6 Nxe6 19. Ne4 Qe5 20. Bg5+ {Navara,David: '!'} Kd7 ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 20... Nxg5 21. Nxg5 $18) (20... Ke8 21. Nf6+ gxf6 22. Rxe5 $18) 21. Nc5+ {Navara,David: '!' Navara,David: '!' '!' Clearance to allow e1-e5} (21. Nf6+ $2 {would unnecessarily give away a piece} gxf6 22. Rxe5 fxe5 $13) 21... Bxc5 ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 21... Qxc5 {Theme: Deflection from e6} 22. Qxe6# {# # A classical mating theme}) 22. Qf7+ {Navara,David: '!' Navara,David: 'To decide on 20.g5+ and 21.c5+, it was enough to see 22.xe5 and evaluate it as clearly winning. The text move is even better, but white has to continue accurately!'} (22. Rxe5 {'?!' is the less attractive alternative} Nxe5 23. Qxe5 Bd6 {''} 24. Qf5 (24. Qd5 $5) 24... Raf8 25. Qh3 $18 {is just winning, but the game continuation is both stronger and more effective.}) 22... Kd6 ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 22... Kc8 {the last chance for counterplay} 23. Rxe5 $18 Nxe5 {ChessCalisthenics (Mikhalevski,Viktor) ChessCalisthenics} 24. Qxe6+ Nd7 25. cxd4 Bb6 $18) (22... Be7 23. Rxe5 Nxg5 24. Rxg5 $18) (22... Ne7 23. Rxe5 (23. Bxe7 $18) 23... Nxg5 24. Rxg5 $18) 23. Be7+ ({Navara,David: 'Black resigned in view of'} 23. Be7+ {Navara,David: '!' Navara,David: 'Without this move 22.xe5 would make no sense!' Navara,David: '!' Navara,David: 'Without this move 22.xe5 would make no sense!' '!!' the final blow} Kd5 ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Polgar'} 23... Nxe7 {A deflection} 24. Rxe5 {Annihilates a defender: e5} Kxe5 25. Re1+ Kd5 (25... Kd6 26. Qxe6#) 26. Qxe6# {#}) (23... Kd7 24. Bxc5+ $18) 24. Qf3+ Kc4 25. b3# {#}) ({White would have even lost after} 23. Rxe5 $4 {'?' looks tempting, but} Nxe5 24. Qe7+ $2 (24. Qh5 Nxg5 25. Qxg5 d3 $19) 24... Kd5 {, as the queen is trapped!}) 1-0

