the kingside, but this plan seems too clumsy. My main line runs'} 18. Rh1 Qg6 19. Qh4 f6 20. g4 Kh8 21. f5 Qg7 22. g5 fxg5 23. Nxg5 h6 24. Rg1 Rf6 25. Nh3 $1 {intending Nf4; it is doubtful that Black can defend.}) (17... Na5 18. Rh1 Qg6 19. Qh4 {was mentioned by Polgar. Black's position looks precarious: for instance,} Rfc8 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander'} 20. Rh3 Qf6 (20... Kh8 21. f5 Qg7 22. Ng5 Kg8 23. Rg3 Kh8 24. f6 $1 exf6 25. Nf3 {wins for White}) 21. Rg3+ Kh8 22. Ng5 Qg7 23. Nxh7 $1 Qxh7 24. Qxe7 Rg8 25. Rh3 {and White should win the ending.}) (17... Rfc8 $1 {looks best, when White must make a tough decision: Polgar,Judit: '(Finkel,Alexander) looks best, when White must make a tough decision:'} 18. Z0 ({. The first point to note is that White definitely has at least a draw to fall back on:} 18. Rd5 Nd8 (18... Na5 $2 {is worse:} 19. Bd7 $1 Qf6 20. Rg5+ Kf8 21. Qxh7 e6 22. Rg8+ Ke7 23. Bxc8 Qxf4+ 24. Kb1 {and White is winning}) 19. Nd4 (19. Bd3 Rxc3 {seems okay for Black, who can always sacrifice on d3 if the bishop threatens anything terrible}) 19... Qxe4 20. Rg5+ Kh8 21. Bd3 Qe1+ 22. Kb2 Qxc3+ 23. Kb1 Qe1+ {and the game ends in perpetual check.}) ({. There are other ways for White to play the position, but to tell the truth I have not found anything absolutely convincing: for example, after} 18. Bxc6 Rxc6 19. Nd4 Qxe4 ({if} 19... Rc5 20. Qxc5 bxc5 21. Nxe6 fxe6 22. Kd2) 20. Qg5+ Kf8 21. Nxc6 Qxc6 22. Rd3 {it seems doubtful that White can hope for any more than a draw, as his exposed king will be vulnerable to checks.}) ({. Another idea is} 18. Nh4 $5 {, which leads to unclear play after} Qf6 19. Nf5 e6 20. Nxd6 Qxf4+ 21. Kb2 {. I doubt that White is really better here, but the game goes on. --- In conclusion, it is possible that Black can narrowly maintain the balance with precise play. Should this dissuade you from playing this line with White? It depends on your own outlook, but unless your opponent is exceptionally well-prepared, I would say it is unlikely to matter much. Naturally, too, I would encourage the reader to conduct his own research and look for improvements in the above lines. If he is still unsatisfied, there are plenty of other ways to fight for the advantage, such as the untested 12 Nd5!? as well as the earlier deviation 8 d5, both of which were noted earlier.})))) 15. Qxh5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'White has a king attack'''} Bg7 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'This looks forced, as the threat was e5 followed by Bd3.'''} ({Andrew Greet: 'It is worth noting that' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'It is worth noting that''' James Vigus: 'It is too late for'} 15... Bxc3 $2 {Andrew Greet: '??' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: '??''' James Vigus: 'since now'} 16. Ng5 $1 $18 {Andrew Greet: '!' Andrew Greet: 'wins at once, as pointed out by Finkel.' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: '!' Andrew Greet: 'wins at once, as pointed out by Finkel.''' James Vigus: 'is decisive.'}) (15... Nb4 16. a3 $5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} (16. e5 {Polgar,Judit} Bg7 17. a3 Nc6 18. Bd3 h6 19. Qf5 $18) 16... Rxc3 17. axb4 $1 Re3 18. e5 Bg7 19. Bd3 h6 20. Qf5 Re8 (20... Rxd3 21. Qxd3 Qc8 22. exd6 exd6 23. Qxd6 $16) 21. Qh7+ Kf8 22. Bb5 $16) 16. e5 $1 {[%cal Gf1d3] Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!'' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!' Finkel,Alexander: '!' Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'The imminent Bd3 remains a serious concern for Black.' Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'White wins space'''} Qe8 $6 {More persistant was Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '?!' Finkel,A: 'More persistant was'' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '?!' Finkel,A: 'More persistant was' Finkel,Alexander: '?!''} ({James Vigus: 'If Black stops Ng5 by playing'} 16... h6 $5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!?'' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!?' Finkel,Alexander: '!?'' James Vigus: 'the knight takes a different route:'} 17. Bd3 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} (17. g4 {Polgar,Judit: '