1. e4 {Papp, Petra Erenburg Papp, Petra: 'Erenburg,Sergey'} d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nbd7 4. f4 {Papp, Petra: '!'} e5 5. Nf3 {Papp, Petra: '!'} exd4 6. Qxd4 c6 {Papp, Petra: 'The best move for Black. The plan is clear, playing for ..d6-d5, and opening up the a5-d8 diagonal.'} 7. Be3 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: 'A strong, but natural developing move that prepares 0-0-0.'} d5 {Papp, Petra: '!?' Papp, Petra: 'The most critical move. Black opens up the diagonal for his f8 with a pawn sacrifice, in order to complicate matters.'} (7... Be7 $6 {Papp, Petra is not a challenging move, as now White can accomplish her plan.} 8. O-O-O O-O 9. e5 $1 {A dynamic move is usual and best. Black's position is quite cramped, his c8 is very passive.} Ng4 10. Bg1 $1 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nb6 12. Qe4 $1 {It's important to not trade the queens. Now White can attack the misplaced g4 with with h3, then with g4 creating a more superior position on the kingside.} Qe8 13. h3 Nh6 14. g4 $16 {Groszpeter-Mikac, Austria 1995}) (7... Qb6 $6 {Papp, Petra Trading queens does not improve Black's position. The usual space gaining advance e4-e5 is coming and will give White a dominating position.} 8. O-O-O $1 Qxd4 9. Bxd4 Be7 10. e5 $1 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nh5 12. Be3 $1 $16 {Reutsky-Burmakin, Kharkov 2005}) 8. exd5 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: 'I like this move the most, White accepts the challenge. Black's pieces will come out with tempo, ..c5, e7,g4, so White must play precisely to gain an advantage.'} (8. e5 {Papp, Petra The other main move also gives White some advantage.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd3 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: 'The best square for the queen. On d2 the queen would be vulnerable to some tempo gaining moves.'} (9. Qd2 $6 {Papp, Petra} Qe7 $1 10. Nd4 Nb6 11. dxc6 O-O 12. O-O-O Bb4 $1 $132) 9... Qe7 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: 'Pinning the e3 is the main point of Black's play.'} (9... O-O $6 {Papp, Petra Just loses time, and Black will have no compensation for the sacrificed pawn.} 10. dxc6 bxc6 11. O-O-O Qb6 12. Bd4 $1 $16 {Goncharenko-Bocanegra Moreno, ICCF email 2008; An important and clever move. White doesn't want to take on c5, which would develop the d7-knight but instead he improves his position and theatens a4 now. The position is clearly better for White.}) 10. Nd4 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: 'Breaking the attack on the pinned bishop with this centralising move is the only way to play, but it's quite logical at the same time. This is the critical position, where Black has numerous options to choose from.'} Nb6 {Papp, Petra: 'The main move. Black sacrifices another pawn, but usually White cannot keep it as Black has good compensation due to fast development. White does best to keep just one extra pawn by consolidating the position with some precise moves. '} 11. dxc6 {Papp, Petra: '!'} O-O (11... Nbd5 $2 {Papp, Petra: 'Erenburg,Sergey'} 12. cxb7 Qxb7 13. Qb5+ Qxb5 14. Bxb5+ Bd7 15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Bf2 O-O-O 17. Ba6+ Kc7 18. Ne6+ Bxe6 19. Bxc5 Nxf4 20. O-O $18 {1-0 Bauer,C (2625)-Strikovic,A (2492)/Calvia 2005/EXT 2006 (56)}) (11... bxc6 {Papp, Petra} 12. O-O-O $1 (12. Nxc6 $2 {Of course the pawn cannot taken, as the e3 is hanging.} Qxe3+ 13. Qxe3+ Bxe3 $19) 12... Ba6 $5 {An interesting tactical move. The bishop cannot taken, as the e3 is hanging.} (12... O-O {Just transposes to the main game.}) 13. Qd2 $1 Bxf1 14. Bf2 $3 {I like this calm move. The bishop avoids the ..c4 attack and threatens e1 while the f1 remains hanging.} (14. Rhxf1 $4 Nc4 $19) 14... O-O (14... Nc4 {The d2 has only one square to go to but this is enough. Black temporarily has an extra piece, but must return it or to avoid having a weakened king position.} 15. Qe1 $1 Bxd4 (15... Qxe1 16. Rxe1+ Kd7 17. Rhxf1 $16 {Schmaltz-Reinhardt,Germany 2018}) 16. Qxe7+ Kxe7 17. Rxd4 c5 18. Rd1 $1 Bxg2 19. Rhg1 Bf3 20. Bxc5+ Ke6 (20... Ke8 21. Rd4 Na5 22. Re1+ $18) 21. Rd4 Nb6 22. f5+ $1 Kxf5 23. Rf1 h5 24. Rxf3+ Kg6 25. Rd1 $16 {Castro-Groot, Internet 2015}) 15. Rhxf1 Qc7 16. g3 Rfd8 (16... Rad8 17. Qe2 $16 {Ljubicic-Johansen, Internet 2011}) 17. Qe2 Nbd5 18. Nxd5 cxd5 19. Nb5 Qb6 20. Bxc5 Qxc5 21. Nd4 $16 {Poli-Pegg, Internet 2017}) 12. O-O-O {Papp, Petra: '!'} (12. cxb7 $2 {Papp, Petra This would be too gready as Black gets great compensation for the pawn.} Bxb7 13. O-O-O Nbd5 $132) 12... bxc6 {Papp, Petra: 'Just transposes to the main game.'} (12... Bg4 {Papp, Petra: 'Erenburg,Sergey'} 13. Be2 Rfe8 14. Bxg4 Nxg4 15. Bg1 bxc6 16. h3 Nf6 17. Nxc6 Qc7 18. Bxc5 Qxc6 19. Bd4 $18 {1-0 Rodriguez Lopez,R (2299)-Strikovic,A (2536)/Sanxenxo 2009/CBM 131 Extra (48)}) 13. Bg1 $1 {Papp, Petra: 'A non-standard square for the bishop. The point is that on g1 it's protected by the h1, so the pinned d4 can now move, and lastly the c6-pawn is hanging.'} (13. Bf2 $5 $14 {Papp, Petra Also a good move, but the drawback is that the d4 move, as the vulnerable bishop on f2 is hanging, but White can manoeuvre his bishop to h4, where it has more potential.}) 13... Qc7 (13... Qb7 {Papp, Petra Protecting the c6-pawn and threatening ..d8.} 14. Nb3 $1 {A logical move. After the trade of the dark squared bishop, the c5-square is weakened a lot, and with an extra pawn White's position is clearly better.} Bxg1 15. Rxg1 Nbd5 16. Qd4 $1 $16 {This pleasant position has occured in two corr games, where White won both. He needs to consolidate the position with c4, d2, and then exploit the dark square weaknesses. The games shows clearly how White needs to proceed.} Bg4 (16... Rd8 17. Bc4 Bf5 18. Rd2 Qc7 19. Bxd5 Nxd5 20. g3 $16 {Pakfolska-Korinek, Internet 2014}) 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. Rd2 Qc7 19. g3 a5 20. h3 Be6 21. Qc5 Qb7 22. Nd4 $16 {Metsemakers-Hendri Sevano, Internet 2017}) 14. g3 {Papp, Petra: 'The simplest move, just protecting the pawn, however White had another good but more complicated move.'} (14. Ne6 $5 {Papp, Petra A nice and good move, that has not appeared yet in the practice. White returns the extra pawn, but enters a favourable endgame with the bishop pair, and Black has a weak c6-pawn.} Bxe6 15. Bxc5 Qxf4+ 16. Qd2 $1 Qxd2+ 17. Rxd2 Rfe8 18. Bd4 $16) 14... Rd8 $4 {Papp, Petra: 'This loses on the spot. Strangely this move had been played against Judit Polgar already in the same year by another GM.'} (14... Bg4 15. Be2 Bxe2 16. Ndxe2 $16 Rfd8 {Papp, Petra} 17. Bd4 $1 Bxd4 18. Nxd4 Nbd5 (18... c5 $2 19. Ndb5 $18) 19. Rhe1 c5 20. Ndb5 Qa5 21. Qc4 $16 {Roubaud-Deneuville, ICCF email 2013}) (14... Nbd5 $2 {Papp, Petra Trading the pieces is favourable for White.} 15. Bg2 Bxd4 16. Bxd4 Bb7 17. Rd2 Qa5 18. a3 $18 {Wehner-May, Germany 2014}) (14... Bd7 $6 {Papp, Petra doesn't create any problems for White to consolidate the position.} 15. Nb3 $1 Bxg1 16. Rxg1 Nfd5 17. Qd4 Rfe8 18. Bd3 a5 19. a3 Nxc3 20. Qxc3 $16 {Ebeling-Schenaoui, Baku 2016}) (14... Bb4 $5 {Papp, Petra The most challenging move, where White has to be careful. Black intends to play ..c5, g4.} 15. Bg2 Bg4 {(Heiermann-Schulenberg, Internet 2017)} 16. Nxc6 $3 {A strong improvement over the game. The hardest move in this combination is the 18th one for White.} Bxc3 $1 (16... Bxd1 $2 17. Nxb4 $18) 17. Qxc3 Bxd1 18. Qe5 $1 {A very difficult move. White has just two pawns right now for the rook, but Black cannot save his d1.} (18. Kxd1 $2 Nbd5 19. Qc5 Rac8 $19) 18... Rac8 $1 (18... Qxe5 $2 19. fxe5 Nfd7 20. Kxd1 $18) 19. Qxc7 Rxc7 20. Bxb6 $1 axb6 21. Rxd1 $16 {The subsequent unbalanced endgame is more favourable for White. The c6 is dominating and in the near future White can easily advance his queenside pawns.}) 15. Ndb5 $18 {Papp, Petra: '!' Papp, Petra: '(1-0 J.Polgar-Rivas Pastor, Dos Hermanas 57/(130) 1993)  (1-0 J.Polgar-Rivas Pastor, Dos Hermanas 57/(130) 1993)  Erenburg,Sergey: '+-'' Papp, Petra: ' The strongest female player ever, Judit Polgar, beat two grandmasters with this nice move. In that time information spread slowly, so Black was unaware of this predecessor game. Anyway it is quite surprising to miss this 15.db5 tactical blow.'} Rxd3 (15... cxb5 {Papp, Petra} 16. Qxd8+ $18) 16. Nxc7 Rxd1+ 17. Nxd1 {Papp, Petra: '+-' Papp, Petra: 'The a8 and c5 pieces are hanging so Black cannot protect them both, he just loses material and the game.'} Bxg1 18. Nxa8 Nxa8 19. Rxg1 1-0

