1. d4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bg5 c5 6. Rc1 h6 7. Bh4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 b6 {It would be very optimistic to believe, that this game will enhance the popularity of this side line. Black scores much better with the move 8...c6.} 9. e3 Bb7 10. Ndb5 $1 Na6 11. Nd6 Bxd6 12. Qxd6 {At this moment the omens are not especially good for Black. The absence of the dark squared bishop will be felt for the rest of the game.} Rc8 (12... Qc7 13. Nb5 $16) 13. f3 Rc6 14. Qd4 Qe7 15. Be2 (15. Rd1 Rd8 16. Be2 $16) 15... g5 $6 {Polgar shows her teeth, but weakens the kingside forever. Ponomariov will demonstrate his attacking skills with great confidence.} (15... Nc7 16. O-O d5 17. cxd5 exd5 18. Bd3 $16) 16. Bg3 d5 (16... Nh5 17. Rd1 (17. Bf2 f5 18. h4 $16) 17... Nxg3 18. hxg3 Rd8 19. Ne4 $16) 17. h4 (17. Be5 Nd7 18. cxd5 exd5 19. Bg3 $16) 17... Nh5 (17... dxc4 $2 18. hxg5 hxg5 19. Ne4 Nh7 20. Bd6 $18) 18. Bf2 Ng7 19. hxg5 hxg5 (19... Qxg5 20. cxd5 exd5 21. g4 $16) 20. Qe5 $5 {[%mdl 128] This funny looking move aims to relocate the white queen to the open h-file. Black is slowly facing the impossible task of defending the hopeless cause.} (20. cxd5 exd5 21. Bb5 Rc5 22. a3 $16) 20... f6 (20... dxc4 $2 21. Nd5 $18) 21. Qh2 (21. Nxd5 Qd8 22. Qh2 exd5 23. Qh7+ Kf7 24. Rd1 $40) 21... dxc4 (21... Nc7 22. f4 $16) 22. f4 g4 {Moves like this are not made with love; Black desperately tries to avoid the impending disaster.} (22... Kf7 23. fxg5 fxg5 24. Bh5+ Nxh5 25. Qxh5+ Kg8 26. Bg3 $18) (22... gxf4 $4 23. Qh7+ Kf7 24. Bh5#) 23. Bxg4 Rc5 24. e4 Kf7 (24... Rc7 $4 25. Qh7+ Kf7 26. Bh5#) (24... Ra5 25. e5 Kf7 26. Bh5+ Nxh5 27. Qxh5+ Kg8 28. Rh4 $18) 25. Bxc5 {Any decent computer program would be ashamed to grab the material, since the direct attack was winning much more swiftly.} (25. e5 $5 Rc7 (25... Ke8 26. Bh4 Nb4 27. Be2 Nd3+ 28. Bxd3 cxd3 29. exf6 $16) 26. Rd1 Ke8 27. Bh4 $18) 25... Qxc5 (25... Nxc5 26. Be2 Rd8 27. Kf1 Nd3 28. Rd1 $18) 26. Qh7 Ke7 (26... Bxe4 27. Qxe4 f5 28. Qb7+ $18) (26... Qe3+ 27. Ne2 Rg8 28. Bxe6+ $18) 27. Qxg7+ {Once again it made more sense to refrain from consuming the the spoils, but Ponomariov is simply spoilt for choice...} (27. Rd1 $5 Rf7 (27... Qe3+ 28. Ne2 Nc5 29. e5 Ne4 30. Qxg7+ Rf7 31. Qxf7+ $1 (31. Rd7+ Kxd7 32. Qxf7+ $18) 31... Kxf7 32. Rd7+ Ke8 33. Bxe6 $18) 28. Qg8 Ne8 29. Bh5 $18) 27... Rf7 28. Qg8 Nb4 (28... Qe3+ 29. Ne2 Nc5 30. Rd1 Nd3+ 31. Rxd3 cxd3 32. Rh8 $18) 29. Ke2 (29. Rd1 Nd3+ 30. Rxd3 cxd3 31. Bh5 $18) 29... Nd3 30. Rcf1 Qb4 31. Rh8 $1 Qxb2+ 32. Ke3 {The race on the board doesn't look nice for Black. White's king sits in relative safety while Black is hopelessly exposed.} Kd6 (32... Qxc3 33. Qd8#) 33. e5+ $5 (33. Qb8+ $1 Rc7 (33... Kc6 34. Qe8+ $18) 34. Rh7 $18) 33... fxe5 34. fxe5+ Nxe5 (34... Kxe5 35. Qb8+ $18) 35. Qb8+ Rc7 (35... Kc6 36. Qxe5 Rxf1 37. Qxe6+ $18) 36. Rd1+ (36. Rd1+ Kc6 37. Qe8+ Rd7 38. Bf3+ $18) 1-0

