1. d4 {Belov Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 O-O 5. Bg5 ({A relatively solid line, while the main lines of the KID are starting with} 5. e4 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert}) 5... d6 6. e3 ({In more recent years Mamedyarov has given preference to} 6. Qd2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert}) 6... Nbd7 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'There are different setups possible, but true to her style, Polgar proceeds in the spirit of this opening.'} (6... c5 $5 7. Be2 cxd4 8. exd4 (8. Nxd4 $10 {Belov: '='}) 8... h6 9. Bf4 Bf5 10. O-O Ne4 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. Qd2 g5 13. Be3 e6 $1 $15 {Smyslov,V-Epishin,V/Rostov/1993/0:1 (50) Inf 57/543/}) 7. Be2 e5 8. O-O (8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Qc2 c6 10. O-O Qe7 11. Nd2 h6 12. Bh4 g5 13. Bg3 Ne8 $5 14. Bg4 (14. Rab1 f5 15. f3 b6 16. b4 Bb7 17. c5 bxc5 18. bxc5 Nxc5 19. Nb3 Bc8 20. Na5 Ne6 21. Na4 c5 $13 {Lechtynsky,J-Shirov,A/BRD/1992/0:1 (40)/}) 14... Nd6 15. e4 f5 $1 16. Bxf5 Nxf5 17. exf5 Nc5 18. Nde4 Bxf5 $10 {Burlov,V-Belov,I/Moscow/1987/0:1 (38)/ Belov: '='}) 8... h6 (8... Re8 9. Qc2 c6 10. Rfd1 Qe7 11. Ne4 $1 $14 {Bisguier,A-Biyiasas,J/USA/1978/ Inf 25/704/}) 9. Bh4 g5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'Sooner or later this moves has to played in order to get of the pin.'} 10. Bg3 Nh5 $10 {Belov: '='} 11. dxe5 Nxg3 (11... dxe5 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'allows'} 12. Bxe5 $1 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. Bxh5 $16 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '''' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'and White is a pawn up.'}) 12. hxg3 dxe5 ({Objectively a better idea is, but then} 12... Nxe5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} 13. Qc2 {followed by Rad1 White can claim an edge.}) 13. Qc2 (13. e4 c6 (13... Nc5 14. b4 Ne6 15. c5 Nd4 $13) 14. b4 Qe7 15. c5 Rd8 16. Nd2 Nf8 17. Qc2 Ne6 $15) 13... f5 14. Rad1 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''?!''} (14. b4 $5 c6 15. c5 {f3-d2-c4/}) 14... c6 15. Nd2 ({The critical moment of the game. White has a very solid position and hopes to exploit Black's aggressive expansion on the kingside. However, in the present game Polgar shows Black's attacking potential in a very convincing way. One the of main problems for White are the lack of nice squares for the knights and in order to activate these pieces White should opt for} 15. g4 $1 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} fxg4 16. Nd2 (16. Nh2 {has been played in a few earlier, games, but on d2 it's more active}) 16... Nf6 17. Nde4 Qe7 18. c5 $1 {offers White tremendous play on the light-squares, while the d6-square is a great outpost for either the knight or rook (to double on the d-file). In the game Ruban first brings the knight to d2, preparing g3-g4 with the idea of controlling the e4-square, but Polgar has a creative solution thwarting White's plan.}) 15... h5 $5 (15... Qe7 16. a3 a5 $15) 16. e4 ({Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'The point of Black's last move is that'} 16. Bxh5 $2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'fails to'} g4 17. e4 Qg5 $19 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''-+'' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'and the bishop is trapped.'}) 16... f4 17. gxf4 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '?'} ({Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'Perhaps more stubborn is'} 17. Bxh5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'though after'} Nf6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'the minus pawn isn't really felt.'} 18. Be2 fxg3 19. fxg3 Qb6+ 20. Kh1 Bg4 $17) 17... gxf4 ({Recapturing away from the centre with} 17... exf4 $1 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert is a better option, opening up the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop and clearing the e5 square for the knight. After} 18. Bxh5 Ne5 19. Nf3 Qe7 {Black is in great shape. One of the plans is to use the h-file for a kingside attack.}) 18. Bxh5 ({Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'Grabbing such a pawn is asking for problems, as the opening of the h-file could only really favor Black. A more sound option is'} 18. b4 $5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'planning c5, Nc4 etc.'} Nf6 (18... h4 19. Bg4) 19. c5 Qe7 20. Nc4 h4 $15) 18... Qh4 19. Bf3 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''?!''} ({Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'In case of'} 19. Be2 $5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''!?'' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'a strong idea is'} Rf6 (19... Nf6 20. Nf3 Qh5 21. c5 $13) 20. Nf3 Qh8 $8 {h6 / Belov: '' Belov: 'h6/'} (20... Qh7 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert followed by Rh6 when the threats against the white king become unbearable.}) (20... Qh5 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} 21. Nd4 {and the knight can be installed on f5.})) 19... Nf6 (19... Rf6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '?' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'is less impressive this time, because after'} 20. Rfe1 Rh6 21. Kf1 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'the white king can still be evacuated.'}) 20. Qb3 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''?!''} (20. c5 $5) 20... Kh8 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'A useful move, as the king leaves the a2-g8 diagonal and the rook may join the attack along the g-file.'} 21. Rfe1 Ng4 $1 22. Bxg4 Bxg4 23. f3 (23. Nf3 $2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'is strongly met by'} Bxf3 24. gxf3 Qh3 $1 $19 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''-+!'' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'and since the king can't escape mate within a few moves can't be avoided.'}) 23... Rad8 $1 ({Another fantastic move: ignoring your opponents threat by generating a counterattack. After something like} 23... Be6 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} 24. Kf1 {followed by Ke2 White still puts up resistance.}) 24. Nf1 $6 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '?' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''?!'' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'White succumbs to the pressure.'} (24. fxg4 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'is met by'} Rxd2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!'} 25. Rxd2 Qxe1+ $19 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''-+'' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'and Black will be a rook up.'}) ({Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'White's best chance is to overprotect the knight on d2, but then after'} 24. Qc2 $5 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''!?''} Be6 $17 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '-+' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '''' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'White can never take on b7 any longer, while Black's attack simply continues (Rg8, Bf8-c5 etc).'}) 24... Bxf3 $1 25. gxf3 Rg8 $19 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: ''-+''} (25... Bf6 $2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert isn't clear at all, since after} 26. Nh2 $1 Rg8+ 27. Kh1 Rd2 (27... Qh3 $2 28. Qc2 $18) 28. Re2 $1 {everything is well covered.} (28. Rxd2 $2 Qxe1+ 29. Nf1 Qxf1+ 30. Kh2 Qg1+ 31. Kh3 Qg3#)) 26. Nh2 (26. Rxd8 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'runs into'} Bf8+ {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!'} 27. Ng3 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} Qxg3+ 28. Kf1 Qg2#) 26... Bf8+ {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'Another advantage to start with Rg8 is that the bishop can join the attack via c5.'} 27. Kh1 Bc5 28. Re2 (28. Qc2 Rd7 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!?' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'with the idea of Rdg7 also suffices'} (28... Qg3 $1 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert with the idea to take on d1, deflecting defenders from either the g2 or g1 square.} 29. Rc1 Rd7 30. Na4 Bf2 {wins for Black.}) 29. Rf1 Rdg7 30. Na4 Qh3 31. Nxc5 Rg2 $19) 28... Rd7 $1 29. Na4 (29. Rxd7 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} Rg1#) 29... Bf2 $1 (29... Bf2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: '!' Belov,Igor Ris,Robert: 'and here White had seen enough, as White can't do anything against the threat of ...Qxh2+, e.g.'} 30. Qc2 (30. Rxf2 {Belov,Igor Ris,Robert} Qxf2 31. Ng4 Rh7+ {and mate.}) 30... Qxh2+ $1 31. Kxh2 Rh7#) 0-1

