1. Nf3 Nf6 2. d4 g6 3. Bg5 Bg7 4. Nbd2 d5 5. e3 O-O 6. Bd3 c5 7. c3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Bxf6 $5 {Apparently this move has not been played before} Bxf6 10. Qe2 Bg7 11. Rad1 Nd7 12. Rfe1 c4 $6 {The beginning of a strategically dubious plan.} 13. Bc2 f5 14. Ng5 $1 Rf6 15. f4 $1 {[%csl Rb7] In a blocked position white now has a clear advantage because: he has the good bishop - black has one very bad bishop(b7). White has to s and s are stronger than s in closed positions - especially here where e5 could be a good square for the s. Should one  be changed on e5 then after d4xe5 the square d4 looks like a good place for the other  to go.} b5 16. g4 e6 17. Qg2 Qe7 18. Ndf3 h6 19. Nh3 a5 20. Ne5 g5 $6 {Objektively speaking this is tactically a bad move. However the idea is sound. To open the diagonal for blacks bad b7} 21. gxf5 Nxe5 (21... exf5 22. fxg5 hxg5 23. Nxd7 Qxd7 24. Qxg5 $16 {Black has lost a  and b7 is still a useless piece}) 22. dxe5 Rxf5 23. Bxf5 exf5 24. fxg5 d4 {Black has managed to open the diagonal a8-h1 for b7. The cost has been to high though. Exchange,  and bad -position - for one good bishop-} 25. e4 dxc3 26. bxc3 $2 {So far Smyslov has been able to use is outstanding experience in strategical positions. However when a game is decided normally it is neccesary to switch into calculating winning lines. Of which the first seems to be:} (26. gxh6 cxb2 27. Qg6 $1 Bxe4 28. Ng5 $18) 26... hxg5 27. Nxg5 b4 28. e6 Bc6 29. Nf7 $2 {The last clear win was probably:} (29. Rf1 Rf8 30. Qg3 $1 {[%cal Re4f5,Rg3h4] exf5/h4}) 29... Qf6 30. Rd6 Qxc3 31. Red1 Bxe4 32. Rd8+ Rxd8 33. Rxd8+ Kh7 34. Ng5+ Kh6 35. Nf7+ {A interesting struggle between a strategical and  a tactical player.} 1/2-1/2

