1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 {Colin Crouch: 'Simple enough. White wants to bring his kingside pieces into play. There is a slight problem that the dark-squared bishop cannot develop, and this indeed was a significant factor later in this game.'} (4. Qc2 {, keeping open a later Bg5, is also popular. See Bareev-Efimenko, and Kramnik-Leko.}) ({Among other possibilities,} 4. Nf3 d5 {is seen in Wang Yue-Movsesian.}) 4... O-O {Colin Crouch: 'One of many alternatives.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'If Black wants to try the Nimzowitsch plan of doubling the c-pawns, and playing against them, the best option would be'} 4... c5 {Colin Crouch: ', aiming for'} 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 d6 {.}) ({and} 4... b6 {are both main lines, but not covered in this collection.}) 5. Bd3 ({The most common, although} 5. Nge2 {is also popular, planning to recapture with the knight, rather than the pawn, on c3. The knight is slightly misplaced after} d5 6. a3 Be7 {, but in compensation Black has lost a tempo with the bishop. White usually tries} 7. cxd5 {, and then either recapture is playable.}) 5... d5 {Colin Crouch: 'Aiming for a Queen's Gambit type of position. Black's bishop is more aggressive on b4 than on e7, but he will often be forced to exchange, giving away the bishop pair. However, White no longer has Bg5 possibilities.'} 6. a3 {Colin Crouch: 'Sokolov's favoured approach, setting up a Sämisch structure.'} (6. Nf3 {,.}) (6. cxd5) ({and, though less often played,} 6. Nge2 {are also good.}) 6... Bxc3+ {Colin Crouch: 'The natural reply.'} (6... Be7 {is an alternative, and is playable but not very inspiring. Black argues that in comparison with the Queen's Gambit (for example, 1 d4 d5 2 c4 e6 3 Nf3 Nf6 4 Bg5), White does not have the Bg5 development. Black has, however, lost a 'semi-tempo', and White's a3 is often useful.}) (6... dxc4 {allows extra transpositional possibilities, allows extra transpositional possibilities, for example,} {for example,} 7. Bxc4 ({but White too has an alternative, with} 7. Bxh7+ {followed by axb4, with a slight but secure edge}) 7... Bd6 {.}) 7. bxc3 {Colin Crouch: 'So Black has given up the bishop pair. This is of course standard in the Nimzo-Indian. Black has free development with his, or her, pieces, and has every opportunity of gaining parity in the centre.'} dxc4 (7... e5 $5 {has been tried a few times: has been tried a few times: White usually prefers} {White usually prefers} 8. Ne2 ({The tactical point is that after} 8. dxe5 dxc4 9. Bxc4 $2 (9. exf6 Qxd3 10. Qxd3 cxd3 {is about equal}) 9... Qxd1+ 10. Kxd1 Ng4 {Black is doing well, and has recovered the pawn. White's isolated queenside pawn pair will become something of a problem as we get closer to the endgame.}) 8... e4 9. Bc2 {, and after} dxc4 {there are some similarities to the pawn structure in our main game.}) (7... c6 {, as played in I.Sokolov-S.Movsesian, Sarajevo 2007, is a quiet alternative, aiming for gradual equality after} 8. Nf3 b6 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Qe2 Bb7 11. O-O Qc8 {, followed by ...Ba6. The game was later drawn.}) 8. Bxc4 c5 (8... e5 {is again playable, but no longer has the possibility of an ...e4 hit against the bishop. White could perhaps consider} 9. Nf3 e4 10. Ne5 Nbd7 11. f4 $5 exf3 {:} 12. Nxf3 $1 {is better, and Black might well have a few problems over the f7-square (0-0, Ng5 or Ne5, etc). Perhaps such thoughts help explain why Black does not try an early ...e5.} (12. Qxf3 $2 {is natural, but a serious blunder. Black has a winning attack after} Nxe5 13. dxe5 Bg4 $1 14. Qf4 Qd1+ 15. Kf2 Qc2+ 16. Kg1 Ne4 {.} (16... Z0))) 9. Ne2 (9. Nf3 {leads to mainline positions, often reached too via 6 Nf3. Sokolov attempts what he hopes will become a more flexible development, covering any problems on the c3-square, and maybe even thinking about gaining pawn space with f3. The trouble is that he no longer keeps control of e5, and Polgar gains a second hit in the centre, with ...e5 as well as ...c5. leads to mainline positions, often reached too via 6 Nf3. Sokolov attempts what he hopes will become a more flexible development, covering any problems on the c3-square, and maybe even thinking about gaining pawn space with f3. The trouble is that he no longer keeps control of e5, and Polgar gains a second hit in the centre, with ...e5 as well as ...c5.}) 9... Qc7 {Colin Crouch: 'Gaining a minor tempo, with the threatened hit on the bishop after ...cxd4.'} ({'The threat is greater than the execution', as the great chess thinker Aron Nimzowitsch noted. Here} 9... cxd4 $6 10. cxd4 Qc7 11. Ba2 {gives nothing for Black. It is better to keep the good pawn in play.}) (9... Nc6 {is a good alternative, keeping the tension. Black will usually play ...Qc7 at some stage, though.}) 10. Ba2 {Colin Crouch: 'Keeping the bishop out of harm's way, and reminding Black that if she tries a quick ...e5, White still has pressure against f7.'} (10. Bd3 {is the main alternative. Then} e5 {is well-timed, and should lead to equal play.}) {Colin Crouch: 'The next stage of the development plan. Black has to decide what to do with the queenside minor pieces. '} 10... b6 {Colin Crouch: 'The bishop goes first, either to b7 or a6.'} (10... e5 {has been tried several times. Then the simple} 11. d5 {might be best. The passed pawn is not a big threat, especially so early in the game, but it demands some respect. Later White may try to bolster the pawn with c4, and possibly f3 followed by e4. Polgar's move seems more logical, aiming to develop her pieces while giving away nothing. might be best. The passed pawn is not a big threat, especially so early in the game, but it demands some respect. Later White may try to bolster the pawn with c4, and possibly f3 followed by e4. Polgar's move seems more logical, aiming to develop her pieces while giving away nothing.}) 11. O-O ({Or maybe} 11. Bb2 {, but on the other hand, while we know that White has to castle kingside, we do not quite know whether the bishop is best on b2. It is better to keep flexibility, and to castle immediately.}) 11... Ba6 {Colin Crouch: 'Did the reader assume that Black was going to fianchetto immediately on the long diagonal? Black is not really threatening to attack on g2, not least because White is often quite happy to close the diagonal with f3, if required, and then e4. The a6-f1 diagonal, on the other hand, is more of an irritation. White's doubled c-pawns have gone, but he still has the problem that the c4-square is a slight weakness, making it difficult to play c4, especially if Black's bishop is pressing on this square. ' Did the reader assume that Black was going to fianchetto immediately on the long diagonal? Black is not really threatening to attack on g2, not least because White is often quite happy to close the diagonal with f3, if required, and then e4. The a6-f1 diagonal, on the other hand, is more of an irritation. White's doubled c-pawns have gone, but he still has the problem that the c4-square is a slight weakness, making it difficult to play c4, especially if Black's bishop is pressing on this square.} 12. Re1 {Colin Crouch: 'To free the knight.'} Nc6 {Colin Crouch: 'The natural square. She completes her development, before pushing the pawn with ...e5. '} 13. Ng3 (13. Nf4 {, seemingly more aggressive, has not gained much attention.} e5 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. Bxd5 Rad8 16. dxc5 Ne7 $1 {leaves Black comfortable.}) 13... Rad8 {Colin Crouch: 'In such positions, it is often difficult to decide which rook should be placed on the d-file.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'In previous games, Black had tended to play'} 13... Rfd8 {Ü 89/(463) Colin Crouch: ', with the option of placing rooks on the c-file and d-file. Polgar has decided instead that she wants to add extra cover for the f7-square, after she has opened up White's diagonal with ...e5.'}) 14. Bb2 (14. Nh5 Nxh5 15. Qxh5 Bd3 {Colin Crouch: '!' Colin Crouch: ', followed perhaps by ...Bg6, covers any attack by White on Black's kingside. White prefers to complete his development, before attacking.' '!' , followed perhaps by ...Bg6, covers any attack by White on Black's kingside. White prefers to complete his development, before attacking.}) 14... e5 {Colin Crouch: 'She takes over the initiative in the centre, but loosens some light squares.'} (14... Na5 15. e4 Nc4 16. Bc1 cxd4 17. cxd4 Qd7 18. Bxc4 Bxc4 19. Bb2 {still gives White a slight edge, with the idea of d5 followed by breaking up the pawn structure with Bxf6.}) 15. Qc2 $146 (15. Rc1 Na5 16. Qf3 Rfe8 17. e4 Bc4 18. Bb1 Nb3 {was tried in M.Illescas-F.Vallejo Pons, Spanish Team Championship 2004. It might look as though Black has gained an edge, attacking the rook, and putting great pressure on the pawn on d4, but White sacrificed the exchange with} 19. d5 $1 Nxc1 20. Bxc1 {, keeping a strong pawn centre, and being ready to attack on the kingside. Vallejo opted for safety by returning the exchange, with} Rd6 21. Nf5 Nd7 22. Nxd6 Qxd6 {, draw agreed. The computer suggests that White may still be slightly better at the time of the draw agreement, but a strong human player would find it difficult to make any progress. It is difficult to activate the bishop pair, and it is difficult to open up the pawns. Any exchange of the light-squared bishops will leave Black's knight more effective than White's remaining bishop. Also, Black has an extra queenside pawn, in compensation, obviously, for White's extra central pawn, and Black can try to expand with a later ...c4, or a more general push with ...b5 and ...a5. Probably the position is, as the players agreed, just equal. , draw agreed. The computer suggests that White may still be slightly better at the time of the draw agreement, but a strong human player would find it difficult to make any progress. It is difficult to activate the bishop pair, and it is difficult to open up the pawns. Any exchange of the light-squared bishops will leave Black's knight more effective than White's remaining bishop. Also, Black has an extra queenside pawn, in compensation, obviously, for White's extra central pawn, and Black can try to expand with a later ...c4, or a more general push with ...b5 and ...a5. Probably the position is, as the players agreed, just equal.}) ({Colin Crouch: 'In 'Informator', Polgar suggests as better'} 15. d5 $1 Na5 (15... e4 16. c4) 16. e4 Bc4 {Colin Crouch: '. White's connected passed pawn is of course useful, but this is not enough to force an edge of itself. To aim for a realistic plus, White would need to push pieces to the kingside, maybe starting off with'} 17. Bc1 {, taking advantage that Black's knight on a5 is far away from the kingside. Black is not yet equal.}) 15... Rd7 {Colin Crouch: 'With great tension on the d-file, most obviously on the d4-pawn, but also an attempt to restrain the pawn from playing to d5.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'The immediate liquidation, with'} 15... cxd4 {Colin Crouch: '?!' '?!'} 16. cxd4 exd4 17. Bxd4 {Colin Crouch: ', would give White a comfortable slight edge with the help of the bishop pair.'}) ({Colin Crouch: 'If'} 15... Na5 $5 {Colin Crouch: ', Polgar suggests'} 16. dxe5 Bd3 17. Qc1 Qxe5 18. c4 Qe6 19. e4 $36 {Colin Crouch: 'with chances of attack for White.'}) (15... Bc8 {was suggested by the computer, with the idea that if} 16. Rad1 Be6 $5 {, Black's isolated pawn structure is no real weakness, and the knight on c6 does not have to reach to a5 to offer a bishop exchange. The simple} 17. Bb1 $1 {leaves White slightly better.}) {Colin Crouch: 'White has no pin on the d-file, as opposed to the interpolated' Colin Crouch: 'In the suggested improvement, White might still have a slight edge after'} 16. Rad1 (16. Ne4 $1 Nxe4 17. Qxe4 {looks like an improvement.}) 16... Rfd8 {Colin Crouch: 'White no longer has an edge on the d-file.'} 17. h3 {Colin Crouch: '!?' Colin Crouch: 'White's position is fully playable, and remains so for the next few moves. There is no need to criticize Sokolov's quiet move here. Polgar plays dramatic attacking chess later on, and this may easily create the impression that Sokolov's quiet move early on was a mistake of judgement, causing problems much later. Playing through the position move-by-move such an assessment would seem not so clear. We are in a classic Nimzo-Indian early middlegame, in which both pawns and pieces are finely balanced. There are four possibilities, two on either side, of central pawn exchanges, but neither side would gain from initiating the pawn exchange, and indeed would lose ground. White wants to keep his d-pawn, this being clearly his best and most effective pawn, while Black would not want to exchange with ...cxd4 or ...exd4, when White could recapture with a pawn on d4. The only possibilities of making progress in the centre would either be d5 for White, too early and it would lose a pawn, or ...e4 and/or ...c4 for Black, which would gain space but would release the central pawn tension. Both players will want to keep in mind these ideas.' '!?' White's position is fully playable, and remains so for the next few moves. There is no need to criticize Sokolov's quiet move here. Polgar plays dramatic attacking chess later on, and this may easily create the impression that Sokolov's quiet move early on was a mistake of judgement, causing problems much later. Playing through the position move-by-move such an assessment would seem not so clear. We are in a classic Nimzo-Indian early middlegame, in which both pawns and pieces are finely balanced. There are four possibilities, two on either side, of central pawn exchanges, but neither side would gain from initiating the pawn exchange, and indeed would lose ground. White wants to keep his d-pawn, this being clearly his best and most effective pawn, while Black would not want to exchange with ...cxd4 or ...exd4, when White could recapture with a pawn on d4. The only possibilities of making progress in the centre would either be d5 for White, too early and it would lose a pawn, or ...e4 and/or ...c4 for Black, which would gain space but would release the central pawn tension. Both players will want to keep in mind these ideas.} ({Colin Crouch: 'In the meantime, it is unclear how White should play his next couple of piece moves.'} 17. Ne4 $5 {Colin Crouch: 'is a possibility;'} Nxe4 18. Qxe4 {Colin Crouch: 'when Black may safely try'} Ne7 {Colin Crouch: '.'}) (17. Bb1 g6) ({but so too is} 17. Nf5 {. It is unclear as yet which would be the better square for the knight. The bishop would be good on the b1-h7 diagonal, but it is also good on the a2-g8 diagonal, and it is not totally clear that an extra bishop move would be worth a tempo. Other piece moves are also possible, but do not improve their positions.}) ({This leaves off-centre pawn moves. The two main candidates are} 17. f3 $5 {or 17 h3!?, as played. The 17 f3 idea is fully playable, preventing Black from trying ...e4 or a possible threat with ...Ng4 after White makes a knight move. White is, however, weakening his dark squares. Sokolov instead pushes his h-pawn, cutting out possible weaknesses on the back rank, and also cutting out ...Ng4.}) (17. d5 Ne7 18. c4 {.}) 17... c4 $5 {Colin Crouch: 'Polgar takes the initiative in the centre, not perhaps because of aggression intent, but more from a sense that if play drifts on, Black will have fewer good quiet moves than White.'} ({Colin Crouch: 'Polgar suggests'} 17... Na5 {White could continue the theme of trying to play constructive quiet moves with} 18. dxe5 ({White could continue the theme of trying to play constructive quiet moves with} 18. Bb1 Nc4 19. Bc1 {. It is not fully clear that the knight is better on c4 than on c6. The position is probably about equal, or the usual very slight edge for White.}) 18... Qxe5 19. c4 Qe6 20. Bb1 {Colin Crouch: 'as a possibility.'} (20. Z0)) 18. a4 $2 {Colin Crouch: '?!' Colin Crouch: 'The first real question mark given by Judit Polgar. It is still more of an indicator that White could have played the line better, rather than any indication that White would be worse.' '?!'}