e its position.'} Rg5 {Colin Crouch: 'Continuing to attack. This is do-or-die chess, and soon Polgar is obliged to drop a pawn to gain any chance of carrying on the attack against the king. '} 22. Nf4 {Colin Crouch: 'A good square for the knight, assisting both the defence of h3 and g2, and helping a pawn attack with d5. '} Bc8 {Colin Crouch: '?!' Colin Crouch: 'Polgar has played aggressively, but so far has not been taking risks. She has allowed White to set up a strong passed pawn, but so long as the pawn is not charging forward, that is acceptable, provided she has reasonable counterplay. Her next move takes risks, though, as no longer is Black's bishop covering the c4-pawn.' '?!' Polgar has played aggressively, but so far has not been taking risks. She has allowed White to set up a strong passed pawn, but so long as the pawn is not charging forward, that is acceptable, provided she has reasonable counterplay. Her next move takes risks, though, as no longer is Black's bishop covering the c4-pawn.} (22... Qd7 $5 {Colin Crouch: ', suggested by Polgar in her notes, is safer if less ambitious. Black would probably try ...Ne7, and then one of the knights to d5. Black looks at least equal. My reading of the position is that Polgar's move is a mistake, overpressing, but that she soon recovers well, by aiming for a full-blown attack. See the notes to White's 25th, below.' , suggested by Polgar in her notes, is safer if less ambitious. Black would probably try ...Ne7, and then one of the knights to d5. Black looks at least equal. My reading of the position is that Polgar's move is a mistake, overpressing, but that she soon recovers well, by aiming for a full-blown attack. See the notes to White's 25th, below.}) 23. Qe2 {Colin Crouch: 'Aiming for the pawn.'} ({Polgar notes the alternative,} 23. Ba2 $5 {Instead, after} Na5 (23... Nd5 {Colin Crouch: '?!' '?!'} 24. Bxc4 Nxf4 25. exf4 Qxf4 26. Re3 {Colin Crouch: ', also given by Polgar, favours White.'}) (23... Ne7 $5 24. Bxe7