. h4 ({Colin Crouch: 'White has no defending pieces supporting his king, and this could easily lead to fatal exposure. If, for example,'} 27. Kh2 {Colin Crouch: ', Polgar gives'} Bxh3 $1 28. gxh3 (28. Qb5 Qd8 $19 {Colin Crouch: '!' '!'}) 28... Qf5 29. f4 Rh5 $19 {Colin Crouch: 'with a winning attack.'}) ({If instead} 27. Qd6 {'!?' , Polgar instead gives} Qf5 {, playing for the attack, with a win after} ({Black could of course simplify, and recover the pawn with} 27... Qxd6 28. Bxd6 Bxh3 29. Bg3 {with possibly Black keeping a slight edge after} Bg4 30. Rc1 Nc4 {, and if} 31. Ba2 Nb2 32. Rc2 Nd3 33. Rb1 Bd7 {. Black has the more active pieces, but she must be careful about White's passed pawn and bishop pair.} (33... Z0)) 28. Kh1 (28. h4 Rg6 29. Qf4 Nc4 $19) ({There are possible improvements for White, though, for example with} 28. Qh2 Qxh3 29. Ba2 Qxh2+ 30. Kxh2 Bd7 31. d5 Bxa4 32. Rd4 Bb3 33. Re2 Bxa2 34. Rxa2 {, although then} Nb3 $1 {is still advantageous to Black, and if} 35. Rb4 Nc5 {.}) 28... Nc4 29. Qc6 Bd7 $1 30. Qxc4 Rxg2 31. Kxg2 Qf3+ 32. Kh2 Bxh3 33. Rg1 Be6 $19 {. The counterattack with} 34. Rxg7+ Kxg7 35. Qf1 Qh5+ 36. Kg1 Bh3 {does not work.} (36... Z0)) 27... Rg4 {Colin Crouch: 'With an attack on the h4-pawn.'} (27... Rh5 {is a possibility, but then White can force the exchange of queens:} 28. Qb5 $5 Qxb5 29. axb5 Rxb5 {with unclear play, but no chances of attack.}) 28. Qb5 {Colin Crouch: 'Naturally Sokolov would still like to swap the queens.'} Qd8 $1 {Colin Crouch: 'An unusual starting point for a vicious kingside attack. All Black's pieces are on the periphery, with only a solitary pawn in the centre. Black's queen and bishop are, however, in the process of regrouping. Polgar is aiming for a direct attack.'} (28... Qxb5 $5 29. axb5 Nc4 30. Bc1 Bd7 {is also good, with a positional edge for Black. But an effective mating attack speeds things up.}) 29. g3 {Colin Crouch: '?' Colin Crouch: 'This is a weak reply. In a couple of moves time, Black can take the h4-pawn anyway, and White has loosened the pawns in front of his king.' '?'} (29. Bd6 {'!?' at least brings the bishop to the kingside, and keeps the play live. Polgar gives} Bd7 {, without further analysis.} (29... Qxd6 $4 30. Qxe8+) {. After} 30. Qb4 Qxh4 31. Bg3 {, Black has: , Black has: So maybe} {So maybe} Qg5 ({She could consider sacrificing the exchange with} 31... Rxg3 32. fxg3 Qxg3 {, maybe about equal, but it seems sensible to wait for the sacrifice until more pieces are developed.} (32... Z0)) 32. d5 h5 33. Kh1 Nb7 (33... h4 $6 34. Bf4 {is premature}) 34. Bc2 {(the computer suggests this as best, but it is not so clear that it is better or worse than a few alternatives)} Qg6 35. Rg1 {(otherwise ...h4 is threatened)} Nc5 36. a5 Nd3 37. Bxd3 exd3 38. Qb1 Rxg3 39. fxg3 Qxg3 40. Rge1 Ba4 41. Rxd3 Bc2 42. Qxc2 Qxe1+ 43. Kh2 Re4 44. g3 h4 {with a winning attack. Not forced, but White would have to find a considerable improvement to hold this.}) 29... a6 {Colin Crouch: '!' Colin Crouch: 'Forcing the queen from the b5-e8 diagonal.' '!'} 30. Qb4 ({Colin Crouch: 'What Sokolov had presumably missed was that if'} 30. Qh5 Nc4 31. Bc1 g6 32. Qh6 Nd6 { f5 Colin Crouch: ', and White's queen is about to be trapped with ...Nf5. It takes four moves by the knight to move from the a-file to the h-file, and sometimes such a manoeuvre may easily be overlooked.'}) 30... Qxh4 {Colin Crouch: 'Recapturing the pawn, and also re-establishing the rook sacrifice.'} 31. Bxe4 ({If} 31. Qd6 Nc4 32. Qc6 Rxg3+ 33. fxg3 Qxg3+ 34. Kh1 Qh3+ 35. Kg1 Bd7 $1 36. Qxc4 Qg3+ 37. Kh1 Re6 {(probably the simplest)} 38. Qxe6 Bxe6 {, and the queen and bishop still create havoc. White's two rooks and extra bishop, in return for the queen, do not coordinate well, and at the very least White will have to sacrifice a bishop to slow down the attack slightly.}) 31... Rxg3+ $1 {Colin Crouch: 'This rook sacrifice is hardly by now unexpected, but the new feature is no longer the complete demolition of the white pawns, but rather the great pressure on the long diagonal.'} 32. fxg3 Qxe4 {Colin Crouch: 'Now the queen is fully centralized, and there are worrying mate threats, with ...Bb7 or ...Qf3 and ...Qxg3+, combined perhaps with ...Bg4 or ...Bh3. This is extremely dangerous, and Black has excellent compensation for the exchange sacrifice. Is Black winning? Maybe the players might have thought so. ' Now the queen is fully centralized, and there are worrying mate threats, with ...Bb7 or ...Qf3 and ...Qxg3+, combined perhaps with ...Bg4 or ...Bh3. This is extremely dangerous, and Black has excellent compensation for the exchange sacrifice. Is Black winning? Maybe the players might have thought so.} 33. Rd2 {Colin Crouch: '!' Colin Crouch: 'White has to find tough and resourceful defensive play, but this isn't it.' '!'} ({Colin Crouch: 'The alternatives were: Polgar gives' The alternatives were: Polgar gives} 33. d5 Nc4 34. Rd4 Qf3 35. Qxc4 Qxg3+ 36. Kf1 Bh3+ 37. Ke2 Rxe3+ 38. Kd2 Qxe1+ 39. Kc2 Re2+ $19 {Colin Crouch: 'with a winning attack. As promised, the queen and bishop work successfully together.'}) (33. Qb2 {is a logical defensive plan, making use of the second rank, but the problem is, of course, the knight fork with} Nc4 {. If then} 34. Qa2 Nxa3 35. Qxa3 Bb7 36. d5 Bxd5 37. Rxd5 Qxd5 {, and Black has recovered her material, is a pawn up with active play, and her opponent's pawns are all isolated. It will eventually be a loss, as with various positions where White does not take back the knight after ...Nxa3. White slightly prolongs the game, but does not hold it.}) (33. Bc1 $5 {, cutting out any ...Nc4xa3 threats, is a sensible reply:} Nc4 $1 {is an attractive surprise reply, sacrificing the knight for nothing, and not even attacking anything – a positional sacrifice in the midst of an attack!} ({If, for example,} 33... Bb7 34. d5) ({or} 33... Bh3 34. Rd2 {, White's position is holding.} (34. Z0)) {A more resilient reply is} 34. Re2 $5 ({The point is that if} 34. Qxc4 Bb7 {, White cannot play} {, White cannot play} 35. d5 $4 {, as the queen is no longer defended.} ({White could still try} 35. Re2 Qh1+ 36. Kf2 Qxd1 {, but essentially his defence is broken.})) 34... Bb7 35. Rf1 {, aiming to spirit the king to e1 and beyond.} Re6 $1 {is the most accurate response, bringing the rook into the attack. is the most accurate response, bringing the rook into the attack. If instead} {If instead} 36. Kf2 {, White is checkmated after} ({If then} 36. Qxc4 {, Black wins after} Qh1+ 37. Kf2 Rf6+ {.} (37... Z0)) 36... Rf6+ 37. Ke1 Rxf1+ 38. Kxf1 Qh1+ 39. Kf2 Qg2+ 40. Ke1 Qg1# {. It is only a small consolation that the king finally reaches e1.}) ({Is there any further try? It is too early to give up on White just yet.} 33. Qb1 $1 Qf3 34. Qa2 $1 {seems to give good chances of holding, provided White does not mind returning his extra exchange. After} Nc4 35. Bc1 {Black will not be able to force a direct win, but there are two good choices for an endgame edge: Black will not be able to force a direct win, but there are two good choices for an endgame edge: Alternatively,} {Alternatively,} Qxg3+ $1 {has the merit of setting up two passed pawns, beneficial in an endgame:} ({One idea is} 35... Bg4 36. Qf2 $1 Qxf2+ 37. Kxf2 Bxd1 38. Rxd1 f5 39. Ke2 ({it is too early to hurry with} 39. d5) 39... Kf7 40. Kd3 b5 41. axb5 axb5 42. Rf1 Ke6 (42... Kg6 43. Rf4 {holds}) 43. e4 fxe4+ 44. Kxe4 Kd6+ 45. Kd3 {and White, with care, should draw.}) 36. Qg2 Qxg2+ 37. Kxg2 Bg4 38. Rd3 Bf5 39. e4 (39. Rdd1 $2 Bc2 {leaves Black a clear pawn up}) 39... Bxe4+ 40. Kf2 f6 41. Rg3 Kf7 42. Rg4 f5 43. Rh4 g6 44. Rh5 {. White has defended well so far, but one would expect that Black's three connected passed pawns would eventually break through. So it seems that Polgar did not have a quick winning attack with best play, but that her opponent had to make concessions. . White has defended well so far, but one would expect that Black's three connected passed pawns would eventually break through. So it seems that Polgar did not have a quick winning attack with best play, but that her opponent had to make concessions.}) 33... Qf3 {Colin Crouch: 'With a threatened invasion on g3.'} 34. Kh2 Qh5+ {Colin Crouch: 'A repetition before the time control.'} 35. Kg1 Qf3 {Colin Crouch: 'Back to move 33. '} 36. Kh2 (36. Qd6 $5 {could again have been tried. sets up a trap. . Black would need to play more carefully to force the win:} {. Black would need to play more carefully to force the win:} Bb7 $1 {, making use of the long diagonal. Then White has to defend against the mating attack, and only then think about ...Nc4. Play might continue} ({The natural triple knight fork with} 36... Nc4 37. Rf2 $1 {Or} {Or} Qxf2+ ({and then} 37... Qe4 38. Qc7 {(threatening the check on f7)} Be6 39. Bc1 Bd5 40. Kh2 {provides for Black some degree of positional compensation for the exchange, but no dangerous attack.} (40. Z0)) 38. Kxf2 Nxd6 39. Bxd6 Re6 40. Bf4 Bd7 41. Ra1 Rc6 42. Rb1 {, and it would be difficult for Black to force a win in the opposite-coloured bishop endgame.}) 37. d5 Nc4 38. Rf2 Qg4 39. Qc7 Bxd5 40. Bd6 Qe4 41. Rh2 Nd2 {, and Black wins.}) 36... Bf5 {Colin Crouch: '!' Colin Crouch: 'Back to normal play. The threat is 37...Be4, and White has no real resistance. ' '!'} 37. e4 ({White no longer has time to try} 37. Qd6 $2 Nc4 {, as Black is simply winning material, Rf2 no longer being an option.}) ({On other moves, ...Be4 is the main threat: for example,} 37. Red1 Be4 38. d5 Qh5+ 39. Kg1 Qh1+ 40. Kf2 Qf3+ 41. Ke1 Bc2 $1 {with a quick win. Sokolov tries a desperate exchange sacrifice, but it doesn't work. with a quick win. Sokolov tries a desperate exchange sacrifice, but it doesn't work.}) 37... Bxe4 38. Rxe4 Qxe4 {Colin Crouch: 'Black is now a pawn up, with good centralization and continued chances of an attack on the king. '} 39. d5 {Colin Crouch: 'White's only hope is his passed pawn.'} Nc4 {Colin Crouch: '. But this squeezes any counterplay.'} 0-1

