1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Bb4 9. f3 Ne5 {The main line, but there have also been important developments in two sidelines:} ({a) Nisipeanu has made good use of} 9... Ne7 {, but after} 10. Nde2 b5 11. Bf4 $1 e5 12. Bg5 Qb6 $6 ({a new idea, but I'm not so sure why Nisipeanu wanted to avoid the} 12... h5 $5 13. Kb1 Ba5 14. Nc1 {of Karjakin-Nisipeanu, Foros 2008, when Black might well exchange on c3, as we've noted before}) 13. a3 Bc5 14. b4 $1 Bf2 15. Qd6 Qxd6 16. Rxd6 Neg8 17. Ng3 {White was in full command in Shirov-Nisipeanu, Bazna 2009.}) ({b) However,} 9... O-O $5 {continues to hold up well:} 10. g4 b5 11. g5 Nh5 (11... Ne8 {is the alternative, after which the new try} 12. a3 Be7 13. Nf5 $5 exf5 14. Nd5 Qb7 15. exf5 Nc7 16. f6 Bd8 17. Nb6 Rb8 {gave White decent play for his piece, and was most unclear in Vachier Lagrave-A.Sokolov, French Championship, Nimes 2009}) 12. Kb1 ({Volokitin's} 12. Nde2 $5 {may well be more critical}) 12... Rb8 ({Polgar herself had previously preferred} 12... Nxd4 {- see Anand-J. Polgar, Canada de Calatrava (rapid) 2007, in the Archive}) 13. Nxc6 dxc6 14. f4 $5 e5 $1 15. f5 Bb7 16. Be2 Nf4 17. Bf3 c5 18. Qd6 Qxd6 19. Rxd6 c4 {was about equal in Short-Satyapragyan, Kolkata 2009.}) 10. Nb3 b5 11. Qe1 {For coverage of the arguably less-critical alternatives, the subscriber should consult Vachier Lagrave-Vitiugov, Plovdiv 2008, and earlier games in the Archive.} Be7 {And this in turn is probably the best reply, making way for a queenside attack.} 12. f4 Nc4 $6 ({A notable but not wholly convincing choice. Not for Polgar} 12... Ng6 {, as preferred by Vitiugov and advocated in 'The Safest Sicilian'.}) 13. e5 Ng4 14. Bd4 Bb7 15. Bxc4 $1 {By no means the only choice, but this may have been preparation for Polgar had already reached this position earlier in the same event:} ({a)} 15. Kb1 O-O 16. Ne4 $6 Nce3 $1 17. Bxe3 Bxe4 18. Bd3 Bxd3 ({quite possibly Polgar had subsequently decided she could grab:} 18... Bxg2 $5 19. Rg1 Bf3 {and Black can defend with either .. . f5 or ...Bh5-g6}) 19. Rxd3 Rac8 20. Rc3 Qb7 {was about equal at this stage in Najer-J.Polgar, Natanya (rapid) 2009.}) ({b) Preparing h3 and g4 with} 15. Bd3 $5 {also makes a fair degree of sense, after which} f5 16. h3 Nh6 17. Rg1 Rc8 18. g4 {might be a little better for White.}) 15... bxc4 16. Nd2 Nh6 17. Qe2 Rc8 18. Nde4 {It feels like White's centralized pieces should give him the advantage, but presumably Polgar had considered the position at home and wasn't so sure.} Nf5 ({Naturally} 18... O-O $6 19. g4 {was well worth avoiding.}) 19. g4 Nxd4 20. Rxd4 O-O $5 ({A novelty. It doesn't fully solve Black's problems, but does improve over} 20... f6 $6 21. Rhd1 fxe5 (21... Bc6 22. exf6 $1 Qxf4+ 23. Kb1 gxf6 24. Nd6+ Bxd6 25. Rxf4 Bxf4 26. Ne4 {is also pretty good for White}) 22. Rxd7 $1 Qxd7 23. Rxd7 Kxd7 24. fxe5 Rhf8 25. a3 {, which left Black firmly on the defensive and suffering in Volokitin-Macieja, Bermuda 2005.}) 21. g5 $5 ({I'm not too sure what was wrong with the simple} 21. Rhd1 Rfd8 22. Nd6 Bxd6 23. Rxd6 {when White must have an edge, but Sutovsky remains true to his style and introduces dangerous ideas of Nf6+.}) 21... Bxe4 22. Nxe4 Qa5 {Black too can aim to attack.} 23. Kb1 c3 24. Rhd1 $1 Rb8 $2 ({A lazy move, albeit just the type of mistake which is so easy to make in a fast game. It turns out that Black is unable to exploit the b2-square and that c3 will actually become weak. Thus Polgar should have preferred either} 24... cxb2 25. Rxd7 Bc5 26. R7d3) ({or} 24... Rc7 $5 {when White might be a little better, but plenty of play most certainly remains.}) 25. b3 Rfc8 26. Qd3 Bb4 27. Rc4 {Sutovsky is quick to target the weakness on c3. Indeed, he must or risk being mated.} Rxc4 28. Qxc4 d5 $5 {A decent attempt at randomising, especially as White may have already been running low on time.} 29. exd6 Qa3 30. Nxc3 Bxd6 31. Ne4 Bf8 32. Rd7 ({Not bad, but} 32. Qc7 $1 {followed by 33 Nd6 would have been an even better way to maintain control.}) 32... Qa5 33. a4 $4 ({Sutovsky did not enjoy the best of times in this event, and the text is an unfortunate blunder. Even here} 33. Qc7 $1 {would still have been quite strong.}) 33... Qe1+ 34. Ka2 Rb4 {Black picks up a piece.} 35. Qc8 Rxe4 36. Rb7 ({There's no counter for White against the black king, and} 36. Rd8 Qb4 {would also defend.}) 36... Qc1 $1 37. b4 Re3 0-1

