1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Be7 9. f3 b5 10. g4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bb7 12. g5 Nh5 13. Be5 Qxe5 14. Qxd7+ Kf8 15. Qxb7 Bxg5+ {So far this move has been played almost automatically, either on this move or after 15...b8 16.d7.} ({However, Black's struggles in this game led GM Van Kampen to suggest a very unexpected} 15... Qb8 16. Rd7 Qxb7 17. Rxb7 h6 $5 $146 {as a potential improvement in his video series on this variation. This has been already tested in a practical game:} 18. g6 fxg6 19. a4 Kf7 20. axb5 ({Van Kampen cites} 20. Bd3 bxa4 21. Nxa4 a5 $11 {as the main line}) 20... axb5 21. Kb1 b4 22. Nb5 Rhd8 (22... Rhb8 $5 {is a possible improvement}) 23. Bc4 Ra5 24. c3 bxc3 25. bxc3 Nf4 26. Kc2 {Bjerre,J (2083)-Hecht,C (2259) Dortmund 2015. Objectively, the position is almost equal, even though White almost managed to win it later on.}) 16. Kb1 Qb8 17. Rd7 Qxb7 18. Rxb7 Bf6 19. a4 Bxc3 20. bxc3 bxa4 21. Bxa6 g5 22. Bb5 {[%mdl 4100] This is clearly the critical position for the whole 10...xd4 variation. It is so imbalanced that different players tend to give it completely opposite evaluations! For example, GM Andriasian wrote the following in his book "The English Attack Against the Taimanov Sicilian": "When this game was played and I saw this position, I thought White should have a large advantage because of his bishop and passed pawns, plus the fact that the black king is cut off." In his review of this book in "New In Chess" #4/2015, GM Sadler notes: "Funny how two players can have such a different gut feeling about the position! I thought at once that this could turn out pretty disgusting for White - king's rook on c8, knight round to e5. I just couldn't imagine White being better here". Actually, the quote from Andriasian's book was cut short, as in the next very sentence he concedes: "However, once I looked more deeply into the positon, I understood that things are not so simple." I must admit that my own evaluation was probably closer to Andriasian than to Sadler. Based on my experience in the "old line", I knew how dangerous the doubled pawns on the c-file can become, so I was worried whether Black has enough time to generate counterplay on the kingside. This game proves that the task is not so easy, as one of the strongest grandmasters in the world fails to hold this!} Kg7 (22... Nf4 $5 {is a recent attempt to improve Black's play that was analysed in Andriasian's book. So far it was played in just one game that lasted only two more moves:} 23. c4 ({Andriasian looks at two other options for White in his book:} 23. h4 h5 $1 $132 {This is a standard idea for Black in this line; we will see it in the next game as well}) ({and} 23. Rd1 Kg7 24. Rdd7 Raf8 $1 25. Bxa4 Ng6 $132) 23... Ng6 {Only this is a novelty in a true sense of the word. Sadler praises this move: "Bringing the knight to e5, which covers the f7-square and thus prevents White from forcing an exchange of rooks by threatening to invade on the 7th. Black can also simply try and take the pawn on f3 and then push his kingside pawns, being willing to sacrifice an exchange or even a rook for the c-pawn. The knight on f3 is ideally placed to shepherd home Black's kingside pawns."} ({In the aforementioned book, GM Andriasian analyses only} 23... Ne2 24. c5 Nc3+ 25. Kb2 Nxb5 26. Rxb5 {, saying that "White is slightly better, but rook endings are notorious for their drawing tendencies"}) 24. Rf1 {Quesada Perez,Y (2637)-Chandra,A (2459) Philadelphia 2015, and the players agreed to a draw in spite of the White player being almost 200 Elo points stronger.} ({The strongest test of Black's idea is the direct} 24. c5 $5 Ne5 25. c6 Nxf3 26. c7 {The computer thinks that this is perfectly fine for Black after} Kg7 {and I guess it must be right. However, I am not sure I would be able to defend this over the board. Let me provide just one illustrative variation:} 27. Bd7 Nd2+ 28. Kc1 Nxe4 29. Bxe6 Nc5 30. c8=Q Rhxc8 31. Bxc8 Rxc8 {It looks like Black can hold this by pushing his pawns on the kingside and some equestrian equilibristics. Side note: when I was working on this article, I learned that this position, which never arose on the board so far, has already been analysed by at least three different people! (I was the 4th :) It is amazing how much one can learn about the current state of theory these days just by checking the cloud function of ChessBase...} 32. Ra7 Nb3+ 33. Kb2 Nd4 $11)) 23. Rd1 (23. c4 Rac8 {- see Falatowicz-Terekhov, corr 2014.}) 23... Rhb8 $2 {[%mdl 16384] Sadler: "With both pairs of rooks on the board, Black makes it much more difficult for the white king to advance in support of the passed c-pawn. For this reason I liked the manoeuvre that Chandra played very much".} ({In my opinion, Black can still get enough counterplay by} 23... Nf4 24. c4 (24. Rdd7 $6 {would probably just give an extra tempo for Black once he gets to play f4-g6-e5:} Rhf8 25. c4 Rac8) 24... Rac8 $1 {following the same plan as in Falatowicz-Terekhov. This line has been suggested in "The Most Flexible Sicilian" book by Delchev & Semkov. Black's plan is quite standard for this endgame: h5, g6-e5, threatening ...xf3 or even ...g4} ({Delchev & Semkov also mention} 24... Ng6 $5 25. c5 Ne5 26. c6 Nxf3 27. c7 Nxh2 {evaluating it as "rather unclear". Note that we have already seen this position in the notes to Quesada Perez-Chandra game above, only here White has already committed his rook to d1, which might prove to be not the most useful move})) 24. Rxb8 Rxb8 25. c4 Nf4 26. Kb2 {Andriasian: "The computer considers the position to be equal, but such positions are extremely difficult for Black to play, especially when a pair of rooks have been exchanged. The c-pawn becomes very dangerous. I would not recommend Black to enter this position, where he has to play very accurately for a long time, just to make a draw". In fact, at a greater depth of analysis the computer's evaluation starts to grow in White's favour. For experienced correspondence players this is a sure sign of trouble!  In the past, this type of position was commonly evaluated as "/+-"} Ng6 27. Ka3 Ne5 28. Kxa4 Nxf3 $2 {This natural move seems to be a decisive mistake already. This goes to show how difficult and unenviable Black's task is in this endgame!} ({After the mathematically strongest} 28... Rc8 29. Kb4 Kf6 30. c5 Ke7 $1 {Black is still fighting for survival.}) 29. c5 Ne5 30. c6 Rc8 31. Rd6 Kf6 32. Kb4 $18 {Sooner or later Black would have to give his knight for the c6-pawn. Unfortunately, White has one more pawn on the same file!} g4 33. Kc5 Ke7 34. Rd1 h5 35. Kb6 $6 {A slight inaccuracy, which, however, does not spoil anything.} (35. c4 $1 {allowed White to keep Black's counterplay in check.}) 35... Rb8+ 36. Kc5 Rc8 37. Kb6 ({Again missing} 37. c4 $1) 37... Rb8+ 38. Ka6 Nxc6 39. Bxc6 Rb2 40. Ba4 Rb4 41. Bb5 Rxe4 42. Rc1 h4 43. c4 Re2 44. c5 Rxh2 45. c6 Rd2 46. c7 Rd8 47. cxd8=Q+ Kxd8 48. Be2 f5 49. Rc4 {The shortest way to the victory is to simplify into a  vs  endgame.} Kd7 50. Bxg4 fxg4 51. Rxg4 Kc6 52. Ka5 Kd5 1-0

