1. d4 {Tyomkin,D Tyomkin,D} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Rc1 {Topalov's variation! However this move hasn't brought him any single win yet- few draws and a loss in this game. 8.e4 is the most popular, but also 8.Qc2 and 8.Bg2 were tried by some strong players.} d5 $146 {Judit invents a novelty, while it seems like black got a good play in previous games. I am not sure that Judit prepared this move at her home preparation- she probably more expected 1.e4 from Topalov in this game.  The strongest looks 8...c6 following with d5;} (8... c6 $1 9. e4 d5 10. Bd3 dxe4 (10... dxc4 $5 11. bxc4 e5 {Tyomkin,D: '!' '!' and black look very good here. Tyomkin,D: 'and black looks great here.'}) (10... dxc4 $5 11. bxc4 e5 $1 {and black looks great here.}) 11. Nxe4 Nbd7 12. Nxf6+ Nxf6 13. Bc3 c5 14. dxc5 Bxc5 15. Qe2 Bb7 16. O-O h6 (16... Re8 $5 {[%cal Ge6e5]} 17. b4 e5 18. Ng5 $6 Bd4 19. Ne4 Rc8 20. Nxf6+ Qxf6 $15 {0-1 Bologan,V-Tkachiev,V/Belfort 2004/EXT 2005 (35)}) 17. b4 Be7 18. Rfd1 Qc7 19. Bxf6 Bxf6 20. Be4 Bxe4 21. Qxe4 a5 $1 $11 {½-½ Topalov,V-Gelfand,B/Monaco 2004/CBM 099 ext (50)}) (8... Bb7 9. Bg2 d5 10. Ne5 Na6 11. cxd5 exd5 12. O-O c5 13. Be3 Re8 14. Nd3 Qd7 15. Na4 cxd4 16. Bxd4 Ne4 $11 {½-½ Topalov,V-Kramnik,V/Monaco 2004/CBM 099 ext (41)}) (8... Ba3 {Finkel} 9. Rc2 (9. Rb1 d5 10. cxd5 (10. b4 c5 11. bxc5 bxc5 12. cxd5 exd5 13. Qa4 cxd4 14. Qxa3 dxc3 15. Bxc3 Ne4 $13) 10... exd5 11. Bg2 c5 12. O-O Nc6 13. Bg5 Re8 $132) 9... Nc6 10. Bc1 Bb4 (10... Bxc1 11. Rxc1 Bb7 12. Bg2 Ne7 13. O-O d6 14. Qd3 Re8 15. e4 $14) 11. Bg2 (11. a3 Bxc3+ 12. Rxc3 Bb7 13. Bg2 Ne7 14. O-O d6 $13) 11... b5 $5 {Beliavsky is famous for his uncompromising style, that's why he starts a fight for the initiative from the very first moves.} 12. cxb5 Bxb5 13. Bd2 (13. O-O Bxc3 14. Rxc3 Ne4 (14... Rb8 15. Re1 Ne4) 15. Re3 d5 16. a4 Ba6 17. Ba3 $14) 13... Rb8 14. O-O a5 {Black's play on the queenside is based on the advance of an a-pawn.} 15. Nxb5 Rxb5 16. Bg5 Be7 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Qd2 Nb4 19. Rcc1 c6 20. Rfd1 Qa8 21. e3 (21. e4 d5 22. e5 Be7 23. Rc3 Rb6 24. Rdc1 $13) 21... Rfb8 22. Ne5 $6 {I don't like the idea to give black a chance to get rid of the knight.} Bxe5 23. dxe5 R5b7 24. Rc4 Nd5 {½-½ Ionov,S-Beliavsky,A/Sochi 2004/CBM 101/[Finkel,A] (81)}) 9. cxd5 Nxd5 (9... exd5 10. Bg2 $14) 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Bg2 Re8 12. Rc2 {This rook will serve to protect e2- white intends to move the bishop d2 and then to castle if possible... But this game got completely different scenario!} Bd6 13. Bg5 Bb4+ $1 {This intermediate check prevents white from castling in this game! Topalov definitely expected this move.} 14. Kf1 f6 15. Bf4 Nd7 $6 {15...Bd6 looks safe and logical, while Judit goes for the fast development.} (15... Bd6 16. Ne5 $1 Bb7 17. Nd3 a5 $1 $11 {[%cal Gb8a6,Ga6b4]}) 16. Ne1 $6 {iT seems like 16.a3 leads to white's advantage, while the game move allows black to regroup.} (16. a3 $5 Bd6 (16... Bxa3 $2 17. Qa1 Qe7 18. Ng1 $1 $16 {[%csl Ya3,Ya6,Rd5][%cal Rc2a2]}) (16... Bf8 17. Ng1 $1 $16 {[%csl Rc7,Rd5]}) 17. Nh4 Bxf4 (17... Bb7 18. Bxd6 cxd6 19. Bf3 $16 {[%csl Rd6,Rd7][%cal Gf1g2,Yh4f5]}) 18. Bxd5+ Kh8 19. gxf4 Rc8 20. Bc6 $16) (16. Bxc7 Rxe2 (16... Bxe2+ 17. Rxe2 Qxc7 18. Ne1 $1 Rxe2 19. Qxe2 (19. Bxd5+ $6 Kf8 20. Qxe2 Re8) 19... Kf8 20. Nd3 Re8 21. Qd1 $14) 17. Rxe2 Qxc7 18. Kg1 Bxe2 19. Qxe2 Qc1+ $11) 16... c6 17. Nd3 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 Rc8 19. Bh3 g6 $1 {[%cal Gf6f5,Gd7f6] black intends to block light square bishop of white with f5.} 20. a3 Bf8 {The swap of a-pawns in 20...Bxa3 line is in white's favor.} (20... Bxa3 21. Ra2 Bf8 22. Rxa7 f5 23. Kg2 $14) 21. e3 $6 {Very risky move which locks f4. 21.b4 would protect everthing and white would have time to bring Rh1 into the game.} (21. Kg2 f5 22. g4 $140 g5 {[%cal Gf5f4]}) (21. b4 $142 f5 22. Kg2 $14 {[%cal Gh1c1,Yg2g1]}) 21... Qe7 (21... g5 $6 22. Qf5 $1) 22. Bg4 {No way back. after 22.g4 black can safely grab a3 pawn.} (22. g4 Qxa3) 22... g5 23. Qf5 ({Fritz suggests crazy but worthless} 23. Bb8 Nxb8 24. Bxc8 Rxc8 25. Qf5 Qd7 $1 $17) (23. Bxg5 fxg5 24. h4 gxh4 25. Rxh4 Kh8 $1 $17) 23... gxf4 {It seems like white doesn't get enough compensation in 23...Red8, while also 23...gxf4 keeps an advantage (only if my analysis of 24.Qxd7 line is accurate!)} (23... Red8 24. Rxc6 Rxc6 25. Qxd5+ Kh8 26. Qxc6 gxf4 27. gxf4 Nb8 $1 $15) 24. gxf4 $4 {This move actually loses a game. I guess Topalov didn't see any good for him in 24.Qxd7 line, however this was a best chance for him. According my analysis of 24.Qxd7 line, the best black can achieve in it is some slight advantage in the ending.} (24. Qxd7 $1 Qe4 25. Rg1 Qxc2 (25... fxe3 26. Bf5 Rc7 $1 27. Qxc7 Qxf5 28. Re2 Bxa3 (28... c5 29. Qf4 $1 Qd3 30. fxe3 Re4 31. Qxf6 cxd4 32. Qg5+ Kh8 33. Qf6+ Kg8 34. Qg5+ $11) 29. Qf4 Qd3 30. Qxf6 exf2 31. Qg5+ Kh8 32. Qf6+ Kg8 33. Qg5+ $11) 26. Bf5 Rc7 $1 (26... Qd1+ 27. Kg2 Qh5 28. Be6+ Kh8 29. Bf7 Re7 (29... Red8 30. Qxc8 Rxc8 31. Bxh5 $11) 30. Qxe7 Bxe7 31. Bxh5 fxe3 32. fxe3 $11) 27. gxf4+ Kh8 28. Qxe8 Qd1+ 29. Kg2 Rg7+ 30. Kh3 Qxg1 31. Qxf8+ Rg8 32. Qxf6+ Qg7 (32... Rg7 33. Qf8+ $11) 33. Qxg7+ Rxg7 34. b4 Rc7 {[%cal Gc6c5] black is better here-after c5 break pawn a3 of white will become a target, but white has good chances to escape!} 35. a4 c5 36. dxc5 bxc5 37. bxc5 Rxc5 38. Kg4 {and i think white can draw it here.}) 24... Kh8 $1 $19 {Tough defense- 25.Qxd7 doesn't work anymore!} 25. Rg1 (25. Qxd7 Qe4 26. Rg1 Qxc2 27. Bf5 Qd1+ 28. Kg2 Qh5 $19) 25... Rc7 26. Bf3 Nb8 $1 {connecting black major pieces and overprotecting c6. White is down a knight for only one pawn, despite of that Topalov fought hard for another 27 moves- I believe many players would resign much earlier.} 27. Rg3 Qd6 28. b4 b5 29. Rc1 a5 {White pieces are far away from the queenside, so Judit opens it there.} 30. bxa5 Qxa3 31. Rc5 (31. Qxf6+ $4 Bg7 {[%csl Rc1]}) 31... Rg7 (31... Bxc5 $4 32. Qxf6+) 32. Bh5 Rd8 33. Qxf6 {White have won f6 but black king is safe now- too many black pieces are around to protect the king.} Qxa5 34. Rc2 b4 35. Ra2 Qc7 36. Be2 {36.Ra7 is more resistant, but white must win there anyhow.} (36. Ra7 $142 Qxa7 37. Rxg7 Qxg7 38. Qxd8 Nd7 (38... Na6 39. Qb6 Qd7 $1 40. Qxa6 Qh3+ 41. Ke1 Qxh5 42. Qxc6 Qf5 $19) 39. Be8 Nf6 40. Bxc6 Qe7 $19) 36... Qe7 37. Qf5 Qe4 38. Qxe4 dxe4 39. Ra8 b3 40. Bc4 b2 41. Ba2 c5 $1 42. dxc5 b1=Q+ 43. Bxb1 Rd1+ 44. Kg2 Rxb1 {White has only three pawns for two light pieces of black, but the fight continues!} 45. Ra4 Re7 {in case of x white would get a pawns' diamond!} (45... Rxg3+ 46. hxg3) 46. Rg5 Nc6 47. Ra6 Re6 48. h4 Be7 49. f5 (49. Ra8+ Rb8) 49... Rf6 50. Rg4 Rb4 51. Ra8+ Rf8 52. Rxf8+ Bxf8 53. f6 Ne5 0-1

