1. e4 {Marin,M Marin,M} e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. Nc3 Bf5 9. Re1 Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 O-O {Marin,M: 'For general ideas of this variation as well as for a small survey see the notes to Cheparinov,I (2670) -Roiz,M (2630)/Kemer 2007/CBM 121/[Marin] (1-0, 58)' For general ideas of this variation as well as for a small survey see the notes to Cheparinov,I (2670)-Roiz,M (2630)/Kemer 2007/CBM 121/[Marin] (1-0, 58)} 12. Rb1 Rb8 13. c4 $146 {Marin,M: 'Since both Topalov and his second, Cheparinov, play this line with White, the last move must be the fruit of their home analysis. The insertion of the moves Rb1 and ...Rb8 is supposed to favour White, because after the opening of the centre Black's rook would belong to the central d-file, while the white rook could still be useful on the b-file.' Since both Topalov and his second, Cheparinov, play this line with White, the last move must be the fruit of their home analysis. The insertion of the moves b1 and ...b8 is supposed to favour White, because after the opening of the centre Black's rook would belong to the central d-file, while the white rook could still be useful on the b-file.} (13. Qf5 {Gashimov,V (2664)-Dominguez Perez,L (2683)/Barcelona 2007 (½-½, 35) Marin,M: 'Gashimov,V (2664)-Dominguez Perez,L (2683)/Barcelona 2007 (1/2-1/2, 35) is also examined in the notes to the aforementioned game.' is also examined in the notes to the aforementioned game.}) 13... Bf6 {Marin,M: 'Before releasing the tension, Black increases the pressure against the enemy centre. However, opening the e-file and leaving the a3-f8 diagonal undefended before having completed development is not without drawbacks as we shall see.' Before releasing the tension, Black increases the pressure against the enemy centre. However, opening the e-file and leaving the a3-f8 diagonal undefended before having completed development is not without drawbacks as we shall see.} ({Marin,M: 'One of the differences induced by the rook moves is that after' One of the differences induced by the rook moves is that after} 13... dxc4 14. Qxc4 Bd6 {Marin,M: '(as played with the queen rooks on their initial positions)' (as played with the queen rooks on their initial positions)} 15. d5 {Marin,M: 'the b4-square is not available (...Nb4 would threaten ...Nxd5!, winning time), while' the b4-square is not available (...b4 would threaten ...xd5!, winning time), while} Na5 $6 {Marin,M: 'would be bad because of' would be bad because of} 16. Qa4 b6 17. Bd2 $16 {Marin,M: 'Black has to retreat with his knight to e7, thus allowing White to look for ways to develop his initiative in the centre.' Black has to retreat with his knight to e7, thus allowing White to look for ways to develop his initiative in the centre.}) 14. c3 ({Marin,M: 'Topalov probably considered that White's initiative after' Topalov probably considered that White's initiative after} 14. Ba3 dxc4 15. Qxc4 Re8 {Marin,M: 'is of a temporary nature. Indeed, having been developed with gain of time, the a3-bishop might remain out of play. In the game, it will be developed to f4, keeping the backward c7-pawn under constant pressure.' is of a temporary nature. Indeed, having been developed with gain of time, the a3-bishop might remain out of play. In the game, it will be developed to f4, keeping the backward c7-pawn under constant pressure.}) 14... dxc4 {Marin,M: 'It seems that Black has to release the tension in the centre already, since the generally useful moves do not work out properly here. However, by this time it is not entirely clear whether this really is a concession, because the queen is not especialy well placed on c4, being exposed to the attack of the enemy knight with ...Na5.' It seems that Black has to release the tension in the centre already, since the generally useful moves do not work out properly here. However, by this time it is not entirely clear whether this really is a concession, because the queen is not especialy well placed on c4, being exposed to the attack of the enemy knight with ...a5.} (14... b6 {[%csl Gc6] Marin,M: 'leaves the knight undefended, restricting Black's possibilities after' leaves the knight undefended, restricting Black's possibilities after} 15. Ba3 {Marin,M: 'to' to} dxc4 16. Qxc4 Na5 17. Qa4 Re8 {Marin,M: ', when White can maintain stable domination with' , when White can maintain stable domination with} 18. Bb4 Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Nc4 20. Qc6 Nd6 21. a4 $14) (14... Na5 {Marin,M: 'loses material to' loses material to} 15. Ba3 dxc4 16. Qf5 $1 $16 {[%csl Ga5,Gf8]}) 15. Qxc4 Qd7 16. Bf4 {Marin,M: 'The pressure against the c7-pawn is aimed to contribute to the queen's stability on c4.' The pressure against the c7-pawn is aimed to contribute to the queen's stability on c4.} Rbd8 17. h3 b6 18. Re4 {[%cal Gb1e1]} Na5 $5 {Marin,M: 'White threatened to increase his pressure gradually and Black decided that this was the right moment to free herself by tactical means.' White threatened to increase his pressure gradually and Black decided that this was the right moment to free herself by tactical means.} ({Marin,M: 'The preliminary exchange of one pair of rooks with' The preliminary exchange of one pair of rooks with} 18... Rfe8 19. Rbe1 Rxe4 20. Rxe4 {Marin,M: 'before playing' before playing} Na5 {Marin,M: 'would leave the enemy queen more freedom of action than in the game:' would leave the enemy queen more freedom of action than in the game:} 21. Qxc7 Qd5 22. Re2 Nc4 23. Qxa7 $16) (18... Ne7 {Marin,M: 'is safer, but slightly passive.' is safer, but slightly passive.} 19. Be5 $14 (19. Qxc7 $2 Qf5 {[%cal Ge7d5]})) 19. Qxc7 $1 {Marin,M: 'This move required some very precise calculation. We have seen Topalov's queen infiltrating herself to the same dangerous looking area of the board in the game Topalov,V (2783)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Morelia/Linares 2007/CBM 117 [Marin] (1/2-1/2, 25), when the courageous incursion led to a forced draw by repetition.' This move required some very precise calculation. We have seen Topalov's queen infiltrating herself to the same dangerous looking area of the board in the game Topalov,V (2783)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Morelia/Linares 2007/CBM 117 [Marin] (½-½, 25), when the courageous incursion led to a forced draw by repetition.} Qd5 20. Rbe1 $14 {Marin,M: 'It would be redundant to attach an exclamation mark to this natural move, but we shall see that it is absolutely necessary in order to keep troubles away from the queen.' It would be redundant to attach an exclamation mark to this natural move, but we shall see that it is absolutely necessary in order to keep troubles away from the queen.} Nc4 ({Marin,M: 'Topalov's fine point is that' Topalov's fine point is that} 20... Rd7 {Marin,M: 'fails to trap the queen because of the unexpected' fails to trap the queen because of the unexpected} 21. Qc8 $3 $16 {Marin,M: 'threatening Re8 and preparing the queen evacuation through a6 if necessary.' threatening e8 and preparing the queen evacuation through a6 if necessary.} Rxc8 $2 {Marin,M: 'is impossible, of course, because of' is impossible, of course, because of} 22. Re8+ Rxe8 23. Rxe8#) 21. Qxa7 $4 $19 {Marin,M: 'Having won the battle of principle in the phase of transition from the opening to the middlegame, Topalov lets be himself carried away by euphoria, caused by the false sensation that the queen is invulnerable.' Having won the battle of principle in the phase of transition from the opening to the middlegame, Topalov lets be himself carried away by euphoria, caused by the false sensation that the queen is invulnerable.} ({Marin,M: 'White had several reasonable moves here, but I would prefer' White had several reasonable moves here, but I would prefer} 21. Bg5 $5 {Marin,M: ', getting rid of the potentially "bad" bishop and weakening the defence of the enemy kingside.' , getting rid of the potentially "bad" bishop and weakening the defence of the enemy kingside.} Bxg5 22. Nxg5 h6 (22... Qxg5 23. Qxc4 $16) (22... Nd6 23. Re5 Qxa2 24. Re7 $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rg7,Rh7][%cal Gg5e6]} Qc2 $6 {[%csl Gh7]} 25. Nxf7 $1) 23. Nf3 Nd6 24. R4e3 Qxa2 25. Ne5 $14 {Marin,M: 'Black has reestablished the material equality, but White's position remains more active. After a further knight move to c6, Black will be very passive.' Black has reestablished the material equality, but White's position remains more active. After a further knight move to c6, Black will be very passive.}) 21... Ra8 22. Qc7 Bd8 {Marin,M: 'What could he have missed when he captured on a7? It is curious that this is not the first game in which Topalov allowed Judit to trap one of his pieces. At Wijk aan Zee in 2005, he played a suicidal e2-e3, leaving the f4-bishop without an escape after ...g6-g5.' What could he have missed when he captured on a7? It is curious that this is not the first game in which Topalov allowed Judit to trap one of his pieces. At Wijk aan Zee in 2005, he played a suicidal e2-e3, leaving the f4-bishop without an escape after ...g6-g5.} 23. Re5 Bxc7 24. Rxd5 Bxf4 {Marin,M: 'White will get some vague saving chances if he could manage to drive the enemy bishop to a passive position and exchange the wonderful c4-knight. However, this will be tactically impossible, as we shall see.' White will get some vague saving chances if he could manage to drive the enemy bishop to a passive position and exchange the wonderful c4-knight. However, this will be tactically impossible, as we shall see.} 25. Re2 (25. g3 Bd2 $1 {Marin,M: 'forces White give his knight away for the bishop, leaving the black knight unchallenged master of the weak queenside squares.' forces White give his knight away for the bishop, leaving the black knight unchallenged master of the weak queenside squares.}) 25... Ra3 26. Rc2 (26. g3 Rxc3 $1 $19 {[%csl Gf3]}) 26... Rfa8 27. Kf1 (27. g3 Bxg3 $1 28. fxg3 Ne3 $19 {Marin,M: 'On three consecutive moves, the advance of the g-pawn has been impossible for three different reasons!' On three consecutive moves, the advance of the g-pawn has been impossible for three different reasons!}) 27... Kf8 28. Ke2 Rxa2 29. Rxa2 Rxa2+ 30. Kd3 Nd2 31. Ne5 Bxe5 32. Rxe5 Nb3 (32... Nf1 $2 33. Re2 $1 {[%csl Rf1]}) 33. c4 Na5 34. c5 Nc6 0-1

