1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 b6 $6 $14 {Joe Gallagher: '!?' '!?' Joe Gallagher: 'A novelty on move 9 in the Mar del Plata. That is almost a miracle. Will it catch on? I doubt it. Before embarking on the usual kingside attack Judith decides to take prophylactic action against White's queenside breakthrough c5.' Joe Gallagher: 'A novelty on move 9 in the Mar del Plata. That is almost a miracle. Will it catch on? I doubt it. Before embarking on the usual kingside attack Judith decides to take prophylactic action against White's queenside breakthrough c5. Tsesarsky: '?!'' Strange and bad novelty. Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Strange and bad novelty.''} ({Main theoretical lines are:} 9... a5) (9... c5) (9... Ne8) (9... c6) 10. Nd3 {[%cal Gf2f4] Best reaction on Black's poor play. Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Best reaction on Black's poor play.''} Ne8 $6 $16 {Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: '?!''} ({Why not} 10... Nd7 $14 {preventing f2-f4.}) 11. f4 $1 {Joe Gallagher: 'A logical reaction. Central play is more effective after Black has wasted a tempo with b6.' This position should be compared with theoretical one which easily might be ctreated after 9...e8 10.d3 f5 11. f4. Nobody will consider to play here 11...b7-b6? In the game b7-b6 useless move was done instead of useful f7-f5. Of course, White caught an advantage... Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'This position should be compared with theoretical one which easily might be ctreated after 9...e8 10.d3 f5 11. f4. Nobody will consider to play here 11...b7-b6? In the game b7-b6 useless move was done instead of useful f7-f5. Of course, White caught an advantage...''} f5 12. Kh1 ({Possibly} 12. fxe5 dxe5 13. b4 Nd6 14. Nf2 $16) 12... Kh8 13. fxe5 dxe5 14. b4 Nd6 15. Nf2 fxe4 {Joe Gallagher: 'Black concedes the e4 square but gets outposts of her own in return. It wasn't really possible to hold up the white pawns as after'} (15... c5 16. bxc5 bxc5 17. Be3 $16 {[%csl Rc5][%cal Ge3c5,Gc3a4]} (17. Na4 {the pawn on c5 is too weak. For example} Qc7 (17... Qa5 18. Bg5 Qc7 19. Nxc5) 18. Ba3 Nb7 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. d6 $16)) 16. c5 $1 {Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: '!'' White centre became powerful. Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'White centre became powerful.''} (16. Nfxe4 Bf5 17. Bd3 $16 {[%csl Re5,Rg7]}) 16... Ndf5 17. Nfxe4 a5 $2 $18 {Joe Gallagher: '?' '?' Joe Gallagher: 'This is to slow and it also weakens the b6 square. White now obtains an excellent game through a series of simple but powerful moves. Better was' Joe Gallagher: 'This is to slow and it also weakens the b6 square. White now obtains an excellent game through a series of simple but powerful moves. Better was Tsesarsky: '+-?'' The idea of this move is to destroy White's central  chain. It's refutation isn't difficult, but obvious: Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'The idea of this move is to destroy White's central  chain. It's refutation isn't difficult, but obvious:''} ({Perhaps, better was Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Perhaps, better was''} 17... Nd4 18. Rxf8+ Qxf8 {Joe Gallagher: 'with a playable game for Black'} 19. Bc4 Bf5 20. Qd3 $14) 18. d6 $1 {Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: '!'' Of course... Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Of course...''} cxd6 19. cxd6 Nc6 20. Bg5 {White's initiative is developed from itself without any serious resistance... Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'White's initiative is developed from itself without any serious resistance...''} Qe8 21. Nd5 {[%cal Gd5c7,Gd5b6]} Ncd4 ({Joe Gallagher: 'After' Or Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Or''} 21... Rb8 22. Ndf6 {Joe Gallagher: '!' '!' Joe Gallagher: 'Black is in trouble, e.g.'} Qf7 (22... Qe6 23. Qc1 Bb7 24. Bc4 Qc8 25. d7 Qc7 26. Be6 $18) 23. g4 {Joe Gallagher: '?!' '?!'} (23. d7 Bb7 24. Nd6) 23... h6 (23... Ncd4 24. gxf5 Bb7 {is far from clear}) 24. gxf5 hxg5 25. Nh7 Bxf5 26. Nexg5 {[%cal Gh7f8]} Qb7 27. Nxf8 $18) 22. Nc7 Qc6 23. Bd3 {Joe Gallagher: '!' '!'} ({Not} 23. Nxa8 $2 Qxe4 24. Bf3 Nxf3 25. Qxf3 Nxd6 26. Qxe4 Nxe4 27. Be7 Rxf1+ 28. Rxf1 Ba6 29. Rc1 Bh6 $13) 23... Ra7 24. Rc1 ({Possibly also} 24. Be7 Ne3 25. Rxf8+ Bxf8 26. Qc1 Bxe7 27. dxe7 Bd7 28. Qxe3 Nf5 (28... Rxc7 29. Qh6 $18) 29. Qg5 Rxc7 30. Bb5 Qe6 31. Bxd7 $18) 24... Qd7 25. Nd5 axb4 (25... Qe6 26. Nxb6 $18) (25... Rb7 26. Be7 Nxe7 27. Rxf8+ Bxf8 28. dxe7 Bxe7 29. Nxe7 Qxe7 30. Rxc8+) 26. Be7 Nxe7 27. dxe7 Re8 28. Nxb6 Qe6 29. Nxc8 {Rapid games quality is always worse than normal ones quality. But J. Polgar plays rapid games usually very strong! not that time... Joe Gallagher: 'Tsesarsky: 'Rapid games quality is always worse than normal ones quality. But J. Polgar plays rapid games usually very strong! not that time... ''} Rxc8 30. Rxc8+ Qxc8 31. Nd6 $18 {1-0 (31) Vera Gonzalez Quevedo,R (2544)-Polgar,J (2685) Benidorm 2002 CBM 093 [Tsesarsky,Ilya]} 1-0

