{For some time Black enjoyed considerable success in answering the 5.b6 Benko with 5...a5!?, the idea being to throw in ...Ba6 before White has time to play e4. However the attacking plan shown in this game, plus the sneaky move-order idea invented by Ivan Sokolov and shown in the note to move four, has effectively killed all ...a5 based plan, at least for the time being.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 b5 4. cxb5 (4. Nf3 {Ivan Sokolov has enjoyed considerable success with the subtle move order} g6 (4... e6 {would be a Blumenfeld Gambit.}) 5. cxb5 a6 6. b6 {Now 6...a5 has been recommended by theoreticians as eminent as Igor Stohl because White has blocked his f pawn and cannot play as Zueger does in this game. However} a5 $6 {walks into a vicious attack which has picked up quite a few victims...} 7. Nc3 Ba6 8. e4 Bxf1 9. Kxf1 d6 10. Qa4+ (10. e5 dxe5 {is also good for White, so long as it is followed up with} 11. Qa4+ $1 {, e.g.} Nfd7 12. b7 Ra6 13. Nxe5 Bg7 14. Nc6 Qc7 15. Bf4 Qxb7 16. Nxb8 {1-0, Sokolov-Wiersma, Hoogwoud rapid 1997.}) 10... Nbd7 11. e5 $1 dxe5 12. Nxe5 Qxb6 13. Bg5 $1 Qxb2 {Desperation, but on} (13... Ra7 14. Re1 {is killing.}) 14. Rb1 Qxc3 15. Bxf6 Rd8 16. Qxd7+ {1-0, Sokolov-Van der Weide, Leeuwarden 1998.}) 4... a6 5. b6 a5 $5 6. Nc3 Ba6 {This is the point behind Black's fifth move. If White now plays quietly he risks finishing up in a typical Benko Gambit position, with the rather serious difference that White is without an extra pawn.} 7. f4 $1 {The most dangerous idea, intending to play e4 only after Nf3, then recapture on f1 with a rook and blow Black away with e5. Other moves for White are more commonly seen but tend to play into Black's hands:} ({(i)} 7. Bg5 $5 Qxb6 8. Qc2 g6 9. e4 Bxf1 10. Kxf1 Bg7 {causes Black no problems since} 11. e5 $6 {is well met by} Ng4) ({(ii)} 7. e4 Bxf1 8. Kxf1 d6 {Now White has no time to transpose to the Ivan Sokolov line, since Black has not wasted a tempo on ...g6.However} 9. b7 $5 (9. g3 $6 Qxb6 10. Kg2 g6 11. Nf3 Bg7 12. Nd2 O-O 13. Nc4 Qa6 14. Qe2 a4 15. Re1 Nbd7 16. Bd2 Rfb8 17. Rac1 Nb6 18. Nxb6 Rxb6 19. Qxa6 Raxa6 {is about as good as life gets for Black in a Benko and he won comfortably in Hsu Li Yang-Rogers, Jakarta 1993.}) 9... Ra7 10. f4 {may still cause some problems for Black, who is probably best advised to react actively with} e6 11. dxe6 fxe6 12. Nf3 Qd7 13. Qe2 Qxb7 14. Ng5 Qd7 15. f5 e5 16. Ne6 Nc6 17. Bg5 Nd4 18. Qc4 Nxe4 $1 {with complications satisfactory for Black in Kacheishvili-Shevelev, Groningen Open 1993.}) ({(iii) Trying to hang onto the pawn with} 7. Qb3 {is doomed to failure, e.g.} d6 8. e4 Bxf1 9. Kxf1 Nbd7 10. b7 Rb8 {with ...Qc7 to follow.}) ({(iv)} 7. Nf3 {cannot be compared to the Sokolov line as Black has not played .. .g6. As mentioned earlier White cannot afford quiet play, e.g.} d6 8. g3 g6 9. Bg2 Bg7 10. O-O O-O 11. Re1 Nbd7 12. e4 Ng4 13. Bg5 h6 14. Be3 Qxb6 15. Qb3 Rfb8 16. Qxb6 Rxb6 17. Bc1 Nde5 18. Rd1 Nd3 {with another near perfect Benko for Black, Meier-Rogers, Baden Casino Open1998.}) 7... d6 (7... e6 {allows} 8. e4 Bxf1 9. Kxf1 Qxb6 10. d6 $5) 8. Nf3 Qxb6 (8... Nbd7 {intending to recapture on ...b6 with the knight and, through pressure on d5 keep White from playing e5, fails in its objective for two reasons.} 9. e4 (9. b7 $5 Rb8 10. e4 Bxf1 11. Rxf1 Nb6 (11... Qb6 {is even worse -} 12. e5 dxe5 13. fxe5 Ng4 14. e6 fxe6 15. Ng5 h5 16. dxe6 Rxb7 17. exd7+ Rxd7 18. Qb3 {1-0, Delemarre-Nieuwenhuis, Netherlands tt 1995.}) 12. e5 $1 dxe5 13. fxe5 Nfxd5 14. Ng5 Qd7 (14... f6 $2 15. Ne6 Qd7 16. Nxd5 Nxd5 17. Qxd5 {loses a piece, while}) (14... Nb4 15. Qxd8+ Rxd8 16. Nxf7 {also wins for White.}) 15. Nxf7 Rg8 16. Nxd5 Qxd5 17. Qc2 {and White was well on top in Seirawan-Z.Polgar, Monaco 1994.}) 9... Bxf1 10. Rxf1 Nxb6 11. Qe2 Rb8 (11... Qd7 12. Kf2 g6 13. Rd1 Bg7 14. e5 {worked out well for White in Postl-Beck, Austria tt 1994.}) 12. Be3 g6 13. Rd1 Bg7 14. e5 Ng4 {Atalik-Knoppert, Tilburg 1993, and now} (14... dxe5 15. Nxe5) 15. Bc1 $1 {would have kept White on top according to Knoppert.}) 9. e4 Bxf1 10. Rxf1 g6 (10... Qb4 {doesn't hold up e5 for long after the typical manoeuvre} 11. Qe2 g6 12. Kf2 $1 Bg7 13. Kg1) (10... e6 {while} 11. dxe6 fxe6 12. Ng5 {is also ugly for Black.}) 11. e5 $1 Nfd7 (11... dxe5 12. fxe5 Nfd7 {is even worse after} 13. e6 fxe6 14. Ng5 Bg7 15. Nxe6 Bf6 16. d6 Na6 17. Nd5 Qxd6 18. Nxf6+ Nxf6 19. Qxd6 exd6 20. Ng7+ Ke7 21. Bg5 Rhf8 {Rogers-Claessen, Ostend 1992, and now} 22. Kd2 $1 Rf7 23. Rae1+ Kd7 24. Bxf6 Raf8 25. Ne6 Re8 26. Ng5 Rxe1 27. Kxe1 {would have won cleanly for White.}) 12. e6 fxe6 13. dxe6 (13. Ng5 {is also promising but Zueger has an even more ambitious idea in mind.}) 13... Nf6 14. f5 $1 gxf5 15. Ng5 $1 h6 16. Nf7 Rg8 17. Rxf5 Rxg2 ({Black has little choice as} 17... d5 {walks into} 18. Qa4+ $1) 18. Rxf6 $3 exf6 19. Qh5 {Now Black has no good defense to the discovered checks, since the e7 square is out of bounds.} Rg1+ 20. Kd2 Qb4 21. Kc2 $1 Qg4 22. Nxd6+ Ke7 23. Qxc5 Rg2+ 24. Bd2 $2 ({Finally Polgar's persistent defence enjoys some reward.} 24. Kb1 Qg6+ 25. Nf5+ {would have won on the spot, whereas now White is lucky not to lose.}) 24... Rxd2+ 25. Kxd2 Qf4+ 26. Kc2 Qxh2+ 27. Kb3 Qxd6 28. Nd5+ Kd8 29. Qxd6+ Bxd6 30. Rg1 $1 {By a magnificent accident, the mate threat on g8 means that White regains the piece with a draw looming.} Nd7 31. exd7 Rb8+ 32. Kc4 Kxd7 33. Nxf6+ Ke6 34. Ne4 Bf4 35. Nc5+ Kf5 36. Nd3 Be3 37. Rf1+ Ke4 38. Nc5+ Bxc5 39. Kxc5 Rxb2 40. Rh1 Rxa2 41. Rxh6 a4 1/2-1/2

