{[%evp 0,41,40,40,68,61,78,70,67,27,30,32,55,34,19,6,-4,1,14,-20,-20,-29,17,17,20,-25,10,-12,76,88,93,92,126,116,113,127,146,186,351,336,839,1045,29989,549] It is time to destroy the Najdorf "Through the eyes of Judit"} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bd3 {I was happy to find this nice miniature with this underrated line! I hope the readers will be inspired to take up the line with White.} e5 ({One of Black's most ambitious ways is to play for the Dragon type development with} 6... g6 7. f3 $1 Bg7 8. Be3 {The fabled English attack setup...but with the bishop on d3 :).} Nc6 9. Qd2 Bd7 10. O-O-O {White was better in (Xiong-Carlsen INT 2020)}) ({The Classical recipe with} 6... Nc6 {is met by} 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. b3 $1 {My favorite move in any position :)! I can't help...If you don't know what I am talking about :) !} g6 9. O-O Bg7 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Na4 $14 {with a slight edge for White due to ideas like e5 and more space.}) ({Schveningen players might go for} 6... e6 {which can be met by} 7. f4 $14 {with a slight pull.}) 7. Nde2 {The knight will go to g3 to cause Black some inconvenience.} Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. f4 {Of course! All the white pieces are pointing towards the kingside!} Nc6 {Not the typical Najdorf move. The knight would have been better placed on d7.} ({Anish Giri in his Najdorf course recommends} 9... b5 10. Ng3 Nbd7 {and here I would like to propose} 11. a3 $5 ({Another interesting direction is} 11. Nf5 $5 b4 (11... Bb7 {(Karthik-Verstraeten 2019)} 12. Qe1 $16) 12. Ne2 d5 {One sample line of how things could progress would be} 13. Neg3 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Nc5 $1 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Nxf6+ Qxf6 17. fxe5 Qxe5 {leading to major liquidation...though there is still some messy fight after} 18. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 19. Bf4 Qe7 20. Bd6 Qe3+ 21. Kh1 Qg5 $1 {Not an easy move to make!} 22. Qc1 $1 (22. Bxf8 Bb7 $1 {was the brilliant point with double attack on g2 and f8.}) 22... Qd5 23. Qd1 {with an equal game.}) 11... exf4 12. Bxf4 Ne5 13. Qe1 {Preparing Rd1.} Be6 14. Rd1 Qc7 15. Kh1 {Although the position is very close to 0.00, I think our huge concentration of forces on the kingside should give us some hope of gaining some attacking chances on the kingside.}) 10. f5 {A risky decision..."Only one can live to see the daylight!"} h6 {After this, Judit doesn't give any chance for Black.} ({Black had to find} 10... Nb4 $1 {One sample line could continue} 11. a3 (11. Bg5 d5 $1 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 {with a slightly more comfortable game for Black.}) 11... Nxd3 12. Qxd3 b5 13. Bg5 Bb7 ({A cool way to keep the d5-square under check is} 13... Ng4 $5 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Qf3 Nf6 {Black needn't worry about light square domination anymore!}) 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Nd5 Bxd5 16. Qxd5 Rc8 17. Nc3 Qb6+ 18. Kh1 Rc4 {White's control of the d5-square is offset by the activity via the c-file.}) 11. Nd5 $1 {Taking control of the important d5-square and exchanging a key defensive piece for the black side.} Nb4 (11... Nxd5 12. exd5 Nb8 13. Ng3 $16 {with a big advantage due to the space and due to a possible pawn storm on the kingside thanks to the f5-pawn.}) 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. Ng3 {The knight will go to h5 to wreak havoc.} Bg5 14. Bxg5 Qxg5 15. Bc4 $1 {Judit makes it look so easy! In addition to Black's kingside issues, now he also needs to worry about the d6-pawn.} h5 (15... Rd8 16. a3 Nc6 17. Rf3 {followed by Rd3 next with a winning advantage.}) 16. a3 {Kicking the only active/developed piece in Black's territory.} h4 17. axb4 hxg3 18. Ra3 $1 {A rude awakening! Opening the h-file will be Black's undoing!} Qh4 19. Rxg3 Qxe4 20. f6 $1 {No need to hesitate anymore...the end is near.} g6 21. Bd5 {The black queen can't protect g6 anymore...A powerful demolition of one of Black's most trusted lines against 1.e4.} 1-0

[Event "London Classic 4th"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "2012.12.09"]
[Round "8"]
[White "McShane, Luke J"]
[Black "Polgar, Judit"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "A37"]
[WhiteElo "2713"]
[BlackElo "2705"]
[Annotator "Adhiban,Baskaran"]
[PlyCount "66"]
[EventDate "2012.12.01"]
[EventType "tourn"]
[EventRounds "9"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]
[EventCategory "21"]
