{[%evp 0,85,58,32,58,21,46,46,56,44,178,121,121,5,11,13,13,-6,0,53,53,52,43,-47,7,-23,7,7,-3,-18,3,0,15,6,6,-29,-54,-88,-84,-43,-43,-50,-32,-32,-21,-56,-14,-56,-32,-63,-38,-38,-41,-88,-13,-70,3,-20,-11,-23,8,-48,-36,-41,-13,-41,-41,0,-5,-1,-9,20,98,59,59,50,113,134,142,105,174,197,187,306,236,-166,90,-208]} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4 {This move introduces the MacCutcheon Variation leading to a sharp game.} ({A more solid alternative is} 4... dxe4) 5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 {The old mainline.} ({In recent years the move} 6. Be3 {has become a popular alternative intending} Ne4 7. Qg4 $1 {followed by a3 next in order for white to keep the dark-squared bishop in the game.}) 6... Bxc3 7. bxc3 {White builds up a compact centre and intends to put the forces on the kingside where black is rather weak.} Ne4 8. Qg4 $1 {A key move which forces black to some concessions by either weakening the pawn structure or giving up the right to castle.} Kf8 {A positional move which doesnt weaken the pawn structure.} ({The more popular} 8... g6 {is the main alternative.}) 9. Bd3 {White heads for quick development and does not mind to part with his bishop. In fact, the trade actually helps white to develop quicker and it's proven that white's king remains pretty solid due to the closed French centre.} Nxd2 10. Kxd2 c5 11. h4 {Expanding on the kingside where a rook lift also may be an option.} Nc6 12. Nf3 c4 {The main continuation closing the position further while slightly reducing white's activity for the moment.} 13. Be2 b5 {Black now tries to break the position open with the advance ...b4.} 14. a3 a5 15. Qf4 {White opens the way for the g-pawn in order to break the kingside open.} Qe7 16. h5 {Fixing the pawn on h6 and getting ready for g4-5.} Ra7 {Black on his side is now prepared to launch the b4-break.} 17. g4 b4 18. Qe3 {Defending the king while preparing a doule exchange on b4 if black allows.} bxa3 {This move temporarily wins a pawn for black while also giving him a passed a-pawn. However, it also makes white's king more secure which is now well covered with pawns while there is no longer any way to break open for black.} ({The alternative} 18... bxc3+ 19. Qxc3 {also favours white slightly but now her king is a bit more open for attack later on.}) 19. Ra2 {White needs to take care about the a-pawn to start with and the attack on the kingside has to wait for later.} a4 20. Rha1 Bd7 21. Rxa3 Nb8 {Black now has a passed protected a-pawn but lacks coordination between his pieces and especially between the rooks. Apart from having to keep an eye on the a-pawn White has few worries.} 22. R3a2 $1 {Making the rook on a1 free to move over to the kingside where the attack can continue.} Rb7 23. Rg1 Na6 {Black starts to regroup his passive knight} 24. g5 hxg5 25. Nxg5 {White is now about to create a dangerous attack along the half open g-file.} Nc7 26. Qg3 Ne8 {Black sets for a solid defence while remaining with bad coordination and passive pieces.} 27. f4 {White grabs more space all the time and now black finally decides to do something active.} f5 {This move closes the centre completely which makes it hard to break through for white. However, it also gives black an even more passive position where he will now basically just have to "sit and wait" while hoping that white is not able to find any way forward.} 28. Qg2 Rb6 29. Qh1 {Perhaps intendning Rb1 to take over the b-file.} Qd8 {Black is now ready to counter Rb1 with ...Qb8.} 30. h6 $5 {An interesting pawn sacrifice aiming for an opening of the kingside where white can use her forces to attack.} ({Also possible was} 30. Rb1 {intending} Qb8 31. Rxb6 Qxb6 32. Qa1 {followed by Rb2 when white takes over the b-file having a strong initiative.}) 30... Rxh6 31. Qg2 {Unfortunately black's knight on e8 covers g7, if not 31.Qxh6 would have lead to a nice checkmate.} Bb5 32. Rb1 Bc6 33. Rh1 {Now the kingside is about to be opened.} Ke7 34. Rxh6 gxh6 35. Nh7 Kd7 {Black is now forced to escape with his king giving up the extra pawn.} 36. Nf8+ Kc8 37. Nxe6 Qh4 $6 (37... Qe7 38. Nc5 {and now} Qh4 {would have been a better way for black.}) 38. Ra1 Kb8 $2 (38... a3 $1 {was necessary intending} 39. Rxa3 ({Instead} 39. Rh1 $1 Qe7 40. Nc5 $14 {keeps an advantage for white.}) 39... Rb1 $132 {with counterplay for black.}) 39. Bf3 (39. Rh1 Qe7 40. Nc5 $16) 39... Qe7 $2 {After this there is no longer any return for black.} ({Once again} 39... a3 $1 {was the best opportunity to create active counterplay.}) 40. Nc5 Qh4 41. Ke3 $1 {Using the king to prect after which black's position will collapse very soon.} Rb2 (41... a3 42. Qg1 $1) 42. Qg6 Rxc2 {This loses on the spot but} (42... Kc7 {would not last long either following} 43. Rh1 Qe7 44. Rxh6 Rb6 45. Nxa4 $18) 43. Rb1+ 1-0

