{A very sharp and exiting game where despite the big Elo difference White kept the tension of battle and was very close to victory!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 {As usual, Judit goes for the sharp and double-edged Sicilian Defence.} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 (6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3 {ts considered by theory as a main line in Taimanov Variation.}) 6... Nge7 (6... Nxd4 $5 7. Qxd4 b5 {was probably a safer option.}) 7. Nf3 b5 (7... d5 8. e5 {was possible, with a strange kind of French Defence, but Polgar is a Sicilian player!}) 8. a3 Bb7 9. Bd3 Ng6 {The position is quite unusual and many different options are available, for example} (9... g6 $5) 10. O-O (10. Be3 $5 Qc7 11. Qd2 {would be a bit more precise.}) 10... Qc7 11. Ng5 {And now it all starts! White starts aggression and Black took the challenge. A solid alternative could be} (11. Ne2 $5) 11... f6 $1 (11... h6 12. Nxf7 $1 Kxf7 13. Qh5 Nce7 14. Be3 {with a strong initiative was the point of Ng5.}) 12. Nh3 Bc5+ 13. Kh1 O-O-O $5 {Raising stakes to the maximum! After the calmer} (13... O-O $5 {Black is completely OK according to computer, but from a human point of view it could looks a bit scary after 14.Qh5.}) 14. Qh5 {One of interesting points of this game is that both sides try to be as aggressive as possible! And it make honor specialy to the white player who had a much lower rating and showed himself to be a very brave player.} (14. Bd2 $5) 14... Nce7 15. Qe2 e5 (15... Kb8 $5 16. Be3 Bxe3 17. Qxe3 d5 $1 $132 {was strategically sounder.}) 16. fxe5 (16. f5 $1 Nh4 17. Qg4 g5 18. fxg6 Nhxg6 19. Rxf6 {Black does not have enough compensation for the pawn.}) 16... Nxe5 17. Nf4 {A typical opposite sides castling situation has arisen and the question is: Who will be faster?} h5 $1 18. a4 $1 g5 $1 19. axb5 $1 {No retreating is allowed} (19. Nfd5 $2 Nxd5 20. Nxd5 Bxd5 21. exd5 Ng4 22. g3 Rde8 23. Qf3 Nxh2 24. Kxh2 h4 $19) 19... gxf4 (19... a5 $5 {is probably too "coldblooded" for a human, even for Judit.}) 20. bxa6 Bc6 21. Bxf4 {Continuing in to keep up the pressure} (21. b4 $5 Bd4 22. Ra3 {was quite a strong option actually.}) 21... Rdg8 ({The computer indicates} 21... N7g6 $1 22. Bg3 h4 23. Be1 h3 24. g3 f5 25. a7 Bxa7 26. Ba6+ Kb8 27. Nd5 Bxd5 28. exd5 Ng4 $1 {with crazy and balanced position.}) 22. Bc4 {Again an attacking move!} ({Both the simple} 22. h3 $5 {and more the forcing}) (22. Nb5 $5 Bxb5 23. Bxb5 $16 {secured an advantage for White.}) 22... Rg4 {Black is preparing counterattacking ..Rxf4 idea in some variations.} 23. Nd5 (23. Bd5 $5) 23... Bxd5 24. Bxd5 Nxd5 25. exd5 {The position looks quite chaotic, but it is dynamically equal acoording to engines. The next calm moves of the black king are impressive!} Kb8 $1 26. Rf3 (26. b4 $1 Ba7 27. c4 Rhg8 28. c5 Rxg2 29. Qb5+ Ka8 {kept balance.}) 26... Ka8 $1 27. h3 Rhg8 {Objectively this is mistake, as Black was already better after some defensive moves, but this move is so much in the spirit of all this game (no retreating!) that this hardly deserves serious criticism.} (27... d6 $1 28. Bxe5 fxe5 29. Raf1 Rg7 $17) 28. d6 $1 {From now on White plays the best moves and comes just one move short of a sensational victory!} Qc6 {Finally oversteping the line. After the objectively correct} (28... Qxd6 29. hxg4 ({Or} 29. Qe4+ Qc6 30. Qxc6+ dxc6 31. hxg4 Nxf3 32. gxf3 hxg4 33. Kg2 $1 gxf3+ 34. Kxf3 Ka7 {looks holdable for black.}) 29... hxg4 30. Qe4+ Qc6 31. Qxc6+ Nxc6 32. Rc3 Rh8+ 33. Bh2 Bd4 $1 34. Rd3 Bf2 35. g3 Ne5 $1 36. Kg2 Nxd3 37. cxd3 Ba7 {Black has chances to survive.}) 29. hxg4 $1 hxg4 30. Rc3 $1 Rh8+ 31. Bh2 Qxd6 {The white king still in trouble, but he finds a solution} 32. Qe4+ $1 Nc6 33. g3 $4 {The culmination of a hard tactical battle! Just one more strong move was required} (33. Rh3 $1 {and White would win this game! And it would be fully deserved, but, at the same time, Black also deserved luck for her brave play.}) 33... Rxh2+ $1 {A beautiful mating combination, which Judit was exellent at finding from early childhood. This was a uite painful for White, but such is chess life.} 34. Kxh2 Qd2+ $1 {And White resigns due to mate in two} 35. Kh1 (35. Qg2 Qh6+ 36. Qh3 Qxh3#) 35... Qh6+ 36. Kg2 Qh3# 0-1

