{[%evp 0,65,27,11,55,40,53,20,82,58,79,-22,-22,-22,-15,-25,-18,-38,-3,8,6,-10,-22,-42,-30,-152,-192,-192,-192,-215,-139,-157,-149,-111,-64,-87,-86,-102,-90,-104,-31,-16,67,-48,-46,-35,49,-11,9,-54,117,126,153,186,234,206,245,222,222,222,215,280,325,355,412,492,837,900]} 1. e4 {A special encounter between the two stars, especially if one has heard about their debate on the genders from 2015.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Nce2 {The second most topical variation.} (5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 {has been tried more often.}) 5... c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. f4 cxd4 $6 {Releasing the tension was a bit early.} (7... Qb6 {is the very mainline.}) 8. cxd4 f5 {Closing the position entirely allows White time to develop her pieces just as she prefers.} (8... Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Qb6 10. Nf3 O-O 11. h4 $1 {Making Rh3 possible.} f6 12. a3 Bxd2+ 13. Qxd2 fxe5 14. fxe5 Na5 {Rother - Riefner, Bayern 2009} 15. Qc3 $16) (8... f6 9. Nf3 fxe5 10. fxe5 Bb4+ 11. Bd2 O-O 12. Bxb4 Nxb4 13. Qd2 a5 {happened in Shabalov - Ni,Hua, Beijing (blitz) 2008.} 14. a3 Nc6 15. Nf4 Nb6 16. h4 $1 $16 {and Ng5 will be a painful threat.}) 9. Nf3 Nb6 10. h3 $5 {A quite committing decision, showing clearly White's intention (pushing g2-g4).} (10. Kf2 Bd7 11. h3 Be7 12. g4 O-O 13. Ng3 Rc8 14. Rh2 a5 15. Kg1 a4 16. Rg2 Be8 17. gxf5 exf5 18. Kh1 Qd7 {and a draw was agreed in Zakharov - Makarjev, Protvino 1992. However, White could have played on with} 19. h4 Kh8 20. h5 Qe6 21. Rh2 $36) 10... Be7 11. g4 $5 {A risky advance, because the future of the white king has not been solved.} (11. Kf2 {was more cautious and after} O-O 12. g4 {it could have led to a similar game like above.}) 11... Bh4+ 12. Nxh4 $6 ({Funnily dancing with the king} 12. Kd2 Nc4+ 13. Kc2 Be7 14. b3 Na3+ 15. Kd2 Bd7 16. Ke3 Rc8 17. Kf2 $13 {in such an unique way was more advisable.}) 12... Qxh4+ 13. Kd2 Qf2 (13... Nc4+ {This check makes much sense, if one sees the tactical potential few moves later.} 14. Kc2 (14. Kc3 b5 15. b3 Qf2 $1 16. Rg1 (16. bxc4 Qf3+) 16... b4+ 17. Kc2 Na3+ 18. Bxa3 bxa3 19. Qd2 Ba6 20. Rg2 Qf3 21. Qc3 Rc8 $1 22. Qxf3 Nxd4+ 23. Kd1 Nxf3 $17) 14... fxg4 15. a3 $1 ({On} 15. b3 Nb4+ 16. Kb1 Qh5 $3 {would be the point of the whole idea!} (16... Nb6 $2 17. a3 Nc6 18. Rg1 $1 gxh3 19. Rh1 Bd7 20. Nc3 O-O-O 21. Rxh3 Qe7 22. a4 Be8 23. Ba3 $16) 17. bxc4 (17. Ng3 Qg6+ 18. f5 exf5 19. Bf4 Ne3 $1 {Another important tactical blow.} 20. Bb5+ (20. Bxe3 f4+) 20... Bd7 21. Bxd7+ Kxd7 22. Qd2 Nec2 23. a3 a5 24. axb4 Nxa1 25. Qd3 Rhc8 (25... axb4 $2 26. Rc1 Ra5 27. Nxf5 $44) 26. Kxa1 axb4+ 27. Kb2 Qb6 $1 $19) 17... Qg6+ 18. Kb2 Nd3+ 19. Kc3 Nf2 20. Qc2 (20. Qe1 $2 {runs into} Qd3+ 21. Kb2 Nd1+ {which proves perfectly how tricky piece the knight is!}) 20... Nxh1 21. f5 (21. Be3 gxh3 22. Bxh3 Ng3 {and the cavalier successfully escapes.}) 21... Qxf5 22. Qxf5 exf5 23. Be3 g5 24. Bxg5 Nf2 $17) 15... Bd7 16. b3 Nb6 17. Bd2 O-O 18. Kb2 a5 19. a4 $13) 14. b3 $2 {Proceeding in her known, adventurous style.} ({The engines consider the fire-fighting attempt with} 14. Qb3 Nxd4 15. Qg3 $1 {However, this is far from the spirit of Judit's attitude.} Qxg3 16. Nxg3 O-O 17. Bd3 $44 {hoping that the dark squares provide White reasonable compensation.}) 14... Nxd4 15. Kc3 Qf3+ $2 {Falling into the trap by becoming too greedy.} (15... Nf3 $1 {would have kept a huge advantage.} 16. Ba3 d4+ 17. Kb2 Nd5 $19) 16. Kb2 Qxh1 17. Nxd4 Qh2+ 18. Kb1 O-O 19. a4 $1 a5 $2 (19... fxg4 20. Ra2 Qh1 21. hxg4 Rxf4 {would have posed more tasks.} 22. Bxf4 Qe4+ 23. Bd3 Qxf4 24. a5 Nd7 25. Nxe6 Qxe5 26. Re2 Qd6 27. Bxh7+ $3 {A powerful continuation that destroys Black's position quickly.} Kxh7 28. Ng5+ Kg8 {Here White needs to find two strong moves still:} 29. Re6 $1 {An essential intermediation, everything else just loses.} Qc5 30. Qf3 Nf6 31. Re8+ $1 $18 {The final finess that crowns the attacking process.}) 20. Ra2 {All of a sudden, the dreadful rook appears from the second rank and becomes an extremely useful piece.} Qg3 21. Rg2 Qc3 22. gxf5 exf5 23. Bb2 Qc7 24. e6 Rf6 25. Nxf5 $1 {The overwhelming part has arrived.} Rg6 (25... Rxf5 26. Qd4 $18) 26. Be5 Qd8 27. Nxg7 d4 28. f5 {The black queenside is just packed.} Rxg2 29. Bxg2 Qg5 30. Qh5 Qxh5 31. Nxh5 Ra6 32. Bxd4 Na8 33. Bd5 1-0

