{[%evp 0,47,19,38,40,41,41,32,28,27,46,29,90,90,77,58,63,37,46,32,54,58,46,69,50,0,32,33,43,33,43,43,57,57,58,79,76,95,95,73,242,37,154,381,408,419,423,430,434,460] I don't remember already why and how Jan and Maria Macieja, parents of GM Bartek Macieja and the managers of our Polonia Warsaw chess club, came to the idea of inviting Judit Polgar to our team for the 2002 European Club Cup. Judit made an excellent impression when making a debut in our team at the Polish Supercup, where she convidently defeated (2-0) legendary GM Wlodzimierz Schmidt. And then Greece and Judit's excellent performance (5.5/7 on Board 2). It is not her fault that our team couldn't earn more than a bronze medal that year. Judit won a brilliant game in the decisive round against GM Alexander Motylev; it was annotated in detail by GM Dorian Rogozenco for CBM 92. She started her excellent streak with a victory in the first round against her compatriot GM Jozsef Horvath - the game I present to your attention.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Bb4 9. f3 {This system started gaining popularity in those times. Nowadays it's probably White's main weapon against the Paulsen.} d5 (9... Ne5 {is considered safer today.}) 10. a3 $1 Bxc3 (10... Ba5 11. Nb3 $1 {, and Black should take on c3 anyway.}) 11. Qxc3 dxe4 12. fxe4 (12. Nxc6 {is a good alternative, e.g.} Qxc6 13. Qxc6+ (13. Qe5 $5 O-O 14. Bg5 Nd7 15. Qxe4 Qxe4 16. fxe4 e5 17. Bc4 h6 18. Bh4 g5 19. Bg3 $16 {1-0 (35) Caspi,I (2438)-Garbea,B (2270) Eforie Nord 2010}) 13... bxc6 14. fxe4 Nxe4 15. Bd3 {, and White keeps strong pressure despite the exchange of queens:} Nf6 16. Bd4 O-O 17. Rhf1 Nd5 18. c4 Ne7 19. Bc5 Re8 20. Be4 {1-0 (45) Reznicek,T (2265)-Vujovic,M (2310) Imperia 1996}) 12... Nxe4 {At first glance, this capture doesn't look risky but in fact, it was better to continue development:} (12... O-O 13. Nxc6 Nxe4 $1 (13... bxc6 14. Bh6 $5 $16) 14. Qe5 Qxc6 15. Bd3 f5 {1/2 (37) Brilej,J (2177)-Ivanussa,B (2097) Slovenia 2019} 16. Bd4 Rf7 17. Rhe1 $36) ({or, even safer,} 12... Bd7 13. Nf3 (13. Bd3 $142 O-O 14. h3 $14) 13... Rc8 14. Bd3 O-O 15. h3 e5 16. Kb1 b5 17. Qc5 Be6 18. Ng5 Rfe8 $11 {1/2 (30) Landenbergue,C (2424)-Kelecevic,N (2341) Switzerland 2007}) 13. Qd3 $1 f5 {It turns out that Black's knight has no comfortable retreat:} (13... Nf6 14. Nxc6 bxc6 15. Bc5 {, and Black's king will get under attack even if it manages to castle, e.g.} Nd5 16. c4 Ne7 (16... Qf4+ 17. Rd2 $18 {1-0 (19) Brzeski,M (2356)-Kuciel,W (2162) Polanica Zdroj 2007}) 17. h4 $5 O-O 18. h5 h6 19. g4 $40) (13... Nc5 {(relatively best)} 14. Qc4 Qe5 (14... Ne5 15. Qc3 $16 {1-0 (43) Charmeteau,S (2346)-Sabirov,R (2125) PlayChess INT 2021}) (14... Nd7 15. Nxc6 Qxc6 16. Qg4 $16 {1-0 (48) Kramnik V (2809)-Ljubojevic Lj (2570) Monaco (rapid) 2003 2003 [Kramnik,V ]}) 15. Qc3 (15. Bg1 $5 $16) 15... Nd7 16. g3 Ne7 (16... Qe4 17. Nxc6 Qxh1 18. Qxg7 Rf8 19. Ne5 $18 {1-0 (32) Arizmendi Martinez,J (2534)-Ivanov,J (2391) Andorra 2004}) 17. Bg2 (17. Bf4 $2 {1-0 (30) Cheparinov,I (2567)-Ivanov,J (2391) Ortigueira 2004} Nd5 $1 18. Bxe5 Nxc3 19. Bxg7 Rg8 20. bxc3 Rxg7 $11) 17... O-O 18. Rhf1 $16 {[%csl Ra8,Rc8]}) 14. Qc4 {[%mdl 128]} (14. Nxc6 $5 bxc6 15. g4 $40) 14... e5 {Black renounces castling as} (14... O-O 15. Nxf5 $1 Rxf5 16. Qxe4 {is clearly favorable for White}) ({but leaving his king in the centre is hardly a better option.} 14... Ke7 {was equally poor, e.g.} 15. Bd3 Nf6 16. Rhe1 g6 17. Bg5 e5 18. Nxc6+ bxc6 19. Qc5+ Kf7 20. Rxe5 $18 {1-0 (24) Arizmendi Martinez,J (2516)-Valensi,B (2154) Istanbul 2003}) 15. Nxc6 Qxc6 (15... bxc6 16. Be2 (16. g4 $5)) 16. Rd5 $1 Qe6 (16... Qxc4 17. Bxc4 $18 {[%cal Rd5e5]}) 17. Ra5 $1 Nd6 {Black still can't castle:} (17... O-O 18. Rxe5 $18) 18. Qc3 O-O {Finally! But White's development advantage and bishop pair must bring her an easy victory.} 19. Rxe5 Qa2 $6 {This desperate attempt suddenly (almost) succeeds.} (19... Qf6 20. Qb3+ Kh8 (20... Qf7 21. Qxf7+ Rxf7 22. Bc5) 21. Bd4 {etc. with full domination.}) 20. b3 $2 {Judit was probably confused by her opponent's "crazy" move.} (20. Qc7 $1 {was quite possible. After} Be6 21. Qxd6 Qa1+ 22. Kd2 Rad8 {White has} 23. Bd3 $1 (23. Rxe6 Rxd6+ 24. Rxd6 {is not bad either}) 23... Qxh1 24. Qxe6+ $18) 20... Qxa3+ $2 (20... Bd7 $1 {[%cal Ya8c8] was Black's last chance. White has no clear win and must play Qc3-b2 at once or later. Of course, she keeps an advantage but now needs a lot of effort to convert it.}) 21. Kb1 {[%cal Re3c5,Re3c1] Black's queen is simply trapped now.} Nb5 ({Or} 21... Ne4 22. Rxe4 fxe4 23. Bc5 $18) 22. Qe1 Rd8 23. Bc4+ Kh8 24. Bd2 $1 {[%cal Re5e8,Rd2b4] Not blundering mate (24...Rd1+) and depriving Black of any chances.} (24. Bd2 Qf8 25. Bb4 Nd6 26. Bxd6 Qxd6 27. Re8+ $18) 1-0

