{[%evp 0,101,28,17,37,36,41,27,27,28,28,10,19,32,21,-13,9,-15,-6,3,5,-9,-8,-28,-24,-32,-34,-34,-10,-10,-10,-10,-14,-58,0,-23,11,11,141,143,135,131,159,162,142,56,129,113,86,74,116,57,113,117,149,61,67,53,61,72,72,50,71,76,105,72,129,110,110,109,113,-34,147,146,165,88,200,200,178,185,183,270,267,263,491,485,555,501,469,407,407,415,427,376,623,657,665,672,604,610,810,840,830,972]} {The French Winawer System. Until nowadays, there’s plenty of life left in the variations with h2-h4.  So for this nominee, I decided to investigate two brilliant victories of Judit Polgar, played back in the distant 90s. These (rapid) games have not been commented on before, but I think they deserve closer attention for any player using this system with white pieces.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. h4 $5 {This creative option has become popular since the game Short-Korchnoi, Wijk aan Zee 1987. It allows White to avoid the highly theoretical lines following 7.g4 c7 or 7...0-0. White first increases his resources on the kingside.} Qc7 $5 {The most solid answer. Black temporarily prevents 8.g4? (8...cxd4!) and prepares the fundamental scheme ...b7-b6 and ...a6.} ({Straightaway} 7... b6 {see the next game, Polgar-Bareev, Moscow 1996}) (7... Qa5 $5 {would be Black’s ultra-dynamic move in the main Winawer.}) 8. h5 $5 (8. Rh3 Nbc6 $5 {scores nicely for Black}) ({and, for example} 8. Nf3 b6 $5 {leads us back to old classical lines of the Winawer.}) 8... h6 (8... cxd4 {Rustemov’s sharp line is highly unclear:} 9. cxd4 Qc3+ 10. Bd2 Qxd4 11. Nf3 Qe4+ 12. Be2 Nf5 $13 {by transposition this is also a possible sub-line after 7...a5.}) 9. Nf3 {It should be noted that in both games Judit preferred to develop the minor pieces in the first place. However, modern theory (computer science) already considers that this move is not flexible enough.} (9. Rb1 $5 {after the Candidates game, Vachier-Lagrave-Nepomniachtchi, Yekaterinburg 2020, this became White’s most popular line in the Winawer in 2020!}) 9... Bd7 (9... b6 {is the main alternative.}) 10. Bd3 {Again, pieces first.} (10. a4 {, preventing ...a4 seems more logical option.}) 10... Ba4 {In the complex Winawer system it is always difficult to predetermine which order is more precise.} ({Black could blockade the centre with} 10... c4 $5 {and then 11...a4.}) 11. dxc5 $1 {An absolutely correct practical solution. White needs to open the game as soon as possible.} Nd7 (11... Qxc5 12. Rh4 $2 (12. Bd2 $142 $14) 12... Qxc3+ 13. Bd2 Qxd3 $15 {½-½ (69) Minakov,Y-Pyshkin,A corr 1995}) 12. Rh4 $1 {The black bishop becomes vulnerable on a4.} Bc6 (12... Nxc5 $2 13. Rxa4 $18 {1-0 (27) Tupy,M (2235)-Kubala,M (2265) Plzen 1997}) 13. Bf4 (13. Rg4 $5 $146 {a possible improvement.}) 13... O-O-O {A very responsible decision of GM Nikolic (a great expert of the French Defence). More often the black king feels safer in the centre.} (13... Nxc5 $5 14. Rg4 $13 {1-0 (34) Behling,H-MacDonald,D ICCF corr 2000}) 14. Rg4 Rdg8 {[%cal Gg7g5]} 15. Rb1 Nxc5 (15... g5 $5) 16. Be3 Nd7 $2 {It's hard to believe, but this is a decisive mistake. The knight's return is too slow, so the black fortress collapses after Polgar's vigorous play.} (16... Ne4 $5) 17. Rgb4 $1 $40 {Intending 18.xa7.} Kb8 18. Nd4 $1 Ka8 19. Nxc6 Nxc6 20. Rxb7 $1 {After this exchange combination, the white queen will dominate the black pieces for the rest of the game.} Qxb7 21. Rxb7 Kxb7 22. Qb1+ (22. Bb5 $5) 22... Ka8 23. Bb5 Rc8 24. f4 Rc7 25. Qb3 Rhc8 26. Qa4 Ncb8 27. Bd3 Nc5 28. Bxc5 Rxc5 29. c4 $1 Nc6 30. cxd5 exd5 31. Kf2 Rc7 32. g4 Rb7 33. g5 Rb6 34. f5 hxg5 35. e6 (35. f6 $5 $18) 35... fxe6 36. fxe6 Kb7 37. Kg3 (37. Qg4 $18) 37... Ne7 38. Qd4 Rc7 39. Qe5 a6 40. Kg4 Ng8 41. Kxg5 Nh6 42. Qxd5+ Ka7 43. c4 Re7 44. Bf5 Nxf5 45. Kxf5 Rc7 46. Ke5 Rbc6 47. Qxc6 $1 {The simplest.} Rxc6 48. e7 Rc8 49. Ke6 Kb7 50. Kf7 Rc7 51. Kf8 {Judit play with initiative is impressive. Starting from the 17th move, Black did not have a single chance to save the game!} 1-0

[Event "Moscow PCA-GP Kremlin Stars"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1996.04.27"]
[Round "1.1"]
[White "Polgar, Judit"]
[Black "Bareev, Evgeny"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C18"]
[WhiteElo "2675"]
[BlackElo "2645"]
[Annotator "Moskalenko,Viktor"]
[PlyCount "55"]
[EventDate "1996.04.??"]
