{[%evp 0,55,25,29,38,32,32,33,33,26,26,28,25,25,25,-4,25,-3,-3,-16,-16,-17,-3,-40,-46,-52,-35,-45,-37,-37,0,0,0,25,68,99,115,128,263,266,266,134,165,191,167,155,161,44,37,37,35,35,35,35,49,20,29997,29998]} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. h4 $5 {This advance may look strange - why isn’t White developing his pieces? The idea of h2-h4 is to gain space on the kingside - the area where White is stronger (thanks to the e5-pawn) and where normally he should play.} b6 $6 {This old set-up considered as premature here, since it allows White the attack - g4!} ({So,} 7... Qc7 $5 {(as in the previous game, Polgar-Nikolic) or 7...a5!? are preferible options for Black.}) 8. h5 h6 9. Qg4 $1 {Black is too passive and he scores poorly here. This game is good illustration of how White should progress.} Kf8 10. Nf3 {Once again, developing the minor pieces first.} ({After} 10. a4 $5 {a nice miniature followed:} Qc7 11. Rh3 $1 {a typical rook maneuver} Ba6 12. Rf3 (12. Bb5 $5) 12... Kg8 13. Qf4 Nec6 14. Bb5 $1 $36 Rh7 15. Ne2 Na5 $2 16. Be8 $1 {1-0 Nepomniachtchi,I (2626)-Shimanov,A (2535) St Petersburg 2009}) 10... Qc7 (10... Ba6 {has an uncompromising score of 6:4 with no draws!}) 11. Bd3 (11. a4 $5) 11... cxd4 12. cxd4 {Other recommended options:} (12. Qxd4 $5 $146) (12. O-O $5 {} Qxc3 $4 13. Bd2 Qc7 14. Bb4 $18 {1-0 (35) De Silva,L (2085)-Sviridova,V (2144) Lichess.org INT 2021}) 12... Qc3+ 13. Ke2 Ba6 $5 (13... Qxa1 {accepting material looks very dangerous:} 14. Bxh6 Qxh1 15. Qxg7+ Ke8 16. Qxh8+ Kd7 {and now} 17. Be3 $5 {} Nbc6 18. h6 Bb7 $2 19. Qg7 $16 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Gh6h7]}) 14. Ra2 Qc6 $2 {Black prepares 15...c4, but it is actually a wrong (too slow) plan.} (14... Kg8 15. Bd2 $13 {½-½ (25) Rivera Kuzawka,D (2365)-Del Rio de Angelis,S (2395) Spain 1998}) 15. a4 $1 {[%cal Gc1a3] An important attacking resource. White now retains the initiative on the entire board.} Bc4 16. Ra1 Nd7 17. Ba3 $1 Rc8 18. Nd2 b5 19. axb5 Bxb5 20. Rhc1 $5 $18 {[%csl Rf8][%cal Gc2c4] Opening the position would be fatal for the black king.} a6 21. c4 dxc4 22. Nxc4 Qd5 23. Nb2 $6 {An inaccuracy (normal for each rapid game).} ({After} 23. Ke1 $5 $18) ({or} 23. Ne3 $5 Bxd3+ 24. Kd2 $1 $18 {White wins immediately.}) 23... Rxc1 24. Rxc1 Bxd3+ 25. Nxd3 Qa2+ 26. Bb2 Nb6 27. Qh4 Nbd5 $4 {A good move for any stable position, however, Bareev missed the opponent's tactics.} ({After} 27... Qd5 $8 {Black could still continue the fight.}) 28. Rc8+ $1 {Knockout!} (28. Rc8+ Nxc8 29. Qd8#) 1-0

