{[%evp 0,82,30,28,30,-10,-3,-3,25,30,25,18,23,23,23,-3,-3,6,2,9,-28,-16,-15,-6,-16,2,15,24,26,1,22,-14,-4,-33,-2,-31,-55,-9,-9,-17,-10,0,-3,-3,-2,-3,-10,-8,-2,-5,-11,-6,-6,-21,-22,-85,-151,-224,-194,-333,-374,-376,-391,-408,-91,-409,-32,-23,-24,-322,-294,-332,-293,-302,-302,-296,-296,-296,-296,-274,-296,-296,-279,-279,-284] I played a rapid match against Judit Polgár back in 2010 and lost it 2-6. Some of her wins have already been published, but this one is lesser known. This game was played when the score was 3.5-0.5 in Judit Polgár's favour.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. Nc3 Bb7 5. Bg5 Bb4 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 g5 {Black is ready to weaken her kingside. In return she gains control over the e4-square. It is important that black can still choose where to hide her king. The monarch can also stay in the centre or move to the queenside.} 8. Bg3 Ne4 9. Qc2 d6 10. Bd3 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Nxg3 12. fxg3 {The doubled pawns are not weak there, although the c4 could become an issue in the endgame. Black would like to attack along the f-file.} Nd7 13. O-O Qe7 14. Qa4 $5 {White prevents the long castle with this unusual move. It costs some time, but it does not matter that much, as the position is relatively closed. I had known this motif from an earlier game. White has a plenty of decent alternatives but probably no way to an edge. The unforced nature of the position makes the opening preparation less important and puts more emphasis on the subsequent stages of the game.} ({After} 14. Be4 {, black can safely hide her king on the kingside:} Bxe4 (14... O-O-O $6 15. Nd2 $14 {leaves black's king exposed. White might play a2-a4, when a7-a5 might invite a pawn sacrifice c4-c5 with a dangerous attack.}) 15. Qxe4 O-O $11 {. Without the bishops there will never be mate on h7.}) ({It makes sense to insert} 14. a4 a5 {at some point, but even here} 15. Be4 Bxe4 16. Qxe4 O-O {can hardly bring white more than equality.}) ({White also has many useful semi-waiting moves like} 14. Rab1 {, waiting for black showing her intentions. Long castling is good in most of such cases.}) 14... f5 ({After} 14... O-O {white could play} 15. e4 {with some initiative. Creating a battery with d3-b1, a4-c2 is always an option, but it pays of to rule out f7-f5 first. The e4 could advance to e5 later on. If black plays e6-e5, white can transfer the knight to e3 and ideally to f5. That said, such a plan costs a lot of time and black can regroup before the knight reaches f5.} {A possible scenario is} e5 16. Rae1 Nf6 17. Qd1 (17. h3 Nh5 $5 18. Kh2 g4 {makes white take on g4 with unclear play:} 19. hxg4 Nf6 20. Nh4 Nxg4+ 21. Kg1 Bc8) 17... c5 $14 {, when a knight transfer to f5 could be met with c8 or with taking on d4. White's position is easier to play, though.}) 15. c5 {White obviously needs to open the position before black consolidates, but this is not the best way to achieve that.} ({Critical was} 15. Rae1 $1 O-O 16. e4 f4 17. e5 $1 {, when it is black who has to be more careful about maintaining the balance:} fxg3 18. hxg3 Bxf3 $1 {A surprising exchange in my eyes, but it allows black to get rid of the e5.} ({The immediate exchange} 18... dxe5 {would have left white with a stable advantage after} 19. Nxe5 Rxf1+ 20. Bxf1 Nxe5 21. Rxe5 Rf8 22. Qc2 $16 {. Black is left with numerous kingside weaknesses and cannot compensate for them by invading along the f-file.}) 19. gxf3 (19. Rxf3 Rxf3 20. gxf3 dxe5 21. Qc6 Rf8 $13) 19... dxe5 20. Qc6 Rad8 $1 21. Qxc7 exd4 22. cxd4 Qf6 {with mutual chances.}) (15. g4 $5 O-O $1 16. gxf5 exf5 17. Rae1 Be4 18. Bxe4 fxe4 19. Nd2 Nf6 $11) 15... bxc5 16. g4 O-O $1 ({Black's king would be very unsafe after} 16... fxg4 $6 17. Bg6+ Kd8 18. Nd2 {.}) 17. gxf5 Nb6 $1 {An important intermediate move.} ({The other exchange operation} 17... exf5 18. Qb3+ Kg7 19. Qxb7 Qxe3+ 20. Kh1 Qxd3 21. Qxc7 Rf7 22. Qxd6 $14 {would have given white an edge.}) 18. Qb3 c4 $1 {Now white has to part with his bishop.} 19. Bxc4 Nxc4 20. Qxc4 Bd5 $1 {Black would like to recapture on f5 with a pawn.} 21. f6 $1 ({I was reluctant to live with a backward e-pawn after} 21. Qd3 exf5 22. Nd2 Rae8 23. Rae1 $15 {.}) ({It was also possible to try} 21. Qb5 {, when} c6 $6 {again allows} 22. f6 $1 $11 {.}) 21... Rxf6 ({White should equalize after} 21... Bxc4 22. fxe7 Rfe8 23. Rfb1 Rxe7 24. Nd2) 22. Qd3 (22. Qe2 $11 {was a more circumspect alternative. The advance} e5 {only leads to equality after} 23. dxe5 dxe5 24. Rad1 {.}) 22... Bxf3 $1 {This surprising exchange gives black slight initiative. White wanted to play e3-e4 and possibly f3-d2, limiting black's bishop.} (22... Raf8 23. e4 Bc6 24. Nd2 $11) 23. Rxf3 Rxf3 24. gxf3 Qf6 25. Rb1 $6 ({There was nothing wrong with} 25. Kg2 Rf8 26. Rf1 {, but I wanted to play actively.}) 25... Rf8 (25... Qxf3 $4 26. Qg6+ $18) 26. Rb7 Rf7 27. Rxa7 $2 {This was overly optimistic, I also miscalculated.} (27. Qe2 {White should hold without problems after} Qxf3 28. Qxf3 Rxf3 29. Rxa7 $11 {.}) 27... Qxf3 $1 {Black's queen protects the a8-square now. Moreover, there is no perpetual and white's king turns out to be very weak.} 28. Qg6+ Rg7 $1 29. Qxe6+ ({It is telling that computer gives} 29. Qc2 Qxe3+ 30. Qf2 {as white's best option. It is very bad after most of black's moves and just lost after} Qc1+ 31. Qf1 (31. Kg2 Rf7 $1 32. Qe2 Qxc3 $19) 31... Qxc3 32. Ra8+ Kh7 33. Qb1+ Rg6 {, when white has no compensation for the missing pawns and even tricks do not work:} 34. Rg8 $1 Qxd4+ $1 35. Kg2 Qd5+ 36. Kg1 Kxg8 37. Qxg6+ Kf8 $19) 29... Kh7 $1 $19 {Black is threatening 30...f7 as well as 30...g4 with a subsequent 31...g3. There is no adequate defence.} 30. Qc4 Qxe3+ 31. Kg2 Qe4+ ({GM Marek Vokac demonstrated a quicker way to win, namely} 31... Qd2+ 32. Kg1 d5 $1 33. Qxd5 Re7 34. Qf5+ Kg7 $19 {.}) 32. Kg1 d5 $2 {We have to keep in mind that this was just a rapid game after all.} ({It was not late for} 32... Qe3+ {, yet.}) 33. Qf1 $1 g4 34. Rb7 $2 ({White could have hold after} 34. Qf2 $1 $11 {, neutralizing the threat of g4-g3.}) 34... g3 $1 35. Qb1 gxh2+ 36. Kxh2 Rg2+ 37. Kh3 Rc2 $1 38. Rb2 ({After} 38. Rxc7+ Kg6 39. Rc6+ Kh5 40. Qd1+ Re2 $19 {white has no more checks, whereas black is threatening five(!) mates in 1.}) 38... Qg2+ 39. Kh4 Qe4+ {There were other ways as well, but this one is very convincing.} ({Computer suggests} 39... Qh2+ 40. Kg4 h5+ 41. Kg5 Qg3+ 42. Kf6 {, but} (42. Kxh5 Qg6+ 43. Kh4 Rh2+ $1 44. Rxh2 Qxb1 {is admittedly a nice line.}) 42... Qg6+ 43. Ke7 Rxb2 44. Qxb2 h4 $19 {does not really look like an obvious win. That said, white's king is placed so poorly that black could easily promote her pawn. With the kings on the same or neighbouring lines, white's checks can often be met with counterchecks and a decisive queen exchange.}) 40. Kh3 Rxc3+ 41. Kh2 Rd3 $1 {This makes a nice echo to 37...c2.} 42. Rb3 Rd2+ 43. Kg3 Rg2+ {This is safe and good enough.} ({Quicker was} 43... Rc2 $1 {(The rook prevents the queen swap for the third time, but this time the c3 is no longer there.)} 44. Rb2 Qd3+ $1 45. Kh4 Qxd4+ {, as demonstrated by GM Vokac in his notes for SACHinfo.}) 44. Kh3 Qxb1 45. Rxb1 Rxa2 46. Rc1 {White had many options, but no realistic chance.} (46. Kg4 Ra4 47. Rd1 Kg6 $19) (46. Rb5 c6 47. Rc5 Ra6 $5 {is easily winning for black, as white's pieces cannot protect the d4 and block the h-pawn at the same time.}) 46... Ra4 47. Rxc7+ ({White would like to make his rook invisible after} 47. Kg4 Rxd4+ 48. Kh5 Rc4 $19 {, as there is no stalemate.}) 47... Kg6 48. Kg3 Rxd4 49. Rd7 Kf5 50. Rd8 h5 51. Rf8+ Ke4 52. Rh8 Rd3+ 53. Kg2 Re3 {Black wins safely by cutting off white's king.} 54. Rxh5 d4 55. Kf2 Kd3 $1 56. Ra5 {The position would have been drawn if all the other pieces were shifted by one file to the kingside, but here white's rook has too little space for side checks, as the pawn is too close. Needless to say, Judit Polgár had known this when giving away her h-pawn.} Re8 57. Ra3+ Kc2 58. Ra2+ Kb3 59. Ra7 {Here I lost on time, but it did not matter. The position is easily winning for black, who can push her pawn to d2 and then liberate the king from d1 if needed. The details can be found in any endgame manual. With this win Judit Polgár also won the whole match long before its end.} 0-1

