08}) 18. Na2 Bd5 19. Nb4 Nc6 {results in a draw by liquidation -} 20. Nxc6+ Bxc6 21. Rxc4 Ba4 $1 22. Rb4 (22. Bd3 Rxb2 23. O-O Bb5 24. Rc3 Bxd3 25. Rxd3 Ra2 $11) 22... Rxb4 23. axb4 Bb5 24. f3 Ra1+ {½, Karpov,A (2651)-Carlsen,M (2786) Cap d'Agde 2008}) (9. Nf3 Qa5+ 10. Bd2 (10. Nd2 Nxd2 ({Playing it safe, but Black has good compensation after the more aggressive} 10... Nd4 $5 11. Qd3 e5 12. b4 Qa4 $44) 11. Bxd2 Qxc5 12. e3 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Ne5 14. Bb3 Bd7 15. O-O Qxc2 16. Bxc2 Bc6 17. Rfd1 O-O 18. Bc3 Nd7 19. f3 Rfd8 20. e4 Nc5 21. a4 Rxd1+ 22. Bxd1 a5 23. Kf2 e5 24. Ke3 f6 25. b3 Bd7 26. Rc1 b6 27. Bb2 Be6 $11 {Mamedyarov,S (2808)-Karjakin,S (2782) Leuven rpd 2018}) 10... Qxc5 (10... Nxd2 11. Nxd2 Qxc5 12. b4 Qe7 13. e3 O-O 14. Bd3 h6 15. O-O Ne5 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Rac1 Be6 18. Qc3 Qf6 19. Be2 Rfc8 20. Qd4 Rxc1 21. Rxc1 {So,W (2770)-Duda,J (2743) Chess.com blitz INT 2020} Nc4 $5 $132) 11. e3 Nxd2 12. Nxd2 dxc4 13. Bxc4 O-O 14. Rc1 Ne5 15. Be2 Qxc2 16. Rxc2 Rd8 17. Nf3 Nc6 18. O-O Bd7 19. Rfc1 Rac8 20. Kf1 Kf8 $11 {Mamedyarov,S (2772)-Karjakin,S (2754) Bucharest rpd 2019}) 9... exd5 10. e3 $5 {Kasimdzhanov revived the interest in this move in 2007.} Qf6 {Polgar emulates Ivanchuk, but here the queen sortie is less effective. Black had better options:} (10... Bf5 11. Bd3 Qg5 $142 (11... Ne5 $6 12. Nf3 Nxd3+ 13. Qxd3 Ng3 14. Qb5+ $1 ({More ambitious than} 14. e4 Nxe4 15. O-O O-O 16. Qd4 $14 {Kasimdzhanov,R (2682)-Asrian,K (2634) France 2007}) 14... Bd7 15. Qxb7 Nxh1 16. c6 Be6 17. c7 Qc8 18. Qc6+ Bd7 19. Qd6 {and with the trapped h1 Black is in big trouble:} a5 (19... Qb7 20. Bd2 Qxb2 21. Rc1 f6 22. Qxd5 Rc8 23. Rc4 Qb1+ 24. Bc1 Bf5 25. g4 Bxg4 26. Ne5 $1 fxe5 27. Qxe5+ Kd7 28. Qxg7+ Kd6 29. Qd4+ {1-0, Steamboat,B (2015)-Bascetta,B (2004) email 2009}) 20. Bd2 Ra6 21. Qe5+ Kf8 22. Rc1 f6 23. Qxd5 Be6 24. Qc5+ Kf7 25. Nd4 Re8 26. Kf1 $18 {mal biuely jasne navrch, Morozevic - Adams, MS blic Moskva 2007. ½-½ (51) Morozevich,A (2755)-Adams,M (2729) Moscow 2007}) 12. f3 Qh4+ 13. Kf1 Ng3+ 14. hxg3 Bxd3+ 15. Qxd3 Qxh1 16. b4 O-O 17. Bb2 Rfe8 (17... Rad8 18. Kf2 Qh6 19. Ne2 Rfe8 20. Nf4 Qg5 21. Rh1 g6 22. Rd1 Qe7 23. b5 Ne5 24. Qd4 f6 25. Nxd5 $18 {Karpov,A (2651)-Hou,Y (2578) Cap d'Agde rpd 2008}) 18. Kf2 Qh6 19. Ne2 $44 Ne5 20. Qb3 (20. Bxe5 Rxe5 21. Nd4 Rae8 22. Re1 Qf6 23. b5 h5 24. Qc3 h4 25. g4 g6 26. c6 Qd6 27. cxb7 R5e7 28. Qc8 Qg3+ 29. Kf1 {½, Figura,A (2045)-Krauss,H (2114) email 2011} h3 30. gxh3 Qxh3+ $11) 20... a5 21. Bxe5 Rxe5 22. Rd1 axb4 23. axb4 Qa6 24. f4 Ree8 25. Rxd5 Rad8 $11 {Kasimdzhanov,R (2683)-Aronian,L (2750) Mainz rpd 2007}) (10... Qa5+ 11. b4 ({Carlsen played the exchange sacrifice later in 2008. White wants more, than the modest} 11. Bd2 Nxd2 12. Qxd2 Qxc5 13. Nf3 O-O 14. Be2 Bg4 $11) 11... Nxb4 12. axb4 Qxa1 13. Bb5+ Kf8 14. Ne2 a6 $1 (14... a5 $143 $6 15. f3 Nf6 16. O-O Qe5 17. e4 $1 {is very risky:} dxe4 18. Bf4 Qf5 19. g4 Qg6 20. Qd2 Be6 21. Bb8 $1 $40 Nd5 22. Nf4 Qh6 23. Nxe6+ Qxe6 24. Bd6+ Kg8 25. Bc4 exf3 26. Bxd5 $18 {Kasimdzhanov,R (2690)-Karpov,A (2670) Vitoria Gasteiz 2007}) 15. Bd3 (15. Ba4 Bf5) 15... Bd7 16. f3 Ba4 17. Qb2 Qxb2 18. Bxb2 Ng5 19. Nd4 (19. Kd2 Rc8 20. Ra1 Bd7 21. Nf4 Bc6 22. Bf5 Re8 23. Ne2 Ne6 24. h4 $44 {Kasimdzhanov,R (2690)-Karpov,A (2670) Moscow blitz 2007 White's compensation was sufficient only for equality.}) 19... Bd7 20. Kf2 f6 21. Ra1 Ke7 22. Bc2 Rhd8 23. h4 Nf7 24. Bxh7 Rh8 25. Bc2 Rxh4 26. Bb3 {Carlsen,M (2765)-Adams,M (2729) Baku 2008} Ne5 $1 27. Bc3 Nc6 $11 {Ftacnik}) ({Finally, Magnus himself subsequently played} 10... O-O 11. Nf3 Bf5 12. Bd3 Qa5+ 13. Ke2 (13. Nd2 Nxd2 14. Bxd2 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 Qxc5 16. Rc1 Qb6 17. O-O Rad8 18. f4 Rfe8 19. b4 h6 20. h3 a6 21. Rc5 a5 22. Rfc1 axb4 23. axb4 Re4 24. Kh2 Rc4 $11 {Morozevich,A (2771)-Carlsen,M (2776) Wijk aan Zee 2009}) 13... Rfe8 14. Rd1 Bg4 15. b4 Qc7 16. h3 Bxf3+ 17. gxf3 Ng5 18. Bb2 d4 19. h4 Rxe3+ 20. fxe3 Qh2+ 21. Kf1 Qh1+ 22. Kf2 Qh2+ 23. Kf1 Qh1+ 24. Kf2 Qh2+ {½, Nakamura,H (2789)-Karjakin,S (2759) Wijk aan Zee 2014 For more details about this line see the notes in CBM 159 by Krasenkow.}) 11. f3 {The only way to fight for an advantage.} (11. Bd3 $6 Ne5 $1 $36 {} 12. Bxe4 dxe4 13. Qxe4 Bf5 14. Qa4+ {Fisette,R-Tsuboi,E (2271) Registro 2013} Bd7 15. Qb3 (15. Qd4 Bb5) 15... Bc6 16. f3 O-O-O $19) (11. Nf3 Bg4 {- 10.f3} 12. Be2 (12. Nd4 Bd1 $5 (12... O-O $32 {seems fine as well}) 13. Kxd1 Nxf2+ 14. Ke1 Nxh1 {shouldn't worry Black, even as White picks up the h1 his lead in development gives him a lot of compensation:} 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. g3 O-O 17. Bg2 Nxg3 18. hxg3 Rfe8 19. Qf2 Qd4 20. e4 Qd3 (20... Qc4 $2 21. Bf4 dxe4 22. Rc1 Qe6 23. Qe3 Rad8 24. Bd6 h5 25. Rc2 Qa2 26. Bf1 Qb1+ 27. Qc1 Qxc1+ 28. Rxc1 $18 {Grigore,G (2517)-Parligras,M (2588) Bucharest 2008}) 21. Qe3 (21. Be3 d4 22. Bf4 (22. Bxd4 Rxe4+ 23. Bxe4 Re8 $19) 22... g5 23. Bxg5 Rxe4+ 24. Bxe4 Re8 25. Qd2 Qf3 26. Qf2 Rxe4+ 27. Kf1 Qh1+ 28. Qg1 Qf3+ 29. Qf2 $11) 21... Qc2 22. Qf2 Qd3 $11) 12... O-O 13. O-O (13. Bd2 Rac8 $1 (13... Rfe8 14. Bc3 $5 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 16. bxc3 Rac8 17. Rb1 Nd8 18. Kd2 Re7 19. Rb4 {Karpov,A (2668)-Georgiev,K (2660) Valjevo 2007} {and here Black should hold the balance with} Bxf3 20. Bxf3 Rxc5 $132) 14. O-O (14. Bc3 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 16. bxc3 Na5 $11) 14... Ne5 $1 15. Nxe5 Bxe2 16. Nd7 Qa6 17. Nxf8 Bd3 18. Qd1 Bxf1 19. Qxf1 Qxf1+ 20. Rxf1 Nxd2 21. Rd1 Rxc5 22. Nd7 Rc2 23. b4 {Lysyj,I (2615)-Sargissian,G (2675) Rijeka 2010} f6 $1 $15 {Krasenkow}) 13... Rfe8 14. Bd2 d4 15. Rad1 Nxd2 {A more recent example is} 16. Rxd2 (16. Qxd2 Rad8 17. Qc1 d3 (17... h5 $5 $44 {Ivanchuk}) 18. Rxd3 Rxd3 19. Bxd3 Bxf3 20. gxf3 Ne5 21. Be4 Nxf3+ 22. Bxf3 Qxf3 23. Qd1 Qf5 24. b4 h6 $44 {[%csl Gg1] Bareev,E (2683)-Ivanchuk,V (2741) Havana 2006}) 16... dxe3 17. Rd6 Re6 18. fxe3 Rxd6 19. cxd6 Bxf3 20. Bxf3 Qxd6 21. Qb3 Qe7 22. Bd5 Nd8 23. Rc1 Qd7 24. Qc4 Ne6 25. Bxe6 fxe6 26. Qd4 Qxd4 27. exd4 Rf8 $11 {Mamedyarov,S (2809)-So,W (2799) Berlin Candidates 2018}) 11... Qh4+ 12. g3 Nxg3 13. Qf2 Nf5 14. Qxh4 Nxh4 15. b4 {White has swapped s to achieve a position with an active bishop pair and promising mobile queenside majority. However, to consolidate his positional trumps he needs to finish his development and coordinate his forces. Carlsen's last move was a natural novelty, opening the long diagonal for the unpopposed c1.} ({An earlier game went} 15. Kf2 Ne5 16. Bd2 f6 (16... g5 17. Bc3 f6 $13 {/}) (16... Bf5 $5 $132) 17. f4 {½, Dydyshko,V (2575)-Hracek,Z (2605) Czechia 2007 Despite the peaceful outcome White seems to be on top, for example} Nc4 18. Bc3 Bf5 19. Ne2 Be4 20. Bh3 $5 (20. Rg1 Nf5 $14) 20... Bf5 (20... Bxh1 21. Rxh1 {[%csl Rc4,Rh4][%cal Yh3e6]} Kf7 22. b3 $1 Nxa3 23. Nd4 a6 24. Be6+ Ke7 25. Rg1 g6 26. c6 $1 $18 {[%cal Rc3b4,Rc6b7]}) 21. Bxf5 Nxf5 22. Nd4 Nxd4 23. exd4 $16 {[%csl Rc4]}) 15... a6 $6 {The whole line requires first and foremost energetic and concrete counterplay, Carlsen will convincingly prove Black doesn't have enough time for the text move.} (15... O-O 16. Kf2 Re8 $6 (16... Ne5 $142 17. Bb2 f6 $14 {/}) 17. Ne2 Bf5 18. Rg1 Bg6 19. Nf4 Ne5 20. Be2 Rad8 21. Rd1 Kf8 22. Ra2 f6 23. Rad2 $16 {/+-,Ju,W (2397)-Harika,D (2462) China 2008}) ({Interesting is the provocative} 15... a5 {, which was tested 9 years later:} 16. b5 Ne5 17. Kf2 O-O 18. Bb2 f6 19. Rd1 Be6 20. Ne2 Nexf3 $5 (20... Nhxf3 21. Nf4 Bf7 22. Bg2 Nh4 23. Bxd5 Nc4 24. Bd4 Bxd5 25. Nxd5 Rad8 (25... Nxa3 26. Nc7 Rac8 27. b6 Nc4 28. Rc1 Ne5 29. Bxe5 fxe5+ 30. Ke2 Nf3 31. c6 bxc6 32. Ne6 Rf7 33. b7 Rxb7 34. Kxf3 $18) 26. Nc7 Rd7 27. b6 Ng6 28. Ke2 Ne7 29. Rc1 Ne5 30. Bxe5 fxe5 31. Rhd1 $18 {L'Ami,E (2614)-Harika,D (2521) Reykjavik 2017}) 21. Nf4 Bf7 22. Nxd5 Rfd8 $2 (22... Rae8 {or}) (22... Ng5 {are both stronger. While White might be better even here, Black's counterplay is more real.}) 23. e4 Ng5 24. Bd3 f5 25. Nb6 (25. Bc1 $5 Nxe4+ 26. Bxe4 fxe4 27. Ne7+ Kf8 28. Rxd8+ Rxd8 29. Bg5 $18 {[%cal Rg5h4,Re7g6]}) 25... fxe4 26. Be2 Rf8 27. Nxa8 $2 (27. Kg3 $1 Nf5+ 28. Kg4 h6 29. Nxa8 Ne3+ 30. Kg3 Nxd1 31. Rxd1 Rxa8 32. c6 $18 {[%csl Gc6] T,Paunovic}) 27... Bc4+ 28. Ke3 Ng2+ 29. Kd2 e3+ 30. Kc1 Bxe2 31. Rd7 Ne6 32. Nc7 Bxb5 $1 $11 33. Nxb5 e2 34. Kd2 Rf1 35. Kxe2 Rxh1 36. Rxb7 Ngf4+ 37. Kf3 Rf1+ 38. Ke3 Re1+ 39. Kf3 Rf1+ {½, Morozevich,A (2675)-Harikrishna,P (2737) Biel 2017}) ({Another try in the same active spirit is} 15... d4 16. b5 (16. Kf2 $142 $1 Be6 (16... O-O 17. exd4 Nxd4 18. Bg5 $36) 17. e4 $5 $14) 16... Na5 17. Rb1 $6 (17. exd4 Nb3 18. Rb1 Nxd4 19. Kf2 Be6 20. Bg5 Ng6 $132 (20... Nhxf3 $2 21. Nxf3 Nxf3 22. Kxf3 Bd5+ 23. Kf2 Bxh1 24. Re1+ Kf8 25. Bc4 $18 {[%cal Re1h1,Re1e7]})) 17... dxe3 18. Bxe3 Be6 19. Kf2 Nf5 20. Bf4 Rc8 $15 {Shen,Y (2468)-Wang,J (2380) Hangzhou 2018}) ({Also developing moves such as} 15... Be6 {or}) (15... Bf5 $5 {deserve attention and are stronger, than the text.}) 16. Kf2 Ne5 17. Bb2 f6 18. Rd1 Be6 19. Ne2 $1 {An important move, possibly underestimated by Judit from afar. White can ignore his f3 and starts getting out his pieces to take control of the position, with the aforementioned strategic factors playing a pivotal role.} Bf7 (19... Nhxf3 20. Nf4 Bd7 21. Bg2 $36) ({More resilient was} 19... g5 $5) 20. Rg1 $1 {Winning another tempo.} Nc4 {Here Black must prepare the g5 advance.} (20... O-O $6 21. f4 Neg6 (21... Nc4 22. Bxf6) 22. Nd4 Ne7 23. Bh3 $18 {and Black's knights are offside}) (20... Nhxf3 21. Rxg7 $18 {[%csl Rf6,Rh7] only creates new weaknesses.}) 21. Bc1 (21. Bc3 $5 {is also promising, but Carlsen has a different idea - c3 remains free for the knight.}) 21... g5 (21... O-O 22. Rd4 (22. Nc3 Rfd8 23. Nxd5 $18) 22... Ng6 (22... Nf5 23. Rf4 Ne7 24. Rxf6) 23. Nc3 $18 {[%csl Rc4,Rd5][%cal Yc3d5,Ye3e4]}) 22. Nc3 O-O-O 23. e4 $1 dxe4 $6 {Now the game will beyond saving.} (23... d4 24. Nd5 Ne5 25. f4 gxf4 26. Rg7 $5 $16) ({or the immediate} 23... Ne5 $5 24. f4 $16 {is no fun either, but probably does offer stiffer resistance.}) 24. Rxd8+ Rxd8 25. Nxe4 {Black has no time to protect the f6 and loses material.} Kc7 (25... Ne5 {gives more practical chances, but White still has a pleasant choice:} 26. f4 (26. Nd6+ Kc7 (26... Rxd6 27. cxd6 Nhxf3 28. Bh3+ $18) 27. f4 Nef3 28. Nxf7 Rd1 29. Be3 Nxg1 30. fxg5 $1 (30. Kxg1 $2 Nf5 $17) 30... Ngf3 31. Bf4+ Kd7 32. gxf6 Ke6 33. Nd6 Kxf6 34. Be2 $18) 26... gxf4 (26... Nef3 27. Rg3 Nxh2 28. Be2 $18 {[%csl Rh2]}) 27. Bxf4 Be6 28. Nxf6 $18 (28. Nd6+ $5 Kc7 29. Rg7+ Rd7 30. Ne8+ Kc6 31. Rxd7 Nxd7 32. Nc7 Bf5 33. Bd6 $18 {[%cal Rb4b5]})) 26. Nxf6 h6 27. f4 Nd2 {A desperate bid for counterplay, but it's too late.} 28. Be2 Nb3 29. Be3 $18 Nd4 (29... gxf4 $2 {fails to} 30. Bxf4+ Kc6 31. Rg7 $18 {so Black loses another pawn. The rest is a matter of technique, which has always been Magnus's forte.}) 30. Bxd4 (30. fxg5 $18) ({or} 30. Bd1 $5 {are roughly equivalent.}) 30... Rxd4 31. fxg5 Rf4+ 32. Ke1 hxg5 33. Nh7 $1 Bd5 34. Nxg5 Kc6 35. Rf1 Rxf1+ (35... Rd4 36. Rf6+ Kc7 37. Ne6+ $18) 36. Bxf1 a5 37. Kd2 axb4 38. axb4 b6 39. cxb6 Kxb6 40. Bd3 Bc6 41. Kc3 Bd7 42. Be4 Bg4 43. Nf7 Bf3 44. Bd3 $1 {Another classy move, Carlsen is patient and doesn't rush into piece exchanges. First of all he unblocks the distant h2.} Bc6 45. Ne5 Bd5 46. Ng6 Ng2 47. Kd4 Bb7 48. h4 Bf3 49. Bf1 Ne1 50. Ne5 Nc2+ 51. Kc3 Be4 52. h5 {Now the pawn is unstoppable.} 1-0

