1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f3 Qb6 $5 {This is the only extra option offered to Black after 6.f3 instead of 6.Be3. This of course does not account for the various limitations White has already forced on himself; 6...e5 cannot be met with 7.Nf3, 6...e6 not with 7.g4, and so on. Luckily, if you do find a good way to play after 6.Be3 Ng4, you can transpose well between variations, while building up a repertoire with these extra options, should you wish so.} 7. g4 ({An equally valid move order is:} 7. Nb3 e6 8. Qe2 {There is one alternative set-up for Black.} Qc7 {Black has a good score with this flexible approach, but I believe in White's chances.} ({After} 8... Nc6 9. g4 {we have transposed to our main line.}) 9. Be3 b5 10. O-O-O Nbd7 {Now we play similarly to the main line.} (10... b4 $2 {is premature.} 11. Na4 Nbd7 12. Qc4 $1 Qb8 13. Na5 Ne5 14. Qd4 Nfd7 {Manole - Hajbok, Brasov 2011.} 15. Bd2 $1 $146 {Winning a pawn. The key point is:} Nc5 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Qd8+ $3 Kxd8 18. Bg5+ Kc7 19. Bd8#) 11. a3 $1 {Having defended the b6-square, Black was threatening ...b4.} Nb6 {The most flexible.} (11... Ne5 12. Qf2 (12. g4 $5 $146 Nc4 13. g5 Nd7 14. Bd4 $14 {also feels pleasant for White}) 12... Rb8 {Delorme - Gerard, Sautron 2001.} 13. g4 $1 $146 Nfd7 14. Rg1 $14) 12. g4 Be7 13. g5 Nfd7 14. f4 $5 (14. h4 Nc4 15. Bd4 $14 {also looks great.}) 14... Bb7 15. Rg1 Na4 16. Bd2 Rc8 17. Rg3 $1 $14 {Wurschner - Szczepankiewicz, email 2012. If Black castles, White arrives quickly with f4-f5 and a serious attack.}) 7... Nc6 8. Nb3 e6 9. Qe2 {Placing the queen on e2 is not much worse than placing it on d2. The reason for this is that it often belongs on f2 or g2. This usually costs a tempo. The real question about this variation is if it is worth it for Black to spend time pushing the knight from d4 to b3. My guess is that it is not.} Qc7 10. Be3 b5 11. O-O-O Nd7 12. Qf2 $1 b4 ({The only decent alternative for Black is probably:} 12... Nce5 13. a3 $1 {Black's counterplay on the queenside seems slower than White's kingside attack. We will analyse a) 13...Bb7?! and b) 13...Be7. a)} Bb7 $6 {This looks very natural, but most Najdorf players know that the bishop needs to "do something" here for this move to be good, and at the moment the e4-pawn is rock solid, so this is not the case. Usually Black's most obvious counterplay is with ...Rb8 and ...b4.} ({b)} 13... Be7 {can be met in several ways. Normally I would say "one good option is enough", but learning about the different methods available here should help to enhance your understanding of these positions in general.So we will consider b1) 14.Rg1!?N, b2) 14.g5!?N and b3) 14.h4. b1)} 14. Rg1 $5 $146 {The idea here is to launch the f-pawn.} ({b2)} 14. g5 $5 $146 {I also like this move, which has an interesting strategic concept behind it.} Nc5 15. Nxc5 dxc5 16. Qg3 $1 {The h2-pawn defends the queen, which is rather important.} O-O (16... b4 $6 {leads to a poor position after:} 17. Bf4 $1 bxc3 18. Bxe5 cxb2+ 19. Kxb2 Qb6+ 20. Ka2 O-O 21. Bc4 Ra7 22. h4 $16) 17. Bf4 f6 18. h4 $14 Rb8 (18... b4 $2 19. Nb1 $16 {White is preparing Bc4.} c4 $5 20. Bxc4 Qxc4 21. gxf6 Bxf6 22. Bxe5 Bxe5 23. Qxe5 $16) 19. Rd5 $3 exd5 20. Nxd5 Qd6 21. gxf6 Bxf6 22. Nxf6+ Qxf6 23. Bxe5 Qh6+ 24. Kb1 $14 {White has given up an exchange for a pawn, but the dark-squared bishop is more powerful than any minor piece I have seen recently.}) ({b3)} 14. h4 {This leads to a further split between b31) 14.. .0-0, b32) 14...Nc4 and the untested b33) 14...Nc5!N. The last option is Black's only decent way to create counterplay, as far as I can see. b31)} O-O ({b32)} 14... Nc4 {occurred in Nijboer - Van der Stricht, Vlissingen 2002, when White should have played:} 15. Bxc4 $1 $146 {White is simply better in this structure. For example:} bxc4 16. Nd2 Rb8 17. Ba7 Rb7 18. Bd4 $14 {But obviously the game has just begun.}) ({b33)} 14... Nc5 $1 $146 15. g5 $1 $14 {Analysis has convinced me that this is the strongest continuation. Play could continue in many directions, but White generally wants to play Bd4 and f3-f4. I have analysed the most direct continuation in detail.} Nxb3+ 16. cxb3 b4 $5 (16... Rb8 17. b4 $14) 17. axb4 Rb8 18. Bd4 O-O (18... Rxb4 19. f4 Nc6 20. Bc4 $1 {is very similar.} (20. Bxg7 Rg8 21. Bd4 Rxb3 {is probably also to White's advantage, but seems less clear to me}) 20... Na5 21. Bxg7 Rg8 22. Bd4 Nxc4 23. bxc4 Qxc4 24. Kb1 Bb7 25. Rhe1 a5 26. e5 d5 27. f5 $16) 19. f4 Nc6 20. Bc4 $1 Nxb4 (20... Nxd4 $2 21. Qxd4 Rxb4 22. h5 $16 {gives White good attacking chances.}) 21. f5 $1 exf5 22. g6 $1 hxg6 $6 (22... d5 23. exd5 Bd6 $16 {is the last try according to the computer, but hardly something that scares us. After} 24. gxh7+ Kh8 25. Rhg1 f6 26. h5 Bd7 27. Qh4 $18 {White is ready for both Rg2 and Rdg1, and the more direct Rxg7!?.}) 23. h5 d5 24. hxg6 $1 dxc4 25. Rh7 $1 Bg5+ 26. Kb1 fxg6 27. Rxg7+ Qxg7 28. Bxg7 Kxg7 29. Qg3 {White wins.}) 15. g5 {is probably all bad news.} Nc4 (15... Rb8 16. Rg1 $14 b4 17. axb4 Rxb4 18. f4 Nc4 19. Bxc4 Rxc4 20. f5 $1 $16) 16. Bxc4 bxc4 17. Nd2 a5 (17... Rb8 18. Ba7 Ra8 19. Bd4 $14) 18. Kb1 $1 $14 {The king goes to a1; White has a more dangerous attack on the kingside.}) 14... Nc5 15. f4 Nxb3+ 16. cxb3 Nc6 17. b4 $14 O-O 18. g5 a5 19. bxa5 Nxa5 20. f5 Bd7 21. Kb1 $1 (21. f6 {allows the amazing} Rfb8 $3 22. fxe7 b4 23. axb4 Rxb4 {with crazy complications:} 24. Bd3 Be8 25. Rde1 Qxe7 26. Rg3 Qb7 27. g6 Nb3+ 28. Kd1 hxg6 29. Rh3 Ra5 $13) 21... b4 $1 22. Bb6 $1 ({I checked} 22. axb4 {but only found equality after:} Rfb8 23. g6 fxg6 24. fxg6 hxg6 25. Rxg6 Bf8 26. bxa5 Qxa5 27. Bc4 Qxc3 28. Rf1 Qxc4 29. Rxg7+ $11) 22... Qb7 23. axb4 Nc6 24. b5 Rfb8 25. fxe6 fxe6 26. Bd4 Nxd4 27. Qxd4 $14 {White keeps the better chances although the position remains complicated.}) 14. g5 Be7 ({The way to make sense of 13...Bb7 is to try to blow up the centre, but after} 14... d5 15. exd5 Bxa3 16. Bd4 Be7 {White has a lot of tempting options. I like:} 17. Bh3 $1 O-O 18. f4 $14 {White has an advantage here; only one variation looks critical.} b4 $6 (18... Ng6 19. Rhf1 $14) 19. Rhe1 $3 bxc3 20. dxe6 Nf3 (20... fxe6 21. Bxe6+ Kh8 22. Bxd7 Bd6 23. Bc5 cxb2+ 24. Kb1 Bxc5 25. Qxc5 Qxc5 26. Nxc5 Nxd7 27. Nxb7 Nb6 28. Rd4 $16) 21. exd7 $16 Nxe1 $6 22. Rxe1 Bb4 23. Bf5 $1 cxb2+ 24. Bxb2 Bxe1 25. Qd4 f6 26. Be6+ Kh8 (26... Rf7 27. f5 $18) 27. gxf6 Rxf6 28. Qxf6 $1 Qxd7 (28... gxf6 29. Bxf6#) 29. Qh6 Qe7 30. Bf5 $1 {White has a winning attack.}) 15. h4 Rb8 16. Rg1 (16. Kb1 $1 $146 {followed by h4-h5 looks more accurate to me.}) 16... Nc4 17. Bxc4 bxc4 18. Nd2 O-O 19. h5 d5 20. exd5 exd5 21. Bd4 $14 {Black survived in Juan - Fenollar Jorda, email 2010, but his prospects look grim to me.}) 13. Na4 $1 Rb8 ({As ever, there is an alternative:} 13... Be7 14. g5 Nce5 {This is very natural, but I like White's chances here as well.} (14... Rb8 15. Rg1 {is similar to our main illustrative game.}) 15. Nb6 Rb8 16. Nxc8 Rxc8 17. Kb1 a5 18. Nd4 $14 (18. h4 $14) 18... Nc5 $2 (18... O-O 19. h4 $14 {with a complicated game was essential, even if more attractive for White.}) 19. Bb5+ Ned7 20. h4 $16 Qb7 21. Qe2 O-O 22. h5 a4 23. Rhg1 Bd8 24. Bf2 Rb8 25. Bg3 Ne5 26. f4 Ned7 27. g6 b3 28. cxb3 axb3 29. a4 Nf6 30. e5 dxe5 31. Nc6 Rc8 32. gxh7+ Kxh7 33. Bh4 exf4 34. Qe5 Rg8 35. h6 g6 36. Bxf6 Bxf6 37. Qxf6 Rxc6 38. Bxc6 Qxc6 39. Qxf7+ Kh8 40. Qxf4 Qxa4 41. Qe5+ {1-0 Engwall - Edwards, email 2014.}) 14. Rg1 $1 {A flexible move that discourages either knight from coming to e5.} Be7 ({We should also consider the following options:} 14... h5 15. gxh5 Rxh5 16. Kb1 Ne7 17. Nd2 d5 18. h4 e5 19. Qh2 Bb7 20. Nb3 Qc6 21. Nac5 Nxc5 22. Bxc5 Qf6 23. Qd2 Ba8 24. f4 $16 {Fagerstrom - Slawinski, email 2009.}) (14... Nce5 {If Black was allowed two moves in a row, then ...Qc6 would be winning.} 15. f4 {But now we have time for this, and we are better:} Nc4 16. Ba7 Rb7 17. Bxc4 Qxc4 18. Nb6 $1 Nxb6 19. Bxb6 Qc6 20. Be3 Rd7 (20... Qxe4 21. Rd4 Qg6 22. f5 Qf6 23. h4 $16) 21. Rd4 $14) 15. Kb1 Nd8 $6 {This looks like a bad move, even though it is of course played with an idea. But I think it is just too slow.} (15... Nce5 16. f4 Ng6 {is the suggestion of many engines. White looks good to me after} 17. Nd4 $14 {with the point:} Nc5 $6 18. Nxc5 dxc5 19. Nf5 $1 $16) 16. Nd2 $1 $16 Qc6 17. b3 O-O {This moves the king into the firing line, but it is also hard to continue the game with the king stuck in the centre.} 18. g5 Nb7 19. f4 Nbc5 20. Nxc5 Nxc5 21. f5 $1 $18 Re8 22. g6 $5 {This is very powerful and enough to win the game, but there was an even stronger and somewhat unusual continuation, which is worth remembering for another time.} (22. f6 Bf8 23. g6 $3 hxg6 24. Rxg6 {Black is mated in short order.}) 22... fxg6 23. fxg6 Rf8 24. gxh7+ Kh8 25. Qg2 Bf6 {Despite missing a direct win, Leko still managed to win the game with a nice trick.} 26. e5 $1 Qxg2 27. Bxg2 Bxe5 28. Nc4 Bf4 29. Rgf1 g5 30. Nxd6 Bb7 31. Bd4+ Kxh7 32. Nxb7 Nxb7 33. Ba7 Kh6 34. Rd7 Rfd8 35. Bxb8 Rxd7 36. Bxf4 gxf4 37. Rxf4 a5 38. Bxb7 Rxb7 39. Rf6+ Kg5 40. Rxe6 Rb5 41. Kb2 Rc5 42. Re4 Kf6 43. Rc4 Rf5 44. a3 Rb5 45. axb4 axb4 46. h4 Ke6 47. h5 Kf6 48. h6 Kg6 49. Rc6+ Kh7 50. c3 {6...Qb6 is a serious alternative to the standard Scheveningen/Najdorf lines, but with good preparation, White should leave the opening with sunny prospects. The real downside of playing 6.f3 against the Najdorf is the loss of 6...e5 7.Nf3 and 6. ..e6 7.g4, but as we recommend neither line in this book, that is not a serious drawback for us.} 1-0

