1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. g3 {Mickey claims he was inspired to try this line based on Gawain's successes with it and the coverage in his book - that move order runs 3.Qe2 but we soon transpose.} Nc6 4. Bg2 Nf6 5. Qe2 d5 6. exd5 ({Adams noted that} 6. e5 {was an alternative. White has scored poorly after} Nd7) 6... Nxd5 7. O-O Be7 8. Rd1 {An interesting position which was tested by Kuzubov in 3 games.} O-O {After this Black is left in the type of passive position against which Adams in normally lethal.} (8... Nd4 {was Adams' home analysis.} 9. Nxd4 cxd4 10. c4 {with a reversed Benoni.} Nc7 (10... Nb4 11. d3 a5 {is "certainly a good Benoni" according to Adams, though he does not sound sure that it's enough for an advantage.}) 11. d3 O-O 12. Nd2 a5 13. f4 Na6 14. Nf3 Nb4 {looked okay for Black in Y.Kuzubov, Y (2627) - K.Miton (2600), Reykjavik 2011 (1-0 in 37).}) (8... e5 $5 {is a dynamic attempt to prevent d4.} 9. c3 ({Black is fine after} 9. Nxe5 Nd4 10. Qf1 f6 11. Nc3 Nb4 12. Nc4 Ndxc2 13. Rb1 O-O) 9... Bg4 10. h3 Bh5 {and Black had no complaints in Y.Kuzubov (2615) - H. Gretarsson (2460), Reykjavik 2012 (1/2-1/2 in 58).}) (8... Qb6 {also puts the brakes on d4.} 9. c3 Bd7 10. Na3 O-O 11. Nc4 Qc7 12. d3 h6 13. a4 Rad8 14. a5 {with perhaps an edge for White, but Black was very solid in Y.Kuzubov (2627) - M.Dzhumaev (2465), Tashkent 2011 (1-0 in 62).}) 9. d4 cxd4 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 ({After} 10... Qb6 {Adams planned} 11. Nf3 {intending c4} (11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. c4 Ba6 13. b3 Bf6 14. Bb2 Bxc4 $1 {is a cute tactic pointed out by Mickey})) 11. Rxd4 Bf6 12. Rd1 Qc7 13. c4 {Black is faced with a difficult choice of where to move the knight.} Nb4 (13... Ne7 14. Nc3 Bxc3 15. bxc3 Nc6 16. Ba3 Re8 (16... Rd8 17. Bxc6 bxc6 18. Qe5 Qb6 19. Bc5 Qa5 20. Bxa7 {wins (Adams)}) 17. Bd6 Qa5 18. Qb2 e5 {leaves White with several active possibilities,} 19. Qb5 $5 (19. Rd5 $5) (19. Bd5 $5)) (13... Nb6 {was "maybe the best chance" according to Adams, since} 14. Na3 Bd7 {(intending ...Bc6)} 15. Bf4 Qc8 (15... e5 16. Be3 {is excellent for White.}) 16. Nb5 e5 17. Nd6 Bg4 {"starts to get a bit random". Continuing a bit further,} 18. f3 Qc7 19. Be3 Be6 20. b3 {still looks nice for White.}) 14. Nc3 a6 15. Bf4 {Provoking ...e5 which, as noted by Adams, weakens the light squares (particularly d5) and blocks the f6-bishop.} e5 16. Be3 Be6 (16... Bf5 {(Adams) "looks dubious but I didn't completely refute it". Let's try:} 17. a3 Nc2 18. Nd5 Qc8 (18... Qb8 19. Nxf6+ gxf6 20. Rac1 Nxe3 21. fxe3 {and Black's kingside is shattered.}) 19. Nb6 Qc7 20. Nxa8 Rxa8 21. Rac1 Nxe3 22. Qxe3 {where, despite the two bishops, Black has insufficient compensation for the exchange.}) 17. Nd5 Nxd5 18. cxd5 {Adams had already seen the possibility of Bb6 (preventing ...Bd8 since e5 is hanging prepares Rac1).} Bf5 19. d6 Qd7 20. Bb6 Rac8 21. Bc7 Bg4 22. Bf3 h5 23. Rac1 Rfe8 {Adams didn't like this (d7 possibilities later) but didn't suggest alternatives.} 24. Bxg4 hxg4 25. Qe4 Bg5 26. Rc5 Bd8 27. Qxb7 Re6 $8 (27... f5 28. Qxc8 Qxc8 29. d7 {(Adams)}) 28. Rdd5 (28. Qc6 $4 {allows} Rxc7 {with a draw, as noted by Adams.}) 28... Rf6 29. Qxa6 {Now Polgar is playing for tricks.} Qf5 30. Qe2 Qb1+ 31. Rd1 Qxa2 32. Rxe5 Bxc7 33. Re8+ Rxe8 34. Qxe8+ Kh7 35. dxc7 Qa7 ({Adams was going to meet} 35... Qxb2 {with the simple} 36. Rf1 {.}) 36. Qe4+ {An excellent win by Adams, in a line which has caused some problems for Black. The alternatives at move 10 certainly appear preferable to Polgar's treatment.} 1-0

