{John Emms: 'Worth A Pawn:- White grabs an early pawn in return fro the initiative: A dangerous strategy against such an attacking player as Judit Polgar.'} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Bd2 {John Emms: 'This is less common than 7 Qc2 or 7 e3.'} Nd7 8. Nxd5 exd5 (8... Bxd5 9. Qc2 c5 10. e4 Bb7 11. d5 $1 {gave White an edge in Anand-Almasi, Dortmund 1998.}) 9. g3 Bd6 10. Bg5 Be7 {John Emms: 'Black loses a move by moving his bishop twice, but manages to obtain more space by exchanging a pair of minor pieces.'} (10... f6 11. Bf4 $14 {looks slightly better for White.}) 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Rc1 c5 13. Bh3 {John Emms: '!?'} (13. dxc5 bxc5 14. Bh3 Nb6 15. b4 cxb4 16. Qd4 O-O 17. Qxb4 Qxb4+ 18. axb4 a5 19. bxa5 Rxa5 20. O-O Ba6 21. Nd4 Bc4 22. Rb1 Ra6 23. Rb4 g6 24. Rc1 Rfa8 25. Bf1 {was drawn in Krasenkow-Gelfand, Polanica Zdroj 1998.}) 13... O-O {John Emms: '!?' Black must play actively, as the centre is to White's advantage.} 14. dxc5 (14. Bxd7 Qxd7 15. dxc5 d4 {creates counterplay, as} 16. O-O d3 17. Ne5 {can be met by} Qd5) 14... bxc5 15. Bxd7 Qxd7 16. Rxc5 Qh3 {John Emms: 'This position has been assessed as "unclear". Certainly Black has some compensation the pawn and this is very much the type of position which Polgar like to play.' The fact that White cannot castle is a considerable handicap, but the black bishop could become bad, and a pawn is a pawn.} (16... d4 17. O-O d3 18. Ne5) 17. Qd3 $1 {White wants to keep d4 free for the knight, but must be ready to meet Qg2 with Qf3.} (17. Qd4 Rfe8 18. Qh4 $2 Qg2 $19 {wins for Black.}) 17... Rac8 {John Emms: '!'} 18. Rxc8 Rxc8 {John Emms: 'Akopian now struggles to find an effective plan and it does seem hard to come up with a solution to White's development problems.'} 19. Qb5 $2 (19. Kd2 {was much better, and White holds the advantage.} d4 (19... Qh6+ 20. Qe3) (19... Qg2 20. Rd1 Qxf2 21. Qd4 {with a big positional advantage - when the queens come off the king belongs in the centre.}) 20. Qxd4 (20. Rd1 Qh6+ 21. Ke1 Bxf3 22. Qxf3 Qxh2 {is less clear.}) 20... Qh6+ 21. Qe3 Qd6+ 22. Ke1 {and White moves his king around to g2.}) 19... Bc6 20. Qd3 (20. Qa6 d4 {with a big initiative.}) 20... Ba4 21. Qe3 h6 {With the back rank problem covered the rook threatens to cause damage.} 22. Nd4 Qg2 23. Rf1 Bd7 $1 {John Emms: 'Preparing ...Bh3.' A neat switchback breaks down the defenses.} 24. Nf3 (24. f4 Bh3 25. Rg1 (25. Rf2 Qg1+) 25... Rc1+ {wins.}) 24... d4 25. Qf4 (25. Nxd4 Bh3 {and Rg1 is no longer available.}) 25... g5 {John Emms: '!'} 26. Nxg5 {John Emms: 'This is desperate, but'} (26. Qd2 Re8 {John Emms: '!'} 27. Ng1 {looks desperate, but after the piece is lost its all over.} Qe4 {leaves White struggling to find a decent move.}) 26... hxg5 27. Qxg5+ Kf8 28. Qh6+ Ke8 29. f3 Rc6 30. Qf4 Qxh2 31. Qb8+ Ke7 32. Kd2 Rc2+ 33. Kxc2 Qxe2+ 34. Kb3 Qxf1 35. Qb4+ Ke6 {and there are no more checks.} 0-1

