1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 c5 9. Ne2 Qc7 {The most common choice for Black. If the queen is going to settle on c7, it makes sense to do so now because it sets up an x-ray attack on the c4-bishop and White has to deal with this straight away.} 10. Bd3 (10. Ba2 {is the alternative.}) 10... Nc6 {Preparing ...e5.} ({The immediate} 10... e5 {is also possible. It usually transposes, but 10...e5 does have the merit of preventing the possibility of 10...Nc6 11 f4!? (see the next note). White can deviate with} 11. dxe5 (11. O-O Nc6 {transposes to the main game}) 11... Qxe5 {but I feel Black should be okay here. For example,} 12. Qc2 Be6 13. Ng3 ({or} 13. f4 Qd6 {followed by ...Rd8}) 13... c4 $1 14. Be2 Nbd7 15. O-O Nc5 16. f4 Qc7 17. e4 Nd3 18. Be3 Ng4 19. Qd2 Nxe3 20. Qxe3 f6 $15 {, H.Hoffmann-H. Staudler, ICCF corr 1998.}) 11. O-O (11. f4 {prevents ...e5. It's certainly White's most ambitious option here, and there are some interesting possibilities:} b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. e4 (13. Ng3 Ne7 14. Qe1 Nf5 $5 {offered a positional pawn sac in A.Koblencs-I.Bondarevsky, Leningrad 1949 - if White accepts with} 15. Nxf5 exf5 16. Bxf5 {, then} Be4 $1 {and Black gets some action on the light squares}) 13... Ne7 $146 ({I prefer this move to} 13... cxd4 14. e5 Nd5 15. cxd4 Rad8 16. Qc2 h6 17. Bd2 f5 18. exf6 Rxf6 $14 {which looked a touch better for White in Pham Minh Hoang-K.Georgiev, Cappelle la Grande 2004.}) (13... Ne7 14. Ng3 cxd4 15. e5 Nfd5) 14. Ng3 ({if} 14. e5 {then} Qc6 15. Rf3 Ne4 {intending ...Nf5}) 14... cxd4 15. cxd4 Qc3 $1 16. Qe2 $5 ({Black's idea is to meet} 16. Be3 $2 {with} Ba6 $1 17. Bxa6 Qxe3+ 18. Kh1 Nxe4 $17) 16... Qxa1 (16... Qxd4+ 17. Be3 $44) 17. Bb2 Qa2 18. Bc4 Ba6 19. Bxa6 b5 $1 {and Black seems to have successfully emerged from the complications - but this new analysis needs checking.}) 11... e5 {I can't see any point in delaying this move any longer, and if Black does White might be tempted to play f4.} 12. Qc2 {White wants to prevent the possibility of ...e4, but I feel maybe this move should be encouraged rather than prevented! The problem with 12 Qc2 is that it allows Black tactical opportunities on the c-file.} ({I prefer} 12. Bb2 {. For example,} e4 $5 ({there's nothing wrong with} 12... Be6 {intending ....Rfd8, ...Rac8, ...Na5, etc}) 13. Bb1 Na5 14. Ba2 c4 15. Nf4 b6 16. a4 Re8 17. Re1 $2 ({Gavrikov suggests the immediate} 17. Ba3 $1 {intending Bb4, and this looks like a significant improvement}) 17... Bb7 18. Ba3 Nd5 $15 {and Black's ambitious play left him with the edge in M.Sadler-M. Chandler, London 1991, but overall I don't think White should be so eager to prevent ... e4.}) 12... Be6 $1 {Simple stuff. Black plans ...Rc8 and it's difficult for White to hold his centre. It has to be said that Aleksandrov doesn't even try. ..} 13. dxe5 $6 $15 {Surely there must be something better than this!} ({It's not} 13. f4 $6 {, though:} c4 $1 14. Bf5 Bxf5 15. Qxf5 exd4 16. cxd4 Rac8 17. Bd2 Rfe8 18. Nc3 a6 19. Rf3 Ne7 20. Qc2 Qd7 21. Re1 Nf5 22. h3 h5 {with an iron grip on the light squares, E.Gausel-M.Petursson, Gausdal 1990.}) ({I feel that} 13. a4 {preparing Ba3 looks the most sensible, although after} Rfc8 $1 {White has to face up to the problems of his queen being on c2:} 14. dxc5 $5 ({not ideal, but} 14. Ba3 {allows Black to use tactics on the c-file:} cxd4 15. cxd4 Nxd4 $5 16. Nxd4 exd4 ({or even} 16... Qxc2 17. Nxc2 e4 18. Bxe4 Nxe4 $11) 17. Qxc7 Rxc7 18. exd4 Bc4 $11) 14... Na5 15. Ba3 Nb3 16. Rab1 Nxc5 17. Bxc5 Qxc5 $11 {, K.Lerner-F.Hellers, New York 1990. Note here that} 18. Rxb7 $2 {is strongly answered by} e4 $1) 13... Nxe5 14. Nf4 Nxd3 15. Qxd3 Rad8 $1 {Black manages to dominate the light squares even without his bishop. By the time White is able to activate his dormant c1-bishop, Black is in complete control.} 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Qc4 Qc6 18. Rb1 Nd7 19. Qb3 b6 20. c4 Ne5 21. Bb2 Nd3 22. Rbd1 Qe4 23. Qc2 Qg6 24. Bc3 Rd7 25. f3 Rfd8 26. Rd2 $6 Qh6 27. Re2 e5 28. Rd1 Qh4 29. Bd2 $6 e4 $1 30. fxe4 $2 Qg4 $1 {Winning material.} 31. Be1 Nf4 32. exf4 Rxd1 33. e5 Qxf4 34. e6 Rf8 {A model game from Black's point of view.} 0-1

