1. d4 {Krasenkow,M Krasenkow} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qc2 c5 6. d5 exd5 7. cxd5 Bb7 8. Bg2 Nxd5 9. O-O Be7 10. Rd1 Nc6 {This line was examined by GM Evgeny Postny in his theoretical survey in CBM116. See also my annotations to Bologan-A.Ivanov in CBM117.} 11. Qf5 Nf6 12. e4 d6 $146 (12... g6 13. Qf4 O-O (13... h6 $5 14. e5 Nh5 15. Qg4 Ng7 16. Nc3 Ne6 17. Be3 h5 18. Qa4 a6 19. Rd2 (19. Nd5 $44) 19... b5 20. Qd1 c4 21. Nd5 Nb4 22. Bb6 $44 {0-1 Loeffler,S-Sakaev,K/Dresden GER 2007/The Week in Chess 648 (38)}) 14. e5 Nh5 15. Qg4 $1 (15. Qh6 Nd4 16. Nxd4 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 cxd4 18. g4 Rc8 $3 19. Rxd4 Ng7 20. Nc3 f5 21. Qd2 Bc5 22. Rxd7 Qh4 $17 {½-½ Aronian,L-Leko,P/Moscow RUS 2006/The Week in Chess 627 (27)}) 15... d5 16. exd6 Bf6 $6 (16... Bxd6 17. Bg5 $44) 17. Nc3 Nd4 18. Nxd4 Bxg2 19. Nf5 Bc6 20. d7 Qc7 21. Nd5 Bxd5 22. Rxd5 Rfd8 23. Be3 Bxb2 24. Nh6+ Kf8 25. Rad1 Bg7 26. Rxh5 gxh5 27. Qf5 {1-0 Carlsen,M-Ivanchuk,V/Monte Carlo MNC 2007/The Week in Chess 646}) 13. e5 Qd7 14. Qxd7+ Nxd7 15. exd6 Bf6 16. Re1+ Kf8 17. Nc3 Nb4 {White's d6-pawn is the main strategical problem in this kind of position. It can be weak or strong. As Black's h8 rook is out of play, the former possibility is more probable.} 18. Bg5 $6 {A dubious exchange sac.} (18. Rd1 $14) 18... Bxf3 $6 {Black "believes" her opponent - and wrongly so!} (18... Nc2 $1 19. Bxf6 Nxf6 20. Re7 Bxf3 21. Bxf3 Re8 $1 22. Rxe8+ Kxe8 23. Bc6+ Kd8 24. Rd1 Nd4 $15 {[%csl Rd6]}) 19. Bxf3 Rd8 20. Bxf6 Nxf6 21. Re7 Nc2 22. Rd1 Nd4 23. Kg2 g6 24. Rxa7 Rxd6 $14 {[%mdl 32] In this position White's bishop is very strong. Compared to the game Gelfand-Kasimdzhanov from this issue, there is much more material on the board. Still, if Black's h8 rook safely comes out without her having to make any concessions, the position will almost equalize.} 25. Be2 $1 Kg7 26. Bc4 Rd7 27. Rxd7 Nxd7 {[%mdl 4096]} 28. Re1 Kf8 $6 {Cutting off his own rook once again. Black's further attempts to free it take valuable time and the invasion of White's rook, combined with the advance of his pawns, decides. The lesser evil was to remove his strong knight from d4:} (28... Nf5 $142 29. Bd3 Nd6 30. Nb5 $14) 29. a4 h5 30. Nd5 h4 31. g4 g5 {[%csl Rg5]} 32. Nc7 {[%cal Re1e8,Re8e7]} Nf6 33. h3 Kg7 34. Re5 Kh6 (34... Kg6 $142 $1 35. Bd3+ Kh6 36. f4 Rg8 37. Nb5 Nxb5 38. axb5 gxf4 39. Kf3 $16) 35. f4 Nh7 36. f5 Nc6 (36... f6 37. Rd5 {[%cal Yd5d6]}) 37. Rd5 Kg7 (37... Rd8 38. Rxd8 Nxd8 39. Nd5) 38. f6+ $5 Kh6 39. Bb5 Nb4 $6 (39... Nd4 40. Rd6 Nxb5 41. axb5 Rb8 42. Nd5 Ra8 43. Nc3 Rb8 44. Kf2 $16) 40. Rd6 Rb8 41. Na6 Nxa6 42. Bxa6 Nf8 43. Bc4 {The advantage of the bishop over the knight is now obvious.} Rb7 44. Kf3 Nd7 (44... Ng6 45. Ke4 Nf4 46. Kf5 Nxh3 47. Rd8 Kh7 48. Rf8 $18) 45. Ke4 Kg6 46. Bd3 c4 47. Bxc4 Nxf6+ 48. Kd4 Kg7 49. Bb5 Re7 {Desperation.} (49... Rb8 50. b4 Rb7 51. Bc4 {[%cal Ra4a5]} Ra7 52. Rxb6 Rxa4 53. Rb7 Ra3 54. b5 Rxh3 55. b6 Rh2 56. Rxf7+ Kg6 57. b7 Rb2 58. Re7 Nxg4 (58... h3 59. Bd3+ Kh6 60. Rf7) 59. Kc3 Rxb7 60. Rxb7 h3 61. Bd3+ Kf6 62. Kd4 $18) 50. Rxb6 Re4+ 51. Kd3 Rb4 52. Kc2 Nxg4 53. Kc3 Re4 54. hxg4 h3 55. Rd6 h2 56. Rd1 Rxg4 57. Rh1 Rg3+ 58. Kb4 Rg2 59. Kb3 g4 60. a5 g3 61. Bc6 Re2 62. a6 Re7 63. Ra1 Ra7 64. Bg2 1-0

