1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. g3 e6 5. Bg2 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. d4 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Bg5 $6 {This is not too promising for White, compared to} (11. Qc2 Bb6 12. Ng5 g6 13. Qd2) 11... Qd7 {This move may look strange but Black avoids creating weaknesses in her position after the "main"} (11... f6) {And the white g5 bishop will have to retreat anyway.} 12. Ne1 {Too "academic" but} (12. Rc1 Bb6 {[%cal Yd5d4] doesn't promise too much either.}) 12... Re8 $1 $146 {[%mdl 8] A novelty in a well-known theoretical position! This move was recommended by GM Alon Greenfeld but not tested in practice before the present game.} (12... d4 {is the main theoretical move but White keeps an edge:} 13. Nd3 Bb6 14. a4 $5 {The most dangerous plan in such positions.} (14. Bd2 Qe7 (14... Re8 15. Re1 Qd6 16. a4 a6 17. Qb3 Na5 18. Qd5 Qxd5 19. Bxd5 Bf5 20. b4 Nc6 21. a5 Ba7 22. b5 axb5 23. a6 Rad8 24. Bg2 Be4 25. Bxe4 Rxe4 26. axb7 Bb8 27. Rec1 Re6 28. Ra8 {1-0 Schandorff,L-Greenfeld,A/Saint Vincent 2005/CBM 110}) 15. Nf4 Be6 16. Qa4 Rac8 17. Rac1 Rfd8 18. Rfe1 h6 19. a3 Qd7 20. Nxe6 Qxe6 21. Qc4 $14 {½-½ Kramnik,V-Anand,V/Monte Carlo 1995/CBM 046 ext (50)}) (14. Bf3 Qf5 15. Bd2 Rd8 16. a4 $1 Be6 17. Nf4 a5 18. Nxe6 Qxe6 19. Bf4 h6 20. h4 Rac8 21. Rc1 Ne5 $1 22. Bxe5 Rxc1 23. Qxc1 Qxe5 24. Kg2 Qe7 $1 $11 {½-½ Kalinin,A-Atalik,S/Wijk aan Zee 1997/CBM 057/[Atalik] (39)}) (14. Rc1 Re8 (14... Qf5 15. Bd2 Rd8 16. a4 Ne5 17. b4 a5 18. Nc5 axb4 19. Nxb7 Bxb7 20. Bxb7 Ra7 21. Bg2 Qd7 22. Bxb4 Qxa4 23. Qxa4 Rxa4 24. Be7 Re8 25. Bd6 $14 {1-0 Utemov,V-Titenko,V/Moscow 1990/EXT 2004 (39)}) 15. Re1 Qf5 16. Bd2 Be6 17. Qa4 Rad8 18. Nf4 Bc8 19. Bh3 Qe5 20. Nd3 Qh5 21. Nf4 Qg5 22. Bxc8 Rxc8 23. Qc2 Qg4 $132 {0-1 Cherniak,L-Magerramov,E/Moscow 1991/TD (50)}) 14... Qf5 15. Bd2 Bd7 16. b4 a6 17. Nf4 Qe5 18. Nd5 Bd8 19. Bf4 Qe6 20. Re1 Qg6 21. b5 axb5 22. axb5 Rxa1 23. Qxa1 Ne7 24. Qxd4 $16 {1-0 Topalov,V-Hansen,C/Istanbul 2000/EXT 2001/[Tsesarsky] (65)}) (12... h6 $5 {is quite good, in order to keep the isolated central pawn on d5 for a while, leaving the a7-g1 diagonal and d4 square free:} 13. Bd2 (13. Bf4 Rd8 14. Nd3 Bb6 15. Bd2 Qe7 16. Nf4 Be6 (16... Qe5 17. a4 Bf5 18. a5 Bc7 19. a6 bxa6 20. Rc1 Ne7 21. Bc3 d4 22. Bb4 Rac8 23. Bb7 Bb6 24. Bxc8 Nxc8 25. Nd3 $14 {½-½ Lie,K-Hansen,L/Copenhagen 2004/CBM 101 ext (45)}) 17. Qa4 Rd7 18. Rfd1 Rad8 19. Be1 Qf6 20. Bc3 d4 21. Be1 Bg4 22. h3 g5 23. Nd5 Rxd5 24. hxg4 Re5 25. Qc4 Rde8 $132 {½-½ Hauchard,A-Kinsman,A/France 1998/EXT 2001 (33)}) 13... Bb6 14. Nd3 Qd6 $1 (14... Rd8 15. a4 a5 16. Nf4 d4 17. Nd5 Ra6 18. Rc1 Ba7 19. Bf4 Kh7 20. Nc7 Rb6 21. Nb5 Bb8 22. Bxb8 Nxb8 23. Rc4 Qe8 24. Rxd4 Rxd4 25. Qxd4 Rg6 26. Nd6 Nc6 27. Qd3 {1-0 Gustafsson,J-Hertneck,G/Germany 2002/EXT 2003}) 15. Bf4 Qf6 16. Bxd5 Bg4 $132 17. Kh1 Rad8 18. Bg2 Nb4 19. Qb3 $1 Nxd3 20. exd3 Be2 21. Rfe1 Bxd3 22. Be3 Bxe3 23. Rxe3 Ba6 $11 {0-1 Schmidt,W-Greenfeld,A/Moscow 1994/CBM 045 (29)}) 13. Nd3 (13. Bxd5 $6 Re5 14. Bxc6 Qxc6 15. Bf4 Re8 $17) (13. Rc1 Bb6 14. Bxd5 Re5 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Nf3 Qxd1 17. Rfxd1 Rxe2 $15) 13... Bb6 14. Bd2 Qd6 {The same idea as after 12...h6 but Black's rook is already on e8, which is, of course, favorable for her.} 15. Nf4 Bg4 16. a4 Rad8 17. a5 Bc5 (17... Bd4 $15 {was even stronger.}) 18. Re1 a6 $6 {An unnecessary move, after which White consolidates his position and Black's d5 pawn becomes weak.} (18... Bf5 $142 {[%cal Yf5e4] , e.g.} 19. Rc1 (19. Qb3 $2 Nd4) (19. Nxd5 $2 Be4) (19. Bxd5 $2 g5 20. Nh5 Bg4) 19... Be4 20. e3 d4 $1 $36) 19. Rc1 Ne5 $2 (19... Bf5 {was still possible, e.g.} 20. Bxd5 Ne5 $44 (20... g5 21. Nh5 Bg4 {is not good now due to} 22. Rxc5 $1 Qxc5 23. Nf6+ Kf8 24. Bxc6 bxc6 25. Nxe8)) 20. h3 Bf5 (20... Be6 $142 $14) 21. Qb3 {[%csl Rb7,Rd5] Suddenly Black is in trouble.} Nc4 22. Red1 Be6 (22... Be4 {is too late due to} 23. Bxe4 Rxe4 24. Bb4 $1 Bxb4 25. Rxd5 $16) (22... b5 23. axb6 Qxb6 24. Qxb6 Bxb6 25. Bb4 $16) 23. Be1 (23. Nxd5 $1 {was even simpler:} Bxd5 24. Bxd5 Qxd5 25. Qxc4 $16) 23... b5 24. axb6 Nxb6 25. Qa2 Bb4 26. Bxb4 Qxb4 27. Qxa6 {and White gradually converted his extra pawn:} Nc4 28. Nxd5 Bxd5 29. Rxd5 Rxd5 30. Bxd5 Nb6 31. Ba2 h6 32. Rc7 Rd8 33. Rxf7 Kh8 34. Qa3 1-0

