1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 a6 7. Nge2 c6 {Diagram } 8. a4 {A relatively calm line. White prevents ...b5.} a5 ({This is a standard answer, but sometimes Black is playing also} 8... Nbd7 {where the queen can go to a5 in some variations.}) 9. Ng3 ({Somewhat more usual is} 9. Nc1 Na6 {as in Korchnoi-Williams, London Staunton Mem 2009. It followed} 10. Be2 Nd7 11. O-O e5 12. Nb3 exd4 13. Bxd4 Ndc5 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Qd4+ Kg8 $11 {(the game was annotated in CT-3205).}) 9... Na6 {Diagram } 10. Be2 Qc7 11. O-O e5 12. d5 {As a rule, having a weakened b4 square, White is not pretending for a serious advantage, but may still have hopes to develop a slight pressure.} h5 $146 ({Previously,} 12... Nd7 $5 {was tried in Dekic-O.Kim, Moscow OL wom 1994.}) 13. Qd2 h4 14. Nh1 Nh5 15. Nf2 {Diagram } f5 ({Instead, quite an important option was} 15... Nf4 $5 16. Bxf4 exf4 17. Qxf4 {where Black has some uncertain long-term compensation for a pawn.}) 16. Nd3 ({White could have tried} 16. exf5) ({or maybe} 16. Bg5) 16... f4 17. Bf2 {Diagram  Black's position, likely, remains playable.} Ng3 ({Possibly better than} 17... h3 18. c5 $1 (18. g4 fxg3 19. hxg3 $13 Qf7)) ({or} 17... Qe7 18. c5 dxc5 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. d6) 18. Rfd1 (18. hxg3 $6 {favours Black} hxg3 19. Bxg3 $2 fxg3 20. Qg5 {because of} Qb6+ $1) 18... c5 $6 ({Preferable was} 18... Nxe2+ {with a rather double edged play:} 19. Qxe2 h3 20. c5 hxg2) 19. hxg3 hxg3 20. Bxg3 $1 fxg3 21. Qg5 $1 {Diagram  Black does not get enough for a pawn, mainly because his knight is far away from the kingside.} Qf7 ({Another option was} 21... Rf6) 22. Nb5 Qf6 23. Qxg3 Bh6 24. Kf2 $1 Nb4 $6 ({A lesser evil, objectively, was} 24... Kg7 25. Rh1 Rh8 ({or maybe} 25... Bd7) 26. Rh4 Bd7 {where White has} 27. Nxd6 $1 Qxd6 28. Qxe5+ Qxe5 29. Nxe5) 25. Rh1 $16 Kg7 {Diagram } 26. Qh4 $6 ({Missing a stronger continuation:} 26. Rxh6 $1 Kxh6 {followed by the attack on the h-file:} 27. Rh1+ Kg7 28. Qh2 $16 {and then, for example,} Nxd3+ 29. Bxd3 Qe7 $6 30. Nxd6 $1 Qxd6 31. Qh7+ Kf6 32. Rh6 Kg5 33. g3 $1 $18 {threatening with xg6+!.}) 26... Qxh4+ $2 ({Black had to play} 26... g5 $1 {after which White is still better but there is surely a fight ahead.}) 27. Rxh4 $18 {Diagram } Bg5 ({Or} 27... Nxd3+ 28. Bxd3 Ra6 29. Rah1 Rh8 {and White should gradually win after, say,} 30. g3 $5 ({or maybe even} 30. g4 $5 g5 31. Rh5 {sooner or later followed by f4!})) 28. Rh2 $1 Be3+ {This tactic does not help, but anyway Black has a bad position.} 29. Kxe3 Nc2+ 30. Kd2 Nxa1 31. Kc3 $1 $18 {Diagram } Ra6 ({Completely hopeless is} 31... Rh8 32. Rxh8 Kxh8 33. Nxd6 {, etc.}) 32. Rh1 Bd7 33. Rxa1 Bxb5 34. cxb5 Raa8 35. b3 Rh8 36. b6 Rh2 37. Rg1 Ra6 38. Nb2 Rxb6 39. Bb5 $1 {Diagram } 1-0

