1. d4 {Ftacnik,L Hansen, Carsten} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. Nc3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Qb6 6. g3 Bc5 7. e3 Nc6 8. Bg2 {Hansen, Carsten: '!?'} ({Aside from this gambit continuation, White has tried a number of things: A)} 8. Nb3 {(Hansen, Carsten) is rather tame, and doesn't do anything to bother Black} Be7 (8... Bb4 9. Bd2 (9. Qe2 Ne4 10. Bd2 Nxd2 11. Qxd2 O-O 12. a3 $6 (12. Bg2 Qa6 13. Qe2 b6 $15) (12. Be2 $5 d5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. O-O (14. Qxd5 Bh3 $44) 14... Rd8 15. Qd3 Be6 $13) 12... Be7 13. Qc2 d6 14. Bg2 Ne5 15. Nd2 Bd7 16. O-O Rac8 17. b3 Qc7 {is very confortable for Black, Romero Holmes, A-Nogueiras, J/Yucatan 1999}) 9... Ne5 10. Qe2 O-O 11. f4 Ng6 12. e4 d5 13. cxd5 exd5 14. e5 Bg4 $15 {Granda Zuniga, J-Suba, M/Zaragoza 1995}) 9. Bg2 (9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O d6 11. e4 Qc7 12. Be3 b6 13. Rc1 Ne5 $11 {Hoeschele, H-Suetin, A/Grieskirchen 1998}) 9... O-O 10. O-O d6 11. e4 Ne5 12. Be3 Qb4 13. c5 dxc5 14. a3 Qc4 15. Rc1 Rd8 16. Nd5 exd5 17. Rxc4 dxc4 18. Nd2 b5 {, and Black has enough for the queen, Paunovic, T-Rogulj, B/Belgrade 1988}) ({B) White's main alternative is likely} 8. Na4 $5 {(Hansen, Carsten) , e.g.} Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Qa5 10. Nc3 Nxd4 11. exd4 Qb6 12. a3 (12. Nb5 $6 Be7 13. Qf3 O-O 14. a4 $6 a6 15. a5 axb5 16. axb6 Rxa1+ 17. Ke2 bxc4 18. Bg2 Ra2 19. Qc3 d5 {with a very good game for Black, Dumitrescu, D-Ionescu, D/ Bucharest 1997}) 12... Bxc3 13. Bxc3 Ne4 14. Qc2 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 O-O 16. Bg2 d6 17. O-O Bd7 18. c5 {, with the better chances for White, Timoshenko, G-Brodsky, M/Kherson 1991}) ({C)} 8. Nf3 {Hansen, Carsten} O-O 9. Bg2 d5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Na4 Bb4+ 12. Bd2 Qa6 13. Bf1 b5 $15 {, and Black is clearly in the driver's seat, Meenakshi, S-Foisor, O/Triesen LIE 2007}) 8... Nxd4 ({Black has tried a couple of other things aside from taking the pawn: A)} 8... Be7 {Hansen, Carsten} 9. O-O d6 10. b3 a6 11. Bb2 Bd7 12. Rc1 Rc8 13. Ne4 $14 {Kiss, A-Titz, H/Szentgotthard 2001}) ({B)} 8... O-O $5 {Hansen, Carsten} 9. Na4 (9. O-O $1 {again invites Black to take the pawn, though in the inferior version as chosen by Polgar in our main game}) 9... Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Qa5 11. Nc3 Qc5 {, Gluszko, T-Kuzmicz, K/Grodzisk Mazowiecki POL 2007, and now} 12. Nxc6 (12. a3 Bxc3 13. Bxc3 Qxc4 14. Qe2 (14. Rc1 Ne5 15. Qe2 d5 16. O-O Qxe2 17. Nxe2 Nc6 18. b4 {with some compensation for the pawn}) 14... Qxe2+ 15. Kxe2 Nxd4+ 16. Bxd4 d5 17. Rhc1 {, and White has compensation for the pawn, but not much more than that.}) 12... dxc6 13. Qe2 e5 14. a3 Bxc3 15. Bxc3 a5 $11 {, Black has solved his opening problems successfully.}) 9. exd4 Bxd4 10. O-O O-O {Hansen, Carsten: '?!' Judit plays a dangerous line, but in the critical position even avoids the main defence 10...e5 that has yet to be refuted. White has a dangerous initiative for the pawn, but can he aspire to more than the equality?} (10... a6 11. Na4 Qa7 12. Bf4 $1 d5 (12... e5 {Hansen, Carsten: '?'} 13. Bxe5 $1 Bxe5 14. Re1 {Hansen, Carsten: '' Hansen, Carsten: '/+- Greenfeld, A-Rocius, M/Saint Vincent ITA 2005'} O-O 15. Rxe5 d5 16. cxd5 b5 17. Nc3 b4 18. Na4 Bb7 19. Qd2 a5 20. b3 Rad8 21. Rd1 {1-0 Greenfeld,A (2549)-Rocius,M (2240)/Saint Vincent 2005 (25)}) 13. Bd6 $1 e5 (13... dxc4 14. Rc1) 14. c5 Bg4 15. Qd2 Rd8 16. Rae1 $16 {Umansky-Schmittdiel/Absam 2002/} Be6 {Hansen, Carsten} 17. Qg5 (17. Bxe5 $5 Bxe5 18. Rxe5 $16) 17... Rxd6 18. Qxg7 Rg8 19. Qxf6 Rd8 {Umansky, M-Schmittdiel, E/ Austrian Tch 2002} 20. Rd1 $16) ({Hansen, Carsten: 'B)'} 10... Bxc3 $6 11. bxc3 O-O 12. Ba3 $1 $14 {Hansen, Carsten: '' Hansen, Carsten: ', and White's strong bishops along with the weak dark squares in Black's camp leaves White with more than adequate compensation'}) ({This causes immediate problems for Black. The alternatives are: A)} 10... e5 {Hansen, Carsten} 11. Nb5 (11. Qc2 {is covered in the notes to Greenfeld-Ward, Jersey Open 2004 - see the PGN archives}) 11... O-O (11... Bc5 12. b4 (12. Bg5 {is covered in the notes to the above mentioned Greenfeld-Ward}) (12. Qe1 {was Greenfeld-Ward}) 12... Bxb4 13. Rb1 Bc5 14. Qe1 d6 15. Bd2 a5 16. Bg5 O-O 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Nc3 Qc7 19. Qe2 {, and White has compensation for the pawn, Ashley, M-Wojtkiewicz, A/Philadelphia 1989}) 12. a4 $5 (12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Qd3 (13. b3 d5 14. Bb2 (14. Ba3 Re8 15. c5 Qa5 16. Bb2 Qxc5 17. Bxd4 Qd6 18. Bxf6 Qxf6 19. Qxd5 Qe7 $11 {, and a draw was soon agreed upon, Haba, P-Sax, G/ German Bundesliga 1993}) 14... dxc4 15. Bxd4 (15. Qxd4 {quickly terminated down to a drawn rook endgame:} cxb3 16. Qxb6 axb6 17. axb3 Be6 18. Bxb7 Rxa1 19. Bxa1 Bxb3 20. Rb1 Rb8 21. Rxb3 Rxb7 22. Bxf6 gxf6 23. Rb5 {1/2-1/2 Nogueiras, J-Psakhis, L/Szirak 1986}) 15... Qa6 16. Bxf6 (16. Qf3 Rd8 17. Bxf6 Qxf6 18. Qxf6 gxf6 19. bxc4 Be6 20. Rfb1 Rab8 21. c5 Rdc8 22. Rb5 a6 23. Rxb7 Rxb7 24. Bxb7 Rxc5 25. Bxa6 Ra5 {with a drawn endgame, although White continued to try for quite a while, Van Dijk, H-Fritz, M/ICCF Email2001}) 16... Qxf6 17. bxc4 Rb8 18. c5 Rd8 19. Qa4 Bd7 20. Qxa7 Bc6 {, thus far Petursson, M-Nogueiras, J/Saint John 1988, and now} 21. Rad1 Bxg2 22. Kxg2 $16 {leaves White with an extra pawn and decent winning chances}) 13... d5 (13... Re8 14. Bg5 d5 15. cxd5 Bg4 16. Rae1 h6 17. Bf4 (17. Bd2 Bh5 $11) 17... Qxb2 18. Be5 Qc3 19. Qb1 $14) 14. cxd5 Bg4 15. h3 Bd7 $2 (15... Bh5 16. Bf4 Rfd8 17. d6 Ne8 18. Be4 Nxd6 19. Bxh7+ Kh8 20. Be5 $14) 16. Rd1 Rfe8 {was played in Raetsky, A-Tunik, G/Podolsk 1992, and now} 17. Kh2 $1 $16 {prepares to pick up the d4-pawn, e.g.} Rac8 18. b3 $1 Bb5 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. Rxd4 Re1 21. Rb4 $1 Rcxc1 (21... a6 22. Bb2 $18) 22. Rxc1 Rxc1 23. Rxb5 $18) 12... a5 (12... Re8 $5 {(Ribli)} 13. a5 Qc5 14. Bg5 Qxc4 15. Nd6 Qe6 16. Nxe8 Nxe8 17. Ra4 $1 {is problematic for Black, e.g.} Bc5 (17... d6 $2 18. Rxd4 $1 exd4 19. Re1 $18) 18. Bd5 Qd6 19. Qb3 Qf8 20. Be3 $1 d6 21. Bxc5 dxc5 22. a6 $18) (12... Bc5 13. Qe1 $5 {A clever multi-purpose move, threatening the e5-pawn and the advance b2-b4 at the same time.} (13. a5 Qd8 14. Be3 $1 {with excellent positional compensation for the sacrificed pawn} (14. a6 $6 Rb8 {is fine for Black})) 13... a5 $6 {Black prevents the b2-b4 advance, but at the same leaves no option for kicking the b5-knight away.} (13... a6 $5 14. a5 Qd8 15. Nc3 $44) 14. Qxe5 d6 15. Qf4 $14 {, White has both the initiative and the better pawn structure, Rahman, Z-Hossain, E/Dhaka 1999}) 13. Nxd4 exd4 14. b3 d5 15. c5 Qc6 16. Qxd4 Bf5 17. Bb2 Rfe8 18. Rfe1 $14 {Nielsen, P-Gelfand, B/playchess.com INT 2004}) ({D)} 10... Be5 {Hansen, Carsten} 11. Qe2 Bb8 12. Be3 Qd8 13. c5 O-O 14. Rfd1 Qe7 15. Rac1 Rd8 16. Nb5 $5 ({White can improve with} 16. Bg5 $1 Qf8 {(Nd5 was threatened)} 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Ne4 Qh6 19. Nd6 Bxd6 20. cxd6 {, and Black is completely tied up}) 16... Nd5 17. Bd4 Qf8 18. Be5 b6 19. Bd6 Bxd6 20. Nxd6 bxc5 21. Bxd5 exd5 22. Rxd5 $16 {Marin, M-Ionescu, C/Predeal ROM 2006}) 11. Bg5 $146 ({Hansen, Carsten: 'This is a new move. In an earlier game, White had proved that'} 11. Na4 {Hansen, Carsten: '!' Hansen, Carsten: 'is uncomfortable for Black:'} Qd6 12. Bf4 $5 {Hansen, Carsten: '!'} (12. Be3 Be5 13. Qe2 {Hansen, Carsten} Qe7 14. Rfd1 d5 15. cxd5 Bd6 16. Nc3 exd5 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. Rxd5 Be6 19. Rb5 {leaves White with a fair-sized initiative, Green, P-Martin, B/Dunedin 1988}) 12... e5 13. Be3 {Hansen, Carsten: ''} Qb4 14. Bxd4 exd4 15. b3 (15. Rc1 $5 {Hansen, Carsten} d5 $2 (15... d6 $5) 16. a3 Qa5 17. cxd5 $16) 15... d5 16. a3 Qa5 17. cxd5 Nxd5 18. Qxd4 $14 {1-0 Bricard,E (2460)-Olafsson,H (2270)/Reykjavik 1993 (52) Hansen, Carsten: 'Bricard, E-Olafsson, H/Reykjavik 1993'}) 11... e5 {Hansen, Carsten: '?!'} (11... Qc5 $5 {Hansen, Carsten} 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. Ne4 Qxc4 14. Nxf6+ gxf6 15. b3 Qb4 16. a3 Qb5 {, and White has some compensation for the pawn thanks to Black's weakened kingside pawn structure, and problematic queenside development}) 12. c5 $5 {Carlsen is uncorking a dangerous idea, that would be challenging enough without the limitations of the blindfold game..} Qxc5 $6 (12... Qxb2 $6 13. Bxf6 Qxc3 14. Rc1 $1 (14. Qg4 g6 15. Rac1 Qd2 $1) 14... Qb2 (14... Qb4 15. a3 $1) 15. Qg4 g6 16. Rc4 Qd2 17. Qh4 h5 (17... Re8 $2 18. Rc2 $1 $18) 18. Rxd4 Qxd4 (18... exd4 19. Bxd4 g5 (19... a5 20. Qf6 Qh6 21. Bd5 $16) 20. Qe4 $16) 19. Qg5 Kh7 20. Bxe5 $5 f6 21. Bxd4 fxg5 22. Re1 Rf7 23. Bd5 Rg7 24. Re8 $18) (12... Bxc5 $5 13. Na4 Qa5 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Qg4+ Kh8 16. Qh4 Kg7 17. Be4 Rh8 18. Qg4+ Kf8 $13) (12... Qe6 $6 13. Nb5 Qf5 14. Be3 $1 Bxe3 15. fxe3 Qg5 16. Qb3 $14) (12... Qa6 13. Be3 (13. Rc1 d5 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Nxd5 Qd8 $15) 13... Bxc3 14. bxc3 d5 15. cxd6 Rd8 $13) 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Nd5 (14. Qg4+ Kh8 15. Qh4 $1 Kg7 (15... Bxc3 $2 16. Be4 $1 $18) 16. Be4 Rh8 17. Nd5 Qd6 18. Rad1 $1 h5 19. Rd3 $16) 14... d6 $2 {[%mdl 256] Hansen, Carsten: '??' Polgar feels, that developing the bishop from c8 is imperative, but unfortunately it is too slow. Suddenly the black queen is cut off from the kingside and White will just win.} (14... f5 $1 15. Rc1 (15. Qh5 Qd6 16. Qxf5 Qh6 $13) 15... Qd6 16. Nc7 (16. Qh5 {Hansen, Carsten} Bxb2 17. Rc2 Bd4 18. Qxf5 Qh6 19. Rfc1 Kh8 20. Ne7 {, and just when it looks like White is winning, Black has} Bb2 $1 {Hansen, Carsten: '=!' Hansen, Carsten: 'is of course a draw by repetition'} 21. Rd1 Bd4 22. Bd5 $5 (22. Rdc1 Bb2 $1 $11 {is of course a draw by repetition}) 22... Re8 23. Nxc8 Rexc8 24. Rxc8+ Rxc8 25. Qxd7 Bxf2+ $1 26. Kxf2 Rc2+ {Hansen, Carsten: '='} 27. Kf3 Rc3+ 28. Kg4 (28. Kf2 Rc2+ $11) 28... Qg6+ 29. Kh4 Qh6+ 30. Kg4 Qg6+ $11) 16... Rb8 17. Nb5 Qb4 18. Nxd4 exd4 19. Qh5 $40) 15. Qh5 Kg7 (15... f5 $2 16. Qg5+ Kh8 17. Qf6+ Kg8 18. Ne7#) 16. Be4 h6 (16... Rh8 17. Qh4 $18) 17. Rac1 Bxf2+ (17... Qa5 18. Rxc8 Rfxc8 19. Qf5 Rg8 20. Qxf6+ Kf8 21. Qxh6+ Ke8 22. Qxd6 $18) 18. Kg2 Qd4 19. Qh4 $1 {Black has grabbed three pawns, but her position is completely hopeless.} f5 (19... Rg8 20. Qxf6+ Kf8 21. Qxd6+ Kg7 22. Qf6+ Kf8 23. Qxh6+ $18) 20. Qf6+ Kg8 (20... Kh7 21. Bxf5+ Bxf5 22. Qxf5+ Kg7 23. Nf6 $18) 21. Ne7+ Kh7 22. Bxf5+ Bxf5 23. Qxf5+ ({Hansen, Carsten: 'After'} 23. Nxf5 {Hansen, Carsten: '!' Hansen, Carsten: 'it is immediate curtains'} Qe4+ 24. Kxf2 $18 {Hansen, Carsten: ', and Black doesn't have anything better than'} Qxf5+ {Hansen, Carsten} 25. Qxf5+) 23... Kg7 24. Rxf2 e4 25. Rc4 $1 {Hansen, Carsten: '!?' Carlsen is enjoying himself and tries to win with an impressive attack.} ({Hansen, Carsten: 'Strictly speaking, the strongest move is'} 25. Qh5 $18 {Hansen, Carsten: '!'} Qxf2+ {Hansen, Carsten} 26. Kxf2 {, though the text move is more than adequate as well.}) 25... Qxc4 26. Qf6+ Kh7 27. Nf5 $1 1-0

