{I got the chance to play in the 2011 World Cup at Khanty-Mansiysk due to having qualified from the European Championship which was organised in France, Aix les Bains during Spring. Not only did I qualify but I played very well and ended up tying for first which left me with the bronze medal. My first visit to Siberia was in the World Cup in 2009, when the weather was very memorable; one of the nights we went back to the hotel and it was -38 Celsius! My second visit was the Olympiad in 2010. I consider it was the best organised Olympiad so I have played in (I have playedin 8 olympiads in my life so far). This was my third occasion to play in Khanty-Mansiysk (by this time I had no problems pronouncing it either:). The playing conditions were excellent, a well organised event and fantastic live coverage during the event. We played in the Chess Academy which was built specially to organise chess events. It is very modern and in different parts of the building you can feel that chess is respected and is in the center of attention, even the bathrooms have chess motifs on the tiles:) The live coverage was something which would be fantastic to have in future events. In the playing hall there were 5 cameras following the games and the players facial expression s. One could really feel the tension during the games while watching the transmission live. During the round, commentaries were in two languages - in Russian and English. The press room was always ready for a live press conference. Let's move on to the game! This match was in round 4. I had won my previous matches, the first against Corrales from Cuba, round 2 against Movsesian. I won my first game in round 3 against Karjakin who was the number 1 seeded player of the World Cup. In game 2 Sergey was not able to equalise the match and I moved on to round 4! Against Dominguez I had lost my first game with White, which meant that I was in a must-win situation. I did not prepare much for the game, but was very much focused on trying to play a long game and keep the game going. Thinking in the long run I might get some chances. Obviously to win with Black against a very strong and solid player as Dominguez is almost impossible.} 1. e4 {Polgar,J Polgar,J Polgar,J Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J''} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qb6 {I had played this line previously, but it is not my main weapon.} 5. Nb3 Nf6 6. Nc3 e6 {I have great memories in this line, remembering my game against Motylev in Khalkhidiki Club Cup 2003.} 7. Bg5 {Trying to play similarly to a Rauzer with d2. I thought, if I would go ...e7 and later d6. In this position 7.d3 and e3 are the most played moves. For some time 7.e2 was considered the best reply (as Motylev had played against me).} a6 {Not deciding yet where to go with my bishop - to b4 or e7.} 8. Qf3 {I was expecting 8.d2.} ({In game 8 of our match it was already the 5 minutes playoff, White turned to 8.d2. We were both very tired in that game, as we had played 5 games previously and the tension was very high. I would not like to comment on the quality of this game, but I won, so I was very happy to be able to move on to the next stage of the event !} 8. Qd2 Qc7 9. O-O-O b5 10. f3 Be7 11. Kb1 O-O 12. g4 Ne5 13. Bf4 b4 14. Ne2 Rd8 15. Ned4 Bb7 16. Qe1 Rac8 17. h4 d5 18. exd5 Rxd5 19. h5 Nxf3 20. Qg3 e5 21. Bg2 exf4 22. Qxf3 Rxd4 23. Nxd4 Bxf3 24. Bxf3 Nd7 25. Rhe1 Bf6 26. Nc6 Ne5 27. Nxe5 Qxc2+ 28. Ka1 Bxe5 29. Rb1 f6 30. Be4 Qd2 31. Bf5 Bxb2+ {0-1 Dominguez Perez,L-Polgar,J Khanty-Mansiysk 2011}) 8... Be7 9. Qg3 {Of course, it is not easy to play with White against a sharp Sicilian when the draw is all you need to qualify for the next stage of the World Cup.} d6 10. O-O-O {It was clear after the queen move, that White aims to castle long. I was happy for it and was looking forward to a sharp game! After all I have to win to be able to continue the match!} O-O 11. Kb1 {I think 11.e2 was a move to consider and then h6 would threat to force my knight back to e8.} (11. Bxf6 {I was happy to offer a pawn, because Black has great compensation after} Bxf6 12. Rxd6 Qc7 {- fantastic compensation for the sacraficed pawn.}) 11... Rd8 12. f4 Qc7 13. Bd3 {Of course by this time it was clear, that my opponent had not chosen to play solidly, but to play in the spirit of the Sicilian and make it a sharp game! Nearly 50 minutes had been used by him.} b5 {I felt very comfortable during the game because I thought that could not have dreamt of a better game after the opening. I felt that I would be able to have chances. In this position there are always basic questions like to play a3 or allow Black to play ...b4 and ...a5 to push the pawns. Lenier decided to go in the sharp direction, which was very brave, but actually a good decision as I did not react the best way.} 14. Qh4 {After making this move White was left with 28 minutes. It shows how a great a player Lenier is: when needing only a draw to move on to the next stage in the World Cup, he goes in for a sharp piece sacrifice! He is also not only a very strong and well prepared player but also has great nerves! White is putting pressure on the h7-pawn and has two ideas in mind to open the diagonal of the d3-bishop with e5 or d5.} h6 15. Bxh6 gxh6 $2 {I made this move too quickly} (15... Nxe4 $1 {is what I should have played} 16. Qh3 Nxc3+ 17. bxc3 g6 {with a complicated game. White could continue} (17... gxh6 {to take it gives a forced draw} 18. Qxh6 f5 19. Qg6+ {at least a draw for White}) 18. Qg3 {White has different ideas in mind, xg6 the concrete one, but also h4 or g5.} Bf6 19. Bg5 Bxg5 20. fxg5 Ne5 21. h4 Qxc3 22. Qf4 Bb7 23. h5 Nxd3 24. Rxd3 Qe5 25. Qh4) 16. Qxh6 Ne8 {Looking for oportunities to play on, but...} (16... Ng4 {would lead to a repetition of moves only:} 17. Qh3 Nf6 18. Qh6) 17. e5 {The most logical continuation.} (17. Nd5 $1 {was the best move, but with great complications; most likely though it leads to an advantage for White.} exd5 18. exd5 f5 {this is the way obviously the game would continue because ...f6 would lead to a perpetual check immediately, which would mean that I was out of the World Cup...} 19. dxc6 {with great advantage for White, but still a lot of work has to be done from the white side to prove it at the board.} Bf6 {White has the initiative and great attacking chances after} 20. Rhe1 {but it would be a long and complicated game.}) 17... f5 18. Bxf5 {Fantastic play by White! Lenier had 9 minutes left after this move until the time control (move 40 plus 30 second per move). It is also interesting in such a situation which line do you start calculating, because if the first line looks good enough, you might simply have no time and motivation to look for more. In these kind of situations intuition plays an important role. I believe Lenier thought that this was maybe not the very best, but it would be good for a draw at least ...} (18. Qg6+ {looks very promising, but it's not clear how White will break through} Ng7 19. g4 {for example when Black can lose the thread immediately by playing the logical ...f8 then the trouble comes after xf5 exf5 and d5 with a decisive attack by White} dxe5 20. gxf5 exf5 21. Rhg1 Bf8 {and in the following lines somehow Black can save.}) (18. g4 Bf8) (18. exf6 {I think this would be the most promising continuation for White!} Bxf6 19. Ne4 Qg7 20. Nxf6+ Nxf6 21. Qh4 $44 {with good compensation for White.}) 18... exf5 19. Nd5 {Starts to get really scary from the black side... I really have to watch my step, if ...d7 then d3 and it is over, but I found my way to make the game go on.} (19. Qg6+ Ng7 20. Nd5 {would allow Black to play} Qd7 21. Nf6+ Bxf6 22. exf6 Qf7 {and Black is taking over} 23. Qg5 Ra7) 19... Bf8 {Forcing White to enter an endgame with unbalanced material.} (19... Qa7 20. Rd3 Bf8 21. Rg3+ Bg7 22. Nf6+ Kf8 23. Nh7+ $11) 20. Nxc7 Bxh6 21. Nxa8 Bxf4 {Now White has a rook and 2 pawns for the two bishops. This would give a clear advantage to White under normal circumstances, but the a8-knight gives White difficulties.} 22. exd6 Bxd6 23. Nb6 {White has to hurry up to get his knight into safety.} Be6 24. Nd5 Kf7 {I was not sure about the exact evaluation during the game, but I thought that it was pretty balanced and if I could activate my pieces I could get some practical chances.} 25. Ne3 {White is playing for safety and provokes me to push my pawn.} (25. h4 {was worth considering, it is always nice to have a passed pawn.}) 25... Nf6 {I was happy to get some activity and aimed to get more space if I could reach the e4-square with my knight.} 26. g3 {Due to Black having very few pawns there are not many chances to win, but if the battle goes on White might get nervous and then might make unneccesery mistakes.} Ng4 {This was an unexpected desicion on my part, but I thought that to change the pawn structure can creat more options and the pawn on g4 will blockade the pawns on the kingside.} (26... Ne4 27. Rhf1 Kg6 28. Nd2 {would give White a comfortable game.}) 27. Nxg4 fxg4 28. Nd4 {White is eager to exchange pieces.} (28. Rhe1 {would be the best move, the 2 rooks in the central files are always good! Black will not be able to get out from the pin on the d-file with out exchanging the rooks and then the winning chances for Black are very slim.}) 28... Nxd4 29. Rxd4 Bc7 {I can not avoid the rook exchange anyway so I offered it.} 30. Rf1+ Ke7 31. Re4 {A strange decision.} ({The best choice would have been for White to exchange the rooks} 31. Rxd8 Bxd8 32. c3 {and it is very difficult to imagine that Black would win in this game.}) 31... Rg8 {With the rooks on the board there can be more opportunities for Black to create some winning chances, if White makes a mistake.} 32. a4 {Of course, White is looking to exchange pawns and with each exchange he gets closer to the draw.} Bd6 33. axb5 axb5 34. Rf5 b4 35. Rh5 Rg6 {Trying to free my king with ...d7-c6.} 36. h3 {This is White made his rook manoeuvre to h5. I thought that after this it's a draw, but of course I was going to fight on.} gxh3 37. Rxh3 Kd7 38. Rh7+ (38. Rhh4 {would threaten to get rid of the last black pawn on b4 and to enter a theoretical drawn endgame.} Rxg3 {now the simplest draw is} 39. Rh7+ Be7 40. b3) 38... Kc6 39. b3 {White had already been in serious time trouble for the last 7 moves and there was great tension due to the match situation... after all the draw for White would mean winning the match and moving on to the next round!} ({And now finally White could have forced a theoretical draw by} 39. Rhh4 $1 {and grab the b4-pawn by sacrificing back an exchange.}) 39... Bd5 {It is always difficult when you have many options which look all the same as here where to move the rook? The clock is ticking and ticking and you see that you only have seconds left...} 40. Re3 (40. Reh4 $1 {This is not an easy move to complete the time control. It would be still save the game because I can not take the g3-pawn with either of my pieces in a good way.} Re6 {I could fight on by avoiding the exchange of my last pawn on the board} (40... Rxg3 {to} 41. R7h6 {is the move and White will then play xd6 and take my pawn on b4 with a drawn game.}) (40... Bxg3 41. Rxb4)) 40... Bxg3 {And now after the time trouble White still is able to reach the theoretical draw, but maybe my opponent underestimated my chances. White has to be quick, otherwise if my bishop can reach the h8-a1 diagonal White will be in big trouble. So White should have played now c3.} 41. Ra7 {White had to realise that once I blockade the c2-pawn, it is practically winning for Black!} (41. c3 $1 {with a worse version than before, but White could still force the exchange of the pawns and transpose to a rook-bishop against rook and pawn endgame.} Bf4 42. Rhe7 Bxe3 43. Rxe3) 41... Rg4 42. Ra4 {White is playing of course for c3, but while looking the best moment to do so he misses the last moment for it.} (42. c3 $1 Bf2 43. Rae7 {only draws} Bxe3 44. Rxe3) 42... Bf4 43. Re1 (43. Re2 {was giving more difficulties for Black.}) 43... Bd2 {Now finally I got the winning position. White is very passive and does not have counterplay, so has to sit and wait, which is really very unpleasant for White.} 44. Rd1 Bc3 45. Ra6+ Kb7 46. Ra5 Be4 {It is clear that Black's main target is the c2-pawn and the weekness of the back rank, but I will need the help of my king.} 47. Ra4 {White's only option to wait to see whether Black can or cannot break through.} Rg2 48. Ra2 Kb6 49. Rd6+ Kb5 50. Rd1 Bf3 51. Rf1 {I was kind of nervous already because I felt that the position is winning, but did not have the patience to figure it all out and think for a while, I was very excited and played pretty fast.} Kc5 52. Ra7 Be4 53. Rc1 {It was time to go with my king forward to d4, but I still made some unnecessery moves before doing so.} Kb6 {White can not leave the a-file with the rook becaue then I would bring my rook back to g8 and the plan would be ...a8 and ...a1 mate.} 54. Ra2 Rg3 55. Rf1 Bg7 56. Kc1 Rg2 57. Kb1 Rd2 58. Kc1 Rh2 59. Kb1 Bc3 60. Rd1 {Practically I had not made progress in the last moves so it was all about time.} Bf3 61. Rf1 Kc5 {Finally I bring in my king, and in endgames the king is one of the strongest attacking pieces.} 62. Ra7 Be4 63. Rc1 Kd4 64. Rd7+ Ke3 65. Re7 Rh6 66. Ra7 Bd2 67. Rg1 Kf2 68. Rd1 Ke2 69. Rg1 Be3 {By this part of the game I was really excited about levelling the match!} (69... Rh3 {was also possible to attack the b3-pawn} 70. Kb2 Rc3) 70. Re7 {White is fighting and looking for the only moves to fight for a draw, but...} Rh4 {Now White can not keep his defence on the 1st rank with his rook, it has to move.} 71. Rg8 Bd4 72. Ka2 {trying to escape from the mate on the 1st rank} Kd2 73. Rd7 Bxc2 {Suddenly the unexpected ...b1+ is the threat and then h1-a1 mate!} 74. Rh8 {A very good practical try and of course I was surprised by this move.} (74. Rb8 {After the game Zoli said b8 is a draw,...but :)} Bb1+ {which unfortunately I did not see. I was already very tired...} 75. Kxb1 Rh1+ 76. Ka2 Ra1#) 74... Rxh8 {Yes I could have finish the game by playing ...c1 with a study like mating net after xh4 b1 mate!! But I thought that I could reach the theoretical win in the rook, bishop against rook endgame. Actually I was right, but not exactly the way I did it...} (74... Kc1 $1 {At this point I was so excited at the thought of winning the game, that did not realise that ...c1 would win on the spot!}) 75. Rxd4+ Bd3 76. Rxb4 (76. Kb2 Rb8 {wins}) 76... Kc3 {When I took the h8-rook I was aware that I will reach the winning Philidor position, but not exactly as I was did it...} 77. Ra4 Rh2+ (77... Rb8 $3 {This move crossed my mind, but I calculated and rejected it in favour of the following line:} 78. Ra3 Rb4 79. Ra4 {this is why I did not do this, and at this point I did not see the ...b4 stalemate!!} Rxb3 {It is a theoritical winning position with this material because of the badly placed king and rook.} 80. Ra3 {I thought this was a draw:((( but ...b1+ and wins the rook.}) 78. Ka3 {At this point I realised that I messed it up, but} Rb2 {At this point White had a minute left, so it was possible to make a mistake but I had realised already that there would be a beautiful stalemate idea by playing b4.} 79. Rg4 $2 (79. Rb4 $3 Rb1 {and} 80. Ka2 Kxb4 {I like these kind of beautiful things, but not during this game I guess.}) 79... Rxb3+ 80. Ka4 {I knew it exactly that this is a winning position, but along the way somehow I did get confused. I do not have to do any hard work to get the win, and had it right there, but I had also few minutes left only and nerves are also part of the game...} Rb1 81. Ka5 Rb5+ 82. Ka4 Rf5 83. Rg3 Rf4+ $2 (83... Rf1 $1 {wins on the spot! Of course I am asking myself how is it possible that I did not see it duiring the game, but...} 84. Ka5 Rf6 {Over.}) 84. Ka3 Rf1 85. Rg2 {Here I started to get nervous. Knowing that I have a win, how is it possible that I can not do it when there is so much at stake. Actually this was exactly the reason that my brain could not think properly... I guess.} Rh1 (85... Rb1 $1 {Zugzwang! This is the position I got finally after the 106th move.}) 86. Rb2 {This made me nervous, because I forgot this move:( and here I started to get mad and angry with myself.} Ra1+ 87. Ra2 Rb1 88. Rg2 Rb3+ 89. Ka4 Rb4+ 90. Ka3 Rb6 (90... Rb1 $22) 91. Rg4 Ra6+ 92. Ra4 Rb6 93. Rg4 Rb7 94. Rh4 Rb1 95. Rh2 Rb6 (95... Bf1 $22) 96. Rh4 Bf1 {I started to get the point but still not the right way.} 97. Rg4 Rb5 98. Rg3+ Bd3 99. Rg4 Rb1 100. Rg2 Rb3+ 101. Ka4 Rb5 102. Rg4 Rf5 {I had exactly the same position at move 82, so I really made myself and my fans very nervous...} 103. Ka3 Rf1 104. Rg2 Rb1 {Finally I got the point, but could I reach it in time?} 105. Rh2 Bf5 {Immediately when I completed my move I realised that I had to go to f1.} 106. Rg2 Bd3 {Polgar,J: '!' '' '!' '' '!' '''Polgar,Judit: '!' '' 'Polgar,J: '!' '''' Polgar,J: 'Zugzwang! This'Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J: 'Zugzwang! This is the position I got finally after the 106th move.''' is the position I got finally after the 106th move.'} 107. Rh2 {It is amazing, that finally when I reached the winning position, White could have stopped the clock and asked for a draw from the arbiter by the rule of 3 fold repetition!! The strange thing is that Domingues was checking the scoresheet carefully in the last 20 moves, probably for a possible repetition, but on the other hand was not sure exactly which position really occurred 3 times. The reality is that by making the move h2 this was the third time that we had it on the board in move 95-105 and now... Maybe he was afraid to claim because he was not sure, if he is not correct the game stops for some minutes and I would figure things out.... well luck is something you need in chess as well as in life!} Bf1 {Polgar,J: '' '' '''Polgar,Judit: '' 'Polgar,J: '''' Yessss.....!!!! I did it, when I think already everybody had given up thinking that I would be able to prove the win...} 108. Rf2 Bc4 109. Rf3+ Bd3 110. Rf2 Rb3+ 111. Ka2 (111. Ka4 Rb6 {[%csl Yf5]}) 111... Rb6 112. Ka1 Rg6 {Oh I was really happy to be able to win this game and equalise the match. I was only happy and not thinking about the following day, about the playoff. Just enjoy the victory!} (112... Rg6 113. Rg2 {was the last chance but} Re6) 0-1

