1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Qc7 5. O-O Nd4 6. d3 ({We'll consider the more important option,} 6. Re1 {, in Kovacevic-Lenic.}) ({I'm less convinced by d3 here than a move earlier, though, and neither should} 6. Bc4 {be too problematic for Black:} e6 7. d3 Ng4 $1 8. g3 Nxf3+ 9. Qxf3 Ne5 10. Qe2 Be7 11. Be3 Nxc4 12. dxc4 O-O 13. Rad1 a6 14. Bf4 Qc6 15. Rd3 b5 {gave the second player good counterplay in Buscher-Naiditsch, European Club Cup, Plovdiv 2010.}) 6... a6 ({Polgar is happy to play by analogy with the 2 Nc3 Nc6 3 Bb5 Nd4 variation. Here too one can make a case for} 6... Ng4 {, although after} 7. g3 Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3 Ne5 {White must be slightly for choice with} 9. Qe2 {followed by f4,} ({whereas} 9. Qh5 g6 10. Nd5 Qd6 11. Qe2 Bg7 12. Bg5 f6 $1 {enabled Black to solve his problems with} 13. Bd2 e6 14. Ne3 a6 15. f4 Nf7 16. Ba4 b5 17. Bb3 Bb7 {in Tseshkovsky-Vaisser, Arco 2010.})) 7. Ba4 b5 8. Bb3 Nxb3 $1 {Black removes the bishop, rather than allow White to exchange on d4 and obtain a bit of pressure after 10 Ne2.} 9. axb3 Bb7 ({This quickly gives rise to an unbalanced, complex middlegame. An alternative approach is} 9... b4 {and after} 10. Nb1 (10. e5 $5 {, with the idea of} bxc3 11. exf6 cxb2 12. Bxb2 gxf6 13. Re1 {and decent pressure for the pawn, could well be a stiffer test}) 10... g6 11. Nbd2 Bg7 12. Nc4 d5 $1 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Re1 O-O {Black was pretty comfortably placed in Voronov-S.Zhigalko, Alushta 2010.}) 10. Bg5 ({White often goes here in the closely-related 2 Nc3 Nc6 3 Bb5 system. Here he might have played more aggressively in the centre, but after} 10. e5 $5 Nd5 $1 ({better than} 10... Ng4 11. Bf4 e6 12. Qe2 {when White has definite chances to obtain the upper hand}) 11. Nxd5 ({White's position looks a little overextended if anything after} 11. Ne4 e6 12. c4 Nb4) 11... Bxd5 12. c4 Bb7 13. d4 cxd4 14. Qxd4 e6 {while matters wouldn't have been at all clear, it would be a surprise if Black was worse with his light-squared bishop an especially useful asset.}) 10... e6 11. Bxf6 gxf6 12. Qe2 Bd6 $1 {Polgar develops her second bishop as actively as possible and now we have a classic tussle between White's knights and slightly better structure and Black's bishops. I can think of several players who would be happy to slowly manoeuvre in White's shoes, but Black's position is hardly bad and does contain a fair amount of dynamic potential.} 13. Rfd1 $6 ({This doesn't achieve too much. Better would have been the immediate} 13. g3 {, aiming to expand while restricting Black's bishops with Nh4 and f4.}) 13... Rg8 14. g3 ({Was Edouard really initially intending} 14. d4 cxd4 15. Rxd4 Bc5 16. Rd3 Qf4 {, which looks like a pretty decent version of an Open Sicilian for Black, and only here changed his mind?}) 14... Rc8 15. Nh4 Rg5 $5 16. Re1 ({Black's aggressive-looking last causes White to reroute his other knight, but he might also have followed through with} 16. Kf1 Ke7 17. f4 {when} Rg7 18. Qh5 {would have kept the lid on Black's bishops and potential for the time being.}) 16... Ke7 17. Nd1 Rcg8 18. Ne3 Kd8 19. Neg2 ({I'm not particularly convinced by White's knight manoeuvre, especially if going to g2 is the best he can do. By now} 19. Kf1 {was no longer such a good option, though, with} f5 $5 20. exf5 Qc6 21. f3 Rh5 {then one possibility, offering Black some pressure and full compensation for the pawn.}) 19... Qc6 20. Rad1 f5 $1 {Having improved her king and then queen, it's time for Polgar to begin to lever open White's camp, solid though his defences still appear.} 21. c3 $2 ({White's play over the last five or so moves has likely not been too accurate, although we shouldn't forget that this was a rapid game. Here he would have done better with} 21. Kf1 $5 {, one important idea of which is that} f4 22. Nf3 R5g7 23. Nxf4 Bxf4 24. gxf4 f5 25. Qe3 {keeps everything defended and seems fine for White.}) 21... f4 $1 22. d4 $5 ({The Frenchman goes for counterplay and who can blame him? Had White defended with, say,} 22. Nf3 Rg4 23. Kf1 {then} fxg3 24. hxg3 f5 {would have seen the second f-pawn coming into play with some effect.}) 22... cxd4 23. cxd4 $2 ({I dare say that Black's slightly-cheeky 24th came as a surprise to White who was no doubt mainly focussed on the pressure down the g-file. Here he had to go} 23. Rxd4 {, preparing counterplay down the d-file with} fxg3 24. hxg3 Rg4 25. Red1 {.}) 23... fxg3 24. hxg3 Bb4 $1 {Polgar's keen eye has spotted that White's powerful-looking central pawns are actually pretty weak.} 25. Rc1 Qb6 26. Red1 Rg4 {Winning a pawn.} 27. e5 Rxd4 28. Qh5 $6 ({Once again} 28. Kf1 {would have been a stiffer defence, rather promising for Black though this would have been with her extra pawn and line-moving pieces. Edouard tries to obtain some counterplay, but only makes matters worse for himself.}) 28... Rxg3 29. Qxf7 Be7 30. Kf1 Rgg4 $6 ({This seems strong, but gives White a resource. Instead} 30... Rxd1+ 31. Rxd1 Rh3 $1 32. Qg8+ Kc7 33. Rc1+ Bc6 {would have concluded matters on the spot.}) 31. Qxh7 $6 ({Missing the chance to grovel with} 31. Nf3 Rxd1+ 32. Rxd1 Qc6 33. Nge1 {, but no doubt both players were low on time by this point and Edouard may well also have been pretty demoralised.}) 31... Rxd1+ 32. Rxd1 Qc6 33. f3 Rg3 34. Qd3 Rh3 $1 {Now Polgar hits on the right idea and goes after White's rather shaky king.} 35. Qd4 Kc8 (35... Rxf3+ $5 36. Nxf3 Qxf3+ 37. Ke1 Qg3+ {would also have done the business.}) 36. Qf4 Qc2 37. Rd2 Qc1+ 38. Ke2 Rh1 39. Kf2 Qg1+ 0-1

