1. e4 {Psakhis,L} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. Bxd7+ Nxd7 5. O-O Ngf6 6. Qe2 e6 {*} 7. b3 Be7 8. Bb2 O-O 9. c4 a6 (9... Re8 10. d4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 a6 12. Nc3 Qc7 13. f4 (13. Rac1 Rad8 14. Kh1 Qc5 15. Rfd1 Bf8 16. Nc2 Qc7 17. Ne3 Qb8 18. Ng4 Nxg4 19. Qxg4 b5 20. Qe2 b4 {½-½ Svetushkin,D-Ristic,N/EUCup Krunica 1997/CBM 62 (88)}) (13. Rad1 Bf8 $1 ({exploiting the white queen's being on e2 to threaten 14...d5, because} 13... d5 $2 {<xml:en>doesn't yet quite work due to</:en/xml>} 14. cxd5 exd5 15. Nf5 $1 dxe4 16. Nxe4 $1 {<xml:en>which is rather strong and neatly exploits the pressure down the long diagonal, as</:en/xml>} Nxe4 17. Rxd7 $1 Qxd7 18. Qg4 {<xml:en>wins the black queen</:en/xml>}) 14. f4 $5 Rad8 15. Kh1 g6 {reaches a tense position. This whole variation often hinges around whether White can achieve a successful e5-break as, due to the absence of light-squared bishops, playing on the queenside is rarely on for c4 becomes too weak.}) 13... Qc5 14. Na4 Qh5 15. Qxh5 Nxh5 16. f5 Bf6 17. fxe6 fxe6 18. Rad1 Nc5 19. g4 Nxa4 20. bxa4 Bxd4+ 21. Bxd4 e5 22. gxh5 exd4 23. Rxd4 $14 {1-0 Tal,M-Miles,A/Riga (izt) (54)}) 10. d4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Rc8 {<xml:en>Black hopes that the rook will prove useful here, with the pressure down the c-file assisting a successful ...b5 or ...d5 break. However, this doesn't have to be played immediately and Black can, and quite possibly should, prefer to first decide where to deploy his queen:</:en/xml>} ({<xml:en>a)</:en/xml>} 11... Qb6 $5 {<xml:en>again prevents 12 Nc3 and, just as in our previous game, is a little awkward for White to meet. Then</:en/xml>} 12. Rd1 {enables 13 Nc3, but White wouldn't usually want the king's rook here, and} Rfe8 13. Nc3 Rad8 $1 {(preparing to play in the centre, while White continues to sort out his position)} 14. Kh1 Ne5 $1 {(a key Hedgehog strategy for Black is to tempt the white pawns forward; such a plan must be well calculated, but here Ftacnik has realised that an advance of the f-pawn doesn't really help White, but does create weaknesses)} 15. f4 $6 Ng6 16. f5 ({or} 16. Qf3 e5 $1) 16... exf5 17. exf5 Bf8 18. Qf1 Ne5 19. h3 d5 $1 {saw Black achieve his main break and be left with the better and more harmonious position in S.Movsesian-L.Ftacnik, Prague (blitz) 1999.}) ({b)} 11... Qc7 12. Nc3) 12. Nc3 Qc7 13. Rad1 Rfe8 14. Kh1 Bf8 15. f4 {Both sides have developed consistently with their overall approaches and we are now at a critical juncture with Black needing to find a way to deal with 16 Nf3 and 17 e5.} Qa5 {'?!'} ({The queen continues to cover e5 from here, but Fressinet is happy to sacrifice his e-pawn after which the queen is not too happy on a5. However, Black should here avoid the tempting break} 15... e5 $6 {due to} 16. Nf5 $1 exf4 17. Rxf4 {when} Ne5 18. Qd2 {keeps up the pressure and favours White.}) ({Probably Black's best is simply not to panic with De Firmian's calm} 15... h6 $5 {(usefully preventing the f3-knight from leaping to g5)} 16. Nf3 Red8 $1 {. Then} 17. e5 dxe5 18. fxe5 Ne8 {(covering d6)} 19. Ne4 Nc5 $1 {prepared exchanges and was fine for Black after} 20. Ng3 Rxd1 21. Rxd1 Rd8 22. Bd4 a5 $1 {in R.Fontaine-N.de Firmian, Stockholm 2004.}) 16. Nf3 $146 (16. Rd3 g6 17. Nf3 Nh5 18. g3 b5 19. cxb5 axb5 20. e5 d5 21. Nd4 b4 22. Na4 Qa6 23. g4 Ng7 24. Nf3 Rc7 {0-1 Fette,M-Gallagher,J/Krumbach op 1991 (36)}) (16. Ba1 {Ü 61/(204) Ü 61/(204)}) 16... Red8 17. e5 {'!'} dxe5 18. fxe5 Ng4 19. Ng5 {'!'} Ndxe5 {'!'} ({Black must try to exchange rooks, whereas It was to dangerous to play} 19... Ngxe5 $2 20. Qe4 $1 {would have been rather strong as after} (20. Qh5 Nf6 21. Rxd8 Nxh5 22. Rxc8 Nf6 23. Rd1 $13) 20... g6 ({or} 20... f5 21. Qh4 Nf6 22. Nxe6 Rxd1 23. Nxd1 $1 {and White is much better}) {there is the thematic sacrifice} 21. Nxf7 $1 Nxf7 22. Qxe6 Nde5 23. Nd5 $1 Rc6 $8 24. Ne7+ Bxe7 25. Qxe7 $16 {[%csl Gb2,Ge7,Gf1,Rg8] (Psakhis) when White's colossal pressure ensures that he will regain the piece while maintaining a crushing position. but saving herself from one threat, Black is under a new one.}) 20. Nd5 ({Continuing to play for the attack, although the simpler} 20. Nxf7 $1 {'!?'} Rxd1 (20... Nxf7 21. Rxd8 Nxd8 22. Qxg4 $16 {[%csl Gb2,Gf1,Gg4]}) 21. Nxd1 $5 {'!'} Nxf7 (21... Nxh2 22. Nxe5 (22. Kxh2 Nxf7 23. Qxe6 Qh5+ $1) 22... Nxf1 23. Qxf1 $16) 22. Qxg4 Re8 $14 23. a3 {would have left White with a pretty pleasant advantage.}) 20... exd5 $6 ({The exchanges don't help Black and so she should have tried to change the course of the struggle with Possibly Judit could play superbrave} 20... Qxa2 $5 {'!' <xml:en>, intending</:en/xml> for example:} 21. Qc2 Ng6 22. Ra1 $140 Qxa1 23. Bxa1 Rxd5 $13 {<xml:en>with reasonable compensation for the queen.</:en/xml>}) 21. Bxe5 Nxe5 (21... dxc4 $2 22. Qxg4 Rxd1 23. Qxd1 Qxe5 24. Qh5 $18) 22. Qxe5 Rc7 $5 23. cxd5 $6 ({<xml:en>Here Kaufman's</:en/xml> Black experiences much more difficulties after} 23. Qf4 $5 {[%cal Gd1d8,Gf1f7] <xml:en>was much stronger when f7 falls and Black is close to being lost after</:en/xml> for example} Bd6 $140 (23... Bb4 24. Rxd5 Rxd5 25. cxd5 Qb6 26. Nxf7 {<xml:en>due to the extra d-pawn and to the kingside threats. In the game Fressinet does force some kingside weaknesses, but Polgar defends strongly and in the end it's White who's left having to force the draw:</:en/xml>}) 24. Qf5 g6 25. Qh3 h5 26. Rxd5 $18) 23... Re7 (23... Qb6 $5 {bringing queen to the king side.}) 24. Qf5 $6 {Facing stubborn defense, Fressinet loses his rate of attack.} (24. Qf4 $140 $1 Rdd7 $140 25. d6 Re8 26. Nxf7 $18) 24... g6 25. Qh3 ({Worth considering} 25. Qf6 $5 Rdd7 26. d6 Re8 27. Nxf7 Bg7 28. Qf4 $14 {although, the lion's share of advantage had been already missed.}) 25... f5 $8 (25... h6 26. d6 Rxd6 27. Rxd6 Qxg5 28. Rd8 $16) 26. d6 Red7 27. g4 (27. Qd3 Re8 28. Qc4+ Kh8 $13) 27... f4 $5 28. Ne6 (28. Ne4 Kh8) 28... Re8 29. g5 $5 {It looks like White has managed to cause serious threats, but it is just an illusory thing.} ({The following does not give anything to White} 29. Nxf8 Rxf8 30. g5 Qf5) 29... Rxd6 $1 (29... Bxd6 30. Nxf4 {[%cal Gh3d7]} Rdd8 31. Ne6 Rd7 32. Nf8 $1 Rde7 33. Rxd6 Rxf8 34. Rxf8+ Kxf8 35. Rf6+ Rf7 $8 36. Rxf7+ Kxf7 37. Qxh7+ $16) (29... Qb5 $5) 30. Nxf8 Rxd1 31. Qxh7+ Kxf8 32. Qh8+ Kf7 (32... Ke7 $2 33. Qg7+ Kd8 34. Rxd1+ Kc8 35. Qd7+ $18) 33. Qf6+ Kg8 34. Qxg6+ Kf8 35. Qh6+ Kg8 36. Qg6+ (36. Rxd1 Re1+ 37. Rxe1 Qxe1+ 38. Kg2 Qe2+ 39. Kh3 f3) 36... Kf8 37. Rxd1 Re1+ 38. Rxe1 Qxe1+ 39. Kg2 Qe2+ 40. Kh3 f3 41. Qf5+ Ke7 42. Qf6+ Kd7 43. Qf7+ Kd6 44. Qf4+ Kc6 45. Qc4+ Kb6 46. Qd4+ Kc6 {½-½ (46) Fressinet,L-Polgar,J Enghien les Bains (Game 26) 2003 White queen and Black king had strange and very long dance together!} 1/2-1/2

