{B00: Queen's Fianchetto Defence, Nimzowitsch Defence} 1. e4 a6 {James Schuyler: '?!'} 2. d4 b5 {James Schuyler: '?!'} 3. a4 {James Schuyler: '!' James Schuyler: 'Not exactly a refutation, but this makes it clear from the very beginning that White will not be tolerating any nonsense. By fighting to take back Black's queenside space, Polgar nullifies Black's only asset. '} b4 4. Nf3 $146 (4. Nd2 g6 5. Ngf3 Bg7 6. Bc4 e6 7. Qe2 Ne7 8. e5 d5 9. Bd3 c5 10. dxc5 Nd7 11. Nb3 Qc7 12. Bf4 Nxc5 13. Nxc5 Qxc5 14. O-O h6 15. h4 Bb7 16. Qd2 Rd8 17. Be3 Qa5 18. c3 Nc6 {Svetushkin,D (2471)-Nanu,C (2420)/Bucharest 1999/CBM 069 ext/0-1 (43)}) (4. c4 bxc3 5. bxc3 e6 6. Nf3 Nf6 7. Bd3 d5 8. e5 Nfd7 9. Ng5 Be7 10. Qh5 g6 11. Qg4 c5 12. h4 Nc6 13. Qf4 Bxg5 14. hxg5 cxd4 15. cxd4 Nb4 16. Qd2 Qb6 17. Ba3 Rb8 18. Rh4 Nxd3+ {Ionescu,C (2456)-Nanu,C (2434)/ Bucharest 2005/CBM 108 ext/1-0}) 4... Bb7 5. Bd3 e6 6. O-O c5 7. Nbd2 cxd4 8. Nxd4 {James Schuyler: 'This is already a Sicilian gone wrong for Black. White's queen's knight is flexibly placed on d2, rather than stuck as a target on c3. '} Nf6 9. e5 Nd5 10. Nc4 Qc7 11. Qg4 Nc6 12. Nf3 g6 13. Qg3 h6 14. a5 {James Schuyler: 'From the opening moves it could be predicted that White would have a central space advantage, but the fact that Black is also cramped on the queenside is extremely ironic. '} Be7 15. Bd2 g5 16. Rfe1 Rg8 17. Nd6+ {James Schuyler: '!'} Bxd6 18. exd6 Qd8 19. Be4 Nf6 20. Bxc6 Bxc6 21. Ne5 Bd5 22. Bxb4 Rb8 23. Bc3 h5 24. Rad1 Rb5 25. Qd3 Bb7 26. Qd4 Rd5 27. Qa7 Qc8 $2 (27... Rxd1 28. Rxd1 Qa8 29. Qxa8+ Bxa8 30. Nd3 $18) 28. Nc4 {James Schuyler: '?' James Schuyler: 'Not at all the cleanest way to win, but that only emphasizes how terrible Black's position is. '} Rxd1 29. Rxd1 Nd5 30. Rxd5 Bxd5 31. Qc7 Qd8 32. Nb6 Bc6 33. Qa7 f5 34. Qxa6 g4 35. Nc8 Qg5 36. Ne7 Bb7 37. Qf1 Rf8 38. a6 Be4 39. b4 f4 $4 (39... h4 $142 40. Qe1 g3 $18) 40. Qc4 Ba8 (40... Bxc2 41. Qc8+ Kf7 42. Qxd7 $18) 41. Qc8+ Kf7 42. Qxd7 f3 (42... h4 $18) 43. Nd5