{Alexei Shirov: 'The game was annotated in November 1996 and published in 'New In Chess'. Before the present game I had lost two games in a row and had dropped from the lead almost to the middle of the field. A free day helped me to relax, but who knows what the story would have been if Judit hadn't fallen into some of my old analysis. Anyway, I am satisfied that after so many setbacks (after this game I also lost to Piket) I still managed to finish the tournament in third place by winning in the last round. '} 1. e4 {Shirov Shirov Boensch Shirov Shirov Boensch B84: Sicilian Scheveningen: 6 Be2 a6, lines without early Be3} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 ({After some bad games with} 6. Be3 {I decided to switch to a 'less aggressive' set-up.}) 6... e6 7. O-O Be7 8. f4 O-O 9. Be3 Qc7 {Alexei Shirov: 'A provocative line. As Vishy Anand once stated there is nothing to be expected from me in this position but the advance of the g-pawn.'} 10. g4 $1 {Alexei Shirov: 'Sutovsky had smashed Van Wely and Polgar earlier in the same tournament with this move, giving me even more confidence in White's attacking chances.'} {Alexei Shirov: 'The text (with a different move-order) occurred in Shirov-Anand, Buenos Aires 1994. After' Alexei Shirov: 'Nowadays after'} Re8 {Alexei Shirov: 'I continued'} (10... d5 {Boensch} 11. e5 (11. exd5 Nxd5 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Bd3 Bc5 14. c3 Nc6 15. f5 Re8 $11 {1-0 Renner,C-Siepelt,H/Bayern-ch 1991 (43)}) 11... Ne4 12. Nxe4 (12. Bd3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Qxc3 14. Rb1 $132 {1-0 Lauk,U-Odeev,H/Moscow olm 1994 (36)}) 12... dxe4 13. Qe1 (13. g5 Rd8 $1 (13... Nc6 14. c3 Bc5 15. Qc2 b5 16. Qxe4 Bb7 17. Bf3 $14 {½-½ Bologan,V-Novikov,I/New York op 1993 (45)}) 14. c3 Nd7 15. Qc2 Nc5 16. Rad1 b5 17. Nb3 Bb7 18. Rxd8+ Rxd8 19. Nxc5 Bxc5 $15 {½-½ Nevednichy,V-Timoshchenko,G/Biel op 1994 (44)}) 13... b5 14. Rc1 Nd7 15. c4 b4 16. Nb3 a5 17. c5 a4 18. Nd2 Bb7 $11 {1-0 Olafsson,F-Stein,L/Stockholm izt 1962 (31)}) ({Alexei Shirov: 'Van Wely continued'} 10... b5 {Boensch} 11. g5 Nfd7 12. Bd3 $1 (12. f5 $6 b4 13. fxe6 bxc3 14. exf7+ Kh8 $5 (14... Rxf7 $2 15. Rxf7 Kxf7 16. Ne6 $2 (16. Qf1+ $1 Nf6 (16... Kg8 $2 17. Bc4+ $18) 17. Bc4+ Ke8 18. gxf6 Bxf6 19. bxc3 Bb7 20. Ne6 Qe7 21. Qh3 Bxe4 22. Bb6 $18) 16... Qc6 17. Bh5+ Kg8 18. Bf7+ Kxf7 19. Qf3+ Nf6 $19 {0-1 Pogats,J-Orgovan,S/HUN-chT 1992 (42)}) 15. Ne6 Qb7 16. Bd4 Ne5 17. Nxf8 Bxf8 18. Bxc3 Qxe4 $132) 12... Re8 13. Qh5 g6 14. Qh4 b4 15. Nce2 Bb7 16. Rf3 h5 17. Ng3 Bf8 18. Nxh5 gxh5 19. Qxh5 Bg7 20. f5 exf5 21. Nxf5 Nf8 22. Nxg7 Kxg7 23. Bd4+ Re5 24. Raf1 {1-0 Sutovskij,E-Van Wely,L/Tilburg Fontys 1996}) (10... Nfd7 {Boensch} 11. g5 Nc6 12. f5 Nde5 (12... Nxd4 13. Qxd4 Re8 14. fxe6 $1 fxe6 15. Bh5 Rf8 16. Rxf8+ Bxf8 17. Rf1 Ne5 18. Nd5 $1 Qd8 19. Nb6 $16 {Short,N-Ljubojevic,L Novi Sad olm (11) ENG-JUG 1990 1-0 43}) 13. f6 Bd8 14. Bd3 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 Qa5 16. fxg7 Kxg7 17. Kh1 Bb6 18. Bxe5+ Qxe5 19. Qh5 Be3 20. Rf3 Bxg5 21. Rg1 f6 22. h4 {1-0 Sutovskij,E-Polgar,J/Tilburg Fontys 1996}) ({and Polgar} 10... Nc6 {.}) {Alexei Shirov: 'We shall see that 11...Bf8? just loses. As I remember, the main reason why I rejected'} 11. f5 $5 {Alexei Shirov: '...but then I decided not to play it. This time I saw less objection to the idea.' Alexei Shirov: 'against Anand was'} (11. g5 {Boensch} Nfd7 {Alexei Shirov: ','} 12. Bd3 {Alexei Shirov: 'seems more critical (instead of 12 Bh5) and this was the way Sutovsky played his games. Why didn't I follow his example? Because I remembered that during my game against Anand I was seriously considering the alternative...'} (12. Bh5 {Alexei Shirov: '!?'} g6 (12... Bf8 $4 13. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 14. Qh5+ Ke7 15. f5 $18) (12... Nc6 $2 13. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 14. Qh5+ $18) 13. Bg4 {Alexei Shirov: 'and won a complicated battle.'} Bf8 (13... Nc6 $2 14. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 15. Bxe6+ $16) (13... Bd8 $5 14. Qd2 $6 Nc6 15. Rad1 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 b5 17. Qf2 Bb7 18. Rd3 Nf8 19. b4 Qc4 20. a3 Rc8 21. Qh4 h5 22. Bxh5 gxh5 23. Qxh5 Qxd4+ $3 $17 {Xie Jun-Bischoff,K Baden Baden-B (08) 1992 0-1 34}) 14. f5 (14. Qf3 Nc6 15. Qh3 Nb6 16. f5 exf5 17. exf5 d5 18. Rad1 Bc5 $132 {Zigouris,H-Rychagov,M Katerini op 1992 0-1 28}) (14. a4 Nc6 15. Qe2 Nxd4 16. Bxd4 b6 17. Qf2 Bb7 18. Rae1 Re7 19. h4 Rae8 20. Bh3 e5 21. fxe5 Nxe5 $10 {Polgar,J-Polugaevsky,L (3) Aruba m 1991 ½ 28}) 14... Ne5 15. fxe6 fxe6 (15... Bxe6 $2 16. Bxe6 fxe6 17. Nxe6 $1 Rxe6 18. Qd5 Qc4 19. Qxb7 Qc6 20. Rxf8+ $1 Kxf8 21. Qxh7 $18) 16. Rf6 Nxg4 17. Qxg4 Nc6 $2 18. Nxc6 bxc6 19. e5 $1 $16 {Shirov,A-Anand,V Buenos Aires 1994 1-0 47}) 12... Nc6 13. Qh5 g6 14. Qh4 Bf8 15. Rf3 Bg7 16. Nde2 b5 17. Rh3 (17. Raf1 Bb7 18. Rh3 Nf8 19. f5 Ne5 20. f6 Bh8 21. Nf4 Rac8 22. Bd4 Red8 23. Bxe5 dxe5 24. Nxg6 fxg6 25. f7+ $18 {Shchekachev,A-Newerovski,G Kstovo op (05) 1994 1-0 33 - EXP 42}) 17... Nf8 18. f5 Ne5 19. f6 Bh8 20. a3 $1 $16 {1-0 Kasparov,G-Anand,V/Moscow PCA-GP 1996 (34)}) 11... Bf8 $6 {Alexei Shirov: '?' Alexei Shirov: 'This is what Anand suggested in our post-mortem but later the same evening I found a crushing piece sacrifice. Of course, I kept my discovery more or less secret, but when I learned that it had occurred in the rapid game Wolff-Rychagov (Hastings PCA qualifier, 1995), I no longer expected to be able to play it myself. Fortunately the Wolff game didn't get into the main databases.'} (11... h6 {Alexei Shirov: 'with the idea'} 12. h4 $6 d5 $1 {.}) 12. g5 Nfd7 {} 13. Nxe6 $1 {Alexei Shirov: 'This is the killer move. Curiously, the position after Black's 12th move occurred in some other games (Nevednichy-Nisipeanu, Romania 1995, for example) played at a normal time control, and White didn't find this refutation of Black's setup.'} (13. fxe6 {Boensch} fxe6 14. Bh5 g6 15. Bg4 Ne5 16. Rf6 (16. Nxe6 Rxe6 $1 $8 17. Nd5 Qd8 18. Nb6 Nxg4 19. Qxg4 Re8 20. Qf3 Be6 21. Nxa8 Nd7 $17) 16... Nxg4 17. Qxg4 Nd7 18. Rxe6 (18. Raf1 Ne5 19. Qh3 Bg7 20. Nxe6 Qd7 21. Nd5 Rxe6 22. Qxe6+ Qxe6 23. Rxe6 Bxe6 24. Nc7 Nc4 25. Bf4 Bh3 $15) 18... Ne5 19. Rxe8 Nxg4 20. Rf1 Qf7 21. Rxf7 Kxf7 22. Rxf8+ Kxf8 23. Bf4 Ne5 24. Bxe5 dxe5 25. Nf3 Be6 26. Nxe5 {½-½ Nevednichy,V-Nisipeanu,L/Bucharest Ciocaltea 1995}) 13... fxe6 14. Bh5 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'By attacking the rook White gains time to create decisive mating threats. Black has no defence because her pieces are completely misplaced.'} g6 (14... Qd8 {Alexei Shirov: 'loses by force after'} 15. fxe6 Nc5 (15... Ne5 16. Bf7+ Kh8 17. Bxe8 Qxe8 18. Nd5 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins'}) 16. Bf7+ Kh8 17. Bxe8 Qxe8 18. Bxc5 dxc5 19. Nd5 Ra7 20. Qh5 g6 21. Qf3 Bg7 22. e7 Kg8 23. Qb3 Kh8 24. Nf6 $18) ({Alexei Shirov: 'and Black's fate is rather similar after'} 14... Re7 15. fxe6 Nb6 16. Qf3 Qd8 17. Bxb6 Qxb6+ 18. Rf2 $18 {Alexei Shirov: '.'}) 15. fxg6 Re7 (15... hxg6 {Boensch} 16. Bxg6 Re7 17. Qh5 $18) 16. Nd5 $1 {Alexei Shirov: 'I don't know whether White has other ways to win the game but this one is simple and effective.'} exd5 17. Qxd5+ Kh8 18. gxh7 $1 {Alexei Shirov: 'After long thought, I didn't go in for another sacrifice.'} ({Alexei Shirov: 'In fact'} 18. Rxf8+ $6 Nxf8 19. Rf1 {Alexei Shirov: 'would probably also have won but I preferred to keep it simple. The following variations justify the second offer, but are not so easy to calculate and be sure about during a game:'} Nxg6 $1 (19... Ne6 20. Bd4+ Kg8 (20... Rg7 21. Rf7 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins'}) 21. Qf5 $1 Qd7 22. Qf6 Rg7 23. Bg4 Qe7 24. gxh7+ Rxh7 25. Bxe6+ Bxe6 26. Qg6+ Rg7 27. Bxg7 Qxg7 28. Qxe6+ Kh8 29. Qe8+ Kh7 30. Rf7 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'is decisive.'}) (19... Z0) 20. Bd4+ ({Alexei Shirov: 'not'} 20. Bxg6 $2 Be6 21. Bd4+ Kg8 22. Bxh7+ Kxh7 23. g6+ Kg8 24. Qh5 Rg7) 20... Re5 21. Bxg6 Qe7 22. Bxe5+ (22. Rf7 Qxg5+ 23. Kf2 Qh4+ 24. Kg1 Qg5+ $10 {Alexei Shirov: 'is a draw'}) 22... dxe5 23. Bxh7 Qxg5+ 24. Kh1 Nc6 (24... Kxh7 25. Qf7+ Qg7 26. Qh5+ Kg8 27. Qe8+ Kh7 28. Qxc8 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins'}) 25. Rg1 Qxg1+ 26. Kxg1 Kxh7 27. Qf7+ $16 {Alexei Shirov: 'with a clear advantage for White.'}) 18... Rxh7 (18... Kxh7 {Boensch} 19. Rf7+ Kh8 20. Bd4+ Re5 21. Bg6 $1 Bg7 22. Rxg7 Rxd5 (22... Kxg7 23. Qf7+ Kh8 24. Qh7#) 23. Rh7+ Kg8 24. Rh8#) (18... Rg7 {Boensch} 19. Bd4 Kxh7 (19... Ne5 20. Rxf8+ Kxh7 21. g6+ Nxg6 22. Bxg6+ Kxg6 (22... Rxg6+ 23. Kh1 $18) 23. Rh8 $18) 20. Qf5+ Kh8 21. Bg6 $18) 19. Bg6 $5 {Alexei Shirov: 'A long time ago Vladimir Bagirov taught me (I believe that he himself was taught it by Vladimir Makogonov) about brilliancy in chess – 'A brilliancy is when you attack a piece and there is nothing to defend it'. This is exactly the case with the rook on h7; once again the co-ordination of Black's pieces speaks for itself.'} ({Alexei Shirov: 'However White had a second winning line which would have been even better:'} 19. g6 $1 Rg7 (19... Re7 20. Bg4 {wins}) 20. Bg4 Rxg6 21. Bd4+ {Alexei Shirov: '!' Alexei Shirov: '(I considered the move 19 g6 during the game but I probably overlooked this very important check)'} Nf6 (21... Ne5 22. Rxf8+ Kh7 23. Bxe5 Rxg4+ 24. Kh1 dxe5 25. Rf7+ Rg7 26. Rxc7 Rxc7 27. Qxe5 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins'}) 22. Rxf6 Rxf6 23. Bxf6+ Bg7 24. Qh5+ Kg8 25. Qe8+ Kh7 26. Bxc8 Bxf6 27. Bf5+ $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'finishes the game.'}) 19... Bg7 {'?' '?'} ({Alexei Shirov: 'The point of White's play was that'} 19... Qc6 {Alexei Shirov: 'is impossible due to'} 20. Rxf8+ Nxf8 21. Bd4+ Rg7 22. Bxg7+ Kxg7 23. Qf7+ Kh8 24. Qxf8# {Alexei Shirov: '.'}) ({Alexei Shirov: 'Nor could Black move his rook, e.g.:'} 19... Rg7 20. Bd4 Nc6 (20... Qd8 {Boensch} 21. Rf4 $18) 21. Rf4 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'with inevitable mate'}) ({Alexei Shirov: 'or'} 19... Re7 20. Rf4 $18 (20. Rf3 Nf6 $18) 20... Bh6 21. Rh4 {and White wins.}) 20. Bxh7 {Threatening mate... how?} Qd8 (20... Kxh7 21. g6+ Kxg6 (21... Kh8 {Boensch} 22. Qh5+ Kg8 23. Qh7#) 22. Qf5# {Alexei Shirov: '.'}) 21. Bf5 {Threatening mate: Rf3} ({Alexei Shirov: 'I was slightly lacking in self-confidence and so chose the more secure continuation instead of the forced win'} 21. Qf7 $1 Kxh7 ({Alexei Shirov: 'or'} 21... Ne5 22. Qh5 Bg4 23. Qh4 $18) 22. Qh5+ Kg8 23. g6 Nf6 (23... Nf8 24. Rxf8+ Kxf8 25. Rf1+ Ke8 26. Qd5 Qe7 27. Qg8+ Kd7 28. Rf7 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins'}) 24. Rxf6 Qxf6 25. Rf1 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'and Black is lost.'}) 21... Ne5 22. Qd1 {Do you see the mate threat? Alexei Shirov: '!'} Bxf5 ({Alexei Shirov: 'The alternatives'} 22... Qe8 23. g6 $18) ({Alexei Shirov: 'and'} 22... Kg8 23. Qh5 Bxf5 24. exf5 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'are no better.'}) 23. exf5 (23. Rxf5 $6 Qe8 $16) 23... Qe8 24. g6 {Alexei Shirov: '!' Alexei Shirov: 'The simplest.'} Ng4 (24... Nxg6 25. Qh5+ Kg8 26. Rae1 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'wins.'}) (24... Kg8 {doesn't change anything anymore} 25. Qd5+ Kh8 26. Qg2 Nxg6 27. Qh3+ (27. Qxg6 $6 Qxe3+ 28. Kh1 Qe4+ (28... Bxb2 $4 {A poison bait which should not be taken} 29. Rae1 Nc6 30. Rxe3 Bg7 31. f6 Bh6 32. Qxh6+ Kg8 33. f7#) 29. Qg2 Qxg2+ 30. Kxg2 Nd7 $11) (27. fxg6 Qxe3+ 28. Kh1 Nd7 $18) 27... Kg8 28. f6 $18) 25. Bd4 (25. Bg5 {seems even better} Qe5 26. Qxg4 Qd4+ 27. Qxd4 Bxd4+ 28. Kg2 Nd7 $18) 25... Qe4 (25... Nf6 {Boensch} 26. Re1 Qd7 (26... Qf8 27. Bxf6 $18) 27. Re6 $18) 26. f6 Nc6 ({Alexei Shirov: 'It was a pity not to be allowed to finish the game off with the queen sacrifice'} 26... Nxf6 27. Qh5+ $1 Kg8 (27... Nxh5 28. Rf8#) 28. Bxf6 Qe3+ 29. Rf2 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'and wins.'}) ({Alexei Shirov: 'Of course,'} 26... Bxf6 27. Bxf6+ Nxf6 28. Rxf6 $18 {Alexei Shirov: 'is also hopeless.'}) (26... Nd7 {hoping against hope} 27. fxg7+ Kg8 $18) 27. fxg7+ Kg8 28. Rf8+ {'!' Theme: Clearance for d1-f1 '!'} Rxf8 29. gxf8=Q+ (29. gxf8=B $4 Nxd4 30. Qd3 Ne2+ 31. Kf1 Qh1+ 32. Kxe2 Qxh2+ 33. Kd1 Nf2+ 34. Ke2 Nxd3+ 35. Kxd3 Qg3+ 36. Kc4 Qf4+ 37. Kc3 Kxf8 $19) (29. gxf8=R+) 29... Kxf8 30. Qf1+ {Alexei Shirov: '. Black resigned as she gets mated.'} (30. Qf1+ {Boensch} Ke8 (30... Nf2 31. Qxf2+ Qf3 32. Qxf3+ Ke8 33. Qf7+ Kd8 34. Bb6+ Kc8 35. Qc7#) 31. Qf7+ Kd8 32. Bb6+ Kc8 33. Qc7#) 1-0

