1. e4 {Atlas,Valeri Atlas,Valeri Atlas,Valeri  Atlas,Valeri: 'Atlas,V  ' Atlas,Valeri Atlas,Valeri Atlas,Valeri  Atlas,Valeri: 'Atlas,V  ' B80: Sicilian Scheveningen: 6 g3 and 6 Be3, including English Attack} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. g3 a6 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O d6 9. Re1 Bd7 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Na4 Rd8 12. c4 c5 13. Bf4 ({The most active comtinuation.} 13. Nc3 Be7 14. Bf4 Bc6 15. Qe2 O-O 16. Rad1 Nd7 17. g4 Ne5 {does not lead to any advantage: Gruenfeld,Y-Franzoni,G/Biel-JMT 1986 (48)}) 13... Be7 ({An accurate move. An attempt to prevent 14.e5 by} 13... Bc6 {admits a beautiful refutation:} 14. Nxc5 $1 e5 15. Nxa6 Qb6 16. b4 Bd7 17. b5 exf4 18. e5 dxe5 19. Rxe5+ Be6 20. Bc6+ Ke7 21. Qe2 fxg3 22. hxg3 Nd7 23. c5 Qa5 24. Rxe6+ fxe6 25. Re1 $18 {Wedberg,T-Ogaard,L/Oslo 1980 (29)}) 14. e5 $5 dxe5 15. Bxe5 Qa5 16. Nc3 O-O {[%cal Gg2a8,Re5b8] White has a slight, but enduring advantage, retaining control over the important diagonals.} 17. Qf3 Bc8 18. Rad1 Rxd1 19. Rxd1 Rd8 20. h3 {Prevents intrusion on g4} Rxd1+ 21. Qxd1 Nd7 22. Bf4 Qb4 $6 ({This active move is an inaccuracy.} 22... Qd8 {was more stubborn.}) 23. Qe2 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Re2e7] Threatening d5.} Bf8 24. Ne4 {Now the threat is d6.} Qb6 ({After} 24... e5 25. Bc1 {white knight comes via c3 to d5.}) 25. Qd2 {Resuming the threat d6.} Nf6 $6 {There was no need to weaken the kingside pawn structure. It would be better to choose 25...e5, although after 26.e3 White has good chances to use the square d5.} (25... e5 26. Be3 $14) 26. Nxf6+ {'' ''} gxf6 27. b3 {Preparing 27.d1} Be7 $6 {Black misplaces his bishop. Now the kngside is seriously diminished, and White developes a dangerous attack. Better was} (27... Bg7 $142 {} 28. Qd6 (28. Bd6 Qd8) 28... Qxd6 29. Bxd6 Bf8 {and although White retains better chances, it is not clear that he will win.}) 28. Qd1 $1 {Now the threat is g4 and h6.} (28. Qe2 e5 $16) 28... f5 (28... e5 29. Be3 $14) 29. g4 $1 e5 30. Qe2 $2 ({Not the best way. After the most natural} 30. Bxe5 Qe6 (30... fxg4 31. hxg4 Qe6 {''} 32. Bc3 Qxg4 33. Qxg4+ Bxg4 34. Bb7 $18) {White gets excellent winning chances by} 31. Bc3 {(31.d5 fg4 32.e6 e6 33.hg4 g4 34.b7 must also suffice)} fxg4 32. hxg4 Qxg4 33. Qxg4+ Bxg4 34. Bb7 $18 {Atlas,Valeri: '+-' '+-' '+-' 'Atlas,V: '+-''}) 30... f6 31. Bd5+ {The white bishop is well posted.} Kh8 $2 {'??' '??'} ({Black returns the compliment. It was essential to continue} 31... Kg7 $1 {'' would bring relief '' , retaining good counterchances, e. g.:} 32. g5 (32. gxf5 Bxf5 33. Qh5 Bg6) 32... Qd6 (32... fxg5 $5 33. Bxe5+ Bf6) 33. Bg3 (33. Qh5 $6 fxg5 (33... exf4 $140 $4 34. Qf7+ Kh8 35. Qg8#) 34. Bxg5 $140 Bxg5 35. Qxg5+ (35. Qf7+ $4 Kh6 $19) 35... Qg6 $17) 33... Be6 (33... f4 $143 34. Qh5 $1 fxg5 35. Qf7+ Kh6 36. Be4 $18) 34. Bxe6 Qxe6) {[%tqu "En","Which continuation yields a decisive attack?","","","g4g5","The real point of the combination.",10]} 32. g5 $3 {'+-!' Theme: Clearance for e2-h5 '+-!' The real point of the combination.} Qc7 {[%csl Re5,Rf6][%cal Ge2h5,Gh5e8]} (32... Qd6 {doesn't help because of} 33. Qh5 $1 fxg5 34. Qf7 {, and mate is ineviatble.}) (32... fxg5 {A deflection} 33. Bxe5+) {[%tqu "En","","","","e2h5","The final blow.",10]} 33. Qh5 $1 {The final blow.} Bf8 (33... fxg5 34. Qf7 $18) (33... exf4 $4 {Black should leave the bishop alone} 34. Qe8+ Bf8 35. Qxf8#) 34. gxf6 $1 {[%csl Gf7] After 34...gf4 35.e8 d6 36.f7 mate is inevitable.} Bb7 35. Qe8 $18 {1-0 (35) Polgar,J (2665)-Milos,G (2605) Sao Paulo 1996 CBM 056 [Atlas,V]} 1-0

