1. c4 {Hansen,Cu Hansen,Cu Hansen,Cu Petursson} c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. e3 Nf6 4. d4 d5 5. dxc5 $6 {A rather unambitious move which allows black to take the pawn back by f8xc5 - without losing a tempo. Normal is 5.f3 e6 6.a3 after which 6..d6 7.dxc5 xc5 would give white the gamecontinuation one tempo up. That is why black normally waits with 6..a6.} e6 6. a3 Bxc5 7. b4 Bd6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Nb5 Bb8 11. Bb2 (11. Be2 Re8 12. O-O a6 13. Nbd4 Qd6 14. Bb2 Bg4 15. g3 $13 {Karolyi,Tibor-Kristensen/Ballerup (04) 1985 (45)}) 11... a5 $1 12. bxa5 Ne4 $15 13. Be2 Qxa5+ 14. Kf1 {White was not allowed to castle so black should be better. However only if she plays energitically. White controls the important square d4, which makes it quite difficult for black to start an attack} Rd8 15. h3 Qb6 16. g3 Re8 17. Qe1 Na5 18. Bd4 Qg6 19. Kg2 Nc4 {White has succeded in in getting the  into safety and connecting the s. However he had to pay a price. To avoidl c8-h3+ Van Wely had to play h2-h3 which has made it neccesary to place the  rather passively on e1(to avoid sacrifices on g3).} 20. Nh4 Qc6 21. a4 Ra6 22. Bg4 f5 $5 {A intesting -sacrifice. Whites somewhat weak -structure f2-g3-h3 will come under strong pressure after the f is opened.} 23. Bxf5 Rf8 24. Bxc8 $2 (24. Bxe4 dxe4 25. Qb4 Ne5 $1 $44) 24... Qxc8 25. Ra2 Rh6 $17 26. Qd1 (26. Nc3 Qe6 $17) 26... g5 27. Nf3 g4 28. hxg4 $2 (28. Ng1 gxh3+ $1 29. Rxh3 (29. Nxh3 Rxh3 $1 30. Rxh3 Rxf2+ 31. Rxf2 Qxh3+ 32. Kxh3 Nxf2+ 33. Kg2 Nxd1 $19) 29... Nxf2 $1 $17 {Tournament-bulletin} (29... Rxf2+ $1 {Petursson} 30. Rxf2 Rxh3 31. Rf8+ $1 Kxf8 32. Qf3+ Kg8 33. Nxh3 $15)) 28... Rxh1 29. Qxh1 Qxg4 30. Nh2 (30. Qd1 {Petursson} Bxg3 $19) 30... Rxf2+ 0-1

