{Kramnik,V: 'Inf 71/157'} 1. e4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Hecht Baburin Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin Hecht Baburin'} c5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = B33_17 Sveshnikov Var 8.a3 b5 / Sveshnikov Var 8.a3 b5'} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Unlike the rather feeble sidesteps from the last chapter, we now enter our greatest danger from the main line. White "threatens" Nd6+, forcing Black to part with the dark-squared bishop.'} ({QUESTION: Why is the word "threatens" in quotes? --- ANSWER: In the final chapter we dare to challenge this assumption with the Ulfie Variation (championed by GM Ulf Andersson, and also your writer - if Andersson is the mad scientist, then at least I get to be his equally mad Igor!), where we play} 6... h6 $5 {, allowing entry with} 7. Nd6+ Bxd6 8. Qxd6 Qe7 {. The position looks strategically awful for Black, but please reserve your judgment until you have examined the line, as it is much better than outer appearances suggest.}) 7. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White introduces the threat of Nc3-d5, which pretty much forces our next move (since 7...Be6 is clearly inferior).'} ({The immediate} 7. Nd5 {is the subject of Chapter Five;}) ({with the rarely-played options} 7. Be3) ({and} 7. a4 {featuring briefly in the notes to Sadatnajafi-Shariyazdanov.}) 7... a6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The aim is to wedge White's knight into a corner on a3.'} 8. Na3 b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening to fork the enemy knights with ...b5-b4.'} 9. Nd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'allows Black to go for a repetition draw with'} ({We examine White's major and sharper alternative,} 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 {, in Chapter Four.}) ({Retreating the knight with} 9. Nab1 {is every bit as cumbersome as the traditional route of c2-c3 (after Nd5) and Nc2. For example,} Be7 10. Bxf6 ({not} 10. Be2 $6 {due to} b4 $1 {, when after} 11. Nd5 ({White doesn't have time to chop with} 11. Bxf6 {in view of} bxc3 $1 12. Bxe7 cxb2 {, winning on the spot}) 11... Nxe4 12. Be3 Rb8 {, White lacks full compensation for the missing pawn.}) 10... Bxf6 11. Nd5 O-O 12. a4 bxa4 ({in keeping with our main line in this chapter, though} 12... b4 {is also possible here, when Black stands no worse}) 13. Rxa4 Bg5 14. Nd2 Kh8 15. Bd3 a5 16. O-O Be6 17. Nf3 Bh6 18. Bb5 Rc8 19. c3 f5 20. exf5 Rxf5 21. Re1 Rf8 22. Qd3 Na7 23. Bc4 Bd7 24. Raa1 Bg4 25. Qe4 (25. Nd2 $4 {hangs a piece to} Bxd2 26. Qxd2 Rxc4) 25... Bxf3 26. gxf3 {and White's kingside pawns have been compromised, B.G.Christensen-L.McShane, Copenhagen 2003.}) 9... Be7 ({We won't be covering} 9... Qa5+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'again'} 10. Bd2 Qd8 11. Bd3 (11. Bg5 {allows Black to go for a repetition draw with} Qa5+ {again}) 11... Nxd5 12. exd5 Ne7 13. c4 g6 14. cxb5 Bg7 {, as I don't believe in Black's full compensation for the pawn after} 15. Qb3 {.}) 10. Bxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Destroying a key defender of d5.'} ({Instead,} 10. Nxe7 {can be met by} Nxe7 $1 ({more active than} 10... Qxe7 $2 {, which is also playable}) 11. Bxf6 gxf6 {and ...f6-f5. For example,} 12. c4 ({or} 12. Qf3 f5 13. exf5 d5) 12... f5 13. exf5 Nxf5 14. Bd3 ({or} 14. cxb5 Nd4 15. bxa6 Bxa6 16. Bxa6 Qa5+ 17. Qd2 Qxa6 18. Rc1 Rg8 19. f3 Ke7 {with loads of compensation for the pawn; if} 20. Rc7+ Kf8 {, White is out of useful defensive moves}) 14... Nd4 15. O-O Bb7 16. f3 Qg5 $5 ({simply} 16... O-O {looks fine too}) 17. Kh1 Rg8 18. Rf2 Qh4 {and a draw was agreed in this dynamically equal position, E.Alekseev-S.Zhigalko, Baku 2013.}) 10... Bxf6 11. c3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White prepares to bring his temporarily offside knight back to relevance via c2, where it eyes both the e3- and b4-squares.'} ({We examine White's various 11th move alternatives:} 11. c4) (11. Be2) (11. Nxf6+) (11. Bd3) ({and} 11. g3 {in Chapter Three.}) 11... O-O ({In this book we concentrate on castling, which simplifies our study, though we have a quick look at} 11... Ne7 $5 {in the final game of the chapter.}) ({QUESTION: Shouldn't we first play our bishop to g5 to preserve it? --- ANSWER: Yes, it is perfectly possible to play} 11... Bg5 {, though Nxf6+ hardly counts as a threat to our side, since it loses way too much time. White nearly always replies} 12. Nc2 Rb8 (12... O-O {transposes to our main line; Topalov-Kasparov and Karjakin-Topalov did in fact take this route}) 13. a4 $1 {(this is the move Black hoped to prevent with ...Rb8)} bxa4 14. Ncb4 Nxb4 15. Nxb4 Bd7 16. Bxa6 O-O 17. O-O ({not yet} 17. Qxd6 $6 Rb6 18. Qxe5 Re8 19. Qd5 Rd6 $1 20. Qxd6 Rxe4+ 21. Kf1 Bb5+ 22. Bxb5 Qxd6 23. Bxa4 Qd2 24. Bb5 g6 {and although White has enough material for the queen, his problem is piece coordination}) 17... Bf4 $5 18. Bc4 ({Sevillano told me later that GM Alex Ivanov unsuccessfully played} 18. Qxd6 $5 {against him, but Komodo prefers}) (18. g3 Bg5 19. Qxd6 {and now that I know better, I would take White here}) 18... Qg5 19. Kh1 $2 ({a serious mistake;} 19. Qd3 {was necessary}) 19... Bg4 $2 ({missing} 19... Qh4 $1 20. g3 Bxg3 $1 21. fxg3 Qxe4+ 22. Kg1 Qxc4 {and Black is winning}) 20. Qd3 Kh8 21. h3 f5 $1 22. Bd5 $1 ({not} 22. hxg4 $2 fxe4 23. Qh3 Rf6 {and ...Rh6 follows}) 22... Qh5 23. Rfe1 fxe4 24. Bxe4 Bg5 25. Kg1 Qh4 26. Qg3 $5 {(my instinct is to always bail out into any ending!)} Qxg3 27. fxg3 Bd7 28. Rad1 Rb6 29. Rf1 Re8 {and White stood a microbe better in the endgame, which was eventually drawn, C.Lakdawala-E.Sevillano, Century City 2005.}) 12. Nc2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's best move, which contains two points: -- 1. The knight move frees an a2-a4 push, which tends to weaken Black's queenside more than White's. -- 2. It enables White to reinforce the d5-knight, via either b4 or e3, depending upon circumstances.'} ({Lesser played alternatives are: -- a)} 12. Be2 Bg5 13. O-O Ne7 {(our play is rather easy in this line: we challenge d5 with a one-track mind)}) ({b)} 12. Bd3 Bg5 13. O-O Ne7 14. Nxe7+ Qxe7) ({c)} 12. h4 {spends a tempo to deprive Black of ...Bg5, but he can reorganize effectively; for example,} Be6 ({not} 12... Bxh4 $2 13. Qh5 {and wins}) 13. Nc2 Rb8 14. Nce3 Be7 15. Bd3 Qd7 16. a4 Bd8 {(clearing the way for ...Ne7 to challenge White's grip on d5)} 17. axb5 axb5 18. h5 Bg5 {and Black stands no worse, H.Nakamura-A.Keatinge Clay, Budapest 2000.}) ({d)} 12. g3 Bg5 13. Bg2 {(increasing White's control of the d5-square, but apart from that the bishop is not very active here)} Ne7 14. Nc2 (14. Nxe7+ Qxe7 15. O-O {transposes to the note to Black's 13th move in Yatsishin-Mikhaletz}) 14... Nxd5 15. Qxd5 Be6 16. Qd1 a5 $1 {(a well-timed ...b5-b4 break is often a rich source of counterplay for our side)} 17. O-O Qb6 18. h4 Bh6 19. Re1 b4 20. cxb4 axb4 21. Ne3 g6 22. Nd5 Qc5 23. Re2 Bxd5 24. exd5 $6 ({White was better off entering an inferior ending after} 24. Qxd5 {etc}) 24... Rfc8 25. Be4 f5 26. Rc2 Qb6 27. Rxc8+ Rxc8 28. Bc2 e4 {and Black has a clear advantage, with a mobile kingside pawn majority and the superior opposite-coloured bishop, Z.Zvan-D.Solak, Slovenian Team Championship 2007.}) ({e) In ANSWER to your previous question,} 12. Nxf6+ Qxf6 {offers White less than nothing; for example,} 13. Be2 Ne7 14. Nc2 $6 Bb7 15. Bf3 Qg6 16. Qe2 $6 d5 17. exd5 Nxd5 18. O-O-O Nf4 {and White already stands significantly worse, A.Gempe-C.Beyer, German League 2013.}) 12... Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'We seize a good diagonal and take aim at e3, with the option of removing a white knight there (see Karjakin-Topalov, for example), and clear the way for our f-pawn to advance.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'transposes to our main line; Topalov-Kasparov and Karjakin-Topalov did in fact take this route'} 13. a4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This is White's best continuation, isolating Black's a-pawn.'} ({Others: -- a)} 13. h4 Bh6 14. g4 $5 {(when White goes bonkers like this, don't be afraid to play ...Bf4, even if it costs you a pawn, since e5 then becomes available for our knight)} Bf4 $1 15. Qf3 Be6 16. Nxf4 exf4 $5 ({the safer} 16... Qf6 {is also possible}) 17. Qxf4 Rb8 18. a3 a5 19. Be2 b4 {(White's main problem is that his king is unsafe across the board)} 20. axb4 axb4 21. Nd4 Ne5 22. Qg3 {, D.Smerdon-V.Mikhalevski, Queenstown 2009, and after} bxc3 23. bxc3 Qc8 $1 24. f4 Nd7 {, Black has huge compensation for the pawn; White's king will never rest easy and his pawns will be perpetual targets.}) ({b)} 13. Bd3 Ne7 14. Nce3 (14. Nxe7+ Qxe7 15. O-O {transposes to 12 Bd3 above} Be6 16. Qe2 g6 17. a4 bxa4 18. Rxa4 a5 19. Rfa1 Qb7 20. b4 axb4 21. cxb4 Rxa4 22. Rxa4 Bb3 23. Ra6 Bxc2 24. Bxc2 Qxb4 {and Black's extra pawn isn't much, but he is the only one with winning chances, A.Komora-L.Salai, Slovakian Team Championship 2012.}) 14... Be6 15. Bc2 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Rc8 17. O-O Rc5 18. Bb3 a5 19. a4 bxa4 20. Rxa4 Qb8 21. Ra2 {, S.Orehek-K.Novak, Slovenian Team Championship 2005, and now} Qb7 {, intending ...Rb8, when White is already on the defensive.}) ({c)} 13. Be2 Ne7 14. Ncb4 (14. O-O {transposes to 12 Be2 above} Nxd5 15. Qxd5 Be6 16. Qd3 d5 {and Black stands at least even having achieved the thematic ...d6-d5 break, Yu Yangyi-Vl.Kovalev, Moscow 2010.}) 14... a5 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Nd5 Qb7 17. Qd3 b4 18. cxb4 axb4 19. Qb3 Be6 20. Bc4 Rac8 21. O-O Qc6 $1 22. Rac1 Bxd5 $1 ({certainly not} 22... Bxc1 $4 23. Ne7+ {, picking off the queen}) 23. exd5 Qc5 24. Rc2 g6 {½-½ V.Anand-V.Kramnik, Dortmund 1997. I think Black could have played on.}) ({d)} 13. Nce3 Be6 14. Be2 Ne7 15. O-O Nxd5 16. Nxd5 {with an even position, which was agreed drawn at this point in R.Cherevatenko-A.Zatonskih, Kharkov 1999.} Qb8 {, followed by ...Qb7 is one idea for Black.}) ({e)} 13. g3 Ne7 14. Ncb4 a5 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Nd5 Qb7 17. Qb3 {and again the players agreed to a draw, S.Tiviakov-Y.Yakovich, Beijing 1998. I actually prefer Black here and would play} Rb8 18. h4 Bd8 19. Rd1 Be6 {. It seems to me that White has no plan and must await Black's intent.}) 13... bxa4 ({QUESTION: Why do we agree to an isolated a-pawn, rather than play} 13... Rb8 {- ? --- ANSWER: Three reasons to avoid ...Rb8 here: -- 1. First, your pawn is isolated anyway after White captures on b5. -- 2. A white knight may sit on b4, unlike the game's continuation, where ...a6-a5 is available. -- 3. Black's b5-pawn is quite vulnerable and White may later be able to apply pressure with such ideas as Bd3 and Qe2, followed by b2-b4 and Na3.}) 14. Rxa4 a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'We suppress b2-b4.'} ({The position after} 14... Rb8 15. b4 a5 16. b5 Ne7 17. Nxe7+ Bxe7 18. Ne3 {doesn't score very well for Black.}) 15. Bc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The bishop takes up its optimal post, overprotecting d5.'} ({Kasparov once tried} 15. Bb5 $5 {, but Black can equalize after this move:} Ne7 ({more flexible than} 15... Bb7 16. Nce3 Bxe3 17. Nxe3 Ne7 18. O-O {, as in G.Kasparov-L.Van Wely, Wijk aan Zee 1999; I prefer White's position here, since Nc4 is an issue and Black experiences problems with his isolated a-pawn if he plays for a ...d6-d5 break}) 16. Ncb4 Bd7 17. Nxe7+ Bxe7 18. Nc6 Qe8 $1 (18... Bxc6 19. Bxc6 Rb8 20. Ra2 {is better for White, who owns the stronger opposite-coloured bishop and the slightly superior structure}) 19. Qd5 (19. Nxe7+ Qxe7 20. Bxd7 Qxd7 21. O-O Rfb8 {is fine for Black}) 19... Be6 20. Qd3 Bd7 21. Qd5 Be6 22. Qd3 Bd7 {with a draw by repetition, J.Polgar-A.Shirov, Wijk aan Zee 2003.}) 15... Rb8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'We finally reach the key tabiya position for this chapter.'} 16. b3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White covers his b-pawn.'} ({The alternative defence,} 16. Ra2 {, is examined in Anand-Kasparov and Karjakin-Topalov.}) 16... Kh8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black needs counterplay, so he prepares the ...f7-f5 break, usually by playing ...g7-g6 first as well.'} 17. O-O ({White has an important alternative here in} 17. Nce3 {, and we look at this in Granara Barreto-Felgaer.}) 17... g6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Preparing the advance of the f-pawn.'} ({QUESTION: Why can't Black just play ...f7-f5 straight away? --- ANSWER: He can; and in fact} 17... f5 {is the most popular move here - but I'm not a fan of this plan, mainly because e4xf5 grants White's pieces free access to the e4-square. For example,} 18. exf5 Bxf5 19. Nce3 Bg6 20. Bd3 {(otherwise 20...e4!? is often a good move)} Bf7 $6 ({the safe course is} 20... Bxd3 21. Qxd3 Bxe3 22. fxe3 Rxf1+ 23. Qxf1 Rxb3 24. Rc4 Rb5 {, as long as Black is happy with a draw after} 25. Nc7 Rb6 26. Nd5 Rb5 {etc}) 21. Be4 Bg8 22. Ra3 {and White was slightly better, P.Leko-V.Topalov, Monte Carlo (blindfold rapid) 2002. --- Later in the chapter, we will see another example of Topalov playing ...f7-f5 without ...g7-g6, albeit in the 16 Ra2 line.}) 18. Kh1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Polgar plans the risky idea of taking on f5, followed by f2-f4, with the intention of exposing Black's king.' Kramnik,V: 'N'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'In the next three games we at' Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin: 'Shirov tried''} 18. Qd3 {Kramnik,V: '— 70/(145)' Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin: 'vs. Kramnik, in a blindfold game in Monaco 1995 and eventually won after''} Bd7 19. Raa1 f5 20. Rfd1 Bh6 21. Nde3 Rf6 22. f3 Ne7 23. Kh1 Bf4 {Shirov,A-Kramnik,V/Monaco blind 1995/EXP 46/1-0 (54)} (23... Bf4 {Baburin} 24. b4 a4 25. Nd5 Nxd5 26. Bxd5 Qf8 27. g3 Bg5 28. c4 f4 29. g4 h5 30. gxh5 Qh6 31. b5 Qxh5 32. Rxa4 Bh4 33. Rda1)) (18. Qe2) ({and} 18. Nce3 {respectively.}) 18... Bh6 ({Retreating before the pending f2-f4 cuts down White's options, whereas the immediate} 18... f5 {Kramnik,V: '!?'} 19. exf5 gxf5 20. f4 (20. Qh5 Bd7 21. Raa1 Be8) 20... Bh6 {can be met by} 21. Qh5 {.} Bg7) 19. Qe2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'last book move'} Bd7 {Baburin: With many pieces on the board it's usually a lot easier for Black to deal with White's knight on d5 in the Sicilian Defence. That knight is not doing much right now, while Black's pieces are moving 'around' it.} (19... f5 $5) 20. Raa1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Just so that the rook won't be surprised by discovered attacks from d7.'} (20. Ra2 f5 21. exf5 gxf5 22. f4 Bg7 {½-½ Dani,P-Kocsis,G/Zalakaros op 1996/EXP 52 (43)}) 20... f5 {Challenging White's control in the centre.} 21. exf5 (21. f3 $142 Qh4 $5 22. g3 Qd8 $5 (22... Qh5 $132)) 21... gxf5 22. f4 Bg7 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Kramnik,V: ''} (22... Qh4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Kramnik,V: '!?'} 23. Qe1 (23. Rf3 $5 exf4 24. Rh3 Qg5 25. Rf1 $13) (23. g3 Qd8 $1) (23. Qf2 Qxf2 24. Rxf2 exf4 25. Nxf4 Ne5 $36) 23... Qxe1 24. Raxe1 $11) ({QUESTION: Why not create a protected passed pawn with} 22... e4 {- ? --- ANSWER: This move looks natural, yet is perhaps a strategic error from Black, since he violates the principle: "Keep the pawn structure fluid, when you own the bishop pair". In any case, the passer is firmly blockaded after} 23. Nce3 {, when I don't see an active plan for Black, since his kingside chances have been shut down.}) 23. Rad1 Ne7 (23... Qh4) 24. Nce3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '='} (24. fxe5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} dxe5 25. Nce3 Ng6 $11) ({It's tempting to eliminate a potential attacker with} 24. Nxe7 Qxe7 {and then follow with} 25. Ne3 {, except that White loses material after} exf4 26. Nd5 Qxe2 27. Bxe2 Rxb3 {.}) 24... Ng6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Kramnik brings another potential attacker to the kingside.' Kramnik,V: '!?'} (24... Nxd5 25. Nxd5 $11) 25. Qh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '...as does Polgar!'} Qe8 (25... exf4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Kramnik,V: '!?'} 26. Nxf4 Nxf4 27. Rxf4 $14 Qe8 $13) 26. fxe5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'f5 becomes the focus of attention' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Playing for a win when we don't have an inherent advantage is similar to the religious farmer who fails to plant seeds, and then prays for the miracle of a good crop. I think the edge now swings to Black.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Polgar should be content with'} 26. Nc7 {etc. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Qc8 27. Ncd5 {, when Black has nothing better than to agree to a repetition draw after} (27. Ned5 Nxf4 28. Nxf4 (28. Rxf4 exf4 29. Ne7 Be8 $1) 28... exf4 29. Nd5 a4 $5)) 26... Qxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This way Black's queen covers f5, while seizing control over f4.'} ({Not} 26... dxe5 $4 {which just hangs a key pawn to} 27. Nxf5 {.}) (26... Nxe5 $15) 27