Rfe1 Bxc4 32. Bxc4 a4) 31... Nxc4 32. bxc4 Be8 33. g3 Be5 {. Admittedly, Black retains serious winning chances there as well.}) 30... hxg6 31. Rf3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Kramnik,V: '!'} (31. Ne7 $5 Kh7 32. Rxd6 Bxc4 33. bxc4 g5 34. Rd5) (31. Rxf4 $2 {Baburin} Rxf4 32. Nxf4 Bxc4 (32... a4 $5) 33. bxc4 Bxc3 $19 {Kramnik,V: ''}) (31. Nxf4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s) Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met by'} Kh7 $11 (31... Kg8 $1 {, intending} 32. Nxg6 $2 Rxf1+ 33. Rxf1 a4 {and White is busted. Kramnik,V: ''})) 31... Be5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Opting to keep the light-squared bishop.'} (31... Bxc4 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 32. bxc4 g5 (32... Be5 {offers Black even better chances in the ending.} 33. Ra1 $11)) 32. Nxe5 dxe5 33. c4 Bc6 34. Re1 Rfe8 (34... Rbe8 35. g3 $5) 35. g3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Polgar is desperate to break up Black's central pawns. In doing so, she relies too heavily on the strength of the d5-knight.'} (35. h4 $1 $15 { g3}) 35... g5 36. gxf4 exf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A skilled con artist tries to fit the lies as plausibly as possible to the facts. Sometimes a move looks like a mundanely unremarkable mistake and then, a few moves later, we see the true craft behind it. Kramnik sets himself up for a future undermining with h2-h4, having seen deeper into the position.'} 37. Rxe8+ Rxe8 { Hecht: Hauptmotiv: Fesselung} 38. h4 {Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin: '?'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A move made with the thought that if a splinter digs into your flesh, it's wise to pluck it out as quickly as possible. Defence of such difficult positions is a trial of endurance and alertness.' Kramnik,V: '?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's activity is desperately scarce, yet she was better off foregoing this thrust in favour of'} 38. Kg1 $1 {Hecht Cyrus Lakdawala: ', removing the king from the bishop's diagonal.' Kramnik,V: ''} Re4 $1 39. Kf2 Kg7 $15) {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning) : It looks as if White, in a single stroke, confronts her most pressing issue, as she apparently breaks up the connected passed pawns. This is an illusion. Come up with a sequential plan which gives Black a winning position. --- ANSWER: Step 1: Undermine c4.'} 38... a4 {Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin: '!!'' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When we discover a combinational sequence, it is the same perfect-fit feeling of slipping a key into a lock.' Kramnik,V: '!'} (38... Re3 39. Kg2 $11) 39. bxa4 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Step 2: Attack c4, leaving White's knight, rook and king lined up along the long diagonal.' Kramnik,V: '?' Erst danach kommt Schwarz in Vorteil.} (39. hxg5 $142 {Kramnik,V: ''} a3 {Baburin} (39... Re3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 40. Rxf4 axb3 $11) 40. Rf2 f3 (40... Rb8 {Hecht} 41. Ra2 Rxb3 42. Ra1 $13 {Kramnik,V: ''}) (40... Re3 41. Ra2) 41. Nb4 Re2 42. Kg1 Rb2 $19) 39... Re4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Black begins to deal mighty blows' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Kramnik begins to work mischief on the lone support of White's unfortunate knight.'} 40. Kg1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): ''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Too late; but'} 40. Rc3 {Hecht Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met by the crushing'} Rxc4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(the rook hopes to make a profit from his shady transaction; this kind of trap has been around since Adam and Eve)'} 41. Rxc4 (41. Rd3 Rc5 {and it becomes painfully obvious that bishop and knight are horribly mismatched in their ability to perform their respective duties. "I never understand why God loves the sinner who repents, more than one who has been righteous for all of his life," ponders the bishop, while eyeing the pinned knight with obvious censure.}) 41... Bxd5+ 42. Kg1 Bxc4 43. hxg5 Kg7 (43... Bd5 44. Kf2 f3 $19) 44. Kf2 Bd5 $19) (40. Rd3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} Rxc4) (40. hxg5 Rxc4 41. Rd3 Rc5) 40... Rxc4 {Khalifman: 'Alexander Baburin: '-+''} 41. Rd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White manages to save her knight, but not the game - the way a person accused of double homicide gets a not guilty verdict for one murder and sent to death row for the other. --- Step 3: Create two connected passed pawns.'} g4 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For Polgar, the board is littered with disappointments. Black's two passed pawns (who defy chess gravity by remaining connected), coupled with his superior rook and bishop team, forces the win.'} 42. a5 (42. Nc3 g3 43. Ne2 Rc2 $1) 42... g3 43. Rd1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White defends her first rank.'} (43. a6 {is met by} f3 $1 44. Rxf3 Rc1+ $1 45. Kg2 Bxd5 {and Black wins.}) (43. Nxf4 Rc1+) 43... f3 44. Ne7 f2+ {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=??'} (44... Be4 $142 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} 45. h5 Ra4 $19) 45. Kf1 Bb5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Do you see the mate threat?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Taking aim at the f1 promotion square.'} 46. Kg2 Rc2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The mate threat is f1Q' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '. --- SUMMARY: In the critical position, play the ...g7-g6 and ...f7-f5 plan, so that if White captures on f5, you can recapture with your g-pawn. If White then follows with f2-f4, don't be tempted to create a passed pawn with ...e5-e4?!. Instead, keep the centre fluid for your bishop pair.'} 47. Kxg3 {Fritz 5.00 (5s)} f1=Q 48. Rxf1 Bxf1 $19 0-1

