1. d4 {Tisdall Tisdall,Jonathan D Tisdall,Jonathan D Tisdall,Jonathan D Hansen,Cu Tisdall,Jonathan D: 'Polgar,Judit Hansen,Cu'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bg5 {Hansen,Cu: 'This was played in games 7 and 11 of the world championship match between Karpov and Kasparov in 1985. Karpov tried 5. - d6 and 5. - c5. The crucial point is which pin plays the most important role in the strategic battle. The one on the  h4-d8 or the one on the  a5-e1. I would say the pin of f6 gains in strength the moment black places his  on g8. However it seems Kasparov and Timman have different opinions about how this should be exploited' Hansen,Cu: 'This was played in games 7 and 11 of the world championship match between Karpov and Kasparov in 1985. Karpov tried 5. - d6 and 5. - c5. The crucial point is which pin plays the most important role in the strategic battle. The one on the  h4-d8 or the one on the  a5-e1. I would say the pin of f6 gains in strength the moment black places his  on g8. However it seems Kasparov and Timman have different opinions about how this should be exploited' Tisdall,Jonathan D: 'Hansen,Cu: 'This was played in game 7 and 11 of the world-championship match between Karpov and Kasparov in 1985. Karpov tried 5. - d6 and 5. - c5. The crucial point is which pin plays the most important role in the strategical battle. The one on the  h4-d8 or the one on the  a5-e1. I would say the pin of f6 gains in strength the moment black places his  on g8. However it seems Kasparov and Timman have different opinions about how this should be exploited''} c5 6. d5 {Hansen,Cu: 'Timman may have played this because of his many games with the Leningrad Variation (4.g5) where Black normally does not castle so quickly. In game 11 of the above mentioned match Kasparov preferred:' Hansen,Cu: 'Timman may have played this because of his many games with the Leningrad Variation (4.g5) where Black normally does not castle so quickly. In game 11 of the above mentioned match Kasparov preferred:' Tisdall,Jonathan D: 'Hansen,Cu: 'Timman may have played this because of his many games with the Leningrad-variation (4.Lg5) where black normally does not castle so quickly. In game 11 of the above mentioned match Kasparov preferred:''} (6. e3 {Hansen,Cu} cxd4 7. exd4 h6 8. Bh4 d5 (8... Qa5 $132 {see Wojtaszek-Berkes see Wojtaszek-Berkes}) 9. Rc1 {with some kind of Nimzo-Indian type of position with some kind of Nimzo-Indian type of position Tisdall,Jonathan D: 'with some kind of Nimzoindian type of position'}) 6... d6 {Judit avoids the critical reaction in this type of position, with an immediate kicking away of the bishop with ...h6. This and the gambit reaction ...b5 is the combination of ideas that I imagine put most White players off using this line more often. Play can easily transpose into one of Timman's pet variations (the true Leningrad Nimzo, 4.g5), but there is relatively little experience with this gambit with the inclusion of the moves f3 and ...0-0. Judit avoids the critical reaction in this type of position, with an immediate kicking away of the bishop with ...h6. This and the gambit reaction ...b5 is the combination of ideas that I imagine put most White players off using this line more often. Play can easily transpose into one of Timman's pet variations (the true Leningrad Nimzo, 4.g5), but there is relatively little experience with this gambit with the inclusion of the moves f3 and ...0-0. Tisdall,Jonathan D: 'Judit avoids the critical reaction in this type of position, with an immediate kick of the bishop with ...h6. This and the gambit reaction ...b5 is the combination of ideas that I imagine put most white players off using this line more often. Play can easily transpose into one of Timman's pet variations (the true Leningrad Nimzo, 4.g5), but there is relatively little experience with this gambit with the inclusion of the moves f3 and ...0-0.'} (6... h6 7. Bh4 b5 $5 (7... Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 d6 9. e3 e5 10. Nd2 Nbd7 11. B