{Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Hazai: 'In the quiet positional 6.Be2 e5 Najdorf, Svidler has tried the 10.Nd5 and 11. Qd3 line. After Judith's energetic 13...Nfe4 it became clear that the queenside will be closed and Black has chances on the kingside in the long run. This is a fine illustrative game showing Black's blockade strategy on the queenside and her attacking possibilities on the kingside. White's whole plan received a severe blow in this game.'''} 1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Polgar,Judit Ftacnik'Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit Hazai Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik'''} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O ({The related} 8. Be3 Be6 9. Nd5 $5 {is a fairly recent idea which contains a touch of venom. Black must avoid greed with 9...Nxe4?? due to 10 Bb6 and 11 Nc7+, whereas after} Nbd7 {he shouldn't be worried by 10 Nxe7 on account of 10...Qxe7 11 f3 d5 when he has achieved his ideal break. Instead} 10. Qd3 O-O ({steering well clear of the exchange on d5 as} 10... Bxd5 11. exd5 O-O 12. g4 $1 {is White's idea when it is White, not Black as usually occurs after an exchange on d5, who has gained the kingside initiative; here 12...e4!? may well not actually be so bad for Black, but} Nc5 $6 13. Nxc5 dxc5 14. O-O-O e4 15. Qd2 Bd6 16. g5 Nd7 17. h4 {certainly was in A.Khalifman-B.Gelfand, FIDE World Championship, Las Vegas 1999 as Black badly lacked counterplay}) {11 0-0 transposes back into Svidler-Polgar, but the other idea behind the 9 Nd5 moveorder is to have the optionof} 11. c4 {when it looks as if White's bind promises him an excellent game.  TIP: When it appears that White is set to gain full control of the position and thus a large advantage, always look for active counterplay, even at the cost of a pawn.} b5 $1 {is an essential but also strong counter which has halted many players' interest in 9 Nd5. White is loathe to exchange his central e-pawn for the one on b5 and so usually continues} 12. cxb5 axb5 13. O-O {, although after} Bxd5 14. exd5 Nb6 $1 15. Bxb6 Qxb6 16. Qxb5 Qa7 {Black enjoyed full compensation for the pawn in P.Svidler-B.Gelfand, Haifa (rapid) 2000. He has promising Benko-style queenside pressure and will also be able to tie White to the defence of d5.}) 8... O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. Nd5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Hazai: 'The idea is to put pressure on the queenside. Black has to take the N, and after exd5 White has a queenside pawn majority. However to build up the ideal set of pawns a5-b4-c4-d5 is far from easy. The breakthrough on c5 can easily be stopped, while in the meantime Black can advance in the centre with the ... f5 advance.'''} Nbd7 {Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!' Marcin Sieciechowicz: 'Covering b6 and f6 is once again the most flexible move as Black still need not worry about an exchange on e7, after which White cannot both defend e4 and prevent 12...d5. However, White's next does threaten to exchange on e7, after which d6 would become rather vulnerable, and so Black is finally forced to exchange on d5.'} 11. Qd3 {Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Hazai: 'Now c4 is already threatened.'''} Bxd5 12. exd5 Nc5 {Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!' Marcin Sieciechowicz: 'An important concept and one which ensures Black of a good game. Polgar rightly doesn't fear an exchange on c5 as she would then blockade the d5-pawn and be able to utilise her useful kingside majority.'} 13. Qd2 ({Marcin Sieciechowicz: 'Trying to retai n queenside chances like this fails to impress, but Black is also very comfortable after'} 13. Nxc5 {'(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Hazai: '?!' Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} dxc5 {Polgar,Judit: 'Hazai: 'leads to a successful blockade for Black on d6, and the defended passed P on d5 is useless.''} 14. c4 (14. Rad1 {(Polgar,Judit) Hazai} e4 15. Qd2 Bd6 16. a4 Qc7 17. g3 Rae8 18. a5 Nd7 19. Bf4 Ne5 20. c4 f5 {and Black ca n mobilize his kingside pawns, but White cannot do the same on the other flank, King-Browne, Reykjavik 1990.}) (14. Rfd1 {when he can do even better than bring his knight to d6. Instead} e4 $1 15. Qd2 Bd6 16. a4 Qc7 17. g3 Rae8 18. a5 Nd7 {, intending ...Ne5 and ...f5, saw Black build up a strong attacking position in  D.King-W.Browne, Reykjavik 1990.   NOTE: White may possess a passed d-pawn and the bishop-pair, but after the exchange on c5 he often struggles to find any play, whereas Black has a clear-cut plan on the kingside.}) 14... Bd6 15. b4 $5) 13... Nfe4 {Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Hazai: '!' Hazai: 'This is the best continuation.'''} (13... Qc7 {(Polgar,Judit) Ü 56/278} 14. f3 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) controlling the e4 square.} Nfd7 (14... Nxb3 15. axb3 Nd7 (15... a5 16. c4 {and b4 will come again.}) 16. c4 {followed by b4.}) (14... h6 15. c4 Nh7 {Black wants to exchange the B through ...Bg5.} (15... Nfd7 16. Na5 $1 {discouraging ...b6, when there is no good defence against b4.}) (15... b6 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Bd3 {with only a small advantage for White because of the weakened kingside.}) 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Bd3 {preventing Bg5, Apicella-Brunner, EU-chT Debrecen 1992.}) 15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. b4 Nd7 17. c4 b6 {The key square is c5.} 18. a3 a5 19. Rfc1 Ra7 $11 {with the idea of Rfc8 and Black has equalized, Galkin-Voitsekhovsky, RUS-cup Tomsk 1997.}) (13... Nce4 $6 {(Polgar,Judit) (Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Hazai) is met by} 14. Qb4 $1 {and the pressure on the queenside is increasing.} a5 15. Qxb7 Rb8 16. Qc6 a4 17. Nd2 Rxb2 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bd3 {and Black's position is a bit worse.}) (13... Nxb3 {(Polgar,Judit) (Ftacnik,Lubomir) Hazai} 14. axb3 a5 15. c4 {threatening b4 with a fine game for White.}) 14. Qb4 {Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Hazai: 'While the knight is on c5, this is not dangerous any more.'''} a5 {Marcin Sieciechowicz: '!' Marcin Sieciechowicz: 'Gaining a useful tempo as Polgar begins a strong mini-plan to keep the queenside closed against the white pieces.'} (14... b6 {'(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} 15. f3 Nf6 16. Qd2 Qd7 17. f4 Nfe4 18. Qb4 Rfb8 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. fxe5 dxe5 21. Rad1 Bd6 22. Qh4 Rf8 