{[%mdl 2080]} 1. e4 {Finkel,A Finkel,A Finkel,A Bangiev. McShane. Finkel,A Bangiev. McShane. Finkel,A: 'Bangiev. McShane. ' Finkel,A Finkel,A Bangiev. McShane. Finkel,A: 'Bangiev. McShane. ' Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander Bangiev. McShane. '} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 (7... Qb6 {(Finkel,A) (Chandler-Gurevich) is the major alternative.}) ({The old main line with} 7... a6 {(Finkel,A) is not seen so often nowadays.}) 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O {'!' Bangiev. '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''!' Bangiev. '} 10. O-O-O ({In Kasparov-Shirov White inserts} 10. g3 $5 {(Finkel,A) before castling.}) 10... a6 {Finkel,A: 'Byron Jacobs: 'It used to be thought that these middlegames were clearly in White's favour, but after 7...Qb6 fell under something of a cloud, interest in the line with ...Bc5 revived and there are those who believe that expansion on the queenside grants Black adequate chances.''} 11. h4 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 13. Rh3 {McShane: 'This looks a little slow. Pushing on with 13.h5 or 13.g4 may be better.'} ({White plays instead} 13. h5 {(Finkel,A) in the next game.}) 13... b4 {Finkel,A: 'Byron Jacobs: 'A useful gain of tempo.''} ({More natural than} 13... Bb7 {, which we looked at in the previous game. Black hurries on with his attack, giving White's knight the heave-ho from c3.}) 14. Ne2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Believe it or not, I think Black actually stands slightly better after this natural move, where White's knight heads for the d4 blockade square.' Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.' Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.' Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.''' Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.' Finkel,A: 'Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More popular and probably superior is'} 14. Na4 {Finkel,A: '(McShane) may be better, as Shirov's attack came very fast. The problem is that the knight is prone to being attacked by a black bishop.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'which we have looked at in the previous game and I don't believe White extracts anything from that path either.' Finkel,A: '(McShane) may be better, as Shirov's attack came very fast. The problem is that the knight is prone to being attacked by a black bishop.' Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander: '(McShane) may be better, as Shirov's attack came very fast. The problem is that the knight is prone to being attacked by a black bishop.''} Bxd4 15. Qxd4 Qa5 (15... f6 16. Qxb4 $5 fxe5 17. Qd6 Qf6 18. f5 $5 Qh6+ 19. Kb1 Rxf5 20. Rf3 $1 $36 {Kasparov,G-Short,N/Amsterdam VSB 1994 Kasparov,G-Short,N/Amsterdam VSB 1994 Finkel,A: 'Kasparov,G-Short,N/Amsterdam VSB 1994 '}) (15... a5 16. b3 $6 {'!?' Bangiev. '!?' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''!?' Bangiev. '} (16. Bb5 Rb8 (16... Ba6 $2 17. Bxd7) 17. Bd3 (17. Bxd7 Bxd7 18. Nc5 Bb5 19. f5 Qc7 $16 {fc8 fc8 Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'fc8'' fc8 Finkel,A: 'fc8'}) 17... Qc7 (17... f6 $5) 18. Re1 {'!?' Bangiev. '!?' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'f5' '!?' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'f5' Finkel,A: ''!?' Bangiev. Finkel,Alexander: ''!?' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'f5''' Bangiev: 'f5' Finkel,A: 'Bangiev: 'f5''} (18. h5 Bb7 19. g4 Rfc8 20. Rh2 Qc6 21. b3 Ba6 $132 {Fogarasi,T-Luther,T/Kecskemet 1993 Fogarasi,T-Luther,T/Kecskemet 1993 Finkel,A: ' Fogarasi,T-Luther,T/Kecskemet 1993'}) 18... Qc6 19. b3 Ba6 20. Bxa6 Qxa6 21. Rg3 (21. f5 exf5 22. Qxd5 Nb6 $13) 21... Rbc8 (21... Qc6 22. f5) 22. f5 Qc6 (22... exf5 $2 23. e6) 23. Re2 $14 {Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev izt-W 1995 Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev izt-W 1995 Finkel,A: ' Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev izt-W 1995'} (23. Re2 {Bangiev} Kh8 $132 {1-0 Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev 1995/(61)})) 16... Ba6 17. Kb1 Bxf1 18. Rxf1 Qc7 19. h5 Ra6 20. Re3 Rc6 21. Rf2 f6 {Bangiev: '0-1 Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov 1995/(39)'} 22. Ree2 h6 $15 {Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov on Don-W 1995 Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov on Don-W 1995 Finkel,A: ' Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov on Don-W 1995 '}) 16. b3 Bb7 17. Rg3 $5 (17. c3 Rfc8 18. Kb2 (18. Kb1 bxc3 19. Rxc3 Rxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3 21. Nxc3 Rc8 22. Kb2 $14 {Herrera,I-Ramas,L/CUB-ch 1993}) 18... bxc3+ 19. Rxc3 Rxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 21. Nxc3 Kf8 22. a4 Ke7 23. a5 $14 {Nijboer,F-Luther,T/Leeuwarden op 1992 Nijboer,F-Luther,T/Leeuwarden op 1992 Finkel,A: ' Nijboer,F-Luther,T/Leeuwarden op 1992'}) 17... Rfc8 18. Rd2 Bc6 19. f5 Qc7 20. f6 g6 21. Qf4 $5 $40 {Arakhamia,K-Gurevich,M/Helsinki op 1992}) (14. Na4 {(McShane) may be better, as Shirov's attack came very fast. The problem is that the knight is prone to being attacked by a black bishop.}) 14... a5 15. Qe3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Is the plan of adding pressure to c5 really effective for White? After all, Black can cover c5 more times than White can attack it. --- ANSWER: I think the main motivation behind her move is that she wants to reinforce e5 to eventually play for f5.'} (15. Bxc5 {Bangiev} Nxc5 16. Nd4 Ba6 17. Bxa6 Rxa6 18. Qe3 a4 $36) ({After} 15. h5 Ba6 16. Qe3 Qc7 17. Rg3 Rfc8 {, Black's attack already looks quicker.} 18. Rd2 $2 (18. f5 Bxe2 19. Qxe2 Bxd4 20. Rxd4 {had to be tried but even here Black has several paths to an edge}) 18... Bxd4 19. Qxd4 Nc5 $1 {and there is no defence to the coming ...Ne4, which wins material.} 20. Re3 Ne4 21. Rxe4 dxe4 22. Qxe4 {and Black is winning with the material advantage and still possessing the attack, R.Sprangers-D.Stellwagen, Arnhem 2006.}) 15... Qc7 16. Bxc5 Nxc5 17. Nd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Contemplating f5 at a later stage.'} a4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why wasn't Shirov interested in swapping away his bad bishop?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Of course'} 17... Ba6 {Bangiev Cyrus Lakdawala: 'was possible, but strangely enough, the "badness" of the bishop tends to be an overrated selling point for White in this variation. In my research, I noticed several examples where the lone "bad" light-squared bishop more than held its own against a White knight or bishop. Also, if Black wants to play ...f6 at some future point, he may need the light-squared bishop to protect e6. --- Let's see what happens after 17...Ba6:'} 18. Bxa6 Rxa6 19. h5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'was R.Altrock-H.Offenborn, correspondence 2000. At this point, Houdini recommends'} (19. f5 $6 Ne4) 19... a4 (19... Ne4 20. h6 g6 21. Kb1 a4 {with the idea of ...a3, which is pretty close to what happened in the game. White already looks uncomfortable.}) 20. h6 g6 21. Kb1) 18. Kb1 $6 {Serious inaccuracy passing the initiative into black's hands.It was nessasary to prevent black from playing 18...a3.The only way to do it was to play 18.a3!:} (18. a3 $1 Nb3+ (18... b3) (18... bxa3 19. Qxa3 Qb6 (19... Ba6 20. Rc3 Rfc8 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 22. Nb5 Qb6 23. Nd6 Rc7 24. Kb1 $14) 20. h5 Ba6 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 22. h6 g6 23. g4 Ne4 24. Rf3 $13) 19. Kb1 $1 (19. Nxb3 axb3 20. Qxb3 bxa3 21. bxa3 Ba6 22. Qc3 Qb6 $36) 19... Nxd4 20. Qxd4 bxa3 21. Rxa3 Bd7 22. h5 h6 23. g4 $14) (18. h5 {Bangiev} b3 $1) 18... a3 $1 {[%csl Rc3] McShane: 'Shirov's attack is already creating concrete threats and Polgar's attack is barely off the ground.' Finkel,A: 'McShane: 'Shirov's attack is already creating concrete threats and Polgar's attack is barely off the ground.' Byron Jacobs: '!?' Byron Jacobs: 'A fundamental decision as Shirov commits his pawns. Many would have waited until they had developed further.'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move imposes burdensome restrictions upon White's king and the question of his safety.'} (18... b3 $2 {Bangiev} 19. cxb3 axb3 20. Nxb3) 19. b3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This forced weakening move acts as a corrosive acid upon the dark squares surrounding White's king.'} Ba6 {'!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'For so long, the bishop prayed for a sign and now receives it.' '!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. ' '!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. ' Finkel,A: ''!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,Alexander: ''!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev.''} ({The text move is more accurate than} 19... Ne4 20. Nb5 Qa5 21. Bd3 Bd7 22. Bxe4 Bxb5 23. Bf3 Rfc8 24. f5 $1 {when White generates serious kingside counterplay. After} Qc7 25. Rd2 exf5 26. Bxd5 Bc6 27. Qc5 {, chances look balanced.}) 20. Bxa6 (20. Bd3) 20... Rxa6 $15 {Finkel,A: 'Byron Jacobs: 'No bad bishop any more, but still an attacking build-up.''} 21. Qe1 (21. h5 {Bangiev} Ne4 22. Rd3 Rc8 $40) 21... Rb6 $1 {'' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''' Bangiev. Byron Jacobs: 'There is a large hole at e4.'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black threatens ...Ne4, so White's next move looks pretty much forced.' '' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.' Finkel,A: ''' Bangiev. ' '' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.' Finkel,A: ''' Bangiev. ' Finkel,A: ''' Bangiev. Finkel,Alexander: ''' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.''' Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.' Finkel,A: 'Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.''} 22. c3 $6 {White tries to deprive black the control over the square c3,but opens the king,which proves to be crucial. McShane: 'Otherwise Black will play Ne4, threatening Nc3+ and, if need be, break with f6!' '?' Bangiev. Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Worry tends to precede outright fear. White isn't simply indulging in exaggerated precautions, since Black's ...Ne4 and ...Nc3+ threat is very real. Polgar takes steps to absorb the coming wave of attack.' White tries to deprive black the control over the square c3,but opens the king,which proves to be crucial. McShane: 'Otherwise Black will play Ne4, threatening Nc3+ and, if need be, break with f6!' '?' Bangiev. Finkel,A: 'White tries to deprive black the control over the square c3,but opens the king,which proves to be crucial. McShane: 'Otherwise Black will play Ne4, threatening Nc3+ and, if need be, break with f6!' '?' Bangiev. '} (22. g4 Ne4 23. Rdd3 Rc8 (23... f6 24. exf6 Rxf6 25. Ne2 Qc5 26. Rhe3 $13) 24. h5 f6 $1 25. exf6 gxf6 $15) (22. Ka1 {Bangiev} Ne4 23. Rhd3 $15 {Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung. Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung. Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung.'' Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung. Finkel,A: 'Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung.'}) 22... Qb7 {'!' Bangiev. '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''!' Bangiev. '} 23. Rc1 Ne4 24. cxb4 Rxb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White remains on the defensive, having to watch for tricks on b3, c3, d4 and even c4.'} 25. Rd3 {McShane: 'The knight on e4 is White's main problem, as it stops him doubling his rooks on the c-file and posting his queen on a better square'} (25. Nc2 {Bangiev} Rc4 26. Nxa3 Rxc1+ (26... Ra4 $5) 27. Qxc1 Rc8 28. Qe1 (28. Nc2 d4 $19 { d3, a6  d3, a6 Finkel,A: 'Finkel,Alexander: ' d3, a6''  d3, a6 Finkel,A: ' d3, a6'}) 28... Qa7 29. Nc2 Qc5 $19) ({White is also in trouble after} 25. Nc6 Rb6 26. Nd4 Rc8 27. Rh1 Rc4 $1 28. Qe3 Rbb4 29. Ne2 h5 {.}) 25... Rc4 $1 $17 {Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Shirov begins a cold-blooded attack, divorced from emotion.' Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: 'Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. ' Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: 'Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. ' Finkel,A: 'Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. Finkel,Alexander: 'Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev.''} 26. Rxc4 {'?!' Bangiev. '?!' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''?!' Bangiev. '} (26. g4 Rfc8 27. Rc2 Rxc2 28. Nxc2 Qb5 29. Qe3 Nf2 $1 $17 {[%csl Rd3,Rg4]}) ({Finkel,A: 'Byron Jacobs: 'On'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If White plays a waiting game with'} 26. Rdd1 {McShane Cyrus Lakdawala: ','} Rfc8 {threatening Nc3+ Finkel,A: 'threatening Nc3+ Byron Jacobs: 'White is under great pressure, but now Shirov gets him very quickly.'' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'forces'} 27. Ka1 (27. Rxc4 dxc4 28. Ka1 cxb3 29. axb3 h5 {and White's king remains exposed. Her pieces are also tied down to defence of numerous weaknesses like b3, c3, d4 and even the kingside pawns.}) 27... Qb6 $1 {is no better, as the d4 knight must now retreat}) (26. g3 $142 {Bangiev}) 26... dxc4 {[%csl Rb1][%cal Gb7b1]} 27. Rd1 $6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): White's position trembles like a tuning fork: Attackers convene, conspiring devious methods of deposing White's king. How did Shirov continue his attack? --- ANSWER: Double attack. Both b3 and g2 are simultaneously threatened.'} ({It was possible to put up some rsistance after} 27. Re3 $5 Nc5 28. Qe2 cxb3 29. Nxb3 Qb4 30. g3 Rc8 $17) 27... Nc5 {'-+' Bangiev. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Shirov's genius is one of a general who always retains presence of mind, even in the most cluttered battlefields.' '-+' Bangiev. Finkel,A: ''-+' Bangiev. '} 28. Qc3 Qxg2 {McShane: 'The diagonal opens for the queen.'} 29. b4 (29. Qxc4 {McShane} Qb2#) (29. bxc4 Qe4+ 30. Ka1 Qxf4 {is also hopeless for White.}) 29... Nd3 $19 30. Qxa3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (combination alert): When we are in desperation mode, we snatch at any hope which presents itself - however small. White's queen appears to be one of those seemingly vivacious women who we suspect hides her depression from the world. White's two connected queenside passers would be great...if only it were an ending! Examine the position carefully and you will discover something amiss in White's defensive barrier. We don't need Sherlock Holmes' powers of inductive reasoning to find Black's trick.'} (30. b5 {is also hopeless as} Qd5 31. Ka1 Nxf4 32. b6 Qc5 33. Rb1 Nd5 {wins.}) 30... Ra8 {'!' McShane. Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Double attack. White's queen can't afford to leave her protection of a2. --- Black's forces run riot over the queenside.' '!' McShane. Finkel,A: ''!' McShane. '} 31. Rxd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'No choice.'} (31. Qc3 Qxa2# {is mate! The days of the queen's benign indifference are over.}) 31... cxd3 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Also crushing is'} 31... Rxa3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '"I'm afraid your presence is superfluous," Black's rook informs the queen.'} 32. Rxa3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is met by'} Qg1+ (32... g5 $1 {which eliminates back rank vulgarities.} 33. fxg5 Qg1+ 34. Kc2 Qxd4 {and Black wins.}) 33. Kc2 Qf2+ 34. Kb1 Qe1+ 35. Kc2 Qe4+ $19) {McShane: But Shirov's move wipes out all counterchances...} 32. Qxd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The queen performs a clumsy, embarrassed curtsey and quickly leaves the room.'} Qxa2+ 33. Kc1 Qa1+ (33... Ra3 $1 34. Qe4 Qa1+ 35. Kd2 Qc3+ 36. Ke2 Ra2+ {is a quicker finish.}) 34. Kd2 Rd8 35. Ke3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's grievously wounded king is top priority in what is now essentially a triage unit.'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} Qe1+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '"You demand redress for past injustices. Yet how can I, the law itself, be anything but just?" Black's queen asks White's king.'} 36. Kf3 {, White's king, a hunted fawn, breathes raggedly from the chase and} Qxb4 {ends the contest, since after} 37. Ke3 {, Black can nonchalantly trade all the pieces for a won pawn ending if he so wishes. --- SUMMARY: This game raises a side question: Where did White go wrong? Where was White's initial error which allowed her position to degenerate? The answer I suspect may be 14 Ne2 which may be inaccurate. However the superior 14 Na4 leads to no more than dynamic equality for White.} 0-1

