e a premature.' Sonst kann Weiss xf6 spielen, mit deutlichem Positionsvorteil. Sonst kann Weiss xf6 spielen, mit deutlichem Positionsvorteil. Timman,Jan H: 'Ribli: 'Sonst kann Weiss xf6 spielen, mit deutlichem Positionsvorteil.''} ({Ribli,Zoltan Hera,Imre: 'The evaluation of'} 16... axb5 {Ftacnik Ftacnik Timman,Jan H: '(Ribli) Ftacnik' Ribli,Zoltan Hera,Imre: '!!' Ribli,Zoltan Hera,Imre: 'is surprisingly not that obvious. One would consider it as quite strange at first glance. Indeed, it contains a lot of complications, where Black's play is amazingly difficult. Undertaking double pawns and many other unpleasant things are far from the normal attitude.'} 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Bxb5 f5 (18... Ba6 {Ribli,Zoltan Hera,Imre} 19. Bxa6 (19. c4 Bxb5 20. cxb5 {Here I hardly believed to my eyes! Even though White has an extra pawn and besides two connected passers, the computer jauntily parries the danger:} Qc4 $8 21. Rb1 Ra4 $8 22. Qe2 (22. b6 Ke7 23. b7 Rb8 24. Rb6 d6) 22... Qxe4 23. Qxe4 Rxe4+ 24. Kd2 Ke7 25. b6 Rb8 26. b7 Ra4 27. Rb3 Ra7 28. Rhb1 d6 {followed by ...d7-c7 is also fine for Black.}) 19... Rxa6 20. O-O {looks like a hard task for the second player either, though there is some light at the end of the tunnel.} Ke7 21. Rfd1 (21. h4 Rha8 22. Qh5 Rxa3 23. Rxa3 Rxa3 24. Qxh7 Rxc3 25. h5 Rc1 26. Rxc1 Qxc1+ 27. Kg2 Qg5 {In spite of the well-advanced pawn, Black should be able to hold the queen ending. In my opinion, it is impossible from a human to foresee everything so long and clear!} 28. h6 Qg4 29. f3 Qg5 30. Qg7 Qd2+ 31. Kh3 Qd1 32. h7 Qxf3 33. h8=Q Qh1+ 34. Kg4 Qxe4+ $11) (21. Rfb1 Rha8 22. Rb3 Ra4 $44) 21... Rha8 22. c4 Qxc4 23. Rd3 {A lot of positive details are on White's side, such as having a passed pawn, better structure, safer king. On the other hand, converting them would be not that straightforward at all.}) 19. O-O Rb8 20. Rab1 $16) 17. Qxe4 {Timman,Jan H: 'TonyK: 'Clearly forced, but White has calculated well.''} Bb7 {[%cal Rb7h1]} ({Timman,Jan H: 'Ton