1. Nf3 {Knaak,R Knaak Knaak Ftacnik Knaak: 'Knaak,Rainer Ftacnik Knaak,Rainer: 'Knaak Knaak Ftacnik''} Nf6 2. c4 b6 3. d4 e6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Qc2 Nxc3 8. bxc3 Be7 9. e4 O-O 10. Bd3 c5 11. O-O Qc8 12. Qe2 Ba6 13. Rd1 cxd4 ({Black often delays exchanging on d4 with} 13... Bxd3 14. Rxd3 Nd7 {; for example} 15. e5 $5 {(leaving the knight on d7 short of squares)} cxd4 16. cxd4 Qc4 {(Black's queen patrols the light squares)} 17. Bg5 Bd8 $1 18. Rad1 Qd5 19. h4 $5 f6 $5 {(activating pieces at a cost of a weakness)} 20. exf6 Bxf6 21. Re3 h6 22. Bf4 Rae8 23. Bg3 Rf7 {and Black was doing okay in the game Radjabov-Leko, Dortmund 2003: both sides have an isolated pawn in the centre.}) 14. cxd4 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 {John Emms: '!?' Vermutlich eine Unterschätzung der Gegnerin.} ({Mit} 15. Rxd3 {konnte Weiß die Damen auf dem Brett halten.}) ({John Emms: 'Normally White recaptures with the rook on d3:'} 15. Rxd3 {Ftacnik} Nd7 {John Emms: 'with lines similar to the one discussed in the previous note.'} 16. Bb2 (16. Bg5 $6 Bxg5 17. Nxg5 h6 18. Nh3 Qc4 19. Nf4 Nf6 20. Re1 Rfd8 21. Qd1 Rac8 $15 {Peshina-Gurevich,M/Eger op/1987}) 16... Qa6 17. Rad1 Rfe8 18. Qe3 (18. h4 Qb5 19. g3 Rac8 20. Kg2 Qa4 21. R1d2 Bf8 22. Qe3 Rc2 23. Qf4 Rxd2 24. Nxd2 Qc2 25. Rf3 {½-½ Ribli,Z-Almasi,Z/Altensteig/1994/ (42)}) 18... Rac8 19. d5 exd5 20. Rxd5 Nf8 21. h4 Qa4 22. R1d4 {½-½ Bareev,E-Rodriguez,A/Sochi 1988/ (22)}) 15... Qa6 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'Black is not adverse to an exchange of queens, as this greatly reduces White's attacking potential.'} 16. Bb2 (16. d5 {Ftacnik} Qxd3 $1 17. Rxd3 exd5 18. exd5 Bd6 19. Be3 Rc8 20. Re1 Nd7 $11) ({John Emms: 'Because of this, maybe White should consider'} 16. Qe3 $5 {Ftacnik John Emms: ', intending Bb2 etc.'} Nd7 17. Bb2 Rac8 $13) 16... Qxd3 17. Rxd3 {Dieses Endspiel haben beide angestrebt - Polgar aus objektiven Gründen, Timman vielleicht deshalb, weil er glaubt, im Endspiel der Bessere zu sein.} Nd7 18. Rc1 {John Emms: 'Naturally taking control of the open file.'} ({John Emms: 'Obviously Black always has to consider the thrust d4-d5, but here it shouldn't be a problem:'} 18. d5 {Ftacnik} Nc5 {John Emms: '!'} 19. Re3 Rfd8 20. Rd1 Rac8 $11 {John Emms: '(Ftacnik) and Black is well placed.'}) 18... Rfc8 19. Rdc3 Rxc3 20. Rxc3 {Kaum vorstellbar, dass Weiß schlechter steht, doch die schwarze Bauernmehrheit am Damenflügel stellt zumindest eine Gefahr dar.} {John Emms: 'This attack on e4 is quite annoying for White because'} Nf6 {John Emms: '!'} 21. Nd2 (21. e5 $2 {Ftacnik John Emms: 'allows the knight to jump into a juicy outpost with'} Nd5 $17 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: '.'}) 21... b5 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'At first sight it looks as if White's control of the c-file should give him an advantage, but with this move Polgar shows that she also has a trump card - the pawn majority on the queenside.' Ftacnik: 'Polgar shows, that she has potentially passed pawn as well.'} 22. Rc6 ({Nach} 22. Rc7 Kf8 23. Rb7 a6 {'' Ftacnik. muss der weiße Turm den Rückzug antreten.}) 22... Kf8 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'Bringing the king into the action. In some lines it can chase the rook away.'} 23. f3 Ke8 24. Nb3 Nd7 {John Emms: 'All logical stuff from Polgar: the knight eyes the outpost on c4 via b6.'} 25. Kf1 $2 {John Emms: 'The king is unfortunately placed on this square, as we shall see in the coming tactics.'} ({Besser ist} 25. Kf2 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'is more logical, after which the position is still finely balanced.'}) (25. d5 {Ftacnik} exd5 26. exd5 Nb6 $15) 25... Nb6 26. Nc5 $2 {John Emms: 'After this move White is in real trouble. His pieces look impressively placed but this turns out to be a facade.' Ftacnik: 'A serious mistake, since white does nothing against the massive improvement of black pieces. They will attack his weak pawns a3 and d4 to a decisive efffect.' Die weiße Aktivität greift völlig ins Leere.} ({Aber auch nach} 26. Na5 Kd7 27. Rc2 {( Timman) '=' Ftacnik.} Rc8 28. Rxc8 Nxc8 29. Ke2 {hat Weiß wegen der schwarzen Majorität am Damenflügel keinen völligen Ausgleich, aber wenigstens geht kein Material verloren.}) 26... Nc4 $1 27. Bc1 Rd8 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'Suddenly White has problems with his d4-pawn. Worse still, when this drops off White is faced with the threat of ...Rd1+, winning the bishop. White's error of judgement on his 25th move begins to haunt him.'} 28. a4 {John Emms: 'As Ftacnik pointed out, this tactic would have saved White if his king had been on f2 instead of f1.' Dies würde funktionieren, wenn der König im 25. Zug nach f2 gegangen wäre!} (28. Nb3 {can be answered by} a5 $1 {planning ...a5-a4.}) 28... Rxd4 $1 29. Nb3 Rd3 {John Emms: '!'} 30. axb5 Ne5 31. Rc8+ Kd7 {John Emms: 'It's the end of the tactical skirmish and White simply loses a piece.'} 32. Ra8 Rxb3 33. Rxa7+ Ke8 0-1

