1. e4 {Lukacs Lukacs Lukacs} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 e6 {This is a somewhat passive defence.} (5... Ngf6 $142 {is the main line.}) 6. Bd3 Be7 (6... h6 $6 {can be met by the aggressive} 7. Qh5 $1 Qe7 {The  is hindering the development of his own pieces.} 8. N1f3 Ngf6 9. Qh4 Rg8 10. Ne4 g5 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 12. Qg3 $16 {Djurhuus-Agdestein, NOR-ch Oslo 1998}) 7. N1f3 h6 8. Ne4 (8. Nxe6 $6 {can't be correct.} fxe6 9. Bg6+ Kf8 10. Qe2 Bf6 11. Bf4 Ne7 12. Bh5 Qa5+ 13. Nd2 Nb6 14. O-O Nbd5 15. Bg3 Nf5 16. c3 Bd7 17. Ne4 Be7 18. Be5 Nf6 19. Bf3 Nxe4 20. Bxe4 Bf6 $17 {Antoniou-Rodrigues, ITA tt 1998}) 8... Ngf6 9. Nxf6+ {Now Black has to decide which piece will take back on f6.} (9. Qe2 Nxe4 (9... c5 {is the quick way to attack the .} 10. Nxf6+ (10. Nxc5 Bxc5 (10... Nxc5 11. dxc5 {- 10.xf6}) 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Bb5+ Bd7 13. O-O a6 14. Bxd7+ Ncxd7 15. c4 O-O 16. Bf4 Qa5 17. Ne5 $14 {and White is only slightly better, Palac-Dizdarevic, Zadar op 1995.}) 10... Nxf6 11. dxc5 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Qxc5 13. O-O-O {White has the lead in development.} Bd7 14. Kb1 a6 15. a3 Qb6 16. Be3 Bc5 17. Bxc5 Qxc5 18. Ne5 Bb5 19. Bxb5+ axb5 20. Rd3 $16 {Ivanchuk-Karpov, Amber-blind rapid 1995}) 10. Qxe4 Nf6 11. Qe2 c5 (11... b6 {is too slow.} 12. Bd2 Bb7 13. O-O-O Qc7 14. Ne5 Nd7 15. f4 {White has a strong  in the .} Rf8 16. Qg4 Bf6 17. Bb4 c5 18. dxc5 Bxe5 19. fxe5 bxc5 20. Bb5 O-O-O 21. Rxd7 Rxd7 22. Bxd7+ Kxd7 23. Qxg7 Qc8 24. Rd1+ Bd5 {(Wolff-Adams, Preston 1989)} 25. Ba3 $1 Kc6 26. b3 $18) 12. dxc5 {Lukacs: '- 10.xf6'} Bxc5 13. Bd2 { 0-0-0} Qb6 14. O-O-O Bd7 15. Ne5 Bc6 16. f4 Bd5 17. c4 Bd4 18. b3 Bc6 19. Rhe1 O-O {Kwiatkowski-Arkell, Hastings Challengers 1998}) (9. c3 O-O (9... c5 10. O-O cxd4 11. Nxd4 O-O 12. Re1 Re8 {This looks again a bit passive.} 13. Nxf6+ Bxf6 14. Bc2 a6 15. Qg4 {White has good prospects of an .} Kh8 (15... Bxd4 16. Qxd4 Nf6 17. Qe5 $14 {}) 16. Re3 {bringing the  into the  on the third rank.} Nf8 17. Rh3 e5 18. Nf5 g6 19. Bxh6 Kg8 20. Rg3 $16 {King-Adams, London 1989}) 10. O-O c5 11. Ne5 (11. Re1 $5) 11... cxd4 12. cxd4 Qb6 {putting pressure on the isolated d .} 13. Nxf6+ Bxf6 14. Be3 Rd8 15. Qc2 Nf8 16. Rfd1 Bd7 17. d5 Qd6 18. Nxd7 Rxd7 19. dxe6 Qxe6 20. Bc4 {} Rc7 21. Bxe6 Rxc2 22. Bd5 Rxb2 23. Rab1 Rxb1 24. Rxb1 Rd8 25. Bxb7 Bd4 $11 {eliminating White's , Kasparov-Ivanchuk, Novgorod 1995.}) 9... Bxf6 10. O-O b6 {This looks a bit slow now.} (10... c5 11. c3 cxd4 (11... O-O 12. Bc2 { d3 × h7}) 12. Nxd4 $5 (12. cxd4 {creates an isolated , but Black is suffering from the lack of development.} Nb6 (12... O-O 13. Qe2 Re8 14. Re1 $36) 13. Ne5 (13. Re1 Nd5 {blockading × d5.}) 13... Bd7 14. Be4 $1 Nd5 $5 {Cvetkovic} (14... Bxe5 $6 15. dxe5 Bc6 16. Bxc6+ bxc6 17. Qg4 Kf8 18. Be3 $16 {Chandler-Speelman, Reykjavik 1991}) 15. Re1 O-O 16. Qh5 $14 {× h6}) 12... a6 $1 (12... Nc5 13. Bc2 e5 14. Nf5 $14) (12... O-O {can be dangerous because of the typical threats on the b1-h7 .} 13. Bc2 $1 a6 (13... Ne5 14. Qh5 $40) (13... Re8 14. Nb5 {× d6}) 14. Qd3 Re8 {Sad, but true.} (14... g6 $2 {founders on the devastating} 15. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 16. Qxg6+ Bg7 17. Qh7+ Kf7 18. Bg6+ Kf6 19. Bh5 $1 $18) 15. Qh7+ Kf8 16. Re1 $16) 13. Re1 (13. Bc2 Qc7) 13... O-O 14. Bc2 Re8 15. Qg4 {- 9.c3 c5 10.0-0 cxd4 11.xd4} (15. Qd3 Nf8 {defending × h7})) 11. Bf4 (11. Qe2 Bb7 12. Re1 (12. Bf4 {- 11.f4}) 12... c5 (12... O-O $2 {is again dubious because of} 13. Qe4 $18) 13. dxc5 Nxc5 $5 (13... bxc5 14. Bb5 {with threats against the pinned .} a6 (14... Qc7 15. Ne5 Bxe5 16. Qxe5 Qxe5 17. Rxe5 $14) (14... O-O 15. Rd1 Bd5 16. c4 Bxf3 17. Qxf3 Ne5 18. Qh5 Qb6 19. Bf4 $36) 15. Ba4 O-O 16. Rd1 Bxf3 17. Qxf3 Ne5 18. Qe2 Qb6 19. c3 Rfd8 20. Be3 $14 { and weaknesses on the .}) 14. Bb5+ Kf8 15. c3 a6 $1 16. Bc4 b5 $11 {and the  is gone.}) 11... Bb7 12. Be4 (12. Bd6 {is simply met by} Be7) (12. Qe2 $142 $1 {is more flexible. Lukacs: '- 11.f4'} c5 (12... Qe7 13. Rad1 O-O-O 14. Rfe1 $14 {with pressure on the .}) 13. dxc5 bxc5 (13... Nxc5 14. Bb5+ Kf8 15. Rad1 $16) (13... Bxb2 14. Rad1 $1 $36) 14. Rad1 Qb6 15. Bb5 Bc6 16. Bxc6 Qxc6 17. b3 $14 {and Black has weak pawns on the .}) 12... Qc8 (12... Be7 13. Re1 Nf6 14. Bd3 O-O 15. Qe2 $14) 13. Qe2 (13. Re1 O-O 14. c3 c5 {This is the problem. If White exchanges his light-squared , his  is not dangerous anymore.} 15. Bc2 Rd8 {vacating the square for the .} (15... Ba6 $6 {is illogical.} 16. d5 $14) (15... cxd4 $6 16. Qd3 $1 g6 17. Bxh6 $16) 16. Qd3 Nf8 $11) 13... O-O 14. c4 (14. Rad1 c5 $11) 14... c5 15. d5 (15. Bxb7 Qxb7 $11) 15... exd5 16. Bxd5 (16. cxd5 $2 {is a mistake because of} Ba6 17. Bd3 Re8 18. Qc2 Bxd3 19. Qxd3 Bxb2 20. Rae1 Nf6 $17) 16... Re8 17. Qc2 b5 $5 (17... Bxd5 18. cxd5 Qa6 $1 {was a serious alternative activating the .} 19. Be3 (19. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 20. Rxe1 $6 (20. Nxe1 Re8) 20... Qxa2 {Black can bravely take the .} 21. Qf5 Nf8 22. Bxh6 Rd8 23. Rd1 Qa4 24. Re1 Qd7 $17) 19... Rad8 20. Rfd1 Ne5 21. Nxe5 Rxe5 22. d6 Re6 23. Bf4 Qe2 $15 {successfully blockading the d .}) 18. Rae1 Rxe1 19. Rxe1 bxc4 20. Bxf7+ {White is going for a draw by perpetual checks.} (20. Qxc4 Bxd5 21. Qxd5 Nb6 22. Qd2 Qf5 {is not giving anything for White either.}) 20... Kxf7 21. Qxc4+ Kg6 22. Qd3+ Kf7 23. Qc4+ 1/2-1/2

