1. d4 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R Huzman Huzman,Alexander Huzman,Alexander Huzman,Alexander Huzman,Alexander: 'Huzman'} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 Na6 7. O-O Qe8 8. Re1 e5 9. Be3 Ng4 10. Bg5 (10. Bc1 f5 (10... Kh8 11. h3 Nh6 12. Bf1 f6 13. Be3 Nf7 14. Rb1 c5 15. dxc5 dxc5 16. Nd5 Bd7 17. a3 Rc8 18. b4 $14 {1-0 Azmaiparashvili,Z-Danailov,S/Ponferrada 1991/EXT 99 (34)}) 11. exf5 (11. h3 Nf6 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Bf1 e4 14. d5 Nc5 15. Nd4 a5 16. Ndb5 Qd8 17. Be3 Nfd7 18. Qh5 Ne5 $17 {0-1 Aronov,V-Lederer,Y/Match \ Beer-Sheva (Israel) 1998 (35)}) 11... gxf5 12. dxe5 dxe5 13. h3 Nf6 14. c5 e4 15. Bxa6 bxa6 16. Nd4 Qe5 17. Nb3 Kh8 18. Be3 f4 19. Bd4 Qg5 $40 {0-1 Van Wely,L-Gelfand,B/Arnhem 1988/TD (30)}) 10... exd4 (10... f6 11. Bd2 f5 12. exf5 gxf5 13. c5 e4 14. cxd6 Qh5 15. h3 exf3 16. Bxf3 cxd6 17. Bf4 Kh8 18. Nd5 Bd7 19. Bxd6 Rf7 20. Bg3 $16 {½-½ Sulava,N-Milov,V/Geneva 1996/CBM 51 ext (48) Huzman/Quintiliano,R: '½-½ Sulava,N-Milov,V/Geneva 1996/CBM 51 ext (48)'}) (10... h6 11. Bh4 Nf6 12. dxe5 dxe5 13. Nd2 c6 14. Bg3 b6 15. a3 Bb7 16. b4 Rd8 17. Qc2 Nh7 18. Rad1 Ng5 19. Nf3 Nxf3+ 20. Bxf3 Qe7 {½-½ Danielian,O-Makarjev,I/Protvino 1992/EXT 97 (20) Huzman/Quintiliano,R: '½-½ Danielian,O-Makarjev,I/Protvino 1992/EXT 97 (20)'}) 11. Nxd4 (11. Nd5 $5 {(Huzman/Quintiliano,R) is a more complicated variaton, but interesting also - Quintiliano}) 11... Qe5 (11... Nxh2 $2 12. Be3 $18) 12. Nf3 Qc5 13. Bh4 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'In this position, Black seems to be active and developing the pieces well , but it's not so easy. Actually, his pieces can easily be attacked by moves like h3 or a3-b4, and in many variations Black's  is badly placed, occupying the c5-square and then keeping the a6 out of play - Quintiliano'} Ne5 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'One of the natural reactions, because White can play d2 sometimes, attacking the g4 and preparing b3 - Quintiliano'} 14. Nxe5 dxe5 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R: '?!' Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'I don't like this move, closing in the dark-squared , and Black doesn't achieve anything good in return for this - Quintiliano'} (14... Qxe5 15. Rc1 {½-½ Sulava,N-Loncar,R/Pula 1997/EXT 98 (15) Huzman/Quintiliano,R: '½-½ Sulava,N-Loncar,R/Pula 1997/EXT 98 (15)'}) (14... Bxe5 {(Huzman/Quintiliano,R) 1-0 Quintiliano Pinto,R (2443)-Pichot,A (2550) 20th Copa Mercosur 2016 Buenos Aires ARG (2.4) we will see in the next game -}) 15. Rc1 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R: '?!' Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'Now Black has time to develop the pieces - Quintiliano'} (15. Nd5 $1 {[%cal Gh4e7] (Huzman/Quintiliano,R) immediately puts pressure on Black's position} f6 16. Qa4 $1 {[%cal Yb2b4]} c6 17. b4 Qd6 (17... Qd4 18. Rad1 Qxe4 19. Ne7+ Kh8 20. Bg3 {[%csl Re4] and the  is almost trapped}) 18. c5 Qd8 19. Ne3 $14 {[%cal Ye3c4,Yc4d6] -Quintiliano}) 15... Be6 (15... c6 16. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4]}) 16. Nd5 (16. a3 $142 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R} c6 17. b4 Qd4 18. Qc2 $14 {[%cal Ye1d1] Quintiliano}) 16... f6 17. a3 $14 c6 18. b4 Qd4 19. Qxd4 exd4 20. Nf4 $14 {White put f4 on blocading position on d3 after what they have possibility to improve their pawns advantage on the king's side. Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'White has put f4 in a blockading position on d3 after which he has the possibility to improve his pawn advantage on the kingside. Quintiliano: Actually, this position is very balanced. White can successfully blockade Black's passed pawn, but Black can develop his pieces and improve his position also.'} Bf7 21. Bg3 Rfe8 22. Bf1 Rac8 23. Nd3 h5 24. f3 Bh6 25. Rc2 Nb8 $2 (25... Be3+ $142 26. Bf2 Bxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Nc7 28. f4 $14) ({Why not the natural} 25... c5 {(Huzman/Quintiliano,R) ? Black just protects his good passed pawn} 26. bxc5 (26. Bf2 b6) 26... Be3+ $1 27. Bf2 Bxf2+ 28. Kxf2 Nxc5 29. Nxc5 Rxc5 {and Black is totally ok - Quintiliano}) 26. Bf2 (26. Nc5 $1 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R} b6 27. Nb7 {[%cal Yb7d6,Yc4c5] - Quintiliano}) 26... Rcd8 27. Rd1 {[%csl Rd4]} Be3 $2 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'Now Black is just losing the pawn - Quintiliano'} (27... b6 $142 28. g3 Na6 {[%cal Yc6c5]} 29. Bg2 c5 30. f4 $14 Bf8 $15 {Huzman/Quintiliano,R}) 28. Bxe3 dxe3 29. Re1 Rd4 30. Nc5 $16 Rd2 (30... b6 31. Nb3 Rd6 32. Rxe3 $16) 31. Rxd2 exd2 32. Rd1 b6 33. Nb3 (33. Nb7 $5 Re7 34. Nd6 c5 35. b5 Rd7 36. Rxd2 $16) 33... b5 34. Na5 (34. Rxd2 bxc4 (34... Bxc4 35. Bxc4+ bxc4 36. Na5 c3 37. Rc2 $16) 35. Na5 $16 (35. Nc5 $5 $16 {[%csl Rb8]})) 34... Rd8 35. Kf2 bxc4 36. Nxc4 Bxc4 37. Bxc4+ Kf8 38. Ke3 $18 Ke7 39. Rxd2 Nd7 (39... Rxd2 40. Kxd2 Nd7 41. Ke3 g5 42. f4 $18 {[%csl Rh5]}) 40. Rc2 Rb8 (40... c5 41. bxc5 (41. b5 $5 Nb6 42. Be2 Kd6 43. f4 $18) 41... Nxc5 42. Bd5 $18 Rc8 43. f4 Kd6 (43... Rc7 44. e5) 44. Bf7 $18) 41. Be2 Kd6 42. f4 a5 43. Rd2+ Kc7 44. bxa5 Rb3+ 45. Rd3 Rb2 46. Bf3 (46. Rc3 $5 Kd6 $140 47. Bf3 Nc5 48. e5+ $18) 46... Nc5 (46... Rb5 $5 47. Rc3 (47. a6 Ra5 48. Rc3 Rxa6 49. f5 $5) 47... Rxa5 48. e5 fxe5 49. Rxc6+ Kd8 50. Rxg6 Rxa3+ 51. Ke4 Ra4+ 52. Kf5 Rxf4+ 53. Kg5 $16) 47. Rc3 Na4 48. Rc4 Rb3+ 49. Kd2 Rxa3 50. e5 $1 fxe5 51. Rxc6+ Kd7 52. fxe5 {Now it is clear that game is over.White have big positional and material advantage. Huzman/Quintiliano,R: 'Now it is clear that the game is over. White has a big positional and material advantage.'} g5 53. e6+ Ke7 54. a6 Ra2+ 55. Rc2 Ra3 56. a7 Nb6 57. Rc6 Ra2+ 58. Kc1 Na8 59. Rc8 Ra1+ 60. Kb2 Rxa7 61. Rxa8 Rxa8 62. Bxa8 Kxe6 63. Kc3 h4 64. Kd4 Kf5 65. Bd5 g4 66. Ke3 Ke5 67. Bc6 Kf5 68. Bd7+ 1-0

