{Andrew Greet: 'This game also features a dangerous attacking scheme, although this time the play is rather more double-edged as White must be willing to sacrifice some material. Fortunately the potential rewards more than justify the risk.' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'This game also features a dangerous attacking scheme, although this time the play is rather more double-edged as White must be willing to sacrifice some material. Fortunately the potential rewards more than justify the risk.' Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'page 66 B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack''' James Vigus: 'Please fasten your seatbelt!' Andrew Greet: 'This game also features a dangerous attacking scheme, although this time the play is rather more double-edged as White must be willing to sacrifice some material. Fortunately the potential rewards more than justify the risk. '} 1. e4 {Finkel,Alexander Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A Finkel,A: ' '' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander Finkel,A Finkel,A: ' ' Finkel,Alexander: ' Finkel,A Finkel,A '' B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack} (1. d4 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} c5 $11) 1... g6 (1... d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 {1-0 (22) Polgar,J-Smirin,I Istanbul Olympiad 2000 [Andrew Greet] 1-0 (22) Polgar,J-Smirin,I Istanbul Olympiad 2000 Beating Unusual Def: 1 e [Andrew Greet]}) (1... e5 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} 2. a3 $11) 2. d4 (2. Nf3 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} c5 $14) 2... Bg7 (2... d6 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} 3. Be2 $14) 3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} d6 $14) 3... d6 (3... d5 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Bb5+ Nbd7 6. Nf3 $11) 4. f4 {G. Bertola: '1-0 (22) Polgar,J (2656)-Smirin,I (2677) Istanbul 2000'} (4. Nf3 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} Nc6 $14) 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'Black has a cramped position'''} 6. Be3 (6. e5 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} Nfd7 $11) 6... b6 {G. Bertola: 'Le coup princ ipal retenu par la theorie, un carrefour important et au vu de cette partie interessant est :' James Vigus: 'This has for some time been the most popular response to 6 Be3, though that might change owing to the combined onslaught of this game and Game 25. With 6...b6 Black invests a tempo to guarantee that he can play ...c5, at the same time preparing to bring his bishop to b7 to generate Sicilian-style pressure on e4. Black hopes this is justified by the fact that White's e5-push will be met by ...Ng4 (or sometimes ...Nd5), gaining a tempo by attacking the bishop on e3. However, it can happen that manoeuvres with this knight lose precious time.'} (6... Nbd7 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} 7. Bc1 $11 (7. Qd2 c5 8. O-O-O cxd4 9. Bxd4 Bh6 10. h3 Nh5 11. Be3 Ng3 12. Rg1 Nf6 {est incertain selon Tian.})) (6... Na6 {une partie disputee lors de l'Olympiade d4Istanbul s'est averee tresinstructive :} 7. Qd2 c5 8. O-O-O cxd4 9. Bxd4 b6 10. h4 Nc5 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. e5 Bg7 13. h5 Be6 14. hxg6 hxg6 15. Qf2 Qc7 {Arias L. - Aung Aung, et ici :} 16. exd6 exd6 17. Nd4 Rac8 18. f5 {est a considerer.}) (6... Ng4 {, experimental} 7. Bg1 h5 (7... e5 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. h3) 8. h3 Nf6 9. Be3 e6 10. Qd2 Nc6 11. O-O-O a6 12. Qf2 b5 13. e5 Nd5 14. Nxd5 exd5 15. g4 hxg4 16. hxg4 Bxg4 17. Bh3 Bh5 18. Rdg1 {avec attaque sur l'aile roi. Lyubimstev-Ferodov (St. Petersburg 2000)}) (6... c6 7. Qd2 b5 8. Bd3 Bb7 (8... b4 9. Ne2 a5 10. f5 d5 11. exd5 cxd5 12. Ng3 $14 {Nunn}) 9. h3 Nbd7 10. O-O {Une reaction logique face a l'aile dame noire en mouvement} a6 11. e5 Ne8 12. Ne4 Qc7 13. Rae1 Bc8 14. Qf2 Nb6 15. Qh4 Nd5 16. Bd2 Be6 $2 {pas une grande partie pour les Noirs sur le plan strategique et tactique.} 17. Neg5 h6 18. Bxg6 {1-0 (Prahov-Gagnon Montreal 2000)}) 7. Qd2 (7. e5 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander} Ng4 8. Bg1 Na6 $11 (8... c5 9. h3 Nh6 10. d5 Bb7 $1 11. Qd2 Nf5 12. Bh2 dxe5 13. fxe5 e6 $1 14. O-O-O $6 (14. Bc4 exd5 15. Bxd5 Nc6 {is unclear - Anand}) 14... exd5 15. Nxd5 Nc6 16. c3 Ncd4 $1 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 18. cxd4 Bg7 19. d5 c4 $1 20. Be2 Rc8 21. Kb1 Bh6 22. Bf4 Bxf4 23. Qxf4 Bxd5 24. h4 c3 25. bxc3 Rxc3 26. h5 Ne3 27. Ng5 Qc7 28. Nxh7 Rb3+ $1 {0-1, A.Beliavsky-V.Anand, Munich 1991, is the brilliant game that first inspired so much interest in 6...b6. Since then White has discovered that he can defend the bishop with 7 Qd2 before playing e5, and usually does not need to spend a tempo retreating it.})) (7. Bc4 c5 8. Qe2 $5 Nc6 9. e5 Ng4 10. O-O-O Nxe3 $1 11. Qxe3 Bg4 $1 {turned out well for Black in L.Nisipeanu-B.Avrukh, Wijk aan Zee 2000, after} 12. dxc5 $6 ({Avrukh indicates instead} 12. d5 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Nd4 14. Qe4 a6 15. Ne2 dxe5 16. fxe5 Qc7 17. Rhe1 Bxe5 18. Nxd4 Bf4+ 19. Kb1 cxd4 20. Rxd4 Bd6 {with equal chances}) 12... bxc5 13. Bd5 Qb6 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Bxf3 dxe5 {.}) (7. Bd3 {is solid and leads to Sicilian-style positions after} c5 8. Qe2 ({instead} 8. d5 $6 Na6 9. O-O Rb8 {reaches a position akin to Kramnik-Morozevich in the Introduction, in which the move Be3 is not very constructive for White, and after} 10. Nb5 Nb4 $1 11. Nxa7 $6 Bd7 12. c4 Nxd3 13. Qxd3 Nxe4 {Black was already slightly better in the game G.Mahia-F.Peralta, Argentinean Championship, San Luis 2006}) 8... cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bb7 {.}) (7. Qe2 $5 {is discussed in the notes to Game 25.}) 7... c5 {G. Bertola: 'Peu clair est :'} ({James Vigus: 'Polgar's whirlwind attack in the present game has discouraged Black players from repeating this, and during the last few years attention has switched to the developing move'} 7... Bb7 {Andrew Greet: 'was seen in the previous two games. The text move is an important alternative, for which White should be well prepared.' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'was seen in the previous two games. The text move is an important alternative, for which White should be well prepared.''' James Vigus: '(Game 25).'} 8. e5 {G. Bertola: 'Il faut examiner la continuation de Judith apres :'} (8. Bd3 Na6 (8... c5 9. O-O-O {et ici :} cxd4 (9... Ng4 10. Bg1 cxd4 11. Nxd4 e5 12. Nde2 exf4 13. Nxf4 Nd7 14. Be2 Nde5 {Azadmanesh-Pel (Hollande 2000) apres :} 15. Qxd6 Qxd6 16. Rxd6 {et la compensation pour le pion sacrifie est obscure.}) 10. Bxd4 Nc6 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. h4 $1 Nb4 $5 (12... h5 13. f5 $5) 13. h5 Rc8 14. hxg6 fxg6 15. f5 Rxc3 16. Qh6 Rf7 17. fxg6 hxg6 18. bxc3 Nxa2+ 19. Kd2 {etc. avec des complications peu claires.}) 9. a3 c5 10. d5 Nc7 11. h3 b5 12. O-O e6 13. dxe6 Nxe6 $13 {Rosen,W-Nau,G/World Seniors 2000}) 8... Ne4 (8... Ng4 9. O-O-O c5 {G. Bertola: '!?'} 10. dxc5 Nxe3 11. Qxe3 bxc5 12. Bc4 Nc6 13. Ng5 Nd4 14. h4 {G. Bertola: 'Drbohlav-Pribyl (Rep. Tcheque 2000).'} dxe5 15. fxe5 Qc7 $132 {Drbohlav,F-Pribyl,M/Extraliga CZE 2000}) 9. Nxe4 Bxe4 10. Ng5 Bb7 11. O-O-O $36) 8. O-O-O {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'This is the most interesting response;'''} ({Andrew Greet: 'although readers who find the game continuation too wild may wish to investigate' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: 'although readers who find the game continuation too wild may wish to investigate''' James Vigus: 'Alternatively:' Andrew Greet: 'This is the most interesting response, although readers who find the game continuation too wild may wish to investigate'} 8. d5 {Andrew Greet: ', which also offers good chances for an opening advantage.' Polgar,Judit: 'Ü 71/114' Polgar,Judit: 'Ü 71/114 Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: ', which also offers good chances for an opening advantage.''' James Vigus: 'is also quite promising, e.g.'} a6 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} (8... Nbd7 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A'} 9. h3 {James Vigus: '?!'} ({Sadler later thought this a waste of time, preferring} 9. a4 {e.g.} a6 10. Bc4 Rb8 11. Qe2 $1 {, while if Black plays ...Ne8-c7 he will be hit by h4-h5}) 9... Rb8 $5 (9... a6 10. a4 Rb8 11. e5 dxe5 12. fxe5 Nh5 13. e6 fxe6 14. dxe6 Ne5 $13) 10. Bb5 $6 (10. a4) 10... a6 11. Bxd7 Qxd7 $1 12. e5 Ne8 13. O-O $6 Bb7 14. Rad1 Nc7 {James Vigus: 'and by now Black had gained good counterplay, M.Sadler-J.Plaskett, Hastings 1997/8.'} 15. a3 $5 $14 {Sadler,M-Plaskett,J/Hastings Premier 9798}) (8... Bb7 $2 {does not have the desired effect of stopping White's e5-push:}) 9. e5 (9. a4 Bg4 10. Be2 Nbd7 11. e5 Ne8 12. e6 fxe6 13. Ng5 $16) 9... Nfd7 10. h4 dxe5 11. h5 e4 12. Ng5 $40 {Sadler,M-Mestel,A/BCF-ch 1997}) (8. e5 Ng4 9. Bc4 Nxe3 ({but Gallagher suggests} 9... Nc6 {as an improvement for Black}) 10. Qxe3 cxd4 11. Nxd4 dxe5 12. fxe5 Bb7 13. O-O-O {gave White a menacing position in A.Shirov-S.Simonenko, Zabala (rapid) 2001.}) 8... cxd4 {G. Bertola: 'Possible est :'} (8... Nbd7 9. e5 (9. h4 $5) 9... Ng4 10. Bc4 $5 Nxe3 11. Qxe3 cxd4 12. Nxd4 dxe5 13. Nc6 {avec avantage blanc selon Nunn.}) ({After} 8... Bb7 {,} 9. d5 $1 {transposes to Sadvakasov-Dzhumaev, above,} ({whereas} 9. dxc5 bxc5 10. e5 Ng4 {transposes to Game 25.}) 9... Na6 10. e5 Ng4 11. h3 $1 {- White's willingness to exchange his bishop for the sake of tempi and a kingside attack is a typical theme of this line -} Nxe3 12. Qxe3 c4 13. h4 b5 14. h5 b4 15. Ne4 c3 16. hxg6 fxg6 17. Rxh7 $1 Qb6 18. Qd3 Qa5 19. Nf6+ $1 exf6 20. Qxg6 Qc7 21. Bd3 {and Black resigned, D.Sadvakasov-M.Dzhumaev, Hyderabad 2005}) ({Attempts to rehabilitate the 7...c5 line have focused on} 8... Nc6 {, but it looks terribly precarious after} 9. d5 {:} Z0 (9... Na5 10. h3 (10. e5 {at once is possible, but Black's queenside play is slow enough that White can enjoy the luxury of preventing ...Ng4}) 10... Rb8 11. e5 Nd7 12. h4 dxe5 13. h5 e4 14. Nxe4 Nf6 15. Nxf6+ exf6 (15... Bxf6 16. hxg6 hxg6 17. g3 {prepares to swing the queen across to the h-file}) 16. Rh4 f5 17. g3 Re8 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Ne5 {is clearly better for White, N.Ninov-B.Chatalbashev, Tsarevo 2001.}) (9... Nb4 10. h3 (10. e5 Ng4 11. h3 Nxe3 12. Qxe3 {is also good for White,}) ({but again he has the luxury of time to spend on preventing ...Ng4; after} 10. a3 Na6 11. e5 Ng4 12. Bg1 Bh6 13. exd6 exd6 14. h3 Nf6 15. g4 Bg7 16. f5 b5 $5 17. Bxb5 Nc7 18. Bc6 Rb8 19. Qf4 Bb7 20. Bxb7 Rxb7 {Black had a bit of compensation for the pawn, G.Hernandez-K.Movsziszian, Lorca 2004}) 10... Bb7 11. a3 a5 $5 12. Kb1 (12. axb4 $2 axb4 13. Nb1 Nxe4 {is Black's idea}) 12... b5 13. Bxb5 Qb6 14. Qe2 Ba6 15. Bxa6 Nxa6 16. e5 Nd7 {was D.Tyomkin-B.Maryasin, Israeli League 2002, and now Tyomkin suggests} 17. Bc1 {with a clear extra pawn for White.})) 9. Bxd4 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: '!' Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'White has a very active position''' James Vigus: '!' James Vigus: 'An unstereotyped idea: Polgar wants to eliminate Black's knight on f6 in order to hack away at Black's kingside defences.'} (9. Nxd4 {is not a bad move, but the non-stereotypical move seen in the game is the best way to put the opponent under pressure. Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'is not a bad move, but the non-stereotypical move seen in the game is the best way to put the opponent under pressure.''} Bb7 {Polgar,Judit} 10. e5 Ng4 11. e6 f5 12. Nf3 {James Vigus: 'is not so clear according to Polgar.'}) 9... Nc6 ({In the event of} 9... Bb7 $5 {(A.Ashton-C.Beaumont, British League 2007), the most consistent response is Polgar,Judit: '(Finkel,Alexander) (A.Ashton-C.Beaumont, British League 2007), the most consistent response is'} 10. h4 $1 {when possible replies include:} Z0 ({. The e-pawn is poisoned, as revealed by the variations} 10... Bxe4 $4 11. Bxf6 Bxf3 12. Bxg7 Bxd1 13. Bxf8 {,}) ({, and} 10... Nxe4 $4 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. Bxg7 Bxf3 13. Bxf8 Bxd1 14. Bh6 Bh5 ({if} 14... Bg4 15. Qd4) 15. g4 $1 Bxg4 16. Qd4 e5 ({or} 16... f6 17. Qd5+) 17. fxe5 {, when White wins in each instance.}) (10... h5 {weakens the kingside and} 11. f5 $1 {offers White good attacking chances.}) (10... Nbd7 11. e5 (11. h5 $5 {also looks interesting, although I was unable to find anything conclusive after} Nxh5 12. Bxg7 Kxg7) 11... dxe5 12. fxe5 Ng4 ({also promising for White are both} 12... Nd5 13. h5) ({and} 12... Ne4 13. Nxe4 Bxe4 14. h5) 13. e6 Nde5 ({or} 13... Bh6 14. Ng5 Nde5 15. Qe2) 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. h5 {with some initiative for White.})) 10. Bxf6 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: '!''' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Andrew Greet: '!''' G. Bertola: '!?' G. Bertola: 'Un developpement logique, comme le demontre les exemples precedents, qui conduit aux roques opposes et, pour eviter de perdre des temps les blancs, "sacrifient" leur Fou pour accelerer l'attaque.' James Vigus: '!'} Bxf6 {Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,Alexander: 'Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'Black has the pair of bishops'''} 11. h4 $1 {Polgar's play is fantastic:she simply doesn't give Smirin time to breeze! Polgar,Judit: 'N' 'N' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!' Finkel,A: 'Polgar's play is fantastic:she simply doesn't give Smirin time to breeze!'' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '!' Finkel,A: 'Polgar's play is fantastic:she simply doesn't give Smirin time to breeze!' Finkel,Alexander: '!' Finkel,Alexander: 'Polgar's play is fantastic:she simply doesn't give Smirin time to breeze! Polgar's play is fantastic:she simply doesn't give Smirin time to breeze! Andrew Greet: 'The point behind White's 9th move is revealed. It is worth 'sacrificing' the valuable dark-squared bishop for a knight in order to gain time for the attack.' Houdini 1.5a w32 (2s): 'Black has a cramped position''' James Vigus: 'The most incisive.'} ({James Vigus: 'After'} 11. e5 $2 {Polgar,Judit: '?!' '?!' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '?'' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '?' Finkel,Alexander: '?''} dxe5 12. Qxd8 (12. Qe1 {Polgar,Judit} Qc7 13. Nd5 Qb8 14. Bb5 Bb7 15. Bxc6 Bxc6 16. fxe5 Bxd5 17. Rxd5 Bg7 18. Qe4 Qb7 {Black clung on in D.Pavasovic-V.Nevednichy, Croatian Team Championship 2006.}) 12... Rxd8 13. Rxd8+ Nxd8 14. fxe5 Bg7 15. Nd5 Bb7 $17 {Gomez Jurado,L-Efimov,I/Andorra op 1993 Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '' Finkel,A: 'Gomez Jurado,L-Efimov,I/Andorra op 1993 '' Polgar,Judit: 'Finkel,A: '' Finkel,A: 'Gomez Jurado,L-Efimov,I/Andorra op 1993 ' Finkel,Alexander: '''}) (11. Qe3 {(Polgar,Judit) Finkel,Alexander