{After the rather recent, nice Karpov's book about Caro-Can and his own big expirience in the line 5.f3-g5 the present game is like an opening survey showing partialy its developement since 1983 so far:} 1. e4 {Kostakiev,D Kostakiev,D Kostakiev,D Kostakiev,D Kostakiev,D: 'Kostakiev,Dimitar Kostakiev,Dimitar: 'Kostakiev,D''} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 $5 {Semenova 1983} Ngf6 (5... Ndf6 6. Bc4 Nd5 {0-1 Spassky,B-Karpov,A/Belfort 1988/CBM 08 (22)}) 6. Bd3 e6 (6... h6 7. Ne6 Qb6 8. Nxf8 Nxf8 {0-1 Semenova,L-Muresan,M/Bad Kissingen 1983/MCD (47)}) 7. N1f3 Bd6 (7... h6) 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Qc7 {''!?' Speelman,J. ' 'Finkel,A: 'Probably the most precise move.Although 10...Nf6 also leads to absolutely playable position where nevertheless white's chances should be prefered.''} (10... Nf6 11. Qe2 Qc7 12. Bd2 b6 {Copar,A-Sapundjiev,G/EM ICCF 14 ol 200/2001}) ({The immediate} 10... c5 {was played by Karpov in his opening round game with Shirov.}) 11. Qg4 (11. a4 Nf6 12. Qh4 {½-½ Ramesh,R-Galkin,A/Anibal Open, Linares ESP 2001 (20)}) 11... Kf8 {'Lukacs: 'Karpov's favourite  move.''} 12. O-O ({The immediate} 12. Be3 {was Judit's choice in three games against Karpov in a rapid play match in Hungary, 1998, where she scored 2/3.}) 12... c5 {'Lukacs: 'Soon or later Black has to attack White's .''} (12... b6 {has a similar idea to play c6-c5 after b7.} 13. Re1 {Yandemirov-Kharitinov, RUS-Cup 1996 CBM 56} (13. b3 {Arakhamia-Portisch, Cancan Veterans-Women 1996 CBM 67})) 13. dxc5 {'Lukacs: 'The  is vulnerable on g4.''} (13. Re1 cxd4 {0-1 Finkel,A-Yevseev,D/Groningen 1995/CBM 50 ext (64)} (13... b6 14. Ne5 $2 (14. b3) (14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Bf1 (15. Bd2 h5 $1 16. Qh4 Bb7) 15... e5 16. Qh5 {De Firmian,N-Gulko,B/USA-ch 1996 (36)}) 14... cxd4 $1 {1/2-1/2 Khalifman,A-Speelman,J/Hastings 1996/CBM 51/[Speelman,J] (31)})) (13. c3 b6 14. Re1 {'Finkel,A: 'After this move black equalises very easely,but it is not so clear what to do instead.Black has very unpleasent threat Bb7,Nf6 and B:f3 and white tries to prevent it.''} (14. Qh4 {After this move black equalises very easely,but it is not so clear what to do instead.Black has very unpleasent threat Bb7,Nf6 and B:f3 and white tries to prevent it.} Bb7 15. Be4 Bxe4 16. Qxe4 Rd8 17. dxc5 (17. Be3 Nf6 18. Qc2 Ke7 19. dxc5 Bxc5 20. Bxc5+ Qxc5 21. Rad1 $11) 17... Nxc5 18. Qe2 Ke7 $11 19. h3 Rhe8 20. Qc2 {Trying to keep black king in the center.} (20. Be3 Kf8) 20... Nd3 $1 {½-½ Illescas Cordoba,M-Karpov,A/Dos Hermanas 1997/CBM 59/[Finkel,A] (49)}) 14... Bb7 15. h4 Rd8 (15... c4 16. Bc2 b5 17. Bd2 {½-½ Anand,V-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (49)}) 16. Bd2 {1-0 Shirov,A-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (54)}) (13. Qh4 {Lukacs: 'The  is vulnerable on g4.'} b6 14. Be4 Bb7 (14... Rb8 15. Rd1 $1 $146 {0-1 Sadvakasov,D-Karpov,A/Hoogeveen 1999/CBM 74/[Lukacs] (65) White puts pressure on the d  especially against d6.} (15. Bg5 {Eismont-Gutov, RUS-Cup 1996 CBM 56}) (15. b3 Nf6 $6 (15... c4 $6) (15... cxd4 $142 $5 {appears to be the best here.} 16. Nxd4) (15... Be7 16. Qf4 Bd6 {½-½ Leitao,R-Karpov,A/Buenos Aires 2000/CBM 80 no vc (19)}) 16. dxc5 $1 {½-½ Timman,J-Karpov,A/Bali 2000/CBM 77/[Lukacs] (62)} ({If} 16. Bd3 {then Black occupies the long  after} Bb7))) 15. Bxb7 Qxb7 16. dxc5 bxc5 {1-0 Lautier,J-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (48)}) 13... Nxc5 14. Be3 $146 (14. Re1 {0-1 Luther,T-Anastasian,A/Istanbul 2000/EXT 2001 (39)} e5 15. Bf5 e4 16. Nh4 Bxh2+ 17. Kh1 h5 18. Qh3 Be5 19. Be3 g6 20. Bxc8 Qxc8 21. Bxc5+ Qxc5 22. Rxe4 Kg7 23. c3 Bf6 24. Qf3 Rad8 25. Rf4 Qe5 26. g3 Rd2 27. Qxb7 Rb8 28. Qxa7 Rbxb2 29. Kg1 Qd5 30. a4 Be5 31. Rf3 Bf6 32. Rf4 Bxc3 33. Rf1 Be5 34. Rf3 Bf6 35. Rf4 Bg5 36. Ng2 Bxf4 37. Nxf4 Qe5 38. a5 h4 39. a6 Ra2 {0-1 Luther,T-Anastasian,A/Istanbul TUR 2000/The Week in Chess 314 (39).}) 14... Nxd3 15. cxd3 e5 16. Qe4 ({How about} 16. Qh5 $5 Qe7 (16... g6 $6 17. Qh4 {and Black's looking a bit loose.}) 17. Rae1 (17. Bg5 $6 Qe6 $1 {with the idea of ..Qg4 with a good game.}) 17... Kg8 (17... g6 18. Bxh6+ Kg8 19. Qg5 $1) 18. Nd2 g6 19. Qe2 {with the idea of Ne4 and f4 to open up the game.}) 16... g6 17. Rac1 ({Did Judit miss a trick here?} 17. Rfc1 $1 Qe7 18. Bxa7 Kg7 19. Qe3 Bg4 (19... Bf5 20. Bc5 $1) 20. Nd2 {and, with the idea of Nc4 and Bb6, White just seems to be a pawn ahead.}) 17... Qe7 18. Rfe1 ({Now if} 18. Bxa7 Kg7 $1 {see the difference - at the end of the day, White's a-pawn is attacked, and Black has superb piece-play.} 19. Qe3 Bg4 20. Nd2 (20. a3 Bxf3 21. gxf3 Rhc8 $1 {White's pawn structure is completely shattered. Despite being a pawn ahead, the ending may be better for Black.}) 20... b5 $1) 18... Bf5 19. Qa4 Kg7 20. Nxe5 (20. Bf4 f6 21. d4 e4 22. Bxd6 Qxd6 23. Nd2 Rhc8 $1 24. Rxc8 Rxc8 25. Qxa7 Qb4 {and Black's better here.}) 20... Bxe5 21. Bf4 Rhc8 (21... Rac8 22. Rxc8 {½-½ (30) Polgar,J (2676)-Karpov,A (2679) Linares ESP 2001 [Henderson,John]}) 22. Rxc8 Rxc8 23. Rxe5 Rc1+ 24. Bxc1 Qxe5 $11 {} 25. Be3 (25. Qd1 Qd4) 25... f6 26. Qb4 b6 27. h3 (27. d4 $1 Qe4 (27... Qd5 $2 28. Qe7+) 28. h3 {and Black still has a lot of work to do to secure a draw: A pawn ahead, White has a strong, passed d-pawn and the Black pawns are the more vulnerable to attack - especially with the king unprotected on g7.}) 27... g5 28. Qa3 Qc7 29. b4 Qd7 30. Qc3 (30. d4 Be6 {and Black can easily set up a good blockade from d5.}) 1/2-1/2

