{ChessCalisthenics: 'C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and 9 Nbd2' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and 9 Nbd2'' Navara,David: 'Podľa Judit Polgár v New in Chess 6/2016, strana 76.'} {Navara,David: 'My notes are not exactly original, as this game has been annotated by Judit Polgár herself for New in Chess 6/2016 (page 76). Moreover, GM Victor Mikhalevski had mentioned the relevant lines in his comments to the game Svidler - Anand, Dos Hermanas 1999. Funnily enough, Dos Hermanas means "two sisters" in Spanish! I am sure that Judit Polgár's two sisters loved the following game!'} 1. e4 {[%emt 0:00:09] Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics' Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics' C80: Open Ruy Lopez: Sidelines and Nbd2} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 ({It is worth noting that the ancient line} 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 $6 6. Nxf7 (6. d4 $5) 6... Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 {resembles the game.}) 3... a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 9. Nbd2 Nc5 10. c3 d4 {John Shaw: 'This is the critical attempt to exploit White's move order.'} 11. Ng5 {John Shaw: '!' John Shaw: 'This fantastic move was first played by Karpov during his 1978 world championship match against Korchnoi. Nowadays Kasparov is its most significant supporter.' Navara,David: 'This surprising move was first played in the World Championship match Karpov - Kortschnoj, Baguio 1978.'} ({Players seeking a quieter line could consider} 11. Bxe6 Nxe6 12. cxd4 Ncxd4 {and now either} 13. a4 ({or} 13. Ne4 {.})) 11... Bd5 $6 {Mikhalevski,Viktor: '?' '?' '?' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'It's interesting that this objectively bad move brought good results. In fact, black suffered first defeat in this game after winning 2 and drawing 5 games!' John Shaw: '?' John Shaw: 'After this move White develops a massive attack.' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'It's interesting that this objectively bad move brought good results. In fact, black suffered first defeat in this game after winning 2 and drawing 5 games!'} ({John Shaw: 'If Black takes the piece with' Navara,David: 'By now it has become clear that black can hold after'} 11... Qxg5 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor John Shaw: 'White's idea is'} 12. Qf3 {ChessCalisthenics John Shaw: ', when there is no way to hang on to the extra material, so'} O-O-O {John Shaw: 'is normal'} ({if} 12... Kd7 13. Bd5 $1) {John Shaw: '. However, after'} 13. Bxe6+ fxe6 14. Qxc6 Qxe5 15. b4 {John Shaw: '!' John Shaw: 'Black must give up another piece to safeguard his king and hope his passed pawns provide compensation.'} (15. a4 dxc3 16. Nf3 Qd5 17. axb5 Rd6 18. Qe8+ Rd8 19. Qc6 Rd6 20. Qe8+ Rd8 21. Qf7 Nb3 22. Rxa6 Nxc1 23. Rxc1 Bc5 24. Re1 cxb2 25. h3 Rhf8 26. Qxg7 Rxf3 27. gxf3 Qxf3 28. Qg2 Bxf2+ 29. Qxf2 Rg8+ {Iordachescu,V (2603)-Golod,V (2576)/Pori Erin 2006/CBM 114 ext/0-1}) 15... Qd5 16. Qxd5 exd5 17. bxc5 dxc3 18. Nb3 d4 19. Ba3 {John Shaw: 'is the theoretical line. The high-level game (Kasparov-Shirov 2001, Linares 2001) confirmed the verdict that White is better, but there is no denying that the position is murky enough to make a Black improvement possible.'} g6 {Navara,David: '!?'} (19... Be7) 20. Bb4 Bg7 21. a4 {Navara,David: '' Navara,David: '. The lines are complex and the ensuing positions are hard to evaluate. Back then the computers were very weak for today's standards.'} Kd7 22. axb5 axb5 23. Rfd1 Ke6 24. Rac1 Rhe8 25. Kf1 Kf5 26. c6 {Kasparov,G (2849)-Shirov,A (2718)/Linares 2001/CBM 082/1-0 (38)}) ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'and' and} 11... dxc3 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'are the main alternatives.' are the main alternatives.} 12. Nxe6 {ChessCalisthenics} fxe6 13. bxc3 Qd3 14. Bc2 (14. Nf3 {was Karpov's move in the game that started it all – Korchnoi held the draw}) 14... Qxc3 15. Nb3 Rd8 (15... Nxb3 16. Bxb3 Qxa1 ({Anand played} 16... Nd4 {, but after} 17. Qg4 Qxa1 18. Bxe6 {White's attack proved decisive}) 17. Qh5+ {leads to a win after both} Kd7 ({and} 17... g6 18. Qf3) 18. Bxe6+ $1 Kxe6 19. Qg4+ {.}) 16. Bd2 Qxe5 17. Re1 Qd5 18. Qg4 Nd4 19. Nxc5 Bxc5 20. Qxg7 Rf8 21. Bxh7 Rd7 22. Qg6+ Kd8 23. Bh6 Nf3+ 24. gxf3 Qxf3 25. Qg5+ Kc8 26. Qxc5 {Naiditsch,A (2611)-Mamedyarov,S (2660)/Pamplona 2004/CBM 105/1/2-1/2}) 12. Nxf7 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' Mikhalevski,Viktor: '!' '!' '!' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N'' Navara,David: '!' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'This tactical blow combined with Svidler's 14.g4+ is a refutation of 11...d5? It's strange that Black tried to repeat this line after Svidler-Anand.' John Shaw: '!' John Shaw: 'A sacrifice which will appeal to anyone who grew up playing the Fried Liver Attack (1 e4 e5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 Bc4 Nf6 4 Ng5 d5 5 exd5 Nxd5 6 Nxf7).' Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'This tactical blow combined with Svidler's 14.g4+ is a refutation of 11...d5? It's strange that Black tried to repeat this line after Svidler-Anand.' Navara,David: 'This sacrifice does not win by force, but gives white a very strong attack.'} (12. c4 {ChessCalisthenics (Mikhalevski,Viktor) ½-½ Mamedov,N-Mamedyarov,S/Baku 2001/EXT 2003 (25) Mikhalevski,Viktor: '½-½ Mamedov,N-Mamedyarov,S/Baku 2001/EXT 2003 (25)'} bxc4 {ChessCalisthenics} 13. Bxc4 h6 14. Ngf3 Be7 15. b3 Bxc4 16. Nxc4 Qd5 17. Ba3 Rd8 18. Bxc5 Bxc5 19. Rc1 O-O 20. Ne1 d3 21. Qxd3 Qxd3 22. Nxd3 Rxd3 23. Nb2 Rd5 24. Na4 Bxf2+ 25. Rxf2 Nxe5 {1/ 2-1/2 Mamedov,N (2392)-Mamedyarov,S (2479)/Baku 2001/EXT 2003}) ({Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'In the beginning White tried to look for advantage after' In the beginning White tried to look for advantage after} 12. Bxd5 {ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor ChessCalisthenics Mikhalevski,Viktor '!?' must be considered '!?'} Qxd5 {'' ''} 13. Nb3 Nxb3 14. axb3 Be7 15. Nf3 d3 (15... Nxe5 {Mikhalevski,Viktor: 'Anand-Sokolov,Lion,1994 CBM 45[Korchnoi]' Anand-Sokolov,Lion,1994 CBM 45[Korchnoi]}) 16. Be3 O-O 17. b4 {0-1 McDonald