1. e4 {Psakhis,L Psakhis} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ {Hathaway, Mark: 'Moscow Variation -- After this White will either establish a white square pawn formation (Pc4, Pe4) to replace the effect of the bishop (to the extent they can) or aim for c2-c3, d2-d4 and possibly d4-d5 or e4-e5. The move also works to enable O-O quickly. 3. Bc4 is interesting and has been played recently, but 3. d4 is still the most common move.' This being the second game in his match and Kasparov, after victory in the first game, chooses the most solid line. Once in a similiar situation he played against Judit 2.c3, but now, maybe, he considers this move as too risky!} Nc6 {Hathaway, Mark: 'This is unusual, but not necessarily bad. If White captures Nc6 Black can recapture ...b7xc6 and have better central control. Perhaps Polgar is trying to take Kasparov out of familiar territory.'} (3... Bd7 {Judit shows fighting spirit - 3...Bd7 leads to more or less equal positions.}) (3... Nd7) 4. Bxc6+ bxc6 5. O-O (5. d4 cxd4 6. Qxd4 e5 {and Black's better central pawns prevent White from maintaining his Qd4}) 5... e5 {Hathaway, Mark: '?!' Hathaway, Mark: 'This makes Bf8 bad and doesn't prevent White from playing c2-c3, d2-d4 to open the center.'} (5... Bg4 6. c3 e5 7. d3 Be7 8. h3 Bh5 9. Nbd2 Nf6 10. Re1 O-O 11. Nf1 Nd7 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. Be3 Rb8 14. Qc2 Qc7 15. h4 $14 {0-1 Rublevsky,S-Rabiega,R/Frankfurt Masters 2000/The Week in Chess 294 (66)}) ({Black can immediately target Pb2 with ...Bg7 and ...Rb8 (or ...Qb6) , but mostly she should use Bg7 in cooperation with her dark-square pawn chain.} 5... g6 6. e5 d5 7. h3 {and Black should be in good shape while White has to justify his (possibly overextended) Pe5}) 6. c3 {Hathaway, Mark: 'Already Black faces a crisis since White is better developed and Ke8 might soon be exposed.'} g5 {Gawain Jones: '?!' Gawain Jones: 'Excessively risky. Polgar was in a must-win situation in the mini-match which prompted this move, but as we will see, Kasparov wasn't going to shy away from a challenge. Excessively risky. Polgar was in a must-win situation in the mini-match which prompted this move, but as we will see, Kasparov wasn't going to shy away from a challenge.' Hathaway, Mark: 'This could be the move of a complete amateur or of a great player such as Ivanchuk, but it seems out of place and unusual, for Polgar. This may be a small indication of an internal struggle wherein she tries to expand her approach, hopefully for the better. In this case I think it just fails.' Dubious move, but how Black can play for a win with "normal" play.} ({Gawain Jones: 'and' is more common here, though Black can't be too happy with his results, for example} 6... f5 7. exf5 Bxf5 8. d4 e4 9. dxc5 exf3 10. Qxf3 Be6 11. Re1 Kd7 12. cxd6 Qf6 13. Bf4 Qf5 14. Nd2 Nf6 15. Qe2 Re8 16. Qe3 Qa5 17. Nf3 $16 {1-0 Rublevsky,S-Sveshnikov,E/YUG-Cup Herceg Novi 1999 (35)}) ({As well as} 6... Nf6) ({Black has also been known to try: Black has also been known to try: a)} 6... Ne7 {is another rare approach, but was seen in one high-rated clash: is another rare approach, but was seen in one high-rated clash:} 7. d4 Ng6 {was P.Svidler-V.Zvjaginsev, Moscow 2005, when I like the immediate was P.Svidler-V.Zvjaginsev, Moscow 2005, when I like the immediate} 8. dxe5 Ba6 (8... Nxe5 9. Nxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8+ Kxd8 11. c4 {is very ugly, especially as Black has no knight to stick on d4}) 9. Bg5 $1 f6 ({both} 9... Qc7) ({and} 9... Qb8 {can be dealt with by} 10. exd6) 10. exf6 gxf6 11. Be3 Bxf1 12. Qxf1 {which gives White great compensation for the exchange, with a pawn and play on the light squares. which gives White great compensation for the exchange , with a pawn and play on the light squares.}) ({b)} 6... Ba6 {has also been tried from time to time: has also been tried from time to time:} 7. Re1 Bd3 {the only logical continuation of Black's previous move;} ({the only logical continuation of Black's previous move;} 7... c4 {is dealt with efficiently by} 8. Na3 $1 Qa5 9. b4 $1 cxb3 10. axb3 Qc7 11. d4 {and already White has taken full control, M.Etmans-C.Baljon, Leeuwarden 1997 and already White has taken full control, M.Etmans-C.Baljon, Leeuwarden 1997}) 8. Re3 c4 9. Qa4 Rc8 {was seen in I.Rausis-M.Bosboom, Loewenstein 1997, and here White has a choice of which pawn to take: either was seen in I.Rausis-M.Bosboom, Loewenstein 1997, and here White has a choice of which pawn to take: either} 10. Ne1 ({or} 10. Na3 f5 11. exf5 Bxf5 12. Nxc4 {, both with a clear advantage to White. , both with a clear advantage to White.})) (6... Be6 7. d4 cxd4 8. cxd4 f6 9. Qa4 Qd7 10. Nc3 Ne7 {is clearly easier for White to play and Qd7 isn't very mobile, so White should aim for Rd1 and dxe5 to continue opening lines.}) 7. d4 {Gawain Jones: '!' Gawain Jones: 'The move that ...g5 was designed to prevent. If Kasparov can successfully get away with this move then I think we can hope our opponents play ...g5 too. White sacrifices a pawn, but in return Black has a lot of holes in her structure. The move that ...g5 was designed to prevent. If Kasparov can successfully get away with this move then I think we can hope our opponents play ...g5 too. White sacrifices a pawn, but in return Black has a lot of holes in her structure.' Hathaway, Mark: '!' Hathaway, Mark: 'Opening the center is more important than the pawn he appears to be gambitting.'} g4 8. Nfd2 cxd4 9. cxd4 exd4 10. Nc4 {Hathaway, Mark: 'practically begging for ...d6-d5, so he can open the e-file with exd5'} (10. Qa4 Bd7 11. Qxd4 Qf6 {forces simplification or allows Black to develop unmolested. Pd4 and Rh8 aren't the primary target, Ke8 is.}) 10... c5 $146 {Gawain Jones: 'Polgar holds on to her extra pawn so that she at least has something for her suffering. Polgar holds on to her extra pawn so that she at least has something for her suffering.'} ({Gawain Jones: 'The first time this position was reached Black tried'} 10... Bg7 {Gawain Jones: ', but following' Hathaway, Mark: '?'} 11. Bf4 Be6 (11... d5 12. Nd6+ Kf8 13. exd5 cxd5 14. Nxc8 Rxc8 15. Qxg4 {is equally horrible}) 12. Nxd6+ Kf8 13. Nd2 (13. e5 $5 Qb6 14. Nd2 Ne7 15. N2c4 Qc5 16. b4 {also gives White a great initiative}) 13... Ne7 14. Nb3 Qb6 15. Rc1 $16 {Rublevsky,S-Stripunsky,A/RUS-chT Kazan 1995/[Hecht]/1-0 (58)} Ng6 16. Bg3 h5 17. Nf5 {in S.Rublevsky-A.Stripunsky, Kazan 1995, Black had a really ugly position. Initially I thought White had great compensation for his sacrificed pawn, but then realized he's not even a pawn down! And, yes, this is yet another idea invented by Rublevsky. You could do a lot worse than following his recent games for any updates on the theoretical status of the whole 3 Bb5 Sicilian. in S.Rublevsky-A.Stripunsky, Kazan 1995, Black had a really ugly position. Initially I thought White had great compensation for his sacrificed pawn, but then realized he's not even a pawn down! And, yes, this is yet another idea invented by Rublevsky. You could do a lot worse than following his recent games for any updates on the theoretical status of the whole 3 Bb5 Sicilian.}) 11. Re1 {Gawain Jones: '!' Gawain Jones: 'Preparing the e4-e5 break. Black may have an extra pawn, but already her pawn structure has been severely compromised, she's a long way behind in development and there's no safe haven for her king. Initially my computer liked Black's extra pawn, but even the materialistic machine is less optimistic now. Preparing the e4-e5 break. Black may have an extra pawn, but already her pawn structure has been severely compromised, she's a long way behind in development and there's no safe haven for her king. Initially my computer liked Black's extra pawn, but even the materialistic machine is less optimistic now.' Hathaway, Mark: 'quite likely planning 12. e5 d5 13. Nd6+' It's easy to see that White got great compensation for the pawn: better development and an exposed king on e8.'} Be6 (11... f6 {is ugly, but might hold the dark squares well enough to allow some piece developments}) 12. Nba3 (12. Bf4 $