t Kasparov's next more or less refutes it.'} (13... dxe5 {had to be tried, but obviously White has great play: had to be tried, but obviously White has great play:} 14. Nxe5 Qd5 15. Nd3 $1 (15. Nxg4 Nf6 {wouldn't be so clear as Black has successfully coordinated her pieces}) 15... Nf6 16. Bh6 Rg8 17. Qa4+ Bd7 18. Qa5 {and Black's king has been caught in the centre which spells trouble. For example, and Black's king has been caught in the centre which spells trouble. For example,} c4 19. Rxe7+ $1 Kxe7 20. Re1+ Be6 21. Qc7+ Qd7 22. Qxc4 {would leave White with great compensation for the exchange. would leave White with great compensation for the exchange.}) 14. Nd6+ $1 Kf8 {Hathaway, Mark: 'This position doesn't look too good for Black, but Na3 is misplaced and Nd6 is far from coordinating with other White pieces.'} ({Gawain Jones: 'Polgar had to continue her policy of taking the material even if Polgar had to continue her policy of taking the material even if'} 14... Bxd6 15. exd6 Qxd6 16. b4 $1 {Gawain Jones: ', as offered by Kasparov, looks terrible for Black. Black's dark squares are ruined and so her king will never find safety. Psakhis continues the analysis with the line , as offered by Kasparov, looks terrible for Black. Black's dark squares are ruined and so her king will never find safety. Psakhis continues the analysis with the line'} (16. Nb5 Qb8 17. Nxd4 {and Black's position is falling apart} (17. Rxe6+ fxe6 18. Qxg4 Qxb5 19. Qxe6+ Kf8 {and Black appears to survive} (19... Kd8 20. Qxd5+)) 17... cxd4 18. Qxd4) (16. Qxg4 Ne7 17. Qg7 O-O-O) 16... Ne7 17. bxc5 Qxc5 {Gawain Jones: ', but here I think'} {Gawain Jones: 'than his'} 18. Bb2 (18. Nc2 {is more accurate. Then after} Nf5 19. Bb2 Rb8 20. Bxd4 Nxd4 21. Nxd4 O-O 22. Qd2 {White can continue to exploit Black's advanced kingside.}) 18... O-O 19. Nc2 $36) 15. Bf4 {Gawain Jones: '?!' Hathaway, Mark: 'Kasparov just holds onto Nd6 and keeps an eye on how Black is going to proceed, looking for ways to gain advantage.'} (15. b4 $1 {would be a more accurate move order, immediately forcing a concession from Black. would be a more accurate move order, immediately forcing a concession from Black.}) 15... h5 {Gawain Jones: '?!'} (15... Rb8 {had to be played to prevent White's b2-b4 break, even if White keeps a very promising position with had to be played to prevent White's b2-b4 break, even if White keeps a very promising position with} 16. Qd2 Qb6 17. Rac1 {, as} Qxb2 $2 {loses to} 18. Bh6+ $1 ({or} 18. Rb1) 18... Nxh6 19. Qxh6+ Kg8 20. Rb1 {.}) 16. b4 $1 {Gawain Jones: 'Kasparov finds it second time round and Polgar's position collapses. Kasparov finds it second time round and Polgar's position collapses.' White must attack black's pawn center.} c4 (16... cxb4 {would put Polgar two pawns up very temporarily, but after would put Polgar two pawns up very temporarily, but after} 17. Nab5 Qb6 18. Nxd4 {White has a decisive advantage - just compare the quality of the respective minor pieces. White has a decisive advantage – just compare the quality of the respective minor pieces.}) 17. Qxd4 {Gawain Jones: 'Now Kasparov is level on material and definitely up on position. Now Kasparov is level on material and definitely up on position.' Hathaway, Mark: 'Pd5 is in danger of being captured by Na3-b5-c3xd5 and I don't see a very good defense for Black.'} h4 18. Nab5 {[%cal Gb5a7,Gb5c3]} Rh5 {Hathaway, Mark: 'White takes over the center while Black squirms out of the corner. Is this sufficient to prevent White from crashing through the center? Well, first of all Pa7 is en prise, so White has to decide whether there is time to capture it and then return to the main theme.'} 19. Qe3 {Gawain Jones: 'White plays a little superficially, as one might expect in rapid chess, and decides to put all his minor pieces on pretty squares in the centre of the board. White plays a little superficially, as one might expect in rapid chess, and decides to put all his minor pieces on pretty squares in the