.'} 22. b5 Ng6 23. Nc6 {Gawain Jones: '!' Gawain Jones: 'Very visual knights and psychologically damaging for Black as those knights seem to suffocate her. However, she has played well over the last few moves and her pieces have started to coordinate (thanks to the couple of tempi which Kasparov gave her with his Qd4-e3-d2 rather than capturing the a7-pawn). However, here Polgar plays too aggressively. Very visual knights and psychologically damaging for Black as those knights seem to suffocate her. However, she has played well over the last few moves and her pieces have started to coordinate (thanks to the couple of tempi which Kasparov gave her with his Qd4-e3-d2 rather than capturing the a7-pawn). However, here Polgar plays too aggressively.' Not in every game you will see such a nice couple of knights!} c3 {Gawain Jones: '?!' Gawain Jones: 'All this achieves is a loose pawn.' Hathaway, Mark: '!?' Hathaway, Mark: 'chasing White's queen away from control of d4, so ...Bb6 can't be met by Nc6-d4'} ({Taking a time out to improve the position of her king with Taking a time out to improve the position of her king with} 23... Kg8 {was better. Kasparov could give up one of his monster knights with was better. Kasparov could give up one of his monster knights with} 24. Nxd8 ({or} 24. a4 f6 25. exf6 Bxf6 {and Black's pieces somehow make sense}) 24... Rxd8 25. Bg5 {, but then} h3 {isn't clear.}) (23... Bb6 24. Nd4 (24. a4 g3) 24... Nxf4 25. Qxf4 Bxd4 26. Qxd4 g3 27. fxg3 hxg3 28. hxg3 {seems safe enough for White as Black doesn't have much force on the dark squares}) 24. Qc1 (24. Qd4 $5 Bb6 25. Qb4 Kg8 26. Be3 {would also favour White.}) 24... g3 {Gawain Jones: '?' Gawain Jones: 'And this simply doesn't work.' Hathaway, Mark: '?' Hathaway, Mark: 'And, this is clearly too far-fetched.' Typical Judit!} (24... Bb6 25. Be3 $2 Nxe5 26. Bxb6 Qxd6 $17 {is very good for Black} (26... Nf3+ 27. gxf3 gxf3 {and White is in danger, but might well hold on because Qd7 isn't able to get to