iagonal and thus open the net around White's king.}) 30... f5 {[%cal Gd7h7] Gawain Jones: '!?' Gawain Jones: 'The move had to be tried to bring another major piece to the h-file. The move had to be tried to bring another major piece to the h-file.' Hathaway, Mark: '?' Hathaway, Mark: 'She wants to bring Qd7 to the h-file and/or to use ...f5-f4 to chase away Bg3, but king safety is the first priority! Allowing exf6 is extremely risky. If only there was another way, but I see none. I suppose this is where the classicist says, "I told you so." Black is having difficult with Qd7 because White dominates in the center, cramping Black quite a lot. So, when Black fights back those central pieces spring into action!' It is not time for Black to count pawns! Judit as usual fights back to ropes and creates some threats against white's king.} 31. exf6 Qh7 {Hathaway, Mark: 'threatening ...Rxh2+ mating'} 32. f7+ ({Gawain Jones: 'Palliser and Psakhis both suggest'} 32. Nd4 Rxh2+ 33. Kg1 $18 {Gawain Jones: 'as a way to block out Black's bishop, but here Black still has some compensation with as a way to block out Black's bishop, but here Black still has some compensation with' was also enough} Rh4 $1 {. The move played in the game is the most accurate, but unfortunately Kasparov doesn't follow up correctly. . The move played in the game is the most accurate, but unfortunately Kasparov doesn't follow up correctly.}) 32... Bxf7 (32... Kf8 33. Qxh8+ Qxh8 34. Rxe6) 33. Qxh8+ $5 {Gawain Jones: '?!' Gawain Jones: 'Kasparov sacrifices his queen to get rid of the pressure down the h-file and take over the initiative himself. Kasparov sacrifices his queen to get rid of the pressure down the h-file and take over the initiative himself.' Hathaway, Mark: 'forced, to avoid the checkmate' Looks nice, but} ({Gawain Jones: 'The extremely hard--to-spot'} 33. Bh4 $1 {Gawain Jones: '!!' Gawain Jones: '(clearing the third rank so that the queen controls h3) (clearing the third rank so that the queen controls h3)'} Rxh4 34. h3 $18 {[%csl Gc6,Gd6,Gf1] Gawain Jones: 'is pointed out instantly by the computers as completely winning for White. is pointed out instantly by the computers as completely winning for White.' could finish game quicker.}) 33... Nxh8 {Hathaway, Mark: 'After this the position is changed dramatically and Black's king is in as much, or more, danger than White's.'} (33... Kxh8 $2 34. Rxf7 Qg8 35. Re8 $18) (33... Qxh8 34. Rxf7) 34. Ne7+ Kf8 (34... Kg7 35. Ndf5+ Kf8 36. Bd6 $5 $40) 35. Nec8 {Gawain Jones: '?' Gawain Jones: 'This loses control and allows Black's pieces some flexibility. This loses control and allows Black's pieces some flexibility.' Hathaway, Mark: 'This is a marvelous situation: Bf7 is pinned, Nh8 is stuck defending Bf7 and Qh7 & Rh5 can only sit on the h-file, but they can't open it. White has completely foiled Black's plan, but at a cost. The only question is whether White can finally get to the very exposed Kf8 because a failure to do so would probably allow Black to consolidate and win with her queen.'} (35. Nef5 $1 {was stronger, keeping the black pieces in the net in the corner when White would have great compensation for the queen. was stronger, keeping the black pieces in the net in the corner when White would have great compensation for the queen.}) 35... Qd3 $2 {Hathaway, Mark: 'This looks like a time pressure move. It's quite ineffective. The threat is ...Qxg3.' Final mistake} (35... Bc5 $142 $1 {Gawain Jones: '!' Gawain Jones: ', as pointed out by Psakhis and Palliser, would have amazingly given Black the advantage. After , as pointed out by Psakhis and Palliser, would have amazingly given Black the advantage. After'} 36. Re8+ Kg7 37. Re7 (37. Be5+ Rxe5 38. Rxe5 Qd3 {Black is starting to take control and will be able to unravel her extra piece. Black is starting to take control and will be able to unravel her extra piece.}) (37. Rxh8 Qd3 38. Rxf7+ Kxh8 39. Rf8+ Kg7 40. Rf7+ Kh6 41. Bf4+ Rg5 42. Bxg5+ Kxg5 43. Rf5+ $11) 37... Qd3 $132 {leads to an unclear game.}) 36. Rf3 Qc2 {Gawain Jones: '?' Gawain Jones: 'And this is the losing mistake.' Hathaway, Mark: '??'} (36... Qd2 37. Ref1 (37. Nxf7 Nxf7 38. Nd6 $4 {would fail to} ({although here} 38. Ref1 {must still be better for White}) {would fail to} 38... Rxh2+ $1 {.})) 37. Nxf7 Nxf7 38. Nd6 Qg6 ({Polgar is forced to give up her extra queen, since} 38... Rh7 39. Re8+ Kg7 40. Rxf7+ {drops everything, but is now left with a lost endgame two pawns down. drops everything, but is now left with a lost endgame two pawns down. Hathaway, Mark: '+-'}) 39. Rxf7+ Qxf7 (39... Kg8 40. Re8#) 40. Nxf7 {The white knight worked very hard in this game and he deserves a rest.} Kxf7 {Hathaway, Mark: 'After all that it seems terribly unfair that her heroic efforts to get active pieces was all dissipated, only to produce a losing ending.'} 41. Re5 $1 $18 {Hathaway, Mark: 'Getting rid of the rooks makes good sense, as it makes the advance of Pg2 & Ph2 much easier.'} (41. Rf1+ Ke6 42. Bf2 Rf5 (42... d4 43. a4 Kd5 44. g4) 43. Re1+) 41... Rxe5 42. Bxe5 d4 43. Kg1 Ke6 44. Bb8 Kd5 45. Kf1 Kc4 46. Ke2 d3+ (46... Kxb5 47. Kd3 {and Black is pretty much out of offensive weapons, so Pg2 & Ph2 will have their say}) 47. Kd1 Kxb5 48. h4 a5 49. Be5 Bf2 50. h5 Be3 51. Bg7 {Hathaway, Mark: 'Black loses her bishop after h5-h6 and Pg2 will keep Black occupied defensively while White's king gobbles Pd3 & Pa5 to clear the way for Pa2 to advance. This was quite an exciting game with the odd appearances and Polgar's heroic effort to gain piece activity, only to have it fall apart as they neared the time control (assuming that was move 40) .'} 1-0

