1. e4 {Lukacs Lukacs Lukacs Lukacs} c6 2. c4 d5 3. exd5 Nf6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'KEATON: If this gambit offer shouldn't be accepted by White, is there any real benefit for Black in this move order? CYRUS: I see two benefits for Black if we play this move order: 1. White may not know that dxc6 offers Black at least even chances. So our opponents may get greedy and accept our pawn offer.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'KEATON: If this gambit offer shouldn't be accepted by White, is there any real benefit for Black in this move order? --- CYRUS: I see two benefits for Black if we play this move order: 1. White may not know that dxc6 offers Black at least even chances. So our opponents may get greedy and accept our pawn offer.' By this move order Black can avoid the variation} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '2. By playing this move order, we deny White the line'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '2. By playing this move order, we deny White the line'} 3... cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Bb5+ {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'which may or may not throw our opponents off their opening prep.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'which may or may not throw our opponents off their opening prep.' which is not so dangerous on the other hand.}) 4. Nc3 ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'If''} 4. dxc6 $6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!?'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!?'} Nxc6 $44 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '.  KEATON: Does Black get full compensation for the pawn?  CYRUS: Loads of compensation: 1. A big development lead (as Black!). 2. White nurses a hole on d4. 3. White must deal with a backward d-pawn. If White plays d3 later on, we can pile up on it with ...Bf5 and rooks on the d-file, tying White down. 4. After we play ...e5, our side owns extra central space. 5. Our pieces have great leeway and freedom.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. --- KEATON: Does Black get full compensation for the pawn? --- CYRUS: Loads of compensation: 1. A big development lead (as Black!). --- 2. White nurses a hole on d4. --- 3. White must deal with a backward d-pawn. If White plays d3 later on, we can pile up on it with ...Bf5 and rooks on the d-file, tying White down. --- 4. After we play ...e5, our side owns extra central space. --- 5. Our pieces have great leeway and freedom.'}) 4... cxd5 5. cxd5 (5. d4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'transposes to the Panov-Botvinnik Attack from the last chapter.'' transposes to the Panov Attack.}) 5... Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 ({C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Alternatives: a)'} 6... Nxc3 {C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'gives the game a Grünfeld flavour, like our last game: for example,'} 7. bxc3 g6 {is the other main line.} 8. Bc4 {Lukacs} Bg7 9. Ng5 $5 ({most of your opponents will play something calmer, like C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'most of your opponents will play something calmer, like'} 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 Nc6 11. d4 Qc7 {with an acceptable Grünfeld-like position, S.Halili-E.Dizdarevic, Kukes 2013}) 9... O-O 10. Qf3 e6 11. d4 Nc6 12. O-O Na5 13. Bd3 {, A.Morozevich-A.Karpov, Moscow (rapid) 1992. Black's chances look at least even after C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: ', A.Morozevich-A.Karpov, Moscow (rapid) 1992. Black's chances look at least even after'} e5 $1 14. dxe5 Nc6 {. I like Black due to his superior structure. White doesn't have time for} 15. e6 $4 {, which is met with} Ne5 $1 16. exf7+ Kh8 17. Qe3 Qxd3 {winning.}) ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'b) If we play '' In case of} 6... e6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'remember that White doesn't have to transpose to the Panov-Botvinnik with d4 next. He or she can play'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'remember that White doesn't have to transpose to the Panov-Botvinnik with d4 next. He or she can play'} 7. Bc4 Be7 8. O-O O-O {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'and only now transpose with'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'and only now transpose with'} 9. d4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '. KEATON: What is the difference here? CYRUS: The difference is that we just got tricked out of our ...Bb4 lines of the Panov-Botvinnik. This is why I think it would be better for us to play line 'a' and head for the Grünfeld-like position.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. --- KEATON: What is the difference here? --- CYRUS: The difference is that we just got tricked out of our ...Bb4 lines of the Panov-Botvinnik. This is why I think it would be better for us to play line 'a' and head for the Grünfeld-like position.' White can avoid the b4 lines, this is one of the main ideas of this move order.}) 7. Bb5 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'KEATON: What is the point of this move? Isn't the bishop normally better on d3? CYRUS: The bishop is better on d3 only if you are intent on attacking Black's king. A positional player may want to place the bishop on either c4 or b5. On b5, Black must factor in Bxc6 ideas, which hand us an isolated c-pawn. This isn't such a big worry for us, since we get the bishop pair and light-square control as compensation.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'KEATON: What is the point of this move? Isn't the bishop normally better on d3? --- CYRUS: The bishop is better on d3 only if you are intent on attacking Black's king. A positional player may want to place the bishop on either c4 or b5. On b5, Black must factor in Bxc6 ideas, which hand us an isolated c-pawn. This isn't such a big worry for us, since we get the bishop pair and light-square control as compensation.'} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Alternatives are: a)'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Alternatives are: a)'} 7. d4 e6 (7... Bg4) 8. Bd3 (8. Bc4 Bb4) 8... Bb4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'allows us to arrive in our normal Panov-Botvinnik position.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'allows us to arrive in our normal Panov-Botvinnik position.' are the lines that White wants to avoid.}) ({b)} 7. Bc4 {Lukacs} Nb6 8. Bb3 g6 $1 9. d4 Bg7 10. d5 Na5 {allows us to reach our familiar Grünfeld-like set-up, J.Timman-I.Khenkin, Tilburg 1994.}) 7... e6 8. O-O Be7 ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'I would avoid ''} 8... Nxc3 9. bxc3 Bd7 (9... Be7 10. d4 {is very similar to the QG Tarrasch D41 lines.} O-O {Lukacs} 11. Qe2 Bd7 12. Bd3 Rc8 13. Rb1 Qc7 {. Black's king lacks defenders, V.Korchnoi-R.Kuczynski, Polanica Zdroj 1992. C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. Black's king lacks defenders, V.Korchnoi-R.Kuczynski, Polanica Zdroj 1992.'}) 10. d4 Be7 11. Bd3 {This is again a transposition to D41.} O-O 12. Bf4 g6 13. Rb1 b6 14. Re1 Bf6 15. Qd2 Re8 16. h4 $1 $14 {This is the typical attacking move here, Zhang Zhong-Asrian, Elista ol 1998.}) 9. d4 O-O 10. Qe2 $5 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'KEATON: This move seems committal. Is White sure she wants her queen on this square?'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'KEATON: This move seems committal. Is White sure she wants her queen on this square?'} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'CYRUS: I agree. Maybe White should keep her options open with'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'CYRUS: I agree. Maybe White should keep her options open with'} 10. Re1 $1 {looks a bit better.} Nxc3 (10... Bd7 {Lukacs} 11. Nxd5 exd5 12. Bf4 Bf6 13. Ne5 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Bxb5 15. exf6 Qxf6 16. Qxd5 Bc6 17. Qg5 Qxg5 18. Bxg5 {. The game is very drawish, due to the bishops of opposite colours and a lack of targets for either side, V.Ivanchuk-V.Topalov, Nanjing 2008. C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. The game is very drawish, due to the bishops of opposite colours and a lack of targets for either side, V.Ivanchuk-V.Topalov, Nanjing 2008.'}) 11. bxc3 Bf6 12. Bd3 b6 {- D41}) (10. Bd3 {(Lukacs) , rerouting the bishop so that it aims at Black's king, is also played: C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: ', rerouting the bishop so that it aims at Black's king, is also played:'} Bf6 11. Be4 {(White hopes to increase pressure on d5) C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '(White hopes to increase pressure on d5)'} Nce7 12. Re1 Bd7 {. The position is roughly in balance, S.Montella-R.Martin Molinero, correspondence 2008. C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. The position is roughly in balance, S.Montella-R.Martin Molinero, correspondence 2008.'}) 10... Bd7 (10... Qc7 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'is also possible if Black feared structural damage.''} 11. Rd1 Rd8 12. Qe4 Ncb4 13. Bg5 Bxg5 14. Nxg5 Nf6 15. Qe5 Qe7 16. Nge4 Nfd5 17. a3 Nxc3 18. Nxc3 Nd5 19. Bd3 Nxc3 20. bxc3 $14 {Borge-Gausel, VISA Nordic GP Final 1999}) 11. a3 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'A new move. ''} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Previously after ''} 11. Rd1 Rc8 12. Nxd5 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '(White generally gets nothing if he goes symmetrical like this)'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '(White generally gets nothing if he goes symmetrical like this)'} exd5 {In this symmetrical  structure White's advantage is minimal.} 13. Ne5 a6 14. Bd3 Bf6 15. Bf4 g6 16. Bc2 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '(intending to transfer the bishop to b3) ''  b3 × d5} Re8 17. Bb3 Bf5 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Dreev equalized in G.Milos-A.Dreev, Moscow 2001.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Dreev equalized in G.Milos-A.Dreev, Moscow 2001.'} 18. Qf3 Be4 19. Qg3 Ne7 20. Bg5 {This exchange of the dark-squared bishops is a vital part of White's strategy, but here Black gets a counterplay against × d4.} Nf5 21. Bxf6 Qxf6 22. Qf4 g5 $1 23. Qd2 Nxd4 $1 24. Ng4 Nf3+ $1 25. gxf3 Qxf3 26. Qxg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Ke7 28. Qg5+ Kf8 {and the draw by perpetual checks is inevitable,} 29. Qh6+ {½:½ Milos-Dreev, FIDE WCh KO Moscow 2001}) 11... Rc8 12. Bd2 Bf6 13. Ne4 $6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar is somewhat cavalier about the defence of d4. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Polgar is somewhat cavalier about the defence of d4.' Now White has serious problems with her × d4 .} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'She should have played ''} 13. Qe4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '.''} Nce7 $13) (13. Bd3 Qb6 $5 $13) 13... a6 $142 (13... Nxd4 $4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'is an awful blunder: '' loses to} 14. Nxf6+ Qxf6 15. Nxd4 $18 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'and White wins a piece.''}) 14. Bd3 $5 ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar would rather hand over a pawn, than strategic control after '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Polgar would rather hand over a pawn, than strategic control after'} 14. Bxc6 Bxc6 $15 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '. White saves her d-pawn at the cost of handing Black the bishop pair, light-square control, and pretty much killing her own kingside attacking chances.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. White saves her d-pawn at the cost of handing Black the bishop pair, light-square control, and pretty much killing her own kingside attacking chances.' gives Black a firm control over the blockading square d5.}) 14... Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Bxd4 16. Nd6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'KEATON: Does White have sufficient compensation for Black's extra pawn? CYRUS: I think this was Polgar's idea. She threatens Nxc8, as well as Qe4, with a double attack on the h7 mating square, as well as Black's d4-bishop. I have a feeling she overlooked or underestimated Dreev's next move, after which Black stands better. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'KEATON: Does White have sufficient compensation for Black's extra pawn? --- CYRUS: I think this was Polgar's idea. She threatens Nxc8, as well as Qe4, with a double attack on the h7 mating square, as well as Black's d4-bishop. I have a feeling she overlooked or underestimated Dreev's next move, after which Black stands better.'} Bxb2 $1 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Dreev offers an exchange for pawns and a good position. I think it is White who struggles to equalize here. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Dreev offers an exchange for pawns and a good position. I think it is White who struggles to equalize here.' This is the counterblow! Black sacrifices the exchange for pawns and a well-blockaded position.} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar may have expected '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Polgar may have expected'} 16... Rc6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '?'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '?'} 17. Qe4 f5 18. Qxd4 Rxd6 19. Bb4 $14 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!'} Nxb4 {Lukacs} 20. Qxd6 Nxd3 21. Qxd3 {with a clear advantage for White.}) (16... Qh4 17. Nxc8 Rxc8 18. Rab1 $14) 17. Rab1 Bxa3 (17... Rc6 $5 {can be met by} 18. Nxf7 $1 (18. Nxb7 Qf6 (18... Qb6 $2 19. Na5) 19. Na5 Rc7 20. Qe4 g6 $15) 18... Rxf7 19. Rxb2 b5 $44) 18. Nxc8 Qxc8 (18... Bxc8 19. Bxh7+ Kxh7 20. Qd3+ Kg8 21. Qxa3 Qh4 22. Rb3 $11) 19. Bxh7+ $1 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar regains one of her lost pawns with a tactic.''} Kxh7 20. Qd3+ Kg8 21. Qxa3 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'KEATON: How would you assess this? CYRUS: White has a lot to be gloomy about, for the following reasons: 1. Black has two extra and connected pawns for the exchange, which can later be pushed down the board. 2. Black dominates the light squares. 3. Black's king is safe. 4. White's rooks lack targets (besides Black's relatively safe king). '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'KEATON: How would you assess this? --- CYRUS: White has a lot to be gloomy about, for the following reasons: 1. Black has two extra and connected pawns for the exchange, which can later be pushed down the board. --- 2. Black dominates the light squares. --- 3. Black's king is safe. --- 4. White's rooks lack targets (besides Black's relatively safe king).'} Bc6 22. Rfe1 Re8 23. Rb3 $6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'When the known proves to be unpleasant, then we naturally embrace the unknown. Polgar, hoping to extract the maximum from her scant resources, seeks to swing the rook over to g3 to pressure g7, but it never really takes hold. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'When the known proves to be unpleasant, then we naturally embrace the unknown. Polgar, hoping to extract the maximum from her scant resources, seeks to swing the rook over to g3 to pressure g7, but it never really takes hold.' White wants to organize an a against × g7, but this fails.} (23. Qg3 $14) (23. Re5 $5) 23... e5 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'White's counter-argument fails to convince. One look at Black's central control, and we begin to doubt White's kingside attacking chances. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'White's counter-argument fails to convince. One look at Black's central control, and we begin to doubt White's kingside attacking chances.'} 24. Rg3 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'A natural attacker's motives are rarely subtle; g7 is a square which represents temptation. A storm looms overhead. The question is, whose city will it drench? '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'A natural attacker's motives are rarely subtle; g7 is a square which represents temptation. A storm looms overhead. The question is, whose city will it drench?'} (24. Rh3 {(Lukacs) is also possible, planning Qg3 and Qh4. It looks too slow after C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'is also possible, planning Qg3 and Qh4. It looks too slow after'} Nf4 $1 {.}) 24... Qe6 25. h3 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'No more back-rank mates. The drawback is that it prevents White from utilizing the h-file for the attack in the future.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'No more back-rank mates. The drawback is that it prevents White from utilizing the h-file for the attack in the future.'} (25. Rg5 f6 26. Rg6) 25... f6 26. Bh6 (26. Rg6 Re7 27. Qg3 a5 $132) 26... Re7 27. h4 $2 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Somehow it's much easier to be brave when danger for us lies in the future, rather than in the present. Now the h-pawn becomes a target. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '?!' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Somehow it's much easier to be brave when danger for us lies in the future, rather than in the present. Now the h-pawn becomes a target.' White pushes it too hard...} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar isn't willing to play a move like ''} 27. Bd2 $11 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'and simply wait for Black to make progress. The trouble with her move is that the weakening push of the h-pawn fails to endanger Black's king.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'and simply wait for Black to make progress. The trouble with her move is that the weakening push of the h-pawn fails to endanger Black's king.'}) 27... Qf5 $1 $15 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Halting h5 and menacing Qh5.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!'} 28. Bc1 Qh5 29. Qd6 Rd7 30. Qc5 $2 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!?'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!?'} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Polgar again rejects passive defence with ''} 30. Qe6+ Kh8 $15 (30... Kh7 {Lukacs} 31. Rh3 a5 {.})) 30... Ne7 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Dreev goes for a direct assault, shifting his knight to the kingside. ''} ({Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'There was also nothing wrong with '' It was possible to eliminate the weak h , but in rapid games the end is fairly unpredictable...} 30... Qxh4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '.''} 31. Rh3 Qg4 $17) 31. Qc4+ Kh7 32. Qc2+ Nf5 33. Rc3 (33. Rg5 fxg5 34. Qxf5+ Qg6 $17) 33... Qg6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Threatening mate.''} 34. f3 Nxh4 $19 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'The wealth disparity increases. Black picks up pawn number three for the exchange, coupled with an initiative/attack. White can safely resign here. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'The wealth disparity increases. Black picks up pawn number three for the exchange, coupled with an initiative/attack. White can safely resign here.'} 35. Qf2 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Or not. Polgar decides upon the stiff-upper-lip route and plays on, hoping for an error.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Or not. Polgar decides upon the stiff-upper-lip route and plays on, hoping for an error.'} Rd4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!'} 36. Kh2 Qh5 37. Kg1 Ng6 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'Terrible shapes shift and approach from the murk. Dreev's retreat was not a gesture of dismissal for his knight. He clears the way for ...Rh4. '' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'Terrible shapes shift and approach from the murk. Dreev's retreat was not a gesture of dismissal for his knight. He clears the way for ...Rh4.'} 38. Qc2 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: 'It's suicide to allow the rook entry to h4.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '?'} ({The admittedly ugly C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'The admittedly ugly'} 38. g3 {(Lukacs) had to be played.}) 38... Rh4 39. Kf2 Rh2 40. Rg1 Qh4+ 41. Ke2 Bb5+ {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!'} 42. Rd3 (42. Kd1 {Lukacs} Qd4+ {hangs the g1-rook. "You!" croaks the king. "Everyone has their secrets," replies his sister. C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: 'hangs the g1-rook. "You!" croaks the king. "Everyone has their secrets," replies his sister.'}) 42... e4 {Lukacs: 'Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala & Keaton Kiewra: '. White's unfortunate d3-rook reminds us of the squashed frog under the bus wheel. SUMMARY: Try the 3...Nf6 gambit offer. At club level, a lot of your opponents will eagerly grab the pawn and then later suffer for it.'' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '!' C Lakdawala & K Kiewra: '. White's unfortunate d3-rook reminds us of the squashed frog under the bus wheel. --- SUMMARY: Try the 3...Nf6 gambit offer. At club level, a lot of your opponents will eagerly grab the pawn and then later suffer for it.'} 0-1

