1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik,L B90: Sicilian Najdorf: Unusual White 6th moves, 6 Be3 Ng4 and 6 Be3 e5} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 {Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: '*'} 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Bg7 10. h3 Nf6 11. Qe2 $5 {Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'N' 'N' Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'Ftacnik: '!?'' Ponomariov is comming with the whole new concept of treating this famous position. The queen sits more naturally on e2 than on f3. Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'N' Ponomariov is comming with the whole new concept of treating this famous position. The queen sits more naturally on e2 than on f3. Ponomariov is comming with the whole new concept of treating this famous position. The queen sits more naturally on e2 than on f3. Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'Ftacnik: 'Ponomariov is comming with the whole new concept of treating this famous position. The queen sits more naturally on e2 than on f3.''} (11. Qf3 {Ü 84/(208) Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'Ü 84/(208)'}) 11... Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 dxe5 (13... Nd5 14. Nxd5 cxd5 15. O-O-O Qb6 16. c3 dxe5 17. Bxe5 Bxe5 18. Qxe5 O-O 19. h4 $36) 14. Bxe5 O-O 15. h4 (15. g3 Be6 16. Bg2 Qb6 17. O-O-O Rad8 18. f4 $14) 15... g4 $1 {Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'Ftacnik: '!''} 16. g3 Qb6 17. O-O-O (17. Bg2 Qxb2 18. O-O (18. Rb1 Qa3 19. Bxc6 Ra7 20. Qe3 Nd7 $1 $13) 18... Bf5 (18... Ra7 $5) 19. Bxc6 Rad8 (19... Rac8 $2 20. Nd5 Qa3 21. Qe3 $18) 20. Nd5 Qxc2 21. Nxe7+ Kh7 22. Qe3 $14) 17... Be6 18. Bg2 Rfd8 19. Rhe1 (19. f3 Qa5 20. Rxd8+ Rxd8 21. fxg4 Bxg4 22. Qe3 Be6 $15) 19... h5 20. b3 {Prevents intrusion on c4} (20. f3 $1 Qa5 21. Rxd8+ Rxd8 22. fxg4 Bxg4 23. Qe3 $14) 20... Rac8 21. Na4 Rxd1+ (21... Qb5 22. Rxd8+ (22. Qe3) 22... Rxd8 23. Qxb5 cxb5 24. Nc5 $14) 22. Rxd1 Qb5 (22... Qa5 $14) 23. Bf1 $2 {[%mdl 64] White misses small combination of his lady opponent in the very moment, when he had the chance to get serious advantage. White misses small combination of his lady opponent in the very moment, when he had the chance to get serious advantage. White misses small combination of his lady opponent in the very moment, when he had the chance to get serious advantage. Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: 'Ftacnik: 'White misses small combination of his lady opponent in the very moment, when he had the chance to get serious advantage. ''} (23. Qxb5 {'!?' is an interesting alternative} cxb5 (23... axb5 24. Nb6 Rf8 25. Bxc6 $18) 24. Bb7 {''} Rf8 25. Nc5 Bc8 26. Bxa6 $16) (23. Qe3 c5 (23... Bh6 24. Bf4 (24. Qxh6 Qxe5 25. Qg5+ Qxg5+ 26. hxg5 $14) 24... Bg7 (24... Bxf4 25. Qxf4 c5 (25... Bf5 26. Bf1 Qa5 27. Qg5+ Kf8 28. Bd3 $18) 26. Qg5+ Kf8 (26... Kh8 27. Re1 $18) (26... Kh7) 27. Nxc5 $1 Qxc5 (27... Rxc5 28. Rd8+ Ne8 29. Qh6+ Kg8 30. Be4 Bf5 31. a4 $18) 28. Rd8+ Ne8 29. Qh6+ Kg8 30. Be4 Bf5 (30... Qe5 31. Bh7+ Kh8 32. Rxc8 Bxc8 33. Bg6+) 31. Qg5+ Kf8 32. Bxf5 $18) 25. c4 Qb4 (25... Bxc4 26. Nb6 $18) (25... Qf5 26. Nc5 $16) 26. Qc5 $16) 24. c4 $5 (24. Nc3 Qb4 25. Kb2 $14) 24... Qb4 (24... Qa5 25. Bc3 Qc7 26. Qg5 $16) 25. Qc3 $14 Qa3+ $140 26. Qb2 Qb4 27. a3 Qa5 28. Qc3) (23. Qe1 Bh6+ (23... c5 24. c4 Bh6+ (24... Bxc4 $2 25. Nc3) 25. Kc2 Bf5+ 26. Kb2 Qe8 27. Bb7 $16) 24. Kb1 c5 25. Nc3 $14) 23... Bc4 $1 {'='} 24. Qxc4 (24. Qe1 Bxf1 25. Bxf6 Bxf6 26. Qxf1 e6 $15) 24... Qxe5 25. c3 (25. Qxa6 Qa1+ 26. Kd2 Rd8+ 27. Bd3 Ne4+ 28. Ke1 Qe5 $17) (25. Qc3 Qf5 (25... Qc7 26. Bxa6 Ra8 27. Bb5) 26. Bxa6 Rb8 27. Qxc6 Qxf2 28. Qc7 $13) (25. Qd4 Qf5 26. Bxa6 Rb8 $11) 25... Qf5 26. Qc5 (26. Qxa6 Bh6+ 27. f4 gxf3+ 28. Kb2 Rc7 29. Qd3 Qxd3 30. Rxd3 f2 $15) 26... Qf3 27. Bd3 (27. Bxa6 $5 {might be a viable alternative} Ra8 28. Bf1 $11) 27... Bh6+ {''} 28. Kc2 Ne4 29. Qe5 $2 {The centralization of the strongest piece is not necessarily always the best move, much more promising was 29.xh5! The centralization of the strongest piece is not necessarily always the best move, much more promising was 29.xh5!} (29. Qxh5 $1 {'!?' must be considered} Qxf2+ (29... Nxf2 30. Nb2 Nxd1 31. Qxh6 Ne3+ 32. Kc1 Nf5 (32... Qh1+ 33. Kd2 $11) 33. Qg5+ Ng7 34. Qh6 $11) 30. Kb1 Qf3 31. Bc2 Bg7 32. Rd3 Qe2 33. Qa5 Rf8 34. Kb2 $13) 29... Nxf2 (29... Qxf2+ $5 30. Kb1 Qf3 (30... Nxg3 31. Qxe7 $19) 31. Bc2 Nxg3 32. Qxe7 Rf8 $17) 30. Be2 Qg2 (30... Nxd1 31. Bxf3 Ne3+ 32. Kb2 gxf3 33. Qxh5 f2 34. Qxh6 f1=Q (34... Nd1+ 35. Kc2 f1=Q 36. Qg5+ Kf8 37. Qh6+ Ke8 38. Qh8+ Kd7 39. Nb6+ $16) 35. Qxe3 Qg2+ 36. Ka3 $11) 31. Re1 (31. Bf1 Qh2 32. Re1 Nh1+ 33. Kb1 Nxg3 $17 {'-+'}) 31... Rd8 32. Qxe7 {'?'} (32. Bf1 Rd2+ 33. Kb1 {'-+'} Qd5 34. Qxd5 cxd5 35. Bxa6 Ne4 $19) 32... Rd2+ {'-+'} 33. Kb1 Qd5 (33... Qxg3 34. Bxa6 Qf3 35. Qe8+ Kh7 36. Be2 Qf5+ 37. Ka1 $19) 34. Ka1 (34. Nb2 Rxb2+ 35. Kxb2 Qd2+ 36. Ka3 Bf8 $19) (34. Bc4 Rd1+ 35. Kb2 Bc1+ 36. Kc2 Qd2+ 37. Kb1 Ba3+ 38. Rxd1 Qxd1#) 34... Ne4 {With the decisive threat Rxe2 The FIDE world champion is in real trouble, as black pieces are clearly dominating the board. The FIDE world champion is in real trouble, as black pieces are clearly dominating the board.} 35. Qe8+ Kg7 {Black threatens strongly Rxe2} 36. Nb2 (36. Bxg4 hxg4 37. Qxe4 Rd1+ 38. Kb2 Bc1+ 39. Kc2 (39. Ka1 Be3+ 40. Kb2 Rd2+ 41. Ka3 Qxe4 $19) 39... Qd2+ 40. Kb1 Ba3+ 41. Rxd1 Qxd1#) 36... Rxb2 {'!' the knock-out blow} (36... Nd6 37. Bc4 Nxe8 38. Bxd5 cxd5 39. Rxe8 Rg2 40. Na4 Rxg3 $19) (36... Nxg3 {is clearly inferior} 37. Bc4 Qf3 38. Bxf7 $14) 37. Kxb2 {'?' Decoy to b2} (37. Bc4 $1 Rxa2+ 38. Kxa2 Qa5+ (38... Nxc3+ 39. Kb2 Qd2+ 40. Ka3 Nb5+ 41. Bxb5 axb5 42. Qe2 Qd6+ 43. Ka2 Qxg3 $17) 39. Kb2 (39. Kb1 Nxc3+ 40. Kc2 Qa2+ 41. Kxc3 Qd2#) 39... Qxc3+ 40. Ka2 Qa5+ (40... Qc2+ 41. Ka1 $19) 41. Kb2 Qd2+ 42. Ka3 Nd6 43. Qe2 Nxc4+ 44. bxc4 Qc3+ 45. Ka2 Kg8 $17) 37... Qd2+ {Theme: Double Attack} 38. Ka3 Nf6 $1 {Ponomariov,Ruslan; Komarov,D: '0-1 (38) Ponomariov,R (2743)-Polgar,J (2685) Benidorm 2002 CBM 93 [Ftacnik]'} (38... Nf6 $1 39. Rd1 (39. Qxc6 Qxe1 40. Bxa6 Qxg3 $19) 39... Qxd1 40. Bxd1 Nxe8 $19) (38... Qxe1 $6 {is no comparison} 39. Qxe4 Qxg3 40. Qd4+ Kg8 41. Qf6 $19) 0-1

