1. d4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Ftacnik Polgar,Judit Ftacnik' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'This is still Black's most solid way of meeting the 4 a3 variation.'''} 6. cxd5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Other moves we have looked at include 6 Qc2 and 6 Bg5.'''} Nxd5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Keeping the diagonal open for the bishop, this recapture is much more popular than 6... exd5.'''} 7. Qc2 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Preparing in some lines to play e2-e4, while if Black captures on c3, White can recapture with the queen.'''} Nxc3 (7... Be7 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Polgar,Judit) can be seen in Gretarsson-Sargissian, Bled 2002  (ECO code E12).}) 8. Qxc3 h6 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Both 8...Bd6 and 8...Nd7 are met by the slightly awkward 9 Bg5, so Black expends a tempo to prevent this move.''' John Emms: 'Both 8...Bd6 and 8...Nd7 are met by the slightly awkward 9 Bg5, so Black uses up a tempo to prevent this move.'} ({However,} 8... Nd7 {is a very reasonable way to continue. Following} 9. Bg5 Be7 10. Bxe7 {Black is forced to capture with} Kxe7 {as otherwise the c-pawn would be hanging. Still, this is all theory and is acceptable from Black's point of view. For example,} 11. g3 Nf6 12. Bg2 Qd6 $1 {(planning to move the h-rook so that the king can drop back to f8 if it needs to)} 13. O-O {and now:} Z0 (13... Rhc8 14. Qe3 $5 {(a sneaky move)} c5 $2 {(failing to spot White's not-too-subtle threat!)} ({However,} 14... Kf8 {looks okay for Black:} 15. Rac1 c5 16. dxc5 Rxc5 17. Rxc5 Qxc5 18. Qxc5+ bxc5 19. Rc1 Nd7 {and White's advantage is not significant.}) 15. Nh4 $1 {(threatening both Nf5+ and Bxb7)} Qc7 16. Nf5+ Kd7 17. Bxb7 Qxb7 18. Nxg7 {and White won quickly in Nisipeanu-Tiviakov, Saint Vincent 2003.}) (13... c5 {may be even more accurate:} 14. dxc5 Qxc5 15. Qxc5+ bxc5 16. Rac1 Rhc8 17. Rc3 Rab8 18. Rfc1 Nd7 19. b3 Bxf3 20. Bxf3 a5 {with complete equality, Staniszewski-Macieja, Warsaw 2003.})) 9. e3 {John Emms: 'White intends to play b2-b4 and develop the dark-squared bishop on b2.'} ({Another option for White is} 9. Bf4 {, which is best met by} Bd6 {; for example} 10. Ne5 Nd7 11. f3 Rc8 $5 12. e4 O-O (12... c5 $2 {drops a pawn to} 13. Nxf7 $1) 13. Rd1 Nf6 14. Be3 c6 15. g3 Qe7 16. Nd3 Nd7 {with a roughly level position, M.Gurevich-Adorjan, Akureyri 1988.}) 9... Nd7 10. b4 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Preparing to play Bb2 and dissuading Black from striking out with ...c7-c5.'''} ({John Emms: 'An alternative is'} 10. Bb5 $5 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Polgar,Judit) Ü 46/(692) Ftacnik,Lubomir: '(Polgar,Judit) Ü 46/(692) Polgar,Judit: 'Ü 46/(692)''} Bd6 {John Emms: 'and now:'} 11. Bc6 (11. O-O $2 Bxf3 12. Bxd7+ (12. gxf3 $4 Qg5+ {picks up the bishop}) 12... Kxd7 $1 13. gxf3 Qg5+ {and Black has the initiative - Aseev John Emms: 'and Black has the initiative - Aseev.'}) (11. Z0) 11... Bxc6 12. Qxc6 O-O 13. b4 e5 14. Bb2 Qe7 {(Dreev-Aseev, Barnaul 1988)} 15. dxe5 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Bxe5 17. Bxe5 Qxe5 18. O-O a5 $1 19. b5 a4 {, which is assessed as equal by Aseev.}) 10... Be7 11. Bb2 O-O 12. Rd1 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Continuing with the policy of preventing the ...c7-c5 break.'''} (12. d5 $2 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} Bf6 $17) (12. Bd3 $5 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit}) (12. Bb5 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} c6 $1 13. Bxc6 Rc8 14. d5 Bf6 15. Qb3 Bxc6 16. dxc6 Rxc6 17. Nd4 Bxd4 18. Bxd4 Qg5 19. O-O Rfc8 {was completely equal in the game Hergott-Howell, Groningen 1988. John Emms: 'was completely equal in the game Hergott-Howell, Groningen 1988.'}) 12... a5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'N!?' Polgar,Judit: 'John Emms: 'Striking out at the b4-pawn. White's reply is very natural.''' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'N!?' 'N!?'' John Emms: 'Striking out at the b4-pawn. White's reply is very natural.' Polgar is looking for the compensation, that would balance the dangerous battery along the a1-h8 diagonal. Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar is looking for the compensation, that would balance the dangerous battery along the a1-h8 diagonal. Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik: 'Polgar is looking for the compensation, that would balance the dangerous battery along the a1-h8 diagonal.'''} (12... Rc8 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit}) 13. b5 (13. bxa5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} Rxa5 14. Rc1 c5 $15) (13. Be2 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: '!?'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: '!?' '!?''} axb4 14. axb4 Nf6 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: '' '''} (14... Ra4 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} 15. O-O $36) 15. b5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} Nd5 16. Qc4 Bb4+ 17. Kf1 Bd6 $15) 13... Rc8 14. Qb3 {John Emms: '?!' John Emms: 'Karpov begins to drift.'} (14. Be2 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik''} c6 15. bxc6 Bxc6 16. Qd3 $11) ({John Emms: 'Polgar suggests'} 14. Qd2 $5 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit John Emms: ', giving the line'} c6 15. bxc6 Bxc6 16. Ba6 Rc7 17. O-O {John Emms: ', which looks roughly equal.'}) (14. Ne5 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} Nxe5 15. dxe5 Qe8 {isn't a problem for Black, who will break out with ...c7-c6. John Emms: 'isn't a problem for Black, who will break out with ...c7-c6.'}) (14. Bc4 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} c6 15. Qd3 (15. bxc6 Bxc6 16. Qd3 (16. d5 Bf6) 16... Be4 17. Qxe4 Rxc4) 15... cxb5 16. Bxb5 Nf6 17. O-O) 14... c6 15. bxc6 (15. Be2 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} cxb5 16. Bxb5 Be4 $15) 15... Bxc6 16. Bb5 $2 ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'White should be content with''} 16. Be2 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'although Black has equalised after'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Polgar,Judit: 'Ftacnik'' John Emms: ', although Black has more than equalized after'} Bd5 (16... a4 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Polgar,Judit} 17. Qd3 Nf6 1