1. e4 {Rogozenco,D Leko,Peter Ftacnik} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. Qh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What are the ideas behind this early queen move? --- ANSWER: -- 1. White keeps options open of castling long. -- 2. White discourages Black from castling short, as then e4xf5 and f5-f6 tricks are available. -- 3. White discourages us from playing ...Bxd5, since our f5-pawn would fall.'} Rg8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Only with this move do we retain equality.'} ({As just intimated, Black can't play} 12... Bxd5 {here because of} 13. exd5 Ne7 14. Bxf5 {, winning a pawn, since} Nxd5 $4 {loses to} 15. Be6 $1 {.}) ({And while} 12... Bg7 $6 {is commonly played, I don't believe Black can equalize after} 13. O-O {. --- QUESTION: Can't Black just castle? --- ANSWER: It's suicide! After} O-O $4 ({but} 13... f4 {represents a serious concession, since it removes the pressure from White's e4-point}) 14. exf5 Bxd5 15. f6 e4 16. fxg7 Re8 17. Qxd5 exd3 ({or} 17... Nb4 18. Qb3) 18. Qxc6 {, White wins a piece. We will see something similar in the notes to So. Polgar-Nunn, except that there 17...exd3 comes with check which makes all the difference.}) 13. g3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's most popular choice, covering against ...Rxg2 and keeping the option of castling on either wing.'} ({Alternatives: -- a)} 13. O-O-O {, offering the g-pawn for rapid development, led to some brilliant play in M.Brodsky-V.Kramnik, Kherson 1991:} Rxg2 14. f4 Nd4 $1 15. Ne3 Rf2 16. exf5 $2 Bxa2 17. fxe5 dxe5 18. Nxb5 Bh6 $3 19. Rhe1 (19. Qxh6 $4 Rxc2+ 20. Nxc2 Nb3# {is a pretty mate}) 19... axb5 20. Bxb5+ Ke7 21. Qh4+ f6 22. Qxf2 Bf7 $1 23. Bd3 Qb6 $1 24. Be4 Ra2 25. c4 Bxc4 26. Kb1 Qa5 27. Nd5+ Bxd5 28. Qxd4 Ra1+ 29. Kc2 Rxd1 30. Qxd1 Qa4+ 31. Kc3 {and White resigned before} Qc4# {. This game is annotated in 'Kramnik: Move by Move'.}) ({b)} 13. c3 Rxg2 14. Qf3 {(hitting Black's rook and f5-pawn)} Rg4 15. exf5 Bxd5 16. Qxd5 Ne7 17. Qb7 Bh6 18. f6 Ng8 19. Qc6+ Kf8 20. Rd1 Rf4 21. Bxh7 Rxf6 22. Bxg8 Kxg8 23. Rg1+ Kf8 {and Black stands no worse, F.Nijboer-D.Reinderman, Wijk aan Zee 2000.}) ({c)} 13. O-O f4 {(in this instance ...f5-f4 is well timed, despite being a move Black normally avoids, since it removes e4xf5 from the equation and suppresses White's f2-f4 break)} 14. h3 Rg5 $1 {and then:} 15. Nc7+ (15. Qxh7 $2 Rg6 $1 16. Qh5 ({15...Rg6 threatens} 16. Z0 Rh6 17. Qg8 f5 $1 {, trapping White's queen}) 16... Rh6 17. Qd1 Bxh3 $1 18. gxh3 Qh4 19. Qg4 Rg6 20. Qxg6 fxg6 {and Black wins, S.Richkov-S.Seuchter, correspondence 2003. White doesn't have time to grab the a8-rook with} 21. Nc7+ Kf7 22. Nxa8 {in view of} Qxh3 23. f3 Qg3+ 24. Kh1 d5 $1 25. exd5 Bc5 {, forcing mate.}) 15... Kd7 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Qd1 (17. Qxh7+ Bg7 18. h4 Rg4 19. Be2 Qxh4 20. Qxh4 Rxh4 {is awful for White}) 17... Qf6 {, when Black can attack down the open g-file and stands clearly better, V.Taksrud-E.Lindholm, correspondence 2005.}) 13... Rg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 14. Qd1 ({QUESTION: Why didn't White grab on h7? --- ANSWER:} 14. Qxh7 $2 {surrenders the initiative after} Nd4 $1 15. c3 ({or} 15. O-O-O Rg6 16. exf5 Rh6) 15... Nf3+ 16. Ke2 Bxd5 17. exd5 e4 {, when Black is almost winning already; for example,} 18. h4 ({or} 18. Rac1 Rg6 19. Qh3 Qg5 20. Nb1 Rh6 21. Qg2 Qg4 {0-1 S.Khabarov-R.Felgaer, Rethymnon 2011}) 18... Rg6 19. h5 Rg5 20. Bb1 Qf6 21. Nc2 b4 22. Nxb4 Kd7 23. Bc2 Bh6 24. Ba4+ Kc7 25. Nxa6+ Kb6 26. Bc6 Rh8 {0-1 D.Vogel-T.Winckelmann, correspondence 2001.}) 14... Bxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A now-familiar theme; Leko seals d5 with a white pawn.'} 15. exd5 Ne7 16. Nxb5 $6 {Leko,Peter: 'N!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This move looks a lot stronger than it actually is. White doesn't win a pawn, due to Leko's response.' This is a doubtful novelty. Later Polgar switched to the more logical 16.c3. The problem with taking with the knight on b5 in Sveshnikov Sicilian is that even when it wins a pawn, Black often gets good compensation.  However, in this game Leko doesn't even sacrifice the pawn.} (16. c3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is more usual, when'} Bh6 17. Be2 ({or} 17. Nc2 e4 18. Bf1 f4 $1 19. gxf4 Rxd5 20. Qg4 Qd7 21. Qxd7+ Kxd7 {with an unclear endgame, W.Rocha-E.Matsuura, Brazilian Championship, Rio de Janeiro 2004}) 17... Rc8 (17... Qb6 18. Nc2 Kf8 19. a4 bxa4 20. Nb4 f4 21. Qxa4 {, P.Acs-P.Leko, Budapest 2003, and now} e4 22. Qa5 Qb7 23. Bxa6 Qd7 {is a complete mess, where Black's kingside attack provides him full compensation for the pawn, and maybe even a shade more.}) 18. c4 f4 19. cxb5 e4 20. gxf4 Qa5+ 21. Kf1 Rxd5 22. Qe1 Qc7 23. bxa6 Qd7 24. Rg1 Qh3+ 25. Rg2 Bxf4 26. Qb4 Bxh2 27. Bg4 Qd3+ 28. Be2 Qh3 29. Bg4 Qd3+ 30. Ke1 Nc6 31. Qb7 Qd2+ 32. Kf1 Qd3+ 33. Be2 Qh3 34. Rc1 Rg5 35. Bg4 Qd3+ 36. Ke1 e3 37. Bd7+ Ke7 38. Bf5+ {1-0 Polgar,J-Lautier,J/Enghien les Bains 2003.}) 16... Qb6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This simple response regains the pawn.'} (16... e4 17. Be2 Qa5+ 18. Nc3 Bg7 $44) (16... Bg7 $44) ({Not} 16... axb5 $4 {(even a kindergartener would recognize this as a bone-headed move)} 17. Bxb5+ {and Black can resign.}) 17. Na3 {Leko,Peter: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Heading for c4.'} (17. Qc1 Bh6 $1 $36 {Leko,Peter: '!'} (17... Rg6 {Ftacnik} 18. Nc3 Bh6 19. Qb1 (19. f4 exf4 20. gxf4 Rg1+ $17) 19... e4 20. Be2 Bg7 $11)) ({Instead,} 17. Nc3 Qxb2 18. Na4 Qb4+ 19. c3 Qa5 20. O-O e4 21. Bc4 Rc8 22. Bb3 Bg7 {leaves White's pieces oddly posted on the queenside, so I prefer Black, F.Silva-R.Sikorsky, correspondence 2007.}) 17... Qxb2 18. Nc4 Qc3+ 19. Kf1 Rd8 {Ftacnik: 'The modern chess is first of all tough fight with quite a few questionable moments. It is hard to guess why black refrained from the promissing 19...xd5!? '} (19... e4 {Leko,Peter: '!?'} 20. Be2 (20. f4 Rg6 21. Be2 Rc8 {is much better for Black, if comparing with 21.f4 instead of 21.Be2 from the game.}) 20... Bg7 21. Rb1 Kf8 $13 22. Nxd6 Rd8 $36 {[%csl Rd5]}) (19... Rg6 $5) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Another option is simply to capture'} 19... Nxd5 $5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', and if'} 20. Rb1 {Ftacnik} (20. Be2 $1 $44 {Leko,Peter} Rb8 21. Rc1 Nc7 22. Nxd6+ Bxd6 23. Qxd6 Rg6 24. Qd3 Qc6 25. Rg1 e4 {, Black holds a mild initiative.}) 20... Qd4 21. Be2 Qc5 $15) 20. Rb1 e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Opening up the long dark diagonal, but it's inaccurate and gives White a chance to solidify her position.'} (20... Nxd5 {Ftacnik} 21. Be2 Nf6 22. Rb3 Qd4 23. Qxd4 exd4 24. Rb6 $14) (20... Bh6 $5 {Leko,Peter}) ({The more flexible} 20... Bg7 {was correct.}) 21. Be2 $6 {Leko,Peter: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Both parties fail to catch discrepancies in each other's logic.'} ({The typical} 21. f4 {Leko,Peter: '!!' was necessary. Now Black will find a job for the rook on the fifth rank.} Rg6 (21... exf3 22. Qxf3 {Leko,Peter: ''} Bg7 23. Kg2 {is in White's favour.}) (21... exd3 $2 {Leko,Peter} 22. cxd3 $1 Rg6 23. Qa4+ $18) 22. Be2 $13 {[%cal Gf1g2] Leko,Peter: ''} (22. Rb3 Qd4 $2 {runs into} 23. Nxd6+ $1 Rgxd6 24. Bb5+ {, winning the queen})) (21. Rb3 {Ftacnik} Qf6 (21... Qd4 $2 22. Nxd6+ Rxd6 23. Bb5+ $18) 22. f4 Rg6 (22... exf3 23. Nb6 Ng6 24. Qxf3 $16) 23. Be2 Bg7 24. Kg2 $14) 21... f4 $1 {[%cal Gg5d5] Leko,Peter: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Rectifying his mistake. Leko renders White's f2-f4 impossible by playing ...f5-f4 himself.'} 22. Rb3 (22. Qd2 $6 {Leko,Peter} Nxd5 23. gxf4 Rf5 $1 24. Qxc3 $140 Nxc3 25. Rb7 Rb5 $1) (22. Nb6 {Leko,Peter} Bh6 $40 (22... Bg7)) ({Not} 22. gxf4 $4 Qh3+ 23. Ke1 Rxd5 24. Qc1 Bg7 {, when the threat of ...Bc3+ gives Black a winning position.}) 22... Qf6 23. gxf4 {'?' Ftacnik. White had little choice, since protecting the d-pawn by moves like 23.Nb6 (which is probably best) would hardly cross anyone's mind.  Ftacnik: 'This solution is most likely second rate, Leko will get a chance to build up the massive initiative on the kingside. '} (23. Nb6 {Ftacnik} fxg3 (23... f3 24. Bxa6 Nxd5 25. h4 Qh6 26. Kg1 (26. Nxd5 Rxd5 27. Qe1 $11) 26... Rxg3+ 27. fxg3 Nxb6 28. Qd4 $16) 24. hxg3 Nf5 25. Kg2 Bg7 26. Bxa6 $14) 23... Qxf4 {Leko,Peter: '!?' Now White's previous move is fully justified.} (23... Rxd5 $1 24. Qc1 (24. Qb1 {Ftacnik} Qxf4 25. Ne3 (25. Rb8 {Leko,Peter} Nc6 26. Rxd8+ Nxd8 $15) 25... Ra5 $17) 24... Bh6 $17 (24... Rc5 {Ftacnik} 25. Nb6 Nf5 $17)) 24. Ne3 Bg7 (24... Bh6 $5 25. Bxa6 Ra8 26. Bb5+ Kf8 $44) 25. Bxa6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's extra pawn isn't enough to compensate for Black's powerful kingside initiative.' White's extra pawn plays insignificant role for the moment. Black will organise an initiative in the center and on the kingside, using the fact that White has an worse co-oprdination: not easy to connect rooks, bad position of the king and no possibilities for active play.} Nf5 $44 {Leko,Peter: '!' Leko,Peter: '[ Bd4]'} 26. Ng2 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After'} 26. Nxf5 Rxf5 27. Qe2 Bd4 $17 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', Black is clearly better, since f2 falls.'}) 26... Qe5 (26... Rxg2 $2 {Leko,Peter} 27. Kxg2 Bd4 28. Rf1 Nh4+ 29. Kh1 Nf3 30. Rxf3 $11) 27. h4 Rg6 28. Rhh3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Polgar finds a way to activate her sleeping h1-rook.' So she achieved the connection between rooks. The position is double-edged.} (28. Qb1 {Leko,Peter}) 28... Kf8 {Leko,Peter: '' Leko,Peter: ''} (28... Bh6 $5 {(Leko,Peter)  Qg7}) 29. Qb1 {Leko,Peter: '!?' White is trying to create her own play. However, the king on f1 will need soon more pieces to defend him and White's main task was rather to prevent Black's active possibilities. Also pawn d5 remains unprotected now.} (29. c3 $13 {[%cal Gd1e2] would have been better, preventing Nd4. However, psychologically it's a difficult move to make after just having reached the connection between rooks on the third rank.}) ({or} 29. h5 {followed by 30.c3.}) 29... Bf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Giving Black's king air on g7, in case White tries Rb8.'} (29... Bh6 $5 {Leko,Peter}) 30. h5 {Leko,Peter: '?' Leko,Peter: '[g5]'} Rg8 {Leko,Peter: '' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I read enough detective novels to understand how to shadow a suspect: Hang back and stay several car-lengths distance. Sometimes we are relieved when we mistakenly believe we repelled an opponent's attack. In other cases we are fooled, since the opponent is merely regrouping to return and finish us off. All the same, I'm not sure why Leko didn't play his rook to g5 here.'} 31. Be2 {'?' Ftacnik. Leko,Peter: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White hopes for time to push her passed a-pawn down the board. She doesn't get it.' Ftacnik: 'Much more promissing was to change a pair of rooks with the help of 31.b8!?, even though black would still be better.'} (31. c4 $6 {protects pawn d5, but leaves the bishop out of play on a6. Black is clearly better, after say} Nd4 32. Rbe3 {and now even} (32. Rb8 Qg5 33. Rg3 Qxh5) 32... Nf3 $5 33. Rhxf3 exf3 34. Rxe5 fxg2+ 35. Kg1 Bxe5 $17) (31. Rb8 $5 {Ftacnik} Qxd5 (31... Rxb8 32. Qxb8+ Kg7 33. Qb1 Qxd5 34. c4 $11) 32. c4 Qd4 $15) (31. c3 {was necessary to prevent Black's next move.}) 31... Nd4 (31... Qxd5 32. Rb5 Qe6 {was possible too.}) 32. Rbe3 Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'So Leko finds a use for the vacant g5-square.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'He later saw that' Black has a decisive attack after the following forced line:} 32... Nxe2 {Leko,Peter: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 33. Rxe2 Qg5 {Leko,Peter: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is even stronger, when'} 34. Rg3 (34. Rh2 {Leko,Peter} Be5) 34... Qxh5 35. Rxg8+ Kxg8 36. Re1 Kh8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(now ...Qh1+ and ...Qxg2 is a very real threat)'} 37. Ne3 Bc3 38. Rd1 Qh1+ (38... f5 $19 {Leko,Peter}) 39. Ke2 Qf3+ 40. Kf1 f5 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is crushing.' But the game continuation is also strong enough to win.}) 33. Qb6 Re8 34. Reg3 f5 {Leko,Peter: '-+!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening all sorts of nasties with ...f5-f4-f3.'} 35. Nh4 {Leko,Peter: '?!'} (35. Bb5 Re7 {changes little - White is unable to stop the advance of f-pawn.}) (35. Bd1 $5 {Leko,Peter} f4 36. Rxg5 Rxg5 37. Nxf4 Kg8 $1 $19) 35... Nxe2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 36. Kxe2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When we get that "This-isn't-my-day" feeling, we are usually right. White's king is fatally exposed.'} f4 $19 37. Rb3 (37. Rg1 {walks into mate after} f3+ 38. Kd1 Qa1+ {.}) 37... f3+ 38. Kf1 Qxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This threatens mate and wins easily enough, but Leko later found something even stronger. --- EXERCISE (combination alert): Instead of 38...Qxd5, what might Black might have played?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Leko discovered the nice combination'} 38... Bxh4 $5 {Leko,Peter Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 39. Rxh4 e3 $3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} (39... Rg1+ 40. Kxg1 Qg5+ 41. Rg4 $1 Qxg4+ 42. Kf1) 40. fxe3 ({taking with the rook or the queen, e.g. with} 40. Rxe3 {, allows} Qa1+ {and mates}) 40... Rg1+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Black applies the shock therapy route)'} 41. Kxg1 Qg3+ 42. Kf1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(as he is led to the gallows, White's king realizes that it might have been an error of judgment to place a whoopee cushion on his sister's throne)'} Qg2+ 43. Ke1 Qe2# {# Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Polgar's defence is on the brink of exhaustion, and so she plays her last card with'} 39. Nxf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'When desperation whispers dark temptations in our ear, we are quick to modify our definition of propriety. White's last move is made with the thought: If we are unable to obtain 100% of our desires, then we play for the abbreviated version, with percentage points shaved off.' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', hoping for counterplay after'} Qc4+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A skilled prosecutor's cross-examination questions are worded so that any answer the defendant provides entangles her deeper into her own presumed guilt. Zwischenzug; White either loses a full rook, or moves her king into a deadly discovery.'} (39... exf3 40. Rhxf3+ {. Unfortunately, Black doesn't have to take the knight.}) 40. Rd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gulp! If we defend long enough, we become inured to suffering. Handing an opponent a gigantic material concession is to us chess players a kind of plea bargain with the prosecutor's office. I believe it was the poet Burns who wrote (and I paraphrase): "something, something... the best laid schemes o' mice an' men..." and then I can't remember the rest. Anyway, I think what he meant to convey was that life rarely goes the way we originally envision. When we encounter an agonizing choice between A and B, both of which lose, then a crazy C is highly unlikely to succeed either. In this instance, the cost of repairs far exceeds White's bank balance.'} ({Neither king move saves White:} 40. Ke1 exf3+ 41. Re3 Rxe3+ 42. fxe3 Qe2#) ({or} 40. Kg1 Be3+ {(the white queen's lips slowly shape themselves into a silent "No!")} 41. Kh2 Bxb6 {and Black wins.}) 40... exd3 41. Qxd6+ Be7 42. Qh6+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's checks are already at an end after'} Rg7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', and'} 43. Rg3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Would-be attackers follow Black's completely safe king like seagulls after a fishing boat. They will never taste the fish.'} (43. Ne5 {Leko,Peter Cyrus Lakdawala: 'loses to'} d2+ $1 44. Nxc4 d1=Q# {.}) 43... dxc2+ 44. Kg2 Qc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. --- SUMMARY: The early 12 Qh5 is well met by 12...Rg8!.'} 0-1

