1. e4 {Gelfand Gelfand,B Rogozenko} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. c3 {Rogozenko: 'Having lost a principled opening battle against Leko few rounds earlier in the same tournament, Judit changes the variation and plays a line she used years ago. This time she was more successful with it, although the opening outcome was certainly not completely satisfactory for White.'} (11. Bd3 {Rogozenko} Be6 12. Qh5 Rg8 13. g3 Rg5 14. Qd1 Bxd5 15. exd5 Ne7 {was met in round 5, Polgar,J-Leko,P/Budapest 2003.}) 11... Bg7 12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nc2 O-O 14. Nce3 Be6 15. Bd3 f5 16. O-O Ra7 ({Judit's previous game in this line brought White little joy:} 16... Kh8 {Rogozenko} 17. Qh5 Bf7 18. Qh3 e4 19. Bc2 Ne5 20. f3 $6 (20. Rad1 $142) 20... exf3 21. gxf3 Bxd5 22. Nxd5 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 Qd2 24. Bb3 Nd3 25. Qh5 Rae8 $17 {0-1 Polgar,J-Illescas Cordoba,M/Dos Hermanas 1999 (61).}) (16... e4 {(Rogozenko) is another possible option.}) 17. f4 {Gelfand: 'Rogozenko: '17.a4 and 17.Qh5 are the main options for White, the former being probably stronger. Although White scored good results in practice after 17.f4, it can hardly set any problems for Black.'' Rogozenko: '17.a4 and 17.Qh5 are the main options for White, the former being probably stronger. Although White scored good results in practice after 17.f4, it can hardly set any problems for Black.'} (17. a4 {Ü 83/130}) 17... Ne7 $1 $11 {= Gelfand: 'Rogozenko: 'Gelfand deviates first from his own game and easily achieves a comfortable position.'' Rogozenko: 'Gelfand deviates first from his own game and easily achieves a comfortable position.'} (17... e4 {Rogozenko} 18. Bc2 Kh8 19. Kh1 a5 20. Qe2 b4 21. Rad1 bxc3 22. bxc3 a4 23. g4 fxg4 24. Nxg4 Ne7 25. Bxe4 Nxd5 26. Bxd5 Bxg4 27. Qxg4 Bxc3 $11 {½-½ Voitsekhovsky,S-Gelfand,B/Belgrade 1998 (29) Gelfand: '½-½ Voitsekhovsky,S-Gelfand,B/Belgrade 1998 (29)'}) (17... Raf7 {Rogozenko} 18. Kh1 (18. a4 $5) 18... Qb8 19. Bc2 b4 20. Ba4 $1 $14 {G