1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 Ne4 3. Bf4 d5 4. e3 c5 5. Bd3 Nc6 {In view of White's reply this looks imprecise. Besides the moves examined in McShane-Sutovsky there are a couple of other alternatives for Black:} ({a)} 5... Qb6 6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. Nc3 Qxb2 (7... cxd4 8. Qxd4 $1 Qxd4 9. exd4 f5 $6 10. f3 {was great for White in Hodgson-A.Jackson, Southend 1998}) 8. Nge2 Bg4 9. Nxe4 $1 Bxe2 10. Kxe2 cxd4 $6 11. Qd3 $1 f5 $6 12. Ng5 Nc6 13. Ne6 Rc8 14. Rhb1 Qc3 15. Qxc3 dxc3 16. Rxb7 Kf7 17. Nd4 Kf6 18. Bc7 $1 {and White won in Adams-Xie Jun, Hastings 1996/97.}) ({b)} 5... e6 6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. Nc3 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Qxd4 9. exd4 Bb4 10. Nge2 f5 11. O-O-O Nc6 {was played in K.Berg-Prusikin, Morso 2002, and now} 12. d5 {would have been quite strong.}) 6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. d5 e5 (7... Nb4 8. Nc3 e6 9. d6 Nc6 10. Nxe4 {is good for White.}) 8. Bg3 Ne7 9. Nc3 Ng6 ({This is an attempted improvement over} 9... f6 {, when K.Berg-Navara, Morso 2002 continued} 10. Qh5+ Ng6 (10... g6 11. Qh4 Nf5 12. Qxe4 Nd6 {might be worth another look as Black gets some compensation for the pawn}) 11. O-O-O Qa5 12. f4 $1 exf3 13. Nxf3 Qc7 {and now both Wells and Shredder like} 14. Rhf1 {, which leaves Black facing unpleasant threats, for example} Qd7 15. Bxe5 $1 {wins a pawn.}) 10. h4 ({One wonders what Black had in mind in the event of} 10. Qh5 {, for example} Be7 (10... f6 {transposes to Berg-Navara, above}) 11. Bxe5 O-O 12. Bg3 {does not leave enough for the pawn.}) 10... h5 11. f3 (11. Nxe4 Qb6 {gives Black some play for the pawn, but is it enough?}) 11... Be7 12. Nxe4 f5 13. Nc3 Bxh4 ({Both} 13... O-O {and}) (13... Qb6 {look like better tries.}) 14. Bxh4 Nxh4 15. Qd2 Qg5 16. Nh3 Qg3+ (16... Qxg2 17. Qxg2 Nxg2+ 18. Kf2 Nh4 19. Rag1 {would give White more than enough for the pawn.}) 17. Qf2 f4 (17... Nxg2+ 18. Kf1 Qxf2+ 19. Kxf2 Nh4 20. Rag1 {transposes to the previous note.}) 18. Nxf4 Nxf3+ ({This isn't good, but what else?} 18... exf4 19. Qxg3 fxg3 20. Rxh4 {also leaves White with a huge plus in the endgame.}) 19. gxf3 exf4 20. Qxg3 fxg3 21. Ne4 h4 22. Nxg3 O-O 23. Rxh4 Rxf3 24. Ne4 Bf5 25. Ke2 Bxe4 26. Rxe4 Raf8 27. Kd3 b5 28. Re7 R8f7 29. Re8+ Rf8 30. Rxf8+ Kxf8 31. a4 b4 32. Kc4 Rxe3 33. Kxc5 Re2 34. c4 Rxb2 35. Kc6 b3 36. d6 Rd2 37. d7 b2 38. Rf1+ Ke7 39. Re1+ Kf7 40. Kc7 {An efficiently played game by Radjabov.} 1-0

