{[%evp 0,107,80,53,65,72,68,46,52,41,49,49,67,22,58,58,56,41,37,23,23,24,28,28,32,42,22,20,25,13,37,15,8,12,18,12,34,27,20,20,25,16,9,7,11,65,84,-30,66,66,89,103,103,70,85,96,110,58,74,103,81,81,72,65,63,63,131,101,130,124,124,153,172,162,162,82,82,76,73,33,134,77,84,40,84,84,84,52,88,52,116,97,273,40,40,43,43,52,53,103,121,102,121,149,174,192,178,186,187,181] Judit Polgar is an ideal role model for any female chess player. The youngest of the famous sister trio, she was in my opinion clearly the most talented. However, her achievements, which made her the all-time strongest women at the chessboard ever, were certainly also due to her dedication and high work ethic. Playing almost exclusively in men's (understand open) events (the only exceptions I can think of are a few ladies olympiads way back in the eighties and nineties), she overcame Fischer's long-standing record to become a full GM at 15 and certainly didn't stop there. In the best period of her career she was in the top 10 and played in the World Championship tournament in 2005 (Round robin, 8 participants).  I will blow my own trumpet a bit, as I have a lifetime positive score against Judit. However, this was mainly due to the time, when we met - in the early nineties I was simply more experienced, than the up-and-coming teenager. However, she improved very fast! While she was still only 19 at the time the following game was played, she was already out of my league and way ahead...} 1. e4 c6 ({A small illustration how Judit worked on her openings in the pre-engine era:} 1... d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O e5 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. d5 Nd4 10. Nxe5 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 Bxe5 12. Bf4 Bxf4 (12... Bg7 $142 $5 {is definitely safer, Black's  remains better protected.}) 13. Rxf4 Nf5 14. Bxf5 Bxf5 15. Qd4 Bxc2 {leads to a position, in which White certainly has compensation for the pawn, but matters are not fully clear:} {Two years later my colleague Lubo Ftacnik decided to trust my opening choice and analysis, but had to face} 16. Re1 $5 {After} (16. Raf1 Qe7 (16... Bf5 $5) 17. Qd2 Bf5 18. g4 Bc8 19. d6 Qxd6 20. Rd4 $6 ({Allowing Black to escape. Nowadays the computer quickly shows that} 20. Nd5 $142 $1 {} f5 21. Rd4 $1 fxg4 22. Re1 $32 { gives White a powerful attack.}) 20... Qe5 21. Nd5 Be6 22. Nf6+ Kg7 23. b4 Bf5 24. g5 Rfe8 25. Rd1 c6 26. Nxe8+ Rxe8 27. Rd6 {Polgar,J (2550)-Stohl,I (2555) Brno 1991} h6 28. gxh6+ Kh7 $15 {J.Polgar}) 16... f5 $2 ({My original idea is flawed, here Black should have chosen between} 16... Re8 $5 $132 {and}) (16... Bf5 17. g4 Bd7 $13) 17. Qc4 Kh8 {he was hit with} 18. Rc1 $1 ({I analysed only} 18. Nb5 $6 Be4 19. Nxc7 Rc8 20. Qd4+ Qf6 $11) 18... Be4 19. Nxe4 fxe4 20. Qd4+ Kg8 21. Rxf8+ Qxf8 22. Rxc7 e3 23. Qxe3 Re8 24. Qf2 Qxf2+ 25. Kxf2 Rd8 26. Rxb7 Rxd5 27. Ke3 $18 {Polgar,J (2595)-Ftacnik,L (2535) Budapest zt 1993}) 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 {Kortschnoj was an ambitious player who preferred complex positions. His favourites were the French Defence and the Open Ruy Lopez, especially the former brought him success in his matches against Karpov. However, earlier in the same year he essayed the Caro-Cann with success - instead of cautiously neutralizing White's initial edge, he went for active counterplay:} (6. Nf3 Nf6 7. Ne5 Nbd7 8. Nxg6 $6 hxg6 9. Bd3 Qc7 10. Qf3 e6 11. Be3 c5 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Bxc5 Nxc5 14. Bb5+ Ke7 $1 15. O-O-O Rh4 $1 16. Be2 Rc8 17. Kb1 b5 18. Qe3 b4 $40 19. Rd2 $2 Nce4 20. Nxe4 Nxe4 21. Rd4 Qxc2+ 22. Ka1 Nd2 23. b3 Nxb3+ 24. axb3 Rc5 {0-1, Ashley,M (2460)-Kortschnoj,V (2635) San Francisco 1995/ This game just proves the well-known fact that only by advancing his h-pawn White can hope to fight for an advantage. Kortschnoj was even at a ripe age willing to go for complications and learn new things - leaving the king in the centre (e7,f8) in the Caro-Kann used to be a specialty of his old rival Karpov!}) 6... h6 7. h5 {SInce the 1960s this move was played almost automatically. In quite a few games the cramping effect of the h5 persisted well into an endgame, arising usually after both sides castled queenside and Black's liberating c5 lead to numerous exchanges. However, later Black's attention in the London Variation gradually turned to kingside castling (one of the first high-level examples was Game 8 in the Spassky-Karpov match in 1974; after all, in our game Black plays 0-0 as well) and White in turn realized he can sometimes well use the h5-square otherwise. An experience of my own to illustrate this:} (7. Nf3 Nf6 8. Ne5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bf4 Be7 (11... Qa5+ $142 $5 12. Bd2 Qa6) 12. O-O-O Nbd7 13. Qe2 O-O 14. Kb1 Qb6 15. Nxd7 $1 (15. Rd3 Rad8 16. c4 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Rxd3 18. Qxd3 Ng4 19. Qe2 Nxf2 {Shabalov,A (2645)-Yermolinsky,A (2625) Los Angeles 1998} 20. Be3 Nxh1 21. Bxb6 Nxg3 $44) 15... Nxd7 16. Rd3 Qb5 17. Rc1 Rfe8 18. c4 Qa5 19. Bd2 Qd8 20. Nh5 Nf6 $142 $1 {} (20... Bxh4 $2 21. Rh1 Nf8 22. Nxg7 Kxg7 23. Qh5 Ng6 24. Qxh6+ Kg8 25. Rg3 $1 Bxg3 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Bh6+ Ke7 28. Bg5+ Kf8 29. Bxd8 Raxd8 30. fxg3 $18 {Almasi,Z (2665)-Stohl,I (2573) Baden 1999}) 21. Nxg7 $5 (21. Bxh6 gxh6 (21... Nxh5 $2 22. Qxh5 g6 23. Qg4 Bxh4 24. Rh1 Bf6 25. Qh3 $18) 22. Rg3+ Kh8 $1 (22... Kf8 23. Qe3 Ng8 24. Qe5 (24. Rxg8+ Kxg8 25. Qe5 $1 Bf8 26. Rc3 $18) 24... Nf6 25. Qf4 Ng8 26. Rg7 Bf6 (26... f5 27. Qg3 $18) 27. Rxg8+ $18) (22... Kh7 23. Rg7+ Kh8 24. Qe3 Ng8 25. Qe4 f5 26. Qxe6 Nf6 27. Qf7 Qxd4 28. Nxf6 $18) 23. Qe3 Bf8 $1 24. Qe5 Bd6 (24... Be7 $2 25. Qf4) 25. Qe3 (25. Qxf6+ $2 Qxf6 26. Nxf6 Bxg3 27. Nxe8 Bf4 $19) 25... Bf8 26. Qe5 $11) 21... Kxg7 22. g4 $44 {[%cal Rg4g5] is a dangerous positional sacrifice, White will open up the  with g5 - even here he profits from not having the  on h5.}) 7... Bh7 8. Nf3 e6 {Transpostions here are quite numerous and keeping the  on b8 for a while gives Black an extra option, mentioned in the following note.} ({However, the most common move is still} 8... Nd7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 (10... Qc7 {is the older treatment of the London, Black prevents f4 and quickly follows up with 0-0-0. However, currently this development of the bishop is not considered dangerous and as has already been indicated, Black usually has different intentions with his king.}) {Now} 11. Bf4 (11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Kb1 O-O 14. Ne4 c5 15. Be3 Nxe4 16. Qxe4 Nf6 17. Qxb7 Nd5 18. Qa6 Rb8 19. Bd2 cxd4 20. Nxd4 Bf6 21. Nb3 Qc7 22. Rhe1 Rfc8 23. Rc1 Nb6 24. Re4 Nc4 $40 {Duda,J (2757)-Firouzja,A (2728) Stavanger 2020}) 11... Qa5+ 12. Bd2 Bb4 13. c3 Be7 14. c4 Qc7 15. O-O-O Ngf6 16. Kb1 O-O $132 {leads to a currently topical opening position.}) 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nd7 ({An interesting alternative is} 10... Nf6 11. Bd2 {in connection with the immediate central action} c5 $5 (11... Be7 12. O-O-O O-O {or}) (11... Nbd7 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Kb1 O-O {is the more restrained approach, delaying c5 for a while. Originally 0-0 was considered very risky for Black, as his  is already weakened by h6, but practical experience has shown his counterplay after opening up the centre can't be underestimated. One of the reasons is White's attack is slowed down by his knights getting in the way of a possible pawn storm.}) 12. O-O-O Nc6 13. Bc3 Qd5 $1 (13... Be7 14. Ne4 $14) (13... Nd5 14. dxc5 $16) 14. dxc5 Qxd3 15. cxd3 Bxc5 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. d4 Bb6 $11 18. d5 Ne7 19. dxe6 fxe6 20. Ne4 Nd5 21. Nh4 O-O-O 22. g4 Rhf8 23. Kb1 f5 24. gxf5 exf5 $11 {Andreikin,D (2725)-Howell,D (2693) Astana 2019}) 11. Bf4 {Judit tries out the longer diagonal...} (11. Bd2 Ngf6 {leads to the lines we have mentioned above.} (11... Qc7 {is the game position with one move less.})) 11... Qa5+ 12. Bd2 {...but not for long.} (12. c3 $143 Ngf6 {causes White problems with the placement of his king:} 13. a4 Nd5 14. Bd2 Qc7 15. a5 (15. O-O Bd6 16. Ne4 N5f6 $1 $15 {[%csl Rh5] pinpoints the weakness on h5.}) 15... Bd6 16. Ne4 N5f6 17. Nxd6+ Qxd6 18. Rh4 c5 19. Kf1 O-O 20. Bf4 Qc6 21. Ne5 Nxe5 22. Bxe5 Nd7 $11 {Tseshkovsky,V (2520)-Douven,R (2445) Almaty 1989 Black had no problems whatsoever in this game.}) 12... Qc7 ({We have already mentioned the modern treatment, which started appearing only in the 21-st century:} 12... Bb4 $5 13. c3 (13. Ne4 Ngf6 14. Nd6+ Ke7 15. Nxb7 Bxd2+ 16. Nxd2 Qb4 17. Qb3 Qxd4 18. O-O-O Rab8 19. Qa3+ c5 20. Na5 Rhc8 $132) 13... Be7 14. c4 Qc7 15. O-O-O (15. d5 cxd5 16. cxd5 Qd6 17. Qb3 Ngf6 $1 18. dxe6 Qxe6+ 19. Qxe6 fxe6 20. O-O Kf7 $11) 15... Ngf6 16. Kb1 O-O {This position is considered satisfactory for Black, just one example to prove the point:} 17. Rhe1 a5 (17... Rad8 $132) 18. Bc1 Rfd8 19. Qc2 a4 20. Ne5 $6 Nxe5 21. dxe5 Rxd1 22. Qxd1 Nd7 23. f4 Nc5 24. Ne4 Rd8 25. Qc2 Nxe4 26. Rxe4 Qb6 $15 {Leko,P (2753)-Ivanchuk,V (2751) Morelia/Linares 2008 For more details about this game see the notes in CBM 123 by Ftacnik.}) 13. O-O-O Ngf6 14. Ne4 {White's main continuation.} ({Earlier White used to prefer} 14. Qe2 {, then Black came up with} Bd6 $5 (14... O-O-O 15. Ne5 Nb6 16. Ba5 $14) 15. Nf5 Bf4 16. Bxf4 (16. Nxg7+ Kf8 17. Nxe6+ fxe6 18. Qxe6 Re8 {leads to a position where the extra piece is worth more than 3 pawns -} 19. Qf5 Bxd2+ 20. Rxd2 Qd6 $15) 16... Qxf4+ 17. Ne3 {and now:} {More circumspect is following suit with} O-O-O {This leads to an old-style London Caro-Kann position; the exchange of the dark-squared bishops eases Black's defence, but White still has more space and a slight pull:} (17... O-O 18. g3 Qc7 19. g4 c5 20. dxc5 Ne4 21. g5 $1 Ndxc5 (21... hxg5 {Galkin,A (2540)-Khlian,E (2455) Russia 1998} 22. h6 g6 23. Ng4 Qf4+ 24. Qe3 Qxe3+ 25. fxe3 Ndxc5 26. b4 Na4 27. Rd7 $16) 22. gxh6 gxh6 23. Nc4 (23. Rh4 $5 $14 {/}) 23... Qf4+ 24. Ncd2 Nxd2 25. Nxd2 Rfd8 26. Kb1 Rd4 27. Rhg1+ Kf8 $11 {/, Peetoom,F (2024)-Panman,H (2392) email 2008}) 18. Rh4 Qc7 19. Nc4 Rhe8 (19... c5 20. Nce5 $6 (20. Nfe5 Rhf8 21. dxc5 Nxc5 22. b4 $1 $16) 20... Nxe5 21. Nxe5 $6 (21. dxe5 $11) 21... Rd5 22. c4 cxd4 23. f4 b5 24. b3 bxc4 25. bxc4 Nd7 26. Nxf7 Rf8 $17 {Asomaeki,P (2527)-Jorgensen,E (2459) email 2002}) 20. Nfe5 Nb6 21. Nxb6+ axb6 22. Rd3 Re7 23. Rf3 b5 24. Rg3 Ne8 $14 {/=, Timman,J (2610)-Miles,A (2565) London 1984}) 14... Bd6 $5 {The text has been introduced into practice by Seirawan in 1983, but a dozen years later he abandoned his invention and switched to 4...d7 - the reasons will be illustrated below.} ({Korchnoi is no fan of passive positions and the more common} 14... O-O-O 15. g3 Nxe4 (15... Nc5 16. Nxc5 Bxc5 17. c4 Bd6 18. Bc3 $14 {is similar.}) 16. Qxe4 Nf6 17. Qe2 Bd6 18. c4 c5 19. Bc3 cxd4 20. Nxd4 a6 {doesn't attract him. White still preserves a small edge after} 21. Kb1 $14 ({or} 21. Nf3 $14 {and although Black has reasonable chances to withstand the pressure and gradually equalize, the maximum of his efforts is a draw.})) 15. Kb1 {A useful semi-waiting move.} ({The idea behind 14...d6!? is clearly seen if White tries to transpose into the aforementioned lines with} 15. g3 {and now:} {Here Black has} b5 $5 ({One of Judit's subsequent games went} 15... Nxe4 16. Qxe4 O-O-O 17. Qe2 Rhe8 18. Kb1 $14 {and only now} b5 $2 (18... Nf6 $142) 19. c4 bxc4 20. Qxc4 {[%csl Ra7,Rc6,Rc8]} Nb6 21. Qc2 Nd5 22. Rc1 Kb7 23. Ne5 $1 Bxe5 24. dxe5 Rc8 25. Rhe1 Qb6 26. Be3 $1 $16 {Polgar,J (2658)-Adianto,U (2584) Bali 2000 /Just as in our game, the strong d5 doesn't sufficiently compensate for White's attacking chances against the exposed . For more details see the notes to this game in CBM 077 by Lukacs.}) {, demonstrating his flexibility - he doesn't have to play 0-0-0 at all! The d6 is needed to cover the important squares e5 and f4, and as Black intends to attack White's king, he will probably end up castling kingside. His light-square strategy works even tactically, as} 16. Ba5 (16. Kb1 a5 $132 {leads to a double-edged position, Black's counterattack can't be underestimated.}) 16... Qxa5 17. Nxd6+ Ke7 18. Nxf7 Kxf7 19. Ne5+ Kg8 20. Qg6 Qxa2 {gives White only a perpetual after} 21. Qf7+ Kh7 22. Qg6+ Kg8 23. Qf7+ {½, Spangenberg,H (2505)-Seirawan,Y (2595) Buenos Aires 1993}) ({Another way to fight for the advantage is the principled} 15. Nxd6+ Qxd6 16. Qe2 (16. c4 b5 $1 17. cxb5 cxb5 18. Qxb5 O-O $44) 16... b5 17. Ne5 $5 (17. Kb1 Qd5 18. Rh4 a5 19. Ne5 b4 20. f4 a4 21. Be1 $13 {½, Wolff,P (2590)-Seirawan,Y (2605) Durango 1992/ Black has achieved his aim here - he has light-square control and the position is complex and sharp.}) 17... Qd5 18. Kb1 a5 $5 (18... Qe4 19. Qxe4 Nxe4 20. Be3 Rc8 21. g4 Nef6 22. f3 Nd5 23. Bc1 f6 24. Ng6 Rg8 25. f4 $14 {, Adianto,U (2385)-Seirawan,Y (2600) Jakarta 1983}) (18... Qxg2 $6 19. Rde1 $1 {[%cal Re5f7]} (19. Rhg1 Qe4 20. Qxe4 Nxe4 21. Be3 $14) 19... Qd5 20. Rhg1 $40 {[%csl Re8]}) 19. Rh3 Qxg2 (19... Ne4 20. Bf4 $36) (19... b4 $5) 20. Rg3 Qe4 21. Qxe4 $6 (21. Re3 $1 $44) 21... Nxe4 22. Rxg7 Nxe5 23. dxe5 {Uskova,F (2310)-Graf,A (2580) Omsk 1996} Nxd2+ 24. Rxd2 Ke7 $11 {[%cal Ya8d8,Yd8g8]}) 15... Nxe4 ({The difference between 15.g3 and Polgar's move becomes obvious after} 15... b5 $2 16. Ba5 $1 Qb8 (16... Qxa5 17. Nxd6+ Ke7 18. Ne5 Kxd6 19. Qg3 $1 $18 {[%cal Re5c4,Re5g6] is resignable for Black -} Nxe5 20. dxe5+ Ke7 21. exf6+ Kxf6 22. Qf4+ Ke7 23. Qd6+ Kf6 24. Rd3 g5 25. hxg6 Kxg6 26. Rg3+ {1-0, Przybyla,W-Huchla,A corr 1991}) 17. Ne5 $1 $36 {} Nxe5 18. Nxf6+ gxf6 19. dxe5 Bxe5 20. Qd7+ Kf8 21. Qxc6 Kg7 22. Rd7 $16) (15... O-O-O 16. Nxd6+ Qxd6 17. Qe2 $14 {/ is not ideal either.}) ({Seirawan's original idea was} 15... Bf4 $5 {, until Khalifman used his own recommendation against him:} 16. Nxf6+ Nxf6 17. Bxf4 Qxf4 18. Qa3 $1 Qc7 (18... Ne4 19. Rh4 Qf5 20. Ne5 $40 {[%csl Re8]}) 19. Ne5 Qe7 20. Qe3 {More circumspect is} O-O-O $142 (20... O-O $6 21. g4 $40 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Rg4g5]}) (20... Nd7 21. Nd3 (21. f4 O-O-O 22. c3 Nxe5 23. fxe5 Qg5 24. Qf2 f6 25. Rde1 Rhf8 26. Qc2 Rd5 $132 {Baklan,V (2599)-Nikolaidis,I (2512) Ano Liosia 2001}) 21... Qg5 22. f4 Qxg2 23. Rhg1 Qd5 24. Ne5 Rg8 25. c4 Qd6 26. d5 $1 $40 cxd5 27. Nxd7 Qxd7 28. cxd5 Rd8 29. f5 $1 $18 {[%csl Re8] Khalifman,A (2655)-Seirawan,Y (2625) Amsterdam 1995/ For more details about this game see the notes in CBM 049 by Khalifman himself.}) 21. c4 (21. f4 $5) 21... Nd7 22. Nf3 $14 {, but it leads to the typical slightly worse position he has been trying to avoid with 14...d6!? in first place.}) 16. Qxe4 Nf6 {Kicking the centralized e4 is natural.} (16... b5 $6 17. Qg4 Kf8 {seemed to risky even to Kortschnoj. The engine lashes out with} 18. Ne5 $1 Nxe5 19. dxe5 Bxe5 20. Qe4 $32 {}) 17. Qe2 b5 $2 {However, here Viktor couldn't resist striving for complications. This was the last round of a category 16 tournament and after brilliantly scoring 6,5/8, Kortschnoj secured 1st place with a round to spare. One can understand and even sympathize with his optimism, but the text-move is objectively wrong and leads to great trouble.} ({Delayed castling is one of the more complicated problems of modern chess strategy. Here Black, leaving the king in the centre for the time being, seeks in exchange long-term strategical advantages, as light-square control and building up a dominant outpost on d5. All this could be viable under one condition - that his king would remain safe. Judit will convincingly show this is not the case here. More prudent was the less ambitious} 17... O-O-O $142 18. c4 Rhe8 19. Bc3 c5 {This position actually arose by transposition in Kortschnoj's earlier game:} 20. Ne5 cxd4 21. Bxd4 Bxe5 22. Bxe5 Qc6 (22... Qc5 23. f4 Rxd1+ 24. Rxd1 Rd8 25. Rc1 Qa5 26. Rc2 Qc5 27. a3 Qc6 28. Ka2 Ne8 29. g4 {Venkatesh,M (2450)-Iljin,A (2522) Cochin 2004} f6 30. Bc3 Nc7 31. b3 $14 {Venkatesh}) 23. g4 (23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. Qe3 Rxd1+ (24... Qxg2 $6 25. Rdg1 Qc6 26. Qxh6 $16 {[%csl Gh5]}) 25. Rxd1 Rd8 $1 $132 26. Rxd8+ Kxd8 27. Qxh6 Qe4+ $11) 23... Rxd1+ 24. Rxd1 Rd8 25. Rxd8+ Kxd8 26. Bxf6+ (26. f3 Ke7 $14) 26... gxf6 27. f3 Ke8 $11 {Morovic Fernandez,I (2565)-Kortschnoj,V (2585) Mesa 1992}) 18. Ne5 $1 Bxe5 {Such a knight can't be suffered for long. The question is where should Black's king go?} (18... O-O-O 19. c4 $16 {or}) (18... O-O 19. g4 $16 {both give White's attack a head start.}) ({Thematic is} 18... Nd5 {, but after} 19. Rh4 ({or} 19. Rh3 $5 {the rook threatens to attack the g7 pawn and an exchange on e5 is sooner or later forced.}) (19. f4 {[%cal Yf4f5,Ye2g4] is also unpleasant, after} Bxe5 20. fxe5 $16 {Black's problems with  safety continue.})) 19. dxe5 Nd5 {The knight naturally belongs in the centre.} ({After} 19... Nd7 {the sortie} 20. Qg4 {is very disruptive:} Kf8 (20... Nxe5 21. Qxg7 $18 {or}) (20... Qxe5 21. Bc3 Qg5 22. Qd4 $18 {[%cal Rd4g7,Rd4d7] are out of question.}) 21. f4 $40 {with excellent attacking chances, as Black's pieces lack coordination.}) 20. Rh4 $1 $146 {[%mdl 8] A refined multipurpose move, which stops b4 and introduces the idea g4. We already know c4 and g4 are the right reactions to 0-0-0 and 0-0 respectively, but impatience is out of place:} (20. c4 $2 bxc4 21. Qxc4 O-O $15 {[%cal Gb8b1] just opens a file against White's own king.}) ({After} 20. g4 b4 $1 $13 {[%cal Ya7a5,Yc7b6,Yb6b5] the is the bastion of Black's defences, the possible follow-up can be a5, b6-b5. Black can tuck his king away with d7-d7, or even 0-0-0.} (20... Rb8 21. g5 hxg5 22. Bxg5 b4 23. f4 Qa5 24. Qf3 Qc7 25. f5 exf5 26. Qxf5 Qc8 27. e6 $1 O-O {Byrne,R (2530)-Shamkovich,L (2515) Greenville 1980} 28. h6 Qxe6 29. Qf2 $1 Nc3+ (29... g6 30. Rxd5 $18) 30. bxc3 bxc3+ 31. Ka1 Rb2 32. Qxa7 $18)) (20. f4 {[%cal Ye2g4] allows Black to again consider} b4 $5 (20... O-O-O 21. c4 bxc4 22. Qxc4 $14 {/}) {} 21. Qg4 O-O) ({Roughly equivalent is} 20. Rh3 $5 {[%cal Yh3g3] with similar ideas:} O-O 21. g4 Ne7 22. Rd3 Rfd8 23. Rd6 Nd5 24. g5 hxg5 25. Rxd8+ Qxd8 26. Rg1 Nf4 27. Bxf4 gxf4 28. Qg4 Qf8 29. Qxf4 Kh8 30. Rd1 Rd8 31. Rd6 $16 {/+-, Khusnutdinov,R (2458)-Frolov,I (2362) Cheliabinsk 2017}) 20... O-O (20... O-O-O 21. c4 $40 {is considerably weaker, but White is ready also for the text-move and unleashes a swift and deadly attack.}) 21. g4 {[%mdl 128]} ({White can't break through with piece play alone after} 21. Rg4 $6 Kh8) 21... Qe7 $8 ({More or less forced, for example after} 21... a5 22. g5 $40 {/+- White's attack is fast and free of charge.}) 22. g5 $1 {Opening up the  gives White's play a strong impulse.} hxg5 23. Rg4 f6 ({Again the best defence, after} 23... Nf4 24. Bxf4 gxf4 25. Rdg1 f5 26. Rg6 $18 {[%cal Rh5h6] h6 the attack breaks through.}) 24. exf6 {The natural and very human choice - White opens up the position as soon as possible, gaining access the Black's .} ({Computer recommendations as} 24. Rh1 Rf7 25. h6 $16) ({or} 24. f4 fxe5 25. Rxg5 $16 {are also strong, but there is no need to complicate the matters.}) 24... gxf6 ({After} 24... Qxf6 25. Rxg5 {[%csl Rc6,Re6,Rg7,Rg8] Black has numerous weaknesses and his position will soon fall apart:} Kh8 (25... Qxf2 26. Qxe6+ $18 {} Qf7 27. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 28. Qh6+ Kg8 29. Rg1+) 26. h6 $5 (26. Qg4 Rg8 27. Rg6 Qf5 28. Qg2 Ne7 29. Bc3 $1 $18) (26. Rdg1 Qxf2 27. Qe5 Qf6 28. Qg3 Qf3 29. h6 g6 30. Qe5+ Qf6 31. Qe4 $18) 26... g6 27. Rxd5 cxd5 28. Bc3 $18) 25. f4 e5 (25... Kh8 $2 {doesn't improve the king's position, after} 26. fxg5 f5 27. Rgg1 e5 28. g6 $18 {Black is a few tempi down compared with the lines below.}) 26. fxe5 $6 {The strategic choice (taking towards the centre and opening the position for the d2) is somewhat inconsistent and squanders a part of White's advantage. Stronger was the straightforward attacking solution} (26. fxg5 $1 f5 {(there is nothing else)} 27. Rgg1 {Although the g8 is sheltered by White's connected passed pawns, it's like living in the flood zone or on a volcano:} {More natural is} e4 (27... f4 28. g6 (28. Qe4 Qe6 29. g6 Qf5 {wastes time and is less convincing}) 28... f3 29. Qe4 f2 30. Rh1 $18 {[%cal Rh5h6] wins for White, as Black's pawns are immobilized.}) {Polgar prefers the direct} 28. g6 (28. c4 {is rather artificial, but good enough:} bxc4 29. Qxc4 e3 30. Qxc6 Qe4+ 31. Ka1 f4 {somewhat complicates matters, Black's pawns have become dangerous. The only way to win is} (31... exd2 32. h6 $1 (32. Qg6+ Kh8 33. Qh6+ Kg8 34. g6 Nf6 35. g7 Rfc8 36. Qxf6 Qe3 $11) 32... Qe5 33. Rxd2 $18) 32. h6 $1 Rac8 (32... exd2 33. Qb7 Qe5 34. g6 Ne7 35. Qb3+ Qd5 36. Qc3 $18) 33. h7+ Kxh7 (33... Kh8 34. g6 $1 Qe5 35. Qxd5 $1 Qxd5 36. Bc3+ Rxc3 37. g7+ $18) 34. Qh6+ Kg8 35. g6 Qe7 (35... Nf6 36. Rh1) 36. g7 Rfd8 37. Qh8+ Kf7 38. Qh7 $18) 28... f4 29. h6 {and she is right, as all the natural moves lose:} Rf6 (29... Rf5 30. Qg4 Qe6 31. Bxf4 $1 Nxf4 32. Rd6 Qxd6 33. Qxf5 Rf8 34. g7 $1 Rxf5 35. h7+ {leads to mate.}) (29... e3 30. g7 Rf6 31. h7+ $1 Kxh7 32. Qg4 Kg8 33. Qh4 Qe8 {and now the most convincing is} 34. Bb4 $1 (34. Qh8+ Kf7 35. g8=Q+ Qxg8 36. Rxg8 Rxg8 37. Qh5+ Rgg6 38. Bc3 $18) 34... Nxb4 35. Rd7 $1 Qxd7 36. Qh8+ Kf7 37. Qxa8 $18 {[%cal Rg7g8]}) (29... f3 30. Qh2 $19 {[%cal Rg6g7,Rh6h7] loses at once, as now both g7 an h7 are threats.}) 30. Qh5 e3 31. Be1 Rd6 (31... f3 $2 32. Rxd5 f2 33. Rf1 fxe1=Q+ 34. Rxe1 {is insufficient.}) 32. Rxd5 $1 cxd5 33. Bb4 e2 (33... f3 34. Bxd6 Qxd6 35. g7) 34. h7+ Kg7 35. Rh1 Kh8 36. g7+ $1 {and White's pawns (and attack) come in first.}) 26... Kh8 $1 {The best, the king leaves the potentially open file and hides behind the h5-pawn.} (26... Rae8 27. Qd3 $1 Qh7 (27... Qxe5 28. Qg6+ Kh8 29. Qh6+ Kg8 30. Bxg5 {loses immediately.}) 28. Qxh7+ (28. exf6 Qxd3 29. Rxg5+ $18) 28... Kxh7 29. exf6 Ne3 ({Similar is} 29... Nxf6 30. Rxg5 Ne4 31. Rgg1) 30. Bxe3 Rxe3 31. Rxg5 Rxf6 32. Rdg1 $16 {/+- promises Black some counterplay, but not sufficient compensation, in the endgame he has to struggle desperately for a draw. His task is moreover complicated by the fact his king is too far from the queenside action.}) (26... Rfe8 27. Qd3 Qxe5 28. Qg6+ Kf8 (28... Kh8 $2 29. Bxg5) 29. h6 $18 {is not too appealing either.}) (26... Qxe5 $2 27. Rxg5+ $18 {loses immediately}) 27. Re1 (27. Qd3 $2 Qxe5 28. Qg6 Qe7 {leads nowhere.}) 27... Rae8 28. Qd1 (28. e6 $6 Qd6 $14 {/ leads to complex position, in which Black's king is protected by pawns, both White's and his own.}) ({Polgar understandably wants to open up the kingside. A plausible alternative was} 28. Qg2 $5 {, but here exf6 still requires preparation.}) 28... Qd7 29. Rgg1 (29. exf6 $4 Rxe1 30. Bxe1 Nc3+ $19 {would be a costly oversight.}) 29... Rxe5 30. Rxe5 fxe5 31. Bxg5 (31. Rxg5 {weakens the back rank and gives Black more counterplay, than he deserves:} e4 (31... Nf4 32. Rxe5 Rd8 33. Qe1 Qxd2 34. Re8+ $18) (31... Rf5 32. Rg6 Nf4 33. Rh6+ Kg7 34. Qg4+ Kxh6 35. Qg6#) 32. Rg6 Rf6 {and now the best way to keep the momentum is still} 33. Rg1 $5 $16) 31... Qf5 $6 {Now the position has calmed down and we can assess the result of the complications. Black has managed to parry the direct threats and simplify the position, but his vulnerable king still requires full attention. White has the better minor piece and a clear initiative and advantage. Kortschnoj would welcome some further exchanges mainly to safeguard his king, but after the inaccurate text-move he will be able to get the rooks off the board only at the cost of a pawn.} ({Therefore somewhat better was} 31... Rg8 $142 $5 {[%cal Yd7e6,Ye6f5] Black wants to play either Qf5 or Qe6, nevertheless after} 32. Bc1 $5 {his position remains difficult:} (32. Qf3 $143 Qe6 33. Qe4 Nf4 {is less convincing}) {Black needs his knight in the centre, but after} 32... Rxg1 (32... Nf6 {and now:} 33. Qxd7 $5 (33. Rxg8+ Kxg8 34. Qf3 $1 (34. Qxd7 $6 Nxd7 {is premature, Black's king is closer to the centre and with the knight on d5 White will find it difficult to make progress, e.g.} 35. c4 (35. Be3 Nf6 36. h6 Ng4 37. Bxa7 Nxh6 $11 {is drawish as well.}) 35... bxc4 36. Kc2 Nf6 37. Kc3 Nxh5 38. Kxc4 Kf7 $11 {and Black can't lose.}) 34... Qe6 35. b3 $16) 33... Nxd7 34. Rd1 $16 {is also strong, the rook enters via d6 and the  is without any risk and clearly in White's favour.}) (32... Rg2 33. Qe1 {just postpones the inevitable  swap.}) 33. Qxg1 $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rc6,Re5,Gh5,Rh8] White's winning chances remain very real, as his mobile queen can attack Black's weak pawns (a7,c6,e5) and simultaneously help the h-pawn.}) 32. Bc1 {Although the first wave of the attack didn't break through, Polgar keeps her cool, showing patience and technique to convert her advantage - a clear sign she was fast maturing as a top-level player.} Qe6 (32... Rg8 33. Rxg8+ Kxg8 34. Qg1+ {is no better, as after} Kh8 35. Qxa7 (35. h6 Qf7 36. Qg4 $18 {should win as well}) 35... Qxh5 36. Qa8+ Kg7 37. Qxc6 e4 38. Qe6 $18 {[%csl Rg7] Black's e-pawn won't get far due to his weak king.}) (32... Nf4 33. Qd6 Qf6 34. Qc5 $18 {allows White to activate his queen.}) 33. Qe1 Rg8 ({Loses material, but} 33... e4 34. b3 $1 {opens the important attacking diagonal for White, after} Rg8 35. Bb2+ Kh7 36. Rxg8 Kxg8 (36... Qxg8 37. Qxe4+) 37. Qg3+ {[%csl Gh5]} Kf7 38. Qg7+ Ke8 39. h6 $18) 34. Rxg8+ Kxg8 35. Qg1+ Kh8 36. Qxa7 {[%mdl 4096] White is a pawn up and the final phase of the game begins. By a combination of mating and simplification threats Polgar will gradually neutralize Black's counterplay and win the endgame. Capablanca's maxim about + being superior to + is not valid here, as White's b1 is fully safe, contrary to the still exposed h8.} e4 37. h6 Qe5 (37... Qf6 $143 38. Qg1 {[%csl Ge3][%cal Yg1g5] g5 is weaker, as Black doesn't control e3.}) 38. Qa8+ Kh7 39. Qb7+ Kh8 40. Qa8+ {Repetition is a useful method of reaching the time-control.} (40. Qxc6 $2 e3 {gives Black too much play:} 41. a3 (41. Qg6 e2 42. Qg7+ (42. h7 $2 Nf6) 42... Qxg7 43. hxg7+ Kxg7 44. Bd2 Ne3 45. b3 Kf7 46. Kb2 (46. a4 bxa4 47. bxa4 Nxc2 $1 48. Kxc2 Ke6 $11 {and Black's king reaches a8 in time.}) 46... Ng2 (46... Ke6 $5 47. Kc3 Ng2 48. Kb4 e1=Q 49. Bxe1 Nxe1 50. c3 Nd3+ 51. Kxb5 Nc1 $11 {is also possible.}) 47. Kc3 e1=Q 48. Bxe1 Nxe1 $11 {and with the king in the square of the a-pawn Black draws.}) 41... e2 42. Bd2 e1=Q+ 43. Bxe1 Qxe1+ 44. Ka2 Qe5 $11 {keeps more tension in the game, but even here White's winning chances are minimal.}) 40... Kh7 41. Qa7+ Kh8 (41... Kg6 $6 42. Qg1+ {saves White some moves.}) 42. Qc5 $1 {Stops the e-pawn in it's tracks.} (42. Be3 $2 Nxe3 43. Qxe3 Kh7 $14 {would still require superb technique from White.}) 42... Kh7 (42... e3 $2 43. Bxe3 $1 Qxe3 44. Qf8+ $18) ({After} 42... Qf6 {Black doesn't control e3 anymore and} 43. a3 $18 {or} (43. Qg1 $5 $18 {[%cal Yg1g5] g5 should win for White.})) 43. Be3 Qf6 44. Qa7+ Kg6 $8 {Now clearly forced,} (44... Kg8 $2 45. h7+ Kh8 46. Bd4 $18) 45. Bc1 $1 $18 {[%csl Rg6,Gh6] White has made significant progress. The King is misplaced on g6 and the h-pawn has become truly dangerous.} Qe5 ({As now after} 45... e3 $2 46. Bxe3 {White simply interpolates on c1, Black has only one last try left.}) 46. Qg1+ Kh7 47. Qg5 {This clinches the point. Black can't avoid the exchange of queens and with a knight against a long-legged bishop and passed pawns potentially on both flanks his material deficit will be decisive.} Qxg5 (47... Qc7 48. Qf5+ Kh8 49. Qxe4 $18) 48. Bxg5 c5 ({After other moves White also centralizes his king, e.g.,} 48... e3 49. Kc1 Kg6 50. Kd1 (50. a3 $5) 50... e2+ 51. Ke1 $1 ({The simplest, but} 51. Kxe2 Kxg5 52. h7 Nf4+ 53. Ke3 Ng6 54. Kd4 $18 {also wins.}) 51... Nb4 52. Bd2 Nxc2+ 53. Kxe2 $18) 49. Kc1 c4 50. a3 Kg6 (50... e3 51. Kd1 Kg6 52. b3 $18 {is hopeless as well, the a-pawn will divert the knight.}) 51. Kd2 Kh7 52. b3 cxb3 53. cxb3 Kg6 54. Be3 ({White will advance his a-pawn, which allows him to activate his king after} 54. Be3 Kh7 55. a4 bxa4 56. bxa4 Kg6 57. a5 Nc7 58. Kc3 $18) 1-0

