1. e4 {Blatny,P} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 {Adams was one of the main exponents of the Neo-Arkhangelsk with} (5... b5 6. Bb3 Bc5 $5 {However this line is under pressure nowadays, so he returned to the more classical continuations.}) 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 {Judit is not afraid of the Marshall!} (8. a4 {-C88}) ({Of the various anti-gambit attempts let us mention especially} 8. h3 $5 {as it was played by Kasparov himself. However, after} Bb7 9. d3 d6 10. a3 Na5 11. Ba2 c5 12. Nc3 Nc6 13. Bg5 Qd7 14. Nh2 Ne8 15. Bd2 Nc7 16. Nf1 Kh8 17. Ng3 Nd4 18. Nce2 Nde6 19. b4 $6 d5 20. bxc5 Bxc5 21. Bb4 Rfe8 $15 {Kasparov,G-Adams,M/Linares/1999/ it was only White, who had problems.}) 8... d5 $1 {This move appeared for the first time in 1918 in the famous Capablanca-Marshall game and is very much alive even nowadays. (As we saw above, even Garri avoids it.)} 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 {Marshall's former idea} (11... Nf6 12. d4 Bd6 13. Re1 Ng4 14. h3 Qh4 15. Qf3 Nxf2 16. Bd2 $1 $16 {hasn't survived the test of time, but it has been successfully replaced by the positional continuation in the text.} (16. Re2 $5 Bg4 17. hxg4 Bh2+ 18. Kf1 Bg3 19. Rxf2 Qh1+ 20. Ke2 Bxf2 21. Bd2 Bh4 22. Qh3 Rae8+ 23. Kd3 Qf1+ 24. Kc2 Bf2 25. Qf3 Qg1 26. Bd5 c5 27. dxc5 Bxc5 28. b4 Bd6 29. a4 a5 30. axb5 axb4 31. Ra6 bxc3 32. Nxc3 Bb4 33. b6 Bxc3 34. Bxc3 h6 35. b7 Re3 36. Bxf7+ {1-0, Capablanca,J-Marshall,F/New York/1918/})) 12. d3 {Nowadays the most fashionable.} ({The declining popularity of} 12. d4 {has been caused amongst others by the fact, that after} Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 15. Be3 Bg4 16. Qd3 Rae8 17. Nd2 Re6 18. a4 Qh5 $1 19. axb5 axb5 {it's not clear how White can get any advantage.} 20. Ne4 (20. Nf1 Bf5 $5 21. Qd2 Rfe8 22. Bd1 Qg6 23. Bf3 h5 24. Qd1 h4 25. Bd2 Rxe1 26. Bxe1 Bc2 27. Qc1 Nf4 $44 {Kruppa,Y-Ginsburg,G/Simferopol/1990/}) 20... Bf5 $1 21. Bd2 Rxe4 22. Rxe4 Nf6 23. f3 Qg6 $1 {[%cal Rf6e4,Rd6g3] e4,g3} 24. Qf1 Nxe4 25. fxe4 Bxe4 26. Bf4 Bd3 27. Qf2 Bxf4 28. Qxf4 h6 29. Qf2 Re8 30. Re1 Rxe1+ 31. Qxe1 Be4 32. Qf2 $11 {½,Svidler,P-Kamsky,G/Groningen/1995/}) 12... Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 (13... Bf5 $143 {allows White to return the pawn and play for a win without any risk after} 14. Qf3 Qd7 (14... Re8 15. Rxe8+ Qxe8 16. Nd2 Qe1+ 17. Nf1 Bg6 18. g3 Re8 19. Bd1 Qe6 20. Bd2 Qh3 21. a4 b4 {Dolmatov,S-Kamsky,G/Dortmund/1993/} 22. c4 $14) 15. Bxd5 cxd5 16. Bf4 $5 Bxf4 17. Qxf4 Bxd3 18. Nd2 Rfe8 19. h3 h6 20. Qd4 Bg6 21. Nb3 Qc6 22. Nc5 $36 {Anand,V-Adams,M/Paris Immopar/1992/}) 14. g3 Qh3 15. Re4 {White prevents g4 and attempts to chase away Black's annoying . One of the benefits of d3 is that now 15...g5? is impossible.} Qf5 {Possibly the most active move.} ({Adams has had some bad experience with} 15... Nf6 16. Rh4 Qf5 17. Nd2 $1 (17. Bc2 Qg6 18. Nd2 Bg4 19. Nf3 Rfe8 20. Kg2 Nd5 21. d4 Bf5 22. Bxf5 Qxf5 $44 {[%csl Rh4] צh4} 23. Bd2 Nf6 24. Bf4 Bf8 25. Bd2 Bd6 26. Bf4 Bf8 27. Bd2 Bd6 {½,Leko,P-Adams,M/Madrid/1998/}) (17. Bf4 Be7 $1 18. d4 h6 19. Bc2 Qd5 20. Be5 Bb7 21. Bb3 Qd7 22. Nd2 c5 $40 {[%csl Rf3,Rg2,Rh3,Rh4] צh4,,Ariskin,A-Gorbunov,A/Kstovo/1998/}) 17... g5 18. Rh6 $1 (18. Rd4 $143 $6 Bc5 19. Nf3 h6 20. Bf4 {0-1 Ivanchuk,V-Timman,J/Wijk aan Zee 1999 (51)} Re8 $1 21. Bd6 Bb6 {[%cal Rc6c5] c5}) 18... Ng4 19. Ne4 Nxh6 20. Nxd6 Qg6 (20... Qh3 21. Qe2 $5 $44) 21. Ne4 Ng4 22. Bxg5 Bf5 23. Bf4 Rad8 24. Qe2 Rfe8 25. Re1 Kg7 26. f3 Ne5 27. Bc2 c5 28. Qe3 $36 {Svidler,P-Adams,M/Elista olm/1998/}) ({And he also didn't want to defend a slightly inferior endgame after} 15... Qd7 16. Nd2 Bb7 17. Re1 c5 18. Ne4 Be7 19. a4 b4 20. Bg5 Bxg5 21. Nxg5 bxc3 22. bxc3 Rad8 23. Ne4 Qc6 24. Qh5 Nxc3 25. Qxc5 Rxd3 26. Bc4 Qxc5 27. Nxc5 Rd4 28. Bf1 Bc8 29. Ra3 Nd5 30. a5 $1 $14 {Polgar,J-Adams,M/Tilburg/1997/} (30. Bxa6 Nb4 $1)) 16. Nd2 (16. Bc2 Qg6 (16... Qd7 $5 $132) 17. d4 Bf5 18. Re2 Rae8 $44 {[%csl Re4,Rf3,Rg4][%cal Gf5c2,Ge8e1] '' Blatny,P. shows the positional ideas behind the Marshall-white-square control, long-term piece activity,lead in development. It's not easy for White to make his extra pawn tell even after some simplification.}) 16... Qg6 (16... Nf6 $6 17. Re1 {e4} Qxd3 18. Ne4 Qxd1 19. Nxf6+ gxf6 20. Rxd1 $16 {[%csl Rc6,Rf6,Rf7] ק}) 17. Re1 {Blatny,P: '17.f1, 17.a4 - f5-f4 with attacking possibilities'} (17. a4 Nf6 18. Re1 Bg4 19. f3 Bh3 20. Nf1 Rae8 21. Be3 h5 22. d4 h4 23. Bc2 Qh5 24. Bf2 Rxe1 25. Bxe1 c5 $13 {½, Zontakh,A-Pavlovic,M/Podgorica/1993/}) (17. Nf1 Bf5 18. Rd4 Bc5 19. Rxd5 cxd5 20. d4 Bb6 21. Bxd5 Rad8 22. Qf3 Bg4 23. Qg2 Rfe8 24. Ne3 Qd3 $13 {Hansson,D-Hebden,M/London/1984/}) 17... f5 {In this line many alternatives for both sides have been tried, the choice nearly always being a difficult one.} (17... Bg4 $143 $6 18. f3 Bh3 19. Ne4 Rae8 20. Re2 h5 21. Bg5 Bc7 22. Qd2 Kh8 23. Rae1 f6 24. Nf2 $1 {'!' Blatny,P. Blatny,P: 'Anand,V-Adams,M/Groningen WCh/1997'} Rxe2 25. Rxe2 Bf5 26. Bxd5 cxd5 27. Bf4 Bxf4 28. Qxf4 Bxd3 29. Rd2 $16 {Anand,V-Adams,M/Groningen W-ch/1997/}) (17... Bc7 {Blatny,P} 18. Nf3 Bg4 19. Nh4 Qh5 20. f3 Bh3 21. Qe2 $13) 18. Qf3 $5 $146 {[%mdl 8] Finally a new move, although it probably can't endanger Black's concept.} ({Only a few days earlier Adams had to face} 18. a4 Rb8 19. axb5 axb5 20. Nf3 f4 21. Ne5 Bxe5 22. Rxe5 fxg3 23. fxg3 Bg4 $1 24. Qe1 Bh3 25. Be3 Rf1+ 26. Qxf1 Bxf1 27. Rxf1 Rf8 28. Rxf8+ $11 {½,Anand,V-Adams,M/Dos Hermanas/1999/}) (18. Nf3 f4 19. Ne5 Bxe5 20. Rxe5 fxg3 21. hxg3 Bg4 22. Qe1 Bf3 23. Bd2 Rae8 $36 {Leko,P-Svidler,P/Dortmund/1998/ Blatny,P: 'Svidler-Leko/Dortmund/1998'}) (18. f4 $5 Bxf4 19. Qf3 Bb8 $5 20. Bxd5+ cxd5 21. Nb3 (21. Qxd5+ $143 Kh8 22. Qxa8 $2 (22. Nf3 {Blatny,P} Ra7 $13) 22... Qb6+ {b7-+} (22... Qb6+ 23. d4 Bb7 $19)) 21... Qf7 22. Nd4 Ba7 23. Bf4 Bd7 24. Re5 b4 25. Rae1 Rae8 26. Kf1 bxc3 $132 {½,Leko,P-Adams,M/Linares/1999/}) ({Until now the absolute elite has been avoiding the ultra-sharp} 18. c4 $5 f4 19. Ne4 $1 (19. cxd5 $2 fxg3 20. fxg3 Bxg3 $40) 19... fxg3 20. fxg3 Bg4 21. Qc2 bxc4 22. dxc4 Bb4 $142 $5 (22... Rae8 23. cxd5 Bf3 24. dxc6+ Kh8 25. Bd5 (25. Bf4 Bxf4 26. c7 Qb6+ 27. Qf2 Bxc7 28. Nc5 Be5 29. Rf1 Qd6 30. Ne6 Rf6) 25... Bxg3 (25... Qf5 26. Be3 Rxe4 27. Bxe4 Bxe4 28. Qe2 Bxc6 29. Rac1 Qd5 30. Rxc6 Qxc6 31. Bd4) (25... h5 26. Bg5) 26. hxg3 Rxe4 (26... Bxe4 $6 27. Rxe4 Rxe4 28. Bxe4 Qxg3+ 29. Qg2 Qe1+ 30. Kh2 Rf2 31. Bf3 $1 (31. Bf4 Rxg2+ 32. Bxg2 Qh4+ 33. Kg1 Qxf4 34. Rf1 Qe3+ 35. Rf2 Qc1+ 36. Rf1) 31... Qe5+ 32. Kh3 $18) 27. Bf4 $5 Rxe1+ (27... Rfxf4 28. Qf2 Qh5 29. Qh2 Qxd5 30. gxf4) (27... Qh5 28. Qh2 Qxd5 29. Rxe4 Bxe4 30. Rc1 Qd4+ 31. Qf2 Qxf2+ 32. Kxf2 Bxc6 33. Rxc6 g5 34. Rxa6) 28. Rxe1 Qxc2 29. Bxf3 Qxb2 30. c7 $16 {[%csl Gc7] White's c7 will most probably cost Black a whole , which gives White winning chances.}) 23. Bd2 Nf4 $1 24. Bxf4 (24. Bxb4 $4 Nh3+ 25. Kg2 Bf3+ $19) 24... Bxe1 25. Rxe1 Bf5 26. Qg2 Rae8 (26... Rfe8 $6 27. Bc2 Re6 28. g4 Bxg4 29. Rf1 Be2 30. Ng5 Bxf1 31. Bxg6 Bxg2 32. Bf7+ Kf8 33. Bxe6 $18 {Smagin,S-Hebden,M/Moscow/1986/} (33. Bxe6 {Blatny,P} Ke7 34. Kxg2 h6 35. Bg4 hxg5 36. Bxg5+ Kd6 37. b4)) 27. Bc2 Re6 $44 {when the pins give him sufficient counterplay, e.g.} 28. g4 Bxg4 29. Bd2 Bf5 30. Qxg6 Rxg6+ 31. Kh1 Re6 $11) (18. Ne4 {Blatny,P} fxe4 19. dxe4 Bg4 20. Qd4 Qh5 21. exd5 c5 $13) (18. a4 {(Blatny,P) -> Anand,V-Adams,M/Dos Hermanas/1999/ CBM 71}) 18... Kh8 $8 (18... f4 $2 19. Bxd5+ cxd5 20. Qxd5+ Kh8 21. Ne4 $1 $16) 19. Bd1 ({Very risky is} 19. Bxd5 $6 cxd5 20. Qxd5 Rb8 {[%cal Rc8b7,Rb7h1] b7,גa8-h1 Blatny,P: 'b7a8-h1'}) 19... f4 {Black must act, otherwise his compensation dissipates.} (19... Bd7 $143 20. Qh5 Rae8 (20... Qf6 21. Nf3 $16) 21. Rxe8 Rxe8 22. Qxg6 Re1+ 23. Nf1 hxg6 24. Bd2 $16) 20. g4 (20. Qh5 $6 fxg3 21. fxg3 (21. hxg3 {Blatny,P} Bxg3 $1 22. Qxg6 Bxf2+ 23. Kh1 hxg6 $15) 21... Bxg3 $1 22. Qxg6 Bf2+ 23. Kh1 hxg6 $32 {}) 20... h5 21. h3 Nf6 $1 $32 {The right move-order, Black ruthlessly goes after White's .} (21... hxg4 $143 $6 22. hxg4 Nf6 23. Qh3+ $16 {'!' Blatny,P.}) 22. Qg2 $1 ({Playing with fire would be} 22. Qxc6 hxg4 23. hxg4 $142 (23. Qxa8 $2 f3 24. h4 Bf5 $40 25. Qc6 (25. Qa7 Bxd3 $1 26. Qd4 (26. Re6 g3 27. Rxd6 gxf2+ 28. Kxf2 Ng4+ 29. Kg3 Qxd6+ 30. Kxg4 Qf4+ $19) 26... g3 27. Kh1 (27. Bxf3 gxf2+ 28. Kxf2 Bg3+ 29. Ke3 Re8+ $19) 27... Qf5 28. Nxf3 Qh3+ 29. Kg1 g2 $19) 25... g3 26. Qxf3 (26. Qxd6 gxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Ng4+ $19) 26... gxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Bxd3 $1 $17 {[%cal Rf6g4] g4}) 23... Bxg4 24. Qg2 Rae8 $5 (24... Qh5 25. Qh1 Bxd1 26. Rxd1 $14) (24... Qf5 25. Bxg4 Nxg4 26. Nf3 Rae8 27. Bd2 $14) 25. Ne4 (25. Rf1 Qf5 $5 26. Bxg4 (26. Ne4 f3 27. Qh1+ Bh3 28. Bxf3 Nxe4 29. Bxe4 Qg4+ 30. Bg2 Rf3 $1 $17) 26... Nxg4 27. Ne4 Re6 $40) 25... Qh5 {'' Blatny,P.} 26. Qh1 (26. Bxg4 Nxg4 27. f3 Ne5 $17) 26... Qxh1+ 27. Kxh1 Nxe4 $1 28. dxe4 f3 $36 {[%csl Rh1] עh1 White's position is not easy despite the exchange of , as his  is too exposed.}) 22... hxg4 23. hxg4 (23. Bf3 {Blatny,P} g3 24. fxg3 fxg3 25. Bxc6 Rb8 $13) 23... Bxg4 (23... Nxg4 $6 24. f3 Ne5 25. Qxg6 (25. d4 Qh5 $5 26. Rxe5 Bxe5 27. dxe5 Bh3 $132 {'' Blatny,P.}) 25... Nxg6 26. Ne4 $14 { gives White the advantage, as his pieces quickly enter the game.}) 24. Re6 {[%mdl 64] Ambitious, now wild complications arise.} ({Now Black doesn't have to fear} 24. f3 Rae8 25. Ne4 Bf5 $11) 24... Qh5 $1 {} (24... Rae8 $2 25. Bxg4 $18) (24... Rad8 25. Ne4 $5 $18) (24... Bb8 {Blatny,P} 25. Rxf6 Rxf6 26. Bxg4 Qxd3 $13) 25. Bxg4 ({A  is stronger on f3 than a :} 25. Rxf6 $2 Bxd1 26. Rxd6 Rae8 27. Ne4 (27. Qh2 Re1+ 28. Kg2 f3+ 29. Kg3 Rg1+ $1 $19 {'-+' Blatny,P.}) (27. Nf1 Bf3 28. Qh2 Qxh2+ $1 29. Kxh2 Re1 30. Ng3 fxg3+ 31. Kxg3 Rg1+ 32. Kh2 Rf5 $1 $19 {'-+' Blatny,P.}) 27... Bf3 28. Qh2 Re5 29. Qxh5+ Rxh5 {'-+' Blatny,P.} 30. Bxf4 Rxf4 31. Ng3 Rfh4 $1 $19) (25. Rxd6 {Blatny,P} Bxd1 26. Nf1 Rad8 27. Bxf4 Bf3) 25... Nxg4 26. Rxd6 $5 {Judit with 1,5/5 badly wants to win and ignores the safer} (26. Qh1 Qxh1+ 27. Kxh1 Rf6 $11) (26. Qh1 $11 {Blatny,P}) 26... Rae8 27. Ne4 (27. Nf3 Re2 $142 (27... Ne5 $6 28. Nxe5 Rxe5 29. Qh2 (29. Bxf4 $5 Rxf4 30. Rg6 {'' Blatny,P.} Rxf2 31. Kxf2 Re2+ 32. Kg1 Rxg2+ 33. Rxg2 $14) 29... Rg5+ 30. Kh1 Qxh2+ 31. Kxh2 Kh7 32. Kh1 $1 $16 {and White always has the counter-sacrifice f4 at a suitable moment.}) 28. Qh1 (28. Bd2 $6 Rxf2 29. Qxf2 Nxf2 30. Kxf2 g5 31. Re1 Qh3 $1 $36 {[%csl Gf4,Gg5] g,f, '' Blatny,P.} (31... g4 32. Ree6) 32. Rg1 g4 33. Ne5 g3+ 34. Kf3 g2+ 35. Ke4 Qh1 $1 36. Nf3 Qh7+ 37. Kd4 Qa7+ $19) 28... Qxh1+ 29. Kxh1 Nxf2+ $1 (29... Rxf2 30. Rg6 $1 $16) 30. Kg2 (30. Kg1 Nh3+ 31. Kh1 {gives Black at least a draw and he can try to achieve more after} Rf2 $5 $36) 30... Ne4+ 31. Kf1 Ng3+ 32. Kg1 Rf5 33. Bxf4 (33. Nd4 $2 f3 $19) 33... Rxf4 34. Ng5 {'' Blatny,P. Black is slightly better, but a draw is the most probable result.}) (27. Nf1 $2 Re1 28. Rd4 (28. f3 Nh2 $1 29. Bxf4 Nxf3+ $19) 28... Rxf1+ $1 29. Kxf1 Nh2+ 30. Kg1 Nf3+ 31. Kf1 Nxd4 $19) (27. Rxc6 {Blatny,P} Re1+ 28. Nf1 f3 $19) 27... Ne5 $44 28. f3 $6 {Again in the above-mentioned spirit.} ({Necessary was the more modest} 28. Bd2 Nf3+ 29. Kf1 {Although Black can postpone giving a perpetual, he can hardly improve his position, so a draw is a likely result.} Nh2+ {'=' Blatny,P.} (29... Nxd2+ $6 30. Nxd2 f3 31. Nxf3 Rxf3 32. Kg1 Rf6 $5 (32... Re5 $6 33. Rg6 Rff5 (33... Ref5 {Blatny,P} 34. Rg3 $14) 34. Rg3 $16) 33. Rxc6 $1 $16 (33. Rxf6 gxf6 34. Qxc6 Rg8+ 35. Kf1 Re8 $1 $11) (33. Qxc6 Rg8 34. Kf1 Re8)) (29... Re5 30. Rd7 $11) 30. Kg1 Nf3+ $11) 28... Nxf3+ 29. Kf2 Nh4 (29... Ne5 {Blatny,P} 30. Bd2 Qh4+ 31. Kg1 $13) 30. Qh1 $5 (30. Qg5 $6 Qf3+ 31. Kg1 (31. Ke1 Qh1+ 32. Kf2 Qh2+ 33. Kf1 Nf5 $1 34. Qg2 Ne3+ $1 35. Bxe3 fxe3+ $19) 31... Re5 $1 32. Qxh4+ Rh5 33. Rd8 (33. Qxh5+ Qxh5 34. Bd2 f3 35. Nf2 Rf6 $1 {Blatny,P: 'g6+-'} 36. Rxf6 gxf6 $19) 33... Qd1+ 34. Kf2 Rxh4 35. Rxf8+ Kh7 36. Ng5+ Kg6 37. Nf3 Rh1 38. Rxf4 Qc2+ {'-+' Blatny,P.} 39. Kg3 Rxc1 $17) (30. Qh3 $2 g5 {g4 loses an important tempo.}) 30... g5 31. b4 {A logical attempt to develop his sleeping .} (31. Bd2 g4 32. Rg1 (32. Rxc6 {Blatny,P} g3+ 33. Kg1 g2) 32... g3+ 33. Kf1 (33. Ke1 g2 $1 $19 {f3 '-+' Blatny,P. Blatny,P: 'f3'}) 33... Re6 $3 $40 {} (33... Rf6 $1 {Blatny,P} 34. Nxf6 (34. Rxf6 Qd1+ 35. Be1 Qxd3#) (34. Rd4 Rg6 35. Be1 Kg8 {g2+-}) 34... Qe2#) 34. Rxe6 $2 Qd1+ 35. Be1 Qxd3#) (31. b3 $5 {with the same idea was maybe better, not giving Black the possibility bxc4 later.}) 31... g4 32. Bb2 (32. Rxc6 {Blatny,P} g3+ $19) 32... g3+ 33. Kg1 Nf3+ (33... f3 34. c4+ Kg8 35. Nxg3 f2+ 36. Kf1 $18 {'' Blatny,P.}) (33... g2 34. c4+ Kh7 35. Rd7+ $18 {'' Blatny,P.} (35. Qh3 $5 Nf3+ 36. Qxf3 $18)) 34. Kg2 (34. Qxf3 {Blatny,P} Qxf3 35. c4+ Kg8 36. Rg6+ Kh7 37. Ng5+ Kxg6 $17) 34... Nh2 {Adams already rightly has higher ambitions than a draw.} (34... Nh4+ $11) 35. c4+ Kg8 36. Qd1 $2 {[%mdl 8192] '?!' Blatny,P. White buckles under the tension, this loses by force.} ({The only way to fight on was} 36. Kg1 $1 {} {Black would have to go for the sharp, but promising} Qg4 $5 (36... Qe2 37. Rg6+ Kh7 38. Rg7+ Kh6 39. Qg2 $16) (36... f3 $2 37. Nxg3 f2+ 38. Kg2 f1=Q+ (38... Re2 39. Rf1 Nxf1 40. Qxh5 Ne3+ 41. Kh3 $18) 39. Qxf1 $1 (39. Rxf1 $2 Re2+ $1 40. Nxe2 Qxe2+ 41. Kh3 Qh5+ $1 42. Kg2 Rxf1 $19) 39... Rxf1 40. Nxh5 Re2+ 41. Kh3 $18 {'' Blatny,P.}) (36... Rxe4 {Blatny,P} 37. Qxe4 (37. dxe4 Nf3+ 38. Kg2 Nh4+ 39. Kg1 Nf3+ 40. Kg2 Nh4+ 41. Kg1 Qe2) 37... Nf3+ 38. Kg2 Qh2+ 39. Kxf3 Qh5+ 40. Kg2 Qh2+ 41. Kf3 Qh5+ 42. Kg2 Qh2+ 43. Kf3) (36... Re7 {h7} 37. Nf6+ (37. Bf6 Rh7 38. Qg2 Nf3+ 39. Kf1 Qg4 $40) (37. Rf6 Qe2 38. Qg2 Nf3+ 39. Qxf3 Qxf3 40. Rg6+ Kh7 41. Ng5+ Kxg6 42. Nxf3 Kh5 $15) (37. Rf1 Rh7 38. Nf6+ Rxf6 39. Bxf6 f3 40. Rd8+ Kf7 41. Rd7+ Kg6 42. Rxh7 f2+ 43. Rxf2 Qd1+ 44. Kg2 Qxh1+ 45. Kxh1 gxf2 $19) 37... Rxf6 38. Bxf6 Nf3+ 39. Kg2 Re2+ $19) 37. Rh6 $8 (37. Qg2 Nf3+ 38. Kf1 Nh4 $19) 37... f3 38. Rh8+ Kf7 39. Rh7+ Ke6 40. Re1 Kf5 $1 41. Rxh2 (41. Nd6+ Kg6 42. Rg7+ Kh6 $19) 41... gxh2+ 42. Kf2 Qh4+ $1 $17 { '' Blatny,P.} 43. Kxf3 $2 Kg6+ 44. Ke2 Qf2+) (36. Nf6+ $2 {Blatny,P} Rxf6 37. Bxf6 Re2+ $19) 36... f3+ $19 37. 