1. d4 {Postny} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. e4 d5 {This is the most aggressive way to meet white's last move. Karpov as black prefered 5...d6 against I.Sokolov few rounds before. Postny: 'This is the most aggressive way to meet White's last move. Karpov as Black preferred 5...d6 against I.Sokolov a few rounds before. '} 6. e5 Ne4 7. a3 ({The main alternative is} 7. Bd3) 7... Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 c5 9. Bd3 (9. Bb2 cxd4 10. cxd4 Bd7 (10... Qa5+ 11. Ke2 f6 12. f3 Ng5 13. h4 Nf7 14. exf6 gxf6 15. Kf2 Nc6 16. Bd3 dxc4 17. Bxh7+ Kh8 18. Ne2 $16 {1-0 Giorgadze,G-Illescas Cordoba,M/Barcelona 2000/CBM 77 ext (32)}) 11. Bd3 Qa5+ 12. Ke2 Rc8 13. f3 f5 14. exf6 Nxf6 15. c5 Bb5 16. Bc3 Bxd3+ 17. Qxd3 Qa4 18. Kf2 Nbd7 19. Ne2 $11 {½-½ Shariyazdanov,A-Sasikiran,K/Raipur 2002/CBM 87 ext (22)}) 9... cxd4 (9... Qa5 10. Ne2 cxd4 11. cxd5 exd5 12. f3 Nxc3 13. Nxd4 Ne4+ 14. Ke2 Qc3 15. Bxe4 Qxd4 16. Bxh7+ Kh8 17. Bb2 Qh4 18. Bd3 Nc6 19. Kf1 Bd7 20. Re1 Rfe8 $132 {½-½ Belozerov,A-Sakaev,K/Krasnoyarsk 2003/CBM 98 (26)}) (9... Nc6 10. Ne2 cxd4 11. cxd4 Qa5+ 12. Kf1 Nb4 13. axb4 Qxa1 14. f3 f5 15. Qb1 Qxb1 16. Bxb1 a5 17. b5 a4 18. fxe4 fxe4+ 19. Ke1 $16 {1-0 Shariyazdanov,A-Rashkovsky,N/Oberwart 2002/CBM 89 ext (28)}) 10. cxd4 ({Postny: 'Harmless is'} 10. cxd5 exd5 11. cxd4 {Postny: 'and now:'} f6 (11... Qa5+ {Postny is a solid choice:} 12. Bd2 Qxd2+ 13. Qxd2 Nxd2 14. Kxd2 f6 15. exf6 Rxf6 16. f3 Bf5 17. Bxf5 Rxf5 18. Rb1 b6 19. Rc1 Rf6 20. Rc7 Rc6 21. Rxc6 Nxc6 22. Ne2 $11 {1/2, Fedorchuk,D (2114)-Bragiin,S (2125)/Russia/2002/}) ({Black has also} 11... Nc6 $5 {Postny} 12. Ne2 Bg4 13. f3 (13. O-O Bxe2 14. Qxe2 Nxd4 15. Qe3 Nb3 $15) 13... Bxf3 14. gxf3 Qh4+ 15. Ng3 Nxd4 $5 (15... Nxg3 16. Qf2 Nxd4 17. Qxg3 Nxf3+ 18. Kf2 Qd4+ 19. Be3 Qxd3 20. Rhd1 $11) 16. Qg2 Nc5 $44) 12. exf6 Qxf6 13. Be3 Nc6 14. Ne2 Bf5 15. O-O Nd6 $11 {1-0 Sokolov,I-Polaczek,R/Antwerp 1996/EXT 97 (41) Postny: '=!' Postny: 'Sokolov,I-Polaczek,R/Antwerp/1996/'}) 10... Qa5+ 11. Kf1 {White is losing his right to castle, but is hoping to make use of his pair of bishops, as well as better control over the centre.} (11. Bd2 {Postny} Qxd2+ 12. Qxd2 Nxd2 13. Kxd2 dxc4 14. Bxc4 Rd8 15. Ne2 Nc6 16. Ke3 Ne7 $11 (16... b6 $11)) 11... Bd7 {Postny: 'A rare move, the strongest is probably the usual alternative 12...c6.'} (11... f5 12. exf6 Nxf6 13. Bd2 Qc7 14. cxd5 Qxc2 15. Bxc2 exd5 16. Bb4 Re8 17. f3 Nc6 18. Ne2 Bd7 $11 {0-1 Belozerov,A-Lahno,K/Rethymnon 2003/CBM 98 (67)}) (11... Nc6 12. Ne2 Bd7 13. Rb1 f6 14. cxd5 exd5 15. Rb5 Qd8 16. Rxd5 $18 {1-0 El Gindy,E-Pavlovic,M/Linares 2003 (27)}) (11... Qc3 12. Rb1 Qxd4 13. Bb2 Qc5 14. f3 f5 15. exf6 Nxf6 16. Bxf6 Rxf6 17. Bxh7+ Kf7 18. cxd5 Qxc2 19. dxe6+ Rxe6 20. Bxc2 $16 {½-½ Shariyazdanov,A-Mishra,N/Raipur 2002/CBM 87 ext (38)}) 12. Ne2 (12. f3 $5 {(Postny) Shariyazdanov} Ba4 (12... f5 13. Ne2 Nc6 14. cxd5 exd5 15. Qb2 $16 {[%cal Yg2g3,Yf1g2] g3, g2}) 13. Qb1 Nd2+ (13... f5 14. Ne2 Nc6 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Qb2 $14) 14. Bxd2 Qxd2 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Ne2 $1 h6 17. Kf2 $14) 12... Ba4 $146 (12... f6 {Postny: 'leads to positions, which can arise also from the 11...f6 line'} 13. Bxe4 (13. exf6 {Postny} Rxf6 $5 {Postny: '-12...f6'} (13... Nxf6 14. Bd2 Qc7 15. Bf4 Qc8 16. c5 Nc6 17. Bd6 Rf7 18. f3 b6 19. Kg1 bxc5 20. Qxc5 Ne8 21. Bg3 Ne7 22. Qa5 Nf5 23. Rc1 $36 {Doroshkievich,V (2413)-Biriukov,O (2369)/St Petersburg/2003/}) 14. Bxe4) 13... dxe4 14. exf6 Rxf6 15. Be3 (15. Qxe4 {Postny} Bc6 $44 (15... Qa6 $132)) 15... Bc6 {Postny: '' Postny: '/' Postny: '-see the notes to Vallejo-Schandorff'} 16. h4 Nd7 17. Rh3 Rc8 $11 {½-½ Shariyazdanov,A-Pikula,D/Biel 2002/EXT 2003 (42)}) (12... f5 {Postny} 13. Rb1 $1 (13. exf6 Rxf6 {-12...f6}) 13... Na6 14. Rxb7 Ba4 15. Qb1 dxc4 16. Bxc4 Kh8 17. f3 Rac8 18. Bd3 Nac5 19. dxc5 Nxc5 20. Kf2 Nxb7 21. Qxb7 Rfd8 22. Be3 Rc7 23. Qb1 Qxe5 24. Qb4 Bc2 {Vera Gonzalez,R (2461)-Sulskis,S (2555)/Yucatan/2004/} 25. Bc4 $16 (25. Bd4 $16)) 13. Qb2 (13. Qa2 $6 {Postny} Nc6 14. Qb2 f6 15. Bxe4 dxe4 16. exf6 Rxf6 17. Be3 Qa6 18. c5 Na5 19. Kg1 Nc4 20. Qc3 Raf8 $40 {Gupta,A (2412)-Bakre,T (2491)/Visakhapatnam/2004/}) 13... Nc6 $1 14. cxd5 (14. Qxb7 $4 Rab8 15. Qd7 Rfd8 $19 {[%csl Rd7]}) (14. f3 f6 $1 {[%csl Rf1,Gf8][%cal Gf8f1]} 15. cxd5 (15. fxe4 $2 fxe5+ $17 16. Kg1 $4 Qe1#) 15... exd5 {Postny: ' - see 14.f3.'} 16. e6 f5 $15 {[%csl Re6]}) 14... exd5 15. Be3 (15. f3 f6 {- see 14.f3.}) (15. Qxb7 $2 a6 $1 {[%cal Gf8b8]} (15... Nxd4 16. Qb4 (16. Nxd4 $2 Qc3 $17) 16... Qxb4 17. axb4 Bb5 $11) 16. Bxe4 (16. Qb2 Rab8 17. Qa2 Bb3 18. Qb2 Bd1 $1 19. Qa2 Bxe2+ $19 {[%csl Rd4]}) 16... dxe4 $17) (15. Bf4 f6 $36) 15... f6 16. Bxe4 dxe4 17. exf6 Rxf6 18. Kg1 Bb5 $6 ({More to the point is} 18... Rd8 {putting pressure on white's d4. Postny: 'putting pressure on White's d4.'}) 19. Ng3 Bd3 20. Rc1 Raf8 $6 {There is no real point for doubling the rooks on the "f" file, the f2 is very well protected. Postny: 'There is no real point in doubling the rooks on the f-file, the f2 is very well protected.'} (20... Rd8 $142) 21. h4 b6 $6 {Another dubious move, weakening the c6.} (21... R6f7 $142) 22. Qb3+ Kh8 23. h5 h6 24. Rh4 Rd6 ({Very beautiful variation is:} 24... Nxd4 $2 25. Bxd4 Qg5 {[%csl Rc1,Rh4]} 26. Rc7 $3 Qxh4 {[%tqu "En","Please enter a move for white.","","","c7g7","",10]} 27. Rxg7 $1 {[%cal Gg7g6]} Rc8 (27... Kxg7 28. Nf5+ $18 {[%csl Rg7,Rh4]}) 28. Rg8+ $1 (28. Qb2 $1 $18) 28... Rxg8 29. Qf7 Rg7 30. Bxf6 Qg4 31. Qxa7 $18) 25. Rf4 $1 $16 {Suddenly, all white's pieces are very well coordinated. Black is in serious trouble. Postny: 'Suddenly, all White's pieces are very well coordinated. Black is in serious trouble.'} (25. Nxe4 $2 Nxd4) (25. Rxe4 $2 Bxe4 26. Nxe4 Nxd4) 25... Rxf4 (25... Rfd8 26. Rf7 Nxd4 27. Bxd4 Rxd4 28. Nf5 $40) 26. Bxf4 Rf6 27. Be3 Ne7 28. Rc7 Qd5 (28... Nd5 $2 29. Rd7 $18) 29. Qxd5 Nxd5 30. Rxa7 {White is a pawn up, but it's hard for him to achieve progress.} Rf8 31. Kh2 Kg8 32. Kh3 Nf4+ 33. Kh2 {Karpov does not want to take any risk.} (33. Kg4 Nd5 $1 (33... Nxg2 34. d5 {[%csl Gd5]} (34. Nf5 $4 Be2+))) 33... Nd5 34. Rd7 Nf6 35. Rd6 Ra8 36. Rxb6 Rxa3 {Black has reasonable chances for a draw.} 37. Kh3 Ra5 38. Kh4 Rd5 39. Rb7 (39. Bxh6 Rxd4 $16) 39... Bc2 40. Bf4 $2 {This just leads to exchange of pawns, which will make black's defensive task much easier.} (40. Rc7 Bd1 41. Rc5 {and white is keeping chances for a win.}) 40... Rxd4 41. Rxg7+ $6 {This leads to a drawish position by force.} (41. Be5 Rd5 $1 (41... Rd7 42. Rb2 Bd3 43. Bxf6 gxf6 44. Nf5 $16) 42. Bxf6 gxf6 {In this position white still keeps practical winning chances.}) 41... Kxg7 42. Nf5+ Kh7 43. Nxd4 Bd1 {[%csl Rh5]} 44. Nf5 Bxh5 45. Nxh6 Nd5 46. Bc1 e3 $1 {Transforming the position to a drawish endgame with opposite colour bishops.} 47. fxe3 Kxh6 48. e4+ Kg6 49. exd5 Bd1 $1 50. g4 Bb3 51. d6 Be6 52. Bg5 Bc8 53. Be7 Bd7 54. Kg3 Bc8 55. Kf4 Bd7 {This endgame a theoretical draw.} 56. g5 Kf7 57. Ke5 Be8 58. Kd5 Ba4 59. Bd8 Ke8 60. Ba5 Kf7 61. Bc3 Be8 62. Bb2 Ba4 63. Bc1 Be8 64. Bb2 Ba4 65. Kc5 Ke6 66. g6 Kd7 67. g7 (67. g7 Bb3 {followed by 68...Bg8, building fortress.}) 1/2-1/2

