{[%evp 0,41,19,23,105,75,94,74,96,86,73,71,70,63,62,67,38,50,81,53,58,26,35,16,91,32,5,16,-6,-8,14,138,130,140,140,140,141,141,173,173,175,175,709,806]} 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. Bc4 d6 5. Qf3 $5 {Definitely interesting here: it's a bit unusual to see Black play ...d6, ...e6 AND ...c6 in a Modern Defence.} e6 6. Nge2 b5 (6... Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Bb3 O-O 9. Bg5 (9. Qg3 e5 10. Qh4 exd4 11. Nxd4 Nc5 12. Re1 Ng4 13. Bg5 Qb6 14. Nf3 Nxb3 15. axb3 f6 16. Bc1 Ne5 17. Qg3 f5 18. Bf4 fxe4 19. Nxe5 dxe5 20. Be3 Qc7 21. Nxe4 b6 22. Qh4 Bf5 23. Bg5 Bxe4 24. Rxe4 Rf7 25. Rae1 $14 {Yagupov,I-Komliakov,V/Orel/1994/}) 9... h6 10. Bh4 e5 11. Rad1 Qe7 12. Qe3 Re8 13. f4 exd4 14. Qxd4 Nxe4 15. Bxe7 Bxd4+ 16. Rxd4 Nxc3 17. Nxc3 Rxe7 18. Rxd6 Kg7 (18... Nf8 19. Rd8 b6 20. a4 Bb7 21. Rd6 Ba6 22. Rfd1 Rc8 23. a5 bxa5 24. Ra1 $16 {[%csl Ra5,Ra7,Rc6] ׫,Nunn,J-Fauland,A/Vienna/1991/}) 19. f5 gxf5 20. Rxf5 Nf8 21. Rf3 Be6 22. Rg3+ Kh8 23. Ne4 $1 $16 {Nunn,J-Ehlvest,J/Skelleftea WCup/1989/}) 7. Bb3 (7. Bd3 e5 (7... Nd7 8. O-O Ne7 9. Qg3 e5 10. d5 cxd5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. exd5 a6 13. a4 bxa4 14. Nc3 $1 f5 $2 15. Bxf5 gxf5 16. Qxg7 Qf6 17. Qxf6 Nxf6 18. Rxa4 f4 19. f3 Bf5 20. Rf2 Kd7 21. Bxf4 $1 $18 {Vasiukov,E-Zilberman,N/Belgorod/1990/}) 8. Be3 Nf6 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Rd1 Nbd7 11. Nc1 O-O 12. Nb3 Qc7 13. h3 a6 14. Nc5 Nxc5 15. Bxc5 Rd8 16. O-O Nd7 17. Be3 Nf8 18. Ne2 Ne6 19. c3 Bb7 20. Bc2 c5 21. Qg3 Rxd1 22. Rxd1 Rd8 23. Rxd8+ Qxd8 24. f3 Bf6 25. Qe1 Bg5 26. Qc1 h6 27. Kf2 c4 28. Ke1 Kg7 {0,5/Liss,E-Kanstler,B/Rishon le Zion/1994/}) 7... a5 {'?!' Black ignores development, which enables White to open the position to his advantage.} 8. a3 (8. a4 b4 9. Nd1 Ba6 10. Be3 d5 11. exd5 cxd5 12. c4 Ne7 13. cxd5 Nxd5 14. Rc1 O-O 15. Bxd5 exd5 16. b3 Re8 17. O-O Nd7 18. Re1 Nf6 19. Nb2 Ne4 20. Nf4 Ra7 21. Nfd3 Rae7 $36 {[%csl Ga6,Gg7,Rc3,Rd4][%cal Ge7e1] e,×d4,c3,, Geenen,M-Zilberman,N/Manila olm/1992/}) 8... Ba6 9. d5 (9. O-O Ne7 10. Bf4 O-O 11. Rfe1 Qc7 12. Rad1 Nd7 13. Qg3 Nc8 14. Qh4 b4 15. Na4 e5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Bh6 Bb5 18. Nf4 Qd8 19. Qxd8 Rxd8 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. axb4 Bxa4 22. Bxa4 axb4 23. Bb3 Nb6 24. Ra1 Kf6 25. Nd3 Nxd3 26. cxd3 $10 {Sideifzade-Ivanov,A /Tashkent 1L/1980/}) 9... cxd5 (9... exd5 $142 $5 10. exd5 c5 $8 11. a4 (11. Bf4 $5 $36) 11... b4 12. Nb5 Bxb5 13. axb5 Nf6 14. c4 $14) 10. exd5 e5 {Although Black has a nice pawn structure in the center, (d6,e5-d5) he is far behind in development and White uses the d5-wedge to great effect. Also Black can't consider f5, till it would allow White's  to e6.} 11. Ne4 Qc7 (11... Ne7 {would have allowed the beautiful} 12. Qf6 $3 {collecting the d6-pawn after Black castles, as obviously} Bxf6 $4 {loses right away to} 13. Nxf6+ Kf8 14. Bh6#) (11... h6 $1 {followed by ...Nf6 was the correct way to play.} 12. Qg3 {[%csl Re8][%cal Yf2f4] עe8,f4}) (11... f5 $2 12. Ng5 {[%csl Re6] ×e6}) 12. c4 $5 {'!'} (12. Bd2 {was also extremely strong, with Bxa5 ideas hanging in the air, e.g.} Nd7 13. O-O Ngf6 14. Bxa5 $1 Qxa5 15. Nxd6+ Kf8 16. Nxf7 $1 Kxf7 17. d6+ $18) 12... bxc4 (12... Ne7 13. c5 $40) 13. Ba4+ Nd7 (13... Kf8 14. O-O {isn't something Black wants to play!}) 14. N2c3 $5 {'' -12...bxc4} (14. h4 $5 {was definitely a very interesting alternative.}) 14... Ke7 $2 {gf6 Omitting a sacrifice Shirov would have seen at once would have he been sitting on the other side of the board!} (14... h6 {'!?' with the idea to push ...f5 (and prevent Ng5 while enabling ...e4) was again the best move. Here White can play the elegant} 15. Nc5 $5 {but Black can just defend calmly with} (15. Nb5 Bxb5 16. Bxb5 f5 17. Nd2 Ngf6 18. Bc6 $36 {[%csl Ra5,Rd6] ×a5,d6}) 15... Ngf6 $1 $13) (14... f5 15. Ng5 $40) (14... Ne7 15. Nf6+ Bxf6 16. Qxf6 $16) 15. Nxd6 $3 {'!' It's all over!} Qxd6 (15... Kxd6 {'?'} 16. Ne4+ $18 (16. Qxf7 $18) 16... Kxd5 (16... Ke7 17. d6+) 17. Qxf7+ Kxe4 18. Bc2+ Kd4 19. Be3#) (15... f5 16. Ndb5 $18) 16. Ne4 $1 Qxd5 {Otherwise 17.d6+ would win right away.} (16... Qb6 17. d6+ (17. Bxd7 $6 f5 $13) 17... Kf8 18. Bxd7 $16) 17. Bg5+ $1 Ndf6 {''} (17... f6 18. Rd1 $18 Qxd1+ 19. Qxd1 $18) 18. Rd1 Qb7 19. Rd7+ {Black loses his queen and has no hope of saving the game with such a poorly placed king. What a game!} Qxd7 20. Bxd7 {-18...b7} h6 (20... Bb7 $8 21. Ba4 Kf8 (21... h6 22. Qg3 $1 hxg5 (22... Bxe4 23. Qxe5+ Kf8 24. Qd6+ Ne7 25. Bxf6 $18) 23. Qxe5+ Kd8 (23... Kf8 24. Qd6+ Ne7 25. Nxf6 $18) 24. Qd6+ Kc8 25. Nc5 $18 {}) 22. Bc2 $16 {[%csl Rf8] ,עf8}) 21. Qd1 (21. Qd1 {'!'} hxg5 22. Qd6+ Kd8 23. Bb5+ Kc8 24. Qc6+ Kb8 25. Qb6+ Bb7 (25... Kc8 26. Nd6#) 26. Nd6 Ra7 27. Qd8+ Bc8 28. Qxc8#) 1-0

