1. d4 {Tisdall,J Hansen,Cu} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bg5 {Hansen,Cu: 'This was played in game 7 and 11 of the world-championship match between Karpov and Kasparov in 1985. Karpov tried 5. - d6 and 5. - c5. The crucial point is which pin plays the most important role in the strategical battle. The one on the  h4-d8 or the one on the  a5-e1. I would say the pin of f6 gains in strength the moment black places his  on g8. However it seems Kasparov and Timman have different opinions about how this should be exploited' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'This was played in games 7 and 11 of the world championship match between Karpov and Kasparov in 1985. Karpov tried 5. - d6 and 5. - c5. The crucial point is which pin plays the most important role in the strategic battle. The one on the  h4-d8 or the one on the  a5-e1. I would say the pin of f6 gains in strength the moment black places his  on g8. However it seems Kasparov and Timman have different opinions about how this should be exploited''} c5 6. d5 {Hansen,Cu: 'Timman may have played this because of his many games with the Leningrad-variation (4.Lg5) where black normally does not castle so quickly. In game 11 of the above mentioned match Kasparov preferred:' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'Timman may have played this because of his many games with the Leningrad Variation (4.g5) where Black normally does not castle so quickly. In game 11 of the above mentioned match Kasparov preferred:''} (6. e3 {Hansen,Cu} cxd4 7. exd4 h6 8. Bh4 d5 (8... Qa5 $132 {Tisdall,J see Wojtaszek-Berkes}) 9. Rc1 {with some kind of Nimzoindian type of position Tisdall,J: 'with some kind of Nimzo-Indian type of position'}) 6... d6 {Judit avoids the critical reaction in this type of position, with an immediate kick of the bishop with ...h6. This and the gambit reaction ...b5 is the combination of ideas that I imagine put most white players off using this line more often. Play can easily transpose into one of Timman's pet variations (the true Leningrad Nimzo, 4.g5), but there is relatively little experience with this gambit with the inclusion of the moves f3 and ...0-0. Tisdall,J: 'Judit avoids the critical reaction in this type of position, with an immediate kicking away of the bishop with ...h6. This and the gambit reaction ...b5 is the combination of ideas that I imagine put most White players off using this line more often. Play can easily transpose into one of Timman's pet variations (the true Leningrad Nimzo, 4.g5), but there is relatively little experience with this gambit with the inclusion of the moves f3 and ...0-0.'} (6... h6 7. Bh4 Bxc3+ (7... b5 $5 8. e3 Bb7 9. dxe6 fxe6 10. cxb5 Qa5 11. Bxf6 Rxf6 12. Qd2 $6 (12. Qc1 $1 {is better, keeping the a1 protected and adding punch to an eventual a2-a3.} a6 $44 {Tisdall,J}) (12. Qc2 {Tisdall,J} a6 13. bxa6 Nxa6 (13... Bxf3 $5 14. gxf3 d5 $36) 14. Be2 c4 15. O-O d5 16. e4 Raf8 17. exd5 exd5 18. Rad1 Nc7 19. Nd4 Bc5 20. Bf3 Kh8 21. Nce2 Nb5 22. Nxb5 Qxb5 23. Nc3 Qd7 24. Bxd5 Rxf2 25. Rxf2 Rxf2 26. Qxf2 Bxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Qc7 29. Rd4 Qb6 30. Ne2 $11 {Bernasek,J (2513)-Stocek,J (2559) Havlickuv Brod 2015}) 12... a6 13. bxa6 Nxa6 $6 $44 {Tisdall,J: '½-½ Moskalenko,V-Lyrberg,P/Copenhagen 1995/CBM 47 ext (30)' ½-½ Moskalenko,V-Lyrberg,P/Copenhagen 1995/CBM 47 ext (30)} (13... Bxf3 $1 14. gxf3 Nc6 $1 $40 {Tisdall,J: '!'  transposes into Timman-Ligterink, Wijk an Zee 1977.} 15. Be2 {Tisdall,J} d5 $36 { transposes into Timman-Ligterink, Wijk an Zee 1977.})) 8. bxc3 d6 9. e3 e5 10. Nd2 Nbd7 11. Bd3 Re8 12. Qc2 Nf8 (12... Qe7 13. f3 Nf8 14. O-O g5 15. Bf2 Kg7 16. f4 $5 e4 17. Be2 gxf4 18. Bh4 Ng6 19. Bxf6+ Qxf6 20. Bh5 Bf5 $2 21. Bxg6 $16 {Tisdall,J: '1-0 Riemersma,L-Tozer,R/Edinburgh 1989/EXT 97 (60)' 1-0 Riemersma,L-Tozer,R/Edinburgh 1989/EXT 97 (60)}) 13. O-O g5 14. Bg3 Qe7 15. f3 Nh5 16. Bf2 Kg7 17. g4 Nf6 18. h3 Ng6 19. Bg3 Bd7 20. Rf2 $14 {Tisdall,J: '0-1 Blagojevic,D-Milanovic,V/Belgrade 1989/EXT 97 (47)' 0-1 Blagojevic,D-Milanovic,V/Belgrade 1989/EXT 97 (47)}) (6... b5 7. e3 Bb7 8. d6 $5 Qa5 $6 (8... h6 {Tisdall,J} 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Ne4 $132) 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd2 Bxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 12. cxb5 a6 13. Rc1 Qa3 14. b6 $16 {Tisdall,J: '1-0 Kortschnoj,V-Larsen,B/Sousse 1967/IZT (44)' 1-0 Kortschnoj,V-Larsen,B/Sousse 1967/IZT (44)}) (6... exd5 {Tisdall,J} 7. cxd5 h6 (7... d6 8. a3 Ba5 9. e3 Qb6 10. Qd2 Ne4 11. Qc2 Re8 12. Rc1 Bf5 13. Bd3 c4 14. Bxe4 Bxe4 15. Qd2 Nd7 16. O-O Nc5 17. Qd4 Bd3 18. Qg4 Qxb2 19. Bf6 Bg6 20. Bd4 Nb3 21. Rb1 Qc2 22. Nb5 Rad8 23. Bxa7 Qe4 24. Qg5 h6 25. Qg3 Qxd5 26. Nbd4 Bxb1 27. Rxb1 b6 28. Nxb3 cxb3 29. Nd4 b2 30. h4 Qa2 {0-1 (30) Rakic,M (2294)-Kosteniuk,A (2489) Belgrade 2013}) 8. Bh4 d6 9. e3 Bg4 10. Be2 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Nbd7 12. O-O Re8 13. Be2 a6 14. Qc2 Rc8 15. Rad1 Qe7 16. Bd3 Rc7 17. Bf5 g6 18. Bd3 Bxc3 19. Qxc3 Nxd5 20. Qc4 Ne5 21. Bxe7 Nxc4 22. Bxc4 Nxe7 23. Rxd6 Kg7 24. Rfd1 Nc6 $11 {Shulman,Y (2608)-Bhat,V (2511) ICC INT 2011}) 7. Rc1 {Hansen,Cu: 'The normal Leningrad-Nimzoindian move would be: ' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'The normal Leningrad/Nimzo-Indian move would be: ''} (7. e3 {Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu' Tisdall,J: 'but Timman probably fells that since Black has "wasted a move" with castling, he has the time to avoid the doubled c-pawns with the text move'} Bxc3+ (7... exd5 {Tisdall,J} 8. cxd5 Nbd7 9. Bd3 h6 10. Bh4 Qa5 (10... Nb6 11. e4 g5 12. Nxg5 $5 hxg5 13. Bxg5 Qe7 14. O-O Qe5 15. f4 Qd4+ 16. Kh1 Ng4 17. f5 Ne5 18. Qh5 f6 19. Bh6 Rf7 20. Rf3 $1 Bxf5 21. exf5 Nxf3 22. gxf3 Rh7 23. Ne2 {1-0, Babujian,L (2482)-Sibashvili,G (2274) Jermuk 2014}) 11. O-O Bxc3 12. bxc3 Qxc3 13. Bg3 (13. e4 Re8 14. Re1 Ng4 (14... c4 $142 $5 $15) 15. Bg3 Nge5 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. Bb5 Rd8 18. f4 Ng6 19. e5 dxe5 20. fxe5 a6 (20... Be6 $5 $13) 21. Bf1 b5 22. a4 Bb7 23. d6 Bc6 24. e6 $36 {Milov,V (2590)-Gipslis,A (2485) Biel 1994}) 13... Nxd5 (13... c4 14. Be2 Ne4 15. Rc1 Qa5 16. Bxc4 Nxg3 17. hxg3 Nc5 18. Qd4 Re8 19. Qf4 Qd8 20. Nd2 a6 21. Be2 b5 22. a4 Nxa4 23. Ne4 Re5 24. Rc6 Rxd5 25. Nxd6 Be6 26. Rfc1 {Babujian,L (2485)-Bartel,M (2613) Tromsoe olm 2010} Qg5 $132) 14. Bb5 (14. Bxd6 Rd8 15. Bb5 N7b6 16. Bc7 Rf8 17. Bd6 $11) 14... N5f6 (14... N7b6 $5 $11 {is a sensible attempt to transpose into the previous note}) 15. Bxd6 Re8 16. Rc1 Qa5 17. Qb3 Qb6 18. Rfd1 a6 19. Bxd7 Qxb3 20. axb3 Nxd7 21. Bxc5 Nxc5 22. Rxc5 Bg4 $11 {Le,Q (2710)-Leko,P (2740) Tromsoe olm 2014}) 8. bxc3 exd5 $5 9. cxd5 Bg4 10. Be2 Nbd7 11. O-O Re8 $132 {Tisdall,J: '1-0 Slipak,S-Tempone,M/Buenos Aires 1997/CBM 58 ext (25)' 1-0 Slipak,S-Tempone,M/Buenos Aires 1997/CBM 58 ext (25)}) (7. e3 {Hansen,Cu but Timman probably fells that since black has "wasted a move" with castles - he has the time to avoid the doubled c-pawns with the textmove}) 7... exd5 8. cxd5 Nbd7 9. e3 (9. a3 Ba5 $6 (9... Bxc3+ {Tisdall,J} 10. bxc3 h6 11. Bh4 Qa5 $36) 10. Nd2 Nb6 11. e4 Re8 12. Bb5 Bd7 13. Bxd7 Nbxd7 14. O-O Qb8 15. Qa4 Bxc3 16. Rxc3 $14 {Tisdall,J: '1-0 Barden,L-O'Hare,E/Varna 1962/MCD (35)' 1-0 Barden,L-O'Hare,E/Varna 1962/MCD (35)}) 9... h6 10. Bh4 Qa5 11. Nd2 b5 $1 {Hansen,Cu: 'Black has to play as active as possible. The basic idea of the Nimzoindian defence is to threaten to give white a doubled c- and to get better control over the central squares - particulary e4. But now the -structure is almost like in the Ben-Oni - which means the black-squared  - from a positional point of view - is quite misplaced on b4. However tactically b4 is still of good use for black as it plays a role in putting pressure on d5.' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'Black has to play as actively as possible. The basic idea of the Nimzo-Indian defence is to threaten to give White doubled c-pawns and to get better control over the central squares - particulary e4. But now the -structure is almost like in the Benoni - which means the black-squared  - from a positional point of view - is quite misplaced on b4. However tactically b4 is still of good use for Black as it plays a role in putting pressure on d5.''} (11... Bxc3 12. bxc3 (12. Rxc3 $6 Nxd5 13. Ra3 Qb4 14. Bg3 Ne5 $5 {and white's compensation is not apparent. Tisdall,J: 'and White's compensation is not apparent.'}) 12... b5 {could be very similar to the game but there is no reason to surrender the b4 so early or easily.} (12... Qxa2 13. Nc4 {[%csl Ra2]} (13. Ra1 {Hansen,Cu} Qb2 14. c4 $44)) (12... Nxd5 $4 13. Nc4)) 12. Be2 Bb7 (12... Bxc3 $5 {to grab the a-pawn looks playable, but also unnecessary;} 13. bxc3 Qxa2 14. Bg3 (14. Bxb5 Qxd5 15. Nf3 Qe6) 14... Ne5 (14... Qa6 $5) 15. c4 b4 16. Bxe5 dxe5 17. Ra1 Qb2 18. Rb1 Qa3 19. Ra1 $11) 13. e4 Rae8 14. f3 (14. Qc2 {[%csl Rd5,Re2][%cal Ge8e2] prevents the game continuation but leaves white with a problem to solve regarding castling - the looseness of the e2 means that the d5 pawn is tactically weak. Tisdall,J: 'prevents the game continuation but leaves White with a problem to solve regarding castling - the looseness of the e2 means that the d5-pawn is tactically weak.'}) 14... Nh5 {'!' Hansen,Cu.} 15. O-O {White finally gets his king to safety - with understandable relief as things are about to happen on f4 and f5. Was} (15. Bxb5 {possible?} Bxc3 (15... Nf4 16. Bxd7 Nxg2+ (16... Nd3+ 17. Kf1 Nxc1 18. Qxc1) 17. Kf2 Nxh4 18. Nc4 Qd8 19. Bxe8 Rxe8 {and black has some practical compensation for the exchange, but I don't believe this is enough. Tisdall,J: 'and Black has some practical compensation for the exchange, but I don't believe this is enough.'}) 16. Bxd7 Bxb2 $1 {leads to very worrying clouds gathering around the white king.} 17. Rb1 Nf4 18. Bxe8 Rxe8 $44 {[%csl Rd4,Rg2][%cal Ga6f1,Gb7a6]} (18... Bd4 $5)) 15... Nf4 {Hansen,Cu: 'White has been able to support the strong -chain supporting the -d5. However is has given black certain possibilities on the -side where f4 is now well-placed and the thrust f7-f5 - to undermine whites center-s is now in the air. The -structure is - as mentioned before - like in a Ben-Oni and just like in that opening the squares e5 and f4 make good outposts for blacks s ' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'White has been able to support the strong -chain supporting the -d5. However this has given Black certain possibilities on the -side where f4 is now well-placed and the thrust f7-f5 - to undermine White's central s is now in the air. The -structure is - as mentioned before - like in a Benoni and just like in that opening the squares e5 and f4 make good outposts for Black's s ' '} 16. Nb3 Nxe2+ 17. Qxe2 Qa6 {Hansen,Cu: 'Tactically black manages to hang on to his b5-' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'Tactically Black manages to hang on to his b5-''} 18. Rfd1 (18. Qxb5 Bxc3 $17) (18. Nxb5 {is very complicated but looks equally strange, walking into all sorts of pins in search of chaos.} Bxd5 (18... f5 $5) 19. Rfd1 {[%csl Rd6]} Nb6 $1 (19... Be6 20. Rxd6 Qxa2 21. Nc7 $1 Qxb3 22. Nxe6 $11) 20. Qf1 Qxa2 21. exd5 Qxb3 22. Nxd6 Re5 $17) 18... Bxc3 $1 19. bxc3 f5 {[%csl Ra2,Rd5,Re4][%cal Ra4h4,Re8e2] Now white's position comes under pressure from far too many directions.   Hansen,Cu: 'The exchanges has taken the dynamic out of whites position. His center is under attack and because of the misplaced b3 there is no active continution. The conclusion of the opening-battle seems to me to be: From a strategical point of view the build-up of the white -center f3-e4-d5 was a good idea. However that was only possible by a extremely slow build-up a1-c1, f3-d2, e2-e3-e4. Blacks just gained to much extra time and was tactically able to stop white from getting a harmoniously setup of his minor-pieces.' Tisdall,J: 'Now White's position comes under pressure from far too many directions.   Hansen,Cu: 'The exchanges has taken the dynamic out of White's position. His centre is under attack and because of the misplaced b3 there is no active continuation. The conclusion of the opening battle seems to me to be: From a strategic point of view the build-up of the white -centre f3-e4-d5 was a good idea. However that was only possible by an extremely slow build-up a1-c1, f3-d2, e2-e3-e4. Black has just gained too much extra time and is tactically able to stop White from getting a harmoniously setup of his minor-pieces.' '} 20. Nd2 Qa4 (20... Bxd5 {was possible;} 21. Qd3 (21. a4 $5) 21... Be6 22. a4 {creates some counterchances so black rules even this out. Tisdall,J: 'creates some counterchances so Black rules even here.'}) 21. c4 Ba6 {[%csl Rd3][%cal Ga6e2,Gd7e5]} 22. Bg3 {This induces black to give up the pressure against e4, but the time lost means that bad things will still happen elsewhere. White's main headache is keeping the black  out of d3. Tisdall,J: 'This induces Black to give up the pressure against e4, but the time lost means that bad things will still happen elsewhere. White's main headache is keeping the black  out of d3.'} (22. cxb5 Bxb5 23. Qe3 Ne5 $1 $17) 22... f4 23. Be1 Ne5 {[%csl Rc4,Rd3] Hansen,Cu: 'Blacks domination of the white squares is now much more important than the control of the black squares white might be able to get one fine day.' Tisdall,J: 'Hansen,Cu: 'Black's domination of the white squares is now much more important than the control of the black squares White might be able to get one fine day.''} 24. Rc3 Rb8 {Black continues the unpleasant practice of postponing strong moves in preference to maintaining the threat. There was no obvious drawback with the direct} (24... bxc4 $5 {since} 25. Nb1 (25. Ra1 {could transpose to the game.}) 25... Rb8 26. Ra3 {is refuted by} c3 $1 $19) 25. Ra1 bxc4 {The dreaded day has arrived for white, and d3 has fallen decisively into enemy hands. Tisdall,J: 'The dreaded day has arrived for White, and d3 has fallen decisively into enemy hands. '} 26. Nb1 Nd3 27. Na3 Rb2 28. Qd1 Qxd1 29. Rxd1 Rfb8 30. Nxc4 Bxc4 31. Rxc4 Nxe1 32. Rxe1 Rxa2 $19 {The domination of the second rank scores the point.} 33. g3 fxg3 34. hxg3 Rb3 35. e5 dxe5 36. Rd1 Rxf3 37. d6 Rxg3+ 38. Kh1 Rgg2 39. Rcc1 (39. d7 Rh2+ 40. Kg1 Rag2+ 41. Kf1 Rh1+ 42. Kxg2 Rxd1) 39... Rh2+ 40. Kg1 Rag2+ 41. Kf1 Rd2 42. Kg1 Kf7 0-1

