1. d4 {Baburin,A Huzman} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 6. c4 Nc6 7. Nc3 Rb8 8. h3 a6 9. e4 Nd7 $5 {Baburin: Opening theory regards this move as inferior to Black's common choice 9...b5. True to her style, Polgar chooses a fighting continuation and avoids the more played} (9... b5 {when Black can at most hope for a draw in an unpleasant endgame.} 10. e5 dxe5 (10... Nd7 11. e6 $1 fxe6 12. d5 exd5 13. cxd5 Na7 14. Nd4 Ne5 15. Nce2 Bd7 16. Nf4 Rxf4 17. Bxf4 c5 18. dxc6 Naxc6 19. Nxc6 Nxc6 20. Re1 Kh8 21. Rc1 Nd4 22. Bg5 Ne6 23. Qd2 Nxg5 24. Qxg5 Bf6 25. Qd2 b4 26. b3 a5 27. g4 Rb5 28. Re4 Kg7 29. Bf1 $16 {Sandstrom,L-Ernst,T/SWE-ch Haninge/1997/}) 11. dxe5 Qxd1 12. Rxd1 Nd7 13. e6 fxe6 14. cxb5 axb5 15. Bf4 b4 16. Na4 Nb6 17. Nxb6 Rxb6 18. Rac1 Bxb2 19. Bxc7 Ra6 20. Rc4 $36 {Vaganian,R-Nijboer,F/Antwerpen op/1997/}) 10. Be3 ({More aggressive and perhaps better is} 10. Bg5 $5 {The idea is to provoke Black to chase away the  with h6, later winning a tempo by attacking the pawn with d2.} Na5 $142 $5 (10... h6 $6 11. Be3 e5 (11... Na5 12. b3 c5 (12... b5 13. cxb5 axb5 14. Qd2 Kh7 15. Rfd1 e6 16. Rac1 b4 17. Ne2 Ba6 18. Nf4 e5 19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. Nxe5 Bxe5 21. Nd5 c5 22. Bxh6 $16 {Piket,J-Nijboer,F/Eindhoven ch-NL/1991/}) 13. Qd2 Kh7 14. Rac1 Nc6 15. Rfd1 e5 16. dxc5 dxc5 17. Nd5 Nd4 18. Ne1 b6 (18... f5 19. exf5 gxf5 20. f4 e4 21. Nc2 Nxc2 22. Rxc2 b6 23. g4 Nf6 24. Qe2 Qe8 25. Kh2 fxg4 26. Nxf6+ Bxf6 27. hxg4 Rg8 28. Bh3 Qg6 29. Rd6 $16 {Fernandez,F-Perez/Badalona op/1993/}) 19. Nd3 Bb7 20. Bxd4 cxd4 21. b4 Bc6 22. c5 b5 23. f4 $36 {Donchev,D-Ghinda,M/Prague zt/1985/}) (11... Kh7 12. Qc2 Na5 13. b3 b5 14. cxb5 axb5 15. Rad1 b4 16. Na4 Ba6 17. Rfe1 c6 18. h4 Kg8 19. e5 Nb6 20. Nxb6 Qxb6 21. h5 gxh5 22. Qf5 dxe5 23. dxe5 c5 24. Qxh5 Bb5 25. e6 $1 $40 {Horvath,I-Balazs,T/HUN-chT/1994/}) 12. Nd5 Na5 13. dxe5 Nxc4 14. exd6 Nxd6 15. Qc2 c6 16. Bf4 Nxe4 17. Bc7 Qe8 18. Nc3 Bxc3 19. bxc3 Ra8 20. Nd2 Nec5 21. Rfe1 Ne6 22. Bd6 Nf6 23. Nc4 Qd8 24. Rad1 Nd5 25. Bxf8 Nxf8 26. Ne3 $18 {Nikolic,P-Basagic,Z/JUG-ch Subotica/1984/}) 11. b3 c5 12. Qd2 Nc6 13. d5 Nd4 14. Nxd4 Bxd4 15. Rac1 b5 16. cxb5 axb5 17. b4 Ne5 18. Ne2 Bxh3 19. Nxd4 cxd4 20. Qxd4 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 f6 22. Bh6 Re8 23. Bf4 Ra8 24. Bxe5 fxe5 25. Qd2 Rf8 26. Rc6 Ra3 $132 {Smejkal,J-Sznapik,A/Amsterdam IBM-B/1980/}) 10... Na5 (10... e5 {Huzman} 11. Rc1 $14) 11. b3 (11. Qe2 b5 12. cxb5 axb5 13. Rac1 (13. Nxb5 $2 Ba6 14. a4 c6 $19) 13... c6 14. Rfd1 Nc4 15. b3 Nxe3 16. Qxe3 Qb6 17. Qd2 Ba6 18. b4 Qa7 19. a4 bxa4 20. Nxa4 Nb6 21. Nb2 $11 {½,Zander,A-Roos,M/OLO-A/1995/}) (11. Nd2 {Huzman} b5 (11... c5 12. dxc5 dxc5 13. f4 $14 {[%cal Ge4e5]}) 12. cxb5 axb5 13. a3 c5 $36) 11... b5 (11... c5 12. Rc1 b5 $5 (12... cxd4 13. Bxd4 Nc6 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Nd5 Nde5 16. Ne1 f5 17. f4 Nf7 18. exf5 Bxf5 19. Nc2 e6 20. Nde3 Qb6 21. Kh1 Bxc2 22. Nxc2 Rfe8 23. Qd2 Qd8 24. Ne3 h5 25. Nd1 Qf6 26. Nc3 Rbd8 27. Rcd1 Qe7 28. Rfe1 Qc7 29. Qb2 $16 {Steiger,W-Gersinska,J/Eppingen MB/1988/}) 13. cxb5 axb5 14. dxc5 dxc5 15. Qd2 c4 16. b4 Nc6 17. Nd4 Nxd4 18. Bxd4 Ne5 19. Rfd1 Be6 20. a4 bxa4 21. Nxa4 Nc6 22. Bxg7 Qxd2 23. Rxd2 Kxg7 24. Nc5 Rxb4 25. Na6 Rb5 26. Nc7 Rc5 27. Na6 Rh5 28. Nc7 Rc5 29. Na6 Ra5 30. Nc7 Ra4 $15 {Froehlich,P-Maiwald,J/Dresden op/1994/}) 12. cxb5 axb5 13. Rc1 $146 ({Baburin: It's quite interesting that the negative assessment of 9...d7 in Geller's monograph on the KID (the Russian edition of 1980) is based on the game Huebner-Hartston, 1968, which continued:} 13. Qd2 c6 14. Rfd1 (14. Bh6 {Baburin,A} b4 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Ne2 Nf6 17. Qe3 $16 {Baburin: It's probable that Huebner did not want to allow Black to come up with some improvement over that game and that was the reason for choosing 13 c1, a move which looks fine as well.}) 14... b4 15. Na4 c5 16. Nb2 Bb7 17. Ne1 cxd4 18. Bxd4 Nc5 (18... Bxd4 $142 19. Qxd4 Nc6) 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Qxb4 Kg8 21. Nc4 Nxc4 22. Qxc4 Qb6 23. Qd4 Bc6 24. Rac1 Qa5 25. Rc2 Rb4 26. Qd2 Qb6 27. Nd3 $16 {Calvo,J-Gamez,A/ESP-chT/1993/}) 13... b4 (13... c5 $5 $132 {-see Froehlich-Maiwald}) (13... Ba6 14. Re1 c5 15. Bf1 $1 $14 {[%cal Ra2a4] a4}) 14. Nd5 c6 15. Nf4 Ba6 16. Re1 Bb5 $6 (16... e5 $142 $5 17. dxe5 Nxe5 $132) 17. Nd3 (17. h4 $5 {[%cal Gh4h5] Huzman} e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. Nxe5 dxe5 (19... Bxe5 20. Nd3 Bc3 21. Bd2 Bxd2 22. Qxd2 $16 {[%csl Ra5]}) 20. Ne2 Nb7 $11 {[%csl Rb7,Re2]}) 17... c5 $5 {Otherwise Black would have to admit 16...b5 just lost a tempo.} (17... Ba6 18. Qc2 $36) (17... Bxd3 18. Qxd3 $16 {[%csl Rc6,Gd4,Ge3,Ge4,Gg2] ,×c6}) 18. dxc5 Bc3 (18... Bxd3 {Huzman} 19. Qxd3 Nxc5 20. Bxc5 dxc5 21. Qxd8 Rfxd8 22. Rxc5 $16) 19. Bd2 $6 ({Stronger and more testing was the sacrificial  Baburin: It is hard to understand why White did not offer an Exchange sacrifice by playing} 19. cxd6 $142 $5 Bxe1 (19... exd6 20. Bd2 $16) 20. Nfxe1 (20. Ndxe1 $16 {Baburin,A}) 20... Nc6 (20... Bxd3 21. dxe7 (21. Qxd3 {Huzman} Ne5 (21... exd6 22. Qxd6 Re8 23. Rd1 $5 Nf8 24. e5 $44) 22. Qd5 exd6 23. f4 Nd7 24. Qxd6 $44) 21... Qxe7 22. Nxd3 $16) (20... exd6 21. Nxb4 $16 {[%csl Ga2,Gb3,Rd6] ×d6,a,b}) 21. dxe7 Qxe7 22. Nf4 $40) 19... Bxd2 20. Qxd2 Bxd3 21. Qxd3 (21. c6 $4 Nc5 $19) 21... Nxc5 22. Qe3 {[%cal Re4e5] e5} Ne6 {[%csl Rb4,Rf6,Rg7,Rh6] ×b4 Black's -side is slightly weakened, potentially also b4 can be a problem. Although the position is rather simplified and Black has a compact pawn structure, he must defend very carefully, as White's active pieces can pose dangerous threats.His extra central pawn can make in impact in an endgame, but the game will never get that far.} 23. Qh6 $14 {Baburin: Occupation of the h6 square here has the following reasons: White wants to advance his h-pawn while at the same time preventing ...h7-h5; then he might play g2-h3 and if necessary f3-g5.} (23. Nd4 Qb6 $5 (23... Nxd4 24. Qxd4 Qb6 25. Qxb6 Rxb6 26. e5 $1 $36) 24. Nxe6 Qxe3 25. Rxe3 fxe6 26. Rc7 Rb7 $11) ({Baburin: However, the central strategy might be even more fruitful here and White should seriously consider playing} 23. e5 $1 {Baburin,A} d5 24. Red1 {Baburin: with strong pressure in the centre.}) 23... Nb7 $6 {Baburin: The misplaced knight must come back to defend!} (23... Rc8 $142 $5) 24. h4 (24. e5 $142 $5 {[%cal Rc1c4,Rc4h4] c4-h4} d5 25. Red1 Qa5 26. Rxd5 $1 Qxa2 (26... Qxd5 27. Ng5 $18) 27. Rc4 $40) 24... Qa5 25. Bh3 (25. e5 Qxa2 26. exd6 Nxd6 27. Rxe6 Nf5 $1 $17) 25... Nbd8 (25... Qxa2 $2 26. Bxe6 fxe6 27. Ng5 Qxf2+ 28. Kh1 Rf7 29. Rf1 $18 (29. Rf1 {Huzman} Qxf1+ 30. Rxf1 Rxf1+ 31. Kg2 $18)) (25... Nbc5 {Huzman} 26. Re2 $36) 26. Re2 Rb5 27. Rc4 Rc5 28. Rxc5 (28. e5 $6 {Huzman} Rxc4 29. bxc4 dxe5 30. Nxe5 Nd4) 28... Qxc5 {Baburin: Somehow White has let Black out and the lion's share of his advantege has gone now. Black has survived the first wave of the attack, but White's initiative is far from over.} 29. Kg2 (29. e5 Qc3 (29... Nd4 $2 30. Ng5 Nxe2+ 31. Kg2 $18) (29... d5 30. Rd2 $14) 30. Kg2 dxe5 31. Rxe5 Qb2 $11) 29... Qc3 30. h5 (30. e5 dxe5 31. Rxe5 Qc2 $132) 30... Qf6 (30... Qg7 31. Qd2 $16) 31. e5 (31. Qd2 Nc6 $11) 31... dxe5 32. Rxe5 (32. Nxe5 Nc5 (32... Nd4 33. Re4 $1 $40) 33. Qe3 Ndb7 $132) (32. Nxe5 $5 {Baburin,A}) 32... Nc6 33. hxg6 hxg6 (33... Qxg6 $2 34. Rxe6 fxe6 35. Bxe6+ Rf7 36. Qxg6+ hxg6 37. Ng5 $18) 34. Re4 (34. Rxe6 fxe6 35. Ng5 Qxf2+ 36. Kh1 Qe1+ 37. Kh2 Rf2+ (37... Qe2+ 38. Bg2 Qh5+ 39. Qxh5 gxh5 40. Bxc6 $14) 38. Bg2 Rxg2+ (38... Qc3 39. Qh7+ Kf8 40. Qxg6 $18) 39. Kxg2 Qe2+ $11) 34... Ncd4 (34... Ned4 $2 35. Rf4 Nf5 36. Bxf5 gxf5 37. Qxf6 exf6 38. Rxf5 $18) 35. Ng1 $1 {Clever piece play by White, always creating new threats.} (35. Nxd4 Nxd4 36. Rh4 (36. Qe3 $14 {Huzman}) 36... Qf3+ 37. Kh2 Qxf2+ 38. Kh1 Qe1+ $11) (35. Ne5 {Huzman} Rd8 36. Qe3 $132) 35... Rd8 (35... Ra8 36. Bxe6 Nxe6 37. Rxb4 $14) (35... Rb8 36. Qd2 $36 {[%csl Rb4] ×b4}) 36. Bxe6 Nxe6 37. Nf3 $6 {White should have finally transformed his initiative into material, now Black's tenacious defence extricates him from danger.} (37. Rxb4 $142 $1 Nd4 (37... Qb2 38. Nf3 $16 Qxa2 $140 39. Rh4 Qb2 40. Nd4 $18) 38. Rc4 Qf5 (38... Qa6 {Huzman} 39. Qe3 $16) 39. Qe3 $14) 37... Rd3 38. Ne5 Qf5 $1 (38... Ng5 {Huzman} 39. Nxd3 (39. Rc4 Rd8 $17) (39. Re2 Rd5 40. f4 Rxe5 $1 41. Rxe5 Qc6+ 42. Kf2 Qf3+ 43. Ke1 Ne4 $1 $19) 39... Qf3+ 40. Kh2 Nxe4 41. Qe3 $1 $14 {[%csl Rb4]}) (38... Rd4 {Huzman} 39. Qe3 $14) 39. Re1 Rd5 (39... Rc3 $142 $5 $15) (39... Rc3 $1 $15 {Huzman}) 40. Nc6 Rd7 41. Re5 $1 ({Not} 41. Nxb4 $2 Ng5 $40 {Now the game becomes rich with tactics.}) 41... Qf6 42. Qe3 (42. Rb5 Nf4+ 43. Kh2 $1 Ne6 $11 44. Qe3 (44. Rb8+ {Huzman} Rd8 45. Nxd8 Qxf2+ $11) 44... Nd4 $132) 42... Rd1 $5 (42... Nf4+ 43. Qxf4 Qxc6+ 44. Qe4 $11) 43. Re4 (43. Nxb4 $4 Qh8 $19) 43... g5 (43... Qa1 $5 44. Rxb4 Rg1+ 45. Kh2 $11 (45. Kf3 Qd1+ 46. Qe2 Ng5+ 47. Ke3 Re1 $19)) 44. Rxb4 (44. Nxb4 $4 Qh8 $19) 44... Qh6 45. Rh4 $8 Qf6 46. Rb4 (46. Rh1 $2 Nf4+ $1 47. gxf4 Qxc6+ 48. f3 Qc2+ 49. Qf2 Rd2 $19) (46. Rh3 g4 $1 (46... Nf4+ $2 47. gxf4 Qxc6+ 48. Qf3 $16) (46... Rd6 {Huzman} 47. Ne5 Qf5 $44 {[%csl Rh3]}) 47. Rh6 Qa1 48. Qe4 Ng5 49. Nxe7+ Kf8 50. Qa8+ Kxe7 51. Qa7+ Kf8 (51... Rd7 $2 52. Qc5+) 52. Qb8+ (52. Qc5+ Ke8 53. Qb5+ $11) 52... Ke7 $11) 46... Qh6 47. Rh4 Qf6 48. Rb4 Qh6 1/2-1/2

