1. e4 {Ftacnik} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qb6 5. Nb3 a6 (5... d6 {Ftacnik} 6. Bd3 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. Be3 Qc7 9. f4 Nc6 10. Nc3 Bd7 11. g4 h5 12. h3 hxg4 13. hxg4 g5 14. fxg5 Nh7 15. g6 fxg6 16. Nb5 Qb8 17. Bf4 Ng5 18. Kg2 Ne5 19. Be2 Bc6 20. Bxg5 Bxg5 21. Qd4 Bxb5 22. Bxb5+ Ke7 {0-1 Shetty,R-Vaidya,A/Calcutta (10)/1992 (33)}) 6. Nc3 d6 (6... Qc7 {Ftacnik} 7. Be2 Nf6 8. O-O b5 9. Bf3 Nc6 10. g3 Bb7 11. Bg2 d6 12. Re1 Be7 13. a4 b4 14. Na2 O-O 15. Bf4 Rfd8 16. Qd2 Rab8 17. Rac1 Ne5 18. Nxb4 Bxe4 19. Nxa6 Qb7 20. Rxe4 Nxe4 21. Nxb8 Nxd2 22. Bxb7 Nxb3 23. cxb3 Rxb8 24. Rc8+ Rxc8 25. Bxc8 {1-0 Anand,V-Polgar,J/Monaco blind (02)/1995 (32)}) 7. Bf4 Nf6 8. Qd2 Be7 9. O-O-O O-O 10. g4 $5 (10. Bxd6 {Ftacnik} Bxd6 11. Qxd6 Qxf2 $13) 10... Nxg4 11. Rg1 Ne5 (11... Nxf2 $2 {Ftacnik} 12. Be3 $18) (11... Nf6 {Ftacnik} 12. Bh6 Ne8 13. Bxg7 Nxg7 14. Qh6 $18) 12. Qe2 Qc7 13. Be3 b5 14. f4 Nc4 (14... Ng6 {Ftacnik} 15. f5 $40) 15. Bd4 g6 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6] Some holes appear in the Black position.} (15... e5 {Ftacnik} 16. Nd5 Qd8 17. Qg2 g6 18. fxe5 $16) 16. Qh5 $5 {The queen is getting close to Black's king and the promising h6-square.} Nd7 $2 ({More critical would have been} 16... Nc6 $1 {hitting the dangerous d4-bishop, as GM Seirawan correctly pointed out in 'Inside Chess' magazine. Then the play may continue:} 17. f5 (17. Rd3 $1 {Ftacnik} Nxd4 (17... e5 18. Nd5 Qb7 19. f5 Kh8 20. Qh6 Rg8 21. Rh3 Rg7 22. Bf2 $40) 18. Rh3 Nxb3+ 19. axb3 Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kf6 21. e5+ dxe5 22. Ne4+ Kf5 23. Bd3 $18) (17. Qh6 $6 Nxd4 18. Nxd4 Bf6) 17... Nxd4 18. fxg6 (18. Nxd4 $6 Bf6 {is slow and therefore is not satisfactory for White.}) 18... Nxb3+ 19. axb3 fxg6 20. Rxg6+ $1 {It looks to me that this sharp line should result in a draw:} (20. Bxc4 $2 {is not good because of} Bf6 $1 ({but not} 20... bxc4 $2 {when White transposes into a favourable line:} 21. Rxg6+ Kh8 22. Qh6)) {After the inferior move} 20... Kh8 $2 {White's attack is indeed very dangerous:} (20... hxg6 $1 {Strangly enough Seirawan did not consider this move, although the text makes a lot of sense - grabbing as much material as possible and giving some of it back later can be a good defensive technique in such positions.} 21. Qxg6+ Kh8 22. Qh6+ Kg8 {and now White has to settle for a draw by perpetual check -} 23. Qg6+ ({as after} 23. Bxc4 $2 Bf6 {White has insufficient compensation for the rook.})) 21. Bxc4 bxc4 22. Qh6 Rf7 23. Rdg1 Bb7 24. Rg7 Bg5+ 25. R7xg5 {for example:} cxb3 26. Qxe6 bxc2 27. Nd5 Bxd5 28. Qxd5 $16) 17. Qh6 (17. Rd3 $5 {appears to be more direct. Black has only one defence:} e5 {(Seirawan)} (17... Bf6 $2 18. Rh3 Rd8 19. Rxg6+ fxg6 20. Qxh7+ Kf8 21. Bxf6 $18 (21. Bxf6 Nxf6 22. Qxc7 $18)) (17... Nf6 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Rh3 Re8 20. Qxh7+ Kf8 21. Rxg6 $18) (17... Re8 $4 18. Qxh7+ $1 $18) (17... Rd8 18. Qxh7+ $1 Kxh7 19. Rh3+) 18. Nd5 Nf6 {However, after} (18... Qd8 19. Rh3 $18) 19. Nxc7 Nxh5 20. Nxa8 exd4 21. f5 {White keeps the advantage.} (21. Nc7 {Ftacnik} Nxf4 $14)) 17... Bf6 (17... Nf6 $5 {Ftacnik} 18. Bxc4 (18. Rd3 e5 $1) 18... bxc4 19. e5 Ne8 20. Nd2 $13) 18. Rd3 Bxd4 19. Nxd4 (19. Rh3 $2 {Ftacnik} Be3+ 20. Kb1 Nf6 $19) 19... Re8 20. Rh3 Nf8 ({Black had another defensive idea as well:} 20... Nf6 $5 {Here the knight is more vulnerable than on f8, but on the other hand it covers the d5-square, thus making ...e6-e5 possible. The following continuation might be possible:} 21. Bxc4 Qxc4 22. e5 dxe5 (22... Qxd4 $2 23. Ne2 $1 $18) 23. fxe5 Qxd4 24. exf6 Qxf6 25. Ne4 Qh8 $1 (25... Qg7 26. Qg5 e5 27. Rxh7 $1 $40) 26. Qg5 e5 27. Nf6+ Qxf6 28. Qxf6 Bxh3 $13) (20... Nf6 {Ftacnik}) 21. e5 $1 d5 (21... dxe5 {Ftacnik} 22. Ne4 Nd7 23. Rxg6+ fxg6 24. Qxh7+ Kf8 25. Qh8+ Ke7 26. Rh7+ Kd8 27. Nxe6#) 22. Bd3 (22. Bxc4 $1 {would have been more energetic:} {After} Qxc4 (22... bxc4 $2 23. Nd1 $1 {with a further Nd1-e3-g4-f6.} (23. Nf5 f6 24. Nd6 Rd8 {is not so clear.})) 23. Nce2 {White's attack is going to succeed, for example:} Qxa2 24. Nf3 Qa1+ 25. Kd2 Qxb2 26. Ng5 $18) (22. Nf5 {Ftacnik} f6 (22... exf5 23. Nxd5 Qc6 24. Bxc4 (24. Nf6+ $4 Qxf6 25. exf6 Re1#) 24... Re6 25. Bb3 $18) 23. Nh4 Qg7 $15) 22... Nxe5 $1 {This sacrifice is Black's best chance.} (22... Bb7 {Ftacnik} 23. Nce2 $40) (22... b4 {Ftacnik} 23. Nf5 $3 f6 24. Ne4 dxe4 25. Bxc4 fxe5 26. Qg5 $16) 23. fxe5 Qxe5 24. Qh4 Bb7 $2 ({According to Seirawan, Black's best bid was in} 24... Qg7 {when the positions remains unclear. On b7 the bishop is misplaced; as a matter of fact, it did not move any more in this game.}) (24... Qc7 {Ftacnik} 25. Nf5 exf5 26. Nxd5 Qe5 27. Nf6+ Kg7 28. Nxe8+ Qxe8 29. Qh6+ Kg8 30. Re3 Qc6 31. h4 $16) 25. Rf3 Rac8 (25... Qc7 {Ftacnik} 26. Nce2 Rac8 27. Qf6 $16) 26. Nce2 Rc7 {xf7} 27. Rf6 Ree7 (27... Qd6 {Ftacnik} 28. Nf5 $1 Qc5 29. Nh6+ Kg7 30. Rgf1 $16) 28. Kd2 {xe3} Red7 (28... Nd7 $2 {Ftacnik} 29. Rfxg6+ fxg6 30. Qxe7 $18) 29. a3 $1 Kg7 (29... Qd6 {Ftacnik} 30. Nf5 Qc5 31. Nh6+ Kg7 32. Ng4 $16) 30. Nf4 Rc8 (30... Qxf6 {Ftacnik} 31. Nh5+ $18) (30... Qxd4 {Ftacnik} 31. Nh5+ $18) 31. Rg5 $1 $18 Qc7 (31... Qd6 {Ftacnik} 32. Nf5+ exf5 33. Rxd6 $18) 32. Nfxe6+ Nxe6 (32... fxe6 {Ftacnik} 33. Rxf8 $1 $18) 33. Rxe6 (33. Nxe6+ {Ftacnik} fxe6 34. Bxg6 Kh8 35. Qh6 $14) 33... Qa5+ 34. b4 Qxa3 35. Nf5+ Kg8 (35... Kh8 {Ftacnik} 36. Qd4+ $18) 36. Ne7+ Rxe7 (36... Kg7 {Ftacnik} 37. Qd4+ Kh6 38. Nf5+ Kxg5 39. Qh4#) 37. Rxe7 Qc3+ 38. Ke2 d4 39. Qf4 1-0

