1. e4 {[%mdl 512]} c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {This variation is rather risky, and cannot be the objectively best move, but may lead to immense tactical complications favouring Judit's style.} e6 4. Nc3 (4. Be3 Qb6 $5 {- Mitkov-Dreev, EUCup Neum BIH 2000 CBM 80}) 4... Bb4 5. Bf4 Nf6 (5... Qa5 $143 $6 {is useless because of} 6. Nge2 Nf6 7. a3 Be7 {and Black had to retreat.} 8. Ng3 h5 9. Bd3 h4 10. Nge2 Nbd7 11. e5 Nh5 12. Be3 b5 13. g4 $1 $16 {Gallagher-Preziuso, Ticino 1994}) (5... dxe4 $143 {gives up the tension in the  too early.} 6. fxe4 Nf6 7. Qd3 $14 O-O 8. a3 (8. Nf3 c5) 8... Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qa5 10. e5 $1 Nd5 11. Bd2 $14) (5... Ne7 $5 {is safe and logical.} 6. Nge2 (6. Qd3 {The  is rather vulnerable here.} b6 { a6 activating the passive .} 7. Nge2 a5 8. a3 Ba6 9. Qe3 Bd6 10. Ng3 Bxf1 11. Rxf1 Bxf4 12. Qxf4 Ng6 13. Qe3 O-O 14. O-O-O b5 $132 {Geenen-Arlandi, Athens 1997}) (6. Qd2 O-O 7. Nge2 Nd7 8. a3 Ba5 9. e5 (9. g3 {This is not a quiet positional system!} c5 $1 10. exd5 cxd4 11. Qxd4 (11. dxe6 $2 {is not correct:} dxc3 12. exf7+ Rxf7 13. Nxc3 Kh8 14. O-O-O Rf8 15. Bc4 Ng6 {White has no compensation for the piece.} 16. Bd6 Rxf3 17. Qe2 Qg5+ 18. Kb1 Qe3 $19 {W.Watson-Sigfusson, Reykjavik 1989}) 11... Nxd5 $11) 9... c5 {In a French type of position the  on f4, the  on e2 are misplaced.} 10. dxc5 Bc7 11. Nd4 Bxe5 12. Bxe5 Nxe5 13. f4 Nd7 14. b4 b6 15. c6 Nb8 16. b5 a6 $15 {and White cannot hold his "Vorpost" anymore, Gallagher-Khenkin, Geneve 1995.}) 6... Ng6 (6... O-O 7. a3 (7. Qd2 {- 6.d2}) 7... Ba5 8. Qd3 { 0-0-0 making things more wild.} Nd7 9. Bd6 Bc7 10. Bxc7 Qxc7 11. O-O-O b5 12. Qe3 a5 $13 {making profit from the weakness of a3, Murrey-Kharitonov, Moscow 1989.}) (6... Nd7 7. a3 Ba5 8. Qd3 dxe4 9. fxe4 e5 {Black wants to gain control over × e5.} 10. dxe5 Ng6 11. e6 fxe6 12. Bd6 Nde5 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. Qg3 Ng6 $15 {Ainsa-Izeta, Benasque 1996}) 7. Bg3 dxe4 8. fxe4 e5 9. Qd3 Bg4 10. a3 Ba5 11. dxe5 Nd7 12. h3 Bxe2 13. Bxe2 Ngxe5 {The  is excellent on e5.} 14. Qe3 Qb6 15. Qxb6 Bxb6 16. O-O-O f6 $15 {Mitkov-Magem Badals, San Sebastian 1993}) 6. Qd3 b6 (6... c5 7. a3 Qa5 8. Bxb8 {Usually this exchange is not a big deal.} Rxb8 9. e5 Nd7 10. Rb1 Bxc3+ 11. Qxc3 Qb6 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. Qd4 Bd7 14. f4 {We have reached a typical classical French position.} Rc8 15. Qb4 Qc7 16. Nf3 O-O 17. Rd1 $11 (17. c3 $2 {is met by the activization of the black .} Ba4 $1 { c2} 18. Rc1 b5 19. Nd2 f6 $17 {Vallejo Pons-Garcia Padron, Ponferrada 1997})) (6... O-O 7. Nge2 c5 (7... b6 {- 6...b6}) 8. O-O-O cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nbd7 10. Ndb5 e5 11. Bg5 d4 12. Nd5 Be7 13. Nxe7+ Qxe7 14. Qa3 Qxa3 15. Nxa3 Nb6 16. Nc4 Nxc4 17. Bxc4 {The  is a bit better for White because of the .} Nd7 18. Be7 Re8 19. Ba3 $14 {Murey-Hodgson, Sevilla op 1987}) 7. Nge2 (7. O-O-O Ba6 8. Qe3 Bxf1 9. Rxf1 O-O 10. e5 Nfd7 11. Bg5 Be7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. f4 {This is another French structure with more real chances for White on the , than Black's on the other flank.} c5 14. Nf3 Nc6 15. Nb5 (15. h4) 15... a6 16. Nd6 Nxd4 17. Nxd4 cxd4 18. Qxd4 f6 $13 {Hodgson-Orr, London 1988}) 7... O-O (7... Ba6 8. Qe3 O-O (8... Nbd7 $6 {is met by} 9. e5 {and the  has to retreat.} Ng8 10. a3 Be7 {Now the  follows him...} 11. g4 Bh4+ 12. Bg3 Bxg3+ 13. Nxg3 Bxf1 14. Rxf1 Qe7 15. O-O-O b5 16. Nb1 a5 17. f4 b4 18. f5 $16 {Gallagher-Dunn, London 1993}) (8... Bxc3+ 9. Nxc3 Bxf1 10. Rxf1 O-O (10... Qd7 11. O-O-O O-O 12. g4 {Here in the  race White comes first on the .} Rc8 13. Bg5 Ne8 14. f4 dxe4 15. f5 $16 {Gallagher-Kelecevic, Bad Ragaz 1994}) 11. O-O-O Ne8 12. Rfe1 (12. e5 Qc7 13. Kb1 c5 14. Nb5 (14. Ne2 Nc6 15. dxc5 Na5 {Black sacrifices the  in order to open lines against the enemy .} 16. cxb6 axb6 17. Nd4 Nc4 18. Qb3 Qb7 $44 {Gallagher-Groszpeter, Lenk 1992}) 14... Qc6 15. Nd6 Nd7 $13) (12. Bg5 $5) 12... Nd6 13. Qd3 Nc4 $13) 9. O-O-O c5 (9... Be7 10. g4 $5 b5 11. Ng3 b4 12. Nce2 Bc4 $13 {with immense complications, Wall-Hodgson, GB-ch 1990.}) (9... Nbd7 10. h3 Rc8 11. a3 Be7 (11... Bxc3 12. Nxc3 Bxf1 13. Rhxf1 b5 14. Bd6 {The  works well here.} Re8 15. e5 Nb6 16. b3 Nfd7 17. f4 $16 {Murey-Saidy, New York 1989}) 12. g4 Ne8 13. g5 Nd6 14. Bxd6 Bxd6 15. h4 {(W.Bauer-Nickel, corr 1990/91)} b5 $1 16. e5 Be7 $13 {Seirawan}) 10. a3 {- 7...0-0} (10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Qd2 Nc6 {Black has successfully finished his development.} 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. Qxd5 Qxd5 15. Rxd5 Nb4 $11 {Nolan-Hopper, Largs 1998})) 8. O-O-O c5 9. a3 c4 $2 {This closes the position and gives White free hand in the  and on the .} (9... Bxc3 $143 10. Nxc3 cxd4 11. Qxd4 Nc6 12. Qa4 $16) (9... cxd4 $143 10. axb4 dxc3 11. Nxc3 Bb7 (11... Qe7 12. Bg5) 12. Bg5 $16) (9... Ba6 $1 {was more to the point activating the passive .} 10. Qe3 cxd4 (10... Bxc3 11. Nxc3 (11. Qxc3 Qe7) 11... Bxf1 12. Rhxf1 Nc6 $13) 11. Nxd4 Bxc3 12. Qxc3 Bxf1 13. Rhxf1 Qc8 $1 $11 {Conquest-T.Ravi, Madras 1994}) 10. Qe3 Be7 11. g4 {Judit's favourite attacking move!} Bb7 (11... dxe4 $6 12. fxe4 {and White has already a strong .} Bb7 (12... Nxg4 $2 13. Qg3 Na6 14. Rg1 $18) 13. h3 $16) 12. Ng3 dxe4 (12... Na6 13. g5 Nd7 (13... Ne8 14. exd5 exd5 (14... Bxd5 15. h4 $16 {}) 15. Nf5 $1 {White's pieces are dominating the board.} Bd6 16. Bxd6 Nxd6 17. Ne7+ Kh8 18. Nexd5 $16) 14. exd5 (14. Re1 Re8) 14... exd5 15. Re1 (15. Nf5 Re8 16. Re1 Bf8 {- 15.e1}) 15... Re8 16. Nf5 Bf8 17. Qxe8 Qxe8 18. Rxe8 Rxe8 19. Bh3 $14 {× d5, passive b7 .}) 13. g5 $5 (13. fxe4 {was also promising, for example:} Nxg4 14. Qf3 Nf6 15. Bxc4 $16) 13... Nd5 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 (14... exd5 15. Nf5 {Now} (15. fxe4 c3 (15... Re8 16. Bg2 $14) 16. Qxc3 Bxg5 17. Qe3 Bxf4 18. Qxf4 dxe4 19. Rg1 Kh8 20. Nf5 g6 21. Nd6 $16) 15... c3 $6 {is not dangerous:} 16. Qxc3 Bxg5 17. Qe3 Bxf4 18. Qxf4 Qf6 (18... exf3 19. Nxg7 $1 Kxg7 20. Rg1+ Kh8 21. Qh6 Rg8 22. Bd3 $18) 19. Rg1 g6 20. fxe4 dxe4 21. Bc4 $44) 15. fxe4 $16 Qb5 16. d5 {White's advance in the  is combined with  threats.} Nd7 17. Qc3 Rfc8 18. Nh5 Bf8 (18... e5 19. Bxe5 Bxg5+ 20. Kb1 $18) 19. Bd6 $1 {× g7} e5 20. Bxf8 $1 (20. Bxe5 {wins the , but loses the , and this is not exactly Judit's style!} Nxe5 21. Qxe5 Qe8 $1 22. Qxe8 Rxe8 23. Bg2 Re5 24. h4 h6 $14) 20... Rxf8 (20... Kxf8 21. Bh3 $18) 21. Bh3 $1 Rfe8 22. Rhg1 $1 (22. Bxd7 Qxd7 23. Ng3 $16) 22... Nf8 (22... Nc5 $2 {is met by the devastating} 23. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 24. gxf6+ Kh8 (24... Kf8 25. Rg8+ $1) 25. Bf5 Rg8 26. Rxg8+ $18) 23. Rdf1 Ng6 (23... Bc8 24. Qf3 Re7 25. Bxc8 Rxc8 {is punished by} 26. Nxg7 $1 $18) 24. Bf5 Qc5 25. Qf3 Rf8 (25... Qe7 $2 26. d6 $1 Qxd6 27. Bxg6 Qxg6 28. Nf6+ $18) 26. c3 Rad8 27. Qg3 Bc8 28. Qh3 {White's pieces are gathering for the final assault on the .} Kh8 (28... Bxf5 29. exf5 Nf4 {is again met by the opening of the g  after} 30. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 31. gxf6+ Kh8 32. Qh6 Rg8 (32... Qe3+ 33. Kb1 Qd3+ (33... Rg8 {- 32...g8}) 34. Ka2 $18) 33. Rg7 $1 Qe3+ 34. Kb1 Qd3+ 35. Ka2 Qxf5 36. Rfg1 Nh5 (36... Rgf8 37. R7g5 $16) 37. R1g5 Qc2 38. Rxh7+ Qxh7 39. Rxh5 $18) 29. Rg4 Bxf5 ({There is no time for} 29... b5 $2 {because of} 30. Nxg7 $1 Kxg7 31. Qh6+ Kg8 32. Rh4 $18) 30. Rxf5 (30. exf5 {was also strong enough, for example:} Nf4 31. Nxf4 exf4 32. Rgxf4 Qxd5 (32... Rxd5 33. Rh4 $18) 33. Rd4 Qc5 34. Rh4 $18) 30... Kg8 (30... b5 31. Kb1 $1 {The last preparatory move before the final blow.} a5 32. Nf6 $1 gxf6 33. gxf6 Nf4 34. Rfxf4 exf4 35. Rg7 $18) 31. Kb1 $1 (31. Nf6+ {is premature:} gxf6 32. gxf6 Rfe8 $15) 31... Rfe8 32. Rg3 $1 { gf3 × f7 in case.} Qd6 {} (32... Qf8 33. Rgf3 $1 (33. Nf6+ gxf6 34. gxf6 Kh8 $1) 33... Rd7 (33... Re7 34. Nf6+ $1 {gaining a vital tempo.} gxf6 35. gxf6 Red7 36. Rh5 $18) (33... b5 34. Nxg7 $1 {simply winning material.} Qxg7 35. Rxf7 Qh8 36. Rc7 Nf4 37. Rxf4 $1 exf4 38. Qf5 $18) 34. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 35. gxf6 Qc5 36. Rh5 $18) (32... Nf4 {and} 33. Nf6+ $1 $18) (32... Rd6 {are also met by} 33. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 34. gxf6 Rdd8 35. Rh5 Qd6 (35... Qf2 36. Rxh7 Qxf6 37. Qh6 Rd6 38. Rg5 $18 { f5}) 36. Rf3 $1 Qd7 (36... Nf8 37. Rxh7 $18) 37. Rff5 Qa4 38. Kc1 $1 $18) 33. Nxg7 $1 $18 {This is the final blow.} Nf4 (33... Kxg7 34. Qh6+ Kg8 35. Rh3 $1 Nf8 (35... Rd7 36. Qxh7+ Kf8 37. Rh6 $18) (35... Re7 36. Rf6 Qc7 37. Qxh7+ Kf8 38. Rxg6 $1 $18) 36. g6 $1 $18 {is really pretty.}) 34. Nxe8 Rxe8 35. Qh6 Qd7 36. Rg1 Nxd5 37. g6 Nxc3+ 38. bxc3 Qd3+ 39. Kb2 {This is a typical attacking game by the best female player of the world.} 1-0

