{[%evp 0,47,80,23,28,34,10,12,56,31,50,50,43,31,58,42,65,24,36,15,25,-2,3,-16,-30,-41,-41,-49,-126,-317,-299,-274,-132,-81,-81,-81,-94,-390,-52,144,144,172,172,187,202,189,167,183,155,193]} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 (4. e5 {is the another main continuation.}) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bxf6 Bxf6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nd7 9. O-O-O Be7 {This variation used to be a specialty of Evgeny Bareev, who has since retired from tournament activity. Nowadays Matthias Bluebaum plays it many times.} 10. Bd3 {A natural move,} ({but} 10. Bc4) ({and} 10. h4 {are also popular.}) 10... b6 ({Just a few weeks earlier, went} 10... c5 11. Nxc5 Nxc5 12. dxc5 Qd5 13. Kb1 Qxc5 14. h4 {Black should have played the prophylactic} h6 (14... b6 $2 {was careless, as} 15. Ng5 $1 h6 16. Bh7+ Kh8 17. Be4 Rb8 18. Qf4 $1 $18 {with attacking the b8-rook and the f7-pawn simultaneously, Topalov,V (2743)-Bareev,E (2729) Monte Carlo blind 2003.}) 15. g4 Bd7 16. g5 h5 {with a defensible position, Carmeille,L (2297)-Poetsch,H (2198) Germany 2007.}) 11. Neg5 $5 {White is starting an actions on the kingside without preliminary preparations. A new move in very popular position. White is starting an actions on the kingnside without preliminary preparations.} (11. h4 {is the another even more popular continuation.} Bb7 12. Rh3 (12. Qe2 Nf6 13. Neg5 Bxf3 14. Nxf3 Qd6 15. Ne5 c5 16. dxc5 Qxc5 17. Kb1 Bd6 18. Nc4 Rfd8 19. g4 Bf8 20. Nd2 $14 {Van den Doel,E-Sakalauskas,V/Bled 2002}) (12. c3 c5 13. Neg5 (13. dxc5 Qc7 14. Neg5 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qf4+ 16. Kb1 Bxc5 17. g3 Bxf3 18. Nxf3 Qc7 $11 {Al Modiahki,M-Arizmendi Martinez,J/Biel 2002}) 13... Nf6 14. Qc2 g6 15. h5 Nxh5 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Bxg6 Nf4 18. Bxh7+ $40 {Ivanchuk,V-Radjabov,T/Moscow 2002}) 12... c5 13. Rg3 Qc7 14. dxc5 Rfd8 15. Nfg5 $1 bxc5 16. Nd6 Bxd6 17. Bxh7+ Kf8 18. Qxd6+ Qxd6 19. Rxd6 $16 {Gashimov,V-Dizdar,G/Dubai 2003}) (11. Qf4 $5 Bb7 12. h4 Nf6 13. Neg5 Qd6 14. Ne5 Rad8 15. Rh3 h6 16. g4 c5 17. Ngxf7 Qxd4 18. Qxd4 Rxd4 19. g5 $36 {Guliev,L-Gouliev,N/Baku 2001}) 11... h6 {This is not a mistake yet, but it shows Black reluctance to exchange his bishop for the g5-knight, an attitude that will soon makes problems for him.} (11... Bxg5 {was the safer answer,} 12. Nxg5 Nf6 13. Nf3 Bb7 $11 {with a balanced position, Gashimov,V (2594)-Bareev,E (2688) Gothenburg 2005.}) 12. Bh7+ {'!' A brave decision, though thanks for this idea White won so beautifully. It's very important to drag black king to h8 for the further combination. Ftacnik: 'The dreams of a great chessplayers include the desire to ocassionally win the game stright from the preparation. In a well known position Polgar comes with a concept of violent attack on the black king with little concern for the material sacrifices. '} (12. h4 $5 {was also possible to keep the tension.} Bb7 13. Bh7+ Kh8 14. Be4 Bxe4 15. Nxe4 c5 16. d5 exd5 17. Qxd5 Nf6 18. Qf5 Qc8 19. Qxc8 Rfxc8 20. Nxf6 Bxf6 21. g4 $14 {Al Modiahki,M-Abdul,M/Doha 2002}) 12... Kh8 13. Be4 hxg5 $2 {The decisive mistake. Probably Black saw White's reply, but he could underestimated that. My guess is that black overlooked Judit's reply, otherwise he wouldn't dare to take the knight which was beging to be taken.} ({In case of} 13... Rb8 {White would keep the initiative with} 14. h4 $36 Ba6 (14... Nf6 15. Ne5 Nxe4 16. Ngxf7+ Kg8 17. Nxd8 Nxd2 18. Ndc6 $18) 15. Qf4 $36) ({The best reply was} 13... Bxg5 $1 14. Nxg5 Rb8 15. Nf3 Nf6 $11 {with an approximately equal position, Volokitin,A (2652)-Nielsen,P (2663) Germany 2004.}) 14. g4 $3 {'!!' What a brilliant move! White's idea is to open the h-file and create a mating attack against the black king. Black has to spend a tempo to save his rook, allowing White to play h4. Brilliant move! White's idea is to open an h-file and deliver a mate. For this purpose white is ready to waste a precious tempo. However this tempo doesn't help black to build up a defensive line up on the kingside since his rook is attacked and he has to move it.} ({The greedy} 14. Bxa8 $2 {could have been met by} g4 $19 {'' followed by ...g5 and suddenly Black is winning.}) 14... Rb8 (14... c6 {clears the c7-square for the queen, but concretely, there is no way that Black can use this small difference compared to the game.} 15. h4 g6 (15... gxh4 16. g5 $18 {to closing the diagonal of the black bishop and queen. This is also hopeless for Black.}) 16. hxg5+ Kg7 17. Qf4 Rh8 18. Rxh8 Qxh8 {the same move as in the similar line below still works, for slightly different reasons:} 19. Ne5 Nxe5 20. Rh1 Qxh1+ 21. Bxh1 Nd7 22. Bxc6 $18 {and the black rook is trapped.}) 15. h4 g6 ({It's hard to believe that black would've survived also after} 15... gxh4 {doesn't help either, as} 16. g5 f5 (16... Kg8 17. Qf4 f5 18. Bc6 Kf7 19. Qxh4 Bd6 20. Rde1 $40) 17. Qf4 $1 {The shortest way for the quuen to the h-file.} fxe4 18. Qxh4+ Kg8 19. Qh7+ Kf7 20. Qh5+ g6 (20... Kg8 21. g6 $18 {and mate.}) 21. Qh7+ Ke8 22. Qxg6+ Rf7 23. Rh7 {It seems that it is all over, but Black can still try to resist by returning a large amount of material.} Bxg5+ 24. Nxg5 Qxg5+ 25. Qxg5 Rxh7 26. Qg6+ Rf7 27. Qxe6+ Re7 28. Qg6+ Rf7 29. Qxe4+ {Black stands all right materially, but his coordination is poor.} Kd8 (29... Kf8 30. Rh1 $18) 30. Re1 $18 {and White’s attack is winning.}) ({Or if} 15... f5 16. hxg5+ Kg8 17. gxf5 Rxf5 (17... exf5 18. Bd5+ Rf7 19. g6 $18) 18. Bxf5 exf5 19. Qf4 $18 {The material count is more or less equal, but White's attack is decisive.}) 16. hxg5+ Kg7 17. Qf4 $1 {Once again, the best square for the queen transfer. On the way to the h-file, it also puts some pressure on the queenside. White’s attack may seem a bit slow, but Black’s development is even slower! He has a wide choice, but no saving move. Ftacnik: 'It is almost sure that white had this very position on the board during preparation, black pieces are too passive for the sucessful coping with the threats along the open h file.'} Bb7 {Black continues the development and hoping to exchange at least one of White's attacking pieces - the bishop.} (17... Rg8 {allows the smart finish by} 18. Rh7+ $1 Kxh7 19. Qxf7+ $18 {followed by 20.h1 mate.}) ({With Black's major pieces incompletely coordinated, Black would not be successful in his fight for the h-file: An only move not to lose on the spot was} 17... Rh8 {'' however it wouldn't influence the outcome of the game. however it wouldn't influence an outcome of the game.} 18. Rxh8 (18. Rh6 $6 Bxg5 19. Nxg5 Rxh6 20. Qxf7+ Kh8 21. Qf4 Qf8 22. Nf7+ Kh7 $17) 18... Qxh8 19. Ne5 $1 {Despite being a piece down, White keeps attacking with quiet moves. This is possible only because Black is seriously behind in development.} Bxg5 {happened in a game,} (19... Qe8 20. Rh1 $18 {and Black faces with undefendable threats. and black is helpless against h2.}) (19... Nxe5 20. Qxe5+ Kg8 21. Qxc7 Bxg5+ 22. Kb1 $18 {unexpectedly traps the rook. This line illustrates the lack of communication between Black’s opposite wings.}) (19... Qg8 20. Rh1 {Another apparently peaceful move. It may seem that Black can neutralize the attack with:} Bd6 {Black's position seemt to be just okay, but White is ready with some new tactical fireworks.} 21. Rh7+ $3 {It is quite a beautiful one, moreover the only winning move!} Kf8 (21... Kxh7 {leads to forced mate:} 22. Qh2+ Kg7 23. Qh6#) (21... Qxh7 22. Qxf7+ Kh8 23. Nxg6+ $18) 22. Rh8 $1 {and White wins the queen, since} Qxh8 23. Qxf7#) 20. Qxg5 Qh6 21. Qxh6+ Kxh6 22. Nxf7+ Kg7 23. Ne5 $18 {and White had a clear pawn up, Kim,J (2058)-Vignesh,B (2317) Ho Chi Minh City 2019.}) ({Another apparently logical try which would fail because of Black’s lack of development is:} 17... Bd6 18. Ne5 Bxe5 (18... Be7 {could have been met by} 19. Nxf7 $1 Bxg5 (19... Rxf7 20. Rh7+ $1 Kxh7 21. Qxf7+ $18 {and 22.h1 mate.}) 20. Qxg5 Qxg5+ 21. Nxg5 $18 {and White wins again.}) 19. dxe5 {Threatening with 20.h7 xh7 21.h2 g7 22.h6 g8 23.h1+-.} Rh8 20. Rh6 $1 {White maintains the tension, leaving Black completely paralyzed.} ({However the smart} 20. Qf6+ $18) ({and} 20. Rxh8 Qxh8 21. Bc6 $18 {are also winning.}) 20... c5 (20... Bb7 21. Rxd7 $1 {Another nice tactical blow!} Qxd7 22. Qf6+ $18) 21. Bc6 Rxh6 22. gxh6+ Kf8 23. g5 $18 {There is no adequate defence against 24.xd7 followed by 25.f6.}) 18. Rh7+ $3 {'!' Another fantastic tactical blow, which made possible by the fact that the white bishop is still alive and ready to sacrifice on g6 when required.} Kxh7 ({Obviously} 18... Kg8 {is hopeless, because of} 19. Rdh1 $18 {and mate soon.} f5 20. gxf6 Bxf6 21. Rh8+ Bxh8 22. Rxh8+ Kxh8 23. Qh6+ Kg8 24. Qxg6+ Kh8 25. Qh7#) 19. Qh2+ Kg8 ({The bishop’s lethal action is revealed after:} 19... Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kg8 21. Bxg6 $1 Bxg5+ 22. Nxg5 Qxg5+ 23. Qxg5 fxg6 $6 24. Qxg6+ Kh8 25. d5 $1 {Opening the d-file for a decisive invasion.} Bxd5 26. f3 $1 {The second case of interference on the long diagonal.} Bxf3 27. Rxd7 $18 {and mate.}) 20. Rh1 Bxg5+ 21. Nxg5 Qxg5+ ({In the event of the black king tries to escape with} 21... Kg7 {then the following combination must have come as a cold shower:} 22. Nxe6+ $3 {Weakening the g6-pawn and opening the seventh rank, os the black king cannot escape on e7.} fxe6 23. Qh7+ Kf6 24. g5+ $1 {The final strike and the only winning move!} Kxg5 25. Qh4#) 22. f4 Qxf4+ {Otherwise checkmate on the h-file.} 23. Qxf4 Bxe4 24. Qxe4 {and Black resigned. The positon is approximate material equality, but the black king remains hopelessly vulnerable. What a nice victory by Judit Polgar!} 1-0

