1. Nf3 {HarZvi} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. d4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 {HarZvi: this line becomes very popular lately,probably because it has not been analysed as much as all other lines 9.e1,9.d2, but still it is a very interesting line which goes direct for the c5 break} Nh5 (9... a5 {HarZvi}) 10. Re1 {HarZvi: this is a very new move played first in 95 according to my data but here too the main line was 10.g3 which was analysed very deeply} f5 (10... h6 {HarZvi} 11. Nd2 Nf4 12. Bf1 f5 (12... c6 13. dxc6 (13. a4 $5 Kh8 14. a5 cxd5 15. cxd5 f5 16. Nc4 g5 17. b5 Rf6 18. b6 a6 19. Qb3 Rg6 20. Ba3 $16 {Piket,J-Xie Jun Monaco rapid 1996}) 13... Nxc6 (13... bxc6 14. Ba3 $14) 14. a3 a5 15. Rb1 axb4 16. axb4 Nd4 17. Nb3 Nfe6 18. Nb5 Bd7 (18... Nxb5 $6 19. cxb5 b6 20. Rb2 $1 $16) 19. N3xd4 Nxd4 $1 (19... exd4 $6 20. Na3 $1 Ba4 21. Qd2 $16) 20. Nxd4 exd4 21. Bf4 g5 $6 (21... Be5 22. Bg3 Qf6 $10) 22. Bg3 $14 {Lalic,B-Atalik,S Hastings 1995 65/(579) [Lalic,B]}) 13. c5 g5 (13... fxe4 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Ndxe4 Nf5 16. g3 Nh5 $13 {/ Tunik,G-Fedorov,V Vladivostok 1995}) 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Nc4 g4 16. b5 Rf6 17. Qb3 Kh7 $6 (17... Kh8 $14) 18. b6 a6 19. Ba3 $16 {Sokolov,I-Van Wely,L Nederland (ch) 1995 64/552 [Sokolov,I]}) (10... Nf4 {HarZvi} 11. Bf1 Bg4 (11... h6 12. Nd2 {transposes to 10...h6}) (11... f5 $6 12. Bxf4 $1 exf4 13. e5 dxe5 (13... g5 14. Nxg5 dxe5 15. c5 $1 h6 16. Bc4 $1 hxg5 17. d6+ Kh8 18. dxe7 Qxe7 19. Nd5 $16 {Lalic,B-Gicev,B Skopje 1995 65/(579)}) 14. Nxe5 a5 {Komarov,D-Oral,T Leeuwarden 1995 65/(579)} 15. c5 $1 axb4 16. Bc4 $16) 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 f5 14. Qd1 $1 fxe4 (14... Qe8 15. exf5) 15. Nxe4 h6 (15... Nf5 $2 16. g3 Nh5 17. g4) 16. Rb1 $1 Qd7 17. Qg4 $1 $16 {Tukmakov,V-Cvitan,O Zurich 1995 65/579 [Tukmakov,V]}) 11. Ng5 Nf6 (11... Nf4 {HarZvi} 12. Bxf4 exf4 13. Rc1 Kh8 {Sokolov,I-Wiersma,E Nederland (ch) 1995 64/551 [Sokolov,I]} (13... a5 14. b5 Bf6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Bxc3 17. Rxc3 fxe4 18. Bg4 $14 {/ Tukmakov,V-Rupp,M Portoroz 1996}) 14. Bf3 $1 a5 (14... Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Qxg5 16. Nxc7 Rb8 17. Qxd6 Rd8 18. Ne6 $16) 15. b5 {with e6}) 12. Bf3 {This is a new plan here with the idea of e6 after which the bishop on f3 is rather well-placed. The traditional 12.f3 leads to a complicated struggle with temporarily pawn sacrifice after Ne6. Black has no standard attack on the kingside, White gets the initiative, and Black has to make some ugly defensive moves. Psychologically this is not very easy for most of the attacking Kings Indian players.} (12. f3 {HarZvi} c6 $1 {HarZvi: this is white's problem after 12.f3 that is way in recent games we only see 12.f3} (12... Kh8 $6 13. Be3 Neg8 (13... Bh6 14. Nf7+ Rxf7 15. Bxh6) 14. c5 Bh6 15. exf5 $1 gxf5 16. f4 $16 {Piket,J-Polgar,J Aruba (m/3) 1995 65/578 [Piket,J]}) 13. Kh1 (13. Be3 Bh6 $1 14. Bd2 cxd5 15. cxd5 fxe4 16. fxe4 Qb6+ {1-2/1-2 Panno,O-Ricardi,P Boenos Aires Najdorf 1995}) 13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Ne8 16. Qb3 Nc7 17. Be3 Kh7 18. c5 d5 19. exd5 cxd5 20. Rad1 d4 $13 {Degraeve,JM-Howell,J/ Groningen (open) 1995/ ½-½ (42)}) 12... Kh8 (12... fxe4 {HarZvii: Two rounds before Keamnik played this variation against Gelfand who played 12...fxe4 although the game ended in a draw Kramnik got a nice position out of the opening}) (12... c6 13. Bb2 $1 {White is preparing for an open struggle in the centre, which is a bit unusual here.} (13. Qb3 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 Rf6 18. Bb2 (18. b5 Qc8 $1 $15 {Dautov}) 18... Rxe6 19. Rad1 Qc7 20. Qh3 Rf6 21. f4 Rxf4 22. Qe6+ Kh7 23. Qxd6 Rc8 $10 {Astrom-Relange, Yerevan (ol) 1996}) 13... h6 (13... cxd5 14. exd5 (14. cxd5 fxe4 15. Ngxe4 (15. Ncxe4 Nxe4 16. Nxe4 { 15.ge4} (16. Bxe4 h6 17. Ne6 Bxe6 18. dxe6 d5 19. Bc2 Qd6)) 15... Nxe4 16. Nxe4 Nf5 17. a4 Nh4 18. Be2 Bf5 19. Bd3 Rc8 20. a5 a6 21. Rc1 $14 {I.Sokolov-Temirbaev, Yerevan (ol) 1996}) 14... e4 15. Be2 {is better for White according to Dautov.}) (13... Kh8 { 12...h8}) 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Bxe4 $1 (16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Rxe4 {Dautov,R-Kindermann,S Nussloch 1996} (17. Bxe4 $6 d5 $1) 17... Nf5 18. b5 Rc8 19. Re2 Re8 20. bxc6 bxc6 21. c5 d5 22. Bxe5 Bxe5 23. Rxe5 Qf6 {1/2 Dautov-Kindermann, Nussloch 1996}) 16... d5 (16... Nxe4 17. Nxe4 d5 18. Nc5 $1 $16) 17. cxd5 cxd5 18. Bc2 Qd6 19. Bb3 Qxe6 20. Na4 e4 21. Nc5 Qf5 22. f3 Kh7 23. fxe4 Nxe4 24. Bxg7 Qf2+ 25. Kh1 Kxg7 26. Ne6+ $16 {Tukmakov-Hulak, Yerevan (ol) 1996}) (12... fxe4 {HarZvi} 13. Ngxe4 Nf5 14. Bg5 Nd4 15. Nb5 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 Bf5 17. Qb3 Bxe4 18. Rxe4 Qd7 19. Bxf6 Rxf6 20. Rae1 $14 {/ Kramnik,V-Gelfand,B Vienna 1996}) 13. Bb2 a5 (13... c6 14. Qb3 cxd5 15. cxd5 fxe4 16. Ncxe4 Nfxd5 (16... Nxe4 17. Bxe4) 17. Nxd6 Qxd6 18. Rad1 Rxf3 19. Qxf3 Be6 20. Qf7 $1 $16) 14. b5 $1 Rb8 (14... b6 15. exf5 gxf5 16. Ne6 Bxe6 17. dxe6 e4 18. Bh5 Nxh5 19. Qxh5 Qc8 20. Nxe4 $1 $16) 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Ne8 (16... f4 17. c5 dxc5 18. Na4 $16) (16... Qc8 {HarZvi} 17. Nd5) 17. Qb3 c5 (17... Qc8 18. c5 dxc5 19. Na4 (19. Rad1 {HarZvi} Nd6 20. b6 {HarZvi: with more than a very good compensation for the pawn}) 19... b6 20. exf5 Nxf5 21. Bxe5 $16) 18. exf5 gxf5 (18... Nxf5 19. Nd5 Nc7 20. Nxc7 Qxc7 21. b6 Qe7 22. Bd5 $16) (18... Nxf5 {HarZvi}) 19. Bh5 {HarZvi: with more than a very good compensation for the pawn} Nc7 20. Bf7 Qc8 21. Nd5 Ncxd5 22. cxd5 $16 f4 (22... b6 23. f4) 23. Bh5 $1 (23. Rad1 Rxf7 24. exf7 Qf5) 23... b6 24. Rad1 Rf5 25. Bf3 Qg8 26. Be4 Rg5 {HarZvi: white advantage is very big,now he should put a rook on h3 put preasure on h7 and then try to break with g3,it is very simple plan} 27. Rd3 Bf6 28. Rh3 Ng6 29. Qf3 Rb7 30. Rh5 {HarZvi: h5 will be afantastic place for the queen} Rxh5 31. Qxh5 Rg7 32. g3 $18 Ne7 33. Kh1 Qf8 34. Rg1 {HarZvi: the threat gxf4 then xg7 and xf6} Ng8 {defending f6} 35. Qf3 Bg5 36. h4 Bf6 37. Rg2 Qe7 38. Qh5 fxg3 39. fxg3 Qf8 40. Rf2 Qd8 41. g4 Be7 42. g5 c4 43. Rf7 Qe8 44. Qxh7+ (44. Qxh7+ {HarZvi} Rxh7 45. Rxh7#) 1-0

