{[%evp 0,101,20,17,92,39,25,10,12,-3,-10,2,9,-23,25,8,24,22,16,16,33,26,65,48,62,55,97,90,99,111,111,91,102,102,181,194,57,57,94,71,71,-13,4,-5,-11,-90,-54,-100,-83,-22,39,-39,131,131,131,115,93,104,109,109,109,106,101,103,125,128,151,129,122,128,148,148,164,158,164,164,182,182,198,135,143,137,142,138,259,244,276,246,264,279,302,287,267,330,285,285,548,542,634,641,829,770,848,998]} 1. Nf3 c5 2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 e6 6. a3 {Dieser Zug war von Speelman in 1988 in Turnierpraxis eingeführt worden. Mit dieser Fortsetzung verhindert Weiss den Zug b4.} Qc7 $5 {In the spirit of the Taimanov Sicilian, which was not a rare guest in Judit's practice. However, here it is an uncommon sideline. Dieser Zug ist ein seltener Gast in der Turnierpraxis. Die Alternativen scheinen mir besser zur Erlangung von Ausgleich geeignet zu sein.} (6... Be7 {is the main reply.} 7. e4 (7. e3 d5 (7... O-O 8. Be2 d5 9. cxd5 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 exd5 12. O-O Bf6 13. Qd3 Be6 14. Bd2 Bxb2 15. Rab1 Qf6 16. Qb3 Be5 17. Qxb7 Bf5 {½-½ Barlov,D-Matamoros Franco,C/Cala Galdana ESP 1999 (22)}) 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bb5 Bd7 10. Nf3 a6 11. Be2 Bg4 12. h3 Bh5 13. Nh4 Bxe2 14. Nxe2 Ne4 15. Nf3 Bf6 16. O-O O-O 17. Rb1 Qd6 $11 {Kortchnoi,V-Sax,G/Wijk aan Zee (m/9) 1991 (17)}) 7... O-O 8. Be2 (8. Nf3 d6 9. Be2 b6 10. Bf4 Bb7 11. Qd3 Qb8 12. Rd1 Ne5 13. Qb1 Ng6 14. Bg3 Nh5 15. Qc1 Nxg3 16. hxg3 Bf6 {Kortschnoj,V-Akesson,R/Antwerp op 1995/½-½ (51)}) 8... d5 9. exd5 exd5 10. O-O Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Be6 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 (13... Bxd5 {½-½ Kortschnoj,V-Kasparov,G/Horgen CS 1994}) 14. Be3 Bf6 15. Qxd5 Bxd5 {½-½ Panno,O-Kasparov,G/Buenos Aires 1997/EXT 98 (15)}) (6... Nxd4 7. Qxd4 b6 $11 {[%cal Gf8c5,Gc5d4]}) 7. Ndb5 ({In case of} 7. e4 {'?!'} Nxe4 $1 {would be the point.} (7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Bc5 $11) 8. Nxe4 (8. Nxe6 dxe6 9. Nxe4 Be7 {''} 10. Bd3 O-O 11. O-O b6 $15) 8... Qe5 {''} 9. Nb5 Qxe4+ 10. Be2 {occurred in Zeng - Fang, Hefei 2011. Black can improve his play by} Qe5 $146 11. g3 Qb8 12. Bf4 e5 13. Be3 d6 14. O-O Be7 $132) (7. e3 a6 8. Be2 b6 9. O-O Bb7 10. b3 (10. Nxc6 Bxc6 11. b3 Be7 12. Bb2 O-O 13. Bf3 b5 14. cxb5 axb5 15. Qe2 d5 {½-½ Averbakh,Y-Mikenas,V/Moscow 1955/URS-ch (24)}) 10... Be7 11. Bb2 O-O 12. Rc1 Rac8 13. Nf3 Qb8 14. h3 Rfd8 15. b4 d6 16. Qb3 Nd7 {½-½ Xu Jun-Wojtkiewicz,A/Manila 1992/TD (31)}) (7. Bg5 a6 (7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 (9. Qf4 Qxf4 10. Bxf4 O-O 11. Nb5 e5 12. Bxe5 Bxf2+ 13. Kxf2 Ng4+ 14. Kg3 Nxe5 {½-½ Hauchard,A-Berend,F/Brussels 1993/CBM 34 ext (47)}) 9... a5 {[%csl Gc5]} 10. e3 b6 11. Be2 Bb7 $11 {½-½ Hauchard,A-Chomet,P/Meribel 1998/CBM 67/[Ribli] (95)}) 8. Bxf6 (8. e3 Be7 9. Rc1 O-O 10. Be2 b6 11. Bf4 Ne5 12. O-O Bb7 13. Bg3 {1-0 Wojtkiewicz,A-Przewoznik,J/POL-ch 1992/CBM 29 ext (81)}) 8... gxf6 9. e3 f5 10. Be2 Bg7 11. O-O f4 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. exf4 Qxf4 14. c5 Rb8 {½-½ Chuchelov,V-Baklan,V/Vlissingen NED 1999 (34)}) 7... Qb8 8. g3 {Nach diesem Zug droht Weiss 9.f4 zu spielen. (Es ist anzumerken, dass es ähnliche Motive in der Sizilianischen Verteidigung gibt: z.B. nach 1.e4 c5 2.f3 e6 3.d4 cxd4 4.xd4 c6 5.c3 c7 6.g3, wonach 6...f6 nicht so gut wäre wegen 7.f4 und 8.db5, so dass Schwarz mit 6...a6 fortsetzen muss).} a6 9. Nd4 (9. Bf4 $2 e5 {[%csl Rb5,Rf4][%cal Ra6b5,Re5f4]} 10. Bg5 axb5 11. cxb5 Nd4 $17 {Weiss kann dann mit xf6 und g2 fortsetzen, aber seine Kompensation ist auf jeden Fall nicht ausreichend.}) 9... Nxd4 {Schwarz möchte seine Stellung mit diesem Springertausch befreien.} (9... Qc7 10. Bg2 Be7 11. O-O Ne5 12. Bf4 d6 13. Rc1 O-O 14. Na4 Ng6 15. Be3 Ng4 16. Bd2 Bf6 17. e3 N4e5 18. c5 $1 $14 {1-0 Salov,V-Yudasin,L/Madrid 1992/CBM 30/[Ribli] (59)}) (9... d6 10. Bg2 Bd7 11. O-O Be7 12. b3 O-O 13. Bb2 Rc8 14. e3 $14 {1-0 Anic,D-Koch,J/Narbonne 1997/CBM 61/[Ribli] (45)}) 10. Qxd4 b5 $5 (10... b6 {'!?' looks more solid. Diese genügsame Fortsetzung mit der Idee c5 scheint mir logischer als der Textzug.} 11. Bg2 Bc5 12. Qd3 Bb7 13. e4 {and only now} b5 $1 {The pawn is basically poisoned.} 14. O-O (14. cxb5 axb5 15. Qxb5 $4 Qa7 $40) (14. Bf4 e5 15. Bg5 bxc4 16. Qxc4 Bd4 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. O-O Bc6 $13) 14... bxc4 15. Qxc4 Bd6 16. Be3 O-O 17. Rfd1 Be5 $11) 11. e4 $1 {'N' White proceeds dinamically to justify his power in the centre. Actually, this move was already a novelty at that time. Weiss muss energisch spielen, nach 11.g2 hat Schwarz keine Probleme.} (11. Bg2 Bb7 12. O-O Bxg2 13. Kxg2 Qb7+ 14. f3 bxc4 15. Qxc4 Be7 $15 {happened in Tunik - Dvoirys, Moscow 1996.} 16. b4 d5 17. Qd3 O-O 18. Bb2 Nd7 19. Na4 Qb5 {0-1 Tunik,G-Dvoirys,S/Moscow 1996/EXT 99 (48)}) 11... e5 $6 {Although the idea of e4-e5 had to be treated, weakening the d5-square was far from forced. Black intends to handle the game in a very direct way, but it eventually backfired.} (11... b4 {was the precise move order.} 12. axb4 (12. e5 $2 {'!?'} Ng4 (12... bxc3 13. exf6 cxb2 14. Bxb2 $14)) 12... e5 $1 {Circumstances are different to the line with 12...b5-b4 discussed below, because Black obtains more activity.} 13. Qd3 Bxb4 14. Bg2 h6 15. O-O O-O 16. Be3 d6 $132) (11... bxc4 {Ftacnik} 12. e5 Ng8 $1 {A not too appealing, but necessary retreat. In return there will be some counterplay on the long diagonal.} (12... Nd5 $2 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. Bg2 Bb7 15. O-O Qa7 16. Qxa7 Rxa7 17. Rd1 $16 {'=' was mentioned by Ftacnik.}) 13. Ne4 Bb7 14. Bxc4 (14. Bg2 f5 $1 15. exf6 Nxf6 16. Nxf6+ gxf6 17. Bxb7 Qxb7 18. O-O Bg7 {with an acceptable position.}) 14... Qa7 $1 15. Qd3 Qb6 {The queen comes out like a snake. Once it lands on c6, Black could start consolidating his passivity.} 16. O-O Qc6 17. f3 Rc8 18. b3 (18. Qb3 f5 19. exf6 Nxf6 $132) 18... f5 19. Nd6+ Bxd6 20. exd6 Nf6 $13) (11... Bd6 $5 {came into consideration as well.} 12. cxb5 O-O 13. bxa6 Be5 14. Qb4 Bxa6 15. Qxb8 Rfxb8 16. Bxa6 Rxa6 {would give the position rather a character of the Benko Gambit.} 17. f3 g5 $1 $44) 12. Qe3 Qc7 $6 {'!?' Complicating the matters seems a bit daring. Schwarz spielt scharf und opfert einen Bauern.} ({In the event of} 12... b4 {White has already the extra opportunity of} 13. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 14. cxd5 {''} a5 (14... Qc7 15. Bd2 bxa3 16. bxa3 Bc5 17. Qc3 d6 (17... Qd6 18. Rc1 Bb6 (18... Bd4 19. Qxc8+ Rxc8 20. Rxc8+ Ke7 21. Rxh8 Qb6 22. Bd3 Bxf2+ 23. Kd1 $1 $16 (23. Ke2 $2 Bg1 $1 $44)) 19. f4 $1 f6 20. Qb4 $16) 18. Be3 Bd7 19. Bxc5 Qxc5 20. Qxc5 dxc5 21. f4 f6 22. Rb1 Ke7 23. Kd2 Rhb8 24. Bc4 Kd6 25. f5 Bb5 26. Kc3 $36) 15. Bd2 Be7 16. Bd3 O-O 17. O-O $14 {Anyway, this would have been less evil than the game.}) (12... bxc4 {Ftacnik} 13. Bxc4 Bb7 14. Qd3 $36 {'' and the d5-square remains a long-term strategical problem for Black.}) 13. cxb5 Bc5 (13... axb5 {Ftacnik} 14. Bxb5 Bb7 15. O-O $16) 14. Qf3 {A very logical continuation.} ({On} 14. Qd3 axb5 {is the correct answer.} (14... Ng4 {was recommended in an old analysis, which is wrong due to} 15. Qf3 $1 Nxf2 16. Bc4 O-O 17. Rf1 $18) (14... Qb6 {[%cal Rb6f2]}) 15. Nxb5 Qb6 16. Be3 Qa5+ 17. Nc3 Ba6 18. Qd2 Bb4 19. f3 Bxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 21. bxc3 d5 22. exd5 Nxd5 23. Bd2 Bxf1 24. Rxf1 Kd7 25. Kd1 (25. Ke2 Ra4 26. Rfb1 Rha8 27. Rb3 Kc6 $44 {with a huge activity for the pawn.}) 25... Rhc8 26. Kc2 Ra6 $44) 14... Bd4 {Das ist schwarze Hauptidee. Der Bauer auf e4 ist schwach in dieser Stellung.} 15. Bg5 $1 {'!' Ftacnik. The beginning of a well-estimated line. Ftacnik: 'The opening becomes exceptionally tactical, both sides are playing with great phantasy.' Weiss muss so spielen; es entsteht sehr scharfes Spiel.} (15. bxa6 {Ftacnik gives nothing special on account of} Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Qc6 $44 17. Bg2 Bxa6 $14) 15... Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Nxe4 $2 {'!?' Knowing her style, the best female player of all times gladly provokes a tactical battle. However, this concrete resource turned out to be a premature. Sonst kann Weiss xf6 spielen, mit deutlichem Positionsvorteil.} ({The evaluation of} 16... axb5 $3 {Ftacnik is surprisingly not that obvious. One would consider it as quite strange at first glance. Indeed, it contains a lot of complications, where Black's play is amazingly difficult. Undertaking double pawns and many other unpleasant things are far from the normal attitude.} 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Bxb5 Ba6 (18... f5 19. O-O Rb8 20. Rab1 $16) 19. Bxa6 (19. c4 Bxb5 20. cxb5 {Here I hardly believed to my eyes! Even though White has an extra pawn and besides two connected passers, the computer jauntily parries the danger:} Qc4 $8 21. Rb1 Ra4 $8 22. Qe2 (22. b6 Ke7 23. b7 Rb8 24. Rb6 d6) 22... Qxe4 23. Qxe4 Rxe4+ 24. Kd2 Ke7 25. b6 Rb8 26. b7 Ra4 27. Rb3 Ra7 28. Rhb1 d6 {followed by ...d7-c7 is also fine for Black.}) 19... Rxa6 20. O-O {looks like a hard task for the second player either, though there is some light at the end of the tunnel.} Ke7 21. Rfd1 (21. h4 Rha8 22. Qh5 Rxa3 23. Rxa3 Rxa3 24. Qxh7 Rxc3 25. h5 Rc1 26. Rxc1 Qxc1+ 27. Kg2 Qg5 {In spite of the well-advanced pawn, Black should be able to hold the queen ending. In my opinion, it is impossible from a human to foresee everything so long and clear!} 28. h6 Qg4 29. f3 Qg5 30. Qg7 Qd2+ 31. Kh3 Qd1 32. h7 Qxf3 33. h8=Q Qh1+ 34. Kg4 Qxe4+ $11) (21. Rfb1 Rha8 22. Rb3 Ra4 $44) 21... Rha8 22. c4 Qxc4 23. Rd3 {A lot of positive details are on White's side, such as having a passed pawn, better structure, safer king. On the other hand, converting them would be not that straightforward at all.}) 17. Qxe4 Bb7 (17... Qxc3+ 18. Ke2 Qxa1 (18... Qb2+ 19. Bd2 $16) 19. Qxa8 $18 {''}) 18. Qb4 f6 19. b6 $1 {A pure intermediation that creates a dangerous passed pawn.} Qc6 (19... Qc8 {does not help either.} 20. Be3 Bxh1 21. O-O-O Qc6 (21... Bf3 22. Rd6 O-O 23. c4 $18) 22. Bh3 Bd5 23. c4 a5 24. Qb5 $18) 20. O-O-O $1 {An elegant and strong continuation. Weiss opfert den Turm auf h1 und strebt sofortigen Angriff an.} (20. Rg1 fxg5 21. Bg2 e4 {''} 22. O-O-O d5 {may yield to an unclear combat, if White does not find the beautiful:} 23. Bxe4 $3 dxe4 24. Rd6 Qc8 25. Qb3 $1 Ke7 26. Rgd1 $40 {followed by d7+ or b4.}) (20. Bxf6 $5 {Ftacnik} Qxf6 (20... gxf6 21. Rg1 Rc8 22. c4 $18) 21. Rg1 Rf8 22. Ra2 $16) 20... Qxh1 (20... fxg5 {is strongly met by} 21. Bh3 $1 (21. Bc4 $16 {[%csl Re8][%cal Rh1e1,Re1e8,Gb4f8,Gc4f7]}) 21... d5 (21... O-O-O 22. Rxd7 $1 Rxd7 23. Rd1 Rhd8 24. Rxd7 Rxd7 25. Qf8#) 22. Rhe1 e4 {''} {Like in the previous line, a sacrifice works well on e4 again.} 23. Rxe4+ $1 dxe4 24. Rd6 $18 {and the black queen is suddenly captured.}) 21. Bh3 (21. Bxa6 Qe4 22. Bxb7 Qxb7 23. Be3 Kf7 24. Qb3+ Kg6 25. c4 $18 {was also good. Later on White can slowly expand on the queenside.}) 21... Qe4 {'!?'} (21... Qc6 22. Bxd7+ Qxd7 23. Rxd7 Kxd7 24. Qc4 $18 {''}) (21... Qxh2 22. Rxd7 $18) 22. Qd6 $2 {Timman decided to keep the queen, which was consequent to his attacking mood. However, it caused some extra inconvenience.} (22. Rxd7 {'?'} Qxb4 23. axb4 Rb8 24. Rxb7 $3 {Othwerwise Black could turn the table, as ...c8 was the second threat!} Rxb7 25. Be3 Rf8 26. Bf1 $18 {The connected passers supported by the bishops easily overcome the enemy rooks.}) (22. Bxd7+ Kf7 23. Qb3+ Kg6 24. Be3 Rhd8 25. Qc2 Kf7 26. Qxe4 Bxe4 27. c4 Ke7 28. Bc5+ Kf7 29. Kb2 $18 {would have involved the least risk.}) 22... Kf7 {'!' Ftacnik. Ftacnik: 'Polgar defends like a lion, the king is ready to find more peaceful play in front of his pawns. Black queen on e4 holds the position.'} ({On} 22... fxg5 {White chases the king with} 23. Bxd7+ Kf7 24. Be6+ Kg6 (24... Ke8 25. Qd7+ $18) 25. Bf5+ {'!'} ({or} 25. Bd5+) 25... Kxf5 26. Qd7+ Kg6 27. Rd6+ Kh5 28. Qh3+ $18) 23. Qxd7+ Kg6 (23... Kf8 24. Be6 Qg6 25. Be3 Rc8 26. Bc5+ Rxc5 27. Qd6+ Ke8 28. Qd8#) 24. Be6 Rhf8 (24... fxg5 {runs into} 25. Bf7+ Kh6 26. Qh3+) (24... Kxg5 25. Qxg7+ Qg6 26. h4+ {'+-'} Kh5 27. Bf7 $18) (24... Rhg8 $2 25. Be3 h6 (25... h5 26. Qf7+ Kh7 27. Qxh5#) 26. g4 $18 {[%cal Re6f5,Rf5e4,Rf5g6]}) 25. Be3 {As things have subsided a bit, Black has a range of candidates to choose from.} (25. g4 fxg5 (25... Kxg5 26. Qxg7+ Qg6 27. h4+ $18 {[%cal Rg7b7]}) 26. Bf5+ Rxf5 27. gxf5+ Kh6 (27... Qxf5 28. Rd6+ $18) 28. Rd6+ g6 $17) 25... Rad8 $2 {Objectively a mistake, but it was easy to overlook a spectacular reply.} (25... Bc6 {'!?'} 26. Qc7 $1 {This brave move assumes the discovery of the coming one to become really valuable.} (26. Qd3 {Ftacnik is the safer option.} Qxd3 27. Rxd3 {'='} Rfd8 28. Rxd8 Rxd8 29. g4 {is favourable for White thanks to his strong b6-pawn and the powerful bishops. Nevertheless, he still has to work hard for the full point.}) (26. Bd5 Bxd5 27. Qxd5 Qxd5 28. Rxd5 $14 {[%csl Gb6,Ge3] Weiss hat einen gefährlichen Bauern (auf b6) für die Qualität.}) (26. Qe7 Rae8 27. Qd6 Rd8 $17) 26... Qa4 27. Kb2 $3 {An astonishing moment. This is the second exchange sacrifice, its goal is to control the middle of the board completely.} ({The tempting} 27. Bc5 $2 {would spoil out the advantage right away.} Be4 28. Rd2 Rac8 $1 {A very nice diverting idea.} (28... Qb5 29. Rd7 Qb1+ 30. Kd2 Qc2+ 31. Ke1 (31. Ke3 $2 Rfc8 $17) 31... Qxc3+ 32. Kf1 Qc1+ 33. Ke2 Qc2+ $11) 29. Qe7 (29. Bxc8 $4 Qb3 $19) 29... Qa5 (29... Rcd8 30. g4 $8 Rxd2 31. Kxd2 Qc2+ 32. Ke1 Qc1+ $11) 30. Bb4 Qxb6 31. Rd7 Kh5 32. Qxg7 Qxe6 33. f3 $1 Bg6 $8 34. Bxf8 Rxf8 35. Qxf8 Qxd7 36. Qxf6 Kh6 37. Qxe5 $11) 27... Qxd1 28. Qxc6 {The black rooks are far from effective, thus White's only problem is his exposed king.} Qe2+ 29. Kb3 Qd1+ 30. Kb4 {An adventurous route of the king starts.} a5+ 31. Kc5 Qd3 32. b7 Ra6 (32... Qxc3+ 33. Kb5 Qd3+ (33... Qb2+ 34. Ka4 Rad8 35. Qe4+ f5 36. Bxf5+ Kf6 (36... Rxf5 37. g4 Kf7 38. Qxf5+ Kg8 39. Qc8) 37. Bxh7 $18) 34. Qc4 Qxc4+ 35. Bxc4 $18) 33. Qd7 Qxc3+ 34. Bc4 {Finally the bishop shelters the monarch from further, annoying checks.} f5 (34... Qxa3+ 35. Kd5 $18) (34... h5 35. Qd3+ Qxd3 36. Bxd3+ $18) 35. g4 $3 {Another stunning, silent move and the only way for the victory.} Raf6 (35... f4 36. b8=Q {is one of the main motifs.} Rxb8 37. Qf5+ Kh6 38. Qh5#) (35... Qxa3+ 36. Kd5 {It is so unique to see a king dancing in the middle of the board between the enemy heavy pieces, while the position is just winning!} Raf6 37. gxf5+ Kh5 (37... Rxf5 38. Qe6+ R5f6 (38... Kh5 39. b8=Q) 39. Qg4+ Kf7 40. Bc5 $18) 38. Qxg7 Qd6+ 39. Ke4 Qc6+ 40. Kd3 Rd6+ 41. Kc3 $18) 36. gxf5+ Rxf5 37. Qe6+ Kh5 (37... R8f6 38. Qe8+ Rf7 39. b8=Q $18) 38. b8=Q e4+ 39. Kd6 $18) (25... Rac8 $1 {'!?' was the best attempt to save the game.} 26. Kb2 {''} (26. c4 Rxc4+ $1 27. Bxc4 Qxc4+ 28. Kb2 Rf7 (28... Rc8 29. Rc1 Qe2+ 30. Ka1 Rxc1+ 31. Bxc1 Qc4 32. Qe8+ Kf5 33. Qh5+ (33. Qd7+ Kg6) 33... Ke6 34. Qe8+ $11) 29. Qd3+ Qxd3 30. Rxd3 Kf5 31. Bc5 (31. Kc3 Ke6 32. Rd8 Rd7) 31... Kg4 $1 {There is time for seeking counterplay, since the white king is too far.} 32. Kc3 Kh3 33. Kb4 Kxh2 34. Rd8 g5 35. Ka5 Kg2 36. Rb8 h5 37. Rh8 f5 38. Rxh5 g4 39. Rh8 f4 $11) 26... Rxc3 $1 (26... Bc6 27. Bf7+ Rxf7 28. Qxc8 {''} Qc4 29. a4 $3 Qxa4 30. Rd4 $3 {is a fascinating variation.} Bd7 $8 31. Rxa4 Bxc8 {White's position is definitely preferable, but it is possible to block him on the light squares in time.}) 27. Bf5+ (27. Kxc3 Rc8+ 28. Kb2 Qc2+ 29. Ka1 Qc3+ $11) 27... Qxf5 28. Qxf5+ Kxf5 29. Kxc3 Ke6 30. Bc5 Rf7 31. Rd6+ (31. Rd8 Rd7 32. Rh8 h6) 31... Ke7 {and Black can create a solid fortress.}) (25... Bc8 $2 {Ftacnik} 26. Bd5 $1 Qc2+ (26... Qxe3+ 27. fxe3 Bxd7 28. Bxa8 Ba4 29. Be4+ f5 30. Rd6+ $18) 27. Kxc2 Bxd7 28. Bxa8 Ba4+ 29. Kc1 Bxd1 30. Bd5 Be2 31. b7 $18) 26. g4 $3 {A fantastic, generous continuation by the Dutch legend. His great recognition is based on the unfortunate placement of the enemy king.} ({I suppose Judit calculated with} 26. Bf7+ Rxf7 27. Qxd8 Qa4 28. Kb2 Qb5+ $44) 26... Rxd7 (26... Qxe3+ {Ftacnik} 27. fxe3 Rxd7 28. Rxd7 Bc8 29. Bf5+ Kh6 30. Rf7 $1 Rd8 (30... Rxf7 31. Bxc8 Kg5 32. b7 Rxb7 33. Bxb7 $18) 31. Bxc8 Rxc8 32. b7 Rb8 33. c4 $18) 27. Bf5+ Qxf5 (27... Kf7 28. Rxd7+ Ke8 29. Bxe4 $18) 28. gxf5+ Kxf5 29. Rxd7 {Die Stellung ist materiell gleich, aber b7 und g7 hängen, so verliert Schwarz einen Bauern und der b6 ist stark.} Be4 30. Rxg7 Ke6 31. Kb2 {'!' It's time to bring the king to support the pawns. Sehr effektiv gespielt - der König strebt nach a5.} Rd8 (31... Rf7 32. Rg8 f5 33. c4 f4 34. Bd2 Kd6 35. Re8 Bf3 36. Bb4+ Kc6 37. Rxe5 Kxb6 38. Re6+ Kb7 39. Bd6 $18) 32. Kb3 f5 33. Kb4 f4 {Schwarz aktiviert seinen f-Bauern.} 34. Bc5 Rd7 35. Rg8 {Certainly not trading, which would be Black's last hope. Weiss muss - natürlich - die Türme auf dem Brett halten.} (35. Rxd7 $2 Kxd7 $11) 35... Bb7 36. Re8+ Kf5 (36... Kd5 37. c4+ Ke4 38. Rb8 (38. Ka5 $18) 38... Kf3 39. Rxb7 Rxb7 40. Ka5 $18) 37. c4 {Ftacnik: 'All white queenside pawns belong closer to the promoting squares.' Der weise Plan ist klar - die Bauern auf dem Damenfluegel werden vorgetrieben und es droht dann xb7 mit Gewinn.} e4 38. Ka5 h5 39. Rb8 e3 {Schwarz opfert einen Bauern, bekommt aber einen Freibauern auf der f-Linie.} 40. fxe3 f3 (40... fxe3 41. Bxe3 $18 {[%csl Ga5,Gb6,Gb8,Gc4]}) 41. e4+ {Dieser Zug ist auch gut, aber die Alternative wäre schneller. Weiss muss die Diagonale a7-g1 öffnen, sonst wäre die Drohung f3-f2 immer gefährlich.} Bxe4 ({Presumably on} 41... Kxe4 42. Rxb7 Rxb7 43. Kxa6 $18 {was planned.}) 42. Kxa6 Kg4 43. a4 {Weiss hat drei (!) Freibauern am Damenflügel.} Rf7 44. a5 Kh3 ({If} 44... f2 45. Bxf2 Rxf2 46. Rg8+ Kh3 47. b7 {'+-' decides the issue quickly.}) 45. b7 Kxh2 46. Kb6 Rf6+ 47. Ka7 Rf7 48. a6 {'+-' Mit zwei verbundenen Bauern steht Weiss auf Gewinn.} Bd3 49. Rc8 Kh3 (49... Bxc4 50. Bd6+ $18 {[%cal Rd6h2,Rc8c4]}) 50. Kb6 Rf6+ 51. Rc6 1-0

