{[%mdl 512]} 1. d4 {Ftacnik} Nf6 2. Bg5 Ne4 3. Bf4 d5 4. e3 (4. f3 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. e4 Bb4 7. Qd3 c5 8. dxc5 Nc6 9. O-O-O Bxc3 10. Qxc3 d4 11. Qd2 e5 12. Bg5 h6 $132 {Tichy,V-Smejkal,J/Mar.Lazne CS-ch/1978}) (4. Nd2 c5 5. Nxe4 dxe4 6. dxc5 Qa5+ 7. c3 Qxc5 8. Qd4 $2 (8. Qa4+ Qc6 9. Qxc6+ Nxc6 10. f3 e5 11. Be3 Bf5 12. g4 Be6 13. Nh3 f6 14. Nf2 exf3 15. exf3 Ne7 $10 {Tunik,G-Avrukh,B/Beersheba/1996}) 8... Qxd4 9. cxd4 Nc6 10. O-O-O e5 $1 11. dxe5 Be6 12. Kb1 e3 $1 13. Bxe3 Nb4 $17 {Mikhalevski,V-Tseitlin,MD/Beersheba/1996}) 4... c5 (4... c6 5. Bd3 Qb6 6. Bxe4 Qxb2 7. Nd2 dxe4 8. Ne2 Nd7 9. O-O Nf6 10. c4 Qa3 11. Ng3 g6 12. Be5 Bg7 13. Ngxe4 $14 {Hodgson,J-Hjartarson,J/Copenhagen PC/1996}) 5. Bd3 Nc6 {An extremly dangerous continuation demanding from black accurate play to pass the opening in peace!} (5... e6 6. Bxe4 (6. Nd2 Nxd2 7. Qxd2 Nc6 8. c3 Bd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. f4 Bd7 11. Nf3 O-O-O 12. O-O f6 $11) 6... dxe4 7. Nc3 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Qxd4 9. exd4 Bb4 10. Nge2 f5 11. O-O-O Nc6 12. Rhf1 O-O 13. f3 exf3 14. Rxf3 Bd7 $11 {Berg,K-Prusikhin,M/Morso DEN 2002}) (5... Qb6 6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. Nc3 cxd4 (7... Qxb2 $5 8. Nge2 cxd4 (8... Bg4 9. Rb1 Qxc3+ 10. Nxc3 Bxd1 11. Kxd1 cxd4 12. exd4 Nc6 13. d5 O-O-O 14. Ke2 Nd4+ 15. Ke3 $14 {Tu Hoang Thong-West,G/Elista RUS 1998}) 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. Qxd4 Nc6 11. Qxe4 f5 12. Qd5 e6 13. Qd2 e5 14. Nb5 Qe7 15. Bg5 Qf7 $11 {Turzo,A-Hatanbaatar,B/FSGM June, Budapest HUN 1998}) 8. Qxd4 $1 Qxd4 9. exd4 f5 $1 (9... Bf5 10. Nge2 e6 11. O-O-O Nc6 12. d5 exd5 13. Nxd5 O-O-O 14. Nec3 Bc5 15. Be3 $14 {Tu Hoang Thai-Esplana,C/Elista RUS 1998}) 10. Nge2 e6 11. Nb5 Na6 $11 {[%cal Gc8d7]}) ({Quite reliable is} 5... cxd4 $1 6. exd4 (6. Bxe4 dxe4 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. exd4 Nc6 9. c3 b6 10. Nd2 Bb7 11. Ne2 $14 {[%csl Re4][%cal Ge2g3] Lputian,S-Rytshagov,M/Istanbul 2000}) 6... Qb6 $1 {[%cal Gb6b2]} (6... Nc6 7. c3 Bf5 8. Qe2 e6 9. Nd2 Nd6 $11 {Timman,J-Van Wely,L/Breda 1998}) (6... g6 7. Be5 f6 8. Bxb8 Rxb8 9. Ne2 Bh6 10. c4 O-O 11. O-O Be6 12. Bxe4 dxe4 13. d5 Bf7 14. Nbc3 f5 $132 {Akopian,V-Herrera,I/Linares ESP 2001}) 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. Nc3 Nc6 9. d5 e5 $1 10. Be3 Qxb2 (10... Nd4 11. Nge2 Bg4 12. Bxd4 exd4 13. Qxd4 Qxd4 14. Nxd4 $14 {Janssen,R-Visser,Y/Rotterdam 1999}) 11. Nge2 Nb4 $15) (5... Nf6 $5 6. Nf3 e6 7. O-O Nc6 8. c3 Bd6 $11) 6. Bxe4 {White wants to take over the initiative by starting actions in the center.} dxe4 7. d5 (7. c3 Qb6 8. Qb3 Be6 $36) 7... e5 (7... Nb4 8. Nc3 e6 $8 9. d6 Qa5 $1 (9... b6 10. Nb5 Na6 11. Qe2 Bd7 12. O-O-O Qc8 13. Qc4 Qb7 14. Ne2 O-O-O 15. Nec3 $16 {Krueger,A-Bressel,T/Germany 2002}) 10. Nge2 Bd7 (10... Nd5 11. Bg3 Nxc3 12. Nxc3 Bd7 13. O-O Bc6 14. Qh5 Rd8 15. Rfd1 $36 {Mensch,E-Musanti,D/Budapest 2001}) 11. O-O f5 12. a3 Nc6 13. b4 $1 cxb4 $8 14. axb4 Qb6 15. b5 Nd8 $1 $13 {Hodgson,J-Tseitlin,M, Ischia, 1996}) 8. Bg3 Ne7 (8... Nb4 9. Nc3 Bg4 10. Qxg4 Nxc2+ 11. Kd2 Nxa1 12. Nge2 Qb6 13. Kc1 $16) 9. Nc3 (9. c4 f6 10. Nc3 Bf5 11. f3 $5 exf3 12. Nxf3 $36) 9... Ng6 (9... f6 $5 10. Nxe4 (10. Qh5+ Ng6 11. O-O-O Qa5 12. f4 exf3 13. Nxf3 Qc7 14. Nb5 Qd7 15. Nh4 Qg4 16. Nc7+ Kf7 17. Qxg4 Bxg4 18. Nxa8 Bxd1 19. Rxd1 Nxh4 20. Bxh4 Bd6 $11 {Berg,K-Navara,D/Morso 2002}) 10... Nxd5 11. Bxe5 $1 Bf5 $1 12. Ng3 Be6 13. e4 Nb4 $132 {Liang Chong-Dizdar,G/Las Vegas 1999}) 10. h4 (10. Nxe4 $2 {Ftacnik} f5 11. Nc3 f4 $17) 10... h5 11. f3 $5 {[%cal Gg3f2] Taking the pawn on e4 would be just a suicide: Ftacnik: 'In the original position both sides are somewhat disoriented by searching for their own ways.'} (11. Nxe4 {'!?' Ftacnik.} f5 (11... Bf5 $5) (11... Qb6 {Ftacnik} 12. Qc1 Bg4 13. c4 O-O-O 14. Qc3 $14) 12. Nc3 Qb6 13. Rb1 f4 14. exf4 exf4 15. Qe2+ Be7 16. Bh2 Bg4 $36 {and black's initiative is too strong to face.}) 11... Be7 $6 {Slightly weird idea: black is willing to trade his central pawn on e4 for the white one on h4!} (11... exf3 12. Nxf3 Bd6 $13 {'=' Ftacnik.}) (11... Qb6 {Ftacnik} 12. Qc1 exf3 13. Nxf3 Bd6 $11) 12. Nxe4 f5 13. Nc3 Bxh4 (13... f4 14. exf4 exf4 15. Bf2 Nxh4 (15... Bf5 16. Qe2 Qb6 17. O-O-O O-O-O) 16. d6 Bf6 17. Qe2+ Kf7 18. O-O-O $36) 14. Bxh4 Nxh4 15. Qd2 $14 {Now white has a very comfortable advantage.} Qg5 16. Nh3 Qg3+ (16... Qxg2 17. Qxg2 Nxg2+ 18. Kf2 Nh4 19. Rag1 Kf8 20. Rg5 $16 {[%csl Rh4]}) 17. Qf2 f4 $6 {Missing white's reply! It was necessary to play} (17... Qxf2+ 18. Kxf2 Ng6 $14 {with a worse, but defendable position.}) 18. Nxf4 {Ftacnik: 'Radjabov is only 14 and due to that doesn't feel reservations in tactical squirmish with extremely strong Judit. White is better developed and his chances will be preferable in all lines.'} Nxf3+ (18... exf4 19. Qxg3 fxg3 20. Rxh4 g5 21. Rh1 $18) 19. gxf3 exf4 20. Qxg3 fxg3 21. Ne4 h4 22. Nxg3 O-O (22... Ke7 23. Ke2 Bd7 24. Ne4 $18 {[%cal Ga1g1]}) 23. Rxh4 Rxf3 24. Ne4 Bf5 $6 {The final mistake depriving black any hope to hold on. The only chance to fight for a draw was} (24... Rxe3+ $8 25. Kd2 (25. Kf2 Rh3 26. Rxh3 Bxh3 27. Kg3 Bf5 28. Nxc5 Rc8 $16 {[%csl Rc2]}) 25... Rh3 (25... Rf3 26. Rah1 $1 Kf8 27. Rh8+ Ke7 28. Rg8 $18 {[%cal Gh1h8]}) 26. Rxh3 Bxh3 27. Nxc5 Bg2 $1 $16) 25. Ke2 Bxe4 26. Rxe4 Raf8 27. Kd3 $18 b5 28. Re7 R8f7 29. Re8+ Rf8 30. Rxf8+ Kxf8 31. a4 b4 32. Kc4 Rxe3 33. Kxc5 Re2 34. c4 $1 Rxb2 35. Kc6 b3 36. d6 Rd2 37. d7 b2 38. Rf1+ Ke7 39. Re1+ Kf7 40. Kc7 1-0

