1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Qd2 Qxb2 9. Nb3 Qa3 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Be2 Nc6 12. O-O Bg7 $146 {Black has large choice of moves instead; There were few fresh games played in those lines, 12...Na5 and 12...h5 deserve attention- white hasn't got any advantage in both:} (12... Na5 $5 13. Kh1 Nxb3 14. axb3 Qc5 15. f5 (15. Bb5+ $5 {would leave black w/o castle;}) 15... Bd7 16. b4 Qxb4 17. fxe6 fxe6 18. Rfb1 Qc5 19. Rxb7 Be7 20. Rxa6 Rxa6 21. Bxa6 Bc8 22. Rb8 O-O $11 {½-½ Luther,T-Timoshenko,G/Panormo GRE 2001/The Week in Chess 360 (42). (42)}) (12... Bd7 13. Nd1 $5 $146 (13. Kh1 {Tyomkin,D: 'in this variation white should play very well,othewise white hasn't compensation for the sacrificed pawn'} Rc8 (13... h5 14. Rf3 (14. Nd1 {Tyomkin,D} Rc8 15. Ne3 (15. Rf3 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik} Qb4 16. Qe3 Qa3 17. Qb6 Qb4 18. Qf2 Qxe4 19. Bd3 Qa4 20. Nb2 Qa3 21. Nc4 Qb4 22. a3 Qc3 23. Bf1 $18) 15... Qb4 16. c3 Qxe4 (16... Qb6 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik} 17. Nc4 Qc7 18. Rad1 (18. Rfd1 {Tyomkin,D} h4 $5)) 17. Bd3 Qa4 18. Nc4 Rc7 19. Nb6 Qa3 20. Rae1 (20. Nc4 {Tyomkin,D} Qa4 21. Nb6 Qa3 22. Nc4 {½-½ Gulko,B-Psakhis,L/Beersheba 1993/CBM 38}) (20. Qe3 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik} Kd8 $6 21. Na8 d5 22. Qb6 Qd6 $44) 20... Ne7 21. Nc4 Rxc4 22. Bxc4 h4 23. Bd3 {0-1 Short,N-Kasparov,G/PCA-Wch London 1993/CBM 36/[Ftacnik] (40)} (23. Rf3 $5 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik}) (23. Be2 $1 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik} Bg7)) 14... Be7 15. Rd1 Rc8 16. Re3 Qb4 17. a3 Qb6 18. Rh3 {½-½ Sedlak,N-Popovic,D/Subotica 2002/CBM 87 ext (18).}) 14. Nd1 $5 $146 (14. Bh5 {Tyomkin,D} Bg7 15. Rf3 O-O 16. Rg3 $146 (16. Re1 $6 {(Tyomkin,D) Tyomkin,D} Na5 $1 {c} 17. f5 $6 $17 (17. Nd4 $5 {Tyomkin,D} Qc5 18. f5 Nc4) 17... Nxb3 (17... Nc4 $5 {Tyomkin,D}) 18. axb3 Qa5 $6 (18... exf5 $142 {Tyomkin,D} 19. Nd5 (19. exf5 {Tyomkin,D} Qa5 $17 {[%csl Rc3,Rf5]}) 19... Rfe8) 19. Rd3 exf5 (19... Rfd8 {Tyomkin,D} 20. Rd1) 20. Rxd6 Be6 21. Nd5 Qxd2 22. Ne7+ Kh8 23. Rxd2 Rce8 24. exf5 Bxf5 (24... Rxe7 {Tyomkin,D} 25. Rde2 $11) 25. Rde2 Bd7 (25... Be6 $2 {Tyomkin,D} 26. Rxe6 fxe6 27. Bxe8 Rxe8 28. Rxe6) 26. Bf3 b5 27. Ra1 b4 28. Nd5 f5 29. Rd1 {½-½ Liberzon,V-Ribli,Z/Buenos Aires olm 1978/MCD-ol}) (16. Rd1 {(Tyomkin,D) Ftacnik} f5 17. exf5 exf5 $13 {Rodriguez,Am-Vera/Havana/1978/}) 16... Kh8 (16... Na5 $5 {Tyomkin,D} 17. f5 {[%cal Rd2h6]} (17. Nd5 $2 {Tyomkin,D} exd5 18. Nxa5 Qb2 $17) 17... Kh8 18. Nxa5 Qxa5 19. Qxd6 Rxc3 20. Qxd7 exf5 21. exf5 Rxg3 22. hxg3 Qe5 23. Rd1 Qxg3 24. Bf3 (24. Bxf7 {Tyomkin,D} Bh6 $19) 24... Bh6 $17) 17. Rf1 {[%cal Gf4f5]} Ne7 (17... Na5 $5 {Tyomkin,D} 18. f5 Nc4 (18... Nxb3 {Tyomkin,D}) 19. Qf4 Ne5 $15 {/}) 18. f5 exf5 19. exf5 Bxf5 (19... Nxf5 {Tyomkin,D} 20. Rgf3 Nh4 $17) 20. Qe1 Be6 $6 (20... Bg6 {Tyomkin,D} 21. Bf3 (21. Qxe7 {Tyomkin,D} Bxh5 22. Qxb7 Bg6 $17 {}) 21... Rfe8 $17 {[%cal Ge7f5]}) 21. Ne4 {0-1 Shabalov,A-De Firmian,N/Chandler 1997/CBM 61/[Tyomkin,D] (39) White got some initiative for the second pawn}) 14... Bg7 15. Ne3 Qb4 $2 (15... b5 $142 {to prevent Nc4}) 16. c3 Qxe4 17. Nc4 Bf8 18. Nb6 $16 {[%cal Rb6d7,Yb3c5] 1-0 Kotronias,V-Froehlich,P/Linares 2002/CBM 87 ext (28). (27)}) (13. Qe3 Na5 14. Nb1 Qb2 $2 (14... Qa4 15. Nc3 Qa3 16. Nb1 $11) 15. Nxa5 Qxa1 16. Na3 Qxa2 17. Bc4 Qb2 18. Rb1 $18 {1-0 Brkic,A-Ruck,R/Bled SLO 2002/The Week in Chess 400 (48). (47)}) (13. Bh5 Bg7 14. f5 O-O 15. Rf3 Rac8 16. Raf1 Qb4 17. Rg3 Kh8 18. Qe3 Ne5 $17 {Black easily repulses white's attack here;} 19. a3 Qxa3 20. Rxg7 Kxg7 21. Qg3+ Kh8 22. Qh4 Kg7 23. Qg3+ Kh8 24. Qh4 Rg8 $1 25. Bxf7 Nxf7 26. Qxf6+ Rg7 27. fxe6 Ne5 $19 {0-1 Gunnarsson,J-Olafsson,H/Reykjavik 1998 (48). (48)}) 13... Be7 14. Ne3 b5 15. Bh5 Qb4 16. c3 Qxe4 17. f5 e5 18. Nd5 Bd8 19. Rad1 $1 $36 {[%cal Rb3c5] ½-½ Vouldis,A-Shipov,S/Ano Liosia GRE 2000/The Week in Chess 321 (39). (38)}) (12... h5 13. Kh1 h4 14. h3 Bd7 15. Nd1 {[%cal Gd1e3,Ge3c4]} Be7 16. Ne3 Qb4 17. c3 Qb6 (17... Qxe4 18. Bd3 Qa4 19. Nc4 {[%cal Gc4b6]} Rd8 20. Nb6 Qa3 21. Nc4 Qa4 22. Nb6 {with perpetual attack!}) 18. Rad1 Qc7 19. Nc4 Rd8 20. Nxd6+ Bxd6 21. Qxd6 Qxd6 $11 {½-½ De la Paz,F-Herrera,I/Holguin City CUB 2002/The Week in Chess 387 (31). (30)}) 13. Rf3 O-O 14. Kh1 Rd8 $6 {Dubious plan which has led to white's advantage;} (14... b5 15. Rg3 (15. Nb1 Qb4 16. Nc3 {[%cal Ra2a3]} Qa3 $11) 15... Kh8 16. Rd1 Rd8 {[%cal Gc8b7]}) (14... f5 15. exf5 b5 16. Rg3 Kh8 $13) 15. Rg3 d5 $6 {This move just helps white to develp an initiative;} (15... Kh8 $5) 16. exd5 f5 17. Rd1 $16 {There is no way for black to return the pawn now;} Qf8 (17... Kh8 18. Rd3 $16) (17... exd5 18. Nxd5 Be6 19. c4 $16 {[%cal Gg3d3,Gg3g7]}) 18. d6 Kh8 (18... b5 {to prevent Na4 as it has happened in this game;} 19. Bf3 Bd7 20. Nc5 $16) 19. Na4 $1 {[%cal Ga4b6,Gb3c5] There is no white piece to cover those dark squares;} b5 20. Nac5 $16 {[%cal Yd6d7,Ge2f3]} Ra7 21. Rd3 e5 {That's the attempt to create some counterplay, but black can't do much against d-pawn eventually;} 22. Qe3 {[%cal Ge3a7] x-raying e3-a7 diagonal;} exf4 23. Qxf4 Ne5 24. Rd5 {[%cal Gd5e5]} Ng6 25. Qf2 Be6 26. R5d2 {[%cal Rc5e6]} (26. Nxe6 fxe6 27. Qxa7 exd5 28. Qxa6 {but has good chances to save the game here- d-pawn is not dangerous anymore;}) 26... Raa8 27. Nb7 $18 {White gains some material by this move, but d-pawn remains same strong;} Be5 (27... Rd7 28. N3c5 $18) (27... Rdb8 28. N3c5 $18 {[%cal Gd6d7,Gd7d8,Ye2f3]}) 28. Nxd8 Rxd8 29. Nc5 Qh6 (29... Rxd6 30. Rxd6 Bxd6 31. Nxe6 fxe6 32. Qb6 $18 {[%csl Ya6,Gd6,Ye6]}) (29... Bxd6 $2 30. Nb7 {[%csl Yd6,Yd8]}) 30. h3 Nf4 31. Qe3 Qg7 32. Bf3 {White has repulsed all one- move threats of black easily;} (32. Bf1 Nh5 $132 {[%cal Ge5f4,Yh5g3]}) 32... f6 33. Nxe6 Nxe6 {[%cal Ge5f4]} 34. Qe1 $6 {This was better to keep control over both, f4 and g3 spots; Following move whould prevent any counterplay:} (34. Qf2 $142 $1 {[%csl Yf4,Yg3]} Qg5 35. Bc6 (35. Bb7)) {The following stage of the game is rich of unaccuracies and mistakes, while white got the win fairly at the end;} 34... Qg5 35. d7 Qf4 36. Qg1 Ng5 37. Rd3 Kg7 (37... Ne4 $142 38. Bxe4 fxe4 39. Rf1 $1 Qg5 40. Rd5 Qg3 {and black may survive for a while;}) 38. Rf1 $6 (38. g3 $1 {[%cal Gg1b6]} Qxg3 39. Qxg3 Bxg3 40. Kg2 $18 {[%cal Gd3c3,Gc3c8]}) 38... Qc4 39. Bd5 Qxc2 40. Rfd1 Rxd7 41. h4 Nf7 $2 {Just a blunder; Black could save the game by:} (41... Rxd5 $1 42. Rxd5 Ne4 $1 {[%cal Ge4g3] and suddenly black is not worse at all!}) 42. Bb3 $18 {[%csl Rc2,Rd7] Now black is losing on the spot;} Qxd3 43. Rxd3 Rxd3 44. Qa7 Rc3 45. Qxf7+ Kh6 {[%cal Yc3c1]} 46. Kg1 $1 {That's important not to blunder mate over the backrank!} Rc7 47. Qf8+ Kh5 48. Bd1+ Kxh4 49. Qh6+ Kg3 50. Qh2# 1-0

