1. e4 c6 {Of course! Just few times in last years Karpov choose Spanish or Russian defence.} 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {Just in the last game, which was not already important from match point of view, Judit choose her favorite Panov attack- anf got fantastic position!} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 Ngf6 6. Bd3 e6 7. N1f3 Bd6 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Qc7 11. Qg4 Kf8 12. Be3 $5 {This move was never played before on super level.} ({Karpov succesfully defend Blacks position after normal} 12. O-O c5 {for instance} 13. c3 b6 14. Re1 Bb7 15. h4 c4 (15... Rd8 16. Bd2 Nf6 17. Qh3 Bxf3 18. Qxf3 cxd4 19. cxd4 Qd7 20. a4 Be7 21. Bc3 Qd5 $13 {1-0 Shirov,A-Karpov,A/Amber blind 1998 (53)}) 16. Bc2 b5 17. Bd2 a5 18. h5 Bd5 19. Qh3 Rb8 $11 {½-½ Anand,V-Karpov,A/Amber rapid 1998 (48)}) 12... c5 {Maybe this is not a mistake, but not in black,s interest to open position with king on f8.} (12... b6 13. Nd2 $5 (13. O-O-O Bb7 14. Rhe1 Rc8 15. Kb1 Nf6 16. Qh3 c5 17. dxc5 bxc5 18. c4 Bxf3 19. Qxf3 Ke7 $36 {[%cal Gc8b8,Gd6e5] ½-½ Fressinet,L-Speelman,J/Bardeaux 1998 (61)}) 13... Nf6 14. Qe2 Nd5 15. Nc4 Ba6 16. Nxd6 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 Qxd6 18. O-O Rd8 19. Rad1 b5 20. a4 $14 {½-½ Polgar,J-Karpov,A/Budapest m (4) 1998 (57)}) (12... Nf6 $1 13. Qh4 b6 14. O-O-O Bb7 15. Rhe1 c5 16. dxc5 bxc5 17. Nd2 Nd5 18. Nc4 Nxe3 19. Nxe3 Rb8 $13 {½-½ Polgar,J-Karpov,A/Budapest m (6) 1998 (48)}) 13. dxc5 Bxc5 {N} (13... Nxc5 14. O-O-O e5 (14... Nxd3+ 15. Rxd3 e5 16. Qe4 Be6 17. Rhd1 $16) 15. Bf5 Ne6 16. Nh4 $1 Qe7 17. Bxe6 Bxe6 18. Nf5 Bxf5 19. Qxf5 b6 20. Rd5 $16 {1-0 Marciano,D-Arlandi,E/Makarska 1996 (33)}) 14. O-O-O $5 {[%csl Rf8]} Nf6 15. Qh4 (15. Bxc5+ Qxc5 16. Qg3 Bd7 $13) 15... Bxe3+ 16. fxe3 {[%csl Rf8][%cal Gf1f8]} Bd7 (16... e5 $5 17. Qg3 Ng4 (17... Bg4 $2 18. Nxe5) 18. h3 Nxe3 19. Nxe5 {[%cal Re5g6]} Qc5 (19... Qb6 20. Qf3 Be6 21. Rde1 $18) 20. Rde1 (20. Nxf7 $5) 20... Nf5 21. Qf3 $18) 17. Rhf1 Rc8 18. Kb1 Bc6 (18... e5 $5) 19. e4 Kg8 {Bad king + 10 minutes difference ( to Judit favour of cource!) makes situation very difficult for Karpov.} (19... e5 20. Nd2 $5 {[%cal Gd2c4,Gf1f6]}) 20. c3 $5 {[%cal Gd3c2,Gd1d8]} Nd7 21. Bc2 Ne5 22. Nxe5 Qxe5 23. Qf2 $5 f6 (23... Bxe4 $5 24. Qxf7+ Kh7 25. Rfe1 (25. Qxb7 Bxc2+ 26. Kxc2 Qe2+ 27. Kc1 Rb8 $19) 25... Bxc2+ 26. Kxc2 Qxh2 27. Qxb7 $14) 24. Qxa7 Qxh2 (24... Bxe4 25. Qa4 Bxc2+ (25... Bxg2 26. Rfe1 Qc7 27. Rxe6 $40) (25... Bc6 26. Qg4 h5 27. Qh3 $36)) 25. e5 $1 Qxe5 26. Rfe1 {At this moment Karpov had something like 1,5 minutes left on his clock, Judit 9-10 and tremendous initiative and I was sure that Judit will win game very easy and quickly, but unfortunately she started to look more on Karpovs clock than at the position.} Qc7 27. Rxe6 Kf7 28. Bb3 Kg6 29. Qd4 {[%cal Rd4d3,Rd3g6]} Rhd8 30. Qg4+ Kh7 31. Bc2+ $2 ({Why not} 31. Qf5+ Kh8 32. Rde1 Re8 $140 33. Bc2 Kg8 34. Qh7+ Kf8 35. Bg6 $18) 31... Kg8 32. Rxd8+ (32. Rxf6 $1 Rxd1+ 33. Bxd1 Rd8 34. Bb3+ Kh8 35. Rf1 $18) 32... Rxd8 33. Re1 $2 (33. Rxf6 $1 Qh2 $140 $2 34. Rf1 Qxg2 35. Qe6+ Kh8 36. Qf5 $18) 33... Kf8 34. Bg6 $14 Qd6 35. Qc4 Qd5 36. Qe2 Qd7 (36... Ra8 $5) 37. c4 Qd6 {It is very difficult to analysed last part of the game- at this moment Karpov had 0,5 minutes left.} (37... b5 $5) 38. a3 Qd7 39. g4 Kg8 40. Bf5 Qd6 41. Qc2 Re8 42. Rd1 Qc5 43. Bg6 Ra8 44. Qd3 Qe7 45. Qd4 Be8 46. Be4 Bf7 47. c5 (47. Bd5 $5 $14) 47... Rc8 48. Bf5 $2 (48. b4 $1) 48... Rxc5 49. Qd8+ Qf8 $4 $138 (49... Qe8 50. Rd7 (50. Be6 Kh7 51. Bf5+ g6) 50... Re5 (50... b5 $2 51. Be6 $1 $18) 51. Qxe8+ Rxe8 52. Rxb7 $14) 50. Bh7+ 1-0

