1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. d5 d6 5. Nc3 exd5 6. cxd5 g6 7. h3 Bg7 8. e4 O-O 9. Bd3 b5 10. Bxb5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 13. Nxd6 Qa6 14. N2c4 Rd8 $5 {Sly move, which we prepared few hours before game,was not expected by Karpov in his home preparation. First 14 moves he played very quickly, but now he started to think a lot .} 15. Bf4 $1 {This strongest in position move took from Anatoly 8 minutes.} ({Black hasn,t any troubles after} 15. Qe2 Bb7 16. Nxb7 (16. Qe7 $2 Rd7 $1 17. Qe8+ Bf8 $17) 16... Qxb7 17. Bf4 (17. Ne3 Qb4+ 18. Qd2 Qe4 19. O-O Nc6 $44 {1-0 Potapov,A-Hrivnak,D/Ceske Budejovice op 1997 (54)}) (17. Bd2 Qxd5 18. Ne3 Qe4 19. Bc3 Bxc3+ 20. bxc3 Nc6 {½-½ Babula,V-Dinstuhl,V/Berlin op 1996}) 17... Qxd5 (17... Rxd5 18. O-O Nc6 $11) 18. Rd1 Bd4 (18... Qxd1+ 19. Qxd1 Rxd1+ 20. Kxd1 Nc6 21. Be3 $14) 19. O-O Nc6 20. Rfe1 (20. b3 Re8 21. Qd2 $14) 20... Re8 21. Be3 Rab8 22. b3 a5 $11 {0-1 Dautov,R-Womacka,M/BL 1998 (38)}) 15... Nd7 16. O-O Nb6 17. Nxb6 Qxb6 (17... axb6 18. Nxc8 (18. Qd2 $5) 18... Qxc8 19. Qb3 Qf5 20. Bc7 Rxd5 21. Qxb6 Rd2 $44 {½-½ Estremera,S-Izeta,F/Spain ch 1991 (35)}) 18. Nxc8 (18. Nc4 Qb7 19. d6 Qe4 20. Qf3 Qxf3 21. gxf3 Bxh3 22. Rfd1 Be6 $15 {½-½ Schlosser,P-Forster,R/Lippstadt 1995 (40)}) 18... Raxc8 19. Rb1 {Finally players came to famous theoretical position, but instead of normal 1-2 minutes Karpov spent 16!} Qb7 $1 {Important move was found by Topalov in his game against Karpov in Las Palmas 1996.In all other variations White keeps initiative ( and sometimes pawn too!), for example:} (19... Bd4 20. d6 c4 21. Qg4 h5 22. Qg3 Bc5 23. Rfd1 $16) (19... Rd7 20. d6 Bf8 21. Qg4 Qb7 22. Rfd1 Rc6 23. Rd2 Bxd6 24. Rbd1 Qc7 25. Rd5 h5 26. Qf3 Kf8 27. Be5 Qd8 28. b3 Qc7 29. Qd3 Ke7 30. g3 h4 31. Qe3 {1-0,Grabarczyk,M-Oliwa,M/POL-ch Brzeg Dolny/1996/}) (19... Qa6 20. d6 Rd7 21. Qd5 c4 22. Rbd1 Bxb2 23. Rfe1 Bf6 24. Be5 Bxe5 25. Rxe5 Rf8 26. Re7 Rdd8 27. d7 Qxa2 28. Rde1 Qa4 29. Kh2 a6 30. Rxf7 {1-0,Rogozenko,D-Marin,M/Bucharest Ciocaltea/1995/}) 20. d6 Bf8 21. Qd3 (21. Qd2 $6 Bxd6 $1 22. Bxd6 Rc6 23. Rfd1 Qa6 24. Qg5 Rdxd6 25. Rxd6 Rxd6 26. Qxc5 Qxa2 27. Re1 Rd8 28. b4 a6 $11 {½-½ Karpov,A-Topalov,V/Las Palmas 1996 (70)}) 21... Bxd6 22. Bxd6 Rc6 23. Rfd1 Rcxd6 ({Deserves attention also} 23... Qd7 $5) 24. Qxd6 Rxd6 25. Rxd6 c4 $1 {[%cal Gc4c3]} 26. Rd2 c3 27. Rc2 Qe4 28. Rbc1 cxb2 29. Rxb2 a5 {N It is difficult to believe, but just this move is a new one! In two previous games Black drew without big problems, but in this game the task was not so easy.} (29... h5 30. g3 a5 31. Rb3 Qd5 32. Rcb1 Qd2 33. a3 Qc2 34. Rb8+ Kg7 35. Re1 Qc3 36. Re3 Qc1+ 37. Kg2 Qc6+ 38. Kg1 Qc1+ 39. Kg2 Qc6+ {½-½ Shipov,S-Velimirovic,D/Belgrade-Moscow m 1998}) (29... Qa4 30. Re2 Kg7 31. Rcc2 h5 32. Rcd2 Qc4 33. Re1 a5 34. Red1 Qa4 35. Rb1 Qe4 36. Ra1 Qb4 37. Re2 Qc3 38. Rae1 Qa3 39. h4 Kh6 40. g3 f5 41. Kg2 Qa4 42. f4 Qc6+ $11 {½-½ Legky,N-Hamdouchi,H/France 1997 (66)}) 30. Rbc2 h5 $6 ({Stronger was to play} 30... Qa4 $1 31. Rd2 h5 $14 {[%csl Ra2][%cal Ga4a2] and it will be difficult for white to improve position}) 31. Rc4 $1 Qd5 32. a4 $16 Qd2 33. h4 Kg7 34. g3 Qb2 35. Rc5 Qd2 36. Kg2 Qb4 37. R1c4 Qb7+ 38. Kh2 Qb6 39. Kg1 Qb1+ 40. Rc1 Qb4 41. R5c4 Qd2 42. R1c3 $5 {[%csl Rf7][%cal Gc3f3,Gc4f4,Rf3f7]} Qd1+ 43. Kh2 Qd2 44. Rf3 {[%cal Rc4c7,Rc7f7]} Qd5 $1 45. Rcf4 f5 $8 {Finally Karpov pushed Judit to play f7-f5} 46. Rc3 Qd2 47. Rc5 Qd8 48. Rfc4 Qd2 49. Kg2 Qd3 50. Rf4 Qd8 51. Kh2 Qd2 52. Rcc4 Qe1 (52... Kh6 $5) 53. Kg2 Qe7 54. Rfd4 Qb7+ 55. Kg1 Qb1+ $6 (55... Kh6 $5 56. Rc5 Qb2) 56. Kh2 Qb2 57. Rd7+ {Unpleasant surprise! Black,s king has to start dangerous trip to the center of the board} Kf6 (57... Kh6 58. Kg2 Qb8 59. Rcc7 Qa8+ 60. Kg1 Qg8 61. Rd6 $18) 58. Rc6+ Ke5 59. Kg2 f4 $1 {Karpov had just 1-1,5 minutes left on his clocks and Polgar correctly try to make position more complicated, or, at least, more open.} 60. gxf4+ Kxf4 61. Rdc7 (61. Rxg6 $5 Qb4 62. Rg5 Qxa4 63. Rf7+ Ke4 64. Kg3 {[%cal Rf7f4]}) 61... Qb3 62. Rc4+ Ke5 63. Re7+ Kd5 64. Rce4 Kd6 65. R7e5 Qd3 66. Re6+ Kd7 67. Re3 Qd5+ 68. R6e4 g5 $16 {Continue same strategy. White chances are still clearly better, but, I think, the best move for white now was offering a draw. Karpov was not happy with this result, he played, played and tried to find chances for a win ... and lost the control of the situation. Just after move 81 he made a draw proposal, but too late- he had at this moment just 1 (!!) second left.} 69. hxg5 Qxg5+ 70. Kh2 Qf5 71. f3 Kd6 72. Rd3+ Kc5 73. Kg3 Qg6+ 74. Kh4 Qf5 75. Rde3 Qg6 76. Re5+ Kb4 77. R3e4+ Kc3 78. Rc5+ Kd3 79. Rf4 Qg1 80. Rd5+ Ke3 81. Rff5 Qh2+ 82. Kg5 $138 0-1

