1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bxf6 gxf6 7. Nf3 b6 8. Bd3 Bb7 9. Qe2 Qd5 $6 {Dubious variation used from time to time by Volkov.} (9... Nd7 10. O-O-O c6 11. Rhe1 (11. c4 Qc7 12. Kb1 O-O-O 13. d5 Ne5 14. Nxe5 Qxe5 15. dxe6 fxe6 16. g3 $14 {Golubev,M-Berelovich,A/Dutch Open 1999}) 11... Qc7 12. Kb1 O-O-O 13. Ba6 Rhg8 14. Bxb7+ Kxb7 15. g3 {½-½ Kovalev,A-Kiriakov,P/AZ Sanace Cup 1999}) 10. O-O (10. c4 Qa5+ 11. Nc3 Bb4 12. O-O Bxc3 13. bxc3 Nd7 $13 {Klovans,J-Volkov,S/MTO 1999}) 10... Nd7 11. Rfe1 O-O-O 12. a4 $1 {Very natural reply,showing that black doesn't have achnse to create any counterplay.} (12. c4 Qh5 13. Ng3 Qg4 14. c5 $6 (14. Be4 $5) 14... Bxf3 15. gxf3 Qxd4 16. Rad1 Qxc5 17. Qe4 $44 {Bologan,V-Tukmakov,V/Biel op 1995}) 12... f5 $2 {This move is suicide and we don't see very oftenly that players of such a high level lose like this.} (12... Qh5 13. Ba6 f5 14. Ng3 Qg4 15. a5 Nb8 16. Bxb7+ Kxb7 17. axb6 cxb6 18. Qc4 $16) (12... c5 13. Nc3 Qh5 14. Ba6 cxd4 15. Bxb7+ Kxb7 16. Qe4+ Kb8 17. Nxd4 $16) 13. Nc3 Qa5 (13... Qc6 14. Bxf5 {[%cal Ge2e7]} Nf6 15. Bd3 Bb4 16. Ba6 $16) 14. Nb5 $1 $18 {[%csl Ra5][%cal Gc2c3,Gf3d2,Gd2b3] That's it!Suddenly the black queen is trapped on a5 and it's just a matter of time when white will win it.} Rhg8 15. c3 c5 16. g3 {[%cal Gf3d2,Gd2b3] Preparing to transfer the knight to the queenside.} a6 17. Nd2 $1 axb5 ({Nothing changes} 17... Bd5 18. b4 $5 cxb4 19. c4 b3 20. cxd5 axb5 21. axb5 Qb4 22. dxe6 fxe6 23. Qxe6 $18) 18. axb5 Qxa1 19. Rxa1 cxd4 20. cxd4 Bd6 21. Nc4 Bc7 22. Qc2 Nf6 23. Nxb6+ Kb8 24. Qc5 Bd6 25. Qc3 h5 26. Qa5 Bf4 27. Ra4 1-0

