1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Qe2 (3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. g3 g6 (5... Nf6) 6. Bg2 Bg7 7. O-O Nge7 8. Re1) 3... c5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. g3 Nge7 6. Bg2 (6. h4 e5 7. Bg2 f6 (7... Bg4) 8. Na3 Be6 (8... Bg4) 9. c3 Qd7) 6... g6 7. O-O (7. h4 h6 (7... h5 8. O-O Bg7 9. e5 Nf5 10. c3) 8. O-O Bg7 9. e5 Nf5 10. c3 f6 11. exf6 Qxf6 12. Bf4 Bd7 13. Re1 O-O-O) 7... Bg7 8. c3 (8. Nbd2 b6 (8... h6) 9. Re1 h6 10. h4 O-O 11. e5 f6 12. exf6 Rxf6 13. Nf1 Rf7) 8... Qc7 (8... h6 9. Re1 b6 10. h4 (10. e5 g5 11. h3 Ba6) 10... d4 11. e5 (11. cxd4 Nxd4 12. Nxd4 Qxd4) 11... dxc3 12. bxc3 Ba6 13. c4 Qd7 14. Nc3 Rd8) 9. Bf4 (9. Re1 dxe4 10. dxe4 e5 11. Na3 O-O 12. Nb5 Qb8 13. Rd1 {1-0 Boensch,U-Gyorkos,L/Szekszard op 1989 (27)}) 9... e5 10. exd5 exf4 11. dxc6 O-O {This looks stronger than} {This is one of the rare occasions when a knight is better placed at the edge of the board than in the centre!} 12. cxb7 Bxb7 $5 13. Na3 fxg3 (13... Nf5 14. Rfe1 Bc6 15. Nc4 Rae8 16. Qd2 Bh6 17. Qc2 fxg3 18. fxg3 Bxf3 19. Rxe8 Rxe8 20. Bxf3 Ne3 21. Qa4 Re7 22. Nxe3 $18 {Dao Thien Hai-Zaw Oo/Yangon zt 1998/1-0 (46)} Rxe3 23. Rf1 Rxd3 24. c4 Qe5 25. Qxa7 Rd2 26. Kh1 Qxb2 27. Bg2 Rf2 $16 {Hausrath,D-Mohandesi,S/Interteam ch, BEL 1996/1-0 (54)} 28. Rxf2 Qxf2 29. Qb8+ Bf8 30. Qb1) 14. hxg3 Rab8 {Black closes down the position. Now White castles to the q-side and mutual pawn attacks start. However, the poor position of the black a5 knight enables White to come first. Korchnoi nicely underlines the drawbacks of Black's plan.} 15. Nc4 {[%cal Yd1e3,Ye3g4]} Nf5 16. Rfe1 $1 $146 {An interesting novelty. White has two ideas: 17.f4 and 17.b5} Bc6 $6 17. Nfd2 Bb5 18. Qf3 $1 h5 19. a4 Bd7 20. Qd5 {Black now has to do something as otherwise after 20.hxg5 hxg5 21.g4 he will have serious problems.} Rfd8 {[%cal Yb6b4]} 21. Qf3 Be6 {[%cal Yb6d4]} 22. Rxe6 fxe6 {} 23. Re1 $1 Qd7 24. Qe4 {White's plan now is: to remove his king th g2 and eventually capture the c4 pawn. Meanwhile the black pieces are completely motionless due to their awkward positions.} e5 25. Nxe5 Bxe5 26. Qxe5 {27.c1} Re8 {despair} 27. Qd5+ Qxd5 28. Bxd5+ Kg7 29. Rxe8 Rxe8 {With an extra pawn and attack along the g-file White confidently brings home his advantage.} 30. Ne4 Rd8 31. Bc4 Rc8 32. Kg2 Nh6 33. Nd6 Rc6 34. Ne4 Ng4 35. Bb5 Rc7 36. Kf3 Nf6 37. Ng5 Re7 38. Bc4 Nd7 39. Kg2 Kf6 40. Ne4+ Kf5 41. f4 Nb6 42. Ba6 c4 43. a5 cxd3 44. Bxd3 $5 Nd5 45. Kf3 Ke6 46. b4 $44 Rc7 47. Nc5+ {to exchange the "bad" bishop} Kf6 48. Ne4+ Ke7 49. Nc5 {But after the text move Whie starts a strong attack.} Nxc3 50. Bxg6 $1 Nd5 51. Nd3 Rc3 52. Ke4 Nxb4 53. Nxb4 Rc4+ 54. Kf3 Rxb4 55. Bxh5 Rb5 56. Bg6 $13 {Sokolov,I-Atalik,S/New York 1997/½-½(42)} Rxa5 57. Ke2 Rd5 58. Be4 Rd4 59. Ke3 Rd1 60. g4 a5 61. Bc2 $17 {Atalik} Rg1 62. g5 Kd6 63. g6 Kd5 64. f5 Ke5 65. Kd2 Rg3 66. Kc1 Rc3 67. Kb2 Rc7 68. Kb3 a4+ 69. Kb2 a3+ 70. Kb1 a2+ 71. Ka1 $14 {Soppe,G-Malisauskas,V/Erevan olm 1996/1-0 (74)} Rxc2 $5 72. f6 {, after which the black knight disturbs his other pieces. The simplest way to refute it is probably} Kxf6 {, e.g.} 1-0

