1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bg5 Bg7 4. Nbd2 O-O 5. c3 d6 6. e4 h6 7. Bh4 Qe8 8. Be2 Nh5 9. O-O e5 (9... Nf4 10. Bc4) 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Re1 Be6 12. Nc4 Nd7 13. Nfd2 (13. Bf1 f6 14. Nfd2 Qf7 15. b4 Rfe8 16. Ne3) (13. b4) 13... Nf4 14. Bf1 Nc5 15. Qc2 (15. b4 Ncd3 16. Re3 g5 17. Bxd3 $19 {Svidler,P-Kasparov,G/ICC blitz/1998} (17. Bg3 Rd8) 17... gxh4) 15... a5 16. Ne3 {This move is relatively new. It was first met in the game Ivanchuk-Kramnik/London PCA rapid/1994 and now 16.0-0 became one of the main continuations here. The alternatives are 16.Bc2 and 16.Qh5.} (16. a4 g5 17. Bg3 Qc6) 16... b5 $5 $146 {Since the above mentioned game a lot of other games were played, but nobody played this.} 17. b4 (17. Nb3 Qc6 18. f3 a4 19. Nxc5 Qxc5 20. Bf2 Qc6) 17... Na4 18. Nb3 axb4 19. cxb4 c6 20. Rec1 $6 {Now after a forced line Black achieves better prospects.} (20. Rac1 Ra6 21. Red1 Qb8 22. Be7 Rc8 23. Bd6 Qa8) 20... Ra6 21. f3 Qb8 22. Bf2 Qd6 23. Nd1 (23. a3 Bxb3 24. Qxb3 Ne6) 23... Rd8 (23... Qxb4 24. Nc5 Nxc5 (24... Raa8 25. Rab1 (25. Nxe6 Nxe6 26. Qxc6 Nd4 27. a3 Qa5 28. Bxd4 exd4 29. Qxb5 d3) 25... Qa5 26. Nxe6 Nxe6 27. Qxc6) 25. Bxc5 $13 Qa5 {is probably dubious, as it allows Black to create a strong attack, e.g.} 26. Bxf8 Bxf8) 24. Nc5 $6 (24. Na5) 24... Nxc5 $1 25. Bxc5 $2 Qd2 26. g3 Qxc2 $19 27. Rxc2 Bb3 (27... Nh3+ $5 $13 {Asrian,K-Rogozenko,D/Elista (ol)/1998} 28. Kg2 (28. Bxh3 Bxh3 29. g4 Ra3 $15 30. Kf2 f5)) 28. Rcc1 {What else? The Knight on d3 must be exchanged, but Black has enough material to win the game.} Bxd1 29. Rxd1 Rxd1 30. Rxd1 Ne6 31. Rd6 (31. Rd2 Bf8) 31... Nxc5 (31... Bf8 32. Bxb5 Nxc5 $5 $13 {Anand,V-Kramnik,V/Linares/1998} 33. Rxc6 $2 (33. bxc5 Ra5 $146 34. Bc4 Bxd6 35. cxd6 Kf8) 33... Rxc6 34. Bxc6 Ne6) 32. bxc5 Bf8 33. Rd2 Bxc5+ 34. Kg2 Kf8 35. Rc2 Ba3 36. f4 Ke7 $6 {This was not necessary. Now White has some hopes to save the game.} 37. Kf3 f6 38. h4 $6 Kd6 39. f5 gxf5 40. exf5 Bc5 41. Kg4 Ra3 42. Bg2 Re3 43. Bh1 b4 44. Bg2 Rc3 $1 {Very good move, cutting the King.} (44... Re1 45. Kh5 {34. ... Rh6 -+.} e4 46. Kxh6 $17 e3 {was also possible, but the game move looks stronger.} 47. Kg6) (44... h5+ 45. Kxh5 Rxg3 46. Be4 Re3 $11 47. Bg2 e4 48. Kg4 $11 Kd5 {was necessary. Only the practice can give a more or less precise evaluation to this complicated position.} 49. h5 {also comes into consideration.} Bf8 50. Rd2+ Ke5 51. Rd8) 45. Rxc3 bxc3 46. Be4 {Sooner or later White will have to take on a3 and it seems that there is no real difference if doing it now or later, as in the game.} Bf2 47. Kf3 Be1 48. h5 Kc5 49. g4 Kd4 50. a4 Bd2 $19 {Black is too active, so the position is easy winning.} 51. Bc2 Bc1 52. Be4 Ba3 53. a5 Kc5 54. Ke2 Kb5 55. Kd3 Bb4 56. a6 Kxa6 57. Bxc6 Kb6 58. Be4 Kc5 59. Ke3 Ba5 60. Bc2 Kb4 61. Kf3 Ka3 62. Ke2 Kb2 63. Kd1 Bb6 $19 64. Be4 Kb3 65. Bc2+ Kc4 66. Ke2 Bc5 $19 67. Be4 Kb3 68. Kd1 Be3 69. Bc2+ Kb2 70. Be4 Bf4 $19 71. Bc2 Bg5 $1 72. Be4 Kb3 73. Bc2+ $17 Kc4 74. Ke2 Kd4 75. Kf3 Kc4 76. Ke2 Kb4 $17 77. Be4 Kb3 78. Kd1 c2+ 79. Bxc2+ Kc3 80. Ba4 {is winning, of course.} Kd3 81. Ke1 Ke3 82. Bd1 $19 e4 83. Kf1 $19 0-1

