1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh6 11. d5 a5 (11... Nd7) (11... f5) 12. Nd2 {The other alterative is 12.h3 keeping an option to develop the knight on e2 or h3.} Na6 $5 {The plans of both sides are perfectly clear:white intends to create something on the kingside,while black is playing on the queenside,trying to brea white's position with b4.} 13. a3 (13. f3 f5 14. Bf2 Nc5 15. h3) 13... Qe8 (13... Bd7 14. O-O Qe8 15. b3 Nc5 16. b4 Na4 17. Nxa4 Bxa4 18. Qc1 f5 19. f3 $11 {Cvicela,A-Sladecek,J/SVK-chT2 1995} f4 $5 20. Be1 Bd7 21. h3 Bf6 22. c5 axb4 23. axb4 Rxa1 $14 {[%cal Gf4f5] Hegedus,I-Bock,J/Budapest FS07 Elo 1998} 24. Qxa1 Bb5 25. Nc4) 14. f3 (14. b4 f5 15. f3 axb4 16. axb4 Bd7 $14 {Heissler,J-Reefschlaeger,H/BL9596} 17. Rb1 g4) 14... Bd7 15. Bf2 $5 a4 $5 {[%cal Gd7e8,Ge7c7]} (15... Nc5 16. b4 (16. h4 gxh4 17. Rxh4 f5) (16. g4 a4 $132 {Polgar,J-Lautier,J/Madrid 1993}) 16... axb4 17. axb4 $14 {[%cal Gf2f5]} Na4 18. Nxa4 (18. Qc2 Nxc3 19. Qxc3 Rxa1+ 20. Qxa1 f5) 18... Rxa4 19. Rxa4 Bxa4 20. Qa1 Bd7 (20... f5 21. b5 Qa8 $13 {Horvath,A-Almasi,I/Budapest FS12 GM 1997} 22. O-O) 21. g4) (15... f5 16. exf5 (16. h3) 16... Nxf5 17. Nde4 Nd4) 16. Nb5 (16. g4 Nc5 17. h4 Nf7) 16... Qe7 (16... Bxb5 $17 17. cxb5 Nc5 18. Qc2 Qe7 $17 {Better was} 19. Rc1 b6 20. g4) (16... Nf7 17. Qxa4 c6 18. dxc6 bxc6 19. Nc3 Nc5 20. Qd1 Ne6 21. O-O Nd4 22. b4 $14 {with slightly better prospects in the endgame.} c5 $5 23. Nb3) 17. Qxa4 c6 18. Nc3 (18. dxc6 $19 {After} bxc6 $5 19. Nc3 Nc5 (19... f5) 20. Qc2 Ne6 21. O-O Nd4 22. Qd3 f5 $132 {white has more than sufficient compensation for the pawn.}) 18... f5 (18... cxd5 19. Nxd5) 19. Qc2 g4 20. O-O-O (20. O-O f4) 20... gxf3 21. gxf3 $5 (21. Nxf3 Ng4 22. Bg1 fxe4 23. Nxe4 Bf5) (21. Bxf3 Ng4 22. Bxg4 fxg4 $15 {with extremly complicated position,in which black is a rook up.} 23. Be3 $2) 21... fxe4 22. Ndxe4 (22. Ncxe4 cxd5 23. cxd5 $18 {Very serious attention deserved} Rac8 $5 24. Nc3 Nc5 $1) 22... Nf5 23. Rhg1 Kh8 24. Rd3 $5 {Interesting decision:white sacrifices a pawn,but significiently improves position of the pieces.} (24. dxc6 bxc6 (24... Bxc6 25. Kb1) 25. Qd3 Rfd8 (25... Nd4 $11 26. Bxd4 $4 exd4 27. Rxg7 Qxg7 28. Qxd4) 26. Rxg7 (26. Bb6 $132 Bh6+ $4 27. Kb1 Rdb8 $6 28. Nxd6 Rxb6 29. Nxf5 Bxf5 30. Qxf5 $15 Rab8 31. Na4 R6b7) (26. Be3) 26... Qxg7 27. Nxd6 Rf8 28. Nce4) 24... Nd4 {[%csl Rd4]} 25. Qd1 (25. Bxd4 exd4 26. dxc6 Bxc6 27. Nd1 d5 28. cxd5 Bxd5) 25... Nc5 26. Nxc5 (26. Rxd4 exd4 27. Bxd4 Nb3+ 28. Qxb3 Bxd4) 26... dxc5 27. Ne4 (27. dxc6 bxc6 28. Ne4 Bf5) 27... cxd5 (27... cxd5 {[%cal Ga1f1,Gh4f3,Gg4g5]} 28. cxd5 Bb5 $2 (28... Bf5 29. Be3 b5) (28... b5) 29. Re3 Nxe2+ {[%cal Gc3f6,Ga1e1]} 30. Rxe2 $6 Bxe2 31. Qxe2 Qd7 32. Bxc5 Rfc8 33. Kb1 Qxd5 $5 {Now the position is equal and the draw agreement is not far away.} 34. Rd1 Qf7) 1/2-1/2

