1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. f3 d5 (3... c5 4. d5 d6 5. e4 e5) 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nb6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. Nc3 Nc6 (7... O-O) 8. d5 (8. Bb5 O-O (8... Bd7 9. Nge2 O-O 10. O-O Na5 11. Bd3 Nac4 12. Bc1 a6 13. Bc2 c6 14. b3 Nd6 15. Bb2) 9. Nge2 e5 (9... Na5 10. b3 a6 11. Bd3 Nc6 12. Bc2 (12. Rc1) 12... Nb4 13. O-O c6 14. Rc1 Nxc2 15. Rxc2 a5 16. Rd2 a4 17. d5 Qc7 18. Bf4 (18. Nxa4 Nxa4 19. bxa4 Qa5) 18... Be5 19. Bxe5 Qxe5 20. f4 Qd6 21. dxc6 Qc5+ 22. Rd4 bxc6 23. b4 Qh5 24. Qd2 (24. Ng3 Qxd1 25. Rfxd1) 24... Ba6 25. Ng3 Qh4 26. Nce2 e5 27. fxe5 Nc4 28. Qc3 Nxe5 29. Rd6 Rfe8 30. Rf4 (30. Rfd1 Ng4) 30... Qe7 31. Rd4 (31. Rdf6 Rad8 32. Nd4 Qa7) (31. Rd2 Rad8 32. Nd4 Qg5 33. Rdf2 Nd3 34. Rxf7 Qe5) 31... Bxe2 32. Nxe2 Red8 33. Rf1 Rxd4) 10. d5 Ne7 (10... Nb8 11. O-O c6 12. dxc6 Nxc6 13. Bc5 Re8 14. Nd5)) 8... Ne5 9. Bd4 (9. f4 Ng4 10. Bb5+ Bd7 11. Bd4 Bxd4 12. Qxd4 O-O 13. Be2 c6 14. dxc6 bxc6 15. Nf3 Qc7 16. h3 Nf6 17. Qe3) 9... f6 (9... O-O 10. f4 Ned7 (10... Bg4 11. Be2 Bxe2 12. Ngxe2 Ng4 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Qd4+ Nf6 15. O-O-O c5) 11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. Qd4+) 10. f4 (10. a4) 10... Nf7 11. a4 $5 {Definitely a side line. There are two ideas associated with this move. The first is to keep the  on the a2-f7 diagonal by stashing it away on a2. The second is to prevent b4 after c2 and exd4 cxd4. So it´s quite an ambitious try.} e5 12. dxe6 Bxe6 13. a5 Nd7 14. a6 b6 15. Bb5 Qe7 16. Nge2 c5 (16... O-O-O 17. Bc6) 17. Bf2 O-O-O (17... O-O 18. Nd5 Qd6 (18... Bxd5 19. Qxd5 Rfd8 20. O-O-O Nf8 21. Qb7) 19. Nec3 Rfd8 20. O-O) 18. Qa4 f5 19. e5 (19. Bc4 Nb8 20. Bxe6+ Qxe6 21. Nb5 Nc6 (21... Rd7)) 19... g5 20. Bc4 (20. g3 gxf4 21. gxf4 Ndxe5) 20... Ndxe5 (20... Nb8 21. Bxe6+ Qxe6 22. O-O Nc6 23. b4) 21. Bxe6+ Qxe6 22. fxe5 Nxe5 {Opening up the centre is a bit dangerous since White is better developed. Black could well have waited for a while.} 23. O-O $1 {[%mdl 2048] An excellent answer. White´s pieces are more active in the following play. The  on e4 hems Black´s play and is also a weakness.} Qc4 24. b4 (24. Qxc4 Nxc4 25. Nb5 Kb8) (24. Qc2 Qd3) 24... Qxb4 (24... cxb4 25. Nb5 Qxe2 (25... Nf3+ 26. gxf3 Bxa1 27. Rc1 Bc3 28. Rxc3) (25... Kb8 26. Ned4 Nd3 27. Nxa7 Kxa7 28. Nc6+ Ka8 29. Nxd8 Bxa1 30. Qd7 $40 {1-0 Averbakh,Y-Alatortsev,V/Moscow 1946/MCD (39)} Qxa6 {and} 31. Qc6+ {are the usual choices.} Kb8 32. Bxb6 Be5 33. Bc7+ Ka7 34. Bxe5 Nxe5 35. Qc5+) 26. Nxa7+ $17 {0-1 Apicella,M-Bellia,F/Vinkovci 1989/CBM 16 (90)} (26. Rfe1 Qd2 27. Nxa7+ Kb8 28. Nc6+) 26... Kb8 {The second option connected to 11.a3.} 27. Nc6+) 25. Qc2 Nd3 26. Rfb1 Qc4 27. Ra4 Qe6 (27... Qf7 28. Bd4 cxd4 (28... Bxd4+ 29. Nxd4 Rxd4 30. Rxd4 $14 {1-0 Matanovic,A-Watzka,H/Vrnjacka Banja 1967/EXT 98 (38)} cxd4 $2 {this does not mix well with a3.} 31. Qxd3 dxc3 32. Qxc3+ $1) 29. Nd5+ $36 Nc5 (29... Kb8 30. Nxb6) 30. Nxb6+ axb6 31. Rxb6 Qc7 32. Qxf5+) 28. Nb5 Kb8 $2 (28... Nxf2 $11 29. Kxf2 (29. Nxa7+ Kb8 30. Qxc5 Rd1+ $14) 29... Kb8 $15 30. Ng3 Rhf8 $40 31. Ra3 Qd5) 29. Ned4 Qe4 (29... Bxd4 30. Bxd4 Rxd4 31. Rxd4 Qe3+ 32. Kf1 cxd4 (32... Qxd4 33. Nxd4 cxd4 34. Qxd3) 33. Qc7+ Ka8 34. Qb7#) 30. Nc3 Qe8 31. Qxd3 cxd4 32. Bxd4 $6 Qe6 33. Qf3 Qf7 34. Bxb6 (34. Bxb6 $6 axb6 $1 $16 {[%cal Yg6e7,Ya1e5]} 35. Rxb6+ Kc8 36. Qc6+ (36. a7 Kd7 37. a8=Q Bf6 (37... Rxa8 38. Qb7+ Kd8 $1 $11 39. Rxa8#) 38. Ra7+ $16 Ke8 $142 39. Qfc6+ $1 {Judit is not known to scorn this type of attack. Perhaps she missed the follow-up.} Kf8 40. Rxf7+ $3 $18 Kg8 (40... Kxf7 41. Qxf6+ Kg8 $11 42. Qaxd8#) 41. Qe8+ Rxe8 42. Qxe8#) 36... Qc7 37. Rb8+ (37. Qe6+) 37... Kxb8 38. a7+ Kc8 (38... Qxa7+ $18 39. Rxa7 Bd4+ 40. Kf1 Bxa7 41. Nb5) 39. a8=Q#) 1-0

