1. c4 Nf6 2. d4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 O-O (4... c5) 5. e4 (5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne8) 5... d5 6. e5 Nfd7 {Very solid variation,quiet suprising for the Polgar's agressive style!This line was played oftenly on the highest level and proved to be very good way to play for a draw.} 7. cxd5 exd5 8. a3 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 f6 10. exf6 (10. f4 fxe5 11. fxe5 Qh4+ 12. g3 Qe4+) 10... Qxf6 11. Bd3 Nb6 (11... Qh4+ 12. g3 Qh5 13. Bf4 c5 14. g4 Qe8+ 15. Qe2 Nc6 16. Bd6 Qxe2+ 17. Bxe2 Re8 $11 {Levitina,I-Maric,A/Shanghai ctW 1992} 18. dxc5 $6 b6 19. cxb6 Nxb6 20. Kf2 Bb7 21. Bb5 Re6 22. Bc5 Nc4 23. Rb1 N6e5 24. Bxc4 dxc4 $14 {[%csl Rd7] Kasparov,G-Ivanchuk,V/Tilburg 1989} {Nothing promises} 25. Rxb7 Nd3+ 26. Kg3 Nxc5 27. Rc7 Re5 28. Nh3 Rf8 29. Rd1 Nd3 $1 30. Rb1 Re3 31. Ng5 Re5 32. Ne4 Re6 33. h4 {½-½ Ivanchuk,V-Ljubojevic,L/Tilburg 1989} {Another good alternative is} Rb6 34. Rxb6 axb6 35. Nd6 Nf4 36. Rxc4 Nd5 37. Rc6 Ra8 38. c4 Ne7 39. Rxb6 h5 $11 {Karpov,A-Anand,V/Siemens Giants 1999} 40. Rb7 Nc6 41. Rc7) 12. Ne2 Bf5 13. Bf4 Rf7 (13... Bxd3 $11 14. Qxd3 $2 Qe7 $1 {[%csl Rg7]} 15. O-O Nc4 16. Rfe1 Nc6 17. Ng3 Qf7 $1 18. Be5 N6xe5 $14 19. dxe5 Rae8 $11 20. f4 Qxf4 21. Qxd5+ Kh8 22. Qxb7 Rxe5 23. Qxa7 Ra5 24. Qd4 $11 Qxd4+ 25. cxd4 Rxa3 26. Rac1 Ne3 27. Nf1 Nxf1 28. Rxf1 $11 Rxf1+ $5 29. Kxf1 Ra7 30. Ke2 Kg8 $11 {black is doing o'kay.}) 14. Be5 Qg5 $5 {Black doesn't want to take on d4 and defends the bishop to prevent d5 in the nearest future.} 15. Bxf5 Qxf5 16. Ng3 Qd7 17. O-O {White has just run out of usefull moves and he finally takes on c5.} Nc6 {Keeping square e5 under control.but also after} 18. f4 Nc4 19. Qe2 Re8 $5 20. h3 b5 21. Rf3 $5 (21. a4 bxa4 22. Rxa4 N6xe5) 21... a5 22. Nf1 b4 $1 $11 (22... N6xe5 23. fxe5 Rxf3 24. Qxf3 Rf8) (22... a4 23. Nd2 N6a5 (23... Qe7 24. Nxc4 bxc4) 24. Nxc4 Nxc4) 23. axb4 axb4 24. Qd1 bxc3 25. Rxc3 N6xe5 {Black has the only weakness in his position-pawn e6,which he easily defends,and slightly exposed position of white king gives him sufficient counterchanses.} 26. fxe5 c5 27. Ne3 1/2-1/2

