1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd3 d5 8. O-O Nf6 9. Re1 Be7 10. e5 Nd7 11. Qg4 g6 12. Bh6 Rb8 {Larsen and later Smyslov made this line popular for Black;} 13. Qh3 (13. Nd1 Rb4 14. Qe2 Bg5 15. c3 Rb6 16. Bxg5 Qxg5 17. g3 O-O 18. f4 Qe7 19. Nf2 c5) (13. Rab1 Rb4 14. Qe2 Rh4 15. Bd2 O-O (15... Qb6) 16. Bxa6 Bxa6 17. Qxa6 Bc5) 13... Rb4 14. Bg7 $1 {The only way for White to fight for the opening advantage. White allows Black to get his queen to g5, while White manages to repulse Black's attack there. Kramnik was definitely familiar with this line, not sure about Judit..} Rg8 {That's a novelty, and definitely an improvement of Matveeva-Maric game where Black got in trouble very soon.} (14... Rh4 15. Qg3 Rg8 16. Bf6 Bxf6 (16... Rb4 17. Bxe7 Qxe7 18. b3 h5 19. Na4 Rg4 20. Qe3 Qg5 21. g3 Qxe3 22. Rxe3 Rb4) 17. exf6 g5 18. Qd6 Bb7 (18... Nxf6 19. Qxc6+ Kf8 20. Bxa6 Bxa6 21. Qxa6 Qb8 22. g3 Ng4 23. Qb5) 19. g3 Rh6 20. Qb4 Bc8 21. Qd6 Bb7 22. Qb4) 15. Qxh7 Rxg7 {15...f8 is the way to keep tension in the centre, although this doesn't bring black an equal play.} 16. Qxg7 Bf8 17. Qg8 {Exchanging Black's bishop, White gets access to Black's queenside.} Qg5 18. g3 (18. Ne2 Rg4 (18... Nxe5 19. f4) 19. g3 Qxe5 (19... Nxe5 20. f4 Nf3+ 21. Kf2 Qh5 22. Kxf3)) 18... Nxe5 19. f4 $1 {Important accuracy- Black's rook is worse on d8 than on a8;} Nf3+ (19... Rxf4 20. Rxe5 Qxe5 21. gxf4 Qe3+ 22. Kg2 e5 23. Qh8) 20. Kf2 Qh5 {Black pawns are targets, but he still has some hopes on the kingside;} 21. Bxg6 $1 {Bringing the rook to a defense- white intends f4 now. Immediate f4 would lead to a draw only.} (21. Be2 Qxh2+ 22. Kxf3 e5) (21. Re3 d4 (21... Nd4) 22. Rxf3 Qxh2+ 23. Kf1 dxc3 24. bxc3 Rb2) 21... fxg6 22. Rxe6+ {This move freezes Black's attack, as well as a2 joins the defense;} Bxe6 23. Qxe6+ Be7 24. Qxc6+ Kf8 25. Qa8+ {White is safe on the kingside, so Vlad simply picks Black pawns;} (25. Nxd5 Nd4 26. Qc8+ Kf7) (25. Qxd5 Nxh2) 25... Kg7 26. Qxd5 {My Fritzy doesn't understand why Kramnik didn't grab another one....} Nxh2 27. Qxh5 gxh5 28. Rh1 {An extra pawn for Kramnik in endgame means a point in the crosstable in the most cases;} (28. b3 Ng4+ 29. Kf3 Bf6 30. Nd5 Rd4 31. Nxf6 Kxf6 32. Rh1 Kg6 33. Re1 Rd2 34. Re2 Rxe2 35. Kxe2 Kf5 36. c4 Nf6 37. b4 Ne4 (37... Kg4 38. c5 Kxg3 39. f5 h4 40. c6 Nd5 41. f6 h3 42. f7 h2 43. f8=Q h1=Q) 38. b5) 28... Ng4+ (28... Rxb2 29. Rxh2 Rxc2+ 30. Ne2 Kg6 31. a4 Ra2 32. g4 hxg4 33. Kg3 Rxa4 34. Kxg4) 29. Kf3 Nf6 (29... Rxb2 30. Nd5 (30. Rxh5 Rxc2 31. Kxg4 Rxc3) 30... Rxc2 31. Nxe7 Rf2+ 32. Ke4 Re2+ 33. Kd4 Rxe7 34. Rxh5) 30. b3 Rb6 31. Ne4 Nxe4 32. Kxe4 Kg6 33. c4 {White has an extra pawn, all black pawns are weak... And Vlad is sitting in front of you... sounds bad.} Re6+ 34. Kf3 Rd6 35. Re1 Rd3+ 36. Kg2 (36. Re3 Rxe3+ 37. Kxe3 Kf5 38. Kf3 Bc5 39. Kg2 Kg4 40. Kh2 a5 41. Kg2 Bb4 (41... Bd4 42. a3) 42. Kf2 Bd6 43. Kg2 Bc5 44. Kh2 Bf2 45. a3 Bxg3+ 46. Kg2 Bxf4 47. b4 a4) 36... Bf6 37. Re6 a5 (37... Rd2+ 38. Kf3 Rxa2 39. g4) 38. Ra6 Kf5 39. c5 (39. Rxa5+ Kg4) 39... Bd4 (39... Rc3 40. c6 Kg4 41. c7 Rc2+ 42. Kf1 Kf3 43. Ke1 Bd4 44. Rxa5 Be3 45. Rd5 Rc1+ 46. Rd1 Rxc7) 40. Rd6 $1 {following with d3. Vlad makes it looking easy.} Ke4 {Pawn exchanges are favoing the weaker side, but here nothing can help Black;} 41. c6 Rc3 42. Kh3 Bf2 43. Rg6 Bb6 44. Kh4 Rc5 45. Rd6 Bc7 46. Rd7 Rxc6 47. Kxh5 Bd6 48. Kg5 Ba3 49. Ra7 Rc5+ 50. Kg6 Bb4 51. Ra6 Rc3 52. Re6+ Kf3 53. f5 Rc2 54. f6 Rxa2 55. f7 Kxg3 56. Kg7 Rf2 57. Rg6+ (57. Re8 Rf4 58. f8=Q Bxf8+ 59. Rxf8 Rxf8 60. Kxf8 Kf4 61. Ke7) 57... Kh3 (57... Kf4 58. f8=Q+ Bxf8+ 59. Kxf8 $1 $18) 58. Rh6+ Kg2 59. Rh5 Rf3 60. Rg5+ Kf2 61. Kg8 (61. Rxa5 Bc3+ 62. Kg8 Rg3+ 63. Kh7 {[%cal Gg2f3]} Rg7+ 64. Kh6 Rxf7) 61... Rg3 62. Rxg3 {The pawn b5 falls as well.... One more very long and exhausting game for both players in Sofia; From Sofia reports: "Both players looked very tired at the press conference and agreed that one additional day of rest would have been a good idea."} Kxg3 63. f8=Q Bxf8 64. Kxf8 {[%cal Gc3c5]} Kf4 65. Ke7 Ke5 66. Kd7 Kd5 67. Kc7 Kc5 68. Kb7 $18 Kb4 69. Ka6 a4 70. bxa4 Kxa4 1-0

