1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 (5... Nd6) 6. Qe2 Nd6 7. Bxc6 dxc6 (7... bxc6 8. dxe5 Nb7) 8. dxe5 Nf5 9. Rd1 Bd7 10. e6 fxe6 11. Ne5 Bd6 12. Qh5+ g6 13. Nxg6 Ng7 14. Qh6 Nf5 $146 {[%mdl 8] In the main line White often takes the initiative on the -side with a timely a2-a4. So Sokolov concentrates on the -side right away.} 15. Qh3 Rg8 {White is invited to close the -side.} 16. Qxh7 Rg7 17. Qh5 Qf6 18. Ne5+ Ke7 19. Ng4 Qg6 (19... Qh4 $132 20. Qxh4+ {is possible, but it gives Black a clear picture of what to do (play on the -side). Judit prefers to keep more options.} Nxh4 21. h3 e5 22. f3 Bc5+ (22... Bf5) 23. Kf1 Bf5 24. Be3 Bd6 25. Rd2 e4 26. Bf2 Ng6 27. Ne3 $14 {1-0 Karpov,A-Gligoric,S/Leningrad 1973/IZT/[ChessBase] (62)} (27. Nc3) 27... Be6 {is more common, but often it's just a transposition, since d5 comes later anyway.} 28. fxe4 Rf8) 20. Qxg6 Rxg6 21. h3 e5 22. Nd2 (22. c4 Nd4 23. c5 Nc2 24. Be3 Nxa1 25. Na3 Bxg4 26. hxg4 Rxg4 $15 27. Rxa1 Rag8 28. g3 b6 29. cxd6+ cxd6) (22. c3) 22... Nd4 23. c3 Nc2 (23... Ne2+ 24. Kf1 Nf4 (24... Nxc1 25. Raxc1 $18 Bxg4 $5 26. hxg4 Rxg4) 25. Ne4 (25. Nxe5 Bxe5 26. Nc4 Bd6 27. Nxd6 Nxg2 28. Nxb7 Bxh3 $16 29. Ke2 $4 Rf8) 25... Bxg4 26. hxg4 Rxg4 $1 27. g3 Nh5 28. Re1 Rag8) 24. Rb1 Bxg4 25. hxg4 $1 {White must start to attack before Black manages to open lines on the other side.} Rxg4 26. Nf1 Rf8 {[%cal Yc5d3]} (26... Rag8 27. g3 e4 28. Bd2 (28. b4) 28... Bc5 29. Kg2 Rf8 30. Rbc1 Rxf2+ $18 31. Kh3 Rg8 32. Rxc2 Rh8+ 33. Kg4 Rg8+ 34. Kh5 Kf6 $18 {Black is helpless against the attack on the h-file.}) 27. b4 $1 {Else Black jumps in on d3 with serious play coming.} a5 28. Rb2 Na3 $6 29. Ne3 Rgf4 30. bxa5 $1 $14 {Black is subdued on the -side and can only wait to see how White is going to increase the pressure against the .} Ra4 (30... Nb5 31. Rb3 Ke6 (31... Rxf2 32. a4)) 31. Rxb7 Ke6 32. Rd2 Ra8 33. Bxa3 Rxa3 34. Rb3 R3xa5 35. Rbb2 Rc5 36. Rbc2 Ra3 37. Nd1 $1 Rd5 38. g3 Be7 39. c4 1-0

