1. e4 {The first win for Morozevich in Corus 2005 came only in round 8- Moro managed to come back in the tournament after an horrible start for him( Alex had scored 2 draws only in first seven rounds of Wijk aan Zee 2005).} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 6. Qe2 Nd6 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8. dxe5 Nb7 9. Nc3 O-O {Very risky move- black isn't developed and wastes time.. 9...Be7 is much safer and probably gives black an equality.} 10. Re1 Re8 11. Be3 {11.f4 is premature.} (11. Bf4 $16 d5 12. exd6 (12. Nd4 Bc5 {and black survives.} 13. Rad1 $5 Bxd4 (13... Bd7 14. Qd3 Bb6 15. Qg3 Qe7 16. Bg5 Qf8 17. Nce2 c5 18. Nf3 $16 {but this was an excellent chance for black to fight for a draw.} c6 $1 19. Nf4 Bf5 20. c4 d4 21. Nh4) 14. Rxd4 Nc5 15. Bg3 Bf5 $16) 12... cxd6 13. Rad1 (13. Bg5) 13... Bg4 $132 14. h3 $142 Bh5 15. Ne4 Bf8 16. Qd2 (16. Qd3 $16 d5 17. Ng3 $16 {[%cal Ga4e4]} Rxe1+ 18. Rxe1 Nc5) 16... Bxf3 {but white has almost nothing left from his advantage.} 17. gxf3 $5 d5 18. Ng3 Qf6) 11... d5 {11...Rc8 doesn't solve black problems completely, however it's worth a try.} 12. exd6 (12. Rad1 Bd7 13. Na4 Qc8 (13... c5 14. Rxd5) 14. b3 (14. h3 c5) 14... Bg4 15. Qa6 Nd8 16. Qxc8 Rxc8 17. Bxa7 Ra8 $18 18. Be3 $1 {and white's win is still a big question here!} Ne6 19. h3 $1 (19. Rd2 $140 Bxf3 $1 20. gxf3 Bb4 21. c3 $1 $16 Rxa4 $2) 19... Bxf3 20. gxf3 Red8 21. c3 {but white must work hard to win this ending.} c5 $1) 12... cxd6 {Black can sacrifice an a7 pawn by Rd8, but black completes the development and gets a counterplay in that case.} 13. Bg5 Be6 $146 {This move is definitely better than 13...Rc8 previously played in this position, however black suffers in this case as well.} 14. Bxe7 Rxe7 (14... Qxe7 15. Nd4 Qd7 16. Qa6 c5 $18 17. Nxe6 fxe6 {[%cal Gf7f8,Gf7f2]} 18. Rad1 $2 d5 19. Qb5 Qf7 $1 $18 {[%cal Gc4c5,Gb4b5]} 20. Rd3 c4 21. Rf3 Nd6) 15. Qa6 (15. Rad1 $2 {This was necessary to cut off white rook from the kingside!} Re8 (15... Qb6 16. Nd4 Rae8) 16. Nd4 Bd7 $1 $18 {Black king is vulnerable now as well as white pieces cooperate perfectly.} 17. Qd2 Qf6 18. Ne4 Qg6 19. f3 {47...g5 was a last chance for Polgar in this game, but black loses anyhow.} d5 20. Nf2 Nd6 21. b3 c5 22. Ne2 Bc6 (22... d4 23. c3) 23. Nd3 d4 {The rest is simple for white- two connected passers of white decide the game easily.} 24. Ng3 Nb5 25. Nxc5 Nc3 $1 {Moro chooses the most beautiful way to win the game!} 26. Rc1 Rad8 $1) 15... Nc5 $2 {This move leads to serious troubles. 15...f5 looks very risky but that's the best black can do here.} 16. Qe2 (16. Qxc6 Rc8 17. Qb5 Rb7 18. Qe2 Rxb2) 16... Rb7 17. b3 $2 {17.fxe5 would cause black much more troubles!} Bg4 18. h3 Bxf3 19. Qxf3 d5 20. Rad1 Ne6 21. Na4 Qf8 {Judit could try 21...d3 here however white had a way for an advantage in this line.} 22. h4 h6 23. g3 {Moro blew off almost all his advantage with his next moves.} Qb4 24. Re5 Re7 25. c3 Qd6 26. Qe2 Rae8 {Draw is very likely here but Judit messed it up in a time trouble.} 27. Re1 Kf8 {Hard to explain this move of Judit....I think black could safely advance the f-pawn.} 28. Qg4 g6 29. R5e3 h5 30. Qf3 Ng7 31. Rxe7 Rxe7 32. Rxe7 Qxe7 33. c4 dxc4 34. Qxc6 $6 {I am not sure why Moro rejected b2-b4 move- seems like white gets good winning chances that way. While after the game move black stops b2-b4.} cxb3 35. axb3 Ne6 $1 36. Kg2 Qb4 {Judit missed a big chance here! Paradoxial 36...Ra6 freezes the queenside and probably gives black a draw.} 37. Qd5 $1 {Now Moro breaks in.} Qd4 38. Qc6 Kg7 39. Qf3 Qf6 $6 {One more mistake in a time trouble. 39...Rd6 is much more resistant.} 40. Qe4 1-0

