{It was 10 years ago when I last played in the European championship in Ohrid, Macedonia. On that occasion I tied for 3rd but the play-off did not go in my favour so I could not reach a medal. I was looking forward to playing in Aix les Bains the 12th European Championship, I had not been playing in classical chess events since the Olympiad, so I was keen to play and challenge myself to see what can I show in such a tense event where nearly 400 participants were taking place. Obviously the main reason for most of the strong players is to qualify for the World Cup which will be held in Khanty-Mansiysk next August. There were a few players qualified from the previous European Championship, but they were competing to get a medal. It was clear from the first moment that to qualify one has to make at least plus 4 but for a sure spot plus 5 was needed. After round 5 I was quite happy with my 3 wins and 2 draws. In round 6 I got a bad opening and sacrificed a knight to complicate things but it backfired. In round 7 I played with Black against the Georgian young talent Pantsulaia.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 {I had the feeling that my opponent's main idea was to surprise me in the opening, this is a premature move for the queen.} Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 {I realised quickly that after my next move there is only one difference between this position and the game I played against Gelfand in Wijk aan Zee 2008, which I won in memorable style. In that game the bishop was on g2 instead of the badly placed queen.} Bg7 7. Be3 {After the game I found one other game with this position where White continued with 7.f3:} c5 $17 {Here I realised that I am better already which to tell you the truth I did not even dream of being the case after move 7!} 8. Qd2 {Obviously it is very difficult to make a choice with White when the position is worse already at a such an early stage.} O-O {This was the right move to point out the drawback of the early queen move which was made on move 4.} 9. Nf3 Qa5 {I did not really notice during the game the difference between the immediate ...dxc4 and then ...f5 or the other way around. As usual one is more precise as the other. Here the bishop move was more forcing.} 10. Rc1 Rd8 11. d5 {Yes, this is the logical move but White could have got chances to get away from the very bad position by sacrificing the rook!} e6 $1 {This move came to my mind immediately and I loved it, so I did not hesitate too much and played it.} 12. Bg5 {White kind of has to take it, if 12.0-0 I just defend the b5-pawn by ...b8 and beside the fact I have a pawn up I also have the initiative.} f6 13. Be3 {Logical continuation.} Nc6 $1 {It is hard to judge this move I could have played simply ...c6 or ...a6 and have a great advantage. On the other hand with this idea I can stop White from castling and it is almost a mating attack, it looked so good and tempting to me that I could not stop myself trying it out.} (13... exd5 14. exd5 Na6) 14. Bd3 (14. Bc4 Qa4) (14. h4 exd5 15. exd5 Bg4 (15... c4 16. Bxc4 Be6 17. Rd1 Ne7 18. Bb3 (18. dxe6 Rxd2 19. Rxd2 Qxc3 20. Bb3) 18... Nxd5 19. Bd4 Nc7 (19... Qa6) 20. O-O Re8 21. Rfe1 Bxb3 22. axb3 a6) 16. Be2 Bxf3 (16... Ne7 17. c4 Qc7 (17... Qxd2+ 18. Nxd2) 18. Ng5 Bf5 19. Nh3 Nc8 20. g4 Bd7 21. h5 gxh5 22. gxh5) (16... Qa4 17. Bxc5 (17. Qc2) (17. h5) 17... Bxf3 18. gxf3 Ne5 19. c4 Rac8 20. Be7 Rd7 21. d6 Re8 22. Qe3 Nf7 23. Qb3 Qxb3 24. axb3 Nxd6) 17. gxf3 c4 (17... Ne7 18. d6)) 14... exd5 {This was my idea so I did not spend too much time on ...g4 which was also interesting.} 15. exd5 {The only move to bring the a3-knight into defence to c3.} c4 (15... Ne7 16. c4 (16. d6 Nf5)) 16. Bxc4 Be6 {This is the position I was looking forward to when I sacrificed my knight. All my pieces are playing; only the rook is missing but not for long. White has a rook up against only one little pawn, but what an initiative for Black!} 17. Rd1 {Again the only continuation for White to get out from the pin.} Ne7 18. dxe6 Rxd2 {It is very difficult for White to get out from this tight position, the continuation was a logical one though there was an other option too:} 19. Rxd2 {White's plan is to play e3 next and if he had time for d2 afterwards then I would be in big trouble, but it was my turn.} Qxc3 (19... Nf5 20. Rd3) 20. Bb3 Qc1+ {It is unbelievable how strong the d3-bishop can be. It was interesting that in a later round I had Black against Lupulescu and I got a similarly unpleasant situation with the other side, but I made an incredible defence and won the game.} (20... Qa1+ 21. Bd1 Nf5 (21... Qc3 22. O-O Qb4) 22. O-O Qc3 (22... Nxe3 23. e7) 23. Bb3 Kh8 24. Rd7 Re8 (24... Nxe3 25. e7 Qa5 26. fxe3) 25. Bd2 Qb2 (25... Qc5 26. Rc1) 26. g4 (26. e7 Nxe7 27. Re1) 26... Ne7 27. Rc1 Nc6 28. Kg2 (28. g5) 28... h5 29. g5 fxg5 30. Rxg7) 21. Rd1 {White has to continue to rearrange his pieces.} (21. Bd1 Qc4) 21... Qc3+ 22. Rd2 Qc1+ $1 {I was very happy with this move to force White to play d2 before playing e3.} 23. Rd1 {White has to stop the possibility for Black to play  to b1 and control the 1st rank.} Qc3+ {Finally my poor queen got out with the threat ...a5 to put pressure on the c3-pawn. ...c6 was also possible.} 24. Ke2 {Stopping Black from ...a5 and attacking the pawn at the same time.} Qb4 {The main idea for Black is to focus on not allowing White to untangle himself.} (24... a5 25. Rd7 a4 26. Rc1 Qb2+ (26... Qb4 27. Bc4 Rc8 28. Nd2 Bf8 29. Bd4 f5 30. Rb1 Qxb1 31. Nxb1 Rxc4 32. Nc3 Rc6 33. Rxb7 Rxe6+) 27. Rc2 {was too early:} Qb1 {and I made him develop for no reason.} 28. Rd1 $1 (28. Nd2 axb3 29. Nxb1 bxc2 30. Nc3 Nf5 {this was Garry's suggestion after the game when we met him in the park.} 31. Bd2 Re8 {and Black has a huge advantage.} 32. Kd3 $1 {I was simply was not paying enough attention to the fact that White can sac material for an initiative.} (32. Rxb7 Rxe6+ 33. Kd3) 32... b6 33. g4 Nh6 34. h3 Rxe6 35. Kxc2 f5) 28... axb3 29. Rxb1 bxc2 30. Rxb7 Nd5 31. Bd2 Bf8) 25. Rd3 {White had other opportunities like} (25. Rd7 {and next ...h3 with mate on the next move.} Qb5+) 25... a5 {I kind of knew what was going to happen on the next move, or I just hoped for it?} 26. Rc1 $2 {This practically loses the game. White could have tried f1 or g4 but it is really unpleasant to play such a position with White specially in time trouble as well.} a4 $3 {After this move my opponent got very upset, he quickly realised that his position going to fall apart.} 27. Rc4 Qb5 (27... Qa3 28. Bc1) 28. Rc5 Qb4 {White can't stop my queen from helping my rook and the bishop in the attack and once they work together it will be hopeless for White.} 29. Rc4 $138 {White is already in timetrouble, but that is not the only problem he has.} Qb5 30. Rc5 Qb4 {It was interesting to notice that this was the only move in the game where I went backwards!} 31. Bc4 (31. Rc4) 31... Rb8 {The last touch.} (31... Bh6 32. Bd4 {and now shows why g1 was important;} Nc6 {and White saves himself for sure.} 33. e7+ {would be practically losing on the spot to} Kg7 {with the following moves for example:}) 32. Rd7 Bf8 33. Rcc7 b5 34. Bd3 Qa3 {...and if White continues and takes the rook, then d1 wins a piece.  It was such a nice feeling to play this game, that I won my next 3 games and made a draw in the last round. I won my first European Bronze Medal.} 35. Nd2 {would not help either} (35. Rc2 Rc8 (35... b4 36. Bc1) (35... Qb4 36. Nd4 {was still not good to}) 36. Rxc8 Nxc8 {with only a draw, so I decided to continue and bring my last piece into play - my rook!} 37. Rd8 Qxa2+ 38. Kf1) 35... Qxa2 36. Rxe7 Bxe7 {wins} 37. Rxe7 a3 {here e3 still wins but taking the knight would allow White to give a perpetual check by f5-c8.} ({Here I saw the following line} 37... Qb2 38. Rd7 Qe5 39. e7 Re8) 38. Bd4 Kf8 (38... Rf8 39. Ke3 Qd5 40. Ne4 a2 41. Bxf6 {I had seen until this point and I was happy!}) 39. Bc5 (39. Bxf6 Rb6) 39... Kg8 $11 {There was a beautiful line after} 40. Bd4 Kf8 $1 {and if} 41. Bc5 Kg8 $3 42. Ke3 (42. Bd4) 42... f5 {mate} (42... Qa1 $17 43. Bd4 {trying to get some air was also possible, but in all cases I prefer to play it with Black} Qe1+ {this is also an idea to control the black squares and defend the c3-pawn} 44. Be2 {and I really like blacks position} a2 {but then I would play} 45. Bxf6 (45. Ra7 Re8 {and}) 45... a1=Q 46. Rg7+ {and with at least great compensation.} Kh8 {was not a good idea due to} 47. Rxg6+ {and I am not only interested in taking the rook on a1 but in attacking the c3-pawn as well!} Qxf6 {my intention was during the game to play}) 43. Bd4 Kf8 44. Bc5 (44. Bf6) 44... Kg8 (44... Qd5 {and the rook on a1 is killing already.}) 45. Bd4 {was also winning but I did not see after} Kf8 46. Bf6 Rb6 {the move} 47. Rf7+ $1 $19 {which is winning on the spot.} Ke8 48. Re7+ {would win the queen} Kf8 49. Rf7+ Ke8 0-1

