1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 {I had the feeling that my opponent would choose the Caro-Kann but he also plays other moves like 1...e5 on move one.} 3. Nc3 {I have surprised my opponent because it was clear to me it was not what he expected. I have played during my career the Panov attack and 3.c3 also 3.f3 a few times. The game move is one I have tried only once a long time ago in a rapid game.} Qd6 4. d4 {I had prepared this line some time ago and this was the time to test it in a practical game!} Nf6 5. Nf3 {This early d2-b3 manoeuvre came into fashion not long ago. Generally it is always nice to try out new directions specially when there is not a lot of accumulated theory on the line. The idea behind this manoeuvre is to stop Black from playing ...c5 quickly.} g6 (5... a6 {is horrible for Black.}) 6. h3 Bg7 7. Bc4 {My opponent had 2 games on this line previously which I found in my big database collection.} O-O {This move is the most frequently played here. 8 other options have been tried out in tournament practice. The most common other options are 7....e7 which may transpose later on to the ...h6, 7...c8 was played by my opponent in 2010.} 8. O-O a6 9. Ne5 {I was getting prepared for a possible early ...c5 later on.} (9. a4 Nc6 10. Be3 (10. b3 Ne8 11. Ba3 Qd8 12. Re1 {White queens the pawn in the next 3 moves} Nd6 13. Bf1 Nf5 14. Ne2 Re8 15. Bb2 {now white queens on the other side the pawn} {and here if I were to go h5 the trick would be ...} b6 16. Qd2 Bb7 17. Rad1 a5 18. c3 e6 $4 {wow I would just make a draw after} 19. Qc2 Qf6 {...and} 20. Nc1 $3 Nh4 {with stalemate, but 57.b1 for example leave the a7-pawn to be taken and White would queen the h-pawn.} 21. Nxh4 Qxh4 {would lose to} 22. Nd3 e5 23. d5 e4 24. dxc6 {and one of the pawns queens on the next move.} exd3) 10... Bf5 11. Qe2 {I am just in time with my pawn.} Nb4 12. Bb3 Nbd5 13. Nxd5 Nxd5 14. Bg5 {The position is completely lost for Black.} Rae8 {I was kind of surprised that my opponent was playing on...} 15. Rfe1 {47.g3 and going on the kingside was a win as well.} h6 16. Bh4 Nf4 17. Qe3 g5 18. Bg3 e6 19. h4 Qc6 20. hxg5 {I became even more curious as to why my opponent was still playing this dead lost position, but when I realised the trick he is playing for I forgave him:)} hxg5 21. Bxf4 gxf4 22. Qxf4 Qd6 {for practical reasons this was the best} 23. Ne5 f6 24. Qxf5) 9... c5 {It was clear that my opponent is playing for ...c5 to open up the position. There was a game Almasi against Medvegy where Black played ...c5 immediately which was very similar to my game by the way. 9...e7 is the main line.} 10. Be3 {I could only find one game with the f4 idea, which was played after ...e7 instead of ...c8. Clearly there will be many games more to come from strong players in the future on this line too I believe.} cxd4 {I think this is too early, Black wants to save time and bring the bishop out in one move from its initial square. Black could play ...e7 and castle next to play for ...f6 breaking up the centre.} 11. Qxd4 Ne8 (11... Qxd4 {I have reached the 7th rank and was looking to get my a-pawn there as well.} 12. Bxd4 {Otherwise c8 would win the game.}) 12. f4 {It was obvious to take the bishop pair off the board.} Nc6 13. Qc5 Nxe5 14. fxe5 Qxe5 $6 15. Rae1 $1 {I was looking forward to playing this move when I played my previous move g5.} Nd6 {This was the most logical reply.} {I think 16.xd7+ was expected by the audience even probably by my opponent, but I was very much in the mood for sacrificing. I had won my previous 3 games. I had the self confidence to do so. After calculating many lines I decided to go for the complications.} 16. Bb3 $3 Be6 17. Qxe5 Bxe5 {Pretty much forcing.} 18. Bc5 (18. Bh6) 18... Bxc3 19. bxc3 {This is the critical position how should Black defend the e6-pawn. Black has many options to choose from - f7, f8, f7, c4 and I have checked 0-0 as well during the game, because it is very important to know if the black king is forced to stay in the centre or can escape and castle. These are the most difficult things to decide in a practical game with lots of tension, to decide what is the best defence out of the many possibilities.} Bxb3 {This is the wrong decision.} 20. axb3 Rac8 21. Bb4 Rfe8 22. Rf3 {I have another rook to come to the c-file.} Nb5 {Logical move, not to allow White's rook to the 7th rank.} 23. Rd3 (23. c4 Nd4 {It is very difficult to make a move with Black. It makes it specially unpleasant to play this position with Black.} 24. Rf2 {the only move which enables him to play on.} Nc6 25. Bc3 b5) 23... e5 24. Rd7 {Looking at the e7-square for the queen and then following the queen to the 7th rank with the rook to c7.} Nxc3 {To be honest I did not feel at all like taking the badly placed knight at h6 but I decided that I would like to simplify the position. 25.d4 was extremely strong.} 25. Rxb7 Nd5 (25... e4 {Still attacking.}) 26. Bd6 {Basically continuing my plan which I started with 24.b4.} Re6 {The only move to try to kick out my queen from e7.} 27. Bb8 f6 {Looking for counter chances.} 28. c4 {It was about time to start to push my pawns and aim to queen one of them.} Re7 {Looking for regrouping the knight possibly to g6 and reach the long diagonal for the bishop trying to get more active.} 29. Rxe7 Nxe7 30. Ba7 {This was my main point by playing e7.} Nc6 31. Bb6 Rb8 32. c5 {Finally I can bring my knight into the attack; even though it is an endgame the black king can be easily in danger.} Kf7 33. Ra1 Ra8 34. b4 Nxb4 35. Rd1 {I wanted to keep my initiative, but b6 was winning and I know that most of the people would find it more practical.} a5 1-0

