1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Qa4+ Bd7 5. Qb3 dxc4 6. Qxc4 c5 7. g3 (7. e4 Nc6 8. Qxc5 e5 9. Qc4 Rc8 10. Qb3 Bc5 11. Be2 O-O) (7. Ne5 e6 8. g3 Nc6 9. Nxd7 Qxd7 10. Bg2 Nd4 (10... Be7 11. O-O O-O) 11. O-O Rc8 12. a4 Ng4 13. a5 Ne5 14. Qa4 Rb8 15. d3 Qxa4 16. Rxa4) (7. Qxc5 Nc6 8. d3 Rc8) 7... Nc6 8. Bg2 Qb6 (8... Rc8 9. O-O b6 (9... Bg7 10. Qxc5) 10. d4 $17 cxd4 $6 11. Nxd4 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 Bg7 (12... Bc6 13. Bxc6+ Rxc6 14. Qa4) 13. Rd1 O-O 14. Bg5 Bf5 15. Qa4 Qc7 16. Bf4 Qc5 17. Rac1 Ng4 18. Ne4 Qa5 19. Qxa5 bxa5 20. b3 Bb2 21. Rc7 Rfd8 22. Rd2 e5 23. Bg5) 9. O-O Qb4 (9... Be6 $17) 10. Qxb4 cxb4 (10... Nxb4 $1 11. Ne5) 11. Nb5 Rc8 12. b3 e5 (12... Bg7 13. Bb2 a6 14. Nbd4 Ne4 $17) 13. Bb2 Be7 14. d4 e4 15. Ne5 {Anand has a compact opening repertoire, so finding an advantage is not easy. Polgar has made a sensible choice, Black has to fight for complete equality.} Be6 (15... Nxe5 $19 16. dxe5 Bxb5 17. exf6 Rc2 $19 18. Bd4) 16. d5 (16. Rfc1 a6 17. Nxc6 Rxc6 (17... axb5 18. Na7) 18. d5 $19 Rxc1+ 19. Rxc1) 16... Bxd5 17. Bh3 (17. Ng4 Nxg4 18. Bxh8 f6 19. Rad1 Be6 $19 20. h3 Nh6 21. Bxf6 Bxf6 22. Nd6+ $19 Ke7 23. Nxc8+ Bxc8) 17... Be6 18. Bxe6 fxe6 {Black is hurrying with the knight move as otherwise the white queen would complicate the counterplay.} 19. Rfc1 O-O 20. Nxc6 Rxc6 21. Rxc6 $6 {The idea of forcing the bishop to take on f5 is not bad, but somewhat better chances were promised by the more standard move to d5.} bxc6 22. Nxa7 c5 23. Nc6 Nd5 24. a4 bxa3 25. Rxa3 Rc8 $6 {Anand was struggling to get his tournament going and this dubious decision reflects his desire to fight. It was objectively better was to equalise with the help of the 25...e4! advance.} 26. Ra6 Bf8 27. e3 $6 {We should not be too harsh on Judit, since the relocation of the knight is not bad. The sad part lies in a missed chance to punish her great opponent with the precise 27.a1! gaining a serious advantage.} Nc7 28. Ra7 Nd5 29. Ne5 Rb8 $1 {The flow of the game has changed and suddenly White has to look for ways to equalise matters.} 30. Nd7 Rxb3 31. Ba3 Be7 32. Bxc5 Bxc5 33. Nxc5 Rb1+ 34. Kg2 $6 {[%mdl 1024] The mislocated knight will not decide the issue, Anand was better in any line. On the other hand the idea to counterattack the f7-square will turn into a pipe dream and White will go under.} Rb2 35. Ra3 Nf6 {The other very strong idea was to assault the b2-pawn with both black pieces.} 36. Ra8+ Kf7 $1 37. Ra7+ Ke8 38. Ra6 Ng4 {Black could already choose between three different lines with variable levels of catastrophe for White.} 39. Nxe4 Nxe3+ 40. Kf3 Nd5 {Repeatedly Anand refused to contemplate the move b3, even though here it was the strongest line winning outright.} 41. Rxe6+ Kf8 42. Rd6 Ne7 43. Rd8+ Kg7 44. Rd7 $8 Kf8 {In the end the decision will fall in the endgame, where White has perilously little material for the enemy queen.} 45. Nf6 h5 46. Ra7 Rb5 47. Ke4 Rb4+ 48. Ke3 Rb5 49. Ne4 Re5 50. f3 Rb5 51. h3 Nd5+ 52. Kd4 Ne7 53. Ra6 Rf5 {White has no more hopes even for resistance.} 54. Ke3 Nd5+ $19 55. Kf2 $2 Ne7 $19 56. Ra8+ Kg7 57. Ra7 Kf8 58. Rb7 Ra5 $19 59. g4 hxg4 60. hxg4 Rd5 61. Kg3 Ra5 $19 62. Kh4 Re5 63. Nf6 Kf7 64. f4 Ra5 65. Nd7 g5+ (65... Rd5 66. Ne5+ Ke6 (66... Kf6 67. Rb6+ Kg7 68. Re6 $19) 67. Rb6+ Rd6 68. Rxd6+ Kxd6 $17 69. Kg5 $1) 66. fxg5 Ke6 67. Kh5 Rd5 68. Nf8+ 0-1

