1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 {I examined this Anti-Berlin variation in my Opening Database from CBM 120.} Bb4+ {In the article, I recommended Black to delay this move for a while, in order to be able to react with ...d5 under certain cricumstances. However, the game move was not considered unplayable either.} 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Bg2 c6 8. O-O d5 9. Qc2 Nbd7 $146 {Strangely enough, this natural move, aiming to obtain the advantage of the pair of bishops and increase the sphere of activity of Black's own dark-squared bishop is a novelty.} 10. Rd1 O-O 11. a4 c5 12. Na3 Bb7 13. Qb2 Rc8 14. Rac1 Ne4 15. Be1 Bf6 16. b4 {This square would be useful for the knight's transfer to e3.} Ba8 (16... cxd4) 17. e3 (17. Qb1 Qe7 18. cxd5 Bxd5 (18... exd5 19. Nb5 a6 20. Nc3) 19. dxc5 bxc5 20. Nc4 $44 cxb4 21. Bxb4 $1 $14 {1-0 Akopian,V (2713)-Gyimesi,Z (2610)/Crete 2007/CBM 121 (29). One small remark: if Akopian did not repeat the experiment of sacrificing on g5, something might be wrong with it.} {Maybe he should have continued developing with} Ndc5 {I considered} 22. Nfe5 {to be the critical line, as in Akopian,V (2704)-Georgiev,K (2645)/Moscow 2006/CBM 112/[Marin; Postny,E] (0-1, 38)} (22. Nfd2 Nxf2 23. Kxf2 Rb8) 22... Nxf2 23. Kxf2 Rb8 24. Bxd5 Rxb4 25. Nc6) 17... cxd4 $36 {Black's attacking plan is hard to counter now.} 18. exd4 dxc4 19. Ne5 Bg5 $1 {Black should keep this bishop on the active diagonal, no matter how many tempi he will lose in achieving this aim.} (19... Qe7 20. Qe2 Nd6 21. Bxa8 {leads to similar consequences as in the game. The strong b6-bishop and the possible plan of advancing the g- and h-pawns compensate for the pawn weaknesses.} {In a game played after my article was published} Rxa8 {was played.} 22. Nc6 Qe8 23. b5) (19... Bd5 20. Naxc4) (19... Be7 20. Naxc4 $1 f5) 20. f4 (20. Ra1 c3 $19 {[%csl Gd2,Gg4]}) ({If White exchanges queens with} 20. Rxc4 Nxe5 {in order to avoid the attacking plan, the development of the queenside would suddenly become difficult to achieve, because the d2-square is not protected.} 21. Rxc8 Nf3+ 22. Bxf3 Qxc8) 20... Be7 21. Naxc4 (21. d5 $2 {[%cal Gg2g3,Gg1g2]} Nxe5 $1 $19 {[%csl Gg4]} (21... Bxd5 {[%csl Gb6,Gf5][%cal Gc4e3,Gc4b6] and White should not be worse.} {Unfortunately, White cannot maintain the control of the d1-h5 diagonal with} 22. Bxe4) (21... exd5 22. Bxe4 $2 {because of}) (21... c3 $1) 22. fxe5 $5 c3) 21... f5 (21... f6 22. d5 $19 {[%csl Gg4]}) 22. Ne3 Rxc1 23. Qxc1 (23. Rxc1 $2 Nxe5 24. dxe5 {[%csl Gf7]} Qd3 {[%cal Gg5g4,Gg6h5]}) 23... Ndf6 $1 {It appears that the bishop is taboo, because White cannot afford to open the h-file.} (23... Nxe5 24. dxe5 $19) 24. Nc6 Qc7 25. b5 Nd5 (25... Bxc6 26. Qxc6 Qxc6 27. bxc6 Rc8 28. Rc1 Nd5 29. Nxd5 exd5 30. Bh3 g6 31. g4) 26. Nxd5 exd5 27. Nxe7+ Qxe7 28. Qb2 Rc8 {Both sides have completed their regroupment, but White's king's position remains dangerous.} 29. Rc1 {The only way to reduce Black's attacking potential.} Rc4 30. Bf1 Nd6 31. Qa3 {It is hard to criticize this move, because the only possibility of staying alive was far from obvious.} (31. Bxc4 dxc4) 31... Qe4 $19 {Now, Black is just winning.} 32. Bf2 Rxc1 33. Qxc1 Nc4 34. Qd1 Bb7 35. Bd3 Qe6 36. Qh5 Nd6 37. Qe2 Qxe2 38. Bxe2 Kf7 39. Be1 Bc8 40. Kf2 Nc4 41. Bb4 g6 42. Ke1 a6 43. bxa6 Bxa6 {The king should have been evacuated from the dangerous diagonal with} 44. Bc3 $1 {, when} Bb7 {does not yet have deadly effects, for instance} 45. Kf2 Bc6 46. Bd1 {and, for once, the presence of opposite coloured bishops favours the defending side, because the a7-bishop has lost its main target already.} Nd6 47. Ke3 Ke6 48. Bb3 $19 0-1

