{[%evp 0,58,27,32,32,32,65,39,51,36,27,32,32,14,12,-13,81,49,27,17,26,30,47,25,24,13,55,58,22,30,87,4,109,63,86,49,55,58,85,-56,21,46,45,-61,12,12,12,7,0,25,141,46,46,0,20,7,59,-353,-59,-365,-29994]} 1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 c6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Qd3 dxc4 6. Qxc4 b5 {There are different setups possible, but true to her style, Polgar proceeds in the spirit of this opening.} 7. Qd3 Bb7 ({In case of} 7... b4 {'!?' a strong idea is} 8. Ne4 Nxe4 (8... Ba6 $8 {h6 /} 9. Nxf6+ gxf6 10. Qc2 c5 $13 {Grabbing such a pawn is asking for problems, as the opening of the h-file could only really favor Black. A more sound option is} 11. dxc5 $5 {planning c5, Nc4 etc.} Qc7 12. Be3 Bb7 13. Rc1) 9. Qxe4 {followed by Rh6 when the threats against the white king become unbearable.} Bb7 $6 10. Bf4 {and the knight can be installed on f5.}) (7... Be7 $15 8. a3 (8. g3) 8... O-O (8... a6 9. e4 $17 {The point of Black's last move is that} c5 $2 {fails to} 10. dxc5 Qxd3 11. Bxd3 {'-+' and the bishop is trapped.} Bxc5 12. b4 Bb6 $15 {The critical moment of the game. White has a very solid position and hopes to exploit Black's aggressive expansion on the kingside. However, in the present game Polgar shows Black's attacking potential in a very convincing way. One the of main problems for White are the lack of nice squares for the knights and in order to activate these pieces White should opt for} 13. e5 $1 Nfd7 14. Be4 Ra7 15. Bf4) {Recapturing away from the centre with} 9. e4 $1 {is a better option, opening up the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop and clearing the e5 square for the knight. After} c5 (9... Bb7 10. e5 Nfd7 11. Qe4) 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Qxd8 {Black is in great shape. One of the plans is to use the h-file for a kingside attack.} {Perhaps more stubborn is} Rxd8 {though after} 12. Bxb5 {the minus pawn isn't really felt.}) 8. e4 b4 9. Na4 Qa5 {Sooner or later this moves has to played in order to get of the pin.} (9... Ba6 $2 {is less impressive this time, because after} 10. Qc2 Bxf1 11. Kxf1 {the white king can still be evacuated.}) 10. b3 c5 {'='} (10... Nbd7 $5) 11. e5 ({White's best chance is to overprotect the knight on d2, but then after} 11. Nxc5 {'!?'} Bxc5 $19 {'' White can never take on b7 any longer, while Black's attack simply continues (Rg8, Bf8-c5 etc).} {Another fantastic move: ignoring your opponents threat by generating a counterattack. After something like} 12. dxc5 $6 Nxe4 {followed by Ke2 White still puts up resistance.} 13. Bb2 $2 {is strongly met by} O-O 14. Qd4 f6 $1 {'-+!' and since the king can't escape mate within a few moves can't be avoided.}) 11... Nd5 (11... Nfd7 12. dxc5 (12. Bf4 $1 cxd4 13. Rc1 {'-+' and Black will be a rook up.}) 12... Nc6 (12... Nxc5 13. Qb5+ Qxb5 14. Bxb5+ $2 Bc6 $18 15. Nd4 {is met by}) 13. Bf4) 12. Qb5+ Qxb5 13. Bxb5+ Nd7 14. Nxc5 {'?!'} (14. dxc5 Rc8 $1 {everything is well covered.} 15. Bd2 $2) 14... Bxc5 15. dxc5 Rc8 $5 16. c6 (16. Nd2 Rxc5 17. Ne4 Rxb5 18. Nd6+ $2 {isn't clear at all, since after} Ke7 $1 19. Nxb5 Nxe5) 16... Bxc6 17. Bxc6 $2 Rxc6 18. O-O O-O 19. a3 {'?!'} Ra6 20. Bb2 {'?!'} bxa3 {A useful move, as the king leaves the a2-g8 diagonal and the rook may join the attack along the g-file.} 21. Rxa3 Rxa3 $1 22. Bxa3 Rb8 23. Rb1 h6 $1 24. h4 $2 {'?!' White succumbs to the pressure.} a5 $1 25. Bd6 Rb7 $1 {'-+'} 26. Ra1 Rxb3 {Another advantage to start with Rg8 is that the bishop can join the attack via c5.} 27. Rxa5 N7b6 28. Ra7 Nc4 $1 29. Ra8+ Kh7 $1 30. Ra7 $1 {and here White had seen enough, as White can't do anything against the threat of ...Qxh2+, e.g.} Kg8 31. Ra8+ $1 Kh7 32. Rc8 Nb2 33. Nd4 Rd3 34. Nf3 {and mate.} Rd1+ 35. Kh2 Nd3 36. Rc2 $1 {with the idea to take on d1, deflecting defenders from either the g2 or g1 square.} Rf1 37. Kg3 N5f4 38. Bc5 {wins for Black.} Rh1 $5 {with the idea of Rdg7 also suffices} 39. Be3 Nh5+ 40. Kg4 Kg6 41. Rc4 f5+ $19 42. exf6 {runs into} gxf6 $1 0-1

