1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. d4 Bb4 4. Qc2 c5 5. dxc5 O-O 6. a3 Bxc5 7. Nf3 b6 8. Bf4 Nh5 {It would be very optimistic to believe, that this game will enhance the popularity of this side line. Black scores much better with the move 8...c6.} 9. Bg5 Be7 10. h4 $1 Bb7 11. O-O-O Bxf3 12. exf3 {At this moment the omens are not especially good for Black. The absence of the dark squared bishop will be felt for the rest of the game.} (12. gxf3 f5 (12... Nc6 $16 13. Kb1 Rc8 14. e3 Ne5 $16 15. f4 $2 Nxc4 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 17. Qe2 Rxc3 $18 18. bxc3 Nf6 19. c4 Ne4 20. Qc2 $16 Nc5) 13. f4 Nc6 (13... h6 14. Bg2) 14. Qd2 (14. Kb1 h6 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. e3 a6 $40 17. Be2 $2 Nf6 $18 18. Rhg1 Rac8) 14... Nf6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qxd7 Qxd7 17. Rxd7 $18 Bxc3 18. bxc3 Na5 19. Kc2 Rf7 20. Rxf7 Kxf7 21. c5 $18 bxc5 $4 22. e3 Rb8 23. Be2 Rb3 24. a4 $16 Rb6) 12... Bxg5+ 13. hxg5 Qxg5+ 14. Kb1 f5 (14... Nf6 15. g3 (15. Bd3 g6 16. g3 Nc6 $18 17. f4 $5 Qc5) (15. Ne4 Nxe4 16. Qxe4 $18 Qg6 17. Bd3 Nc6 18. Qe3 f5 19. g4 Rac8 20. f4 $16 Qf7 $4) 15... h6 $18 16. f4 (16. b4 $1 Qf5 17. Qxf5 exf5 18. Nd5 $18 Nxd5 19. Rxd5 Re8 $18 20. Bd3 g6 21. Rxh6 Re1+ $18 22. Kb2 Nc6 23. Rxd7 Ne5 $18 24. Rd6 Nxf3) 16... Qc5 (16... Qf5 17. Bd3) 17. Rg1 Nc6 18. g4 Rfd8 19. g5 hxg5 $18 20. fxg5 $5 Nh5 21. Ne4 Qe5 22. g6 $18 fxg6 23. Rg5 Qf4 24. Rxg6 Ne5) 15. f4 Qg6 $6 {Polgar shows her teeth, but weakens the kingside forever. Ponomariov will demonstrate his attacking skills with great confidence.} (15... Nxf4 16. g3 $18 Ng6 17. Bg2 $18 Nc6 18. Rxd7 Rac8 19. Qe2) 16. g3 Nf6 17. Bg2 Nc6 18. Bxc6 (18. Rhe1 $18) (18. Nb5 $1) 18... dxc6 19. Rd6 Qf7 20. Re1 $5 {[%mdl 128] This funny looking move aims to relocate the white queen to the open h-file. Black is slowly facing the impossible task of defending the hopeless cause.} c5 21. Rexe6 Rad8 22. Nd5 Rxd6 {Moves like this are not made with love; Black desperately tries to avoid the impending disaster.} 23. Rxd6 Ne8 24. Rc6 Qd7 25. Qe2 {Any decent computer program would be ashamed to grab the material, since the direct attack was winning much more swiftly.} Kf7 26. Qh5+ Kg8 27. Qe2 {Once again it made more sense to refrain from consuming the the spoils, but Ponomariov is simply spoilt for choice...} Kf7 28. Qh5+ Kg8 29. Rc8 Nf6 30. Nxf6+ gxf6 31. Rxf8+ $1 Kxf8 32. Qf3 {The race on the board doesn't look nice for Black. White's king sits in relative safety while Black is hopelessly exposed.} Kg7 33. Kc2 $5 Qd4 34. Qe2 Qe4+ 35. Kd2 Qxe2+ 36. Kxe2 Kf7 37. Kf3 h5 38. Kg2 Kg6 $18 39. Kh3 Kh6 40. b4 a5 $18 41. bxc5 1-0

