1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Re8 10. d4 Bb7 11. Nbd2 Bf8 {For general ideas of this variation as well as for a small survey see the notes to Cheparinov,I (2670)-Roiz,M (2630)/Kemer 2007/CBM 121/[Marin] (1-0, 58)} 12. Bc2 (12. a4 Na5) 12... d5 (12... Nb8) (12... g6 13. d5 Nb8 14. b3 Nbd7 (14... Bh6 15. c4 b4 16. a3 a5 17. axb4 axb4 18. Rxa8 Bxa8 19. c5 dxc5 20. Bb2 Nfd7 21. Nc4 f6 22. Qa1 Bb7 23. Bd3 Bf8 24. Qa7 Qc8 25. Ra1 Ba6 26. Nfd2) 15. Nf1 c6 16. c4 Nc5 (16... a5 17. Ng3 Nc5 18. Be3 Nfd7 19. Nd2 Rc8) (16... Qc7 17. Ng3 bxc4 18. bxc4 Rec8 19. Bd3 Nc5 20. Bf1 a5 21. Bg5 Bg7 22. Qd2 Ba6 23. Rac1 Qd7 24. Bh6 Bh8 25. Ng5 a4 26. Rc3 Re8 27. Nf3 Rab8 28. Nh2 cxd5 29. cxd5 Bxf1 30. Nhxf1 Rb4) 17. Ng3 bxc4 18. bxc4 Nfd7 19. Be3 Qc7 20. h4 a5 21. h5 Ba6 22. Nd2 Nb6 23. Bxc5 dxc5 24. Ba4 (24. Bd3) 24... Nxa4 25. Qxa4 cxd5 26. cxd5 c4 27. Rac1 Rec8 28. Ne2 Rab8 29. Nc3 Rb2 30. Re2 Bc5) 13. exd5 $146 {Since both Topalov and his second, Cheparinov, play this line with White, the last move must be the fruit of their home analysis. The insertion of the moves b1 and ...b8 is supposed to favour White, because after the opening of the centre Black's rook would belong to the central d-file, while the white rook could still be useful on the b-file.} Qxd5 {Before releasing the tension, Black increases the pressure against the enemy centre. However, opening the e-file and leaving the a3-f8 diagonal undefended before having completed development is not without drawbacks as we shall see.} 14. Ne4 (14. Bb3 Qd6 15. Ng5 (15. dxe5 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Rxe5) 15... Re7 (15... Nd8) 16. d5 Na5 17. Nde4 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Qg6 19. Bc2 f5 20. Nc5) 14... exd4 {It seems that Black has to release the tension in the centre already, since the generally useful moves do not work out properly here. However, by this time it is not entirely clear whether this really is a concession, because the queen is not especialy well placed on c4, being exposed to the attack of the enemy knight with ...a5.} (14... Nxe4 15. Bxe4 Qc4 16. dxe5) (14... Qd8 15. Bg5) 15. Nfg5 (15. Nxf6+ gxf6 16. Qd3 (16. Rxe8 Rxe8 17. Qd3 Qh5) 16... Rxe1+ 17. Nxe1 f5) 15... Nxe4 16. Bxe4 {The pressure against the c7-pawn is aimed to contribute to the queen's stability on c4.} Rxe4 17. Nxe4 d3 (17... f5 18. Ng5 Ne5 19. Qb3 Nc4) 18. Qg4 {[%cal Gb1e1]} Kh8 $5 {White threatened to increase his pressure gradually and Black decided that this was the right moment to free herself by tactical means.} (18... Be7 19. Bh6) 19. Ng5 $1 {This move required some very precise calculation. We have seen Topalov's queen infiltrating herself to the same dangerous looking area of the board in the game Topalov,V (2783)-Carlsen,M (2690)/Morelia/Linares 2007/CBM 117 [Marin] (½-½, 25), when the courageous incursion led to a forced draw by repetition.} f6 (19... h6 20. Qe4 Qxe4 21. Nxe4 Ne5 22. b3 (22. Nc5 Nf3+ 23. gxf3 Bxc5 24. Be3 Bf8 25. Kg2 Rd8) 22... Rd8 23. Bf4 Ng6 24. Bxc7 Rd7 25. Bb6 d2 26. Nxd2 Rxd2 27. Rad1 Rxa2 28. Rd8 Kh7) 20. Qe4 $14 {It would be redundant to attach an exclamation mark to this natural move, but we shall see that it is absolutely necessary in order to keep troubles away from the queen.} Qxe4 21. Nxe4 $4 $19 {Having won the battle of principle in the phase of transition from the opening to the middlegame, Topalov lets be himself carried away by euphoria, caused by the false sensation that the queen is invulnerable.} Ne5 22. b3 Rd8 {What could he have missed when he captured on a7? It is curious that this is not the first game in which Topalov allowed Judit to trap one of his pieces. At Wijk aan Zee in 2005, he played a suicidal e2-e3, leaving the f4-bishop without an escape after ...g6-g5.} 23. Bf4 Ng6 24. Bxc7 (24. Bd2) 24... Rd7 {White will get some vague saving chances if he could manage to drive the enemy bishop to a passive position and exchange the wonderful c4-knight. However, this will be tactically impossible, as we shall see.} 25. Bb6 d2 26. Nxd2 Rxd2 27. Rad1 Rxd1 (27... Rxa2 28. Rd8 Kg8 29. Bc5 Bc6 30. Bxf8 Nxf8 31. Re7) 28. Rxd1 h5 (28... Kg8 29. Rd7 Be4 30. Ra7 Nf4 31. f3 Bb1 32. Rxa6 Ne2+ 33. Kf2 Nxc3 34. a4 bxa4 35. bxa4 Kf7) 29. Rd7 Be4 30. Ra7 Kh7 (30... Nf4 31. f3 Bb1 32. Ra8) 31. Be3 Ba3 32. Rxa6 Bb2 33. a4 (33. c4 bxc4 34. bxc4 Ne5 35. c5) 33... Bxc3 (33... bxa4 34. Rxa4) 34. axb5 Be5 35. b6 $2 Bc6 $1 {[%csl Rf1]} 36. Ra5 h4 $1 37. b4 Ne7 $19 {On three consecutive moves, the advance of the g-pawn has been impossible for three different reasons!} 38. b5 Bd5 $1 $19 {[%csl Gf3]} 39. Ra7 Nf5 $1 {forces White give his knight away for the bishop, leaving the black knight unchallenged master of the weak queenside squares.} {White had several reasonable moves here, but I would prefer} 40. Bc5 $5 {, getting rid of the potentially "bad" bishop and weakening the defence of the enemy kingside.} Be6 41. Ra8 Bd5 42. Rd8 Bb7 43. Rd7 Bc8 44. Ra7 $14 {Black has reestablished the material equality, but White's position remains more active. After a further knight move to c6, Black will be very passive.} Be6 45. Ra6 $16 Bd5 46. Ra4 Kg6 (46... g5 47. Ra7+ Kg6 48. Rd7 Be6 49. Rd8) 47. Ra7 $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rg7,Rh7][%cal Gg5e6]} Be6 $6 {[%csl Gh7]} 48. f3 $1 {Topalov's fine point is that} Kh7 {fails to trap the queen because of the unexpected} (48... Kh6 49. Ra8 (49. Bf2 g5) 49... Bd5 50. Rh8+ Kg6 51. Rd8 Bb7 52. Kf1) 49. Bf2 $3 $16 {threatening e8 and preparing the queen evacuation through a6 if necessary.} Kg6 $2 {is impossible, of course, because of} 50. Ra8 Bd5 51. Rd8 {The preliminary exchange of one pair of rooks with} Bb7 52. Kf1 Kh7 53. Ke2 {before playing} Kg6 {would leave the enemy queen more freedom of action than in the game:} 54. Kd3 Nd6 55. Bxh4 Nxb5 56. Bf2 $16 Nd6 {is safer, but slightly passive.} 57. Rd7 $14 Bc6 $2 58. Rc7 {[%cal Ge7d5]} Bb5+ {[%csl Gc6] leaves the knight undefended, restricting Black's possibilities after} 59. Kc2 {to} Bf1 60. g3 Bxh3 61. b7 Nxb7 {, when White can maintain stable domination with} 62. Rxb7 Be6 63. Ra7 Bd5 64. f4 Bb8 65. Rd7 $14 Bc6 {loses material to} 66. Re7 Bd6 67. Re6 $1 $16 {[%csl Ga5,Gf8]} {Topalov probably considered that White's initiative after} Ba4+ 68. Kc3 Bf8 69. Ra6 {is of a temporary nature. Indeed, having been developed with gain of time, the a3-bishop might remain out of play. In the game, it will be developed to f4, keeping the backward c7-pawn under constant pressure.} {One of the differences induced by the rook moves is that after} Bd1 70. Ra5 Bg4 {(as played with the queen rooks on their initial positions)} 71. Bc5 {the b4-square is not available (...b4 would threaten ...xd5!, winning time), while} Bxc5 $6 {would be bad because of} 72. Rxc5 Kf7 73. Kd4 $16 {Black has to retreat with his knight to e7, thus allowing White to look for ways to develop his initiative in the centre.} Ke6 {Gashimov,V (2664)-Dominguez Perez,L (2683)/Barcelona 2007 (½-½, 35) is also examined in the notes to the aforementioned game.} 74. Rc7 g6 (74... g5 75. Rc6+ (75. Ke4 Bf5+ 76. Kf3 gxf4 77. Kxf4 Bg6 78. Rc6+ Ke7) 75... Kf5 76. Rc5+ Kg6 77. fxg5 fxg5 78. Ke5 Bf3 79. Rc4 Bd1 80. g4 Bf3 81. Rd4 Be2 82. Rd6+ Kg7 83. Kf5) 75. Ke4 0-1

