1. e4 c5 {No Sicilian today.} 2. Ne2 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. c4 Qc7 6. Be2 Nf6 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. O-O O-O 9. Bg5 Bxc3 10. Bxf6 Bb4 11. Bh4 (11. Bg5 Bd6) 11... d6 (11... Bd6 12. Bg3 {[%csl Gf4] Is this the end?} Bxg3 $1 {Not really, just the beginning.} 13. hxg3 d6 14. Qd2 e5 $1 $44 {Despite his material advantage, White might be in trouble already.} (14... Nc6 15. Rfd1 Rd8 $1 {Golubev} 16. Nxc6 bxc6 17. Rac1 Bb7 18. c5) (14... Rd8 $1 {[%csl Rb7,Rc2]} {After} 15. Rfd1 {Black can finally insert} e5 16. Nc2 {and then transpose to an analysis given by Ftacnik in the notes to the game Ivanchuk/Miton}) 15. Nf5 $2 Bxf5 $1 16. exf5 $2 Nc6 $1 {Now,} 17. f6 {is less recommendable because of} gxf6 $1 18. Bf3 Nd4 19. Bd5) 12. Bg3 (12. a3 {[%csl Gf7] Apparently, very dangerous.} Bc5 $5 13. b4 Ba7 (13... Bxd4 {[%csl Gd3]} 14. Qxd4) 14. Ra2 {[%csl Ga5]}) 12... e5 $5 {This (previously rare) move has been brought into fashion by Morozevich.} 13. Nf5 Nc6 14. a3 Bc5 $1 $146 (14... Bxf5 15. exf5 $1 Bc5 16. f6 {Black has moved quite a lot with his queen, but only needs one tempo (...ad8) to complete his development.} gxf6 17. Bd3) 15. b4 Bd4 {Safest.} 16. Nxd4 (16. Rc1 Bxf5 17. exf5 {looks risky, but might be playable, too.} f6 18. Bf3) 16... Nxd4 17. Bd3 Be6 18. Rc1 a5 $11 {Black has a normal development and a central passed pawn. White's structure is more compact, but he is slightly underdeveloped and the weakness of the b3-square suggests permanent prudence.} 19. f4 axb4 (19... f6) 20. f5 Bd7 21. axb4 f6 22. Bf2 Ba4 23. Qe1 Nc6 (23... Bc6 24. Ra1) 24. b5 Nb8 25. c5 Nd7 26. cxd6 Qxd6 27. Qc3 Kh8 28. Be2 Rfd8 29. Qc7 Qb4 30. Qxb7 $6 Rab8 $15 {[%csl Yd3][%cal Gd4d3]} 31. Qc7 $2 {It is not recommended to play with hanging pieces against Judit.} Qxe4 $1 $19 32. Rc4 (32. Bf3) 32... Qa8 (32... Qxe2) 33. Bf3 0-1

