1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. b3 Bb4+ {(!?!) Throughout his carrier, Karpov has been playing all kind of closed systems of the Ruy Lopez. He is supposed to know the open variation very well, too, but mainly from White's point of view, after having faced it repeatedly during his World Championship matches against Korchnoi from 1978 and 1981. With Black, Karpov only captured on e4 twice, between the aforementioned matches. Although his overall result was satisfactory, Karpov must have felt that the opening did not suit his style perfectly. Until now...} 6. Bd2 Be7 7. Bg2 c6 8. Bc3 d5 9. Ne5 Ne4 (9... Nfd7) 10. O-O Nxc3 {This variation had been played by Korchnoi starting with the '50s and has been revitalized by him recently.} 11. Nxc3 {Caught by surprise, Judit deviates from the main course of theory, where, she supposed, Karpov had prepared something.} O-O $5 {Still following the footsteps of his historical rival.} 12. Rc1 Bb4 13. e3 Qe7 (13... f6 14. Nd3 Bd6 15. cxd5 Ba3 16. Rc2 exd5 17. Re1) 14. Re1 f6 {It seems that Karpov likes clarity lately.} 15. Nd3 Ba3 (15... Bd6 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Nxd5) 16. Rc2 dxc4 17. bxc4 Bxc4 {Black has strengthened his centre, but also lost some kingside flexibility. The thematic pawn break ...f6 is not available any more, but on the queenside his pawn majority could become threatening. Although objectively speaking chances might be equal, it is mainly White who will do the attacking in the next phase of the game.} 18. Nb1 Bb5 (18... Bxd3 19. Qxd3 Bd6 20. Rec1 e5 21. Be4 g6 22. Bxc6 Nxc6 23. Rxc6) 19. Nxa3 Qxa3 20. Nf4 Re8 (20... Qe7 21. Rc1 e5 22. Qb3+ Kh8 23. Ne6 Ba4 24. Qxa4 Qxe6 25. Qc2) (20... Ba4 21. Qc1 Qxc1 22. Rcxc1 Kf7 23. d5 e5 24. dxc6 Bxc6 25. Nd5) 21. Qh5 Nd7 (21... Re7 22. d5 g6 23. Qh3) 22. Bxc6 Bxc6 {Restricting the enemy bishop by placing the last pawn on a light square and hoping for a favourable endgame in case of successful defence and mass exchanges.} 23. Rxc6 Qxa2 24. Rc7 Re7 (24... Red8 25. Qg4 f5 26. Qg5) 25. Rec1 e5 (25... Rd8 26. Qb5 e5 27. Nd5 Rf7 28. Rc8) 26. Qf3 {White's attack looks a bit scary, but his king is also slightly vulnerable, which restricts his active possibilities.} Rd8 (26... e4 27. Qg4 Rd8 28. R1c6 Qf7 29. Rxa7) 27. Nd5 (27. Qd5+ Qxd5 28. Nxd5 Rf7 (28... Re6 29. Rxa7 exd4 30. exd4 Rd6 31. Nb4) 29. Rxa7 exd4 30. exd4 Ne5 31. Ne7+ Kf8 32. Rcc7 Re8 33. Nf5) 27... Rf7 (27... Re6 28. Rxd7 Rxd7 29. Rc8+ Kf7 30. Qh5+) 28. Nc3 Qb3 (28... Qe6 29. d5 Qe8 30. Rxa7 f5) 29. Rb1 Qe6 (29... Qc2 30. Qd5) 30. d5 Qe8 31. Rxa7 f5 32. Kg2 (32. d6 e4 33. Qd1 Ne5 34. Rxf7 Qxf7 35. Rxb6 h6) 32... Ra8 (32... e4 33. Qd1 Rf6) 33. Rc7 (33. Rba1 Rxa7 34. Rxa7 Nc5 35. Rxf7 Qxf7) 33... Nc5 $1 $11 (33... e4 34. Qf4 Rd8) 34. d6 e4 (34... Rxc7 35. dxc7 Rc8 36. Qd5+ $15 {Lee,P-Langeweg,K/Bad Aibling 1968/EXT 2001 (0-1, 48)} Qf7 37. Rxb6 Nd7 38. Re6 Rxc7 39. Nb5 Rc5 40. Re8+ Nf8 41. Rxf8+ Kxf8 42. Qxc5+) 35. Qd1 Nd3 (35... Ra3 36. Rxb6 Rxc3 37. Qd4 Nd7 38. Rxc3 Nxb6 39. Qxb6) (35... Rxc7 36. dxc7 Rc8 37. Qd5+ Kh8 38. Rxb6 $1 Rxc7 39. Nb5 Rc8 40. Nd6 Qd7 41. Rb7) 36. Qb3 ({Actually, Black can ignore the bishop for the time being and simply continue his development with} 36. Rxb6 Kh8 37. Nd5) 36... Rd8 (36... Kf8 $2 37. Nd5 Qe6 38. Qxb6 $18 Qxd5 $2 39. Qb8+ $16 {[%csl Rd5][%cal Gf4f5]}) 37. Ra1 (37. Re7 $5 Qf8 38. Qd5 $1) 37... Kf8 38. Rxf7+ (38. Raa7 Rfd7 $11 39. Nb5 $2 Ne1+ 40. Kf1 Nf3 $19 41. Qd5) 38... Qxf7 39. Qxb6 Rc8 (39... Qf6 40. Ra6) 40. Ne2 Qh5 (40... Kg8 41. Nd4 h6 42. Ra7) 41. Nd4 Nxf2 (41... Qe8 42. d7 Qxd7 43. Ra7 Qe8 44. Qb7) 42. d7 1/2-1/2

