{[%evp 0,41,40,40,68,61,78,70,67,27,30,32,55,34,19,6,-4,1,14,-20,-20,-29,17,17,20,-25,10,-12,76,88,93,92,126,116,113,127,146,186,351,336,839,1045,29989,549] It is time to destroy the Najdorf "Through the eyes of Judit"} 1. c4 (1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. O-O d5 5. c4 c6 6. b3 Ne4 7. d4 O-O 8. Bb2) 1... Nf6 2. g3 c6 3. Bg2 d5 4. Nf3 g6 5. b3 Bg7 6. Bb2 {I was happy to find this nice miniature with this underrated line! I hope the readers will be inspired to take up the line with White.} O-O 7. O-O {The knight will go to g3 to cause Black some inconvenience.} Nbd7 8. d4 Ne4 9. Nbd2 {Of course! All the white pieces are pointing towards the kingside!} (9. Nc3 Ndf6 (9... Nxc3 10. Bxc3 Nf6 11. Ne5 Ng4 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. e3 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Be6 15. Rc1 Qd7 16. Qd4) 10. Nxe4 dxe4 (10... Nxe4 11. Ne5 a5 12. Qc2 Ng5 13. cxd5 cxd5 14. Rfc1) 11. Ne5 Bf5 12. Qc1 (12. f3 exf3 13. exf3 Nd7) 12... a5 13. h3 h5 14. Qf4 Qc8 15. Kh2 a4) 9... Nxd2 {Not the typical Najdorf move. The knight would have been better placed on d7.} (9... Ndf6 $16 {One of Black's most ambitious ways is to play for the Dragon type development with} 10. Ne5 Bf5 $1 11. f3 Nxd2 {The fabled English attack setup...but with the bishop on d3 :).} 12. Qxd2 Nd7 13. cxd5 (13. Nxd7 Qxd7 14. e4 Bh3) 13... cxd5 (13... Nxe5 14. dxe5 Qxd5 15. Qxd5 cxd5) 14. Rac1 Qb6 15. e3 Rac8 16. g4 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Bd7 18. h3) 10. Nxd2 {A risky decision..."Only one can live to see the daylight!"} (10. Qxd2 $1 dxc4 11. bxc4 {with an equal game.} e5 12. dxe5 $1 {was the brilliant point with double attack on g2 and f8.} Nxe5 {(Karthik-Verstraeten 2019)}) 10... Nf6 {After this, Judit doesn't give any chance for Black.} 11. Rc1 $1 {Taking control of the important d5-square and exchanging a key defensive piece for the black side.} (11. e3 Bf5 {leading to major liquidation...though there is still some messy fight after} 12. Qe2 $1 Rc8 13. Rfc1 Qd6 14. a4 Rfe8 15. a5 a6 $1 {Not an easy move to make!}) 11... a5 (11... Bf5 $1 12. e3 Qa5 13. a4 Qb6) 12. cxd5 Nxd5 (12... cxd5 {and here I would like to propose} 13. e4 $5 (13. Nf3 Bf5) 13... dxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Bxe4 {Preparing Rd1.} a4 16. Bc3 axb3 17. Qxb3 {Although the position is very close to 0.00, I think our huge concentration of forces on the kingside should give us some hope of gaining some attacking chances on the kingside.} (17. axb3 {One sample line of how things could progress would be} Qb6) {Another interesting direction is} 17... Bxd4 $5 18. Rfd1 e5) 13. Nc4 {The knight will go to h5 to wreak havoc.} Nb6 14. a4 Be6 15. e3 $1 {Judit makes it look so easy! In addition to Black's kingside issues, now he also needs to worry about the d6-pawn.} Bd5 16. Nxb6 {Kicking the only active/developed piece in Black's territory.} Qxb6 17. Bxd5 cxd5 18. Ba3 $1 {A rude awakening! Opening the h-file will be Black's undoing!} Rfc8 19. Rxc8+ Rxc8 20. Bxe7 $1 {No need to hesitate anymore...the end is near.} Rc3 21. Bc5 {The black queen can't protect g6 anymore...A powerful demolition of one of Black's most trusted lines against 1.e4.} Qxb3 22. Qg4 h5 23. Qc8+ {followed by Rd3 next with a winning advantage.} Kh7 24. Qd7 b6 25. Bd6 $16 {with a big advantage due to the space and due to a possible pawn storm on the kingside thanks to the f5-pawn.} {Black had to find} Qc2 $1 {One sample line could continue} 26. Qxf7 Qf5 27. Qb7 Qc8 28. Qxb6 Rc6 29. Qb8 Qxb8 30. Bxb8 Rc4 31. Ra1 g5 32. Bd6 g4 33. Be7 Kg6 {White's control of the d5-square is offset by the activity via the c-file.} {A cool way to keep the d5-square under check is} 34. Bd8 $5 Kf5 35. Bxa5 Ke4 36. Be1 {Black needn't worry about light square domination anymore!} Bf8 37. a5 $1 Rc7 38. a6 {with a slightly more comfortable game for Black.} {Anish Giri in his Najdorf course recommends} Ra7 39. Kf1 1-0

