{It is a great pleasure and a honor to play Judit Polgar, the greatest female player in the history of the game! Before the game began it was announced that Judit was celebrating her birthday. I must say that in the thousands of tournament games I've played, I simply can't recall playing an opponent on their birthday. It must have happened -- but this was the first time I was aware of it. After the generous applause had died down, Judit, smiling at me, gestured towards my pieces and "invited" me to offer "any one of them" as a "present." Hmm. I thought about that one for a moment and hesitated. After all hadn't the great Bobby Fischer offered "Knight odds" to any female player? (An offer he withdrew when challenged.) Certainly, Bobby wasn't thinking about such a great player as Judit when making his challenge! Anyway, after reflection, I of course, reached across the board and removed her g8-knight! Judit burst into laughter, "Oh my! You are not a gentleman!" She exclaimed I had to solemnly agree. I would also like to take this moment, to sincerely thank GM Benjamin Finegold for having assisted me in my preparations for the games in Ningbo. Ben was simply terrific. In a cruel irony, our preparations seemed to be wasted as my opponents ducked, dodged and weaved their way past our well-prepared plans. No matter! For each game, I went to the board in a highly motivated mood. Thank you Ben!} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Ne7 6. O-O {So far, so good. Our expectation was for 6...b6, with lengthy variations dead ahead.} c5 $5 {Definitely throwing a wrench into what I had in mind. The text introduces an entirely different plan of development for Black. With the text Black aims for a possible ...e6-e5, central break, even at the cost of a pawn in order to develop the bishop along the c8-h3 diagonal. The drawback to this otherwise flexible system is that if Black reverts back to a bishop's fianchetto, she may want to challenge White in the center with ...d6-d5, in which case a tempo might be lost.} 7. c4 {There was a great temptation to play 7.g3, fianchettoing my own bishop and hence ruling out ... Black's own fianchetto. Nice plan but I couldn't make up my mind whether I wanted to face Black's activity:} (7. dxc5 {when the h4-knight will be trapped.} Nec6 8. Be3 Nd7 9. c4 dxc4 10. Nbd2 c3 11. bxc3 Bxc5 12. Nd4 O-O {which leads to a fascinating strategic position. Both players have trumps, for the moment, the g3-bishop looks "offside" but Black's rigid center should mean some kind of White advantage. Also, the role of the a6-bishop feels stranded as well.} {The alternative:} 13. Nc4 (13. Nxf5 exf5 14. Bxc5 Nxc5 15. Nc4 Qe7 16. Qd6 Qxd6 {when Black is harmoniously developed and prepared to break in the center with either ...d6-d5 or ...e6-e5, with nice play.} 17. exd6 $5 Rfe8 18. Bf3 Ne5 19. Nxe5 Rxe5) 13... Be4 14. Nd6 Nxd4 $5 {This is the tempo loser mentioned earlier but it certainly seems well timed to me.} 15. Bxd4 Bc6 16. Qd2 Qe7 17. Bxc5 Nxc5 18. Qe3 b6 19. f4 f6 20. Rad1 fxe5 $1 21. Qxe5 Qf6 {had that depressing air of a variation that hoovers all the pieces off the board leading to a forcing draw.}) 7... Nbc6 8. Na3 a6 {A key alternative for Black was to decisively deal with the pin by expanding on the kingside.} 9. dxc5 d4 {Going for what I've dubbed the "extended fianchetto." Since the diagonal b7-h1, will become blocked, Black aims for immediate play against the c4-pawn with ...c7-c5 and opening the c-file.} 10. Qa4 Ng6 $5 {Fixing the structure -- but not necessarily for my own advantage. Here I was tempted to play 11.d1, keeping the center flexible and by implication suggesting I'll castle short, which is several tempi away. I couldn't reconcile whether my rook would be good or merely a target on the d4-square:} 11. Rd1 Bxc5 12. b4 Ba7 {As a consequence of blocking the c-file I've had to allow the opening of the b-file. Now it is my turn to face a very consequent decision, where to put my king? Castling long? That would be like waving a red flag to Judit to come and get me. On the other hand, the king is a strong piece and can help protect the b2-pawn. It is sure to lead to an interesting double-edged position!} (12... Bxb4 13. Nxd4 $1 $18 Qa5 $6 14. Qxa5 Nxa5 15. Nxf5 exf5 16. Nc2 Bc3 (16... Bc5 17. Rd5 {White has two pawns for a knight as well as excellent coordination. I couldn't come to a firm conclusion about the position and so I ducked the line. Playing "solid" for the team...} b6 $5 18. Be3 Bxe3 19. Nxe3 Nf4 (19... f4 20. Nf5 O-O 21. Rb1) 20. Rd2 {This position struck me as promising. Now three pawns for the knight, but I began to have some doubts. After 25...c6, Black's counterplay down the b-file, freezes my rooks and Black may be able to quickly reset with ...e8-f6 and ...c6-e8, with unclear consequences.} Nxe2+ $13 {The final "nail" for this variation for me, is that I saw ...b6-a6, in the not too distant future, when White wouldn't have a convenient way to defend the c4-pawn.} 21. Rxe2 $6) 17. Rb1 f4 (17... O-O $5 18. f4 Rfd8 19. Be3 Rxd1+ {Suddenly I had my doubts that it had been so clever to allow the doubling of the h-pawns after all. In any case, as expected, Judit played the most principled variation.} 20. Rxd1 $5 Rc8 $8 {Black has no choice. Capturing the h4-bishop will leave the knight high and dry.} 21. c5) 18. Rd3 Bxe5 19. Rd5 $1 Bc3 20. Bb2 $15 {In this position it is interesting to note that the "powerful" e6-bishop may end up playing no role whatsoever.} {Trying to stop the d4-occupation in another way wasn't attractive:} Bxb2 $5 21. Rxb2 Nc6 22. Rxb7 O-O 23. c5 {when Black's position looked perfectly okay to me.}) 13. c5 O-O {For the next few moves, both players will face some awkward decisions. On the one hand the text costs Black a tempo but by making this supportive move, Judit keeps some central flexibility. Black could easily conjure up a promising initiative if she can manage ...d6-d5-d4, chasing away the c3-knight. My main alternative was for Black to double or treble on the b-file while I'd do the same on the d-file:} 14. Nc4 $1 {Another key decision. White puts an immediate stop to any plans of advancing the d6-pawn but as Viktor Kortschnoj oftentimes reminds me, "pawns don't move backwards." The text offers Black a magnificent d4-outpost which will act as a magnet attracting a black knight. A new strategic battle flares up, Judit will maneuver to get to the d4-square, I'll try to stop her while trying to tie up her forces to the defense of the d6-pawn.} Be4 15. Be3 d3 (15... b5 16. cxb6 $18 Bxb6 17. Nxb6 Qxb6 18. Nxd4 Qxb4 19. Qxb4 Nxb4 20. f3 Bd5 (20... Bb7 21. Rdb1 {Now the difference of two open files on the kingside is clear. White's rooks are ready to flood into Black's position. By the way, in this line, it is important to note that the b2-pawn is taboo.} (21. Rab1 $1 Nd5 22. Bf2 (22. Bc1 Nc3 23. Rxb7 Rfd8 24. Re1 $1 Rxd4 $1 $14 {I'd evaluate this highly unusual position as only slightly better for White with all three results possible.} 25. Bf1 $2 Nxa2) 22... Bc8) 21... Nd5 $4 22. Bc1 Bc8 23. Ba3 Re8 24. Bd6 Ngf4 25. Bf1 Ne3 26. a4) 21. a3 $18 Nc6 22. Nxc6 Bxc6 23. f4) 16. Ne1 Qd5 (16... Ngxe5 17. Nxe5 Nxe5 18. Nxd3 Nxd3 19. Bxd3 Bxd3 20. Qb3 Qh4 {After the game, Alexander Grischuk came up to me to say, "I was watching the last stages and noted your remarkable composure at the board. An exchange ahead and so sure. Then I looked again, and saw that you had two rooks for the knight!" - "Yes," I said, "the extra rook, certainly helped my composure!"} 21. Rxd3 $1 Rad8 22. Rad1 $1) 17. Nd6 Ngxe5 {Both players are going through a maneuvering phase trying to maximize their pieces. On the surface it may appear as if Black's pieces are going backwards, but appearances can be deceiving. Black is doing her best to maneuver a knight to the c6-square, the gateway to the central outpost. The text envisions a ...f8-g6-e7 (e5)-c6-d4, journey.} 18. Nxe4 Qxe4 19. Nxd3 Nxd3 {Here comes the knight!} (19... Bb8 {Spoiling my fun. Judit wasn't interested in helping me celebrate her birthday. Clearly the normal alternative is a total disaster.} 20. Nxe5 $6 {I was too intent on a g6-invasion, in fact a b7-invasion is simply stronger.} Bxe5 21. Rab1 a5 (21... Nd4 22. Bxd4 Bxd4 23. Bf3 Qf5 24. Qd7) 22. Bf3 {My engines enjoy pointing out my human weaknesses that I missed "stronger" continuations. Perhaps so, but to my mind, capturing the g5-pawn and doubling on the g-file seemed the most natural and straight-forward win.} Qh4 23. g3 Qf6) 20. Bxd3 $6 {Sometimes we can be too clever for our own good. The text caught my attention for two reasons. Firstly, I reasoned that if my bishop was captured, that would put an end to any ambitions of bringing a knight to the d4-square and simply open the g-file. Secondly, by refusing to make the "natural" retreat 20.g3, I wouldn't step in front of my g2-pawn. (I felt its burning ambition to advance.)  Actually, my "main" move leading up to this position had been: 20.g4!?} Qxb4 $1 21. Qc2 Qh4 (21... g6 $4 {Unnecessarily opening up the kingside. Put another way, Black is facing the problem of the g-file being opened (only) and therefore should make plans to evacuate her king. That means moving the d8-rook out of the way.} 22. Rab1 Qa5 $2 {Same mistake as before, unneccessarily opening up the kingside. Once more, 31...b7, and hanging tough was necessary but in this case the damage of an open h-file has been committed.} 23. Rxb7 $1 {A sweet clearance sacrifice. I knew there was a reason why I liked the queen on c2, in the Nimzo-Indian!}) 22. g3 Qh5 23. Rab1 Rab8 $1 {At the time, I thought this move best. It perfectly fits with Black's plan to occupy the magnetic d4-square with a knight. On the other hand, while Judit was thinking, I began to wonder whether the dangers on the g-file were really that severe?} 24. Be2 Qe5 $1 {An important move as it will cost White a number of tempi to get in the g4-g5 break. A false reading of the position is that, "yes but after White plays g4-g5, won't more lines be opened?" Such an objection requires precise calculation as well as an intuitive feeling that Black can withstand such an assault. Judit understood that timing was far more important.} 25. Rd7 {There is no going back now and I must allow a knight to appear on the d4-outpost.} Rfd8 26. Rxd8+ Rxd8 27. Rxb7 $1 Rb8 28. Bxa6 Nb4 29. Qa4 $1 Nd5 $1 30. Qd4 {Up till now I thought that we had both played a good game but now Judit committed a griveous error.} 1-0

