1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. Qh5 Bg7 13. c3 {White has to play very patiently against the clever development by Black. Mamedov has chosen a line that is very solid despite the fact that development of the light-squared bishop has been postponed.} (13. O-O f4 $16) 13... f4 14. O-O O-O 15. Nc2 f5 (15... Kh8 16. a4) 16. a4 (16. Ncb4 $18) 16... Bxd5 {The first truly critical position in the game. The defender had to decide, if he prefers the solid position without queens or the more flexible fight after the retreat to d8.} 17. exd5 $1 Ne7 18. axb5 e4 19. Bc4 (19. Be2 f3 20. Bc4 $18 (20. gxf3 Ng6) 20... fxg2 $1 (20... Ng6 21. g3 Qc8 22. Ne3 f4 23. gxf4 $18 Rxf4 (23... Nxf4) 24. Rxa6) 21. Kxg2 axb5 22. Bxb5 $16 Ng6 $5) 19... axb5 20. Bxb5 Rb8 {The real problems for the second player will take some time to become truly visible. If he can not find any counterplay White will slowly advance her pawns on the kingside and at the suitable moment play b2-b4.} 21. c4 (21. Qe2 Kh8 22. Nb4 Rg8 $18 23. Kh1 f3 24. gxf3 exf3 $18 25. Qc4 Be5 26. Rg1 Qb6 $18) 21... Nxd5 22. Ra7 {Polgar has been enjoying her successful comeback to the chess scene. With greater maturity she sometimes plays more cautiously than was the case in her younger years.} (22. Rfd1 $18 Nc7) 22... Nc7 23. Rfa1 (23. Rd1 Nxb5 24. cxb5 Rxb5 (24... Qb6 25. Rxg7+ Kxg7 26. Nd4) 25. Nd4) 23... Nxb5 $6 {Mamedov had every reason to worry about the future, but the advance of a pawn will not bring the counterplay he was hoping for. White will get concrete play for her well coordinated pieces.} 24. cxb5 Qf6 (24... Rxb5 25. Nd4) 25. Rc7 Rbc8 26. Raa7 Rxc7 27. Rxc7 Qxb2 28. Qe2 $1 d5 29. h3 (29. h4 {It is not so common for the stronger party to queen the pawn after the opponent with a decisive effect.} Kh8 30. Qh5 f3 31. Ne3 Qa1+ 32. Nf1 fxg2 33. Kxg2 f4 34. Qg4 f3+ 35. Kg1 {In the end the technical ending is very easy to win, since White enjoys the presense of the passed pawn b3.} Rg8 36. Rc8 Bf8) 29... Kh8 30. Qh5 $6 {Tactics in the service of strategic ideas is a well known and very efficient tool. Judit plays a solid but academic move completely ignoring the golden chance to decide the game immediately with the help of tactical flair.} f3 31. Ne3 Qa1+ 32. Nf1 {The strongest woman player in the history falls in love with clever tactics again, but not at the best moment. It was much easier to simply advance the a-pawn, as the black rook has abolutely no squares.} fxg2 33. Kxg2 f4 $6 {Black should have punished the great opponent by taking the queenside pawns. Winning in a technical ending would be an unpleasant task for White.} 34. Qg4 f3+ 35. Kg1 $1 Rg8 36. Rc8 Bf8 1-0

