1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. exd5 exd5 {A big surprise for me, because I was certain that this line was a one-game try and we had already had this in our first rapid encounter.} 5. Bd3 Nf6 (5... Nc6 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 Nge7 8. Qh5) 6. Nge2 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. f3 {Simple chess! I had already played this way against the specialist of this line Paco Vallejo in Heviz 2008.} (8. a3 Bxc3 9. bxc3 Nbd7 10. f3 Bh5 11. Nf4 Bg6 12. Nxg6 hxg6) 8... Bh5 9. Nf4 Bg6 (9... c6 10. Nxh5 Nxh5 11. g4 Nf6 12. g5 Ne8) 10. Nxg6 hxg6 $6 {I don't like this move, which in my opinion is just wasting time.} 11. Bg5 c6 (11... c5 12. dxc5 Bxc3 13. bxc3 Nbd7) 12. f4 Qb6 13. Na4 {I was aiming to be able to play quickly and strongly in this game, since winning was my only option at this point in the match. Therefore a position with a clear plan and zero tactics suited me well.} (13. Ne2 Nh7 14. Bh4 Nd7 15. f5 g5 16. Bg3 Rfe8 17. c3 Bf8 18. Qd2 Ne5) (13. f5 Qxd4+ 14. Kh1 Nbd7 15. fxg6 fxg6) 13... Qxd4+ 14. Kh1 Ne4 (14... Bd2 15. Qxd2 Ne4 (15... Qxa4 16. f5 Ne4 17. Bxe4 Qxe4 18. Rf4) 16. c3 (16. Qa5 b6 17. Nxb6 Qxb6 18. Qxb6 axb6 19. Bxe4 dxe4 20. Rfe1 Re8 21. Kg1 f6 22. Bh4 Na6 23. a3 Nc7 24. c4 f5 25. Rad1 Ne6 26. g3 Ra4 27. Rc1 Nc5) 16... Qxa4 17. Bxe4 Qxe4 (17... dxe4 18. f5 gxf5 19. Rxf5 f6 20. Bxf6) 18. f5 Nd7 (18... Qg4 19. f6 (19. fxg6 f6 20. Qe3 Qe4) 19... Nd7 20. fxg7 Rfe8 21. h3 Qh5 22. Qf4 f5 23. Rfe1) 19. Rf4 (19. Rae1 Qg4 20. Be7 Rfe8 21. Rf4 Qh5 22. Rh4 Qxf5 23. Rf4 Qh5) 19... Qe5 20. Re1 Qc7 21. fxg6 f6 22. Bh4 Rfe8) 15. c3 (15. Bxe4 Qxe4 16. f5 f6 17. Rf4 Qe1+ 18. Qxe1 Bxe1 19. Bxf6 Bd2 20. Rf2 Be3 21. Rf3 d4) 15... Nf2+ 16. Rxf2 {White has a very pleasant edge in this endgame and by simply pushing his pawns according to 'the book' will create big practical problems for Black. My ideal pawn configuration on the queenside would be a5, b3, c4 and on the kingside e4, f3, g4, h5 and if White can get it Black will suffocate surely.} Qxf2 17. cxb4 (17. Rc1 Bxc3 (17... Bd6 18. Rc2 Qe3 19. Re2) 18. bxc3 Re8) 17... f6 18. Qg4 fxg5 19. Qe6+ (19. g3 gxf4 20. Bxg6 fxg3 21. Qe6+ Rf7 22. Qc8+ Rf8 23. Qe6+ Rf7) 19... Kh8 20. Nc5 {Just to avoid any e6 followed by d6-d5 ideas.} (20. Rf1 Qh4 21. g3 (21. Qe7 Na6 22. Qxb7 Rae8 23. Qxa6 Rxf4 24. Rg1 Qf2 25. Nc3 Rh4 26. h3 g4 27. Rf1 Re1) (21. Rf3 Na6 22. Rh3 Rae8 23. Rxh4+ gxh4) 21... Qh6 22. fxg5 (22. Qe7 Nd7 23. Qxd7 gxf4) 22... Rxf1+ 23. Bxf1 Qxg5 $19 24. Qc8+ Kh7 25. Qxb7 Qc1 26. Kg1 Qe3+ 27. Kg2 Qd2+ 28. Kg1 Nd7 29. Qxa8 Ne5 30. Qf8 Ng4 31. Ba6 Qxh2+ 32. Kf1 Qd2 33. Be2 Ne3+ 34. Kf2 Nd1+ 35. Kf1) (20. Qxg6 Qh4 21. Nc5 (21. fxg5 Nd7 22. g3 Qh3) (21. g3 Qh6) 21... Qh6 22. Qxg5 Qxg5 23. fxg5 Rf2 24. Nxb7 Nd7) 20... Qxb2 21. Qh3+ Kg8 22. Qe6+ {No f7-f5 please!} Kh8 23. Rf1 Qf6 24. Qh3+ Kg8 25. g3 (25. Ne6 g4 $16 {Better seems} 26. Qxg4 Nd7 27. Ng5 (27. Qh3 {and White has only a very small edge.} Rfe8 {was played in the first rapid game by Judit against me, but after} 28. Ng5 $1 {White kept a small but stable advantage due to his bishop pair.} {I took this line seriously after seeing the game Kramnik-Vallejo Amber 2005 (blind) which went} Nf8) 27... Rfd8 28. Qh4 Nf8 29. f5 gxf5 30. Bxf5 $1 Qh6) (25. Ne4 dxe4 (25... Qf5 26. g4) 26. Bc4+ Rf7 27. Qc8+ Kh7 28. Qh3+) 25... Re8 26. Nxb7 {First step complete, now White only has to play a4, c2-c4, f2-e1-b4 and b6 for a complete squeeze.} gxf4 (26... Nd7 27. Qxd7 Re7) 27. Rxf4 {Overprotection never hurts!} (27. Nc5 a5 28. Qg4 (28. Rxf4 $14 Qg5)) 27... Re1+ (27... Qe6) 28. Kg2 Qe6 29. Qxe6+ (29. Rf8+ {Black is still facing an unpleasant defence.} {Another option and possibly a better one was} Kxf8 30. Qh8+ Qg8) 29... Rxe6 30. Nc5 Re7 (30... Rd6 31. b5 $18 {Maybe a better way to fight against White's plan was} cxb5 32. Bxb5 Rb6 {but after} 33. Be8 Rf6) 31. b5 {Since Black is in a sort of strange zugzwang, it makes no sense to hurry, especially in a rapid game. Anyway this move opens the way for the bishop to reach b4.} (31. Rg4 Nd7 32. Nxd7 Rxd7 33. Rxg6 Rc7 {followed by c5 should be winning.} 34. Rd6 Kf8 35. h4 a5) 31... Nd7 $2 {A blunder, but the position should be lost anyway.} (31... cxb5 32. Bxb5 Re5) 32. Nxd7 Rxd7 33. bxc6 Rd6 34. Bb5 $18 Re8 35. Rd4 (35. Ba4 Rc8) 35... Kf7 36. Rf4+ (36. Bc4 Ke7 37. Rxd5 (37. Bxd5 Kd8) 37... Rxd5 38. Bxd5 Kd6 39. Bb3 Re2+ 40. Kh3 Kxc6) 36... Ke6 37. Rg4 Ke5 38. Kf3 Rf6+ 39. Ke3 $1 {The easiest way to break down Black's defences.} Kd6+ 1-0

