[SourceVersionDate "2011.11.11"]
[SourceQuality "1"]

{I got the chance to play in the 2011 World Cup at Khanty-Mansiysk due to having qualified from the European Championship which was organised in France, Aix les Bains during Spring. Not only did I qualify but I played very well and ended up tying for first which left me with the bronze medal. My first visit to Siberia was in the World Cup in 2009, when the weather was very memorable; one of the nights we went back to the hotel and it was -38 Celsius! My second visit was the Olympiad in 2010. I consider it was the best organised Olympiad so I have played in (I have playedin 8 olympiads in my life so far). This was my third occasion to play in Khanty-Mansiysk (by this time I had no problems pronouncing it either:). The playing conditions were excellent, a well organised event and fantastic live coverage during the event. We played in the Chess Academy which was built specially to organise chess events. It is very modern and in different parts of the building you can feel that chess is respected and is in the center of attention, even the bathrooms have chess motifs on the tiles:) The live coverage was something which would be fantastic to have in future events. In the playing hall there were 5 cameras following the games and the players facial expression s. One could really feel the tension during the games while watching the transmission live. During the round, commentaries were in two languages - in Russian and English. The press room was always ready for a live press conference. Let's move on to the game! This match was in round 4. I had won my previous matches, the first against Corrales from Cuba, round 2 against Movsesian. I won my first game in round 3 against Karjakin who was the number 1 seeded player of the World Cup. In game 2 Sergey was not able to equalise the match and I moved on to round 4! Against Dominguez I had lost my first game with White, which meant that I was in a must-win situation. I did not prepare much for the game, but was very much focused on trying to play a long game and keep the game going. Thinking in the long run I might get some chances. Obviously to win with Black against a very strong and solid player as Dominguez is almost impossible.} 1. e4 g6 (1... d6 2. d4 Nf6 $44 {with good compensation for White.} 3. Nc3 $1 {was the best move, but with great complications; most likely though it leads to an advantage for White.} g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 {this is the way obviously the game would continue because ...f6 would lead to a perpetual check immediately, which would mean that I was out of the World Cup...} 5. h3 {with great advantage for White, but still a lot of work has to be done from the white side to prove it at the board.} O-O {White has the initiative and great attacking chances after} 6. Be3 {but it would be a long and complicated game.} c6 {would lead to a repetition of moves only:} 7. a4 Nbd7 (7... b6 $1 {is what I should have played}) 8. a5) 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 c6 {I had played this line previously, but it is not my main weapon.} 5. a4 Nf6 6. h3 Nbd7 {I have great memories in this line, remembering my game against Motylev in Khalkhidiki Club Cup 2003.} 7. Nf3 {Trying to play similarly to a Rauzer with d2. I thought, if I would go ...e7 and later d6. In this position 7.d3 and e3 are the most played moves. For some time 7.e2 was considered the best reply (as Motylev had played against me).} O-O {Not deciding yet where to go with my bishop - to b4 or e7.} 8. a5 {I was expecting 8.d2.} Qc7 9. Qd2 {Of course, it is not easy to play with White against a sharp Sicilian when the draw is all you need to qualify for the next stage of the World Cup.} (9. Be2 e5) 9... Re8 ({The best choice would have been for White to exchange the rooks} 9... e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 {and it is very difficult to imagine that Black would win in this game.} (10... dxe5 {I think this would be the most promising continuation for White!} 11. Bc4) 11. Be2 {would be the best move, the 2 rooks in the central files are always good! Black will not be able to get out from the pin on the d-file with out exchanging the rooks and then the winning chances for Black are very slim.} (11. Nxe5 dxe5) 11... Be6 12. O-O (12. Nd4 Qe7 13. Nxe6 {and Black is taking over} Qxe6 14. O-O Nc4 {looks very promising, but it's not clear how White will break through} 15. Bxc4 Qxc4 {for example when Black can lose the thread immediately by playing the logical ...f8 then the trouble comes after xf5 exf5 and d5 with a decisive attack by White} 16. Ra4 Qe6 17. Rd1 Rfd8 18. Rd4 {and in the following lines somehow Black can save.}) 12... Rfd8 13. Bg5 {would give White a comfortable game.} Rd7 {was worth considering, it is always nice to have a passed pawn.} 14. Rfd1 Nxf3+ 15. Bxf3 d5 16. Bf4 Qd8 17. exd5 Nxd5 $11 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. c4 {would allow Black to play} Be6 20. Qb4) 10. Bc4 {It was clear after the queen move, that White aims to castle long. I was happy for it and was looking forward to a sharp game! After all I have to win to be able to continue the match!} (10. Be2 e5 11. dxe5 dxe5 (11... Nxe5 {to} 12. O-O {is the move and White will then play xd6 and take my pawn on b4 with a drawn game.} Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 {And now finally White could have forced a theoretical draw by} Be6 $1 {and grab the b4-pawn by sacrificing back an exchange.} 14. Bf4 {would threaten to get rid of the last black pawn on b4 and to enter a theoretical drawn endgame.} Bf8 {now the simplest draw is} 15. Rfd1 Red8 16. Ra4) 12. O-O Bf8 13. Bc4 (13. Ng5 {After the game Zoli said b8 is a draw,...but :)} h6 {which unfortunately I did not see. I was already very tired...} 14. Nf3 Kh7 15. Nh2 Nc5 16. Bd3 {was also possible to attack the b3-pawn} Be6 17. f4 Rad8 {was giving more difficulties for Black.} 18. f5 $1 gxf5 19. exf5 {only draws} Bc4) (13. Ra4 Nc5 $1 {with a worse version than before, but White could still force the exchange of the pawns and transpose to a rook-bishop against rook and pawn endgame.} 14. Rc4 Rd8 15. Qc1 Ne6 16. b4 $1 {This is not an easy move to complete the time control. It would be still save the game because I can not take the g3-pawn with either of my pieces in a good way.} Nh5 {I could fight on by avoiding the exchange of my last pawn on the board}) 13... Nc5 14. Ng5 Be6 15. Qe2 {I had exactly the same position at move 82, so I really made myself and my fans very nervous...} (15. Bxc5 Bxc4) (15. Nxe6 {this is why I did not do this, and at this point I did not see the ...b4 stalemate!!} Nxe6 {It is a theoritical winning position with this material because of the badly placed king and rook.} 16. Bxe6 {I thought this was a draw:((( but ...b1+ and wins the rook.} Rxe6 17. Rfd1 {wins} Bb4 $1 {At this point I was so excited at the thought of winning the game, that did not realise that ...c1 would win on the spot!}) 15... h6 (15... Bxc4 16. Qxc4 {[%csl Yf5]} b5 $22 17. axb6 $22 axb6 $1 {Zugzwang! This is the position I got finally after the 106th move.} 18. b4 $1 {wins on the spot! Of course I am asking myself how is it possible that I did not see it duiring the game, but...} b5 19. Qxf7+ {Over.} Qxf7 $3 20. Nxf7 {and} Kxf7 21. bxc5 {I like these kind of beautiful things, but not during this game I guess.} Nd7 $3 {This move crossed my mind, but I calculated and rejected it in favour of the following line:}) 16. Bxc5 (16. Nxe6 Nxe6 {was the last chance but} 17. Qf3) 16... Bxc5 17. Nxe6 {Finally I got the point, but could I reach it in time?} fxe6 18. g3 {Immediately when I completed my move I realised that I had to go to f1.} Kg7 19. Kg2 Rad8 {It is amazing, that finally when I reached the winning position, White could have stopped the clock and asked for a draw from the arbiter by the rule of 3 fold repetition!! The strange thing is that Domingues was checking the scoresheet carefully in the last 20 moves, probably for a possible repetition, but on the other hand was not sure exactly which position really occurred 3 times. The reality is that by making the move h2 this was the third time that we had it on the board in move 95-105 and now... Maybe he was afraid to claim because he was not sure, if he is not correct the game stops for some minutes and I would figure things out.... well luck is something you need in chess as well as in life!} 20. Bd3 {Yessss.....!!!! I did it, when I think already everybody had given up thinking that I would be able to prove the win...} Bd4 21. Na4 a6 22. c3 Ba7 23. b4 Nh5 24. Nb2 Qe7 25. Rad1 {Oh I was really happy to be able to win this game and equalise the match. I was only happy and not thinking about the following day, about the playoff. Just enjoy the victory!}) 10... Nxe4 (10... Rb8 11. Bxf7+ Kxf7 12. Ng5+ Kg8 {I started to get the point but still not the right way.} 13. Ne6) 11. Nxe4 {I think 11.e2 was a move to consider and then h6 would threat to force my knight back to e8.} (11. Bxf7+ Kxf7 12. Nxe4 Nf6 13. Ng3 Kg8 14. O-O Be6 15. Ng5 Bd7 (15... Bd5 16. Qd3) 16. c4 b5) 11... d5 12. Bb3 (12. Bf4 {When I took the h8-rook I was aware that I will reach the winning Philidor position, but not exactly as I was did it...} e5 13. dxe5 (13. Bh2) 13... Nxe5 {At this point I realised that I messed it up, but} 14. Bb3 {At this point White had a minute left, so it was possible to make a mistake but I had realised already that there would be a beautiful stalemate idea by playing b4.} Bf5 $2 (14... Nxf3+ 15. gxf3 Be5) 15. Nd4 (15. Nfg5) 15... Bxe4 {I knew it exactly that this is a winning position, but along the way somehow I did get confused. I do not have to do any hard work to get the win, and had it right there, but I had also few minutes left only and nerves are also part of the game...}) (12. Bd3 dxe4 $2 (12... e5 {Here I started to get nervous. Knowing that I have a win, how is it possible that I can not do it when there is so much at stake. Actually this was exactly the reason that my brain could not think properly... I guess.}) 13. Bxe4 e5) (12. Bxd5 cxd5 {This made me nervous, because I forgot this move:( and here I started to get mad and angry with myself.} 13. Nc3 e5) 12... dxe4 13. Ng5 {Of course by this time it was clear, that my opponent had not chosen to play solidly, but to play in the spirit of the Sicilian and make it a sharp game! Nearly 50 minutes had been used by him.} Rf8 {I felt very comfortable during the game because I thought that could not have dreamt of a better game after the opening. I felt that I would be able to have chances. In this position there are always basic questions like to play a3 or allow Black to play ...b4 and ...a5 to push the pawns. Lenier decided to go in the sharp direction, which was very brave, but actually a good decision as I did not react the best way.} (13... e6) 14. Nxe4 {After making this move White was left with 28 minutes. It shows how a great a player Lenier is: when needing only a draw to move on to the next stage in the World Cup, he goes in for a sharp piece sacrifice! He is also not only a very strong and well prepared player but also has great nerves! White is putting pressure on the h7-pawn and has two ideas in mind to open the diagonal of the d3-bishop with e5 or d5.} Nf6 15. Nxf6+ (15. Nc5 {White is fighting and looking for the only moves to fight for a draw, but...} Nd5 {Now White can not keep his defence on the 1st rank with his rook, it has to move.} 16. O-O Nxe3 17. fxe3 {trying to escape from the mate on the 1st rank} Bf5 18. e4 Bxh3 {Suddenly the unexpected ...b1+ is the threat and then h1-a1 mate!} 19. gxh3 {A very good practical try and of course I was surprised by this move.} Bxd4+ {Yes I could have finish the game by playing ...c1 with a study like mating net after xh4 b1 mate!! But I thought that I could reach the theoretical win in the rook, bishop against rook endgame. Actually I was right, but not exactly the way I did it...} 20. Qxd4 Qg3+) 15... Bxf6 $2 {I made this move too quickly} 16. h4 (16. O-O Rd8 (16... Qd6 17. Rfe1 {By this part of the game I was really excited about levelling the match!}) 17. Rfe1 e5 18. c3 exd4 19. cxd4 Be6 20. Bxe6 fxe6) 16... Qd6 {Looking for oportunities to play on, but...} (16... c5 17. h5 Rd8 {It was time to go with my king forward to d4, but I still made some unnecessery moves before doing so.} 18. hxg6 {White can not leave the a-file with the rook becaue then I would bring my rook back to g8 and the plan would be ...a8 and ...a1 mate.} hxg6 19. Bf4 Qd7 20. d5 Bxb2 21. Ra4 Bg7 22. Bh6 Bf6 23. Bg5 Qf5 24. Bxf6 Qxf6 {Practically I had not made progress in the last moves so it was all about time.} 25. Rah4 Qa1+ 26. Ke2 {Finally I bring in my king, and in endgames the king is one of the strongest attacking pieces.} Qe5+ 27. Qe3 Qxe3+ 28. Kxe3) 17. h5 {The most logical continuation.} Be6 18. hxg6 {Fantastic play by White! Lenier had 9 minutes left after this move until the time control (move 40 plus 30 second per move). It is also interesting in such a situation which line do you start calculating, because if the first line looks good enough, you might simply have no time and motivation to look for more. In these kind of situations intuition plays an important role. I believe Lenier thought that this was maybe not the very best, but it would be good for a draw at least ...} (18. Bxe6 {White's only option to wait to see whether Black can or cannot break through.} Qxe6 19. O-O (19. O-O-O Qa2 20. Qb4 Rfd8 21. c3 {I was kind of nervous already because I felt that the position is winning, but did not have the patience to figure it all out and think for a while, I was very excited and played pretty fast.} b6) 19... Rfd8) 18... hxg6 19. Bxe6 {Starts to get really scary from the black side... I really have to watch my step, if ...d7 then d3 and it is over, but I found my way to make the game go on.} Qxe6 {Forcing White to enter an endgame with unbalanced material.} 20. O-O Rfd8 21. Qb4 (21. Rfe1 Rd5 22. f3 Rad8 {It is clear that Black's main target is the c2-pawn and the weekness of the back rank, but I will need the help of my king.}) 21... Rd7 {Now White has a rook and 2 pawns for the two bishops. This would give a clear advantage to White under normal circumstances, but the a8-knight gives White difficulties.} 22. Rfd1 Kg7 23. c4 {White has to hurry up to get his knight into safety.} Rh8 24. Qc5 Qe4 {I was not sure about the exact evaluation during the game, but I thought that it was pretty balanced and if I could activate my pieces I could get some practical chances.} 25. d5 {White is playing for safety and provokes me to push my pawn.} Qh4 {I was happy to get some activity and aimed to get more space if I could reach the e4-square with my knight.} 26. f4 {Due to Black having very few pawns there are not many chances to win, but if the battle goes on White might get nervous and then might make unneccesery mistakes.} Qh2+ {This was an unexpected desicion on my part, but I thought that to change the pawn structure can creat more options and the pawn on g4 will blockade the pawns on the kingside.} 27. Kf2 Rh3 28. Rg1 {White is eager to exchange pieces.} cxd5 29. Kf1 (29. cxd5 Qg3+ {Now finally I got the winning position. White is very passive and does not have counterplay, so has to sit and wait, which is really very unpleasant for White.} 30. Ke2 Rxd5) 29... Rh5 {I can not avoid the rook exchange anyway so I offered it.} 30. Qc8 Rd6 31. Qxb7 {A strange decision.} Re6 {With the rooks on the board there can be more opportunities for Black to create some winning chances, if White makes a mistake.} 32. Qxa7 {Of course, White is looking to exchange pawns and with each exchange he gets closer to the draw.} d4 33. Bd2 Qg3 34. Ra3 Qg4 35. Rf3 Qf5 {Trying to free my king with ...d7-c6.} 36. g4 {This is White made his rook manoeuvre to h5. I thought that after this it's a draw, but of course I was going to fight on.} Qb1+ 37. Kg2 Re2+ 38. Rf2 Qe4+ 39. Kf1 {White had already been in serious time trouble for the last 7 moves and there was great tension due to the match situation... after all the draw for White would mean winning the match and moving on to the next round!} Rxf2+ {It is always difficult when you have many options which look all the same as here where to move the rook? The clock is ticking and ticking and you see that you only have seconds left...} 40. Kxf2 Bh4+ {And now after the time trouble White still is able to reach the theoretical draw, but maybe my opponent underestimated my chances. White has to be quick, otherwise if my bishop can reach the h8-a1 diagonal White will be in big trouble. So White should have played now c3.} 41. Rg3 {White had to realise that once I blockade the c2-pawn, it is practically winning for Black!} Bxg3+ 42. Kxg3 {White is playing of course for c3, but while looking the best moment to do so he misses the last moment for it.} Qd3+ 0-1

