1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bg5 {This is what I call the Eros variation subline - a big survey was published in CBM 140. By choosing the text move White retains the possibility of a well-timed c4, but Black should face no problems if she reacts at the right moment with the typical ...d5 advance.} c5 7. Bxc4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Qa5 10. Bb5+ Nbd7 $6 11. Bxf6 Qxc3+ 12. Kf1 gxf6 13. Nxe6 $6 (13. h4) (13. Rc1) 13... Qe5 $1 {Probably White missed this strong and energetic move, which allows Black to obtain even play.} (13... fxe6 14. Rc1 Qe5 15. Rxc8+) 14. Nd4 (14. Ng7+ Kf8 15. Nf5 (15. Bxd7 Bxd7 16. Nf5) 15... Qxb5+ 16. Kg1 Qe5 17. h4 Rg8 18. Rh3 Rg6 19. Rc1 Nb6 20. Qd8+ Qe8 21. Qd6+ Kg8 22. Re3 Be6 23. h5 Rg5) 14... O-O {Accepted!} (14... a6 15. Ba4 O-O 16. Nf3 Qe7 17. Qe2 Ne5 18. Nxe5 Qxe5 19. Re1 Be6 20. Bb3 Bxb3 21. axb3 Rac8 22. h4 Rc3 23. Rh3 Rd8 24. Kg1 Rxh3 25. Qg4+ Kh8 26. Qxh3) 15. Qd3 (15. Rc1 Nb6 16. Qd3 a6 (16... Rd8 17. Nf3)) (15. Rb1 Nc5 16. Nf3 Qxe4 17. Rc1 b6 18. Qd6 Be6 19. Re1 Qb4 20. Be2 Rfd8) (15. Be2 Nc5 16. Nf3 Qxe4 17. Qd6 Ne6 18. h4 Nf4 19. Re1 Be6 20. Ba6 Qa4 21. Bxb7 Rad8) (15. a4 Rd8 16. Nf5 Nf8 17. Qg4+ Ng6 18. Re1 a6 19. Be2 Rd4 20. Bf3 Bxf5 21. Qxf5 Rxa4) (15. Qg4+ Kh8 16. Rd1 Nc5 17. Qh4 (17. Nf5 Bxf5 18. Qxf5 Nxe4 19. Qxe5 fxe5 20. Re1 Nc3 21. Rxe5 Nxa2 22. Re7 Nc3 23. Bc4 b5 24. Bxf7 Rfd8) 17... Ne6 18. Bc4 (18. Be2 Rd8 (18... Nxd4 19. f4 Qd6 20. e5) 19. Nxe6 Rxd1+ 20. Bxd1 Bxe6) 18... Nxd4 (18... Rd8 19. Bd5 Nxd4 20. f4 Nf5 21. exf5 Qxf5) 19. f4 Qd6 20. e5 fxe5 21. fxe5 Qxe5 22. Rxd4 Bf5) 15... Nc5 16. Qg3+ Kh8 (16... Qxg3 17. hxg3 Rd8 18. Nb3 Nxe4) 17. Qxe5 fxe5 {An invitation to a draw. If White wanted to continue he should go for} 18. Nf3 a6 (18... f6 19. Nd2 Rd8 20. Ke2 Be6) (18... Nxe4 19. Nxe5 Be6) 19. Bc4 Nxe4 {when a sharp battle is on the cards.} 20. Nxe5 $2 f6 $18 {I think here White could gain the upper hand with} 21. Nf3 Bf5 22. h3 $1 ({c)} 22. Nd4 {- the Athena variation} {d)} Bg6 {- the Zeus variation.} (22... Nd2+ $1 {and Black is doing well according to my extensivly published analysis.} 23. Ke2 Nxc4 24. Nxf5) 23. Bd5 {is also a good alternative:} Nd2+ 24. Ke2 Rad8) 22... b5 23. Bb3 Nc3 24. Nd4 {.} Bd3+ 25. Kg1 $11 {A novelty. Simpler is} a5 26. Kh2 a4 27. Bd1 Bc4 (27... b4 28. Bf3 Rac8 29. Nc6 b3 30. axb3 axb3 31. Ra3 $14 {Milos,G-Morozevich,A Internet 2000.} Be4 32. Rxb3 Bxf3 33. Ne7 Bd5 34. Rxc3 {transposes to more 'main' lines, where White can choose between: a)} Rxc3 {- the Hermes variation} {b)} 35. Nxd5 {- the Hestia variation}) 28. a3 Rad8 {and Black is fine, as in Timoshenko,G-Epishin,V Tbilisi 1989.} 29. Nc6 $5 Rd2 30. Bf3 Rxf2 31. Rhc1 Na2 32. Re1 $11 {The main alternative is} Rc8 33. Re7 (33. Ne7 Rd8 34. Nf5) 33... Nc3 34. Nb4 f5 35. Rae1 (35. Re3) 35... Rg8 36. R7e5 (36. Rd7 Ne4) 36... Be2 37. Rxf5 (37. Bc6 Ne4 $13 {Dworakowska,J-Arakhamia Grant,K Leon 2001} 38. Rg1 Bf1 $1 39. Re8 Rxe8 $15 40. Bxe8 f4 41. Bc6 Nd2) (37. R1xe2 Nxe2 38. Nd3 Rxf3 39. gxf3 $11 {Wehmeier, S-Illner,A Germany 2008} {This is objectively the best continuation. Black has also tried} Nd4 40. Ne1) 37... Bxf3 38. Rxf3 Rgxg2+ 39. Kh1 Rh2+ 40. Kg1 $11 {.} Ne2+ 1/2-1/2

