{[%evp 0,82,30,28,30,-10,-3,-3,25,30,25,18,23,23,23,-3,-3,6,2,9,-28,-16,-15,-6,-16,2,15,24,26,1,22,-14,-4,-33,-2,-31,-55,-9,-9,-17,-10,0,-3,-3,-2,-3,-10,-8,-2,-5,-11,-6,-6,-21,-22,-85,-151,-224,-194,-333,-374,-376,-391,-408,-91,-409,-32,-23,-24,-322,-294,-332,-293,-302,-302,-296,-296,-296,-296,-274,-296,-296,-279,-279,-284] I played a rapid match against Judit Polgár back in 2010 and lost it 2-6. Some of her wins have already been published, but this one is lesser known. This game was played when the score was 3.5-0.5 in Judit Polgár's favour.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 Na6 7. O-O e5 {Black is ready to weaken her kingside. In return she gains control over the e4-square. It is important that black can still choose where to hide her king. The monarch can also stay in the centre or move to the queenside.} 8. Re1 Qe8 9. Bf1 Bg4 10. d5 Nh5 (10... Nc5 11. b4 Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Na4 {Black wins safely by cutting off white's king.} 13. Nb5 Qd7 14. Bg5 Nh5 $1 15. Qd1 {The position would have been drawn if all the other pieces were shifted by one file to the kingside, but here white's rook has too little space for side checks, as the pawn is too close. Needless to say, Judit Polgár had known this when giving away her h-pawn.}) (10... Nd7 11. Be2 (11. h3 {, but} Bxf3 12. Qxf3 f5 13. exf5 gxf5 $19 {does not really look like an obvious win. That said, white's king is placed so poorly that black could easily promote her pawn. With the kings on the same or neighbouring lines, white's checks can often be met with counterchecks and a decisive queen exchange.}) 11... f5 12. Ng5 Bxe2 13. Rxe2 {Here I lost on time, but it did not matter. The position is easily winning for black, who can push her pawn to d2 and then liberate the king from d1 if needed. The details can be found in any endgame manual. With this win Judit Polgár also won the whole match long before its end.} {White would like to make his rook invisible after} Ndc5 14. Rb1 h6 15. Nf3 $19 {, as there is no stalemate.} fxe4 16. Nd2 Nb4 17. Ndxe4 $19 Nxe4 18. Rxe4 Qf7 19. Re2 $5 {is easily winning for black, as white's pieces cannot protect the d4 and block the h-pawn at the same time.} {Quicker was} Qf5 $1 {(The rook prevents the queen swap for the third time, but this time the c3 is no longer there.)} 20. Ne4 Nxa2 $1 21. Bd2 a5 {, as demonstrated by GM Vokac in his notes for SACHinfo.} {Computer suggests} 22. Qb3 Nb4 23. Bxb4 axb4 24. Qxb4) 11. h3 Bd7 (11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 f5 $1 {This makes a nice echo to 37...c2.} 13. exf5 gxf5 14. Be2 Nf6 {This is safe and good enough.} 15. Bd3 f4 16. Bf5 Qh5 17. Qxh5 {White had many options, but no realistic chance.} Nxh5 18. Bc2 Bf6 19. Bd2 Bh4 20. a3 Ng7 21. b4 Nf5 22. Nb5) 12. a3 {The doubled pawns are not weak there, although the c4 could become an issue in the endgame. Black would like to attack along the f-file.} Nc5 (12... f5 $2 {We have to keep in mind that this was just a rapid game after all.} 13. b4 $1 Bf6 14. c5 $2 Nb8 $1 15. a4 Qe7 16. Nd2 f4 17. Nb5 Bh4 $1 18. Nf3 Bxb5 19. axb5 Nd7 {There were other ways as well, but this one is very convincing.} 20. c6) 13. b4 Na4 14. Nb5 $5 {White prevents the long castle with this unusual move. It costs some time, but it does not matter that much, as the position is relatively closed. I had known this motif from an earlier game. White has a plenty of decent alternatives but probably no way to an edge. The unforced nature of the position makes the opening preparation less important and puts more emphasis on the subsequent stages of the game.} Bxb5 15. cxb5 {White obviously needs to open the position before black consolidates, but this is not the best way to achieve that.} Nb6 16. Be3 Qe7 $1 17. Rc1 h6 $1 {An important intermediate move.} 18. a4 Rac8 $1 {Now white has to part with his bishop.} 19. a5 Nd7 20. Bxa7 f5 $1 {Black would like to recapture on f5 with a pawn.} 21. exf5 $1 gxf5 22. Nd4 Nf4 $1 {This surprising exchange gives black slight initiative. White wanted to play e3-e4 and possibly f3-d2, limiting black's bishop.} 23. g3 Qg5 24. Ne6 Nxe6 25. dxe6 $6 Nf6 26. b6 f4 27. Qb3 $2 {This was overly optimistic, I also miscalculated.} Kh8 $1 {Black's queen protects the a8-square now. Moreover, there is no perpetual and white's king turns out to be very weak.} 28. a6 Nh5 $1 29. g4 Nf6 $1 $19 {Black is threatening 30...f7 as well as 30...g4 with a subsequent 31...g3. There is no adequate defence.} 30. axb7 Nxg4 31. hxg4 Qxg4+ 32. Bg2 0-1

