1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Be7 7. Be3 Nd7 8. Qd2 O-O 9. O-O-O c6 10. h4 Re8 11. Bd3 d5 12. Ng5 Nf8 13. h5 Bf6 (13... h6 14. Nf3 Ne6 $1 15. g4 Ng5 16. Nxg5 Bxg5 17. f4) 14. Nf3 Bg4 {Judit Polgar had this position twice in the same tournament.} 15. Rde1 (15. Rdg1 $8 Bxf3 $1 (15... Rc8 $15 {[%csl Rf4]}) (15... b5) 16. gxf3) 15... Bxf3 16. gxf3 Ne6 17. f4 (17. Kb1) (17. h6 g6 $15 {[%csl Rg2]} 18. f4 Qd6 19. f5 $6 Nc5 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Gd8g8]}) 17... h6 18. a3 Qa5 19. Qd1 Nc5 (19... c5 $6) 20. Bf5 $14 (20. Rhg1 Na4 21. Bd4 Bxd4 22. cxd4 Qb6 23. b3 Nc3 24. Qd2 $11 {1-0 Vokarev,S (2529)-Geller,J (2484)/Alushta 2004/CBM 102 ext (35)}) 20... Na4 21. Qd3 (21. Bd4) 21... Nc5 (21... Nxc3 22. Bd2 (22. bxc3 Bxc3 $11 {½-½ Motylev,A (2640)-Makarov,M (2530)/Sochi 2004/EXT 2005 (44)} 23. Kd1 d4) 22... d4 23. Rxe8+ Rxe8 24. Re1 Rd8 $16 25. Re5 Bxe5 26. fxe5 Rd5) 22. Qd1 Qb5 (22... Ne4 23. Bd4 Bxd4 24. Qxd4 $13 Qc7 $2) 23. Qe2 (23. Bd4 $15 Na4 $5 {This is a possible improvement for Black.} 24. b3 Nxc3 25. Bxf6 Nxd1 26. Reg1 Kf8 27. Rxg7 Re6 28. Bxe6 fxe6 {Black has sufficient compensation for the pawn thanks to the pair of powerful bishops.} 29. Rhg1) (23. Bd3 Nxd3+ 24. Qxd3 Qxd3 25. cxd3) 23... Qa4 24. Qd1 (24. Rhg1 Ne4 25. Qg4) 24... Ne4 25. Bxe4 Rxe4 26. Rhg1 (26. f3 Rxe3 27. Rxe3 Qxf4 $15 28. Qd2 $2 (28. Rhe1 d4) 28... Bg5 $19 29. Rhe1 Qf5) 26... Rae8 27. Rg3 Kh8 28. Reg1 Qc4 29. Kb1 c5 30. Qd3 ({The following line illustrates well the potential of the pair of bishops:} 30. Qf3 $6 R4e6 (30... d4 31. Qg2) (30... Qb5 31. Qg2 Rg8 32. Bc1) 31. Qg2 Rb6 32. Bc1 Rg8 $1) 30... b6 31. Qxc4 (31. Rd1 Qa4 $14) 31... Rxc4 32. Rd1 d4 33. cxd4 cxd4 34. b3 dxe3 35. bxc4 exf2 36. Rf3 (36. Rh3 Re1 $14 37. Rh1 Bh4 38. f5 g5) 36... Re1 37. Kc1 Bd4 38. c3 Be3+ 39. Kc2 f5 40. a4 a5 41. c5 Bxc5 $36 {[%csl Rd7] 1-0 Volokitin,A (2685)-Macieja,B (2618)/Bermuda 2005/CBM 106 (44)} 0-1

