1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 4. cxd5 exd5 {White has won some space, but Black can evolve counterplay - that's the French Defence.} 5. Nf3 {[%cal Gc2c3] Intending to keep the pawn chain, though slowing down the development. This move is having a revival it seems.} Nc6 6. g3 Nf6 {Black wants to start to undermine the opponent's centre with f6, but he can not to do it immediately due f4.} 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O {To give the centre back is safe and not bad at least at this moment.} O-O 9. Bg5 c4 10. Ne5 {[%csl Re5] There is a hole on e5. However, Black should be more or less ok, if this  moves up and then down...} Be6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. b3 Qa5 {Of course, Black has many moves here... and the choice of Mr Korchnoi would not surprise anybody from his fan club and never ever me. I like his aggressive style. And the flank operation is logical with the closed centre. (Black only can decide to open it with e6-e5, but will not want in the chosen setup.)} 13. Qd2 (13. Na4) (13. Qc2) 13... Bb4 (13... Rad8 14. Rfd1 (14. Na4 Qxd2 15. Bxd2 cxb3 16. axb3 Ne4 17. Ba5 Rb8 18. Rfb1 Bf5 19. f3 Ng5 20. Rb2 Ne6) (14. bxc4 dxc4 15. Bxc6 Bb4 16. Bxf6 (16. Rac1 Qb6 17. d5 Rxd5 18. Bxd5 Nxd5 19. Be3 Qb7 20. Bd4 Nxc3 21. Bxc3 Bxc3 22. Rxc3 Bh3 23. f3 Bxf1 24. Kxf1 Qc7) 16... gxf6 17. Rfc1 Qb6 18. Be4 Qxd4) 14... Bb4 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Rdc1 (16. Rac1 c5 17. Qc2 (17. bxc4 cxd4 18. Qxd4 Bxc3 19. Qxc3 Qxc3 20. Rxc3 dxc4) 17... cxb3 18. axb3 cxd4 19. Na4 Rc8 20. Qd3 Bc3 21. Rc2 Qb4 22. Rdc1 Qxb3 23. Nxc3 Rxc3 24. Rxc3 dxc3 25. Rxc3 Qb4) 16... Ba3 (16... c5 17. bxc4 dxc4 (17... cxd4 18. Qxd4 Bxc3 19. Rxc3 dxc4 20. Qxf6) 18. d5 Bxd5 19. Bxd5 Bxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3 21. Bxf7+ Kxf7 22. Rxc3) 17. Rc2 Bf5 18. e4 Bg6 19. bxc4 dxc4 20. Qe3 Rfe8 21. h4 f5 22. Nb1 Bf8 23. Nd2 fxe4 24. Rxc4 Qd5 25. Rac1 c5) 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Rfc1 $6 {This looks like a waste of time.} Rad8 $1 ({The normal} 15... Ba3 {allows White to build up the pawn chain on the kingside too; e.g.:} 16. Rc2 Bf5 17. e4 dxe4 18. Bxe4 $5) (15... Rac8 16. bxc4 {[%cal Gh3e6]} {Therefore a more sophisticated try is} dxc4 {if we want to compare the example here above.} 17. a3 (17. e4 Rfd8 18. Qe3 Rc7 19. e5 Be7 20. Qf4 Kg7 21. Ne2 fxe5 22. dxe5 Rcd7) 17... Bxc3 18. Rxc3 (18. Qxc3 Qxc3 19. Rxc3 Rfd8 20. e3 c5 21. d5 Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Rxd5 23. Rxc4 Rb8 24. Ra2 Rb3 25. Kg2) 18... Rfd8 19. e3 $6 (19. Rac1 Rd6 20. Qf4 Rcd8 21. e3 Kg7 22. h4 c5 23. d5 Bxd5 24. Bxd5 Rxd5 25. Qxc4) 19... c5 {[%csl Rd4,Re5,Rf2,Rf3][%cal Gf6e5,Gc5d4] and} 20. d5 {[%cal Gh3e6,Yb6e6] makes no sense.} Bxd5 $5 21. Bxd5 $2 Rxd5 {[%cal Gf4e6,Gd1h5]} 22. Qc2 {is main line.}) 16. bxc4 (16. a3 Bxc3 17. Qxc3 Qxc3 $40 {1/2 Nikel,B (2289)-Siewert,W (2278)/Chessfriend.com 2005/Corr 2008 (49)} 18. Rxc3 $5 c5) 16... dxc4 $6 17. e3 c5 18. d5 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Gh6g5] Interesting, we have the same position as after 14.h3, but with the pawn on h6. Anyway I hardly can extrapolate that this was the idea of one of the players...} Bxc3 19. Rxc3 {White has the better pawn stucture, so Black has to act...} Bxd5 $5 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Gg5g4] You can shout: "wow!", but it is not so criminal (maybe just a little). Remember, the centre is closed...} 20. Bxd5 $1 {[%cal Gb5d6,Yb5d4] Ouch, Judit found an exact sequence to solve her problems with chances to claim an advantage.} Rxd5 21. Qc2 $1 {Avoiding  exchange} Rb8 22. Rxc4 $1 {No more g5-g4.} Rb4 23. Rb1 Rxb1+ 24. Qxb1 Qb6 25. Qc2 $6 f5 $2 26. Rf4 Qc6 27. h3 (27. Rxf5 Rxf5 28. Qxf5 c4) 27... h6 28. Kh2 a6 29. a4 $16 {[%csl Re6,Rg7]} Kg7 30. a5 Qe6 31. Qc3+ $1 Qe5 32. Qc4 Qe6 33. h4 {Winning the e6 and making the transition to an endgame.} Kg8 34. Qc2 $16 {White is a pawn up, however she still has a long way to go before signing the score sheets. It would be good to exchange the rooks for her. Black should try to exchange the pawns on the queenside and possibly the bishops (under reasonable circumstances).} Kg7 35. e4 $1 fxe4 36. Rxe4 Qf5 (36... Qd6) 37. Qb2+ $1 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Yc5e3,Ye3g5]} Rd4 (37... Kh7 38. Rf4 Qd7 39. Qf6) (37... Kg6 38. Rf4 Qe6 39. Qh8) (37... f6 $44 {1-0 Polgar,J (2658)-Hernandez,G (2560)/Merida 2000/CBM 078 (32)} 38. Re7+ $5 Kg6 $1 39. Qe2) 38. Rxd4 cxd4 39. Qxd4+ f6 (39... Kh7) 40. Qb6 {It's always right to drag the opponents piece to a passive positon.} h5 41. Kg1 Qd3 42. Qb7+ Kg6 43. Kg2 Qc4 44. Qb1+ Kg7 45. Qf5 Qc6+ 46. Kh2 $1 {[%csl Ra6,Rb7,Yc5] Black can not exchange the queenside pawns and the white  has a strong outpost now.} Kh6 47. Kg1 $1 Qc1+ 48. Kg2 $1 {[%csl Ye4]  belongs in the centre!} Qc6+ 49. Qf3 Qd6 50. Kf1 Kg6 51. Qe4+ Kg7 52. Qf5 Qc6 53. Kg1 $1 $18 {[%cal Gd3d7,Yd3d6]} Kh6 $8 54. Kh2 Qd6 55. Kh3 Qc6 56. f3 Qc3 57. g4 hxg4+ 58. Kxg4 Qb2 59. Qf4+ Kg7 60. Qc7+ Kg6 (60... Kh6 61. Kf5 Qg2 62. f4 Qg6+ 63. Ke6 f5+ 64. Kd7) 61. h5+ Kh6 62. Kf5 {Please imagine this position without the b-pawns. Black could draw by taking on g5. Look the line 34...xb2.} Qb5+ 63. Kxf6 Qg5+ 64. Ke6 Qe3+ 65. Kd7 ({or} 65. Qe5 {And after} Qxf3 {(in the spirit of the White's plan, but it looks risky to me). The White Lady could find herself in Black's preparation because she had already played 8.f4...} 66. Kd7) 65... Kxh5 66. f4 {[%cal Gg6g7,Gd5e6,Ge6d7,Gd7c8]} Kg4 67. Kc8 Qb3 68. Qe5 Qb4 $5 {[%csl Ra3,Ra8][%cal Ra3a8] with the drawish idea White , a3,  /Black } 69. Kc7 Qb3 70. Kc6 {[%cal Gb7b2,Gb7b6,Yb6a6,Yb6h6]} Qb4 71. Qg5+ Kf3 $1 {tempo} 72. Qh5+ Kg3 73. Qg5+ Kf3 74. Kc7 Qb3 {and Black has improved the material structure on the board. The question is if his pawns are not too weak now...} 75. Qf6 Qb4 76. Qe5 Kg4 77. f5 Kg5 78. Kc6 Qb3 79. Qc5 (79. f6+ $14 {[%csl Rd5]} {After} Kg6 {can Black solve his problems with} 80. Kc7 Qb4 81. Kc8 Qb3 82. Qe7) 79... Qb8 80. Kd7 Qb7+ {White is again still very far from the victory.} 81. Ke6 $5 Qb3+ 82. Kd7 Qb7+ 83. Kd6 Qb3 (83... Kf6 {is pretty ok for Black.} 84. Qe5+ Kg5 {is more common.}) ({Another setup - to rid of the weak e-pawn and move} 83... Qb8+ $5 {is possible naturally, though it' just play for draw. And White is a bit better.} 84. Kc6 Kf6) 84. Kc7 Qf7+ (84... Qb2 85. f6+ Kg6 86. Qe7) 85. Kb6 Qe8 86. Kxa6 Qa8+ 87. Kb6 Qb8+ 88. Kc6 Qc8+ 89. Kd5 (89. Kd6 Qf8+ $11 (89... Qd8+ 90. Ke6) 90. Kd5) 89... Qd7+ $5 90. Kc4 $2 Qa4+ 91. Kc3 Qa1+ 92. Kb4 Qb2+ (92... Qb1+ 93. Kc3 {[%csl Ra8,Rb8,Rg7][%cal Ga1a3]} (93. Ka4 Qe4+ 94. Kb5 Qb7+) 93... Qa1+ $143 94. Kc4 Qa4+ 95. Kd3 {[%cal Gd1d3]} Qd1+ $5 {[%cal Yd1h5,Rf3g2] and the white  is not in real trouble yet.} 96. Ke4 Qa4+ 97. Ke5 {allows} Qe8+ $13 98. Kd6 $2 Qf8+ {Why not} 99. Kd5 $1 Qg8+ 100. Kd4 Qd8+ 101. Kc3) 93. Ka4 (93. Kc4 $1) 93... Qa2+ (93... Qe2 94. Kb4 Qe4+ {[%csl Ra8,Rb8,Rg7]} 95. Kc3 Qe1+ {[%csl Rg7][%cal Yc2h7]} 96. Kd4) 94. Kb5 Qb3+ 95. Kc6 Qa4+ {[%csl Rf6]} {Black coud play the very strong move} 96. Kc7 $142 $1 {[%cal Ga6b5,Yb8a7]} (96. Kd6 {[%cal Yc2h7]} Qf4+ $5 97. Ke7 $5) 96... Qf4+ {[%csl Rb4]} 97. Kb7 $1 (97. Kd7) 97... Qe4+ 98. Ka7 {[%cal Gf7f2]} Qe8 99. Qd5 Qe7+ 100. Kb8 Qe8+ 101. Kc7 $44 Qe7+ $143 102. Kc8 Qa7 103. f6+ Kxf6 (103... Kg6 104. f7 $14 Qf2 $142 $1 $16 {[%cal Ya1a3]}) 104. Qd8+ 1-0

