{A very sharp and exiting game where despite the big Elo difference White kept the tension of battle and was very close to victory!} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 {As usual, Judit goes for the sharp and double-edged Sicilian Defence.} 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ndf3 Qb6 8. a3 a5 9. b3 Be7 {The position is quite unusual and many different options are available, for example} 10. h4 f6 (10... cxd4 11. cxd4 f6) 11. Bd3 {And now it all starts! White starts aggression and Black took the challenge. A solid alternative could be} Qc7 $1 (11... f5 12. Ne2 h5 $17 13. c4 $1 (13. O-O cxd4 14. Nexd4) 13... cxd4 14. cxd5 exd5 15. O-O Nc5 16. Kh2 O-O {kept balance.} 17. Nexd4 $5 {Both the simple} Nxd4 $5 {and more the forcing} 18. Nxd4 $5 Bxh4 19. Be3 $16 {secured an advantage for White.} {The computer indicates} Qg6 $1 20. Kg1) (11... cxd4 12. cxd4 f5 (12... O-O 13. Ne2 $1 $132 {was strategically sounder.} Rf7 $5 (13... Kh8 {would be a bit more precise.} 14. Bb1 $5 (14. Bb2) 14... Rf7 15. Qd3 {was possible, with a strange kind of French Defence, but Polgar is a Sicilian player!} Nf8 $5 16. h5 Bd7 {was probably a safer option.} 17. Qe3 Kg8 18. Bd2 {ts considered by theory as a main line in Taimanov Variation.}) 14. h5 $5 {Black is completely OK according to computer, but from a human point of view it could looks a bit scary after 14.Qh5.} Nf8 15. g4 $1 (15. h6) 15... fxe5 16. dxe5 Bd7 17. g5 {with a strong initiative was the point of Ng5.} (17. Bd2 $5) (17. Kf1 $5)) 13. Ne2 h5 14. Be3 (14. Kf2) 14... g6 (14... Bxa3) 15. Kf2 Kf7 (15... Qd8 16. g3 {Black does not have enough compensation for the pawn.} Nb6 $5) 16. Qd2 $1 {with crazy and balanced position.} Qd8 $5 (16... Ndb8) 17. Nc3 (17. Ng5+ Bxg5 18. fxg5) 17... Kg7 {was quite a strong option actually.} 18. g3 $5 {is probably too "coldblooded" for a human, even for Judit.} Nb6 $2 19. Rhc1 Bd7 20. Ng5 Bxg5 21. fxg5 Rf8 22. a4 Nb4 23. Ne2 Nxd3+ 24. Qxd3 $19 Bc6 $1) 12. Ne2 (12. Be3 $1 Rb8) 12... Rb8 (12... cxd4 13. cxd4 Nb6 14. h5 a4 15. b4 Nc4 $1 16. Bxc4 dxc4 17. h6 {looks holdable for black.}) 13. a4 (13. Qc2 f5 (13... Nf8 14. exf6 {Black has chances to survive.} {Or} gxf6 15. c4) 14. Ng5 Nf8 15. dxc5 Bxc5 $1 16. b4 (16. Nd4 $1 Bxd4 17. cxd4) 16... Ba7 17. Nf3) 13... cxd4 $5 {Raising stakes to the maximum! After the calmer} 14. cxd4 {One of interesting points of this game is that both sides try to be as aggressive as possible! And it make honor specialy to the white player who had a much lower rating and showed himself to be a very brave player.} Nb4 15. O-O Nxd3 (15... O-O 16. Ba3 Qd8 $1 {and White would win this game! And it would be fully deserved, but, at the same time, Black also deserved luck for her brave play.} 17. Bxb4 Bxb4 18. Qc2 f5 19. g4) 16. Qxd3 O-O 17. Ba3 {A typical opposite sides castling situation has arisen and the question is: Who will be faster?} (17. Bd2) 17... Bxa3 $1 18. Rxa3 $1 Nc5 $1 19. Qc3 $1 {No retreating is allowed} Na6 20. Qxc7 Nxc7 21. h5 {Continuing in to keep up the pressure} h6 22. exf6 {Again an attacking move!} gxf6 {Black is preparing counterattacking ..Rxf4 idea in some variations.} 23. f5 e5 24. Nh4 Bd7 25. Ng6 {The position looks quite chaotic, but it is dynamically equal acoording to engines. The next calm moves of the black king are impressive!} Rfe8 $1 26. Raa1 Na6 $1 27. Rac1 Kf7 {Objectively this is mistake, as Black was already better after some defensive moves, but this move is so much in the spirit of all this game (no retreating!) that this hardly deserves serious criticism.} 28. g4 $1 {From now on White plays the best moves and comes just one move short of a sensational victory!} b5 {Finally oversteping the line. After the objectively correct} 29. Rfe1 $1 bxa4 30. bxa4 $1 Rb4 31. Kf2 exd4 {The white king still in trouble, but he finds a solution} 32. Nef4 $1 Rb2+ 33. Kf1 $4 {The culmination of a hard tactical battle! Just one more strong move was required} Re3 $1 {A beautiful mating combination, which Judit was exellent at finding from early childhood. This was a uite painful for White, but such is chess life.} 34. Nxd5 Rf3+ $1 {And White resigns due to mate in two} 35. Kg1 Rg3+ 36. Kf1 Rf3+ 37. Kg1 0-1

