{[%evp 0,85,37,6,79,79,44,38,41,32,32,32,95,45,120,48,84,55,51,45,69,35,82,19,30,-5,15,15,46,-35,8,-25,-12,-12,-7,-26,-9,-77,-56,-96,-127,-106,-106,-106,-106,-106,-106,-123,-123,-145,-79,-147,-147,-147,-146,-147,-159,-159,-116,-140,-140,-150,-166,-166,-162,-179,0,0,98,55,98,53,53,58,58,67,372,422,422,151,154,149,149,77,78,80,80,75] Back in 1996, Judit Polgar was already among the best players in the world. She was used to participating in the strongest tournaments in the World, and beating the other top players. In this game, she is going to have a highly interesting encounter with Vassili Ivanchuk!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 {It's absolutely no surprise to see that Judit employs 1.e4 and follows with the aggressive and complicate lines of the Open Sicilian. And of course Ivanchuk is not someone who goes away from the battlefield.} cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 {At the time, the Classical Sicilian was more popular than nowadays. It's only a few years later that the Najdorf got the statut of the obvious main Sicilian.} 6. Bg5 {The Richter Rauzer variation has always been the most complicated variation and the one that gives Black the most worries.} e6 7. Qd2 a6 8. O-O-O h6 {This line isn't really played anymore, but that's not because of the variation of the game.} (8... Bd7 {Recently, it clearly became the main line. Also 8...Nxd4 or 8...Be7 are possible continuations for Black.} 9. f4 Be7 10. Nf3 b5 11. Bxf6 gxf6 {A necessity to keep the protection of the d6-pawn.} 12. Kb1 Qb6 13. f5 O-O-O {Often the right side to castle for Black when he had to take back with the g-pawn on f6. As in the following game, even the current World Champion recently tried this variation.} 14. Bd3 Kb8 15. Ne2 Bc8 16. Nf4 Ne5 17. Be2 d5 $132 {0-1 (45) Tari,A (2633)-Carlsen,M (2863) Stavanger 2020}) 9. Be3 {The most played move. White juste replaces the bishop, and he considers that the pawn on h6 will help him in the attack in order to push g4-g5 quickly. That's especially true if Black castles, but Ivanchuk will avoid that.} (9. Nxc6 $1 {I consider it's the most dangerous continuation here.} bxc6 10. Bf4 d5 11. Qe3 {Practice has shown that the position is difficult for Black.} Bb4 12. Be2 $1 {An important waiting move that prevents annoying jumps by the f6-knight in case if e4-e5.} O-O 13. e5 $40 {White chases the knight away, and with the help of the pawn on e5, he will get a dangerous attack on the kingside.}) 9... Be7 10. f4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 {Ivanchuk is in no rush of castling. He knows it would just help Polgar's attack with g4-g5.} 12. g4 $6 {The only time this move has been played! Black has tried many moves. This one is ambitious, but probably a bit too much for now.} (12. Kb1 {This kind of move, or 12.Bd3 or even 12.Rg1 seems more reasonable for now.} Bb7 13. Bd3 O-O 14. e5 {White plays simple and strong. He forces Black to get into a passive position.} dxe5 15. fxe5 Nd7 16. Ne4 Bxe4 {The knight is too strong, the bishop has to take it even if it means that the white dark-squares bishop will be a monster.} 17. Bxe4 Rb8 18. c3 $14 {Black could hold in, but his position isn't entirely satisfying., as in ½-½ (41) Kasparov,G (2795)-Kramnik,V (2740) Novgorod 1997.}) 12... Bb7 13. Bg2 e5 $1 {I like a lot his reaction. Ivanchuk feels that White wasn't totally ready to open push the pawns, and he reacts immediately on the center.} 14. Bg1 $1 {The bishop needs to avoid the e3 and f2 square, not to be under attack in case of 14...Nxg4.} (14. fxe5 {This is leading to a worse endgame for White.} dxe5 15. Bxe5 Qxd2+ 16. Rxd2 Nxg4 {[%cal Re7g5] Taking on g7 isn't possible because Black will have ...Bg5 in the end, winning the exchange.} 17. Bf4 g5 18. Bg3 Ne3 $17) 14... Rc8 $1 {[%cal Rc8c3] Black prepares his next move.} 15. Kb1 {Sooner or later, this move will be necessary because of the weakened c1-h6 diagonal where White has the king and the queen.} Rxc3 $1 {A thematical sacrifice totally justified in this situation. Black gives up the exchange but the center explodes.  So far, we can't say that the opening is going smoothly for White, but Polgar keeps the track of the game, and she will fight back.} 16. Qxc3 Nxe4 17. Qe1 Nc3+ 18. bxc3 $1 {I like this reaction. Polgar accepts a poor pawn structure to keep the initiative.} (18. Qxc3 {This would lead to major simplifications.} Bxg2 19. fxe5 Bxh1 20. exd6 {[%cal Rc3g7,Rd6e7] There are many threats, and Black should give up the material back.} O-O $1 (20... Bf6 $6 {Trying to keep the material doesn't help} 21. Bd4 $1 Ba8 (21... Qxd6 $1 22. Re1+ Kd7 23. Bxf6 $40 {And that's far from easy for Black.}) 22. Bxf6 gxf6 23. d7+ Kf8 24. h4 $18 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Rf8g7,Rg4g5] Black is actually hopeless here, the pawn on d7 is too strong.}) 21. Bb6 $1 Qxb6 22. dxe7 Re8 23. Rxh1 Rxe7 $15 {In the end, Black is probably slightly better due to the better activity of the rook, but that should be holdable for White.}) 18... Bxg2 19. fxe5 Bh4 $1 {This forces a decision by White.} (19... Bxh1 $4 20. exd6 $18 {[%cal Re1e8]}) 20. Bf2 $6 {Very natural, but objectively inferior.} (20. Qe2 $1 {[%cal Re2g2] That was another interesting option.} Bxh1 21. exd6+ Kd7 (21... Kf8 22. d7 Be7 $13 {The computer claims a small to White, but I am not sure he is going to make real progress.}) 22. Bf2 $1 Bb7 23. Qd3 $13 {[%cal Rd3f5] Everything can happen here.}) 20... Bxf2 21. Qxf2 Bxh1 22. Rxh1 dxe5 {Black is finally a pawn with a better pawn structure. That's obvious that if he manages to castle, he will reach a winning position. The only solution is to prevent it.} 23. Qc5 $1 {[%cal Rc5e5]} f6 $1 {Black defends the e5-pawn, but also prepares ...Kf7 to bring the rook into play.} 24. Kc1 $1 {[%cal Rh1d1] This is the only way to take the c-file.} Qd7 (24... Kf7 $6 25. Rd1 Qe7 26. Qd5+ Qe6 27. Qb7+ $13 {[%cal Rd1d7] That's too dangerous because the rook is coming to invade the seventh rank.}) 25. h3 {A mistake, but a very understandable one. It looks scary to give up the g4-pawn.} (25. Rd1 $1 {Such a hard move.} Qxg4 {It seems difficult for White to keep something. He can try 26.Qc6+-xa6 but then the queen will be out play, and the king goes to f7. The center of pawns is going to be amazing. But the computer finds an "only move" that is really surprising.} 26. h4 $1 $11 {And it seems it's hard to find a move for Black. For example.} Kf7 $4 27. Qa7+ Kg6 28. Rg1 $18 {[%cal Rg1g6]}) 25... Kf7 $1 26. Rd1 Qb7 27. Rd6 Rc8 28. Qe3 {So far, Ivanchuk has played an excellent game, and he is going to being winning. But he already had to face a lot of pressure, and Polgar is going to make him collapse.} Rc4 $6 (28... Rc7 $1 29. Qd3 Ke7 {That's hard to play such moves, but everything holds for Black.} 30. Rd8 Qh1+ 31. Kb2 Qc6 $19) 29. Qd3 Ke7 30. Rd8 $1 {[%csl Rd6][%cal Rd3d6] The situation is getting out of hands as the rook invades the 8th rank.} Qc6 31. Rg8 Rxc3 $1 (31... Kf7 $4 {This doesn't help as the g7-pawn falls anyway.} 32. Qh7 $18) 32. Qg6 $1 e4 $2 (32... Kd6 $1 {The only way to keep an edge, but that's so difficult to find.} 33. Rxg7 Qf3 34. Qe8 Kc5 $17 {And eventually, the king looks more or less safe when he reaches the queenside.}) 33. Rxg7+ Kd6 (33... Ke6 34. Qf5+ Kd6 35. Qxf6+ {goes back to the game.}) 34. Qxf6+ Kd5 35. Qf5+ Kd4 (35... Kd6 $4 36. Rg6+ $18) 36. Rd7+ Kc4 $1 {Black still finds the only moves to survive.} (36... Ke3 $4 37. Rd2 {[%cal Rf5f2] Black is defenceless against the mating threat on f2.}) 37. a3 $1 {Such a nice move. Polgar simply prevents the possibility of ...Kb4, and she closes the mating net.} b4 (37... Rf3 $8 {After this accurate move, everything tends to lead to a draw. But there are too many things to calculate for Ivanchuk.} 38. Qe5 (38. Rc7 Rxf5 39. Rxc6+ Kd5 40. Rxh6 Rf1+ 41. Kd2 Rf2+ 42. Kd1 Kd4 $11) 38... Rf1+ {[%cal Rc6d7]} 39. Rd1 Rxd1+ 40. Kxd1 b4 $1 $11) 38. Qf1+ Rd3 $1 (38... Kc5 39. Qf8+ $1 {[%cal Rf8b4] A nice geometrical manoeuver.} Kb6 40. Qb8+ Kc5 41. Qxb4#) 39. Rxd3 (39. cxd3+ $4 Kb3+ $1 {Suddenly, with the counter-check, that's even White who should be careful.} 40. Kd1 Qxd7 $11) 39... exd3 40. Qxd3+ Kc5 41. axb4+ Kxb4 42. Qb3+ Ka5 (42... Kc5 43. Qc3+ Kb6 44. Qxc6+ Kxc6 45. h4 Kd5 46. g5 hxg5 47. hxg5 Ke5 48. c4 Kf5 49. c5 $18) 43. c3 {Ivanchuk resigns because he understands his situation is hopeless. An impressive game by Judit Polgar which shows her strength and the amount of pressure she put in her games that can make players like Ivanchuk fall even when they are objecively better.} (43. c3 {After this move, Black can't do much but to wait and prevent the mate on b4.} Qc5 (43... Qh1+ 44. Kb2 Qh2+ 45. Ka3 {[%cal Rb3b4]} Qd6+ 46. Qb4+ Qxb4+ 47. cxb4+ Kb5 48. h4 $18) 44. Kb2 $1 {Black can only wait, and White can start to push his pawns on the kingside.} (44. Qb4+ $5 {Complicated but winning with accurate play.} Qxb4 45. cxb4+ Kxb4 46. h4 Kc4 47. g5 hxg5 48. h5 g4 49. Kd2 $1 $18 {Would be also winning.}) (44. h4 $2 Qg1+ 45. Kb2 Qxg4 $11 {[%csl Rb4][%cal Rg4b4]}) 44... Qd6 45. h4 Qc5 46. Qb4+ Qxb4+ 47. cxb4+ Kxb4 48. g5 hxg5 49. hxg5 $18) 1-0

