1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bd3 Bd6 (5... Bg4 6. Qe2+ (6. c3 Qd7 7. O-O Bd6 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 O-O-O 10. Nbd2 h5 11. Qc2 Nge7 12. Rfe1 Bf5 13. Nf1 Bxd3 14. Qxd3 Nf5 15. Ne3 Nce7 16. Re2 g5 17. Bg3 Bxg3 18. fxg3 Nxe3 19. Qxe3 Nf5 $15 {½-½ Gubanov,A-Lobach,P/St.Petersburg Chigorin 1995 (57)}) 6... Qe7 7. Be3 Nb4 8. Bb5+ c6 9. Ba4 a5 10. c3 Bxf3 11. gxf3 Na6 12. Nd2 Qe6 13. Bc2 Bd6 14. O-O-O Ne7 15. Rde1 Nc7 16. f4 O-O-O 17. Rhg1 g6 18. Nf3 Nf5 19. Bd2 Qxe2 20. Rxe2 Ng7 21. f5 Nxf5 22. Bxf5+ gxf5 23. Rg5 Rde8 24. Rxe8+ Nxe8 25. Rxf5 f6 26. Bf4 $14 {½-½ Iruzubieta,J-Romero Holmes,A/ESP-ch Canete 1994 (37)}) (5... Nb4 6. Bb5+ Bd7 7. Qe2+ Be7 8. Bxd7+ Qxd7 9. Ne5 Qc8 10. a3 Na6 11. Nc3 c6 12. Bd2 Nf6 13. O-O O-O 14. Rab1 Bd8 15. Qd3 Nc7 16. f4 Nce8 17. Ne2 a5 18. f5 Ne4 19. g4 N8d6 20. Bf4 $36 {1-0 Milov,V-Barsov,A/NRW-II 1996 (38)}) 6. O-O (6. h3 h6 7. O-O Nge7 8. c4 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Bf5 10. Nc3 Qd7 11. d5 Ne5 12. Bb5 c6 13. dxc6 N5xc6 14. Nd4 Bxh3 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. gxh3 cxb5 17. Nxb5 Qxb5 18. Qxd6 Rd8 19. Qa3 Rd7 20. Re1 O-O $15 {Bakhtadze,G-Croonenborghs,T/EU-chJM U20 Holon 1995/0-1 (60)}) 6... Nge7 7. c4 dxc4 8. Bxc4 O-O 9. h3 Be6 $5 (9... Bf5 10. Nc3 Qd7 11. Re1 $14) 10. Bxe6 fxe6 {[%csl Gd4,Ge6] Although 9...Be6 created a weakness on e6 it has many positive sides, such as:1.Eleminating white's very active bishop 2.Openning the "f" file for his rooks 3.Preventing white's isolated pawn (d4) from moving etc.} 11. Nc3 (11. Qb3 Qc8 12. Re1 Nd5 13. Nc3 $140 $2 Rxf3 $1 14. gxf3 Nxd4 15. Qd1 e5 $3 16. Nxd5 Qxh3 17. Re3 e4 $1 18. f4 Nf3+ 19. Rxf3 exf3 20. Qf1 Qg4+ 21. Kh2 Qh5+ 22. Qh3 Qxd5 $19) 11... Qe8 $1 {Moving the queen to g6 (or f5) where she is best placed for defence and offence.} 12. Re1 Qg6 13. Re4 $5 {From here the rook presses on e6,defends on d4 and can olso "jump" to the king side when needed.} (13. d5 exd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. Qxd5+ Kh8 $13) 13... Rae8 14. Qe2 Nd5 15. Rg4 (15. Ne5 $2 Nxe5 16. dxe5 Bc5 $17 {[%csl Gf2]}) 15... Qf5 16. Bh6 (16. Be3 Nxe3 17. fxe3 e5 18. d5 (18. e4 Qf7 19. d5 Nd4 20. Nxd4 exd4 21. Nb5 Be5 $17) 18... e4 19. dxc6 exf3 20. Qxf3 Qxf3 21. gxf3 Rxf3 22. cxb7 Rxh3 $17 {[%csl Gb7,Ge3]}) 16... Rf7 {Although her pieces seem to be well placed Polgar struggles to find a good plan to continue with.} 17. Nh4 {Forces black to take on c3, but white's pieces are in 'offside' and  Kramnik exploits it brilliantly.} (17. Ne4 Bf4 $1 $15) (17. Nd1 e5 $1 (17... Bf4 18. Ne3 Qh5 19. Nxd5 Bxh6 20. Ne5 $1 $16) 18. Ne3 Nxd4 $1 19. Nxd4 exd4 20. Nxf5 Rxe2 21. Rxg7+ (21. Nxd6 cxd6 22. Rxd4 Rfxf2 $19) 21... Rxg7 22. Bxg7 d3 23. Bd4 Be5 24. Rd1 Bxd4 25. Nxd4 Rxb2 26. Rxd3 Rxa2 27. Ne6 Re2 (27... c6 28. Rf3 $1 h6 29. Rf8+ Kh7 30. Rf7+ Kg6 31. Rxb7 $13) 28. Rxd5 Rxe6 $19) 17... Nxc3 (17... Qf6 $4 18. Bxg7 $1 Rxg7 19. Nxd5 $18) 18. bxc3 Qa5 19. Bd2 e5 $1 {White doesn't succeed in bringing all his pieces back.} 20. Nf3 Ree7 {[%cal Ge5d4]} 21. d5 {Polgar prefers to give up a pawn rather than open the position since black pieces are just too active.} (21. dxe5 Nxe5 (21... Rxf3 22. Qxf3 Nxe5 23. Qxb7 Nxg4 24. Qc8+ Kf7 25. Qxg4 $16) 22. Nxe5 Bxe5 23. Re4 Bf6 $17 {[%csl Ga2,Gc3]}) 21... Qxd5 22. c4 Qc5 $17 23. Ng5 (23. Rb1 b6 24. Rb5 Qa3 $17) 23... Rf5 24. Ne4 Nd4 25. Qf1 (25. Qd3 Qa3 26. Qxa3 Bxa3 $19) 25... Qc6 26. c5 {Polgar sacrifises another pawn hoping to get some compensation.} (26. Rc1 Ref7 $17) 26... Bxc5 27. Rc1 b6 28. Qa6 (28. Nxc5 bxc5 29. Qc4+ Ref7 30. Be3 Qb6 $19 {[%cal Gb6b2]}) 28... h5 29. Rh4 Qg6 $1 30. Qc4+ (30. Rxc5 Nf3+ 31. Kh1 Nxh4 $19) 30... Ref7 31. Rc3 Nf3+ 32. Rxf3 Rxf3 $19 {Here white could stop the clock.} 33. Be1 Be7 34. Nd2 Bxh4 35. Nxf3 Bf6 36. Nd2 Kh7 37. Ne4 Rd7 38. Bc3 c5 39. Qe2 Kg8 40. Qc4+ Qf7 41. Qe2 c4 42. f4 Rd3 43. f5 Be7 44. g4 Rxh3 0-1

