{This game was played in the 9th round. Kramnik had 5.5 points, I had 5 and Topalov, Karpov and Polgar had 4.5. Kramnik played Topalov. I knew that if they drew, then I would have a chance to win the tournament.} 1. Nf3 {Huzman} Nf6 2. c4 g6 {I had studied the King's Indian quite well, since I knew that if I played 1.f3, then there would be a few players who would play the King's Indian. Shirov opted for the Slav, but finally a KID appears!} 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. d4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 {Quite the rage these days. I like it because there are only a few critical positions to study, and so far, there doesn't seem a clear way for Black.} a5 10. Ba3 b6 {Looked new for a second, but I didn't see any difference between this and 10...axb4, (except that the same position can arise after move 11 or move 12!) so...} 11. bxa5 Rxa5 12. Bb4 Ra8 13. a4 Nh5 {This doesn't seem right. 13...e8 is played more often and rightly so, in my opinion. Now c7 is weak.} (13... Ne8 {[%csl Rc7] Huzman}) 14. a5 {During the game, I thought that it was important to just go ahead, but now I think that this is imprecise.} (14. Re1 {Quite simple. White can always play a4-a5 on the next move. White's light squared bishop is very important.}) (14. Re1 $5 {Huzman} Nf4 15. Bf1 {[%cal Ya4a5]}) 14... bxa5 15. Bxa5 Nf4 16. Nd2 {Once again, just e1 was better. Black has to decide right away whether she is going to take the bishop.} Qd7 $1 {Of course fairly obvious, but I was mesmerised by the "x-ray" on d8! When she played this, I suddenly felt uncomfortable on the h3 square.} 17. Re1 Bh6 $2 {A really bad move. Even passing would have been better.} (17... Nxg2 $1 {Having played d7, why not use it?} 18. Kxg2 Rxa5 19. Rxa5 Qh3+ 20. Kg1 Qxc3 21. Ra7 {I thought this was very strong, but then I saw Black's next...} Qd4 $1 (21... Qb4 $1 {Huzman} 22. Qc2 Bh6 23. Rd1 Qb6 24. Raa1 f5 $132) 22. Ra3 $1 {And White is certainly better, but Black is doing well on the dark squares and can start counterplay with f5.} (22. Rxc7 Qb6 $1 23. Rxe7 Bf6)) 18. Bf1 c6 (18... f5 $142 {Huzman} 19. c5 $16 (19. g3 Nh5 20. exf5 Nxf5 21. Nb5 Nf6 22. Bxc7 (22. Ne4 Nxe4 23. Rxe4 c6)) 19... dxc5 20. Nb3 (20. Nc4 fxe4 $132)) 19. c5 $1 {This would not work with the bishop on g7.} cxd5 (19... dxc5 20. Nc4 { b6}) 20. exd5 $1 dxc5 (20... Nexd5 21. Nxd5 Nxd5 22. Ne4 Bb7 23. Qxd5 $1 $18) 21. Nde4 Bg7 (21... f5 {Huzman} 22. Nxc5 Qd6 23. Bb4 Rxa1 24. Qxa1 Nexd5 25. Bc4 Kh8 (25... Rd8 26. Nxd5 Nxd5 27. Rxe5 $18) 26. Bxd5 Nxd5 27. Rd1 $18) 22. Bb5 Qb7 23. Nxc5 $6 {I saw that d6 would win a piece, but decided to try for more...} (23. d6 Nf5 24. d7 Nd4 25. dxc8=Q {Black has some , but eventually the extra piece should tell.}) 23... Qb8 $1 {And wound up with less.} 24. Bf1 $6 (24. d6 $1 Ned5 $1 {Is what I had missed.} 25. d7 (25. Nxd5 Qxb5 $13 (25... Qxb5 26. Ne7+ Kh8 27. d7 Bxd7 28. Nxd7 Rfe8 29. Bc3 $11)) 25... Rxa5 $1 (25... Nxc3 26. Bxc3 Rxa1 (26... Bb7 27. Bf1 $16) 27. Bxa1 Qxb5 28. d8=Q Qxc5 29. Q8d6 $16) 26. Rxa5 Nxc3 (26... Nxc3 {Huzman} 27. Qb3 Bxd7 28. Nxd7 Qb7 29. f3 e4 $132)) 24... Bf5 (24... Rxa5 25. Rxa5 Qb4 26. Qa1 $1 $18 (26. Rb5 {Huzman} Qxc3 27. d6 $14)) 25. d6 $1 Nc6 26. Bc7 {White has to start all over again, but at least I don't have to push the d- to d6 again!} Rxa1 (26... Qb4 $5 {Huzman} 27. N5e4 $16) 27. Qxa1 Qb4 28. N5e4 Qb3 29. Rb1 Qe6 30. Qa4 Nd4 (30... Bxe4 31. Nxe4 Nd4 32. Kh1 $1 $16 {Stepping out of any tricks with the s.}) 31. Bc4 $1 {Now the queens go.} Qd7 32. Qxd7 Bxd7 33. Nd5 (33. Nc5 Bc6) 33... Be6 $1 34. Nb6 Bxc4 35. Nxc4 Nfe6 36. Nb6 $1 {Black's  has nowhere to go.} f5 37. Nc3 e4 38. Ncd5 $2 {Another one. After f1, Black would have no defence to cd5, but now she gets some relief by exchanging one pair of s.} (38. Kf1 $1 $16 {[%cal Yc3d5] Huzman}) 38... Ne2+ (38... Kf7 {Huzman} 39. Kf1 $16) 39. Kf1 Nc3 $1 40. Rc1 Nxd5 41. Nxd5 Be5 {Now I realized that White was in danger of losing his advantage entirely. From here on however, I think I played quite well.} (41... Kf7 {This doesn't help Black.} 42. Bb6 $1 { d7} Ke8 (42... Bh6 {Huzman} 43. Ra1 $16) 43. Nc7+ $1 Kd7 (43... Nxc7 44. dxc7 Kd7 45. Rd1+ $18) 44. Nxe6 Kxe6 45. d7 $1 Kxd7 (45... Bf6 46. Rc8 $18) 46. Rc7+ $18) 42. Bb6 Rf7 43. Rc8+ Nf8 44. Ne7+ {White must keep the  boxed in as long as possible.} Kg7 45. Bc5 Nd7 46. Bb4 Kf6 (46... Bxh2 {Huzman} 47. Rc7 Nf6 48. Rb7 $16 {[%cal Yd6d7]}) 47. Re8 $1 {White eventually wants to get his  to d8 to dislodge the Black rook.} Bb2 48. Nd5+ Kg7 49. Nc7 { 50.e6+ f6 51.d8!} Ne5 {Black has cleared d7 for her , but now her bishop is running out of squares. When I saw that after e6+, Black has to play f6, White's next move became apparent.} 50. Bd2 $1 { 51.e6+ f6 52.g5#} h6 51. Rb8 $1 Ba1 (51... Ba3 52. Bc3 Bxd6 53. Ne8+ $18) (51... Nc4 52. Rxb2 $1 $18) 52. Ra8 Nc4 (52... Bb2 53. Ra2 $18 (53. Ra2 {Huzman} Nc4 54. Rxb2 $18)) 53. Ne8+ $1 {The Black king must be forced to h7 for the subsequent fork on f6.} Kh7 54. Rxa1 $1 Nxd2+ 55. Ke2 Nb3 56. Rd1 Nc5 57. d7 Ne6 (57... Nxd7 58. Rxd7 $18) 58. d8=Q Nxd8 59. Rxd8 g5 60. Rd7 Rxd7 61. Nf6+ Kg6 62. Nxd7 Kf7 63. Kd2 Ke6 64. Nb6 h5 65. h4 $1 f4 (65... gxh4 66. Ke3 Ke5 67. Nc4+ Kd5 68. Na5 $1 Ke5 69. Nc6+ Kd5 (69... Kd6 70. Nd4 Ke5 71. Ne2) 70. Ne7+ $18) 66. hxg5 Kf5 67. Nc4 Kxg5 68. Nd6 e3+ 69. fxe3 h4 70. Ne4+ (70. e4 $4 {Huzman} f3 71. gxf3 h3 72. e5 Kf4 73. Ne4 Kxf3 74. Ng5+ Kf4 $11) 70... Kg4 71. Ke2 Kf5 (71... fxe3 72. Kxe3 h3 73. Nf2+ $18 (73. Nf2+ {Huzman} Kg3 74. gxh3 $18)) 72. Nf2 {. With this win, my score went to 6 points. I then remembered the tournament in Madrid 1993 where I won my last round game against Judit and tied with Kramnik and Topalov, who had just drawn their last round game...!} 1-0

