1. Nf3 {Psakhis} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. d4 d6 5. e4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. Re1 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. Bf3 c6 13. Be3 $5 {[%cal Ye3a7] N/ Strong novelty in wellknown position. Kramnik preferes to increase preasure on queen side, than to put bishop on b2, where Black,s defence is not so easy. For example} (13. Bb2 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 (17. Rxe4 Nf5 18. b5 $1 Rc8 $1 19. Re2 Re8 20. bxc6 bxc6 21. c5 d5 22. Bxe5 Bxe5 23. Rxe5 Qf6 $10 {½-½ Dautov,R-Kindermann,S/Nussloch 66/533 1996 (24)}) 17... Nf5 18. b5 Qe8 19. Re2 Rc8 20. Qa4 Qxe6 21. bxc6 bxc6 22. Bxc6 Kh7 23. Bd5 Qe7 24. Rd1 $16 {1-0 Ionov,S-Cvitan,O/EU-Cup Budapest 1996 (53)}) (13. b5 cxd5 14. cxd5 h6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 fxe4 (16... Rc8 $5 17. Qb3 d5 $5 18. Rd1 (18. exd5 e4 19. d6 Qxd6 20. Ba3 Qc7) 18... d4 $13) 17. Nxe4 (17. Bxe4 $13 d5 $5) 17... Nxe4 18. Bxe4 d5 {½-½ Danielian,O-Golubev,M/Moscow 1996 (22)}) (13. Qb3 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 Rf6 $13) 13... cxd5 (13... f4 {Usually it is not good for Black to close king side.} 14. Bc1 (14. Bd2 h6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Qc8) 14... cxd5 15. cxd5 Qb6 16. Qb3 $36 {[%cal Yg5e6,Yb3g8]}) (13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Ne8 16. Qb3 Qc8 17. c5 fxe4 $140 18. cxd6 exf3 $6 19. dxe7 Rf5 20. Rad1 $18) 14. cxd5 h6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 fxe4 17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. Bxe4 d5 19. Bc2 (19. Bc5 dxe4 20. Qxd8 Rfxd8 21. Bxe7 Re8 (21... Rd4 $2 22. Rac1 $16) 22. Bc5 $5 (22. Bh4 Rxe6 23. Rxe4 (23. Rac1 Ra6) 23... Rc8 $11) 22... b6 23. Be3 Rxe6 24. a4 $44) 19... e4 ({After} 19... d4 20. Bd2 d3 21. Bb3 Qb6 22. Be3 (22. Re3 e4 23. Rc1 Bd4) 22... Qxb4 23. Rc1 Rfd8 (23... Rac8 24. Qxd3 $16) 24. Bc5 Qh4 (24... Qxe1+ 25. Qxe1 d2 26. Qd1 dxc1=Q 27. Qxc1 Rac8 28. Qa3 $16) 25. Re3 e4 26. Rc4 $16 {advantage also on the white side.}) 20. Rc1 {[%cal Re4e3,Re3c5,Re3e4,Rc2b3]} (20. Bd4 $6 Qc8 $1 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Qd4+ Rf6 $15) 20... d4 {Judit playing in her usual active stile, but , unfortunately, she could't give answer on question: how to protect her,s pawns in the center.} 21. Bd2 e3 {Best chance.} (21... d3 22. Bb3 Bd4 23. Be3 Bxe3 24. Rxe3 Qd4 25. Qd2 Rac8 (25... Nd5 26. e7 $1 Rf7 27. Rc4 Qa1+ 28. Re1 $18) 26. Rce1 Rf4 27. g3 $18 {lose without any fight.}) 22. fxe3 d3 23. Bb3 Qd6 24. Qg4 Be5 25. Rf1 $1 {h2 pawn is less important than possible Black,s activity after} (25. Qh4 Rf5 $1) 25... Bxh2+ 26. Kh1 Qg3 (26... Be5 27. b5 $1 {[%cal Rd2b4]} Qb6 28. Rf7 Rxf7 29. exf7+ Kg7 30. Rf1 Rf8 31. Bb4 $18) 27. Qd4 $1 (27. Qxg3 Bxg3 28. Rf7 Rxf7 (28... Bd6 29. Rc3 Bxb4 30. Rxd3 Bxd2 31. Rxd2 Rxf7 32. exf7+ Kg7) 29. exf7+ Kg7 $16 {is good for White, but move in the game is stronger.}) 27... Qe5 (27... Qd6 28. Rf7) 28. Rf7 $1 Rxf7 {} (28... Nf5 29. Rxf5 $1 (29. e7 $4 Rxf7 30. Qd8+ Kg7 31. Bxf7 Ng3+ 32. Kxh2 Ne2+ $19) 29... Rxf5 (29... gxf5 30. Qd7 $18) 30. e7+ (30. Qd7 Qb8 31. e7+ Kh8 32. g4 $1 Re5 33. Bf7 $18) 30... Kh7 31. Qd7 Qg7 (31... Rf7 32. Bxf7 Bg3 33. Be6 $18)) (28... Rae8 29. Rcf1 $18 (29. Bc3 $5)) 29. exf7+ Kh7 30. Qxe5 $1 {Simple ans strong.} Bxe5 31. Rc5 Bd6 (31... Bg7 32. Rc7 $18) 32. Rc4 {[%cal Rc4d4,Rd4d7]} Nc6 (32... Nf5 33. g4 Ng3+ 34. Kg2 Ne2 35. Re4 Rf8 36. Bc4 $18) 33. b5 Ne5 34. Rd4 $18 Bf8 35. Re4 Bg7 36. Bb4 {[%csl Re5][%cal Rf7f8]} d2 37. Rxe5 (37. Bxd2 Rf8 38. Bb4 Rxf7 39. Bxf7 Nxf7 40. Re7 $18) 37... Rd8 38. Re8 (38. Re8 d1=Q+ 39. Bxd1 Rxd1+ 40. Kh2) 1-0

