{[%mdl 2]} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 4. g3 b6 5. Bg2 Bb7 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 d6 8. e4 Nbd7 (8... a6 9. d4 cxd4 10. Nxd4 Qc7 {is a more usual move order in this line.}) 9. d4 cxd4 10. Nxd4 {[%cal Re4e5] e5} Rb8 $6 {Black's 8-th move forces him to prevent White's immediate threat, Polgar does it in a strange way.} ({Also risky is} 10... Qb8 11. Nd5 $5 exd5 (11... Bd8 12. Nb4 $1 $16) 12. exd5 Kd8 13. Nc6+ Bxc6 14. dxc6 $16 {Chumfwa,S-Uribe,M/Istanbul olm/2000/}) (10... Nc5 11. b3 O-O 12. Bb2 Qc7 13. Rc1 a6 14. f4 Rac8 15. f5 e5 16. Nc2 b5 17. cxb5 axb5 18. Nb4 Rfe8 19. h3 Qa5 20. a3 Qa8 21. Ncd5 Bxd5 22. exd5 Nfd7 23. Kh2 Bf6 24. Bf1 Qb7 25. Nc6 e4 26. Bxf6 Nxf6 27. b4 Ncd7 28. Qe2 $14 Qb6 $4 29. Ne7+ {1-0,Paunovic,D-Micic,C/Becici/1993/}) (10... Qc8 11. f4 $5 (11. Ndb5 Qb8 12. f4 O-O 13. Be3 a6 14. Nd4 Qc7 15. Rc1 {leads to the main line positions}) 11... O-O 12. Be3 $14) ({More natural, and an attempt to justify the (temporary) omission of a6 is} 10... Qc7 $5 11. Be3 (11. Ndb5 {gives Black a choice between} Qb8 {and} (11... Qc6 $5 $13)) (11. b3 Rc8 $5 12. Bb2 O-O 13. Rc1 Qb8 14. Rc2 Rfe8 15. Rd2 a6 16. f4 Bf8 17. Bf3 h6 18. h4 h5 $1 19. Bxh5 Nxh5 20. Qxh5 Nf6 21. Qd1 b5 $44 {Ruck,R-Prusikhin,M/Griesheim/2003/}) 11... Rc8 12. Rc1 Qb8 13. b3 Nc5 14. f4 $5 (14. f3 a6 15. Bf2 O-O 16. f4 Qa8 17. Qe2 Rfe8 18. g4 g6 19. f5 $6 exf5 20. gxf5 Bf8 21. fxg6 hxg6 22. Nd5 Bxd5 23. cxd5 Nfxe4 $17 {Garcia Paolicchi,R-Marin,M/Andorra la Vella/2002/}) 14... Ncxe4 15. Nxe4 Bxe4 16. Bxe4 Nxe4 17. f5 e5 18. Qg4 Qb7 19. Nb5 g6 20. Qf3 gxf5 21. Qxf5 a6 22. Nd4 $44 {Cacho Reigadas,S-Suba,M/ESP-chT/2001/}) 11. b3 $6 $146 {Too soft, but one has to realize this is a 5 minute game.} (11. f4 O-O 12. Ndb5 a6 13. Nxd6 Bxd6 14. Qxd6 Nc5 15. Qxd8 Rfxd8 16. e5 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Ne8 18. Be3 $16 {Christiansen,L-Wallace,J/Internet/2001/}) (11. Ndb5 $1 Ne5 (11... O-O 12. Nxa7 Qc7 13. b3 $16) 12. Nxa7 O-O 13. b3 $16 {and White seems to have a healthy extra .}) 11... a6 12. Bb2 O-O 13. h3 Qc7 {Now despite Black's 10...b8 we have a normal Hedgehog position with a full board of pieces and the typical possibilities for both sides. Ponomariov doesn't have too much experience with the White side of the English Opening yet, he opts for a direct  assault against the , something typical for the Sicilian.} 14. Qd2 Nc5 15. Rad1 Rfe8 16. f4 Ba8 17. Qf2 Ncd7 ({Possibly safer was} 17... Rbd8 $5 {} 18. e5 dxe5 19. fxe5 Nfd7 20. b4 Bxg2 21. Qxg2 Nxe5 22. bxc5 Bxc5 23. Ne4 Nxc4 $13) 18. g4 {Here comes the aforementioned bayonet attack.} (18. Kh2 $14 {/= was a typical Karpovian noncommittal waiting move.}) ({The immediate tactical break is unclear:} 18. e5 dxe5 19. fxe5 Nxe5 20. Ncb5 Qd7 $5 (20... axb5 21. Nxb5 Qc8 22. Bxe5 Bc5 23. Nd4 $14) 21. Rxe5 (21. Nf5 $2 Bc5 $17) (21. Nc6 $6 Bc5 22. Qxc5 bxc5 $15 {/}) 21... axb5 22. Rxb5 Bxg2 23. Qxg2 Bc5 $13) 18... h6 19. g5 $5 {A double-edged move, now Black gets more  influence (the e5-square!), but also his  becomes vulnerable.} hxg5 20. fxg5 Nh5 (20... Nh7 21. g6 Nhf6 22. gxf7+ Kxf7 23. Qg3 $14 {}) 21. g6 Bf6 $5 {[%csl Rd6] This active move costs Black material in the end (×d6), but resolves the  tension.} (21... Nhf6 {-20...h7}) 22. gxf7+ Kxf7 23. Bf3 (23. Ndb5 axb5 24. Nxb5 Qc5 25. Nxd6+ Ke7 26. Bxf6+ gxf6 27. Nxe8 Rxe8 $13 {/ is not too worrying for Black, his pieces are well coordinated, while White's  are still far from dangerous.}) 23... Nf4 24. Bg4 g5 25. Nde2 Qc5 $1 (25... Nxe2+ 26. Rxe2 $40 {} (26. Nxe2 $5 {d4}) 26... Ke7 27. Nd5+ $1 exd5 28. Bxd7 $18) 26. Nxf4 (26. Qxc5 $5 Nxc5 (26... bxc5 27. Rxd6 Ne5 (27... Ke7 28. Rxa6 $16) 28. Nxf4 gxf4 29. Na4 $14) 27. Nxf4 gxf4 28. Bh5+ Ke7 29. Bxe8 Rxe8 30. Nd5+ $5 Bxd5 (30... exd5 31. exd5+ Be5 32. Bxe5 Rg8+ 33. Kf2 dxe5 34. Rxe5+ $14 {/}) 31. Bxf6+ Kxf6 32. exd5 e5 33. b4 Nd7 $14 {}) 26... gxf4 27. Qxc5 bxc5 28. Rxd6 (28. Bh5+ Ke7 29. Bxe8 Rxe8 $44 {[%csl Re4,Rh3][%cal Rf6d4,Rd7e5] d4,e5,×e4,h3 gives Black excellent play on the dark squares.}) 28... Bd4+ 29. Kf1 Ne5 30. Rxa6 $6 {Seems too greedy.} (30. Na4 $13 {/ keeps White well in the game.}) 30... Nxg4 31. hxg4 Rh8 $40 {All of a sudden Black has a dangerous attack.} 32. Ra7+ $2 {In the fast tempo of a blitz game White isn't up to his defensive task. This thoughtless check just opens the f for Black's b8.} (32. Kg2 $5 Rbg8 (32... f3+ 33. Kxf3 Bxc3 34. Bxc3 Rh3+ 35. Kf4 Rxc3 36. Ke5 $16) 33. Ra7+ Kf6 34. Nd5+ Ke5 $5 $36 (34... exd5 $6 35. Bxd4+ cxd4 36. exd5 $16)) 32... Kg6 33. Kg2 f3+ $1 34. Kg3 (34. Kxf3 Rbf8+ 35. Ke2 (35. Kg3 Bf2+ $19) 35... Rf2+ 36. Kd1 $8 Rxb2 $19) 34... Be5+ 35. Kxf3 (35. Kf2 Rh2+ $19) 35... Rh3+ 36. Ke2 (36. Kf2 Rf8+ 37. Kg1 Rg3+ $19 38. Kh1 Rh8+ $19) 36... Rh2+ 37. Ke3 Rxb2 38. Na4 $2 {The final mistake, although the game could hardly be saved anyway.} (38. Ne2 $142 Kg5 $17 {/-+}) 38... Rxa2 $19 {[%cal Re5d4] d4} 39. Rh1 Bd4+ 40. Kf3 Rxb3+ 41. Kf4 e5# 0-1

