1. e4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. exd5 Nf6 {By this move order Black can avoid the variation} (3... cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Bb5+ {which is not so dangerous on the other hand.}) 4. Nc3 (4. dxc6 $6 Nxc6 $44) 4... cxd5 5. cxd5 (5. d4 {transposes to the Panov Attack.}) 5... Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 g6 {is the other main line.}) ({In case of} 6... e6 7. Bc4 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. d4 {White can avoid the b4 lines, this is one of the main ideas of this move order.}) 7. Bb5 (7. d4 e6 (7... Bg4) 8. Bd3 (8. Bc4 Bb4) 8... Bb4 {are the lines that White wants to avoid.}) 7... e6 8. O-O Be7 (8... Nxc3 9. bxc3 Bd7 (9... Be7 10. d4 {is very similar to the QG Tarrasch D41 lines.}) 10. d4 Be7 11. Bd3 {This is again a transposition to D41.} O-O 12. Bf4 g6 13. Rb1 b6 14. Re1 Bf6 15. Qd2 Re8 16. h4 $1 $14 {This is the typical attacking move here, Zhang Zhong-Asrian, Elista ol 1998.}) 9. d4 O-O 10. Qe2 $5 (10. Re1 $1 {looks a bit better.} Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bf6 12. Bd3 b6 {- D41}) 10... Bd7 (10... Qc7 11. Rd1 Rd8 12. Qe4 Ncb4 13. Bg5 Bxg5 14. Nxg5 Nf6 15. Qe5 Qe7 16. Nge4 Nfd5 17. a3 Nxc3 18. Nxc3 Nd5 19. Bd3 Nxc3 20. bxc3 $14 {Borge-Gausel, VISA Nordic GP Final 1999}) 11. a3 (11. Rd1 Rc8 12. Nxd5 exd5 {In this symmetrical  structure White's advantage is minimal.} 13. Ne5 a6 14. Bd3 Bf6 15. Bf4 g6 16. Bc2 { b3 × d5} Re8 17. Bb3 Bf5 18. Qf3 Be4 19. Qg3 Ne7 20. Bg5 {This exchange of the dark-squared bishops is a vital part of White's strategy, but here Black gets a counterplay against × d4.} Nf5 21. Bxf6 Qxf6 22. Qf4 g5 $1 23. Qd2 Nxd4 $1 24. Ng4 Nf3+ $1 25. gxf3 Qxf3 26. Qxg5+ Kf8 27. Qh6+ Ke7 28. Qg5+ Kf8 {and the draw by perpetual checks is inevitable,} 29. Qh6+ {½:½ Milos-Dreev, FIDE WCh KO Moscow 2001}) 11... Rc8 12. Bd2 Bf6 13. Ne4 $6 {Now White has serious problems with her × d4 .} (13. Qe4 Nce7 $13) (13. Bd3 Qb6 $5 $13) 13... a6 $142 (13... Nxd4 $4 {loses to} 14. Nxf6+ Qxf6 15. Nxd4 $18) 14. Bd3 $5 (14. Bxc6 Bxc6 $15 {gives Black a firm control over the blockading square d5.}) 14... Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Bxd4 16. Nd6 Bxb2 $1 {This is the counterblow! Black sacrifices the exchange for pawns and a well-blockaded position.} (16... Rc6 17. Qe4 f5 18. Qxd4 Rxd6 19. Bb4 $14) (16... Qh4 17. Nxc8 Rxc8 18. Rab1 $14) 17. Rab1 Bxa3 (17... Rc6 $5 {can be met by} 18. Nxf7 $1 (18. Nxb7 Qf6 (18... Qb6 $2 19. Na5) 19. Na5 Rc7 20. Qe4 g6 $15) 18... Rxf7 19. Rxb2 b5 $44) 18. Nxc8 Qxc8 (18... Bxc8 19. Bxh7+ Kxh7 20. Qd3+ Kg8 21. Qxa3 Qh4 22. Rb3 $11) 19. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 20. Qd3+ Kg8 21. Qxa3 Bc6 22. Rfe1 Re8 23. Rb3 $6 {White wants to organize an a against × g7, but this fails.} (23. Qg3 $14) (23. Re5 $5) 23... e5 24. Rg3 Qe6 25. h3 (25. Rg5 f6 26. Rg6) 25... f6 26. Bh6 (26. Rg6 Re7 27. Qg3 a5 $132) 26... Re7 27. h4 $2 {White pushes it too hard...} (27. Bd2 $11) 27... Qf5 $1 $15 28. Bc1 Qh5 29. Qd6 Rd7 30. Qc5 $2 (30. Qe6+ Kh8 $15) 30... Ne7 ({It was possible to eliminate the weak h , but in rapid games the end is fairly unpredictable...} 30... Qxh4 31. Rh3 Qg4 $17) 31. Qc4+ Kh7 32. Qc2+ Nf5 33. Rc3 (33. Rg5 fxg5 34. Qxf5+ Qg6 $17) 33... Qg6 34. f3 Nxh4 $19 35. Qf2 Rd4 36. Kh2 Qh5 37. Kg1 Ng6 38. Qc2 Rh4 39. Kf2 Rh2 40. Rg1 Qh4+ 41. Ke2 Bb5+ 42. Rd3 e4 0-1

