1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 b6 $6 $14 {Strange and bad novelty.} ({Main theoretical lines are:} 9... a5) (9... c5) (9... Ne8) (9... c6) 10. Nd3 {[%cal Gf2f4] Best reaction on Black's poor play.} Ne8 $6 $16 ({Why not} 10... Nd7 $14 {preventing f2-f4.}) 11. f4 $1 {This position should be compared with theoretical one which easily might be ctreated after 9...e8 10.d3 f5 11. f4. Nobody will consider to play here 11...b7-b6? In the game b7-b6 useless move was done instead of useful f7-f5. Of course, White caught an advantage...} f5 12. Kh1 ({Possibly} 12. fxe5 dxe5 13. b4 Nd6 14. Nf2 $16) 12... Kh8 13. fxe5 dxe5 14. b4 Nd6 15. Nf2 fxe4 (15... c5 16. bxc5 bxc5 17. Be3 $16 {[%csl Rc5][%cal Ge3c5,Gc3a4]}) 16. c5 $1 {White centre became powerful.} (16. Nfxe4 Bf5 17. Bd3 $16 {[%csl Re5,Rg7]}) 16... Ndf5 17. Nfxe4 a5 $2 $18 {The idea of this move is to destroy White's central  chain. It's refutation isn't difficult, but obvious:} ({Perhaps, better was} 17... Nd4 18. Rxf8+ Qxf8 19. Bc4 Bf5 20. Qd3 $14) 18. d6 $1 {Of course...} cxd6 19. cxd6 Nc6 20. Bg5 {White's initiative is developed from itself without any serious resistance...} Qe8 21. Nd5 {[%cal Gd5c7,Gd5b6]} Ncd4 ({Or} 21... Rb8 22. Ndf6 Qf7 23. g4 h6 24. gxf5 hxg5 25. Nh7 Bxf5 26. Nexg5 {[%cal Gh7f8]} Qb7 27. Nxf8 $18) 22. Nc7 Qc6 23. Bd3 ({Not} 23. Nxa8 $2 Qxe4 24. Bf3 Nxf3 25. Qxf3 Nxd6 26. Qxe4 Nxe4 27. Be7 Rxf1+ 28. Rxf1 Ba6 29. Rc1 Bh6 $13) 23... Ra7 24. Rc1 ({Possibly also} 24. Be7 Ne3 25. Rxf8+ Bxf8 26. Qc1 Bxe7 27. dxe7 Bd7 28. Qxe3 Nf5 (28... Rxc7 29. Qh6 $18) 29. Qg5 Rxc7 30. Bb5 Qe6 31. Bxd7 $18) 24... Qd7 25. Nd5 axb4 (25... Qe6 26. Nxb6 $18) 26. Be7 Nxe7 27. dxe7 Re8 28. Nxb6 Qe6 29. Nxc8 {Rapid games quality is always worse than normal ones quality. But J. Polgar plays rapid games usually very strong! not that time...} 1-0

