{[%mdl 1]} 1. e4 {Wells} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qb6 5. Nb3 Nf6 6. Nc3 e6 7. Qe2 Bb4 8. Bd2 O-O 9. a3 Be7 10. O-O-O d5 (10... d6 {is more "sicilian". On the other hand after 10...d6 White can easier organise the kingside attack, since the center is not open yet.} 11. g4 a6 12. g5 Nd7 13. h4 Qc7 14. f4 b5 15. Kb1 b4 16. axb4 Nxb4 17. f5 Rb8 18. Bf4 exf5 19. exf5 Ne5 20. Bh3 Re8 21. g6 $1 $36 {Anand,V-Kramnik,V/Mainz 2001 (blitz)}) 11. exd5 (11. Be3 Qc7 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. g4 Ne5 15. h3 Nc4 16. Bd4 Be6 17. Bg2 Rfc8 18. Rhe1 Bf8 $13 {Konguvel,P-Sherbakov,R/Calcutta 2002}) (11. e5 Nd7 12. f4 a6 13. Qh5 f5 14. Bd3 Nd4 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 16. Rhe1 Qb6 17. Re3 g6 18. Qh6 Nc5 19. Rh3 Nxd3+ 20. Rxd3 Rf7 {½-½ Mulyar,M-Yermolinsky,A/Seattle 2002}) 11... Nxd5 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Bc3 (13. Be3 Qc7 {transposes into 11.Be3.}) (13. Qf3 Bf6 14. Be3 d4 15. Bf4 Be6 16. Bd3 Rfc8 17. Kb1 $13 {Gofshtein-Grivas, Panormo 2001 (rapid)}) 13... Be6 14. Qf3 Rac8 15. Qg3 g6 16. h4 {Here and on the next move I would rather prefer to block the d-pawn with Nd4.} (16. Nd4 {Wells} Bf6 $1 {also leads nowhere fast for white.}) 16... Rfd8 17. h5 {Looks like White has a strong attack, but in fact after the next move Black is the one who creates threats quicker.} d4 $1 {Wells: 'Most obviously this enhances the light-squared bishop, but there are tactical elements to consider here, and Judit is really setting herself up a very pleasing exchange sacrifice which is really much more about the dark squares.'} 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Nxd4 (19. Bd2 Bxb3 20. Qh3 (20. cxb3 Na5+ 21. Kb1 Qxb3 $17) 20... Bf6 21. cxb3 d3 $1 (21... Na5+ 22. Kb1 Qxb3 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Bb4+) 22. Bc3 Ne5 {and White's position looks critical anyway.}) 19... Nxd4 20. Rxd4 Rxc3 $1 {[%mdl 896] Wells: 'The point. It is not too hard to see, especially when the long-diagonal combines with the open h-file that this is the absolute key piece in the position. Still, the way in which the necessary defensive aspects of this sacrifice combine with very potent attacking resources too is a delight to watch. '} (20... Rxd4 21. Qe5 Bg5+ $8 22. Qxg5 Rxc3 23. bxc3 Qd8 $14 (23... Rd5 {Wells} 24. Qh6 {with some advantage to white.} (24. Qf6 $2 {Wells} Rd1+ $1))) 21. bxc3 {Wells: 'Regrettably forced from white's standpoint. There is a rather better hidden tactical point underlying black's sacrifice namely that'} (21. Rxd8+ Qxd8 22. Qxc3 Bg5+ $19) (21. Qe5 {(Wells) fails to} Bf6 $1 22. Qxf6 Rxc2+ $1 23. Kxc2 Bb3+) 21... Rxd4 (21... Bxa3+ {Wells} 22. Kd2 Rxd4+ {is also possible, but the text has a very unhurried feel about it which emphasises black's utter confidence that her dark-square domination will carry the day.}) 22. cxd4 Qxd4 {Black is objectively better and from the practical point of view White has almost no chances to survive against Black's strong attack.} 23. c3 (23. Qb8+ Bf8 $17) 23... Qc5 (23... Bxa3+ 24. Kc2 Qb6 25. Qe5 $1 {for another pawn White trades queens, which keeps him out of danger.} Qxf2+ 26. Be2 Qf5+ 27. Qxf5 Bxf5+ 28. Bd3 {and the draw is most likely result.}) 24. Kd2 ({preferable is} 24. Be2 Qxa3+ 25. Kd2 Qb2+ 26. Ke1 {although Black is better here too.}) 24... Bg5+ (24... Bf6 $5) 25. Kc2 (25. Ke1 {again he had to run to e1.} Bf6 26. Be2 $15 {[%cal Ge1f1] /}) 25... Kg7 {'!' Wells. Wells: 'The patience that comes from such a qualitative superiority of force. I'm sure Judit didn't feel as if she was material down here. Her pieces have an enduring superiority which belies the simple number-crunching of relative material values.'} 26. Bd3 (26. Be2 Qd5 $1 {[%cal Gd5a2]}) 26... Bf6 27. Rb1 (27. Be2 Qxa3 $19) 27... Qxc3+ 28. Kd1 Bg5 29. Ke2 Bg4+ $1 30. Kf1 Bf4 $1 {The triumph of black bishops.} (30... Bf4 31. Qxf4 Qxd3+) 0-1

