1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Be3 a6 7. f3 b5 8. g4 {These pawn advances, which anticipate castling on opposite sides, are typical of this variation of the Sicilian.} Bb7 9. Qd2 Be7 10. O-O-O Nc6 11. Nxc6 Bxc6 12. Ne2 {A useful regrouping, putting the knight on a more active square.} O-O 13. g5 Nd7 14. Nd4 Bb7 15. h4 d5 $1 {White's attack is obviously slightly closer to hitting home, so Polgar seeks counterplay in the centre.} 16. Bh3 {The fight for the initiative is underway.} (16. exd5 {is too automatic and hands over the initiative, for example} Bxd5 17. Kb1 Ne5 18. Be2 Qc7 {with Ne5-c4 to follow.}) 16... Ne5 (16... dxe4 17. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 18. Qxd7 {is bad news for Black's bishops.}) 17. Qe2 Qa5 18. Kb1 dxe4 (18... Rfd8 {allows the interesing} 19. Nxe6 fxe6 20. Bxe6+ Kh8 21. exd5 {when three pawns look like providing good compensation for the piece.}) 19. f4 Nc4 20. Bc1 {Black has won a pawn, but now White will soon get his say with f4-f5} e3 21. Rhf1 Rad8 22. f5 e5 (22... Qb6 {was an alternative, but after} 23. f6 Bxf6 24. gxf6 Rxd4 25. Qh5 $1 {White has undeniable attacking compensation.} (25. fxg7 $2 Rfd8 {seals Black's king in safety.})) 23. f6 $1 Rxd4 (23... gxf6 $2 24. Nf5 {gives White an enormous attack.}) 24. fxe7 Re8 25. Bxe3 Rxd1+ 26. Rxd1 Rxe7 {The dust has settled and Black is a pawn up, but White retains some initiative.} 27. Bc5 Re8 (27... Rc7 $4 28. Rd8# {is not the only variation where Black's weak back rank is critically important}) 28. b3 Qc3 (28... Na3+ 29. Bxa3 Qxa3 30. Bd7 $1 Rd8 (30... Re7 31. Bxb5 $1 {also regains the pawn.}) 31. Qxe5 {looks better for White. Even worse is}) (28... Qc7 29. Qf2 $1 Na5 {(the only square)} 30. Rd7 {and the attack on f7 wins.}) 29. bxc4 Qxh3 30. Qxe5 $1 Bc6 $6 (30... Qc8 $1 {Offered Black better chances due to White's weakened king position.} 31. Qxe8+ (31. Qd4 {may be better}) 31... Qxe8 32. Bb6 Bd5 $5 (32... f5 33. Rd8 Qxd8 34. Bxd8 bxc4 35. Bc7 {and I suspect White can hold the endgame.}) 33. cxd5 {and White's strong passed pawn will make winning very tough for Black.} (33. Rxd5 Qe1+ 34. Kb2 h6 {is bad for White.})) 31. Qd6 Qf3 {Now Gelfand forces a draw by a perpetual attack along the d-file.} 32. Rd3 Qf1+ 33. Rd1 Qe2 34. Rd2 Qe4 35. Rd4 Qh1+ 36. Rd1 Qg2 37. Rd2 Qh1+ {Black has no way to keep the game going.} 1/2-1/2

