1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 dxe5 5. Nxe5 c6 6. Be2 Bf5 7. g4 Be6 (7... Bc8 $5 {This retreat has its logic: Black is ready to accept the loss of two tempi in order to demonstrate the negative sides of the advance g2-g4. Only future practice can show if White is able to make use of his space advantage. In the following example Black achieved sufficient counterplay:} 8. c4 Nc7 9. Nc3 Nd7 10. Be3 (10. Nf3 $5 g6 11. Be3 Bg7 12. Qd2 $13) 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 Qxd1+ 12. Rxd1 f6 13. Bf4 f5 14. h3 g6 15. Be3 Bg7 16. f4 g5 $13 {/=, 0-1 Chen Fan (2354)-Ye Jiangchuan (2648)/Wuxi 2005 (53)}) 8. f4 (8. c4 Nb6 9. b3 (9. c5 Nd5 10. Nc3 Nd7 11. Nc4 Qc7 $11) 9... f6 $1 {White loses either the pawn on d4 or that on g4.} 10. Nd3 Qxd4 11. Bb2 Qd8 (11... Qd7 12. Nc3 Na6 $15) 12. Nc3 Na6 13. Qd2 Qc7 14. O-O-O O-O-O 15. Qe3 Bf7 16. f4 Kb8 17. h4 e6 18. Ne4 Nd7 19. h5 h6 20. g5 Be7 21. g6 Be8 22. Kb1 Qb6 23. Bd4 c5 24. Bb2 Ka8 25. Ng3 Ndb8 26. Rhe1 Bd7 27. Nf5 exf5 28. Qxe7 Rhg8 29. Bf3 Bc6 30. Bxc6 Qxc6 31. Qe6 Qxe6 32. Rxe6 Nc6 33. Nf2 Nc7 $17 {½-½ Kasparov,G (2838)-Short,N (2682)/Moscow (rapid) 2002 (56)}) 8... f6 9. Nd3 Bf7 (9... Nd7 10. c4 Nc7 11. b3 Bf7 12. Nc3 (12. O-O $142 g6 $13) 12... e5 $1 13. fxe5 fxe5 14. O-O $6 exd4 15. Ne4 Be7 $17 {0-1 Niemi,K (2108)-Cornette,M (2366)/Budva 2003 (25)}) 10. O-O Na6 (10... Qb6 11. Nf2 e5 12. fxe5 fxe5 13. c4 $36 {½-½ Almasi,Z (2672)-Luther,T (2538)/Germany 2002(78)}) 11. Nc3 (11. c4 $2 Nb6 {[%csl Rc4,Rd4]}) 11... Nxc3 12. bxc3 g6 13. Qe1 Bg7 14. f5 Qd7 15. Qf2 gxf5 (15... O-O-O $5) 16. Qxf5 (16. gxf5 $6 Rg8 $40 {[%csl Rg1]}) 16... Qxf5 17. gxf5 Rg8 18. Kf2 O-O-O 19. Be3 Bf8 (19... Rd5 $5 20. Ke1 Ra5 21. a4 Bf8 $13) 20. Bf3 Nc7 21. a4 Nd5 22. Bd2 (22. c4 $2 Nxe3 23. Kxe3 Bh6+ $19) 22... e6 (22... Nb6 23. Nc5 Nc4 24. Bf4 $16) 23. fxe6 Bxe6 $11 1/2-1/2

