1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. Qh5 Rg8 13. g3 Rg5 (13... Rg4) 14. Qd1 {One of the newest attempts to fight against Sveshnikov Sicilian, although 14.Qd1 was first played back in 1980. If one needs new ideas, the simplest way to discover them is to look at old games... The arising positions are extremely sharp and strategically complicated.} (14. Qxh7 $6 {Here Black has a lot of attractive continuations, the most energetic being 14...Nd4, which points best the isolated position of white queen on h7.}) 14... Bxd5 (14... Rg4 $6 {Here this move makes practically no sense if comparing with 13...Rg4, where it has the main idea to cut White's queen off on h5.} 15. f3 Rg8 16. Qd2 Rc8 17. c3 h5 18. Nc2 $16 {1-0 Vallejo Pons,F-Moreno Carnero,J/Cala Galdana 1999 (36)}) 15. exd5 Ne7 16. c3 {White's point of the maneuver Qd1-h5-d1 is that black rook on g5 is too exposed and it is not easy at all for Black to coordinate his pieces.} (16. Nxb5 Qb6 17. Na3 Qxb2 18. Nc4 Qc3+ {and Black was doing fine, although mutual innacuracies followed in Polgar,J-Leko,P/Budapest 2003.}) 16... Bh6 {Leko's move. After other continuations Black couldn't show a clear route to equality yet, although I believe that after both 16...Bg7 and 16...Qb6 Black has enough resources.} (16... Bg7 17. h4 (17. Be2 Rc8 18. Nc2 Rc5 19. a4 Nxd5 20. axb5 axb5 21. Nb4 $44 {/=, ½-½ Medvedev,V-Klovans,J/URS-chT1 corr 1988 (42)}) 17... Rg6 18. Nc2 e4 19. Be2 Qc7 20. a4 b4 $1 21. h5 Rh6 22. cxb4 Qb7 23. b5 Bxb2 24. Rb1 Bc3+ 25. Kf1 a5 $13 {1-0 Sideif Sade,F-Yurtaev,L/Dnepropetrovsk 1980 (42). This was the first game which saw the retreat 14.Qd1.}) (16... e4 17. f4 Rg6 (17... Rg8 18. Be2 Qb6 19. Nc2 Bg7 20. Qd2 Bf6 21. a3 $14 {1-0 Bezgodov,A-Tolstikh,N/Samara 2000/CBM 78 (80)}) 18. Be2 $14 {½-½ Bezgodov,A-Smirnov,P/Samara 2000 (88)}) (16... Qb6 17. Nc2 e4 18. Be2 f4 19. Qd2 Rxd5 20. Qxf4 Re5 21. O-O Bg7 22. Kh1 $13 {1-0 Grischuk,A-Krasenkow,M/Wijk aan Zee 2003 (48)}) 17. Be2 ({The point of 16...Bh6 can be seen after} 17. Nc2 e4 18. Be2 f4 {when White does not have the typical Qd2, see for instance Grischuk-Krasenkow (16...Qb6).}) 17... Rc8 $146 (17... Qb6 18. Nc2 Kf8 19. a4 $36 {1-0 Acs,P-Leko,P/Budapest 2003 (37)}) 18. c4 (18. Nc2 Rc5 19. Nb4 (19. a4 Rxd5 20. Qb1 {is an interesting idea, pointed out by Acs, when White has compensation for the pawn.}) 19... Qa8 (19... Kf8 $5) (19... a5 20. f4 (20. Na6 Rxd5 21. Bxb5+ Kf8 $17 (21... Rxb5 $2 22. Qa4 Qd7 23. Qxb5 Qxb5 24. Nc7+ $18)) 20... Rg6 21. Nc6 Qa8 22. Bf3 e4 $132) 20. f4 Rg8 (20... Rg6 $5) 21. fxe5 dxe5 $13) 18... f4 {"Cleaning" the fifth rank to justify the rook on g5.} (18... Qa5+ 19. Kf1 Kf8 (19... b4 20. Nc2 Qc5 {or 20...Qb6, looks also good for Black.}) 20. cxb5 axb5 21. Nxb5 Rc5 22. Nc3 Nxd5 $13 {Of course all these unbalanced positions are impossible to evaluate precisely, but it looks like everywhere Black's chances are not worse.}) (18... b4 $2 19. Qa4+ Kf8 20. Qxb4) 19. cxb5 e4 20. gxf4 Qa5+ (20... Rxd5 $5 21. Qa4 Bg7 (21... axb5 22. Nxb5 Kf8 23. Nc3 Ra5 24. Qxe4 Bg7 {with some compensation as well}) 22. bxa6+ Kf8 23. Nc4 Ng6 $44) 21. Kf1 Rxd5 22. Qe1 Qc7 {The plan to transfer the queen on h3 turned out to be wrong. White will place the rook on g2 and Black won't have enough time to use the pin. In fact the position is still unclear and even in endgame Black has compensation for minimal material disadvantage. 22...Qc7 is not bad at all, but it was connected with the wrong idea.} (22... Qxe1+ 23. Rxe1 axb5 24. Bxb5+ Kf8 25. Rxe4 Rb8 $44 ({or} 25... Ng6)) (22... Qd2 $5 23. Qxd2 Rxd2 24. bxa6 d5 $5 $44) 23. bxa6 Qd7 $2 {This is the real mistake.} ({Black had to play} 23... Bxf4 24. Qb4 (24. Bb5+ Kf8 25. Qxe4 Bc1 $1 {and suddenly Black has counterplay. Besides the threat Bxb2, which by the way isn't at all easy to parry, Black also has additional idea Rd1+.} 26. Rb1 (26. Rg1 $2 Rd1+ 27. Kg2 Rxg1+ 28. Kxg1 Bxb2) (26. Qxh7 $2 Rxb5 27. Nxb5 Qc4+ 28. Kg2 Qd5+ 29. Kg1 Qg5+ 30. Kf1 Qxb5+ $19) 26... Bxb2 $1 $13) 24... f5 25. f3 {with a total mess.}) 24. Rg1 $1 Qh3+ 25. Rg2 $16 Bxf4 26. Qb4 $1 {[%csl Re4] As usual, the losing side fails by just one tempo to create enough counterthreats.} (26. Bg4 Qd3+ 27. Kg1 $16 {was also possible, but 26.Qb4 is even stronger.}) 26... Bxh2 (26... Rg5 27. Qxe4 d5 28. Qf3 $18) (26... f5 {The drawback of this advance is that Rd5-g5 won't be possible anymore.} 27. a7 $18) 27. Bg4 Qd3+ 28. Be2 Qh3 29. Bg4 Qd3+ 30. Ke1 $18 Nc6 (30... e3 31. Bxc8 Nxc8 (31... exf2+ 32. Kxf2 $18) 32. f3 {[%cal Gb4e4]}) (30... f5 31. Bh5+ Kd7 32. Rxh2 $18) 31. Qb7 Qd2+ 32. Kf1 Qd3+ 33. Be2 Qh3 34. Rc1 Rg5 35. Bg4 {The final subtlety. Even tactical motifs work out for White...} Qd3+ 36. Ke1 e3 37. Bd7+ Ke7 38. Bf5+ 1-0

[Event "Budapest Hunguest Hotels"]
[Site "Budapest"]
[Date "2003.04.11"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Kortschnoj, Viktor Lvovich"]
[Black "Polgar, Judit"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "E12"]
[WhiteElo "2632"]
[BlackElo "2715"]
[Annotator "Quintiliano,Renato"]
[PlyCount "92"]
[EventDate "2003.04.11"]
