{[%evp 0,92,8,50,26,-42,32,23,22,24,21,27,4,-34,-18,-19,36,25,31,-8,27,0,30,20,24,18,21,12,38,-47,1,-36,-38,-38,-67,-120,-53,-110,-110,-101,-219,-219,-170,-219,-184,-184,-154,-154,-148,-214,-214,-214,-149,-149,-149,-149,-152,-152,-169,-194,-194,-190,-183,-196,-213,-213,-207,-207,-207,-209,-203,-173,-175,-187,-200,-198,-191,-200,-187,-204,-203,-164,-164,-154,-154,-222,-212,-215,-212,-222,-231,-230,-209,-215,-231] Going through Polgar's games this one immediately draw my attention. I think the reason will be clear after seeing the game, but one can always expect a true fight in a game between two legends.} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 b6 3. Nc3 Bb7 4. d4 e6 5. Bg5 {Definitely Kortschnoj favourite move.} (5. Bf4) (5. g3) (5. Qc2) (5. e3) (5. a3 {all of these moves can be found in his games as well, though the text is by far the most seen.}) 5... h6 (5... Bb4 {transposes to the Nimzo.}) (5... Be7 {is also possible.}) 6. Bh4 ({The last game played by Kortschnoj in this line went} 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 {½-½ Kortschnoj,V (2564)-Ekstroem,R (2496) SUI-ch Lenzerheide 2010 (2) but it is difficult to believe that White gets something for the bishop pair.}) 6... g5 $5 {Polgar goes for the ambitious plan of pursuing this bishop.} ({In two games of Kortschnoj's practice Black went for a more solid position with} 6... Be7 7. Qc2 (7. e3 Ne4 8. Bg3 Bb4 9. Qc2 d6 10. Bd3 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Nxg3 12. hxg3 Nd7 $11 {1-0 Kortschnoj,V (2635)-Ligterink,G (2445) Hoogovens Wijk aan Zee 1984 (1) seems balanced.}) 7... d5 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. cxd5 exd5 10. g3 O-O 11. Bg2 c5 12. dxc5 bxc5 13. Rd1 Na6 14. O-O Qe7 $1 $13 {½-½ Kortschnoj,V (2626)-Tkachiev,V (2625) Biel GM 2002 (5) with a double-edged position.}) 7. Bg3 Nh5 8. e3 Nxg3 9. fxg3 $6 {Sometimes, this highly unusual recapture may give dynamic chances for White along the f-file, but unfortunately for Kortschnoj this is not the case. Perhaps he was trying to avoid the bad memories from a previous loss in this position.} ({In a game played 14 years before, he chose the strategically safe} 9. hxg3 Bg7 10. Bd3 Nc6 11. g4 Qe7 12. a3 O-O-O 13. Qc2 {but Black was able to take the initiative now by means of a typical idea:} h5 $1 14. gxh5 g4 15. Nd2 f5 16. Ne2 Qg5 $36 {0-1 Kortschnoj,V (2655)-Sax,G (2580) Clermont Ferrand 1989 and Black eventually won.}) 9... Bg7 ({The computer says that} 9... g4 $1 {with the aim of exploiting White's damaged structure immediately is even stronger. For instance:} 10. Nh4 Qg5 11. Qd2 h5 12. O-O-O Bh6 13. Re1 Nc6 $17 {and White's position seems very unpleasant.}) 10. Bd3 Nc6 (10... d6 11. O-O Nd7 {is another possible set-up, but the development chosen by Polgar in the game was also good, and faster.}) 11. O-O Qe7 12. a3 $2 {White prepares an offensive on the queenside, maybe in an attempt of provoking Polgar to place her king on the other side. Objectively speaking, though, this seems like a loss of time.} (12. Rc1 {was probably better, then if} O-O-O 13. c5 $1 {White is a move ahead compared to the game. That makes a huge difference, as one tempo often changes the evaluation dramatically in opposite castling positions. For example:} h5 14. Qa4 $1 g4 15. Nh4 {and now Black shouldn't play} bxc5 $4 ({Instead, after} 15... Kb8 16. Ba6 Bh6 17. Rfe1 Ba8 18. b4 $13 {the position remains very complicated.}) 16. Ne4 $1 cxd4 17. Nc5 $18 {when White has a powerful queen on a4 instead of a useless pawn on a3, which makes the position completely lost for Black.}) 12... O-O-O $1 {With her incredible feeling for the initiative, Polgar sees that the tempo wasted by White in his previous move will cost him dearly.} 13. Rc1 (13. b4 $2 {is immediately refuted by} g4 14. Nh4 Nxd4 $1 15. Qxg4 Nf3+ $1 16. Nxf3 Bxc3 $17) 13... h5 $1 {It's Black who comes faster in the attack now.} (13... f5 14. c5 f4 $40 {seems also promising.}) 14. c5 {This advance seems quite logical, but it doesn't works now.} (14. d5 Ne5 15. c5 $1 {would be the best way to execute White's idea.} Qxc5 ({In case of} 15... exd5 16. cxb6 axb6 17. Nd4 Nxd3 18. Nf5 $1 Qe5 19. Qxd3 Bf8 20. Nb5 $44 {White's initiative can't be underestimated.}) 16. Nd4 Nxd3 17. Qxd3 {[%cal Gc3b5] and Black has to find a way of dealing with the threat of Ncb5. For example:} Bxd4 18. exd4 Qe7 19. Nb5 Kb8 20. Rxc7 exd5 21. Rcc1 $13) 14... g4 15. cxb6 $2 {I think Kortschnoj probably trusted in some tactical motifs and evaluated the arising complications as favourable for White.} ({Or did he simply disliked} 15. Nh4 {but the analysis show that this was White's best practical chance. The engine says Polgar could have accepted the challenge here with the cold-blooded} bxc5 $1 (15... Bh6 16. Re1 Bg5 {seems more human, but in that case White ignores the knight on the edge and plays} 17. Qa4 $1 $132) 16. Ne4 Bh6 $1 {Black is getting more active now than in the lines analysed in the previous comments.} 17. Re1 cxd4 18. Nc5 $1 Rde8 $1 {a difficult move, vacating d8 for the king.} ({But not} 18... Bxe3+ 19. Rxe3 $1 dxe3 20. Qb3 {and Black is forced to force a draw with} Na5 21. Qc3 Nc6 22. Qb3 $11) 19. Qb3 Ba8 20. Ba6+ Kd8 {and White finds it difficult to continue the attack, for example:} 21. Nb7+ $2 (21. Bb7 {is the best attempt.} Bxb7 22. Nxb7+ Kc8 23. Nc5 Qd6 $1 $15 {keeping the escape route d8-e7 free.}) 21... Bxb7 22. Qxb7 Qd6 $19 {[%cal Gd8e7] and the king has a path to safety on e7. Still, I think this was White's best chance, but such things are easier to say in hindsight.}) 15... gxf3 16. bxc7 Kxc7 {Polgar has also calculated the next moves accurately, and saw an important detail.} ({The defensive strategy of using White's pawn in front of the king as a shield is a mistake here, because White has plenty of open lines to reach the king:} 16... Rdg8 $2 17. Rxf3 Bh6 18. Qa4 h4 19. Ba6 $18 {and White has a winning attack.}) 17. d5 Qg5 $1 {As Black is forced to give back the piece, Polgar takes the chance of playing actively.} (17... Bxc3 18. dxc6 dxc6 $1 19. Rxc3 fxg2 $17 {was also good for Black, but the game continuation is clearly stronger.}) 18. dxc6 (18. Rxf3 $2 exd5 $19 {is obviously worse.}) 18... Qxe3+ 19. Kh1 (19. Rf2 $4 dxc6 $19 {[%cal Rd8d1] and White loses more material.}) 19... fxg2+ 20. Kxg2 Bxc6+ 21. Be4 {I'm not sure whether Kortschnoj missed Black's strong 17th move or he trusted on this move to keep the tension. One way or another, the brilliant resource found by Polgar now completely refutates White's idea:} Bxc3 {The only move.} ({Other moves would keep White in the game, for example:} 21... d5 22. Nb5+ $1 Kb6 23. Bd3 Bxb5 24. Bxb5 {and the bishop can't be taken, as if} Kxb5 $2 25. Rf4 d4 26. Rf3 $1 $18 {Black will be mated.}) (21... h4 22. Rf3 Qb6 23. Bxc6 dxc6 24. Rxf7+ Kb8 25. Qe2 $13 {seems also complicated.}) 22. Bxc6 Qd2+ $1 {The detail spotted by Polgar in the end of this long forced variation. Thanks to this move, Black is able to prevent all threats and keeps a decisive advantage.} 23. Kh3 {A desperate attempt.} (23. Qxd2 Bxd2 $19 {[%cal Gd7c6,Yd8d2] and Black will emerge a piece up.}) (23. Kh1 {could be simply answered by} dxc6 24. Rxf7+ Kc8 $19 {and Black keeps a piece advantage here as well.}) 23... h4 $1 {Something that I've always admired in Polgar's games is how she is always ready to reject material gains to go for the ultimate goal of the game: the checkmate.} ({Kortschnoj likely put his last hopes in the careless} 23... dxc6 $2 {when White is suddenly back to the game after} 24. Rxf7+ Kc8 $4 ({Correct is} 24... Rd7 25. Rxd7+ Kxd7 26. bxc3 $11) 25. Qa4 $1 $18 {and we can see the main difference of placing the king on h3, as the Rc1 is not lost with check.}) ({But Black had the more solid alternative} 23... Qxd1 24. Rcxd1 (24. Rfxd1 Bxb2 25. Rc2 dxc6 $19) 24... Kxc6 25. bxc3 f5 $19 {with a technically winning endgame, although the conversion of Black's advantage would still require some time.}) 24. Rxc3 ({White can't avoid the king's hunt by means of} 24. g4 $2 {as after} dxc6 25. Rxf7+ Kc8 $19 {[%cal Rd8d3] the rook is coming to d3 to decide the game.}) 24... hxg3+ 25. Kg4 (25. Kxg3 Rdg8+ 26. Kf3 Rh3+ 27. Ke4 Rh4+ 28. Kf3 Rf4#) 25... Rhg8+ 26. Kf3 dxc6 {Kortschnoj has managed to avoid a quick mating attack, but the resulting position doesn't improves his situation, as the endgame offers no chance of salvation.} 27. Qxd2 Rxd2 28. hxg3 Rxb2 29. Ke4 {The rest was easy.} f5+ 30. Ke5 Rg6 31. Rh1 (31. Rfc1 Rb5+ 32. Kf4 Kd7 $1 33. Rxc6 $2 e5+ $19 {[%cal Rg6c6] is a nice trap.}) 31... Re2+ 32. Kf4 Re4+ 33. Kf3 Reg4 34. Kf2 e5 35. Rf3 Kd6 36. Rh7 (36. Rxf5 Rxg3 37. Rhh5 Rg2+ 38. Kf1 e4 $19 {also doesn't offers any hope for White.}) 36... a6 37. Ra7 Ra4 38. Rd3+ Ke6 39. Rc3 Kd5 40. Rd7+ Rd6 41. Rf7 f4 42. Rb7 Rc4 43. Rd3+ Kc5 44. Rdb3 Rd2+ 45. Kf3 fxg3 46. Kxg3 Rdc2 {An impressive demonstration of accurate calculation and strong tactical vision by Polgar.} 0-1

