1. e4 {Ftacnik} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd5 f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. Qh5 Rg8 13. g3 Rg5 14. Qd1 Bxd5 15. exd5 Ne7 16. Nxb5 $6 {This is a doubtful novelty. Later Polgar switched to the more logical 16.c3. The problem with taking with the knight on b5 in Sveshnikov Sicilian is that even when it wins a pawn, Black often gets good compensation.  However, in this game Leko doesn't even sacrifice the pawn.} (16. c3 Bh6 17. Be2 Rc8 18. c4 f4 19. cxb5 e4 20. gxf4 Qa5+ 21. Kf1 Rxd5 22. Qe1 Qc7 23. bxa6 Qd7 24. Rg1 Qh3+ 25. Rg2 Bxf4 26. Qb4 Bxh2 27. Bg4 Qd3+ 28. Be2 Qh3 29. Bg4 Qd3+ 30. Ke1 Nc6 31. Qb7 Qd2+ 32. Kf1 Qd3+ 33. Be2 Qh3 34. Rc1 Rg5 35. Bg4 Qd3+ 36. Ke1 e3 37. Bd7+ Ke7 38. Bf5+ {1-0 Polgar,J-Lautier,J/Enghien les Bains 2003.}) 16... Qb6 (16... e4 17. Be2 Qa5+ 18. Nc3 Bg7 $44) (16... Bg7 $44) 17. Na3 (17. Qc1 Bh6 $1 $36 (17... Rg6 {Ftacnik} 18. Nc3 Bh6 19. Qb1 (19. f4 exf4 20. gxf4 Rg1+ $17) 19... e4 20. Be2 Bg7 $11)) 17... Qxb2 18. Nc4 Qc3+ 19. Kf1 Rd8 {Ftacnik: 'The modern chess is first of all tough fight with quite a few questionable moments. It is hard to guess why black refrained from the promissing 19...xd5!? '} (19... e4 20. Be2 (20. f4 Rg6 21. Be2 Rc8 {is much better for Black, if comparing with 21.f4 instead of 21.Be2 from the game.}) 20... Bg7 21. Rb1 Kf8 $13 22. Nxd6 Rd8 $36 {[%csl Rd5]}) (19... Rg6 $5) (19... Nxd5 $5 20. Rb1 {Ftacnik} Qd4 21. Be2 Qc5 $15) 20. Rb1 e4 (20... Nxd5 {Ftacnik} 21. Be2 Nf6 22. Rb3 Qd4 23. Qxd4 exd4 24. Rb6 $14) 21. Be2 $6 ({The typical} 21. f4 {was necessary. Now Black will find a job for the rook on the fifth rank.} Rg6 (21... exf3 22. Qxf3 Bg7 23. Kg2 {is in White's favour.}) 22. Be2 $13 {[%cal Gf1g2]}) (21. Rb3 {Ftacnik} Qf6 (21... Qd4 $2 22. Nxd6+ Rxd6 23. Bb5+ $18) 22. f4 Rg6 (22... exf3 23. Nb6 Ng6 24. Qxf3 $16) 23. Be2 Bg7 24. Kg2 $14) 21... f4 $1 {[%cal Gg5d5]} 22. Rb3 Qf6 23. gxf4 {'?' Ftacnik. White had little choice, since protecting the d-pawn by moves like 23.Nb6 (which is probably best) would hardly cross anyone's mind.  Ftacnik: 'This solution is most likely second rate, Leko will get a chance to build up the massive initiative on the kingside. '} (23. Nb6 {Ftacnik} fxg3 (23... f3 24. Bxa6 Nxd5 25. h4 Qh6 26. Kg1 (26. Nxd5 Rxd5 27. Qe1 $11) 26... Rxg3+ 27. fxg3 Nxb6 28. Qd4 $16) 24. hxg3 Nf5 25. Kg2 Bg7 26. Bxa6 $14) 23... Qxf4 {Now White's previous move is fully justified.} (23... Rxd5 $1 24. Qc1 (24. Qb1 {Ftacnik} Qxf4 25. Ne3 Ra5 $17) 24... Bh6 $17 (24... Rc5 {Ftacnik} 25. Nb6 Nf5 $17)) 24. Ne3 Bg7 (24... Bh6 $5 25. Bxa6 Ra8 26. Bb5+ Kf8 $44) 25. Bxa6 {White's extra pawn plays insignificant role for the moment. Black will organise an initiative in the center and on the kingside, using the fact that White has an worse co-oprdination: not easy to connect rooks, bad position of the king and no possibilities for active play.} Nf5 $44 26. Ng2 (26. Nxf5 Rxf5 27. Qe2 Bd4 $17) 26... Qe5 27. h4 Rg6 28. Rhh3 {So she achieved the connection between rooks. The position is double-edged.} Kf8 29. Qb1 {White is trying to create her own play. However, the king on f1 will need soon more pieces to defend him and White's main task was rather to prevent Black's active possibilities. Also pawn d5 remains unprotected now.} (29. c3 $13 {[%cal Gd1e2] would have been better, preventing Nd4. However, psychologically it's a difficult move to make after just having reached the connection between rooks on the third rank.}) ({or} 29. h5 {followed by 30.c3.}) 29... Bf6 30. h5 Rg8 31. Be2 {'?' Ftacnik. Ftacnik: 'Much more promissing was to change a pair of rooks with the help of 31.b8!?, even though black would still be better.'} (31. c4 $6 {protects pawn d5, but leaves the bishop out of play on a6. Black is clearly better, after say} Nd4 32. Rbe3 {and now even} (32. Rb8 Qg5 33. Rg3 Qxh5) 32... Nf3 $5 33. Rhxf3 exf3 34. Rxe5 fxg2+ 35. Kg1 Bxe5 $17) (31. Rb8 $5 {Ftacnik} Qxd5 (31... Rxb8 32. Qxb8+ Kg7 33. Qb1 Qxd5 34. c4 $11) 32. c4 Qd4 $15) 31... Nd4 (31... Qxd5 32. Rb5 Qe6 {was possible too.}) 32. Rbe3 Bg5 ({Black has a decisive attack after the following forced line:} 32... Nxe2 33. Rxe2 Qg5 34. Rg3 Qxh5 35. Rxg8+ Kxg8 36. Re1 Kh8 37. Ne3 Bc3 38. Rd1 Qh1+ 39. Ke2 Qf3+ 40. Kf1 f5 $19 {But the game continuation is also strong enough to win.}) 33. Qb6 Re8 34. Reg3 f5 35. Nh4 (35. Bb5 Re7 {changes little - White is unable to stop the advance of f-pawn.}) 35... Nxe2 36. Kxe2 f4 $19 37. Rb3 f3+ 38. Kf1 Qxd5 39. Nxf3 Qc4+ 40. Rd3 exd3 41. Qxd6+ Be7 42. Qh6+ Rg7 43. Rg3 dxc2+ 44. Kg2 Qc3 0-1

