{After the rather recent, nice Karpov's book about Caro-Can and his own big expirience in the line 5.f3-g5 the present game is like an opening survey showing partialy its developement since 1983 so far:} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 $5 {Semenova 1983} Ngf6 (5... Ndf6 6. Bc4 Nd5 {0-1 Spassky,B-Karpov,A/Belfort 1988/CBM 08 (22)}) 6. Bd3 e6 (6... h6 7. Ne6 Qb6 8. Nxf8 Nxf8 {0-1 Semenova,L-Muresan,M/Bad Kissingen 1983/MCD (47)}) 7. N1f3 Bd6 (7... h6) 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Qc7 {''!?' Speelman,J. ' 'Finkel,A: 'Probably the most precise move.Although 10...Nf6 also leads to absolutely playable position where nevertheless white's chances should be prefered.''} (10... Nf6 11. Qe2 Qc7 12. Bd2 b6 {Copar,A-Sapundjiev,G/EM ICCF 14 ol 200/2001}) 11. Qg4 (11. a4 Nf6 12. Qh4 {½-½ Ramesh,R-Galkin,A/Anibal Open, Linares ESP 2001 (20)}) 11... Kf8 {'Lukacs: 'Karpov's favourite  move.''} 12. O-O c5 {'Lukacs: 'Soon or later Black has to attack White's .''} (12... b6 {has a similar idea to play c6-c5 after b7.} 13. Re1 {Yandemirov-Kharitinov, RUS-Cup 1996 CBM 56} (13. b3 {Arakhamia-Portisch, Cancan Veterans-Women 1996 CBM 67})) 13. dxc5 {'Lukacs: 'The  is vulnerable on g4.''} (13. Re1 cxd4 {0-1 Finkel,A-Yevseev,D/Groningen 1995/CBM 50 ext (64)} (13... b6 14. Ne5 $2 (14. b3) (14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Bf1 (15. Bd2 h5 $1 16. Qh4 Bb7) 15... e5 16. Qh5 {De Firmian,N-Gulko,B/USA-ch 1996 (36)}) 14... cxd4 $1 {1/2-1/2 Khalifman,A-Speelman,J/Hastings 1996/CBM 51/[Speelman,J] (31)})) (13. c3 b6 14. Re1 {'Finkel,A: 'After this move black equalises very easely,but it is not so clear what to do instead.Black has very unpleasent threat Bb7,Nf6 and B:f3 and white tries to prevent it.''} (14. Qh4 {After this move black equalises very easely,but it is not so clear what to do instead.Black has very unpleasent threat Bb7,Nf6 and B:f3 and white tries to prevent it.} Bb7 15. Be4 Bxe4 16. Qxe4 Rd8 17. dxc5 (17. Be3 Nf6 18. Qc2 Ke7 19. dxc5 Bxc5 20. Bxc5+ Qxc5 21. Rad1 $11) 17... Nxc5 18. Qe2 Ke7 $11 19. h3 Rhe8 20. Qc2 {Trying to keep black king in the center.} (20. Be3 Kf8) 20... Nd3 $1 {½-½ Illescas Cordoba,M-Karpov,A/Dos Hermanas 1997/CBM 59/[Finkel,A] (49)}) 14... Bb7 15. h4 Rd8 (15... c4 16. Bc2 b5 17. Bd2 {½-½ Anand,V-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (49)}) 16. Bd2 {1-0 Shirov,A-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (54)}) (13. Qh4 {Lukacs: 'The  is vulnerable on g4.'} b6 14. Be4 Bb7 (14... Rb8 15. Rd1 $1 $146 {0-1 Sadvakasov,D-Karpov,A/Hoogeveen 1999/CBM 74/[Lukacs] (65) White puts pressure on the d  especially against d6.} (15. Bg5 {Eismont-Gutov, RUS-Cup 1996 CBM 56}) (15. b3 Nf6 $6 (15... c4 $6) (15... cxd4 $142 $5 {appears to be the best here.} 16. Nxd4) (15... Be7 16. Qf4 Bd6 {½-½ Leitao,R-Karpov,A/Buenos Aires 2000/CBM 80 no vc (19)}) 16. dxc5 $1 {½-½ Timman,J-Karpov,A/Bali 2000/CBM 77/[Lukacs] (62)} ({If} 16. Bd3 {then Black occupies the long  after} Bb7))) 15. Bxb7 Qxb7 16. dxc5 bxc5 {1-0 Lautier,J-Karpov,A/Monte Carlo 1998/CBM 63 ext (48)}) 13... Nxc5 14. Be3 $146 (14. Re1 {0-1 Luther,T-Anastasian,A/Istanbul 2000/EXT 2001 (39)}) 14... Nxd3 15. cxd3 e5 16. Qe4 g6 17. Rac1 Qe7 18. Rfe1 Bf5 19. Qa4 Kg7 20. Nxe5 Bxe5 21. Bf4 Rhc8 22. Rxc8 Rxc8 23. Rxe5 Rc1+ 24. Bxc1 Qxe5 $11 {} 25. Be3 (25. Qd1 Qd4) 25... f6 26. Qb4 b6 27. h3 g5 28. Qa3 Qc7 29. b4 Qd7 30. Qc3 1/2-1/2

