1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Nb3 Be7 9. Qf3 Nbd7 10. O-O-O Qc7 11. Bd3 b5 12. a3 Rb8 13. Rhe1 h6 {[%mdl 4] That's actually an improvement of Kasparov's 13...b4 from the first round against the same Leko. Polgar's move leads to the comfortable position for black.} (13... b4 14. axb4 Rxb4 15. Kb1 Bb7 16. Qh3 Nc5 17. Nxc5 dxc5 18. e5 c4 $5 (18... Nd5 $6 {½-½ Leko,P-Kasparov,G/Linares 2001/CBM 82_01 (55)})) 14. Qh3 ({Probably, white can look for something only in the line:} 14. Bh4 g5 {but it should be good for black.}) 14... e5 $1 {[%cal Gc8h3][%mdl 4] Black threatens to move the knight from d7, attacking white queen over the diagonal c8-h3.} 15. f5 $146 {This move doesn't let black castle, but black doesn't suffer from it at all because the center is closed after f4-f5. In the game Leko-Kasparov, the pawn structure in the center was flexible, and white could break through the center.} (15. g4 {Tyomkin,D} Nb6 16. fxe5 dxe5 (16... Bxg4 $2 17. exd6 Qxd6 (17... Bxd6 18. e5) 18. e5 (18. Qe3 $5) 18... Qc6 19. Qg3 Nh5 20. Qxg4 Bxg5+ 21. Kb1 Nf4 22. h4 $16) 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. exd5 O-O 20. Nd2 $2 (20. d6 $5) 20... Rd8 21. Ne4 Be7 22. d6 Bxd6 23. Nxd6 Rxd6 24. Rxe5 Bxg4 $1 {[%csl Re5][%cal Gd6d3]} 25. Qe3 {[%cal Re5e8]} (25. Rde1 Re6 26. Rxe6 Bxe6 27. Rxe6 fxe6 28. Qxe6+ Kf8 29. Qxa6 Qxh2 30. Bxb5 Qg1+ $17) 25... Re6 $17 {Zaitsev Igor A-Mitenkov Alexey/It (cat.9), Moscow (Russia) 1996/0-1 (43)}) 15... b4 ({Of course not:} 15... O-O $6 16. Bxh6 $1 {[%csl Rg8] and white's attack is too strong.}) 16. axb4 Rxb4 17. Bd2 Nb6 {Black has a good choice of moves instead- in all cases black can be just better:} (17... Bb7 $5 18. Kb1 (18. g4 Rxb3 $1 19. cxb3 Nc5 $44) 18... Nc5 19. Nxc5 Qxc5 (19... dxc5 $6 20. Nd5 $1) 20. Na2 Rxe4 $5 $44) (17... Nc5 $5 18. Nxc5 Qxc5 $13 (18... dxc5 19. Nd5 $1)) 18. g4 {[%cal Rg4g5]} Rg8 $1 {The exactest reply- black doesn't mind to keep the king in the center. White can not do too much here, on the other hand black may go on over the queenside.} 19. Kb1 (19. g5 hxg5 20. Bxg5 Bb7 {[%cal Gb6c4,Gb6a4]} (20... Nxe4 $2 21. Bxe7 Nxc3 22. bxc3 Rxb3 23. Bxd6 $1 $18)) 19... Nc4 20. Bxc4 Rxc4 (20... Qxc4 $6 21. g5 hxg5 22. Bxg5 Bb7 $140 23. Na5 {trading the worst knight for the good bishop.}) 21. g5 hxg5 22. Bxg5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Bb7 {[%csl Yd5] Black is in time to control d5-square.} 23. Bxf6 Bxf6 24. Nd5 Bxd5 25. Rxd5 $11 Ke7 {Finally the rook g8 can be moved into the game.} ({The pawn c2 was poisonous:} 25... Rxc2 26. Qd3 Rxh2 (26... Rc6 27. Na5 Rc5 28. Rxc5 dxc5 29. Qxa6 $16) 27. Rc1 {[%csl Re8][%cal Gd5d6]} Qb7 28. Rxd6 $16) 26. Qd3 Rc8 27. Re2 Qb6 {Both sides can not achieve any progress over here.} 1/2-1/2

