1. e4 {Baburin} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Nd5 Be7 10. Bxf6 Bxf6 11. c3 O-O 12. Nc2 Rb8 13. h4 {Baburin: This move is designed not only to take away the g5-square from the bishop, but also to prepare the following favourable exchange.} g6 {Eine Alternative ist e7,=> Kasparov-Kramnik/CBM/43;Kasparov-LautierCBM/45} 14. g3 Be6 (14... h5 $5) 15. Bh3 {Baburin: The point of the previous play.} Bg7 (15... Bxd5 16. Qxd5 Ne7 17. Qd3 d5 $14) 16. h5 a5 17. Nce3 Qg5 $6 (17... Ne7 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Bxe6 $6 (19. Qd3 Nxd5 20. Nxd5 Qd7 $13) (19. Nxe7+ Qxe7 20. Qd3 $14) 19... fxe6 20. Nxe7+ Qxe7 21. Qg4 Qf6 $13) 18. Nc7 $1 {Baburin: This manoeuvre forces the exchange of the light-squared bishops, which increases White's control over the critical d5-square.} Bxh3 19. Rxh3 Rfd8 20. Kf1 $1 b4 21. Ncd5 Rdc8 22. Qa4 $1 bxc3 23. bxc3 Qd8 24. Kg2 $16 Ne7 25. hxg6 hxg6 26. Rhh1 Nxd5 27. Nxd5 {Baburin: White is strategically winning here, having a glorious knight on d5 vs an inefficient bishop.} Rc5 28. Rab1 Rxb1 29. Rxb1 Qa8 30. c4 Bf8 31. Qd7 Qc8 32. Qa7 $1 $18 Rxd5 (32... Kg7 33. Rb8 Qe6 34. Qa8 Be7 35. Rh8 $18) (32... Rxc4 33. Rb8 Qe6 34. Qa8 $18) 33. cxd5 Qc2 34. Re1 Qxa2 35. Re3 Qa4 36. Rf3 Qe8 37. Qxa5 Qd7 38. Qa8 Kg7 39. Qc6 Qe7 40. Qc8 Qa7 41. Rb3 1-0

