1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 {First game and first surprise! It is really very strange, but Judit ( with her superactive stile) never played this interesting opening.} 4. d5 {Interesting, but Karpov also has not great expierence in Benoni, maybe because very often he met 3...c5 with 4.e3 or 4.g3.} d6 5. Nc3 exd5 6. cxd5 g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. h3 O-O 9. Bd3 b5 $1 {Clealy the best move! In all other variations white keep some advantage without bog problems.} 10. Bxb5 {Popular in recent practice 10.xb5 Re8, but Karpov likes move in the text.} Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Qa5+ 12. Nfd2 Qxb5 13. Nxd6 Qa6 14. N2c4 Nd7 {[%cal Gd7b6,Gd7e5] 14... d8!? was played in 3th game.} 15. O-O Nb6 16. Nxb6 Qxb6 17. Nxc8 Raxc8 18. Rb1 Rfd8 {[%csl Rd5][%cal Gg7d4]} 19. Bg5 (19. Bf4 {according to theory strongest move ( Karpov played this move in 3th game) and difficult to argue with this conclusion.}) 19... Rd7 20. Qg4 Qb7 21. Rfd1 h5 22. Qc4 {N/} ({Black hasn,t any troubles and after} 22. Qf3 {for example} Bd4 23. b4 (23. Rxd4 cxd4 24. Qf6 Qb6) 23... Qxd5 24. Qxd5 Rxd5 25. Be3 Rcd8 26. bxc5 Bxe3 27. Rxd5 Rxd5 28. fxe3 Rxc5 {½-½ Ibragimov,I-De Firmian,N/Reykjavik op 1994}) 22... Bd4 23. Be3 Qxd5 (23... Rxd5 24. b4 $5) 24. Qxd5 (24. Rxd4 cxd4 25. Qxc8+ Rd8 26. Qc7 dxe3 27. fxe3 Rd7 28. Qb8+ Kg7 29. Ra1 (29. Qb3 Qxb3 30. axb3 Rb7 $11) 29... Qe6) 24... Rxd5 25. Rd2 a5 $1 26. Kf1 (26. Rbd1 Rb8 27. b3 a4 28. bxa4 Rb4 29. a5 Ra4 $11) 26... Rb8 27. Rbd1 Rb4 28. a3 (28. Ke2 Re5 $11) (28. Bxd4 cxd4 29. Ke2 a4 $11) 28... Rb3 29. Bxd4 cxd4 30. Rxd4 Rxd4 31. Rxd4 Rxb2 32. Rd5 Rb1+ 33. Ke2 Rb2+ (33... Rb2+ 34. Ke3 Rb3+ 35. Rd3 Rb2 $11) 1/2-1/2

