1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Bd2 Nd7 8. Nxd5 exd5 9. g3 Bd6 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Rc1 c5 13. Bh3 O-O {Black must play actively, as the centre is to White's advantage.} 14. dxc5 (14. Bxd7 Qxd7 15. dxc5 d4 {creates counterplay, as} 16. O-O d3 17. Ne5 {can be met by} Qd5) 14... bxc5 15. Bxd7 Qxd7 16. Rxc5 Qh3 {The fact that White cannot castle is a considerable handicap, but the black bishop could become bad, and a pawn is a pawn.} (16... d4 17. O-O d3 18. Ne5) 17. Qd3 $1 {White wants to keep d4 free for the knight, but must be ready to meet Qg2 with Qf3.} Rac8 18. Rxc8 Rxc8 19. Qb5 $2 (19. Kd2 {was much better, and White holds the advantage.} d4 (19... Qh6+ 20. Qe3) (19... Qg2 20. Rd1 Qxf2 21. Qd4 {with a big positional advantage - when the queens come off the king belongs in the centre.}) 20. Qxd4 (20. Rd1 Qh6+ 21. Ke1 Bxf3 22. Qxf3 Qxh2 {is less clear.}) 20... Qh6+ 21. Qe3 Qd6+ 22. Ke1 {and White moves his king around to g2.}) 19... Bc6 20. Qd3 (20. Qa6 d4 {with a big initiative.}) 20... Ba4 21. Qe3 h6 {With the back rank problem covered the rook threatens to cause damage.} 22. Nd4 Qg2 23. Rf1 Bd7 $1 {A neat switchback breaks down the defenses.} 24. Nf3 (24. f4 Bh3 25. Rg1 (25. Rf2 Qg1+) 25... Rc1+ {wins.}) 24... d4 25. Qf4 (25. Nxd4 Bh3 {and Rg1 is no longer available.}) 25... g5 26. Nxg5 (26. Qd2 Re8 27. Ng1 {looks desperate, but after the piece is lost its all over.}) 26... hxg5 27. Qxg5+ Kf8 28. Qh6+ Ke8 29. f3 Rc6 30. Qf4 Qxh2 31. Qb8+ Ke7 32. Kd2 Rc2+ 33. Kxc2 Qxe2+ 34. Kb3 Qxf1 35. Qb4+ Ke6 {and there are no more checks.} 0-1

