1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Bg5 Na6 7. f4 Nh5 $6 {This move seems to be too risky. Black wants to fight against the weaknesses on f4 and g3, but loses time, and his  may get trouble.} (7... Qe8 {is the theoretical continuation preparing for the standard push e7-e5.} 8. Nf3 (8. e5 Nh5 {is already not the same!}) 8... e5 $1 {This nice move is well-known also from the 4  Attack!} 9. fxe5 dxe5 10. Nxe5 c5 $1 {blowing up White's mighty .}) 8. Nge2 h6 9. Bh4 f5 {This is again an original idea, but it is not in the spirit of the position.} 10. exf5 gxf5 11. g3 {Completing the development with the  fianchetto. Suddenly all white pieces work together harmoniously.} Qe8 { e5} 12. Bg2 e5 13. dxe5 {This gives up the tension in the  a bit.} (13. O-O exd4 (13... exf4 14. Nxf4 Qe3+ 15. Kh2) 14. Nd5 c5 15. Re1 {was an interesting option.}) 13... dxe5 14. fxe5 Qxe5 15. Qd5+ Qxd5 16. Bxd5+ (16. Nxd5 {looks more agressive, for example:} Bxb2 17. Rb1 Bg7 18. Ne7+ Kh7 19. Nxc8 Raxc8 20. Bxb7 Rb8 21. O-O Nc5 22. Bf3 $14) 16... Kh7 17. Bf3 Nf6 18. O-O-O Nc5 19. Nf4 Nfe4 20. Be7 Re8 21. Ncd5 Be5 22. Rhe1 c6 23. Bxc5 cxd5 24. Rxd5 Bxf4+ 25. gxf4 Be6 26. Re5 Bxc4 ({White can still continue the game after} 26... Bxc4 27. Rxe8 Rxe8 28. b3 Bd5 29. Kb2 $14 {}) 1/2-1/2

