1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Bb4 9. f3 O-O $5 ({Both a new move for the site and a pretty rare move in general. Previously the Fed has concentrated on} 9... Ne5 {. Here's a up of some recent developments after that critical and theoretical alternative:} 10. Nb3 ({White's main move instead the positionally-ambitious} 10. Kb1 Bxc3 11. Qxc3 $5 Qxc3 12. bxc3 d5 13. Nb3 dxe4 14. Bd4 Nc6 15. fxe4 Nxd4 16. Rxd4 Nd7 17. Nd2 Ne5 18. Nf3 Nxf3 19. gxf3 Bd7 {was rather complex and unclear in Nedev-Kozul, Negotino 2007}) 10... b5 11. Kb1 ({also critical is} 11. Qe1 Be7 12. f4 Ng6 13. e5 Ng4 14. Bd2 Bb7 15. Bd3 Rc8 $1 {and the assessment of this key position remains as unclear Svidler tried} 16. Qe2 f5 17. exf6 $5 {, but after} Nxf6 18. Rhf1 O-O 19. g3 b4 20. Ne4 Nxe4 21. Bxe4 a5 {Black had plenty of counterplay in Svidler-Vitiugov, Moscow 2006}) 11... Nc4 12. Bxc4 bxc4 13. Nc1 ({another recent development is} 13. Nd4 $5 {, although} Rb8 14. Ka1 O-O 15. g4 d6 16. h4 e5 $1 17. Nf5 Bxf5 18. gxf5 d5 {gave Black good counterplay in Korneev-Vehi Bach, Seville 2007}) 13... Rb8 14. N1e2 O-O 15. Bf4 e5 16. Bg5 Ne8 17. Ka1 d6 18. a3 a5 ({despite having played this himself, in 'The Safest Sicilian' Delchev recommends} 18... Ba5 $5 {when I too quite like Black's position}) 19. axb4 $5 axb4 20. Na2 Be6 21. Nxb4 Ra8+ 22. Kb1 Qa5 23. c3 Qa1+ {forced perpetual in Kritz-Schlosser, Bundesliga 2006.}) 10. g4 ({Getting on with the attack must be critical. White has also tried} 10. a3 {, but I'm always a little suspicious of this move in opposite-side castling situations. Here} Be7 11. g4 b5 $1 ({critical and probably better than} 11... d6 12. g5 Nd7 13. f4 Nxd4 14. Bxd4 Re8 15. h4 b5 {which reminded one of a pure English Attack in Henry-Reeve, Edmonton 2005}) 12. g5 Ne8 13. Nxc6 $5 dxc6 14. h4 {was Pilgaard-Ivanovic, Subotica 2003, and now} a5 15. h5 b4 16. Nb1 c5 {would have been rather unclear note that an immediate} 17. g6 fxg6 18. hxg6 h6 19. Bxh6 $2 {fails to} gxh6 20. Qxh6 Qf4+ {- White's b1-knight is a good defender, but the king's position on c1 gives Black a number of useful resources along the c1-h6 diagonal.}) (10. Kb1 {has also been tried when} Be7 11. g4 Ne5 12. Rg1 b5 13. g5 Nh5 14. f4 Nc4 15. Bxc4 bxc4 16. f5 {led to another rather murky position in Erdogdu-Miladinovic, Istanbul 2002.}) 10... b5 11. g5 Nh5 12. Kb1 ({Once again} 12. a3 {may not be such a bad choice, although} Be7 13. Nxc6 (13. Nce2 $5 {is perhaps ore critical try, although I'm far from certain that White was better after} Na5 14. Ng3 e5 $1 15. Nxh5 $5 exd4 16. Kb1 dxe3 17. Qd4 f6 {in Arizmendi Martinez-Arbakov, Linares 2003 that continued} 18. gxf6 Bxf6 19. Nxf6+ {and now} gxf6 $5 20. Qd5+ Kh8 21. Qxa8 Nc6 22. a4 Rd8 23. axb5 Bb7 24. Qxd8+ Nxd8 25. bxa6 Bc8 {would have remained rather murky, but Black should be doing well here as he is able to round up a6}) 13... dxc6 14. f4 Rd8 15. Qf2 Rxd1+ 16. Nxd1 e5 $1 {broke up White's advancing pawns and gave Black a good game after} 17. f5 Nf4 {in Ramesh-Zhang Pengxiang, Dubai 2005.}) 12... Nxd4 ({A novelty, although whether this was prepared isn't so clear. Previously Black had preferred the more aggressive} 12... Ne5 $5 {, but perhaps Polgar didn't like the look of} 13. Ncxb5 $5 (13. Be2 Rb8 14. f4 Nc4 15. Bxc4 bxc4 16. Nde2 {wasn't so clear in I. Rogers-Bjelobrk, Brisbane 2005, although Black must be very careful in such a situation that his h5-knight doesn't cost him}) 13... Qa5 (13... Bxd2 14. Nxc7 Bxe3 15. Nxa8 {also seems to leave Black coming up short}) 14. Nc3 {when Black has some play for the pawn, but White can keep the queenside fairly closed with} Rb8 15. Nb3 {.}) 13. Qxd4 ({Now Black gets to change the structure and is never really in any trouble. Thus White should investigate} 13. Bxd4 $1 {which looks like a much better try for an edge for example,} Bc5 (13... d6 $6 {runs into} 14. Nd5 $1 exd5 15. Qxb4) ({and Black might prefer to regroup with} 13... Bd6 {, but then} 14. Ne2 $1 Be5 15. Bxe5 Qxe5 16. Bh3 {retains some advantage}) 14. Bxc5 Qxc5 15. f4 (15. Bh3 $5 {might be more challenging}) 15... Qc7 16. e5 $5 {(positionally ambitious, but White is after a bind and also wants to exploit the black knight's position on the rim)} Bb7 17. Rg1 b4 18. Ne2 Rfc8 19. Nd4 d6 20. exd6 Qxd6 21. Rg4 Be4 {and Black has gained some counterplay, but the suspicion remains that this variation should be a little more pleasant for White}) 13... Bxc3 $1 14. Qxc3 Qxc3 15. bxc3 d5 {Breaking up White's centre and thereby preventing Anand from gaining a grip on the position White now struggles to find a good role for his bishops.} 16. Be2 ({White is too late to be able to capture on h5, but} 16. exd5 exd5 17. Rxd5 Bb7 18. Rd6 Bxf3 19. Rg1 Rfe8 {would also have given Black plenty of counterplay.}) 16... g6 ({Enabling the knight to be redeployed, although} 16... Bb7 17. f4 g6 18. Bxh5 gxh5 19. exd5 Bxd5 20. Rhg1 Rab8 {also would have been fine for Black.}) 17. exd5 exd5 18. Rhe1 Re8 19. Bd3 (19. Bf1 {was the alternative, but just as in the game} f6 $1 {ensures Black of counterplay and White isn't at all better after} 20. Rxd5 Bb7 21. Rd6 fxg5 {.}) 19... Bd7 20. Re2 f6 $1 21. Rde1 Kf7 {Anand's play has been a little pedestrian over the last few moves, not that the white position was especially easy to handle in a more active vein. Black has clearly fully equalised and indeed if anyone now needs to be careful, it's White due to his split structure.} 22. Kb2 Rac8 23. gxf6 Nxf6 24. Bd4 Rxe2 25. Rxe2 Ne8 {Trying to bring a piece to c4 or f5, but White's bishops are well centralised and he is able to hold the balance without any real difficulty.} 26. Be5 Rc6 27. h4 Nd6 28. Bf4 Nc4+ (28... Bf5 $5 29. Bxd6 Rxd6 30. Bxf5 gxf5 31. Re5 Kf6 32. Re8 d4 {should also be a draw, although perhaps Black might have tried this.}) 29. Kc1 Bf5 30. Bg5 Re6 31. Bxc4 $1 {The opposite-coloured bishops ensure the draw. This was quite a success for the rare 9...0-0, but should the line become more popular we'll probably start seeing some tests of the critical 13 Bxd4.} 1/2-1/2

