1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 b6 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. Ne2 Nbd7 10. Qc2 h6 11. Bh4 c5 12. dxc5 bxc5 13. Nc3 d5 14. O-O-O $6 {Morozevich avoided this sharp move, and going by the evidence of this game he was wise to do so!} (14. Rd1 {is covered in Morozevich-Moiseenko, Porto Carras 2011.}) 14... Qb6 $1 {Just like Moiseenko did against Morozevich, Black breaks the pin on the f6-knight, although here Polgar chooses the b6-square for her queen. This makes a lot of sense, as ...d4 certainly comes into consideration in a number of lines.} 15. Bg3 ({Black's plan can be seen in action after} 15. Be2 d4 $1 16. exd4 cxd4 17. Na4 Qd6 18. Bg3 (18. c5 $4 Qf4+ $1) 18... e5 {If White had castled kingside then his queenside pawn majority might have counterbalanced Black's central advantage, but here White can't really push his queenside pawns without the risk of his king getting exposed. Advantage Black. Kacheishvili's move discourages ...d4 - for the moment - because the black queen no longer has the d6-square, but somehow it all looks a bit artificial for White. Certainly it does after White's next move.}) 15... Bc6 $1 {Now ...d4 is on again, as the knight will be vulnerable on a4.} 16. f4 {Kacheishvili wants to stop ...d4 by preventing Black from supporting the pawn with ...e5. However, Bg3 followed by f2-f4 looks incredibly strange, and it's hardly surprising that Black has a route to a good position that doesn't directly involve ...d4.} ({Golod gives the line} 16. Bd3 d4 17. Ne4 Nxe4 18. Bxe4 Bxe4 19. Qxe4 f5 20. Qc2 e5 {and "" although this assessment might even be generous to White!}) 16... Ng4 $1 $17 {A logical and strong way to exploit the weakness 16 f4 created. White's position is a mess.} ({White's idea was} 16... d4 17. exd4 cxd4 18. Bf2 {(Golod).}) 17. Bf2 Ndf6 $5 (17... Nxf2 18. Qxf2 Nf6 $17 {is simple and strong.}) 18. Bg1 $5 {The bishop has completed a miserable transformation. Playing Bg1 wouldn't have been part of Kacheishvili's plans when he went 7 Bg5! At least this retreat forces Black to continue forcefully, although it's hardly a challenge for Polgar.} (18. h3 Nxf2 19. Qxf2 Rfd8 {followed by ...Rab8 and ... d4 (Golod) is all too easy for Black.}) 18... Rfd8 $1 19. h3 Nxe3 20. Bxe3 d4 21. Bd3 ({If} 21. Bd2 dxc3 22. Bxc3 {then} Rxd1+ 23. Qxd1 Rd8 {is awkward for White.} 24. Qe1 {loses to} Qb3 $1 {when either ...Ba4 or ...Be4 will be decisive.}) 21... dxe3 22. Rde1 (22. Rhe1 {might be better, but} Rd4 {is still very good for Black.}) 22... Nh5 $1 {White can only round up the e3-pawn at the cost of giving up another pawn.} 23. Rxe3 Nxf4 $19 24. Bh7+ Kh8 25. Rf1 {A last desperate attempt.} Nxg2 26. Rg3 Qc7 27. Ne2 Qe5 28. Qc3 (28. Rxf7 {is the only consistent move to White's previous play, but it loses to the shot} Ba4 $1 {, e.g.} 29. Qxa4 (29. b3 Ne3) 29... Qxe2 30. Rfxg7 Qe1+ 31. Kc2 Ne3+ 32. Rxe3 Rd2+ 33. Kb3 Rb8+ 34. Ka2 Rbxb2# {(Golod)}) 28... Qxc3+ 29. Nxc3 Kxh7 30. Rxf7 Rg8 31. Rc7 Rac8 32. Rgxg7+ Rxg7 33. Rxc8 Bf3 {In summary, Kacheishvili's 14 0-0-0 looks too optimistic here. Perhaps the most revealing fact is that Morozevich, who isn't averse to taking the odd risk or two, prefers 14 Rd1 and castling short!} 0-1

