1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. Bc4 d6 5. Qf3 e6 6. Nge2 b5 {The main line.} 7. Bb3 (7. Bd3 {is weaker as it allows} e5 $1 {with good prospects for Black, for example:} 8. Be3 Nf6 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Rd1 Nbd7 11. Nc1 O-O 12. Nb3 Qc7 13. h3 a6 $1 {(intending ...c5-c4)} 14. Nc5 Nxc5 15. Bxc5 Rd8 16. O-O Nd7 17. Be3 Nf8 $1 {Aiming for the d4 square. Black is already slightly better.} 18. Ne2 Ne6 19. c3 Bb7 20. Bc2 c5 21. Qg3 Rxd1 22. Rxd1 Rd8 23. Rxd8+ Qxd8 24. f3 Bf6 25. Qe1 Bg5 {1/2,Liss - Kanstler, Rishon le Zion, 1994}) 7... a5 8. a3 (8. a4 $6 {is weaker because White's Knight is driven back into a passive position. The game Geenen - Zilberman, Manila(ol), 1992 continued:} b4 9. Nd1 Ba6 10. Be3 d5 11. exd5 cxd5 12. c4 Ne7 13. cxd5 Nxd5 14. Rc1 O-O 15. Bxd5 exd5 16. b3 Re8 17. O-O Nd7 18. Re1 Nf6 19. Nb2 Ne4 20. Nf4 Ra7 21. Nfd3 Rae7 {, and Black is clearly better.}) 8... Ba6 9. d5 $1 {The correct approach: White counters flank activity by action in the centre.} cxd5 (9... b4 $6 {is insufficient:} 10. dxe6 $1 fxe6 11. Nd1 d5 12. Nf4 Qe7 13. Qh3 {and Black's seemingly strong central pawns are in fact very good targets}) (9... exd5 $5 {deserves certain attention. Black's ideas here are similar to those Nunn - Shirov:} 10. exd5 c5 11. O-O (11. a4 {is hardly good:} b4 12. Nb5 Bxb5 13. axb5 Nf6 14. c4 O-O 15. O-O Nbd7 {intending ...Nb6, ...a4, with good prospects.}) 11... Nf6 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bh4 g5 14. Bg3 O-O 15. a4 b4 16. Nb5 Ne8 17. c3 f5 18. h4 f4 19. Bh2 {Now White's Bishop is out of play.} Nd7 20. hxg5 hxg5 21. Qh5 Qf6 22. Qh3 Ne5 23. Qe6+ Kh8 24. Qxf6 Rxf6 25. Rfd1 Rb8 {and Black is clearly better.}) 10. exd5 e5 {Black has a nice pawn structure in the center and if he manages to complete his development he'll be better. So White should play very aggressively.} 11. Ne4 $1 Qc7 $6 {After this seemingly natural move Black's position is barely defensible.} ({Of course,} 11... f5 $2 {is bad in view of} 12. Ng5) 12. c4 $1 bxc4 (12... Ne7 {is insufficient in view of} 13. c5 $1) ({and} 12... Nd7 13. N2c3 bxc4 14. Ba4 {leads to the same position as in the game.}) 13. Ba4+ Nd7 (13... Ke7 14. N2c3) 14. N2c3 Ke7 $2 {Black wants to play ...Ngf6, completing development, but this move allows brilliant combination which leads to a forced win for White.} ({Both} 14... f5 15. Ng5) ({and} 14... Ne7 15. Nf6+ Bxf6 16. Qxf6 {are very bad for Black}) ({To avoid an immediate loss Black should try either} 14... h6 15. Nb5 Bxb5 16. Bxb5 f5 17. Nd2 Ngf6 18. Bc6) ({or} 14... Kf8 15. Bc6 Rb8 16. a4 $1 {(intending 17 Nb5 Bxb5 18 axb5!), although in both lines White's advantage is beyond any doubt.}) 15. Nxd6 $3 {After this blow Black's position crumbles to dust.} Qxd6 (15... Kxd6 $2 {leads to a spectacular mate in the centre of the board:} 16. Ne4+ Kxd5 (16... Ke7 17. d6+) 17. Qxf7+ Kxe4 18. Bc2+ Kd4 19. Be3# $1) 16. Ne4 Qxd5 (16... Qb8 {loses on the spot:} 17. d6+ Ke8 18. Bxd7+) 17. Bg5+ Ndf6 (17... f6 18. Rd1 Qxd1+ 19. Qxd1) 18. Rd1 Qb7 (18... Qe6 {is no better:} 19. Rd6 $1 (19. Rd7+ Kf8 {is not so clear.}) 19... Qf5 20. Rd7+ $1 Kf8 21. Nd6 {winning material:} Qxd7 (21... Qxf3 22. Rxf7#) (21... Qb1+ 22. Bd1 Ne7 23. Bxf6) 22. Qxa8+ Ke7 23. Bxd7) 19. Rd7+ Qxd7 20. Bxd7 h6 $2 (20... Bb7 {was more stubborn as White has to find a rather difficult solution:} 21. a4 $1 h6 22. Bb5 $1 hxg5 23. Qa3+ Kd8 24. Qd6+ Kc8 25. Nc5 {with a quick mate.}) 21. Qd1 $1 ({In view of unavoidable mate after} 21. Qd1 hxg5 22. Qd6+ Kd8 23. Bb5+ {Black resigned. An excellent attack!}) 1-0

