{In the quiet positional 6.Be2 e5 Najdorf, Svidler has tried the 10.Nd5 and 11. Qd3 line. After Judith's energetic 13...Nfe4 it became clear that the queenside will be closed and Black has chances on the kingside in the long run. This is a fine illustrative game showing Black's blockade strategy on the queenside and her attacking possibilities on the kingside. White's whole plan received a severe blow in this game.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. Nd5 {The idea is to put pressure on the queenside. Black has to take the N, and after exd5 White has a queenside pawn majority. However to build up the ideal set of pawns a5-b4-c4-d5 is far from easy. The breakthrough on c5 can easily be stopped, while in the meantime Black can advance in the centre with the ... f5 advance.} Nbd7 11. Qd3 {Now c4 is already threatened.} Bxd5 12. exd5 Nc5 13. Qd2 (13. Nxc5 $6 dxc5 {leads to a successful blockade for Black on d6, and the defended passed P on d5 is useless.} 14. Rad1 e4 15. Qd2 Bd6 16. a4 Qc7 17. g3 Rae8 18. a5 Nd7 19. Bf4 Ne5 20. c4 f5 {and Black ca n mobilize his kingside pawns, but White cannot do the same on the other flank, King-Browne, Reykjavik 1990.}) 13... Nfe4 $1 {This is the best continuation.} (13... Nce4 $6 {is met by} 14. Qb4 $1 {and the pressure on the queenside is increasing.} a5 15. Qxb7 Rb8 16. Qc6 a4 17. Nd2 Rxb2 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bd3 {and Black's position is a bit worse.}) (13... Nxb3 14. axb3 a5 15. c4 {threatening b4 with a fine game for White.}) (13... Qc7 14. f3 {controlling the e4 square.} Nfd7 (14... Nxb3 15. axb3 Nd7 (15... a5 16. c4 {and b4 will come again.}) 16. c4 {followed by b4.}) (14... h6 15. c4 Nh7 {Black wants to exchange the B through ...Bg5.} (15... Nfd7 16. Na5 $1 {discouraging ...b6, when there is no good defence against b4.}) (15... b6 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Bd3 {with only a small advantage for White because of the weakened kingside.}) 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Bd3 {preventing Bg5, Apicella-Brunner, EU-chT Debrecen 1992.}) 15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. b4 Nd7 17. c4 b6 {The key square is c5.} 18. a3 a5 19. Rfc1 Ra7 $11 {with the idea of Rfc8 and Black has equalized, Galkin-Voitsekhovsky, RUS-cup Tomsk 1997.}) 14. Qb4 {While the knight is on c5, this is not dangerous any more.} a5 15. Qb5 Qc7 {Black has to blockade the dark squares.} 16. Rfd1 (16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. Qc4 Nf6 {The B is also strong now on d6.} 18. Rfd1 Bd6 19. a3 Nd7 20. Rab1 Rac8 21. g4 {This is directed against ...f5, but weakening the dark squares is not a sensible strategy.} Qd8 22. Kh1 Qh4 {Here is the punishment!} 23. Rg1 e4 24. Rg2 Rce8 {with chances for a kingside attack, Nikolenko-Voitsekhovsky, Zepter op Minsk 1998.}) 16... b6 17. Qc4 f5 {Black's pawns can easily be mobilized.} 18. Bd3 Qd8 19. Nxc5 Nxc5 20. a3 Rc8 21. Qb5 e4 22. Bf1 {This light-squared B has no future in this pawn structure.} Bf6 {activating the B.} 23. Rab1 Be5 24. b4 axb4 25. Rxb4 ({or} 25. axb4 Nd7) 25... Nd7 26. Rc4 Rxc4 27. Qxc4 Qe8 28. Re1 Nf6 29. h3 $6 {This is the beginning of a faulty plan.} Nd7 30. Qc7 $2 f4 {Now the avalanche of the black pawns cannot be stopped.} 31. Bc1 (31. Bxb6 Nxb6 32. Qxb6 e3 {destroying White's defence.} 33. fxe3 f3 {and the opposite coloured B is the decisive factor in the attack.}) 31... e3 $1 32. Bb5 $2 {This loses outright.} (32. fxe3 {was the persistent defence, although Black has a fierce attack anyway.} f3 $1 {Again this motif crushes White's defence.} 33. gxf3 Qg6+ 34. Bg2 (34. Kh1 $2 Qg3 35. Re2 (35. f4 Qxe1) 35... Qxf3+ {and wins.}) 34... Qg3 35. Kf1 ({or} 35. Re2 Rxf3) 35... Rxf3+ $1 36. Bxf3 Qxf3+ 37. Kg1 Nf8 $1 38. Bd2 (38. Qxb6 $2 Bh2+ $1) 38... Qg3+ 39. Kf1 Qxh3+ 40. Ke2 Bg3 41. Rb1 Qg2+ 42. Kd3 Qxd5+ {and Black is much better.}) 32... exf2+ 33. Kxf2 f3 $1 {followed by Bg3, winning.} 0-1

