1. e4 {Polgar,J Polgar,J Polgar,J Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J'' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J''} c6 2. d4 d5 {Polgar,J: 'I had the feeling that my opponent would choose the Caro-Kann but he also plays other moves like 1...e5 on move one. '} 3. e5 {Polgar,J: 'I have surprised my opponent because it was clear to me it was not what he expected. I have played during my career the Panov attack and 3.c3 also 3.f3 a few times. The game move is one I have tried only once a long time ago in a rapid game.'} Bf5 4. Nd2 {Polgar,J: 'I had prepared this line some time ago and this was the time to test it in a practical game!'} e6 5. Nb3 {Jones, Gawain: 'This knight manoeuvre has become very topical. White's idea is to dissuade ...c6-c5 and slow Black's queenside counterplay before developing his kingside.' Polgar,J: 'This early d2-b3 manoeuvre came into fashion not long ago. Generally it is always nice to try out new directions specially when there is not a lot of accumulated theory on the line. The idea behind this manoeuvre is to stop Black from playing ...c5 quickly.'} Nd7 ({Last year I looked at} 5... c5 {but this forces Black to cede the bishop pair after} 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. Nxc5 ({A recent miniature between two GMs continued} 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. Ne2 Nge7 $4 {Overlooking the idea behind White's previous.} (8... Bb6 {was necessary}) 9. Nxc5 Qa5+ 10. Nc3 {1-0 Rublevsky, S (2678)-Lintchevski, D (2539) Olginka 2011.}) 7... Qa5+ 8. c3 Qxc5 {when following} 9. Qa4+ {and Black failed to equalise in Vachier Lagrave, M (2718)-Mchedlishvili, M (2613) Muelheim 2010. However Mchedlishvili has since done a survey in NiC Yearbook 95 in which he thinks Black is doing satisfactorily.}) 6. Nf3 Bg6 {Jones, Gawain: 'Making way for the knight to jump to f5.'} (6... Ne7 {is the most common and has been seen in a few recent encounters.} 7. Be2 Nc8 {A typical knight retreat, opening up the e7 square for the Black bishop and thus he's able to complete his kingside development. This is an important position for the variation and is examined in Lars Schandorff's Caro Kann book.} ({Actually I had this position myself and opted for the unusual} 7... a6 $5 {which is probably a little premature.} 8. O-O Nc8 9. Be3 Be7 10. Nfd2 O-O 11. f4 Bg6 12. c3 Qc7 13. Qe1 c5 14. h4 {The point of the previous move but probably over-ambitious.} c4 15. h5 Bc2 {Nicolai had overlooked this square was available for my bishop.} 16. Nc1 Qb6 17. b3 Qa5 18. Nf3 b5 19. Qd2 Be4 20. Ng5 Bxg5 21. fxg5 Qa3 {and I had good queenside counterplay but still had to be careful to stop his kingside initiative. Getz, N (2333)-Jones, G (2578) Fagernes 2011.}) 8. a4 (8. O-O Be7 (8... Ncb6 {was Wojtaszek's previous try which I looked at in the notes last time I covered this variation.}) 9. a4 O-O 10. Be3 Qc7 (10... f6 {is Schandorff's suggestion} 11. a5 Qc7 12. exf6 Rxf6 $5 {with a complex position.}) 11. Nfd2 c5 {when the experienced Russian continued with the dynamic} 12. c4 $5 Ncb6 13. a5 Nxc4 14. Bxc4 dxc4 15. Nxc4 cxd4 (15... Rfd8 {would be more circumspect}) 16. Qxd4 Rfd8 17. Rfc1 Nc5 $6 (17... Nb8 $2 {doesn't work either} 18. Nb6 $1 Qxc1+ 19. Rxc1 Rxd4 20. Bxd4 axb6 21. Rc8+ Bf8 22. Bxb6 {is very good for White.}) (17... Nf6 $1 18. Nd6 Qb8 19. Nxf5 exf5 20. Qf4 Nd5 {and Black would be fine.}) 18. Nd6 $1 Nxb3 19. Rxc7 Nxd4 (19... Bxd6 $5 20. Qxd6 Rxd6 21. exd6 Nxa1 22. Rxb7 {also leaves White with very good winning chances.}) 20. Rxe7 Nc2 21. Rc1 {and White had a great position. Rublevsky, S (2678)-Wojtaszek, R (2726) Warsaw 2010.}) 8... Be7 9. a5 a6 $1 {I think this is an important idea. Not only does Black prevent the a pawn advancing any further but also prepares ... Nc8-a7 and thence either to ...c6 or ...b5 when Black's pieces start to coordinate.} ({Instead} 9... O-O {was played in Olszewski, M (2458)-Macieja, B (2606) Warsaw 2008 examined by John Watson in the archives.}) 10. O-O O-O 11. c4 Na7 ({The immediate} 11... dxc4 {is offered by Schandorff but the game continuation is very similar.}) 12. h3 dxc4 13. Bxc4 c5 $5 (13... Nb5 {followed by ...Nc7, ... b7-b5 is Schandorff's treatment.}) 14. Qe2 Nc6 15. Rd1 Qc7 16. Bf4 Nb4 {Jones, Gawain: 'is just equal.'} 17. dxc5 {Following the exchanges Black stands well as he can start targeting the a5 and e5 pawns.} (17. Rac1 Na2 18. Ra1 Nb4 {is just equal.}) (17. Bd2 $5 Nc6 18. Bc3 {is an interesting idea, preparing the advance d4-d5 looks a little better for White.}) 17... Nxc5 18. Nxc5 ({Keeping material on with} 18. Nbd4 {should probably be preferred.}) 18... Bxc5 19. Nh4 {I presume Kurnosov believed he was gaining the bishop pair but in fact the tactics favour Black.} Bc2 20. Rdc1 Qd8 21. Bg3 Be7 22. Qg4 $6 (22. Nf3 Bd3 (22... Bg6 $5) 23. Bxd3 Qxd3 24. Qxd3 Nxd3 $11) 22... Rc8 23. b3 g6 {and White's pieces lacked coordination. Kurnosov, I (2662)-Rodshtein, M (2625) Moscow 2011.}) 7. Be2 {Jones, Gawain: 'Rublevsky is the main practitioner of the White side of this variation and has very good results.' Polgar,J: 'My opponent had 2 games on this line previously which I found in my big database collection.'} Nh6 {Jones, Gawain: 'And this is normal, the knight gets ready to jump into f5' Polgar,J: 'This move is the most frequently played here. 8 other options have been tried out in tournament practice. The most common other options are 7....e7 which may transpose later on to the ...h6, 7...c8 was played by my opponent in 2010.'} ({Again I don't believe} 7... c5 {good enough to equalise.} 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Nxc5 Nxc5 10. Be3 Rc8 11. c4 dxc4 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. Bxc4 {and White's bishop pair gave him a definite edge. Rublevsky, S (2699) -Bareev, E (2655) Cala Mayor 2008.}) (7... Be7 {normally transposes but following} 8. O-O h5 $5 {was an interesting try, gaining space on the kingside.} 9. Ne1 h4 10. Nd3 Nh6 11. Nf4 Bf5 12. Nh5 g6 13. Ng7+ Kf8 14. Nxf5 Nxf5 15. c3 c5 {and Black's position looked respectable enough to me. Kurnosov, I (2669) -Epishin, V (2590) St Petersburg 2009.}) (7... a5 {should probably be met by} 8. a4 {which I feel favours White as ...c5 is now harder to achieve with the gaping hole on b5.} ({Instead} 8. Be3 a4 9. Nc1 Bb4+ 10. Kf1 a3 11. b3 Ne7 12. h4 Nf5 13. Bd2 Bxd2 14. Qxd2 Bh5 15. Ng5 h6 $1 16. Bxh5 hxg5 17. Bg4 gxh4 {gave White insufficient compensation for the pawn. Andriasian, Z (2564)-Dreev, A (2688) Budva 2009.})) 8. O-O ({In Pacher, M (2399)-Berczes, D (2535) Cappelle la Grande 2011, White chose to delay castling with} 8. Bf4 {so that he could meet} Nf5 {with} 9. g4 $5 Ne7 $5 {Hoping to later exploit White's kingside expansion.} (9... Nh4 {must of course be a decent alternative.}) 10. h4 h6 ({Claiming the f5 square back with} 10... h5 {is more critical.}) 11. h5 Be4 12. Bd3 c5 {and Black's play in the centre gave him a reasonable position.}) 8... Nf5 {Jones, Gawain: 'We've reached a position that is also commonly reached from the mainline Short Variation (1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.Nf3 e6 5.Be2 Ne7 6.0-0 Bg6 7.Nbd2 Nf5 8.Nb3 Nd7).'} (8... Be7 {is the other main option for Black here when White has tried two main paths.} 9. Bxh6 $5 {is the most principled. White gives up the bishop pair but in return hopes to exploit Black's compromised structure. It's notable that Rublevsky has been happy to repeat the White side here.} (9. a4 $5 {has been the choice of Areschenko.}) 9... gxh6 10. Qd2 O-O $5 {With c2 hanging the pawn doesn't need immediate defending.} (10... h5 11. c3 Qc7 12. Nc1 O-O-O 13. Nd3 Rdg8 14. Nf4 h4 15. Qe3 Kb8 16. a4 Qd8 17. a5 a6 18. c4 $1 Be4 19. cxd5 exd5 20. Rfc1 Ka7 21. Bd3 {and White's play on the queenside had made much more progress than Black's kingside initiative. Rublevsky, S (2645)-Lastin, A (2583) Kazan 2005.}) 11. c3 Kg7 12. Nc1 $1 {Rerouting the knight around to the useful f4 square.} c5 13. Nd3 cxd4 14. cxd4 Rc8 15. Rac1 {and White had the easier game as Black has to watch out for a swift attack. Rublevsky, S (2678)-Panarin, M (2526) Olginka 2011.}) (8... Rc8 $5 {is an alternative move order but reasonable likely to transpose to other lines covered.}) 9. c3 {Polgar,J: 'I was getting prepared for a possible early ...c5 later on.'} (9. Bd2 {has been the mainline, with ideas to control the a5 square preparing for a4-a5.} Be7 ({As in the game,} 9... Rc8 {can also be played. It's useful to check out a couple of Rublevsky's games:} 10. Rc1 Be7 11. Ba5 $5 {It's interesting that Rublevsky declined to play the thematic g2-g4 this time and instead provokes a weakness in Black's queenside.} ({In an earlier game he had tried} 11. g4 Nh6 (11... Nh4 12. Nxh4 Bxh4 13. f4 f5 {Is similar to a position examined with rooks back on a1 and a8, bishop on c1 but c3 played. This version looks to favour White as he can break with} 14. c4 {as was played in Lahno, K (2479)-Anastasian, A (2560) Yerevan 2008.} (14. Bb4 $5) ({and} 14. exf6 {also look like decent alternatives.})) 12. h3 O-O 13. Bd3 Kh8 14. Qe2 Ng8 15. Kg2 c5 16. Bxg6 fxg6 {Rublevsky, S (2695) -Asrian, K  (2630) Dagomys 2008 when} 17. Nxc5 Nxc5 18. dxc5 Bxc5 19. c4 {would have given White the edge.}) 11... b6 12. Bd2 O-O 13. c4 Bh5 14. Re1 dxc4 15. Bxc4 b5 16. Be2 Nb6 17. Ba5 Qd7 18. h3 Bxf3 19. Bxf3 Nc4 20. Bc3 Qd8 21. Qe2 a5 22. Rcd1 Qb6 23. Be4 a4 24. Nc5 $1 Bxc5 25. dxc5 Qxc5 26. Bxf5 exf5 {Rublevsky, S (2678) -Hovhannisyan, R (2560) Aix-les-Bains 2011 when the most clinical finish would have been} 27. e6 $1 {as} fxe6 28. Qxe6+ Kh8 29. Bxg7+ $1 Kxg7 30. Rd7+ Kh8 31. Qh6 {mates}) (9... h5 {prevents White's typical g2-g4 but} 10. Rc1 Rc8 11. c4 dxc4 12. Bxc4 Be7 13. Ba5 Nb6 14. Nc5 Bxc5 15. dxc5 Qxd1 16. Rfxd1 Nd7 17. Bf1 {saw White with a definite edge. Rublevsky, S (2687)-Morozevich, A (2730) Sochi 2006.}) 10. g4 Nh4 11. Nxh4 Bxh4 12. f4 Be4 ({Rublevsky, S-Jobava, B Khanty-Mansiysk 2005 continued} 12... f6 13. f5 Bf7 {when John van der Wiel recommends} 14. exf6 $1 Nxf6 15. fxe6 Bxe6 16. Nc5 Qe7 17. Nxe6 Qxe6 18. g5 Ne4 19. Bg4 {followed by 20.Rf5 with the initiative.}) 13. Bf3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 {and White's extra space on the kingside and attacking potential gives him the better chances. I've left the rest of the game in to illustrate how White might go about winning this position.} Be7 15. f5 O-O 16. Rae1 c5 17. c3 c4 18. Nc1 Bg5 19. Bxg5 Qxg5 20. Ne2 exf5 21. Qxf5 Qe7 22. Nf4 Nb6 23. Nh5 Qe6 24. Qg5 Qg6 25. Qh4 Rae8 26. Rf5 f6 27. e6 Re7 28. Nf4 Qe8 29. g5 fxg5 30. Qxg5 Rxf5 31. Qxf5 Qc6 32. Qg5 Qd6 33. Re5 Re8 34. Qh5 g6 35. Qg4 Re7 36. h4 Kh8 37. Qg5 {1-0 Rublevsky, S (2678)-Vuckovic, B (2626) Aix-les-Bains 2011.}) (9. Ne1 $5 {was looked at by John Watson in Alekseev, E (2716)-Berkes, F (2606) Budapest 2008.}) (9. a4 $5 {has also been played a couple of times recently, albeit without great results.}) 9... Rc8 {Jones, Gawain: 'Black does not yet want to commit to ...Be7 so as to lessen the power of g2-g4.' Polgar,J: 'It was clear that my opponent is playing for ...c5 to open up the position. There was a game Almasi against Medvegy where Black played ...c5 immediately which was very similar to my game by the way. 9...e7 is the main line.'} ({Ceding the bishop pair with} 9... Bh5 10. h3 Bxf3 {cannot be good enough to equalise and following} 11. Bxf3 c5 12. Bg4 g6 13. Nxc5 Nxc5 14. dxc5 Bxc5 15. Be2 O-O 16. Bd3 {White had a definite edge. Cheparinov, I (2660)-Forsaa, E (2301) Caleta 2010.}) ({Against} 9... Be7 10. g4 {must be critical when} Nh4 11. Nxh4 Bxh4 12. f4 f5 {is the important position and has been debated in a few strong encounters.} 13. exf6 (13. Be3 $5 Be7 14. c4 {was an aggressive bid but not totally convincing.} Nb6 15. Na5 dxc4 16. Nxb7 Qd7 17. Na5 fxg4 18. Nxc4 Nxc4 19. Bxc4 Bf5 20. Bd3 g6 21. Qc2 Kf7 22. Be4 Bxe4 23. Qxe4 Qd5 {and a draw was agreed but only Black can be better with White's terrible bishop. Erenburg, S (2582)-Sakaev, K (2668) Khanty Mansiysk 2005.}) 13... Bxf6 ({Lukacs gives} 13... Qxf6 14. Nd2 $1 h5 15. g5 Qf5 16. Nf3 Qg4+ 17. Kh1 Be4 18. Bd3 {when the bishop on h4 is a major headache for Black. He would have to try the tactical} Bg3 $1 {with a highly complicated position. Black's pieces look rather precarious but it may be playable.}) 14. Bd3 Bxd3 15. Qxd3 {and White should have had a slight edge as he can put pressure on e6. Delchev, A (2583)-Schlosser, P (2534) France 2003.}) (9... c5 {Polgar,J} 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Nxc5 Nxc5 12. Bb5+ Nd7 13. Bg5 Qc7 14. c4 a6 15. Bxd7+ Qxd7 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Qd2 O-O 18. h3 h6 19. Bf4 Rfd8 20. Rad1 Ne7 21. Nd4 Nc6 22. e6 $1 fxe6 23. Rfe1 Nxd4 24. Qxd4 Rac8 25. b3 Rc2 26. Re3 {and White won in very nice style, Almasi-Medvegy,Z Hungary 2008.}) 10. Bf4 $146 {Jones, Gawain: 'Polgar's innovation over a previous game by the Moldovan GM.' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: 'N'' Polgar,J: 'I could only find one game with the f4 idea, which was played after ...e7 instead of ...c8. Clearly there will be many games more to come from strong players in the future on this line too I believe.'} ({Jones, Gawain: 'That game continued with the aggressive'} 10. g4 {Ü 108/(97) Polgar,J: '(Polgar,Judit) Ü 108/(97)'} Ne7 (10... Nh4 {is the alternative when I presume White was planning} 11. Nxh4 Qxh4 12. f4 {which looks rather dangerous. The computer informs me that} h5 13. g5 {is completely fine for Black but I would feel rather uneasy with my queen trapped on the side of the board on h4.}) 11. Nh4 c5 12. Nxg6 (12. Nxc5 {is the computer's choice:} Nxc5 13. dxc5 Rxc5 (13... Nc6 14. Nxg6 hxg6 15. b4 Nxe5 {is very messy. White has the bishop pair and probably some edge although the pawn on g4 is looking really out of place.}) 14. Be3 Nc6 $1 15. Nf3 (15. Bxc5 Bxc5 {gives Black great compensation.}) 15... h5 16. g5 Qc7 {and it seems to me that Black has great compensation for the exchange as e5 is dropping and White's king rather vulnerable.}) 12... hxg6 13. f4 Nc6 14. Be3 cxd4 15. cxd4 Nb6 16. Nd2 Bb4 17. Nf3 Nc4 18. Bc1 {and already a draw was agreed. Kurnosov, I (2674)-Iordachescu, V (2621) Rijeka 2010.}) (10. Bd2 $5 {Ü 108/(97) Polgar,J: '(Polgar,Judit) Ü 108/(97)' Jones, Gawain: 'was tried by another strong aggressive GM. This looks slightly misplaced in conjunction with c2-c3 but it leaves open the f4 square for our b3 knight.'} Be7 11. Nc1 c5 12. Nd3 Bh5 (12... cxd4 13. cxd4 Bh5 {might be more accurate, when the computer offers the odd looking} 14. Nde1 {to keep control of the d4 pawn.}) 13. dxc5 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Nxc5 15. Nxc5 Bxc5 16. Be2 Ne7 17. Bd3 Nc6 18. Qg4 g6 19. Rae1 {and White's pieces had coordinated well. Nisipeanu, L (2661) -Popov, V (2585) Rijeka 2010.}) 10... c5 $6 {Jones, Gawain: 'The logical continuation of Black's previous couple of moves but now White can claim an edge.' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?!'' Polgar,J: 'I think this is too early, Black wants to save time and bring the bishop out in one move from its initial square. Black could play ...e7 and castle next to play for ...f6 breaking up the centre. '} (10... Be7 {Jones, Gawain: 'is of course a reasonable alternative. Now' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'} 11. Qd2 (11. Nc1 c5 12. Nd3 {is no longer logical as the knight cannot jump into f4. White could argue that following} c4 13. Nde1 {White has tempted Black into fixing the centre but I don't think Black should have too many problems as White has used so many tempi with his knight.}) (11. g4 $5 {doesn't seem so logical if White cannot continue with f2-f4 but still playable.} Nh4) (11. a4 {gaining space on the queenside would be normal.}) 11... O-O { f6 Polgar,J: ' f6'}) 11. dxc5 (11. c4 $5 {is another possibility but Polgar's play gives White a dynamic advantage.}) 11... Bxc5 (11... Nxc5 {would keep the bishop pair but} 12. Nxc5 Bxc5 13. Qa4+ Kf8 14. Rad1 {is rather awkward.}) 12. Nxc5 {Polgar,J: 'It was obvious to take the bishop pair off the board.'} Nxc5 ({Polgar,J: 'After '} 12... Rxc5 {Polgar,J: 'White is slightly better.'} 13. h3 $14 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'for example is an option to stop Black from playing ...h5, then I would develop with my queen and bring the rook to d1.'}) 13. Bb5+ Nd7 14. Bg5 Qc7 $6 (14... Ne7 $5 {Jones, Gawain: 'would be another way of dealing with the threat when the computer offers the interesting idea' Polgar,J: 'would avoid the big complications'} 15. Nd4 (15. Bxd7+ $5 Qxd7 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 (16... Qxe7 $2 17. Qa4+) 17. Qb3 {when White's safer king and superior knight vs bishop offers her the better chances.}) 15... a6 {Polgar,J: 'is a must, losing some time but...'} (15... O-O $2 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'has concrete problems after'} 16. Qa4 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} Nxe5 17. Qb4 {Polgar,J: 'taking advantage of the pin'} Rc7 18. Bf4 $16 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'now another pin is causing trouble for Black.'}) 16. Bxd7+ Qxd7 17. Bxe7 Qxe7 {Polgar,J: 'I think White has a pleasant game.'} 18. Qd2 $14 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'}) 15. c4 $1 {Jones, Gawain: 'Dynamic play. Polgar needs to act fast before Iordachescu can bring his king to safety.' Polgar,J: 'I was looking forward to playing this move when I played my previous move g5.'} a6 {Polgar,J: 'This was the most logical reply.'} (15... dxc4 $2 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'would lose on the spot because of the immediate '} 16. Rc1 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'and Black is losing material within a few moves.'}) {Polgar,J: 'I think 16.xd7+ was expected by the audience even probably by my opponent, but I was very much in the mood for sacrificing. I had won my previous 3 games. I had the self confidence to do so. After calculating many lines I decided to go for the complications. '} 16. cxd5 $3 {Jones, Gawain: '!' '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Ignoring the piece in order to keep the initiative and a dangerous attack.'} axb5 17. Rc1 Qb8 {Polgar,J: 'Pretty much forcing.'} ({Polgar,J: 'After'} 17... Nc5 {Polgar,J: 'both moves b4 and g4 are very good.'} 18. b4 $16 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'}) 18. dxe6 fxe6 19. Qb3 {Polgar,J: 'This is the critical position how should Black defend the e6-pawn. Black has many options to choose from - f7, f8, f7, c4 and I have checked 0-0 as well during the game, because it is very important to know if the black king is forced to stay in the centre or can escape and castle. These are the most difficult things to decide in a practical game with lots of tension, to decide what is the best defence out of the many possibilities.'} Nf8 $2 {Jones, Gawain: '?!' '?!' Jones, Gawain: 'After this Black's position is hopeless.' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'This is the wrong decision.'} (19... O-O $2 {Polgar,J: 'would be losing to '} 20. Qxe6+ Rf7 21. Rxc8+ Qxc8 {Polgar,J: 'and now my other rook is coming into play on the c-file'} 22. Rc1 Qf8 (22... Qb8 23. Qd5 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'I have seen it until this point during the game'}) 23. Qb3 {Polgar,J: 'and e6 comes very soon to decide the game.'} Nc5 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'} 24. Qd5 $18) (19... Bf7 $2 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'would be too passive'} 20. Rxc8+ Qxc8 21. Rc1 Qb8 {Polgar,J: 'and now after'} 22. g4 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!'' Polgar,J: 'I blundered during the game in thinking that 0-0 is a nice defensive try'} O-O (22... Ne7 {Polgar,J: 'which would be the natural reply '} 23. Qb4 Nd5 {Polgar,J: 'and'} 24. Qd6 $1 $40 {Jones, Gawain: '!' '!' Jones, Gawain: 'would leave Black virtually paralysed. White threatens 25.Qxb8+ and 26.Rc8+ so' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!'' Polgar,J: 'is a great spot for the queen with a fantastic position for White'} Rg8 {is forced but now} 25. Nd4 $1 {leaves White clearly on top.}) 23. Qxb5 Be8 24. gxf5 Rxf5 25. Qb3 $16 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'with advantage for White.'}) (19... Rc4 $5 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!?'' Jones, Gawain: 'looks odd but does parry the threat to e6 and might be Black's best although White keeps fantastic play. A sample line might go' Polgar,J: 'yes, ...c4 and ...f7 are the 2 choices to stay in the battle'} 20. Rxc4 (20. Qxb5 {Polgar,J: 'then'} Rxc1 21. Rxc1 {Polgar,J: 'and'} h6 $13 {Jones, Gawain: '=' '=' 'Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J: 'and d1 back'''} 22. Bd2 Ne7 23. Bb4 Nc6 24. Bd6 {when the bishop on d6 provides enough compensation for the piece for two pawns.} Qa7 25. Nh4 $5 Bf7 26. Rxc6 $5 bxc6 27. Qxc6 Qb6 28. Qc8+ Qd8 29. Qc4 {is an interesting position where White has just three pawns for the piece but it's still not easy to see how Black is to complete his development and in the meantime White can start pushing his queenside pawns.}) (20. Rfd1 h6 21. Qxb5 Rc7 $1 22. Rxc7 Qxc7 23. Rc1 Qb8 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' 'Polgar,Judit: 'Polgar,J: 'and d1 back'''} 24. Rd1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'} Qc7 $11) 20... bxc4 21. Qxc4 {Polgar,J: 'and now without the rooks already to play '} Bf7 {Polgar,J: 'is much better for Black then with the rooks on the c-file'} 22. g4 $40 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'would definitely come with a lot of complications to come, and White has great compensation.'}) (19... Kf7 $1 {Polgar,J: '!?' '!?' '!?' Polgar,J: 'It seems to me now that it is the best reply in this position.'} 20. Qxb5 Rc6 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} 21. Rxc6 (21. g4 $5) 21... bxc6 22. Qxc6 Nxe5 23. Nxe5+ Qxe5 24. Qb7+ Kf8 $8 $44 {Polgar,J: '' '' '' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '''} 25. Rd1 Be8 26. Bd2 $1 h5 27. Bc3 Qb5 28. Qxb5 $5 Bxb5 29. Rd8+ Be8 30. a4 {and Black has traded queens but still not solved all his problems.}) 20. Qxb5+ Kf7 21. Rxc8 Qxc8 22. Rc1 {Polgar,J: 'I have another rook to come to the c-file.'} Qb8 {Polgar,J: 'Logical move, not to allow White's rook to the 7th rank.'} (22... Qa8 $2 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'would lose on the spot after'} 23. g4 {Polgar,J: 'when '} Nh6 {Polgar,J: 'then'} 24. Rc7+ Kg8 25. Bxh6 gxh6 {Polgar,J: 'and'} 26. Qb4 $1 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-!'' Polgar,J: 'wins.'}) (22... Qd7 $2 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '?'' Polgar,J: 'is unfortunate, but is losing on the spot as well:'} 23. Qxd7+ Nxd7 24. Rc7 $1 {Polgar,J: 'and Black is losing material because on ...e8, c8+ would win the rook in the corner!'} Ke8 25. Rc8+ $18) (22... Qe8 {Polgar,J: 'is not better either:'} 23. Qxb7+ Kg8 24. Rc7 h6 {Polgar,J: 'and now'} 25. g4 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!'' Polgar,J: 'with a winning attack.'}) 23. g4 {Jones, Gawain: '!' '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Removing Black's sole well placed piece.'} (23. Qb6 {Polgar,J would have been another option to reach the 7th rank with the rook.}) 23... Nh6 (23... Ne7 {Jones, Gawain: 'would be more logical but now' Polgar,J: 'was the other option for Black but it was not very tempting for him I guess'} 24. Bxe7 Kxe7 25. Qc5+ {Polgar,J: 'is best of the different continuations for White'} (25. Qb6 Nd7 26. Rc7 {looks decisive but in fact the weird} Kd8 $1 27. Rxb7+ Nxb6 28. Rxb8+ Nc8 {gives Black some chances to hold the ending although still very good for White.}) 25... Kf7 (25... Ke8 26. Qb6 $16) 26. Ng5+ Kg8 {Polgar,J: 'and now a beautiful touch to the attack'} 27. Nxe6 $3 {Jones, Gawain: '!?' '!?'} (27. Qe7 Qxe5 28. Rc8 Qf6 (28... Qf4 29. h3 Qf6) 29. Qb4 {threatens 30.Nxe6 so} Bf5 {is forced but} 30. Nf3 $1 {leaves White on top, Black has to give the piece to prevent g4-g5 and Ne5.}) 27... Nxe6 (27... Bf7 28. Nxf8 Qxf8 29. Qxf8+ Kxf8 30. Rc8+ Be8 31. Rc7 {is another great endgame for White.}) 28. Qc8+ Qxc8 29. Rxc8+ {Polgar,J: 'and the material advantage wins the game after ...f7, xh8.'} Nf8 30. e6 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'e7 is indefensible.'}) 24. Qb4 {Jones, Gawain: '!' '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Preparing to take the seventh rank.' Polgar,J: 'Looking at the e7-square for the queen and then following the queen to the 7th rank with the rook to c7.'} Kg8 {Polgar,J: 'To be honest I did not feel at all like taking the badly placed knight at h6 but I decided that I would like to simplify the position. 25.d4 was extremely strong. '} (24... Ng8 {would prevent the queen swooping into e7 but} 25. Qb6 $1 Be4 26. Rc7+ Kg6 27. Nh4+ $1 Kxg5 28. Qe3+ Kxh4 29. Qg3+ Kg5 30. h4+ Kg6 31. Qf4 {sets up a mating net.}) 25. Bxh6 gxh6 26. Qe7 $1 {Jones, Gawain: 'A highly picturesque position. Black is a piece for two pawns up but his rook on h8 has no future while the knight on f8 isn't brilliantly placed. White has a simple plan of taking the 7th rank, picking up the b7 pawn and pushing the queenside pawns. Iordachescu discovers that even without queens he is powerless to prevent White's idea.' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!'' Polgar,J: 'Basically continuing my plan which I started with 24.b4.'} Qe8 $8 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'The only move to try to kick out my queen from e7. '} (26... Bf7 27. Rc7 Qe8 28. Qf6 {is equally hopeless.}) 27. Qxb7 Qa4 {Polgar,J: 'Looking for counter chances. '} (27... Bd3 28. h3 Qg6 29. Rc8 {and with so much pressure on the seventh and eighth ranks Black won't last long.}) 28. b4 {Jones, Gawain: '!' '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Defending the kingside and getting the passed pawn nearer home.' Polgar,J: 'It was about time to start to push my pawns and aim to queen one of them.'} Be8 {Polgar,J: 'Looking for regrouping the knight possibly to g6 and reach the long diagonal for the bishop trying to get more active.'} ({Of course the pawn is invulnerable.} 28... Qxa2 $2 29. Rc7 $18) 29. Qe7 ({Rerouting the knight to the f6 outpost with} 29. Nd2 $5 {Polgar,J: 'with the idea to attack the king by e4-f6 came seriously in consideration, which is what the engine suggests.'} Nd7 30. Ne4 {is also very strong but the game continuation is more forcing.}) 29... Qd7 {Jones, Gawain: 'The queen exchange doesn't lessen Black's woes.'} (29... Bc6 {Polgar,J: 'This move would have been the logical continuation of the previous black move'} {Polgar,J: 'I have planned d4 but the computer suggestion is quite interesting here:'} 30. h3 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} (30. Nd4 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'}) (30. Qf6 {also leaves White with a decisive attack.}) 30... Bxf3 31. Rc8 Qd1+ 32. Kh2 Qh1+ 33. Kg3 Qg2+ 34. Kf4 Qh2+ {Polgar,J: 'unbelievable '} 35. Ke3 Qxe5+ 36. Kxf3 Qd5+ 37. Kg3 Qe5+ 38. Kh4 Qg5+ 39. Qxg5+ hxg5+ 40. Kxg5 h6+ 41. Kh5 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'but who can calculate all this? Would anybody be brave enough to enter such a line when the position is just great for White?'}) 30. Rc7 $1 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!'' Polgar,J: 'This was my main point by playing e7.'} Qxe7 31. Rxe7 Bc6 32. Nd4 {Polgar,J: 'Finally I can bring my knight into the attack; even though it is an endgame the black king can be easily in danger.'} Bd5 33. b5 {Jones, Gawain: 'The chief problem for Black is that despite the exchanges he still cannot develop that h8 rook and so cannot stop the connected passed pawns.'} Ng6 34. Rc7 Nxe5 35. f4 {Jones, Gawain: '!?' '!?' Jones, Gawain: 'Judit continues to play tactically choosing the most aggressive moves, even in this endgame with few pieces left on the board.' Polgar,J: 'I wanted to keep my initiative, but b6 was winning and I know that most of the people would find it more practical.'} ({Jones, Gawain: 'There was nothing wrong with the simple'} 35. b6 {Jones, Gawain: 'e.g.'} Nf7 36. Rc8+ Kg7 37. Nxe6+ $1 {Jones, Gawain: 'Deflecting the bishop.' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '!''} Kf6 (37... Bxe6 38. Rxh8 Kxh8 39. b7 $18) 38. Rxh8 Nxh8 {Polgar,J: 'and'} 39. Nd8 $1 $18 {Polgar,J: '!' '!' '!' Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-!'' Polgar,J: 'practically wins the game, Black cannot stop b7 and then the d5-bishop has to be given for the b-pawn. Then White has too many pawns and should win shortly.'} (39. Nc5 $18 {and the extra pawns decide.})) 35... Nf7 (35... Ng6 36. f5 {Polgar,J: 'is horrible for Black.'} exf5 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'} 37. gxf5 Ne5 38. Rc8+ Kg7 39. f6+ $18) 36. f5 {Polgar,J: 'Still attacking.'} exf5 37. Nxf5 {Jones, Gawain: 'Reminding Black that there are still mating threats around.' Polgar,J: 'It is very difficult to make a move with Black. It makes it specially unpleasant to play this position with Black.'} Be6 $8 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: ''' Polgar,J: 'the only move which enables him to play on. '} 38. b6 Bxf5 39. gxf5 Kg7 40. b7 {Polgar,J: 'I have reached the 7th rank and was looking to get my a-pawn there as well.'} Rb8 {Polgar,J: 'Otherwise c8 would win the game. '} 41. a4 Kf6 42. a5 Nd6 43. a6 {Polgar,J: 'I am just in time with my pawn.'} Kxf5 (43... Re8 {Polgar,J: 'would lose to'} 44. Rc6 Ke5 45. Rxd6 Kxd6 46. a7 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'and one of the pawns queens on the next move.'}) 44. a7 Rg8+ 45. Kf2 Nxb7 46. Rxb7 $18 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '+-'' Polgar,J: 'The position is completely lost for Black.'} Ra8 {Jones, Gawain: 'Black has managed to stop the pawns but only to find himself in a trivially lost rook and pawn ending.' Polgar,J: 'I was kind of surprised that my opponent was playing on... '} 47. Ke3 {Polgar,J: '47.g3 and going on the kingside was a win as well.'} (47. Kg3 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit'}) 47... Ke5 48. Rxh7 Rc8 49. Kd3 Kd5 50. Rxh6 Kc5 51. Ra6 Ra8 52. h4 {Polgar,J: 'I became even more curious as to why my opponent was still playing this dead lost position, but when I realised the trick he is playing for I forgave him:) '} Kb5 53. Ra1 Kb6 54. Kc4 (54. Kc4 {Polgar,J: 'for practical reasons this was the best '} Kb7 55. Kb5 Kc7 (55... Rh8 56. a8=Q+) (55... Rxa7 56. Rxa7+ Kxa7 57. h5 {Polgar,J: 'White queens the pawn in the next 3 moves'}) 56. Ka6 Rh8 57. Rc1+ Kd6 58. Kb7 {Polgar,J: 'now white queens on the other side the pawn'}) ({Polgar,J: 'and here if I were to go h5 the trick would be ...'} 54. h5 Kb7 55. h6 Rd8+ 56. Ke4 Ka8 57. Rb1 (57. h7 $4 {Polgar,J: 'wow I would just make a draw after'} Rh8 58. Rh1 {Polgar,J: '...and'} Rxh7 $3 59. Rxh7 $11 {Polgar,J: 'Polgar,Judit: '='' Polgar,J: 'with stalemate, but 57.b1 for example leave the a7-pawn to be taken and White would queen the h-pawn.'})) (54. Ke4 {A highly entertaining game with the top woman of all time playing at her absolute best. The 4.Nd2 lines are still causing Black some problems.}) 1-0

