1. e4 {Mikhalevski, V} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {Kramnik was able to eliminate Kasparov's white pieces in the London 2000 match with the help of this ambitious defence. Black is playing without castling and with the worse pawn structure, but he can lean back on the advantage of the bishop pair.} 9. Nc3 Ke8 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Surprisingly all 6 games played with the Berlin system in the World Cup 2011 saw this line.'} 10. h3 h5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'The most popular continuation, which is directed against g4.'} 11. Rd1 {Mikhalevski, V: 'One of the few main lines.' So far there is no patent which leadis to the gaining of an advantage, so in every game white players try to squeeze from the position some concrete benefits.} (11. Bg5 Be7 12. Rfd1 {Mikhalevski, V: 'is a more popular alternative. It occurred in two games in the current World Cup.'} Be6 13. g3 (13. Rd2 h4 (13... Rd8 14. Rad1 Rxd2 15. Rxd2 h4 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Ne2 Bd5 18. Nfd4 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 g6 20. b3 a5 21. c4 Be6 $11 {1-0 Anand,V (2788)-Karjakin,S (2723)/Moscow 2009 (51)}) 14. Rad1 Rh5 15. Bxe7 Nxe7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'with a reasonable position for Black. Robson, R (2560)-Bacrot, E (2710)/ Khanty-Mansiysk RUS 2011'} 16. Nd4 Bd5 17. f4 a6 18. b3 b6 19. Kf2 Rc8 20. Rd3 $14 {½-½ Robson,R (2560)-Bacrot,E (2710)/Khanty Mansiysk 2011 (26)}) (13. Bxe7 {Mikhalevski, V} Kxe7 14. Ne2 Bd5 15. Nfd4 Nxd4 16. Rxd4 a5 17. Nf4 Be6 18. Nxe6 Kxe6 {draw. Ivanov, A (2538)-Lysyj, I (2629)/ Khanty-Mansiysk RUS 2011}) 13... Rd8 14. Rxd8+ (14. Kg2 f6 15. Bf4 Bb4 16. exf6 Bxc3 17. f7+ Bxf7 18. bxc3 Rxd1 19. Rxd1 Bxa2 20. Ra1 Bd5 21. Rxa7 Rf8 22. Rxb7 $2 Nh4+ $19 {0-1 Zhigalko,A (2591)-Matlakov,M (2603)/Sestao 2010 (26)}) 14... Bxd8 15. Rd1 Be7 16. b3 b6 17. Bxe7 Kxe7 18. Ne2 a5 19. Nf4 c5 20. Ng5 Rh6 21. c3 $16 {1-0 Navara,D (2722)-Kryvoruchko,Y (2635)/Reykjavik 2010 (44)}) ({Earlier we considered} 11. Ne2 {(Mikhalevski, V) Carlsen, M (2772)-Jakovenko, D (2760) /Dortmund GER 2009/ [Mikhalevski, V]}) ({and} 11. b3 {(Mikhalevski, V) Shirov, A (2749)-Anand, V (2800)/Bilbao ESP 2010/[Mikhalevski, V].}) 11... Be7 12. Ne4 {Mikhalevski, V: 'This move was introduced by the Hungarian GM Balogh, so the fact that Polgar has chosen it isn't surprising. The idea behind the move is to prepare for 12...Be6.'} (12. Ne2 {(Mikhalevski, V) is another important line. Curiously it occurred in a game between the same players in the last Olympiad. However that game wasn't too interesting as a draw was agreed a few moves later.} Be6 13. Nf4 Bc8 {draw. Polgar, J (2682)-Karjakin, S (2747)/ Khanty Mansiysk 2010.}) 12... Bd7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'A novelty. Black prefers to keep this bishop in safety.'} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Earlier Black tested'} 12... Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 14. Ng5 Bf5 15. c4 Rf8 16. Rd4 Bxg5 17. Bxg5 f6 18. exf6 {Mikhalevski, V: 'with a slight edge for White, who has an extra pawn on the kingside. Balogh, C (2595)-Delorme, A (2434)/ Arinsal 2009.'} gxf6 19. Re1+ Kf7 20. Rf4 Kg6 21. Bh4 Rad8 22. Re3 $14 {1-0 Balogh,C (2595)-Delorme,A (2434)/Arinsal 2009 (68)}) ({Mikhalevski, V: 'and the principled'} 12... Be6 13. Nfg5 Bd5 (13... Rh6 14. b3 Rg6 15. Bf4 {Mikhalevski, V: 'with a complex position in Szabo, K (2523)-Kovacs, G (2500)/ Szombathely 2010.'} Nh4 16. Nxe6 Rxe6 17. Bh2 Ba3 18. Rab1 a5 19. c3 Be7 20. a4 Rb8 21. b4 axb4 22. cxb4 $11 {½-½ Szabo,K (2523)-Kovacs,G (2500)/Szombathely 2010 (126)}) 14. b3 Rd8 15. c4 Bxe4 16. Rxd8+ Bxd8 17. Nxe4 h4 18. Bb2 Be7 19. Rd1 {Mikhalevski, V: '' Mikhalevski, V: 'with some edge for White in Vachier Lagrave, M (2710)-Gozzoli, Y (2524)/Marseilles 2010.'} Rh6 20. Rd3 c5 21. Kf1 $14 {1-0 Vachier Lagrave,M (2710)-Gozzoli,Y (2524)/Marseilles 2010 (41)}) 13. b3 {Mikhalevski, V: 'It's not clear if this move is really necessary. It seems that Judith prepares c4.'} h4 {Nobody is able to tell Black sensibly which danger should be addressed as a priority. The fixing of the kingside pawns lowers the danger of the advance of his majority. The other competing concept was to gradually advance the queenside with the hope of generating real counterplay.} (13... c5 $5 14. Bg5 b6 $14) 14. Bg5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'In general, exchanging the dark-squared bishops should be in White's favor as Black holds the advantage of a bishop pair. On the other hand it will solve the problem of Black's king, which sometimes fails to find a good square.'} Rd8 15. c4 b6 (15... Bxg5 16. Nfxg5 b6 17. Rd2 Be6 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Rxd8+ Kxd8 20. Rd1+ $14) 16. Rd2 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Polgar is improving her position while retaining the tension.'} Bc8 {Mikhalevski, V: 'At the same time Karjakin is trying to solve his problems by means of exchanges.'} (16... Bxg5 17. Nfxg5 Be6 $14) 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 (17... Bxd8 18. Bf4 (18. Rd1 Bxg5 19. Nfxg5 (19. Nexg5 {Mikhalevski, V} Ke7 {was probably more accurate, as in the game White saved the dark-squared bishop and started to create problems on the queenside.}) 19... Ke7 $11) 18... c5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'looks ok for Black as the c7-pawn is protected and White can't play 19.e6 as she did in the game.'} 19. Rd1 Rh5 $14) 18. Rd1+ Ke8 19. Bf4 {Mikhalevski, V: '!' Mikhalevski, V: 'Probably Karjakin missed or underestimated this retreat.'} c5 (19... Be6 20. Kf1 $14) (19... Bd8 $5 {(Mikhalevski, V) preventing 20.e6 deserved attention, but then 17...Bxd8 was clearly a better version of the game position. Sergey doesn't want to admit his "mistake" and continues according to his general plan.} 20. Nfg5 Rf8 21. c5 f6 22. Nh7 Rf7 23. Re1 fxe5 24. Bxe5 Re7 {and Black should be able to solve his problems.}) 20. e6 $5 {[%mdl 32] Mikhalevski, V: 'An interesting exchange of the central pawn for a doubled pawn. The point behind the exchange is the fact that Black's king and rook are far away from the queenside and it won't be easy to prevent a direct attack on the queenside pawns.' A remarkable moment in the battle of two concepts. The white player is seemingly illogically giving up the pawn majority on the kingside; the black bishops automatically gain in importance. The curse of the defence is at this moment the passive location of the rook on the other side of the board.} (20. Nc3 {(Mikhalevski, V) with the idea of Nb5 was a reasonable alternative. Now in case of} c6 {the knight comes back to e4 in order to exploit the weakness of the d6-square.} 21. Ne4 $14) 20... Bxe6 $2 {Mikhalevski, V: '?!' Mikhalevski, V: 'This might be inaccurate.' Karjaking is a brilliant talent from the Ukraine, but currently playing for Russia. The change of the federation did not come cheap - the conditions for him had an implicit guarantee of the Russian government. The goal is to groom a player with the potential to win the world championship title. In World Cup he had the bad luck of meeting Judit Polgar who was coming back after longish maternity leave at the height of her powers. The strongest woman player in history is not afraid of any opponent. The Russian hope missed the chance to apply an unusual solution. After the less appealing recapture with a pawn chances would have remained balanced.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Many commentators have already suggested'} 20... fxe6 {Mikhalevski, V: ', which is the first line of computer engines, but the move is not so easy to make for a human being as it creates a permanent weakness on e6. Anyway, the point is to meet'} 21. Bxc7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'with'} Bb7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'and White doesn't have time to attack the a7-pawn by means of Bb8.'} 22. Nfg5 Rh6 23. Bb8 Rg6 24. f4 a6 $11) 21. Bxc7 f6 {Mikhalevski, V: '?!' Mikhalevski, V: 'It seems that both players missed tactics connected with the hanging position of the rook on h8 after 23. Bc7.'} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Otherwise Black would probably play'} 21... Bc8 {Mikhalevski, V: '!?' Mikhalevski, V: 'opening the sixth rank for the rook and bringing the bishop to b7, where it's much more active. Still White's chances after'} 22. Nc3 Bb7 23. Nd5 {Mikhalevski, V: '' Mikhalevski, V: 'are better.'} Rh5 24. Bb8 Nd4 25. Nxd4 Bxd5 26. Nb5 Bb7 27. Bxa7 Rg5 28. Nc7+ Kf8 29. Nd5 $16) 22. Bb8 a6 ({Mikhalevski, V: 'An attempt to trap White's bishop on a7 doesn't seem to work.'} 22... Bd8 23. Bxa7 Bc7 24. Nc3 Kf7 25. Nb5 Ra8 26. Nxc7 (26. a4 $14 {(Mikhalevski, V) and I don't see much compensation for the pawn, though the game is hardly better.}) 26... Rxa7 27. Nxe6 Kxe6 28. a4 $16) 23. Ba7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Either Judith was happy with what she gets after 23.Ba7 or simply missed 24. Nxc5! in the 23.Bc7-line. However the latter sounds unlikely, especially when talking about Polgar.'} ({I'm a bit surprised that White didn't play} 23. Bc7 {Mikhalevski, V} b5 24. Nxc5 $1 $14 {White seems to be a healthy pawn up.}) 23... Bd8 (23... b5 24. cxb5 axb5 25. Bxc5 $16) 24. Nc3 {Mikhalevski, V: '!' Mikhalevski, V: 'White definitely connected her hopes on this strong move. The knight is either coming to a4 or d5 with good chances to win a pawn. It's not the full truth as Black could have protected his weak pawns with the help of a knight manoeuvre, which you'll see below.'} Kf7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Karjakin accepts the loss of a pawn, hoping to compensate it with a pair of bishops.'} (24... Bc7 25. Na4 b5 26. Nxc5 Bc8 27. Rc1 $16) ({Black had a clever defense, which starts with} 24... Ne7 $5 {Mikhalevski, V} 25. Na4 (25. Rxd8+ Kxd8 26. Bxb6+ Kd7 27. Bxc5 Nf5 {doesn't look entirely clear.}) 25... Nc8 26. Bxb6 $1 Bxb6 27. Nxb6 Nxb6 28. Rd6 Bc8 29. Rxb6 {It may seem that White won a pawn for nothing, but suddenly White's rook gets trapped. The latter allows Black to create counterplay.} Kd7 $1 30. b4 $1 {The only move.} Rh5 $1 {with counterplay thanks to the weakness of the c4-pawn.}) 25. Na4 $1 {The position has significantly moved towards understandable problems without original features. Black is losing a pawn and has to look for the most promising form of resistance.} b5 26. Nxc5 Bc8 ({Mikhalevski, V: 'After'} 26... bxc4 27. Nxe6 Kxe6 28. bxc4 {Mikhalevski, V: '' Mikhalevski, V: 'The game reaches a technical stage, though White's win is far from being easy.'} Be7 29. Kf1 $16) 27. cxb5 axb5 28. a4 $5 (28. Ne4 Be7 29. Nc3 $14) 28... bxa4 (28... b4 29. Nd2 Be7 30. Nc4 Bxc5 31. Bxc5 Be6 32. Na5 Kg6 33. Rd3 Rb8 34. g4 hxg3 35. fxg3 $18) 29. bxa4 {Mikhalevski, V: '' Mikhalevski, V: 'White has won a pawn. Now she has to prepare its advance to a8.'} Re8 {Knowledge of the course of the game enables us to guess, that somewhat better chances for a real fight were offered by the idea of opening the d-file for counterplay.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Probably Black could set more problems with'} 29... Bc7 $5 30. Rb1 Rd8 31. Bb6 Rd5 (31... Bxb6 {Mikhalevski, V} 32. Rxb6 Rd1+ 33. Kh2 Ke7 $14) 32. Bxc7 Rxc5 33. Bb6 Rc2 34. Rb4 g5 35. a5 Bb7 36. Nd4 $16) 30. Rb1 $1 {Mikhalevski, V: 'A direct approach. White is going to exchange the dark-squared bishop in order to open a way to the a-pawn.'} g5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Freeing the knight from the defense of the h-pawn.'} (30... Bc7 31. Bb8 Bd8 32. Rb5 Be7 33. Bc7 $16) (30... Re7 $5 {Mikhalevski, V} 31. Bb6 Bxb6 32. Rxb6 Rc7 {would be a tougher defense.}) 31. Bb6 {Mikhalevski, V: '!'} Be7 (31... Bxb6 32. Rxb6 Re2 33. a5 Ra2 34. Ne4 $18) 32. a5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'The first advance didn't require much effort.'} Bxc5 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Black hopes that the bishops of opposite colors increase his chance of a draw, but this doesn't happen.' When the position is sliding the defender should try keep das far away from the abyss as possible. Defence with both bishops would have enabled Polgar to dream about the ideal location for the knight on the c7-square.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Black could try to set a trap. Now after'} 32... Nd6 33. Nd2 (33. a6 {Mikhalevski, V} Nc4 34. a7 Bf5 $1 35. Rb5 Ra8 $1 {Black equalizes.}) ({However} 33. Rc1 $16 {(Mikhalevski, V) allows White to retain a big edge.}) 33... Nb7 34. Na6 Bf5 35. Ra1 Rc8 36. Nc7 Bc5 37. Bxc5 Nxc5 38. a6 Rxc7 39. a7 Rxa7 40. Rxa7+ $18) 33. Bxc5 Re6 34. Rb6 {Mikhalevski, V: '!' Mikhalevski, V: 'Surprisingly White is looking for an exchange of rooks. However it turns out that all Black's pawns are on dark square, which explains White's idea.'} (34. Rb8 Ba6 35. Bb4 Rc6 $16) 34... Ng7 {The blockade on the light square is seriously complicating the advance of the a-pawn, but Polgar will easily refocus on the pawn weaknesses of the kingside.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Nevertheless,'} 34... Rxb6 {Mikhalevski, V: 'deserved attention.'} 35. Bxb6 Ke6 36. Nd2 Nd6 37. f3 Ba6 38. Bd4 f5 39. Nb3 $16) 35. Be3 (35. Nd4 Rxb6 36. axb6 Ne6 37. Nb3 Ke8 38. Bb4 f5 39. Na5 Nd8 40. f3 Kd7 41. Bd2 f4 42. Bc3 $18) 35... Nf5 (35... Rxb6 36. axb6 Nf5 37. Bc5 Ke6 38. Nd2 Kd5 39. Ba3 $16) 36. Rb8 Re8 (36... Nxe3 37. Rxc8 Nd5 38. Rc5 $18) 37. Ra8 {Mikhalevski, V: '!?' Mikhalevski, V: 'A simple, but clever idea, which prepares an advance of her a-pawn to a7.'} Bb7 {Mikhalevski, V: '?' Mikhalevski, V: 'A decisive mistake in a difficult position.' The choice of the endgame, in which the defender could stand a chance to save the difficult position is a very complicated task. The battle of the bishop against the knight in the event of an exchange on e3 would not have been pretty for Black.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'Black should have started with'} 37... Nxe3 38. fxe3 Bf5 (38... Bb7 {Mikhalevski, V} 39. Ra7 Re7 $16 {staying in the game.}) 39. Rxe8 Kxe8 40. Nd2 Kd7 41. e4 Be6 42. e5 fxe5 43. Ne4 Kc6 44. Nxg5 Bd5 45. Nf3 $18) 38. Ra7 Re7 39. Bc5 {Mikhalevski, V: '!' Mikhalevski, V: 'Probably Black missed this intermediate move.'} Rd7 40. a6 Bc6 41. Rxd7+ Bxd7 42. Nd2 {Mikhalevski, V: '+-!' Mikhalevski, V: 'White is going to advance the a-pawn to a7 and bring the knight to b6. Black can't do much about these two ideas.'} (42. Nd4 Nxd4 43. Bxd4 Ke6 44. a7 Bc6 45. f3 f5 46. Kf2 $16) 42... Ke6 (42... Bc6 {Mikhalevski, V} 43. Nc4 {doesn't change much.}) 43. Nc4 Bc6 {The last moment when the final part of the game might have developed differently. Unfortunately also the fight of the black knight against the bishop would have been hopelessly lost.} ({Mikhalevski, V: 'After'} 43... Kd5 44. Na5 $1 (44. Nb6+ {Mikhalevski, V} Kxc5 45. Nxd7+ Kc6 46. Nxf6 Kb6 47. Ne4 $18 {White would have a clearly winning position.}) 44... Bc6 45. Nxc6 Kxc6 46. Kf1 Ng7 47. Be7 Nh5 (47... Ne8 48. Bd8 $18) 48. Ke2 $18) 44. Nb6 {Mikhalevski, V: 'The rest is easy.'} Nd6 (44... Ng7 45. a7 Kf5 46. a8=Q Bxa8 47. Nxa8 Ne6 48. Bd6 $18) 45. Bxd6 Kxd6 46. a7 Kc7 47. a8=Q Bxa8 48. Nxa8+ Kb7 {Mikhalevski, V: 'Black retains the material balance, but just for a few moves as his king is too far away from the kingside. Judith proves the point in an elegant way.'} 49. f4 $1 {Mikhalevski, V: 'and Black resigned as he loses all of his kingside pawns. A great win from Judith Polgar, but to be fair it should be mentioned that Karjakin played way below his usual level, although 12...Bd7 deserves further tests.' The charming finish is reminiscent of the sharp intake of breath you experience at the moment, when everything is beautiful around you. White will win the pawn endgame without any effort, since the black king has to go to the far away a8-square.} (49. f4 $1 Kxa8 (49... gxf4 50. Kf2 Kxa8 51. Kf3 Kb7 52. Kxf4 Kc6 53. Kf5 $18) 50. fxg5 fxg5 51. Kf2 Kb7 52. Kf3 Kc6 53. Kg4 $18) 1-0

