{It is a great pleasure and a honor to play Judit Polgar, the greatest female player in the history of the game! Before the game began it was announced that Judit was celebrating her birthday. I must say that in the thousands of tournament games I've played, I simply can't recall playing an opponent on their birthday. It must have happened -- but this was the first time I was aware of it. After the generous applause had died down, Judit, smiling at me, gestured towards my pieces and "invited" me to offer "any one of them" as a "present." Hmm. I thought about that one for a moment and hesitated. After all hadn't the great Bobby Fischer offered "Knight odds" to any female player? (An offer he withdrew when challenged.) Certainly, Bobby wasn't thinking about such a great player as Judit when making his challenge! Anyway, after reflection, I of course, reached across the board and removed her g8-knight! Judit burst into laughter, "Oh my! You are not a gentleman!" She exclaimed I had to solemnly agree. I would also like to take this moment, to sincerely thank GM Benjamin Finegold for having assisted me in my preparations for the games in Ningbo. Ben was simply terrific. In a cruel irony, our preparations seemed to be wasted as my opponents ducked, dodged and weaved their way past our well-prepared plans. No matter! For each game, I went to the board in a highly motivated mood. Thank you Ben!} 1. d4 {John Emms Seirawan,Y John Emms} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 {So far, so good. Our expectation was for 6...b6, with lengthy variations dead ahead.} d6 $5 {John Emms: 'As we've seen previously on this site, this line isn't always exactly the same as 6...b6 7 Bg5 Bb7, because Black can still choose to put the bishop on a6, which is indeed what Polgar does. However, White can still play a la Bareev with Ne2.' Definitely throwing a wrench into what I had in mind. The text introduces an entirely different plan of development for Black. With the text Black aims for a possible ...e6-e5, central break, even at the cost of a pawn in order to develop the bishop along the c8-h3 diagonal. The drawback to this otherwise flexible system is that if Black reverts back to a bishop's fianchetto, she may want to challenge White in the center with ...d6-d5, in which case a tempo might be lost.} ({It's definitely also worth noting that the game position can be reached via the move order} 6... b6 {John Emms} 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. Ne2 Nbd7 10. Qd3 ({instead of} 10. Qc2) 10... Ba6 {and only now} {instead of the more usual} 11. Nc3 {John Emms}) 7. Bg5 {There was a great temptation to play 7.g3, fianchettoing my own bishop and hence ruling out ... Black's own fianchetto. Nice plan but I couldn't make up my mind whether I wanted to face Black's activity:} (7. g3 e5 $1 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Bg2 (9. Qxe5 {This brazen grab made me recall the wise words of my trainer, IM Nikolay Minev, "White will die with a full stomach."} Nc6 10. Qc3 Ne4 11. Qd3 Bf5 12. Bh3 Qf6 13. Bxf5 Qxf5 14. Nf3 Rad8 15. Qb1 Nd4 {and I had "seen" enough.}) 9... Qe7 10. Nf3 Nc6 11. O-O Bg4 {when in this particular position I wasn't satisfied with my development. In particular, I couldn't reconcile how great an asset my two bishops are as the role of the c1-bishop has to be solved.}) 7... Nbd7 8. e3 ({My preparations (against an immediate fianchetto by Black) had focused on lines with f2-f3, in order to build a big center. Against Black's current setup, it simply makes no sense.} 8. f3 $6 h6 9. Bh4 e5 10. e4 Re8 {Preparing the capture of the e4-pawn. I saw a line that went:} 11. Bf2 d5 $5 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. Qd2 exd4 14. Qxd4 N5b6 $5 (14... N5f6 15. O-O-O Qe7 16. Qc3 c5 $5) 15. O-O-O Qe7 {when I had a sinking feeling in my stomach that I was seriously lagging in development. Unfortunately, in my 'mind's eye' I hadn't realized that I had stopped the perceived threat of ...d7-c5, which simply puts a piece en prise.}) 8... b6 {A key alternative for Black was to decisively deal with the pin by expanding on the kingside.} (8... h6 $5 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Ne4 11. Qc2 f5 12. O-O-O Qe7 13. f3 Nxg3 14. hxg3 Kg7 {Black might not be thrilled about the safety of her king but on the other hand Black's play is relatively simple, to play ...d7-f6 and to just bring out the queenside pieces. The kingside "weaknesses" are not so easy to exploit. As Viktor Kortschnoj would say, "A weakness is only a weakness if it can be attacked." For the moment, Black's h6-pawn certainly appears safe enough.}) 9. Ne2 Ba6 {Going for what I've dubbed the "extended fianchetto." Since the diagonal b7-h1, will become blocked, Black aims for immediate play against the c4-pawn with ...c7-c5 and opening the c-file.} ({Black can even delay the bishop development further, e.g.} 9... c5 {John Emms} 10. Rd1 (10. Qc2) 10... Qc7 11. Qc2 {and only now} Ba6 {- see the annotations to Laznicka-Khairullin, Moscow 2008.}) 10. Qc2 {John Emms: '!?' John Emms: 'when both sides have "lost" a tempo.'} c5 $5 {Fixing the structure -- but not necessarily for my own advantage. Here I was tempted to play 11.d1, keeping the center flexible and by implication suggesting I'll castle short, which is several tempi away. I couldn't reconcile whether my rook would be good or merely a target on the d4-square:} 11. dxc5 {John Emms: '!' '!' John Emms: 'Choosing the same approach as Morozevich. How will the different placement of Black's light-squared bishop alter things, if at all?'} ({John Emms: 'If'} 11. Rd1 {John Emms: 'there's an argument that Black gets more play against c4 because the bishop is on a6. For example,'} cxd4 12. Rxd4 h6 13. Bh4 Qc7 14. Nc3 Rac8 {John Emms: '"when Black is harmoniously developed and prepared to break in the center with either ... d6-d5 or ...e6-e5, with nice play" - Seirawan.' when Black is harmoniously developed and prepared to break in the center with either ...d6-d5 or ...e6-e5, with nice play.} 15. Qd1 {John Emms} Qb7 16. Rxd6 Bxc4 17. f3 Bxf1 18. Rxf1 b5 19. Rf2 b4 $11 {J.Kociscak-J.Stocek, Prague 2007.}) 11... bxc5 12. Nc3 Qb6 {As a consequence of blocking the c-file I've had to allow the opening of the b-file. Now it is my turn to face a very consequent decision, where to put my king? Castling long? That would be like waving a red flag to Judit to come and get me. On the other hand, the king is a strong piece and can help protect the b2-pawn. It is sure to lead to an interesting double-edged position!} 13. O-O-O {John Emms: 'Seirawan makes the same decision as Kacheishvili did against Polgar - to castle queenside. Let's see how it works this time!'} ({John Emms: 'If White plays a la Morozevich with' The alternative:} 13. Rd1 {John Emms: ', the evidence suggests that Black's queenside counterplay is accelerated by the presence of ...Ba6.'} Rab8 14. Rd2 d5 $5 {John Emms: 'and White has no time to hold c4.' This is the tempo loser mentioned earlier but it certainly seems well timed to me.} 15. cxd5 Bxf1 16. Kxf1 exd5 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. Rxd5 Qxb2 19. Qxb2 Rxb2 20. g4 f6 $1 21. Bf4 Ne5 {John Emms: '"had that depressing air of a variation that hoovers all the pieces off the board leading to a forcing draw" - Seirawan. In fact, I feel it's White is who is having to work harder to equalise here.' had that depressing air of a variation that hoovers all the pieces off the board leading to a forcing draw.}) 13... Bb7 {John Emms: 'Polgar prepares the ...d5 (-d4) idea which worked so well against Kacheishvili. But because Black has spent two moves to get the bishop to b7, surely White is now a tempo up on that game? Can Seirawan make use of the extra move?' For the next few moves, both players will face some awkward decisions. On the one hand the text costs Black a tempo but by making this supportive move, Judit keeps some central flexibility. Black could easily conjure up a promising initiative if she can manage ...d6-d5-d4, chasing away the c3-knight. My main alternative was for Black to double or treble on the b-file while I'd do the same on the d-file:} ({John Emms: 'The alternative for Black is treble on the b-file, against which White doubles on the d-file:'} 13... Rab8 14. Rd2 Rb7 (14... Rfe8 {John Emms} 15. Be2 h6 16. Bh4 Bb7 17. Rhd1 (17. e4 $5) 17... d5 18. f4 $5 Bc6 19. Bf3 dxc4 20. Rd6 Rec8 $2 (20... Bxf3 $1 21. gxf3 Qc7 $14) 21. Bxc6 Rxc6 22. Rxd7 Nxd7 23. Rxd7 $16 {I.Krush-N.Medvegy, Beijing 2008}) 15. Be2 Rfb8 16. Rhd1 Ne8 17. Bf4 e5 18. Bg3 Qa5 {John Emms: '"Both players have trumps, for the moment, the g3-bishop looks "offside" but Black's rigid center should mean some kind of White advantage. Also, the role of the a6-bishop feels stranded as well" - Seirawan.' which leads to a fascinating strategic position. Both players have trumps, for the moment, the g3-bishop looks "offside" but Black's rigid center should mean some kind of White advantage. Also, the role of the a6-bishop feels stranded as well.}) 14. e4 $1 {John Emms: 'Yes he can! The key difference between this game and Polgar's game with Kacheishvili is that ...d5 is prevented, so Black is much less active and finds it more difficult to generate counterplay. The only problem for White, albeit a significant one, is that e3-e4 gives away the d4-square, and getting a knight to this outpost becomes an obvious goal for Black and something White must try to prevent. The position remains very complex, but my feeling is that White might have an edge here. At the very least, I already feel that the plan of dxc5 and 0-0-0 looks like a challenging option against some ...Ba6 move-orders.' Another key decision. White puts an immediate stop to any plans of advancing the d6-pawn but as Viktor Kortschnoj oftentimes reminds me, "pawns don't move backwards." The text offers Black a magnificent d4-outpost which will act as a magnet attracting a black knight. A new strategic battle flares up, Judit will maneuver to get to the d4-square, I'll try to stop her while trying to tie up her forces to the defense of the d6-pawn.} Rab8 15. Rd2 Rfc8 16. Be2 Ne8 17. f4 Nf8 {John Emms: '"Both players are going through a maneuvering phase trying to maximize their pieces. On the surface it may appear as if Black's pieces are going backwards, but appearances can be deceiving. Black is doing her best to maneuver a knight to the c6-square, the gateway to the central outpost. The text envisions a ...Nf8-g6-e7 (e5)-c6-d4, journey" - Seirawan.' Both players are going through a maneuvering phase trying to maximize their pieces. On the surface it may appear as if Black's pieces are going backwards, but appearances can be deceiving. Black is doing her best to maneuver a knight to the c6-square, the gateway to the central outpost. The text envisions a ...f8-g6-e7 (e5)-c6-d4, journey.} 18. Rhd1 f6 19. Bh4 Ng6 {Here comes the knight!} 20. g3 $6 {John Emms: 'Seirawan wasn't too concerned about the possibility of ...Nxh4, as it's this knight which wants to get to d4. Nevertheless, after the game he questioned this decision.' Sometimes we can be too clever for our own good. The text caught my attention for two reasons. Firstly, I reasoned that if my bishop was captured, that would put an end to any ambitions of bringing a knight to the d4-square and simply open the g-file. Secondly, by refusing to make the "natural" retreat 20.g3, I wouldn't step in front of my g2-pawn. (I felt its burning ambition to advance.)  Actually, my "main" move leading up to this position had been: 20.g4!?} ({John Emms: 'Another option for White, as well as the obvious 20 Bg3, is'} 20. Bg4 $5 f5 $8 {Black has no choice. Capturing the h4-bishop will leave the knight high and dry.} (20... Nxh4 $6 21. Bxe6+ Kh8 22. Bxc8 Bxc8 23. f5 {John Emms: '"when the h4-knight will be trapped"' when the h4-knight will be trapped.}) 21. exf5 Nxh4 22. fxe6 {John Emms: '"White has two pawns for a knight as well as excellent coordination. I couldn't come to a firm conclusion about the position and so I ducked the line. Playing "solid" for the team..." - Seirawan.' White has two pawns for a knight as well as excellent coordination. I couldn't come to a firm conclusion about the position and so I ducked the line. Playing "solid" for the team...} Ng6 $5 23. Bf5 Rc7 24. Bxg6 hxg6 25. Qxg6 {This position struck me as promising. Now three pawns for the knight, but I began to have some doubts. After 25...c6, Black's counterplay down the b-file, freezes my rooks and Black may be able to quickly reset with ...e8-f6 and ...c6-e8, with unclear consequences.} Bc6 $13 {The final "nail" for this variation for me, is that I saw ...b6-a6, in the not too distant future, when White wouldn't have a convenient way to defend the c4-pawn.}) 20... Rd8 $1 21. Bg4 e5 22. Be6+ Kf8 23. f5 Ne7 $1 {John Emms: 'Finally the knight is coming to d4.' At the time, I thought this move best. It perfectly fits with Black's plan to occupy the magnetic d4-square with a knight. On the other hand, while Judit was thinking, I began to wonder whether the dangers on the g-file were really that severe?} (23... Nxh4 $5 24. gxh4 Bc6 25. h5 h6 {Suddenly I had my doubts that it had been so clever to allow the doubling of the h-pawns after all. In any case, as expected, Judit played the most principled variation.}) 24. g4 h6 $1 {John Emms: 'Slowing down White's g5 break.' An important move as it will cost White a number of tempi to get in the g4-g5 break. A false reading of the position is that, "yes but after White plays g4-g5, won't more lines be opened?" Such an objection requires precise calculation as well as an intuitive feeling that Black can withstand such an assault. Judit understood that timing was far more important.} 25. Bf2 {There is no going back now and I must allow a knight to appear on the d4-outpost.} (25. Nd5 $6 Nxd5 26. exd5 Ba6 27. Qa4 Nc7 28. Rc2 Qb3 29. Qxb3 Rxb3 30. Be1 Na8 $1 31. Ba5 Nb6 $15 {In this position it is interesting to note that the "powerful" e6-bishop may end up playing no role whatsoever.}) ({Trying to stop the d4-occupation in another way wasn't attractive:} 25. Bd5 $5 Nxd5 26. Nxd5 Bxd5 27. Rxd5 Rd7 {when Black's position looked perfectly okay to me.}) 25... Nc6 26. Nd5 Qa5 27. h4 $1 Nd4 28. Bxd4 cxd4 29. g5 $1 Bxd5 $1 30. exd5 {Up till now I thought that we had both played a good game but now Judit committed a griveous error.} hxg5 $4 {John Emms: 'It's counter-intuitive to open up the kingside, although I suspect Polgar simply missed Seirawan's 32nd move.' Unnecessarily opening up the kingside. Put another way, Black is facing the problem of the g-file being opened (only) and therefore should make plans to evacuate her king. That means moving the d8-rook out of the way.} ({John Emms: 'As Seirawan indicates, Black should be making plans to evacuate her king.'} 30... Rb7 $1 31. Rd3 Rdb8 32. gxh6 gxh6 (32... Rxb2 $2 33. Qxb2 Rxb2 34. h7 $1 $18 {John Emms: '!' '!'}) 33. b4 Qa6 34. Rg1 Ke7 $1 35. Kb2 $1 $14 {John Emms: '"I'd evaluate this highly unusual position as only slightly better for White with all three results possible" - Seirawan.' I'd evaluate this highly unusual position as only slightly better for White with all three results possible.}) 31. hxg5 fxg5 $2 {Same mistake as before, unneccessarily opening up the kingside. Once more, 31...b7, and hanging tough was necessary but in this case the damage of an open h-file has been committed.} (31... Rb7 {John Emms: 'is still the best, although obviously the second open file on the kingside helps White.'} 32. gxf6 gxf6 33. Rh1 Rdb8 34. Rg2 {Now the difference of two open files on the kingside is clear. White's rooks are ready to flood into Black's position. By the way, in this line, it is important to note that the b2-pawn is taboo.} Rxb2 $4 35. Rg8+ Ke7 36. Rh7+ Kd8 37. Rd7+ Kc8 38. Rxe8+ Qd8 39. Rexd8#) 32. f6 $1 {John Emms: 'This is the move I'm sure Polgar must have overlooked. If White didn't have this move, Black would still be well in the game. As it is, a queen entry into g6 in conjunction with Rh1 is going to be decisive, so much so that Polgar now decides to give up a rook just to distract White:' A sweet clearance sacrifice. I knew there was a reason why I liked the queen on c2, in the Nimzo-Indian!} Rxb2 {Spoiling my fun. Judit wasn't interested in helping me celebrate her birthday. Clearly the normal alternative is a total disaster.} (32... Nxf6 33. Qg6 Rd7 34. Rh1 {John Emms: 'and White wins everything.'} Ke7 35. Qxg7+ Kd8 36. Qxf6+ Kc7 37. Bxd7 $18) 33. Kxb2 $6 {I was too intent on a g6-invasion, in fact a b7-invasion is simply stronger.} (33. Qxb2 $1 Nxf6 34. Qb7 $1 Qxa3+ 35. Kb1 $18 {John Emms: '(Seirawan) is even stronger, but the text is still more than good enough.'}) 33... Nxf6 34. Ka2 Qc7 35. Rg2 {My engines enjoy pointing out my human weaknesses that I missed "stronger" continuations. Perhaps so, but to my mind, capturing the g5-pawn and doubling on the g-file seemed the most natural and straight-forward win.} Rc8 36. Bxc8 Qxc8 37. Rxg5 Nxd5 38. Qf5+ Qxf5 39. Rxf5+ Nf6 40. c5 Ke7 41. c6 Nd5 42. Rg1 d3 43. Kb3 Ke6 44. Rfg5 {After the game, Alexander Grischuk came up to me to say, "I was watching the last stages and noted your remarkable composure at the board. An exchange ahead and so sure. Then I looked again, and saw that you had two rooks for the knight!" - "Yes," I said, "the extra rook, certainly helped my composure!"} 1-0

