{Jones, Gawain: 'An interesting match where the players were obliged to play the Sicilian every game. For round 2, Kaidanov plumped for the Dragon.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O Rc8 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. Kb1 Re8 {Jones, Gawain: 'So we reach possibly the most important Dragon position from the last couple of years.'} 13. h4 h5 14. g4 {Carsten Hansen: 'The text move is White's most aggressive and dangerous approach.'} ({Carsten Hansen: 'In our next main game, we will discuss '} 14. Bh6 {was played in the only other recent game I could find and seems to be a relatively risk-free way of playing for White.} Nc4 {Carlsen's choice but Black has to be happy to defend so maybe it's here that Black can investigate the other choices.} 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Nd5 e5 18. Nxf6 Qxf6 19. Nb3 (19. Ne2 {was Karjakin's choice against Carlsen but didn't get any real advantage.}) 19... Re6 20. Qa5 Ra4 21. Qc7 Bc6 22. Rd3 (22. Rd2 {is suggested by Ponomariov and Komarov (and de la Villa in Dismantling the Sicilian) with the conclusion that White is slightly better.}) 22... Bb5 $6 {Seeing as Black just has to retreat his bishop next move this seems dubious.} (22... Ra6 {seems very passive but it's not clear how White improves either} 23. Rhd1 $5 Bb5 24. R3d2 Rb6 25. Qb8 Qxh4 26. Qxa7 Qd8 27. Nc5 Re7 {and Black seems to be holding.}) 23. Rd2 Bc6 24. a3 Ra6 25. Nc1 (25. Rhd1 Qxh4 26. Rxd6 {is starting to be unpleasant}) 25... Qf4 26. Rhd1 Qxh4 27. Rxd6 Qe7 28. Qb8 Rxd6 29. Rxd6 Bb5 30. Rd5 (30. Rd8 $5 $16) 30... Bf1 31. Rxe5 $6 Qd6 32. Qe8 Rb6 $6 (32... Bxg2 33. Re7 Qf6 34. e5 Qxf3 {is fine for Black as} 35. e6 $2 Rxe6 $17) 33. Rd5 Qf6 34. e5 $6 (34. Nb3 Bxg2 35. Nd4 $16) 34... Qe6 35. Qxe6 Rxe6 36. Nd3 Bxg2 37. Nf4 Bxf3 38. Nxe6+ fxe6 {This trade has left Black in a winning endgame with his two connected passed pawns and he went on to win although not due to the opening, Agopov-Fruebing, Berlin 2010}) 14... hxg4 15. h5 Nxh5 16. Rdg1 {Jones, Gawain: 'Deviating from 16.Bh6 which we've seen a fair amount of in the archives, most recently in Dominguez-Smerdon in November where David held very comfortably. White switches her moves around although it's not clear what difference this makes. On the one hand Kasimdzhanov's 16...Kh7 is now no longer playable, although it's been somewhat discredited recently. On the other hand Black now has more options here.' Jones, Gawain: 'Deviating from 16.Bh6 which we've seen a fair amount of in the archives, most recently in Dominguez-Smerdon in November where David held very comfortably. White switches her moves around although it's not clear what difference this makes. On the one hand Kasimdzhanov's 16...Kh7 is now no longer playable, although it's been somewhat discredited recently. On the other hand Black now has more options here.' Carsten Hansen: '?!' Carsten Hansen: 'White plays for a direct attack by taking aim at the g6-pawn as well as preparing at some point, the Nf5 sacrifice. However, this approach can be improved. '} ({More precise is} 16. Bh6 e6 ({instead,} 16... Bf6 17. fxg4 Bxg4 18. Rdg1 {was J.Cubas-A.Fier, Campinas 2009, and now Black should have played} e6 19. Ndb5 Rc5 20. Be3 Be7)) 16... e6 (16... Qa5 $5 17. fxg4 (17. Bh6 Bf6 {:} {White has a better option in} 18. fxg4 (18. f4 $4 {. EXERCISE: What did White overlook when he played this move? See if you can work out the entire sequence of forced moves that leads to a much better position for Black. ANSWER: Black presses on the queenside with} Nc4 19. Qd3 $6 (19. Bxc4 Rxc4 20. Nb3 Qa6 {leaves White two pawns for nothing}) 19... Qc5 20. Nde2 Nxb2 $1 21. Kxb2 Bb5 $1 {and White will have to give up his queen to stay in the game, A.Nukin-E.Kanter, Izhevsk 2011.}) 18... Nxg4 $6 19. Nf5 $1 Bxf5 20. exf5 Qxf5 21. Qd1 Bxc3 22. Qxg4 Qxg4 23. Rxg4 e6 24. bxc3 {and although there is plenty of work left to be done, the two bishops should promise White decent winning chances in the long run}) 17... Bxg4 (17... Nxg4 $2 18. Nf5 $1 $40 {and Black's extra couple of pawns aren't going to help when he gets mated.}) 18. Bh6 e6 (18... Bf6 19. Bg5 Bg7 {gives White at least a draw} 20. Rh4 Rc5 (20... Nf6 {has also proven to be playable}) 21. Bh6 e6 22. Rhxg4 ({or} 22. Bxg7 Kxg7 23. Rhxg4 Nxg4 24. Rxg4 Nf6 25. Rf4 Rh5 26. Rf1 Qe5 {with a complex position where Black cannot be worse}) 22... Nxg4 23. Bxg7 Kxg7 24. Rxg4 Nf6 25. Rg1 Rxc3 26. bxc3 Nxe4 27. Qe3 Nxc3+ 28. Kb2 Na4+ 29. Bxa4 Qxa4 {and Black has sufficient compensation for the piece, R.Cvak-L.Mezera, correspondence 2011.}) 19. Rh4 Bxh6 20. Qxh6 Rxc3 21. bxc3 Qxc3 22. Nxe6 Rxe6 (22... Bxe6 23. Rxh5 Nc4 24. Qh7+ Kf8 25. Qh8+ Qxh8 26. Rxh8+ Ke7 27. Rxe8+ Kxe8 28. Bxc4 Bxc4 29. Kb2 {gives White a slightly better ending although Black should hold.}) 23. Rhxg4 (23. Bxe6 Bxe6 24. Rxh5 Bxa2+ 25. Kxa2 (25. Kc1 Qa1+ 26. Kd2 Qd4+ {would turn the tables on White}) 25... Qxc2+ $11) 23... Nxg4 24. Rxg4 {and Black has held on}) (16... Rxc3 {would be the logical choice although} 17. bxc3 (17. Qxc3 Nxf3 $15) 17... a5 18. Bh6 {looks too scary to be playable.} Bxh6 19. Qxh6 a4 20. Rxh5 gxh5 21. fxg4 $1 $18) (16... gxf3 {is far too dangerous} 17. Rxh5 gxh5 18. Bh6 $18) 17. Bh6 {Jones, Gawain: 'So we've transposed back to Dominguez-Smerdon but Black doesn't have the opportunity to play Kasimdzhanov's 16...Kh7 although that has been somewhat discredited.'} (17. fxg4 Nf6 18. g5 (18. Bh6 Nfxg4 19. Bxg7 Kxg7) 18... Nfg4 (18... Nh5)) 17... Qf6 ({Other choices are:} 17... g3 {is favoured by the computer, but it is not entirely easy to play either:} 18. Rxh5 (18. Nce2 $1 Qf6 19. Bxg7 Qxg7 ({or} 19... Kxg7 20. Rxh5 gxh5 21. Nxg3 Kf8 22. Nxh5 Qh8 23. Qg5 {with clearly better chances for White, Y.Gonzalez Vidal-Y.Borges Feria, Ciego de Avila 2009}) 20. Nxg3 Nxg3 21. Rxg3 a5 22. a3 (22. f4 Nc4 23. Qf2 {is a suggestion of Pavlovic and if Black now plays} e5 $2 {, then} 24. f5 {decides the game}) 22... a4 23. Ba2 Nc4 24. Bxc4 Rxc4 25. Ne2 (25. c3 $5 {is also worthy of consideration}) 25... e5 26. Rg2 Rec8 27. Qxd6 Be6 28. Nc3 {and Black is struggling to stay in the game, G.Pezzica-T.Habermehl, correspondence 2008.}) 18... gxh5 19. Rxg3 Ng6 20. Nf5 Bf6 21. Nxd6 Rc6 22. Nxe8 Bxe8 23. Qh2 h4 24. Rg2 Bxc3 25. bxc3 Qd1+ 26. Bc1 Qxf3 27. Rg1 Kh7 28. Qb8 {was M.Vachier Lagrave-R.Kasimdzhanov, San Sebastian 2009, and now Black could have secured good counterplay with Vigorito's suggestion of} h3 $1 {.}) ({We have seen} 17... Rxc3 {many times by now, but here it is not particularly good as after} 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Qxc3 Qb6 20. fxg4 Nf6 21. Qh3 Kf8 22. Nf3 Qc5 23. Ng5 a5 24. Rf1 Nexg4 25. Nxf7 $1 {, White should be winning, L.Aroshidze-O.Perez Mitjans, Barcelona 2011.}) 18. fxg4 ({The impatient} 18. Bxg7 {only helps Black after} Kxg7 19. fxg4 Qf4 $1 {and Black is already in charge of the game, which concluded with} 20. Qd1 $6 (20. Qe1 Nf6 21. Rf1 Qg5 {leaves Black better; whereas taking the g4-pawn will energise White's initiative on the kingside}) 20... Ng3 21. Nde2 Nxe2 22. Nxe2 Qxe4 23. Ng3 $2 Qe3 24. Qxd6 Bc6 25. Nh5+ gxh5 26. gxh5+ Kh7 {(that was the end of that attack)} 27. Re1 Bxh1 28. Rxe3 Red8 {and White resigned in Z.Stanojoski-M.Parligras, Budva 2009.}) 18... Bxh6 19. Qxh6 Qg7 20. Qd2 $146 {Jones, Gawain: 'And this finally deviates from the aforementioned game with Polgar's second novelty of the game! Polgar, true to her nature, goes for the most aggressive plan.'} (20. Qe3 {Ü 107/71 Ü 107/71} Nf6 21. g5 Nh5 22. Ndb5 (22. Nde2 a5 (22... b5 23. Nf4 $1 Nxf4 24. Qxf4 a5 25. Rg2 $1 {followed by Rgh2 and it is game over for Black}) 23. a4 ({Rogozenco offers the following computer-generated line:} 23. Rxh5 gxh5 24. Nf4 Qh8 25. g6 fxg6 26. Nxg6 Nxg6 27. Nd5 exd5 28. Rxg6+ Kf7 29. Rh6 Qg7 {and now my analysis continues with} 30. Qf4+ Kg8 31. Bxd5+ Be6 32. Bxe6+ Rxe6 33. Rxe6 Rf8 34. Qc1 Rf6 35. Rxf6 Qxf6 {which is assessed as slightly better for Black by Houdini, but defending against Black's passed h-pawn will not be much fun}) 23... Rb8 24. Nd4 Qf8 25. Rd1 Rec8 26. Ndb5 {and White's pressure promises him a small plus.}) 22... Bxb5 23. Nxb5 Red8 24. Qxa7 (24. Rd1 {lead to a draw by repetition after} Ng4 25. Qf3 Ne5 26. Qe3 Ng4 {in I.Gaponenko-C.Koepke, German League 2009}) 24... Nf3 25. Rg2 Qe5 26. Qe3 (26. Qxb7 $2 Rb8 {is not an option}) 26... Ne1 $1 {was a strong novelty against which Dominguez failed to find any advantage.} ({a small, but important detail; the white rook is lured away from the h-file and if Black lets White keep it on h1, then Black will run into problems after} 26... Qxb5 27. Qxf3 Qe5 28. Rxh5 $5 gxh5 29. g6 {with a strong attack for White}) 27. Rxe1 Qxb5 28. c3 {(intending to attack the h5-knight with Bd1)} Qe5 {(Black's counterplay will consist of either ...Rc4 or ...d6-d5, thus White keeps the bishop on b3)} 29. a3 b5 30. Ka2 (30. Bd1 $5 d5 31. Bxh5 gxh5 32. Qf3 d4 33. Rf1 Rd7 34. Rd2 Rcd8 35. Rxd4 Qxg5 36. Ka2 {and Black's position with an open king is considerably more difficult to play than White's}) 30... Kg7 $1 31. Rf1 ({now} 31. Bd1 {brings nothing due to} Rh8) 31... Rc7 32. Qf3 Ra8 33. Rh1 Rh8 {, and the players repeated the move a couple of times before agreeing to a draw, L.Dominguez Perez-D.Smerdon, Khanty-Mansiysk 2009.}) 20... Nf6 21. g5 Nh5 22. Nce2 {Jones, Gawain: 'So White shows her hand. She plans on trading the h5 knight and tripling on the h file. Initially when I looked at this game I thought this idea was too basic but the deeper I look the more problems I find for Black. The extra pawn doesn't seem very useful.'} Nc4 $2 {Carsten Hansen: 'QUESTION: Why do you call this a bad move? It is after all a normal move in the Dragon.   ANSWER: Just that a move is normal does not necessarily mean it is good. In this case, Black gives up a nicely placed knight for a bishop which has literally no prospects and is biting on the e6-pawn.'} (22... a5 {was another of my attempts for counterplay.} 23. Qxa5 Nc6 (23... Bc6 24. Rh4 Nd7 25. Qd2 Nc5 26. Qe3 {and I can't really see Black's counterplay.}) 24. Qd2 Qe5 25. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 26. g6 {is another killing attack for White.}) ({I' prefer to keep the knight on e5 to try and protect the king. However the problem is it's hard to generate any aggressive potential without trading the b3 bishop.} 22... b5 23. Ng3 Nxg3 24. Rxg3 Nc4 (24... Kf8 25. Rgh3 Ke7 26. Qf4 $1 (26. Rh7 Rh8 {exploits White's back rank problems.}) 26... a5 27. Rh7 Rh8 28. Rxh8 Rxh8 29. Qf6+ Qxf6 30. gxf6+ Kxf6 31. Rxh8 a4 32. c4 axb3 33. cxb5 $18) 25. Bxc4 Rxc4 26. Nf3 {and it seems unlikely Black will survive long.} (26. Rgh3 e5)) ({Instead Black should consider} 22... Bc6 {, and now:} 23. Ng3 (23. Qe3 d5 $6 ({Black should be able to improve with} 23... b5 $5 {, for instance} 24. Ng3 Ng4 25. Qd2 Nxg3 26. Rxg3 Bxe4 27. Rh4 Bf5 28. Rhxg4 Bxg4 29. Rxg4 Rc5 {with a playable position, although White clearly is the side with the better chances}) 24. Ng3 Nxg3 25. Rxg3 dxe4 26. Nxc6 Nxc6 27. Rgh3 Ne7 28. Qxe4 a5 29. Bxe6 fxe6 30. Qxe6+ {and Black resigned in P.Konguvel-V.Antonio, Delhi 2010.}) (23. Rxh5 $5 {is fun and possibly excessive as after} gxh5 24. Nf4 Ng6 25. Nxh5 Qe5 26. Nf3 Qc5 27. Rf1 Bb5 28. Rh1 Kf8 {, while White probably is better, nothing is clear.}) 23... Nxg3 24. Rxg3 Bxe4 25. Rh4 Bf5 26. Rg1 Rc5 27. a3 Bg4 28. Rhxg4 Nxg4 29. Rxg4 Qh8 30. Rg1 Qh3 31. Qf2 Re5 32. Rf1 Re7 $4 (32... Qh7 {is ugly, but better}) 33. Qf6 $1 {(now it is game over)} Rd7 34. Ba4 b5 35. Bxb5 Rb7 36. Bc6 Rc7 37. Rh1 {and there is little left for Black to do other than resign in I.Ortiz Suarez-D.Arenas Vanegas, Cali 2010.}) 23. Bxc4 (23. Qb4 $2 a5 24. Qxb7 Bc6) 23... Rxc4 24. b3 Rc5 25. Ng3 Nxg3 (25... Nf4 $2 {is an attempt to slow the White attack but after} 26. Rh2 {followed by Rgh1 is winning.}) 26. Rxg3 Rec8 ({Carsten Hansen: 'Also hopeless is '} 26... e5 27. Nf5 Bxf5 (27... gxf5 28. Qxd6) 28. exf5 gxf5 29. g6 $1 (29. Rgh3 {Carsten Hansen: 'as Black cannot stop White's decisive action on the h-file following'} Re6 30. Qh2 Kf8 31. Rh8+ Ke7 32. Qh4 {and Black's king can't survive, for example and Black's king can't survive, for example Carsten Hansen: '(threatening g5-g6) '} Kd7 (32... Rg6 33. Qa4 {Carsten Hansen: 'and Black is without a defence.'} Rxg5 34. Qe8+ Kf6 35. Qd8+ Ke6 36. Re8+ Kd5 37. Rd1+ Ke4 38. Qxd6 $18) 33. Qa4+ Kc7 34. b4 $1 (34. R1h7 {also looks good}) 34... Rc6 (34... Rc3 35. Qa5+) 35. b5 $1 Rc5 36. Qa5+ {with mate next move}) 29... fxg6 30. Rgh3 $1) ({The other options are hopeless: White wins swiftly after} 26... Bc6 27. Nxc6 Rxc6 28. Rgh3 Rec8 {. EXERCISE: How can White close out the game quickly?   ANSWER: Control the h8-square further by plugging the f6-square with the queen with} 29. Qf4 Rc3 30. Rh6 {and Black resigned because 31 Qf6 is coming next, N.Galopoulos-A.Pavlidis, Athens 2014.}) 27. Rgh3 $1 {Jones, Gawain: '+-' Jones, Gawain: 'Funnily enough, all Black's moves have matched the computer's, and then in this position it suddenly switches to White winning. Indeed there's no defense.'} e5 (27... Kf8 28. Rh7 Qe5 29. Qf2 $18) 28. Rh4 exd4 (28... Bg4 29. Qh2 Bh5 30. Nf5 $18) ({Black has in fact also tried} 28... f5 {, but after} 29. Qh2 {, he resigned in A.Abreu Delgado-L.Lorenzo de la Riva, Barbera del Valles 2010.}) 29. Qh2 Kf8 30. Qxd6+ Kg8 31. Qxd7 d3 ({Carsten Hansen: 'Desperation, but nothing works. For example, '} 31... Rxc2 32. Qh3 {followed by 33.Rh8+ wins} Kf8 33. Rh8+ Ke7 34. Rxc8 {is curtains.}) 32. c4 (32. cxd3 Rc1+ 33. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 34. Kxc1 Qa1+ 35. Kd2 Qb2+ 36. Ke3 Qc1+ 37. Kd4 Qg1+ 38. Kc4 Qc1+ 39. Kb4 Qe1+ 40. Kc5 Qxh4 41. Qe8+ Kg7 42. Qe5+ Kg8 43. Kd6 $18) 32... Qc3 33. R4h2 b5 {Jones, Gawain: 'It looks like Black has suddenly got some counterplay but after'} ({Carsten Hansen: 'After '} 33... R5c7 34. Qd6 Rc6 35. e5 (35. Qd5 Rc5 36. Qxb7 d2 37. Qd7 Qe5 38. Rh4 {, White wins.}) 35... Qc2+ 36. Rxc2 dxc2+ 37. Kxc2 Rxd6 38. exd6 $18) {Carsten Hansen: 'EXERCISE: What is White's best move?   ANSWER: By interfering with Black's defence of the h8-square, White forces the black queen away from c3, and White can get the decisive attack going. '} 34. e5 $1 {Carsten Hansen: 'Now, White gains time to place a rook on the seventh rank with devastating effect.'} Qxe5 35. Rh7 R5c7 (35... Rf8 36. Qd6 d2 37. Rh8+ Qxh8 38. Rxh8+ Kxh8 39. Qxf8+ Kh7 40. Qxf7+ Kh8 41. Kc2) 36. Qd6 {Jones, Gawain: '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Black resigned as it's forced mate. Polgar's idea looks very dangerous and I have to admit I can't find a good solution for Black. Against 16.Rg1, 16...Qa5 is a possibility but this idea seems to refute 16... e6 and 17...Qf6. Let's hope not!' Jones, Gawain: '!' Jones, Gawain: 'Black resigned as it's forced mate. Polgar's idea looks very dangerous and I have to admit I can't find a good solution for Black. Against 16.Rg1, 16...Qa5 is a possibility but this idea seems to refute 16... e6 and 17...Qf6. Let's hope not!' Carsten Hansen: '!'} ({Carsten Hansen: 'Black cannot stop mate as after '} 36. Qd6 Qc3 (36... Qxd6 37. Rh8+ Kg7 38. R1h7#) 37. Qf6 {Jones, Gawain: '!'} Qc2+ 38. Ka1 {Carsten Hansen: ', it is the end.'}) 1-0

