1. d4 {Krasenkow,M Krasenkow} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Ba6 5. Qa4 Bb7 6. Bg2 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 (7... bxc5) 8. O-O O-O 9. Nc3 Be7 10. Rd1 Ne4 $6 $146 {[%mdl 8] Van Delft/Ris: '?!' Van Delft/Ris: 'The key match of the 3rd round in Khanty-Mansiysk was no doubt between top seed Gelfand and Polgar. A Queen's Indian could have been expected, although the fashionable 5.Qa4 might have come as a slight surprise for Polgar. Recently Gelfand has employed 5.Qc2 almost without exception, but very likely he didn't wish to follow the game Giri-Polgar, Essent 2009, where the latter had a clear advantage. This time, Polgar recaptured the pawn with her bishop, whereas 7...bxc5 is a topical subject (see CVO 16). Even more surprisingly is that in a well-known position she came up with the novelty 10...Ne4. At a first glance, simplifications may be favourable for Black, as she has to deal with a lack of space. On the other hand, White is ahead in development and so easily can exert pressure along the d-file. Gelfand perfectly sensed this by exchanging the dark squared Bs, clearing d6 as entry point for the R. Not in a mood for a long torture, Polgar changed the character of the position with 16...Bd5, although without the desired result. After a forced sequence, Gelfand reached a winning RB vs RN ending.' A new but hardly good continuation in this well-known position. White seizes the d6 square and initiative.} (10... Na6 {is usually played} 11. Bf4 Nc5 12. Qc2 Qc8 13. Rd4 (13. Rac1 Nce4 14. Nd4 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 a6 16. Qb3 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Qb7+ 18. Qf3) 13... d5) (10... d6) (10... a6) ({and} 10... Qc8 {have also been seen in GMs' practice.}) 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. Bf4 Qc8 (12... Nc6 13. c5 $1) (12... d6 13. Rd4 $1 f5 (13... Bc6 14. Qd1) 14. Rxe4 $1 fxe4 15. Nd4 Qd7 (15... e5 16. Bxe4) 16. Qxd7 Nxd7 17. Nxe6 Rf7 18. Bxe4 $16) 13. Rac1 (13. Bd6 $5 Bxd6 14. Rxd6 {may be even more precise as White's a1 rook will go to d1.}) 13... Rd8 (13... Qb7) 14. Bd6 (14. c5 $6 Qb7) 14... Bxd6 (14... Bf6 15. b4 $1 Bb2 16. Rc2 Bxc2 17. Qxc2 Bf6 18. Nd2 Nc6 19. Ne4) 15. Rxd6 Qc5 (15... Qb7 16. Rcd1 Nc6 17. Bf1 h6 (17... Bxf3 $6 18. exf3 Ne5 $2 19. Bg2 Nxf3+ 20. Kh1) 18. Nd2 Bg6 19. Bg2 $16) 16. Qd1 (16. Rcd1 $5) 16... Bd5 $2 {It was relatively better to give up the d7 pawn to seize the d-file:} (16... Nc6 17. Rxd7 h6 $16) (16... Bc6 17. Qd2) 17. Rxd5 $1 {[%mdl 64]} exd5 18. Ng5 {[%cal Rg2d5,Rd1d5,Gd5f7,Gd5a8]} (18. Ne5 $142 $1 $18 {[%cal Rg2d5,Rd1d5,Gd5f7,Gd5a8]} Rf8 $140 19. Bxd5 Nc6 20. Nxd7) 18... Rf8 (18... Nc6 19. Bxd5 (19. cxd5 Qe7 20. h4 h6 21. Nh3)) 19. Bxd5 Nc6 20. Qd3 g6 21. Ne4 Qd4 $1 {Black now holds a material balance by tactical means but his position remains compromised.} (21... Qe7 22. Qc3 Qe5 23. Qxe5 Nxe5 24. Bxa8 Rxa8 $18 {}) 22. Qxd4 Nxd4 23. Rd1 $1 (23. Bxa8 {Van Delft/Ris: '?'} Nxe2+) 23... Nc6 (23... Nxe2+ 24. Kf1) 24. Nf6+ Kg7 25. Nxd7 Rfc8 26. Rb1 $1 {[%cal Rb2b4]} (26. b3 $6 Rc7 27. Ne5 Rd8 $1 {That's why the R has to leave the d-file.}) (26. b4 Rc7 27. b5 Rxd7 28. bxc6 Rc7) 26... Rc7 $8 (26... f6 {(to cover e5)} 27. b4 Rc7 28. b5 Rxd7 29. Bxc6 Rad8 30. Bxd7 Rxd7 31. Rb3 $18) 27. Ne5 Nxe5 28. Bxa8 Rxc4 {White has won a pawn (although not in the best version) and gradually converts his material advantage:} 29. Bd5 Rc2 30. Kf1 g5 31. Bb3 Rd2 32. Ke1 Rd7 33. h3 {Van Delft/Ris: '!' Van Delft/Ris: 'Restricting the possibilities of the N.'} (33. f4 gxf4 34. gxf4 Ng4 35. h3 Nf6) 33... h6 34. f4 Ng6 35. e3 Nf8 36. Rd1 Re7 37. Kf2 Nd7 38. Kf3 Nc5 39. Bc2 Rc7 40. Rd2 Nd7 41. e4 Nf8 42. e5 Nd7 43. Kg4 b5 44. h4 gxf4 45. gxf4 a5 46. Kh5 a4 47. Rg2+ Kf8 48. Kxh6 Rc4 49. Kg5 Nc5 50. Bf5 Rc1 51. h5 (51. Rc2 $6 Ne6+) 51... Ke7 52. h6 Rh1 53. h7 Ne6+ 54. Kg4 Nf8 55. Rc2 Nxh7 56. Rc7+ Ke8 57. Rb7 Nf8 58. Rb8+ Ke7 59. Rb7+ Ke8 60. Rxb5 Rg1+ 61. Kf3 Rf1+ 62. Ke4 1-0

