1. e4 {Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter: 'Nisipeanu' Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter Nisipeanu,Liviu Dieter: 'Nisipeanu'''} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 {A big surprise for me, because I was certain that this line was a one-game try and we had already had this in our first rapid encounter.  Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'A big surprise for me, because I was certain that this line was a one-game try and we had already had this in our first rapid encounter. ''} 5. Nb5 a6 {Jonas Hacker: 'The Löwenthal Variation is on the board.'} ({For the Kalashnikov} 5... d6 {see Games 55-59.}) 6. Nd6+ Bxd6 7. Qxd6 Qf6 {Jonas Hacker: 'REMEMBER: If Black develops the queen to f6 we should swap it off, as otherwise Black can play 8...Qg6, preventing our development on the kingside.'} ({The main alternative,} 7... Qe7 {, is the subject of the next game.}) 8. Qxf6 {Simple chess! I had already played this way against the specialist of this line Paco Vallejo in Heviz 2008. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'Simple chess! I had already played this way against the specialist of this line Paco Vallejo in Heviz 2008.''} ({I took this line seriously after seeing the game Kramnik-Vallejo Amber 2005 (blind) which went} 8. Qd1 Qg6 9. Nc3 Nge7 10. h4 h5 11. Rh3 d5 12. Rg3 Bg4 13. f3 dxe4 14. fxg4 Rd8 15. Bd2 f5 16. Re3 hxg4 $19) 8... Nxf6 9. Nc3 Nb4 (9... d5 {was the sharp line in the pre-computer era, but now there is a straightforward solution:} 10. exd5 Nb4) 10. Bd3 h6 $6 {Jonas Hacker: '!?' Jonas Hacker: 'The modern approach; Black accepts that White has the bishop pair and simply limits the dark-squared bishop by taking away its best square. Nisipeanu: '?!'' I don't like this move, which in my opinion is just wasting time. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'I don't like this move, which in my opinion is just wasting time.''} ({Better seems} 10... d6 11. Bg5 Be6 12. O-O-O Ng4 13. Bh4 g5 14. Bg3 Nxd3+ $1 (14... Ke7 {was played in the first rapid game by Judit against me, but after} 15. Be2 $1 {White kept a small but stable advantage due to his bishop pair.}) 15. Rxd3 Ke7 {and White has only a very small edge.}) (10... d5 11. exd5 {is the same as 9...d5 above.} Nxd3+ 12. cxd3 Bf5 13. O-O {and White is better.}) 11. Be3 d6 12. O-O-O Be6 13. a3 {I was aiming to be able to play quickly and strongly in this game, since winning was my only option at this point in the match. Therefore a position with a clear plan and zero tactics suited me well. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'I was aiming to be able to play quickly and strongly in this game, since winning was my only option at this point in the match. Therefore a position with a clear plan and zero tactics suited me well.''} ({Another option and possibly a better one was} 13. Be2 d5 (13... Rc8 14. Rxd6 Nxa2+ 15. Nxa2 Bxa2 16. Rb6 $16) 14. a3 d4 15. axb4 dxe3 16. fxe3 Rc8 $14) 13... Nxd3+ ({Retreating with} 13... Nc6 {seems interesting seeing as the white bishop has no job on d3. In this case we have the simple and effective plan of doubling rooks and then retreating the bishop out of the way with} 14. f3 Ke7 15. Rd2 $1 Rhc8 16. Rhd1 b5 17. Bf1 Ne8 18. b3 {. Of course White cannot increase the pressure on the d6-pawn any more, but we can slowly improve with Kb2 and Nd5 before finally opening the queenside for the bishops; e.g.} Rc7 19. Kb2 Rac8 20. Nd5+ Bxd5 21. exd5 Nd4 22. c4 bxc4 23. Bxd4 exd4 24. Bxc4 {with a big plus.}) 14. Rxd3 Ke7 15. Rhd1 Rhd8 16. Bb6 {White has a very pleasant edge in this endgame and by simply pushing his pawns according to 'the book' will create big practical problems for Black. My ideal pawn configuration on the queenside would be a5, b3, c4 and on the kingside e4, f3, g4, h5 and if White can get it Black will suffocate surely. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'White has a very pleasant edge in this endgame and by simply pushing his pawns according to 'the book' will create big practical problems for Black. My ideal pawn configuration on the queenside would be a5, b3, c4 and on the kingside e4, f3, g4, h5 and if White can get it Black will suffocate surely.''} Rd7 17. f3 {Jonas Hacker: 'Black is now completely stuck.'} Rc8 18. a4 Rc6 19. a5 Bc4 ({Maybe a better way to fight against White's plan was} 19... Nh5 20. R3d2 Nf4 {but after} 21. h4 f6 22. Kb1 {Black is still facing an unpleasant defence.}) 20. Re3 {Just to avoid any e6 followed by d6-d5 ideas. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'Just to avoid any e6 followed by d6-d5 ideas.''} Ne8 21. b3 Be6 22. g4 {No f7-f5 please! Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'No f7-f5 please!''} Rc8 23. Kb2 Nf6 24. Red3 {Jonas Hacker: 'From this point White’s play is just incredible to watch: slowly expanding further on both wings before thinking about the final push.'} Ne8 25. h4 f6 26. h5 {First step complete, now White only has to play a4, c2-c4, f2-e1-b4 and b6 for a complete squeeze. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'First step complete, now White only has to play a4, c2-c4, f2-e1-b4 and b6 for a complete squeeze.''} Nc7 27. R1d2 {Overprotection never hurts! Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'Overprotection never hurts!''} Na8 28. Na4 Rc6 29. Bf2 Rdc7 30. c4 Rc8 31. Rd1 {Since Black is in a sort of strange zugzwang, it makes no sense to hurry, especially in a rapid game. Anyway this move opens the way for the bishop to reach b4. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'Since Black is in a sort of strange zugzwang, it makes no sense to hurry, especially in a rapid game. Anyway this move opens the way for the bishop to reach b4.''} Nc7 $2 {Jonas Hacker: 'This loses material but it was already hard to suggest an improvement. Nisipeanu: '?'' A blunder, but the position should be lost anyway. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'A blunder, but the position should be lost anyway.''} (31... R8c7 32. c5 dxc5 33. Rd8 Rc8 34. Bxc5+ Kf7 35. Rxc8 Rxc8 36. Bb4 {followed by c5 should be winning.}) ({Jonas Hacker: 'If Black simply passes White can break through with something like'} 31... Bf7 32. c5 (32. Ba7 Be6 33. Nc3 Bf7 34. Na2 {, planning Nb4 with crushing effect; e.g.} Rd8 35. c5 Ke6 36. Nb4 Rcc8 37. cxd6 {and wins.}) 32... dxc5 33. Rd7+ Ke8 34. Rxb7 $18) {Jonas Hacker: 'QUESTION: Can you discover how White continued? ANSWER:'} 32. Nb6 {Jonas Hacker: '!'} Rb8 33. Nd5+ Kf7 {Jonas Hacker: 'Taking the knight would cost Black at least a piece. But now White wins the exchange anyway and the game is over. '} 34. Nb4 $18 {Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: '+-''} Ne8 35. Nxc6 bxc6 36. Kc3 Ke7 37. b4 Rc8 38. Bb6 Ra8 39. c5 $1 {Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: '!'' The easiest way to break down Black's defences. Jonas Hacker: 'Nisipeanu: 'The easiest way to break down Black's defences.''} d5 40. exd5 Bxd5 41. Rxd5 {Jonas Hacker: '!' Jonas Hacker: 'The simplest way to win.'} cxd5 42. Rxd5 Ke6 43. Kc4 Rc8 44. Rd1 f5 45. gxf5+ Kxf5 46. b5 1-0

