,Evgeny} 35. Rfxc1 (35. Bxf5 $6 Nd4 $1 {[%cal Ge8e1]}) 35... dxc1=Q+ 36. Rxc1 fxe4 37. Rc4 $16) 35. f4 Rxb1 36. Bxb1 Rc1 37. Nf2 $18) (31... d1=Q 32. Rfxd1 Qe2 33. Qxb7 (33. Bg2 Rxg4 34. Qxb7 $16) 33... Nc5 34. Qe7 Qxg4+ 35. Bg2) 32. Bf3 h5 33. Nh2 Rxh4 34. Rfd1 $16) 31... Rc1 {Postny,Evgeny: 'Now White is forced to give a whole rook for the passed pawn.'} 32. Raxc1 dxc1=Q 33. Rxc1 Qxc1+ 34. Kg2 h5 35. Nh2 Nc5 {Postny,Evgeny: '!' '!' Postny,Evgeny: 'White still got a pawn for the exchange, but his king is in trouble. When you got a vulnerable king against Judith, usually you have no chance to survive. This game is no exception.'} 36. Bxb7 (36. Bxh5 {Postny,Evgeny was slightly more stubborn, but still after} Qc2 {White's position is in bad shape.}) 36... Qc2 {Postny,Evgeny: '-+' '-+'} 37. Bd5 Qg6+ 38. Kh1 Nd3 {Postny,Evgeny: 'Queen + knight is a well-known nasty duo.'} 39. Nf3 Nxf2+ 40. Kh2 Ng4+ 0-1

