{The following game between two top-rank players tests an important line that is virtually forced if Black is to avoid a definite positional advantage. It's significant that Judit takes up the challenge, which indicates that she must feel that Black suffers only a temporary inconvenience. However, Van Wely plays a new move that I believe might have caused some more serious problems. At any rate, this potentially critical variation is still worthy of investigation.} 1. c4 {*} c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. g3 d5 5. d4 e6 (5... cxd4 6. Nxd4 dxc4 7. Nxc6 Qxd1+ 8. Nxd1 bxc6 9. Bg2 Nd5 10. Ne3 e6 11. Nxc4 Ba6 {is the Kramnik mainline, see the eBook.}) 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Bg2 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nxc3 (8... Be7 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. O-O O-O 11. Be3 {gave White a small advantage in Stohl-Janos, Martin 2003.}) 9. bxc3 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. cxd4 Bb4+ {Stohl,Igor: 'Further simplification helps the defender. A beutiful technical display was'} (11... Bd6 $143 12. O-O Rb8 13. e4 O-O 14. e5 Be7 15. Be3 Bd7 16. Rfc1 Rfc8 17. Rxc8+ Bxc8 18. Rc1 Kf8 19. Bh3 $5 (19. d5 $14) 19... Ke8 20. d5 Bd7 21. d6 Bd8 22. Bg2 b6 23. f4 Rc8 24. Kf2 Rxc1 25. Bxc1 Bb5 26. Be4 h6 27. Be3 Kd7 28. Ke1 Bc6 29. Bd3 Bd5 30. a3 $16 {and White gradually converted his permanent advantage:} f6 31. Kd2 fxe5 32. fxe5 Kc6 33. Kc3 Bf3 34. Bc4 Bd5 35. Ba6 Bf3 36. Kd4 Bd5 37. a4 Bb3 38. Bb5+ Kb7 39. Bd7 Bd5 40. Kc3 Ba2 41. Kb4 Bd5 42. h4 Ba2 43. Bd2 Bd5 44. Bc1 Ba2 45. Kc3 Bd5 46. Ba3 Ba2 47. Be8 Bd5 48. d7 Bc6 49. Bf8 Bxa4 50. Bxg7 Kc7 51. Bxh6 Bxd7 52. Bf7 Kc6 53. h5 Kd5 54. Bg7 Bg5 55. g4 Ke4 56. h6 Bxh6 57. Bxh6 Kxe5 58. g5 Kf5 59. g6 Kf6 60. Bg5+ Kg7 61. Kd4 Ba4 62. Ke5 Bc2 63. Bf6+ Kf8 64. Kf4 {Kramnik,V-Lautier,J/Horgen/1995/}) 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Kxd2 Ke7 {Stohl,Igor: 'Black has managed to reach an  as early as move 13, but still has to defend carefully - White has more  and a lead in development. On the other hand his edge is very small and due to the simplified nature of the position most games in this line result in draws.'} ({The alternatives are} 13... Kd8 14. Rab1 $5 (14. Rhc1 Re8 15. Rc5 (15. Rc3 Re7 16. Rac1 Rb8 17. f4 b6 18. g4 h6 19. g5 hxg5 20. fxg5 Bb7 21. Bxb7 Rexb7 22. h4 g6 23. h5 gxh5 24. Rh1 Ke7 25. Rxh5 {Navara,D-Alekseev,E/Lausanne YM/2004/} Rd7 26. e3 e5 $132 {/=}) 15... Rb8 (15... Re7 16. Rac1 Rb8 17. g4 h6 18. Rh5 Bd7 19. h4 b6 20. g5 hxg5 21. hxg5 g6 22. Rh8+ Re8 23. Rh7 Re7 24. Ke3 Rc8 25. Rxc8+ Kxc8 26. Kf4 $16 {Nielsen,P-Golod,V/EU-ch 5' Panormo/2002/}) 16. Ra5 a6 17. Rb1 b6 18. g4 h6 19. h4 Re7 20. e3 (20. f4 $1 $14) 20... Kc7 21. Ra3 e5 22. Rc1+ Kd8 23. dxe5 Rxe5 24. Rd3+ {½-½ Christiansen,L-Ribli,Z/Germany 1994/GER-chT (41)} Ke8 $5 $11 {Ribli}) 14... Rb8 15. Rb3 b6 16. Ra3 Bb7 17. e4 Ra8 18. g4 h6 19. h4 Ke7 20. Rc1 Rhd8 21. Ke3 Rd7 22. Bf1 (22. f4 $5 {} f5 23. gxf5 exf5 24. d5 $1 fxe4 25. Bh3 $16) 22... Rc8 23. Rxc8 Bxc8 24. Rc3 Kd8 25. f4 Bb7 26. f5 $36 {Tukmakov,V-Sax,G/Pula op/1999/}) ({Probably a more accurate version of this defensive set-up is} 13... Rb8 $5 {[%cal Yb7b6,Yc8b7] b6,b7} 14. Rhc1 Kd8 15. Rc3 Re8 16. Ra3 a6 17. f4 Re7 18. g4 Rd7 19. Ke3 b5 20. g5 Rb6 21. Be4 Rbd6 22. Rc1 Bb7 23. Bxb7 Rxb7 24. Ke4 Rbd7 25. Rd1 Rc6 26. Rd2 h6 $1 27. h4 (27. gxh6 f5+ $11) 27... hxg5 $11 {Chernin,A-Sax,G/HUN-ch Budapest/1997/}) 14. Rhc1 Rd8 15. Rc4 {Stohl,Igor: 'A rare move, White mostly played'} (15. Rc7+ {Ü 65/404} Rd7 16. Rac1 Kd8 {when Black can apparently surmount his problems after} 17. Rxd7+ Kxd7 {, eg.,} 18. g4 (18. f4 Rb8 19. d5 (19. e4 b6 20. Ke3 Bb7 21. Bh3 Kd8 $1 (21... Rc8 $2 22. Rxc8 Bxc8 23. d5 Kc7 24. e5 $1 $16 {Benko,P-Donner,J/Wijk aan Zee/1970/}) 22. f5 Rc8 $11) 19... exd5 20. Bxd5 f6 21. e4 Kd6 22. Ke3 Bd7 23. Kd4 Rc8 24. e5+ fxe5+ 25. fxe5+ Ke7 26. Rxc8 Bxc8 27. Kc5 Be6 $11 {Huzman,A-Psakhis,L/Haifa act/2000/}) 18... h6 19. f4 Rb8 20. g5 $5 (20. e4 b6 21. f5 Bb7 22. Ke3 {might give Black some long-term discomfort, even though the position is undoubtedly theoretically drawn}) 20... hxg5 ({The game that made 5.d4 popular was Kramnik-Leko, Belgrade 1995:} 20... b6 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' '!?' '!?' Stohl,I: '!?'} 21. gxh6 gxh6 22. Rc3 Bb7 23. Bxb7 Rxb7 24. Rh3 Rc7 25. Rxh6 Rc4 26. Rh3 Rxd4+ {Stohl,Igor: '?' '?' '?' Stohl,I: '?'} (26... Ra4 $1 27. a3 Rxd4+ $11) 27. Rd3 {Stohl,Igor: '+-!' '+-!' '+-!' Stohl,Igor: 'Kramnik,V-Lautier,J/Beograd/1995/' Stohl,I: '+-!'} Rxd3+ 28. Kxd3 {and White went on to win}) 21. fxg5 b6 22. h4 g6 23. Bf3 {1/2-1/2 Kohlweyer-I Farago, Porto San Giorgio 2002. Even here, [go to 20.e4]} (23. Ke3 Ba6 24. Bf3 Rh8 25. Rh1 Bc4 26. Kf4 b5 $132 {Psakhis,L-Zilberman,Y/Ramat Aviv/1998/}) (23. Bc6+ Kd6 24. Be8 Ke7 25. Bb5 a6 26. Bd3 Kd6 27. Rf1 Ke7 28. Rc1 Kd6 {½-½ Volke,K-Farago,I/Budapest 2004/CBM 100 ext (28)})) 15... Rd7 $146 {Stohl,Igor: ''N''} ({Stohl,Igor: 'Black could have saved tempi with'} 15... Rd6 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' '!?' '!?' Stohl,I: '!?'} 16. Rac1 Kd8 17. Ke3 Rb8 18. g4 h6 19. h4 Bd7 20. g5 Ra6 21. gxh6 gxh6 22. R1c2 Rc8 23. Rxc8+ Bxc8 24. Kf4 Ra5 25. e4 f6 26. e5 fxe5+ 27. dxe5 Ra4+ 28. Kg3 b6 $11 {Vaganian,R-Bauer,C/Bundesliga 9798/1998/}) 16. a4 (16. Rac1 Kd8 17. Be4 $1 h6 {, for example,} (17... e5 18. e3 $1 {with the idea Bf5}) 18. f4 $5 (18. a4 Rb8 19. a5) 18... Rb8 19. e3 b6 20. f5 e5 21. f6 $5 g6 22. Bg2 $1 Rd6 23. Rc6 Rxc6 24. Bxc6) 16... Rb8 17. a5 Kd8 {Stohl,Igor: '!' '!' '!' Stohl,I: '!'} 18. Rac1 $14 {Stohl,Igor: ''''} Rd6 19. f4 $5 {Here} (19. Ke3 {also stops ...e5 ideas and looks more promising, e.g., .} Ra6 20. Rc5 b5 21. axb6 Raxb6 22. Be4) 19... Ra6 20. Rc5 b6 21. axb6 (21. Rc7 Bd7 22. axb6 axb6 $11 {[%cal Yb8c8] c8}) 21... axb6 22. Rh5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Just as in the previous examples, White can't achieve progress solely on the , so he tries to create some targets on the other flank. However, Polgar remains alert.'} h6 23. g4 Ra2+ 24. Ke3 (24. Rc2 Rxc2+ 25. Kxc2 Ba6 $132) 24... Bb7 {Stohl,Igor: '=' '=' '=' Stohl,I: '=' Stohl,Igor: 'Forces the exchange of  and finally achieves equality.'} 25. Bxb7 Rxb7 {Black has equalised, but things are still interesting.} 26. g5 hxg5 27. fxg5 g6 28. Rh8+ Ke7 29. Rcc8 Rd7 $6 (29... Ra5) 30. Rhe8+ (30. Rb8 $1) 30... Kd6 31. Rb8 Rb2 32. Kd3 Rb3+ 33. Ke4 (33. Kc4 Rb2 34. e4 Rc7+ $11) 33... Rb2 34. e3 Kc6 (34... Rxh2 $6 35. Rxb6+ Kc7 36. Reb8 $36 {needlessly gives White extra chances.}) 35. h4 Rh2 36. Rec8+ Kb5 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' '!?' '!?' Stohl,I: '!?'} (36... Rc7 37. Rxc7+ Kxc7 38. Rf8 (38. Rh8 Kd6 $11) 38... Rxh4+ 39. Ke5 Rh7 40. e4 $44) 37. Rd8 $2 (37. Rh8 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' '!?' '!?' Stohl,I: '!?' Stohl,Igor: 'keeps more tension in the position, although a draw is the most likely result after'} Rh3) 37... Rxh4+ 38. Ke5 Rxd8 39. Rxd8 Kc4 $2 {Stohl,Igor: 'Now Black's  will be passive and the defence requires some accuracy.'} (39... Rh2 $1) (39... Rh3 $142 $5 40. e4 Kc6 $11) 40. Kf6 $1 Rh7 (40... Re4 41. Kxf7 Rxe3 42. Kxg6 $16) 41. e4 b5 42. d5 exd5 43. exd5 Kc5 (43... b4) 44. d6 Kc6 45. Ke7 (45. d7 Kc7 {Stohl,Igor: '?' '?' '?' Stohl,I: '?'} (45... Rh1 $1 46. Kxf7 (46. Rb8 Kxd7 47. Rxb5 Rf1+) 46... Rd1 47. Ke6 Re1+ 48. Kf6 Rd1 49. Kxg6 Rxd7 $11) 46. Rb8 Kxd7 47. Rxb5 $16 {/+- Black's  remains passive and this gives White winning chances.}) 45... Kd5 {Stohl,Igor: '!' '!' '!' Stohl,I: '!'} (45... f5+ $2 46. Ke6 $18) 46. Kf6 (46. d7 f5+ 47. Kf6 Kd6 48. Kxg6 Rxd7 $11) 46... Kc6 47. Ke5 b4 48. Rc8+ Kd7 49. Rc7+ Kd8 50. Kd5 (50. Rb7 $4 f6+ $19) 50... Rh2 $1 {Stohl,Igor: 'Once again activating the  saves the day.'} (50... b3 51. Rb7 Rh3 $11) 51. Rxf7 b3 52. Rb7 b2 53. Ke6 Re2+ 54. Kf6 Rd2 55. d7 Rd6+ {Stohl,Igor: '!' '!' '!' Stohl,I: '!'} (55... Rxd7 $2 56. Rb8+ $1 Kc7 57. Rxb2 Rd6+ 58. Kf7 $18) 56. Ke5 {Stohl,Igor: '' '' '' Stohl,I: ''} Rxd7 57. Rxb2 Ke7 58. Rb6 {½-½ (58) Van Wely,L (2679)-Polgar,J (2728) Wijk aan Zee NED 2005 [Watson,J]} (58. Rh2 Rd1 59. Rh7+ Kf8 $11) 58... Kf7 $11 *

