{Inf 93/(251)} 1. e4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net) Huzman: 'Huzman,Alexander Huzman,Alexander: 'Huzman Huzman''} e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. c4 Nb4 9. Be2 O-O 10. a3 Nc6 11. cxd5 Qxd5 12. Nc3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Bf5 14. Re1 Rfe8 15. Bf4 Rac8 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net): ' The main opening tabia in Sofia. After two painful losses Kramnik repeats the line once again. In an interview to the Bulgarian TV he said that he did not want to finish using the opening, which served him well for years, with two losses. His aim was to avoid defeat in the his last Black game in Sofia and then, after returning home, to decide what to do with the Petroff in the future. At the press conference, I asked Vladimir what he would do if, hypothetically, the tournament would start again: to play the Sveshnikov or to stay with Petroff. Kramnik answered that of course he would playthe Sveshnikov - he can not look anymore at these Petroff positions! Below I will recall all previous Kramnik's games, played in the position after 15...ac8.  '} 16. Bd3 {*} (16. Bg3 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Bf6 ({Note that} 16... Bd6 {was tried in another game of the 9th round: Adams-Anand. Please find it in today's game file!}) 17. Nd2 Qa5 18. Qc1 Bxd4 19. cxd4 Nxd4 20. Bc4 Nc2 21. Rxe8+ Rxe8 22. Rb1 Re1+ 23. Qxe1 Nxe1 24. Rxe1 Kf8 25. Nf3 f6 26. Rd1 Qc5 27. Bf1 Ke8 28. Nd4 Bd7 29. Rd3 a5 30. h3 b5 31. Nb3 Qxa3 32. Bxc7 a4 33. Bd6 Qb2 34. Nc5 a3 35. Re3+ Kf7 36. Nd3 Qb1 37. Bxa3 Be6 38. Nf4 b4 39. Bxb4 Qxb4 40. Nxe6 g6 41. g3 {1-0 Adams-Kramnik, Sofia 2005}) (16. Qc1 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Na5 17. c4 Qe4 18. Bd1 Qd3 19. Re3 Qxc4 20. Re5 {1-0 Anand-Kramnik, Sofia 2005}) (16. c4 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Qe4 17. Be3 Qc2 18. d5 Na5 19. Nd4 Qxd1 20. Rexd1 Bd7 21. Bd2 Bf6 22. Bxa5 Bxd4 23. Rxd4 Rxe2 {½-½ Leko-Kramnik, Brissago 2004 (3)}) (16. h3 {Mikhail Golubev (www.chesstoday.net)} Be4 (16... Bf6 17. Nh2 Qa5 18. Bd2 Rc