1. e4 {Polgar,Judit Ftacnik,L TonyK} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 7. g4 {TonyK: 'A sharp alternative to'} (7. f3 {(TonyK) , but very much in keeping with Judit's daring style.}) 7... e5 {TonyK: '!?' TonyK: 'Moving the e-pawn a second time, but for purely tactical reasons: White must sacrifice a piece, or lose her g-pawn.'} (7... h6 {(TonyK) is a safer choice, especially against a great attacking player like Shirov, or Polgar.}) 8. Nf5 g6 9. g5 {TonyK: 'The dangerous Perenyi piece sac.'} gxf5 10. exf5 d5 11. Qf3 d4 12. O-O-O Nbd7 13. Bxd4 {TonyK: '!?' TonyK: 'An unusual try. We have only considered'} (13. Bd2 {(TonyK) before, Polgar,Judit: 'Ü 93/181'} dxc3 14. Bxc3 Bg7 15. Rg1 {which was analysed in Polgar,J-Anand,V/Dos Hermanas 1999 - see the archives.}) (13. Rxd4 $6 {TonyK} exd4 14. Bxd4 {is doubtful after} Bg7 15. Bd3 O-O) (13. gxf6 {(TonyK) was once the main move,} dxc3 14. Bc4 Qxf6 15. Rhg1 h6 {but Black went on to win in Khalifman,A-Gelfand,B/Shenyang 2000.}) 13... exd4 14. Rxd4 (14. Bc4 Qc7 15. Rhe1+ Kd8 16. Rxd4 Bc5 17. Rd3 Re8 18. Red1 Nh5 19. Bxf7 Qf4+ 20. Kb1 Qxf3 21. Rxf3 {1-0 Farago,S-Habibi,A/Budapest 1994/ (58)}) 14... Bg7 $146 {[%mdl 1024] TonyK: '!?' TonyK: 'The FIDE World Champ produces a new move.' Modern chess has many different faces. This irrational position features attampt to compensate the absence of two light pieces with activity and three pawns.} (14... Bc5 {TonyK: 'has been the previous choice, gaining a tempo on the rook,' Polgar,Judit: '!?'} 15. Rd2 {Polgar,Judit: ''} Qc7 (15... O-O {TonyK: '?' TonyK: 'is a mistake,'} 16. gxf6 Qxf6 (16... Kh8 {TonyK} 17. Ne4 {is also unpleasant for Black}) 17. Rg1+ Kh8 18. Ne4 Qe7 {TonyK: '?!' TonyK: 'Firt,S-Sobek,J/Czechia 1996, and now'} 19. f6 (19. Qg4 {TonyK} f6 20. Bc4 {is the most incisive,} h6 {(the only sensible way to stop Qg8+)} 21. Qf4 Qh7 22. Rg6 {which is devastating}) 19... Nxf6 20. Nxc5 Bg4 21. Qe3 Rae8 22. Qxe7 Rxe7 23. Bg2 {1-0 Firt,S-Sobek,J/Czechia 1996/ (54)}) 16. gxf6 Nxf6 17. Bc4 Be7 18. Bb3 O-O 19. Re1 Kh8 {TonyK: 'Pulkkinen,K-Oll,L/Helsinki 1990, when'} 20. Qe3 (20. Rd4 {(TonyK) looks strongest, using the rook along the 4th rank. Still, Judit may well have prepared another idea at home.}) 20... Bd8 21. Qd4 Bxf5 {0-1 Pulkkinen,K-Oll,L/Helsinki 1990/ (28)}) 15. Rg1 {TonyK: 'Simply dissuading Black from castling.'} Kf8 {TonyK: '!' TonyK: 'The king was too exposed on the open e-file, and'} (15... O-O {TonyK: '?' Polgar,Judit: '?'} 16. gxf6 Qxf6 17. Rdg4 $18 {TonyK: 'is crushing.'}) (15... Rg8 $5 {Polgar,Judit} 16. Qe3+ $5 (16. Bc4 $5)) 16. Qe3 (16. Bc4 $5 {TonyK}) 16... Qe7 {TonyK: 'Black is eager to exchange queens.'} (16... b6 17. Bg2 Rb8 18. Re1 $36) (16... Rg8 $5 {Polgar,Judit} 17. Bc4 (17. gxf6 Bxf6 18. Rxg8+ (18. Qh6+ Bg7 19. Qd6+ Qe7 $19) 18... Kxg8 19. Re4 (19. Rg4+ Bg7 20. Nd5 Kh8 21. Bg2 Nf6 22. Rxg7 Kxg7 23. Qg5+ Kh8 24. Nxf6 Qf8 25. Be4) 19... Kf8 20. Nd5 $40)) 17. Qd2 {TonyK: '!' TonyK: 'On principle it seems right to play for the attack.'} (17. Qxe7+ {TonyK: 'is tempting, but'} Kxe7 18. gxf6+ Bxf6 (18... Kxf6 {Polgar,Judit} 19. f4 $1) 19. Nd5+ Ke8 {TonyK: '!' Polgar,Judit: '!'} (19... Kf8 $2 {TonyK} 20. Nxf6 Nxf6 21. Rd8+ Ne8 22. f6 {leaves Black completely tied up,} b6 23. Bc4 {etc.}) 20. Re4+ (20. Nc7+ {TonyK} Kd8 21. Rxd7+ Bxd7 22. Nxa8 {and although White has two extra pawns, the f5 one will soon drop, the knight is misplaced, and Black has the bishop pair.} Bd4 {Polgar,Judit}) (20. Nxf6+ {TonyK} Nxf6 21. Bc4 Bd7 22. Re1+ Kf8 {is fine for Black}) 20... Kd8 21. Bh3 $13) 17... h6 {TonyK: '!?' TonyK: 'Black wants to provoke the capture on f6, but' Polgar,Judit: '?' Kasimdzhanov forces a crises with his pawn move, Polgar is forced to finally consumate one piece.} (17... Rg8 $5 {TonyK: 'might be superior, the idea is that after' Polgar,Judit: '!'} 18. Bg2 (18. gxf6 {TonyK} Qxf6 {is possible, as} 19. Rxd7 $2 Bxd7 20. Qxd7 $2 {loses to} Bh6+ {and the rook is hanging on g1.}) (18. Rg3 $1 {TonyK Polgar,Judit: ''}) 18... Qd8 $13) 18. gxf6 Nxf6 {TonyK: '?' TonyK: 'Perhaps it is a good time to return some more material.'} (18... Bxf6 {Polgar,Judit: '?!'} 19. Nd5 Qe5 20. Nxf6 Qxf6 21. Rg3 $18 {Polgar,Judit: ''}) (18... Qxf6 19. Rd6 (19. Rxd7 $6 Bxd7 20. Qxd7 Qd4 (20... Qc6 {(TonyK) is safer.}) 21. Qxd4 Bxd4 22. Ne4 Re8 23. Bd3 Rg8 $15) 19... Qe7 (19... Qxf5 $2 20. Rd3 $1 (20. Nd5 $40 {Polgar,Judit}) 20... Qe6 21. Bh3 f5 22. Rf3 $18) 20. Rg3 $40) 19. Rd8+ Ne8 20. Bb5 {TonyK: '!' TonyK: 'White sacrifices the bishop for a tempo!' Polgar,Judit: '!!'} (20. Bh3 Bf6 21. Rd3 Qb4 $13) (20. Rxe8+ $2 {Polgar,Judit} Kxe8 (20... Qxe8 $2 21. Nd5) 21. Rxg7 (21. Nd5 Qc5 22. Rxg7 Bxf5) 21... Bxf5 22. Bc4 Bg6 23. Nd5 Qg5) 20... axb5 (20... Bxf5 {Polgar,Judit} 21. Rxa8 axb5 22. Nd5 Qd6 23. Rxe8+ Kxe8 24. Rxg7 $18) 21. Re1 b4 {TonyK: '?!' TonyK: 'This should lose immediately, but the alternatives were not too thrilling either:' Polgar,Judit: '?'} (21... Bf6 22. Rxe7 (22. Rxe8+ $1 {TonyK} Qxe8 (22... Kxe8 23. Nd5 $1 Qxe1+ 24. Qxe1+ Kf8 25. Nxf6) 23. Rxe8+ Kxe8 {Black has a material advantage, but the queen and knight co-operate well in such situations:} 24. Ne4 {Polgar,Judit: '!'} (24. Nd5 {Polgar,Judit} Bd8 25. Qe3+ Kf8) 24... Bd8 (24... Be7 25. f6 Rg8 {Polgar,Judit} 26. Nd6+) 25. Nd6+ Kf8 (25... Ke7 {Polgar,Judit} 26. Qb4 Rg8 27. Nxc8+ Kd7 28. Na7 $3 $18) 26. Qd5 Bg5+ (26... Rh7 {Polgar,Judit} 27. Qxb5 Bg5+) 27. Kb1 Rh7 28. Qxb5 {and the threat of Qe8+ is decisive.}) 22... Bxe7 23. Qd4 f6 24. Nxb5 Bxd8 25. Qxd8 Kf7 $16) (21... Be5 {TonyK: 'blocks the e-file, but'} 22. f4 f6 (22... Bxf4 23. Rxe8+ $18) (22... b4 {TonyK} 23. Rxe5 bxc3 24. Qd4 f6 25. Rxe7 cxb2+ 26. Kxb2 Kxe7 27. Rd5 $1 {and the black king is too exposed, Qc5+ will win.}) (22... Bf6 {(TonyK) is like the next variation,} 23. Rxe8+ $1 Qxe8 (23... Kxe8 {Polgar,Judit} 24. Nd5) 24. Rxe8+ Kxe8 25. Ne4 (25. Nd5 {Polgar,Judit} Bd8 26. Qe3+ Kf8 27. f6 Bg4 28. Qg1 $18) 25... Be7 26. f6 Bd8 (26... Rg8 {Polgar,Judit} 27. Nd6+ $1 Kd7 28. Nxc8+ Kxc8 29. Qc3+ Kd7 30. fxe7 $18) 27. Nd6+ Kf8 28. Qd5 Be6 29. Qxb7 Rxa2 30. Qb8 {winning}) (22... Bd6 {Polgar,Judit} 23. Rxd6 $1 Qxe1+ 24. Qxe1 Nxd6 25. Qe5 $18) 23. fxe5 fxe5 24. Qd5 $18) (21... Bf6 22. Rxe7 Bxe7 23. Qd4 Bxd8 24. Qxh8+ Ke7 25. Qxh6 $18) 22. Nb5 $2 {TonyK: 'A staggering blunder!' Polgar,Judit: '??'} (22. Rxe8+ $1 {TonyK: 'is perhaps the simplest:'} Kxe8 (22... Qxe8 {TonyK: '?'} 23. Qd6+ $18) 23. Rxe7+ (23. Nd5 {TonyK} Qxe1+ 24. Qxe1+ Kf8 25. Nc7 {winning the rook, as Qe8 mate is the menace.}) 23... Kxe7 24. Nd5+ $18 {TonyK: 'is also convincing'} Kf8 {Polgar,Judit} 25. Nb6 Bf6 26. Nxa8 $18) (22. Rxe7 {(TonyK) is the move most weak players would find without any problem!} Kxe7 23. Ne4 Be5 24. f6+ Nxf6 25. Rxh8 Nxe4 26. Qxb4+ Nd6 27. Qe1 f6 28. f4 {gaining a material advantage.}) 22... Bxb2+ $2 {TonyK: 'Returning the compliment!' In sharp positions it is very typical that both players will ignore some more or less hidden tactical possibilty. The exworld champion could have played much better with 22...e5! winning time for inclusion of his rook via a2.} (22... Be5 $1 {TonyK: 'is stronger now, as' Polgar,Judit: '-+!'} 23. f4 $140 {TonyK: '??' TonyK: 'loses on the spot to'} (23. Kb1 {(TonyK) is obviously better, but} Kg7 24. Rd5 (24. f4 {Polgar,Judit} Bxf4) 24... Qg5 $1 {swaps queens}) (23. Qd5 {Polgar,Judit} Bf4+ 24. Kd1 Be6) 23... Rxa2 {TonyK: 'with a nasty threat of mate on a1,'} 24. Kb1 Rxb2+ 25. Kc1 Bxf5 {TonyK: 'and amazingly it is Black who has the winning attack!!'} 26. Rxe5 Rxc2+ 27. Qxc2 Bxc2 28. Rxe8+ Qxe8 29. Rxe8+ Kxe8 30. Kxc2 Kd7 $19) 23. Kxb2 (23. Kb1 $2 {Polgar,Judit} b3) 23... Qf6+ 24. Qd4 {TonyK: '!' TonyK: 'Did Black miss this move? White can play the endgame because she is sure to win the knight on e8.'} Kg7 (24... Qxd4+ 25. Nxd4 Kg7 26. Rexe8 $18) 25. Rexe8 Rxe8 26. Rxe8 {TonyK: 'Threatening Rg8+!.'} Qxd4+ 27. Nxd4 {The otcoume of the risky undertaking is very encouraging for white. Polgar enjoyes an extra pawn and terrible pin along the 8th rank.} Kf6 (27... b6 28. Kb3 Ra3+ 29. Kxb4 Ra4+ 30. Kxa4 Bd7+ 31. Kb4 Bxe8 32. c4 Kf6 33. f4 Bd7 34. a4 $18) 28. f4 {TonyK: 'White only has one extra doubled pawn for the moment, but the awkward pin on the bishop means she wins easily.'} b6 29. Rd8 Bb7 {TonyK: 'Black has to activate the two pieces.'} (29... Ke7 {TonyK} 30. Rh8 (30. Nc6+ {Polgar,Judit} Kf6 31. Nxb4) 30... Bb7 31. Rxa8 {is similar.}) 30. Rxa8 (30. Rd6+ Kg7 31. Rxb6 Bd5 32. Rb5 {TonyK: '!'} Bxa2 33. Rxb4 $18 {TonyK: 'should also win.'}) 30... Bxa8 31. Kb3 {TonyK: '!'} Bd5+ 32. Kxb4 Bxa2 33. Kb5 Bb1 34. c3 $1 {Excellent and very logical move, lady is saving the c pawn for later advance towards the promotion square.} (34. Kxb6 $2 {TonyK: 'is careless,'} Bxc2 35. Nxc2 Kxf5 $132 36. Kc5 {TonyK} Kxf4 37. Kd4 Kf3 {and draws.}) 34... Ke7 (34... Bxf5 35. Nxf5 Kxf5 36. Kxb6 Kxf4 37. c4 f5 38. c5 $18) 35. Kxb6 Kd6 36. c4 Bd3 37. c5+ {TonyK: 'The c-pawn is too strong.'} Kd5 38. Nc6 $1 Ke4 (38... Bxf5 39. Ne7+ $18) 39. Ne7 Bc2 (39... Kxf4 40. c6 $18) 40. c6 Ba4 41. c7 Bd7 42. Kc5 {TonyK: '!' TonyK: 'A nice touch! White plans Kd6 when the bishop has no squares.'} (42. Kc5 Kxf4 43. Kd6 $18) 1-0

