1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4 5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. bxc3 Ne4 8. Qg4 Kf8 9. Bd3 Nxd2 10. Kxd2 c5 11. h4 Nc6 12. Nf3 {The other alterative is 12.h3 keeping an option to develop the knight on e2 or h3.} (12. Rh3 c4 13. Be2 (13. Bf1 $5 {[%cal Gg1e2]} Bd7 14. Ne2 b5 15. a3 a5 16. Nf4 Ne7 17. Nh5 Nf5 18. Qf4 $16 {Golubev,M-Hess,C/Chemnitz op 1998}) 13... b5 14. a3 Bd7 15. Rf3 a5 16. Nh3 Ne7 17. Qf4 Be8 18. h5 b4 19. axb4 axb4 20. Rxa8 bxc3+ $13 {Arnold,L-Almasi,I/Budapest FS08 GM 1997}) 12... c4 $5 {The plans of both sides are perfectly clear:white intends to create something on the kingside,while black is playing on the queenside,trying to brea white's position with b4.} 13. Be2 b5 14. a3 Bd7 (14... a5 15. Qf4 (15. h5 Rb8 16. Rhb1 Qe7 17. Qf4 (17. Qh4 $5 Ba6 18. g4 Qxh4 19. Nxh4 Rb6 20. f4 Ne7 21. Rf1 $14 {[%cal Gf4f5] Hegedus,I-Bock,J/Budapest FS07 Elo 1998}) 17... b4 18. axb4 axb4 19. cxb4 Rxb4 20. Qe3 Ke8 21. Qc3 Rxb1 $11 {Cvicela,A-Sladecek,J/SVK-chT2 1995}) 15... Qe7 (15... Rb8 16. Rhb1 Qe7 17. g4 Bd7 18. g5 b4 19. axb4 axb4 20. cxb4 Rxb4 21. c3 $14 {Heissler,J-Reefschlaeger,H/BL9596}) 16. h5 Ra7 17. g4 b4 18. Qe3 bxa3 19. Ra2 a4 20. Rha1 Bd7 $132 {Polgar,J-Lautier,J/Madrid 1993}) 15. Qf4 $5 (15. h5 a5 16. Qf4 b4 17. g4 bxc3+ 18. Ke3 Be8 19. Rag1 Ke7 20. g5 $13 {Horvath,A-Almasi,I/Budapest FS12 GM 1997}) 15... Ke7 $5 {[%cal Gd7e8,Ge7c7]} 16. h5 (16. Qg3 Rg8 17. h5 Be8 18. Rh4 a5 19. Rg4 Kf8 20. Nh4 $14 {[%cal Gf2f5]}) 16... Be8 17. Nh4 (17. Rh4 $5) 17... a5 18. Qg3 Rg8 19. Qe3 Bd7 20. f4 b4 21. Rhb1 $5 ({Better was} 21. axb4 axb4 22. cxb4 Nxb4 23. Qc3 Nc6 24. Rxa8 Qxa8 25. Ra1 Qb7 26. Qa3+ Qb4+ 27. Qxb4+ Nxb4 28. c3 $14 {with slightly better prospects in the endgame.}) 21... bxc3+ 22. Qxc3 Qc7 23. g4 Rgb8 24. f5 $5 {Interesting decision:white sacrifices a pawn,but significiently improves position of the pieces.} Qa7 {[%csl Rd4]} 25. f6+ (25. Ke3 Nxe5 $17) (25. Nf3 exf5 26. gxf5 Bxf5 $17) 25... gxf6 26. exf6+ Kxf6 27. Rf1+ Ke7 28. Rf4 {[%cal Ga1f1,Gh4f3,Gg4g5]} a4 29. Bxc4 $2 ({After} 29. Raf1 $5 Be8 30. Nf3 Qa5 31. Qxa5 Rxa5 32. g5 hxg5 33. Nxg5 $132 {white has more than sufficient compensation for the pawn.}) 29... dxc4 30. d5 {[%cal Gc3f6,Ga1e1]} Qa5 $6 (30... exd5 $4 31. Qf6+ Ke8 32. Re1+ Be6 33. Rxe6+ fxe6 34. Qf8+ Kd7 35. Rf7+ $18) ({Very serious attention deserved} 30... e5 $5 31. Re1 Be6 $1 32. dxe6 (32. Nf5+ $2 Bxf5 33. Rxf5 Qd4+ 34. Qxd4 Nxd4 $19) 32... exf4 33. Nf5+ Kd8 34. Qf6+ Kc8 35. exf7 Qd7+ 36. Kc1 Kb7 $15 {with extremly complicated position,in which black is a rook up.}) 31. dxc6 Bxc6 32. Qxa5 Rxa5 33. Re1 Bd5 34. Kc3 $5 {Now the position is equal and the draw agreement is not far away.} (34. Nf5+ Kf6 35. Ne3+ Kg7 36. Nxc4 $11) 34... Kf8 35. Ref1 Rb7 36. Ng6+ Kg8 37. Ne5 Bg2 38. Re1 (38. Nxc4 $6 Rc5 39. Re1 Bd5 40. Kd3 Rbc7 $15) 38... Rc7 39. Kb4 Ra8 40. Rxc4 Rb8+ 41. Kc3 (41. Kxa4 $4 Ra7#) 41... Rxc4+ 42. Nxc4 Kg7 43. Ne3 Bc6 44. Rf1 Rb5 45. Rf4 Re5 46. Kd3 Ra5 47. Kd2 (47. c4 Kf8 48. Kc3 Ke7 49. Rf2 Re5 $132) 47... Rc5 48. Kd3 1/2-1/2

