{[%mdl 2080]} 1. e4 {Ziegler Ari Bangiev. McShane.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 (5. Nce2 {Ziegler Ari} c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Nf3 {Gives us a position which we ahve explored earlier.}) (5. Nf3 {Ziegler Ari} c5 6. dxc5 Nc6 7. Bf4 Bxc5 8. Bd3 f6 {Gives Black an easy game.}) 5... c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 ({According to our concept Black should try ...f6 somewhere here. It has never been tried though. Let's look at it.} 6... f6 {Ziegler Ari} 7. exf6 ({Of course,} 7. f5 {must also be analysed:} cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nxe5 9. Nxe6 Bxe6 10. fxe6 {Black will probably win the e6-pawn, but White will not be without compensation.}) 7... Nxf6 (7... Qxf6 {White could now try to attack Black right away:} 8. Qe2 {threatens to take on d5.} (8. Be3 Bd6 (8... cxd4 9. Qxd4 Bc5 10. Qxf6 gxf6 11. Bxc5 Nxc5 12. g3 {White has a slightly better position.}) 9. dxc5 Bxf4 10. Bxf4 Qxf4 11. Qe2 Nf6 (11... Nxc5 12. Nxd5) 12. g3 Qb4 {Black seems to cope.}) 8... Be7 9. Nb5 Na6 10. Ng5 Nb6 {Men det ser inte ut att ge så mycket.}) 8. Be3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nc6 10. Qd2 Bd6 11. O-O-O O-O 12. g3 Bd7 13. Bh3 {gives White a tiny edge.}) 7. Be3 {Ziegler Ari: 'Luc Winants calls this "Boleslavsky's variation".'} cxd4 ({The other main line is:} 7... a6 {Ziegler Ari}) (7... f6 $6 {Ziegler Ari} 8. exf6 Qxf6 $6 9. Nb5 Rb8 10. Ng5 {And, actually, Black can resign!}) 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O {'!' Bangiev.} 10. O-O-O {Ziegler Ari: 'Black would like to play f6, but right now it is not playable.'} a6 11. h4 (11. Nb3 {Ziegler Ari}) 11... Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 (12... f6 {Ziegler Ari} 13. exf6 Bxd4 14. Qxd4 Qxf6 {Actually, Black has too few pieces left to be able to enjoy the position.}) 13. Rh3 {McShane: 'This looks a little slow. Pushing on with 13.h5 or 13.g4 may be better.'} (13. h5 {(Ziegler Ari) To me h5 looks better, but all the stars prefers the textmove.} b4 14. Na4 Bxd4 15. Qxd4 Qa5 16. b3 Bb7 17. f5 Bc6 {This looks very good for Black, White has to find improvements earlier.}) 13... b4 14. Ne2 {Bangiev: 'Ein neuer Zug in dieser Stellung. Der weiße Plan ist allerdings nicht neu. Die Überführung des Springer nach d4 ist für Weiß in der französischen Verteidigung eine Herausforderung.'} (14. Na4 Bxd4 15. Qxd4 Qa5 {Ziegler Ari: ''} (15... f6 16. Qxb4 $5 (16. exf6 {Ziegler Ari} Qxf6 17. Qxf6 Rxf6 18. Rf3 Rb8 (18... Bb7 {This could give some problems with the bishop after:} 19. Rd4 a5 {Black threatens Bc6,} 20. Bb5 e5 21. fxe5 Rxf3 22. gxf3 Nxe5 23. Nc5 {White has a clear advantage.}) 19. Rd4 {The only problem is that Balck's position is little bit boring though.}) 16... fxe5 17. Qd6 Qf6 18. f5 $5 Qh6+ 19. Kb1 Rxf5 20. Rf3 $1 $36 {Kasparov,G-Short,N/Amsterdam VSB 1994}) (15... a5 16. b3 $6 {'!?' Bangiev.} (16. Bb5 Rb8 (16... Ba6 $2 17. Bxd7) 17. Bd3 (17. Bxd7 Bxd7 18. Nc5 Bb5 19. f5 Qc7 $16 {fc8}) 17... Qc7 (17... f6 $5) 18. Re1 {'!?' Bangiev. Bangiev: 'f5'} (18. h5 Bb7 19. g4 Rfc8 20. Rh2 Qc6 21. b3 Ba6 $132 {Fogarasi,T-Luther,T/Kecskemet 1993}) 18... Qc6 19. b3 Ba6 20. Bxa6 Qxa6 21. Rg3 (21. f5 exf5 22. Qxd5 Nb6 $13) 21... Rbc8 (21... Qc6 22. f5) 22. f5 Qc6 (22... exf5 $2 23. e6) 23. Re2 $14 {Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev izt-W 1995} (23. Re2 {Bangiev} Kh8 $132 {1-0 Arakhamia,K-Matveeva,S/Kishinev 1995/(61)})) 16... Ba6 17. Kb1 Bxf1 18. Rxf1 Qc7 19. h5 Ra6 20. Re3 Rc6 21. Rf2 f6 {Bangiev: '0-1 Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov 1995/(39)'} 22. Ree2 h6 $15 {Kulish,I-Matveeva,S/Rostov on Don-W 1995}) 16. b3 Bb7 17. Rg3 $5 (17. c3 Rfc8 18. Kb2 (18. Kb1 bxc3 19. Rxc3 Rxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3 (20... Qd8 $1 {Ziegler Ari} 21. g3 Rc8 22. Qd3 Qc7 {The position is difficult to assess. Black can try ...f6 or slowly activate the bishop after the exchange of knights. Black is howeve in no danger at all.}) 21. Nxc3 Rc8 22. Kb2 $14 {Herrera,I-Ramas,L/CUB-ch 1993 Ziegler Ari: 'White has an microscopic advantage. Herrea-Ramas,1993.'}) 18... bxc3+ 19. Rxc3 Rxc3 20. Qxc3 Qxc3+ (20... Qd8 $1 {Ziegler Ari} 21. g3 Rc8 22. Qd3 Qc7 {This is also sligthly advantageous for Black.}) 21. Nxc3 Kf8 22. a4 Ke7 23. a5 $14 {Nijboer,F-Luther,T/Leeuwarden op 1992}) (17. Kb1 {(Ziegler Ari) Now we end up in a position between Ernst and Brynel from the Swedish Championship 1988. Here the players agrees on a draw. Quite amazing, if Black don't play on in posititions like this I wonder why Black should play this at all. Black should be slightly better, but let us take a look at the position:} f6 18. Bd3 Bc6 19. Nb2 Qc5 (19... Bb5 $6 {this don't look very constructive:} 20. exf6 Rxf6 21. Re3 {And Black is at the end of the road. To exchange the bishop on d3 only helps white to activate the knight.}) 20. Qxc5 Nxc5 21. c3 fxe5 (21... bxc3 22. Bxh7+ Kxh7 23. Rxc3 {Is slightly better for White.} Ne4 24. Rxc6) 22. fxe5 a5 23. cxb4 axb4 24. Rc1 Ra5 $5 {and now Black threatens both to double on the a-file and to play ...d4 and activate the bishop. White should be able to cope but he has to be careful!}) 17... Rfc8 (17... Bc6 $1 {Ziegler Ari} 18. f5 Bxa4 19. f6 ({Terror does not work either:} 19. Qg4 g6 20. fxe6 Nxe5 21. Qf5 d4 $1) 19... g6 20. bxa4 Qxa4 21. Kb1 Rfc8 22. Bd3 Nc5 {and Black wins.}) 18. Rd2 Bc6 19. f5 Qc7 (19... Bxa4 {Ziegler Ari}) 20. f6 g6 21. Qf4 $5 $40 {Arakhamia,K-Gurevich,M/Helsinki op 1992 Ziegler Ari: 'Arakhamia-Gurevich, Helsingfors Open 1992.'} Kh8 {Ziegler Ari}) (14. Na4 {(McShane) may be better, as Shirov's attack came very fast. The problem is that the knight is prone to being attacked by a black bishop.}) 14... a5 15. Qe3 (15. Bxc5 {Bangiev Ziegler Ari: 'According to Bangiev this does not give White any advantage.'} Nxc5 16. Nd4 Ba6 17. Bxa6 Rxa6 18. Qe3 a4 $36 {Ziegler Ari: 'and Black's attack looks more dangerous than White's.'}) 15... Qc7 16. Bxc5 Nxc5 17. Nd4 a4 ({Ziegler Ari: 'Bangiev has suggested:'} 17... Ba6 {Bangiev} 18. Bxa6 Rxa6 19. h5 (19. f5 $6 Ne4) 19... a4 20. h6 g6 21. Kb1 {Ziegler Ari: 'With the slightly more favourable chances to Black.'}) 18. Kb1 $6 {Serious inaccuracy passing the initiative into black's hands.It was nessasary to prevent black from playing 18...a3.The only way to do it was to play 18.a3!: Ziegler Ari: 'White has a difficult choice here. How shall he/she react to be able to neutralise those two Kamikazepawns?'} (18. a3 $1 Nb3+ (18... b3 19. c3 {Ziegler Ari} Ba6 20. Bxa6 Rxa6 {looks pretty equal.}) (18... bxa3 19. Qxa3 Qb6 {Ziegler Ari: 'A tiny and innoucent looking queen move which ties up a lot of the energy of the opponent's. The queens is protecting the e6-square, and the bishop can go to a6 for an eventual exchange with the counterpart. The queens also pressurises against b2, which in practice keeps White's queen on the awkward square a3. The queeen also excerts sme pressure on the a7-g1-diagonal. Ths knight on d4 thereby needs protection from the rook on d1 which otherwise happily would perform other duties. On the same diagonal the queen can support an ambitious knight on it's way to f2.'} (19... Ba6 {Ziegler Ari: 'is little bit premature'} 20. Rc3 Rfc8 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 22. Nb5 Qb6 23. Nd6 Rc7 24. Kb1 $14) 20. h5 Ba6 {Ziegler Ari: 'is playable but then Black has to give up his f7-f6-ambitions.'} (20... h6 {Ziegler Ari} 21. g4 Ne4 22. Rf3 {It is slightly uneasy for White.} Ba6 23. Bxa6 Rxa6 24. f5 {only weakens the e5- and g5-squares.} (24. g5 hxg5 25. f5 Rb8) 24... Rb8 25. fxe6 fxe6 {and White will lose the e5-pawn.}) 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 22. h6 g6 23. g4 Ne4 24. Rf3 $13) 19. Kb1 $1 (19. Nxb3 axb3 20. Qxb3 bxa3 21. bxa3 Ba6 22. Qc3 Qb6 $36) 19... Nxd4 20. Qxd4 bxa3 21. Rxa3 Bd7 22. h5 h6 23. g4 $14 {Ziegler Ari: 'Here, Black's play is without push.'}) (18. h5 {Bangiev} b3 $1) 18... a3 $1 {[%csl Rc3] McShane: 'Shirov's attack is already creating concrete threats and Polgar's attack is barely off the ground.' Ziegler Ari: 'We have seen how dangerous it can be with a weakened c3-square.'} (18... b3 $2 {Bangiev} 19. cxb3 axb3 20. Nxb3) 19. b3 Ba6 {'!' McShane. McShane: 'The black bishop has no better role than to exchange off its counterpart.' '!' Bangiev. Ziegler Ari: 'Black is not in a hurry and takes his time fo this bishop sacrifice which is beneficial for him in the long run.'} 20. Bxa6 (20. Bd3) 20... Rxa6 $15 {Ziegler Ari: 'It might appear that the play is locked on the queen's wing, which is partly true. But Black still has play on the c-file at his disposal and especially the two squares c2 and c3. White's major problem is that his king faces an eternal mate threat which is difficult to do much about. White's attacking chances on the King's wing are as we will see controlable. The factor that decides the evaluation of the position is the the knights ability to move. The Black knight is far more flexible in both attack and defence than its counterpart.'} 21. Qe1 {Ziegler Ari: 'White forces Black to spend some time on the defence of the b-pawn.'} (21. h5 {Bangiev} Ne4 22. Rd3 Rc8 $40 {Ziegler Ari: 'And hereafter White will face a difficult task.'}) 21... Rb6 $1 {'' Bangiev. Ziegler Ari: 'But black is in no hurry!' Bangiev: 'Der weiße Eröffnungsplan ist gescheitert. Der Nachziehende steht deutlich besser.'} 22. c3 $6 {White tries to deprive black the control over the square c3,but opens the king,which proves to be crucial. McShane: 'Otherwise Black will play Ne4, threatening Nc3+ and, if need be, break with f6!' '?' Bangiev. Ziegler Ari: 'White does not dare to be cool. Black's next move would be Ne4 eventually followed by a jump into c3. Black can also consider playing ...f6 at any time in order to attack the centre.'} ({Ziegler Ari: 'Was it possible for White to ignore Balck's threats?'} 22. g4 Ne4 23. Rdd3 {Ziegler Ari: 'looks forced'} Rc8 (23... f6 {Ziegler Ari: 'is playable but it is better to put the rook on the c-file first.'} 24. exf6 Rxf6 25. Ne2 Qc5 26. Rhe3 $13 Rc6 {Ziegler Ari} 27. Nd4 Rc7 {Black has an easier position to handle.}) 24. h5 f6 $1 25. exf6 gxf6 $15 {Ziegler Ari: 'White is facing insoluble problems. the f4-pawn is hanging, e6-e5 followed by Qxc2 is in the air. The knight on e4 paralyses White's forces. Black has a winning position.'}) (22. Ka1 {Bangiev} Ne4 23. Rhd3 $15 {Ziegler Ari: 'This could be White's best try, but he is really withouf any play of his/her own.' Weiß steht schlechter, verfügt aber über recht feste Stellung.}) 22... Qb7 {'!' Bangiev.} 23. Rc1 Ne4 24. cxb4 Rxb4 25. Rd3 {McShane: 'The knight on e4 is White's main problem, as it stops him doubling his rooks on the c-file and posting his queen on a better square' Ziegler Ari: 'White would really love to double on the c-file, but the e4-knight...'} (25. Nc2 {Bangiev} Rc4 26. Nxa3 Rxc1+ (26... Ra4 $5) 27. Qxc1 Rc8 28. Qe1 (28. Nc2 d4 $19 { d3, a6}) 28... Qa7 29. Nc2 Qc5 $19) 25... Rc4 $1 $17 {Very strong move revealing white's problems. '!' McShane. McShane: 'Now Shirov attacks the c-file with his rooks and Judit's position crumbles.' '!' Bangiev. Ziegler Ari: 'The rook wasn't very effective on b4. Shirov propose to change it off instead. White's position is clearly worse, but that is not the end of the story. White is also deprived of any counterplay. It is extremely difficult to defend such a position when you face a motivated opponent.'} 26. Rxc4 {'?!' Bangiev.} (26. g4 Rfc8 27. Rc2 Rxc2 28. Nxc2 Qb5 29. Qe3 Nf2 $1 $17 {[%csl Rd3,Rg4]} 30. Rc3 {Ziegler Ari} Qf1+ 31. Ne1 Rxc3 32. Qxc3 h6 {And Black wins a pawn with speed.}) (26. Rdd1 {McShane} Rfc8 {threatening Nc3+} 27. Ka1 Qb6 $1 {is no better, as the d4 knight must now retreat} 28. Rxc4 {Ziegler Ari} Rxc4 29. Ne2 h6 {Some of White's survival chances is built on the fact that she might win a tempi by threatening mate. That is why ...h6 is a very practical move in this position.} 30. Kb1 Rc8 31. Rd3 (31. Rc1 Rxc1+ 32. Nxc1 Qd4 {A mighty queen.}) 31... Qb5 32. Rf3 Qc5 33. Qd1 {White controls all entrysquares on the c-file and all direct threats as well. Black needs to find something creative to cruck White.} h5 $1 {or a waiting move!} 34. g3 d4 35. Nc1 d3 $1 36. Rxd3 (36. Nxd3 Qc3) 36... Nc3+ 37. Rxc3 Qxc3 38. Nd3 Rd8) (26. g3 $142 {Bangiev Ziegler Ari: 'Doesn't really change anything. Black just plays'} Rfc8 {Ziegler Ari with similair play as above.}) 26... dxc4 {[%csl Rb1][%cal Gb7b1]} 27. Rd1 $6 ({It was possible to put up some rsistance after} 27. Re3 $5 {Ziegler Ari: 'Isn't much better:'} Nc5 28. Qe2 cxb3 (28... Nd3 {Ziegler Ari All Black has to do now is to chase the knight away from d4 and then b3 will crash and burn.}) 29. Nxb3 Qb4 30. g3 Rc8 $17) 27... Nc5 {'-+' Bangiev. Ziegler Ari: 'White can't protect b3 and g2 at the same time.'} 28. Qc3 (28. Qg3 $2 {Ziegler Ari} Qe4+ 29. Ka1 Rd8) 28... Qxg2 {McShane: 'The diagonal opens for the queen.'} 29. b4 (29. Qxc4 {McShane} Qb2#) 29... Nd3 $19 {Ziegler Ari: 'White is lost but she tries a little.'} 30. Qxa3 Ra8 {'!' McShane.} 31. Rxd3 cxd3 (31... Rxa3 32. Rxa3 Qg1+ 33. Kc2 Qf2+ 34. Kb1 Qe1+ 35. Kc2 Qe4+ $19 {Ziegler Ari: 'Also wins but Shirov likes the text better.'}) {McShane: But Shirov's move wipes out all counterchances...} 32. Qxd3 Qxa2+ 33. Kc1 Qa1+ 34. Kd2 Rd8 35. Ke3 Qe1+ {Ziegler Ari: 'Judith decided that enough is enough.'} 0-1

